DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"hypoglycin-a": {
 "origin": "Hypoglycin A is not a drug and has no approved US product of any kind. It is a non-protein amino acid made by trees of the soapberry family, above all ackee (Blighia sapida), a West African tree whose genus honors Captain William Bligh, who carried specimens to Kew in 1793. Ackee traveled to Jamaica in the 18th century and the ripe arils became the national dish with saltfish. A seasonal killer of children called the vomiting sickness of Jamaica was described through the 19th century, and Harold Scott's work around 1916 tied it to unripe ackee. In 1954 and 1955 Cedric Hassall and Karl Reyle, at the University College of the West Indies, isolated the responsible compounds and named them hypoglycin A and B. Kay Tanaka, Eric Kean and Barbara Johnson published the metabolic explanation in the New England Journal of Medicine in 1976. A chemical relative, methylenecyclopropylglycine (MCPG), sits in lychee seeds and unripe lychee fruit, and in 2017 it closed one of the great open questions in field epidemiology.",
 "timeline": [
  [
   "1793",
   "William Bligh carries ackee from Jamaica to Kew, and the genus Blighia is later named for him."
  ],
  [
   "1916",
   "Harold Scott links Jamaican vomiting sickness to unripe ackee fruit."
  ],
  [
   "1955",
   "Hassall and Reyle isolate hypoglycin A and B from ackee in Jamaica."
  ],
  [
   "1976",
   "Tanaka, Kean and Johnson describe the block in fatty acid oxidation in two poisoned patients."
  ],
  [
   "1995",
   "Yearly clusters of acute encephalopathy in children begin to be recorded in Muzaffarpur, Bihar, during the lychee harvest."
  ],
  [
   "2017",
   "A case-control study in Lancet Global Health identifies hypoglycin A and MCPG metabolites in the Muzaffarpur children."
  ]
 ],
 "cell": "Hypoglycin A is a protoxin. Mitochondria transaminate it and then run it through the branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase complex, which produces methylenecyclopropylacetyl-CoA (MCPA-CoA). The strained methylenecyclopropyl ring makes MCPA-CoA a mechanism-based, essentially irreversible inhibitor of the flavin acyl-CoA dehydrogenases: short-chain and medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase, isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase and glutaryl-CoA dehydrogenase. Beta-oxidation stops at its first dehydrogenation step. Three things follow. Acetyl-CoA is no longer generated, so ketogenesis fails and pyruvate carboxylase loses its allosteric activator. NADH and FADH2 from fat oxidation disappear, removing the reducing equivalents and ATP that gluconeogenesis runs on. And CoA and carnitine are trapped as MCPA conjugates, so acyl groups spill out as dicarboxylic acids and as MCPA-carnitine and MCPA-glycine in urine. The biochemical picture is an acquired multiple acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency. MCPG from lychee is handled the same way and yields MCPF-CoA, which hits the same enzymes.",
 "body": [
  [
   "Liver",
   "Fatty acid oxidation and gluconeogenesis both fail, and fat accumulates in hepatocytes with rising transaminases, a picture resembling Reye syndrome."
  ],
  [
   "Blood glucose",
   "Once hepatic glycogen is used up there is no second line, so glucose falls steeply, and ketones stay low or absent because acetyl-CoA is not being made."
  ],
  [
   "Brain",
   "The brain loses glucose and has no ketones to fall back on, producing drowsiness, seizures, encephalopathy and coma."
  ],
  [
   "Stomach and gut",
   "Early and often violent vomiting, which gave the illness its old name and also empties out anything left to absorb."
  ],
  [
   "Skeletal muscle",
   "Muscle depends heavily on fat oxidation between meals, so weakness and hypotonia are common and creatine kinase can rise."
  ],
  [
   "Kidney",
   "Clears the accumulated organic acids and conjugates, so urine organic acid testing carries the fingerprint of the poisoning."
  ]
 ],
 "journey": "Hypoglycin A is an amino acid and is absorbed readily from the gut, probably on neutral amino acid transporters, then distributes through body water. It does essentially nothing until mitochondria convert it to MCPA-CoA, which is why there is a latency of hours between the meal and the metabolic collapse. Content in ackee is highest in unripe arils and in the seeds, and falls steeply in the arils once the pod opens naturally on the tree. Elimination is renal, as MCPA-glycine and MCPA-carnitine conjugates plus medium-chain dicarboxylic acids. Because the enzyme inhibition is covalent, recovery waits on new enzyme protein rather than on clearance of the toxin, and low glucose can recur for many hours.",
 "sidefx": "Everything traces to one block. Vomiting comes first and is probably a direct gut and central effect of the toxin. Then the fatty acid oxidation lesion declares itself: hypoketotic hypoglycemia, metabolic acidosis from lactate and dicarboxylic acids, hepatic steatosis with transaminase elevation, muscle weakness and secondary carnitine depletion. Severity tracks glycogen reserve, which is why the illness concentrates in children, in people who are undernourished, and in anyone who ate the fruit and then went without a meal. Seizures and coma are consequences of the glucose level rather than separate toxic effects, and the classic diagnostic trap is a child without diabetes whose encephalopathy is read as infection while the glucose goes unmeasured.",
 "fun": "The Muzaffarpur investigation is a model of field epidemiology: for two decades children in the lychee belt of Bihar died each May and June of an encephalopathy blamed on heat, viruses and pesticides, and the 2017 case-control study found instead that the sick children had eaten lychees in the orchards, skipped the evening meal, and had hypoglycin A and MCPG metabolites in their urine. A metabolic poisoning had been masquerading as viral encephalitis.",
 "src": [
  "Tanaka K, Kean EA, Johnson B. Jamaican vomiting sickness: biochemical investigation of two cases. N Engl J Med, 1976",
  "Shrivastava A et al. Association of acute toxic encephalopathy with litchi consumption in an outbreak in Muzaffarpur, India, 2014: a case-control study. Lancet Global Health, 2017",
  "Hassall CH, Reyle K. Hypoglycin A and B, two biologically active polypeptides from Blighia sapida. Biochem J, 1955",
  "FDA Import Alert 21-11 (ackee)"
 ]
},
"vicine-convicine": {
 "origin": "Vicine and convicine are pyrimidine glucosides made by the fava or broad bean, Vicia faba, and they are food constituents rather than medicines: there is no approved US drug product containing either, and none has ever been sought. Fava beans have been a Mediterranean and Middle Eastern staple since antiquity, and so has the knowledge that they occasionally make someone catastrophically ill. Pythagoras is said to have forbidden beans to his followers, which later writers enjoyed connecting to the disease. Italian physicians described the syndrome carefully in the 1890s and gave it the name favism: a child, usually a boy, eating fresh fava beans and then turning pale and yellow with dark urine within a day. The mechanism came apart in the 1950s. In 1956 Paul Carson, Charles Flanagan and Alf Alving showed in Science that primaquine-induced hemolysis was caused by deficiency of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase, and in 1957 Giuseppe Sansone and Giorgio Segni showed that favism patients had the same enzyme defect. Jacob Mager, Mordechai Chevion and colleagues then identified the aglycones divicine and isouramil as the oxidants doing the damage.",
 "timeline": [
  [
   "1890s",
   "Italian clinicians describe the syndrome in detail and name it favism."
  ],
  [
   "1949",
   "J.B.S. Haldane proposes that malaria has shaped the frequency of human red cell disorders."
  ],
  [
   "1956",
   "Carson, Flanagan, Ickes and Alving identify G6PD deficiency behind primaquine hemolysis."
  ],
  [
   "1957",
   "Sansone and Segni show that favism patients are G6PD deficient."
  ],
  [
   "1982",
   "Chevion, Navok, Glaser and Mager characterize divicine and isouramil as the red cell oxidants."
  ],
  [
   "1989",
   "Breeders identify a fava line with very low vicine and convicine, opening the way to low-toxin varieties."
  ]
 ],
 "cell": "Vicine and convicine are themselves fairly inert. Beta-glucosidases, largely bacterial, hydrolyze them in the gut to the aglycones divicine and isouramil. These are redox-active pyrimidines that autoxidize, cycling between reduced and oxidized forms and generating superoxide and hydrogen peroxide while consuming reduced glutathione. A red cell has one way to regenerate glutathione: NADPH from the oxidative arm of the pentose phosphate pathway, whose rate-limiting enzyme is glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase. With normal G6PD activity the cell keeps up. In G6PD deficiency NADPH supply is capped, glutathione is consumed, and the oxidant front reaches hemoglobin and the membrane. Hemoglobin is oxidized to methemoglobin and then to denatured hemichromes that precipitate as Heinz bodies, which tether to the inner membrane; band 3 and spectrin are cross-linked by disulfide bonds; lipids peroxidize. The cell loses deformability, splenic macrophages bite pieces out of it, leaving bite cells, and the most damaged cells lyse inside the vessels.",
 "body": [
  [
   "Red blood cells",
   "The target. Oldest cells, with the lowest residual G6PD activity, hemolyze first, so the episode is self-limited as that cohort is cleared."
  ],
  [
   "Spleen",
   "Removes Heinz-body-laden cells and produces the bite cells and spherocytes seen on the smear, plus splenic enlargement and tenderness."
  ],
  [
   "Kidney",
   "Free hemoglobin filtered from intravascular hemolysis gives cola-colored urine and can injure tubules, so acute kidney injury is the serious complication."
  ],
  [
   "Liver and skin",
   "Heme load overwhelms conjugation, so unconjugated bilirubin rises and jaundice appears within a day."
  ],
  [
   "Heart and circulation",
   "Abrupt loss of oxygen-carrying capacity produces tachycardia, a flow murmur, and in severe episodes hypotension and high-output failure."
  ],
  [
   "Bone marrow",
   "Responds with reticulocytosis over several days, which is also why a G6PD assay drawn during the crisis can read falsely normal."
  ]
 ],
 "journey": "These are dietary glucosides, so the journey is short. Little intact vicine or convicine is thought to be absorbed; the important step is hydrolysis in the gut lumen by bacterial beta-glucosidases, and the aglycones divicine and isouramil are absorbed and reach the circulation. Onset of hemolysis is roughly 5 to 24 hours after the meal, which fits the time needed for microbial hydrolysis and absorption rather than for any slow distribution. The aglycones are chemically unstable and short lived, which is part of why fresh or raw beans, with the highest glucoside content, are linked to the worst episodes. Hemolysis then runs its own course over one to three days as the vulnerable red cell cohort is destroyed, long after the chemistry has cleared.",
 "sidefx": "There is only one mechanism and therefore one syndrome: acute oxidative hemolysis in a person whose red cells cannot regenerate glutathione fast enough. Pallor, fatigue, jaundice, back or abdominal pain and dark urine all follow from it, and the severity scales with the G6PD variant, the amount eaten and whether the beans were fresh. The Mediterranean variant, with very low residual activity, is classically tied to the severe episodes. People with normal G6PD activity eat fava beans without consequence, which is why this is a food rather than a recognized poison. Reported hemolysis in breastfed infants after maternal intake points to transfer of the aglycones in milk. Infection raises background oxidant stress and can make a given exposure worse.",
 "fun": "Favism is a window onto natural selection. G6PD deficiency is the most common enzyme defect in humans, and its distribution maps onto historical malaria, because the oxidant-poor red cell is a worse home for Plasmodium falciparum; a 1995 Nature study in Africa estimated that the A-minus variant cut severe malaria risk by roughly half. The same genotype that bought protection against malaria is the one that turns a plate of fresh fava beans into a hemolytic crisis.",
 "src": [
  "Luzzatto L, Ally M, Notaro R. Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency. Blood, 2020",
  "Chevion M, Navok T, Glaser G, Mager J. The chemistry of favism-inducing compounds. Eur J Biochem, 1982",
  "Ruwende C et al. Natural selection of hemi- and heterozygotes for G6PD deficiency in Africa by resistance to severe malaria. Nature, 1995",
  "Carson PE, Flanagan CL, Ickes CE, Alving AS. Enzymatic deficiency in primaquine-sensitive erythrocytes. Science, 1956"
 ]
},
"solanine": {
 "origin": "Alpha-solanine is a steroidal glycoalkaloid made by the potato plant, Solanum tuberosum, and it travels with a near-twin, alpha-chaconine. Both are built on the same aglycone, solanidine, with different sugar chains. They are not drugs and there is no approved US product containing them; they are plant defense chemicals and a food safety problem. Solanine was isolated in 1820 by the French pharmacist Desfosses, working with black nightshade berries, and it gave its name to the whole class. The potato makes glycoalkaloids in its leaves, flowers, sprouts and the thin layer under the peel, and production rises sharply with light exposure, mechanical damage, sprouting and poor storage. Light also drives chlorophyll synthesis, which is why green potatoes are a warning sign even though the green pigment itself is harmless: the greening and the glycoalkaloid rise are two consequences of the same light exposure. Human poisoning is usually traced to tubers stored badly, sprouted, or eaten with peel and sprouts included, and the toxin is bitter, which is the plant giving its own warning.",
 "timeline": [
  [
   "1820",
   "Desfosses isolates solanine from nightshade berries, naming the glycoalkaloid class."
  ],
  [
   "1967",
   "The Lenape potato, bred for chipping, is released in the United States."
  ],
  [
   "1970",
   "Lenape is withdrawn after reports of illness, and its glycoalkaloid content is found to be unusually high."
  ],
  [
   "1979",
   "An outbreak at a south London school is investigated and published as suspected solanine poisoning."
  ],
  [
   "1990s",
   "An informal limit near 200 mg total glycoalkaloid per kilogram becomes the working ceiling in potato breeding programs."
  ]
 ],
 "cell": "Solanine and chaconine act in two ways at once. First, they are amphipathic: the solanidine steroid end inserts into membranes and binds 3-beta-hydroxy sterols, mainly cholesterol, while the sugar chains stay in the aqueous phase. Bound molecules aggregate into sterol complexes that distort the bilayer and open pores, which lyses cells outright; chaconine is the more potent of the two and the pair act synergistically. This is the basis of the intestinal injury, and it is the same chemistry that makes these compounds hemolytic in a test tube. Second, both reversibly inhibit cholinesterases, with more effect on butyrylcholinesterase than on acetylcholinesterase. The inhibition is weak compared with pharmaceutical or insecticidal anticholinesterases, so a full cholinergic crisis is not the usual picture, but it contributes the headache, drowsiness, confusion and occasional bradycardia seen in larger exposures. Glycoalkaloids are heat stable and survive boiling, frying and baking, so cooking does not undo either mechanism.",
 "body": [
  [
   "Stomach and small intestine",
   "Membrane disruption injures the epithelium directly, giving a burning sensation in the mouth and throat, vomiting, cramping abdominal pain and diarrhea within hours."
  ],
  [
   "Fluid and electrolytes",
   "Vomiting and diarrhea drive dehydration, hypotension and electrolyte loss, which account for most of the illness in practice."
  ],
  [
   "Central nervous system",
   "Cholinesterase inhibition plus systemic illness produce headache, dizziness, drowsiness and confusion, and in historical severe cases depressed consciousness."
  ],
  [
   "Autonomic system",
   "Cholinergic excess can show as sweating, salivation, small pupils and slowing of the heart, although these are inconsistent."
  ],
  [
   "Red blood cells",
   "Sterol binding is hemolytic in vitro, which is a useful demonstration of the mechanism rather than a prominent clinical finding."
  ]
 ],
 "journey": "Glycoalkaloids are poorly absorbed intact, which is the main reason that ordinary potatoes are safe to eat. Gut bacteria and intestinal enzymes hydrolyze the sugar chains off, giving solanidine, which is better absorbed and is detectable in human plasma with a long residence time measured in days. Hepatic metabolism and biliary plus urinary excretion handle the rest, and fecal loss of unabsorbed glycoalkaloid dominates. Because the injury is a local membrane effect in the gut lumen, symptoms begin within a few hours of the meal and track the dose swallowed rather than any blood level. Peeling removes most of the content, since it concentrates in the peel, the eyes and the sprouts, but heat does not: these molecules are stable to normal cooking temperatures.",
 "sidefx": "The two mechanisms separate the clinical picture into two halves. The membrane-lytic effect gives the gastrointestinal illness, which is early, dose-related and responsible for the dehydration that makes people sick enough to be admitted. The cholinesterase effect contributes the neurologic layer on top, which is what distinguishes a solanine outbreak from ordinary infectious gastroenteritis in a group who shared a meal. A bitter or burning taste often precedes the illness and is the plant's own dose signal. One historical claim has not held up: in 1972 blighted potatoes were proposed as a cause of neural tube defects, and animal work raised the question, but human studies have not confirmed a link.",
 "fun": "The Lenape potato is the cautionary tale of potato breeding. Released in 1967 as a high-solids chipping variety, it was withdrawn in 1970 after people who ate it became ill, and it turned out to carry several times the usual glycoalkaloid load, most likely inherited from a wild Solanum parent used for disease resistance. Breeders have screened for glycoalkaloid content ever since, and Lenape itself survives as a parent in the pedigrees of later varieties.",
 "src": [
  "McMillan M, Thompson JC. An outbreak of suspected solanine poisoning in schoolboys. Q J Med, 1979",
  "Friedman M. Potato glycoalkaloids and metabolites: roles in the plant and in the diet. J Agric Food Chem, 2006",
  "FDA Bad Bug Book, 2nd edition (plant toxins: glycoalkaloids)",
  "Goldman IL et al. historical review of the Lenape potato release and withdrawal"
 ]
},
"persin": {
 "origin": "Persin is a toxin made by the avocado tree, Persea americana, and it is not a drug: there is no approved US product containing it and none has been proposed. The toxicity was known long before the molecule was. Veterinarians had reported for decades that avocado leaves and fruit killed caged birds, caused sudden mastitis and a collapse in milk yield in goats and cattle, and produced head and neck swelling with heart damage in horses. In 1989 Hargis and colleagues published a description of avocado intoxication in caged birds in the Journal of the American Veterinary Medical Association, and in 1995 Peter Oelrichs and co-workers in Australia isolated the responsible compound from avocado leaves, named it persin, and showed that it produced necrosis of the secretory epithelium of the lactating mammary gland and of heart muscle. Persin is a long-chain fatty acid derivative, structurally in the neighborhood of the annonaceous acetogenins. Interest since then has come from two directions: veterinary toxicology, and laboratory cancer research, where persin kills cultured breast cancer cells.",
 "timeline": [
  [
   "1980s",
   "Veterinary reports of avocado killing caged birds and of mastitis and cardiac injury in livestock have accumulated."
  ],
  [
   "1989",
   "Hargis and colleagues publish a clinical and pathologic description of avocado intoxication in caged birds."
  ],
  [
   "1995",
   "Oelrichs and colleagues isolate and name persin and reproduce mammary and myocardial necrosis with it."
  ],
  [
   "2006",
   "Laboratory work reports that persin induces apoptosis in breast cancer cell lines and interacts with tamoxifen in vitro."
  ]
 ],
 "cell": "The mechanism is genuinely incompletely defined, and honest cards say so. What is reproducible is the target tissue: persin produces necrosis of the secretory epithelium of the lactating mammary gland and of cardiac myocytes in susceptible species, with sterile inflammation, edema and, in birds, pericardial effusion. The structural kinship with the annonaceous acetogenins, which are potent inhibitors of mitochondrial complex I, makes mitochondrial poisoning of highly oxidative tissue the leading hypothesis, and it would explain why actively secreting mammary epithelium and bird myocardium, two tissues with extreme metabolic demand, are the ones that die. In cultured human breast cancer cells persin behaves as a microtubule-stabilizing agent and triggers an apoptotic program with ceramide accumulation and caspase activation, and sensitizes cells to tamoxifen. That is cell culture data, at concentrations well above anything a diet provides, and it has not been shown to mean anything in an animal or a person. Human avocado reactions are a separate matter entirely and are immunologic, not toxic.",
 "body": [
  [
   "Mammary gland (animals)",
   "The signature lesion: necrosis of secretory epithelium in lactating goats, cattle, rabbits and mice, with a sterile mastitis and an abrupt fall in milk."
  ],
  [
   "Heart (animals)",
   "Myocardial necrosis with heart failure, pericardial and subcutaneous edema, and sudden death, most dramatic in small caged birds."
  ],
  [
   "Respiratory system (birds)",
   "Caged birds show labored breathing and fluffed feathers before collapse, largely a consequence of cardiac failure and effusion."
  ],
  [
   "Head and neck (horses)",
   "Horses develop striking edema of the face, tongue and neck along with cardiac injury."
  ],
  [
   "Humans",
   "No recognized toxic syndrome from eating avocado fruit; the clinically relevant human problem is allergy to avocado proteins, not persin."
  ]
 ],
 "journey": "Persin is lipophilic and reaches animals by the oral route when they eat leaves, bark, skin, seed or fruit; absorption, distribution and clearance have not been characterized in any species, and no validated assay exists for routine use. Concentration is the whole story. The leaves and bark carry by far the most, the skin and seed less, and the ripe flesh that people eat the least, and Guatemalan-type cultivars are reported to be more toxic to animals than West Indian types. For humans the practical pharmacokinetics are that very little persin is present in the food as eaten, and what is present is diluted into a large body with no identified sensitive target tissue, so the compound does not accumulate anywhere that matters.",
 "sidefx": "In animals the adverse effects and the mechanism are the same thing: the toxin kills the tissues it reaches, and the clinical picture is read off which tissue that is, lactating mammary gland or myocardium. Species sensitivity varies enormously, with small birds at one extreme and dogs and cats relatively resistant, which points to differences in metabolic handling rather than in exposure. In people the two avocado-related problems are both unrelated to persin: allergy, mediated by avocado proteins including the class I chitinase Pers a 1, which cross-reacts with latex hevein and places avocado in the latex-fruit syndrome alongside banana, kiwi and chestnut, and a handful of case reports of avocado intake lowering the INR in patients taking warfarin.",
 "fun": "Avocado is one of the few foods that is genuinely lethal to a pet budgerigar and genuinely harmless to the person feeding it, which makes it a good test of whether someone understands that toxicity is a property of a dose in a species rather than of a molecule. The avocado problem people actually present with is an allergy to a chitinase that happens to look like a latex protein.",
 "src": [
  "Oelrichs PB et al. Isolation and identification of a compound from avocado (Persea americana) leaves which causes necrosis of the acinar epithelium of the lactating mammary gland and the myocardium. Natural Toxins, 1995",
  "Hargis AM et al. Avocado (Persea americana) intoxication in caged birds. J Am Vet Med Assoc, 1989",
  "Butt AJ et al. A novel plant toxin, persin, with in vivo activity in the mammary gland, induces Bim-dependent apoptosis in human breast cancer cells. Mol Cancer Ther, 2006",
  "Sanchez-Monge R et al. Class I chitinases, the panallergens responsible for the latex-fruit syndrome. J Allergy Clin Immunol, 2000"
 ]
},
"phytohemagglutinin": {
 "origin": "Phytohemagglutinin, PHA, is a lectin made by the common bean Phaseolus vulgaris and concentrated in red kidney beans. It is a seed defense protein and a food safety hazard, not a medicine: there is no approved US drug product containing it, and the only legitimate use of purified PHA is as a laboratory reagent. Plant lectins entered science in 1888 when Hermann Stillmark, in Dorpat, found that castor bean extract agglutinated red cells and named the active principle ricin. Bean lectins that clumped erythrocytes were called hemagglutinins, hence the name. PHA then had a second career that had nothing to do with beans: in 1960 Peter Nowell, in Philadelphia, reported that PHA drove normal human leukocytes into mitosis, and that single observation made human cytogenetics practical, because for the first time a clinician could get dividing lymphocytes from a blood sample and look at chromosomes. Human karyotyping, and much of what followed in the genetics of chromosomal disease, rests on a kidney bean protein. Food poisoning outbreaks were characterized separately, notably in British surveillance reported by Noah and colleagues in 1980.",
 "timeline": [
  [
   "1888",
   "Stillmark describes plant hemagglutinins in castor bean, founding lectin biology."
  ],
  [
   "1960",
   "Nowell shows PHA is a mitogen for human lymphocytes, making routine karyotyping possible."
  ],
  [
   "1970s",
   "British surveillance begins recording clusters of rapid-onset vomiting traced to red kidney beans."
  ],
  [
   "1980",
   "Noah and colleagues publish an account of food poisoning from raw red kidney beans in the BMJ."
  ],
  [
   "1980s",
   "Heating experiments show that warming beans below boiling can raise rather than lower their toxicity."
  ]
 ],
 "cell": "PHA is a tetrameric glycoprotein assembled from two subunit types, E and L, giving five isoforms; the E subunit agglutinates erythrocytes and the L subunit acts on leukocytes. Each subunit carries a carbohydrate recognition domain that binds complex N-linked oligosaccharides, so PHA sticks to the sugar side chains of cell surface glycoproteins and, because it is multivalent, cross-links them. In the gut the relevant target is the brush border glycocalyx. PHA binds enterocyte surface glycoproteins, resists digestion by gastric and pancreatic proteases well enough to arrive intact, disrupts microvilli, is taken up into enterocytes, and breaks the epithelial barrier, provoking fluid secretion, mucosal injury and vomiting. On lymphocytes the same cross-linking chemistry has a different consequence: clustering of glycosylated surface receptors including the T cell receptor complex and CD2 delivers a polyclonal activating signal through the normal TCR pathway, which is why PHA is a T cell mitogen in the laboratory and why lymphocyte proliferation to PHA is a standard test of T cell function.",
 "body": [
  [
   "Stomach and small intestine",
   "The site of injury. Lectin binding strips and distorts microvilli and opens the barrier, producing nausea and profuse vomiting one to three hours after the meal, then cramping and diarrhea."
  ],
  [
   "Fluid balance",
   "Vomiting and diarrhea are the clinically important effect, and dehydration is the reason anyone needs care."
  ],
  [
   "Red blood cells",
   "Agglutinates erythrocytes in a test tube, the property it is named for; this is a laboratory phenomenon rather than something that happens in a poisoned person."
  ],
  [
   "Immune system",
   "Cross-links T cell surface glycoproteins and drives polyclonal proliferation, which is the basis of its use as a mitogen in immune function testing and karyotyping."
  ],
  [
   "Recovery",
   "The epithelium turns over quickly, so the illness is typically over within several hours to a day with no lasting injury."
  ]
 ],
 "journey": "There is almost no pharmacokinetics here, because PHA works in the lumen. It is a protein, and a tough one: the lectin fold is compact and partly resistant to gastric acid and to pancreatic proteases, which is exactly what lets a dietary protein reach the small bowel with its binding sites intact. Small amounts are endocytosed by enterocytes and traces can cross into the circulation in animal studies, but systemic exposure is not what makes people sick. Whatever is not bound is digested or passes in the stool. The determinant of illness is how much active lectin survived the cooking, measured in hemagglutinating units: raw beans carry tens of thousands, and thoroughly boiled beans a small fraction of that.",
 "sidefx": "The entire syndrome is the mechanism acting on one tissue, so there is nothing to explain beyond the gut. Onset is fast because no absorption or metabolism is required, vomiting is prominent because the injury is to the upper small bowel, and recovery is quick because enterocytes are replaced within days. Outbreaks come in clusters, since everyone at the table ate the same dish, which is the clue that separates this from a viral illness. Canned kidney beans are already cooked and are not a source. The hazard is dried beans prepared gently, and soaking alone does not inactivate the lectin.",
 "fun": "A slow cooker is the specific hazard, and for a counterintuitive reason: heating kidney beans to around 80 degrees Celsius, the range a low setting reaches, has been reported to make them up to five times more toxic than raw beans, apparently by partly unfolding the protein into a form that binds more avidly while leaving it far from denatured. Reaching a full rolling boil destroys the lectin, so the dangerous preparation is the one that gets warm and stays warm.",
 "src": [
  "Noah ND et al. Food poisoning from raw red kidney beans. BMJ, 1980",
  "Nowell PC. Phytohemagglutinin: an initiator of mitosis in cultures of normal human leukocytes. Cancer Research, 1960",
  "FDA Bad Bug Book, 2nd edition (phytohaemagglutinin)",
  "Pusztai A, Bardocz S. Biological effects of plant lectins on the gastrointestinal tract. Trends in Glycoscience and Glycotechnology, 1996"
 ]
},
"myristicin": {
 "origin": "Myristicin is an alkenylbenzene from nutmeg and mace, the seed and the seed covering of Myristica fragrans, a tree native to the Banda Islands of the Moluccas. It is a spice constituent, not a drug, and there is no approved US product containing it. Nutmeg was one of the most valuable commodities in the world for two centuries, and the Dutch East India Company fought and depopulated islands to hold the monopoly. Its capacity to derange people has been recorded almost as long. A frequently cited 16th century account describes an English woman who became deliriously intoxicated after eating a dozen nutmegs, and in 1829 the Czech physiologist Jan Evangelista Purkinje swallowed three nutmegs and left a careful record of the headache, delirium and days of dullness that followed. Myristicin was isolated from nutmeg oil in the late 19th century. Andrew Weil brought the subject into modern pharmacology with a 1965 review in Economic Botany, and the clearest contemporary data come from poison center case series, notably a ten-year Illinois Poison Center review published in 2014, which found most exposures to be deliberate and most of those in teenagers.",
 "timeline": [
  [
   "1576",
   "An early account describes a woman intoxicated and delirious after eating a dozen nutmegs."
  ],
  [
   "1829",
   "Purkinje records his own prolonged nutmeg intoxication after eating three nutmegs."
  ],
  [
   "1890",
   "Myristicin is isolated and characterized from nutmeg oil."
  ],
  [
   "1965",
   "Andrew Weil reviews nutmeg as an intoxicant in Economic Botany."
  ],
  [
   "2014",
   "A ten-year Illinois Poison Center series describes 32 nutmeg exposures, most intentional and most in adolescents."
  ]
 ],
 "cell": "The mechanism is not settled, and the honest summary is that nutmeg intoxication is a syndrome in search of a receptor. Nutmeg oil is a mixture, and myristicin travels with elemicin, safrole and terpenes, so attributing effects to one molecule is already an assumption. Myristicin is a weak reversible inhibitor of monoamine oxidase in vitro, which could contribute to the sympathetic features. The most repeated hypothesis is hepatic CYP-mediated conversion of myristicin to an amphetamine, 3-methoxy-4,5-methylenedioxyamphetamine, and of elemicin to trimethoxyamphetamine, by amination of the allyl side chain; this is chemically plausible, has been demonstrated in vitro, and has not been confirmed as a meaningful pathway in humans, where the metabolite has not been reliably found. Anticholinergic-like and serotonergic actions have both been proposed on the basis of the clinical picture rather than binding data. Myristicin also inhibits several CYP enzymes and induces others, which is a more solid finding than any receptor claim.",
 "body": [
  [
   "Central nervous system",
   "Hours after ingestion, agitation, anxiety, visual distortions and hallucinations, a characteristic sense of impending doom, and a prolonged foggy aftermath."
  ],
  [
   "Autonomic system",
   "A peripheral picture that mimics anticholinergic poisoning: dry mouth, flushed skin, tachycardia, palpitations and sometimes urinary retention."
  ],
  [
   "Eyes",
   "The informative exception. Pupils are often normal or small rather than dilated, which is what separates this from a true anticholinergic toxidrome."
  ],
  [
   "Stomach and gut",
   "Nausea, repeated vomiting and abdominal pain, prominent enough that gastrointestinal misery dominates the early hours."
  ],
  [
   "Liver",
   "Metabolizes myristicin through CYP enzymes, and myristicin in turn inhibits and induces CYP activity, a theoretical interaction route."
  ]
 ],
 "journey": "Myristicin is lipophilic and absorbed from the gut, but onset is strikingly delayed, commonly three to eight hours after ingestion, which is longer than absorption alone explains and is one of the arguments for an active metabolite. Distribution is wide, with ready entry into the brain. Metabolism is hepatic and CYP-dependent, with demethylenation and side-chain oxidation, and renal excretion of conjugated metabolites. Duration is the other oddity: effects commonly run 24 hours and have been described lasting two to three days, far longer than the parent compound's presence would predict. The combination of long latency and long duration is the practical signature, and it is also the reason people who take more while waiting for something to happen end up considerably worse.",
 "sidefx": "There is not much separation between the effect and the adverse effect here, because the whole exposure is unwanted. The gastrointestinal distress is probably direct irritation from the oil. The tachycardia, flushing and dry mouth follow whatever sympathetic and anticholinergic-like actions the mixture has, and the agitation, distorted perception and dysphoria are central. Severe outcomes are rare, and the deaths reported in the literature generally involved other substances. The long tail of fatigue, headache and dulled thinking over following days is characteristic enough to have its own informal name. Pediatric exposures are usually accidental ingestions of kitchen spice rather than experimentation.",
 "fun": "Nutmeg has a reputation as a free legal high and almost no reputation as a pleasant one, and the case series explain why: the reliable effects are many hours of nausea and vomiting, a racing heart, dry mouth, dread and confusion, then a day or more of feeling poisoned. It is the rare intoxicant whose main deterrent is simply what it does, and repeat exposures in poison center data are uncommon.",
 "src": [
  "Ehrenpreis JE, DesLauriers C, Lank P, Armstrong PK, Leikin JB. Nutmeg poisonings: a retrospective review of 10 years experience from the Illinois Poison Center, 2001-2011. J Med Toxicol, 2014",
  "Weil AT. Nutmeg as a narcotic. Economic Botany, 1965",
  "Goldstein Hoffman R et al. review of myristicin pharmacology and nutmeg intoxication, Clinical Toxicology literature",
  "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies (plants and herbal preparations)"
 ]
},
"dmaa": {
 "origin": "DMAA, 1,3-dimethylamylamine or methylhexanamine, is a synthetic aliphatic amine with an unusual double life. Eli Lilly patented it in 1944 and sold it as Forthane, a nasal inhaler for congestion, and withdrew it from the market in the early 1980s. It reappeared around 2006 as an ingredient in sports supplements, introduced by the chemist Patrick Arnold, who marketed it as geranamine on the premise that it occurs naturally in geranium, Pelargonium graveolens. That premise rested largely on a 1996 Chinese report of trace methylhexanamine in geranium oil, and repeated independent analyses through 2011 and 2012 failed to find it, or found only amounts consistent with contamination. The working consensus is that the DMAA in supplements was synthetic. It has had no approved US drug product in its supplement incarnation, and the FDA has stated that it is not a lawful dietary ingredient. It sold enormously in pre-workout powders such as Jack3d and in weight-loss products, and the history that followed is regulatory and forensic rather than therapeutic.",
 "timeline": [
  [
   "1944",
   "Eli Lilly patents methylhexanamine and later markets it as the nasal inhaler Forthane."
  ],
  [
   "1983",
   "Forthane is withdrawn from the US market."
  ],
  [
   "2006",
   "DMAA appears in sports supplements as geranamine, with a claimed geranium origin."
  ],
  [
   "2010",
   "The World Anti-Doping Agency lists methylhexaneamine as a prohibited specified stimulant."
  ],
  [
   "2011",
   "Two US soldiers die after collapsing during physical training with DMAA detected, and the Department of Defense pulls DMAA products from military exchanges pending a safety review."
  ],
  [
   "2013",
   "FDA sends warning letters to DMAA marketers, declares it not a lawful dietary ingredient, and seizes product; litigation upholding the agency runs to 2019."
  ]
 ],
 "cell": "DMAA is a simple branched aliphatic primary amine, structurally in the family of the old sympathomimetic amines such as tuaminoheptane and propylhexedrine, and lacking the catechol ring of the endogenous transmitters. It acts indirectly. As a substrate for the norepinephrine transporter it is carried into sympathetic nerve terminals, where it both blocks reuptake and displaces stored norepinephrine into the synapse, with weaker activity at the dopamine transporter. The released norepinephrine then does the work: alpha-1 receptor activation on vascular smooth muscle raises peripheral resistance, and beta-1 activation in the heart raises rate and contractility. Direct receptor affinity is low, so the pharmacology is that of a releasing agent rather than an agonist, and potency is far below that of amphetamine. The mechanistic literature is thin, consisting mostly of structural inference and a small number of in vitro and animal studies, which is itself part of the regulatory story: the compound was sold to millions of people without the basic work having been done.",
 "body": [
  [
   "Blood vessels",
   "Alpha-1 mediated vasoconstriction raises systemic vascular resistance and blood pressure, the most consistently documented human effect."
  ],
  [
   "Heart",
   "Beta-1 stimulation raises rate, contractility and myocardial oxygen demand while coronary perfusion time shortens, the setup for ischemia and arrhythmia."
  ],
  [
   "Brain",
   "Central noradrenergic stimulation gives the marketed effects of energy and focus, and at higher exposure agitation, headache, insomnia and psychiatric symptoms; abrupt hypertension is the proposed route to hemorrhagic stroke."
  ],
  [
   "Thermoregulation",
   "Vasoconstriction plus increased metabolic rate impairs heat dissipation, which is why cases cluster around hard exertion in heat."
  ],
  [
   "Liver",
   "Hepatotoxicity has been reported with DMAA-containing products, although several of those products contained other ingredients, so attribution is uncertain."
  ],
  [
   "Nasal mucosa",
   "Its original pharmacology: local vasoconstriction shrinking congested mucosa, which is what it was sold for in the 1940s."
  ]
 ],
 "journey": "Oral absorption is good and effects come on within about half an hour. Human data are limited to a few small studies, which reported a terminal half-life around eight to nine hours, considerably longer than the stimulant experience itself and long enough that repeated daily servings stack. Being a small lipophilic amine, it distributes widely and enters the central nervous system. Metabolism appears limited, and a substantial fraction is excreted unchanged in urine, which is how anti-doping laboratories detect it; routine hospital drug screens are not designed for it, although DMAA has been reported to cross-react on some amphetamine immunoassays. Co-ingested caffeine was almost universal in the products it was sold in, and the combination was shown to raise blood pressure more than either alone.",
 "sidefx": "Everything reported traces back to sympathetic overdrive. The common complaints, palpitations, headache, jitteriness, insomnia, nausea and raised blood pressure, are the mechanism at low exposure. The serious events, hypertensive emergency, hemorrhagic stroke in young people with no vascular risk factors, myocardial infarction with normal coronary arteries, arrhythmia and cardiac arrest, are the same mechanism pushed further, often with caffeine on board and often during intense exertion. Exertional heat illness is the third strand, since the drug works against heat loss while exercise generates it. Psychiatric presentations, including agitation and psychosis, follow the central noradrenergic action.",
 "fun": "The military deaths are what moved this compound. After two soldiers died in 2011 following collapse during physical training with DMAA in their blood, the Department of Defense pulled DMAA products from exchanges and ran its own safety review, and that decision by a customer rather than a regulator did more to end DMAA's commercial life than anything else, with FDA enforcement and seven years of litigation following behind.",
 "src": [
  "FDA. DMAA in dietary supplements (agency statement and warning letters, 2012-2013)",
  "Eliason MJ et al. Report of 2 cases of exercise-induced death in otherwise healthy soldiers using dietary supplements containing 1,3-dimethylamylamine. Military Medicine, 2012",
  "Austin KG et al. Pharmacokinetics of 1,3-dimethylamylamine in humans. Int J Sport Nutr Exerc Metab, 2014",
  "Lisi A et al. Studies of methylhexaneamine in supplements and geranium oil. Drug Testing and Analysis, 2011"
 ]
},
"dmha": {
 "origin": "DMHA is the supplement-industry name for 2-amino-6-methylheptane, also called octodrine, 2-aminoisoheptane and 1,5-dimethylhexylamine. Like DMAA it is a synthetic aliphatic amine, and like DMAA it has no approved US drug product: the FDA has stated that it does not meet the requirements for a lawful dietary ingredient. Octodrine has a pharmaceutical past outside the United States, where it was marketed decades ago in combination preparations as an inhaled decongestant and a pressor agent, at doses well above what supplement labels later suggested. Its modern career began after FDA enforcement cleared DMAA out of the market around 2013, when DMHA appeared in pre-workout and weight-loss products marketed, sometimes explicitly, as the DMAA replacement. The same playbook followed, including plant-origin claims, this time to English walnut bark, Juglans regia, or to Kigelia africana or Aconitum species, none of which has been substantiated. Pieter Cohen and colleagues documented the compound in commercial products in Clinical Toxicology in 2018, and FDA warning letters went out in 2019.",
 "timeline": [
  [
   "20th century",
   "Octodrine is marketed outside the United States as an inhaled decongestant and pressor agent, then falls out of use in the second half of the century."
  ],
  [
   "2016",
   "DMHA appears in US pre-workout and weight-loss supplements following FDA enforcement against DMAA."
  ],
  [
   "2018",
   "Cohen and colleagues publish analyses identifying DMHA and related experimental stimulants in commercial supplements."
  ],
  [
   "2019",
   "FDA sends warning letters stating that DMHA is not a lawful dietary ingredient, and it is listed among ingredients prohibited in products sold at military exchanges."
  ]
 ],
 "cell": "DMHA is a branched seven-carbon primary amine, a near-homolog of DMAA with the amine and methyl groups in different positions, and the pharmacology is assumed from that structure because very little has been measured. The presumed action is that of an indirect sympathomimetic: uptake by the norepinephrine transporter, displacement of stored norepinephrine and inhibition of its reuptake, producing alpha-1 vasoconstriction and beta-1 cardiac stimulation, with no significant direct receptor affinity. Its old use as a decongestant and pressor is consistent with that, since both depend on alpha-1 mediated vasoconstriction. What is missing is specific: there are no good human receptor or transporter data, no characterized metabolism, and no dose-response work. A second problem is chemical identity. Labels have used 2-aminoisoheptane and octodrine interchangeably, and analytical work has found that products differ in which positional isomer they actually contain, so the molecule in a given tub is not reliably the one the pharmacology is being inferred for.",
 "body": [
  [
   "Blood vessels",
   "Expected alpha-1 vasoconstriction with raised peripheral resistance and blood pressure, consistent with its historical use as a pressor."
  ],
  [
   "Heart",
   "Expected beta-1 stimulation raising rate and contractility, with the usual consequences for oxygen demand and rhythm; human reports are sparse."
  ],
  [
   "Brain",
   "Central noradrenergic stimulation accounts for the marketed energy and focus and for reported jitteriness, headache, insomnia and agitation."
  ],
  [
   "Nasal mucosa and airway",
   "The original pharmaceutical target, local vasoconstriction and bronchial effects when inhaled."
  ],
  [
   "Thermoregulation",
   "As with other stimulants, impaired heat loss during exertion is a theoretical concern, stacked on the caffeine these products also contain."
  ]
 ],
 "journey": "Essentially uncharacterized in humans. Oral absorption of a small lipophilic amine is expected to be good, with ready central nervous system entry, and the historical inhaled use implies local mucosal action with some systemic absorption. Onset, peak, half-life, metabolic route and route of elimination have not been established in any published human study, and that gap is the point rather than an incidental omission: a compound with no pharmacokinetic description was sold in doses that analytical surveys found varied widely between products and often did not match the label. Routine hospital and workplace drug screens are not designed to detect it, and specific chromatographic methods are required, which is also why anti-doping laboratories rather than clinical ones generate most of the detection data.",
 "sidefx": "The expected adverse profile is the sympathomimetic one, and the reported human experience is consistent as far as it goes: palpitations, raised blood pressure, headache, insomnia, anxiety and nausea, with hypertension, tachycardia, arrhythmia and agitation as the concern at higher exposure. Serious cardiovascular and cerebrovascular events are anticipated by analogy with DMAA rather than documented in a case series of any size, and that uncertainty cuts both ways: absence of reports in a thinly studied, intermittently legal ingredient is weak reassurance. In practice DMHA rarely arrives alone, since the products combine it with caffeine, yohimbine and other stimulants, which makes attribution of any single event difficult.",
 "fun": "DMHA is a case study in regulatory whack-a-mole. It entered the market as DMAA left it, carried the same unsupported botanical cover story transplanted to walnut bark, and was marketed to the same customers, including the ones in uniform, which is why it joined DMAA on the list of ingredients barred from products sold at military exchanges. Products advertising themselves as DMAA-free were a reasonable place to look for it.",
 "src": [
  "Cohen PA, Travis JC, Keizers PHJ, Boyer FE, Venhuis BJ. The stimulant higenamine in weight loss and sports supplements, and related analyses of DMHA and octodrine. Clinical Toxicology, 2018",
  "FDA. Warning letters regarding DMHA (octodrine) as a new dietary ingredient, 2019",
  "Department of Defense Operation Supplement Safety, prohibited ingredient listings",
  "WADA Prohibited List (octodrine, specified stimulant)"
 ]
},
"papain": {
 "origin": "Papain is a cysteine protease from the latex of the unripe papaya fruit, Carica papaya, and it is sold in the United States as a dietary supplement and a food enzyme rather than as a drug; there is no approved US drug product containing papain. Cultures across the tropics wrapped tough meat in papaya leaves long before anyone named the enzyme, and that remains its largest commercial use. Alexander Balls and Hans Lineweaver, at the US Department of Agriculture, crystallized papain in 1937, and in 1968 Jan Drenth and colleagues in Groningen published its three-dimensional structure in Nature, one of the first enzyme structures ever solved. That structure defined a fold and a catalytic strategy now named for it: the papain family, clan CA, which includes the human cathepsins B, L, K and S, plant and parasite proteases, and the papain-like proteases of coronaviruses. A related papaya enzyme, chymopapain, became a surgical product in the 1980s, and its story is the reason papain is remembered in medicine mostly as an allergen.",
 "timeline": [
  [
   "1937",
   "Balls and Lineweaver crystallize papain at the US Department of Agriculture."
  ],
  [
   "1956",
   "Lewis Thomas observes that injected papain collapses rabbit ears by dissolving cartilage matrix, suggesting a use on intervertebral discs."
  ],
  [
   "1964",
   "Lyman Smith injects chymopapain into a human lumbar disc and publishes the first chemonucleolysis series."
  ],
  [
   "1968",
   "Drenth and colleagues publish the crystal structure of papain, defining the papain protease family."
  ],
  [
   "1982",
   "FDA approves chymopapain as Chymodiactin for chemonucleolysis, and tens of thousands of injections follow."
  ],
  [
   "2008",
   "FDA removes unapproved topical papain debriding products from the US market after reports of serious hypersensitivity reactions."
  ]
 ],
 "cell": "Papain hydrolyzes peptide bonds using a catalytic dyad rather than the serine protease triad. Cysteine 25 sits at the interface of two structural lobes, and its thiol is held as a thiolate-imidazolium ion pair with histidine 159, oriented by asparagine 175. The thiolate attacks the substrate carbonyl to form a covalent acyl-enzyme intermediate through a tetrahedral transition state, the amine leaving group departs, and water then hydrolyzes the thioester to release the carboxy product and regenerate free enzyme. The active site cleft binds about seven substrate residues, and the S2 pocket prefers a bulky hydrophobic side chain such as phenylalanine, but specificity is otherwise broad, which is precisely what makes papain useful for chewing up structural protein and useless as a selective tool. Activity depends on the free thiol, so reducing agents activate it and heavy metals and oxidation kill it. In immunology, protease activity is itself the signal: papain cleaves epithelial substrates and triggers release of the alarmin IL-33, driving a type 2 response, which is why it is a standard experimental allergen.",
 "body": [
  [
   "Mouth and esophagus",
   "Chewable papaya enzyme tablets act locally and can irritate mucosa; meat tenderizer was once put down the esophagus for impacted food, and was abandoned after reports of perforation."
  ],
  [
   "Stomach and intestine",
   "Marketed as a digestive aid for bloating, but it is a protein entering a protease-rich acidic environment, and evidence of benefit is limited and of poor quality."
  ],
  [
   "Immune system",
   "The clinically important effect. Papain is a potent sensitizer, causing IgE-mediated reactions up to anaphylaxis, and cross-reacting within the latex-fruit syndrome."
  ],
  [
   "Lungs",
   "Inhaled papain dust causes occupational asthma and rhinitis in food, laboratory and supplement workers, and papain in enzymatic contact lens cleaners sensitized users."
  ],
  [
   "Intervertebral disc (historical)",
   "Chymopapain injected into the nucleus pulposus depolymerized proteoglycan, reducing disc volume and pressure on the nerve root."
  ],
  [
   "Skin and wounds (historical)",
   "Topical papain-urea ointments digested necrotic protein for debridement before the unapproved products were removed."
  ]
 ],
 "journey": "Papain is a protein of about 23 kilodaltons and is not absorbed in any meaningful amount by mouth. It acts in the lumen where it is placed, is denatured by gastric acid and digested by gastric and pancreatic proteases along with the food, and what survives passes in the stool. There is no systemic distribution, no hepatic metabolism and no renal elimination to describe, which is the whole pharmacokinetic story of an oral enzyme supplement and also the main reason claims for systemic anti-inflammatory effects are difficult to support. The two routes that did produce systemic exposure were the ones that caused trouble: injection into a disc, and inhalation of powder, both of which deliver intact antigenic protein past the gut and sensitize the immune system.",
 "sidefx": "Papain's adverse effects are almost entirely immunologic, and they follow from it being a stable foreign protein with enzymatic activity that itself provokes type 2 immunity. Sensitized people react to papaya, papain supplements and papain dust, and because the papaya allergen Pers a 1 analog family and latex hevein share a class I chitinase structure, papaya sits in the latex-fruit syndrome alongside avocado, banana, kiwi and chestnut. Local effects, mouth and throat irritation and gastric upset, are direct proteolysis. Case reports describe raised INR with papaya products in patients taking warfarin. Occupational asthma reflects repeated inhalation of an aerosolized protease.",
 "fun": "Chemonucleolysis is a clean example of a good idea defeated by its own allergen. Chymopapain injection into a herniated lumbar disc worked, was far less invasive than surgery, and was approved in 1982 to great enthusiasm, but roughly half a percent to one percent of patients had anaphylaxis, with deaths reported, and the technique collapsed within a decade. The enzyme that tenderizes a steak was too good at teaching the immune system to recognize it.",
 "src": [
  "Drenth J, Jansonius JN, Koekoek R, Swen HM, Wolthers BG. Structure of papain. Nature, 1968",
  "Smith L. Enzyme dissolution of the nucleus pulposus in humans. JAMA, 1964",
  "FDA. Topical drug products containing papain: notice regarding unapproved marketed products, 2008",
  "Baur X, Fruhmann G. Allergic reactions, including asthma, to the pineapple protease bromelain and the papaya protease papain. Clinical Allergy and related occupational allergy literature"
 ]
},
"indole-3-carbinol": {
 "origin": "Indole-3-carbinol is not a drug and has no approved US product; it is a breakdown product of a plant defense chemical, sold as a dietary supplement along with its condensation product DIM. The story starts with cabbage. Cruciferous plants, the Brassicaceae, store sulfur-containing glucosinolates in their tissues and keep the enzyme that cleaves them, myrosinase, in separate cells; damage to the leaf brings the two together, which is a chemical weapon detonated by being eaten. The glucosinolate called glucobrassicin yields indole-3-carbinol. In the 1970s Lee Wattenberg at the University of Minnesota showed that feeding rodents cruciferous vegetables, and then purified indoles, induced detoxifying enzymes and reduced carcinogen-induced tumors, work published with Loub in Cancer Research in 1978 and widely credited with launching dietary chemoprevention. H. Leon Bradlow and Jon Michnovicz at the Rockefeller University then reported, around 1990 in the Journal of the National Cancer Institute, that indole-3-carbinol shifted human estradiol metabolism toward 2-hydroxylation, which is the claim the supplement market was built on.",
 "timeline": [
  [
   "1978",
   "Wattenberg and Loub report that dietary indoles induce detoxifying enzymes and inhibit carcinogen-induced tumors in rodents."
  ],
  [
   "1990",
   "Michnovicz and Bradlow show that indole-3-carbinol increases 2-hydroxylation of estradiol in humans."
  ],
  [
   "1991",
   "Bjeldanes and colleagues identify indolocarbazole, formed from indole-3-carbinol in acid, as an extremely potent aryl hydrocarbon receptor ligand."
  ],
  [
   "2000",
   "A small placebo-controlled trial of indole-3-carbinol in cervical intraepithelial neoplasia is published."
  ],
  [
   "2004",
   "Trials in recurrent respiratory papillomatosis report mixed results."
  ]
 ],
 "cell": "Chewing or chopping a cabbage releases myrosinase, which hydrolyzes glucobrassicin to an unstable indol-3-ylmethyl isothiocyanate. That falls apart in water, releasing thiocyanate and leaving indole-3-carbinol. Indole-3-carbinol is itself unstable in acid, and in the stomach it self-condenses into a family of oligomers: mainly 3,3-diindolylmethane, known as DIM, plus a linear trimer and small amounts of indolo[3,2-b]carbazole. These condensation products, not the parent, are what reach the tissues. Several are ligands for the aryl hydrocarbon receptor, and indolocarbazole binds it with an affinity approaching that of dioxin. Activated AhR dimerizes with ARNT and induces the CYP1 family, CYP1A1, CYP1A2 and CYP1B1, along with UGT1A1 and some phase II enzymes. The CYP1A1 induction is what redirects estradiol metabolism from 16-alpha-hydroxylation toward 2-hydroxylation, raising the urinary ratio of 2-hydroxyestrone to 16-alpha-hydroxyestrone, a biomarker whose clinical meaning remains unproven. DIM also shows antiandrogenic and NF-kB effects in cell culture.",
 "body": [
  [
   "Stomach",
   "Not a passive conduit but a reaction vessel: gastric acid converts swallowed indole-3-carbinol into DIM and the other condensation products that do the work."
  ],
  [
   "Liver",
   "Aryl hydrocarbon receptor activation induces CYP1A1, CYP1A2 and CYP1B1, changing the clearance of other CYP1A2 substrates and of steroid hormones."
  ],
  [
   "Endocrine system",
   "Estradiol metabolism shifts toward 2-hydroxylation, measurable as a changed urinary estrogen metabolite ratio in small human studies; whether that changes any outcome has not been shown."
  ],
  [
   "Cervix and airway epithelium",
   "The settings for the small clinical trials, in cervical intraepithelial neoplasia and recurrent respiratory papillomatosis, both HPV-related, with limited and inconsistent results."
  ],
  [
   "Nervous system",
   "High intakes of indole-3-carbinol in one small study produced imbalance and tremor, which resolved on stopping."
  ]
 ],
 "journey": "Very little indole-3-carbinol survives to be absorbed as itself. It is converted in gastric acid within minutes, and after an oral dose the parent compound is barely detectable in plasma while DIM and the other oligomers are. That chemistry is the whole pharmacokinetic story and it has a practical consequence: products labeled indole-3-carbinol and products labeled DIM are not interchangeable, since the first depends on each person's gastric acidity and transit to produce an unpredictable mixture, while the second delivers one defined compound. DIM is lipophilic, reaches peak plasma concentrations a few hours after dosing with a half-life of a few hours, is hydroxylated and conjugated, and is eliminated in bile and urine. Absorption of DIM varies several-fold between formulations, since it dissolves poorly in water.",
 "sidefx": "The reported effects are mild and mostly gastrointestinal, nausea and abdominal discomfort, with rash and a harmless darkening of the urine from DIM. The important risks are mechanistic rather than symptomatic. Inducing CYP1A2 can lower concentrations of narrow-margin substrates such as theophylline, clozapine and tizanidine. Shifting steroid metabolism can interfere with hormone-based therapy, and a small study found that DIM lowered levels of endoxifen, the active metabolite of tamoxifen, which matters for a supplement marketed heavily to women with breast cancer histories. There is also a tumor-promotion signal: in several rodent models indole-3-carbinol given after a carcinogen increased rather than decreased tumors, the opposite of Wattenberg's result when it was given before.",
 "fun": "Indole-3-carbinol is a reminder that the stomach is part of the pharmacology. Swallow it and acid condenses it into a mixture including indolocarbazole, one of the most potent aryl hydrocarbon receptor ligands known, binding the same receptor dioxin uses, which means a cabbage-derived supplement activates the classic environmental-toxin sensor. Whether that is chemoprevention or the opposite has depended, in animal studies, entirely on whether it was given before or after the carcinogen.",
 "src": [
  "Wattenberg LW, Loub WD. Inhibition of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon-induced neoplasia by naturally occurring indoles. Cancer Research, 1978",
  "Michnovicz JJ, Bradlow HL. Induction of estradiol 2-hydroxylation in humans by dietary indole-3-carbinol. J Natl Cancer Inst, 1990",
  "Linus Pauling Institute Micronutrient Information Center, Indole-3-carbinol",
  "Bjeldanes LF et al. Aromatic hydrocarbon responsiveness-receptor agonists generated from indole-3-carbinol in vitro and in vivo. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA, 1991"
 ]
},
"sulforaphane": {
 "origin": "Sulforaphane is an isothiocyanate from broccoli and its relatives, sold as a dietary supplement; it is not a drug and there is no approved US product containing it. It owes its scientific existence to one laboratory. Paul Talalay, a pharmacologist at Johns Hopkins, spent decades on the enzymes that cells use to dispose of electrophiles and set out to find the dietary compounds that turn them on. In 1992 Yuesheng Zhang, Talalay, Cho and Gary Posner reported in PNAS that they had isolated from broccoli the major inducer of these protective enzymes and identified it as sulforaphane. In 1994 they showed it blocked carcinogen-induced mammary tumors in rats. Then in 1997 Jed Fahey, Zhang and Talalay published the finding that made it a consumer product: three-day-old broccoli sprouts contain twenty to fifty times more of its precursor than mature broccoli. Hopkins patented the sprouts, a company was founded, and the patents were later invalidated in court on the ground that one cannot patent growing a known vegetable. Talalay died in 2019.",
 "timeline": [
  [
   "1992",
   "Zhang, Talalay, Cho and Posner isolate sulforaphane from broccoli as the major inducer of protective phase II enzymes."
  ],
  [
   "1994",
   "Sulforaphane is shown to reduce carcinogen-induced mammary tumors in rats."
  ],
  [
   "1997",
   "Fahey, Zhang and Talalay report that young broccoli sprouts carry far more glucoraphanin than mature broccoli."
  ],
  [
   "1999",
   "Itoh, Yamamoto and colleagues identify Keap1 as the cytoplasmic sensor that holds Nrf2 in check."
  ],
  [
   "2014",
   "Broccoli sprout beverage trials in Qidong, China, show increased urinary excretion of airborne pollutant and aflatoxin conjugates."
  ],
  [
   "2014",
   "A small randomized trial reports behavioral changes with sulforaphane in young men with autism spectrum disorder."
  ]
 ],
 "cell": "Broccoli stores the glucosinolate glucoraphanin and keeps myrosinase in separate cells; chewing or chopping ruptures both and the enzyme hydrolyzes glucoraphanin to sulforaphane. Sulforaphane is an electrophile, and its isothiocyanate carbon is attacked by thiols. Its target is Keap1, the substrate adaptor of a Cullin-3 ubiquitin ligase complex that normally grips the transcription factor Nrf2 and keeps it continuously ubiquitinated and destroyed. Sulforaphane modifies reactive cysteines on Keap1, including the sensor cysteine 151, which distorts the complex so that Nrf2 is no longer tagged for degradation. Newly made Nrf2 accumulates, enters the nucleus, pairs with small MAF proteins and binds antioxidant response elements, inducing NQO1, heme oxygenase-1, glutathione S-transferases, glutamate-cysteine ligase, thioredoxin reductase and UGT1A1. The elegance is that the same thiol reactivity that makes sulforaphane a sensor signal also makes it a substrate: glutathione S-transferases conjugate it and send it down the mercapturic acid pathway. Reported histone deacetylase and NF-kB effects come from cell culture.",
 "body": [
  [
   "Liver and gut epithelium",
   "The main site of phase II enzyme induction, raising glutathione conjugation and quinone reductase capacity, measurable as faster urinary excretion of pollutant conjugates in human trials."
  ],
  [
   "Stomach",
   "Small trials in Helicobacter pylori infection reported reduced markers of colonization and gastritis, with inconsistent eradication."
  ],
  [
   "Airways",
   "Studied for Nrf2-mediated protection against oxidant air pollution, with mixed human results."
  ],
  [
   "Brain",
   "The basis of autism and schizophrenia trials, on an oxidative stress and inflammation rationale; the trials are small and the findings preliminary."
  ],
  [
   "Thyroid",
   "Glucosinolate breakdown also yields thiocyanate ion, which competes with iodide at the sodium-iodide symporter, a theoretical goitrogenic concern with concentrated products."
  ],
  [
   "Gut lumen",
   "Isothiocyanates and sulfur breakdown products account for the gas, bloating and sulfurous taste that people notice."
  ]
 ],
 "journey": "The pharmacokinetics are mostly a question of what was swallowed. Preformed sulforaphane is well absorbed, with something like 70 to 80 percent bioavailability and a peak at one to three hours. Glucoraphanin alone is not: without plant myrosinase it depends on the myrosinase-like activity of colonic bacteria, which varies several-fold between people, and bioavailability falls to roughly a tenth. Cooking broccoli inactivates myrosinase, which is why well-cooked broccoli and raw sprouts deliver very different amounts. Absorbed sulforaphane is conjugated with glutathione by glutathione S-transferases and processed through the mercapturic acid pathway to cysteinylglycine, cysteine and N-acetylcysteine conjugates, which are excreted in urine within about twelve hours. The half-life is a few hours, so the enzyme induction outlasts the compound by days.",
 "sidefx": "Acute toxicity in humans appears low, and the common complaints, gas, bloating, nausea and an unpleasant sulfurous taste or odor, come from the sulfur chemistry itself rather than from Nrf2. The mechanistic cautions are more interesting than the symptoms. Nrf2 activation is cytoprotective in normal cells and is also what some tumors use to resist chemotherapy, so chronic pharmacologic activation is not obviously benign. Induction of UGT1A1 and glutathione S-transferases could in principle speed clearance of other drugs, which has been shown in cell systems without clear human consequence. Thiocyanate from glucosinolate breakdown is a theoretical thyroid concern at concentrated intakes. Isothiocyanates are also irritants, which is the same reactivity at work on mucosa.",
 "fun": "Talalay's broccoli sprout finding is the rare piece of nutritional biochemistry that produced both a supermarket product and a lawsuit. Hopkins patented sprouting broccoli seeds for their glucoraphanin content, and in 2002 a federal appeals court threw the patents out, reasoning that growers had been sprouting and selling these seeds for a century and that discovering why that was good for people did not make the vegetable new.",
 "src": [
  "Zhang Y, Talalay P, Cho CG, Posner GH. A major inducer of anticarcinogenic protective enzymes from broccoli: isolation and elucidation of structure. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA, 1992",
  "Fahey JW, Zhang Y, Talalay P. Broccoli sprouts: an exceptionally rich source of inducers of enzymes that protect against chemical carcinogens. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA, 1997",
  "Kensler TW et al. Modulation of the metabolism of airborne pollutants by glucoraphanin-rich and sulforaphane-rich broccoli sprout beverages in Qidong, China. Cancer Prevention Research, 2014",
  "Linus Pauling Institute Micronutrient Information Center, Isothiocyanates"
 ]
},
"piperine": {
 "origin": "Piperine is the alkaloid that makes black pepper hot, and it is a food constituent and supplement ingredient rather than a drug: no approved US product contains it. Pepper, Piper nigrum, came from the Malabar coast of India and was for centuries among the most valuable goods in long-distance trade, so much so that Alaric's ransom for Rome in 408 is recorded as including three thousand pounds of it. The molecule was isolated in 1819 by Hans Christian Oersted, the Danish chemist better known for discovering that an electric current deflects a compass needle, who identified piperine as the pungent principle of pepper; it turned out to be an amide of piperic acid with piperidine. Its modern career came from a different direction. From the late 1970s, Chand Krishen Atal and colleagues at the Regional Research Laboratory in Jammu, India, showed that piperine raised blood levels of co-administered drugs and coined the term bioavailability enhancer. In 1998 Shoba and colleagues reported in Planta Medica that piperine raised curcumin bioavailability roughly twentyfold in humans, which is why it now appears in almost every turmeric supplement sold.",
 "timeline": [
  [
   "1819",
   "Hans Christian Oersted isolates piperine from black pepper."
  ],
  [
   "1985",
   "Atal and colleagues in Jammu describe piperine as a bioavailability enhancer and publish interaction studies."
  ],
  [
   "1991",
   "Small human studies report raised levels of phenytoin, propranolol and theophylline when given with piperine."
  ],
  [
   "1998",
   "Shoba and colleagues report an approximately twentyfold increase in curcumin bioavailability with piperine in humans."
  ],
  [
   "2005",
   "McNamara, Randall and Gunthorpe characterize piperine as an agonist at the human TRPV1 receptor."
  ]
 ],
 "cell": "Piperine does two unrelated things. The first is to interfere with drug disposal. It inhibits CYP3A4 and several other cytochrome P450 enzymes, inhibits P-glycoprotein, the efflux pump that returns absorbed molecules to the gut lumen, and inhibits UDP-glucuronosyltransferases in both intestine and liver. Those three mechanisms act on the same stretch of the first-pass barrier, so inhibiting them together can raise systemic exposure to a co-ingested compound far more than inhibiting any one alone. Curcumin is the showcase case because its clearance is dominated by intestinal and hepatic glucuronidation, exactly the step piperine blocks. Piperine has also been reported to increase enterocyte membrane fluidity and to inhibit monoamine oxidase in vitro. The second action is sensory and entirely separate: piperine is an agonist at TRPV1, the capsaicin receptor, a non-selective cation channel on nociceptive C and A-delta fibers, and also activates TRPA1. Opening TRPV1 admits calcium and sodium, depolarizes the nerve ending and is read centrally as heat and pain.",
 "body": [
  [
   "Small intestine",
   "The main site of the interaction effect: inhibited CYP3A4, P-glycoprotein and glucuronidation in the enterocyte let more of a co-ingested compound through intact."
  ],
  [
   "Liver",
   "Further inhibition of CYP-mediated oxidation and of glucuronidation reduces first-pass clearance of substrates arriving by the portal vein."
  ],
  [
   "Mouth, tongue and throat",
   "TRPV1 and TRPA1 activation on sensory nerve endings produces the burn of black pepper, and inhaled pepper dust triggers the sneeze reflex."
  ],
  [
   "Stomach",
   "Direct irritation and TRPV1-mediated sensory effects explain heartburn and gastric discomfort with concentrated extracts."
  ],
  [
   "Other drugs",
   "Not an organ, but the place the effect actually shows up: concentrations of narrow-margin drugs can rise without the person or prescriber knowing a supplement was involved."
  ]
 ],
 "journey": "Piperine is lipophilic and absorbed well from the gut, with plasma concentrations peaking within a few hours. It is metabolized in the liver by CYP enzymes with oxidation and opening of the methylenedioxy ring, and metabolites are excreted in urine and bile; the parent has a short residence time. For once the pharmacokinetics of the compound matter less than its effect on everything else: piperine does its work during the absorption and first-pass window of whatever it was taken with, so timing relative to the other compound determines the size of the interaction. Culinary pepper delivers small amounts spread through a meal and is not generally regarded as an interaction risk. Concentrated standardized extracts, taken deliberately alongside something else, are the concern.",
 "sidefx": "Piperine alone causes little beyond gastrointestinal irritation, heartburn and the burning sensation that TRPV1 activation produces. The real adverse effect is other drugs becoming more potent than intended, and it traces to exactly the same mechanism that the product is sold for: a bioavailability enhancer cannot raise curcumin absorption without also raising the absorption of CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein substrates that happen to be in the same gut at the same time. Immunosuppressants, some anticonvulsants, and other narrow-margin drugs are the theoretical concern, and phenytoin, propranolol and theophylline have documented increases in small human studies. The interaction is easy to miss because piperine is listed as a minor ingredient, often under a trade name, on a product the patient thinks of as turmeric.",
 "fun": "Two things about piperine are worth keeping. It was isolated by Hans Christian Oersted, the same man who discovered electromagnetism, which makes black pepper an odd footnote in the history of physics. And its presence in turmeric supplements is not a flavoring or a tradition but a deliberate pharmacokinetic intervention: curcumin by itself is almost unabsorbable, and piperine is there to block the glucuronidation that would otherwise dispose of it.",
 "src": [
  "Shoba G, Joy D, Joseph T, Majeed M, Rajendran R, Srinivas PS. Influence of piperine on the pharmacokinetics of curcumin in animals and human volunteers. Planta Medica, 1998",
  "McNamara FN, Randall A, Gunthorpe MJ. Effects of piperine, the pungent component of black pepper, at the human vanilloid receptor TRPV1. Br J Pharmacol, 2005",
  "Atal CK, Dubey RK, Singh J. Biochemical basis of enhanced drug bioavailability by piperine. J Pharmacol Exp Ther, 1985",
  "Bano G et al. Effect of piperine on bioavailability and pharmacokinetics of propranolol and theophylline in healthy volunteers. Eur J Clin Pharmacol, 1991"
 ]
}
});

;
DB.DEEP = DB.DEEP || {};
Object.assign(DB.DEEP, {
"bp-ltowb": {
 "origin": "Whole blood is the oldest transfusion product and, in military medicine, the newest again. Karl Landsteiner described the A, B and O agglutination groups in Vienna in 1901, work that earned him the 1930 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, and Reuben Ottenberg used that typing in New York in 1907 to pick a group O donor whose cells no recipient antibody attacked. In 1917 Oswald Hope Robertson, an American physician serving with the British Expeditionary Force, kept citrate-glucose anticoagulated group O blood in iced ammunition boxes behind casualty clearing stations near Cambrai, creating the first blood depot. Whole blood remained the main resuscitation product through the Second World War, Korea and Vietnam. Component therapy then replaced it in civilian blood banking, and American military practice followed. The wars in Iraq and Afghanistan reversed that. The 75th Ranger Regiment's Ranger O Low titer program and the Armed Services Blood Program built a supply of group O units screened for low anti-A and anti-B antibody titers, so a single bag could go to any casualty before a type was known. Cold-stored low-titer group O whole blood now sits first in the TCCC order for hemorrhagic shock.",
 "timeline": [
  ["1901","Karl Landsteiner describes the ABO agglutination groups in Vienna."],
  ["1917","Oswald Robertson sets up iced citrated group O blood depots behind British casualty clearing stations."],
  ["1930","Landsteiner receives the Nobel Prize for the discovery of the human blood groups."],
  ["1960s","Civilian blood banking shifts to component therapy and whole blood leaves most hospitals."],
  ["2000s","The Ranger O Low titer program returns cold-stored low-titer group O whole blood to the point of injury."]
 ],
 "cell": "A unit of whole blood delivers hemoglobin, plasma proteins and platelets in roughly the proportions a bleeding casualty is losing them. Hemoglobin binds oxygen cooperatively, and its affinity is tuned by 2,3-diphosphoglycerate, a glycolytic intermediate that binds the deoxygenated beta chains and stabilizes the low-affinity T state. Red cells in citrate anticoagulant acidify, glycolysis slows, and 2,3-DPG falls toward zero within about two weeks, so stored cells still bind oxygen well but release it reluctantly: the oxygen dissociation curve shifts left until the cells rebuild 2,3-DPG over hours to a day in the recipient. The rest of the storage lesion is metabolic as well. ATP falls, membrane lipid is shed as microvesicles, the biconcave disc becomes an echinocyte and then a sphere with less capacity to deform through a capillary, potassium leaks down its gradient into the supernatant, and free hemoglobin and vesicles scavenge nitric oxide. The plasma in the unit carries fibrinogen and the full factor cascade. Platelets held cold lose circulating lifespan but become more adhesive, because chilling clusters the GPIb receptor and primes the cell.",
 "body": [
  ["Blood and oxygen delivery","Restored hemoglobin mass raises arterial oxygen content, and restored volume raises stroke volume, so oxygen delivery improves on both terms at once rather than only one."],
  ["Coagulation system","Plasma factors and platelets arrive together with the red cells, so the clotting capacity of the circulation is diluted far less than it is by red cells or crystalloid alone."],
  ["Heart and vessels","Citrate chelates ionized calcium, and calcium is the trigger for cardiac and vascular smooth muscle contraction, so rapid transfusion can lower contractility and vascular tone."],
  ["Kidneys","Free hemoglobin and microvesicles from damaged stored cells scavenge nitric oxide and are filtered into the tubules, which contributes to renal vasoconstriction and pigment injury."],
  ["Immune system","Donor red cell antigens the recipient lacks, Rh D and Kell above all, can drive lasting IgG antibodies that complicate later transfusion and, in females, later pregnancy."]
 ],
 "journey": "Transfused red cells are not metabolized like a drug; they join the circulation and live out whatever remains of their lifespan, with the majority of a stored unit still circulating a day later and the survivors cleared over following weeks as splenic and hepatic macrophages recognize aged, stiffened membranes. The iron released is recycled through transferrin and stored as ferritin and hemosiderin. Plasma proteins have shorter careers: fibrinogen persists for days, while factor V and factor VIII fall away in hours. Citrate is taken up by the liver and metabolized through the citric acid cycle into bicarbonate within minutes in a well-perfused patient, which is exactly why a cold, hypoperfused casualty accumulates it instead. Platelets from a cold unit are pulled from the circulation within hours by hepatic receptors that recognize desialylated surface glycans.",
 "sidefx": "The harms trace either to the chemistry of the bag or to immune recognition. Citrate binds ionized calcium, and calcium is the cofactor that lets clotting factors assemble on phospholipid surfaces and lets muscle contract, so hypocalcemia shows up as coagulopathy and falling blood pressure together. Cold product cools the core, and both the enzymes of the cascade and platelet function slow steeply below normal temperature, which links hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy and hypocalcemia into the lethal diamond, each worsening the others. Potassium that leaked into the supernatant during storage arrives as a bolus. Donor anti-A and anti-B in group O plasma can fix complement on a non-O recipient's own red cells, though the low-titer threshold, soluble A and B substance in recipient plasma and the limited plasma volume keep that uncommon. Donor leukocyte antibodies and bioactive lipids prime recipient neutrophils trapped in pulmonary capillaries, the two-event model behind TRALI.",
 "fun": "Robertson's 1917 blood depot ran on group O blood in ammunition boxes packed with ice and sawdust, and he reported units still working after weeks of storage. Whole blood then held the field for half a century, left it for roughly forty years of component therapy, and came back.",
 "src": [
  "AABB Circular of Information for the Use of Human Blood and Blood Components",
  "Joint Trauma System CPG: Whole Blood Transfusion",
  "Robertson OH, Transfusion with preserved red blood cells, British Medical Journal 1918",
  "TCCC Guidelines 2026"
 ]
},
"bp-fwb": {
 "origin": "Before anticoagulants, transfusion meant a direct connection between two people. Alexis Carrel's vascular suture technique let George Crile in Cleveland join a donor artery to a recipient vein in 1906, with the donor's own blood pressure driving the transfusion. Albert Hustin demonstrated citrated transfusion in 1914, and Richard Lewisohn at Mount Sinai worked out a non-toxic sodium citrate concentration in 1915, which freed blood from the donor's arm. Direct donor-to-casualty transfusion survived anyway, because it needs no refrigeration and no supply line. It carried Korea and Vietnam when the stored supply ran short, was used by Task Force Ranger in Mogadishu in 1993, and was then formalized by Norwegian naval special operations forces and by American special operations into what is now called the walking blood bank: a rostered donor pool inside the unit itself, typed, titered and infection-tested before deployment. Fresh whole blood is not a licensed product. It is blood collected under emergency medical direction, which is why doctrine ties it to trained personnel and why every collection is documented so donor and recipient can be traced later.",
 "timeline": [
  ["1906","George Crile transfuses by joining a donor artery directly to a recipient vein."],
  ["1915","Richard Lewisohn establishes a safe sodium citrate concentration for anticoagulated blood."],
  ["1993","Medics with Task Force Ranger in Mogadishu transfuse fresh whole blood drawn from fellow soldiers."],
  ["2000s","Norwegian and American special operations formalize the rostered walking blood bank."]
 ],
 "cell": "Fresh whole blood is defined by what has not yet happened to it. Platelets collected and held near body temperature keep their discoid shape, intact dense and alpha granules, and a full aggregation response to ADP, thrombin, collagen and thromboxane A2. Factor V and factor VIII, the two labile cofactors, are still near donor levels; both decay in storage because they are large protease-sensitive proteins whose stability depends on carriage by von Willebrand factor and on intact protease inhibition. Red cells have full ATP and full 2,3-diphosphoglycerate, so hemoglobin unloads oxygen at its normal P50 rather than on the left-shifted curve of a stored unit, and the membrane is still a deformable biconcave disc. Nitric oxide handling is intact rather than consumed by free hemoglobin and microvesicles. The trade is immunologic: the unit also holds viable donor T lymphocytes, which gamma irradiation removes from licensed products. An immunocompetent recipient usually clears them, but a donor sharing HLA haplotypes can leave cells the recipient does not recognize as foreign.",
 "body": [
  ["Coagulation system","Warm platelets and undegraded factor V and factor VIII give this product the highest intrinsic clot-forming capacity of any field blood product."],
  ["Blood and oxygen delivery","Red cells with intact 2,3-DPG and normal membrane deformability unload oxygen at tissue capillary level without the lag a stored unit shows."],
  ["Bone marrow and skin","Viable donor lymphocytes that engraft attack marrow, skin and gut in transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease, a reaction that irradiation of licensed components prevents."],
  ["Donor circulation","The donor loses a unit of their own blood volume and red cell mass, with a measurable fall in exercise capacity until plasma volume and then hemoglobin recover."],
  ["Immune system","Unirradiated, non-leukoreduced blood carries the donor's white cells and their cytokines, which raises the chance of a febrile reaction and of HLA sensitization."]
 ],
 "journey": "The components take separate paths once transfused. Red cells circulate for weeks and are cleared by splenic and hepatic macrophages as their membranes age. Platelets transfused warm behave like the recipient's own and survive days rather than the hours a cold-stored platelet lasts, because their surface glycans have not been desialylated by chilling. Factor VIII and factor V are consumed or inactivated within hours, fibrinogen over days, while albumin and immunoglobulin from the donor plasma persist far longer. Citrate from the collection bag is metabolized by the liver to bicarbonate in minutes when perfusion is adequate. Donor lymphocytes are normally eliminated by recipient cytotoxic T cells within days, and infectious agents carried in the unit follow their own incubation, which is why recipient follow-up testing is part of the record rather than an afterthought.",
 "sidefx": "The risks follow from collecting blood outside a blood bank. A typing or labeling error delivers ABO-incompatible red cells, and recipient IgM anti-A or anti-B fixes complement on them, lysing cells inside the vessels; free hemoglobin scavenges nitric oxide and precipitates in tubules while complement fragments and cytokines drive fever, shock and disseminated intravascular coagulation. Field venipuncture and a non-sterile environment raise bacterial contamination, and some skin organisms grow well at ambient temperature. Rapid-test infection screening has a window period that laboratory testing closes, so transmitted infection is a documented later risk. Without irradiation, donor T cells can engraft and attack recipient marrow, skin and gut, a reaction more likely when donor and recipient share HLA haplotypes, as relatives and genetically similar populations do. Citrate still binds ionized calcium, and an unwarmed unit still cools the core.",
 "fun": "In the direct-transfusion era the donor was part of the equipment: Crile's donor and recipient lay side by side with their vessels joined, and no one could measure how much blood had crossed. The walking blood bank is the same idea with a bag, a roster and an anticoagulant.",
 "src": [
  "Joint Trauma System CPG: Whole Blood Transfusion",
  "Armed Services Blood Program guidance on walking blood banks",
  "AABB Circular of Information for the Use of Human Blood and Blood Components"
 ]
},
"bp-prbc": {
 "origin": "Red cells became a separate product because the blood bank needed them to be. In Barcelona in 1936 Frederic Duran-Jorda built the first large-scale stored blood transfusion service, pooling grouped donations into refrigerated glass bottles and moving them to the Aragon front by converted van, while Norman Bethune ran a mobile transfusion unit for the Republican side of the Spanish Civil War. Glass set the limits: splitting a donation into parts without breaking sterility was not practical. Carl Walter and W. P. Murphy introduced the sealed plastic blood bag in 1950, and the satellite bags that followed let one donation be centrifuged and separated into red cells, plasma and platelets in a closed system. Through the 1960s and 1970s hospitals adopted component therapy, which let a single donation serve several recipients, gave each patient only the fraction they lacked, and allowed plasma to be frozen while red cells kept for weeks. CPDA-1 and then additive solutions stretched red cell storage further. For a bleeding trauma patient, though, taking blood apart and putting it back together unevenly produced a resuscitation rich in oxygen carriers and poor in everything else.",
 "timeline": [
  ["1936","Frederic Duran-Jorda organizes the Barcelona stored blood transfusion service during the Spanish Civil War."],
  ["1950","Carl Walter and W. P. Murphy introduce the plastic blood bag, making closed-system separation possible."],
  ["1960s","Component therapy spreads through civilian blood banking and whole blood leaves most hospitals."],
  ["1970s","CPDA-1 and then additive solutions extend red cell storage from weeks toward six weeks."],
  ["2010s","Trauma resuscitation moves off crystalloid onto balanced component ratios, and then back toward whole blood."]
 ],
 "cell": "A red cell is a bag of hemoglobin with just enough metabolism to stay useful. Hemoglobin is a tetramer whose four heme irons bind oxygen cooperatively, and the sigmoid dissociation curve is shifted by protons, carbon dioxide, temperature and 2,3-diphosphoglycerate. 2,3-DPG is a glycolytic side product that binds in the central cavity between the beta chains of deoxyhemoglobin and holds the molecule in its low-affinity state, so it exists to make unloading easy. Storage dismantles that arrangement. Citrate acidifies the unit, phosphofructokinase slows in the acid, and 2,3-DPG falls toward zero in about two weeks, shifting the curve left so a freshly transfused old unit binds oxygen more tightly than the patient's own cells; the levels rebuild over hours to a day. ATP falls alongside, the membrane sheds microvesicles and loses the biconcave shape that lets a cell fold through a capillary, potassium leaks out into the supernatant, and free hemoglobin consumes nitric oxide. That bundle of changes is the storage lesion. What a red cell unit does not carry is meaningful platelet or fibrinogen content.",
 "body": [
  ["Blood and oxygen delivery","Added hemoglobin mass raises arterial oxygen content, though a long-stored unit unloads oxygen poorly for the first hours until 2,3-DPG is regenerated."],
  ["Coagulation system","Red cells suspended in additive solution dilute circulating fibrinogen, factors and platelets, so the clot-forming capacity of the blood falls as the hematocrit rises."],
  ["Microcirculation","Stiffened, spherical stored cells deform less through capillaries, and the free hemoglobin and vesicles they shed scavenge nitric oxide, blunting local vasodilation."],
  ["Kidneys","Pigment load from hemolyzed cells and nitric oxide scavenging in the renal vasculature contribute to tubular injury, more so with older units and large volumes."],
  ["Heart and vessels","Volume arrives faster than it can be accommodated in a small, older or failing heart, raising filling pressures and driving hydrostatic pulmonary edema."]
 ],
 "journey": "Red cells transfused into the circulation are cleared not by metabolism but by senescence. A large majority of a unit's cells survive the first day, and the remainder are removed over the following weeks as splenic macrophages recognize phosphatidylserine exposure, loss of CD47 signaling and reduced deformability. The non-viable fraction, higher in older units, is cleared within hours, which releases free hemoglobin and heme into plasma to be bound by haptoglobin and hemopexin, and iron is recycled to transferrin and stored in macrophage ferritin. The additive solution carries adenine, mannitol, dextrose and phosphate, all of which are handled as ordinary nutrients or renally excreted. Citrate goes to the liver and becomes bicarbonate. Potassium from the supernatant redistributes intracellularly once insulin and the sodium-potassium pump catch up, assuming the patient is not acidotic and the kidneys are working.",
 "sidefx": "An ABO-incompatible unit is destroyed in the circulation by recipient IgM, which fixes complement through to the membrane attack complex; free hemoglobin scavenges nitric oxide and obstructs tubules, and complement fragments with interleukin-6, interleukin-8 and tumor necrosis factor produce fever, hypotension, renal injury and disseminated intravascular coagulation. Antibodies to Kidd, Kell and Duffy antigens can do the same in a sensitized recipient, or surface days later as a delayed hemolytic reaction. Donor antibodies to recipient leukocytes, and bioactive lipids accumulated in storage, prime neutrophils in the pulmonary capillary bed and produce TRALI, while sheer volume in a vulnerable circulation produces the hydrostatic edema of TACO. Citrate lowers ionized calcium. Supernatant potassium can arrive faster than it can be redistributed. Unwarmed units cool the core, slowing cascade enzymes and platelets and feeding the lethal diamond of hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy and hypocalcemia.",
 "fun": "A red cell unit is suspended to a hematocrit near sixty percent and holds essentially no working platelets and little fibrinogen, so resuscitating with red cells alone steadily dilutes whatever clotting capacity is left. That arithmetic is what balanced ratios, and then whole blood, were brought back to fix.",
 "src": [
  "AABB Circular of Information for the Use of Human Blood and Blood Components",
  "Hess JR, Red cell storage, Journal of Proteomics 2010",
  "European guideline on the management of major bleeding and coagulopathy following trauma"
 ]
},
"bp-plasma": {
 "origin": "Plasma was the product that made far-forward transfusion possible, because it carried no red cells and so needed no blood type. Charles Richard Drew, working under John Scudder at Columbia, wrote his 1940 doctoral thesis on banked blood and showed that plasma separated from red cells kept far longer than whole blood and could be given across ABO groups. That year he was made medical supervisor of the Blood for Britain project, which collected plasma in New York and shipped it to a Britain under bombardment, and he designed the collection, sterility and record systems that the American Red Cross national blood program then adopted, with Drew as its first director in 1941. The American military directed that blood from Black donors be excluded, and then that it be collected and labeled separately for segregated use. Drew said plainly that there was no scientific basis for sorting donated blood by race, resigned from the program in 1941, and returned to Howard University, where he trained surgeons until his death in 1950. The segregation policy stayed in place through the war.",
 "timeline": [
  ["1940","Charles Drew defends his thesis on banked blood and takes charge of the Blood for Britain plasma project."],
  ["1941","Drew resigns from the American Red Cross blood program over the segregation of donated blood by race."],
  ["1970s","Closed-system plastic bags make fresh frozen plasma a routine blood bank component."],
  ["2012","PROMMTT reports that higher early plasma to red cell ratios track with lower mortality in bleeding trauma patients."],
  ["2018","PAMPer finds prehospital thawed plasma during air medical transport lowers 30-day mortality."]
 ],
 "cell": "Plasma is the solvent phase of blood and carries the whole coagulation proteome at donor concentration: fibrinogen, prothrombin, factors V, VII, VIII, IX, X, XI and XIII, von Willebrand factor, plus the brakes, which are antithrombin, protein C, protein S and tissue factor pathway inhibitor. Thrombin generated on tissue factor cleaves fibrinopeptides A and B from fibrinogen so the monomers polymerize into protofibrils, and factor XIIIa cross-links the gamma and alpha chains into a clot that resists plasmin. Fibrinogen is the factor that reaches a critical level first in massive hemorrhage: it is consumed in bulk into clot, cleaved by plasmin during hyperfibrinolysis, diluted by whatever is infused, and replaced only slowly by hepatic synthesis, while cold and acidosis further impair its polymerization. Plasma also carries albumin and the sphingosine-1-phosphate and protein components that maintain the endothelial glycocalyx, the sugar-protein layer on the luminal vessel surface that is shed in shock; restoring it reduces capillary leak, which is the leading explanation for the plasma effect in the prehospital trials.",
 "body": [
  ["Coagulation system","Factor and fibrinogen levels rise toward donor concentration, so thrombin generation and clot strength recover in blood that had been diluted and consumed."],
  ["Endothelium","Plasma proteins restore the shed glycocalyx on the luminal vessel surface, reducing capillary permeability and the interstitial edema that follows shock."],
  ["Lungs","Plasma-rich components are the ones most often implicated in TRALI, because donor antibodies to recipient leukocyte antigens travel in plasma rather than in cells."],
  ["Brain","Plasma is given in severe traumatic brain injury for its effect on coagulopathy and the vascular lining rather than for oxygen carriage, since it holds no red cells."],
  ["Heart and vessels","Oncotically active protein holds infused volume in the vascular space, which supports preload but can overload a small or failing circulation."]
 ],
 "journey": "Plasma distributes into the plasma volume it came from, so the infused volume stays largely intravascular while its oncotic protein holds it there. The factors then clear on their own timetables. Factor VII has the shortest span, a matter of hours, followed by factor VIII and factor V; factor IX and factor X persist longer; fibrinogen and factor XIII last days. Each is consumed into clot, inactivated by antithrombin, protein C and plasmin, or cleared by hepatic receptors. Citrate in the unit goes to the liver and is oxidized to bicarbonate, which is rapid in a perfused patient and slow in a cold one. Albumin recirculates through lymph over weeks. Frozen plasma loses some factor V and factor VIII activity in the thaw and more over days of refrigerated storage, so a long-thawed unit carries full fibrinogen with reduced labile cofactors.",
 "sidefx": "Plasma concentrates the immunologic risks of transfusion because the antibodies travel in plasma. Donor antibodies to recipient HLA or human neutrophil antigens bind neutrophils sequestered in pulmonary capillaries; in a patient whose endothelium is already primed by trauma or sepsis, the activated neutrophils release proteases and reactive oxygen species and the capillary barrier fails, giving the non-hydrostatic pulmonary edema of TRALI. Oncotically held volume in a small or failing circulation raises hydrostatic pressure instead, giving TACO. Recipients with IgA deficiency and anti-IgA antibodies can react anaphylactically to donor IgA, and smaller allergic reactions follow donor plasma proteins generally. Group O plasma given to a group A, B or AB recipient delivers anti-A and anti-B that can coat and lyse the recipient's own cells. Citrate chelates ionized calcium, and an unwarmed unit cools the core.",
 "fun": "Drew's reputation outran the facts after his death: the widely repeated story that he bled out after a car crash because a segregated hospital turned him away is not what happened, since he was treated at the nearest hospital in North Carolina. The story persisted because the policy he resigned over was entirely real.",
 "src": [
  "AABB Circular of Information for the Use of Human Blood and Blood Components",
  "Holcomb JB et al, PROPPR trial, JAMA 2015",
  "Sperry JL et al, PAMPer trial, New England Journal of Medicine 2018",
  "Love S, One Blood: The Death and Resurrection of Charles R. Drew"
 ]
},
"bp-dried-plasma": {
 "origin": "Dried plasma carried the Second World War's transfusion logistics. Earl Flosdorf and Stuart Mudd adapted lyophilization to plasma in the late 1930s, and Max Strumia in Pennsylvania developed the field package that followed: a sealed container of plasma powder, a container of sterile water and a transfer set, stable at ambient temperature and usable without typing or crossmatch. Millions of units were produced, and it was the standard American and British forward resuscitation fluid from Pearl Harbor through North Africa to the Normandy beaches. It was also pooled from hundreds of donors per lot, and pooling concentrated a hazard nobody could yet name: serum hepatitis. Recipient attack rates were high enough that the United States stopped producing pooled dried plasma in 1968. Europe kept the idea and changed the manufacturing instead. Germany's LyoPlas N is single-donor and quarantined; the French military blood service produces a pooled, pathogen-reduced freeze-dried plasma treated with amotosalen and ultraviolet light, carried at the point of injury. American forces have used the French product under an FDA emergency use authorization since 2018 while domestic freeze-dried plasma works toward licensure.",
 "timeline": [
  ["1930s","Flosdorf, Mudd and Strumia develop freeze-dried plasma and a field-packaged reconstitution kit."],
  ["1941","Dried plasma becomes the standard American and British forward resuscitation fluid of the Second World War."],
  ["1968","The United States halts production of pooled dried plasma because of serum hepatitis transmission."],
  ["2010s","France adds amotosalen pathogen reduction to its freeze-dried plasma and fields it at the point of injury."],
  ["2018","American forces begin using French freeze-dried plasma under an FDA emergency use authorization."]
 ],
 "cell": "Freeze-drying works by keeping water out of the liquid phase entirely. Plasma is frozen, then held under vacuum so the ice sublimes directly to vapor, and a secondary drying step pulls residual bound water down to a few percent. Proteins survive this because the frozen matrix and its buffer salts and sugars hold them in place while the hydration shell leaves, so the folded conformation is preserved rather than denatured at an air-water interface. Recovery is not complete: fibrinogen and the vitamin K dependent factors come through well, while factor VIII and factor V, the labile cofactors, lose a share of activity in freezing, drying and reconstitution. Once water is added, the protein chemistry is ordinary plasma chemistry. Thrombin cleaves fibrinogen to fibrin, factor XIIIa cross-links it, and the plasma proteins that maintain the endothelial glycocalyx go back to work. Pathogen reduction with amotosalen cross-links nucleic acid so viruses, bacteria and donor lymphocytes cannot replicate, which is effective against enveloped viruses and weaker against non-enveloped ones.",
 "body": [
  ["Coagulation system","Reconstituted plasma raises fibrinogen and factor levels much as liquid plasma does, with somewhat lower factor VIII and factor V activity after drying."],
  ["Endothelium","The plasma proteins that rebuild the shed glycocalyx survive lyophilization, so the capillary-barrier effect attributed to plasma is retained."],
  ["Blood and oxygen delivery","There are no red cells in the product, so oxygen-carrying capacity is unchanged and the hematocrit falls as volume is added."],
  ["Lungs","Donor leukocyte antibodies survive drying, so TRALI remains possible; pooling dilutes any single donor's antibody while exposing the recipient to more donors."],
  ["Immune system","Pooled product from donors of all ABO groups has its anti-A and anti-B diluted by the pool itself, which is why a pooled unit goes to recipients of any type."]
 ],
 "journey": "Once reconstituted, the product follows plasma exactly. It distributes into the plasma volume and is held there by its oncotic protein. Factor VII clears in hours, factor VIII and factor V somewhat longer, factor IX and factor X longer still, and fibrinogen and factor XIII persist for days, each consumed into clot or inactivated by antithrombin, protein C and plasmin or cleared by hepatic receptors. Citrate present from the original collection is oxidized by the liver to bicarbonate. The one step unique to this product happens outside the body: the powder has to dissolve completely, because undissolved protein aggregate is particulate matter rather than a clotting factor, and the label sets the water volume and the window in which the reconstituted plasma is used. Shelf life before reconstitution runs in years at ambient temperature rather than hours or days.",
 "sidefx": "The adverse effects are plasma's, with one historical addition. Donor antibodies to recipient leukocyte antigens survive drying and can prime pulmonary neutrophils to produce TRALI, and the oncotic load can produce the hydrostatic edema of TACO in a vulnerable circulation. Recipients with IgA deficiency and anti-IgA can react anaphylactically. The addition is donor exposure: a pooled lot exposes one recipient to many donors at once, which is what made wartime dried plasma such an efficient vehicle for serum hepatitis before testing or inactivation existed. Modern products answer that differently, with single-donor quarantined manufacture in Germany and photochemical pathogen reduction in France, though amotosalen and ultraviolet treatment acts on nucleic acid and so is less effective against non-enveloped agents such as hepatitis A virus and parvovirus B19. Citrate still binds ionized calcium, and a unit reconstituted with cold water still cools the patient.",
 "fun": "Pooled plasma transmitted an agent for decades before anyone had a name for it. The Australia antigen Baruch Blumberg described in 1965 turned out to be hepatitis B surface antigen, and it finally explained what pooling hundreds of donors into one lot had been doing.",
 "src": [
  "Joint Trauma System CPG: Damage Control Resuscitation",
  "Pusateri AE et al, Dried plasma: state of the science, Transfusion 2016",
  "French Armed Forces Blood Transfusion Center product information for French lyophilized plasma"
 ]
},
"bp-platelets": {
 "origin": "Platelet transfusion began as a byproduct and became a product. William Duke reported in 1910 that whole blood transfusion raised the platelet count and stopped bleeding in thrombocytopenic patients. Emil Freireich and colleagues at the National Cancer Institute showed in the early 1960s that platelet concentrates prevented hemorrhagic death in leukemia, which turned platelets into a supply problem, and the plastic bag made concentrates practical to produce. Scott Murphy and Frank Gardner then settled the storage question in 1969: platelets held at room temperature survived in the recipient's circulation far better than platelets held at refrigerator temperature, and room-temperature storage with continuous agitation became the worldwide standard. That choice bought circulating lifespan and paid for it in bacterial growth and a shelf life of days, which is why platelets are the component hardest to put in a cooler and carry forward. Becker and colleagues had reported in 1973 that cold-stored platelets, though cleared quickly, actually controlled bleeding better. Military interest in bleeding rather than prophylaxis revived that finding, and cold-stored platelet products with longer shelf lives returned for actively bleeding patients.",
 "timeline": [
  ["1910","William Duke reports that whole blood transfusion raises the platelet count and stops bleeding."],
  ["1962","Freireich and colleagues show platelet concentrates prevent hemorrhagic death in leukemia."],
  ["1969","Murphy and Gardner show room-temperature platelets survive in the circulation far longer than refrigerated ones."],
  ["1973","Becker reports that cold-stored platelets, though cleared faster, control bleeding better."],
  ["2015","PROPPR compares 1:1:1 with 1:1:2 and finds more patients reach hemostasis on the platelet-rich ratio."]
 ],
 "cell": "A platelet is an anucleate cytoplasmic fragment built to recognize a hole. The GPIb-IX-V complex catches von Willebrand factor unrolled by shear on exposed subendothelium, GPVI and integrin alpha2beta1 bind collagen, and that contact triggers calcium release, shape change and granule secretion. Dense granules release ADP, which recruits further platelets through P2Y1 and P2Y12, while cyclooxygenase-1 and thromboxane synthase produce thromboxane A2, and alpha granules release fibrinogen, von Willebrand factor and factor V. Integrin alphaIIbbeta3 then shifts to its high-affinity form and bridges platelets through fibrinogen. Scramblase activity flips phosphatidylserine to the outer membrane, and that anionic surface is where the tenase and prothrombinase complexes assemble, so the platelet is also the floor the cascade is built on. Refrigeration changes this. Cold reorganizes membrane lipid rafts, clusters GPIb and primes the cell so it is more responsive to agonists, while enzymatic desialylation of surface glycans exposes galactose residues that hepatic Ashwell-Morell receptors recognize, clearing cold platelets within hours.",
 "body": [
  ["Coagulation system","Platelets form the primary plug and provide the phosphatidylserine surface on which tenase and prothrombinase assemble, so thrombin generation and clot retraction both depend on them."],
  ["Blood vessels","Adhesion, aggregation and granule release at a breached vessel wall close small vessel injuries that fibrin alone would not seal."],
  ["Brain","Intracranial bleeding is particularly sensitive to platelet number and function, since there is no room for a hematoma to expand into."],
  ["Lungs","Platelet units carry a large plasma volume and accumulated cytokines, so they account for a disproportionate share of febrile reactions and TRALI."],
  ["Immune system","Repeated exposure to donor HLA antigens generates antibodies that destroy transfused platelets rapidly, a state called platelet refractoriness."]
 ],
 "journey": "A platelet's normal lifespan in the circulation is about a week, ended by apoptosis and hepatic and splenic clearance, with roughly a third of the body's platelets pooled in the spleen at any moment. Transfused room-temperature platelets behave much the same, surviving days, while cold-stored platelets are cleared within hours by hepatic Ashwell-Morell and macrophage receptors that recognize the desialylated glycans chilling produces. Their functional contribution is not proportional to their survival: a rapidly cleared cold platelet is more adhesive while it is present. Consumption at an injury site shortens everything, since platelets incorporated into a clot are gone from circulation. The plasma that suspends them is handled as plasma, with citrate oxidized by the liver to bicarbonate and factor proteins consumed or cleared on their own schedules. Storage lesion in a platelet unit shows as falling pH, lactate accumulation and loss of the discoid shape.",
 "sidefx": "Room-temperature storage with agitation is a culture condition, so platelets are the component most likely to grow bacteria, and a contaminated unit delivers organisms and endotoxin straight into the bloodstream as fulminant sepsis. The same warm storage lets residual donor leukocytes keep producing interleukin-1, interleukin-6 and tumor necrosis factor, which accumulate in the supernatant and produce febrile non-hemolytic reactions; pre-storage leukoreduction cuts this by removing the cells before the cytokines build. The large plasma volume in an apheresis unit carries donor leukocyte antibodies that prime pulmonary neutrophils in TRALI, and carries anti-A and anti-B that can hemolyze a group A or B recipient's own red cells, a risk that weighs heaviest in small recipients. Repeated HLA exposure produces antibodies that clear later transfused platelets within minutes to hours. Citrate binds ionized calcium as in any transfusion.",
 "fun": "Platelet storage is the one blood bank rule written against intuition. The product kept near body temperature spoils in days, while the refrigerated product that keeps for weeks was set aside in 1969 for a reason, post-transfusion survival, that has little to do with how well it stops bleeding.",
 "src": [
  "AABB Circular of Information for the Use of Human Blood and Blood Components",
  "Murphy S, Gardner FH, Effect of storage temperature on maintenance of platelet viability, New England Journal of Medicine 1969",
  "Holcomb JB et al, PROPPR trial, JAMA 2015"
 ]
},
"bp-cryo": {
 "origin": "Cryoprecipitate was discovered by accident and changed hemophilia care before it had any role in trauma. Judith Graham Pool, working at Stanford, noticed in the early 1960s that when frozen plasma was thawed slowly in the cold, a small gelatinous residue stayed undissolved in the bottom of the bag, and that this cold-insoluble residue held most of the plasma's factor VIII activity in a fraction of the volume. She published the method in 1964 and 1965. For the first time a person with hemophilia A could receive enough factor VIII to cover surgery or a joint bleed without being given an impossible volume of plasma, and home treatment became realistic. Pooled lyophilized factor VIII concentrates followed, and with them the transmission of hepatitis and HIV through the hemophilia population in the late 1970s and early 1980s, which recombinant factor VIII later ended. Cryoprecipitate kept a second job it had held all along: it is the most concentrated fibrinogen a blood bank can produce from plasma. Trauma practice returned to it once fibrinogen was recognized as the factor that reaches a critical level first in massive hemorrhage.",
 "timeline": [
  ["1964","Judith Graham Pool publishes the cold-insoluble plasma fraction rich in factor VIII."],
  ["1970s","Lyophilized factor VIII concentrates largely replace cryoprecipitate in hemophilia A care."],
  ["1980s","Hepatitis and HIV transmission through pooled factor concentrates devastates the hemophilia population."],
  ["2000s","Pasteurized fibrinogen concentrate becomes the European small-volume alternative to cryoprecipitate."],
  ["2010s","Trauma research identifies fibrinogen as the first coagulation factor to reach critical levels in major hemorrhage."]
 ],
 "cell": "Cryoprecipitation is physical chemistry rather than biology. Thawing frozen plasma slowly at refrigerator temperature leaves the largest and least cold-soluble plasma proteins behind as a gel: fibrinogen, the factor VIII and von Willebrand factor complex, factor XIII and fibronectin. Fibrinogen is the substrate of the final common pathway. Thrombin cleaves fibrinopeptides A and B from its alpha and beta chains, exposing polymerization sites so monomers assemble into half-staggered protofibrils that bundle into fibers, and factor XIIIa then cross-links glutamine to lysine residues on the gamma and alpha chains, producing a clot that resists plasmin. Fibrinogen is also the ligand that integrin alphaIIbbeta3 uses to bridge platelet to platelet, so it builds both halves of the clot. It reaches a critical level first in massive hemorrhage for several reasons at once: it is consumed into clot in bulk, cleaved by plasmin during hyperfibrinolysis, diluted by anything infused, and replaced only slowly by hepatic synthesis, while hypothermia and acidosis further impair its polymerization. Von Willebrand factor in the fraction also restores the platelet tether to injured subendothelium.",
 "body": [
  ["Coagulation system","Fibrinogen, factor XIII and von Willebrand factor rise in a small volume, improving clot firmness and cross-linking rather than thrombin generation."],
  ["Blood vessels","Fibrinogen bridges platelets through integrin alphaIIbbeta3, and fibronectin contributes to adhesion at the injured vessel surface."],
  ["Liver","Fibrinogen is a hepatic acute-phase protein, so endogenous replacement depends on a liver that is perfused, warm and not failing."],
  ["Heart and vessels","The infused volume is small, so circulatory overload is a minor consideration compared with plasma, and it carries no oxygen-carrying capacity."],
  ["Immune system","Pooling donations into one dose exposes a recipient to several donors, and the residual plasma carries donor antibodies and small amounts of anti-A and anti-B."]
 ],
 "journey": "The constituents separate immediately after infusion. Fibrinogen distributes into plasma and the extravascular space and circulates with a span of several days, consumed into clot, degraded by plasmin into fibrin degradation products and D-dimer, and replaced by hepatic synthesis that rises as an acute-phase response over a day or more. Factor VIII circulates bound to von Willebrand factor, which protects it from clearance, and falls over hours; free factor VIII without its carrier is cleared much faster. Von Willebrand factor multimers are trimmed by ADAMTS13 under shear and cleared hepatically. Factor XIII, a transglutaminase, persists for days. Fibronectin joins the plasma pool and the extracellular matrix. Citrate carried over from the original collection is oxidized by the liver to bicarbonate. The unit itself is time-limited once thawed because fibrinogen in a thawed bag slowly becomes less soluble and the product is an open system after pooling.",
 "sidefx": "Because the infused volume is small, the dominant risks are immunologic rather than hemodynamic. Residual donor plasma carries antibodies to recipient leukocyte antigens, so TRALI is possible though uncommon, and carries donor anti-A and anti-B that can coat a non-group-O recipient's red cells when many units are pooled. Allergic and anaphylactic reactions follow donor plasma proteins, including IgA in a recipient with IgA deficiency and anti-IgA antibodies. Pooling multiplies donor exposure, which is the same arithmetic that made pooled wartime plasma and pooled factor concentrates efficient vehicles for hepatitis and HIV before testing and inactivation existed. Febrile reactions arise from accumulated donor leukocyte cytokines. Raising fibrinogen is itself a prothrombotic intervention, so the correction of a depleted level carries thrombotic risk once bleeding is controlled. A small volume of cold product has a correspondingly small thermal effect.",
 "fun": "Fibrinogen circulates at a higher concentration than any other clotting factor and still runs out first, because a clot consumes it in bulk, plasmin cleaves it, and the liver replaces it slowly. Pool's leftover residue in the bottom of a thawing bag is still the densest fibrinogen a blood bank can make.",
 "src": [
  "AABB Circular of Information for the Use of Human Blood and Blood Components",
  "Pool JG, Shannon AE, Production of high-potency concentrates of antihemophilic globulin, New England Journal of Medicine 1965",
  "European guideline on the management of major bleeding and coagulopathy following trauma"
 ]
}
});

;
DB.DIS = DB.DIS || {};
Object.assign(DB.DIS, {
"hypertension": {
  name: "Hypertension (high blood pressure)",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["high blood pressure", "HTN", "essential hypertension", "primary hypertension"],
  what: "Hypertension means the pressure of blood pushing on artery walls stays too high. It usually causes no symptoms, but over years it damages the heart, brain, kidneys and eyes, which is why it is called the silent killer.",
  origin: "In 1733 English clergyman and scientist Stephen Hales made the first recorded blood pressure measurement by running a tube into the artery of a horse and watching how high the blood rose. In 1896 Italian physician Scipione Riva-Rocci invented the inflatable arm cuff, and in 1905 Russian surgeon Nikolai Korotkoff described the tapping sounds heard with a stethoscope that are still used to read blood pressure today. For decades many doctors thought high pressure was a helpful response needed to push blood through stiff arteries. The Framingham Heart Study, begun in 1948, proved that higher pressure predicts strokes and heart attacks. Thiazide diuretics arrived in the late 1950s, and Veterans Administration trials in the late 1960s showed that treating high pressure prevents strokes and deaths. ACE inhibitors, calcium channel blockers and ARBs followed, and in 2017 US guidelines lowered the cutoff for hypertension to 130/80.",
  timeline: [["1733", "Stephen Hales measures blood pressure directly in a horse."], ["1896", "Scipione Riva-Rocci introduces the inflatable arm cuff."], ["1905", "Nikolai Korotkoff describes the sounds used to read pressure with a stethoscope."], ["1948", "The Framingham Heart Study begins and later links high pressure to heart disease and stroke."], ["1958", "Chlorothiazide becomes the first thiazide diuretic in wide use."], ["2017", "ACC/AHA guidelines define hypertension as 130/80 or higher."]],
  cell: "About 90 percent of cases are primary (essential) hypertension, a mix of genes, salt intake, weight, age and stress. Blood pressure equals cardiac output times systemic vascular resistance, so anything that raises blood volume or tightens small arteries (arterioles) raises it. Key players are the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (angiotensin II constricts arterioles through AT1 receptors and aldosterone makes the kidney hold sodium), the sympathetic nervous system (norepinephrine on alpha-1 and beta-1 receptors) and kidney sodium handling. Over time, high pressure injures the endothelium, lowering nitric oxide. Small arteries develop hyaline arteriolosclerosis (pink, glassy thickening of the wall) and, in severe cases, hyperplastic onion-skin arteriolosclerosis. Large arteries stiffen and atherosclerosis speeds up. The left ventricle works against higher afterload and thickens (concentric hypertrophy). Secondary hypertension has a specific cause, such as renal artery stenosis, primary aldosteronism, pheochromocytoma, sleep apnea or coarctation of the aorta.",
  body: [["Heart", "Pumping against high pressure makes the left ventricle thicken and stiffen (left ventricular hypertrophy). This can lead to heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, atrial fibrillation and a higher risk of heart attack."], ["Brain", "Damaged small arteries raise the risk of ischemic stroke, bleeding strokes (from tiny Charcot-Bouchard aneurysms) and vascular dementia."], ["Kidneys", "Thickened arterioles reduce blood flow to the nephrons, causing benign nephrosclerosis, protein in the urine and slowly falling kidney function. Hypertension is the second leading cause of end-stage kidney disease in the US."], ["Eyes", "Retinal arteries narrow and can show AV nicking, cotton-wool spots, hemorrhages and, in severe cases, swelling of the optic disc (hypertensive retinopathy)."], ["Arteries", "High pressure speeds atherosclerosis and weakens the aortic wall, raising the risk of peripheral artery disease, aortic aneurysm and aortic dissection."]],
  symptoms: "Most people have no symptoms at all, because arteries and organs are damaged slowly and silently. That is why regular blood pressure checks matter. Symptoms usually appear only when organs are already injured: shortness of breath from heart strain, chest pain from coronary disease, or weakness and speech trouble from a stroke. Very high pressure can cause headache, blurred vision or confusion because of swelling and damage in the brain and retina.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is based on the average of two or more properly measured readings on two or more occasions, ideally confirmed with home or 24-hour ambulatory monitoring to rule out white coat hypertension. Under the ACC/AHA definition, normal is below 120/80, elevated is 120 to 129 systolic, stage 1 is 130 to 139 systolic or 80 to 89 diastolic, and stage 2 is 140/90 or higher. Basic tests look for organ damage and causes: kidney function, electrolytes, glucose, lipids, urinalysis and an ECG. Young age, low potassium or very hard-to-control pressure prompts a search for secondary causes.",
  tx: "Lifestyle care comes first and helps every patient: less salt, the DASH eating pattern, weight loss, regular exercise, limiting alcohol and not smoking. First-line drug classes are thiazide or thiazide-like diuretics (lower blood volume and relax vessels), ACE inhibitors (block angiotensin II formation), ARBs (block the AT1 receptor) and dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (relax arteriolar smooth muscle). Most people need two or more drugs, often in one combination pill. ACE inhibitors or ARBs are preferred when there is diabetes with protein in the urine or chronic kidney disease. For resistant hypertension, a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist such as spironolactone is added. Beta blockers are used when there is another reason, such as heart failure or a past heart attack. ACE inhibitors and ARBs are avoided in pregnancy.",
  drugs: ["chlorthalidone", "hydrochlorothiazide", "indapamide", "lisinopril", "enalapril", "ramipril", "benazepril", "captopril", "losartan", "valsartan", "olmesartan", "irbesartan", "candesartan", "telmisartan", "amlodipine", "nifedipine", "spironolactone", "eplerenone", "metoprolol", "carvedilol", "labetalol", "hydralazine", "clonidine", "aprocitentan"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "First-line drugs are the ABCDs minus B: ACE inhibitor or ARB, Calcium channel blocker, Diuretic (thiazide)."], ["Most-tested fact", "ACE inhibitors cause a dry cough and angioedema because they block the breakdown of bradykinin; ARBs do not raise bradykinin."], ["Pathology clue", "Hyaline arteriolosclerosis is the classic small-vessel change of long-standing hypertension and diabetes."], ["Pregnancy", "ACE inhibitors and ARBs harm the fetal kidneys; labetalol, nifedipine and methyldopa are used in pregnancy instead."]],
  red: "Blood pressure that is very high together with chest pain, shortness of breath, severe headache, confusion, vision loss, weakness or trouble speaking can mean a hypertensive emergency or stroke and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Stephen Hales's horse experiment in 1733 showed the blood rising more than eight feet up a glass tube.",
  src: ["ACC/AHA Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation, and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults (2017)", "AHA/ACC High Blood Pressure Guideline (2025)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Veterans Administration Cooperative Study Group on Antihypertensive Agents. JAMA 1967 and 1970"]
},
"coronary-artery-disease": {
  name: "Coronary artery disease",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["CAD", "coronary heart disease", "CHD", "ischemic heart disease", "atherosclerotic heart disease"],
  what: "Coronary artery disease happens when fatty plaques build up inside the arteries that feed the heart muscle. The narrowed arteries can cause chest pain, and a plaque that breaks open can trigger a heart attack.",
  origin: "In 1768 English physician William Heberden described a strangling chest pain with exertion that he named angina pectoris, though he did not know its cause. In 1912 American physician James Herrick described how a clot in a coronary artery could cause a heart attack that people sometimes survive. In 1913 Russian scientist Nikolai Anichkov fed rabbits cholesterol and produced plaques in their arteries, a first clue that cholesterol drives the disease. In 1958 Mason Sones at the Cleveland Clinic accidentally injected dye straight into a coronary artery and invented coronary angiography. René Favaloro popularized bypass surgery with leg veins in 1967, and Andreas Gruentzig performed the first balloon angioplasty in 1977. Stents in the 1990s and statins from 1987 onward transformed care.",
  timeline: [["1768", "William Heberden describes angina pectoris."], ["1912", "James Herrick links coronary artery clots to heart attacks."], ["1913", "Nikolai Anichkov shows that cholesterol feeding causes atherosclerosis in rabbits."], ["1958", "Mason Sones performs the first selective coronary angiogram."], ["1967", "René Favaloro develops coronary bypass surgery with a saphenous vein graft."], ["1977", "Andreas Gruentzig performs the first coronary balloon angioplasty."]],
  cell: "CAD is caused by atherosclerosis. It begins with endothelial injury from high LDL cholesterol, high blood pressure, smoking, diabetes and inflammation. LDL particles slip into the artery wall (intima) and become oxidized. Endothelial cells express adhesion molecules such as VCAM-1, and monocytes enter the wall, become macrophages and eat oxidized LDL through scavenger receptors, turning into foam cells. These form the fatty streak. Macrophages and T cells release cytokines and growth factors such as PDGF, which draw smooth muscle cells from the media into the intima. Smooth muscle cells make collagen and form a fibrous cap over a soft necrotic lipid core. A stable plaque with a thick cap narrows the lumen slowly and causes stable angina when narrowing reaches about 70 percent. A vulnerable plaque with a thin cap and many macrophages (which release matrix metalloproteinases) can rupture, exposing tissue factor and collagen. Platelets stick, activate and aggregate, and a thrombus forms, causing acute coronary syndrome.",
  body: [["Heart muscle", "When demand for oxygen exceeds the supply through narrowed arteries, the muscle becomes ischemic, causing angina. Complete blockage causes infarction (death) of heart muscle."], ["Heart pumping", "Repeated ischemia or past heart attacks leave scar tissue and weaken the ventricle, a leading cause of heart failure (ischemic cardiomyopathy)."], ["Heart rhythm", "Scar and ischemic tissue can trigger ventricular arrhythmias, so CAD is the most common cause of sudden cardiac death in adults."], ["Other arteries", "Atherosclerosis is body-wide, so people with CAD often also have carotid disease (stroke risk) and peripheral artery disease in the legs."]],
  symptoms: "Many people have no symptoms until a plaque narrows an artery enough to limit blood flow. Chest pressure or tightness (angina) happens when the heart works harder, as with exercise, cold or stress, and blood flow cannot keep up. Ischemic muscle releases chemicals such as adenosine that stimulate pain nerves, so pain can spread to the arm, jaw or back. Shortness of breath happens because an ischemic ventricle stiffens and pressure backs into the lungs. Women, older adults and people with diabetes more often have fatigue, nausea or breathlessness instead of classic chest pain.",
  dx: "Diagnosis starts with symptoms and risk factors. An ECG may show old Q waves or ischemic changes. Stress testing (exercise or drug-induced, with ECG, echocardiogram or nuclear imaging) shows areas that do not get enough blood under strain. Coronary CT angiography can show plaques and narrowing, and a coronary calcium score measures calcified plaque. Invasive coronary angiography is the gold standard for seeing blockages and planning stents or bypass. Lipids, glucose and HbA1c look for risk factors.",
  tx: "Treatment aims to stop plaques from growing, prevent clots and ease symptoms. High-intensity statins lower LDL and stabilize plaques; ezetimibe and PCSK9 inhibitors lower LDL further. Aspirin blocks platelet thromboxane A2 production to prevent clots, and clopidogrel is an alternative. Beta blockers and nitrates relieve angina. Blood pressure and diabetes control, quitting smoking, a heart-healthy diet, exercise and cardiac rehabilitation are central. Some diabetes drugs, such as GLP-1 receptor agonists and SGLT2 inhibitors, also lower cardiovascular events. For severe blockages or symptoms despite medicine, clinicians open arteries with a balloon and stent (PCI) or route blood around them with bypass surgery (CABG).",
  drugs: ["atorvastatin", "rosuvastatin", "simvastatin", "pravastatin", "ezetimibe", "evolocumab", "alirocumab", "inclisiran", "bempedoic-acid", "icosapent-ethyl", "aspirin", "clopidogrel", "ticagrelor", "rivaroxaban", "metoprolol", "carvedilol", "nitroglycerin", "isosorbide-mononitrate", "amlodipine", "ranolazine", "lisinopril", "colchicine", "semaglutide", "empagliflozin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Plaque builds like a pimple: a soft fatty core under a thin cap. Thin caps pop and cause heart attacks."], ["Most-tested fact", "Foam cells are macrophages full of oxidized LDL; they are the first cells of the fatty streak."], ["Most common artery", "The left anterior descending (LAD) artery is the most commonly blocked coronary artery."], ["Often confused with", "Stable angina comes from a fixed narrowing; acute coronary syndrome comes from a ruptured plaque with a new clot."]],
  red: "Chest pain or pressure lasting more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath or pain spreading to the arm or jaw, can mean a heart attack and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Werner Forssmann threaded a catheter into his own heart in 1929, an act that later earned a share of the 1956 Nobel Prize and paved the way for angiography.",
  src: ["ACC/AHA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease (2023)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Herrick JB. JAMA 1912", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"stable-angina": {
  name: "Stable angina",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["angina pectoris", "chronic stable angina", "exertional angina", "effort angina"],
  what: "Stable angina is chest pain or pressure that comes on in a predictable way with exertion or stress and goes away with rest or nitroglycerin. It happens because a narrowed coronary artery cannot deliver enough blood when the heart works harder.",
  origin: "William Heberden gave the classic description of angina pectoris to the Royal College of Physicians in London in 1768, noting a sense of strangling in the chest when walking uphill that eased on standing still. In 1867 Scottish physician Thomas Lauder Brunton found that inhaled amyl nitrite relieved angina, reasoning that it lowered blood pressure. In 1879 William Murrell reported in The Lancet that small amounts of nitroglycerin, the explosive used in dynamite, relieved angina too. In the 1960s James Black developed propranolol, the first beta blocker, partly to reduce the heart's oxygen demand in angina, work that helped earn him a Nobel Prize in 1988. Later, large trials such as COURAGE (2007) and ISCHEMIA (2020) showed that in stable disease, good medical therapy is as effective as stents for preventing heart attacks, with procedures used mainly to relieve symptoms.",
  timeline: [["1768", "William Heberden describes angina pectoris."], ["1867", "Thomas Lauder Brunton uses amyl nitrite to relieve angina."], ["1879", "William Murrell reports nitroglycerin for angina in The Lancet."], ["1960s", "James Black develops propranolol, the first clinically useful beta blocker."], ["2007", "The COURAGE trial finds PCI adds no survival benefit over optimal medical therapy in stable disease."], ["2020", "The ISCHEMIA trial supports an initial medical therapy strategy for stable ischemic disease."]],
  cell: "Stable angina is a supply and demand mismatch. A fixed atherosclerotic plaque with a thick fibrous cap narrows an epicardial coronary artery, usually by 70 percent or more. At rest, arterioles downstream dilate to keep flow normal, but they have little reserve left. When heart rate, contractility or wall tension rise with exercise, oxygen demand increases and flow cannot increase enough. The subendocardium, the inner layer that is farthest from the coronary arteries and squeezed most during systole, becomes ischemic first. Ischemic myocytes switch to anaerobic metabolism, produce lactate and release adenosine, bradykinin and potassium, which stimulate cardiac sympathetic afferent nerves and cause pain. Ischemia also impairs relaxation (diastolic dysfunction) before it weakens contraction. Because the plaque is stable and no clot forms, the ischemia is reversible and stops when demand falls.",
  body: [["Heart muscle", "The inner wall becomes briefly ischemic during exertion, which can show as ST-segment depression on the ECG. The muscle recovers when demand falls, so there is no lasting damage from each episode."], ["Heart function", "During an attack the ventricle relaxes poorly and filling pressure rises, which can cause shortness of breath."], ["Nerves", "Pain signals travel with sympathetic nerves to spinal segments around T1 to T5, so pain is felt in the chest, left arm, neck or jaw (referred pain)."]],
  symptoms: "The pain is usually a pressure, squeezing or heaviness rather than a sharp stab, because it comes from deep visceral nerves. It is brought on by exertion, cold weather, heavy meals or emotional stress, all of which raise heart rate and blood pressure and so oxygen demand. It eases within minutes of rest or nitroglycerin because demand falls or veins dilate and wall tension drops. It is stable when the pattern is predictable; a change to pain at rest or pain that is more frequent, longer or with less effort is unstable angina.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is mostly based on a typical history. A resting ECG is often normal between episodes. Exercise stress testing can provoke ST depression. Stress echocardiography or nuclear perfusion imaging shows areas of poor wall motion or poor blood flow under stress, and drugs such as dobutamine or regadenoson are used when a person cannot exercise. Coronary CT angiography shows plaques and narrowing. Invasive angiography is used when symptoms are severe or noninvasive tests are high risk.",
  tx: "Treatment has two goals: relieving angina and preventing heart attacks. Sublingual nitroglycerin gives quick relief by releasing nitric oxide, which mainly dilates veins and lowers preload, and also dilates coronary arteries. Beta blockers are first-line for prevention because they slow the heart and lower contractility, reducing oxygen demand and lengthening diastole when the heart fills its own arteries. Calcium channel blockers and long-acting nitrates are added or used instead, and ranolazine blocks the late sodium current to reduce calcium overload. To prevent events, clinicians use aspirin, high-intensity statins and control of blood pressure and diabetes. Cardiac rehabilitation and quitting smoking help. PCI or bypass surgery is used for symptoms that persist despite medicine or for high-risk anatomy such as left main disease. Nitrates are never combined with PDE-5 inhibitors such as sildenafil because blood pressure can fall dangerously.",
  drugs: ["nitroglycerin", "isosorbide-mononitrate", "isosorbide-dinitrate", "metoprolol", "atenolol", "bisoprolol", "carvedilol", "amlodipine", "diltiazem", "verapamil", "ranolazine", "aspirin", "clopidogrel", "atorvastatin", "rosuvastatin", "ezetimibe", "lisinopril", "ramipril"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Stable angina is predictable: same trigger, same pain, same relief with rest."], ["Most-tested fact", "Nitroglycerin works mainly by dilating veins, lowering preload and wall tension; it must never be given within a day or two of a PDE-5 inhibitor."], ["Board clue", "Exercise stress test shows ST depression in stable angina (subendocardial ischemia), while ST elevation suggests transmural injury."], ["Often confused with", "Prinzmetal (vasospastic) angina happens at rest from coronary spasm, shows transient ST elevation and is treated with calcium channel blockers and nitrates."]],
  red: "Angina that happens at rest, lasts longer than usual, is not relieved by rest or nitroglycerin, or comes with sweating, fainting or severe breathlessness may be a heart attack and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Alfred Nobel, who made his fortune from nitroglycerin in dynamite, was prescribed nitroglycerin for his own chest pain late in life.",
  src: ["ACC/AHA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Chronic Coronary Disease (2023)", "Heberden W. Some Account of a Disorder of the Breast (1772)", "Boden WE et al. COURAGE. N Engl J Med 2007", "Maron DJ et al. ISCHEMIA. N Engl J Med 2020"]
},
"acute-coronary-syndrome": {
  name: "Acute coronary syndrome (heart attack)",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["ACS", "heart attack", "myocardial infarction", "MI", "STEMI", "NSTEMI", "unstable angina"],
  what: "Acute coronary syndrome is a sudden drop in blood flow to the heart muscle, usually because a plaque in a coronary artery breaks open and a clot forms. It includes unstable angina and heart attacks (NSTEMI and STEMI), and every minute of blocked flow kills more heart muscle.",
  origin: "In 1912 Chicago physician James Herrick argued that a clot blocking a coronary artery causes heart attacks and that patients can survive them. For decades heart attacks were treated with weeks of strict bed rest. Coronary care units with ECG monitoring and defibrillators began in the early 1960s and sharply cut deaths from arrhythmias. In 1980 Marcus DeWood showed with angiography that most early heart attacks involve a fresh clot, which opened the door to clot-busting drugs. The GISSI trial (1986) showed streptokinase saves lives, and ISIS-2 (1988) showed aspirin alone lowers deaths and adds to streptokinase. In the 1990s primary angioplasty and stenting proved even better than clot busters when done quickly. Sensitive troponin tests replaced older enzyme tests and now define a heart attack.",
  timeline: [["1912", "James Herrick describes coronary thrombosis as a survivable cause of heart attack."], ["1960s", "Coronary care units with continuous ECG monitoring are introduced."], ["1980", "Marcus DeWood shows a fresh coronary clot in most early heart attacks."], ["1986", "The GISSI trial shows streptokinase lowers death after heart attack."], ["1988", "ISIS-2 shows aspirin lowers death after heart attack."], ["1990s", "Primary PCI and cardiac troponin testing become standard."]],
  cell: "Most ACS starts when a vulnerable atherosclerotic plaque with a thin fibrous cap and a large lipid core ruptures or erodes. Macrophage matrix metalloproteinases weaken the cap. Rupture exposes collagen and tissue factor. Platelets attach via glycoprotein Ib binding von Willebrand factor, become activated and release ADP and thromboxane A2, which recruit more platelets. Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa receptors bind fibrinogen and link platelets together, while tissue factor starts the coagulation cascade, making thrombin and fibrin. A partially blocking clot causes unstable angina or NSTEMI (subendocardial injury); a fully blocking clot causes STEMI (transmural injury). Without oxygen, myocytes switch to anaerobic metabolism, ATP falls, and contraction stops within about a minute. After 20 to 40 minutes, irreversible injury begins: membranes break and troponin and CK-MB leak into the blood. Dead tissue shows coagulative necrosis, then neutrophils (1 to 3 days), macrophages (3 to 14 days) and finally collagen scar (weeks).",
  body: [["Heart muscle", "Blocked flow kills myocytes from the inside out, starting in the subendocardium. The longer flow is blocked, the more muscle dies and the weaker the heart becomes."], ["Heart rhythm", "Ischemic tissue is electrically unstable and can trigger ventricular fibrillation, the main cause of early death. An inferior MI can damage the AV node and cause heart block."], ["Pumping and circulation", "Large infarcts can cause acute heart failure and cardiogenic shock."], ["Mechanical structures", "Days after the attack, softened dead tissue can tear, causing papillary muscle rupture with acute mitral regurgitation, a ventricular septal rupture or free wall rupture with tamponade."], ["Pericardium", "Inflammation over dead muscle can cause pericarditis within days, or weeks later as an immune reaction (Dressler syndrome)."]],
  symptoms: "Chest pressure, squeezing or heaviness that lasts more than a few minutes comes from ischemic muscle releasing chemicals that stimulate visceral pain nerves; the pain can spread to the left arm, jaw, neck or back. Sweating, pale skin and a fast heart rate come from a surge of sympathetic activity. Nausea and vomiting are common, especially with inferior MIs, because of vagal nerve activation. Shortness of breath happens when the injured ventricle cannot pump or relax, raising pressure in the lungs. Women, older adults and people with diabetes may have fatigue, breathlessness or indigestion-like discomfort instead of classic pain.",
  dx: "A 12-lead ECG is done within 10 minutes of arrival. STEMI shows new ST elevation in contiguous leads (or an equivalent pattern), and the ECG leads involved point to the blocked artery. NSTEMI and unstable angina show ST depression, T wave inversion or a normal ECG. High-sensitivity cardiac troponin separates them: troponin rises in NSTEMI and STEMI but not in unstable angina. Repeated troponin levels show the rise and fall of acute injury. Echocardiography shows wall motion problems and complications, and coronary angiography finds and treats the blockage.",
  tx: "The aim is to open the artery fast and stop the clot from growing. STEMI is treated with emergency PCI (balloon and stent), ideally within 90 minutes of first medical contact; when PCI is not available in time, clinicians give a fibrinolytic such as tenecteplase or alteplase, which turns plasminogen into plasmin to dissolve fibrin. All patients get aspirin (blocks thromboxane A2) plus a P2Y12 inhibitor such as ticagrelor, prasugrel or clopidogrel (blocks ADP-driven platelet activation), and an anticoagulant such as heparin, enoxaparin or bivalirudin. Nitroglycerin eases ischemic pain unless blood pressure is low, right ventricular infarction is suspected or a PDE-5 inhibitor was used. Oxygen is given only if oxygen levels are low. Afterward, high-intensity statins, beta blockers, ACE inhibitors or ARBs, cardiac rehabilitation and quitting smoking lower the risk of another event.",
  drugs: ["aspirin", "ticagrelor", "prasugrel", "clopidogrel", "cangrelor", "heparin", "enoxaparin", "bivalirudin", "fondaparinux", "eptifibatide", "tirofiban", "tenecteplase", "alteplase", "reteplase", "nitroglycerin", "morphine", "fentanyl", "metoprolol", "atorvastatin", "rosuvastatin", "lisinopril", "eplerenone", "amiodarone", "lidocaine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Time is muscle: every minute an artery stays closed, more heart muscle dies."], ["Most-tested fact", "Troponin I or T is the most sensitive and specific marker of heart muscle injury; CK-MB falls sooner and helps spot a reinfarction."], ["Timeline of complications", "Arrhythmias in the first day, papillary muscle or free wall rupture around days 3 to 7 when macrophages soften the tissue, and Dressler syndrome weeks later."], ["Often confused with", "Unstable angina has symptoms of ACS but no troponin rise; NSTEMI has a troponin rise without ST elevation."], ["Right ventricular MI", "Inferior STEMI with low blood pressure and clear lungs suggests right ventricular infarction; it depends on preload, so nitrates are avoided and fluids are used."]],
  red: "Chest pain or pressure lasting more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, nausea, shortness of breath, fainting or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back, needs emergency care (call 911). EMS can send an ECG ahead so the cath lab is ready.",
  fun: "Chewing an aspirin early in a heart attack is one of the cheapest life-saving treatments in medicine; ISIS-2 showed it cut deaths by about a fifth.",
  src: ["ACC/AHA/ACEP/NAEMSP/SCAI Guideline for the Management of Patients With Acute Coronary Syndromes (2025)", "Fourth Universal Definition of Myocardial Infarction (2018)", "ISIS-2 Collaborative Group. Lancet 1988", "DeWood MA et al. N Engl J Med 1980", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"heart-failure": {
  name: "Heart failure",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["congestive heart failure", "CHF", "HFrEF", "HFpEF"],
  what: "Heart failure means the heart cannot pump or fill well enough to meet the body's needs. Fluid backs up into the lungs and legs, and the body feels tired and short of breath.",
  origin: "Doctors described swelling, breathlessness and a weak heart for thousands of years; ancient Egyptian and Greek writers linked fluid buildup, then called dropsy, to the heart. In 1785 English physician William Withering showed that foxglove (digitalis) could ease dropsy, one of the first effective heart failure treatments. In the 1900s diuretics arrived, and in the 1980s and 1990s large trials showed that ACE inhibitors and beta blockers help people live longer, which changed the thinking from simply squeezing the heart harder to protecting it from the body's own stress hormones. Newer drugs such as sacubitril-valsartan (2015) and SGLT2 inhibitors (2020s) added more protection.",
  timeline: [["1785", "William Withering publishes his account of foxglove for dropsy."], ["1950s", "Thiazide and loop diuretics start to remove fluid more reliably."], ["1987", "The CONSENSUS trial shows an ACE inhibitor improves survival in severe heart failure."], ["1990s", "Trials show beta blockers such as carvedilol and metoprolol succinate lower death rates."], ["2015", "Sacubitril-valsartan is approved after the PARADIGM-HF trial."], ["2020s", "SGLT2 inhibitors join standard heart failure treatment."]],
  cell: "Most heart failure starts with injury to heart muscle cells (cardiomyocytes), from a heart attack, long-standing high blood pressure, a valve problem or a cardiomyopathy. Injured cells lose contractile strength or the wall becomes stiff. The body senses lower blood flow and switches on the sympathetic nervous system and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Norepinephrine, angiotensin II and aldosterone tighten blood vessels, hold on to salt and water, and push heart cells to grow and lay down scar tissue. In the short term this props up blood pressure, but over time it overworks the heart, causes more cell death and remodeling, and makes the failure worse. Many modern drugs work by blocking these hormone loops.",
  body: [["Heart", "The left ventricle either cannot squeeze well (reduced ejection fraction) or cannot relax and fill (preserved ejection fraction). Over time the chambers can enlarge or thicken."], ["Lungs", "Back-up from the left side raises pressure in the lung capillaries, so fluid leaks into the air sacs, causing shortness of breath, crackles and sometimes pulmonary edema."], ["Kidneys", "Lower blood flow makes the kidneys release renin and hold salt and water, adding to fluid overload and swelling."], ["Legs and belly", "Right-sided back-up raises pressure in the veins, causing ankle swelling, a swollen liver and fluid in the abdomen."], ["Muscles and energy", "Less blood flow to muscles causes fatigue and poor exercise tolerance."]],
  symptoms: "Shortness of breath happens because fluid collects in the lungs, which is why it gets worse when lying flat (orthopnea) or wakes people at night. Swelling in the legs and quick weight gain come from salt and water the kidneys hold on to. Fatigue comes from low blood flow to the muscles. A fast heartbeat is the body's stress response trying to keep blood moving.",
  dx: "Diagnosis combines the history and exam with tests. A blood test for BNP or NT-proBNP rises when the heart walls are stretched. An echocardiogram measures the ejection fraction and shows valve and wall problems. A chest X-ray can show fluid in the lungs and a large heart, and an ECG looks for rhythm problems or an old heart attack.",
  tx: "Treatment removes extra fluid and protects the heart from harmful hormone signals. Loop diuretics help the kidneys flush salt and water. For reduced ejection fraction, four drug groups are the foundation: an ARNI (sacubitril-valsartan) or ACE inhibitor or ARB to block angiotensin, a beta blocker to calm the sympathetic drive, a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist to block aldosterone, and an SGLT2 inhibitor. Others such as hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate, digoxin, ivabradine and vericiguat are used in specific situations. In the hospital, inotropes like dobutamine or milrinone can support a failing heart.",
  drugs: ["furosemide", "bumetanide", "sacubitril-valsartan", "lisinopril", "enalapril", "losartan", "carvedilol", "metoprolol", "bisoprolol", "spironolactone", "eplerenone", "empagliflozin", "dapagliflozin", "isosorbide-dinitrate-hydralazine", "digoxin", "ivabradine", "vericiguat", "dobutamine", "milrinone", "nitroglycerin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "The four pillars for reduced EF: ARNI, Beta blocker, MRA, SGLT2i. Think of them as the four legs holding up the heart."], ["Left vs right", "Left-sided failure floods the Lungs (both start with L). Right-sided failure swells the legs and liver."], ["Most-tested fact", "Drugs that lower death rates in HFrEF block the body's stress hormones; diuretics ease symptoms but do not lower mortality."]],
  red: "Sudden severe shortness of breath, pink frothy sputum, chest pain, fainting or confusion can mean acute decompensated heart failure and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Foxglove, the plant behind digoxin, is still grown in gardens, and Withering reportedly learned of it from a folk remedy used in Shropshire.",
  src: ["ACC/AHA/HFSA Guideline for the Management of Heart Failure (2022)", "Withering W. An Account of the Foxglove (1785)", "CONSENSUS Trial Study Group. N Engl J Med 1987", "McMurray JJV et al. PARADIGM-HF. N Engl J Med 2014"]
},
"atrial-fibrillation": {
  name: "Atrial fibrillation",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["AFib", "AF", "A-fib"],
  what: "Atrial fibrillation is the most common sustained heart rhythm problem. The upper chambers (atria) quiver chaotically instead of beating, so the heartbeat becomes irregular and often fast, and blood can pool and form clots that cause strokes.",
  origin: "Physicians long noticed a totally irregular pulse, and in 1785 William Withering described foxglove slowing the racing, irregular heart of people with dropsy. After Willem Einthoven invented a practical electrocardiograph in the early 1900s, British cardiologist Thomas Lewis showed in 1909 that the irregular pulse came from fibrillation of the atria, seen on the ECG as no P waves and an irregularly irregular rhythm. For decades treatment focused on slowing the heart with digitalis. Large trials around 1990 proved that warfarin greatly lowers stroke risk in atrial fibrillation. In 1998 French cardiologist Michel Haïssaguerre and colleagues showed that most episodes are triggered by electrical sparks in the pulmonary veins, leading to catheter ablation. Direct oral anticoagulants, starting with dabigatran in the US in 2010, made stroke prevention simpler.",
  timeline: [["1785", "William Withering describes foxglove for a rapid, irregular heart with dropsy."], ["1909", "Thomas Lewis identifies atrial fibrillation on the ECG."], ["1954", "Warfarin is approved for use in people."], ["1990s", "Trials show anticoagulation sharply lowers stroke risk in atrial fibrillation."], ["1998", "Haïssaguerre shows pulmonary vein triggers, leading to ablation."], ["2010", "Dabigatran becomes the first direct oral anticoagulant approved in the US for atrial fibrillation."]],
  cell: "Atrial fibrillation needs a trigger and a substrate. Triggers are rapid ectopic firings, most often from myocardial sleeves in the pulmonary veins where they enter the left atrium. The substrate is a stretched, scarred atrium: high blood pressure, valve disease, heart failure, obesity, sleep apnea, alcohol and aging cause atrial dilation, fibrosis and changes in ion channels. Fibrosis slows and breaks up conduction, allowing many small wandering reentry circuits (multiple wavelets) that fire at 300 to 600 times per minute. The AV node cannot pass every impulse, so the ventricles respond irregularly and often fast. Once AF starts, it shortens the atrial refractory period through calcium-driven changes in ion channels, so AF begets AF. With no organized atrial contraction, blood stagnates, especially in the left atrial appendage, and Virchow's triad (stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulability) favors a thrombus that can embolize to the brain. Hyperthyroidism and acute illness can also trigger it.",
  body: [["Brain", "Clots from the left atrial appendage can travel to the brain and cause large ischemic strokes. AF raises stroke risk about fivefold."], ["Heart", "Losing the atrial kick reduces ventricular filling. A rapid rate for weeks can weaken the ventricle (tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy) and worsen heart failure."], ["Lungs", "Poor filling and fast rates raise pressure in the left atrium and lung veins, causing breathlessness and sometimes pulmonary edema."], ["Other organs", "Clots can also lodge in the arteries of the gut, kidneys or limbs, causing sudden pain and tissue damage."]],
  symptoms: "Palpitations and a fluttering chest come from the irregular, often fast ventricular beats. Fatigue, dizziness and reduced exercise tolerance happen because the heart loses the atrial kick and fills less, so it pumps less blood with each beat. Shortness of breath comes from pressure backing into the lungs. Many people, especially older adults, have no symptoms at all, and the first sign may be a stroke.",
  dx: "The ECG shows an irregularly irregular rhythm with no distinct P waves and a wavy (fibrillatory) baseline. Episodes that come and go can be caught with a Holter monitor, event monitor or wearable device. An echocardiogram looks for a large atrium, valve disease and weak pumping, and a transesophageal echo can look for clot in the left atrial appendage before cardioversion. Thyroid tests, electrolytes and kidney function look for causes and guide drug choice. Stroke risk is estimated with the CHA2DS2-VASc score.",
  tx: "Treatment rests on three pillars: preventing stroke, controlling rate or rhythm, and treating risk factors. Anticoagulants are used when stroke risk is high. Direct oral anticoagulants (apixaban, rivaroxaban, edoxaban) block factor Xa, dabigatran blocks thrombin, and warfarin blocks vitamin K-dependent clotting factors and is preferred with mechanical valves or moderate to severe mitral stenosis. Rate control uses beta blockers or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (diltiazem, verapamil) to slow AV node conduction, with digoxin as an add-on. Rhythm control uses electrical cardioversion, antiarrhythmics such as flecainide, propafenone, amiodarone, dronedarone, sotalol or dofetilide, and catheter ablation, which isolates the pulmonary veins. Unstable patients get immediate synchronized cardioversion. Weight loss, blood pressure control, treating sleep apnea and limiting alcohol reduce AF burden. Left atrial appendage occlusion is an option for some people who cannot take anticoagulants.",
  drugs: ["apixaban", "rivaroxaban", "edoxaban", "dabigatran", "warfarin", "metoprolol", "esmolol", "carvedilol", "diltiazem", "verapamil", "digoxin", "amiodarone", "flecainide", "propafenone", "dronedarone", "sotalol", "dofetilide", "ibutilide", "procainamide", "heparin", "enoxaparin", "idarucizumab", "prothrombin-complex-concentrate", "phytonadione"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Irregularly irregular with no P waves equals AFib."], ["CHA2DS2-VASc", "CHF, Hypertension, Age 75 or older (2 points), Diabetes, Stroke or TIA (2 points), Vascular disease, Age 65 to 74, Sex category female."], ["Most-tested fact", "The left atrial appendage is where most AF clots form."], ["WPW warning", "In AF with Wolff-Parkinson-White (very fast, wide, irregular), AV nodal blockers such as adenosine, verapamil, diltiazem and digoxin can speed conduction down the accessory pathway; procainamide or cardioversion is used instead."]],
  red: "Sudden face drooping, arm weakness, trouble speaking, chest pain, fainting or severe shortness of breath in someone with atrial fibrillation needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "About one in three people of European descent will develop atrial fibrillation at some point in their lifetime, according to large population studies.",
  src: ["ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Atrial Fibrillation (2023)", "Haïssaguerre M et al. N Engl J Med 1998", "Lewis T. Heart 1909 and related papers", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"supraventricular-tachycardia": {
  name: "Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT)",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["SVT", "PSVT", "paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia", "AVNRT", "AVRT"],
  what: "Supraventricular tachycardia is a fast, regular heart rhythm that starts above the ventricles, usually from a short circuit loop involving the AV node. It begins and ends suddenly and is often seen in young, healthy people.",
  origin: "Sudden bouts of racing heartbeat were described in the 1800s as paroxysmal tachycardia. In 1930 Louis Wolff, John Parkinson and Paul Dudley White described young patients with a short PR interval, a wide QRS and attacks of tachycardia, now called Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome, later explained by an extra electrical pathway between the atria and ventricles. In 1929 Alan Drury and Albert Szent-Györgyi showed that adenosine slows the heart, and adenosine was approved in the US in 1989 as a fast way to stop SVT. In the late 1980s and early 1990s radiofrequency catheter ablation made it possible to cure most SVTs by burning away the short circuit. In 2015 the REVERT trial showed that a modified Valsalva maneuver, with the legs raised right after the strain, works much better than the standard maneuver.",
  timeline: [["1929", "Drury and Szent-Györgyi describe the heart-slowing effects of adenosine."], ["1930", "Wolff, Parkinson and White describe the syndrome that bears their names."], ["1989", "Adenosine is approved in the US for paroxysmal SVT."], ["1991", "Reports show radiofrequency catheter ablation can cure accessory pathway tachycardias."], ["2015", "The REVERT trial shows the modified Valsalva maneuver converts more SVT."]],
  cell: "SVT usually depends on reentry, a loop that keeps restimulating itself. In AV nodal reentrant tachycardia (AVNRT, the most common type), the AV node region has two pathways: a fast pathway with a long refractory period and a slow pathway with a short one. A well-timed premature atrial beat finds the fast pathway refractory, travels down the slow one, and returns up the now-recovered fast pathway, creating a loop. In AV reentrant tachycardia (AVRT), an accessory pathway (as in WPW) forms one limb and the AV node the other; most often impulses go down the AV node and up the accessory pathway (orthodromic, narrow QRS). The AV node depends on L-type calcium channels and is slowed by vagal tone, adenosine (A1 receptors open potassium channels and hyperpolarize nodal cells), calcium channel blockers and beta blockers, which is why these break AV node-dependent circuits. Atrial tachycardia and atrial flutter are other supraventricular rhythms that do not need the AV node to continue.",
  body: [["Heart", "Very fast rates shorten diastole, so the ventricles fill less and pump less blood. Episodes lasting a long time can rarely weaken the heart."], ["Blood pressure and brain", "Low output during fast rates can cause lightheadedness and, less often, fainting."], ["Kidneys", "Stretching of the atria releases atrial natriuretic peptide, which can cause frequent urination during or after an attack."], ["Neck veins", "In AVNRT the atria and ventricles contract at nearly the same time against closed valves, causing a pounding feeling in the neck (frog sign)."]],
  symptoms: "Sudden onset and sudden end of a pounding, racing heartbeat reflect a reentry loop switching on and off. Dizziness and shortness of breath come from reduced filling at very fast rates. Chest discomfort can occur because the heart's oxygen demand rises while diastolic time, when the coronary arteries fill, shrinks. Anxiety and sweating come from the sympathetic response.",
  dx: "A 12-lead ECG during an episode shows a regular, narrow QRS tachycardia, usually 150 to 250 beats per minute, often with P waves hidden in or right after the QRS. Between episodes the ECG may be normal or show a delta wave and short PR in WPW. Ambulatory monitors capture episodes that come and go. An electrophysiology study maps the circuit and is usually combined with ablation. Adenosine given during the rhythm can both stop AV node-dependent SVT and unmask flutter waves in atrial flutter.",
  tx: "For stable patients, vagal maneuvers such as the modified Valsalva come first; they increase vagal tone to slow the AV node. If these fail, clinicians give rapid IV adenosine, which briefly blocks the AV node and breaks the loop; people often feel a short, intense sense of doom or chest pressure. If adenosine fails, IV diltiazem, verapamil or a beta blocker slows the node. Unstable patients with low blood pressure, chest pain or altered mental status get synchronized cardioversion. Long-term, catheter ablation cures most cases. Beta blockers or calcium channel blockers, and sometimes flecainide or propafenone, are used to prevent episodes. Caffeine and stimulants are common triggers.",
  drugs: ["adenosine", "diltiazem", "verapamil", "metoprolol", "esmolol", "propranolol", "atenolol", "flecainide", "propafenone", "procainamide", "amiodarone", "sotalol", "digoxin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "SVT is Sudden, Very fast and regular, Terminates suddenly."], ["Most-tested fact", "Adenosine has a half-life of seconds, so it is pushed rapidly through a large IV close to the heart and followed by a saline flush."], ["Most common type", "AVNRT is the most common SVT and is more common in women."], ["WPW clue", "Short PR, delta wave (slurred QRS upstroke) and wide QRS on a resting ECG point to an accessory pathway."]],
  red: "A racing heart with chest pain, fainting, severe shortness of breath or confusion needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Patients given adenosine often describe a brief feeling that their heart has stopped; on the monitor it may truly pause for a few seconds before a normal rhythm returns.",
  src: ["ACC/AHA/HRS Guideline for the Management of Adult Patients With Supraventricular Tachycardia (2015)", "Appelboam A et al. REVERT. Lancet 2015", "Wolff L, Parkinson J, White PD. Am Heart J 1930", "AHA Guidelines for CPR and Emergency Cardiovascular Care"]
},
"ventricular-tachycardia": {
  name: "Ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["VT", "V-tach", "VF", "V-fib", "ventricular arrhythmia", "sudden cardiac death"],
  what: "Ventricular tachycardia is a fast rhythm starting in the ventricles. Ventricular fibrillation is a chaotic quiver of the ventricles that pumps no blood at all. Both can cause sudden cardiac arrest and death within minutes without a shock.",
  origin: "In the late 1880s Scottish physiologist John MacWilliam proposed that sudden death in people often came from ventricular fibrillation rather than the heart simply stopping. In the 1930s engineers and physiologists, including William Kouwenhoven, showed electric shocks could stop fibrillation in dogs. In 1947 American surgeon C. S. Beck performed the first successful human defibrillation, shocking a boy's exposed heart during surgery. In 1956 Paul Zoll showed shocks could be given through the closed chest. In the early 1960s Bernard Lown developed the safer direct current defibrillator and synchronized cardioversion for VT. In 1980 Michel Mirowski's team at Johns Hopkins implanted the first automatic implantable defibrillator (ICD) in a person. Later trials showed ICDs save lives in people at high risk, and public access AEDs brought defibrillation into airports, schools and homes.",
  timeline: [["1889", "John MacWilliam proposes ventricular fibrillation as a cause of sudden death."], ["1947", "C. S. Beck performs the first successful human defibrillation."], ["1956", "Paul Zoll shows external defibrillation works through the closed chest."], ["1962", "Bernard Lown introduces direct current defibrillation and cardioversion."], ["1980", "The first implantable cardioverter-defibrillator is placed in a patient."]],
  cell: "Most VT in adults is scar-related reentry after a heart attack or in cardiomyopathy. Surviving channels of myocytes thread through fibrous scar and conduct slowly, allowing an impulse to circle around the scar and repeatedly excite the ventricle (monomorphic VT, with each QRS looking the same). Other mechanisms are triggered activity and abnormal automaticity. Early afterdepolarizations occur when repolarization is prolonged, as in long QT, and cause polymorphic VT (torsades de pointes). Delayed afterdepolarizations come from calcium overload, as in digoxin toxicity or catecholaminergic polymorphic VT. Acute ischemia causes potassium leak, acidosis and uneven refractoriness, which can trigger polymorphic VT or VF. In VF, many wavelets break up across the ventricles, no coordinated contraction occurs and cardiac output drops to zero. Electrolyte problems (low potassium or magnesium), stimulants and inherited channelopathies such as Brugada syndrome also raise risk.",
  body: [["Heart", "Fast VT shortens filling time and can drop cardiac output. VT often degenerates into VF, which produces no pumping at all."], ["Brain", "Without blood flow, consciousness is lost within seconds and brain cells begin to die after a few minutes, which is why CPR and early shocks are critical."], ["Whole body", "With no circulation, every organ becomes ischemic; survivors can develop kidney injury, liver injury and post-cardiac arrest syndrome."]],
  symptoms: "Stable VT can cause palpitations, chest pain, dizziness and shortness of breath because the fast ventricular rate leaves less time to fill and pump. When output drops further, blood pressure falls and the person faints. VF causes sudden collapse, no pulse and no normal breathing (sometimes gasping called agonal breathing) because the heart pumps no blood.",
  dx: "VT is a wide-complex tachycardia, usually over 100 beats per minute, with QRS complexes 120 ms or wider. Clues that a wide-complex rhythm is VT rather than SVT with aberrancy include AV dissociation, fusion and capture beats, and very wide QRS complexes; in adults with heart disease, a wide-complex tachycardia is treated as VT until proven otherwise. VF shows a chaotic, irregular baseline with no clear QRS. After the event, tests look for the cause: troponin and angiography for ischemia, electrolytes, echocardiography for weak pumping, cardiac MRI for scar and genetic testing when an inherited cause is suspected.",
  tx: "Pulseless VT and VF are treated with high-quality CPR and defibrillation as fast as possible, plus epinephrine during resuscitation and amiodarone or lidocaine for rhythms that keep returning after shocks. VT with a pulse but signs of instability gets synchronized cardioversion. Stable monomorphic VT can be treated with IV amiodarone, procainamide or lidocaine, which slow conduction or prolong refractoriness. Torsades de pointes is treated with IV magnesium sulfate and by correcting low potassium. Long-term, an ICD detects and shocks dangerous rhythms. Beta blockers lower adrenergic triggers, and amiodarone, sotalol or mexiletine reduce episodes. Catheter ablation of the scar circuit reduces recurrences. Any ischemia or electrolyte problem is fixed.",
  drugs: ["amiodarone", "lidocaine", "procainamide", "epinephrine", "magnesium-sulfate", "sotalol", "mexiletine", "metoprolol", "esmolol", "propranolol", "potassium-chloride", "calcium-chloride", "sodium-bicarbonate", "flecainide", "quinidine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "VF has no pulse, no pattern, and needs a shock now."], ["Most-tested fact", "Pulseless VT and VF are the shockable cardiac arrest rhythms; asystole and PEA are not."], ["Shock type", "Synchronized cardioversion for VT with a pulse; unsynchronized defibrillation for pulseless VT, VF and polymorphic VT."], ["Torsades", "Polymorphic VT with a long QT is torsades de pointes, and IV magnesium is the first drug."]],
  red: "A person who collapses and is not breathing normally needs CPR and an AED right away; call 911. Palpitations with fainting, chest pain or shortness of breath also need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Survival from witnessed VF arrest can be very high when bystanders do CPR and use an AED within a few minutes, as seen in casinos where security staff are trained.",
  src: ["AHA/ACC/HRS Guideline for Management of Patients With Ventricular Arrhythmias and the Prevention of Sudden Cardiac Death (2017)", "AHA Guidelines for CPR and Emergency Cardiovascular Care (2020, 2025)", "Mirowski M et al. N Engl J Med 1980", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"bradycardia-heart-block": {
  name: "Bradycardia and heart block",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["slow heart rate", "AV block", "atrioventricular block", "first-degree block", "Mobitz I", "Wenckebach", "Mobitz II", "complete heart block", "sick sinus syndrome"],
  what: "Bradycardia is a heart rate slower than normal, usually under 60 beats per minute. Heart block means the electrical signal from the upper chambers is delayed or stopped on its way to the lower chambers. Some slow rhythms are harmless, while others cause fainting and need a pacemaker.",
  origin: "In 1827 Irish physician Robert Adams described an older man with a very slow pulse who had repeated fainting fits, and in 1846 William Stokes described similar cases; sudden fainting from heart block is still called a Stokes-Adams attack. In 1893 Wilhelm His Jr. described the bundle that carries signals to the ventricles, and in 1906 Sunao Tawara described the AV node. In 1899 Karel Wenckebach described a pattern of gradually lengthening delay before a dropped beat by studying the pulse, and in 1924 Woldemar Mobitz used the ECG to separate second-degree block into type I (Wenckebach) and type II. In 1958 in Sweden, engineer Rune Elmqvist and surgeon Åke Senning implanted the first fully internal pacemaker into Arne Larsson, who lived to age 86 and used many pacemakers over the years.",
  timeline: [["1827", "Robert Adams describes fainting with a slow pulse."], ["1846", "William Stokes describes similar cases, later called Stokes-Adams attacks."], ["1899", "Karel Wenckebach describes progressive conduction delay with dropped beats."], ["1924", "Woldemar Mobitz classifies second-degree AV block into two types."], ["1958", "The first fully implantable pacemaker is placed in Sweden."]],
  cell: "The sinoatrial (SA) node sets the rate using pacemaker (funny) current through HCN channels and calcium currents. The AV node conducts slowly through L-type calcium channels, then the His bundle and Purkinje fibers conduct rapidly using sodium channels. Bradycardia arises from SA node dysfunction (sick sinus syndrome from fibrosis with aging) or conduction block. In first-degree block, AV node conduction is slow but every beat gets through (PR over 200 ms). In Mobitz I (Wenckebach), the AV node tires progressively, so the PR lengthens until a beat drops; it is usually at the node and often vagal. In Mobitz II, conduction fails suddenly without PR lengthening, usually below the node in the His-Purkinje system, and can progress to complete block. In third-degree block, no atrial impulses reach the ventricles; a junctional or ventricular escape rhythm takes over. Causes include fibrosis of the conduction system with age (Lenègre and Lev disease), inferior MI (the right coronary artery usually supplies the AV node), anterior MI, Lyme disease, infiltrative disease, hyperkalemia, hypothyroidism and drugs such as beta blockers, calcium channel blockers and digoxin. Increased vagal tone in athletes or during sleep causes harmless slowing.",
  body: [["Heart", "A slow rate lowers cardiac output, since output equals heart rate times stroke volume. The ventricle tries to compensate with larger beats, but when the rate is very slow output falls."], ["Brain", "Too little blood flow causes lightheadedness, confusion and fainting (Stokes-Adams attacks when sudden)."], ["Lungs and circulation", "Low output can cause fatigue, breathlessness and heart failure; in complete heart block, cannon A waves appear in the neck veins when the atria contract against closed valves."]],
  symptoms: "Many people with mild bradycardia have no symptoms, especially fit athletes. When output falls, the brain gets less blood, causing dizziness, confusion and fainting. Fatigue and poor exercise tolerance happen because the heart cannot speed up to meet demand. Shortness of breath and chest discomfort can occur from low output and backing up of blood. Sudden fainting without warning suggests a high-grade block below the AV node.",
  dx: "The ECG defines the problem. First-degree block has a PR interval over 200 ms. Mobitz I shows gradual PR lengthening then a dropped QRS. Mobitz II shows a constant PR with sudden dropped QRS complexes, often with a wide QRS. Third-degree block shows P waves and QRS complexes marching independently (AV dissociation). Holter or event monitors catch intermittent problems, and exercise testing shows whether the rate rises normally. Labs check potassium, thyroid function, digoxin levels and troponin, and Lyme serology when relevant.",
  tx: "Asymptomatic sinus bradycardia and first-degree or Mobitz I block often need only observation and removal of slowing drugs. For symptomatic, unstable bradycardia, atropine is first; it blocks muscarinic M2 receptors to remove vagal braking, so it works best for SA node and AV node problems and often fails in infranodal block or after heart transplant. If atropine fails, clinicians use transcutaneous pacing or an infusion of epinephrine or dopamine, which stimulate beta-1 receptors. Isoproterenol is used in some settings. Drug overdoses get specific antidotes: glucagon or high-dose insulin therapy for beta blocker overdose, calcium for calcium channel blocker overdose and digoxin immune fab for digoxin toxicity. Mobitz II, third-degree block and symptomatic sinus node disease are usually treated with a permanent pacemaker.",
  drugs: ["atropine", "epinephrine", "dopamine", "isoproterenol", "glucagon", "calcium-chloride", "calcium-gluconate", "digoxin-immune-fab"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "If the R is far from P, then you have a first degree. Longer, longer, longer, drop, then you have a Wenckebach. If some Ps do not get through, then you have Mobitz II. If Ps and Qs do not agree, then you have a third degree."], ["Most-tested fact", "Mobitz II and third-degree block need a pacemaker because the block is below the AV node and can suddenly stop the ventricles."], ["MI link", "Inferior MI (right coronary artery) often causes AV nodal block that responds to atropine."], ["Lyme disease", "Lyme carditis can cause heart block that changes from day to day and often improves with antibiotics."]],
  red: "A slow heart rate with fainting, chest pain, confusion, severe shortness of breath or low blood pressure needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Arne Larsson, the first person to receive a fully implantable pacemaker in 1958, received more than 20 pacemakers during his life and outlived both of the men who implanted the first one.",
  src: ["ACC/AHA/HRS Guideline on the Evaluation and Management of Patients With Bradycardia and Cardiac Conduction Delay (2018)", "AHA Guidelines for CPR and Emergency Cardiovascular Care (2020, 2025)", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"cardiac-arrest": {
  name: "Cardiac arrest",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["sudden cardiac arrest", "SCA", "out-of-hospital cardiac arrest", "OHCA", "code blue"],
  what: "Cardiac arrest is when the heart suddenly stops pumping blood, so the person collapses, has no pulse and stops breathing normally. It is different from a heart attack, though a heart attack can cause it. Without CPR and often a shock from a defibrillator, death follows within minutes.",
  origin: "For centuries people tried to revive the apparently dead with methods such as bellows and rolling over barrels. In the 1950s anesthesiologists James Elam and Peter Safar proved that mouth-to-mouth breathing could keep a person oxygenated. In 1960 William Kouwenhoven, James Jude and Guy Knickerbocker at Johns Hopkins showed that pushing hard on the chest could circulate blood without opening the chest, and combined with rescue breaths this became modern CPR. Paul Zoll's external defibrillator (1956) and Bernard Lown's direct current defibrillator added the shock. The American Heart Association issued its first CPR standards in 1966. In the late 1960s and 1970s paramedic programs such as Seattle's Medic One brought advanced care to the street, and the chain of survival idea (1991) linked fast 911 calls, bystander CPR, early defibrillation and advanced care. Automated external defibrillators (AEDs) placed in public later let untrained bystanders deliver shocks.",
  timeline: [["1950s", "Elam and Safar show mouth-to-mouth ventilation works."], ["1960", "Kouwenhoven, Jude and Knickerbocker publish closed-chest cardiac massage."], ["1966", "The American Heart Association publishes its first CPR standards."], ["1970", "Seattle's Medic One program begins paramedic-level prehospital care."], ["1991", "The chain of survival concept is published by the AHA."], ["2002", "Trials show that cooling after cardiac arrest improves neurologic outcome."]],
  cell: "Cardiac arrest is the end point of many diseases. In adults it is most often caused by VF or pulseless VT from ischemia or scar in coronary artery disease. Other rhythms are asystole (no electrical activity) and pulseless electrical activity (PEA, organized electrical activity without effective contraction), often from reversible causes known as the Hs and Ts: hypoxia, hypovolemia, hydrogen ion (acidosis), hypo- or hyperkalemia, hypothermia, tension pneumothorax, tamponade, toxins, and thrombosis of the coronary or pulmonary arteries. In children, arrest is more often from respiratory failure and hypoxia. Once flow stops, cells lose oxygen, ATP falls, sodium-potassium pumps fail and cells swell. Neurons are most sensitive: calcium floods in, glutamate excitotoxicity occurs and injury starts within minutes. After return of circulation, reperfusion injury with free radicals and inflammation causes post-cardiac arrest syndrome, including brain injury, myocardial stunning and a sepsis-like systemic inflammatory response.",
  body: [["Brain", "Brain cells begin to die within minutes without oxygen. Hippocampal neurons, cerebellar Purkinje cells and the cortex are most vulnerable, and brain injury is the main cause of death after resuscitation."], ["Heart", "Even after the heartbeat returns, the muscle can be stunned and weak for a day or more, causing low blood pressure."], ["Kidneys and liver", "Loss of blood flow can cause acute kidney injury and ischemic hepatitis (shock liver)."], ["Whole body", "Lack of oxygen causes lactic acidosis; reperfusion triggers widespread inflammation and leaky blood vessels."]],
  symptoms: "The person suddenly collapses and does not respond, because the brain stops receiving blood within seconds. There is no pulse. Breathing stops or becomes agonal gasps, which are brainstem reflexes, not effective breathing, and should not be mistaken for normal breathing. Some people have warning signs in the hours or days before, such as chest pain, palpitations, shortness of breath or fainting.",
  dx: "Recognition is clinical: unresponsive, not breathing normally and no definite pulse within 10 seconds. The monitor or AED identifies the rhythm as shockable (VF or pulseless VT) or nonshockable (asystole or PEA). During resuscitation, end-tidal carbon dioxide (capnography) reflects the quality of compressions and a sudden rise suggests return of circulation. Point-of-care ultrasound, blood gases, potassium and glucose help find reversible causes. After return of circulation, a 12-lead ECG looks for STEMI, and CT of the head and chest may look for causes.",
  tx: "Survival depends on the chain of survival: early 911 call, immediate high-quality CPR (hard and fast compressions at 100 to 120 per minute, at least 2 inches deep in adults, with full chest recoil and few pauses), early defibrillation for shockable rhythms, advanced life support and post-arrest care. Epinephrine is given during resuscitation; its alpha-1 effect constricts blood vessels and raises pressure in the aorta, improving blood flow to the heart and brain. Amiodarone or lidocaine is used for VF or pulseless VT that persists after shocks. Specific causes get specific treatment: calcium and sodium bicarbonate for high potassium, magnesium for torsades, naloxone for opioid overdose, needle decompression for tension pneumothorax, pericardiocentesis for tamponade and fibrinolytics for a suspected massive PE. After return of circulation, care includes oxygen and blood pressure support, emergency cardiac catheterization when indicated and careful temperature control to protect the brain.",
  drugs: ["epinephrine", "amiodarone", "lidocaine", "magnesium-sulfate", "calcium-chloride", "sodium-bicarbonate", "naloxone", "alteplase", "tenecteplase", "norepinephrine", "dextrose", "normal-saline"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Push hard, push fast, shock early: compressions at 100 to 120 per minute, at least 2 inches deep."], ["Most-tested fact", "VF and pulseless VT are shockable; asystole and PEA are not, so treatment focuses on CPR, epinephrine and finding the Hs and Ts."], ["Often confused with", "A heart attack is a plumbing problem (blocked artery); cardiac arrest is an electrical problem (the heart stops pumping). A heart attack can lead to cardiac arrest."], ["Capnography", "End-tidal CO2 below about 10 mmHg during CPR suggests poor compressions; a sudden jump suggests return of circulation."]],
  red: "Anyone who suddenly collapses and is not breathing normally needs emergency care right away: call 911, start chest compressions and use an AED as soon as one is available.",
  fun: "Hands-only CPR is often taught to the beat of the song Stayin' Alive, which has a tempo of about 100 beats per minute.",
  src: ["AHA Guidelines for CPR and Emergency Cardiovascular Care (2020, 2025)", "Kouwenhoven WB, Jude JR, Knickerbocker GG. JAMA 1960", "Cummins RO et al. Chain of Survival. Circulation 1991", "Hypothermia after Cardiac Arrest Study Group. N Engl J Med 2002"]
},
"hyperlipidemia": {
  name: "High cholesterol (hyperlipidemia)",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["hypercholesterolemia", "dyslipidemia", "high LDL", "hypertriglyceridemia", "familial hypercholesterolemia", "FH"],
  what: "Hyperlipidemia means there is too much fat, such as LDL cholesterol or triglycerides, in the blood. It causes no symptoms, but over years extra LDL builds up inside artery walls and leads to heart attacks and strokes.",
  origin: "In 1913 Russian scientist Nikolai Anichkov fed rabbits cholesterol and found fatty plaques in their arteries, the first strong link between cholesterol and atherosclerosis. In 1938 Norwegian physician Carl Müller described families with high cholesterol, skin fat deposits and early heart attacks, now called familial hypercholesterolemia. The Framingham Heart Study in the 1950s and 1960s showed that higher blood cholesterol predicts heart disease in the general population. In 1973 Michael Brown and Joseph Goldstein discovered the LDL receptor and how cells pull LDL out of the blood, earning the 1985 Nobel Prize. In the 1970s Japanese scientist Akira Endo found compactin, the first statin, in a fungus, and lovastatin was approved in the US in 1987. The 4S trial in 1994 proved that lowering LDL with a statin saves lives. The discovery of PCSK9 in 2003 led to PCSK9 antibodies approved in 2015.",
  timeline: [["1913", "Nikolai Anichkov links dietary cholesterol to atherosclerosis in rabbits."], ["1938", "Carl Müller describes familial hypercholesterolemia."], ["1973", "Brown and Goldstein discover the LDL receptor."], ["1987", "Lovastatin becomes the first statin approved in the US."], ["1994", "The 4S trial shows simvastatin lowers death in people with heart disease."], ["2015", "The first PCSK9 inhibitor antibodies are approved in the US."]],
  cell: "Cholesterol and triglycerides travel in lipoproteins. The liver makes VLDL (rich in triglycerides), which loses triglycerides to tissues through lipoprotein lipase and becomes IDL, then LDL. LDL carries apolipoprotein B-100, which binds the LDL receptor on liver cells; the complex is taken in by endocytosis and broken down. Liver cells sense low cholesterol through SREBP-2, which increases HMG-CoA reductase (the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis) and LDL receptors. PCSK9 binds the LDL receptor and sends it to be destroyed, lowering receptor numbers. Familial hypercholesterolemia comes from mutations in the LDL receptor gene (most often), APOB or gain-of-function PCSK9, so LDL cannot be cleared. Most high LDL is polygenic plus diet, obesity and hypothyroidism. Excess LDL enters the artery wall, is oxidized, and is taken up by macrophages, which become foam cells and start plaques. High triglycerides come from insulin resistance, diabetes, alcohol and lipoprotein lipase defects; very high levels can cause pancreatitis. HDL removes cholesterol from tissues (reverse cholesterol transport), and lipoprotein(a) is an inherited particle that adds risk.",
  body: [["Arteries", "LDL drives atherosclerosis in the coronary, carotid, leg and other arteries, leading to heart attack, stroke and peripheral artery disease."], ["Skin and tendons", "Very high LDL, as in familial hypercholesterolemia, can cause tendon xanthomas (lumps on the Achilles or knuckle tendons) and xanthelasma (yellow patches on the eyelids)."], ["Eyes", "A gray-white ring at the edge of the cornea (arcus) at a young age can be a sign of high cholesterol."], ["Pancreas", "Very high triglycerides can trigger acute pancreatitis, likely because fatty acids released inside the pancreas damage its cells."], ["Liver", "High triglycerides and insulin resistance are linked to fatty liver disease."]],
  symptoms: "High cholesterol itself causes no symptoms, because plaques build slowly and silently over decades. Symptoms appear only when plaques narrow or block arteries, such as chest pain with exertion, leg pain when walking, or a stroke. People with familial hypercholesterolemia may notice lumps on tendons or yellow eyelid patches from cholesterol deposits. Very high triglycerides can cause belly pain from pancreatitis and eruptive xanthomas, small yellow bumps on the skin.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is made with a blood lipid panel measuring total cholesterol, LDL, HDL and triglycerides; fasting is not usually required unless triglycerides are high. Clinicians estimate 10-year cardiovascular risk with tools such as the Pooled Cohort Equations or the newer AHA PREVENT equations. An LDL of 190 or higher (in mg/dL) suggests familial hypercholesterolemia and leads to family screening. Coronary artery calcium scoring can help refine risk. Tests for secondary causes include thyroid function, glucose, kidney and liver tests. Lipoprotein(a) is measured at least once in many adults.",
  tx: "Lifestyle changes help everyone: a diet low in saturated and trans fats, more fiber and plants, exercise, weight control and not smoking. Statins are the main drugs; they block HMG-CoA reductase so liver cells make less cholesterol, which raises LDL receptors and pulls LDL from the blood. They also lower heart attacks and strokes. Ezetimibe blocks the NPC1L1 cholesterol transporter in the gut. PCSK9 monoclonal antibodies (evolocumab, alirocumab) and the small interfering RNA inclisiran stop PCSK9 from destroying LDL receptors. Bempedoic acid blocks ATP citrate lyase upstream of HMG-CoA reductase. For high triglycerides, fibrates activate PPAR-alpha to increase lipoprotein lipase, and icosapent ethyl (purified EPA) lowers cardiovascular events in selected patients. Bile acid sequestrants bind bile acids in the gut. Rare severe forms may use evinacumab, lomitapide or LDL apheresis.",
  drugs: ["atorvastatin", "rosuvastatin", "simvastatin", "pravastatin", "lovastatin", "pitavastatin", "ezetimibe", "ezetimibe-simvastatin", "evolocumab", "alirocumab", "inclisiran", "bempedoic-acid", "bempedoic-acid-ezetimibe", "fenofibrate", "icosapent-ethyl", "omega-3-acid-ethyl-esters", "niacin", "colesevelam", "cholestyramine", "colestipol", "evinacumab", "lomitapide"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "LDL is Lousy (lower is better), HDL is Healthy."], ["Most-tested fact", "Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, which raises liver LDL receptors; the classic side effects are muscle pain (rarely rhabdomyolysis) and raised liver enzymes."], ["Familial hypercholesterolemia", "Autosomal dominant LDL receptor defect; tendon xanthomas and heart attacks at a young age."], ["Often confused with", "Very high triglycerides cause pancreatitis; very high LDL causes atherosclerosis."]],
  fun: "Akira Endo screened thousands of fungal strains before finding compactin in a Penicillium mold, inspired by Alexander Fleming's discovery of penicillin in another mold.",
  src: ["AHA/ACC Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol (2018)", "Brown MS, Goldstein JL. Nobel Lecture (1985)", "Scandinavian Simvastatin Survival Study (4S). Lancet 1994", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"deep-vein-thrombosis": {
  name: "Deep vein thrombosis (DVT)",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["DVT", "venous thrombosis", "venous thromboembolism", "VTE", "blood clot in the leg"],
  what: "A deep vein thrombosis is a blood clot in a deep vein, usually in the leg or pelvis. It can cause a swollen, painful leg, and part of the clot can break off and travel to the lungs (pulmonary embolism).",
  origin: "In 1856 German pathologist Rudolf Virchow explained that blood clots in veins form because of three factors: slow blood flow, injury to the vessel wall and blood that clots too easily, now called Virchow's triad. He also showed that clots from the legs can travel to the lungs. In 1916 medical student Jay McLean, working in William Howell's lab at Johns Hopkins, found an anticoagulant in liver extracts that became heparin, which entered clinical use in the late 1930s. In the 1940s Karl Paul Link at the University of Wisconsin traced a bleeding disease in cattle to spoiled sweet clover and isolated dicoumarol, leading to warfarin, approved for people in 1954. Low molecular weight heparins arrived in the 1990s, and direct oral anticoagulants such as rivaroxaban and apixaban in the 2010s made treatment at home common.",
  timeline: [["1856", "Rudolf Virchow describes the triad behind venous thrombosis."], ["1916", "Jay McLean discovers the anticoagulant that becomes heparin."], ["1940s", "Karl Paul Link isolates dicoumarol from spoiled sweet clover."], ["1954", "Warfarin is approved for use in people."], ["1990s", "Low molecular weight heparins allow outpatient DVT treatment."], ["2010s", "Direct oral anticoagulants become standard first-line therapy."]],
  cell: "DVT follows Virchow's triad. Stasis (immobility, long travel, surgery, paralysis, heart failure) lets activated clotting factors build up and starves the valve pockets of oxygen. Endothelial injury (surgery, trauma, catheters) exposes tissue factor and collagen. Hypercoagulability can be inherited (factor V Leiden, which resists inactivation by activated protein C; prothrombin G20210A; protein C, protein S or antithrombin deficiency) or acquired (cancer, pregnancy, estrogen therapy, antiphospholipid syndrome, nephrotic syndrome, recent surgery). The clot usually starts in the valve pockets of calf veins. Tissue factor and factor VIIa start the coagulation cascade, thrombin turns fibrinogen into fibrin, and red cells are trapped, forming a red venous thrombus with lines of Zahn. Clots can grow upward into the popliteal, femoral and iliac veins, where they are most likely to break off and embolize. Over weeks, the clot is broken down by plasmin or organized, but valves may be destroyed, causing chronic venous insufficiency.",
  body: [["Leg veins", "The clot blocks outflow from the leg, raising pressure in the veins and capillaries, so fluid leaks out and the leg swells."], ["Lungs", "Pieces of clot can break off and lodge in the pulmonary arteries, causing pulmonary embolism, which can be fatal."], ["Leg long-term", "Damaged valves cause post-thrombotic syndrome, with chronic swelling, pain, skin darkening and sometimes ulcers."], ["Brain (rare)", "A clot can cross into arteries through a patent foramen ovale and cause a stroke (paradoxical embolism)."]],
  symptoms: "One-sided leg swelling happens because the clot blocks blood leaving the leg. Pain, tenderness along the vein and warmth come from inflammation of the vein wall around the clot. The skin can look red or bluish from congested blood. Many DVTs, especially in hospitalized people, cause few or no symptoms, and the first sign may be a pulmonary embolism. A massively swollen, blue, painful leg (phlegmasia cerulea dolens) means the clot blocks almost all venous outflow and can threaten the limb.",
  dx: "Clinicians estimate pretest probability with the Wells score. When probability is low, a normal D-dimer (a breakdown product of fibrin) makes DVT very unlikely, though D-dimer is often high in older adults, cancer, pregnancy and after surgery. Compression ultrasound is the main test; a vein that does not collapse under the probe contains clot. Unprovoked clots may prompt a search for cancer or inherited clotting disorders in selected patients.",
  tx: "Anticoagulation is the core treatment. It stops the clot from growing and lowers the risk of pulmonary embolism while the body's own plasmin system breaks the clot down. Direct oral anticoagulants (apixaban and rivaroxaban, which block factor Xa, or dabigatran and edoxaban after an initial injectable) are preferred for most people. Low molecular weight heparin (enoxaparin) and fondaparinux enhance antithrombin against factor Xa and are used in cancer, pregnancy and other settings. Unfractionated heparin acts quickly and can be reversed with protamine. Warfarin lowers vitamin K-dependent factors and is bridged with heparin at the start because it first lowers protein C. Treatment usually lasts at least three months, longer for unprovoked or ongoing risk. Catheter-directed thrombolysis is reserved for limb-threatening clots, and IVC filters for people who cannot be anticoagulated. Prevention in hospital uses early walking, compression devices and preventive anticoagulants.",
  drugs: ["apixaban", "rivaroxaban", "edoxaban", "dabigatran", "enoxaparin", "dalteparin", "fondaparinux", "heparin", "warfarin", "argatroban", "bivalirudin", "alteplase", "protamine", "idarucizumab", "prothrombin-complex-concentrate", "phytonadione"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Virchow's triad is SHE: Stasis, Hypercoagulability, Endothelial injury."], ["Most-tested fact", "Factor V Leiden is the most common inherited cause of hypercoagulability in people of European descent."], ["Warfarin clue", "Warfarin first lowers protein C (short half-life), causing a brief hypercoagulable state, so heparin is overlapped at the start; this also explains warfarin skin necrosis."], ["Heparin warning", "A falling platelet count 5 to 10 days after starting heparin, with new clots, suggests heparin-induced thrombocytopenia; heparin is stopped and a direct thrombin inhibitor such as argatroban is used."]],
  red: "Sudden shortness of breath, chest pain, coughing blood, fainting or a fast heartbeat in someone with leg swelling can mean a clot has reached the lungs and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Warfarin was first sold as rat poison in 1948, and its name comes from WARF, the Wisconsin Alumni Research Foundation, plus the ending of coumarin.",
  src: ["CHEST Guideline: Antithrombotic Therapy for VTE Disease (2021)", "American Society of Hematology Guidelines for Management of Venous Thromboembolism (2020)", "Wells PS et al. Lancet 1997", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"pulmonary-embolism": {
  name: "Pulmonary embolism",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["PE", "lung clot", "venous thromboembolism", "VTE", "saddle embolus"],
  what: "A pulmonary embolism is a blockage in an artery of the lungs, usually from a blood clot that traveled from a deep vein in the leg. It can cause sudden shortness of breath and chest pain, and a large clot can strain the heart and cause death.",
  origin: "In the 1840s and 1850s German pathologist Rudolf Virchow showed at autopsy that clots in the lungs came from veins in the legs and coined the word embolism. In 1908 German surgeon Friedrich Trendelenburg attempted removing clots from the pulmonary artery, and later his student Martin Kirschner reported the first patient to survive the operation in 1924. In 1960 British physicians David Barritt and Simon Jordan published a small trial in The Lancet showing that heparin and an oral anticoagulant dramatically lowered deaths from PE, one of the first randomized trials in this field. Ventilation-perfusion lung scans became the main test in the 1960s to 1980s, and CT pulmonary angiography took over in the late 1990s and 2000s. Clot-busting drugs, catheter-based clot removal and PE response teams now treat the most severe cases.",
  timeline: [["1850s", "Rudolf Virchow describes embolism of venous clots to the lungs."], ["1908", "Friedrich Trendelenburg attempts pulmonary embolectomy."], ["1960", "Barritt and Jordan show anticoagulation lowers death from PE."], ["1990s", "CT pulmonary angiography begins to replace older tests."], ["2010s", "Direct oral anticoagulants are approved to treat PE."]],
  cell: "Most PEs come from DVTs in the proximal leg or pelvic veins, formed by Virchow's triad of stasis, endothelial injury and hypercoagulability. A piece breaks off, travels through the right heart and lodges in the pulmonary arteries, sometimes across the main split (saddle embolus). The blockage creates dead space: ventilated lung with no blood flow. Platelets on the clot release serotonin and thromboxane A2, which constrict pulmonary vessels and airways. Blood is diverted to other lung regions, causing ventilation-perfusion mismatch and hypoxemia, and the fast breathing it causes lowers carbon dioxide (respiratory alkalosis). Pulmonary vascular resistance rises, so the thin-walled right ventricle must pump against high afterload. It dilates, its wall tension rises, its oxygen supply falls and it can become ischemic, releasing troponin and BNP. The dilated right ventricle pushes the septum toward the left, lowering left ventricular filling and cardiac output, which can spiral into obstructive shock and cardiac arrest (often PEA). Smaller distal clots can cause pulmonary infarction with pleuritic pain. Other emboli include fat (after long bone fractures), air and amniotic fluid.",
  body: [["Lungs", "Blocked arteries create areas with air but no blood flow, so oxygen exchange falls. Small clots near the edge can cause lung infarction and pleural inflammation."], ["Right heart", "Sudden high pressure in the lung arteries strains and dilates the right ventricle. Severe strain causes right heart failure, low blood pressure and shock."], ["Left heart and circulation", "A dilated right ventricle squeezes the left ventricle, so less blood reaches the body, causing fainting, low blood pressure and sometimes cardiac arrest."], ["Long-term", "Clots that do not dissolve can scar the pulmonary arteries and cause chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH)."]],
  symptoms: "Sudden shortness of breath is the most common symptom, caused by dead space and hypoxemia. Fast breathing and a fast heart rate are the body's attempts to make up for low oxygen and low output. Sharp chest pain that worsens with a deep breath comes from lung infarction irritating the pleura. Coughing blood can happen with infarction. Fainting, low blood pressure and distended neck veins signal a large clot straining the right heart. Signs of DVT in a leg may be present.",
  dx: "Clinicians estimate probability with the Wells score or clinical judgment; in low-risk people the PERC rule can rule out PE without testing. A normal D-dimer makes PE unlikely in people at low or moderate risk. CT pulmonary angiography is the main test and shows filling defects in the pulmonary arteries; a V/Q scan is used when contrast is not safe, such as in kidney disease or some pregnancies. The ECG most often shows sinus tachycardia, sometimes signs of right heart strain such as S1Q3T3. Echocardiography shows right ventricular dilation, and troponin and BNP help sort risk. Classic but uncommon chest X-ray signs are Hampton hump and Westermark sign. Severity is graded as low risk, submassive (intermediate) or massive (high risk, with sustained low blood pressure).",
  tx: "Anticoagulation is the foundation: it stops new clot from forming while the body breaks down the existing one. Direct oral anticoagulants such as apixaban and rivaroxaban are preferred for most stable patients, and many low-risk people are treated at home. Low molecular weight heparin is used in cancer and pregnancy, and unfractionated heparin is chosen for unstable patients or when thrombolysis may be needed because it acts fast and can be reversed. Massive PE with low blood pressure is treated with systemic thrombolysis using alteplase, which turns plasminogen into plasmin to dissolve the clot, at the cost of bleeding risk. Catheter-directed therapy or surgical embolectomy is an option when bleeding risk is high or thrombolysis fails. Supportive care includes oxygen and vasopressors such as norepinephrine; large IV fluid amounts are avoided because they can overstretch the right ventricle. IVC filters are for people who cannot take anticoagulants.",
  drugs: ["apixaban", "rivaroxaban", "edoxaban", "dabigatran", "enoxaparin", "dalteparin", "fondaparinux", "heparin", "warfarin", "alteplase", "tenecteplase", "norepinephrine", "epinephrine", "dobutamine", "argatroban", "protamine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "PE classic trio: sudden shortness of breath, pleuritic chest pain and a fast heart rate, often with a leg clot."], ["Most-tested fact", "The most common ECG finding in PE is sinus tachycardia; S1Q3T3 is classic but uncommon."], ["Blood gas clue", "PE often causes hypoxemia with respiratory alkalosis and a widened A-a gradient."], ["Fat embolism", "After a long bone fracture, the triad of hypoxemia, confusion and petechial rash suggests fat embolism."]],
  red: "Sudden shortness of breath, chest pain, coughing up blood, fainting or a racing heartbeat, especially after surgery, travel, pregnancy or with a swollen leg, needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "PE is a well-known risk of long-haul flights, sometimes called economy class syndrome, though the risk applies to any long period of sitting still.",
  src: ["ESC Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Acute Pulmonary Embolism (2019)", "CHEST Guideline: Antithrombotic Therapy for VTE Disease (2021)", "Barritt DW, Jordan SC. Lancet 1960", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"peripheral-artery-disease": {
  name: "Peripheral artery disease",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["PAD", "peripheral arterial disease", "peripheral vascular disease", "PVD", "intermittent claudication", "critical limb ischemia"],
  what: "Peripheral artery disease is narrowing of the arteries outside the heart, most often in the legs, caused by atherosclerosis. It can cause leg pain with walking, slow-healing wounds and, in severe cases, loss of a limb, and it is a strong sign of heart attack and stroke risk.",
  origin: "The word claudication comes from the Latin claudicare, to limp. In 1831 French veterinarian Jean-François Bouley described a horse that limped after running and recovered with rest, with blocked leg arteries found later. In 1858 French neurologist Jean-Martin Charcot described the same pattern in a person, calling it intermittent claudication. In 1940 René Leriche described blockage of the lower aorta and iliac arteries, causing buttock pain and erectile problems (Leriche syndrome). In the 1940s Portuguese surgeon João Cid dos Santos introduced endarterectomy, coring out plaque from arteries. In 1964 American radiologist Charles Dotter opened a blocked leg artery with a catheter, the birth of angioplasty. The ankle-brachial index became a simple bedside test, and in 1999 cilostazol was approved to ease claudication. Supervised exercise therapy and antiplatelet and statin therapy are now core treatments.",
  timeline: [["1831", "Jean-François Bouley describes intermittent limping in a horse."], ["1858", "Jean-Martin Charcot describes intermittent claudication in a person."], ["1940", "René Leriche describes aortoiliac occlusion."], ["1964", "Charles Dotter performs the first percutaneous angioplasty of a leg artery."], ["1999", "Cilostazol is approved in the US for intermittent claudication."]],
  cell: "PAD is atherosclerosis of the leg arteries, driven by the same steps as coronary disease: endothelial injury, LDL entry and oxidation, monocyte recruitment and foam cell formation, smooth muscle migration and fibrous plaque. Smoking and diabetes are the strongest risk factors. Smoking damages the endothelium and lowers nitric oxide; diabetes causes advanced glycation end products, inflammation and calcification, and tends to affect the smaller arteries below the knee. As a plaque narrows the superficial femoral, popliteal or tibial arteries, flow at rest is enough, but during exercise the muscle cannot get more blood. Ischemic muscle switches to anaerobic metabolism and builds up lactate and other metabolites that stimulate pain fibers. Collateral vessels grow over time and partly bypass blockages. With severe disease, flow at rest is too low, causing chronic limb-threatening ischemia, tissue death and gangrene. A clot or embolus on top of a plaque can cause acute limb ischemia.",
  body: [["Leg muscles", "Narrowed arteries cannot meet demand during walking, causing cramping pain in the calf, thigh or buttock that eases with rest."], ["Skin and feet", "Low blood flow causes shiny, hairless, cool skin, thick nails and wounds that heal poorly, especially on the toes and heels."], ["Limb survival", "Severe narrowing can cause pain at rest, ulcers and gangrene, which can lead to amputation."], ["Heart and brain", "PAD is a marker of body-wide atherosclerosis; most people with PAD die of heart attack or stroke rather than limb loss."], ["Pelvis", "Blockage of the aorta and iliac arteries can cause buttock pain and erectile dysfunction."]],
  symptoms: "Claudication is cramping or aching in the calf, thigh or buttock that starts after walking a predictable distance and eases within minutes of rest. It happens because exercising muscle needs more blood than the narrowed artery can deliver. Rest pain in the foot, often worse at night and eased by hanging the leg over the bed, means flow is too low even at rest; gravity helps push blood down. Weak or absent pulses, cool skin, hair loss and slow-healing wounds reflect chronic low flow. Many people, especially with diabetes and nerve damage, have few symptoms until a wound appears.",
  dx: "The ankle-brachial index (ABI) compares blood pressure at the ankle with the arm; a value of 0.90 or less indicates PAD. Very high values over 1.40 suggest stiff, calcified arteries, common in diabetes and kidney disease, and are followed by a toe-brachial index. Exercise ABI testing can reveal disease when resting values are normal. Duplex ultrasound shows where arteries are narrowed. CT angiography, MR angiography or catheter angiography map arteries before procedures. Exam findings include weak pulses, a bruit, pallor on leg raising and redness when the leg hangs down.",
  tx: "Treatment protects the heart and brain and improves walking. Quitting smoking is the single most powerful step. High-intensity statins lower LDL and cardiovascular events. Antiplatelet therapy with aspirin or clopidogrel lowers heart attack and stroke risk, and low-dose rivaroxaban plus aspirin further lowers major heart and limb events in selected patients. Blood pressure and diabetes control and foot care are essential. Supervised exercise therapy trains collateral vessels and muscle metabolism and greatly increases walking distance. Cilostazol, a phosphodiesterase-3 inhibitor, dilates arteries and inhibits platelets to improve walking distance but is not used in heart failure. Revascularization with angioplasty and stents or bypass surgery is used for lifestyle-limiting claudication despite therapy and for chronic limb-threatening ischemia. Acute limb ischemia is an emergency treated with heparin and urgent clot removal or bypass.",
  drugs: ["aspirin", "clopidogrel", "rivaroxaban", "cilostazol", "atorvastatin", "rosuvastatin", "ezetimibe", "evolocumab", "alirocumab", "heparin", "alteplase", "pentoxifylline", "lisinopril", "ramipril", "varenicline", "nicotine", "bupropion"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "The 6 Ps of acute limb ischemia: Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paresthesia, Paralysis and Poikilothermia (cold)."], ["Most-tested fact", "An ABI of 0.90 or below is diagnostic of PAD; above 1.40 means the arteries are too stiff to compress."], ["Often confused with", "Neurogenic claudication from spinal stenosis hurts with standing and eases with sitting or bending forward; vascular claudication eases with simply stopping."], ["Cilostazol warning", "Cilostazol is contraindicated in heart failure of any severity."]],
  red: "A suddenly cold, pale, painful leg or foot, numbness or weakness, or a wound turning black needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Charles Dotter, the father of interventional radiology, was nominated for a Nobel Prize, and his first angioplasty patient, an 82-year-old woman who refused amputation, kept her leg.",
  src: ["ACC/AHA/Multisociety Guideline for the Management of Lower Extremity Peripheral Artery Disease (2024)", "Dotter CT, Judkins MP. Circulation 1964", "Anand SS et al. COMPASS. Lancet 2018", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"aortic-dissection": {
  name: "Aortic dissection",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["dissecting aneurysm", "type A dissection", "type B dissection", "acute aortic syndrome"],
  what: "Aortic dissection is a tear in the inner layer of the aorta, the body's main artery. Blood forces its way between the layers of the wall and splits them apart, which can block blood to organs or burst the aorta. It is a life-threatening emergency.",
  origin: "In 1760 King George II of Great Britain collapsed and died suddenly at Kensington Palace. His physician Frank Nicholls performed an autopsy and described blood within the wall of the aorta that had burst into the sac around the heart, one of the earliest detailed accounts of aortic dissection. In the early 1800s French physician René Laennec used the term dissecting aneurysm. For more than a century the disease was nearly always fatal and diagnosed only after death. In 1955 Michael DeBakey, Denton Cooley and Oscar Creech in Houston reported the first successful surgical repair of an aortic dissection, and DeBakey later created a classification by location. In 1970 surgeons at Stanford proposed the simpler type A and type B system still used today. Modern CT imaging, beta blocker therapy to lower the force on the aorta, and stent grafts placed through the groin have greatly improved survival.",
  timeline: [["1760", "King George II dies; his autopsy describes a dissected, ruptured aorta."], ["1819", "René Laennec uses the term dissecting aneurysm."], ["1955", "DeBakey, Cooley and Creech report the first successful surgical repair."], ["1970", "The Stanford type A and B classification is introduced."], ["1990s", "Endovascular stent grafts begin to be used for the descending aorta."]],
  cell: "The aortic wall has three layers: intima, media and adventitia. The media is made of elastic lamellae, smooth muscle cells and extracellular matrix that resist pressure. Anything that weakens the media or raises wall stress predisposes to dissection. Long-standing hypertension is the most common risk factor; it narrows the vasa vasorum that feed the media and causes medial degeneration. Inherited connective tissue diseases cause cystic medial degeneration (loss of elastic fibers and smooth muscle with accumulation of ground substance): Marfan syndrome (fibrillin-1 mutation with excess TGF-beta signaling), Loeys-Dietz syndrome (TGF-beta receptor mutations), vascular Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (type III collagen) and Turner syndrome. Bicuspid aortic valve, cocaine and methamphetamine use, pregnancy, trauma and prior heart surgery also raise risk. A tear in the intima lets blood enter the media and create a false lumen that spreads along the aorta. The false lumen can compress branch arteries, extend back to the aortic root (causing aortic regurgitation, coronary blockage or tamponade) or rupture outward.",
  body: [["Heart", "Type A dissections can tear back to the aortic root, causing acute aortic regurgitation, blocking a coronary artery (often the right) and causing a heart attack, or bleeding into the pericardium and causing tamponade."], ["Brain", "Involvement of the carotid arteries can cause a stroke, fainting or confusion."], ["Spinal cord", "Blocked intercostal or lumbar arteries can cause sudden leg weakness or paralysis."], ["Kidneys and gut", "Blocked renal or mesenteric arteries can cause kidney failure or bowel ischemia."], ["Limbs", "Reduced flow to the arms or legs causes unequal pulses and blood pressures and can cause limb ischemia."]],
  symptoms: "The classic symptom is sudden, severe tearing or ripping pain in the chest or back that is worst at the moment it starts, because the wall splits all at once. Pain moves as the dissection extends. Ascending (type A) dissections tend to cause front chest pain, while descending (type B) dissections cause pain between the shoulder blades or in the back. A difference in blood pressure or pulse between the arms comes from the false lumen compressing a branch artery. A new diastolic murmur reflects aortic regurgitation. Stroke-like symptoms, leg weakness, abdominal pain or fainting reflect blocked branch arteries or tamponade.",
  dx: "CT angiography of the chest, abdomen and pelvis is the main test and shows an intimal flap separating true and false lumens. Transesophageal echocardiography is fast and accurate in unstable patients and in the operating room, and MRI is used for stable patients and follow-up. A chest X-ray may show a widened mediastinum but can be normal. The ECG helps rule out a heart attack, though dissection can cause one. A negative D-dimer makes dissection less likely in low-risk patients. The Stanford system classifies type A as involving the ascending aorta and type B as not involving it.",
  tx: "The first goal is to lower the force on the aortic wall. IV beta blockers such as esmolol or labetalol come first to lower heart rate and the rate of pressure rise (dP/dt), with a common target heart rate of about 60 and the lowest systolic pressure that keeps organs perfused. If blood pressure stays high, a vasodilator such as nicardipine or nitroprusside is added after beta blockade, since vasodilators alone cause reflex tachycardia that can worsen the tear. Pain control with opioids also reduces sympathetic drive. Type A dissection is a surgical emergency, with replacement of the ascending aorta. Uncomplicated type B dissection is usually treated with medicines and close imaging follow-up; complicated type B (rupture, organ or limb ischemia, uncontrolled pain) is treated with endovascular stent grafts (TEVAR). Long-term blood pressure control, beta blockers and imaging follow-up are needed. Thrombolytics and anticoagulants are avoided because they can cause catastrophic bleeding.",
  drugs: ["esmolol", "labetalol", "metoprolol", "nicardipine", "clevidipine", "nitroprusside", "diltiazem", "fentanyl", "morphine", "losartan"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Type A affects the Ascending aorta and needs an operation; type B is Beyond the arch and is usually treated with medicine."], ["Most-tested fact", "Beta blockers come before vasodilators, to prevent reflex tachycardia that increases shear stress on the wall."], ["Risk factor clues", "Hypertension in older adults; Marfan syndrome, Ehlers-Danlos, bicuspid aortic valve, Turner syndrome or cocaine use in younger people."], ["Often confused with", "Dissection can mimic a heart attack; giving antiplatelets or thrombolytics to a dissection can be fatal."]],
  red: "Sudden, severe chest or back pain, especially tearing pain, with fainting, weakness, trouble speaking or a cold limb needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "King George II of Great Britain died on his toilet (close stool) in 1760 from what is now recognized as an aortic dissection.",
  src: ["ACC/AHA Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Aortic Disease (2022)", "Nicholls F. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society (1761)", "DeBakey ME, Cooley DA, Creech O. Ann Surg 1955", "International Registry of Acute Aortic Dissection (IRAD)"]
},
"aortic-stenosis": {
  name: "Aortic stenosis",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["AS", "calcific aortic stenosis", "aortic valve stenosis", "bicuspid aortic valve disease"],
  what: "Aortic stenosis is narrowing of the aortic valve, the door between the left ventricle and the aorta. The heart has to push harder to get blood through the stiff, calcified valve, and once symptoms start it can quickly become life-threatening without valve replacement.",
  origin: "Physicians in the 1600s, including French physician Lazare Rivière, described hardened aortic valves found at autopsy. In 1904 German pathologist Johann Mönckeberg described calcific aortic stenosis of aging valves, distinct from rheumatic disease. For decades there was no fix. In 1960 Dwight Harken in Boston performed one of the first successful aortic valve replacements with a mechanical valve, and the following decades brought improved mechanical and tissue (bioprosthetic) valves. In 2002 French cardiologist Alain Cribier implanted the first transcatheter aortic valve (TAVR) through a catheter in a patient too sick for surgery. After the PARTNER trials showed strong results, TAVR was approved in the US in 2011 and is now used across the range of surgical risk.",
  timeline: [["1904", "Johann Mönckeberg describes calcific aortic stenosis."], ["1960", "Dwight Harken performs one of the first successful aortic valve replacements."], ["2002", "Alain Cribier performs the first transcatheter aortic valve implantation."], ["2011", "TAVR is approved in the US for patients who cannot have surgery."], ["2019", "Trials support TAVR in patients at low surgical risk."]],
  cell: "In older adults, calcific aortic stenosis was once considered simple wear and tear, but it is an active process that resembles atherosclerosis. Mechanical stress injures the valve endothelium, LDL and lipoprotein(a) enter the leaflets and are oxidized, and macrophages and T cells cause inflammation. Valve interstitial cells change into bone-forming (osteoblast-like) cells and lay down calcium, making the leaflets thick and stiff so they cannot open fully. A congenital bicuspid aortic valve (about 1 to 2 percent of people) is under more stress and calcifies earlier, often by age 50 to 60. Rheumatic heart disease causes commissural fusion and usually affects the mitral valve too. As the valve opening shrinks, the left ventricle faces high pressure overload, and myocytes add sarcomeres in parallel, causing concentric hypertrophy. The thick wall needs more oxygen, has less capillary density and is compressed during systole, so the subendocardium becomes ischemic even with normal coronary arteries. Over time, fibrosis and diastolic dysfunction follow, and finally systolic failure.",
  body: [["Heart", "Pressure overload causes concentric left ventricular hypertrophy, a stiff ventricle, and eventually heart failure. The thick muscle can become ischemic, causing angina."], ["Brain", "During exertion, muscles' vessels dilate but output cannot rise through the fixed narrow valve, so blood pressure drops and a person can faint."], ["Lungs", "A stiff, struggling ventricle raises filling pressure, which backs into the lungs and causes shortness of breath."], ["Gut and blood", "High shear stress across the valve breaks down von Willebrand factor multimers, which can cause bleeding from intestinal angiodysplasia (Heyde syndrome)."]],
  symptoms: "Many people have no symptoms for years while the ventricle compensates. The three classic symptoms are angina, syncope and heart failure. Angina comes from a thick muscle needing more oxygen while getting less flow. Exertional fainting happens because the fixed valve keeps cardiac output from rising when blood vessels in the muscles dilate. Shortness of breath and reduced exercise tolerance come from high pressure in the left atrium and lungs. On exam there is a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border that radiates to the carotids, and a weak, delayed pulse (pulsus parvus et tardus).",
  dx: "Echocardiography is the key test. It measures how fast blood moves across the valve, the mean pressure gradient and the valve area. Severe AS is generally defined by a peak velocity of 4 m/s or more, a mean gradient of 40 mmHg or more or a valve area of 1.0 cm2 or less. Some patients have severe AS with a low gradient because the heart is weak, and dobutamine stress echo or a CT calcium score helps sort this out. An ECG may show left ventricular hypertrophy. CT is used to plan TAVR, and coronary angiography looks for coexisting coronary disease.",
  tx: "No medicine slows or reverses aortic stenosis; statins do not slow its progression. The definitive treatment is replacing the valve, either by surgery (SAVR) or through a catheter (TAVR). Replacement is recommended for severe AS with symptoms, a falling ejection fraction or other high-risk features; the choice depends on age, anatomy, surgical risk and patient preference. Mechanical valves last long but need lifelong warfarin, while tissue and TAVR valves usually need antiplatelet therapy and wear out over time. Before replacement, clinicians treat high blood pressure carefully and use diuretics gently for congestion, because the ventricle relies on preload and too much fluid removal or strong vasodilators can drop blood pressure. Balloon valvuloplasty can serve as a temporary bridge. After a valve replacement, antibiotic prophylaxis is given before certain dental procedures.",
  drugs: ["furosemide", "bumetanide", "nitroprusside", "phenylephrine", "aspirin", "clopidogrel", "warfarin", "amoxicillin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "SAD: Syncope, Angina, Dyspnea. Average survival without replacement after symptoms is roughly 5 years for angina, 3 for syncope and 2 for heart failure."], ["Most-tested fact", "Crescendo-decrescendo systolic ejection murmur at the right second intercostal space radiating to the carotids, with a weak, delayed carotid pulse."], ["Age clue", "Aortic stenosis before about age 70 suggests a bicuspid valve; after that, age-related calcification."], ["Often confused with", "Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy also has a systolic murmur, but it gets louder with Valsalva and standing, while AS gets softer."]],
  red: "Fainting, chest pain, severe shortness of breath or a sudden drop in ability to exercise in someone with aortic stenosis needs prompt medical attention, and chest pain or fainting needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The first TAVR valve was crimped onto a balloon by hand, and the early devices were tested in animal hearts and cadavers before the first human case in Rouen, France.",
  src: ["ACC/AHA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Valvular Heart Disease (2020)", "Cribier A et al. Circulation 2002", "Leon MB et al. PARTNER. N Engl J Med 2010", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"pericarditis": {
  name: "Pericarditis",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["acute pericarditis", "recurrent pericarditis", "constrictive pericarditis", "pericardial effusion", "cardiac tamponade", "Dressler syndrome"],
  what: "Pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardium, the thin sac around the heart. It usually causes sharp chest pain that is worse lying down and better sitting forward. Most cases are viral and get better, but fluid can build up and squeeze the heart.",
  origin: "Ancient physicians, including Galen, described fluid around the heart. In 1669 English physician Richard Lower described a thickened pericardium that restricted the heart, an early account of constrictive pericarditis. In 1873 German physician Adolf Kussmaul described pulsus paradoxus, a large drop in pulse strength with breathing in, in patients with pericardial disease, and the rise of neck veins with inspiration that bears his name. In 1935 American surgeon C. S. Beck described the triad of low blood pressure, distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds in cardiac tamponade. Treatment for most cases remained anti-inflammatory drugs. Italian trials led by Massimo Imazio, including COPE in 2005 and ICAP in 2013, showed that adding colchicine cuts recurrences roughly in half. In 2021 rilonacept, which blocks interleukin-1, was approved in the US for recurrent pericarditis.",
  timeline: [["1669", "Richard Lower describes constrictive pericarditis."], ["1873", "Adolf Kussmaul describes pulsus paradoxus."], ["1935", "C. S. Beck describes the triad of cardiac tamponade."], ["2005", "The COPE trial shows colchicine prevents recurrent pericarditis."], ["2013", "The ICAP trial confirms colchicine benefit in acute pericarditis."], ["2021", "Rilonacept is approved for recurrent pericarditis."]],
  cell: "The pericardium has two layers, the visceral and parietal, with a small amount of lubricating fluid between them. In acute pericarditis, a trigger such as a virus (coxsackievirus and echovirus are classic), autoimmune disease, kidney failure (uremia), recent heart attack, heart surgery, radiation, cancer or tuberculosis causes inflammation. The innate immune system is central: damaged cells release danger signals that activate the NLRP3 inflammasome in neutrophils and macrophages, producing interleukin-1 beta. This drives fever, pain and fibrinous exudate, giving the classic bread and butter appearance at autopsy and the scratchy friction rub. Inflammation also spreads to the outer layer of heart muscle, causing diffuse ST elevation on the ECG. Fluid may build up (effusion); if it gathers fast or in large amounts, pressure in the sac rises above the pressure in the heart's chambers, collapsing the right heart in diastole and blocking filling (tamponade). Chronic scarring and calcification can encase the heart and limit filling (constrictive pericarditis). Colchicine blocks microtubule assembly in neutrophils and inhibits the inflammasome, and IL-1 blockers target the same pathway.",
  body: [["Heart sac", "Inflamed pericardial layers rub together, causing chest pain and a friction rub. Fluid may build up between them."], ["Heart filling", "A rapidly growing effusion causes cardiac tamponade, stopping the chambers from filling and dropping blood pressure. Chronic scarring causes constriction with poor filling."], ["Heart muscle", "Inflammation can spread to the myocardium (myopericarditis), raising troponin and sometimes weakening the heart."], ["Veins, liver and legs", "Constrictive pericarditis backs up blood into the veins, causing distended neck veins, liver congestion, ascites and leg swelling."]],
  symptoms: "The chest pain is sharp and pleuritic, worse with breathing in and lying flat, and better sitting up and leaning forward, because these positions change how the inflamed layers touch. It can spread to the trapezius ridge, since the phrenic nerve carries pain from the pericardium. Fever and fatigue come from systemic inflammation. Shortness of breath and lightheadedness can reflect a growing effusion. Tamponade causes low blood pressure, distended neck veins, muffled heart sounds and pulsus paradoxus (a fall in systolic pressure of more than 10 mmHg on inspiration), because the trapped right ventricle bulges into the left when it fills with breathing in.",
  dx: "Acute pericarditis is diagnosed when at least two of four features are present: typical chest pain, a pericardial friction rub, typical ECG changes and a new or worsening effusion. The ECG classically shows widespread concave ST elevation with PR depression (and PR elevation in aVR), unlike the regional ST elevation of a heart attack. Echocardiography looks for effusion and signs of tamponade, such as right atrial and right ventricular collapse in diastole. CRP is usually high, and troponin may rise with myocarditis. Further tests for cause are used in selected cases, and cardiac MRI or CT shows inflammation or a thick pericardium in constriction.",
  tx: "Most cases of viral or idiopathic pericarditis are treated with an NSAID such as ibuprofen or high-dose aspirin, which block COX enzymes and reduce prostaglandin-driven inflammation and pain, plus colchicine, which cuts the risk of recurrence. Aspirin is preferred after a heart attack because other NSAIDs can impair healing. Exercise is limited until symptoms and CRP settle, longer for athletes and myocarditis. Corticosteroids are reserved for specific causes or when NSAIDs cannot be used, because they increase recurrence. Recurrent pericarditis may be treated with IL-1 blockers such as rilonacept or anakinra. Uremic pericarditis improves with dialysis, and bacterial or tuberculous pericarditis needs drainage and antimicrobials. Tamponade is an emergency treated with pericardiocentesis (draining fluid with a needle) and fluids to support filling. Constrictive pericarditis may need surgical removal of the pericardium.",
  drugs: ["ibuprofen", "aspirin", "colchicine", "naproxen", "indomethacin", "ketorolac", "prednisone", "rilonacept", "anakinra", "normal-saline"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Pericarditis pain is better sitting forward; think of the patient leaning forward on a table."], ["Most-tested fact", "Diffuse ST elevation with PR depression is pericarditis; localized ST elevation with reciprocal depression is STEMI."], ["Beck triad", "Tamponade: low blood pressure, distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds, plus pulsus paradoxus and electrical alternans on the ECG."], ["Post-MI timing", "Pericarditis 1 to 3 days after an MI is from local inflammation; weeks later it is autoimmune Dressler syndrome."]],
  red: "Chest pain with fainting, low blood pressure, severe shortness of breath or confusion can mean cardiac tamponade or a heart attack and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Electrical alternans, where the QRS height switches beat to beat, happens because the heart literally swings back and forth inside a large effusion.",
  src: ["ESC Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Pericardial Diseases (2015)", "Imazio M et al. ICAP. N Engl J Med 2013", "Klein AL et al. RHAPSODY. N Engl J Med 2021", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"endocarditis": {
  name: "Infective endocarditis",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["IE", "bacterial endocarditis", "subacute bacterial endocarditis", "SBE", "acute bacterial endocarditis", "prosthetic valve endocarditis"],
  what: "Infective endocarditis is an infection of the inner lining of the heart, usually a heart valve. Bacteria form clumps called vegetations that destroy the valve and can break off and travel to the brain, kidneys and other organs. It needs weeks of antibiotics and sometimes surgery.",
  origin: "In 1885 Canadian physician William Osler gave his famous Gulstonian Lectures in London on malignant endocarditis, bringing together scattered descriptions and showing that the vegetations contained microbes. Earlier, in 1872, German pathologist Moritz Roth described the retinal hemorrhages with pale centers now called Roth spots, and in 1899 Edward Janeway described painless spots on the palms and soles. Osler described the painful finger nodes that bear his name. Before antibiotics, endocarditis was almost always fatal. Penicillin in the 1940s made it curable for the first time, and heart valve surgery later allowed infected valves to be replaced. In 1994 David Durack and colleagues at Duke published the Duke criteria for diagnosis, revised in 2000 and again in 2023. In 2007 the American Heart Association narrowed dental antibiotic prophylaxis to the highest-risk patients.",
  timeline: [["1872", "Moritz Roth describes the retinal spots now called Roth spots."], ["1885", "William Osler delivers his Gulstonian Lectures on malignant endocarditis."], ["1940s", "Penicillin makes endocarditis curable."], ["1994", "The Duke criteria for diagnosis are published."], ["2007", "AHA limits dental antibiotic prophylaxis to the highest-risk patients."], ["2023", "The Duke-ISCVID criteria update the diagnostic rules."]],
  cell: "Normal valve endothelium resists infection. Damage from turbulent flow (rheumatic, congenital or degenerative valve disease, prosthetic valves, devices) or repeated injection of particles exposes the matrix, and platelets and fibrin form a sterile clot (nonbacterial thrombotic vegetation). When bacteria enter the blood, from the mouth, skin, gut or injection, they stick to it. Viridans group streptococci use surface adhesins to bind fibrin and platelets. Staphylococcus aureus uses fibronectin- and clumping factor-binding proteins and can invade even normal valves, causing rapid destruction. Bacteria multiply inside the vegetation, hidden from neutrophils and coated by fibrin and biofilm, which is why long courses of bactericidal antibiotics are needed. The infection destroys leaflets, causing regurgitation, and can spread to form abscesses in the valve ring. Pieces break off as septic emboli. Immune complexes deposit in kidneys, skin and eyes, causing glomerulonephritis, Osler nodes and Roth spots. S. aureus is the most common cause overall, especially with injection drug use (often the tricuspid valve); viridans strep follows dental bacteremia; enterococci follow GI or urinary procedures; Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis) is linked to colon cancer; coagulase-negative staph infects prosthetic valves; HACEK organisms often cause culture-negative cases.",
  body: [["Heart valves", "Vegetations destroy leaflets and chordae, causing sudden valve leaks and heart failure. Abscesses in the valve ring can cause heart block."], ["Brain", "Septic emboli can cause strokes, brain abscesses and mycotic aneurysms that may bleed."], ["Lungs", "Right-sided endocarditis, usually of the tricuspid valve, sends septic emboli to the lungs, causing multiple nodules and cavities."], ["Kidneys", "Emboli cause infarcts, and immune complexes cause glomerulonephritis with blood in the urine."], ["Spleen", "Emboli cause splenic infarcts or abscesses and left upper belly pain; the spleen may enlarge."], ["Skin, nails and eyes", "Splinter hemorrhages under the nails, painless Janeway lesions on palms and soles, painful Osler nodes on finger pads and Roth spots in the retina."]],
  symptoms: "Fever is the most common sign, from cytokines released as bacteria seed the blood. A new or changed heart murmur reflects valve destruction. Acute endocarditis (often S. aureus) causes high fever, chills and rapid illness over days. Subacute endocarditis (often viridans strep) causes weeks of low fever, night sweats, fatigue and weight loss. Emboli cause sudden strokes, belly or flank pain or cold limbs. Immune complex deposits cause painful finger nodes, joint aches and blood in the urine.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses the Duke criteria, updated in 2023 as Duke-ISCVID. The two major criteria are positive blood cultures with typical organisms (multiple sets are drawn before antibiotics) and imaging evidence of endocardial involvement, such as a vegetation, abscess or new valve leak. Minor criteria include a predisposing heart condition or injection drug use, fever, embolic or vascular signs, immune signs and cultures that do not meet major criteria. Transthoracic echocardiography is done first, and transesophageal echo is more sensitive, especially for prosthetic valves and abscesses. Cardiac CT and PET-CT help in prosthetic valve and device infections.",
  tx: "Treatment is 4 to 6 weeks of IV bactericidal antibiotics chosen by organism, because bacteria inside vegetations are hard to reach. Empiric therapy while cultures are pending often includes vancomycin plus an agent covering streptococci and gram-negatives, such as ceftriaxone. Viridans strep is treated with penicillin G or ceftriaxone. Methicillin-sensitive S. aureus is treated with nafcillin, oxacillin or cefazolin, and MRSA with vancomycin or daptomycin. Enterococci are treated with ampicillin plus ceftriaxone or gentamicin. Prosthetic valve staph infections add rifampin and gentamicin. Surgery is needed for heart failure from valve destruction, abscess or heart block, uncontrolled infection, fungal or highly resistant organisms and large vegetations with high embolism risk. For people who inject drugs, treatment of opioid use disorder and harm reduction are part of compassionate care (SAMHSA helpline 1-800-662-4357). Antibiotic prophylaxis before dental work is given only for the highest-risk patients, such as those with prosthetic valves or prior endocarditis.",
  drugs: ["vancomycin", "ceftriaxone", "penicillin-g", "nafcillin", "oxacillin", "cefazolin", "daptomycin", "ampicillin", "gentamicin", "rifampin", "ampicillin-sulbactam", "amoxicillin", "cefepime", "dalbavancin", "buprenorphine", "buprenorphine-naloxone", "methadone"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "FROM JANE: Fever, Roth spots, Osler nodes, Murmur, Janeway lesions, Anemia, Nail (splinter) hemorrhages, Emboli."], ["Osler vs Janeway", "Osler nodes are Ouchy (painful, immune); Janeway lesions are painless and embolic."], ["Most-tested fact", "S. aureus is the most common cause overall and in people who inject drugs, who often have tricuspid valve infection."], ["Strep gallolyticus", "Streptococcus gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis) endocarditis calls for a colonoscopy to look for colon cancer."], ["Culture-negative", "HACEK organisms (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) and prior antibiotics are classic causes of culture-negative endocarditis."]],
  red: "Fever with sudden weakness, trouble speaking, severe shortness of breath, chest pain or confusion in someone with a heart valve problem or who injects drugs needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "William Osler, who described much of endocarditis, became one of the four founding professors of Johns Hopkins Hospital and is often called the father of modern medicine.",
  src: ["AHA Scientific Statement: Infective Endocarditis in Adults (2015)", "Duke-ISCVID Criteria for Infective Endocarditis (2023)", "AHA Prevention of Infective Endocarditis Guideline (2007, updated 2021)", "Osler W. Gulstonian Lectures on Malignant Endocarditis (1885)", "Durack DT et al. Am J Med 1994"]
},
"cardiomyopathy": {
  name: "Cardiomyopathy",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["dilated cardiomyopathy", "DCM", "hypertrophic cardiomyopathy", "HCM", "HOCM", "restrictive cardiomyopathy", "ARVC", "takotsubo cardiomyopathy", "cardiac amyloidosis"],
  what: "Cardiomyopathy is a disease of the heart muscle itself, not caused mainly by blocked arteries or valve problems. The muscle may become stretched and weak (dilated), thick (hypertrophic) or stiff (restrictive), which can lead to heart failure, dangerous rhythms or sudden death.",
  origin: "In 1957 British cardiologist Wallace Brigden used the word cardiomyopathy for heart muscle diseases of unknown cause. In 1958 British pathologist Donald Teare described young people who died suddenly with an unusually thick, asymmetric heart wall, a landmark description of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. In 1980 a World Health Organization task force grouped the cardiomyopathies as dilated, hypertrophic and restrictive. In 1990 the Seidman laboratory at Harvard found the first gene for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a mutation in beta-myosin heavy chain, showing it is a disease of the sarcomere. Heart transplantation, first done by Christiaan Barnard in 1967, became a treatment for end-stage disease, and implantable defibrillators reduced sudden death. Targeted drugs followed: tafamidis for transthyretin amyloid cardiomyopathy in 2019 and mavacamten, a cardiac myosin inhibitor, for obstructive HCM in 2022.",
  timeline: [["1957", "Wallace Brigden introduces the term cardiomyopathy."], ["1958", "Donald Teare describes asymmetric hypertrophy in young people who died suddenly."], ["1967", "Christiaan Barnard performs the first human heart transplant."], ["1990", "The first HCM gene, in beta-myosin heavy chain, is identified."], ["2019", "Tafamidis is approved for transthyretin amyloid cardiomyopathy."], ["2022", "Mavacamten is approved for obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."]],
  cell: "Dilated cardiomyopathy (most common) has weak, stretched ventricles with eccentric hypertrophy (sarcomeres added in series). Up to a third of cases are genetic, most often truncating mutations in titin, plus lamin A/C and others. Acquired causes include alcohol, doxorubicin and trastuzumab, viral myocarditis, pregnancy (peripartum), Chagas disease, thiamine deficiency, tachycardia and iron overload. Myocytes lose contractile proteins and die, fibrosis follows and neurohormonal activation worsens remodeling. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is usually autosomal dominant mutations in sarcomere genes, most often beta-myosin heavy chain (MYH7) and myosin-binding protein C (MYBPC3). Excess actin-myosin cross-bridge activity makes the muscle hypercontractile, energy-hungry and thick, with disorganized myocyte disarray and fibrosis. Septal thickening plus systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve can obstruct outflow (HOCM). Restrictive cardiomyopathy has stiff walls that fill poorly, from amyloid (transthyretin or light chain fibrils), sarcoidosis, hemochromatosis, radiation or endomyocardial fibrosis. Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy comes from desmosome mutations, with fibrofatty replacement of the right ventricle. Takotsubo cardiomyopathy is reversible apical ballooning after a catecholamine surge from severe stress.",
  body: [["Heart pumping", "Dilated hearts squeeze weakly (reduced ejection fraction); hypertrophic and restrictive hearts squeeze well but fill poorly (diastolic dysfunction)."], ["Heart rhythm", "Scarring, fibrosis and myocyte disarray create circuits for ventricular arrhythmias; HCM is a leading cause of sudden death in young athletes in the US."], ["Lungs", "High filling pressures back up into the lungs, causing shortness of breath and pulmonary edema."], ["Valves", "Stretching of the ventricle pulls the mitral leaflets apart (functional mitral regurgitation); in HOCM the mitral leaflet is drawn into the outflow tract."], ["Brain and body", "Enlarged, poorly contracting chambers and atrial fibrillation increase the risk of clots and stroke. Amyloid can also affect nerves and cause carpal tunnel syndrome."]],
  symptoms: "Shortness of breath, swelling and fatigue come from heart failure: fluid backs up behind a weak or stiff ventricle. In HOCM, chest pain comes from thick muscle needing more oxygen than it gets, and fainting with exertion comes from outflow obstruction that worsens when the heart beats hard and fills less. Palpitations and sudden death come from ventricular arrhythmias. The HCM murmur gets louder with Valsalva or standing, because less filling brings the septum and mitral leaflet closer and worsens obstruction. Many people with HCM have no symptoms, and the first sign may be sudden cardiac arrest.",
  dx: "Echocardiography is the main test: it shows chamber size, wall thickness, ejection fraction, outflow obstruction and filling. Cardiac MRI shows scar and specific patterns, such as diffuse late enhancement in amyloid. The ECG may show high voltage and deep T waves in HCM, or low voltage despite thick walls in amyloid. A technetium pyrophosphate scan, with blood and urine tests to exclude light chain disease, can diagnose transthyretin amyloid without biopsy. Genetic testing and screening of first-degree relatives are recommended in HCM and familial DCM. Coronary imaging rules out ischemic causes, and endomyocardial biopsy is used in selected cases.",
  tx: "Dilated cardiomyopathy with reduced ejection fraction is treated like other HFrEF: an ARNI or ACE inhibitor or ARB, an evidence-based beta blocker, a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist and an SGLT2 inhibitor, with loop diuretics for fluid and removal of toxins such as alcohol. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is treated with beta blockers or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers to slow the heart and improve filling; disopyramide reduces contractility; mavacamten inhibits cardiac myosin ATPase to reduce excess contraction and obstruction. In obstructive HCM, dehydration, vasodilators, nitrates and high-dose diuretics are avoided because they lower preload and worsen obstruction. Septal myectomy or alcohol septal ablation relieves severe obstruction. Transthyretin amyloid is treated with stabilizers (tafamidis, acoramidis) or a gene silencer (vutrisiran). ICDs prevent sudden death in high-risk patients, and advanced disease may need a ventricular assist device or heart transplant.",
  drugs: ["sacubitril-valsartan", "lisinopril", "losartan", "carvedilol", "metoprolol", "bisoprolol", "spironolactone", "empagliflozin", "dapagliflozin", "furosemide", "propranolol", "verapamil", "diltiazem", "disopyramide", "mavacamten", "tafamidis", "acoramidis", "vutrisiran", "amiodarone", "apixaban", "warfarin", "thiamine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Dilated is a floppy balloon, hypertrophic is a thick brick, restrictive is a stiff box."], ["Most-tested fact", "HCM is the most common cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes in the US; it is autosomal dominant, often from beta-myosin heavy chain or myosin-binding protein C mutations."], ["Murmur maneuvers", "Valsalva and standing make the HOCM murmur louder; squatting and handgrip make it softer."], ["Dilated causes", "ABCCCD: Alcohol, Beriberi, Coxsackie B, Chagas, Cocaine, Doxorubicin, plus peripartum and genetic causes."], ["Takotsubo", "Broken heart syndrome: apical ballooning after severe stress, more common in postmenopausal women, usually recovers within weeks."]],
  red: "Fainting during exercise, chest pain, a racing heartbeat with dizziness or sudden severe shortness of breath needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Takotsubo cardiomyopathy is named after a Japanese octopus trap, because the ballooned left ventricle looks like the pot's narrow neck and round base.",
  src: ["AHA/ACC Guideline for the Management of Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (2024)", "ACC/AHA/HFSA Guideline for the Management of Heart Failure (2022)", "Teare D. Br Heart J 1958", "Geisterfer-Lowrance AA et al. Cell 1990", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"pulmonary-hypertension": {
  name: "Pulmonary arterial hypertension",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["PAH", "pulmonary hypertension", "PH", "idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension", "primary pulmonary hypertension"],
  what: "Pulmonary arterial hypertension is high blood pressure in the arteries of the lungs caused by narrowing and thickening of the small lung arteries. The right side of the heart has to push harder and can eventually fail. It is rare and serious but much more treatable than it once was.",
  origin: "In 1891 German physician Ernst von Romberg described thickened lung arteries at autopsy in a patient without heart or lung disease. In 1951 David Dresdale and colleagues in New York used the new technique of right heart catheterization to describe primary pulmonary hypertension. In the late 1960s an epidemic in Europe was linked to aminorex, an appetite-suppressant drug, and in the 1990s the diet drug combination fen-phen was linked to PAH and heart valve disease. In 1981 the US National Institutes of Health began a registry that showed median survival of under three years without treatment. In 1995 epoprostenol, a continuous IV prostacyclin, became the first approved therapy, and in 2000 two groups found that mutations in BMPR2 cause many inherited cases. Bosentan (2001), sildenafil (2005) and other drugs followed, and sotatercept, approved in 2024, targets the cell growth signals that drive the disease.",
  timeline: [["1891", "Ernst von Romberg describes pulmonary vascular sclerosis."], ["1951", "Dresdale and colleagues describe primary pulmonary hypertension by catheterization."], ["1995", "Epoprostenol becomes the first approved PAH therapy in the US."], ["2000", "Mutations in BMPR2 are linked to inherited PAH."], ["2001", "Bosentan becomes the first oral endothelin receptor antagonist approved."], ["2024", "Sotatercept, an activin signaling inhibitor, is approved for PAH."]],
  cell: "PAH (WHO group 1) is a disease of the small pulmonary arteries. Triggers include BMPR2 mutations (reduced bone morphogenetic protein receptor 2 signaling lets cells grow unchecked), drugs and toxins (methamphetamine, older appetite suppressants, dasatinib), connective tissue disease (especially systemic sclerosis), HIV, portal hypertension, congenital heart shunts and schistosomiasis. Three signaling pathways become imbalanced. Endothelin-1, a strong vasoconstrictor and growth factor, is overproduced and acts on ETA and ETB receptors. Nitric oxide production falls, so less cGMP forms in smooth muscle. Prostacyclin production falls, so less cAMP forms. The result is vasoconstriction, smooth muscle and endothelial cell proliferation, resistance to apoptosis, inflammation and in-situ thrombosis. Arteries thicken with medial hypertrophy, intimal fibrosis and plexiform lesions (tangles of abnormal channels). Pulmonary vascular resistance rises, and the right ventricle first hypertrophies, then dilates and fails. Other groups of pulmonary hypertension are caused by left heart disease (most common), lung disease or hypoxia, chronic clots (CTEPH) and miscellaneous causes.",
  body: [["Lung arteries", "Narrowed, thickened small arteries raise resistance to blood flow, so pressure in the pulmonary circulation climbs."], ["Right heart", "The right ventricle thickens, then dilates and fails (cor pulmonale), causing tricuspid regurgitation and low output."], ["Veins, liver and legs", "Right heart failure backs blood into the veins, causing distended neck veins, liver congestion, ascites and leg swelling."], ["Brain and muscles", "Low output from a failing right heart reduces blood flow to the brain and muscles, causing fatigue and fainting with exertion."]],
  symptoms: "Shortness of breath with exertion and fatigue are the earliest and most common symptoms, because the stiff pulmonary circulation cannot increase flow during exercise. Symptoms are often blamed on being out of shape, delaying diagnosis by years. Chest pain comes from a strained right ventricle with reduced blood supply. Fainting with exertion means the right ventricle cannot keep output up. Leg swelling and belly swelling reflect right heart failure. Exam findings include a loud pulmonic component of the second heart sound, a right ventricular heave and a tricuspid regurgitation murmur.",
  dx: "Echocardiography is the first test and estimates pulmonary pressure and right ventricular function. Right heart catheterization is required to confirm PAH: under current ESC/ERS definitions, a mean pulmonary artery pressure above 20 mmHg, a pulmonary artery wedge pressure of 15 mmHg or less and a pulmonary vascular resistance above 2 Wood units. Vasoreactivity testing identifies the small group who respond to calcium channel blockers. A V/Q scan screens for chronic clots (CTEPH). Pulmonary function tests, chest CT, sleep studies, HIV testing, liver tests and autoimmune labs look for causes. BNP, the six-minute walk test and functional class are used to judge risk.",
  tx: "Treatment targets the three imbalanced pathways, usually starting with two drugs together. Endothelin receptor antagonists (bosentan, ambrisentan, macitentan) block endothelin-1; they can cause birth defects, so pregnancy prevention is required. PDE-5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil) prevent breakdown of cGMP, and riociguat directly stimulates soluble guanylate cyclase to make more cGMP; riociguat and PDE-5 inhibitors are never combined. Prostacyclin pathway drugs (IV epoprostenol, treprostinil by IV, under the skin, inhaled or oral routes, inhaled iloprost and the oral receptor agonist selexipag) raise cAMP to relax and slow growth of vessels; IV epoprostenol is used for the highest-risk patients. Sotatercept traps activins to rebalance growth signaling. Calcium channel blockers are used only in proven vasoreactive patients. Supportive care includes diuretics, oxygen, supervised exercise and vaccinations. Lung transplant is an option for advanced disease.",
  drugs: ["ambrisentan", "bosentan", "macitentan", "sildenafil", "tadalafil", "riociguat", "epoprostenol", "treprostinil", "treprostinil-inhaled", "iloprost", "selexipag", "sotatercept", "furosemide", "spironolactone", "nifedipine", "diltiazem", "amlodipine", "warfarin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Three pathways, three drug families: Endothelin (block it), Nitric oxide (boost it with PDE-5 inhibitors or riociguat), Prostacyclin (replace it)."], ["Most-tested fact", "Right heart catheterization is required to diagnose PAH; BMPR2 mutation is the classic inherited cause."], ["Pathology", "Plexiform lesions are the hallmark of severe PAH."], ["Drug safety", "Endothelin receptor antagonists and riociguat are teratogenic; PDE-5 inhibitors must never be combined with nitrates or riociguat."]],
  red: "Fainting, chest pain, coughing blood, severe shortness of breath or sudden worsening swelling in someone with pulmonary hypertension needs emergency care (call 911). Interruption of a continuous IV prostacyclin infusion is also an emergency.",
  fun: "Sildenafil was first studied for angina, then became famous for erectile dysfunction before being approved for PAH under a different brand name.",
  src: ["ESC/ERS Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Pulmonary Hypertension (2022)", "Dresdale DT et al. Am J Med 1951", "Hoeper MM et al. STELLAR. N Engl J Med 2023", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"hypertensive-emergency": {
  name: "Hypertensive emergency",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["hypertensive crisis", "malignant hypertension", "accelerated hypertension", "hypertensive urgency"],
  what: "A hypertensive emergency is severely high blood pressure, usually 180/120 or higher, that is actively damaging organs such as the brain, heart, kidneys or eyes. It needs careful, controlled lowering of blood pressure in a hospital.",
  origin: "In 1914 German physicians Franz Volhard and Theodor Fahr separated benign high blood pressure from a rapidly destructive malignant form that caused kidney failure and death. In 1939 Norman Keith, Henry Wagener and Nelson Barker at the Mayo Clinic graded the eye changes of hypertension and showed that people with the most severe retinal changes, including swelling of the optic disc, nearly all died within a few years without treatment. In the 1950s the first effective blood pressure drugs, such as ganglion blockers and hydralazine, began to change this. Sodium nitroprusside became a mainstay of intensive care from the 1970s, and newer titratable IV drugs such as nicardipine and clevidipine (approved in 2008) gave clinicians smoother control. The modern focus is on organ damage rather than the number alone, and on lowering pressure gradually to avoid starving organs of blood.",
  timeline: [["1914", "Volhard and Fahr describe malignant hypertension and its kidney damage."], ["1939", "Keith, Wagener and Barker classify hypertensive retinopathy and its prognosis."], ["1950s", "The first effective antihypertensive drugs begin to improve survival."], ["1970s", "IV nitroprusside becomes a standard ICU treatment."], ["2008", "Clevidipine, an ultra-short-acting IV calcium channel blocker, is approved."]],
  cell: "A sudden rise in blood pressure, often in someone with poorly controlled chronic hypertension, missed medicines, stimulant use (cocaine, amphetamines), kidney disease, preeclampsia, pheochromocytoma or rebound after stopping clonidine or beta blockers, overwhelms the normal protective response. Normally, small arteries in the brain and kidneys constrict when pressure rises (autoregulation) to keep flow steady. When pressure exceeds the upper limit of autoregulation, vessels are forced open, and high pressure is transmitted to capillaries. The endothelium is injured, causing leakage of plasma and fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles. Damaged vessels release vasoconstrictors and activate the renin-angiotensin system, and pressure-induced natriuresis causes volume depletion, which drives more renin release in a vicious cycle. Red cells are sheared on damaged vessel walls (microangiopathic hemolytic anemia). In small arterioles, smooth muscle proliferates in onion-skin layers (hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis). In people with long-standing hypertension, the autoregulation curve shifts to the right, so rapid lowering to normal levels can cause ischemia of the brain, heart or kidneys.",
  body: [["Brain", "Loss of autoregulation causes brain swelling (hypertensive encephalopathy or PRES), with headache, confusion, seizures and vision loss. Ischemic and hemorrhagic strokes can also occur."], ["Heart", "Sudden high afterload can cause acute left heart failure with pulmonary edema and can trigger chest pain or a heart attack."], ["Aorta", "High pressure and shear forces can tear the aortic wall, causing aortic dissection."], ["Kidneys", "Fibrinoid necrosis of renal arterioles causes acute kidney injury, blood and protein in the urine."], ["Eyes", "Retinal hemorrhages, exudates, cotton-wool spots and swelling of the optic disc reflect damaged retinal vessels."], ["Pregnancy", "Severe preeclampsia and eclampsia are hypertensive emergencies that threaten both parent and baby."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms reflect which organ is under attack. Severe headache, confusion, vomiting, seizures and vision changes come from brain swelling. Chest pain comes from heart strain, ischemia or aortic dissection. Shortness of breath comes from pulmonary edema when the left ventricle cannot push against the high pressure. Reduced urine output reflects kidney injury. Some people with very high pressure have no organ damage; this was called hypertensive urgency and is managed differently, usually with oral medicines.",
  dx: "The diagnosis rests on very high blood pressure, typically above 180/120, plus evidence of new or worsening organ damage. Evaluation includes a neurologic exam, a fundoscopic exam for retinal hemorrhages and papilledema, ECG and troponin for heart injury, chest X-ray for pulmonary edema, kidney function and urinalysis, a blood smear and LDH for hemolysis, and a pregnancy test in people who could be pregnant. CT or MRI of the brain and CT angiography of the chest are used when stroke or aortic dissection is suspected. Toxicology screening can identify stimulant use.",
  tx: "Treatment uses titratable IV drugs in an ICU with close monitoring. For most emergencies, the goal is to lower blood pressure by no more than about 25 percent in the first hour, then gradually toward normal over the next 24 to 48 hours, because the brain and kidneys have adjusted to high pressure. Exceptions are aortic dissection (rapid lowering with beta blockers first), severe preeclampsia or eclampsia, and pheochromocytoma. Common drugs are nicardipine and clevidipine (dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers that dilate arterioles), labetalol (alpha-1 and beta blocker), esmolol (short-acting beta-1 blocker), nitroglycerin (venodilator for acute heart failure and acute coronary syndrome), nitroprusside (releases nitric oxide to dilate arteries and veins, with a risk of cyanide toxicity) and fenoldopam (dopamine-1 agonist). In preeclampsia, IV labetalol, hydralazine or oral nifedipine are used, and magnesium sulfate prevents seizures. For cocaine or pheochromocytoma, benzodiazepines and phentolamine (an alpha blocker) are used, and beta blockers alone are avoided.",
  drugs: ["nicardipine", "clevidipine", "labetalol", "esmolol", "nitroglycerin", "nitroprusside", "hydralazine", "fenoldopam", "phentolamine", "magnesium-sulfate", "nifedipine", "lorazepam", "clonidine", "metoprolol"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Emergency equals End-organ damage; urgency means high numbers without organ damage."], ["Most-tested fact", "Lower mean arterial pressure by no more than about 25 percent in the first hour, except for aortic dissection, where heart rate and pressure are lowered quickly."], ["Cocaine and pheochromocytoma", "Beta blockers alone can leave alpha receptors unopposed and raise pressure; alpha blockade with phentolamine and benzodiazepines are used."], ["Nitroprusside", "Nitroprusside can cause cyanide and thiocyanate toxicity, especially with long use or kidney failure; hydroxocobalamin and sodium thiosulfate are antidotes."]],
  red: "Very high blood pressure with chest pain, shortness of breath, severe headache, confusion, seizure, vision loss, weakness, trouble speaking or back pain needs emergency care (call 911). In pregnancy or after delivery, a severe headache or vision changes need emergency care.",
  fun: "Before modern drugs, malignant hypertension had a one-year survival rate worse than many cancers, which is why it was called malignant.",
  src: ["ACC/AHA High Blood Pressure Guideline (2017)", "AHA/ACC High Blood Pressure Guideline (2025)", "Keith NM, Wagener HP, Barker NW. Am J Med Sci 1939", "ACOG Practice Bulletin: Gestational Hypertension and Preeclampsia (2020)"]
},
"long-qt-syndrome": {
  name: "Long QT syndrome and torsades de pointes",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["LQTS", "prolonged QT interval", "torsades de pointes", "TdP", "drug-induced long QT", "Romano-Ward syndrome", "Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome"],
  what: "Long QT syndrome means the heart's electrical recharge between beats takes too long, seen as a long QT interval on the ECG. It can be inherited or caused by drugs and low electrolytes, and it can trigger a twisting, dangerous ventricular rhythm called torsades de pointes that causes fainting or sudden death.",
  origin: "In 1957 Norwegian physicians Anton Jervell and Fred Lange-Nielsen described a family in which several deaf children had a very long QT interval, fainting spells and sudden death. In 1963 and 1964 Cesarino Romano in Italy and Owen Conor Ward in Ireland separately described families with the same heart problem but normal hearing, an autosomal dominant form now called Romano-Ward syndrome. In 1966 French cardiologist François Dessertenne named torsades de pointes, meaning twisting of the points, for the way the QRS seems to twist around the baseline. In 1995 and 1996 Mark Keating and colleagues identified the first long QT genes, encoding potassium and sodium channels, showing that LQTS is a channelopathy. Since then, many common drugs have been found to block the same potassium channel, and drug-induced QT prolongation has become a major safety issue in drug development.",
  timeline: [["1957", "Jervell and Lange-Nielsen describe congenital deafness with long QT and sudden death."], ["1963", "Romano describes inherited long QT with normal hearing; Ward reports similar families in 1964."], ["1966", "François Dessertenne names torsades de pointes."], ["1995", "The first long QT genes, KCNH2 (hERG) and SCN5A, are identified."], ["1996", "KCNQ1 is identified as the gene for the most common type, LQT1."]],
  cell: "The QT interval reflects ventricular depolarization and repolarization. Repolarization depends mainly on outward potassium currents: IKr (rapid, through the hERG channel encoded by KCNH2) and IKs (slow, encoded by KCNQ1). In LQT1, loss of IKs function means the heart cannot shorten repolarization when adrenaline rises, so events happen during exercise, especially swimming. In LQT2, loss of IKr function makes events happen with sudden noises or emotion and after childbirth. In LQT3, a gain-of-function SCN5A mutation leaves a persistent late sodium current, and events happen at rest or during sleep. Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome is autosomal recessive (two KCNQ1 or KCNE1 mutations) and also causes deafness because the same channel maintains potassium in the inner ear. Acquired long QT is far more common: many drugs block hERG, including class IA and III antiarrhythmics, macrolides, fluoroquinolones, some antipsychotics, methadone, ondansetron and some antifungals, and risk rises with low potassium, low magnesium, low calcium, slow heart rates, female sex and heart disease. Prolonged repolarization lets L-type calcium channels reopen, causing early afterdepolarizations that trigger beats; uneven repolarization across the wall lets these beats reenter, producing torsades de pointes, which can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation.",
  body: [["Heart", "Prolonged and uneven repolarization makes the ventricles prone to torsades de pointes, which can stop by itself or turn into ventricular fibrillation."], ["Brain", "Torsades causes a sudden loss of blood flow to the brain, leading to fainting and sometimes seizure-like jerking, which can be mistaken for epilepsy."], ["Ears", "In Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndrome, the potassium channel defect in the inner ear causes congenital deafness."]],
  symptoms: "Many people with a long QT have no symptoms. When torsades occurs, the heart beats too fast and chaotically to pump well, causing sudden palpitations, dizziness and fainting. If the rhythm does not stop on its own, it can become ventricular fibrillation and cause cardiac arrest. Fainting during exercise, swimming, loud noises such as an alarm clock, or sleep can point to the inherited type. Seizure-like movements during an episode come from brief lack of blood flow to the brain.",
  dx: "The ECG shows a prolonged corrected QT interval (QTc). A QTc above about 470 ms in men and 480 ms in women is prolonged, and above 500 ms carries a much higher risk of torsades. T waves may look broad (LQT1), notched (LQT2) or late-onset (LQT3). Torsades on the monitor shows polymorphic VT with QRS complexes that twist around the baseline, often starting after a short-long-short sequence of beats. The Schwartz score combines ECG findings, symptoms and family history. Genetic testing confirms the type and allows screening of family members. Electrolytes, magnesium and a review of all medicines are checked.",
  tx: "Torsades in a person without a pulse is treated with defibrillation and CPR. IV magnesium sulfate is the first drug for torsades and works even when magnesium levels are normal, by blocking calcium influx that drives early afterdepolarizations. Low potassium is corrected. Speeding up the heart with overdrive pacing or isoproterenol shortens the QT and prevents pauses that trigger torsades, mainly in acquired long QT. All QT-prolonging drugs are stopped. In congenital LQTS, beta blockers such as nadolol or propranolol are the mainstay, especially for LQT1 and LQT2, by blocking adrenergic triggers. Mexiletine blocks the late sodium current and helps in LQT3. High-risk patients may receive an ICD or left cardiac sympathetic denervation. People with LQTS avoid QT-prolonging drugs and keep electrolytes normal, and family members are screened.",
  drugs: ["magnesium-sulfate", "potassium-chloride", "isoproterenol", "nadolol", "propranolol", "metoprolol", "mexiletine", "lidocaine", "epinephrine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "LQT1 swims, LQT2 hears an alarm clock (or has a baby), LQT3 sleeps."], ["Most-tested fact", "IV magnesium sulfate is the first drug for torsades de pointes."], ["Drugs that prolong QT", "Think ABCDE: AntiArrhythmics (class IA and III), AntiBiotics (macrolides, fluoroquinolones), AntiCychotics (haloperidol), AntiDepressants (TCAs), AntiEmetics (ondansetron), plus methadone."], ["Deafness clue", "Long QT with congenital deafness is Jervell and Lange-Nielsen (recessive); long QT with normal hearing is Romano-Ward (dominant)."]],
  red: "Fainting, especially during exercise, swimming, loud noises or sleep, or a seizure in someone with long QT or on QT-prolonging drugs needs emergency care (call 911). A person who collapses and is not breathing normally needs CPR and an AED right away.",
  fun: "Several drugs have been pulled from the US market because they prolonged QT and caused torsades, including the heartburn drug cisapride and the antihistamine terfenadine.",
  src: ["AHA/ACC/HRS Guideline for Management of Patients With Ventricular Arrhythmias and the Prevention of Sudden Cardiac Death (2017)", "Jervell A, Lange-Nielsen F. Am Heart J 1957", "Wang Q et al. Nat Genet 1996", "CredibleMeds QT drug lists", "AHA Guidelines for CPR and Emergency Cardiovascular Care"]
},
"rheumatic-heart-disease": {
  name: "Rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["acute rheumatic fever", "ARF", "RHD", "rheumatic carditis", "Sydenham chorea"],
  what: "Rheumatic fever is an immune reaction that can follow an untreated strep throat. The immune system attacks the heart, joints, skin and brain. Repeated attacks can scar the heart valves for life, which is called rheumatic heart disease.",
  origin: "In 1686 English physician Thomas Sydenham described the jerky movements of children now called Sydenham chorea, later linked to rheumatic fever. In the late 1700s and early 1800s physicians, including David Pitcairn and William Charles Wells, connected rheumatism of the joints with heart disease. In 1904 German pathologist Ludwig Aschoff described the inflammatory nodules in the heart that bear his name. In the 1920s and 1930s Rebecca Lancefield at the Rockefeller Institute classified streptococci into groups, showing that group A strep causes the throat infections that come before rheumatic fever. In 1944 T. Duckett Jones published diagnostic criteria still used, in revised form, today. Around 1950, studies of military recruits at Warren Air Force Base showed that treating strep throat with penicillin prevents rheumatic fever. In wealthy countries rheumatic fever then became rare, but it remains common in many low-income regions and Indigenous communities.",
  timeline: [["1686", "Thomas Sydenham describes the chorea later named after him."], ["1904", "Ludwig Aschoff describes Aschoff bodies in rheumatic hearts."], ["1930s", "Rebecca Lancefield classifies streptococci and identifies group A strep."], ["1944", "T. Duckett Jones publishes the Jones criteria."], ["1950", "Studies at Warren Air Force Base show penicillin for strep throat prevents rheumatic fever."], ["2015", "The American Heart Association revises the Jones criteria and adds echocardiography."]],
  cell: "Rheumatic fever follows group A Streptococcus pyogenes pharyngitis, usually by 2 to 4 weeks, in genetically susceptible people. The bacterial M protein and carbohydrates resemble human proteins such as cardiac myosin, tropomyosin, laminin and proteins on valve endothelium. Antibodies and CD4 T cells made against the bacteria cross-react with these tissues (molecular mimicry), a type II hypersensitivity reaction. Antibodies bind the valve endothelium, upregulate VCAM-1 and let T cells infiltrate the valve, where they release interferon gamma and TNF. The result is pancarditis, inflammation of the pericardium, myocardium and endocardium. Aschoff bodies (foci of fibrinoid necrosis with T cells, plasma cells and activated macrophages called Anitschkow cells, with caterpillar-shaped nuclei) are the hallmark. Antibodies against basal ganglia neurons cause Sydenham chorea. Repeated infections cause repeated attacks, and healing leads to chronic valve scarring: thickened, fused leaflets and commissures, shortened chordae and calcification. The mitral valve is most affected, then the aortic. Skin streptococcal infections can also trigger rheumatic fever in some settings.",
  body: [["Heart valves", "Acute inflammation causes mitral regurgitation. Years of scarring cause mitral stenosis with fish-mouth fused leaflets, and sometimes aortic valve disease."], ["Heart muscle and sac", "Myocarditis can cause acute heart failure, the main cause of death in the acute phase; pericarditis causes chest pain and a rub."], ["Joints", "Migratory polyarthritis moves from one large joint to another, such as knees and ankles, and heals without lasting damage."], ["Brain", "Sydenham chorea causes jerky, purposeless movements and emotional changes, often months after the infection."], ["Skin", "Erythema marginatum (pink rings with clear centers) on the trunk and painless subcutaneous nodules over bony areas can appear."], ["Lungs and atria", "Chronic mitral stenosis raises left atrial pressure, causing atrial enlargement, atrial fibrillation, clots and pulmonary hypertension."]],
  symptoms: "Fever and painful, swollen joints that move from joint to joint come from immune complexes and inflammation in the joint lining, and they respond quickly to anti-inflammatory drugs. Shortness of breath, a new murmur and chest pain come from carditis. Chorea reflects antibodies affecting the basal ganglia. Years later, people with rheumatic heart disease develop shortness of breath, palpitations from atrial fibrillation and fatigue as mitral stenosis worsens, and symptoms often get worse during pregnancy, when blood volume rises.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses the revised Jones criteria, which require evidence of a recent group A strep infection (positive throat culture or rapid test, or raised antistreptolysin O or anti-DNase B antibodies) plus major and minor criteria. Major criteria are carditis (including findings seen only on echocardiography), arthritis, chorea, erythema marginatum and subcutaneous nodules. Minor criteria are fever, joint pain, raised ESR or CRP and a prolonged PR interval. Echocardiography detects valve inflammation and chronic valve damage, and it is used for screening in high-risk regions.",
  tx: "Treatment eradicates the strep infection, controls inflammation and prevents recurrences. Penicillin (or another antibiotic for those with allergy) clears any remaining group A strep. Aspirin or other NSAIDs relieve arthritis and fever, and corticosteroids are sometimes used for severe carditis. Heart failure is treated with diuretics and other standard drugs. Chorea may be managed with medicines such as carbamazepine or valproic acid. The most important step is secondary prophylaxis: regular long-acting benzathine penicillin G injections for years, which prevent new strep infections and new attacks that would further damage valves. Primary prevention means prompt antibiotic treatment of strep throat. Chronic valve disease may need balloon valvotomy for mitral stenosis or valve repair or replacement, and atrial fibrillation with mitral stenosis is treated with warfarin, not a DOAC.",
  drugs: ["penicillin-g-benzathine", "penicillin-v", "amoxicillin", "azithromycin", "cefdinir", "aspirin", "naproxen", "ibuprofen", "prednisone", "furosemide", "carbamazepine", "valproic-acid", "haloperidol", "warfarin", "metoprolol", "digoxin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Jones major criteria spell JONES: Joints, O for a heart shape (carditis), Nodules, Erythema marginatum, Sydenham chorea."], ["Most-tested fact", "Rheumatic fever is caused by molecular mimicry between strep M protein and cardiac proteins (type II hypersensitivity); Aschoff bodies with Anitschkow cells are the hallmark."], ["Valve clue", "Mitral stenosis in a young adult from a low-income region, or in pregnancy, is rheumatic until proven otherwise."], ["Strep sequelae", "Treating strep throat with antibiotics prevents rheumatic fever but does not reliably prevent post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis."]],
  red: "Shortness of breath, chest pain, fainting, a racing or irregular heartbeat or sudden weakness or trouble speaking in someone with rheumatic heart disease needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Rheumatic heart disease still affects tens of millions of people worldwide, even though it is almost entirely preventable with penicillin, one of the oldest and cheapest antibiotics.",
  src: ["AHA Scientific Statement: Revision of the Jones Criteria (2015)", "World Health Organization Rheumatic Heart Disease Fact Sheet", "Denny FW, Wannamaker LW et al. JAMA 1950", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"shock": {
  name: "Shock (hypovolemic, cardiogenic, septic, obstructive)",
  sys: "Heart & Blood Vessels",
  aka: ["circulatory shock", "hypovolemic shock", "hemorrhagic shock", "cardiogenic shock", "septic shock", "distributive shock", "obstructive shock", "anaphylactic shock", "neurogenic shock"],
  what: "Shock is a life-threatening state in which blood flow cannot deliver enough oxygen to the body's cells. It can come from losing blood or fluid, a failing heart, widely dilated and leaky blood vessels or something blocking blood flow. Without fast treatment, organs fail.",
  origin: "The word shock entered medicine in the 1700s from a translation of French surgeon Henri-François Le Dran's writing on gunshot wounds. In 1872 American surgeon Samuel Gross called shock a rude unhinging of the machinery of life. During World War I, physiologists including Walter Cannon studied wounded soldiers in shock. In the 1930s Alfred Blalock showed that traumatic shock was mainly due to loss of blood and fluid into injured tissues, which led to fluid and blood replacement and saved many lives in World War II. In 1971 Max Harry Weil and Herbert Shubin grouped shock into four types still used today: hypovolemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive. In 1999 the SHOCK trial showed that early artery opening saves lives in cardiogenic shock after a heart attack. The Surviving Sepsis Campaign, launched in 2002, standardized early care for septic shock, and the Sepsis-3 definitions were published in 2016.",
  timeline: [["1872", "Samuel Gross describes shock as a rude unhinging of the machinery of life."], ["1930s", "Alfred Blalock shows traumatic shock results from blood and fluid loss."], ["1971", "Weil and Shubin propose the four categories of shock."], ["1999", "The SHOCK trial supports early revascularization in cardiogenic shock."], ["2002", "The Surviving Sepsis Campaign is launched."], ["2016", "The Sepsis-3 definitions of sepsis and septic shock are published."]],
  cell: "Oxygen delivery depends on cardiac output (heart rate times stroke volume), hemoglobin and oxygen saturation. Shock is a failure of this delivery or of cells to use oxygen. Hypovolemic shock (bleeding, vomiting, diarrhea, burns) lowers preload. Cardiogenic shock (large MI, cardiomyopathy, arrhythmia, valve rupture) lowers contractility. Obstructive shock (tension pneumothorax, cardiac tamponade, massive PE) blocks filling or outflow. Distributive shock (sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic injury, adrenal crisis) causes widespread vasodilation; in sepsis, pathogen molecules such as endotoxin bind toll-like receptors on immune cells, releasing TNF, IL-1 and IL-6, inducing nitric oxide synthase and causing vasodilation, capillary leak and microvascular clotting; in anaphylaxis, IgE-triggered mast cells release histamine; in neurogenic shock, loss of sympathetic tone causes vasodilation and bradycardia. In all types, low perfusion triggers baroreceptor-driven sympathetic and renin-angiotensin activation to raise heart rate and squeeze vessels. When cells lack oxygen, they switch to anaerobic glycolysis, producing lactate and acidosis; ATP falls, ion pumps fail, cells swell and die. Ongoing shock causes multi-organ dysfunction.",
  body: [["Brain", "Reduced blood flow causes anxiety, confusion and then lowered consciousness."], ["Heart", "Low pressure reduces coronary flow and acidosis weakens contraction, worsening output in a downward spiral."], ["Kidneys", "Low perfusion lowers urine output and can cause acute tubular necrosis and acute kidney injury."], ["Lungs", "Inflammation, especially in sepsis, makes lung capillaries leaky, causing acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)."], ["Gut and liver", "Diverting blood away from the gut can cause bowel ischemia and let bacteria cross into the blood; the liver can develop ischemic hepatitis."], ["Blood", "Sepsis and massive bleeding can trigger disseminated intravascular coagulation, with both clotting and bleeding."]],
  symptoms: "A fast heart rate and fast breathing are the body's early responses to low oxygen delivery and acidosis. Blood pressure may stay normal at first because vessels constrict, so a falling pressure is a late sign. In hypovolemic and cardiogenic shock, the skin is cold, pale and clammy because blood is shunted away from the skin. In early septic shock, the skin may be warm and flushed from vasodilation. Neurogenic shock causes low blood pressure with a slow heart rate and warm skin. Confusion and low urine output reflect poor brain and kidney perfusion. Cardiogenic shock adds lung crackles and distended neck veins; obstructive shock from tamponade or tension pneumothorax causes distended neck veins with clear lungs.",
  dx: "Shock is a clinical diagnosis: low blood pressure (often a mean arterial pressure below 65 mmHg) or signs of poor perfusion, such as cool skin, slow capillary refill, confusion, low urine output and a raised lactate. The type is sorted by history, exam and bedside ultrasound, which looks at heart function, the inferior vena cava, pericardial fluid, lungs and the abdomen. Labs include lactate, blood cultures, complete blood count, chemistries, troponin, coagulation tests and blood gas. Septic shock under Sepsis-3 is sepsis needing vasopressors to keep a mean arterial pressure of 65 mmHg with lactate above 2 mmol/L despite fluids. The ECG, chest X-ray and CT find specific causes.",
  tx: "Treatment restores oxygen delivery while fixing the cause. Hypovolemic shock gets fluid replacement; hemorrhagic shock gets blood products in a balanced ratio, control of bleeding and tranexamic acid in trauma. Septic shock is treated with early broad-spectrum antibiotics after blood cultures, IV crystalloid fluids, source control and norepinephrine as the first vasopressor (alpha-1 vasoconstriction with some beta-1 support); vasopressin is added next, and hydrocortisone for shock that persists. Anaphylactic shock is treated first with IM epinephrine, which reverses vasodilation and airway swelling. Cardiogenic shock is treated with revascularization for MI, inotropes such as dobutamine or milrinone, vasopressors and sometimes mechanical circulatory support. Obstructive shock needs needle decompression for tension pneumothorax, pericardiocentesis for tamponade or thrombolysis for massive PE. Neurogenic shock is treated with fluids, vasopressors and atropine for bradycardia.",
  drugs: ["norepinephrine", "vasopressin", "epinephrine", "phenylephrine", "dopamine", "dobutamine", "milrinone", "angiotensin-ii", "hydrocortisone", "normal-saline", "lactated-ringers", "plasma-lyte", "albumin-human", "tranexamic-acid", "piperacillin-tazobactam", "vancomycin", "cefepime", "meropenem", "ceftriaxone", "alteplase", "atropine", "methylene-blue", "epinephrine-auto-injector"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Shock types: hypovolemic is an empty tank, cardiogenic is a broken pump, distributive is pipes too wide and leaky, obstructive is a kinked hose."], ["Most-tested fact", "Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor in septic shock; epinephrine is the first drug for anaphylaxis."], ["Hemodynamic patterns", "Hypovolemic and cardiogenic shock have high SVR and low output; septic shock has low SVR and often high output; cardiogenic shock has high wedge pressure."], ["Neurogenic clue", "Low blood pressure with a slow heart rate after a spinal cord injury suggests neurogenic shock."]],
  red: "Confusion, fainting, cold clammy or mottled skin, a racing pulse, fast breathing, very little urine, or severe bleeding, swelling of the face or throat after an allergen, or high fever with feeling extremely ill needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "A 2006 study of septic shock found that each hour of delay in effective antibiotics after low blood pressure began was linked to a lower chance of survival.",
  src: ["Surviving Sepsis Campaign: International Guidelines for Management of Sepsis and Septic Shock (2021)", "Singer M et al. Sepsis-3. JAMA 2016", "Hochman JS et al. SHOCK Trial. N Engl J Med 1999", "ATLS Advanced Trauma Life Support (American College of Surgeons)", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"asthma": {
  name: "Asthma",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["bronchial asthma", "reactive airway disease", "allergic asthma", "exercise-induced bronchoconstriction", "status asthmaticus"],
  what: "Asthma is a long-term inflammation of the airways that makes them swollen, sensitive and prone to tightening. During an attack the airways narrow, causing wheezing, coughing, chest tightness and shortness of breath. Unlike COPD, the narrowing usually reverses with treatment.",
  origin: "The word asthma comes from a Greek word meaning to pant, and Hippocratic writers used it for breathlessness. In the 1100s the physician Maimonides wrote a treatise on asthma for a sultan, advising rest, clean air and chicken soup. In 1860 English physician Henry Hyde Salter, who had asthma himself, described the spasm of airway muscle and triggers such as animals and exercise. In the early 1900s injected adrenaline was found to relieve attacks, and later inhaled bronchodilators followed. Albuterol (salbutamol), a selective beta-2 agonist, was developed in Britain in the 1960s and reached the US in the early 1980s. Inhaled corticosteroids such as beclomethasone, introduced in the early 1970s, changed asthma from a muscle problem to an inflammatory disease to be controlled. The Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) began in 1993. Biologic antibodies, starting with omalizumab in 2003, now target specific inflammatory pathways in severe asthma.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Hippocratic writers use the word asthma for panting and breathlessness."], ["1860", "Henry Hyde Salter publishes On Asthma, describing airway spasm and triggers."], ["1968", "Salbutamol (albuterol) is introduced in the United Kingdom."], ["1972", "Inhaled beclomethasone brings inhaled corticosteroids into asthma care."], ["1993", "The Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) is launched."], ["2003", "Omalizumab becomes the first biologic approved for asthma in the US."]],
  cell: "Most asthma is type 2 (Th2-high) inflammation. In allergic asthma, inhaled allergens such as dust mites, pollen, mold and animal dander are taken up by dendritic cells, which prime CD4 Th2 cells. Airway epithelium also releases alarmins (TSLP, IL-33, IL-25) that activate Th2 cells and type 2 innate lymphoid cells. These release IL-4 and IL-13 (which switch B cells to make IgE and increase mucus) and IL-5 (which recruits and activates eosinophils). IgE coats mast cells through FcεRI receptors. On re-exposure, allergens cross-link IgE and mast cells release histamine, leukotrienes (C4, D4, E4) and prostaglandin D2 within minutes, causing smooth muscle contraction, swelling and mucus (the early phase). Hours later, eosinophils, basophils and Th2 cells arrive and release major basic protein and cytokines (the late phase), keeping airways inflamed and hyperresponsive. Over years, airway remodeling occurs: basement membrane thickening, goblet cell and smooth muscle hyperplasia, and submucosal gland enlargement. Non-allergic triggers include viral infections, cold air, exercise, smoke and aspirin in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, where blocking COX shifts arachidonic acid toward leukotrienes. Some people have type 2-low asthma, with neutrophils, which responds less to steroids.",
  body: [["Airways", "Inflamed bronchi swell, contract and fill with thick mucus, narrowing the airway. Sputum can contain Curschmann spirals (mucus casts) and Charcot-Leyden crystals (from eosinophils)."], ["Lungs", "Air gets trapped behind narrowed airways during exhalation, causing hyperinflation and making breathing hard work. Severe attacks cause hypoxemia."], ["Breathing muscles", "Trapped air flattens the diaphragm and makes breathing inefficient, so the muscles tire; a rising carbon dioxide level means respiratory failure."], ["Heart and circulation", "Large swings in chest pressure during a severe attack cause pulsus paradoxus, a drop in blood pressure with each breath in."], ["Nose and skin", "Asthma often coexists with allergic rhinitis and eczema (the atopic triad); nasal polyps occur with aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease."]],
  symptoms: "Wheezing is the musical sound of air forced through narrowed airways, loudest when breathing out. Coughing, often at night or early morning, comes from inflamed, irritable airways and mucus; nighttime symptoms are worse partly because of natural daily rhythms in cortisol and airway tone. Chest tightness and shortness of breath come from bronchoconstriction and air trapping. Symptoms come and go and are triggered by allergens, viruses, exercise, cold air, smoke or stress. In a very severe attack, the chest may become quiet because almost no air moves, a dangerous sign that the person is tiring.",
  dx: "Diagnosis combines typical, variable symptoms with evidence of variable airflow limitation. Spirometry shows a reduced FEV1/FVC ratio that improves significantly after a bronchodilator (by at least 12 percent and 200 milliliters in FEV1). If spirometry is normal, peak flow variability or a methacholine challenge test can show airway hyperresponsiveness. Fractional exhaled nitric oxide (FeNO) and blood eosinophils identify type 2 inflammation. Allergy testing identifies triggers. In an acute attack, clinicians assess peak flow, oxygen saturation, work of breathing and ability to speak, and a normal or rising carbon dioxide level in a breathless patient signals impending respiratory failure.",
  tx: "Inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) are the foundation; they turn off inflammatory genes and calm airway hyperresponsiveness. Current GINA guidance avoids using a short-acting beta agonist (SABA) alone and favors an ICS-formoterol inhaler as both maintenance and reliever, because formoterol acts fast and every use adds steroid. Beta-2 agonists raise cAMP in airway smooth muscle to relax it; long-acting ones (LABAs) are always combined with ICS in asthma. Other options are long-acting muscarinic antagonists, montelukast (a leukotriene receptor antagonist with a boxed warning about mood changes) and biologics for severe asthma: anti-IgE (omalizumab), anti-IL-5 or IL-5 receptor (mepolizumab, reslizumab, benralizumab), anti-IL-4 receptor alpha (dupilumab) and anti-TSLP (tezepelumab). Acute attacks are treated with repeated inhaled albuterol and ipratropium, systemic corticosteroids and oxygen; severe attacks may add IV magnesium sulfate and epinephrine or terbutaline. Trigger avoidance and an action plan are central.",
  drugs: ["albuterol", "levalbuterol", "budesonide-formoterol", "albuterol-budesonide", "fluticasone-salmeterol", "mometasone-formoterol", "fluticasone-furoate-vilanterol", "fluticasone", "budesonide", "beclomethasone", "ciclesonide", "ipratropium", "albuterol-ipratropium", "tiotropium", "montelukast", "zafirlukast", "omalizumab", "mepolizumab", "benralizumab", "dupilumab", "tezepelumab", "prednisone", "methylprednisolone", "magnesium-sulfate"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Asthma is reversible: the FEV1 bounces back after albuterol. COPD does not fully bounce back."], ["Most-tested fact", "Inhaled corticosteroids are the most effective long-term controller; LABAs are never used alone in asthma because of a higher risk of severe attacks."], ["Danger sign", "A silent chest, drowsiness or a normal to rising carbon dioxide level in a wheezing patient means respiratory failure is near."], ["Samter triad", "Asthma, nasal polyps and aspirin sensitivity is aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, driven by excess leukotrienes."]],
  red: "Severe shortness of breath, trouble speaking in full sentences, blue lips, drowsiness or confusion, a reliever inhaler that is not helping or a quiet chest during an attack needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Albuterol and salbutamol are the same drug: albuterol is the US name and salbutamol is the name used in most other countries.",
  src: ["Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention (2024)", "NAEPP Expert Panel Report 3 and 2020 Focused Updates", "Salter HH. On Asthma: Its Pathology and Treatment (1860)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"copd": {
  name: "COPD (emphysema and chronic bronchitis)",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["chronic obstructive pulmonary disease", "emphysema", "chronic bronchitis", "COPD exacerbation", "alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"],
  what: "COPD is a long-term lung disease that makes it hard to breathe out, usually caused by years of smoking or breathing other harmful smoke and dust. It includes emphysema, where the air sacs are destroyed, and chronic bronchitis, where the airways are inflamed and full of mucus. The damage is mostly permanent but treatment helps.",
  origin: "In 1819 French physician René Laennec, inventor of the stethoscope, described emphysema in lungs that were overinflated and failed to collapse when the chest was opened. In the early 1800s English physician Charles Badham described chronic bronchitis. In 1846 John Hutchinson invented the spirometer to measure how much air the lungs can move. In 1963 Carl-Bertil Laurell and Sten Eriksson in Sweden discovered alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency in people with early emphysema, revealing how unchecked enzymes destroy lung tissue. In 1977 Charles Fletcher and Richard Peto showed that smoking speeds the yearly loss of lung function and that quitting slows it. Trials in 1980 and 1981 showed that long-term home oxygen helps people with severe COPD and low oxygen live longer. The Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) released its first report in 2001. Long-acting inhaled bronchodilators, such as tiotropium in the 2000s, and newer drugs including ensifentrine and dupilumab in 2024, have expanded treatment.",
  timeline: [["1819", "René Laennec describes emphysema."], ["1846", "John Hutchinson invents the spirometer."], ["1963", "Laurell and Eriksson discover alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency."], ["1977", "Fletcher and Peto show how smoking accelerates lung function loss and how quitting helps."], ["1980", "The Nocturnal Oxygen Therapy Trial shows long-term oxygen improves survival in hypoxemic COPD."], ["2001", "The first GOLD report on COPD is published."]],
  cell: "Cigarette smoke is the main cause in the US; worldwide, smoke from burning wood and other biomass fuels, air pollution and workplace dusts also matter. Smoke activates airway epithelial cells and alveolar macrophages, which release chemokines such as IL-8 and leukotriene B4 that recruit neutrophils and CD8 T cells. Neutrophils release neutrophil elastase and macrophages release matrix metalloproteinases, which digest elastin in the alveolar walls. Normally alpha-1 antitrypsin, made in the liver, neutralizes elastase; smoke oxidizes and inactivates it, tipping the protease-antiprotease balance toward destruction. Oxidative stress and apoptosis add to the loss of alveolar walls. In smokers, damage is centriacinar (upper lobes); in alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (SERPINA1 gene, PiZZ genotype), it is panacinar and lower lobe, often before age 45, and the misfolded protein can also damage the liver. Loss of elastic recoil and loss of alveolar attachments let small airways collapse during exhalation, trapping air. In chronic bronchitis, mucous gland hypertrophy (raised Reid index), goblet cell hyperplasia, impaired cilia and small airway fibrosis narrow the airways. Ventilation-perfusion mismatch causes hypoxemia and, in advanced disease, carbon dioxide retention.",
  body: [["Lungs", "Destroyed air sacs and narrowed, mucus-filled airways trap air and reduce the surface for gas exchange, causing low oxygen and sometimes high carbon dioxide."], ["Chest and breathing muscles", "Air trapping causes a barrel chest and flattened diaphragm, so breathing takes more work, and people use neck muscles and pursed lips to breathe out."], ["Heart", "Chronic low oxygen constricts lung arteries, causing pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure (cor pulmonale) with leg swelling."], ["Blood", "Low oxygen stimulates erythropoietin release from the kidneys, sometimes causing polycythemia (too many red cells)."], ["Muscles and weight", "Chronic inflammation and the high energy cost of breathing cause muscle wasting and weight loss in advanced emphysema."], ["Whole body", "COPD raises the risk of heart disease, lung cancer, osteoporosis, depression and anxiety."]],
  symptoms: "Shortness of breath that slowly worsens over years, first with exertion and later at rest, comes from air trapping and loss of gas exchange surface. A chronic cough with sputum comes from enlarged mucous glands and damaged cilia. Wheezing comes from narrowed airways. Pursed-lip breathing keeps pressure in the airways so they stay open longer during exhalation. Flare-ups (exacerbations), often triggered by viral or bacterial infections or air pollution, bring more breathlessness, cough and sputum. Advanced disease can cause blue-tinged lips from low oxygen, morning headaches and drowsiness from carbon dioxide retention, and leg swelling from right heart failure.",
  dx: "Diagnosis requires spirometry: a post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC ratio below 0.70 confirms persistent airflow obstruction. The severity of airflow limitation is graded by FEV1 as a percent of predicted (GOLD 1 to 4), and symptoms and exacerbation history guide treatment groups. Lung volumes show air trapping, and a low diffusing capacity (DLCO) points to emphysema. A chest X-ray may show hyperinflated lungs with a flat diaphragm, and CT shows the pattern of emphysema. Everyone with COPD is tested once for alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. Pulse oximetry and arterial blood gases assess oxygen and carbon dioxide levels, and blood eosinophils help decide on inhaled corticosteroids.",
  tx: "Quitting smoking is the most important step and clearly slows lung function decline; nicotine replacement, varenicline and bupropion help. Long-acting bronchodilators are the foundation: LAMAs such as tiotropium block M3 receptors on airway muscle, and LABAs raise cAMP to relax it, often both in one inhaler. Inhaled corticosteroids are added for frequent exacerbations with high blood eosinophils, though they raise pneumonia risk. Roflumilast (a PDE-4 inhibitor) and long-term azithromycin reduce exacerbations in selected patients. Ensifentrine inhibits PDE-3 and PDE-4, and dupilumab treats COPD with type 2 inflammation. Flare-ups are treated with short-acting bronchodilators, a short steroid course, antibiotics when sputum changes, oxygen targeting a saturation of 88 to 92 percent and noninvasive ventilation for rising carbon dioxide. Pulmonary rehabilitation and vaccines help, and long-term oxygen improves survival in chronic low oxygen. Alpha-1 antitrypsin augmentation, lung volume reduction and transplant help some.",
  drugs: ["tiotropium", "umeclidinium", "aclidinium", "revefenacin", "umeclidinium-vilanterol", "tiotropium-olodaterol", "glycopyrrolate-formoterol", "aclidinium-formoterol", "fluticasone-umeclidinium-vilanterol", "budesonide-glycopyrrolate-formoterol", "salmeterol", "formoterol", "arformoterol", "olodaterol", "albuterol", "albuterol-ipratropium", "ipratropium", "roflumilast", "ensifentrine", "dupilumab", "azithromycin", "prednisone", "alpha-1-proteinase-inhibitor", "varenicline"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Pink puffer (emphysema): thin, breathing hard with pursed lips, near-normal oxygen. Blue bloater (chronic bronchitis): cough, low oxygen, cor pulmonale. Most patients have features of both."], ["Most-tested fact", "Only quitting smoking and long-term oxygen (for chronic low oxygen) have been shown to improve survival in COPD."], ["Alpha-1 antitrypsin clue", "Panacinar emphysema in the lower lobes in a young person or nonsmoker, sometimes with liver disease, suggests alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency."], ["Oxygen in exacerbations", "Oxygen is titrated to a saturation of about 88 to 92 percent; too much oxygen can worsen carbon dioxide retention by worsening V/Q mismatch and the Haldane effect."], ["Often confused with", "Asthma is reversible and starts younger; COPD is persistent obstruction in people with long exposure, usually after age 40."]],
  red: "Severe shortness of breath, trouble speaking, blue lips or face, confusion or unusual drowsiness, or chest pain during a COPD flare needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Laennec invented the stethoscope in 1816 by rolling paper into a tube, reportedly to avoid placing his ear directly on a young woman's chest.",
  src: ["Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) Report (2025)", "Fletcher C, Peto R. BMJ 1977", "Nocturnal Oxygen Therapy Trial Group. Ann Intern Med 1980", "Laurell CB, Eriksson S. Scand J Clin Lab Invest 1963", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
}
});

;
DB.DIS = DB.DIS || {};
Object.assign(DB.DIS, {
"pneumonia": {
  name: "Pneumonia",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["community-acquired pneumonia", "CAP", "hospital-acquired pneumonia", "HAP", "ventilator-associated pneumonia", "VAP", "lung infection"],
  what: "Pneumonia is an infection of the lung's tiny air sacs. The sacs fill with fluid, pus and immune cells, so oxygen has a harder time getting into the blood. It causes cough, fever and shortness of breath.",
  origin: "Ancient Greek physicians described a fever with chest pain and cough that they called peripneumonia. In 1816 French physician Rene Laennec invented the stethoscope and soon described the crackles and other lung sounds of pneumonia. In the early 1880s German scientists Carl Friedlander and Albert Fraenkel identified bacteria in the lungs of people who had died of pneumonia, and Fraenkel's organism became known as the pneumococcus (Streptococcus pneumoniae). While studying these lung samples, Danish scientist Hans Christian Gram developed the Gram stain in 1884, still used every day. William Osler called pneumonia the captain of the men of death, because it killed so many people before antibiotics. Penicillin in the 1940s turned a frequently fatal illness into a treatable one. Pneumococcal vaccines followed, with a polysaccharide vaccine in 1983 and conjugate vaccines for children starting in 2000.",
  timeline: [["1816", "Rene Laennec invents the stethoscope, letting doctors hear pneumonia in the chest."], ["1884", "Hans Christian Gram publishes his staining method while studying pneumonia lung tissue."], ["1928", "Frederick Griffith's experiments with pneumococcus reveal bacterial transformation, a step toward finding DNA as the genetic material."], ["1940s", "Penicillin becomes available and sharply lowers pneumonia deaths."], ["1983", "A 23-valent pneumococcal polysaccharide vaccine is licensed in the US."], ["2000", "The first pneumococcal conjugate vaccine for infants is licensed in the US."]],
  cell: "Germs usually reach the alveoli by microaspiration of mouth and throat secretions, by inhaled droplets, or less often through the blood. Normally mucus, cilia, cough and alveolar macrophages clear them. When the load is high or defenses are weak (smoking, alcohol, a recent viral infection, older age, a ventilator tube), bacteria multiply. Streptococcus pneumoniae, the most common bacterial cause, has a polysaccharide capsule that resists phagocytosis and a toxin, pneumolysin, that damages cells. Alveolar macrophages sense the germs through Toll-like receptors and release TNF-alpha, IL-1 and IL-8. These cytokines call in neutrophils and make capillaries leaky, so fluid, fibrin, red cells and neutrophils fill the alveoli. In classic lobar pneumonia this passes through congestion, red hepatization, gray hepatization and resolution. Filled alveoli still receive blood but no air, creating a shunt and ventilation-perfusion mismatch that lowers blood oxygen. Cytokines entering the blood cause fever and, in severe cases, sepsis.",
  body: [["Lungs", "Air sacs fill with pus and fluid (consolidation). Gas exchange drops, causing low oxygen, fast breathing and crackles. Fluid can collect around the lung (parapneumonic effusion) and sometimes become infected (empyema)."], ["Pleura", "Inflammation of the lining around the lung causes sharp chest pain that worsens with a deep breath (pleuritic pain)."], ["Blood and whole body", "Bacteria or their products can enter the bloodstream, causing bacteremia and sepsis, with low blood pressure and organ injury."], ["Heart", "Low oxygen and inflammation strain the heart, and pneumonia raises the short-term risk of heart attack, arrhythmias and heart failure, especially in older adults."], ["Brain", "Low oxygen, fever and sepsis can cause confusion, which is a common sign of pneumonia in older adults."]],
  symptoms: "Cough with sputum happens because inflamed airways and air sacs make mucus and pus that the body tries to clear. Rust-colored sputum in pneumococcal pneumonia comes from red blood cells leaking into the alveoli. Fever and chills come from cytokines such as IL-1 and TNF acting on the hypothalamus. Shortness of breath and fast breathing occur because fluid-filled air sacs cannot exchange oxygen. Sharp chest pain on breathing comes from irritated pleura. Older adults may have only confusion or weakness because their immune response is blunted.",
  dx: "Diagnosis rests on symptoms plus a new infiltrate on chest X-ray. Exam may show crackles, bronchial breath sounds, dullness to percussion and increased tactile fremitus over consolidation. Pulse oximetry checks oxygen. Severity scores such as CURB-65 and the Pneumonia Severity Index help decide on hospital care. In sicker patients, sputum Gram stain and culture, blood cultures, urine antigen tests for pneumococcus and Legionella, and viral PCR tests help find the cause. Lung ultrasound and CT are also used.",
  tx: "Antibiotics kill the bacteria, chosen by setting and risk. ATS/IDSA guidance lists amoxicillin, doxycycline or, where resistance is low, a macrolide for healthy outpatients. Patients with other illnesses or in the hospital typically receive a beta-lactam (such as ceftriaxone or amoxicillin-clavulanate), which breaks bacterial cell walls, plus a macrolide or doxycycline to cover atypical germs like Mycoplasma and Legionella, or a respiratory fluoroquinolone alone. Vancomycin or linezolid are added when MRSA is a concern, and anti-Pseudomonas drugs such as piperacillin-tazobactam or cefepime when that germ is likely. Some guidelines now suggest hydrocortisone in severe community-acquired pneumonia to calm the inflammatory response. Supportive care includes oxygen, fluids and fever control. Pneumococcal, influenza and COVID-19 vaccines and smoking cessation help prevent it.",
  drugs: ["amoxicillin", "amoxicillin-clavulanate", "doxycycline", "azithromycin", "clarithromycin", "ceftriaxone", "cefpodoxime", "cefuroxime", "ampicillin-sulbactam", "levofloxacin", "moxifloxacin", "ceftaroline", "vancomycin", "linezolid", "piperacillin-tazobactam", "cefepime", "meropenem", "hydrocortisone", "acetaminophen", "pneumococcal-21-valent-conjugate-vaccine", "pneumococcal-conjugate-vaccine", "pneumococcal-polysaccharide-vaccine", "influenza-vaccine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most common bacterial cause of community-acquired pneumonia."], ["Classic sputum clues", "Rust-colored sputum suggests pneumococcus. Currant jelly sputum suggests Klebsiella, classically in people with alcohol use disorder."], ["Walking pneumonia", "Mycoplasma pneumoniae causes milder atypical pneumonia in young adults, and beta-lactams do not work on it because it has no cell wall."], ["Exam findings", "Consolidation gives dullness, increased tactile fremitus and bronchial breath sounds. A pleural effusion gives dullness with decreased fremitus."]],
  red: "Trouble breathing, blue lips, confusion, a very fast heart rate, low blood pressure or chest pain with fever can mean severe pneumonia or sepsis and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Hans Christian Gram's famous stain was developed while he looked at lung tissue from people who had died of pneumonia, and it is still the first test run on many samples today.",
  src: ["ATS/IDSA Guideline for Community-Acquired Pneumonia in Adults (2019)", "IDSA/ATS Guideline for Hospital-Acquired and Ventilator-Associated Pneumonia (2016)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "CDC Pneumococcal Vaccine Recommendations"]
},
"tuberculosis": {
  name: "Tuberculosis",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["TB", "consumption", "phthisis", "latent TB infection", "LTBI", "Pott disease (spinal TB)"],
  what: "Tuberculosis is an infection caused by the bacterium Mycobacterium tuberculosis that spreads through the air. It most often attacks the lungs, can hide quietly in the body for years, and is curable with several months of antibiotics.",
  origin: "TB is ancient. Signs of spinal TB have been found in Egyptian mummies, and Hippocrates described phthisis, a wasting disease of the lungs. In the 1700s and 1800s it was called consumption and caused a large share of deaths in Europe and North America. On March 24, 1882, German physician Robert Koch announced that he had found the cause, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, and that date is now World TB Day. Before drugs, patients were sent to sanatoriums for rest and fresh air. Albert Calmette and Camille Guerin developed the BCG vaccine, first given to people in 1921. In 1943 Albert Schatz, working in Selman Waksman's lab, isolated streptomycin, the first antibiotic active against TB. Isoniazid followed in 1952, and rifampin around the end of the 1960s, which made shorter combination therapy possible. Drug-resistant TB led to newer drugs such as bedaquiline (2012) and pretomanid (2019).",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Spinal TB is seen in Egyptian mummies, and Greek physicians describe phthisis."], ["1882", "Robert Koch announces Mycobacterium tuberculosis as the cause of TB."], ["1921", "BCG vaccine is first given to a person."], ["1943", "Streptomycin is isolated in Selman Waksman's lab, the first effective TB drug."], ["1952", "Isoniazid enters use and becomes the backbone of TB treatment."], ["2012", "Bedaquiline is approved for multidrug-resistant TB."]],
  cell: "M. tuberculosis is an acid-fast rod with a waxy cell wall rich in mycolic acids. Inhaled droplet nuclei carry it to the alveoli, where alveolar macrophages engulf it. Cord factor and other wall lipids help it block fusion of the phagosome with the lysosome, so it survives and multiplies inside macrophages. After a few weeks, dendritic cells present its antigens and macrophages release IL-12, which drives CD4 Th1 cells to make interferon-gamma. Interferon-gamma activates macrophages to kill bacteria and turn into epithelioid cells, and TNF-alpha helps organize them into granulomas with Langhans giant cells and a center of caseous necrosis. The first lesion, a Ghon focus in the lower or middle lung plus hilar node involvement, forms the Ghon complex. In most people the granuloma walls off the bacteria (latent infection). If immunity drops later, as in HIV, TNF-alpha inhibitor therapy, diabetes or older age, bacteria reactivate, usually in the oxygen-rich lung apices, forming cavities that release bacteria into the airways.",
  body: [["Lungs", "Granulomas and caseous necrosis destroy lung tissue, forming cavities in the upper lobes in reactivation TB. Cavities can bleed (hemoptysis) and spread bacteria by coughing."], ["Lymph nodes", "Hilar and neck nodes enlarge with granulomas. Neck node TB (scrofula) can drain through the skin."], ["Spine and bones", "Spread through the blood can infect vertebrae (Pott disease), causing back pain, collapse and a hunched spine."], ["Brain and meninges", "TB meningitis inflames the base of the brain, causing headache, cranial nerve palsies and hydrocephalus. It is most dangerous in young children."], ["Whole body (miliary TB)", "Blood spread seeds many tiny granulomas like millet seeds across the lungs, liver, spleen and bone marrow."], ["Adrenals, kidneys and other organs", "TB can destroy the adrenal glands (causing adrenal insufficiency) and infect the kidneys, pericardium and gut."]],
  symptoms: "A cough lasting more than three weeks, sometimes with blood, comes from inflamed and cavitating lung tissue. Fever, night sweats and weight loss come from the long-running cytokine response, especially TNF-alpha, which was once called cachectin because it drives wasting. Fatigue reflects chronic inflammation. People with latent TB have no symptoms and cannot spread the infection because the bacteria are contained inside granulomas.",
  dx: "Infection is detected with a tuberculin skin test (PPD) or an interferon-gamma release assay (IGRA) blood test, but neither tells latent from active disease. Active TB is assessed with a chest X-ray (upper lobe infiltrates or cavities), sputum smears for acid-fast bacilli, mycobacterial culture (the gold standard), and nucleic acid amplification tests such as Xpert MTB/RIF, which also detects rifampin resistance. Biopsy of other organs shows caseating granulomas. HIV testing is recommended for everyone with TB.",
  tx: "TB needs several drugs at once for months because the bacteria grow slowly and resistance develops easily with a single drug. Standard drug-sensitive TB treatment uses isoniazid (blocks mycolic acid synthesis), rifampin (blocks bacterial RNA polymerase), pyrazinamide (works in acidic areas inside macrophages) and ethambutol (blocks arabinogalactan wall synthesis) for two months, then isoniazid and rifampin for four more. A four-month regimen of rifapentine and moxifloxacin with isoniazid and pyrazinamide is also an option for some patients. Pyridoxine is given with isoniazid to prevent nerve damage. Latent TB is treated with shorter regimens such as rifapentine plus isoniazid weekly, or rifampin alone. Drug-resistant TB uses combinations such as bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid and moxifloxacin. Directly observed therapy and airborne isolation in negative-pressure rooms with N95 respirators help cure patients and protect others.",
  drugs: ["isoniazid", "rifampin", "pyrazinamide", "ethambutol", "rifapentine", "moxifloxacin", "pyridoxine", "rifabutin", "bedaquiline", "pretomanid", "linezolid", "levofloxacin", "amikacin", "streptomycin", "cycloserine", "ethionamide", "bcg-vaccine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "RIPE: Rifampin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol."], ["Side effects", "Rifampin turns body fluids orange and induces liver enzymes. Isoniazid causes hepatitis and neuropathy (prevented with vitamin B6). Pyrazinamide raises uric acid. Ethambutol harms the Eyes (red-green color vision)."], ["Most-tested fact", "Reactivation TB favors the lung apices because they have the highest oxygen levels, and M. tuberculosis is an obligate aerobe."], ["Infection control", "TB is airborne: negative-pressure room and N95 respirator, not just a surgical mask."]],
  red: "Coughing up a large amount of blood, severe shortness of breath, or headache with a stiff neck and confusion in someone with TB needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Robert Koch announced his discovery on March 24, 1882, and that date is marked every year as World TB Day.",
  src: ["ATS/CDC/ERS/IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines: Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis (2016)", "CDC Tuberculosis Treatment Guidance (4-month rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen, 2022)", "WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Koch R. Die Aetiologie der Tuberculose (1882)"]
},
"ards": {
  name: "Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["ARDS", "acute lung injury", "noncardiogenic pulmonary edema", "diffuse alveolar damage", "shock lung"],
  what: "ARDS is a sudden, severe lung injury where both lungs fill with leaky fluid even though the heart is working. Oxygen levels fall very low, and most people need a breathing machine in the ICU.",
  origin: "Doctors treating wounded soldiers in World War II and later the Vietnam War noticed that some survived their injuries only to develop stiff, waterlogged lungs, sometimes called shock lung. In 1967 David Ashbaugh, Thomas Petty and colleagues in Denver published a Lancet report of 12 adults with sudden breathlessness, low oxygen despite extra oxygen, stiff lungs and patchy infiltrates on X-ray, and gave the condition its name. A 1994 American-European consensus conference created a shared definition, which the Berlin definition updated in 2012 with mild, moderate and severe grades. The biggest treatment breakthrough came in 2000, when the ARDS Network trial showed that ventilating with smaller breaths lowered deaths, proving that the ventilator itself can injure lungs. In 2013 the PROSEVA trial showed that lying patients face down (prone positioning) also saves lives in severe cases.",
  timeline: [["1967", "Ashbaugh, Petty and colleagues describe and name the syndrome in The Lancet."], ["1994", "The American-European Consensus Conference publishes a shared definition."], ["2000", "The ARDS Network trial shows lower tidal volume ventilation reduces deaths."], ["2012", "The Berlin definition grades ARDS as mild, moderate or severe."], ["2013", "The PROSEVA trial shows prone positioning improves survival in severe ARDS."], ["2020s", "COVID-19 causes ARDS in huge numbers worldwide, and a 2023 global definition expands criteria."]],
  cell: "ARDS starts with a direct lung insult (pneumonia, aspiration, inhalation injury, near drowning) or an indirect one carried in the blood (sepsis, pancreatitis, major trauma, transfusion). Damage signals and cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6, IL-8 and TNF-alpha activate the capillary endothelium and recruit neutrophils into the lung. Neutrophils release proteases, reactive oxygen species and neutrophil extracellular traps that injure both the capillary lining and the alveolar epithelium, especially the thin type I pneumocytes. With the barrier broken, protein-rich fluid floods the alveoli (noncardiogenic edema). Plasma proteins and dead cells form hyaline membranes along the alveolar walls, the hallmark of diffuse alveolar damage. Injury to type II pneumocytes cuts surfactant production and fluid clearance, so alveoli collapse. Flooded and collapsed alveoli create a large shunt and stiff lungs. After about a week, a proliferative phase begins as type II cells and fibroblasts repair, and in some patients this leads to fibrosis.",
  body: [["Lungs", "Both lungs become heavy, wet and stiff. Large areas cannot exchange gas, causing severe low oxygen that responds poorly to extra oxygen. Survivors may have lasting scarring and reduced lung function."], ["Heart", "Low oxygen and high pressures in the lung vessels raise strain on the right ventricle, which can fail (acute cor pulmonale)."], ["Kidneys and other organs", "Inflammation, low oxygen and low blood pressure often cause multiorgan failure, including acute kidney injury."], ["Muscles and nerves", "Long ICU stays with sedation and immobility cause ICU-acquired weakness, which can take months to recover."], ["Brain and mood", "Survivors often have memory problems, depression, anxiety or PTSD after critical illness (post-intensive care syndrome)."]],
  symptoms: "Severe shortness of breath and fast breathing begin within hours to days after the trigger, because fluid-filled alveoli cannot pass oxygen into the blood. Oxygen levels stay low even with high-flow oxygen because blood is shunted past flooded air sacs. The lungs are stiff (low compliance), so breathing takes great effort. Crackles are heard throughout both lungs. Cyanosis and confusion reflect very low oxygen.",
  dx: "The Berlin definition requires onset within one week of a known insult or new or worsening symptoms, bilateral opacities on chest imaging not fully explained by effusions, collapse or nodules, and respiratory failure not fully explained by heart failure or fluid overload. Severity is graded by the PaO2/FiO2 ratio on at least 5 cm H2O of PEEP: mild 200 to 300, moderate 100 to 200, and severe 100 or less. Echocardiography or BNP helps exclude cardiogenic pulmonary edema. A 2023 global definition also allows pulse oximetry ratios and high-flow nasal oxygen.",
  tx: "Treatment supports the lungs while the cause is treated, for example antibiotics for pneumonia or source control for sepsis. Lung-protective ventilation uses small tidal volumes, limits plateau pressure and uses PEEP to keep alveoli open, which reduces ventilator-induced injury. Prone positioning for many hours a day improves oxygenation and survival in moderate to severe ARDS by opening the back of the lungs. A conservative fluid strategy reduces lung water once shock resolves. Sedatives and opioids such as propofol, dexmedetomidine and fentanyl help patients tolerate the ventilator. Neuromuscular blockers such as cisatracurium may be used briefly in severe cases. Recent guidelines suggest corticosteroids such as dexamethasone or methylprednisolone to damp inflammation. ECMO, which oxygenates blood outside the body, is used for refractory cases at specialized centers. Clot prevention, nutrition and early mobility are part of care.",
  drugs: ["propofol", "dexmedetomidine", "fentanyl", "midazolam", "cisatracurium", "rocuronium", "dexamethasone", "methylprednisolone", "norepinephrine", "furosemide", "heparin", "enoxaparin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "ARDS: Acute onset, Ratio low (PaO2/FiO2 300 or less), Diffuse bilateral opacities, not caused by heart failure (no Systolic failure as the explanation)."], ["Most-tested fact", "The hallmark pathology is diffuse alveolar damage with hyaline membranes, and the key proven therapy is low tidal volume ventilation."], ["Often confused with", "Cardiogenic pulmonary edema, which comes from high pressure in a failing heart. ARDS is leaky capillaries with normal heart pressures."], ["Common triggers", "Sepsis is the most common cause, followed by pneumonia, aspiration, trauma and pancreatitis."]],
  red: "Rapidly worsening shortness of breath, blue lips, confusion or inability to speak in full sentences, especially in someone with sepsis, pneumonia or a major injury, needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The 2000 ARDS Network trial was stopped early because the smaller-breath group had clearly fewer deaths, a finding that changed how ventilators are set for nearly all ICU patients.",
  src: ["ATS Clinical Practice Guideline: Treatment of ARDS (2024)", "ARDS Definition Task Force. Berlin Definition. JAMA 2012", "ARDS Network. N Engl J Med 2000", "Guerin C et al. PROSEVA. N Engl J Med 2013", "Ashbaugh DG et al. Lancet 1967"]
},
"pneumothorax": {
  name: "Pneumothorax",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["collapsed lung", "spontaneous pneumothorax", "tension pneumothorax", "traumatic pneumothorax"],
  what: "A pneumothorax is air trapped in the space between the lung and the chest wall. The air lets the lung collapse partly or fully. A tension pneumothorax, where pressure keeps building, is a life-threatening emergency.",
  origin: "The word pneumothorax was coined in 1803 by French physician Jean Itard, a student of Rene Laennec. Laennec described the clinical picture in detail in 1819 using his new stethoscope. For most of the 1800s, nearly all cases were blamed on tuberculosis, which can eat through the lung surface. In 1932 Danish physician Hans Kjaergaard showed that many cases occur in otherwise healthy young people from rupture of small air blisters on the lung surface, which became known as primary spontaneous pneumothorax. Through the 20th century, chest tubes with underwater seals became standard for draining air, and in the 1990s video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS) made bleb removal and pleurodesis less invasive. Trauma care and military medicine refined fast needle decompression for tension pneumothorax, now a core skill in EMS.",
  timeline: [["1803", "Jean Itard coins the term pneumothorax."], ["1819", "Rene Laennec describes the clinical features of pneumothorax."], ["1932", "Hans Kjaergaard describes primary spontaneous pneumothorax in healthy people."], ["1990s", "Video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery becomes common for recurrent pneumothorax."]],
  cell: "Normally the pleural space holds a thin film of fluid at negative pressure, which keeps the elastic lung stretched against the chest wall. When air enters that space, the pressure rises toward atmospheric and the lung's elastic recoil makes it collapse. Primary spontaneous pneumothorax happens when apical subpleural blebs or bullae rupture. These form in tall, thin young adults, where distending forces at the lung apex are greatest, and smoking adds small airway inflammation and breakdown of elastic fibers. Secondary pneumothorax occurs in diseased lungs, such as COPD with bullae, cystic fibrosis, Pneumocystis pneumonia or certain cystic lung diseases. Traumatic pneumothorax follows rib fractures, penetrating wounds or procedures. In tension pneumothorax, a one-way valve lets air in with each breath but not out. Rising pressure pushes the mediastinum to the other side, compresses the great veins and cuts venous return to the heart, producing obstructive shock.",
  body: [["Lungs", "The affected lung partly or fully collapses, lowering the area for gas exchange and causing shunting and low oxygen, especially in people with existing lung disease."], ["Pleura", "Air in the pleural space irritates the pleura, causing sudden sharp chest pain. Blood may also collect (hemopneumothorax) after trauma."], ["Heart and great vessels", "In tension pneumothorax, pressure squeezes the vena cava and shifts the heart, reducing blood return, which causes low blood pressure and can lead to cardiac arrest."], ["Neck and skin", "Air can track into the tissues of the chest and neck (subcutaneous emphysema), causing a crackling feeling under the skin."]],
  symptoms: "Sudden one-sided sharp chest pain comes from air irritating the pleura. Shortness of breath reflects loss of working lung. Breath sounds are reduced on that side because the collapsed lung is no longer next to the chest wall, and percussion sounds hyperresonant because of trapped air. In tension pneumothorax, low blood pressure, a fast heart rate, distended neck veins and, late, a trachea shifted away from the affected side occur because pressure pushes the mediastinum over and blocks venous return.",
  dx: "Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis based on respiratory distress and shock with signs on one side of the chest. Otherwise, an upright chest X-ray shows a thin white visceral pleural line with no lung markings beyond it. Point-of-care ultrasound shows absent lung sliding, a barcode or stratosphere sign in M-mode, and a lung point, which confirms the diagnosis. CT is the most sensitive test and can show blebs that predict recurrence.",
  tx: "Small, stable primary pneumothoraces can be observed, and extra oxygen speeds reabsorption of the trapped air by lowering nitrogen in the blood. Larger or symptomatic ones are treated by needle aspiration or by placing a small-bore catheter or chest tube connected to a one-way valve or underwater seal. Lidocaine numbs the chest wall for tube placement, and analgesics such as opioids treat pain. Tension pneumothorax is treated immediately by needle decompression or finger thoracostomy, followed by a chest tube. Recurrence is prevented with VATS to remove blebs plus pleurodesis, which scars the pleural layers together. Smoking cessation lowers recurrence. People are usually advised by clinicians to avoid flying until the lung has healed and to avoid scuba diving unless definitive surgery was done.",
  drugs: ["lidocaine", "fentanyl", "morphine", "ketamine", "acetaminophen", "ibuprofen", "varenicline", "nicotine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis, and it is decompressed right away without waiting for an X-ray."], ["Exam findings", "Pneumothorax: hyperresonant, decreased breath sounds, decreased fremitus. Tension adds trachea shifting away from the affected side, JVD and hypotension."], ["Classic patient", "A tall, thin young man who smokes and has sudden one-sided chest pain and breathlessness at rest."], ["Often confused with", "Hemothorax, which is dull to percussion because blood fills the space instead of air."]],
  red: "Sudden chest pain with worsening shortness of breath, fast heartbeat, low blood pressure, blue lips or confusion, especially after chest trauma, can mean tension pneumothorax and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Before antibiotics, doctors deliberately collapsed tuberculous lungs by injecting air into the chest (artificial pneumothorax) to rest the lung and help it heal.",
  src: ["British Thoracic Society Guideline for Pleural Disease (2023)", "ACCP Delphi Consensus Statement on Management of Spontaneous Pneumothorax (2001)", "ATLS Advanced Trauma Life Support, 10th edition", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "Kjaergaard H. Spontaneous pneumothorax in the apparently healthy. Acta Med Scand 1932"]
},
"cystic-fibrosis": {
  name: "Cystic fibrosis",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["CF", "mucoviscidosis"],
  what: "Cystic fibrosis is an inherited disease in which a faulty protein makes mucus thick and sticky. The mucus clogs the lungs, pancreas and other organs, causing repeated lung infections and trouble absorbing food.",
  origin: "European folklore warned that a child who tasted salty when kissed on the brow was bewitched and would soon die, likely an early description of CF. In 1938 New York pathologist Dorothy Andersen described the disease in detail from autopsies of infants and named it cystic fibrosis of the pancreas for the scarred, cystic pancreas she saw. In the late 1940s Paul di Sant'Agnese noticed that children with CF became very ill during a New York heat wave and found their sweat contained far too much salt. In 1959 Lewis Gibson and Robert Cooke standardized the sweat test still used today. In 1989 teams led by Lap-Chee Tsui, John Riordan and Francis Collins found the CFTR gene. Treatments such as dornase alfa (1993) and inhaled tobramycin improved lung care, and in 2012 ivacaftor became the first drug to fix the faulty protein itself. The triple combination elexacaftor-tezacaftor-ivacaftor (2019) now helps most people with CF.",
  timeline: [["1938", "Dorothy Andersen describes cystic fibrosis of the pancreas."], ["1959", "Gibson and Cooke publish the standardized pilocarpine sweat test."], ["1989", "The CFTR gene is identified."], ["1993", "Dornase alfa is approved to thin CF mucus."], ["2012", "Ivacaftor becomes the first CFTR modulator approved."], ["2019", "Elexacaftor-tezacaftor-ivacaftor is approved for people with at least one F508del variant."]],
  cell: "CF is autosomal recessive and caused by variants in the CFTR gene on chromosome 7. CFTR is an ATP-binding cassette protein that works as a cAMP-regulated chloride and bicarbonate channel on the apical surface of epithelial cells. The most common variant, F508del, causes the protein to misfold, so the endoplasmic reticulum tags it for destruction and little reaches the cell surface (a class II defect). Other variants block channel opening (gating, such as G551D) or production. In the airways, less chloride secretion and increased sodium absorption through ENaC dehydrate the airway surface liquid, so mucus becomes thick and cilia cannot clear it. Trapped bacteria, first Staphylococcus aureus and later mucoid Pseudomonas aeruginosa, cause chronic infection. Neutrophils pour in and release elastase and DNA, which damage airway walls and thicken mucus, leading to bronchiectasis. In the sweat duct, CFTR normally reabsorbs chloride, so its loss makes sweat salty. In the pancreas, bile ducts, intestine and vas deferens, thick secretions block ducts and scar tissue.",
  body: [["Lungs and airways", "Thick mucus, chronic infection and neutrophil inflammation cause bronchiectasis, gradual loss of lung function and eventually respiratory failure, the main cause of death."], ["Pancreas", "Blocked ducts destroy the exocrine pancreas, so fat and protein are poorly digested. Later, damage to islets can cause CF-related diabetes."], ["Intestines", "Newborns may have meconium ileus, a bowel blockage from thick stool. Older patients can develop distal intestinal obstruction syndrome."], ["Liver and gallbladder", "Thick bile blocks small bile ducts, causing focal biliary cirrhosis and gallstones in some patients."], ["Sinuses", "Chronic sinusitis and nasal polyps are common because sinus mucus is also thick."], ["Reproductive system", "Most males have congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens and are infertile, while sperm production is normal. Females may have thicker cervical mucus."], ["Sweat glands", "Salt is lost in sweat, which can cause dehydration and low sodium in hot weather."]],
  symptoms: "A chronic wet cough and repeated chest infections occur because thick mucus traps bacteria in the airways. Greasy, bulky, foul-smelling stools (steatorrhea) and poor weight gain happen because the pancreas cannot deliver enzymes to digest fat. Deficiency of the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K follows. Digital clubbing reflects chronic low oxygen and lung inflammation. Salty-tasting skin comes from the sweat duct's failure to reabsorb chloride.",
  dx: "All US states screen newborns, usually by measuring immunoreactive trypsinogen in a heel-stick blood spot followed by CFTR variant testing. Diagnosis is confirmed with a sweat chloride test: a value of 60 mmol/L or higher supports CF, and 30 to 59 is intermediate and needs further testing. Genetic testing identifies the two CFTR variants, which guides modulator choice. Fecal elastase shows pancreatic insufficiency, and sputum cultures and lung function tests track lung disease.",
  tx: "CFTR modulators treat the root defect. Correctors (elexacaftor, tezacaftor, lumacaftor, vanzacaftor) help misfolded CFTR reach the cell surface, and potentiators (ivacaftor, deutivacaftor) hold the channel open. These can greatly improve lung function and nutrition. Airway clearance with vests or chest physiotherapy, plus inhaled dornase alfa (cuts neutrophil DNA in mucus) and hypertonic saline or inhaled mannitol (draws water into the airway), helps move mucus. Albuterol opens airways before clearance. Inhaled antibiotics such as tobramycin suppress chronic Pseudomonas, and long-term azithromycin reduces flare-ups. Pancreatic enzyme replacement with meals and fat-soluble vitamins treat malabsorption, and insulin treats CF-related diabetes. Care in accredited CF centers, a high-calorie diet and lung transplant for advanced disease are also key.",
  drugs: ["elexacaftor-tezacaftor-ivacaftor", "vanzacaftor-tezacaftor-deutivacaftor", "ivacaftor", "tezacaftor-ivacaftor", "lumacaftor-ivacaftor", "dornase-alfa", "hypertonic-saline-inhaled", "mannitol-inhaled", "albuterol", "tobramycin-inhaled", "tobramycin", "azithromycin", "amikacin-liposome-inhaled", "pancrelipase", "ursodiol", "insulin-glargine", "cholecalciferol", "vitamin-e", "phytonadione"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "The most common CFTR variant is F508del, a misfolding defect that leads to the protein being degraded before it reaches the cell surface."], ["Memory trick", "Sweat is salty because CFTR cannot pull chloride back in from the sweat duct, while in the airways CFTR cannot push chloride out."], ["Classic germs", "Staph aureus in childhood, then Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which becomes mucoid and hard to clear. Burkholderia cepacia is feared because it can spread between CF patients."], ["Infertility", "Males have absent vas deferens (obstructive azoospermia) but normal sperm production."]],
  red: "Coughing up a lot of blood, sudden chest pain with breathlessness (possible pneumothorax), severe abdominal pain with vomiting and no stool (possible bowel blockage), or rapidly worsening breathing in someone with CF needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Because of better care and CFTR modulators, median predicted survival for people with CF in the US is now well into adulthood, a huge change from early childhood in the 1950s.",
  src: ["Cystic Fibrosis Foundation Clinical Care Guidelines", "Cystic Fibrosis Foundation Diagnosis Consensus Guidelines (2017)", "Andersen DH. Am J Dis Child 1938", "Riordan JR, Rommens JM, Kerem B et al. Science 1989", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"pulmonary-fibrosis": {
  name: "Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["IPF", "usual interstitial pneumonia", "UIP", "cryptogenic fibrosing alveolitis", "interstitial lung disease"],
  what: "Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is a long-term disease in which scar tissue slowly builds up in the walls of the air sacs. The lungs get stiff and cannot move oxygen into the blood well. Idiopathic means the cause is unknown.",
  origin: "In 1944 Louis Hamman and Arnold Rich at Johns Hopkins described patients with rapidly fatal scarring of the lungs, and for years many fibrosing lung diseases were lumped together as Hamman-Rich syndrome (that rapid form is now called acute interstitial pneumonia). In 1969 pathologist Averill Liebow classified interstitial pneumonias by their microscopic pattern, naming usual interstitial pneumonia, the pattern seen in IPF. For decades IPF was treated with steroids and immune-suppressing drugs, but in 2012 the PANTHER-IPF trial showed that combining prednisone, azathioprine and N-acetylcysteine actually increased deaths and hospital stays. This shifted thinking from inflammation to abnormal wound healing. In 2014 the FDA approved the first two antifibrotic drugs, pirfenidone and nintedanib, which slow the loss of lung function. International ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT guidelines in 2011 and later updates standardized diagnosis using high-resolution CT.",
  timeline: [["1944", "Hamman and Rich describe rapidly progressive lung fibrosis."], ["1969", "Averill Liebow classifies interstitial pneumonias, including usual interstitial pneumonia."], ["2011", "ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT guidelines standardize IPF diagnosis with HRCT."], ["2012", "The PANTHER-IPF trial shows that steroid-based triple therapy is harmful."], ["2014", "The FDA approves pirfenidone and nintedanib."]],
  cell: "IPF is thought to start with repeated small injuries to the alveolar epithelium in a person with a genetic and age-related tendency toward faulty repair. Risk factors include older age, male sex, cigarette smoking, a common variant in the MUC5B promoter, and variants that shorten telomeres (TERT, TERC). Damaged type II alveolar cells undergo stress and senescence and fail to regenerate the lining. Instead they release profibrotic signals, especially activated TGF-beta, plus PDGF and other growth factors. These signals recruit fibroblasts and turn them into myofibroblasts, which gather in fibroblast foci and lay down excess collagen and extracellular matrix. The scarring is patchy and of different ages side by side (temporal heterogeneity), concentrated in the lower and outer (subpleural) lung. Over time alveoli are replaced by cystic spaces lined with bronchiolar cells (honeycombing), and contracting scar pulls airways open (traction bronchiectasis). Inflammation is mild, which is why immune suppression does not help.",
  body: [["Lungs", "Scar tissue thickens the walls between air sacs, blocking oxygen transfer and making the lungs small and stiff (restrictive disease). Acute exacerbations can cause sudden severe worsening."], ["Heart", "Chronic low oxygen tightens lung blood vessels, causing pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain (cor pulmonale)."], ["Fingers", "Digital clubbing is common, likely linked to chronic hypoxia and growth factors."], ["Whole body", "Low oxygen and the work of breathing cause fatigue, weight loss and reduced exercise tolerance. IPF also raises the risk of lung cancer."]],
  symptoms: "Breathlessness with exertion that slowly worsens happens because thickened alveolar walls slow oxygen diffusion, which shows most during exercise when blood moves faster through the lungs. A dry cough comes from distorted airways and changes in nerve signals. Fine Velcro-like crackles at the lung bases are heard as stiff, collapsed small airways pop open on breathing in. Clubbing and low oxygen reflect long-term disease.",
  dx: "Diagnosis needs exclusion of known causes of lung scarring, such as autoimmune disease, drug toxicity, occupational dusts and hypersensitivity pneumonitis. High-resolution CT showing a usual interstitial pneumonia pattern (subpleural, lower-lobe reticulation with honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis) can be enough for diagnosis in the right setting. When CT is not typical, lung biopsy may be used. Pulmonary function tests show restriction with reduced FVC and a low diffusing capacity (DLCO). Diagnosis is ideally made by a multidisciplinary team.",
  tx: "Antifibrotic drugs slow the decline in lung function but do not reverse scarring. Pirfenidone reduces TGF-beta signaling and fibroblast growth. Nintedanib blocks tyrosine kinase receptors for VEGF, FGF and PDGF that drive fibroblasts. Supplemental oxygen treats low oxygen with exertion or rest, and pulmonary rehabilitation improves strength and breathing efficiency. Vaccines against influenza, pneumococcus, COVID-19 and RSV lower infection risk. Steroids combined with azathioprine are avoided as standard therapy because they caused harm. Lung transplant is the only cure for eligible patients, and palliative care helps with breathlessness and cough.",
  drugs: ["pirfenidone", "nintedanib", "influenza-vaccine", "pneumococcal-21-valent-conjugate-vaccine", "rsv-vaccine", "covid-19-vaccine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "IPF has a usual interstitial pneumonia pattern: subpleural, basal honeycombing with patchy fibrosis of different ages."], ["PFT pattern", "Restrictive: low FVC, normal or high FEV1/FVC ratio, and low DLCO."], ["Classic patient", "A man over 60 with a history of smoking, slowly worsening breathlessness, dry cough, clubbing and Velcro crackles."], ["Treatment twist", "Antifibrotics, not steroids. Steroid triple therapy increased deaths in the PANTHER-IPF trial."]],
  red: "Sudden worsening of breathlessness over days, chest pain, coughing up blood or blue lips in someone with pulmonary fibrosis needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The MUC5B promoter variant is the strongest known genetic risk factor for IPF, yet people who carry it and develop IPF may live longer than those who do not.",
  src: ["ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT Clinical Practice Guideline: Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (2022 update)", "Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis Clinical Research Network. PANTHER-IPF. N Engl J Med 2012", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"sleep-apnea": {
  name: "Obstructive sleep apnea",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["OSA", "obstructive sleep apnea-hypopnea syndrome", "sleep-disordered breathing"],
  what: "Obstructive sleep apnea is a condition in which the throat repeatedly collapses during sleep, partly or fully blocking airflow. Oxygen drops and the brain briefly wakes to reopen the airway, many times a night, leaving people tired by day.",
  origin: "In his 1837 novel The Pickwick Papers, Charles Dickens described Joe, a fat boy who snored and kept falling asleep. In 1956 physician C. Sidney Burwell and colleagues used the name Pickwickian syndrome for obesity with daytime sleepiness and poor breathing. In 1965 French researcher Henri Gastaut and colleagues used overnight recordings to show that these patients had repeated obstructive apneas during sleep. Early on, the only reliable treatment was a tracheostomy that bypassed the throat. In 1981 Australian physician Colin Sullivan published in The Lancet his invention of continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP), a mask that blows air to splint the throat open, and it became the main therapy. The same year Shiro Fujita described a throat surgery called uvulopalatopharyngoplasty. Later came oral appliances, hypoglossal nerve stimulation (FDA approval in 2014) and, in 2024, tirzepatide as the first drug approved for moderate to severe OSA in adults with obesity.",
  timeline: [["1956", "Burwell and colleagues describe the Pickwickian syndrome."], ["1965", "Gastaut and colleagues record obstructive apneas during sleep."], ["1981", "Colin Sullivan reports CPAP for obstructive sleep apnea."], ["2014", "The FDA approves hypoglossal nerve stimulation for OSA."], ["2024", "Tirzepatide is approved for moderate to severe OSA in adults with obesity."]],
  cell: "OSA results from an anatomically narrow upper airway combined with loss of muscle tone during sleep. Fat around the neck and tongue, large tonsils, a small or set-back jaw and nasal blockage narrow the airway. Awake, dilator muscles such as the genioglossus keep the pharynx open, but during sleep, especially REM sleep, their activity falls and the floppy pharynx collapses under the negative pressure of breathing in. Each apnea or hypopnea causes intermittent hypoxemia and hypercapnia. Chemoreceptors and the effort of breathing trigger a brief arousal that restores muscle tone and airflow, then the cycle repeats. Repeated hypoxia and reoxygenation create oxidative stress, activate inflammatory pathways (NF-kappa B) and impair endothelial nitric oxide, promoting atherosclerosis. Each arousal causes a sympathetic surge, raising blood pressure and heart rate. Strong negative pressure in the chest during obstructed breaths stretches the atria and increases afterload. Low arousal threshold and unstable breathing control (high loop gain) also contribute.",
  body: [["Upper airway", "The pharynx collapses repeatedly during sleep, causing loud snoring, gasping and witnessed pauses in breathing."], ["Brain and sleep", "Constant arousals fragment sleep, causing daytime sleepiness, poor concentration, mood changes and a higher risk of motor vehicle crashes."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Sympathetic surges and hypoxia cause hypertension that is often resistant to treatment, and raise the risk of atrial fibrillation, heart failure, coronary disease and stroke."], ["Lungs", "Repeated hypoxia can raise pressure in the lung arteries (pulmonary hypertension). In obesity hypoventilation syndrome, carbon dioxide stays high even during the day."], ["Metabolism", "Sleep fragmentation and hypoxia worsen insulin resistance, linking OSA with type 2 diabetes and fatty liver disease."], ["Bladder", "Atrial stretch releases natriuretic peptide, which increases urine output at night (nocturia)."]],
  symptoms: "Loud snoring comes from vibration of the narrowed, floppy throat. Witnessed pauses and choking or gasping reflect full airway collapse and the arousals that end it. Daytime sleepiness and morning headaches arise because sleep is broken into fragments and carbon dioxide rises overnight. Poor memory and irritability come from lost deep and REM sleep. Nighttime urination results from atrial stretch releasing natriuretic peptide.",
  dx: "Screening tools such as STOP-BANG (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, blood pressure, BMI, age, neck size, male sex) estimate risk. Diagnosis is made by in-lab polysomnography or, in uncomplicated adults, a home sleep apnea test. The apnea-hypopnea index (AHI) counts breathing events per hour of sleep: 5 to 15 is mild, 15 to 30 is moderate, and 30 or more is severe. An AHI of 15 or more, or 5 or more with symptoms or related conditions, establishes OSA. Exam often shows a large neck and crowded throat.",
  tx: "CPAP is first-line for moderate to severe OSA. It acts as a pneumatic splint, keeping the throat open so oxygen stays steady and sleep is no longer interrupted. Weight loss shrinks fat around the airway and can greatly lower the AHI; tirzepatide, a GIP and GLP-1 receptor agonist, is FDA approved for moderate to severe OSA with obesity. Mandibular advancement oral appliances pull the jaw and tongue forward. Sleeping on the side helps when apneas occur mostly on the back. Hypoglossal nerve stimulation activates the genioglossus during sleep for selected patients who cannot use CPAP. Surgery to the tonsils, palate or jaw helps some, and tonsillectomy is first-line for most children with OSA. Avoiding alcohol and sedatives at night prevents more collapse. Solriamfetol, modafinil and armodafinil treat lingering daytime sleepiness despite therapy but do not fix the airway.",
  drugs: ["tirzepatide", "solriamfetol", "modafinil", "armodafinil"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "STOP-BANG: Snoring, Tired, Observed apnea, Pressure (high blood pressure), BMI over 35, Age over 50, Neck size large, Gender male."], ["Most-tested fact", "OSA is a common cause of secondary and resistant hypertension, and CPAP is the first-line treatment."], ["Often confused with", "Central sleep apnea, where the brain fails to send the breathing signal and there is no breathing effort, often seen with heart failure or opioids."], ["Children", "In children, enlarged tonsils and adenoids are the main cause, and symptoms can look like ADHD."]],
  red: "Falling asleep while driving, chest pain, severe shortness of breath, or leg swelling with worsening breathlessness in someone with sleep apnea needs urgent medical care, and chest pain or trouble breathing needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Colin Sullivan built his first CPAP device from a vacuum cleaner blower motor and tubing attached to a mask glued to the patient's face.",
  src: ["AASM Clinical Practice Guideline for PAP Treatment of OSA (2019)", "AASM Clinical Practice Guideline for Diagnostic Testing for Adult OSA (2017)", "Sullivan CE et al. Lancet 1981", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "AAP Clinical Practice Guideline: Childhood OSA (2012)"]
},
"croup": {
  name: "Croup",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["laryngotracheobronchitis", "viral croup", "spasmodic croup"],
  what: "Croup is a viral infection in young children that swells the voice box and windpipe. It causes a barking, seal-like cough, a hoarse voice and sometimes a high-pitched noise when breathing in, called stridor.",
  origin: "The word croup comes from an old Scottish word meaning to cry hoarsely. In 1765 Scottish physician Francis Home published An Inquiry into the Nature, Cause and Cure of the Croup, one of the first detailed accounts. For more than a century, croup was a loose label that included deadly diphtheria (membranous croup) as well as milder viral illness. Vaccination against diphtheria in the 20th century separated the two. In the late 1950s researchers isolated parainfluenza viruses from children with croup and showed they were the main cause. Nebulized racemic epinephrine came into use in the 1970s to shrink airway swelling quickly. In 1978 the Westley croup score was published to grade severity. Trials in the 1980s and 1990s showed that a single dose of a corticosteroid, especially dexamethasone, reduces symptoms, return visits and hospital stays, and it is now given for croup of every severity.",
  timeline: [["1765", "Francis Home publishes an early detailed account of croup."], ["1950s", "Parainfluenza viruses are isolated and linked to croup."], ["1970s", "Nebulized racemic epinephrine becomes a standard treatment for moderate to severe croup."], ["1978", "The Westley croup score is published."], ["1990s", "Trials establish dexamethasone as a standard treatment for croup of all severities."]],
  cell: "Most croup is caused by parainfluenza virus, especially type 1, with RSV, influenza, adenovirus, human metapneumovirus and SARS-CoV-2 as other causes. The virus enters through the nose and throat and infects the ciliated epithelium of the larynx and trachea. Parainfluenza's hemagglutinin-neuraminidase protein binds sialic acid on airway cells, and its fusion protein lets it enter. Infected cells release cytokines that draw in lymphocytes, neutrophils and other immune cells, and capillaries leak, so the mucosa and submucosa swell. The key site is the subglottis, the part of the airway surrounded by the cricoid cartilage, which is a complete, rigid ring that cannot expand outward, so swelling narrows the opening inward. In a young child this area is already the narrowest point of the airway. By Poiseuille's law, resistance rises with the fourth power of a smaller radius, so a small amount of swelling greatly increases resistance. Turbulent airflow through this narrowing makes stridor, and inflamed vocal cords make the barking cough and hoarse voice.",
  body: [["Larynx and subglottis", "Swelling below the vocal cords narrows the airway, causing stridor and increasing the work of breathing."], ["Vocal cords", "Inflammation causes hoarseness and the classic barking cough."], ["Trachea and bronchi", "Inflammation can extend down the airways, adding cough and, in some children, wheeze."], ["Lungs and blood oxygen", "Most children keep normal oxygen levels. In severe obstruction, tiring breathing muscles lead to low oxygen and high carbon dioxide."]],
  symptoms: "Croup usually begins with a runny nose and mild fever for a day or two, then a sudden barking cough, often at night. The bark and hoarse voice come from swollen vocal cords and subglottis. Stridor, a harsh noise on breathing in, comes from air rushing through the narrowed subglottis, which collapses further inward during inspiration. Chest wall retractions show the effort of pulling air through a narrow tube. Symptoms often worsen when a child cries or is upset, because faster airflow increases turbulence. It usually affects children from about six months to three years old and lasts a few days.",
  dx: "Croup is a clinical diagnosis based on the typical barking cough, stridor and hoarseness after a cold in a young child. Severity is graded with tools such as the Westley score, which looks at level of consciousness, cyanosis, stridor, air entry and retractions. Imaging is not needed in typical cases. When done, a front-view neck X-ray may show the steeple sign, a narrowing of the subglottic airway. Lack of improvement, drooling, high fever or toxic appearance raise concern for bacterial tracheitis, epiglottitis or a foreign body.",
  tx: "Corticosteroids are used for all severities. Dexamethasone, given by mouth or injection, binds glucocorticoid receptors and reduces airway inflammation and swelling over several hours, lowering return visits and hospital admissions. Nebulized budesonide or oral prednisolone are alternatives. For moderate to severe croup with stridor at rest, nebulized racemic or L-epinephrine stimulates alpha-1 receptors to constrict swollen mucosal blood vessels, quickly shrinking subglottic edema. Its effect fades within about two hours, so children are observed for a few hours for rebound. Keeping the child calm, in a parent's arms, reduces turbulent airflow. Oxygen is given for low oxygen, and heliox may help in severe cases. Mist or humidified air has not been shown to help. Fever reducers ease discomfort. Rarely, intubation with a smaller tube is needed.",
  drugs: ["dexamethasone", "dexamethasone-oral", "racemic-epinephrine", "epinephrine", "budesonide", "prednisolone", "acetaminophen", "ibuprofen"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Croup: barking Cough, Ring (cricoid) narrows, Under three years, Parainfluenza. Steeple sign on X-ray."], ["Most-tested fact", "Parainfluenza virus is the most common cause, and the X-ray finding is the steeple sign."], ["Often confused with", "Epiglottitis: sudden high fever, drooling, tripod position, no bark, and a thumbprint sign on lateral X-ray. It is now rare because of Hib vaccination."], ["Treatment pearl", "After racemic epinephrine, observe for a few hours because the swelling can return when the drug wears off."]],
  red: "Stridor at rest, severe chest retractions, drooling or trouble swallowing, blue or gray lips, unusual sleepiness, or a child who is too tired to cry needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Croup symptoms often seem to improve on the drive to the hospital in cool night air, but studies have not shown that mist or cool air actually treats the swelling.",
  src: ["Bjornson CL, Johnson DW. Croup in children. CMAJ 2013", "Cochrane Review: Glucocorticoids for croup in children", "Westley CR et al. Am J Dis Child 1978", "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics", "Home F. An Inquiry into the Nature, Cause and Cure of the Croup (1765)"]
},
"bronchiolitis": {
  name: "Bronchiolitis (RSV)",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["RSV bronchiolitis", "respiratory syncytial virus infection", "viral bronchiolitis"],
  what: "Bronchiolitis is a common viral infection of the smallest airways in babies and toddlers, most often caused by RSV. The tiny airways swell and fill with mucus, causing wheezing, fast breathing and trouble feeding.",
  origin: "Doctors long recognized winter outbreaks of wheezing illness in infants. In 1956 researchers at the Walter Reed Army Institute isolated a new virus from chimpanzees with colds, and in 1957 Robert Chanock and colleagues recovered the same virus from infants with lower respiratory illness. They named it respiratory syncytial virus because infected cells fuse into large multinucleated cells called syncytia. In the 1960s a trial of a formalin-inactivated RSV vaccine had a tragic result: vaccinated children who later caught RSV became sicker, and two died, a phenomenon called enhanced respiratory disease. This set back vaccine work for decades. In 1998 palivizumab, a monthly antibody shot for high-risk infants, was approved. In 2023 the long-acting antibody nirsevimab and the first RSV vaccines, for older adults and for pregnant people to protect newborns, were approved, followed by the antibody clesrovimab in 2025.",
  timeline: [["1956", "RSV is first isolated from chimpanzees at Walter Reed."], ["1957", "Robert Chanock and colleagues isolate RSV from infants and name the virus."], ["1960s", "A formalin-inactivated RSV vaccine causes enhanced disease, halting vaccine development for years."], ["1998", "Palivizumab is approved to prevent severe RSV in high-risk infants."], ["2023", "Nirsevimab and the first RSV vaccines, including a maternal vaccine, are approved."], ["2025", "Clesrovimab is approved as another long-acting RSV antibody for infants."]],
  cell: "RSV is an enveloped, negative-sense RNA virus. Its G protein helps it attach to airway cells, and its fusion (F) protein, in its prefusion shape, merges the viral and cell membranes so the virus can enter. F protein on infected cells also fuses neighboring cells into syncytia. RSV infects ciliated epithelial cells of the nose and then the small bronchioles. Infected cells die and slough into the airway, cilia stop working, and the body responds with neutrophils, lymphocytes and edema of the airway wall. Sloughed cells, mucus and fibrin form plugs. Because an infant's bronchioles are tiny, even a thin layer of swelling and mucus greatly increases resistance. Partial plugs act as one-way valves that trap air (hyperinflation), and complete plugs cause small areas of collapse (atelectasis). The resulting ventilation-perfusion mismatch lowers oxygen. Young or premature infants can also have apnea, likely from immature breathing control. Immunity after infection is incomplete, so reinfection is common throughout life.",
  body: [["Small airways (bronchioles)", "Inflammation, dead cells and mucus narrow and plug the bronchioles, causing wheeze, air trapping and patchy collapse."], ["Lungs and oxygen", "Uneven ventilation lowers oxygen levels, and tiring breathing muscles can lead to respiratory failure in severe cases."], ["Nose and upper airway", "Thick nasal secretions block the nose, which matters because young infants breathe mainly through the nose and need it clear to feed."], ["Brain and breathing control", "Very young or premature infants may have apnea, pauses in breathing, sometimes before wheezing appears."], ["Feeding and hydration", "Fast breathing and a blocked nose make feeding hard, leading to dehydration."]],
  symptoms: "Illness starts with a runny nose and mild fever, then over two to three days spreads to the lower airways, causing cough, wheezing and crackles. Wheezing comes from air moving through narrowed bronchioles. Fast breathing, nasal flaring, grunting and retractions show the extra work of moving air through plugged airways. Poor feeding happens because babies cannot breathe and suck at the same time with a blocked nose. Symptoms often peak around days three to five. A wheezy cough can last two to four weeks while the airway lining regrows.",
  dx: "Bronchiolitis is diagnosed clinically in a child under two years with a viral cold followed by cough, wheeze or crackles and increased breathing effort. The AAP guideline advises against routine chest X-rays, blood tests and viral testing in typical cases, since results rarely change care. Pulse oximetry and assessment of hydration and breathing effort guide decisions about hospital care. Rapid RSV tests or viral panels may be used for infection control or in special situations. Risk factors for severe disease include prematurity, age under three months, chronic lung disease, heart disease and immune problems.",
  tx: "Treatment is mainly supportive because no drug shortens typical bronchiolitis. Nasal suctioning clears the nose for breathing and feeding. Fluids by mouth, feeding tube or IV prevent dehydration. Oxygen is given when oxygen levels are low, and heated high-flow nasal cannula or CPAP supports breathing in more severe cases. The AAP advises against routine albuterol, epinephrine, corticosteroids and antibiotics, since studies show little benefit. Nebulized hypertonic saline is sometimes used for hospitalized infants to draw water into thick mucus. Ribavirin, an antiviral, is reserved for rare severe cases in immunocompromised patients. Prevention is the biggest advance: long-acting antibodies (nirsevimab and clesrovimab) and palivizumab bind the RSV F protein and block entry, and a maternal RSV vaccine in pregnancy passes protective antibodies across the placenta.",
  drugs: ["nirsevimab", "rsv-monoclonal-clesrovimab", "palivizumab", "rsv-vaccine", "hypertonic-saline-inhaled", "ribavirin", "acetaminophen", "saline-nasal-spray"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "RSV is the most common cause of bronchiolitis and the leading cause of infant hospitalization in the US."], ["Memory trick", "Bronchiolitis care is SOS: Suction, Oxygen if needed, Support hydration. Bronchodilators and steroids are not routine."], ["Prevention", "Nirsevimab and palivizumab are antibodies (passive immunity), not vaccines. They target the RSV F protein."], ["Often confused with", "Asthma: bronchiolitis is a first wheezing episode with a viral cold in a child under two, and it usually does not respond to albuterol."]],
  red: "Pauses in breathing, blue or gray lips or skin, severe retractions, grunting, very fast breathing, inability to feed, or signs of dehydration (few wet diapers) in an infant need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "RSV got its name from the way infected cells fuse together into giant cells called syncytia, which can be seen under the microscope.",
  src: ["AAP Clinical Practice Guideline: Bronchiolitis (2014, reaffirmed)", "CDC RSV Immunization Recommendations (nirsevimab and maternal vaccine, 2023)", "Chanock R, Roizman B, Myers R. Am J Hyg 1957", "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics", "Hammitt LL et al. Nirsevimab. N Engl J Med 2022"]
},
"influenza": {
  name: "Influenza (flu)",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["flu", "seasonal influenza", "influenza A", "influenza B", "pandemic influenza"],
  what: "Influenza is a contagious viral infection of the nose, throat and lungs. It comes on suddenly with fever, body aches, cough and exhaustion, and it can lead to pneumonia and hospital stays, especially in older adults, young children and pregnant people.",
  origin: "Epidemics of sudden fever and cough have been described since ancient times. The name comes from Italian, where outbreaks were blamed on the influence of the stars. The 1918 pandemic infected about a third of the world's people and killed tens of millions. In 1933 British scientists Wilson Smith, Christopher Andrewes and Patrick Laidlaw infected ferrets with throat washings from sick people and isolated the human influenza virus for the first time. Inactivated vaccines were developed in the 1930s and 1940s, used in the US military in World War II and licensed for civilians in 1945. Later pandemics came in 1957, 1968 and 2009, each caused by a new mix of virus genes. Amantadine was the first flu antiviral in the 1960s, but resistance is now widespread. The neuraminidase inhibitors zanamivir and oseltamivir were approved in 1999, and baloxavir, which blocks a different viral enzyme, in 2018.",
  timeline: [["1918", "A pandemic of H1N1 influenza kills tens of millions worldwide."], ["1933", "Smith, Andrewes and Laidlaw isolate human influenza virus in ferrets."], ["1945", "Inactivated influenza vaccine is licensed for civilian use in the US."], ["1999", "Zanamivir and oseltamivir, the first neuraminidase inhibitors, are approved."], ["2009", "A new H1N1 strain causes the first pandemic of the 21st century."], ["2018", "Baloxavir is approved as a single-dose flu antiviral."]],
  cell: "Influenza A and B are enveloped viruses with eight segments of negative-sense RNA. The surface protein hemagglutinin (HA) binds sialic acid on respiratory epithelial cells; human strains prefer sialic acid linked in the alpha-2,6 position found in the upper airway, while bird strains prefer alpha-2,3 linkages deeper in the lung. The virus is taken in by endocytosis. The M2 ion channel (the target of amantadine) lets acid into the virus so its genome is released, and HA fuses the viral and endosome membranes. The viral polymerase works in the nucleus and steals 5' caps from host messenger RNA (cap snatching, blocked by baloxavir) to make viral proteins. New viruses bud from the cell, and neuraminidase cuts sialic acid so they can be released and spread (blocked by oseltamivir and zanamivir). Infected ciliated cells die, exposing the airway to bacteria. Interferons, IL-6 and TNF-alpha cause fever and aches. Small mutations in HA and NA (antigenic drift) cause yearly epidemics, while swapping whole gene segments between strains (antigenic shift) can cause pandemics.",
  body: [["Upper airway", "Virus destroys ciliated cells in the nose, throat and trachea, causing sore throat, cough and lost mucus clearance."], ["Lungs", "Primary viral pneumonia or a secondary bacterial pneumonia (often Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pneumoniae) can follow, because damaged airways let bacteria take hold."], ["Muscles", "Cytokines cause severe body aches, and in some children influenza causes myositis with calf pain or, rarely, rhabdomyolysis."], ["Heart", "Inflammation raises the risk of heart attack in the weeks after flu, and myocarditis can occur."], ["Brain", "Rarely influenza causes encephalitis. Aspirin use in children with flu is linked to Reye syndrome, with liver failure and brain swelling."], ["Chronic conditions", "Flu often worsens asthma, COPD, heart failure and diabetes."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms start suddenly, one to four days after exposure. High fever, chills, headache and intense muscle aches come from interferons and cytokines acting on the brain and muscles. Dry cough and sore throat come from damaged airway lining. Profound tiredness can linger for weeks. Older adults may have less fever and more confusion or weakness. Worsening after early improvement, with new fever and productive cough, suggests a secondary bacterial pneumonia.",
  dx: "During flu season, diagnosis is often clinical. Molecular tests such as RT-PCR on a nasal or throat swab are the most accurate and can distinguish influenza A and B and test for COVID-19 and RSV at the same time. Rapid antigen tests are quick but miss many cases, so a negative result does not rule out flu. Chest X-ray is used if pneumonia is suspected. Hospitalized patients are tested with molecular assays.",
  tx: "Antivirals work best when started within 48 hours of symptoms, but CDC recommends them for anyone hospitalized or at high risk regardless of timing. Neuraminidase inhibitors (oral oseltamivir, inhaled zanamivir and IV peramivir) stop new virus particles from leaving infected cells. Baloxavir blocks the cap-dependent endonuclease of the viral polymerase so the virus cannot make its proteins. Amantadine and rimantadine are no longer recommended because of resistance. Acetaminophen or ibuprofen ease fever and aches, and aspirin is avoided in children and teens because of Reye syndrome. Rest, fluids and staying home lower spread. Annual vaccination is recommended for everyone six months and older, with high-dose or adjuvanted vaccines preferred for adults 65 and older. Antivirals are also used to prevent flu after exposure in some high-risk people.",
  drugs: ["oseltamivir", "baloxavir", "zanamivir", "peramivir", "influenza-vaccine", "influenza-vaccine-high-dose", "acetaminophen", "ibuprofen"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Hemagglutinin helps it Hop on (attach); Neuraminidase lets it Nip off (release). Oseltamivir and zanamivir block the N."], ["Drift vs shift", "Drift is small mutations causing seasonal epidemics. Shift is gene segment swapping between strains causing pandemics, and it happens only in influenza A."], ["Most-tested fact", "Aspirin in a child with a viral illness such as flu or chickenpox is linked to Reye syndrome."], ["Common complication", "Bacterial pneumonia after flu, classically Staphylococcus aureus, is a major cause of flu deaths."]],
  red: "Trouble breathing, chest pain, confusion, seizures, inability to stay awake, blue lips, severe weakness, or fever and cough that improve then return worse need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Ferrets are still used as the main animal model for human influenza because they catch it from humans and show similar symptoms, including sneezing.",
  src: ["CDC Influenza Antiviral Medications: Summary for Clinicians", "CDC/ACIP Prevention and Control of Seasonal Influenza with Vaccines", "IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines on Seasonal Influenza (2018)", "Smith W, Andrewes CH, Laidlaw PP. Lancet 1933", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"covid-19": {
  name: "COVID-19",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["coronavirus disease 2019", "SARS-CoV-2 infection", "coronavirus", "long COVID (post-COVID condition)"],
  what: "COVID-19 is an infection caused by the coronavirus SARS-CoV-2. Most people get a cold or flu-like illness, but some develop pneumonia, low oxygen and blood clots, and some have symptoms that last for months.",
  origin: "In December 2019 a cluster of pneumonia cases was reported in Wuhan, China. In January 2020 Chinese scientists sequenced the new coronavirus and shared its genome, which let tests and vaccines be designed quickly. The World Health Organization declared a pandemic on March 11, 2020. In June 2020 the UK RECOVERY trial showed that dexamethasone lowered deaths in patients who needed oxygen, the first drug proven to save lives. The FDA authorized the first mRNA vaccines in December 2020, built on decades of mRNA research by scientists including Katalin Kariko and Drew Weissman, who won the 2023 Nobel Prize. Remdesivir was approved in October 2020, and the oral antiviral nirmatrelvir-ritonavir was authorized in December 2021 and fully approved in 2023. New variants such as Delta and Omicron changed how the virus spread and caused illness. WHO ended the global health emergency in May 2023, and COVID-19 continues to circulate.",
  timeline: [["2019", "Pneumonia cases caused by a new coronavirus are reported in Wuhan, China."], ["2020", "WHO declares a pandemic, RECOVERY shows dexamethasone saves lives, and mRNA vaccines are authorized."], ["2021", "Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir is authorized as an oral antiviral, and the Omicron variant emerges."], ["2023", "WHO ends the COVID-19 global health emergency, and mRNA vaccine pioneers receive the Nobel Prize."]],
  cell: "SARS-CoV-2 is an enveloped, positive-sense RNA betacoronavirus. Its spike protein binds the ACE2 receptor on nasal epithelial cells, type II pneumocytes, endothelial cells and other tissues. The host protease TMPRSS2 (or endosomal cathepsins) cleaves spike so the viral and cell membranes can fuse. The RNA genome is translated into polyproteins that the main protease (Mpro, the target of nirmatrelvir) cuts into working parts, including the RNA-dependent RNA polymerase (the target of remdesivir). The virus delays the early interferon response, letting it replicate before the immune system reacts. In severe disease a late, overactive immune response drives IL-6 and other cytokines, inflammatory monocytes and neutrophils into the lungs, causing diffuse alveolar damage like ARDS. Injury to endothelial cells, complement activation and neutrophil extracellular traps promote blood clots in small and large vessels (immunothrombosis). Loss of smell happens when the virus infects support cells in the olfactory epithelium. Lasting symptoms may involve persistent viral pieces, immune changes, autoimmunity and vascular injury.",
  body: [["Lungs", "Viral pneumonia with patchy ground-glass opacities can progress to ARDS, with low oxygen and need for a ventilator."], ["Blood and vessels", "COVID-19 raises the risk of clots, including pulmonary embolism, deep vein thrombosis, stroke and clots in small vessels."], ["Heart", "Myocarditis, arrhythmias and heart attacks can occur, related to inflammation, low oxygen and clotting."], ["Nose and senses", "Infection of olfactory support cells causes sudden loss of smell and taste, which usually recovers."], ["Kidneys", "Acute kidney injury is common in severe illness from low blood pressure, inflammation and direct effects."], ["Brain and nerves", "Headache, confusion, delirium and stroke can occur. Long COVID can include brain fog and fatigue."], ["Children", "A rare multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C) can appear weeks after infection."]],
  symptoms: "Fever, cough, sore throat, fatigue and body aches result from the immune response to infected airway cells. Shortness of breath and low oxygen mean the infection has reached the alveoli. Some people have silent hypoxemia, low oxygen with little sense of breathlessness. Loss of smell reflects olfactory epithelium damage. Diarrhea can occur because ACE2 is found in the gut. Symptoms lasting more than three months after infection are called long COVID, with fatigue, brain fog and worsening after exertion common.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses nucleic acid amplification tests such as RT-PCR, which are very sensitive, or rapid antigen tests, which are less sensitive, so repeat testing is used after a negative result in someone with symptoms. Chest imaging in pneumonia often shows bilateral, peripheral ground-glass opacities. Labs in severe disease may show low lymphocytes and high CRP, D-dimer and ferritin. Pulse oximetry tracks oxygen and guides treatment decisions.",
  tx: "Outpatients at higher risk of severe illness can be treated early with nirmatrelvir-ritonavir: nirmatrelvir blocks the viral main protease, and ritonavir slows its breakdown by inhibiting CYP3A4, which causes many drug interactions. A short course of IV remdesivir, which blocks the viral RNA polymerase, is an alternative. Hospitalized patients needing oxygen typically receive dexamethasone to calm the harmful inflammatory response, often with remdesivir. Those who keep worsening may get baricitinib (a JAK inhibitor) or tocilizumab (an IL-6 receptor blocker). Preventive doses of heparin or enoxaparin lower clot risk in hospitalized patients. Oxygen, high-flow nasal oxygen, prone positioning and lung-protective ventilation support the lungs. Vaccines train the immune system to recognize spike protein and lower the risk of severe illness, hospitalization and death.",
  drugs: ["nirmatrelvir-ritonavir", "remdesivir", "dexamethasone", "baricitinib", "tocilizumab", "heparin", "enoxaparin", "covid-19-vaccine", "acetaminophen"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "SARS-CoV-2 enters cells through the ACE2 receptor, with spike activated by the protease TMPRSS2."], ["Drug pearl", "Ritonavir in nirmatrelvir-ritonavir is a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor, so drug interactions must always be checked."], ["Treatment timing", "Antivirals help most early, when the virus is replicating. Steroids and immune modulators help later, when inflammation drives the damage."], ["Often confused with", "Influenza and RSV, which look similar. Combined molecular tests tell them apart."]],
  red: "Trouble breathing, chest pain or pressure, new confusion, inability to stay awake, or pale, gray or blue lips, skin or nail beds need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The genome sequence of SARS-CoV-2 was shared publicly in January 2020, and the first mRNA vaccine was designed within days of its release.",
  src: ["NIH COVID-19 Treatment Guidelines (archived 2024)", "IDSA Guidelines on the Treatment and Management of COVID-19", "RECOVERY Collaborative Group. Dexamethasone. N Engl J Med 2021", "CDC COVID-19 Clinical Care", "Hammond J et al. EPIC-HR. N Engl J Med 2022"]
},
"common-cold": {
  name: "Common cold",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["upper respiratory infection", "URI", "viral rhinitis", "acute nasopharyngitis", "head cold"],
  what: "The common cold is a mild viral infection of the nose and throat. It causes a runny or stuffy nose, sneezing, sore throat and cough, and it usually gets better on its own in about a week to ten days.",
  origin: "People have described colds since ancient times, and the name comes from the old belief that cold weather or a chill caused them. In 1946 the British Medical Research Council opened the Common Cold Unit near Salisbury, England, where volunteers were deliberately infected to study colds, and it ran until 1989. In the 1950s researchers, including Winston Price in the US and teams at the Common Cold Unit, grew the first rhinoviruses, now known to cause most colds. In 1965 David Tyrrell and Malcolm Bynoe at the unit grew a new cold virus in organ cultures of human airway tissue, and in 1967 June Almeida's electron microscope images showed its crown-like shape, giving coronaviruses their name. Over time scientists showed that more than 200 different viruses can cause colds, which is one reason there is no single vaccine or cure. Research has focused on symptom relief and on how colds spread by hands, surfaces and the air.",
  timeline: [["1946", "The Common Cold Unit opens in England to study colds in volunteers."], ["1950s", "Rhinoviruses are first isolated and grown in the laboratory."], ["1965", "Tyrrell and Bynoe grow a human cold coronavirus in airway tissue cultures."], ["1967", "June Almeida images coronaviruses and helps name them for their crown-like look."], ["1989", "The Common Cold Unit closes after more than four decades of research."]],
  cell: "Rhinoviruses cause most colds, with seasonal coronaviruses, adenoviruses, parainfluenza viruses, RSV and others causing the rest. Rhinoviruses are small, non-enveloped positive-sense RNA viruses. Most bind ICAM-1 (the major group), while others use the LDL receptor family or CDHR3. They grow best at the cooler temperature of the nose rather than the warmer lungs. Infection starts in the back of the nose and adenoid area after virus is carried there by hands or inhaled droplets. Unlike influenza, rhinovirus damages few epithelial cells. Instead, infected cells release IL-8, IL-6 and IL-1 and trigger kinins such as bradykinin, which dilate nasal blood vessels, make them leaky and stimulate nerves. Parasympathetic reflexes increase gland secretion. Neutrophils move into the mucus and their enzymes make it thicker and yellow or green, which does not mean bacterial infection. Short-lived, type-specific immunity and the huge number of virus types explain why adults catch two to three colds a year and children even more.",
  body: [["Nose", "Swollen blood vessels and gland secretions cause congestion, runny nose and sneezing. Mucus can turn thick and colored as neutrophils arrive."], ["Throat", "Postnasal drip and inflammation cause a scratchy or sore throat, often the first symptom."], ["Sinuses and ears", "Swelling can block sinus and eustachian tube openings, setting up bacterial sinusitis or middle ear infections, especially in children."], ["Lower airways", "Colds are a leading trigger of asthma and COPD flare-ups, because the virus increases airway inflammation and twitchiness."]],
  symptoms: "A sore or scratchy throat often comes first, followed by sneezing, runny nose and congestion. Congestion comes from swollen venous sinusoids in the nasal lining, and the runny nose from leaky vessels and stimulated glands. Cough is driven by postnasal drip and irritated airway nerves and may last a few weeks. Fever is usually mild or absent in adults but more common in young children. Symptoms peak in two to three days.",
  dx: "Colds are diagnosed by their typical symptoms, and testing is rarely needed. The main task is telling a cold from other conditions: influenza (sudden high fever and body aches), strep throat (fever and sore throat without cough), allergic rhinitis (itchy eyes, no fever, seasonal pattern) and bacterial sinusitis (symptoms lasting more than ten days without improvement, severe facial pain with high fever, or worsening after initial improvement). Respiratory viral panels may be used in hospitals.",
  tx: "There is no cure, and antibiotics do not help because colds are viral. Care focuses on symptoms. Decongestants such as pseudoephedrine and oxymetazoline stimulate alpha-adrenergic receptors to shrink swollen nasal vessels; nasal sprays are limited to a few days to avoid rebound congestion. FDA advisers concluded in 2023 that oral phenylephrine does not work as a decongestant. First-generation antihistamines such as chlorpheniramine, combined with a decongestant, can dry a runny nose through anticholinergic effects. Ipratropium nasal spray, an anticholinergic, reduces runny nose. Acetaminophen and ibuprofen ease sore throat, headache and aches. Saline sprays rinse the nose. Honey can ease cough in children over one year old and is avoided in infants because of infant botulism. Over-the-counter cough and cold products are not recommended for young children. Handwashing reduces spread.",
  drugs: ["acetaminophen", "ibuprofen", "pseudoephedrine", "oxymetazoline", "ipratropium-nasal", "chlorpheniramine", "brompheniramine", "diphenhydramine", "dextromethorphan", "guaifenesin", "benzonatate", "saline-nasal-spray"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Rhinovirus is the most common cause of the common cold, and most rhinoviruses bind ICAM-1."], ["Myth buster", "Green or yellow mucus comes from neutrophil enzymes, not proof of a bacterial infection."], ["Drug pearl", "Topical nasal decongestants used for more than a few days cause rebound congestion (rhinitis medicamentosa)."], ["Infant safety", "No honey under 12 months because of infant botulism."]],
  red: "Trouble breathing, wheezing, blue lips, a high fever that lasts, severe headache with a stiff neck, chest pain, or a baby under three months with fever needs prompt or emergency care (call 911 for trouble breathing).",
  fun: "Rhinoviruses grow best at about the cool temperature inside the nose, which helps explain why they usually stay in the upper airway.",
  src: ["CDC Common Cold Clinical Overview", "Heikkinen T, Jarvinen A. The common cold. Lancet 2003", "AAP Guidance on Cough and Cold Medicines in Children", "FDA Nonprescription Drugs Advisory Committee on Oral Phenylephrine (2023)", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"pertussis": {
  name: "Pertussis (whooping cough)",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["whooping cough", "Bordetella pertussis infection", "100-day cough"],
  what: "Pertussis is a highly contagious bacterial infection of the airways that causes long fits of violent coughing. In babies it can cause breathing pauses and death. Vaccines for children, teens, adults and pregnant people protect against it.",
  origin: "French physician Guillaume de Baillou described a Paris epidemic of whooping cough in 1578, one of the first clear accounts, published decades later. The Chinese name means the 100-day cough, after how long it lasts. In 1906 Belgian scientists Jules Bordet and Octave Gengou grew the bacterium, later named Bordetella pertussis, on a special blood-potato agar. In the 1930s Pearl Kendrick and Grace Eldering, working at the Michigan health department, developed and tested an effective whole-cell vaccine in a large field trial. By the late 1940s it was combined with diphtheria and tetanus toxoids as DTP and given to US infants, and cases fell dramatically. Concerns about side effects of the whole-cell vaccine led to acellular vaccines containing purified bacterial proteins, used for all US childhood doses by 1997. Tdap boosters for teens and adults followed in 2005, and in 2012 US advisers recommended Tdap during every pregnancy to protect newborns.",
  timeline: [["1578", "Guillaume de Baillou describes an epidemic of whooping cough in Paris."], ["1906", "Bordet and Gengou isolate Bordetella pertussis."], ["1930s", "Pearl Kendrick and Grace Eldering develop and test an effective whole-cell vaccine."], ["1940s", "Whole-cell pertussis vaccine is combined with diphtheria and tetanus toxoids (DTP)."], ["1990s", "Acellular pertussis vaccines (DTaP) replace whole-cell vaccines for US children."], ["2012", "ACIP recommends Tdap during every pregnancy."]],
  cell: "Bordetella pertussis is a small, aerobic Gram-negative coccobacillus spread by respiratory droplets. It attaches only to ciliated airway cells using adhesins such as filamentous hemagglutinin, pertactin and fimbriae. Pertussis toxin is an AB toxin that ADP-ribosylates the alpha subunit of the inhibitory G protein (Gi), locking it off. Without Gi braking adenylyl cyclase, cyclic AMP rises in many cells. This impairs immune cell function, keeps lymphocytes from leaving the blood (causing marked lymphocytosis), increases insulin release (hypoglycemia in infants) and raises sensitivity to histamine. Adenylate cyclase toxin enters phagocytes and makes cAMP directly, crippling their killing ability. Tracheal cytotoxin, a fragment of bacterial cell wall, kills ciliated cells and stops cilia from beating, so mucus builds up and triggers severe cough. Because the toxins have already damaged the airway and nerves, the cough lasts weeks even after bacteria are cleared.",
  body: [["Airways", "Ciliated cells are destroyed and mucus builds up, causing paroxysmal coughing that can last two to three months."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonia, from pertussis or from secondary bacteria, is the most common cause of pertussis deaths. In infants, very high white blood cell counts can clog lung vessels and cause pulmonary hypertension."], ["Brain", "Infants can have apnea, seizures and brain injury from lack of oxygen during coughing fits."], ["Blood", "Pertussis toxin causes a striking lymphocytosis, and very high counts predict severe disease in infants."], ["Chest, eyes and abdomen", "Forceful coughing can cause rib fractures, hernias, broken blood vessels in the eyes, nosebleeds and urinary leakage."]],
  symptoms: "Illness runs in three stages. The catarrhal stage, lasting one to two weeks, looks like a cold with runny nose and mild cough, and it is the most contagious time. The paroxysmal stage follows with bursts of many rapid coughs in a row, because damaged cilia cannot clear mucus. After a fit, a sudden deep breath through a narrowed throat makes the whoop, and vomiting after coughing is common. People often look well between fits. The convalescent stage brings slow recovery over weeks to months. Infants may not whoop and may instead have apnea, turn blue or gag.",
  dx: "PCR testing of a nasopharyngeal swab is the main test and works best in the first three weeks of cough. Culture on special media (Bordet-Gengou or Regan-Lowe agar) is very specific but slower and less sensitive. Serologic antibody testing helps later in illness. A high white blood cell count with lymphocytosis supports the diagnosis, especially in infants. CDC case definitions use a cough of at least two weeks with paroxysms, whoop, post-tussive vomiting or apnea.",
  tx: "Macrolide antibiotics are first-line: azithromycin, clarithromycin or erythromycin block the bacterial 50S ribosome. Azithromycin is preferred for infants under one month because erythromycin is linked to infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is an alternative for people who cannot take macrolides, but not for infants under two months. Antibiotics clear the bacteria and stop spread, and they shorten illness mainly when started early; once paroxysms begin, the cough continues because toxin damage has already occurred. Close contacts receive post-exposure antibiotics. Infants often need hospital care for oxygen, suctioning, feeding support and monitoring for apnea. Prevention relies on DTaP for children, Tdap boosters for teens and adults, and Tdap in each pregnancy so the mother's antibodies protect the newborn.",
  drugs: ["azithromycin", "clarithromycin", "erythromycin", "trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole", "dtap-vaccine", "tdap-vaccine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Pertussis toxin turns off Gi, so cAMP goes up. Think: the inhibitor is inhibited."], ["Most-tested fact", "Pertussis causes marked lymphocytosis, unusual for a bacterial infection."], ["Stages", "Catarrhal (cold-like, most contagious), Paroxysmal (whoop and vomiting), Convalescent (slow recovery)."], ["Infant pearl", "Azithromycin is preferred in young infants because erythromycin is linked to pyloric stenosis."]],
  red: "A baby who stops breathing, turns blue or gray, has trouble feeding, or has a seizure, or anyone with severe trouble breathing after coughing fits, needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Pearl Kendrick and Grace Eldering ran their vaccine trial during the Great Depression with very little funding, visiting children's homes to track who caught whooping cough.",
  src: ["CDC Pertussis Clinical Overview and Treatment Guidance", "CDC/ACIP Recommendations for Tdap in Pregnancy (2012)", "Red Book: Report of the Committee on Infectious Diseases (AAP)", "Bordet J, Gengou O. Ann Inst Pasteur 1906", "Murray Medical Microbiology"]
},
"lung-cancer": {
  name: "Lung cancer",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["bronchogenic carcinoma", "non-small cell lung cancer", "NSCLC", "small cell lung cancer", "SCLC", "lung adenocarcinoma", "squamous cell lung cancer"],
  what: "Lung cancer is the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in the lungs. It is the leading cause of cancer death in the US. Most cases are linked to cigarette smoking, but it also affects people who never smoked.",
  origin: "Lung cancer was considered rare before the 20th century, and in 1912 physician Isaac Adler published a book noting how uncommon it was. As cigarette smoking became widespread, cases rose sharply. In 1933 surgeon Evarts Graham performed the first successful one-stage removal of a whole lung for cancer. In 1950 two landmark studies, by Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill in Britain and by Ernst Wynder and Graham in the US, linked smoking to lung cancer. The 1964 US Surgeon General's report confirmed that smoking causes lung cancer, leading to warning labels and public health campaigns. Treatment relied on surgery, radiation and platinum chemotherapy until 2004, when researchers found EGFR mutations that predict response to targeted drugs, starting the era of precision medicine. In 2011 the National Lung Screening Trial showed that low-dose CT screening lowers lung cancer deaths. Immune checkpoint inhibitors, approved starting in 2015, have helped some people live for years with advanced disease.",
  timeline: [["1933", "Evarts Graham performs the first successful one-stage pneumonectomy for lung cancer."], ["1950", "Doll and Hill, and Wynder and Graham, publish studies linking smoking to lung cancer."], ["1964", "The US Surgeon General's report concludes that smoking causes lung cancer."], ["2004", "EGFR mutations are found to predict response to EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors."], ["2011", "The National Lung Screening Trial shows low-dose CT screening lowers lung cancer deaths."], ["2015", "The first PD-1 checkpoint inhibitor is approved for lung cancer."]],
  cell: "Tobacco smoke contains carcinogens such as polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (for example benzo[a]pyrene) and nitrosamines (such as NNK). After activation by cytochrome P450 enzymes they bind DNA, forming adducts that cause mutations, often G to T changes, in genes such as TP53 and KRAS. Radon, asbestos, air pollution and family history add risk. Damage accumulates in stages from hyperplasia to dysplasia, carcinoma in situ and invasive cancer. Non-small cell lung cancer includes adenocarcinoma (most common overall and in never-smokers, usually in the outer lung) and squamous cell carcinoma (central, strongly linked to smoking, can secrete PTHrP). Adenocarcinoma in never-smokers often has a single driver change such as an EGFR mutation, an ALK or ROS1 fusion, or RET, MET, BRAF, HER2 or KRAS G12C alterations that keep growth signaling switched on. Small cell lung cancer is a neuroendocrine tumor almost always caused by smoking, with loss of TP53 and RB1. It grows fast, spreads early and can make hormones like ADH and ACTH. Many tumors express PD-L1, which switches off attacking T cells by binding PD-1.",
  body: [["Lungs and airways", "Tumors block airways, causing cough, coughing up blood, wheeze and pneumonia behind the blockage. Fluid can collect around the lung (malignant pleural effusion)."], ["Chest structures", "Tumors can press on the superior vena cava (facial and arm swelling), the recurrent laryngeal nerve (hoarseness), the phrenic nerve (raised diaphragm) or the esophagus (trouble swallowing)."], ["Lung apex (Pancoast tumor)", "A tumor at the top of the lung can invade the sympathetic chain and brachial plexus, causing Horner syndrome and shoulder and arm pain."], ["Brain", "Brain metastases are common and cause headaches, seizures, weakness or personality change."], ["Bones, liver and adrenals", "Spread to bone causes pain and fractures. Liver and adrenal glands are other frequent sites."], ["Hormones and nerves", "Paraneoplastic syndromes include SIADH and Cushing syndrome (small cell), hypercalcemia from PTHrP (squamous) and Lambert-Eaton syndrome (small cell)."]],
  symptoms: "A new or changing cough, coughing up blood and chest pain arise from tumor growth in airways and nearby tissue. Shortness of breath comes from blocked airways, pleural fluid or loss of working lung. Weight loss and fatigue reflect cytokines from the tumor and the body's response. Hoarseness, facial swelling, bone pain and neurologic symptoms come from local spread or metastases. Many early lung cancers cause no symptoms, which is why screening matters.",
  dx: "Lung cancer is often found on a chest X-ray or CT. The USPSTF recommends yearly low-dose CT screening for adults 50 to 80 with a 20 pack-year smoking history who currently smoke or quit within the past 15 years. Suspicious findings lead to PET-CT and biopsy by bronchoscopy, endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) or CT-guided needle. Pathology determines the type, and molecular testing looks for EGFR, ALK, ROS1, KRAS, BRAF, MET, RET, HER2 and NTRK changes plus PD-L1 level. Staging uses the TNM system, and brain MRI checks for metastases.",
  tx: "Early-stage NSCLC is treated with surgery, often a lobectomy, or with stereotactic radiation. Chemotherapy and immunotherapy before or after surgery lower the risk of return, and osimertinib after surgery helps EGFR-mutated tumors. Stage III disease is often treated with chemotherapy plus radiation followed by durvalumab. Advanced NSCLC with a driver change is treated with targeted drugs such as EGFR inhibitors (osimertinib), ALK inhibitors (alectinib, lorlatinib), KRAS G12C inhibitors (sotorasib, adagrasib) and others, which block the overactive growth signal. Without a driver, checkpoint inhibitors (PD-1 or PD-L1 antibodies like pembrolizumab, nivolumab, atezolizumab) release the brakes on T cells, alone or with platinum chemotherapy, which damages tumor DNA. Small cell lung cancer is treated with platinum and etoposide plus atezolizumab or durvalumab, and later options include tarlatamab, which links T cells to tumor cells, and lurbinectedin. Smoking cessation, palliative care and symptom control are essential parts of care.",
  drugs: ["osimertinib", "pembrolizumab", "nivolumab", "atezolizumab", "durvalumab", "cisplatin", "carboplatin", "pemetrexed", "paclitaxel", "docetaxel", "etoposide", "alectinib", "lorlatinib", "crizotinib", "sotorasib", "adagrasib", "amivantamab", "lazertinib", "selpercatinib", "capmatinib", "dabrafenib", "trametinib", "tarlatamab", "lurbinectedin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Sentral (central) tumors: Squamous and Small cell. Squamous makes PTHrP (hypercalcemia). Small cell makes SIADH and ACTH and causes Lambert-Eaton."], ["Most-tested fact", "Adenocarcinoma is the most common type overall and the most common in people who never smoked."], ["Pancoast tumor", "An apical tumor can cause Horner syndrome: ptosis, miosis and anhidrosis on the same side."], ["Screening", "Annual low-dose CT for ages 50 to 80 with a 20 pack-year history who smoke now or quit within 15 years (USPSTF 2021)."]],
  red: "Coughing up more than a small amount of blood, sudden severe shortness of breath, swelling of the face, neck and arms, new confusion or seizures, or sudden weakness in the legs with back pain (possible spinal cord compression) needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Evarts Graham's first pneumonectomy patient, a physician, outlived him. Graham, a longtime smoker who helped prove the link between smoking and lung cancer, died of lung cancer in 1957.",
  src: ["NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Non-Small Cell and Small Cell Lung Cancer", "USPSTF Lung Cancer Screening Recommendation (2021)", "National Lung Screening Trial Research Team. N Engl J Med 2011", "Doll R, Hill AB. BMJ 1950", "US Surgeon General. Smoking and Health (1964)"]
},
"anaphylaxis": {
  name: "Anaphylaxis",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["anaphylactic shock", "severe allergic reaction", "anaphylactoid reaction"],
  what: "Anaphylaxis is a sudden, severe allergic reaction that affects the whole body. It can swell the throat, tighten the airways and drop blood pressure within minutes. It is a life-threatening emergency, and epinephrine is the first treatment.",
  origin: "In 1901 French scientists Charles Richet and Paul Portier, invited aboard the yacht of Prince Albert I of Monaco, studied the toxin of the Portuguese man-of-war. Back in Paris, they gave dogs small doses of a sea anemone toxin, expecting to build protection. Instead, dogs given a second dose weeks later collapsed and died within minutes. In 1902 Richet named this anaphylaxis, meaning against protection, and he won the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1913. In 1966 and 1967 Kimishige and Teruko Ishizaka in the US, and S. G. O. Johansson and Hans Bennich in Sweden, discovered immunoglobulin E (IgE), the antibody behind most allergic reactions. Epinephrine had been purified around 1900 and became the main treatment. The EpiPen auto-injector, adapted from military auto-injector technology, was approved in 1987, letting people carry epinephrine with them. In 2024 the FDA approved the first needle-free epinephrine nasal spray.",
  timeline: [["1902", "Charles Richet and Paul Portier describe and name anaphylaxis."], ["1913", "Richet receives the Nobel Prize for his work on anaphylaxis."], ["1967", "IgE is identified as the antibody behind allergic reactions."], ["1987", "The epinephrine auto-injector is approved for public use."], ["2024", "The FDA approves the first epinephrine nasal spray for anaphylaxis."]],
  cell: "Classic anaphylaxis is a type I (immediate) hypersensitivity reaction. On a first exposure to an allergen such as peanut, insect venom or a drug, Th2 cells release IL-4 and IL-13, which push B cells to make allergen-specific IgE. IgE binds the high-affinity receptor FcepsilonRI on mast cells and basophils, sensitizing them. On re-exposure, allergen cross-links neighboring IgE molecules, triggering a signaling cascade and calcium influx that causes degranulation within seconds. Preformed mediators include histamine and tryptase. Newly made mediators include leukotrienes C4 and D4, prostaglandin D2 and platelet-activating factor. Histamine acting on H1 receptors dilates vessels, makes capillaries leaky, constricts bronchial smooth muscle and causes itching, while H2 effects add vasodilation. A large share of plasma volume can leak out within minutes, causing distributive shock. Some reactions skip IgE and directly activate mast cells, for example through MRGPRX2 with some drugs, or through complement. A late-phase or biphasic reaction can occur hours later.",
  body: [["Airway and lungs", "Swelling of the tongue, throat and larynx can block airflow, and bronchoconstriction causes wheezing and low oxygen. Airway obstruction is a leading cause of death in food-induced anaphylaxis."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Widespread vasodilation and fluid leak cause low blood pressure and shock, and the heart may beat fast or, in late shock, slow. Reduced coronary blood flow can cause chest pain."], ["Skin and mucosa", "Hives, flushing, itching and angioedema occur in most cases, though they can be absent, especially in severe shock."], ["Gut", "Smooth muscle contraction and fluid shift cause cramping, vomiting and diarrhea, especially with food allergy."], ["Brain", "Low blood flow causes dizziness, fainting, confusion and a sense of impending doom."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms usually start within minutes to an hour of exposure. Hives and flushing come from histamine dilating skin vessels. Swelling of lips, tongue and throat, hoarseness and stridor come from leaky vessels in the upper airway. Wheeze and chest tightness come from bronchoconstriction by histamine and leukotrienes. Dizziness and fainting come from vasodilation and fluid loss lowering blood pressure. Vomiting and cramps come from gut smooth muscle contraction. People on beta blockers may have more severe reactions that respond poorly to epinephrine.",
  dx: "Anaphylaxis is a clinical diagnosis made quickly at the bedside. Widely used criteria describe sudden skin or mucosal symptoms plus breathing trouble or low blood pressure, or two or more organ systems involved after a likely allergen, or low blood pressure after a known allergen. Newer criteria also include sudden breathing involvement or low blood pressure after a known allergen even without skin signs. A serum tryptase drawn within a few hours can support the diagnosis but is not needed to treat. Later, an allergist uses skin and blood IgE testing to identify the trigger.",
  tx: "Epinephrine is the first and most important treatment and is given into the outer thigh muscle as soon as anaphylaxis is recognized; delay is linked to deaths. It acts on alpha-1 receptors to tighten blood vessels and reduce swelling, on beta-1 receptors to strengthen the heart, and on beta-2 receptors to open airways and reduce further mediator release from mast cells. Doses can be repeated. Patients lie flat with legs raised and receive oxygen and IV fluids. Antihistamines (H1 blockers like diphenhydramine or cetirizine, and H2 blockers like famotidine) treat itching and hives but do not treat airway swelling or shock. Albuterol helps persistent wheeze. Corticosteroids are sometimes given but have not been shown to prevent biphasic reactions. Glucagon is used for patients on beta blockers who do not respond to epinephrine. An epinephrine infusion or other vasopressors treat refractory shock. People at risk carry two epinephrine devices and an action plan. Omalizumab lowers the risk of reactions after accidental exposure in IgE-mediated food allergy.",
  drugs: ["epinephrine", "epinephrine-auto-injector", "epinephrine-nasal", "diphenhydramine", "cetirizine", "famotidine", "albuterol", "methylprednisolone", "prednisone", "glucagon", "normal-saline", "lactated-ringers", "norepinephrine", "vasopressin", "omalizumab"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Epinephrine IM in the anterolateral thigh is first-line. Antihistamines and steroids are add-ons, never replacements."], ["Beta blocker pearl", "Patients on beta blockers can resist epinephrine; glucagon raises cAMP in the heart without beta receptors."], ["Lab clue", "Elevated serum tryptase supports mast cell activation and anaphylaxis."], ["Biphasic reaction", "Symptoms can come back hours later without new exposure, so patients are observed after treatment."]],
  red: "Throat tightness, trouble breathing or swallowing, wheezing, swelling of the tongue or lips, fainting, or a sudden feeling of doom after a sting, food or medicine is anaphylaxis and needs epinephrine and emergency care (call 911), even if symptoms improve.",
  fun: "Richet coined anaphylaxis from Greek words meaning against protection, because his dogs became more sensitive to the toxin instead of protected against it.",
  src: ["AAAAI/ACAAI Joint Task Force Anaphylaxis Practice Parameter Update (2023)", "World Allergy Organization Anaphylaxis Guidance (2020)", "Sampson HA et al. NIAID/FAAN Second Symposium on Anaphylaxis. J Allergy Clin Immunol 2006", "Portier P, Richet C. De l'action anaphylactique de certains venins (1902)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"sarcoidosis": {
  name: "Sarcoidosis",
  sys: "Lungs & Breathing",
  aka: ["sarcoid", "Besnier-Boeck-Schaumann disease", "Lofgren syndrome", "pulmonary sarcoidosis", "cardiac sarcoidosis"],
  what: "Sarcoidosis is an inflammatory disease in which small clusters of immune cells, called granulomas, form in organs. It most often affects the lungs and the lymph nodes in the chest, but it can involve the skin, eyes, heart and nerves.",
  origin: "In the 1870s English surgeon Jonathan Hutchinson described patients with purple skin plaques, one of the first accounts. In 1889 French dermatologist Ernest Besnier described lupus pernio, a purple rash on the nose and cheeks. In 1899 Norwegian dermatologist Caesar Boeck described skin lesions with a microscopic look he thought resembled sarcoma and called them sarkoid, giving the disease its name. In the 1910s Swedish dermatologist Jorgen Schaumann showed that it was a body-wide disease affecting the lungs, lymph nodes and other organs. In the 1940s Swedish physician Sven Lofgren described a form with bilateral hilar lymph nodes, erythema nodosum and joint pain that usually resolves on its own, now called Lofgren syndrome. After cortisone became available in the 1950s, corticosteroids became the main treatment. The cause is still unknown, and recent research has focused on immune responses to environmental and microbial antigens and on TNF-alpha blockers for severe disease.",
  timeline: [["1870s", "Jonathan Hutchinson describes skin lesions now recognized as sarcoidosis."], ["1889", "Ernest Besnier describes lupus pernio."], ["1899", "Caesar Boeck names the skin lesions sarkoid."], ["1910s", "Jorgen Schaumann shows sarcoidosis is a systemic disease."], ["1950s", "Corticosteroids become the main treatment for sarcoidosis."]],
  cell: "Sarcoidosis is thought to arise when a genetically susceptible person (certain HLA-DRB1 types, for example) meets an unknown antigen, possibly from microbes such as mycobacteria or Cutibacterium, or from inorganic dusts. Antigen-presenting cells show the antigen on MHC class II molecules to CD4 helper T cells, which become Th1 and Th17 cells. These release IL-2 and interferon-gamma, which activate macrophages. Macrophages make TNF-alpha and IL-12, keeping the loop going, and transform into epithelioid histiocytes that fuse into multinucleated giant cells. They cluster into tight, non-caseating granulomas surrounded by lymphocytes, sometimes containing asteroid bodies and Schaumann bodies. Bronchoalveolar fluid shows many CD4 cells with a high CD4 to CD8 ratio, while blood lymphocytes may be low and skin tests show anergy because T cells concentrate in tissues. Activated macrophages express 1-alpha-hydroxylase, which converts vitamin D to active calcitriol, raising calcium absorption and causing hypercalcemia and kidney stones. Macrophages also release angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE). In some patients granulomas persist and turn into scar tissue.",
  body: [["Lungs and chest lymph nodes", "Granulomas enlarge hilar lymph nodes on both sides and infiltrate the lung, causing cough and breathlessness. Chronic disease can lead to fibrosis and pulmonary hypertension."], ["Skin", "Erythema nodosum (tender red bumps on the shins), lupus pernio (purple plaques on the nose and cheeks) and other papules or plaques can appear."], ["Eyes", "Uveitis causes eye pain, redness and blurred vision and can threaten sight if untreated. Dry eyes and enlarged tear glands also occur."], ["Heart", "Granulomas in the heart muscle can cause heart block, dangerous ventricular arrhythmias, heart failure and sudden death."], ["Nerves and brain", "Neurosarcoidosis can cause facial nerve palsy, meningitis, seizures and pituitary problems such as diabetes insipidus."], ["Kidneys and calcium", "High calcium from excess calcitriol causes kidney stones, nephrocalcinosis and kidney injury."], ["Liver, spleen and joints", "Granulomas can enlarge the liver and spleen, and arthritis, especially of the ankles, is common."]],
  symptoms: "Many people have no symptoms and are found on a chest X-ray done for another reason. Dry cough, breathlessness and chest discomfort come from granulomas in the lungs and nodes. Fatigue, fever and weight loss reflect cytokines such as TNF-alpha. Red, painful eyes and blurred vision come from uveitis. Tender shin bumps come from erythema nodosum. Palpitations or fainting suggest heart involvement. Thirst, frequent urination and kidney stones can come from high calcium.",
  dx: "Diagnosis rests on three parts: a compatible clinical and imaging picture, non-caseating granulomas on biopsy, and exclusion of other causes of granulomas such as tuberculosis, fungal infections, lymphoma and drug reactions. Chest X-ray or CT often shows bilateral hilar lymph nodes, graded with Scadding stages. Biopsy is often obtained by endobronchial ultrasound needle aspiration. Lofgren syndrome can be diagnosed without biopsy. Screening includes an eye exam, ECG, calcium and kidney tests. A high ACE level is common but not specific enough for diagnosis. Cardiac MRI or PET evaluates suspected heart involvement.",
  tx: "Many people, especially with Lofgren syndrome, improve without treatment and are monitored. Treatment is used for significant symptoms or organ threat, such as worsening lung function, eye, heart or nerve involvement, or high calcium. Corticosteroids such as prednisone are first-line; they bind glucocorticoid receptors and turn off the cytokines that build granulomas. Steroid-sparing drugs reduce long-term steroid harm: methotrexate (blocks folate metabolism in immune cells) is the most common, with azathioprine, leflunomide and mycophenolate as alternatives. Hydroxychloroquine helps skin disease and high calcium. TNF-alpha blockers such as infliximab or adalimumab are used for severe or refractory disease because TNF-alpha holds granulomas together. Cardiac involvement may need a pacemaker or implantable defibrillator. Severe lung disease may lead to lung transplant.",
  drugs: ["prednisone", "methylprednisolone", "methotrexate", "azathioprine", "leflunomide", "mycophenolate", "hydroxychloroquine", "infliximab", "adalimumab", "corticotropin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "GRAIN: Gammaglobulins up, Rheumatoid arthritis-like joint pain, ACE up, Interstitial fibrosis, Non-caseating granulomas."], ["Most-tested fact", "A young Black woman with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy and non-caseating granulomas is the classic presentation."], ["Hypercalcemia", "Granuloma macrophages make 1-alpha-hydroxylase, raising calcitriol and calcium."], ["Lofgren syndrome", "Bilateral hilar nodes, erythema nodosum, ankle arthritis and fever. It has a good prognosis and often resolves on its own."]],
  red: "Fainting, a very slow or irregular heartbeat, chest pain, sudden vision loss or severe eye pain, or severe shortness of breath in someone with sarcoidosis needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Boeck named the disease sarkoid because he thought the skin lesions looked like sarcoma under the microscope, even though sarcoidosis is not a cancer.",
  src: ["ATS Clinical Practice Guideline: Diagnosis and Detection of Sarcoidosis (2020)", "ERS Clinical Practice Guidelines on Treatment of Sarcoidosis (2021)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "Grunewald J et al. Sarcoidosis. Nat Rev Dis Primers 2019"]
},
"stroke-ischemic": {
  name: "Ischemic stroke",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["stroke", "cerebrovascular accident", "CVA", "brain attack", "cerebral infarction", "acute ischemic stroke", "AIS"],
  what: "An ischemic stroke happens when a blood clot or narrowed artery blocks blood flow to part of the brain. Brain cells start dying within minutes, causing sudden weakness, numbness, trouble speaking or vision loss. It is an emergency where every minute counts.",
  origin: "Hippocrates described apoplexy, a sudden collapse with paralysis, about 2,400 years ago. In 1658 Swiss physician Johann Jakob Wepfer studied the brains of people who died of apoplexy and showed that some had bleeding while others had blocked arteries that stopped blood supply. In the 1800s Rudolf Virchow described how clots form in vessels and travel as emboli. For most of the 20th century there was no treatment that could reopen a blocked brain artery. That changed in 1995, when the NINDS trial showed that the clot-dissolving drug alteplase (tPA), given within three hours, improved recovery, and the FDA approved it in 1996. In 2008 the ECASS III trial extended the window to four and a half hours for many patients. In 2015 a series of trials proved that mechanical thrombectomy, pulling the clot out with a catheter, greatly improves outcomes for large vessel blockages. In 2018 the DAWN and DEFUSE 3 trials extended thrombectomy to as late as 24 hours in selected patients, and in 2025 tenecteplase was approved for stroke.",
  timeline: [["1658", "Johann Jakob Wepfer links some apoplexy to blocked brain arteries."], ["1995", "The NINDS trial shows alteplase improves stroke recovery."], ["1996", "The FDA approves alteplase for acute ischemic stroke."], ["2015", "Several trials prove mechanical thrombectomy helps large vessel occlusion strokes."], ["2018", "The DAWN and DEFUSE 3 trials extend thrombectomy to as late as 24 hours in selected patients."], ["2025", "Tenecteplase is FDA approved for acute ischemic stroke."]],
  cell: "Ischemic stroke has three main causes: thrombosis on a large artery atherosclerotic plaque (such as the carotid or middle cerebral artery), embolism from the heart (often from atrial fibrillation) or from a plaque upstream, and small vessel (lacunar) disease, where long-standing hypertension causes lipohyalinosis of tiny penetrating arteries. Neurons depend on a steady supply of glucose and oxygen and have almost no reserve. Within minutes of lost flow, ATP falls and the sodium-potassium ATPase fails. Cells depolarize, sodium and water rush in (cytotoxic edema), and glutamate floods the synapse. Glutamate overstimulates NMDA and AMPA receptors, letting calcium pour into neurons. Calcium activates proteases, phospholipases and nitric oxide synthase, and damages mitochondria, producing free radicals. This excitotoxic cascade kills cells by necrosis and apoptosis. The ischemic core dies quickly, but the surrounding penumbra receives some collateral flow and can be saved if blood flow returns fast. Over days, inflammation and blood-brain barrier breakdown cause vasogenic edema, and dead tissue undergoes liquefactive necrosis.",
  body: [["Brain", "The damage depends on the artery. Middle cerebral artery strokes weaken the opposite face and arm, and cause aphasia (dominant side) or neglect (non-dominant side). Anterior cerebral strokes weaken the opposite leg. Posterior circulation strokes cause vertigo, imbalance, double vision or visual field loss."], ["Brain swelling", "Large strokes swell over two to five days and can raise pressure in the skull, causing herniation and death unless treated."], ["Heart", "Atrial fibrillation, a recent heart attack or a heart valve problem can be the source of the clot. Stroke itself can also cause heart rhythm changes and troponin rise."], ["Swallowing and lungs", "Weak throat muscles cause dysphagia, leading to aspiration pneumonia, a major cause of death after stroke."], ["Muscles and joints", "Paralysis leads to spasticity, contractures, shoulder pain and loss of mobility."], ["Mood and thinking", "Post-stroke depression, anxiety and cognitive decline are common and slow recovery."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms start suddenly because brain tissue stops working within seconds of losing blood flow. One-sided face drooping, arm or leg weakness and numbness come from damage to the motor and sensory pathways on the opposite side of the brain. Slurred or confused speech comes from damage to speech areas. Sudden vision loss, double vision, vertigo and loss of balance come from strokes in the back of the brain. Ischemic stroke is usually painless, unlike many hemorrhagic strokes.",
  dx: "A fingerstick glucose is checked first because low blood sugar can mimic a stroke. A noncontrast head CT is done right away to rule out bleeding before clot-dissolving drugs. CT angiography looks for a large vessel occlusion that could be removed by thrombectomy, and CT perfusion or MRI helps show salvageable brain, especially when the onset time is unknown. MRI diffusion-weighted imaging is the most sensitive test for new infarction. The NIH Stroke Scale scores severity. ECG, heart monitoring, echocardiogram, lipids and A1c look for the cause.",
  tx: "Time is brain. Eligible patients receive IV thrombolysis with alteplase or tenecteplase within four and a half hours of last known well. These drugs convert plasminogen to plasmin, which breaks down fibrin in the clot. Blood pressure must be below 185/110 before and kept below 180/105 after thrombolysis, often with labetalol, nicardipine or clevidipine. Mechanical thrombectomy removes large vessel clots and can be done up to 24 hours in selected patients. Patients not receiving thrombolysis usually start aspirin within 24 to 48 hours. For minor stroke, short-term dual antiplatelet therapy (aspirin plus clopidogrel or ticagrelor) lowers early recurrence. Long-term prevention includes high-intensity statins, blood pressure control, anticoagulants (apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, warfarin) for atrial fibrillation, carotid surgery or stenting for tight symptomatic stenosis, diabetes control and smoking cessation. Stroke units, swallowing screens and early rehabilitation improve recovery.",
  drugs: ["alteplase", "tenecteplase", "aspirin", "clopidogrel", "ticagrelor", "aspirin-dipyridamole", "atorvastatin", "rosuvastatin", "apixaban", "rivaroxaban", "dabigatran", "edoxaban", "warfarin", "labetalol", "nicardipine", "clevidipine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "BE FAST: Balance, Eyes, Face, Arm, Speech, Time to call 911."], ["Most-tested fact", "A noncontrast head CT is done first to rule out hemorrhage before thrombolysis."], ["Blood pressure numbers", "Below 185/110 to give thrombolysis, and below 180/105 for 24 hours after."], ["Vulnerable cells", "The hippocampus (CA1), Purkinje cells of the cerebellum and cortical layers are most sensitive to low oxygen."], ["Often confused with", "Stroke mimics: hypoglycemia, seizure with Todd paralysis, complex migraine and conversion disorder."]],
  red: "Sudden face drooping, arm or leg weakness, trouble speaking, vision loss, loss of balance or a sudden severe headache needs emergency care (call 911). Noting the time symptoms began, or when the person was last seen normal, helps decide treatment.",
  fun: "During a large vessel stroke, roughly two million neurons are estimated to die each minute, the origin of the phrase time is brain.",
  src: ["AHA/ASA Guidelines for the Early Management of Acute Ischemic Stroke (2019 update)", "AHA/ASA Guideline for Prevention of Stroke in Patients with Stroke and TIA (2021)", "NINDS rt-PA Stroke Study Group. N Engl J Med 1995", "Saver JL. Time is brain, quantified. Stroke 2006", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"stroke-hemorrhagic": {
  name: "Hemorrhagic stroke and subarachnoid hemorrhage",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["intracerebral hemorrhage", "ICH", "subarachnoid hemorrhage", "SAH", "brain bleed", "ruptured brain aneurysm"],
  what: "A hemorrhagic stroke happens when a blood vessel in or around the brain bursts. Bleeding inside the brain is called intracerebral hemorrhage, and bleeding into the space around the brain, often from a ruptured aneurysm, is subarachnoid hemorrhage. Both are emergencies.",
  origin: "In 1658 Johann Jakob Wepfer showed at autopsy that some people who died of apoplexy had bleeding into the brain. In 1868 Jean-Martin Charcot and Charles Bouchard described tiny aneurysms on small brain arteries in people with brain hemorrhage, now called Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms and linked to high blood pressure. Aneurysms at the base of the brain were recognized as a cause of subarachnoid bleeding, and in 1937 American neurosurgeon Walter Dandy performed the first planned clipping of a brain aneurysm. In 1968 William Hunt and Robert Hess published a grading scale for subarachnoid hemorrhage still used today. In the 1980s trials showed that nimodipine improves outcomes after aneurysm rupture. In 1991 Guido Guglielmi introduced detachable coils that seal aneurysms from inside the artery, and the 2002 ISAT trial showed coiling often gives better outcomes than surgery. For intracerebral hemorrhage, recent advances include faster reversal of blood thinners and careful blood pressure lowering.",
  timeline: [["1658", "Johann Jakob Wepfer shows that some apoplexy is caused by bleeding in the brain."], ["1868", "Charcot and Bouchard describe microaneurysms in hypertensive brain hemorrhage."], ["1937", "Walter Dandy performs the first planned clipping of a brain aneurysm."], ["1968", "The Hunt and Hess grading scale for subarachnoid hemorrhage is published."], ["1991", "Guglielmi detachable coils are introduced to treat aneurysms from inside the vessel."], ["2002", "The ISAT trial compares coiling and clipping for ruptured aneurysms."]],
  cell: "In intracerebral hemorrhage, long-standing high blood pressure damages small penetrating arteries such as the lenticulostriate branches. Smooth muscle is replaced by collagen and fibrinoid material (lipohyalinosis), and Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms form, which can rupture into the basal ganglia (putamen most often), thalamus, pons or cerebellum. In older adults, cerebral amyloid angiopathy deposits beta-amyloid in the walls of cortical vessels, making them brittle and causing lobar hemorrhages; APOE e2 and e4 raise risk. Anticoagulants, vascular malformations, tumors and drugs such as cocaine also cause bleeds. The hematoma can keep expanding in the first hours, then thrombin, hemoglobin breakdown products and iron trigger inflammation and edema around it, raising intracranial pressure. In subarachnoid hemorrhage, a saccular (berry) aneurysm forms at a branch point of the circle of Willis, most often the anterior communicating artery, where the wall lacks a normal media and internal elastic lamina. Risk rises with smoking, hypertension, polycystic kidney disease and connective tissue disorders. When it ruptures, arterial blood floods the subarachnoid space and pressure spikes. Blood breakdown products later trigger vasospasm through endothelin release and loss of nitric oxide, and blood blocks CSF absorption, causing hydrocephalus.",
  body: [["Brain tissue", "The hematoma destroys tissue directly and compresses surrounding brain. Putamen bleeds cause opposite-side weakness, thalamic bleeds cause sensory loss and eye changes, and cerebellar bleeds cause vomiting, imbalance and rapid brainstem compression."], ["Skull pressure", "Blood and edema raise intracranial pressure, which can push brain tissue through openings in the skull (herniation), causing coma and death."], ["Brain arteries", "After subarachnoid hemorrhage, vasospasm narrows arteries, typically between days three and fourteen, causing delayed cerebral ischemia and new strokes."], ["CSF flow", "Blood blocks the arachnoid granulations or ventricles, causing hydrocephalus with drowsiness and confusion."], ["Heart", "A surge of catecholamines can cause ECG changes, arrhythmias and stress cardiomyopathy (neurogenic stunned myocardium)."], ["Salt and water", "Subarachnoid hemorrhage often causes low sodium from cerebral salt wasting or SIADH, which can worsen brain swelling."]],
  symptoms: "Subarachnoid hemorrhage classically causes a sudden thunderclap headache, often described as the worst of a person's life, because arterial blood suddenly irritates pain-sensitive meninges and raises pressure. Neck stiffness and light sensitivity come from blood irritating the meninges. Brief loss of consciousness reflects a sudden pressure spike. Intracerebral hemorrhage causes sudden weakness or numbness on one side, trouble speaking, vomiting and headache, often worsening over minutes as the hematoma grows. Drowsiness and coma come from rising intracranial pressure.",
  dx: "A noncontrast head CT is the first test and shows fresh blood clearly. It is very sensitive for subarachnoid hemorrhage within the first six hours. If CT is negative but suspicion remains, a lumbar puncture looks for red blood cells that do not clear and xanthochromia (yellow fluid from hemoglobin breakdown). CT angiography or catheter angiography finds the aneurysm or other vascular cause. A spot sign on CT angiography predicts hematoma growth. Severity is graded with the ICH score, Hunt and Hess, WFNS and modified Fisher scales. Coagulation tests show whether blood thinners are involved.",
  tx: "For intracerebral hemorrhage, blood pressure is lowered quickly but smoothly, often with nicardipine, clevidipine or labetalol, to limit hematoma growth. Anticoagulants are reversed: warfarin with four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (4F-PCC) plus vitamin K, dabigatran with idarucizumab, factor Xa inhibitors such as apixaban and rivaroxaban with 4F-PCC, and heparin with protamine. Platelet transfusion is not routinely used for people on antiplatelet drugs unless surgery is planned. Raised intracranial pressure is managed with head elevation, hyperosmolar therapy (mannitol or hypertonic saline) and sometimes a ventricular drain. Large cerebellar bleeds are removed surgically. For subarachnoid hemorrhage, the aneurysm is secured early by endovascular coiling or surgical clipping to prevent rebleeding. Oral nimodipine, a calcium channel blocker, improves neurologic outcome. Patients are kept euvolemic and watched closely for vasospasm, hydrocephalus and low sodium. Seizures are treated when they occur, often with levetiracetam.",
  drugs: ["nicardipine", "clevidipine", "labetalol", "prothrombin-complex-concentrate", "phytonadione", "idarucizumab", "protamine", "nimodipine", "mannitol", "hypertonic-saline-3-percent", "levetiracetam", "acetaminophen", "ondansetron"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Thunderclap worst headache of life equals subarachnoid hemorrhage until proven otherwise. CT first, then LP if CT is negative."], ["Aneurysm location", "The anterior communicating artery is the most common site. A posterior communicating artery aneurysm can compress cranial nerve III, causing a blown pupil and down-and-out eye."], ["Hypertensive bleed sites", "Putamen is most common, then thalamus, pons and cerebellum. Lobar bleeds in older adults suggest amyloid angiopathy."], ["Drug pearl", "Nimodipine is given after SAH to improve outcomes. Its benefit is thought to be neuroprotective rather than simply reversing vasospasm."], ["Associations", "Berry aneurysms are linked to autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, Ehlers-Danlos syndrome and coarctation of the aorta."]],
  red: "A sudden, severe headache unlike any before, especially with vomiting, stiff neck, fainting, seizure, confusion, or sudden weakness or trouble speaking, needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Walter Dandy, who clipped the first brain aneurysm, also invented ventriculography, an early way of imaging the brain by injecting air into its fluid spaces.",
  src: ["AHA/ASA Guideline for the Management of Spontaneous Intracerebral Hemorrhage (2022)", "AHA/ASA Guideline for the Management of Aneurysmal Subarachnoid Hemorrhage (2023)", "Molyneux A et al. ISAT. Lancet 2002", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Hunt WE, Hess RM. J Neurosurg 1968"]
},
"tia": {
  name: "Transient ischemic attack (TIA)",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["TIA", "mini-stroke", "warning stroke", "amaurosis fugax (transient monocular blindness)"],
  what: "A transient ischemic attack is a brief episode of stroke symptoms caused by a short-lived blockage of blood flow to the brain, spinal cord or eye. Symptoms go away, usually within minutes, and no permanent damage shows on brain imaging, but it is a strong warning of a coming stroke.",
  origin: "In the 1950s Canadian-American neurologist C. Miller Fisher, working in Boston, showed that many strokes are caused by disease of the carotid artery in the neck, and that brief warning spells often come first. He described transient monocular blindness and brief one-sided weakness as signs of carotid disease and helped popularize the idea of the transient ischemic attack. In the 1960s and 1970s TIA was defined as symptoms lasting less than 24 hours, an arbitrary cutoff. MRI later showed that many such spells actually cause small permanent infarcts, so in 2009 the American Heart Association adopted a tissue-based definition: a brief episode without acute infarction on imaging. In 1991 the NASCET trial proved that carotid endarterectomy prevents strokes in people with severe symptomatic carotid narrowing. In 2007 the ABCD2 score was published to estimate early stroke risk, and trials such as CHANCE (2013) and POINT (2018) showed that short-term aspirin plus clopidogrel after a high-risk TIA prevents strokes.",
  timeline: [["1950s", "C. Miller Fisher links brief warning spells to carotid artery disease."], ["1991", "The NASCET trial shows carotid endarterectomy prevents stroke after symptomatic severe stenosis."], ["2007", "The ABCD2 score is published to predict early stroke risk after TIA."], ["2009", "The AHA/ASA adopts a tissue-based definition of TIA."], ["2018", "The POINT trial confirms short-term dual antiplatelet therapy after high-risk TIA."]],
  cell: "A TIA follows the same causes as ischemic stroke: an embolus from a carotid or aortic plaque, a clot from the heart in atrial fibrillation, or narrowing of a small penetrating artery. The difference is timing. Brain cells stop working electrically at a higher level of blood flow than the level at which they die. When flow drops below the electrical failure threshold, ATP falls, ion pumps falter and neurons stop firing, so symptoms appear. If flow returns quickly, because the clot breaks up, moves downstream or collateral vessels take over, the cells regain energy and function before the cascade of calcium overload and excitotoxic death becomes permanent. In amaurosis fugax, a small embolus, sometimes a cholesterol fragment visible as a Hollenhorst plaque, briefly blocks the retinal artery. Because the underlying plaque or heart source remains, the next clot may not dissolve in time. Stroke risk is highest in the first 48 hours after a TIA.",
  body: [["Brain", "Temporary loss of function in a brain region causes brief weakness, numbness, speech trouble or imbalance. Up to a third of classic 24-hour spells show small infarcts on MRI, which are then classified as strokes."], ["Eye", "Amaurosis fugax causes painless loss of vision in one eye, often described as a curtain coming down, from a brief retinal artery blockage."], ["Carotid arteries", "Atherosclerotic plaque at the carotid bifurcation is a key source of emboli, and its severity guides surgery."], ["Heart", "Atrial fibrillation, which may come and go, can throw clots to the brain. Long-term heart rhythm monitoring often finds it."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms are the same as stroke because the mechanism is the same, but they resolve, often within minutes. One-sided weakness or numbness, slurred speech or trouble finding words, sudden loss of vision in one eye, double vision and loss of balance reflect the region briefly starved of blood. Because symptoms are gone by the time many people see a clinician, the diagnosis often depends on the history. Symptoms like isolated dizziness, confusion or fainting are less likely to be a TIA.",
  dx: "Diagnosis combines the history of a sudden, focal, temporary deficit with imaging that shows no acute infarct. MRI with diffusion-weighted imaging is preferred. Vessel imaging of the head and neck (carotid ultrasound, CT angiography or MR angiography) looks for narrowing. ECG and heart monitoring look for atrial fibrillation, and echocardiography looks for a cardiac source. Glucose, lipids and A1c are checked. The ABCD2 score (Age, Blood pressure, Clinical features, Duration, Diabetes) estimates short-term stroke risk. Evaluation is done urgently, ideally within 24 hours.",
  tx: "The goal is to prevent a stroke, and urgent treatment greatly lowers that risk. Antiplatelet drugs stop platelets from sticking together: aspirin blocks COX-1 and thromboxane A2, while clopidogrel and ticagrelor block the P2Y12 ADP receptor. After a high-risk TIA, aspirin plus clopidogrel (or ticagrelor) is used for a short period, about three weeks, then a single agent continues. If atrial fibrillation is found, an anticoagulant such as apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran or warfarin is used instead. High-intensity statins stabilize plaque and lower LDL. Blood pressure is lowered with drugs such as ACE inhibitors, thiazides or calcium channel blockers. Carotid endarterectomy or stenting is done soon, ideally within two weeks, for significant symptomatic carotid narrowing. Smoking cessation, diabetes control, exercise and healthy eating complete prevention.",
  drugs: ["aspirin", "clopidogrel", "ticagrelor", "aspirin-dipyridamole", "atorvastatin", "rosuvastatin", "apixaban", "rivaroxaban", "dabigatran", "edoxaban", "warfarin", "lisinopril", "chlorthalidone", "amlodipine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "ABCD2: Age 60 or older, Blood pressure 140/90 or higher, Clinical (weakness or speech trouble), Duration, Diabetes."], ["Most-tested fact", "TIA is now defined by tissue: brief symptoms with no infarct on imaging, not by the old 24-hour rule."], ["Amaurosis fugax", "Transient one-eye vision loss like a curtain points to an embolus from the same-side carotid artery."], ["Key point", "Stroke risk is highest in the first 48 hours after a TIA, so evaluation is an emergency."]],
  red: "Any sudden face drooping, arm weakness, speech trouble or vision loss needs emergency care (call 911), even if it goes away, because it can be a warning sign of a major stroke in the coming hours to days.",
  fun: "C. Miller Fisher was a Canadian navy surgeon who spent time in a prisoner of war camp during World War II before becoming one of the most influential stroke neurologists of the century.",
  src: ["AHA/ASA Scientific Statement: Definition and Evaluation of TIA (2009)", "AHA/ASA Guideline for Prevention of Stroke in Patients with Stroke and TIA (2021)", "Johnston SC et al. ABCD2 score. Lancet 2007", "Johnston SC et al. POINT. N Engl J Med 2018", "NASCET Collaborators. N Engl J Med 1991"]
},
"epilepsy": {
  name: "Epilepsy and seizures",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["seizure disorder", "convulsions", "focal seizures", "generalized tonic-clonic seizure", "absence seizures", "grand mal", "petit mal"],
  what: "A seizure is a burst of abnormal, synchronized electrical activity in the brain that changes movement, feeling, awareness or behavior. Epilepsy is a brain condition with a lasting tendency to have repeated seizures that are not caused by a temporary trigger.",
  origin: "Ancient Babylonian tablets described seizures in detail, often blaming spirits. Around 400 BC the Hippocratic text On the Sacred Disease argued that epilepsy came from the brain, not the gods. In 1857 Charles Locock reported that potassium bromide reduced seizures, the first effective drug. In the 1870s English neurologist John Hughlings Jackson defined a seizure as a sudden, excessive discharge of brain gray matter and showed that focal seizures can march along the body as the discharge spreads. Phenobarbital was introduced for epilepsy in 1912, and in 1938 H. Houston Merritt and Tracy Putnam found phenytoin, which controlled seizures without heavy sedation. In 1929 Hans Berger published human EEG recordings, which allowed seizures to be seen as electrical events. Epilepsy surgery was refined by Wilder Penfield in Montreal from the 1930s. Many newer drugs followed from the 1990s on, and the International League Against Epilepsy updated its definition in 2014 and its seizure classification in 2017.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Babylonian and Hippocratic writings describe epilepsy, and On the Sacred Disease places its cause in the brain."], ["1857", "Charles Locock reports bromide for seizures."], ["1912", "Phenobarbital is introduced as an antiseizure drug."], ["1929", "Hans Berger publishes the first human EEG recordings."], ["1938", "Merritt and Putnam discover the antiseizure effect of phenytoin."], ["2017", "The ILAE publishes an updated seizure classification."]],
  cell: "Seizures arise when excitation outweighs inhibition in networks of neurons. Glutamate acting on AMPA and NMDA receptors excites neurons, while GABA acting on GABA-A chloride channels inhibits them. In an epileptic focus, groups of neurons show a paroxysmal depolarization shift: a large, long depolarization driven by glutamate receptors and voltage-gated calcium channels that triggers bursts of sodium-channel action potentials. Recurrent excitatory connections synchronize these bursts, and when surrounding inhibition fails, the activity spreads. Causes include genetic channel defects (for example SCN1A in Dravet syndrome), structural lesions (hippocampal sclerosis, cortical malformations, tumors, stroke, trauma), infections, autoimmune encephalitis and metabolic problems. In temporal lobe epilepsy, hippocampal sclerosis shows neuron loss and gliosis, and surviving circuits rewire. Absence seizures come from thalamocortical circuits, where T-type calcium channels in thalamic neurons drive the rhythmic three-per-second spike-and-wave pattern. Seizures end through GABA release, potassium currents, adenosine and exhaustion of the neurons, followed by a postictal period.",
  body: [["Brain", "Focal seizures affect one region and cause symptoms that match it, such as jerking of one hand or a rising feeling in the stomach. Generalized seizures involve both hemispheres from the start. Repeated poorly controlled seizures can harm memory and mood."], ["Muscles", "Tonic-clonic seizures cause stiffening then rhythmic jerking, which can lead to injury, muscle breakdown and lactic acidosis."], ["Heart and breathing", "Seizures can disturb breathing and heart rhythm. Sudden unexpected death in epilepsy (SUDEP) is rare but linked to frequent uncontrolled tonic-clonic seizures."], ["Mouth and bladder", "Tongue biting, often on the side, and loss of bladder control are common in generalized tonic-clonic seizures."], ["Mood and daily life", "Depression and anxiety are common, and driving limits and stigma affect work and quality of life."], ["Pregnancy", "Some antiseizure medications, especially valproate, raise the risk of birth defects, so medication planning matters."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms depend on where the discharge starts and spreads. Motor cortex seizures cause jerking or stiffening on the opposite side. Temporal lobe seizures cause an aura such as deja vu, fear or a rising stomach feeling, then staring, lip smacking and impaired awareness. Absence seizures cause brief staring spells in children with immediate recovery. Generalized tonic-clonic seizures cause loss of consciousness, stiffening and jerking because both hemispheres are involved. Afterward, confusion and sleepiness (the postictal state) reflect exhausted neurons, and temporary weakness (Todd paralysis) can follow focal seizures.",
  dx: "Diagnosis starts with a detailed description from the person and witnesses, since many events, such as fainting, can look like seizures. Labs check glucose, sodium, calcium and toxins for provoked causes. EEG may show epileptiform discharges that support the diagnosis and the seizure type, and video EEG records events. MRI with an epilepsy protocol looks for structural causes. By the ILAE definition, epilepsy is present after two unprovoked seizures more than 24 hours apart, one unprovoked seizure with a high chance of more, or an epilepsy syndrome. Psychogenic nonepileptic events are identified with video EEG.",
  tx: "Antiseizure medications are chosen by seizure type, age, other conditions and pregnancy plans. Sodium channel blockers (lamotrigine, carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, phenytoin, lacosamide) limit rapid repetitive firing. Levetiracetam and brivaracetam bind the synaptic vesicle protein SV2A to reduce neurotransmitter release. Valproate, topiramate and zonisamide have several mechanisms. Benzodiazepines and phenobarbital enhance GABA-A receptors. Ethosuximide blocks T-type calcium channels for absence seizures. Valproate is avoided in pregnancy when possible because of neural tube defects and lower child IQ, and carbamazepine is linked to severe skin reactions in people with HLA-B*15:02. About a third of people keep having seizures despite medicines; for them, epilepsy surgery, vagus nerve stimulation, responsive neurostimulation, deep brain stimulation and the ketogenic diet are options. Rescue medicines such as nasal midazolam or diazepam treat clusters. Seizure first aid means turning the person on their side, timing the seizure and never putting anything in the mouth.",
  drugs: ["levetiracetam", "lamotrigine", "valproic-acid", "carbamazepine", "oxcarbazepine", "eslicarbazepine", "phenytoin", "lacosamide", "topiramate", "zonisamide", "ethosuximide", "brivaracetam", "cenobamate", "perampanel", "clobazam", "phenobarbital", "gabapentin", "fenfluramine", "stiripentol", "ganaxolone", "midazolam-nasal", "diazepam-nasal", "lorazepam", "diazepam"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Ethosuximide for absence: think ABSENCE of T (T-type calcium channels blocked)."], ["Most-tested fact", "Absence seizures show three-per-second spike-and-wave on EEG and are treated with ethosuximide or valproate."], ["Pregnancy", "Valproate carries the highest risk of neural tube defects. Lamotrigine and levetiracetam are often preferred in pregnancy."], ["Seizure vs syncope", "Seizures cause postictal confusion and lateral tongue biting. Syncope has quick recovery and often a trigger such as standing or pain."]],
  red: "A seizure lasting more than five minutes, repeated seizures without waking up between them, a first seizure, a seizure in water, trouble breathing afterward, or a seizure after a head injury or in pregnancy needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Hughlings Jackson's idea of the Jacksonian march came partly from watching his wife's seizures, which spread along her body in a fixed order.",
  src: ["ILAE Operational Classification of Seizure Types (2017)", "Fisher RS et al. ILAE Practical Clinical Definition of Epilepsy. Epilepsia 2014", "AAN/AES Practice Guideline Update on Antiepileptic Drugs (2018)", "Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology", "Merritt HH, Putnam TJ. JAMA 1938"]
},
"status-epilepticus": {
  name: "Status epilepticus",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["SE", "convulsive status epilepticus", "nonconvulsive status epilepticus", "refractory status epilepticus", "prolonged seizure"],
  what: "Status epilepticus is a seizure that lasts too long or seizures that repeat without the person recovering in between. A convulsive seizure lasting more than five minutes is treated as status epilepticus. It is a medical emergency that can cause brain injury and death.",
  origin: "Ancient Babylonian texts already described seizures that followed one another without recovery. By the 1800s physicians at the Salpetriere hospital in Paris used the term etat de mal for it, and Desire-Magloire Bourneville described it in detail in the 1870s. For decades, treatment relied on bromides, barbiturates and paraldehyde. In 1965 Henri Gastaut and colleagues reported that IV diazepam could stop status epilepticus, starting the benzodiazepine era. The 1998 Veterans Affairs trial showed lorazepam worked better than phenytoin alone as a first treatment. In 2001 a San Francisco trial showed that paramedics giving benzodiazepines before hospital arrival stopped more seizures, and in 2012 the RAMPART trial showed IM midazolam by autoinjector was at least as effective as IV lorazepam in the field. In 2015 the ILAE defined time points for treatment, and in 2019 the ESETT trial found fosphenytoin, valproate and levetiracetam about equally effective as second-line drugs.",
  timeline: [["1965", "Gastaut and colleagues show IV diazepam stops status epilepticus."], ["1998", "A VA cooperative trial shows lorazepam is a strong first treatment."], ["2001", "A trial shows prehospital benzodiazepines by paramedics stop more seizures."], ["2012", "The RAMPART trial shows IM midazolam is as effective as IV lorazepam prehospital."], ["2015", "The ILAE defines status epilepticus with time points for treatment."], ["2019", "The ESETT trial finds three second-line drugs about equally effective."]],
  cell: "Most seizures stop on their own within two minutes because inhibitory mechanisms take over. Status epilepticus happens when these stopping mechanisms fail or excitation is too strong. As a seizure goes on, synaptic GABA-A receptors containing beta-2, beta-3 and gamma-2 subunits are pulled into the cell by endocytosis, so fewer targets remain for GABA and for benzodiazepines. This is why benzodiazepines work best early and lose power with time. At the same time, more NMDA and AMPA receptors move to the synapse, increasing excitation. Ongoing glutamate release causes calcium overload, mitochondrial failure and excitotoxic death of neurons, especially in the hippocampus, thalamus and cortex. Early on the body compensates with a surge of catecholamines, high blood pressure and high glucose. After about 30 minutes, compensation fails, leading to low blood pressure, low oxygen, hyperthermia, lactic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis and low glucose. Common causes include low antiseizure medication levels, alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal, stroke, infection, metabolic problems, toxins and autoimmune encephalitis.",
  body: [["Brain", "Continuous seizures cause neuronal death, brain swelling and lasting problems with memory, thinking and new epilepsy. Damage risk rises after about 30 minutes."], ["Airway and lungs", "Breathing is ineffective during convulsions, causing low oxygen and high carbon dioxide. Aspiration and neurogenic pulmonary edema can occur."], ["Heart", "Catecholamine surges cause fast heart rates, arrhythmias and stress cardiomyopathy."], ["Muscles and kidneys", "Constant muscle contraction causes rhabdomyolysis, high potassium, lactic acidosis and acute kidney injury from myoglobin."], ["Temperature and metabolism", "Muscle activity causes hyperthermia, and glucose first rises then can fall as reserves are used up."]],
  symptoms: "Convulsive status epilepticus shows continuous or repeated stiffening and jerking without regaining consciousness, because the abnormal discharge keeps going. Nonconvulsive status epilepticus shows confusion, staring, subtle twitching of the face or eyes or coma without obvious jerking, and it is often missed without EEG. Fever, fast heart rate and high blood pressure reflect the stress response. Over time, visible jerking can fade even though the brain is still seizing (subtle status), which is why continuous EEG matters.",
  dx: "Status epilepticus is recognized clinically when a convulsive seizure lasts five minutes or longer, or seizures repeat without recovery. A fingerstick glucose is checked right away. Labs include electrolytes, calcium, magnesium, blood count, kidney and liver tests, toxicology and antiseizure medication levels. Continuous EEG detects nonconvulsive status, especially in people who do not wake up after convulsions stop. Brain CT or MRI looks for stroke, bleeding or tumor, and lumbar puncture is done when infection is suspected.",
  tx: "Treatment follows a timed pathway, as in the American Epilepsy Society guideline. In the first minutes, care focuses on airway, breathing, circulation, oxygen, monitoring and a glucose check, with thiamine before glucose in people at risk of deficiency. From about five minutes, a benzodiazepine is given: IM midazolam, IV lorazepam or IV diazepam, or intranasal or buccal midazolam or rectal diazepam when there is no IV. These enhance GABA-A receptor activity. If seizures continue, a second-line IV drug is given: fosphenytoin (sodium channel blocker), valproate or levetiracetam (SV2A binding), with lacosamide and phenobarbital as other options. Refractory status is treated with continuous infusions of midazolam, propofol, pentobarbital or ketamine (an NMDA antagonist), usually with intubation and continuous EEG. The cause is treated at the same time, such as pyridoxine for isoniazid toxicity, magnesium sulfate for eclampsia and hypertonic saline for severe low sodium.",
  drugs: ["midazolam", "lorazepam", "diazepam", "midazolam-nasal", "diazepam-nasal", "fosphenytoin", "phenytoin", "levetiracetam", "valproic-acid", "lacosamide", "phenobarbital", "propofol", "ketamine", "thiamine", "dextrose", "pyridoxine", "magnesium-sulfate", "hypertonic-saline-3-percent", "rocuronium"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Benzodiazepines are first-line, and they work best when given early, because GABA-A receptors are pulled off the synapse as seizures continue."], ["Timeline", "Five minutes: treat as status. Twenty minutes: second-line drug. Forty minutes and beyond: refractory, anesthetic infusion."], ["EMS pearl", "IM midazolam is a fast, effective first treatment when IV access is not available (RAMPART)."], ["Special causes", "Isoniazid toxicity needs pyridoxine, eclampsia needs magnesium sulfate, hypoglycemia needs dextrose."]],
  red: "A seizure lasting more than five minutes, a second seizure without waking up in between, a seizure with trouble breathing, or a person who stays unresponsive after a seizure needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "In the RAMPART trial, paramedics stopped seizures more often with IM midazolam than with IV lorazepam, largely because the shot could be given faster than starting an IV in a seizing patient.",
  src: ["American Epilepsy Society Guideline: Treatment of Convulsive Status Epilepticus (2016)", "Trinka E et al. ILAE Definition and Classification of Status Epilepticus. Epilepsia 2015", "Silbergleit R et al. RAMPART. N Engl J Med 2012", "Kapur J et al. ESETT. N Engl J Med 2019", "Neurocritical Care Society Guideline for Status Epilepticus (2012)"]
},
"migraine": {
  name: "Migraine",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["migraine headache", "migraine with aura", "migraine without aura", "chronic migraine", "hemicrania"],
  what: "Migraine is a common brain disorder that causes repeated attacks of moderate to severe, often throbbing headache, usually with nausea and sensitivity to light and sound. Some people first have an aura, such as flashing or zigzag lights.",
  origin: "In the second century AD, Greek physician Aretaeus of Cappadocia described one-sided headaches with nausea. Galen used the term hemicrania, meaning half the head, which became migraine in French and English. In the 1600s Thomas Willis suggested that blood vessel changes caused migraine. In the 1930s and 1940s American neurologist Harold Wolff studied migraine in patients and promoted a vascular theory, supported by the effect of ergotamine, which had been isolated from ergot fungus in 1918 by Arthur Stoll. In 1944 Brazilian scientist Aristides Leao described cortical spreading depression, later linked to aura. In 1990 Lars Edvinsson and Peter Goadsby showed that the neuropeptide CGRP is released during migraine attacks, shifting the theory toward the trigeminal nerve and brain. Sumatriptan, the first triptan, reached the US in 1992. OnabotulinumtoxinA was approved for chronic migraine in 2010, and in 2018 erenumab became the first antibody designed to block CGRP signaling, followed by the gepant pills.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Aretaeus describes one-sided headache, and Galen names it hemicrania."], ["1918", "Arthur Stoll isolates ergotamine, an early migraine treatment."], ["1944", "Aristides Leao describes cortical spreading depression, later linked to aura."], ["1992", "Sumatriptan, the first triptan, is approved in the US."], ["2018", "Erenumab is approved as the first CGRP pathway antibody for migraine prevention."], ["2019", "Ubrogepant and lasmiditan are approved for acute migraine."]],
  cell: "Migraine is a neurovascular disorder of the brain with strong genetic influence; familial hemiplegic migraine is linked to CACNA1A, ATP1A2 and SCN1A variants that make neurons hyperexcitable. Attacks often begin in the hypothalamus and brainstem, which explains early symptoms like yawning, food cravings and fatigue. Aura is caused by cortical spreading depolarization, a slow wave of intense neuronal and glial depolarization followed by suppressed activity, moving across the cortex, usually starting in the visual cortex. Spreading depolarization and brainstem changes activate the trigeminovascular system. Trigeminal nerve fibers around meningeal blood vessels release CGRP, substance P and PACAP, which dilate vessels and cause neurogenic inflammation, sensitizing pain fibers (peripheral sensitization) and producing throbbing pain. Signals relay through the trigeminal nucleus caudalis to the thalamus and cortex, where neurons become sensitized (central sensitization), causing allodynia. Triptans act on 5-HT1B receptors on vessels and 5-HT1D receptors on trigeminal nerve endings to block CGRP release, and ditans act on 5-HT1F receptors without constricting vessels.",
  body: [["Brain and nerves", "Trigeminal pain pathways become sensitized, causing throbbing head pain that worsens with movement. Aura causes temporary visual, sensory or speech symptoms."], ["Eyes", "Visual aura includes zigzag lines, shimmering spots and blind areas that spread over 5 to 60 minutes. Light sensitivity is common."], ["Stomach and gut", "Nausea and vomiting come from brainstem activation, and gastric emptying slows during attacks, which can delay absorption of oral drugs."], ["Skin", "Central sensitization makes the scalp and skin tender, so brushing hair or wearing glasses can hurt (allodynia)."], ["Blood vessels", "Migraine with aura is linked to a modestly higher risk of ischemic stroke, especially with smoking and estrogen-containing contraceptives."], ["Daily life", "Migraine is a leading cause of disability worldwide, especially in young and middle-aged women."]],
  symptoms: "A prodrome of yawning, mood change, food cravings or neck stiffness can come hours before pain because the hypothalamus is involved early. Aura reflects the spreading depolarization wave moving across the cortex. The headache is often one-sided and pulsating and worsens with activity, because sensitized trigeminal fibers fire with each pulse and movement. Nausea, vomiting and sensitivity to light, sound and smell come from activated brainstem and thalamic pathways. A postdrome of tiredness and fogginess can follow the attack.",
  dx: "Migraine is diagnosed clinically using International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-3) criteria. Migraine without aura needs at least five attacks lasting 4 to 72 hours with at least two of these: one-sided location, pulsating quality, moderate or severe intensity, or worsening with routine activity, plus nausea or vomiting, or both light and sound sensitivity. A normal neurologic exam supports the diagnosis. Imaging is not needed for typical migraine but is used when red flags are present, such as a sudden thunderclap onset, new headache after age 50, neurologic deficits, fever or a change in pattern. Chronic migraine means headache on 15 or more days a month.",
  tx: "Acute treatment works best early in an attack. NSAIDs such as ibuprofen and naproxen block prostaglandin production, and acetaminophen helps milder attacks. Triptans (sumatriptan, rizatriptan and others) are 5-HT1B/1D agonists that block CGRP release and constrict cranial vessels; they are avoided in coronary or other vascular disease. Gepants (ubrogepant, rimegepant, zavegepant) block CGRP receptors without constricting vessels. Lasmiditan, a 5-HT1F agonist, also lacks vessel effects but causes drowsiness and driving limits. Dihydroergotamine treats longer attacks. In emergency departments, metoclopramide or prochlorperazine (dopamine antagonists) treat pain and nausea, often with ketorolac. Opioids are avoided. Prevention for frequent attacks includes propranolol, topiramate, amitriptyline, candesartan, CGRP antibodies (erenumab blocks the receptor; fremanezumab, galcanezumab and eptinezumab bind CGRP), atogepant or rimegepant, and onabotulinumtoxinA for chronic migraine. Regular sleep and meals and limiting acute drugs to avoid medication overuse headache also matter.",
  drugs: ["sumatriptan", "rizatriptan", "zolmitriptan", "eletriptan", "ubrogepant", "rimegepant", "zavegepant", "lasmiditan", "dihydroergotamine", "ibuprofen", "naproxen", "acetaminophen", "ketorolac", "metoclopramide", "prochlorperazine", "propranolol", "topiramate", "amitriptyline", "candesartan", "erenumab", "fremanezumab", "galcanezumab", "eptinezumab", "atogepant"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "POUND: Pulsating, hOurs (4 to 72), Unilateral, Nausea, Disabling."], ["Most-tested fact", "Triptans are 5-HT1B/1D agonists and are contraindicated in coronary artery disease and other vascular disease."], ["Aura", "Aura comes from cortical spreading depolarization and typically lasts 5 to 60 minutes before or with the headache."], ["Often confused with", "Tension-type headache (bilateral, pressing, no nausea) and cluster headache (short, severe, around one eye with tearing, in men more often)."], ["Medication overuse", "Using acute headache drugs too often can cause daily rebound headaches."]],
  red: "A sudden thunderclap headache, the worst headache of a person's life, headache with fever and stiff neck, new weakness, confusion, vision loss or trouble speaking, or a new headache after a head injury needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Some scholars think Lewis Carroll, who had migraine with aura, may have drawn on aura experiences for the size changes in Alice in Wonderland, and Alice in Wonderland syndrome is named for this kind of distorted perception.",
  src: ["International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd edition (ICHD-3, 2018)", "American Headache Society Consensus Statement on Integrating New Migraine Treatments (2021)", "AHS Guideline: Acute Treatment of Migraine in the Emergency Department (2016)", "Goadsby PJ et al. Pathophysiology of Migraine. Physiol Rev 2017", "Edvinsson L, Goadsby PJ. Ann Neurol 1990"]
},
"parkinsons-disease": {
  name: "Parkinson's disease",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["Parkinson disease", "PD", "paralysis agitans", "shaking palsy", "parkinsonism"],
  what: "Parkinson's disease is a slowly progressive brain disease in which nerve cells that make dopamine die. It causes slowness of movement, stiffness, a resting tremor and balance problems, plus non-movement symptoms like loss of smell, constipation and sleep changes.",
  origin: "In 1817 London surgeon James Parkinson published An Essay on the Shaking Palsy, describing six people with resting tremor, a stooped posture and shuffling walk. Decades later French neurologist Jean-Martin Charcot added rigidity to the description and named it after Parkinson. In 1912 Friedrich Lewy described protein inclusions in the brain, now called Lewy bodies, and in 1919 Konstantin Tretiakoff showed loss of neurons in the substantia nigra. In 1957 and 1958 Swedish scientist Arvid Carlsson showed dopamine is a neurotransmitter and that L-DOPA reversed movement loss in animals, work that earned a Nobel Prize in 2000. In 1960 Oleh Hornykiewicz found dopamine was very low in the brains of people with Parkinson's. In 1967 George Cotzias showed that oral levodopa dramatically helped patients, and carbidopa-levodopa reached the US market in the 1970s. In 1982 contaminated street drugs containing MPTP caused sudden parkinsonism in young people, revealing a toxic model. In 1997 SNCA mutations were found and alpha-synuclein was shown to make up Lewy bodies. Deep brain stimulation was approved for Parkinson's in 2002.",
  timeline: [["1817", "James Parkinson publishes An Essay on the Shaking Palsy."], ["1912", "Friedrich Lewy describes the inclusions later called Lewy bodies."], ["1960", "Oleh Hornykiewicz finds low dopamine in the brains of people with Parkinson's."], ["1967", "George Cotzias shows oral levodopa dramatically improves symptoms."], ["1997", "Alpha-synuclein is linked to inherited Parkinson's and found in Lewy bodies."], ["2002", "Deep brain stimulation is FDA approved for Parkinson's disease."]],
  cell: "Parkinson's disease is defined by loss of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta and by Lewy bodies, round inclusions of misfolded alpha-synuclein. Misfolded alpha-synuclein aggregates and seems to spread from cell to cell, and the Braak hypothesis proposes that pathology starts in the olfactory bulb and the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus before reaching the midbrain and cortex. Mitochondrial complex I dysfunction, oxidative stress from dopamine metabolism, and failure of lysosomes and autophagy also contribute. Genetic forms involve SNCA, LRRK2, GBA1, PRKN (parkin) and PINK1, and the MPTP toxin destroys these neurons by blocking complex I. By the time symptoms appear, a large fraction of nigral neurons are already lost. In the basal ganglia, dopamine normally excites the direct pathway through D1 receptors and inhibits the indirect pathway through D2 receptors, both of which favor movement. Without dopamine, the internal globus pallidus over-inhibits the thalamus, so movement becomes slow and small. A relative excess of striatal acetylcholine contributes to tremor, explaining why anticholinergics can help.",
  body: [["Basal ganglia and movement", "Dopamine loss causes bradykinesia, rigidity and resting tremor, and later postural instability and freezing of gait. Small handwriting (micrographia) and a soft voice reflect small, slow movements."], ["Gut", "Lewy pathology in the enteric nerves and vagus causes constipation, often years before movement symptoms, and slowed stomach emptying."], ["Smell and sleep", "Loss of smell and REM sleep behavior disorder, acting out dreams, often precede diagnosis."], ["Autonomic system", "Orthostatic hypotension, bladder urgency, sweating changes and erectile dysfunction come from involvement of autonomic neurons."], ["Mood and thinking", "Depression, anxiety and apathy are common. Many people develop dementia over many years as Lewy pathology reaches the cortex. Hallucinations can occur, sometimes from medicines."], ["Swallowing and speech", "Weak, slow throat muscles cause soft speech, drooling and dysphagia, raising the risk of aspiration pneumonia."]],
  symptoms: "Resting tremor, often a pill-rolling motion of the hand that fades with action, comes from abnormal rhythmic activity in basal ganglia circuits. Rigidity, felt as cogwheel stiffness, comes from increased tone in opposing muscle groups. Bradykinesia, the core sign, shows as slow, shrinking movements, a masked face and reduced blinking, because the thalamus is overly inhibited. Shuffling gait, reduced arm swing and later falls come from impaired automatic movement and balance reflexes. Symptoms usually start on one side and stay worse on that side.",
  dx: "Parkinson's disease is diagnosed clinically using Movement Disorder Society criteria: bradykinesia plus resting tremor, rigidity or both, with supporting features such as a clear response to levodopa and an absence of red flags for other conditions. A DaTscan, a SPECT scan of dopamine transporters, shows reduced uptake in the striatum and helps distinguish Parkinson's from essential tremor or drug-induced parkinsonism, though not from other degenerative parkinsonisms. MRI helps exclude stroke, hydrocephalus or other causes. Alpha-synuclein seed amplification assays on spinal fluid or skin are newer tests.",
  tx: "Levodopa, the most effective drug, crosses the blood-brain barrier and becomes dopamine; carbidopa blocks its breakdown outside the brain, so more reaches the brain with less nausea. Over time wearing off and dyskinesias develop. Dopamine agonists (pramipexole, ropinirole, rotigotine) stimulate dopamine receptors but can cause sleepiness and impulse control disorders. MAO-B inhibitors (selegiline, rasagiline, safinamide) and COMT inhibitors (entacapone, opicapone, tolcapone) slow dopamine or levodopa breakdown. Amantadine reduces dyskinesias. Trihexyphenidyl, an anticholinergic, helps tremor in younger people. Istradefylline (adenosine A2A blocker), apomorphine for sudden off episodes and subcutaneous foscarbidopa-foslevodopa infusion help fluctuations. Pimavanserin treats Parkinson's psychosis, droxidopa treats neurogenic orthostatic hypotension, and rivastigmine treats Parkinson's dementia. Deep brain stimulation helps selected people. Exercise and physical, occupational and speech therapy are key. Dopamine-blocking drugs are avoided.",
  drugs: ["carbidopa-levodopa", "carbidopa-levodopa-entacapone", "foscarbidopa-foslevodopa", "pramipexole", "ropinirole", "rotigotine", "rasagiline", "selegiline", "safinamide", "entacapone", "opicapone", "tolcapone", "amantadine", "istradefylline", "apomorphine", "trihexyphenidyl", "benztropine", "pimavanserin", "droxidopa", "rivastigmine", "midodrine", "fludrocortisone"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "TRAP: Tremor (at rest), Rigidity (cogwheel), Akinesia or bradykinesia, Postural instability."], ["Most-tested fact", "Parkinson's is loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta with Lewy bodies made of alpha-synuclein."], ["Drug pearl", "Carbidopa does not cross the blood-brain barrier; it blocks levodopa breakdown outside the brain."], ["Often confused with", "Essential tremor, which is an action tremor that improves with alcohol and often runs in families, with no bradykinesia."], ["Drugs to avoid", "Dopamine blockers like haloperidol, metoclopramide and prochlorperazine can worsen Parkinson's."]],
  red: "Sudden confusion, high fever with severe rigidity (which can happen if levodopa is stopped suddenly), a fall with head injury, choking, or new trouble breathing in someone with Parkinson's needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Some people with Parkinson's can ride a bicycle or climb stairs smoothly even when they freeze on flat ground, because visual and rhythmic cues use different motor pathways.",
  src: ["Postuma RB et al. MDS Clinical Diagnostic Criteria for Parkinson's Disease. Mov Disord 2015", "AAN Practice Guideline: Dopaminergic Therapy for Motor Symptoms in Early Parkinson Disease (2021)", "Parkinson J. An Essay on the Shaking Palsy (1817)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Adams and Victor's Principles of Neurology"]
},
"alzheimers-disease": {
  name: "Alzheimer's disease and dementia",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["Alzheimer disease", "AD", "dementia", "major neurocognitive disorder", "senile dementia", "mild cognitive impairment (MCI)"],
  what: "Dementia is a decline in memory, thinking and daily abilities severe enough to interfere with independent life. Alzheimer's disease is its most common cause: abnormal proteins build up in the brain, nerve cells die, and memory and thinking slowly worsen over years.",
  origin: "In 1901 German psychiatrist Alois Alzheimer began caring for Auguste Deter, a woman in her early fifties with memory loss, confusion and paranoia. After her death, he examined her brain and in 1906 described plaques and tangled fibers inside nerve cells. In 1910 his colleague Emil Kraepelin named the disease after him. For decades it was thought to be a rare early-onset condition, while dementia in older people was called senility. In the 1970s researchers recognized that most late-life dementia was the same disease and found a loss of acetylcholine-making neurons. In 1984 George Glenner and Caine Wong isolated beta-amyloid from plaques, and in 1986 tau was identified as the main part of tangles. Genetic studies found APP mutations in 1991 and the APOE e4 risk gene in 1993. Cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil (1996) and memantine (2003) offered symptom relief. In 2023 lecanemab and in 2024 donanemab became the first drugs approved that clear amyloid and modestly slow decline in early disease.",
  timeline: [["1906", "Alois Alzheimer describes plaques and tangles in Auguste Deter's brain."], ["1984", "Glenner and Wong isolate beta-amyloid from Alzheimer's plaques."], ["1993", "APOE e4 is identified as a major genetic risk factor."], ["1996", "Donepezil is approved for Alzheimer's disease."], ["2003", "Memantine is approved for moderate to severe Alzheimer's disease."], ["2023", "Lecanemab receives full FDA approval as an anti-amyloid therapy for early Alzheimer's."]],
  cell: "Amyloid precursor protein (APP) is a membrane protein. When beta-secretase (BACE1) and then gamma-secretase (whose core is presenilin) cut it, they release beta-amyloid peptides, especially the sticky 42-amino-acid form. Beta-amyloid clumps into soluble oligomers and protofibrils, which harm synapses, and then into extracellular plaques. This amyloid buildup begins up to two decades before symptoms and drives changes in tau, a protein that normally stabilizes microtubules in axons. Hyperphosphorylated tau detaches, misfolds into paired helical filaments and forms neurofibrillary tangles inside neurons. Tau spreads in a predictable pattern from the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus to the neocortex, and tangle burden matches symptoms better than plaques do. Microglia, influenced by genes such as TREM2, react with inflammation. Synapses and neurons are lost, especially cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert, lowering acetylcholine. Early-onset familial disease comes from APP, PSEN1 or PSEN2 mutations, and people with Down syndrome have an extra APP copy on chromosome 21. In late-onset disease, APOE e4 raises risk and APOE e2 lowers it. Other dementias include vascular, Lewy body and frontotemporal dementia.",
  body: [["Hippocampus and memory", "Early tau and neuron loss in the hippocampus and entorhinal cortex impair forming new memories, so recent events are forgotten while old memories remain."], ["Cerebral cortex", "As disease spreads, language, judgment, planning, visual-spatial skills and recognition of people and objects decline. Brain scans show shrinking of the cortex and enlarged ventricles."], ["Behavior and mood", "Depression, apathy, anxiety, agitation, wandering, sleep changes and sometimes delusions or hallucinations develop."], ["Brain blood vessels", "Amyloid in vessel walls (cerebral amyloid angiopathy) can cause small bleeds, which also raises the risk of side effects with anti-amyloid drugs."], ["Whole body", "In late stages, people lose the ability to walk, speak and swallow, leading to weight loss, infections and aspiration pneumonia, the most common cause of death."]],
  symptoms: "Short-term memory loss, such as repeating questions or misplacing items, appears first because the hippocampus is affected early. Word-finding trouble, getting lost in familiar places and difficulty managing money or medicines come later as the cortex is affected. Personality and behavior changes reflect damage to frontal and limbic areas. Unlike delirium, symptoms develop slowly over months to years and attention is preserved early. People may not recognize their own memory problems.",
  dx: "Diagnosis starts with a history from the person and someone who knows them well, plus cognitive tests such as the MoCA or MMSE. Blood tests look for treatable contributors such as low vitamin B12, thyroid disease and other conditions. Brain MRI or CT excludes strokes, tumors and hydrocephalus and may show hippocampal shrinking. Biomarkers confirm Alzheimer's biology: amyloid PET, spinal fluid showing low beta-amyloid 42 and high phosphorylated tau, and newer blood tests of phosphorylated tau. Dementia means the decline interferes with independence, while mild cognitive impairment means changes without that loss. Confirmed amyloid is required before anti-amyloid therapy.",
  tx: "No treatment cures Alzheimer's. Cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine) slow the breakdown of acetylcholine, giving a modest boost in thinking and daily function; side effects include nausea and slow heart rate. Memantine, an NMDA receptor antagonist, dampens excess glutamate signaling and is used in moderate to severe disease. Anti-amyloid antibodies, lecanemab (binds protofibrils) and donanemab (binds a modified form of beta-amyloid in plaques), remove amyloid and modestly slow decline in mild cognitive impairment or mild dementia due to Alzheimer's. They require MRI monitoring for amyloid-related imaging abnormalities (ARIA), brain swelling or small bleeds, which are more common in people with two APOE e4 copies. Brexpiprazole is approved for agitation in Alzheimer's dementia, with a boxed warning about higher death rates with antipsychotics in older adults with dementia. Suvorexant treats insomnia. Non-drug care is central: routines, safety at home, hearing and vision correction, exercise, managing blood pressure and diabetes, and support for caregivers.",
  drugs: ["donepezil", "rivastigmine", "galantamine", "memantine", "lecanemab", "donanemab", "brexpiprazole", "suvorexant"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Plaques are extracellular beta-amyloid. Tangles are intracellular hyperphosphorylated tau. Tangles correlate better with cognitive decline."], ["Genetics", "APP (chromosome 21), PSEN1 and PSEN2 cause early-onset familial disease. APOE e4 raises late-onset risk, and APOE e2 is protective."], ["Down syndrome link", "An extra chromosome 21 means an extra APP gene, so people with Down syndrome often develop Alzheimer's earlier."], ["Often confused with", "Delirium (sudden onset, fluctuating attention, often reversible) and depression, which can mimic dementia (pseudodementia)."], ["Reversible causes", "Low B12, hypothyroidism, normal pressure hydrocephalus and medication side effects can cause or worsen memory problems."]],
  red: "Sudden confusion or a sudden change in thinking or behavior can mean delirium, stroke or infection and needs urgent medical care, and sudden weakness, trouble speaking or unresponsiveness needs emergency care (call 911). Caregivers in crisis or anyone with thoughts of self-harm can reach the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline.",
  fun: "Alois Alzheimer's original slides of Auguste Deter's brain were rediscovered in Munich in the 1990s, and re-examination confirmed the plaques and tangles he had described.",
  src: ["NIA-AA Revised Criteria for Diagnosis and Staging of Alzheimer's Disease (2024)", "Alzheimer's Association Facts and Figures", "van Dyck CH et al. Lecanemab in Early Alzheimer's Disease. N Engl J Med 2023", "Sims JR et al. Donanemab. JAMA 2023", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"multiple-sclerosis": {
  name: "Multiple sclerosis",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["MS", "relapsing-remitting MS", "RRMS", "primary progressive MS", "PPMS", "secondary progressive MS", "demyelinating disease"],
  what: "Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune disease in which the immune system attacks the myelin coating around nerve fibers in the brain, spinal cord and optic nerves. Signals slow or stop, causing vision problems, numbness, weakness and fatigue that can come and go or slowly worsen.",
  origin: "One of the earliest well-documented cases is Augustus d'Este, a grandson of King George III, whose diaries from 1822 to 1846 describe vision loss, weakness and numbness that came and went. In the 1830s and 1840s pathologists Robert Carswell and Jean Cruveilhier drew the hardened patches in the spinal cord. In 1868 Jean-Martin Charcot described the disease in detail, named it sclerose en plaques and noted features such as nystagmus, intention tremor and scanning speech. In 1916 James Dawson described the inflammation around veins in MS lesions. MRI in the 1980s transformed diagnosis, and the McDonald criteria, first published in 2001 and revised several times since, use MRI to allow earlier diagnosis. In 1993 interferon beta-1b became the first disease-modifying therapy. Natalizumab (2004), fingolimod, the first pill (2010), and ocrelizumab, the first approved for primary progressive MS (2017), followed. In 2022 a study of millions of US military members showed that Epstein-Barr virus infection sharply raises the risk of MS.",
  timeline: [["1868", "Jean-Martin Charcot describes and names multiple sclerosis."], ["1980s", "MRI begins to show MS lesions in the brain and spinal cord."], ["1993", "Interferon beta-1b becomes the first approved disease-modifying therapy."], ["2001", "The McDonald diagnostic criteria are first published."], ["2017", "Ocrelizumab becomes the first drug approved for primary progressive MS."], ["2022", "A large military cohort study links Epstein-Barr virus infection to MS risk."]],
  cell: "MS begins when autoreactive T and B cells that recognize myelin antigens are activated outside the brain, possibly through molecular mimicry with Epstein-Barr virus proteins, in people with genetic risk such as HLA-DRB1*15:01. Risk also rises with low vitamin D, smoking and adolescent obesity. Activated CD4 Th1 and Th17 cells express alpha-4 beta-1 integrin (VLA-4), which binds VCAM-1 on brain vessel walls and lets them cross the blood-brain barrier. Inside, they release interferon-gamma and IL-17 and recruit macrophages and microglia that strip myelin and damage oligodendrocytes. B cells present antigen, make antibodies and organize inflammation; their importance is shown by the strong effect of anti-CD20 drugs. CD8 T cells directly injure axons. Demyelinated axons lose saltatory conduction, so signals slow, fail or misfire, and heat worsens this (Uhthoff phenomenon). Lesions form around venules in the periventricular white matter, corpus callosum, brainstem, cerebellum, spinal cord and optic nerves. Remyelination is often incomplete. Over time, axonal loss and smoldering inflammation behind an intact blood-brain barrier drive progressive disability.",
  body: [["Optic nerves", "Optic neuritis causes painful vision loss in one eye, worse with eye movement, often with a relative afferent pupillary defect and loss of color vision."], ["Brainstem", "Lesions cause double vision, internuclear ophthalmoplegia from damage to the medial longitudinal fasciculus, vertigo, facial numbness and trigeminal neuralgia."], ["Spinal cord", "Lesions cause numbness, weakness, spasticity and Lhermitte sign, an electric shock down the spine when bending the neck."], ["Cerebellum", "Lesions cause imbalance, intention tremor, nystagmus and scanning speech."], ["Bladder, bowel and sexual function", "Spinal cord involvement causes urinary urgency, retention, constipation and sexual dysfunction."], ["Brain and mood", "Fatigue, slowed thinking, depression and anxiety are common and often among the most disabling symptoms."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms depend on where lesions form and come from blocked or slowed nerve conduction. A relapse develops over days, lasts at least 24 hours and then partly or fully recovers as inflammation settles and some remyelination occurs. Vision loss, double vision, numbness, tingling, weakness and imbalance are common first symptoms. Fatigue is common and may reflect the extra effort of damaged circuits. Symptoms often flare temporarily with heat or fever because warm demyelinated axons conduct even less well.",
  dx: "MS is diagnosed with the McDonald criteria, which require evidence of lesions disseminated in space (in different parts of the central nervous system) and in time (occurring at different times). MRI shows typical lesions in periventricular, juxtacortical or cortical, infratentorial and spinal cord areas, and gadolinium-enhancing lesions show active inflammation. Spinal fluid oligoclonal bands, not found in the blood, show antibody production in the CNS and can substitute for dissemination in time. Tests exclude mimics such as neuromyelitis optica (AQP4 antibodies), MOG antibody disease, vitamin B12 deficiency, infections and other inflammatory diseases.",
  tx: "Relapses are treated with high-dose corticosteroids such as IV methylprednisolone, which shorten the attack but do not change long-term course; plasma exchange is used for severe relapses that do not respond. Disease-modifying therapies lower relapses and new MRI lesions. Interferon beta and glatiramer acetate modulate immune activity. Teriflunomide blocks pyrimidine synthesis in dividing lymphocytes. Dimethyl fumarate activates the Nrf2 antioxidant pathway. S1P receptor modulators (fingolimod, siponimod, ozanimod, ponesimod) trap lymphocytes in lymph nodes. Cladribine depletes lymphocytes. High-efficacy infusions or injections include natalizumab, which blocks alpha-4 integrin so cells cannot enter the brain (with a risk of PML linked to JC virus), anti-CD20 antibodies that deplete B cells (ocrelizumab, ofatumumab, ublituximab), and alemtuzumab, which targets CD52. Ocrelizumab is approved for primary progressive MS. Dalfampridine, a potassium channel blocker, improves walking, and baclofen and tizanidine ease spasticity. Rehabilitation, exercise and not smoking are part of care.",
  drugs: ["ocrelizumab", "ofatumumab", "ublituximab", "natalizumab", "interferon-beta-1a", "glatiramer", "dimethyl-fumarate", "teriflunomide", "fingolimod", "siponimod", "ozanimod", "ponesimod", "cladribine-oral", "alemtuzumab", "methylprednisolone", "corticotropin", "dalfampridine", "baclofen", "tizanidine", "oxybutynin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Charcot triad: SIN, Scanning speech, Intention tremor, Nystagmus."], ["Most-tested fact", "MS lesions are in white matter of the CNS, often periventricular (Dawson fingers), and CSF shows oligoclonal bands."], ["Classic patient", "A woman in her 20s to 40s with painful vision loss in one eye or an internuclear ophthalmoplegia, especially when it appears on both sides."], ["Drug pearl", "Natalizumab carries a risk of PML from JC virus. Fingolimod can slow the heart with the first dose."], ["Often confused with", "Neuromyelitis optica, an AQP4 antibody disease with severe optic neuritis and long spinal cord lesions."]],
  red: "Sudden loss of vision, sudden severe weakness, trouble breathing or swallowing, or new confusion needs emergency care (call 911). Depression is common in MS, and anyone with thoughts of suicide can call or text the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline.",
  fun: "MS is more common farther from the equator, and people who move from a high-risk to a low-risk area in childhood seem to take on the risk of their new home.",
  src: ["Thompson AJ et al. Diagnosis of MS: 2017 Revisions of the McDonald Criteria. Lancet Neurol 2018", "AAN Practice Guideline: Disease-Modifying Therapies for Adults with MS (2018)", "Bjornevik K et al. EBV and MS. Science 2022", "Charcot JM. Histologie de la sclerose en plaques (1868)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"myasthenia-gravis": {
  name: "Myasthenia gravis",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["MG", "ocular myasthenia", "generalized myasthenia gravis", "myasthenic crisis"],
  what: "Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease in which antibodies block or destroy the receptors that let nerves signal muscles. Muscles weaken with use and improve with rest, often starting with droopy eyelids and double vision.",
  origin: "In 1672 English physician Thomas Willis described a woman whose weakness worsened through the day and who sometimes could not speak, often cited as the first account. In the late 1800s Samuel Wilks, Wilhelm Erb and Samuel Goldflam described more cases, and in 1895 German neurologist Friedrich Jolly named it myasthenia gravis pseudoparalytica, meaning grave muscle weakness. In 1934 Mary Walker, a doctor at St Alfege's Hospital in London, noticed that the weakness resembled curare poisoning and tried physostigmine, a cholinesterase inhibitor, with dramatic results, later called the miracle at St Alfege's. In 1939 Alfred Blalock removed a thymus tumor from a woman with MG, who improved, and thymectomy became a treatment. In 1973 researchers showed that people with MG had fewer acetylcholine receptors and that immunizing rabbits with the receptor caused MG-like weakness, and in the following years antibodies against the receptor were found in patients. In 2016 the MGTX trial proved thymectomy helps. Since 2017 complement inhibitors and FcRn blockers have expanded targeted treatment.",
  timeline: [["1672", "Thomas Willis describes fatigable weakness."], ["1895", "Friedrich Jolly names myasthenia gravis."], ["1934", "Mary Walker shows physostigmine improves MG."], ["1973", "Studies show loss of acetylcholine receptors and an autoimmune cause."], ["2016", "The MGTX trial shows thymectomy improves outcomes."], ["2017", "Eculizumab becomes the first complement inhibitor approved for generalized MG."]],
  cell: "Myasthenia gravis is a type II hypersensitivity disease of the neuromuscular junction. In most people with generalized MG, IgG1 and IgG3 autoantibodies target the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (AChR) on the muscle end plate. They cause damage in three ways: they activate complement, forming membrane attack complexes that destroy the folded postsynaptic membrane; they cross-link receptors so they are pulled inside and broken down faster; and some block acetylcholine binding. Other patients have antibodies to MuSK (IgG4, which does not activate complement but disrupts receptor clustering) or LRP4. With fewer working receptors, the end plate potential is smaller. Normally there is a safety margin, but with repeated firing the amount of acetylcholine released per impulse falls, so the potential drops below threshold and muscle fibers stop contracting. This creates fatigable weakness. The thymus plays a central role: many patients have thymic hyperplasia with germinal centers, and about 10 to 15 percent have a thymoma, where faulty T cell education lets AChR-reactive cells escape tolerance. Maternal antibodies can cross the placenta and cause transient neonatal MG.",
  body: [["Eyes and eyelids", "Ptosis and double vision are the first symptoms in most people, because eye muscles fire rapidly and have a smaller safety margin. Pupils are spared."], ["Face and throat (bulbar muscles)", "Weakness causes trouble chewing, swallowing and speaking, a nasal voice and a flat smile, raising the risk of aspiration."], ["Limbs and neck", "Proximal arm and leg weakness and neck weakness worsen with use, such as climbing stairs or lifting the arms."], ["Breathing muscles", "Weakness of the diaphragm and chest muscles can cause myasthenic crisis, respiratory failure that needs a ventilator."], ["Thymus", "Thymic hyperplasia or thymoma is often present, and a thymoma can grow and spread like other tumors."], ["Other autoimmune conditions", "Autoimmune thyroid disease and other autoimmune diseases are more common in people with MG."]],
  symptoms: "Weakness gets worse with repeated use and later in the day, then improves with rest, because each nerve impulse releases less acetylcholine as activity continues and fewer receptors are left to respond. Droopy eyelids and double vision are often first. Trouble chewing, swallowing and speaking, and weakness of the arms, legs and neck can follow. Sensation, reflexes and pupils are normal because only the muscle side of the neuromuscular junction is affected. Infections, surgery, pregnancy and some medicines can trigger worsening.",
  dx: "Diagnosis combines typical fatigable weakness with tests. Blood tests find AChR antibodies in most people with generalized MG, with MuSK and LRP4 antibodies in some others. Repetitive nerve stimulation shows a decreasing muscle response with repeated stimulation. Single-fiber EMG shows increased jitter and is the most sensitive test. The ice pack test, in which cooling a droopy eyelid improves it because cold slows acetylcholinesterase, is a quick bedside clue. Chest CT or MRI looks for thymoma. In crisis, breathing strength is followed with vital capacity and negative inspiratory force.",
  tx: "Pyridostigmine, an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor, slows the breakdown of acetylcholine so more is available at the junction, improving strength. Muscarinic side effects such as cramping and diarrhea may be eased with glycopyrrolate. Corticosteroids such as prednisone suppress the immune attack, though they can briefly worsen weakness when started. Steroid-sparing agents such as azathioprine, mycophenolate and tacrolimus are used long-term. Thymectomy helps AChR antibody positive generalized MG and is required for thymoma. For myasthenic crisis or rapid worsening, IVIG and plasma exchange quickly lower harmful antibodies, with ventilator support as needed. Newer targeted drugs include complement C5 inhibitors (eculizumab, ravulizumab, zilucoplan) for AChR antibody positive MG, which require meningococcal vaccination, and FcRn blockers (efgartigimod, rozanolixizumab, nipocalimab) that speed IgG breakdown. Rituximab helps MuSK antibody positive MG. Drugs that worsen MG, such as aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, magnesium and some beta blockers, are avoided or used carefully.",
  drugs: ["pyridostigmine", "prednisone", "azathioprine", "mycophenolate", "tacrolimus", "intravenous-immune-globulin", "efgartigimod", "rozanolixizumab", "nipocalimab", "eculizumab", "ravulizumab", "zilucoplan", "rituximab", "glycopyrrolate", "neostigmine", "meningococcal-acwy-vaccine", "meningococcal-b-vaccine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "MG is a type II hypersensitivity against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors, causing weakness that worsens with use."], ["Often confused with", "Lambert-Eaton syndrome: antibodies to presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels, often with small cell lung cancer, and strength improves with repeated use."], ["Thymus link", "Look for thymoma or thymic hyperplasia on chest imaging; thymectomy can improve MG."], ["Crisis pearl", "In myasthenic crisis, breathing is tracked with vital capacity and negative inspiratory force, because oxygen levels drop late."], ["Drugs that worsen MG", "Aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, magnesium and beta blockers can trigger worsening."]],
  red: "Trouble breathing, a weak cough, trouble swallowing or handling saliva, or rapidly worsening weakness in someone with myasthenia gravis can mean myasthenic crisis and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Mary Walker made her famous discovery as a junior doctor by reasoning that if MG looked like curare poisoning, the curare antidote physostigmine might help, and it did.",
  src: ["International Consensus Guidance for Management of Myasthenia Gravis (2020 update)", "Wolfe GI et al. MGTX Thymectomy Trial. N Engl J Med 2016", "Walker MB. Lancet 1934", "Gilhus NE. Myasthenia gravis. N Engl J Med 2016", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
}
});

;
DB.DIS = DB.DIS || {};
Object.assign(DB.DIS, {
"guillain-barre": {
  name: "Guillain-Barre syndrome",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["GBS", "acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy", "AIDP", "Landry ascending paralysis", "Miller Fisher syndrome (variant)"],
  what: "Guillain-Barre syndrome is a sudden attack by the immune system on the nerves outside the brain and spinal cord. It causes weakness that usually starts in the legs and climbs upward, sometimes reaching the breathing muscles.",
  origin: "In 1859 French physician Jean Landry described a rapidly climbing paralysis that could kill by stopping breathing. In 1916, during World War I, Georges Guillain, Jean-Alexandre Barre and Andre Strohl examined two French soldiers with weakness and lost reflexes who recovered. Using the new lumbar puncture, they found high protein in the spinal fluid but normal cell counts, a pattern still called albuminocytologic dissociation. Strohl's name later dropped off the eponym. In the 1990s researchers linked many cases to a recent Campylobacter jejuni gut infection and showed that antibodies against the bacterium cross-react with nerve gangliosides. Treatment changed in 1985, when a North American trial showed plasma exchange speeds recovery, and in 1992, when a Dutch trial showed intravenous immune globulin works about as well.",
  timeline: [["1859", "Jean Landry describes acute ascending paralysis."], ["1916", "Guillain, Barre and Strohl report high spinal fluid protein with normal cell counts."], ["1956", "C. Miller Fisher describes the variant with eye muscle weakness, ataxia and lost reflexes."], ["1985", "A North American trial shows plasma exchange improves recovery."], ["1992", "A Dutch trial shows IV immune globulin is about as effective as plasma exchange."]],
  cell: "GBS is a post-infectious autoimmune disease of peripheral nerves. About two thirds of people had a respiratory or gut infection in the weeks before, most classically Campylobacter jejuni, and also CMV, Epstein-Barr virus, Zika and others. Through molecular mimicry, the immune system makes antibodies and T cells against microbial sugars that look like gangliosides on nerve membranes. In the common Western form (AIDP), macrophages and complement strip the myelin made by Schwann cells, so nerve signals slow or block. In the axonal forms (AMAN, AMSAN), anti-GM1 or anti-GD1a antibodies bind the axon at the nodes of Ranvier, fix complement and disrupt sodium channels. In Miller Fisher syndrome, anti-GQ1b antibodies target nerves to the eye muscles. Because nerve roots are inflamed and the blood-nerve barrier is weakest there, protein leaks into the spinal fluid.",
  body: [["Peripheral nerves", "Demyelination or axonal injury blocks motor signals, causing symmetric weakness that often starts in the legs and ascends, with lost or reduced reflexes."], ["Breathing muscles", "When the phrenic and intercostal nerves are affected, the diaphragm weakens. Up to about a quarter of patients need a ventilator."], ["Autonomic nerves", "Damage to autonomic fibers causes swings in blood pressure, abnormal heart rhythms, sweating changes and urinary retention, which can be dangerous."], ["Cranial nerves", "Facial weakness on both sides and trouble swallowing are common. In Miller Fisher syndrome the eye muscles are weak."], ["Sensory nerves", "Tingling in the hands and feet and deep back or limb pain come from inflamed sensory fibers and nerve roots."]],
  symptoms: "Weakness rises over days to about four weeks because more and more nerve segments lose their myelin or axon function. Reflexes disappear early because the reflex arc depends on fast conduction in large fibers. Pain and tingling come from inflamed nerve roots. Shortness of breath and a weak cough mean the diaphragm is failing. Heart rate and blood pressure swings reflect autonomic nerve injury.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical: progressive, fairly symmetric weakness with lost reflexes. Spinal fluid often shows high protein with a normal white cell count, though protein may be normal in the first week. Nerve conduction studies show slowed conduction and conduction block in demyelinating forms or low amplitudes in axonal forms. Anti-GQ1b antibodies support Miller Fisher syndrome. Forced vital capacity and negative inspiratory force are tracked to catch breathing failure early.",
  tx: "Immune therapy shortens the illness when started early. Intravenous immune globulin floods the body with donor antibodies that block Fc receptors and speed breakdown of the harmful autoantibodies. Plasma exchange physically removes the antibodies and complement from the blood. The two work about equally and are not usually combined. Corticosteroids do not help in GBS. Supportive care is essential: close monitoring of breathing with early intubation when needed, heart monitoring, blood clot prevention with heparin or enoxaparin, pain control with drugs such as gabapentin or pregabalin, and physical and occupational therapy.",
  drugs: ["intravenous-immune-globulin", "enoxaparin", "heparin", "gabapentin", "pregabalin", "acetaminophen"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "GBS goes up the stairs: weakness ascends from the feet toward the chest."], ["Most-tested fact", "High CSF protein with a normal cell count (albuminocytologic dissociation) after a Campylobacter infection."], ["Watch the breathing", "Falling vital capacity predicts respiratory failure. Patients can look comfortable until the diaphragm suddenly fails."], ["Often confused with", "CIDP, which develops over more than eight weeks and does respond to corticosteroids, and myasthenia gravis, which spares reflexes and sensation."]],
  red: "Weakness that spreads quickly, trouble breathing or swallowing, a weak cough, or a racing or very slow heartbeat needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "In 1976 a US swine flu vaccination campaign was halted partly because of a small rise in GBS cases, which led to the vaccine safety monitoring systems used today.",
  src: ["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Guillain G, Barre JA, Strohl A. Bull Soc Med Hop Paris 1916", "van der Meche FG et al. N Engl J Med 1992", "Willison HJ et al. Lancet 2016"]
},
"als": {
  name: "Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["ALS", "Lou Gehrig's disease", "motor neuron disease", "MND"],
  what: "ALS is a disease in which the nerve cells that control muscles slowly die. Muscles weaken and waste away, and over time people lose the ability to move, speak, swallow and breathe, while thinking and feeling often stay intact.",
  origin: "French neurologist Jean-Martin Charcot described ALS in 1869 and named it in the 1870s. He linked the wasting muscles (amyotrophy) to scarring (sclerosis) in the side (lateral) columns of the spinal cord where motor fibers run. In the United States it became known as Lou Gehrig's disease after the New York Yankees star was diagnosed in 1939. In 1993 a team led by Daniel Rosen and Robert Brown found the first ALS gene, SOD1. In 2006 TDP-43 protein was found clumped in the motor neurons of most patients, and in 2011 a repeat expansion in C9orf72 was found to be the most common inherited cause. Riluzole became the first approved ALS drug in 1995, edaravone followed in 2017, and in 2023 tofersen became the first gene-targeted therapy, for SOD1 ALS.",
  timeline: [["1869", "Jean-Martin Charcot describes the disease."], ["1939", "Lou Gehrig is diagnosed and retires from baseball."], ["1993", "SOD1 mutations are identified as the first ALS gene."], ["1995", "Riluzole is approved by the FDA."], ["2011", "The C9orf72 repeat expansion is identified as the most common genetic cause."], ["2023", "Tofersen is approved for ALS caused by SOD1 mutations."]],
  cell: "ALS kills both upper motor neurons in the motor cortex and lower motor neurons in the brainstem and spinal cord anterior horn. About 90 percent of cases are sporadic and about 10 percent familial. Key genes include C9orf72 (a GGGGCC repeat that produces toxic RNA and dipeptide repeat proteins), SOD1 (misfolded superoxide dismutase that gains a toxic function), TARDBP and FUS. In nearly all sporadic cases, the RNA-binding protein TDP-43 leaves the nucleus and forms ubiquitinated clumps in the cytoplasm, disrupting RNA processing. Other contributors are glutamate excitotoxicity (from reduced astrocyte EAAT2 glutamate uptake, the rationale for riluzole), oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction, impaired axonal transport and harmful activity from microglia and astrocytes. Dying corticospinal axons leave the lateral columns scarred, and denervated muscle fibers atrophy.",
  body: [["Muscles of arms and legs", "Lower motor neuron loss causes weakness, wasting and fasciculations. Upper motor neuron loss causes stiffness, spasticity and brisk reflexes."], ["Speech and swallowing", "Bulbar motor neuron loss weakens the tongue, lips and throat, causing slurred speech, choking and drooling. The tongue may twitch and shrink."], ["Breathing", "Loss of motor neurons to the diaphragm causes progressive respiratory failure, which is the usual cause of death."], ["Brain and behavior", "Up to half of people have some change in thinking or behavior, and some develop frontotemporal dementia, especially with C9orf72."], ["Emotional expression", "Pseudobulbar affect, sudden uncontrollable laughing or crying, results from damage to pathways that regulate emotional expression."]],
  symptoms: "Early signs are often painless weakness in one hand or foot, such as tripping or dropping things, because motor neurons die in one region first and the loss then spreads. Twitching (fasciculations) and cramps come from unstable, dying motor units. Stiffness and brisk reflexes reflect upper motor neuron loss, while wasting reflects lower motor neuron loss. The mix of both in the same limb is the hallmark. Sensation, bladder control and eye movements are usually spared because those neurons are relatively resistant.",
  dx: "There is no single test. Diagnosis rests on finding progressive upper and lower motor neuron signs across body regions, often using the Gold Coast or revised El Escorial criteria. EMG shows widespread denervation and reinnervation. MRI of the brain and spine and blood tests rule out mimics such as cervical spine compression, multifocal motor neuropathy and thyroid disease. Genetic testing is offered, especially when there is a family history.",
  tx: "There is no cure, but some drugs slow decline. Riluzole lowers glutamate release and modestly extends survival. Edaravone is a free radical scavenger thought to reduce oxidative stress. Tofersen is an antisense oligonucleotide given into the spinal fluid that lowers production of SOD1 protein in SOD1 ALS. Symptom care includes dextromethorphan-quinidine for pseudobulbar affect, baclofen or tizanidine for spasticity, and glycopyrrolate for drooling. Multidisciplinary clinics provide noninvasive ventilation, which improves survival, feeding tubes for nutrition, speech devices, and palliative care.",
  drugs: ["riluzole", "edaravone", "tofersen", "dextromethorphan-quinidine", "baclofen", "tizanidine", "glycopyrrolate"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "ALS is both upstairs and downstairs: upper and lower motor neuron signs together, with sensation spared."], ["Most-tested fact", "Riluzole lowers glutamate excitotoxicity. SOD1 was the first gene, and C9orf72 is the most common genetic cause."], ["Often confused with", "Cervical myelopathy, which causes sensory and bladder changes, and spinal muscular atrophy, which is a lower motor neuron disease that starts in childhood."]],
  red: "Choking on food or saliva, shortness of breath, morning headaches from poor breathing at night, or a chest infection in a person with ALS needs urgent medical care (call 911 for trouble breathing).",
  fun: "The 2014 Ice Bucket Challenge raised over 100 million dollars for the ALS Association, which helped fund research that found new ALS genes.",
  src: ["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Rosen DR et al. Nature 1993", "Brown RH, Al-Chalabi A. N Engl J Med 2017", "American Academy of Neurology ALS practice parameters"]
},
"meningitis": {
  name: "Meningitis",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["bacterial meningitis", "viral meningitis", "aseptic meningitis", "meningococcal disease"],
  what: "Meningitis is inflammation of the membranes (meninges) and fluid that surround the brain and spinal cord. It is usually caused by an infection. Bacterial meningitis is a medical emergency that can kill or disable within hours.",
  origin: "In 1805 Gaspard Vieusseux described an outbreak of fever with stiff neck in Geneva, one of the first clear accounts of epidemic meningitis. In 1887 Anton Weichselbaum in Vienna identified the bacterium now called Neisseria meningitidis in spinal fluid. In the early 1900s Simon Flexner in New York lowered death rates with horse serum given into the spinal canal. Sulfa drugs in the late 1930s and penicillin in the 1940s turned a mostly fatal disease into a treatable one. Vaccines then reshaped it: conjugate vaccines against Haemophilus influenzae type b in the late 1980s nearly wiped out Hib meningitis in US children, and pneumococcal and meningococcal vaccines followed. In 2002 a European trial by de Gans and van de Beek showed that dexamethasone given with antibiotics lowers death and disability in adults.",
  timeline: [["1805", "Gaspard Vieusseux describes epidemic meningitis in Geneva."], ["1887", "Anton Weichselbaum identifies the meningococcus."], ["1940s", "Penicillin becomes the main treatment for bacterial meningitis."], ["1987", "The first Hib conjugate vaccine is licensed in the US."], ["2002", "A European trial shows dexamethasone improves outcomes in adults."]],
  cell: "Bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis and Haemophilus influenzae colonize the nasopharynx, invade the bloodstream and resist complement because of their polysaccharide capsules. They cross the blood-brain barrier at the choroid plexus or cerebral capillaries. Spinal fluid has few antibodies and little complement, so bacteria multiply quickly. Bacterial cell wall parts and toxins, such as pneumococcal teichoic acid and meningococcal lipooligosaccharide, trigger TNF-alpha, IL-1 and other cytokines. Neutrophils pour in, the blood-brain barrier leaks, and vasogenic, cytotoxic and interstitial edema raise intracranial pressure. Inflammation of vessels causes stroke, and blocked CSF flow causes hydrocephalus. Bacteria and neutrophils consume glucose, so CSF glucose falls. Antibiotics that lyse bacteria briefly increase this inflammation, which is why dexamethasone is given before or with the first dose. Viral meningitis, often from enteroviruses, causes a milder lymphocytic reaction.",
  body: [["Meninges and brain", "Inflammation raises intracranial pressure and can cause brain swelling, seizures, stroke, hydrocephalus and confusion or coma."], ["Ears", "Inflammation of the inner ear and cochlear nerve can cause permanent hearing loss, the most common lasting complication, especially after pneumococcal meningitis."], ["Blood and skin", "Meningococcal bacteria in the blood release endotoxin that causes septic shock, disseminated intravascular coagulation and a purple rash that does not fade under pressure."], ["Adrenal glands", "Severe meningococcemia can cause bleeding into both adrenal glands (Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome) and adrenal failure."], ["Limbs", "Clotting and shock in meningococcal disease can cut off blood flow to fingers, toes and limbs, sometimes requiring amputation."]],
  symptoms: "Headache and neck stiffness come from inflamed meninges that hurt when stretched, which is also why bending the neck or straightening the knee causes pain (Brudzinski and Kernig signs). Fever reflects cytokines acting on the hypothalamus. Confusion and drowsiness come from raised pressure and cortical inflammation. Light sensitivity and vomiting are linked to meningeal irritation and pressure. A petechial or purpuric rash suggests meningococcal bloodstream infection. Babies may only be irritable, feed poorly or have a bulging fontanelle.",
  dx: "Lumbar puncture is key. Bacterial meningitis shows high opening pressure, many neutrophils, low glucose and high protein, while viral meningitis shows lymphocytes with normal glucose. Gram stain, culture and PCR identify the organism. Blood cultures are drawn right away. A head CT is done first only in certain people, such as those with focal deficits, papilledema, seizure, immune compromise or reduced consciousness, and antibiotics are not delayed for imaging.",
  tx: "Bacterial meningitis is treated with antibiotics as fast as possible. Typical empiric adult therapy is vancomycin to cover resistant pneumococcus plus a third-generation cephalosporin such as ceftriaxone or cefotaxime, which kill bacteria by blocking cell wall synthesis. Ampicillin is added for newborns, older adults and immunocompromised people to cover Listeria. Dexamethasone given before or with the first antibiotic dose dampens the cytokine surge and reduces hearing loss and death, mainly in pneumococcal disease. Acyclovir is added when herpes encephalitis is possible. Close contacts of meningococcal cases receive prophylaxis with rifampin, ciprofloxacin or ceftriaxone. Vaccines against Hib, pneumococcus and meningococcus prevent most cases.",
  drugs: ["ceftriaxone", "vancomycin", "cefotaxime", "ampicillin", "dexamethasone", "acyclovir", "cefepime", "meropenem", "penicillin-g", "rifampin", "ciprofloxacin", "meningococcal-acwy-vaccine", "meningococcal-b-vaccine", "haemophilus-b-vaccine", "pneumococcal-conjugate-vaccine"],
  mem: [["Classic triad", "Fever, neck stiffness and altered mental status. Most adults with bacterial meningitis have at least two of these plus headache."], ["Most-tested fact", "Bacterial CSF: neutrophils, low glucose, high protein. Viral CSF: lymphocytes, normal glucose."], ["Do not delay", "Blood cultures, then dexamethasone and antibiotics right away. A CT scan should never delay the first dose."], ["Listeria clue", "Add ampicillin for the very young, the old, pregnant people and the immunocompromised, because cephalosporins do not cover Listeria."]],
  red: "Fever with a stiff neck, severe headache, confusion, seizures, or a purple rash that does not fade when pressed needs emergency care (call 911). In infants, a bulging soft spot, poor feeding or extreme sleepiness also needs emergency care.",
  fun: "The glass test, pressing a clear glass against a rash, became a well-known public health message because a meningococcal rash usually does not fade under pressure.",
  src: ["IDSA Practice Guidelines for the Management of Bacterial Meningitis (2004)", "IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines for Healthcare-Associated Ventriculitis and Meningitis (2017)", "de Gans J, van de Beek D. N Engl J Med 2002", "CDC", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"encephalitis": {
  name: "Encephalitis",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["HSV encephalitis", "viral encephalitis", "autoimmune encephalitis", "anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis", "meningoencephalitis"],
  what: "Encephalitis is inflammation of the brain tissue itself. It is most often caused by a virus or by the immune system attacking the brain, and it can cause confusion, seizures, personality changes and coma.",
  origin: "Brain fever has been described since ancient times, but encephalitis became famous during the encephalitis lethargica epidemic that Austrian neurologist Constantin von Economo described in 1917. It left many survivors frozen with parkinsonism, as later told in Oliver Sacks's book Awakenings. Herpes simplex virus was recognized as the leading cause of sporadic severe encephalitis in the mid-1900s, and without treatment most patients died. Acyclovir, developed by Gertrude Elion's team, was shown in 1986 trials led by Richard Whitley to work better than vidarabine, cutting death rates sharply. Elion shared the 1988 Nobel Prize. In 1999 West Nile virus reached New York. In 2007 Josep Dalmau and colleagues described anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis, showing that antibodies can cause a treatable psychiatric and neurologic illness.",
  timeline: [["1917", "Constantin von Economo describes encephalitis lethargica."], ["1986", "Trials show acyclovir beats vidarabine for herpes encephalitis."], ["1988", "Gertrude Elion shares the Nobel Prize for drug discoveries including acyclovir."], ["1999", "West Nile virus is first detected in the US, in New York."], ["2007", "Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis is described by Josep Dalmau and colleagues."]],
  cell: "In herpes simplex encephalitis, HSV-1 reaches the brain, likely along the olfactory or trigeminal pathways, either during first infection or by reactivation from latency. It infects neurons and glia of the temporal and orbitofrontal lobes, causing necrotizing, hemorrhagic inflammation with Cowdry type A intranuclear inclusions. Defects in TLR3 signaling and type I interferon can make children more susceptible. Arboviruses such as West Nile infect neurons in the brainstem, thalamus and anterior horn cells, causing perivascular lymphocytic cuffing and microglial nodules. In anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis, IgG antibodies against the GluN1 subunit cause the receptors to be internalized, reducing glutamate signaling without killing neurons, which explains why many people recover. It is sometimes triggered by an ovarian teratoma containing neural tissue or after HSV encephalitis.",
  body: [["Temporal lobes", "HSV favors the temporal lobes and limbic system, causing memory loss, personality change, language problems and sometimes smell or taste hallucinations."], ["Brain overall", "Swelling raises intracranial pressure and can cause seizures, coma and herniation."], ["Brainstem and spinal cord", "West Nile and some enteroviruses damage brainstem and anterior horn motor neurons, causing a polio-like flaccid weakness."], ["Autonomic system", "In anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis, swings in heart rate, blood pressure and temperature and slowed breathing are common."], ["Mind and behavior", "Autoimmune encephalitis often starts with anxiety, psychosis, agitation or insomnia, then progresses to abnormal movements and seizures."]],
  symptoms: "Fever and headache come from infection and cytokines. Confusion, odd behavior and memory problems arise because inflamed neurons, especially in the limbic system, cannot work normally. Seizures happen because injured cortex becomes hyperexcitable. Focal weakness or speech trouble points to the area involved. Unlike pure meningitis, encephalitis changes brain function itself, so altered mental status is central.",
  dx: "MRI is the most sensitive imaging and often shows temporal lobe swelling in HSV encephalitis. Spinal fluid shows lymphocytes and high protein, sometimes with red blood cells, and PCR for HSV and other viruses identifies the cause. EEG may show temporal periodic discharges. Testing of spinal fluid and blood for antibodies such as anti-NMDA receptor identifies autoimmune forms, and pelvic imaging looks for a teratoma. Serology for West Nile IgM is used in summer outbreaks.",
  tx: "Because HSV encephalitis is treatable and deadly, IV acyclovir is started as soon as the diagnosis is suspected, without waiting for test results. It is a nucleoside analog activated by viral thymidine kinase that stops viral DNA polymerase. Ganciclovir or foscarnet are used for CMV. Most other viral causes, including West Nile, have no specific antiviral and are managed supportively. Seizures are treated with antiseizure drugs such as levetiracetam, and brain swelling with hyperosmolar therapy. Autoimmune encephalitis is treated with immunotherapy: high-dose corticosteroids, IV immune globulin or plasma exchange, then rituximab or cyclophosphamide if needed, plus removal of any tumor. Vaccines prevent Japanese, tick-borne, measles and rabies encephalitis.",
  drugs: ["acyclovir", "ganciclovir", "foscarnet", "methylprednisolone", "intravenous-immune-globulin", "rituximab", "cyclophosphamide", "levetiracetam", "lorazepam", "fosphenytoin", "hypertonic-saline-3-percent", "mannitol", "japanese-encephalitis-vaccine", "tick-borne-encephalitis-vaccine", "mmr-vaccine", "rabies-vaccine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "HSV-1 is the most common cause of sporadic fatal encephalitis in the US and targets the temporal lobes."], ["Memory trick", "Meningitis hurts the coverings (neck stiffness), encephalitis changes the person (confusion, seizures, behavior)."], ["Classic case", "A young woman with new psychosis, seizures, abnormal mouth movements and autonomic instability: think anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis and look for an ovarian teratoma."], ["Do not wait", "Start acyclovir on suspicion. A negative early HSV PCR does not always rule it out."]],
  red: "Fever with confusion, strange behavior, seizures, trouble speaking, weakness or decreasing alertness needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Encephalitis lethargica caused some survivors to stay frozen for decades, and some of them briefly awakened when treated with levodopa in 1969.",
  src: ["IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines: The Management of Encephalitis (2008)", "Whitley RJ et al. N Engl J Med 1986", "Dalmau J et al. Ann Neurol 2007", "Graus F et al. Lancet Neurol 2016", "CDC"]
},
"traumatic-brain-injury": {
  name: "Traumatic brain injury",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["TBI", "head injury", "concussion", "epidural hematoma", "subdural hematoma", "diffuse axonal injury"],
  what: "Traumatic brain injury is damage to the brain from a blow, jolt or penetrating injury to the head. It ranges from a mild concussion to severe injury with bleeding, swelling and coma.",
  origin: "The Edwin Smith Papyrus from ancient Egypt describes skull fractures and head wounds with remarkable accuracy, and skull trepanning is even older. In the late 1700s and early 1800s Alexander Monro and George Kellie argued that the skull is a closed box, so any added blood or swelling must squeeze out something else. This Monro-Kellie doctrine still guides care. Harvey Cushing described the rise in blood pressure and fall in heart rate from rising brain pressure in the early 1900s. In 1974 Graham Teasdale and Bryan Jennett in Glasgow created the Glasgow Coma Scale, which made it possible to compare patients worldwide. Large trials then changed practice: the CRASH trial in 2004 showed steroids raise death rates in TBI, and CRASH-3 in 2019 showed early tranexamic acid lowers head injury deaths in mild to moderate TBI. In 2005 Bennet Omalu reported chronic traumatic encephalopathy in a former NFL player.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "The Edwin Smith Papyrus describes head injuries and their outcomes."], ["1974", "Teasdale and Jennett publish the Glasgow Coma Scale."], ["2004", "The CRASH trial shows corticosteroids increase death after TBI."], ["2005", "Bennet Omalu reports CTE in a former professional football player."], ["2016", "The Brain Trauma Foundation publishes its fourth edition guidelines."], ["2019", "CRASH-3 shows early tranexamic acid reduces head injury deaths in mild to moderate TBI."]],
  cell: "Primary injury happens at impact: contusions where the brain hits the skull (coup and contrecoup), torn bridging veins (subdural hematoma), torn middle meningeal artery (epidural hematoma) and shearing of axons from rotational forces (diffuse axonal injury). Secondary injury unfolds over hours to days. Stretched neurons release a flood of glutamate, which over-activates NMDA and AMPA receptors and lets calcium pour in. Calcium activates calpains, phospholipases and nitric oxide synthase, damages mitochondria and creates free radicals, leading to cell death. Ion pumps fail, causing cytotoxic edema, and a damaged blood-brain barrier adds vasogenic edema. Microglia and cytokines drive inflammation. As swelling grows inside the fixed skull, intracranial pressure rises and cerebral perfusion pressure (mean arterial pressure minus ICP) falls, causing ischemia. Low oxygen and low blood pressure sharply worsen this secondary injury. Repeated injury can lead to tau buildup in CTE.",
  body: [["Brain", "Bruising, bleeding and swelling raise intracranial pressure. If unchecked, the brain shifts and herniates, compressing the brainstem."], ["Pupils and eyes", "Uncal herniation compresses the third cranial nerve, causing a fixed, dilated pupil on the side of the lesion."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Rising ICP triggers the Cushing reflex: high blood pressure, slow heart rate and irregular breathing."], ["Lungs", "Reduced consciousness risks aspiration, and severe TBI can cause neurogenic pulmonary edema."], ["Blood clotting", "Injured brain releases tissue factor, which can trigger coagulopathy and make bleeding worse."], ["Mood, thinking and hormones", "Survivors may have memory, attention, mood and sleep problems, and damage to the pituitary can cause hormone deficiencies."]],
  symptoms: "Confusion, amnesia and loss of consciousness reflect disrupted signaling in the cortex and the brainstem arousal network. Headache, vomiting and drowsiness that worsen point to rising pressure. In an epidural hematoma, a person may wake up briefly (lucid interval) before arterial bleeding compresses the brain. Weakness on one side, unequal pupils and posturing signal a mass effect or herniation. Concussion symptoms such as fogginess, dizziness and light sensitivity come from a temporary energy crisis in neurons.",
  dx: "The Glasgow Coma Scale grades severity (13 to 15 mild, 9 to 12 moderate, 8 or lower severe). Noncontrast head CT finds bleeding and fractures: an epidural hematoma looks lens-shaped and does not cross sutures, and a subdural hematoma is crescent-shaped and crosses sutures. Decision rules such as the Canadian CT Head Rule help decide who needs a scan after mild injury. MRI better shows diffuse axonal injury. Severe TBI may need an ICP monitor.",
  tx: "Care aims to prevent secondary injury. EMS and hospitals avoid low oxygen and low blood pressure, and avoid routine hyperventilation, which narrows brain vessels. Raised ICP is treated by raising the head of the bed, sedation with drugs such as propofol and fentanyl, and hyperosmolar therapy with mannitol or hypertonic saline, which draw water out of the brain. Expanding hematomas need surgical evacuation, and refractory swelling may need decompressive craniectomy. Tranexamic acid given early can reduce bleeding in mild to moderate TBI. Levetiracetam or phenytoin prevent early seizures. Anticoagulants are reversed, using 4F-PCC for warfarin or factor Xa inhibitors. Corticosteroids are not used. Concussion care centers on brief rest and a gradual return to activity.",
  drugs: ["hypertonic-saline-3-percent", "mannitol", "tranexamic-acid", "levetiracetam", "phenytoin", "fosphenytoin", "propofol", "fentanyl", "ketamine", "norepinephrine", "prothrombin-complex-concentrate", "phytonadione", "acetaminophen"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Epidural = Emergency artery, lens shape, lucid interval. Subdural = Slow veins, crescent shape, often older adults or alcohol use."], ["Most-tested fact", "Cerebral perfusion pressure equals mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure."], ["Cushing triad", "High blood pressure, low heart rate and irregular breathing mean the brain is herniating."], ["EMS fact", "A single episode of low blood pressure or low oxygen roughly doubles the risk of death after severe TBI."]],
  red: "After a head injury, repeated vomiting, a worsening headache, confusion, drowsiness, a seizure, unequal pupils, weakness or clear fluid from the nose or ears needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The Glasgow Coma Scale, created in 1974, is now used worldwide and is one of the most cited scores in clinical medicine.",
  src: ["Brain Trauma Foundation Guidelines for the Management of Severe TBI, 4th ed. (2016)", "Teasdale G, Jennett B. Lancet 1974", "CRASH-3 Trial Collaborators. Lancet 2019", "CRASH Trial Collaborators. Lancet 2004", "CDC"]
},
"spinal-cord-injury": {
  name: "Spinal cord injury",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["SCI", "paraplegia", "tetraplegia", "quadriplegia", "neurogenic shock", "central cord syndrome"],
  what: "A spinal cord injury is damage to the bundle of nerves inside the spine, usually from trauma. It can cause loss of movement, feeling and body control below the level of injury, and may be permanent.",
  origin: "The Edwin Smith Papyrus from ancient Egypt described neck injury with paralysis as an ailment not to be treated, and for thousands of years most people with complete spinal cord injury died within weeks from infections and bed sores. In 1944 German-born British neurologist Ludwig Guttmann opened the spinal unit at Stoke Mandeville Hospital in England. He insisted on regular turning, bladder care and rehabilitation, and survival rose dramatically. In 1948 he organized the Stoke Mandeville Games, which grew into the Paralympics. In 1990 the NASCIS II trial suggested high-dose methylprednisolone helped, but later analysis questioned the benefit, and current guidelines do not recommend it routinely. The ASIA Impairment Scale, refined in the 1980s and 1990s, standardized how injuries are graded.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "The Edwin Smith Papyrus describes spinal injury with paralysis."], ["1944", "Ludwig Guttmann opens the spinal injuries center at Stoke Mandeville."], ["1948", "The Stoke Mandeville Games begin, later becoming the Paralympics."], ["1990", "The NASCIS II trial reports on methylprednisolone in acute SCI."], ["2013", "AANS/CNS guidelines recommend against routine methylprednisolone."]],
  cell: "Primary injury from fracture, dislocation or compression crushes and tears neurons, axons and blood vessels in the cord. Secondary injury then spreads over hours to weeks. Hemorrhage and vessel spasm cause ischemia, and injured cells release glutamate, which over-activates receptors and floods neurons and oligodendrocytes with calcium. Free radicals and lipid peroxidation damage membranes, and neutrophils and macrophages release cytokines and proteases. Oligodendrocytes die by apoptosis, so even surviving axons lose myelin. Over weeks, reactive astrocytes form a glial scar and inhibitory molecules such as chondroitin sulfate proteoglycans and myelin-associated Nogo block axon regrowth, because central nervous system axons regenerate poorly. A cyst often forms at the injury site.",
  body: [["Muscles", "Loss of corticospinal signals causes weakness or paralysis below the injury. At first the limbs are flaccid (spinal shock), then become spastic with brisk reflexes."], ["Breathing", "Injury at C3 to C5 can paralyze the diaphragm, and higher thoracic injuries weaken coughing, raising pneumonia risk."], ["Heart and blood pressure", "Injury above about T6 cuts sympathetic outflow, causing neurogenic shock with low blood pressure and slow heart rate, and later autonomic dysreflexia."], ["Bladder and bowel", "Interrupted spinal pathways cause urinary retention or a spastic bladder and loss of bowel control, raising risk of infection and kidney damage."], ["Skin", "Lack of sensation and movement causes pressure injuries over bony areas, a major cause of complications."], ["Blood clotting", "Immobility leads to high rates of deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism."]],
  symptoms: "Weakness and numbness below a clear level happen because the tracts carrying motor and sensory signals are cut at that level. Patterns tell the location: central cord syndrome weakens the arms more than the legs, Brown-Sequard syndrome causes same-side weakness and opposite-side pain loss, and anterior cord syndrome spares vibration and position sense. Low blood pressure with a slow pulse after a neck injury reflects lost sympathetic tone. Autonomic dysreflexia, a sudden dangerous rise in blood pressure with headache and flushing above the injury, is triggered by a full bladder or other stimulus below the level.",
  dx: "A detailed neurologic exam using the ASIA Impairment Scale (A complete to E normal) defines the level and completeness. CT is the first imaging test for spinal fractures, and MRI shows cord swelling, bleeding, disc herniation and ligament injury. Decision rules such as NEXUS and the Canadian C-spine Rule guide who needs neck imaging. Spinal shock can mask function early, so prognosis is judged after it fades.",
  tx: "Prehospital care uses spinal motion restriction and protects the airway and breathing. In the hospital, blood pressure is supported with fluids and vasopressors such as norepinephrine, because good cord blood flow limits secondary injury, and atropine treats severe bradycardia. Early surgical decompression and stabilization relieve pressure on the cord. Methylprednisolone is no longer used routinely. Enoxaparin prevents blood clots. Long-term care includes rehabilitation, bladder catheter programs, bowel programs, pressure injury prevention, and baclofen or tizanidine for spasticity. Autonomic dysreflexia is treated by sitting up, removing the trigger and using fast-acting blood pressure drugs such as nitroglycerin or nifedipine.",
  drugs: ["norepinephrine", "phenylephrine", "dopamine", "atropine", "enoxaparin", "heparin", "baclofen", "tizanidine", "dantrolene", "gabapentin", "pregabalin", "nitroglycerin", "nifedipine", "methylprednisolone", "oxybutynin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive."], ["Neurogenic vs hypovolemic shock", "Neurogenic shock: low blood pressure with a slow heart rate and warm, dry skin. Hemorrhagic shock: low blood pressure with a fast heart rate and cool, clammy skin."], ["Most-tested fact", "Autonomic dysreflexia occurs with injuries at or above T6; the first step is to sit the person up and find the trigger, often a blocked catheter."], ["Brown-Sequard", "Hemisection causes same-side weakness and vibration loss and opposite-side pain and temperature loss."]],
  red: "After a fall, crash or diving injury, neck or back pain with numbness, tingling, weakness or trouble breathing needs emergency care (call 911), with the person kept still. In someone with a known SCI, a pounding headache with flushing and very high blood pressure can be autonomic dysreflexia and needs urgent care.",
  fun: "Ludwig Guttmann's 1948 archery contest for injured veterans was held on the same day as the opening of the London Olympics.",
  src: ["AANS/CNS Guidelines for the Management of Acute Cervical Spine and Spinal Cord Injuries (2013)", "International Standards for Neurological Classification of SCI (ASIA)", "Bracken MB et al. NASCIS II. N Engl J Med 1990", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"peripheral-neuropathy": {
  name: "Peripheral neuropathy",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["polyneuropathy", "diabetic neuropathy", "diabetic peripheral neuropathy", "stocking-glove neuropathy", "small fiber neuropathy"],
  what: "Peripheral neuropathy is damage to the nerves outside the brain and spinal cord. It often starts as numbness, tingling or burning in the feet and can cause pain, balance problems and weakness.",
  origin: "Ancient physicians described numb, ulcerated limbs in leprosy, still a major cause of neuropathy worldwide. In the 1890s Dutch physician Christiaan Eijkman, working in Java, noticed that chickens fed polished white rice developed a nerve disease like beriberi, and that brown rice prevented it. This led to the discovery of thiamine (vitamin B1), and Eijkman shared the 1929 Nobel Prize. In 1886 Jean-Martin Charcot, Pierre Marie and Howard Tooth described the inherited neuropathy now called Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease. As diabetes became common in the 1900s, it became the leading cause of neuropathy in the US. Modern treatment of nerve pain began with antidepressants and antiseizure drugs; duloxetine and pregabalin became the first FDA-approved drugs for painful diabetic neuropathy in 2004. Gene-silencing drugs for hereditary transthyretin amyloid neuropathy arrived in 2018.",
  timeline: [["1886", "Charcot, Marie and Tooth describe hereditary motor and sensory neuropathy."], ["1890s", "Christiaan Eijkman links polished rice to beriberi neuropathy."], ["2004", "Duloxetine and pregabalin are approved for painful diabetic neuropathy."], ["2018", "Patisiran and inotersen are approved for hereditary transthyretin amyloid polyneuropathy."]],
  cell: "Most polyneuropathy is length-dependent, meaning the longest axons fail first because their distant ends depend on transport of proteins and energy from the cell body. In diabetes, high glucose drives the polyol pathway (aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol), forms advanced glycation end products, increases oxidative stress and activates protein kinase C. Damage to the small blood vessels (vasa nervorum) starves nerves of oxygen. Small unmyelinated C fibers and thin A-delta fibers are hit early, causing pain and temperature loss, then large myelinated fibers fail, causing numbness and loss of vibration sense. Other causes work through different targets: B12 deficiency damages myelin, alcohol and chemotherapy drugs injure axons and microtubules, and transthyretin amyloid deposits in nerves. Damaged nerves fire spontaneously because sodium channels such as Nav1.7 and Nav1.8 are overexpressed, and central sensitization amplifies pain.",
  body: [["Feet and hands", "Numbness, tingling and burning start in the toes and rise to the shins before reaching the fingers, the stocking-glove pattern."], ["Skin and feet", "Loss of protective sensation means unnoticed injuries, ulcers and infections. Diabetic foot ulcers are a leading cause of amputation."], ["Joints", "Repeated unfelt trauma can destroy foot joints (Charcot neuroarthropathy)."], ["Balance", "Loss of position sense from large fibers causes unsteadiness, especially in the dark, and raises fall risk."], ["Autonomic nerves", "Autonomic neuropathy causes dizziness on standing, slow stomach emptying, bladder problems, erectile dysfunction and abnormal sweating."]],
  symptoms: "Burning, electric or stabbing pain happens because injured small fibers fire on their own and the spinal cord amplifies their signals. Numbness and loss of vibration and position sense reflect large fiber loss. Symptoms start in the feet because those axons are longest. Weakness in the ankles and toes and lost ankle reflexes appear as motor fibers fail. Foot ulcers happen because pain no longer warns of injury.",
  dx: "Diagnosis starts with the pattern and the exam: reduced pinprick, temperature, vibration and monofilament sensation and reduced ankle reflexes. Screening labs look for common causes: glucose and A1c, vitamin B12, thyroid tests, kidney and liver tests, and serum protein electrophoresis for a monoclonal protein. Nerve conduction studies and EMG separate axonal from demyelinating disease. A skin biopsy measuring nerve fiber density can confirm small fiber neuropathy.",
  tx: "Treatment targets the cause and the pain. Good glucose control slows diabetic neuropathy, B12 replacement treats deficiency, and stopping alcohol or a toxic drug can help. For nerve pain, first-line drug classes include gabapentinoids (pregabalin, gabapentin), which bind the alpha-2-delta subunit of calcium channels to reduce neurotransmitter release; SNRIs (duloxetine, venlafaxine), which strengthen descending pain-inhibiting pathways; and tricyclic antidepressants (amitriptyline, nortriptyline). Topical capsaicin defunctionalizes pain fibers and lidocaine patches block sodium channels locally. Opioids are generally avoided. Hereditary transthyretin amyloid neuropathy is treated with gene silencers such as patisiran, vutrisiran and eplontersen. Daily foot checks, proper footwear and podiatry prevent ulcers.",
  drugs: ["pregabalin", "gabapentin", "duloxetine", "venlafaxine", "amitriptyline", "nortriptyline", "capsaicin-topical", "lidocaine-patch", "tramadol", "tapentadol", "cyanocobalamin", "thiamine", "patisiran", "vutrisiran", "eplontersen", "inotersen", "intravenous-immune-globulin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Common causes are DANG THE RAPIST: Diabetes, Alcohol, Nutritional (B12), Guillain-Barre, Trauma, Hereditary, Endocrine, Renal, Amyloid, Porphyria, Infection (leprosy, HIV), Systemic disease, Toxins."], ["Most-tested fact", "Diabetes is the most common cause of peripheral neuropathy in the US, and annual monofilament testing is part of diabetes care."], ["B12 clue", "B12 deficiency can damage both peripheral nerves and the spinal cord dorsal columns, with anemia and high methylmalonic acid."]],
  red: "Rapidly spreading weakness or numbness over days, trouble walking or breathing, or a foot wound that is red, swollen, draining or smells bad needs urgent medical care (call 911 for trouble breathing).",
  fun: "Christiaan Eijkman's discovery began when the hospital's chickens got sick after a new cook started feeding them leftover polished rice meant for patients.",
  src: ["ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes (2025)", "AAN Guideline: Treatment of Painful Diabetic Neuropathy (2022)", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"bells-palsy": {
  name: "Bell's palsy",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["idiopathic facial nerve palsy", "acute peripheral facial palsy", "facial nerve paralysis"],
  what: "Bell's palsy is sudden weakness or paralysis of one side of the face caused by swelling of the facial nerve. Most people recover fully within weeks to months.",
  origin: "Scottish surgeon and anatomist Charles Bell described the function of the facial nerve in papers to the Royal Society in 1821, showing that cutting it paralyzed facial expression. The condition of sudden one-sided facial paralysis without another cause took his name. For a long time the cause was unknown. In 1996 a Japanese team found herpes simplex virus DNA in the fluid around the facial nerve during surgery, supporting the idea that viral reactivation triggers nerve swelling. In 2007 a large Scottish trial led by Frank Sullivan showed that prednisolone started within three days improved recovery, while acyclovir alone did not. Guidelines now recommend early corticosteroids, with antivirals as an optional add-on.",
  timeline: [["1821", "Charles Bell describes the facial nerve's role in facial expression."], ["1996", "Herpes simplex virus DNA is detected around the facial nerve in Bell's palsy."], ["2007", "A Scottish trial shows early prednisolone improves recovery."], ["2013", "The AAO-HNS publishes its clinical practice guideline on Bell's palsy."]],
  cell: "The leading theory is reactivation of latent herpes simplex virus type 1, and sometimes varicella-zoster virus, in the geniculate ganglion of the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII). Inflammation and swelling of the nerve trap it inside the narrow bony facial canal, especially at the labyrinthine segment, the tightest point. Compression causes ischemia and demyelination, and in severe cases axonal degeneration. Because the nerve is injured after the facial motor nucleus, all branches on that side fail, including the branch to the forehead. When axons regrow, they can reconnect to the wrong targets, causing synkinesis or crocodile tears.",
  body: [["Facial muscles", "All muscles of facial expression on one side weaken, so the forehead cannot wrinkle, the eye cannot close and the mouth droops."], ["Eye", "Incomplete eye closure and reduced blinking dry the cornea and can cause abrasions or ulcers."], ["Ear", "Weakness of the stapedius muscle makes normal sounds seem too loud (hyperacusis), and pain behind the ear is common."], ["Taste and saliva", "Involvement of the chorda tympani branch reduces taste on the front two thirds of the tongue and can reduce saliva."], ["Tear glands", "Fibers to the lacrimal gland may be affected, changing tear production."]],
  symptoms: "Weakness develops over hours to about three days because swelling within the bony canal progressively compresses the nerve. The forehead is weak because the lesion is in the nerve itself, below the brainstem, so no backup signal from the other side of the brain can reach those muscles. Pain near the ear, altered taste and sound sensitivity map to branches that leave the nerve inside the canal. Drooling and trouble keeping food in the cheek come from lip and cheek weakness.",
  dx: "Bell's palsy is a diagnosis of exclusion based on rapid onset of one-sided facial weakness involving the forehead with no other neurologic signs. The exam looks for causes such as stroke (which spares the forehead), ear vesicles of Ramsay Hunt syndrome, parotid tumors, Lyme disease and middle ear infection. Lyme testing is done in endemic areas. Routine labs and imaging are not needed in typical cases, but imaging is done if weakness is atypical, recurrent or not improving after a few months.",
  tx: "Oral corticosteroids such as prednisone or prednisolone, started within 72 hours of onset, reduce nerve swelling and improve the chance of full recovery. Antivirals such as valacyclovir or acyclovir may be added in severe cases, though their benefit alone is unproven. Eye protection is critical: lubricating drops and ointment by day, and taping the eye shut or using a moisture chamber at night prevent corneal injury. Physical therapy and facial exercises may help. People who do not recover may be offered surgery or botulinum toxin for synkinesis.",
  drugs: ["prednisone", "prednisolone", "valacyclovir", "acyclovir", "onabotulinumtoxina"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Stroke spares the forehead; Bell's takes the whole half of the face."], ["Most-tested fact", "Lower motor neuron lesion of CN VII: forehead involved. Upper motor neuron lesion (stroke): forehead spared because it gets input from both sides of the brain."], ["Often confused with", "Ramsay Hunt syndrome, caused by varicella-zoster, with ear pain and vesicles in the ear canal and a worse prognosis."], ["Associations", "Bell's palsy is more common in pregnancy, diabetes and after upper respiratory infections. Bilateral facial palsy suggests Lyme disease, sarcoidosis or GBS."]],
  red: "Facial drooping with arm or leg weakness, trouble speaking, confusion, vision loss or a severe headache may be a stroke and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Charles Bell was also a talented artist who painted wounded soldiers after the Battle of Waterloo.",
  src: ["AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Bell's Palsy (2013)", "Sullivan FM et al. N Engl J Med 2007", "Murakami S et al. Ann Intern Med 1996", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"trigeminal-neuralgia": {
  name: "Trigeminal neuralgia",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["tic douloureux", "TN", "facial neuralgia"],
  what: "Trigeminal neuralgia causes sudden attacks of severe, electric shock-like pain on one side of the face. The attacks last seconds and are often set off by light touch, chewing, talking or brushing the teeth.",
  origin: "In 1756 French surgeon Nicolas Andre described the condition and called it tic douloureux, painful tic, because of the facial wince it causes. In 1773 English physician John Fothergill gave a detailed account of the pain. For two centuries treatments were crude, including cutting or destroying the nerve. In the 1930s American neurosurgeon Walter Dandy noticed blood vessels pressing on the trigeminal nerve root in many patients. In 1962 carbamazepine was reported to control the pain, and it remains the first-choice drug. In the 1960s and 1970s neurosurgeon Peter Jannetta developed microvascular decompression, an operation that moves the offending artery off the nerve and can bring lasting relief.",
  timeline: [["1756", "Nicolas Andre names the condition tic douloureux."], ["1773", "John Fothergill describes the painful affection of the face."], ["1930s", "Walter Dandy observes vessels compressing the trigeminal nerve root."], ["1962", "Carbamazepine is reported to relieve trigeminal neuralgia."], ["1960s", "Peter Jannetta develops microvascular decompression surgery."]],
  cell: "In classic trigeminal neuralgia, a blood vessel, most often the superior cerebellar artery, presses on the trigeminal nerve root near where it enters the pons. This root entry zone is where peripheral Schwann cell myelin changes to central oligodendrocyte myelin and is especially vulnerable. Constant pulsation causes focal demyelination. Bare axons lie next to each other and can pass signals directly (ephaptic transmission), so a light touch signal carried by large A-beta fibers can jump into pain fibers. Damaged axons also become hyperexcitable from changed sodium channel expression and fire in bursts after a trigger. In multiple sclerosis, a demyelinating plaque in the pons can cause the same problem, and a tumor can compress the nerve in secondary forms.",
  body: [["Trigeminal nerve", "Usually the maxillary (V2) and mandibular (V3) branches are affected, causing pain in the cheek, jaw, teeth, gums or lips on one side."], ["Face", "Light touch, wind or a cold breeze over trigger zones near the nose and mouth can set off an attack."], ["Eating and mouth care", "Fear of triggers can lead people to avoid eating, talking or brushing teeth, causing weight loss and dental problems."], ["Mood and sleep", "Repeated unpredictable pain can lead to anxiety, depression and poor sleep."]],
  symptoms: "Pain is brief, sharp and electric because demyelinated fibers discharge in sudden bursts that then stop. It is triggered by innocuous touch because touch fibers cross-talk with pain fibers. It stays on one side and follows trigeminal branches because only one nerve root is compressed. Between attacks there may be no pain, although some people develop a constant background ache. A brief refractory period after an attack reflects nerve fibers recovering their excitability.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical, based on the International Classification of Headache Disorders criteria: paroxysmal one-sided pain in trigeminal branches lasting under a second up to two minutes, severe, electric or stabbing, and brought on by innocuous stimuli. MRI with special sequences looks for vascular compression, multiple sclerosis or a tumor. A sensory deficit, onset at a young age or pain on both sides raises concern for a secondary cause.",
  tx: "Carbamazepine is first-line and oxcarbazepine is a common alternative; both block voltage-gated sodium channels and quiet the hyperexcitable nerve. Before carbamazepine is started in people of Asian ancestry, HLA-B*15:02 testing is done because this gene raises the risk of severe skin reactions. Add-on or alternative drugs include baclofen, lamotrigine, gabapentin and pregabalin. When drugs fail or cause side effects, microvascular decompression moves the compressing vessel away, while procedures such as Gamma Knife radiosurgery, balloon compression and glycerol or radiofrequency rhizotomy damage part of the nerve to block pain signals.",
  drugs: ["carbamazepine", "oxcarbazepine", "baclofen", "lamotrigine", "gabapentin", "pregabalin", "phenytoin", "fosphenytoin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Tic douloureux: a tic of pain, seconds of lightning in the face set off by a breeze."], ["Most-tested fact", "Carbamazepine is the drug of choice; monitor for low sodium, blood count changes and skin reactions."], ["Red flag", "Trigeminal neuralgia in a young adult, especially on both sides, raises concern for multiple sclerosis."], ["Often confused with", "Dental pain, TMJ disorder, cluster headache (around the eye with tearing, lasting longer) and postherpetic neuralgia."]],
  red: "Facial pain with new numbness, weakness, vision changes, hearing loss or trouble swallowing, or a sudden severe headache, needs urgent medical care (call 911 for sudden severe symptoms).",
  fun: "Trigeminal neuralgia is often called one of the most painful conditions known to medicine, and its nickname, the suicide disease, reflects how severe untreated attacks once were.",
  src: ["International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd ed. (ICHD-3, 2018)", "AAN/EFNS Guideline on Trigeminal Neuralgia Management (2008)", "Bendtsen L et al. Lancet Neurol 2020", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"huntingtons-disease": {
  name: "Huntington's disease",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["Huntington disease", "HD", "Huntington's chorea"],
  what: "Huntington's disease is an inherited brain disease that slowly destroys nerve cells. It causes uncontrolled dance-like movements, changes in mood and behavior, and a decline in thinking, usually starting in midlife.",
  origin: "In 1872, at age 22, American physician George Huntington published a clear description of an inherited chorea he had seen in families on Long Island, New York, with his father and grandfather. He noted that it passed from parent to child and never skipped a generation. In the 1970s Nancy Wexler, whose mother had the disease, helped lead studies of a huge family in the villages around Lake Maracaibo, Venezuela. Blood samples from these families let James Gusella's team map the gene to chromosome 4 in 1983, one of the first disease genes found by genetic mapping. In 1993 the Huntington's Disease Collaborative Research Group identified the gene and its expanded CAG repeat. Tetrabenazine became the first FDA-approved drug for HD chorea in 2008, followed by deutetrabenazine in 2017 and valbenazine in 2023.",
  timeline: [["1872", "George Huntington describes hereditary chorea."], ["1983", "The HD gene is mapped to chromosome 4 using samples from Venezuelan families."], ["1993", "The HTT gene and its expanded CAG repeat are identified."], ["2008", "Tetrabenazine is approved for HD chorea."], ["2017", "Deutetrabenazine is approved for HD chorea."], ["2023", "Valbenazine is approved for HD chorea."]],
  cell: "HD is autosomal dominant. The HTT gene on chromosome 4 contains a CAG repeat. With 40 or more repeats the disease is fully penetrant, and 36 to 39 repeats give reduced penetrance. CAG codes glutamine, so the huntingtin protein gets a long polyglutamine tract. The mutant protein misfolds, is cut into toxic fragments and forms intranuclear inclusions. It disrupts gene transcription (including reduced BDNF), mitochondrial function, axonal transport and protein clearance, and makes neurons more sensitive to glutamate excitotoxicity through NMDA receptors. GABAergic medium spiny neurons of the striatum, especially in the caudate, die first. Early loss of the indirect pathway neurons (which express D2 receptors and enkephalin) reduces inhibition of movement, producing chorea. The repeat is unstable and can expand when passed on, especially from the father, so symptoms appear earlier in the next generation (anticipation).",
  body: [["Basal ganglia", "Atrophy of the caudate and putamen disrupts movement control, causing chorea early and later rigidity and slowness."], ["Cerebral cortex", "Loss of cortical neurons and frontostriatal circuits causes trouble planning, organizing and shifting attention, leading to dementia."], ["Mood and behavior", "Depression, irritability, apathy, anxiety and obsessive thinking are common and can come before movement symptoms. Suicide risk is increased."], ["Swallowing and speech", "Poor coordination of the mouth and throat causes slurred speech and choking, and aspiration pneumonia is a common cause of death."], ["Weight and metabolism", "Many people lose weight despite eating well, from constant movement and metabolic changes."]],
  symptoms: "Chorea, quick irregular movements that flow from one body part to another, happens because loss of indirect pathway neurons lets the thalamus send too much go signal to the cortex. Motor impersistence, such as being unable to keep the tongue out, reflects the same circuit failure. Thinking slows because frontal-striatal loops fail. Mood and behavior changes come from limbic and prefrontal circuit damage. In later stages and in juvenile HD, rigidity and slowness dominate as the direct pathway also degenerates.",
  dx: "Diagnosis combines typical motor signs with a family history and is confirmed by a genetic test measuring the CAG repeat length. Predictive testing for at-risk relatives is offered with genetic counseling because of the major emotional impact. MRI may show atrophy of the caudate, which makes the lateral ventricles look boxed out.",
  tx: "There is no cure or drug yet proven to slow HD. Chorea is treated with VMAT2 inhibitors (tetrabenazine, deutetrabenazine, valbenazine), which reduce dopamine packaging into vesicles and so lower dopamine signaling. These carry a boxed warning for depression and suicidality in HD, so mood is closely watched. Antipsychotics such as haloperidol, olanzapine and risperidone block D2 receptors and help chorea plus psychosis or irritability. SSRIs treat depression and anxiety. Speech, swallowing, physical and occupational therapy, nutrition support, genetic counseling and palliative care are key. Huntingtin-lowering therapies are being studied.",
  drugs: ["tetrabenazine", "deutetrabenazine", "valbenazine", "haloperidol", "olanzapine", "risperidone", "aripiprazole", "quetiapine", "sertraline", "citalopram", "fluoxetine", "mirtazapine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Hunting 4 CAGs: Huntington is on chromosome 4, with a CAG repeat and Caudate atrophy."], ["Most-tested fact", "Autosomal dominant with anticipation, mainly through the father, and a 50 percent chance of passing it to each child."], ["Neurotransmitters", "Huntington: dopamine up, GABA and acetylcholine down (the opposite pattern from Parkinson disease)."]],
  red: "Thoughts of suicide, choking, or sudden confusion in a person with HD need emergency care (call 911). The 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline is available by call or text.",
  fun: "Folk singer Woody Guthrie died of Huntington's disease in 1967, and his wife Marjorie went on to found what became the Huntington's Disease Society of America.",
  src: ["Huntington G. The Medical and Surgical Reporter 1872", "Huntington's Disease Collaborative Research Group. Cell 1993", "Gusella JF et al. Nature 1983", "AAN Evidence-based Guideline: Pharmacologic Treatment of Chorea in HD (2012)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"restless-legs": {
  name: "Restless legs syndrome",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["RLS", "Willis-Ekbom disease", "WED"],
  what: "Restless legs syndrome is an urge to move the legs, usually with uncomfortable creeping or crawling feelings. It happens at rest, is worst in the evening and night, and gets better with movement, so it often disrupts sleep.",
  origin: "English physician Thomas Willis described the condition in 1672, writing of patients whose legs leapt and could not rest in bed. In 1945 Swedish neurologist Karl-Axel Ekbom gave a detailed description and named it restless legs, so it is also called Willis-Ekbom disease. In the 1980s levodopa was found to relieve symptoms, and later the dopamine agonists ropinirole (2005) and pramipexole (2006) became the first FDA-approved drugs for RLS. Over time doctors learned that long-term dopamine drugs can make symptoms worse, a problem called augmentation. Studies of brain iron in the 2000s showed low iron in key brain areas. In 2024 the American Academy of Sleep Medicine updated its guideline to favor iron and alpha-2-delta ligands over dopamine agonists for most people.",
  timeline: [["1672", "Thomas Willis describes restless legs."], ["1945", "Karl-Axel Ekbom names and characterizes the syndrome."], ["2005", "Ropinirole becomes the first FDA-approved drug for RLS."], ["2007", "Genome studies identify the first RLS risk genes, including MEIS1 and BTBD9."], ["2024", "AASM guidelines favor iron and alpha-2-delta ligands over dopamine agonists."]],
  cell: "RLS is linked to low iron in the brain and changes in dopamine signaling. Even with normal blood iron, the substantia nigra and other areas have low iron, shown on MRI and autopsy. Iron is a cofactor for tyrosine hydroxylase, the enzyme that makes dopamine, and it affects dopamine receptor and transporter function. Brain iron deficiency leads to changes in dopamine signaling that follow a daily rhythm, with dopamine activity naturally lowest in the evening, matching the timing of symptoms. Increased glutamate activity and changes in adenosine and opioid systems also add to hyperarousal and sensory symptoms. Risk genes such as MEIS1 and BTBD9 affect iron handling and spinal cord development. Secondary RLS is common in iron deficiency, pregnancy and kidney failure.",
  body: [["Legs", "Uncomfortable urges and sensations deep in the legs, usually in the calves, make it hard to stay still. Arms can be involved in severe cases."], ["Sleep", "Symptoms delay sleep onset, and periodic limb movements of sleep cause repeated awakenings and daytime tiredness."], ["Mood", "Chronic poor sleep raises the risk of depression and anxiety."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Repeated limb movements at night come with spikes in heart rate and blood pressure, which may add to cardiovascular risk."]],
  symptoms: "The urge to move appears at rest because sensory and motor circuits become hyperexcitable when not being used. Movement relieves it by providing competing sensory input. Symptoms peak in the evening because dopamine and iron levels follow a daily rhythm and are lowest at night. Jerking leg movements during sleep, periodic limb movements, come from the same spinal and brain network changes. Iron deficiency, pregnancy and kidney failure make symptoms worse by further lowering brain iron.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical and uses five essential criteria: an urge to move the legs, beginning or worsening at rest, relief by movement, worse in the evening or night, and not explained by another condition such as cramps or arthritis. Iron studies, including ferritin and transferrin saturation, are checked in everyone. Kidney function and a medication review look for secondary causes. Sleep studies are not needed routinely but can show periodic limb movements.",
  tx: "Treatment starts by fixing reversible causes and checking iron. Oral or IV iron (ferrous sulfate, ferric carboxymaltose) is given when ferritin is low or borderline, because restoring brain iron can improve symptoms. Alpha-2-delta ligands (gabapentin enacarbil, gabapentin, pregabalin) reduce neuronal excitability and are now preferred first-line drugs. Dopamine agonists (pramipexole, ropinirole, rotigotine patch) work quickly but can cause augmentation, so guidelines now recommend against routine use. Low-dose opioids are used for severe refractory RLS. Avoiding triggers such as caffeine, alcohol, sedating antihistamines and some antidepressants and antipsychotics also helps.",
  drugs: ["ferrous-sulfate", "ferric-carboxymaltose", "iron-sucrose", "gabapentin", "pregabalin", "pramipexole", "ropinirole", "rotigotine", "tramadol", "methadone"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "URGE: Urge to move, Rest makes it worse, Gets better with movement, Evening and night are worst."], ["Most-tested fact", "Check ferritin in every patient with RLS. Iron deficiency is the most common reversible cause."], ["Augmentation", "Long-term dopamine agonist use can make symptoms start earlier in the day, spread to the arms and become more intense."], ["Triggers", "Diphenhydramine, many antidepressants and dopamine-blocking drugs such as metoclopramide can worsen RLS."]],
  fun: "RLS affects a few percent of adults and is about twice as common in women, partly because pregnancy can trigger it, though it usually fades after delivery.",
  src: ["AASM Clinical Practice Guideline: Treatment of RLS and PLMD in Adults (2024)", "International RLS Study Group Diagnostic Criteria (2014)", "Ekbom KA. Acta Med Scand 1945", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"narcolepsy": {
  name: "Narcolepsy",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["narcolepsy type 1", "narcolepsy with cataplexy", "narcolepsy type 2", "hypocretin deficiency syndrome"],
  what: "Narcolepsy is a long-term sleep disorder in which the brain cannot properly control sleep and wake. People have overwhelming daytime sleepiness and may suddenly lose muscle strength with strong emotions (cataplexy).",
  origin: "German physician Carl Westphal described a patient with sleep attacks and emotion-triggered weakness in 1877, and in 1880 French physician Jean-Baptiste-Edouard Gelineau named the condition narcolepsy, from Greek words meaning seized by sleep. In the 1960s researchers found that people with narcolepsy enter REM sleep almost immediately after falling asleep. In 1998 two teams independently discovered a new brain chemical made in the hypothalamus, naming it hypocretin and orexin. In 1999 Emmanuel Mignot's group found a mutated orexin receptor gene in narcoleptic dogs, and Masashi Yanagisawa's group showed mice without orexin had narcolepsy. In 2000 people with narcolepsy and cataplexy were shown to have lost their orexin neurons. Sodium oxybate was approved for cataplexy in 2002, and newer wake-promoting drugs followed.",
  timeline: [["1880", "Jean-Baptiste-Edouard Gelineau names narcolepsy."], ["1998", "Hypocretin (orexin) is discovered by two research groups."], ["1999", "Orexin defects are shown to cause narcolepsy in dogs and mice."], ["2000", "Loss of hypocretin neurons is found in people with narcolepsy."], ["2002", "Sodium oxybate is approved by the FDA for cataplexy."]],
  cell: "Type 1 narcolepsy is caused by loss of the roughly 70,000 orexin (hypocretin) neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. Orexin normally excites wake-promoting neurons, such as noradrenergic locus coeruleus, histaminergic tuberomammillary, serotonergic raphe and dopaminergic cells, and stabilizes the switch between wake and sleep. Without it, the switch becomes unstable, so people slip into sleep during the day and wake often at night, and parts of REM sleep intrude into wakefulness. Cataplexy is REM atonia appearing while awake: strong emotions activate the amygdala, which, without orexin balance, triggers the brainstem pathways that inhibit spinal motor neurons. The loss of orexin neurons is thought to be autoimmune, given the strong link to HLA-DQB1*06:02 and to T-cell receptor variants, and a rise in cases after the 2009 H1N1 flu pandemic. In type 2, orexin levels are usually normal and the cause is less clear.",
  body: [["Brain sleep-wake system", "An unstable sleep-wake switch causes daytime sleep attacks and fragmented night sleep with frequent awakenings."], ["Muscles", "Cataplexy causes brief, sudden loss of muscle tone, from jaw sagging and knee buckling to full collapse, with consciousness preserved."], ["Perception", "Vivid hallucinations when falling asleep (hypnagogic) or waking (hypnopompic) come from REM dreaming intruding into wakefulness."], ["Sleep paralysis", "On falling asleep or waking, people can be briefly unable to move because REM atonia persists while awake."], ["Metabolism", "People with type 1 narcolepsy tend to gain weight, likely because orexin also influences appetite and energy use."]],
  symptoms: "Excessive daytime sleepiness, the core symptom, happens because without orexin the brain cannot keep wake-promoting systems firing steadily. Cataplexy is triggered by laughter, surprise or anger and lasts seconds to minutes. Hallucinations and sleep paralysis are REM features escaping their normal timing. Night sleep is disrupted because the same unstable switch flips the other way. Memory lapses and automatic behaviors happen during microsleeps.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses an overnight sleep study (polysomnography) followed the next day by a multiple sleep latency test. Narcolepsy shows short average time to fall asleep during daytime naps and two or more sleep-onset REM periods. Low hypocretin-1 levels in spinal fluid confirm type 1. HLA-DQB1*06:02 testing supports but does not confirm the diagnosis. Other causes of sleepiness such as sleep apnea, insufficient sleep and medication effects are ruled out.",
  tx: "There is no cure, but treatment controls symptoms. Wake-promoting drugs include modafinil and armodafinil, which increase dopamine signaling by blocking its reuptake; solriamfetol, which blocks dopamine and norepinephrine reuptake; and pitolisant, which blocks histamine H3 autoreceptors to boost histamine release. Stimulants such as methylphenidate and amphetamines release dopamine and norepinephrine. Oxybates (sodium oxybate and lower-sodium oxybate), taken at night, act on GABA-B receptors to consolidate deep sleep and reduce cataplexy and sleepiness. Antidepressants such as venlafaxine and fluoxetine suppress REM sleep and reduce cataplexy. Scheduled naps, regular sleep times and safety planning around driving are important.",
  drugs: ["modafinil", "armodafinil", "solriamfetol", "pitolisant", "methylphenidate", "amphetamine-dextroamphetamine", "dextroamphetamine", "venlafaxine", "fluoxetine", "clomipramine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "CHESS: Cataplexy, Hallucinations, Excessive daytime sleepiness, Sleep paralysis, Sleep disruption."], ["Most-tested fact", "Narcolepsy type 1 equals loss of orexin (hypocretin) neurons in the lateral hypothalamus, with low CSF hypocretin."], ["Often confused with", "Seizures or fainting. In cataplexy the person stays fully aware and recovers within seconds to minutes."]],
  red: "Falling asleep while driving or a sudden collapse with injury needs urgent medical care, and any collapse with loss of consciousness, chest pain or trouble breathing needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Narcoleptic Doberman pinschers and Labrador retrievers at Stanford, which could collapse with cataplexy when excited by food, were key to finding the orexin receptor gene.",
  src: ["AASM Clinical Practice Guideline: Treatment of Central Disorders of Hypersomnolence (2021)", "International Classification of Sleep Disorders, 3rd ed.", "Lin L et al. Cell 1999", "Chemelli RM et al. Cell 1999", "Nishino S et al. Lancet 2000"]
},
"tension-headache": {
  name: "Tension-type and cluster headache",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["tension-type headache", "TTH", "tension headache", "cluster headache", "trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia", "suicide headache"],
  what: "Tension-type headache is the most common headache, a dull, band-like pressure on both sides of the head. Cluster headache is rare but extremely painful, with stabbing pain around one eye that comes in daily bouts over weeks.",
  origin: "Ordinary pressing headaches have been described since antiquity. In 1939 Mayo Clinic physician Bayard Horton described a severe one-sided headache with a red, watering eye and called it histaminic cephalalgia, and for years cluster headache was called Horton headache. In 1962 an NIH ad hoc committee classified headache types, using the term muscle contraction headache for what is now tension-type headache. The International Headache Society's 1988 classification created clear criteria for both, updated in ICHD-3 in 2018. Oxygen for cluster attacks was reported in the 1950s and confirmed in a 2009 randomized trial. In 1998 Arne May, Peter Goadsby and colleagues showed with PET scans that the posterior hypothalamus switches on during cluster attacks. In 2019 galcanezumab became the first FDA-approved drug to prevent episodic cluster headache.",
  timeline: [["1939", "Bayard Horton describes histaminic cephalalgia (cluster headache)."], ["1988", "The International Headache Society publishes its first headache classification."], ["1998", "PET imaging shows hypothalamic activation during cluster attacks."], ["2009", "A randomized trial confirms high-flow oxygen aborts cluster attacks."], ["2019", "Galcanezumab is approved for episodic cluster headache."]],
  cell: "Tension-type headache is thought to come from sensitized pain pathways. Tender pericranial muscles send more signals through peripheral nociceptors, and in frequent or chronic forms, neurons in the trigeminal nucleus caudalis and higher centers become sensitized, while descending pain inhibition from the brainstem weakens. Stress, poor sleep and muscle tension feed this loop. Cluster headache involves the trigeminovascular system, the trigeminal autonomic reflex and the hypothalamus. The posterior hypothalamus, which controls circadian rhythms, activates during attacks, explaining the clockwork timing and seasonal bouts. Activation of trigeminal pain fibers releases CGRP and other neuropeptides around the eye's blood vessels, and a reflex through the superior salivatory nucleus drives parasympathetic outflow through the sphenopalatine ganglion, causing tearing and nasal congestion. Swelling of the carotid wall can compress sympathetic fibers, producing a partial Horner syndrome.",
  body: [["Head and scalp", "Tension-type: pressing pain on both sides like a tight band. Cluster: severe, boring pain in or around one eye or temple."], ["Neck and scalp muscles", "In tension-type headache, the muscles of the neck, shoulders and scalp are often tender to touch."], ["Eye and face", "In cluster attacks the eye on the same side tears and turns red, the eyelid droops and the pupil may shrink."], ["Nose", "Cluster attacks cause a runny or blocked nostril on the painful side from parasympathetic overactivity."], ["Behavior", "People with cluster headache are restless and pace or rock, unlike migraine, where people lie still."]],
  symptoms: "Tension-type pain is mild to moderate, pressing and does not worsen with routine activity, because it comes from sensitized muscle and central pain signals rather than intense trigeminovascular activation. It lacks the nausea and strong light sensitivity of migraine. Cluster pain is excruciating and lasts 15 minutes to three hours, often at the same time each day or waking people from sleep, because of hypothalamic clock involvement. Tearing, nasal symptoms and eyelid droop on the same side come from the trigeminal autonomic reflex.",
  dx: "Both are diagnosed clinically using ICHD-3 criteria and a normal neurologic exam. Tension-type headache is bilateral, pressing, mild to moderate and not worsened by activity, with no more than one of light or sound sensitivity and no nausea. Cluster headache requires repeated severe unilateral orbital attacks with at least one autonomic sign or restlessness. MRI is done for a first cluster presentation or any atypical features to exclude pituitary or other lesions. Headache diaries help track patterns and medication use.",
  tx: "Tension-type attacks are treated with acetaminophen, aspirin or NSAIDs such as ibuprofen and naproxen, which block prostaglandin production. Frequent or chronic forms are prevented with amitriptyline, which modulates central pain pathways, plus stress management, sleep, exercise and physical therapy. Overusing pain relievers can cause medication overuse headache. Cluster attacks are stopped with high-flow oxygen through a non-rebreather mask, injectable sumatriptan or nasal zolmitriptan, which act on serotonin 5-HT1B/1D receptors to constrict vessels and block CGRP release. Prevention uses verapamil, with ECG monitoring for heart block, and galcanezumab, which blocks CGRP. A short course of prednisone or an occipital nerve block can bridge until prevention works, and lithium is an alternative.",
  drugs: ["ibuprofen", "naproxen", "acetaminophen", "aspirin", "amitriptyline", "nortriptyline", "sumatriptan", "zolmitriptan", "verapamil", "galcanezumab", "prednisone", "lithium", "lidocaine", "dihydroergotamine", "topiramate"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Cluster headache: one eye, one side, same time each day, can't sit still. Tension-type: a band around both sides, can keep working."], ["Most-tested fact", "First-line abortive therapy for cluster headache is high-flow oxygen; verapamil is the first-line preventive."], ["Who gets it", "Cluster headache is more common in men and in smokers, while tension-type headache is slightly more common in women."], ["Often confused with", "Migraine (one-sided, throbbing, nausea, prefers to lie still in the dark) and trigeminal neuralgia (seconds-long electric jolts)."]],
  red: "A sudden worst headache of life, headache with fever and stiff neck, weakness, confusion, vision loss, a headache after a head injury, or a new headache after age 50 needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Many people with cluster headache can predict their attacks almost to the minute, and bouts often return in the same season each year.",
  src: ["International Classification of Headache Disorders, 3rd ed. (ICHD-3, 2018)", "American Headache Society Guideline for Treatment of Cluster Headache (2016)", "Cohen AS et al. JAMA 2009", "May A et al. Lancet 1998"]
},
"delirium": {
  name: "Delirium",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["acute confusional state", "acute brain dysfunction", "ICU delirium", "encephalopathy"],
  what: "Delirium is a sudden change in attention and thinking caused by an illness, a drug or another stress on the brain. It comes on over hours to days and goes up and down through the day. It is common in older adults in the hospital and is a medical emergency.",
  origin: "Ancient Greek physicians described phrenitis, a feverish confusion, and the Roman writer Celsus used the word delirium in the first century, from the Latin for going out of the furrow. For centuries it was seen mainly as a feature of fever or alcohol withdrawal. In 1959 George Engel and John Romano showed that delirium reflects a global brain malfunction, with slowing on EEG that improves as the cause is treated. In 1990 Sharon Inouye and colleagues published the Confusion Assessment Method (CAM), a quick bedside tool, and in 2001 Wes Ely's team adapted it for ventilated ICU patients (CAM-ICU). In 1999 Inouye's Hospital Elder Life Program showed that simple steps like orientation, sleep, mobility and hearing aids prevent delirium. In 2018 the MIND-USA trial found that haloperidol and ziprasidone did not shorten ICU delirium.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Greek and Roman physicians describe feverish confusion, named delirium by Celsus."], ["1959", "Engel and Romano link delirium to global brain dysfunction seen on EEG."], ["1990", "The Confusion Assessment Method is published."], ["1999", "A trial shows a multicomponent program prevents delirium in older patients."], ["2018", "The MIND-USA trial finds antipsychotics do not shorten ICU delirium."]],
  cell: "Delirium is acute brain network failure. Several mechanisms overlap. A deficit of acetylcholine, needed for attention, explains why anticholinergic drugs cause it. Relative dopamine excess contributes to agitation and hallucinations. Systemic inflammation from infection, surgery or injury sends cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6 and TNF-alpha across a leaky blood-brain barrier, activating microglia that disrupt synapses. Low oxygen, low blood flow and low glucose reduce neuronal energy supply. Electrolyte and metabolic problems, such as low sodium, uremia and liver failure with ammonia, change neuronal excitability. GABAergic drugs such as benzodiazepines and alcohol withdrawal disturb the balance of inhibition and excitation, and poor sleep and melatonin disruption worsen it. Brains with less reserve, as in dementia, older age or prior stroke, fail with smaller stressors.",
  body: [["Brain", "Attention, awareness and orientation fail. Delirium increases the risk of lasting cognitive decline and new or faster dementia."], ["Sleep-wake cycle", "The day-night rhythm breaks down, with daytime sleepiness and nighttime agitation (sundowning)."], ["Behavior", "Hyperactive delirium causes agitation and pulling out lines, while hypoactive delirium causes quiet withdrawal and is often missed."], ["Whole body", "Delirium predicts longer hospital stays, falls, pressure injuries, nursing home placement and higher death rates."]],
  symptoms: "The hallmark is inattention: people cannot follow a conversation or count backward, because the networks that sustain focus are failing. Symptoms come and go over the day as underlying causes and sleep-wake cycles change. Disorganized thinking, rambling speech, disorientation, misperceptions and visual hallucinations reflect disrupted cortical processing. Some people are restless and agitated, others drowsy and slow, depending on the dominant chemical imbalance and cause.",
  dx: "Delirium is a clinical diagnosis. The CAM requires an acute onset with a fluctuating course and inattention, plus either disorganized thinking or an altered level of consciousness. The CAM-ICU and 4AT are used in specific settings. A search for causes follows: vital signs and oxygen, glucose, electrolytes, kidney and liver tests, blood count, urinalysis and cultures, a medication review, and an ECG. Head imaging, EEG or lumbar puncture are done when there are focal signs, head injury, fever with neck stiffness or no clear cause. It is distinguished from dementia, which develops slowly with normal attention early.",
  tx: "Treatment centers on finding and fixing the cause, such as infection, dehydration, low oxygen, pain, urinary retention, constipation or a harmful drug. Deliriogenic drugs such as anticholinergics, benzodiazepines and sedating antihistamines are stopped when possible. Nondrug care is the foundation: frequent reorientation, glasses and hearing aids, family presence, early mobility, a quiet night with daylight by day, and avoiding restraints. Antipsychotics such as haloperidol or quetiapine, which block D2 receptors, are reserved for severe agitation that endangers the person and do not shorten delirium. In the ICU, dexmedetomidine, an alpha-2 agonist, is preferred over benzodiazepines for sedation. Benzodiazepines are the treatment of choice for alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal, thiamine is given for possible Wernicke encephalopathy, and physostigmine may be used for anticholinergic toxicity.",
  drugs: ["haloperidol", "quetiapine", "olanzapine", "dexmedetomidine", "thiamine", "lorazepam", "diazepam", "physostigmine", "melatonin", "dextrose"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Causes are I WATCH DEATH: Infection, Withdrawal, Acute metabolic, Trauma, CNS pathology, Hypoxia, Deficiencies, Endocrine, Acute vascular, Toxins or drugs, Heavy metals."], ["Most-tested fact", "Delirium is acute, fluctuating and centered on inattention. Dementia is slow and progressive with attention preserved early."], ["Often missed", "Hypoactive delirium, the quiet and sleepy type, is more common in older adults and carries a worse prognosis."], ["Beers list", "Anticholinergics, benzodiazepines and sedative-hypnotics are top drug causes in older adults."]],
  red: "Sudden confusion, extreme sleepiness, agitation, or new hallucinations, especially with fever, a fall, a new medicine or an older age, needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Delirium affects about a quarter or more of older adults in the hospital and up to most patients on ventilators in the ICU, yet it is missed in a large share of cases.",
  src: ["Inouye SK et al. Ann Intern Med 1990", "Inouye SK et al. N Engl J Med 1999", "Girard TD et al. MIND-USA. N Engl J Med 2018", "SCCM PADIS Guideline (2018)", "American Geriatrics Society Beers Criteria (2023)"]
},
"neuroleptic-malignant-syndrome": {
  name: "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome",
  sys: "Brain & Nerves",
  aka: ["NMS", "syndrome malin des neuroleptiques"],
  what: "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is a rare, life-threatening reaction to drugs that block dopamine, mostly antipsychotics. It causes high fever, severe muscle rigidity, confusion and unstable vital signs.",
  origin: "Soon after the first antipsychotic, chlorpromazine, entered use in the 1950s, French psychiatrists noticed a dangerous reaction in some patients. In 1960 Jean Delay and colleagues described it after starting haloperidol and named it syndrome malin des neuroleptiques, malignant syndrome of neuroleptics. It was little known in the English-speaking world until the 1980s, when case series and diagnostic criteria spread awareness. A similar syndrome was recognized when Parkinson disease patients suddenly stopped levodopa. Dantrolene, first used for malignant hyperthermia, and bromocriptine, a dopamine agonist, were reported to help in the early 1980s. Better recognition, earlier withdrawal of the drug and supportive care have lowered death rates since then. An international expert panel published consensus diagnostic criteria in 2011.",
  timeline: [["1950s", "Chlorpromazine and other antipsychotics come into wide use."], ["1960", "Jean Delay and colleagues describe the syndrome with haloperidol."], ["1980s", "Diagnostic criteria and treatment with dantrolene and bromocriptine are reported."], ["2011", "An international expert panel publishes consensus diagnostic criteria."]],
  cell: "NMS results from sudden, strong blockade of dopamine D2 receptors, or sudden loss of dopamine signaling when a dopamine agonist or levodopa is stopped. In the hypothalamus, loss of dopamine signaling disrupts the temperature set point and heat loss. In the nigrostriatal pathway, D2 blockade causes severe rigidity, and contracting muscles produce extra heat and break down, releasing creatine kinase and myoglobin. Dopamine blockade also unbalances sympathetic nervous system control, leading to labile blood pressure, fast heart rate and sweating. Muscle injury can cause rhabdomyolysis and acute kidney injury, and high calcium release inside muscle adds to heat production. Risk rises with high-potency first-generation antipsychotics, rapid dose increases, long-acting injections, dehydration, agitation and prior NMS, but it can happen with any antipsychotic and with antiemetics like metoclopramide.",
  body: [["Muscles", "Severe lead-pipe rigidity breaks down muscle tissue, causing very high creatine kinase and rhabdomyolysis."], ["Temperature control", "Hypothalamic dopamine blockade plus heat from rigid muscles causes high fever that can exceed 40 degrees Celsius."], ["Brain", "Confusion, agitation, stupor or coma develop as the brain becomes dysfunctional."], ["Autonomic system", "Blood pressure swings, a fast heart rate, heavy sweating and urinary incontinence reflect sympathetic instability."], ["Kidneys", "Myoglobin from damaged muscle can block kidney tubules and cause acute kidney injury."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms usually build over one to three days. Rigidity comes from D2 blockade in the basal ganglia and usually appears early. Fever follows from hypothalamic dysfunction and muscle heat. Mental status changes reflect brain dysfunction. Fast heartbeat, unstable blood pressure and sweating come from autonomic imbalance. Reflexes tend to be normal or reduced and pupils normal, which helps tell it apart from serotonin syndrome.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical: recent exposure to a dopamine blocker (or withdrawal of a dopamine agonist), high fever, rigidity, altered mental status, high creatine kinase and autonomic instability, as in the 2011 consensus criteria. Labs often show high white count, metabolic acidosis, low serum iron and myoglobin in the urine. Tests rule out infection (such as meningitis), heat stroke, serotonin syndrome, malignant hyperthermia and anticholinergic toxicity.",
  tx: "The causative drug is stopped right away, and if NMS followed stopping a Parkinson drug, that drug is restarted. Supportive care is central: ICU monitoring, aggressive IV fluids, active cooling, and correction of electrolytes and kidney injury. Benzodiazepines such as lorazepam reduce agitation and rigidity. In moderate to severe cases, dantrolene blocks the ryanodine receptor to stop calcium release in skeletal muscle and lower heat production, and bromocriptine or amantadine restore dopamine signaling. Electroconvulsive therapy is used for refractory cases. Antipsychotics are restarted cautiously, usually after at least two weeks, with a lower-potency agent.",
  drugs: ["dantrolene", "bromocriptine", "amantadine", "lorazepam", "diazepam", "normal-saline", "enoxaparin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "FEVER: Fever, Encephalopathy, Vitals unstable, Elevated CK, Rigidity."], ["Most-tested fact", "NMS has lead-pipe rigidity and slow onset over days. Serotonin syndrome has clonus, hyperreflexia, diarrhea and onset within hours."], ["Often confused with", "Malignant hyperthermia, which occurs during anesthesia with volatile agents or succinylcholine and also uses dantrolene."]],
  red: "High fever, stiff muscles, confusion, heavy sweating or a racing heart in someone taking an antipsychotic or an anti-nausea drug like metoclopramide needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Acetaminophen does little for NMS fever, because the heat comes mainly from rigid muscles and a disrupted hypothalamus, not from the prostaglandin-driven fever of infection.",
  src: ["Gurrera RJ et al. International expert consensus criteria. J Clin Psychiatry 2011", "Delay J et al. Ann Med Psychol 1960", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"major-depression": {
  name: "Major depressive disorder",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["depression", "clinical depression", "MDD", "unipolar depression", "major depression", "postpartum depression (with peripartum onset)"],
  what: "Major depressive disorder is a common, treatable medical illness that causes a persistent low mood or loss of interest, along with changes in sleep, appetite, energy, focus and self-worth. It lasts at least two weeks and makes daily life hard. It is not a weakness or a choice.",
  origin: "Ancient Greek physicians called it melancholia, black bile, and saw it as an illness of the body. In the late 1800s German psychiatrist Emil Kraepelin separated recurring depression from other mental illnesses. Modern drug treatment began by accident in the 1950s: iproniazid, a tuberculosis drug, lifted mood and turned out to block monoamine oxidase, and in 1957 Swiss psychiatrist Roland Kuhn reported that imipramine relieved depression. These findings led to the monoamine hypothesis in the 1960s. Electroconvulsive therapy had been introduced in 1938 by Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini. DSM-III in 1980 created today's criteria. Fluoxetine, the first widely used SSRI, was approved in the US in 1987. Newer treatments act faster: esketamine nasal spray (2019) and, for postpartum depression, brexanolone (2019) and zuranolone (2023).",
  timeline: [["1938", "Cerletti and Bini introduce electroconvulsive therapy."], ["1957", "Roland Kuhn reports that imipramine relieves depression."], ["1980", "DSM-III defines major depressive disorder."], ["1987", "The FDA approves fluoxetine."], ["2019", "Esketamine nasal spray and brexanolone are approved."], ["2023", "Zuranolone becomes the first oral drug approved for postpartum depression."]],
  cell: "Depression arises from a mix of genes, stress and brain biology. Heritability is about 35 to 40 percent, spread across many genes of small effect. The monoamine hypothesis holds that low serotonin, norepinephrine and dopamine signaling contributes, but antidepressants raise monoamines within hours while mood improves over weeks, which points to slower changes downstream. Chronic stress overactivates the HPA axis: high cortisol and impaired glucocorticoid feedback reduce BDNF, shrink dendrites in the hippocampus and prefrontal cortex, and reduce new neuron growth. Inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6 and TNF-alpha shift tryptophan toward kynurenine, lowering serotonin synthesis. Brain imaging shows an overactive amygdala and subgenual cingulate cortex and an underactive dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. The rapid effect of ketamine, an NMDA receptor blocker, suggests that a glutamate surge restoring synapses through BDNF and mTOR signaling is a key path to recovery.",
  body: [["Brain and mood", "Altered emotion circuits cause persistent sadness, emptiness or irritability and loss of pleasure (anhedonia)."], ["Thinking", "Reduced prefrontal function slows thinking and impairs concentration, memory and decision-making, sometimes mimicking dementia in older adults."], ["Sleep and energy", "Disrupted circadian and HPA rhythms cause insomnia with early waking or oversleeping, and constant fatigue."], ["Appetite and weight", "Hypothalamic changes reduce or increase appetite, with weight loss or gain."], ["Heart and body", "Depression raises the risk of heart disease and worsens outcomes after a heart attack, partly through inflammation, sympathetic activation and reduced self-care."], ["Pain", "Shared serotonin and norepinephrine pain pathways mean depression often comes with headaches, back pain and other aches."]],
  symptoms: "Low mood and anhedonia reflect underactive reward circuits and reduced dopamine signaling in the ventral striatum. Sleep and appetite change because the hypothalamus and circadian clock are disrupted by stress hormones. Fatigue and slowed movement (psychomotor retardation) or restlessness reflect changes in motor and energy circuits. Guilt, worthlessness and hopelessness come from negative bias in how the brain processes information about the self. Thoughts of death or suicide can arise and are a sign of serious illness that is treatable.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses DSM-5 criteria: at least five of nine symptoms for two weeks or more, including depressed mood or loss of interest, causing distress or impairment. The other symptoms are changes in sleep, appetite or weight, energy, concentration, movement, guilt or worthlessness, and thoughts of death. Screening tools such as the PHQ-2 and PHQ-9 are used in primary care. Labs such as TSH, blood count and sometimes B12 rule out medical causes, and clinicians ask about past mania to rule out bipolar disorder and assess suicide risk.",
  tx: "Psychotherapy (cognitive behavioral therapy, interpersonal therapy, behavioral activation) and antidepressants work about equally for mild to moderate depression, and combining them works best for more severe depression. SSRIs (sertraline, escitalopram, fluoxetine) block the serotonin transporter. SNRIs (venlafaxine, duloxetine) block serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake. Bupropion blocks dopamine and norepinephrine reuptake, mirtazapine blocks alpha-2 and certain serotonin receptors, and vortioxetine and vilazodone combine reuptake blockade with receptor effects. Tricyclics and MAOIs work but have more side effects. For partial response, clinicians add an atypical antipsychotic (aripiprazole, brexpiprazole, quetiapine) or lithium. Esketamine treats treatment-resistant depression, and zuranolone and brexanolone, GABA-A modulators, treat postpartum depression. Electroconvulsive therapy and transcranial magnetic stimulation are used for severe or resistant cases. Antidepressants carry a boxed warning about suicidal thoughts in people under 25, so early weeks are closely monitored.",
  drugs: ["sertraline", "escitalopram", "fluoxetine", "citalopram", "paroxetine", "venlafaxine", "desvenlafaxine", "duloxetine", "levomilnacipran", "bupropion", "mirtazapine", "vortioxetine", "vilazodone", "trazodone", "dextromethorphan-bupropion", "gepirone", "amitriptyline", "nortriptyline", "phenelzine", "selegiline-transdermal", "aripiprazole", "brexpiprazole", "esketamine", "zuranolone"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "SIG E CAPS: Sleep, Interest, Guilt, Energy, Concentration, Appetite, Psychomotor change, Suicidal thoughts, plus depressed mood."], ["Most-tested fact", "Antidepressants take about four to six weeks for full effect, and the risk of suicide can be highest as energy returns before mood improves."], ["Often confused with", "Bipolar depression, where an antidepressant alone can trigger mania, and hypothyroidism, which can mimic depression."], ["Serotonin syndrome", "Combining serotonergic drugs, especially an MAOI with an SSRI, can cause dangerous serotonin syndrome."]],
  red: "Thoughts of suicide or self-harm, a plan, or saying goodbye to people needs immediate help: call or text 988 (the Suicide and Crisis Lifeline) or call 911 for immediate danger.",
  fun: "Imipramine was originally being tested as a possible antipsychotic, and its antidepressant effect was discovered by careful observation of patients.",
  src: ["APA Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients with Major Depressive Disorder (2010)", "VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for MDD (2022)", "DSM-5-TR (2022)", "USPSTF Depression Screening Recommendation (2023)", "Kuhn R. Am J Psychiatry 1958"]
},
"bipolar-disorder": {
  name: "Bipolar disorder",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["bipolar I disorder", "bipolar II disorder", "manic depression", "manic-depressive illness", "cyclothymia (related)"],
  what: "Bipolar disorder is a lifelong but treatable mood illness with episodes of mania or hypomania, periods of unusually high energy, mood and activity, and usually episodes of depression. Between episodes many people feel well.",
  origin: "Greek physician Aretaeus of Cappadocia described mood swings between melancholia and mania in the first or second century. In 1854 French psychiatrists Jean-Pierre Falret and Jules Baillarger separately described circular insanity and double-form insanity, illnesses alternating between mania and depression. Emil Kraepelin grouped these as manic-depressive insanity in the late 1890s. In 1949 Australian psychiatrist John Cade reported that lithium calmed manic patients, the first effective mood stabilizer. Danish psychiatrist Mogens Schou proved its value in trials in the 1950s and 1960s, and the FDA approved lithium in 1970. The term bipolar disorder entered DSM-III in 1980. Valproate was approved for mania in 1995, lamotrigine for maintenance in 2003, and several atypical antipsychotics are now approved for mania and bipolar depression.",
  timeline: [["1854", "Falret and Baillarger describe alternating mania and depression."], ["1949", "John Cade reports lithium for mania."], ["1970", "The FDA approves lithium for mania."], ["1980", "DSM-III introduces the term bipolar disorder."], ["2003", "Lamotrigine is approved for bipolar maintenance."]],
  cell: "Bipolar disorder is among the most heritable psychiatric illnesses, with heritability around 60 to 85 percent across many genes, including CACNA1C (an L-type calcium channel) and ANK3. Brain imaging shows altered connections between the prefrontal cortex and limbic areas such as the amygdala, so emotion is less well regulated. Mania involves increased dopamine signaling in reward circuits, which is why antipsychotics help and why stimulants can trigger mania. Disrupted circadian clock genes and sleep loss can set off episodes. At the cellular level, there is evidence of mitochondrial dysfunction, oxidative stress, abnormal intracellular calcium and altered second messengers. Lithium inhibits inositol monophosphatase (dampening phosphoinositide signaling) and glycogen synthase kinase-3, and raises neuroprotective proteins such as BDNF and Bcl-2.",
  body: [["Mood and energy", "Mania brings elevated or irritable mood and high energy; depression brings low mood, slowness and hopelessness."], ["Thinking", "Racing thoughts and distractibility in mania, slowed thinking in depression, and some lasting attention and memory problems between episodes."], ["Sleep", "During mania people feel rested with very little sleep, and sleep loss can also trigger mania."], ["Behavior and safety", "Impulsive spending, risky sex, reckless driving and substance use during mania can have serious consequences."], ["Whole body", "People with bipolar disorder have higher rates of heart disease, diabetes and obesity, linked to the illness and some medications."]],
  symptoms: "Mania causes a decreased need for sleep, pressured speech, racing thoughts, grandiosity and goal-directed overactivity because reward and arousal circuits are overactive and prefrontal control weakens. Severe mania can include psychosis. Hypomania is milder and does not cause major impairment. Depressive episodes look like major depression but are often marked by oversleeping and slowness. Mixed features, high energy with despair, carry a high suicide risk.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses DSM-5 criteria. Bipolar I requires at least one manic episode lasting a week or more (or any length if hospitalized). Bipolar II requires at least one hypomanic episode lasting four or more days plus at least one major depressive episode. A careful history, often with family input, is key because people usually seek help during depression. Labs and drug screens rule out thyroid disease, stimulant use and steroid effects, and the Mood Disorder Questionnaire can help screen.",
  tx: "Mood stabilizers are the foundation. Lithium reduces mania and depression and lowers suicide risk; it has a narrow therapeutic range, so blood levels, kidney and thyroid function are monitored. Valproate and carbamazepine, antiseizure drugs, treat mania; valproate is avoided in pregnancy because of birth defects. Lamotrigine prevents depressive episodes. Atypical antipsychotics (quetiapine, olanzapine, risperidone, aripiprazole, cariprazine) block D2 and 5-HT2A receptors and treat mania, and quetiapine, lurasidone, cariprazine, lumateperone and olanzapine-fluoxetine are approved for bipolar depression. Antidepressants alone are avoided because they can trigger mania or rapid cycling. Psychotherapy, psychoeducation, a regular sleep schedule and family support reduce relapse. Electroconvulsive therapy helps severe or resistant episodes.",
  drugs: ["lithium", "valproic-acid", "lamotrigine", "carbamazepine", "quetiapine", "lurasidone", "cariprazine", "lumateperone", "olanzapine", "olanzapine-fluoxetine", "risperidone", "aripiprazole", "ziprasidone", "asenapine", "haloperidol", "lorazepam"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "DIG FAST for mania: Distractibility, Irresponsibility, Grandiosity, Flight of ideas, Activity increase, Sleep decreased, Talkativeness."], ["Most-tested fact", "Lithium toxicity causes tremor, vomiting, confusion and ataxia; NSAIDs, thiazides, ACE inhibitors and dehydration raise lithium levels."], ["Lithium side effects", "Hypothyroidism, nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and, with first-trimester exposure, Ebstein anomaly risk."], ["Often confused with", "Borderline personality disorder (mood shifts over hours triggered by stress) and ADHD (chronic, not episodic)."]],
  red: "Thoughts of suicide, extreme risky behavior, not sleeping for days, or losing touch with reality needs urgent help: call or text 988 (Suicide and Crisis Lifeline) or call 911 for immediate danger. Confusion, severe tremor, vomiting or unsteadiness in someone on lithium can be toxicity and needs emergency care.",
  fun: "John Cade first gave lithium to guinea pigs and noticed they became calm and placid before he tried it in manic patients.",
  src: ["CANMAT/ISBD Guidelines for the Management of Bipolar Disorder (2018)", "APA Practice Guideline for Bipolar Disorder", "DSM-5-TR (2022)", "Cade JF. Med J Aust 1949"]
},
"generalized-anxiety": {
  name: "Generalized anxiety disorder",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["GAD", "chronic anxiety", "anxiety disorder"],
  what: "Generalized anxiety disorder is ongoing, hard-to-control worry about many everyday things, such as health, work, money or family, on most days for at least six months. It comes with physical symptoms such as muscle tension, poor sleep and fatigue.",
  origin: "Persistent worry and nervousness were described long ago, and in 1895 Sigmund Freud described anxiety neurosis as a separate condition. In the 20th century anxiety was treated with barbiturates and then meprobamate in the 1950s. In 1960 chlordiazepoxide, discovered by Leo Sternbach, became the first benzodiazepine, followed by diazepam in 1963, which became one of the most prescribed drugs in the world. Concerns about dependence led to newer options: buspirone was approved in 1986, and later SSRIs and SNRIs became first-line. Generalized anxiety disorder was first defined as its own diagnosis in DSM-III in 1980, and DSM-III-R in 1987 set the six-month duration still used. Cognitive behavioral therapy, developed from the work of Aaron Beck in the 1960s and 1970s, became a leading treatment.",
  timeline: [["1895", "Sigmund Freud describes anxiety neurosis."], ["1960", "Chlordiazepoxide, the first benzodiazepine, is introduced."], ["1980", "DSM-III defines generalized anxiety disorder."], ["1986", "Buspirone is approved in the US."], ["1999", "Venlafaxine extended-release becomes the first antidepressant approved for GAD."]],
  cell: "GAD reflects an overactive threat-detection system with weak top-down control. The amygdala responds strongly to uncertain or ambiguous signals, and the prefrontal and anterior cingulate cortex do not regulate it well. The bed nucleus of the stria terminalis, which handles sustained anxiety rather than brief fear, is also implicated. Neurotransmitter changes include reduced GABA-A inhibitory tone, altered serotonin signaling from the raphe nuclei, and increased noradrenergic activity from the locus coeruleus. The HPA axis and corticotropin-releasing factor may be overactive. Heritability is about 30 percent, overlapping with depression, and temperament such as behavioral inhibition, plus stressful life events, add risk. Treatments work on these systems: benzodiazepines enhance GABA-A signaling, SSRIs and SNRIs slowly retune serotonin and norepinephrine circuits, and CBT strengthens prefrontal regulation of the amygdala.",
  body: [["Brain and mind", "Constant worry, trouble concentrating, irritability and feeling on edge come from an overactive threat network."], ["Muscles", "Ongoing muscle tension causes aches, headaches and jaw clenching."], ["Sleep", "Worry and arousal make it hard to fall or stay asleep, and sleep feels unrefreshing."], ["Gut", "Autonomic activation causes nausea, stomach upset, and often irritable bowel symptoms."], ["Heart", "Sympathetic activation can cause a fast heartbeat and sweating, and long-term anxiety is linked to higher cardiovascular risk."]],
  symptoms: "Worry feels uncontrollable because the amygdala keeps signaling threat while the prefrontal cortex cannot shut it down. Restlessness, muscle tension and fatigue come from long-lasting sympathetic and muscular arousal. Poor concentration happens because attention is pulled toward possible threats. Sleep problems come from a brain that stays alert at night. Unlike panic disorder, symptoms are steady and constant rather than sudden attacks.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses DSM-5 criteria: excessive anxiety and worry more days than not for at least six months, hard to control, with at least three of six symptoms (restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension, sleep disturbance), causing distress or impairment. The GAD-7 questionnaire screens for and tracks it. Clinicians look for medical and substance causes such as hyperthyroidism, caffeine or stimulant use, alcohol withdrawal and heart rhythm problems, and for coexisting depression.",
  tx: "Cognitive behavioral therapy is a first-line treatment that teaches people to recognize and challenge worry patterns, tolerate uncertainty and reduce avoidance. First-line medicines are SSRIs (escitalopram, paroxetine, sertraline) and SNRIs (venlafaxine extended-release, duloxetine), which take several weeks to work. Buspirone, a 5-HT1A partial agonist, is another option without dependence risk. Hydroxyzine, an antihistamine, offers short-term relief. Pregabalin, which reduces calcium channel-driven release of excitatory neurotransmitters, is used off-label in the US. Benzodiazepines such as lorazepam, alprazolam and diazepam act within minutes by enhancing GABA-A signaling but are reserved for short-term use because of tolerance, dependence and fall risk. Exercise, sleep and limiting caffeine and alcohol help.",
  drugs: ["escitalopram", "sertraline", "paroxetine", "venlafaxine", "duloxetine", "buspirone", "hydroxyzine", "pregabalin", "lorazepam", "alprazolam", "diazepam", "clonazepam"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Six months of worry plus three of six symptoms: restless, tired, unfocused, irritable, tense and sleepless."], ["Most-tested fact", "First-line drugs for GAD are SSRIs or SNRIs; buspirone takes weeks to work and does not cause dependence."], ["Often confused with", "Hyperthyroidism, which causes anxiety with weight loss and heat intolerance, and panic disorder, which features sudden discrete attacks."]],
  red: "Chest pain, fainting, or trouble breathing needs emergency care (call 911). Thoughts of suicide or feeling unable to stay safe needs immediate help: call or text 988 (Suicide and Crisis Lifeline).",
  fun: "Leo Sternbach's first benzodiazepine sat untested on a shelf for about two years before a lab cleanup led a colleague to test it and discover its calming effect.",
  src: ["APA Clinical Practice Guideline for Anxiety Disorders", "NICE Guideline CG113: Generalised Anxiety Disorder and Panic Disorder in Adults", "DSM-5-TR (2022)", "Craske MG, Stein MB. Lancet 2016"]
},
"panic-disorder": {
  name: "Panic disorder",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["panic attacks", "anxiety attacks", "panic disorder with agoraphobia"],
  what: "Panic disorder means having repeated, unexpected panic attacks, sudden waves of intense fear with a pounding heart, shortness of breath and a sense of doom, plus ongoing fear of having more. It is common and very treatable.",
  origin: "In 1871 American physician Jacob Mendes Da Costa described soldiers in the Civil War with episodes of racing heart, chest pain and breathlessness without heart disease, which he called irritable heart. Similar syndromes were called soldier's heart and effort syndrome in World War I. In the early 1960s American psychiatrist Donald Klein noticed that imipramine stopped panic attacks but not the constant worry between attacks, suggesting that panic is a distinct condition. This insight led DSM-III in 1980 to create panic disorder as its own diagnosis. In 1993 Klein proposed the false suffocation alarm theory, explaining why carbon dioxide can trigger attacks. Exposure-based cognitive behavioral therapy, developed by David Barlow and David Clark in the 1980s, became a first-line treatment.",
  timeline: [["1871", "Jacob Da Costa describes irritable heart in Civil War soldiers."], ["1964", "Donald Klein reports imipramine blocks panic attacks."], ["1980", "DSM-III introduces panic disorder as a diagnosis."], ["1993", "Klein proposes the false suffocation alarm theory."]],
  cell: "Panic attacks come from sudden activation of the brain's fear network. The amygdala, hypothalamus, periaqueductal gray and locus coeruleus fire together, triggering a surge of norepinephrine and a sympathetic fight-or-flight response. People with panic disorder appear to have a hypersensitive alarm system: the amygdala and brainstem chemosensors detect rising CO2 or falling pH and react as if suffocating. Acid-sensing ion channels (ASIC1a) in the amygdala may contribute. Lower GABA-A receptor function and reduced serotonin regulation of these circuits lower the threshold for attacks. The insula, which tracks body sensations, is overactive, so normal sensations like a faster heartbeat are read as danger, creating a loop of fear and more arousal. Heritability is about 40 percent.",
  body: [["Heart", "A surge of epinephrine and norepinephrine causes pounding heartbeat and chest discomfort, often mistaken for a heart attack."], ["Lungs", "Rapid breathing (hyperventilation) lowers CO2, causing breathlessness and a feeling of choking."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Low CO2 makes blood alkaline and lowers ionized calcium, causing tingling around the mouth and fingers and sometimes muscle spasms."], ["Brain", "Dizziness, feelings of unreality and fear of losing control or dying come from intense arousal and reduced brain blood flow from hyperventilation."], ["Daily life", "Fear of new attacks leads many people to avoid places where escape is hard, called agoraphobia."]],
  symptoms: "Attacks peak within minutes because the sympathetic surge is fast and self-limited. Racing heart, sweating, trembling and chest tightness come from adrenaline. Shortness of breath and tingling come from hyperventilation and low CO2. A feeling of doom reflects amygdala activation. Afterward, people worry about the next attack and avoid triggers, which keeps the cycle going. Attacks can happen during sleep, which shows they are not only triggered by thoughts.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses DSM-5 criteria: recurrent unexpected panic attacks, with at least four of 13 physical or mental symptoms peaking within minutes, followed by at least a month of persistent worry about attacks or a change in behavior. Because symptoms mimic serious illness, clinicians often rule out heart disease with an ECG and sometimes troponin, and check for hyperthyroidism, asthma, arrhythmias, low blood sugar, stimulant or caffeine use and substance withdrawal.",
  tx: "Cognitive behavioral therapy with interoceptive exposure, in which people safely bring on body sensations like a fast heartbeat, teaches the brain these sensations are not dangerous and is highly effective. First-line medicines are SSRIs (sertraline, paroxetine, fluoxetine, escitalopram) and the SNRI venlafaxine, which raise serotonin and over weeks calm the fear circuit. Low starting doses are used because these drugs can briefly increase jitteriness. Benzodiazepines such as clonazepam and alprazolam, approved for panic disorder, enhance GABA-A signaling and work quickly but are used cautiously because of dependence and withdrawal. Tricyclics such as imipramine also work. Reducing caffeine and alcohol and practicing slow breathing help.",
  drugs: ["sertraline", "paroxetine", "fluoxetine", "escitalopram", "citalopram", "venlafaxine", "clonazepam", "alprazolam", "lorazepam", "imipramine", "clomipramine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "SSRIs plus CBT are first-line. Benzodiazepines act fast but are not first-line for long-term care."], ["Hyperventilation", "Breathing off CO2 causes respiratory alkalosis, lowering ionized calcium, which explains the tingling lips and fingers."], ["Often confused with", "Heart attack, arrhythmia, hyperthyroidism, pheochromocytoma and asthma. Medical causes are ruled out, especially in a first attack."]],
  red: "Chest pain, shortness of breath, fainting or a first-ever attack, especially in someone with heart risk factors, needs emergency care (call 911), because a heart problem cannot be ruled out without testing. Thoughts of suicide need immediate help: call or text 988.",
  fun: "Panic attacks can be triggered in a lab by breathing air with extra carbon dioxide, and people with panic disorder are much more sensitive to this than others.",
  src: ["APA Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients with Panic Disorder", "NICE Guideline CG113: Generalised Anxiety Disorder and Panic Disorder in Adults", "DSM-5-TR (2022)", "Klein DF. Arch Gen Psychiatry 1993"]
},
"ptsd": {
  name: "Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD)",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["PTSD", "shell shock (historical)", "combat stress", "complex PTSD"],
  what: "PTSD is a condition that can develop after living through or witnessing a terrifying event. The brain stays stuck in danger mode, causing unwanted memories, nightmares, avoidance, negative mood and feeling constantly on guard for more than a month. It is a normal brain's response to abnormal stress and is treatable.",
  origin: "Accounts of lasting distress after combat go back to ancient times, and the Greek historian Herodotus described an Athenian soldier at Marathon who went blind after seeing a comrade killed, without being wounded himself. In the 1800s doctors described railway spine after train crashes and soldier's heart in the Civil War. In World War I British psychologist Charles Myers used the term shell shock in 1915, and in World War II it was called combat fatigue. Advocacy by Vietnam veterans and research on survivors of rape and disasters led the American Psychiatric Association to add post-traumatic stress disorder to DSM-III in 1980. The FDA approved sertraline (1999) and paroxetine (2001) for PTSD. Trauma-focused therapies such as prolonged exposure, cognitive processing therapy and EMDR, developed in the 1980s and 1990s, became first-line treatments.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Herodotus describes a soldier blinded by terror at the Battle of Marathon."], ["1915", "Charles Myers describes shell shock in British soldiers."], ["1980", "PTSD is added to DSM-III."], ["1999", "Sertraline becomes the first FDA-approved drug for PTSD."], ["2013", "DSM-5 moves PTSD to a new trauma- and stressor-related disorders chapter."]],
  cell: "PTSD reflects a fear memory that is too strong and fails to extinguish. During trauma, high norepinephrine and cortisol strengthen amygdala-dependent memory consolidation. In PTSD the amygdala becomes overactive, while the ventromedial prefrontal cortex, which normally signals that a danger has passed, is underactive, and the hippocampus, which places memories in context, is smaller and less active. This means reminders trigger fear as if the event is happening now. The locus coeruleus releases too much norepinephrine, causing hyperarousal and nightmares. Paradoxically, baseline cortisol is often low or normal with enhanced glucocorticoid receptor sensitivity and strong negative feedback on the HPA axis. Genes such as FKBP5, which regulates glucocorticoid receptor sensitivity, interact with childhood adversity to raise risk. Treatments that promote extinction learning and reduce noradrenergic drive target these mechanisms.",
  body: [["Brain and memory", "Intrusive memories, flashbacks and nightmares replay the trauma because fear memories are not filed as past events."], ["Nervous system", "Constant sympathetic arousal causes jumpiness, a strong startle response, irritability and hypervigilance."], ["Sleep", "Nightmares and hyperarousal disrupt sleep, which further weakens fear extinction."], ["Heart and metabolism", "Long-term PTSD is linked to higher rates of high blood pressure, heart disease and diabetes."], ["Mood and relationships", "Negative beliefs, guilt, shame and emotional numbing strain relationships, and depression and substance use often coexist."]],
  symptoms: "Intrusions such as flashbacks and nightmares happen because the amygdala reactivates the fear memory with little hippocampal context. Avoidance of reminders is learned because it briefly reduces distress, but it prevents the brain from learning that reminders are safe. Negative mood, blame and detachment reflect changes in reward and self-related circuits. Hyperarousal, including poor sleep, startle and anger, comes from excess norepinephrine.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses DSM-5 criteria: exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence, followed by symptoms in four clusters (intrusion, avoidance, negative changes in thinking and mood, and changes in arousal and reactivity) lasting more than one month and causing impairment. Symptoms lasting three days to one month are called acute stress disorder. The PC-PTSD-5 screens in primary care, and the PCL-5 and CAPS-5 measure severity. Coexisting depression, substance use and suicide risk are assessed.",
  tx: "Trauma-focused psychotherapies are the most effective treatments: prolonged exposure, cognitive processing therapy and EMDR help the brain process the memory and learn safety. SSRIs (sertraline and paroxetine are FDA-approved) and the SNRI venlafaxine are first-line medicines that reduce intrusion, avoidance and arousal. Prazosin, an alpha-1 blocker that reduces the brain's response to norepinephrine, is used for trauma nightmares, though study results are mixed. Benzodiazepines are avoided because they can interfere with recovery and add risks. Treating co-occurring sleep problems, depression and substance use, and peer and family support, improve outcomes.",
  drugs: ["sertraline", "paroxetine", "venlafaxine", "fluoxetine", "prazosin", "mirtazapine", "trazodone"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Four symptom clusters, all lasting over a month: Intrusion, Avoidance, Negative thoughts and mood, and Arousal."], ["Most-tested fact", "Under one month is acute stress disorder; over one month is PTSD. Trauma-focused therapy is first-line, and benzodiazepines are not recommended."], ["Often confused with", "Adjustment disorder, which follows a stressor that is not life-threatening and has fewer specific symptoms."]],
  red: "Thoughts of suicide or harming others, or feeling unable to stay safe, needs immediate help: call or text 988 and press 1 for the Veterans Crisis Line, or call 911 for immediate danger.",
  fun: "Most people who experience trauma do not develop PTSD; feeling shaken in the first weeks is common, and many recover naturally as the brain processes the event.",
  src: ["VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for PTSD and Acute Stress Disorder (2023)", "APA Clinical Practice Guideline for the Treatment of PTSD (2017)", "DSM-5-TR (2022)", "National Center for PTSD"]
},
"ocd": {
  name: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["OCD", "obsessive-compulsive neurosis (historical)"],
  what: "Obsessive-compulsive disorder causes unwanted, distressing thoughts, images or urges (obsessions) and repeated behaviors or mental acts (compulsions) done to ease the distress. The cycle takes up a lot of time and interferes with life. It is a brain-based illness, not a personality quirk.",
  origin: "Religious writers in the Middle Ages described scrupulosity, tormenting doubts about sin. In 1838 French psychiatrist Jean-Etienne Esquirol described a woman with relentless doubts and rituals, and in 1903 Pierre Janet described psychasthenia with obsessions. Freud wrote about obsessional neurosis in his 1909 case of the Rat Man. The modern era began in the 1960s. In 1966 British psychologist Victor Meyer reported that exposure with response prevention helped patients, and in Europe the tricyclic clomipramine was found to reduce obsessions. Clomipramine was approved in the US for OCD in 1989, and SSRIs such as fluoxetine and fluvoxamine followed in the 1990s. Brain imaging in the 1980s and 1990s linked OCD to overactive circuits between the orbitofrontal cortex and the basal ganglia. Deep brain stimulation received a humanitarian device exemption in 2009, and deep TMS was cleared for OCD in 2018.",
  timeline: [["1838", "Jean-Etienne Esquirol describes obsessive doubt and rituals."], ["1966", "Victor Meyer reports exposure with response prevention."], ["1989", "Clomipramine is approved for OCD in the US."], ["2009", "Deep brain stimulation receives an FDA humanitarian device exemption for severe OCD."], ["2018", "Deep transcranial magnetic stimulation is cleared for OCD."]],
  cell: "OCD involves overactivity in the cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) loop. The orbitofrontal cortex and anterior cingulate, which detect errors and threats, send excess signals to the caudate nucleus. The direct pathway through the basal ganglia is relatively stronger than the indirect pathway, so the thalamus is less filtered and feeds the signal back to cortex, creating a loop of worry and the urge to act. Compulsions briefly quiet the loop, reinforcing them. Serotonin is implicated because serotonin reuptake inhibitors uniquely help, while noradrenergic antidepressants do not. Glutamate excess in the loop and dopamine involvement (which is why antipsychotic augmentation helps some) also play roles. Heritability is about 40 to 50 percent. In some children, abrupt onset after streptococcal infection (PANDAS) is thought to involve antibodies against basal ganglia, a topic still under study.",
  body: [["Brain", "Overactive error-detection circuits generate intrusive thoughts and a feeling that something is not right."], ["Skin", "Excessive washing can cause cracked, raw, irritated skin on the hands."], ["Daily function", "Rituals can take hours each day, disrupting work, school, sleep and relationships."], ["Mood", "Depression is common, and the distress and shame of OCD raise suicide risk."]],
  symptoms: "Obsessions feel intrusive and unwanted because the brain's threat and error signals fire inappropriately, and people often recognize they are excessive. Common themes include contamination, harm, symmetry and taboo thoughts. Compulsions such as washing, checking, counting or mental reviewing relieve anxiety briefly, which reinforces them through negative reinforcement. Avoidance grows over time. Doubt that persists despite checking reflects failure of the brain's sense of completion.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses DSM-5 criteria: obsessions, compulsions or both that are time-consuming (for example more than an hour a day) or cause distress or impairment, and are not due to a substance or another condition. Insight is rated as good, poor or absent. The Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS) measures severity. Clinicians look for related conditions such as tic disorders, body dysmorphic disorder and hoarding, and for depression.",
  tx: "Exposure and response prevention (ERP), a form of CBT, is the most effective therapy: people face feared triggers gradually while resisting compulsions, so the brain learns the anxiety fades on its own. First-line medicines are SSRIs (fluoxetine, fluvoxamine, sertraline, paroxetine, escitalopram), often at higher doses and for longer trials than for depression, because changes in the CSTC loop take time. Clomipramine, a tricyclic with strong serotonin reuptake blockade, is effective but has more side effects. For partial response, low doses of antipsychotics such as risperidone or aripiprazole are added. Severe, treatment-resistant OCD may be treated with deep TMS or deep brain stimulation.",
  drugs: ["fluoxetine", "fluvoxamine", "sertraline", "paroxetine", "escitalopram", "citalopram", "clomipramine", "risperidone", "aripiprazole"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Obsessions are thoughts that come in; compulsions are actions that go out to push them away."], ["Most-tested fact", "OCD is ego-dystonic (unwanted). Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder is ego-syntonic, with perfectionism the person sees as right."], ["Treatment fact", "First-line is ERP and SSRIs; clomipramine is the classic tricyclic for OCD."], ["Associations", "OCD often coexists with tic disorders and Tourette syndrome."]],
  red: "Thoughts of suicide, or being unable to eat, drink or leave home because of rituals, needs urgent help: call or text 988 (Suicide and Crisis Lifeline) or call 911 for immediate danger.",
  fun: "Many people have occasional odd intrusive thoughts; what makes OCD different is how sticky, distressing and time-consuming they become.",
  src: ["APA Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients with OCD (2007)", "DSM-5-TR (2022)", "Stein DJ et al. Nat Rev Dis Primers 2019", "Meyer V. Behav Res Ther 1966"]
},
"schizophrenia": {
  name: "Schizophrenia",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["psychotic disorder", "dementia praecox (historical)", "schizoaffective disorder (related)", "first-episode psychosis"],
  what: "Schizophrenia is a serious, long-term brain illness that changes how a person thinks, perceives and relates to the world. It can cause hallucinations, delusions, disorganized thinking and loss of motivation. With treatment and support, many people live meaningful lives.",
  origin: "In 1893 German psychiatrist Emil Kraepelin described dementia praecox, a psychosis starting in youth with a declining course. In 1908 Swiss psychiatrist Eugen Bleuler coined the word schizophrenia, meaning split mind, to describe a splitting of mental functions, not a split personality, and stressed that outcomes vary. Treatment was limited until 1952, when French doctors Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker showed that chlorpromazine calmed psychosis, leading many people to leave long-stay hospitals. Haloperidol followed in 1958 from Paul Janssen's lab. Arvid Carlsson's research on dopamine supported the dopamine hypothesis, and he shared the 2000 Nobel Prize. Clozapine, the most effective drug for treatment-resistant illness, was approved in the US in 1989 after a landmark trial by John Kane. In 2024 xanomeline-trospium became the first approved antipsychotic that does not block dopamine receptors.",
  timeline: [["1893", "Emil Kraepelin describes dementia praecox."], ["1908", "Eugen Bleuler introduces the term schizophrenia."], ["1952", "Delay and Deniker report chlorpromazine for psychosis."], ["1989", "Clozapine is approved in the US for treatment-resistant schizophrenia."], ["2016", "The C4 complement gene is linked to excessive synaptic pruning in schizophrenia."], ["2024", "Xanomeline-trospium, a muscarinic agonist, is approved."]],
  cell: "Schizophrenia is a neurodevelopmental disorder with heritability around 80 percent, involving hundreds of common gene variants and rare copy number variants such as the 22q11.2 deletion. Environmental factors include prenatal infection and malnutrition, birth complications, childhood adversity, migration and heavy cannabis use in adolescence. The dopamine hypothesis holds that excess dopamine release in the mesolimbic (associative striatum) pathway causes positive symptoms, while reduced dopamine in the mesocortical pathway to the prefrontal cortex contributes to negative and cognitive symptoms. The glutamate hypothesis proposes NMDA receptor hypofunction on GABA interneurons, which disinhibits glutamate and downstream dopamine; NMDA blockers such as PCP and ketamine reproduce symptoms. Complement C4 variants increase synaptic pruning by microglia in adolescence, reducing dendritic spines and gray matter. Imaging shows enlarged lateral ventricles and reduced cortical and hippocampal volume.",
  body: [["Perception", "Hallucinations, most often hearing voices, arise when the brain misattributes internal signals as coming from outside."], ["Thinking and beliefs", "Delusions and disorganized speech reflect abnormal salience: ordinary events feel deeply significant."], ["Motivation and emotion", "Negative symptoms such as reduced motivation, flat affect, less speech and social withdrawal often cause the most disability."], ["Cognition", "Problems with attention, working memory and planning are common and often begin before psychosis."], ["Heart and metabolism", "People with schizophrenia have a shorter life expectancy, mostly from heart disease, diabetes and smoking, partly worsened by some antipsychotics."]],
  symptoms: "Positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions, disorganization) come from excess dopamine signaling that assigns importance to random thoughts and perceptions. Negative symptoms come from weak prefrontal and reward circuit activity. Cognitive symptoms reflect prefrontal and hippocampal dysfunction. Illness often begins in late teens to mid 20s, earlier in men, often after a prodrome of withdrawal and declining function, timed with adolescent synaptic pruning. Many people do not recognize they are ill (anosognosia), which reflects brain changes, not stubbornness.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses DSM-5 criteria: at least two of five symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior, negative symptoms) for a significant part of one month, with at least one being delusions, hallucinations or disorganized speech, and continuous signs of illness for at least six months. Symptoms lasting one to six months are schizophreniform disorder. Workup rules out substances, thyroid disease, brain tumors, infections, autoimmune encephalitis and mood disorders with psychosis, often with labs, a drug screen and sometimes brain imaging.",
  tx: "Antipsychotics are the core treatment. First-generation drugs such as haloperidol strongly block D2 receptors, which treats positive symptoms but can cause extrapyramidal side effects and tardive dyskinesia. Second-generation drugs such as risperidone, olanzapine, aripiprazole, quetiapine, lurasidone and paliperidone also block 5-HT2A receptors or act as D2 partial agonists, with fewer movement effects but more weight and metabolic effects in some. Long-acting injections help keep treatment steady. Clozapine is the most effective option for treatment-resistant illness and lowers suicide risk; blood counts are monitored for neutropenia. Xanomeline-trospium stimulates M1 and M4 muscarinic receptors to reduce dopamine release indirectly. Benztropine treats acute stiffness, and VMAT2 inhibitors treat tardive dyskinesia. Coordinated specialty care for first-episode psychosis, CBT for psychosis, family education, supported employment and housing improve recovery.",
  drugs: ["risperidone", "paliperidone", "olanzapine", "aripiprazole", "quetiapine", "ziprasidone", "lurasidone", "cariprazine", "brexpiprazole", "haloperidol", "chlorpromazine", "clozapine", "xanomeline-trospium", "lumateperone", "olanzapine-samidorphan", "risperidone-long-acting", "paliperidone-palmitate", "aripiprazole-long-acting", "haloperidol-decanoate", "olanzapine-pamoate", "benztropine", "valbenazine", "deutetrabenazine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Timeline by duration: under one month is brief psychotic disorder, one to six months is schizophreniform, over six months is schizophrenia."], ["Most-tested fact", "Clozapine is used for treatment-resistant schizophrenia and reduces suicide but can cause agranulocytosis, myocarditis, seizures and severe constipation."], ["Dopamine pathways", "Mesolimbic excess causes positive symptoms; mesocortical deficit causes negative symptoms; nigrostriatal blockade causes movement side effects; tuberoinfundibular blockade raises prolactin."], ["Stigma check", "Schizophrenia does not mean split personality, and people with schizophrenia are more often victims of violence than perpetrators."]],
  red: "Thoughts of suicide, commands from voices to harm self or others, not eating or drinking, or severe confusion needs urgent help: call or text 988 (Suicide and Crisis Lifeline) or call 911 for immediate danger. High fever with rigid muscles on an antipsychotic can be neuroleptic malignant syndrome and needs emergency care.",
  fun: "Chlorpromazine was first studied by French surgeon Henri Laborit as a way to calm patients before surgery, which led to its trial in psychiatry.",
  src: ["APA Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients with Schizophrenia (2020)", "DSM-5-TR (2022)", "Kane J et al. Arch Gen Psychiatry 1988", "Sekar A et al. Nature 2016", "NIMH"]
},
"adhd": {
  name: "ADHD",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder", "ADD", "attention deficit disorder", "hyperkinetic disorder"],
  what: "ADHD is a common brain development condition that affects attention, activity level and impulse control. It starts in childhood, often continues into adulthood, and can make school, work and relationships harder, but it is very treatable.",
  origin: "In 1798 Scottish physician Alexander Crichton described children with mental restlessness who could not keep their attention on one thing. In 1902 British pediatrician George Still gave lectures describing children with poor self-control. In 1937 Charles Bradley in Rhode Island found by chance that the stimulant Benzedrine improved behavior and schoolwork in children. Methylphenidate was synthesized in 1944 and marketed as Ritalin in the 1950s. The condition was called hyperkinetic reaction of childhood in DSM-II (1968), attention deficit disorder in DSM-III (1980), and attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder in DSM-III-R (1987). The 1999 MTA study showed carefully managed medication improved core symptoms. Atomoxetine became the first nonstimulant approved in 2002, and research showed ADHD often continues into adulthood.",
  timeline: [["1902", "George Still describes children with defects of moral control."], ["1937", "Charles Bradley reports Benzedrine improves children's behavior."], ["1987", "DSM-III-R introduces the name attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder."], ["1999", "The MTA study compares medication and behavioral treatment."], ["2002", "Atomoxetine becomes the first nonstimulant approved for ADHD."]],
  cell: "ADHD is highly heritable, around 70 to 80 percent, with many small-effect genes, including variants near dopamine receptor and transporter genes such as DRD4 and DAT1 (SLC6A3). Premature birth, low birth weight and prenatal exposure to tobacco or alcohol add risk. The core problem is weaker regulation by frontostriatal and frontoparietal networks that support attention, working memory and inhibitory control. Dopamine and norepinephrine signaling in the prefrontal cortex is less efficient: norepinephrine acting on alpha-2A receptors strengthens the right signals, and dopamine acting on D1 receptors filters out noise. Imaging studies show cortical maturation is delayed by a few years on average, especially in prefrontal regions, and the default mode network does not switch off well during tasks. Stimulants increase synaptic dopamine and norepinephrine by blocking DAT and NET or releasing them, and alpha-2A agonists strengthen prefrontal signaling.",
  body: [["Attention and planning", "Weak executive function causes trouble sustaining attention, organizing tasks, managing time and finishing work."], ["Activity and impulse control", "Reduced inhibitory control causes fidgeting, restlessness, interrupting and acting before thinking."], ["Emotions", "Emotional regulation is often harder, with frustration and quick shifts in mood."], ["Sleep", "Delayed sleep timing and trouble winding down are common."], ["Safety and health", "Impulsivity raises the risk of injuries, car crashes and substance use, risks that treatment lowers."]],
  symptoms: "Inattention, such as careless mistakes, losing things and being easily distracted, happens because the prefrontal cortex does not filter and hold information well. Hyperactivity and impulsivity come from weak brakes in frontostriatal circuits. Symptoms change with interest: hyperfocus on engaging tasks happens because novelty and reward boost dopamine, which is why ADHD is not about effort or intelligence. Hyperactivity often fades with age, while inattention persists.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses DSM-5 criteria: at least six symptoms of inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity (five for age 17 and older) for six months, several present before age 12, in two or more settings, with clear impairment. Rating scales completed by parents and teachers (such as the Vanderbilt) or by adults gather information. Presentations are predominantly inattentive, predominantly hyperactive-impulsive or combined. Clinicians screen for vision and hearing problems, learning disorders, sleep problems, anxiety, depression and autism, which can mimic or coexist with ADHD.",
  tx: "For preschool children, parent training in behavior management is first-line. For school-age children, teens and adults, medication combined with behavioral support works best. Stimulants are the most effective drugs: methylphenidate blocks dopamine and norepinephrine reuptake, and amphetamines (including mixed amphetamine salts and lisdexamfetamine) block reuptake and also release these chemicals from nerve endings. They are Schedule II controlled substances because of misuse risk. Nonstimulants include atomoxetine and viloxazine, which block norepinephrine reuptake, and guanfacine and clonidine extended-release, alpha-2A agonists that strengthen prefrontal signaling. Side effects such as reduced appetite, sleep problems and higher heart rate and blood pressure are monitored. School supports such as an IEP or 504 plan, organizational coaching and CBT for adults help.",
  drugs: ["methylphenidate", "dexmethylphenidate", "serdexmethylphenidate", "amphetamine-dextroamphetamine", "lisdexamfetamine", "dextroamphetamine", "atomoxetine", "viloxazine", "guanfacine", "clonidine", "bupropion"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Symptoms must appear before age 12 and in at least two settings, such as home and school."], ["Treatment fact", "Behavioral parent training is first-line under age 6; stimulants are first-line for older children and adults."], ["Often confused with", "Absence seizures, hearing loss, sleep apnea, anxiety, trauma and learning disabilities can all look like inattention."], ["Nonstimulant clue", "Atomoxetine carries a boxed warning for suicidal thoughts in children and teens; guanfacine and clonidine can cause rebound high blood pressure if stopped suddenly."]],
  red: "Chest pain, fainting, a racing or irregular heartbeat, or new hallucinations in someone taking a stimulant needs emergency care (call 911). Thoughts of suicide need immediate help: call or text 988.",
  fun: "Charles Bradley was originally trying to treat headaches after spinal taps when he noticed Benzedrine made children calmer and more focused in school.",
  src: ["AAP Clinical Practice Guideline for ADHD (2019)", "CDC", "DSM-5-TR (2022)", "MTA Cooperative Group. Arch Gen Psychiatry 1999", "Faraone SV et al. Nat Rev Dis Primers 2015"]
},
"autism": {
  name: "Autism spectrum disorder",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["ASD", "autism", "Asperger syndrome (former term)", "pervasive developmental disorder (former term)"],
  what: "Autism spectrum disorder is a lifelong difference in brain development that affects social communication and interaction, and includes repetitive behaviors, focused interests and sensory differences. It is a spectrum: support needs range from minimal to very high.",
  origin: "In 1943 Austrian-American psychiatrist Leo Kanner described 11 children with autistic disturbances of affective contact, and in 1944 Austrian pediatrician Hans Asperger described boys with social differences and intense interests. In the 1950s and 1960s autism was wrongly blamed on cold parenting, the refrigerator mother theory. A 1977 twin study by Susan Folstein and Michael Rutter showed autism is strongly genetic. In 1998 a paper by Andrew Wakefield falsely linked the MMR vaccine to autism; it was retracted in 2010 after being found fraudulent, and many large studies have shown vaccines do not cause autism. DSM-5 in 2013 combined autistic disorder, Asperger syndrome and related diagnoses into one autism spectrum disorder. Risperidone (2006) and aripiprazole (2009) were approved for irritability associated with autism. The neurodiversity movement has emphasized respect, acceptance and support.",
  timeline: [["1943", "Leo Kanner describes early infantile autism."], ["1944", "Hans Asperger describes autistic psychopathy in children."], ["1977", "Folstein and Rutter's twin study shows autism is highly heritable."], ["2010", "The fraudulent 1998 paper linking MMR vaccine to autism is retracted."], ["2013", "DSM-5 unites the diagnoses as autism spectrum disorder."]],
  cell: "Autism is mostly genetic, with heritability estimates around 80 percent. Hundreds of genes are involved, many affecting synapse formation and function (SHANK3, neuroligins NLGN3 and NLGN4, neurexin NRXN1), chromatin remodeling and gene regulation (CHD8, ARID1B) and neuronal signaling (SCN2A). Known single-gene conditions with autism include fragile X syndrome (FMR1), tuberous sclerosis (TSC1/TSC2, overactive mTOR) and Rett syndrome (MECP2). Prenatal factors such as advanced parental age, prematurity and valproate exposure add risk. A leading model is an imbalance of excitation and inhibition in cortical circuits, with altered GABAergic interneuron function. Some children show early brain overgrowth and differences in long-range connectivity, with relatively stronger local and weaker long-distance connections. Altered synaptic pruning and differences in how the brain predicts and filters sensory input may explain sensory sensitivity.",
  body: [["Social communication", "Differences in reading facial expressions, tone and social cues, and in back-and-forth conversation, reflect differences in social brain networks."], ["Language", "Language may be delayed, absent or advanced, and some people communicate best with alternative tools."], ["Behavior and interests", "Repetitive movements, routines and deep focused interests help with regulation and predictability."], ["Senses", "Sensory input such as noise, light, textures and tastes may feel overwhelming or under-registered."], ["Co-occurring conditions", "ADHD, anxiety, epilepsy, sleep problems, gut symptoms and intellectual disability occur more often in autistic people."]],
  symptoms: "Social communication differences, such as limited eye contact, reduced pointing or showing, and difficulty with unwritten social rules, come from differences in how social information is processed. Repetitive behaviors, insistence on sameness and intense interests may reflect a brain that relies more on predictability and finds change stressful. Sensory differences arise from altered sensory filtering. Signs usually appear by age two, though some people, often girls, are diagnosed later because they learn to mask.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses DSM-5 criteria: persistent deficits in social communication and interaction, plus at least two types of restricted, repetitive behaviors or interests, present from early development and causing impairment, with severity levels based on support needs. Pediatricians screen all toddlers with tools such as the M-CHAT-R/F at 18 and 24 months. Specialists confirm with developmental history and observation, often with the ADOS-2. Hearing tests, genetic testing (chromosomal microarray and fragile X testing) and evaluation for co-occurring conditions are recommended.",
  tx: "No medicine changes core autism, and the goal is support, skills and quality of life rather than cure. Early intervention matters: speech-language therapy, occupational therapy, developmental and behavioral approaches such as ABA-based and naturalistic developmental behavioral interventions, social skills support and special education services. Augmentative and alternative communication helps people who speak little. Medicines treat co-occurring problems: risperidone and aripiprazole, which block or partially stimulate D2 receptors, are approved for severe irritability and aggression; stimulants, guanfacine or atomoxetine for ADHD symptoms; SSRIs for anxiety; and melatonin for sleep. Family support and autism-friendly environments are central.",
  drugs: ["risperidone", "aripiprazole", "methylphenidate", "guanfacine", "clonidine", "atomoxetine", "melatonin", "sertraline", "fluoxetine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Autism requires both social communication differences and restricted, repetitive behaviors, with signs from early childhood."], ["Myth buster", "Vaccines do not cause autism. The 1998 paper claiming a link was fraudulent and retracted."], ["Screening fact", "The AAP recommends autism-specific screening at 18 and 24 months."], ["Genetic clue", "Fragile X syndrome is the most common known single-gene cause of autism; genetic testing is offered to every autistic child."]],
  red: "A seizure, sudden loss of skills, self-injury causing harm, or going missing (wandering) needs emergency help (call 911). Thoughts of suicide, which are more common in autistic teens and adults, need immediate help: call or text 988.",
  fun: "CDC tracking has found autism in about 1 in 31 eight-year-olds in recent US data, a rise largely explained by broader criteria and better awareness and screening.",
  src: ["AAP Clinical Report: Identification, Evaluation and Management of Children with ASD (2020)", "CDC Autism and Developmental Disabilities Monitoring Network", "DSM-5-TR (2022)", "Lord C et al. Lancet 2018", "Folstein S, Rutter M. J Child Psychol Psychiatry 1977"]
},
"insomnia": {
  name: "Insomnia",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["insomnia disorder", "chronic insomnia", "sleeplessness", "sleep onset insomnia", "sleep maintenance insomnia"],
  what: "Insomnia is trouble falling asleep, staying asleep or waking too early despite enough chance to sleep, causing daytime tiredness, poor focus or irritability. It is chronic when it happens at least three nights a week for three months.",
  origin: "Sleeplessness has been described since ancient times, and remedies like opium, alcohol and herbal teas were used for centuries. In the 1800s chloral hydrate and bromides became early sleep drugs. Barbiturates arrived in the early 1900s but carried high risk of overdose and dependence. Benzodiazepines replaced them in the 1960s and 1970s. In 1987 Arthur Spielman proposed the 3P model, explaining insomnia through predisposing, precipitating and perpetuating factors, which shaped cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I). Zolpidem was approved in the US in 1992, ramelteon, a melatonin receptor agonist, in 2005, and suvorexant, the first orexin receptor antagonist, in 2014. In 2016 the American College of Physicians recommended CBT-I as first-line treatment, and in 2019 the FDA added a boxed warning to zolpidem, eszopiclone and zaleplon about complex sleep behaviors.",
  timeline: [["1987", "Arthur Spielman proposes the 3P model of insomnia."], ["1992", "Zolpidem is approved in the US."], ["2014", "Suvorexant becomes the first approved orexin receptor antagonist."], ["2016", "The American College of Physicians recommends CBT-I as first-line treatment."], ["2019", "The FDA adds a boxed warning on complex sleep behaviors with Z-drugs."]],
  cell: "Chronic insomnia is best explained by hyperarousal. People with insomnia show higher whole-body metabolism, faster heart rate, higher evening cortisol and ACTH, more high-frequency beta activity on EEG during sleep, and higher brain glucose metabolism during sleep than good sleepers. Wake-promoting systems, including orexin neurons in the lateral hypothalamus and monoaminergic and cholinergic nuclei, stay too active, while the sleep-promoting ventrolateral preoptic nucleus, which uses GABA and galanin to switch off arousal centers, does not fully take over. The sleep drive depends on adenosine buildup during waking, and the circadian clock in the suprachiasmatic nucleus times sleep through melatonin. Conditioned arousal is key: the bed becomes linked with frustration and alertness, and behaviors like long time in bed and napping weaken sleep drive, perpetuating the problem.",
  body: [["Brain and thinking", "Poor sleep impairs attention, memory, reaction time and mood regulation."], ["Mood", "Insomnia is both a symptom and a risk factor for depression and anxiety, and raises suicide risk."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Chronic short sleep with insomnia is linked to higher blood pressure and cardiovascular risk."], ["Metabolism", "Sleep loss affects appetite hormones (leptin and ghrelin) and insulin sensitivity."], ["Safety", "Daytime sleepiness and sleep drugs raise the risk of falls and car crashes, especially in older adults."]],
  symptoms: "Trouble falling asleep happens when the arousal system stays switched on at bedtime, often with racing thoughts. Waking during the night or too early happens when the brain's arousal threshold is low and stress hormones rise in the early morning. Daytime fatigue, irritability and poor concentration result from fragmented, light sleep. Worry about sleep becomes part of the problem by increasing arousal at night.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical using ICSD-3 or DSM-5 criteria: difficulty starting or maintaining sleep, or early waking, at least three nights a week for at least three months, with daytime impairment, despite adequate opportunity to sleep. Sleep diaries over one to two weeks and tools like the Insomnia Severity Index help. Clinicians look for causes and contributors such as depression, anxiety, pain, sleep apnea, restless legs, caffeine, alcohol and medications. Polysomnography is not routine but is used when sleep apnea or another sleep disorder is suspected.",
  tx: "CBT-I is the first-line treatment and works as well as medicines in the short term and better in the long term. It includes stimulus control (using the bed only for sleep), sleep restriction to build sleep drive, cognitive work on worries about sleep, relaxation and sleep hygiene. When medicines are used, options include Z-drugs (zolpidem, eszopiclone, zaleplon), which enhance GABA-A receptor activity; dual orexin receptor antagonists (suvorexant, lemborexant, daridorexant), which block wake-promoting orexin; ramelteon, which activates melatonin MT1 and MT2 receptors to help with falling asleep; and low-dose doxepin, which blocks histamine H1 receptors to help staying asleep. Benzodiazepines such as temazepam are used less because of dependence and fall risk. Sedating antihistamines and trazodone are commonly used but not recommended by AASM for chronic insomnia. Older adults are especially sensitive to sedative side effects.",
  drugs: ["zolpidem", "eszopiclone", "zaleplon", "suvorexant", "lemborexant", "daridorexant", "ramelteon", "doxepin", "temazepam", "triazolam", "trazodone", "melatonin", "mirtazapine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "The 3 Ps of insomnia: Predisposing (temperament), Precipitating (stress), Perpetuating (bad habits that keep it going)."], ["Most-tested fact", "CBT-I is first-line for chronic insomnia, before medication."], ["Older adults", "The Beers Criteria advise avoiding benzodiazepines, Z-drugs and diphenhydramine in older adults because of falls, fractures and confusion."], ["Often confused with", "Delayed sleep phase syndrome (sleeps well but late), sleep apnea and restless legs syndrome."]],
  red: "Sleepwalking, sleep-driving or other complex behaviors on a sleep medicine, severe breathing pauses during sleep, or thoughts of suicide need urgent help; call or text 988 for suicidal thoughts, or call 911 for immediate danger.",
  fun: "Sleep restriction, one part of CBT-I, works by briefly limiting time in bed, which seems backward but builds a stronger sleep drive.",
  src: ["ACP Clinical Practice Guideline: Management of Chronic Insomnia Disorder in Adults (2016)", "AASM Clinical Practice Guideline for Pharmacologic Treatment of Chronic Insomnia (2017)", "International Classification of Sleep Disorders, 3rd ed.", "American Geriatrics Society Beers Criteria (2023)", "Spielman AJ et al. Psychiatr Clin North Am 1987"]
},
"anorexia-nervosa": {
  name: "Anorexia nervosa and bulimia",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["anorexia", "AN", "bulimia nervosa", "BN", "eating disorder"],
  what: "Anorexia nervosa and bulimia nervosa are serious eating disorders. Anorexia involves restricting food leading to low body weight with intense fear of gaining weight. Bulimia involves repeated binge eating followed by actions like vomiting or laxative use. Both are treatable illnesses, not choices.",
  origin: "In 1689 English physician Richard Morton described nervous consumption, wasting without a physical cause, in a young woman. In 1873 English physician William Gull and French physician Charles Lasegue independently described the condition, and Gull named it anorexia nervosa. In the 20th century psychiatrist Hilde Bruch described the need for control and distorted body image. In 1979 British psychiatrist Gerald Russell described bulimia nervosa as an ominous variant of anorexia, and bulimia entered DSM-III in 1980. In the 1980s the Maudsley Hospital in London developed family-based treatment for adolescents with anorexia. Fluoxetine was approved by the FDA for bulimia in the 1990s, still the only approved drug for it. In 2019 a large genome-wide study led by Hunna Watson and Cynthia Bulik found that anorexia has metabolic as well as psychiatric genetic roots.",
  timeline: [["1689", "Richard Morton describes nervous consumption."], ["1873", "William Gull and Charles Lasegue describe anorexia nervosa."], ["1979", "Gerald Russell describes bulimia nervosa."], ["1980", "DSM-III includes bulimia as a diagnosis."], ["2019", "A large genetic study links anorexia to both psychiatric and metabolic genes."]],
  cell: "Eating disorders arise from genetic risk (heritability about 50 to 60 percent), brain biology and environment. In anorexia, altered serotonin signaling may link to anxiety and perfectionism, and altered dopamine reward responses make food less rewarding and restriction reinforcing. Genetics also suggest metabolic factors. Starvation itself then changes the body: low leptin from lost fat suppresses hypothalamic GnRH, causing low estrogen and missed periods; the thyroid shifts to low T3; cortisol and growth hormone rise; and bone formation falls. In bulimia, binge-purge cycles involve reward circuit and impulse control differences. Vomiting loses stomach acid, causing hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, and laxative misuse causes potassium and fluid loss. When nutrition restarts after starvation, insulin release drives phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells, which can cause dangerous refeeding syndrome.",
  body: [["Heart", "Starvation thins heart muscle, slows the heart rate and lowers blood pressure. Low potassium from purging causes arrhythmias and QT prolongation, a leading cause of death."], ["Bones", "Low estrogen, high cortisol and poor nutrition cause bone loss and fractures, which may not fully reverse."], ["Hormones and reproduction", "Hypothalamic suppression causes missed periods, infertility and low testosterone in males."], ["Mouth, teeth and glands", "Vomiting erodes tooth enamel, swells the parotid glands and can cause calluses on the knuckles (Russell sign)."], ["Gut", "Slow stomach emptying and constipation are common in anorexia; vomiting can tear the esophagus."], ["Brain and blood", "Starvation shrinks brain volume and slows thinking, and suppresses the bone marrow, causing low blood counts."]],
  symptoms: "In anorexia, weight loss, feeling cold, fatigue, dizziness and fine body hair (lanugo) result from starvation and the body conserving energy. Distorted body image and fear of weight gain are core psychological features and often intensify with starvation. In bulimia, weight is often normal, so the illness can be hidden; repeated binges with a sense of loss of control are followed by compensatory behaviors, with puffy cheeks, dental erosion, weakness and palpitations from low potassium. Both conditions carry high rates of depression, anxiety and suicide.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses DSM-5 criteria. Anorexia: restriction leading to significantly low body weight, intense fear of weight gain or behavior preventing it, and disturbed body image, with restricting or binge-purge subtypes. Bulimia: recurrent binge eating with loss of control and inappropriate compensatory behaviors, both at least once a week for three months, with self-worth tied to shape and weight. The SCOFF questionnaire can screen. Workup includes vital signs including orthostatic changes, weight and BMI, electrolytes (especially potassium, phosphate, magnesium), kidney and liver tests, blood counts, an ECG, and bone density testing in prolonged anorexia.",
  tx: "Care uses a team of medical, mental health and nutrition professionals. In anorexia, nutritional rehabilitation restores weight, with close monitoring of phosphate, potassium and magnesium and supplements such as potassium phosphate and thiamine to prevent refeeding syndrome. Medically unstable patients are treated in the hospital. For adolescents, family-based treatment is first-line, and adults benefit from enhanced CBT and other specialized therapies. No drug is approved for anorexia; olanzapine is sometimes used off-label to help weight gain and reduce anxious thinking. For bulimia, CBT is first-line and fluoxetine, an SSRI, reduces binge-purge cycles. Bupropion is avoided in eating disorders because of a higher seizure risk. Correcting electrolytes and addressing depression, anxiety and substance use are part of care.",
  drugs: ["fluoxetine", "olanzapine", "potassium-chloride", "potassium-phosphate", "sodium-phosphate", "magnesium-sulfate", "thiamine", "cholecalciferol", "calcium-carbonate"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Bulimia vomiting causes metabolic alkalosis with low potassium and chloride; laxatives cause low potassium and can cause acidosis."], ["Most-tested fact", "Refeeding syndrome is defined by low phosphate, which can cause heart failure, arrhythmias, weakness and seizures as nutrition restarts."], ["Drug fact", "Fluoxetine is the only FDA-approved drug for bulimia. Bupropion is contraindicated because of seizure risk."], ["Often confused with", "Binge-eating disorder (binges without purging), which is treated with CBT and can be treated with lisdexamfetamine."]],
  red: "Fainting, chest pain, a very slow or irregular heartbeat, confusion, vomiting blood or severe weakness needs emergency care (call 911). Thoughts of suicide need immediate help: call or text 988 (Suicide and Crisis Lifeline).",
  fun: "Anorexia nervosa has one of the highest death rates of any mental illness, yet with early specialized treatment many people make a full recovery.",
  src: ["APA Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients with Eating Disorders (2023)", "DSM-5-TR (2022)", "Watson HJ et al. Nat Genet 2019", "Russell G. Psychol Med 1979", "Society for Adolescent Health and Medicine Position Paper (2022)"]
}
});

;
DB.DIS = DB.DIS || {};
Object.assign(DB.DIS, {
"alcohol-use-disorder": {
  name: "Alcohol use disorder and withdrawal",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["AUD", "alcoholism", "alcohol dependence", "alcohol withdrawal syndrome", "delirium tremens", "DTs"],
  what: "Alcohol use disorder is a treatable brain condition in which a person has trouble controlling drinking even when it causes harm. When someone who drinks heavily stops suddenly, the brain can become overexcited, causing withdrawal that ranges from shakiness to seizures and delirium.",
  origin: "Heavy drinking has been described since ancient times, but it was usually treated as a moral failing. In 1784 American physician Benjamin Rush wrote that habitual drunkenness was a disease that could be treated. In 1849 Swedish physician Magnus Huss coined the term alcoholism to describe the physical harm of long-term drinking. Carl Wernicke in 1881 and Sergei Korsakoff in the late 1880s described the brain damage now linked to thiamine deficiency in heavy drinkers. Alcoholics Anonymous was founded in 1935, spreading the idea of peer support. In 1960 E. M. Jellinek published The Disease Concept of Alcoholism. Medicines followed: disulfiram in the late 1940s and early 1950s, naltrexone for alcohol in 1994 and acamprosate in the US in 2004. In 2013 the DSM-5 combined alcohol abuse and alcohol dependence into a single diagnosis, alcohol use disorder, graded mild, moderate or severe.",
  timeline: [["1784", "Benjamin Rush describes habitual drunkenness as a disease."], ["1849", "Magnus Huss introduces the term alcoholism."], ["1935", "Alcoholics Anonymous is founded in Akron, Ohio."], ["1994", "The FDA approves naltrexone for alcohol dependence."], ["2004", "Acamprosate is approved in the United States."], ["2013", "DSM-5 defines alcohol use disorder as one diagnosis with severity levels."]],
  cell: "Alcohol (ethanol) acts on many brain receptors. It boosts the calming GABA-A receptor, which lets more chloride into neurons and quiets them, and it blocks the excitatory NMDA glutamate receptor. It also triggers release of endorphins and dopamine in the reward pathway, from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens, which reinforces drinking. With repeated heavy use the brain adapts: GABA-A receptors become fewer and change their subunits, so they respond less, while NMDA receptors increase. When alcohol is suddenly removed, the brain is left with too little inhibition and too much glutamate, plus a surge of norepinephrine from the locus coeruleus. This causes tremor, a racing heart, sweating, seizures and, at worst, delirium tremens. Each withdrawal episode can make the next one worse, a process called kindling. In the liver, ethanol breakdown by alcohol dehydrogenase produces acetaldehyde and excess NADH, which promotes fat buildup, inflammation and scarring. Poor diet and alcohol together deplete thiamine, a cofactor for glucose metabolism, which injures vulnerable brain regions such as the mammillary bodies and thalamus.",
  body: [["Brain", "Reward circuits change, driving craving. Thiamine deficiency can cause Wernicke encephalopathy (confusion, eye movement problems, unsteady gait) and Korsakoff syndrome (lasting memory loss). Withdrawal can cause seizures and delirium."], ["Liver", "Ethanol metabolism leads to fatty liver, alcoholic hepatitis and eventually cirrhosis, with jaundice, fluid in the abdomen and bleeding risk."], ["Stomach and pancreas", "Alcohol irritates the stomach lining and can trigger acute and chronic pancreatitis, causing severe belly pain and poor digestion."], ["Heart and vessels", "Long-term heavy drinking raises blood pressure, can weaken the heart muscle (alcoholic cardiomyopathy) and can trigger atrial fibrillation."], ["Blood and nutrition", "Alcohol suppresses bone marrow and is linked to low folate, large red cells, low platelets, and low magnesium, potassium and phosphate."], ["Mental health", "Depression, anxiety and sleep problems are common and can both drive and result from heavy drinking."]],
  symptoms: "Craving and loss of control come from changes in the brain's reward and stress circuits. Withdrawal symptoms appear because the brain has adapted to constant alcohol and is suddenly left overexcited. Early on, usually within hours to a day after the last drink, people have tremor, sweating, anxiety, nausea and a fast heart rate from the norepinephrine surge. Withdrawal seizures most often occur within the first two days. Alcoholic hallucinosis can cause hallucinations with a clear mind. Delirium tremens usually appears about two to four days after stopping and combines confusion, hallucinations, fever and unstable blood pressure and heart rate.",
  dx: "Alcohol use disorder is diagnosed with DSM-5 criteria, such as drinking more than intended, failed attempts to cut down, craving, giving up activities, continued use despite harm, tolerance and withdrawal. Two or three criteria mean mild, four or five moderate and six or more severe. Screening tools such as AUDIT-C are used in clinics. Withdrawal severity is often scored with the CIWA-Ar scale. Labs may show raised GGT, an AST higher than ALT, large red blood cells and low magnesium or phosphate. Other causes of confusion, such as low blood sugar, head injury, infection and hepatic encephalopathy, are ruled out.",
  tx: "Withdrawal is treated by replacing the missing calming signal. Benzodiazepines such as chlordiazepoxide, diazepam and lorazepam enhance GABA-A receptors; lorazepam is often chosen when the liver is badly damaged. Phenobarbital, another GABA-A enhancer, is used in severe or resistant cases, and dexmedetomidine can help control agitation as an add-on in the ICU. Thiamine is given before or with glucose to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy, along with folate, magnesium and fluids. For long-term recovery, naltrexone blocks opioid receptors and reduces the reward from drinking, acamprosate is thought to calm overactive glutamate signaling, and disulfiram blocks aldehyde dehydrogenase so alcohol causes an unpleasant reaction. Gabapentin and topiramate are used off-label. Counseling, mutual support groups and treating mental health conditions are central. The SAMHSA National Helpline (1-800-662-4357) offers free, confidential treatment referral.",
  drugs: ["chlordiazepoxide", "diazepam", "lorazepam", "phenobarbital", "dexmedetomidine", "thiamine", "folic-acid", "magnesium-sulfate", "naltrexone", "naltrexone-extended-release-injection", "acamprosate", "disulfiram", "gabapentin", "topiramate", "clonidine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Give thiamine before or with glucose in a malnourished heavy drinker to avoid triggering or worsening Wernicke encephalopathy."], ["Memory trick", "Wernicke triad, COAT: Confusion, Ophthalmoplegia, Ataxia, Thiamine to treat."], ["Withdrawal clock", "Tremor in hours, seizures in the first two days, delirium tremens around days two to four."], ["Often confused with", "Alcoholic hallucinosis has hallucinations with normal vital signs and a clear mind; delirium tremens has confusion and unstable vital signs."]],
  red: "A withdrawal seizure, confusion, hallucinations, high fever, a racing heart, or vomiting blood needs emergency care (call 911). Delirium tremens can be fatal without treatment. For help finding treatment, the SAMHSA National Helpline is 1-800-662-4357.",
  fun: "Disulfiram's effect on drinking was noticed by Danish researchers who were testing it for another use and became ill after drinking alcohol.",
  src: ["DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013)", "APA Practice Guideline for the Pharmacological Treatment of Patients With Alcohol Use Disorder (2018)", "ASAM Clinical Practice Guideline on Alcohol Withdrawal Management (2020)", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "NIAAA"]
},
"opioid-use-disorder": {
  name: "Opioid use disorder",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["OUD", "opioid addiction", "opioid dependence", "opioid withdrawal"],
  what: "Opioid use disorder is a treatable medical condition in which a person keeps using opioids, such as prescription pain pills, heroin or fentanyl, despite harm. It changes brain circuits for reward and stress, and it carries a high risk of fatal overdose.",
  origin: "Opium from poppies has been used for pain and pleasure since ancient times, and its habit-forming power was well known. In the early 1800s German pharmacist Friedrich Serturner isolated morphine. The hypodermic syringe in the mid-1800s and the marketing of heroin by Bayer in 1898 spread addiction. The US Harrison Narcotic Act of 1914 put opioids under federal control. In the 1960s Vincent Dole and Marie Nyswander in New York showed that methadone maintenance helped people with heroin addiction stabilize their lives. Naloxone was approved in 1971 to reverse overdose. In the 1970s scientists including Candace Pert and Solomon Snyder found opioid receptors in the brain. Buprenorphine for opioid use disorder was approved in 2002. Since the 1990s the US has faced waves of overdose deaths, first from prescription opioids, then heroin, and since about 2013 illicit fentanyl. At the end of 2022 Congress removed the special federal waiver once needed to prescribe buprenorphine, and in 2023 naloxone nasal spray became available over the counter.",
  timeline: [["1898", "Bayer markets heroin as a cough medicine."], ["1914", "The Harrison Narcotic Act regulates opioids in the United States."], ["1960s", "Dole and Nyswander show the benefits of methadone maintenance."], ["1971", "Naloxone is approved by the FDA."], ["2002", "Buprenorphine is approved for treating opioid use disorder."], ["2023", "Naloxone nasal spray is approved for sale over the counter."]],
  cell: "Opioids bind mu opioid receptors, which are G protein-coupled receptors linked to Gi. Activation lowers cyclic AMP, opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels, so neurons fire less and release less transmitter. In the ventral tegmental area, opioids quiet GABA interneurons that normally brake dopamine neurons, so dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens rises and creates strong reward and learning signals. With repeated use, receptors become desensitized and internalized, and neurons boost their cyclic AMP machinery to compensate, which produces tolerance and physical dependence. When opioids stop, this compensation is unopposed. The locus coeruleus fires excessively and floods the body with norepinephrine, causing withdrawal. Long-term use also shifts the brain's stress systems and weakens prefrontal control, which keeps craving alive long after withdrawal ends. In overdose, mu receptor activation in brainstem breathing centers, including the pre-Botzinger complex, blunts the response to carbon dioxide, so breathing slows and can stop.",
  body: [["Brain", "Reward and stress circuits change, causing intense craving, loss of control and a high risk of relapse even after long periods without use."], ["Breathing", "Opioids depress the brainstem breathing centers. In overdose, breathing slows or stops, leading to low oxygen, brain injury and death."], ["Eyes", "Opioids activate the parasympathetic pupil pathway, causing pinpoint pupils; withdrawal causes wide pupils."], ["Gut", "Mu receptors in the gut slow movement, causing constipation; withdrawal causes cramps, nausea, vomiting and diarrhea."], ["Skin and infections", "Injection use can cause abscesses, cellulitis, endocarditis, and spread of HIV and hepatitis C through shared equipment."], ["Hormones", "Long-term opioid use can lower sex hormones, causing low libido and irregular periods."]],
  symptoms: "Intoxication causes euphoria, drowsiness, small pupils and slow breathing because mu receptors are activated in reward, arousal and breathing centers. Withdrawal is the mirror image: the norepinephrine surge and gut rebound cause anxiety, yawning, tearing, runny nose, sweating, goosebumps, wide pupils, muscle aches, cramps, vomiting and diarrhea. Withdrawal is very uncomfortable but is rarely life-threatening in otherwise healthy adults. The classic overdose triad is pinpoint pupils, unresponsiveness and slow or absent breathing.",
  dx: "Opioid use disorder is diagnosed with DSM-5 criteria, including using more than intended, craving, failed attempts to cut down, continued use despite harm, and tolerance or withdrawal (these last two do not count when a person takes opioids exactly as prescribed). Severity is graded by the number of criteria. Withdrawal is often scored with the Clinical Opiate Withdrawal Scale (COWS). Urine drug tests support the history, though standard immunoassays may miss fentanyl and other synthetic opioids, which need specific tests. Overdose is a clinical diagnosis based on the triad and the response to naloxone.",
  tx: "Medications for opioid use disorder are the core of treatment and lower the risk of death. Methadone is a long-acting full mu agonist given through opioid treatment programs. Buprenorphine is a high-affinity partial agonist that eases craving and withdrawal with a ceiling on breathing depression; it is often combined with naloxone to discourage injection, and it also comes as a monthly injection. Because buprenorphine displaces other opioids, starting it too early can trigger precipitated withdrawal. Extended-release naltrexone blocks mu receptors after a person has fully withdrawn. Lofexidine and clonidine, alpha-2 agonists, calm the norepinephrine surge of withdrawal, while loperamide and ondansetron ease diarrhea and nausea. Naloxone and nalmefene reverse overdose by displacing opioids from the receptor. Counseling, harm reduction services, treatment of infections and mental health care support recovery. The SAMHSA National Helpline (1-800-662-4357) offers free, confidential referral.",
  drugs: ["buprenorphine-naloxone", "buprenorphine-extended-release-injection", "methadone", "naltrexone-extended-release-injection", "naloxone", "nalmefene", "lofexidine", "clonidine", "loperamide", "ondansetron"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Opioid overdose triad: pinpoint pupils, unresponsiveness and respiratory depression. Naloxone reverses it, but it can wear off before long-acting opioids do."], ["Memory trick", "Withdrawal is everything leaking and wide: tearing, runny nose, sweating, diarrhea, yawning and big pupils."], ["Often confused with", "Precipitated withdrawal happens when buprenorphine or naltrexone is given while full agonists still occupy the receptors."], ["Key fact", "Withdrawal is miserable but rarely deadly; overdose and relapse after lost tolerance are the major dangers."]],
  red: "Someone who will not wake up, has very slow or no breathing, blue lips or pinpoint pupils needs emergency care (call 911). Naloxone can be given while waiting for help. For treatment referral, the SAMHSA National Helpline is 1-800-662-4357.",
  fun: "The word morphine comes from Morpheus, the Greek god of dreams.",
  src: ["DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013)", "ASAM National Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Opioid Use Disorder (2020 Focused Update)", "SAMHSA TIP 63: Medications for Opioid Use Disorder", "CDC", "Dole VP, Nyswander M. JAMA 1965"]
},
"tobacco-use": {
  name: "Tobacco and nicotine dependence",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["tobacco use disorder", "nicotine addiction", "smoking", "vaping dependence"],
  what: "Tobacco and nicotine dependence is an addiction to nicotine, the chemical in cigarettes, cigars, smokeless tobacco and most vapes. Nicotine drives the habit, while tobacco smoke delivers the toxins that cause cancer, lung disease and heart disease.",
  origin: "Indigenous peoples of the Americas used tobacco for centuries in ceremony and medicine. After European contact in the late 1400s, tobacco spread worldwide. In 1560 French diplomat Jean Nicot sent tobacco to the French court, and the plant genus Nicotiana and later nicotine were named after him. German chemists isolated nicotine in 1828. Mass-produced cigarettes in the late 1800s made smoking cheap and common. In 1950 Richard Doll and Austin Bradford Hill in the UK, and Ernst Wynder and Evarts Graham in the US, published studies linking smoking to lung cancer. The 1964 US Surgeon General's report officially stated that smoking causes lung cancer, and the 1988 report declared nicotine addictive. Nicotine gum, patches and other replacement products arrived in the 1980s and 1990s, bupropion was approved for quitting in 1997 and varenicline in 2006. E-cigarettes spread in the 2010s, and in 2019 the US raised the minimum age to buy tobacco to 21.",
  timeline: [["1560", "Jean Nicot sends tobacco to the French court."], ["1828", "Nicotine is isolated from tobacco by German chemists."], ["1950", "Doll and Hill, and Wynder and Graham, link smoking to lung cancer."], ["1964", "The US Surgeon General's report concludes smoking causes lung cancer."], ["1988", "The Surgeon General's report declares nicotine addictive."], ["2006", "Varenicline is approved to help people quit smoking."]],
  cell: "Nicotine reaches the brain within seconds of inhaling and binds nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, especially the alpha4-beta2 type on dopamine neurons in the ventral tegmental area. This releases dopamine in the nucleus accumbens, producing reward and reinforcing the habit, along with release of norepinephrine, glutamate and other transmitters. Nicotine quickly desensitizes these receptors, and with repeated exposure the brain makes more of them. Overnight, receptors recover, so the first cigarette of the day feels strongest and withdrawal craving builds between doses. The harm of smoking comes mostly from other chemicals in smoke. Carcinogens such as polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons and tobacco-specific nitrosamines are activated by liver and lung enzymes and form DNA adducts, leading to mutations in genes such as TP53 and KRAS. Oxidants and smoke particles recruit neutrophils and macrophages that release elastase, overwhelming alpha-1 antitrypsin and destroying alveolar walls (emphysema). Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin, and oxidative stress damages endothelium, promoting atherosclerosis and clotting.",
  body: [["Brain", "Nicotine changes reward circuits, creating craving, withdrawal irritability and strong habits tied to daily routines."], ["Lungs", "Smoke causes COPD (emphysema and chronic bronchitis), worsens asthma and is the leading cause of lung cancer."], ["Heart and vessels", "Smoking damages the vessel lining and promotes clots, sharply raising the risk of heart attack, stroke and peripheral artery disease."], ["Cancer risk", "Tobacco causes cancers of the mouth, throat, larynx, esophagus, bladder, kidney, pancreas, cervix and others because carcinogens travel through the body."], ["Pregnancy", "Smoking in pregnancy raises the risk of low birth weight, preterm birth, placental problems and sudden infant death."], ["Skin, teeth and healing", "Reduced blood flow and toxins cause gum disease, early skin aging and slower wound and bone healing."]],
  symptoms: "Craving, restlessness and the urge to smoke after waking come from nicotinic receptors recovering and signaling a dopamine deficit. Withdrawal causes irritability, anxiety, trouble concentrating, increased appetite and poor sleep, usually peaking in the first week. Smoking-related diseases cause their own symptoms: chronic cough and sputum from airway inflammation, shortness of breath from destroyed alveoli, and chest pain or leg pain from narrowed arteries. In nicotine poisoning, often from children swallowing e-liquid, overstimulation of nicotinic receptors causes vomiting, sweating, a fast heart rate and, at high exposure, seizures and weakness.",
  dx: "Tobacco use disorder is diagnosed with DSM-5 criteria such as craving, failed attempts to quit, using more than intended, tolerance and withdrawal. Clinicians ask about every form of tobacco and nicotine, including vapes. Dependence strength is often estimated by how soon a person smokes after waking or with the Fagerstrom test. Exhaled carbon monoxide or cotinine levels can confirm use. Clinicians also screen for smoking-related disease, such as with spirometry and, for eligible people, low-dose CT lung cancer screening.",
  tx: "Treatment combines medication with counseling, which works better than either alone. Nicotine replacement therapy (patch, gum, lozenge, inhaler, nasal spray) supplies nicotine without smoke, easing withdrawal; combining a long-acting patch with a fast-acting form is common. Varenicline is a partial agonist at alpha4-beta2 receptors: it releases a little dopamine to reduce craving while blocking nicotine's full reward. Bupropion blocks reuptake of dopamine and norepinephrine and also blocks nicotinic receptors, reducing craving and withdrawal. Behavioral support includes brief advice, quitlines (1-800-QUIT-NOW), apps and setting a quit date. Relapse is common and is treated as part of recovery, not failure. The SAMHSA National Helpline (1-800-662-4357) also provides referral.",
  drugs: ["varenicline", "nicotine", "bupropion"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Varenicline is a partial agonist at alpha4-beta2 nicotinic receptors; bupropion lowers the seizure threshold and is avoided in seizure and eating disorders."], ["Key fact", "Smoking is the leading preventable cause of death in the United States."], ["Memory trick", "Nicotine is the hook; smoke is the harm."], ["Often confused with", "Nicotine itself is not the main carcinogen; tar, nitrosamines and other smoke chemicals cause cancer."]],
  red: "Chest pain, sudden weakness or trouble speaking, or coughing up blood needs emergency care (call 911). A child who swallows e-liquid or nicotine products needs urgent help: call Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) or 911 if the child is very sick. For treatment referral, the SAMHSA National Helpline is 1-800-662-4357.",
  fun: "The word nicotine honors Jean Nicot, a French ambassador to Portugal who promoted tobacco as a medicine in the 1500s.",
  src: ["US Surgeon General's Reports on Smoking and Health (1964, 1988, 2020)", "USPSTF Recommendation: Tobacco Smoking Cessation in Adults (2021)", "DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013)", "Doll R, Hill AB. BMJ 1950", "CDC"]
},
"serotonin-syndrome": {
  name: "Serotonin syndrome",
  sys: "Mental Health",
  aka: ["serotonin toxicity", "serotonergic toxicity"],
  what: "Serotonin syndrome is a drug reaction caused by too much serotonin activity in the nervous system, usually after taking two or more drugs that raise serotonin or a large overdose. It causes agitation, sweating, shaking and muscle jerks, and severe cases can cause dangerous fever.",
  origin: "Doctors noticed in the 1950s and 1960s that some patients taking monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) became agitated, shaky and feverish when given other drugs or the amino acid tryptophan, the building block of serotonin. In 1960 John Oates and Albert Sjoerdsma reported neurologic toxicity from tryptophan in people on an MAOI. In 1984 the death of 18-year-old Libby Zion in a New York hospital, after she received meperidine while taking the MAOI phenelzine, drew national attention and later led to limits on resident doctors' working hours. In 1991 Harvey Sternbach proposed the first diagnostic criteria. In 2003 Dunkley and colleagues published the Hunter Serotonin Toxicity Criteria, which focus on clonus and are now preferred. As SSRIs, SNRIs and tramadol became widely used, serotonin syndrome became a well-known emergency diagnosis.",
  timeline: [["1960", "Oates and Sjoerdsma describe toxicity from tryptophan given with an MAOI."], ["1984", "The Libby Zion case links meperidine and phenelzine to a fatal reaction."], ["1991", "Sternbach publishes the first diagnostic criteria."], ["2003", "The Hunter Serotonin Toxicity Criteria are published."], ["2005", "Boyer and Shannon review serotonin syndrome in the New England Journal of Medicine."]],
  cell: "Serotonin (5-HT) is made from tryptophan, released from nerve endings, taken back up by the serotonin transporter (SERT) and broken down by monoamine oxidase A. Serotonin syndrome happens when serotonin builds up in the synapse in the brainstem, spinal cord and peripheral nerves. Drugs can do this in several ways: more production (tryptophan), more release (amphetamines, MDMA), blocked reuptake (SSRIs, SNRIs, tricyclics, tramadol, meperidine, dextromethorphan, methadone, fentanyl), blocked breakdown (MAOIs, linezolid, methylene blue), or direct receptor stimulation (some triptans, LSD). Overstimulation of 5-HT2A receptors is thought to drive the dangerous features such as muscle rigidity and high fever, with 5-HT1A also contributing. Excess serotonin in spinal motor circuits causes hyperreflexia and clonus, and autonomic stimulation causes sweating, fast heart rate and diarrhea. In severe cases, sustained muscle contraction generates heat, leading to hyperthermia, rhabdomyolysis, metabolic acidosis, kidney injury and disseminated intravascular coagulation.",
  body: [["Brain", "Excess serotonin causes restlessness, anxiety, agitation and, in severe cases, confusion and delirium."], ["Muscles and nerves", "Overactive spinal reflexes cause tremor, hyperreflexia and clonus, often worse in the legs. Severe cases cause rigidity and muscle breakdown."], ["Autonomic system", "Overstimulation causes sweating, fast heart rate, high blood pressure, dilated pupils and flushing."], ["Gut", "Serotonin receptors in the gut increase movement, causing diarrhea, cramps and hyperactive bowel sounds."], ["Temperature", "Constant muscle activity creates heat. Temperature can rise quickly and become life-threatening."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms usually start within hours, often within 24 hours, of starting a new drug, raising a dose or combining drugs. The classic triad is mental status change (agitation, anxiety), autonomic overactivity (sweating, fast heart rate, high temperature, dilated pupils, diarrhea) and neuromuscular overactivity (tremor, hyperreflexia, clonus, rigidity). Clonus, including rhythmic eye movements called ocular clonus, is the most specific sign because it reflects overactive serotonin signaling in spinal and brainstem motor pathways. Fever comes from muscle activity, not from the brain's thermostat, which is why fever reducers do not help.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical; no blood test confirms it. The Hunter criteria require exposure to a serotonergic drug plus one of: spontaneous clonus; inducible clonus with agitation or sweating; ocular clonus with agitation or sweating; tremor with hyperreflexia; or hypertonia with a temperature above 38 C (100.4 F) and ocular or inducible clonus. Labs look for complications such as high CK, kidney injury, acidosis and clotting problems. It is distinguished from neuroleptic malignant syndrome (slow onset, rigidity, slow reflexes, linked to dopamine blockers), anticholinergic toxicity (dry skin, absent bowel sounds) and malignant hyperthermia (during anesthesia).",
  tx: "The first step is stopping all serotonergic drugs and giving supportive care, which resolves most mild cases within a day. Benzodiazepines such as lorazepam, diazepam or midazolam reduce agitation, tremor and muscle activity. Cyproheptadine, an antihistamine that also blocks 5-HT2A receptors, is used for moderate cases. Fluids, oxygen and cardiac monitoring are standard. Physical restraints are avoided because struggling against them increases heat and acidosis. Severe hyperthermia is a true emergency treated with rapid cooling, sedation, paralysis with a nondepolarizing agent such as rocuronium and intubation; antipyretics like acetaminophen do not work. Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) can help clinicians.",
  drugs: ["cyproheptadine", "lorazepam", "diazepam", "midazolam", "rocuronium", "normal-saline"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Clonus and hyperreflexia, worse in the legs, point to serotonin syndrome; lead-pipe rigidity with slow reflexes points to neuroleptic malignant syndrome."], ["Memory trick", "Serotonin syndrome is fast (hours); NMS is slow (days). S for speedy."], ["Classic combo", "MAOI plus meperidine, linezolid plus an SSRI, and tramadol plus an SSRI are frequently tested causes."], ["Treatment pearl", "Cyproheptadine is the serotonin antidote; antipyretics do not lower this fever."]],
  red: "High fever, severe muscle stiffness, confusion, seizures or an irregular heartbeat after taking serotonin-raising medicines needs emergency care (call 911). Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) can give advice.",
  fun: "The Libby Zion case helped lead New York State to limit resident doctors' work hours in 1989, which later shaped national rules.",
  src: ["Boyer EW, Shannon M. The serotonin syndrome. N Engl J Med 2005", "Dunkley EJC et al. The Hunter Serotonin Toxicity Criteria. QJM 2003", "Sternbach H. Am J Psychiatry 1991", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies"]
},
"type-1-diabetes": {
  name: "Type 1 diabetes",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["T1D", "juvenile diabetes", "insulin-dependent diabetes", "autoimmune diabetes", "LADA"],
  what: "Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune disease in which the immune system destroys the insulin-making beta cells of the pancreas. Without insulin, sugar cannot enter cells, so blood sugar rises and the body must have insulin every day to survive.",
  origin: "Ancient writers described a wasting illness with heavy urination. Indian physicians noted sweet urine that attracted ants, and the Greek physician Aretaeus of Cappadocia used the word diabetes, meaning to pass through. In the 1670s English physician Thomas Willis noted the urine tasted sweet, later giving the name mellitus, meaning honeyed. In 1889 Oskar Minkowski and Joseph von Mering showed that removing a dog's pancreas caused diabetes. In 1921 Frederick Banting and Charles Best, working in J. J. R. Macleod's lab at the University of Toronto with biochemist James Collip, extracted insulin. In January 1922, 14-year-old Leonard Thompson became the first person treated, and children who were dying began to recover. Banting and Macleod received the 1923 Nobel Prize. In the 1970s islet autoantibodies showed the disease is autoimmune. Human insulin from recombinant DNA arrived in 1982, the DCCT trial in 1993 proved tight control prevents complications, and in 2022 teplizumab became the first drug to delay onset.",
  timeline: [["1889", "Minkowski and von Mering link the pancreas to diabetes."], ["1922", "Leonard Thompson becomes the first person treated with insulin in Toronto."], ["1923", "Banting and Macleod receive the Nobel Prize for discovering insulin."], ["1982", "Recombinant human insulin is approved, the first drug made with genetic engineering."], ["1993", "The DCCT shows intensive glucose control reduces eye, kidney and nerve damage."], ["2022", "Teplizumab is approved to delay stage 3 type 1 diabetes."]],
  cell: "Type 1 diabetes is a T cell-mediated autoimmune disease. Genetic risk is strongest for HLA class II types DR3-DQ2 and DR4-DQ8, which present beta cell proteins to the immune system. Self-reactive CD4 helper T cells and CD8 cytotoxic T cells escape tolerance and attack the islets, causing insulitis. CD8 cells kill beta cells through perforin, granzymes and Fas ligand, while cytokines such as interferon gamma and IL-1 beta add to the damage. B cells make autoantibodies against insulin, GAD65, IA-2 and ZnT8; these are markers of the attack rather than its main cause. The process often starts years before symptoms. Stage 1 is two or more autoantibodies with normal blood sugar, stage 2 adds abnormal glucose, and stage 3 is clinical diabetes, when most beta cell function is lost. With absolute insulin deficiency, muscle and fat cannot take up glucose through GLUT4, the liver keeps making glucose, fat breaks down without restraint and the liver turns fatty acids into ketones, which can lead to diabetic ketoacidosis.",
  body: [["Pancreas", "Beta cells in the islets of Langerhans are destroyed, so insulin and C-peptide fall to very low levels while alpha cells keep releasing glucagon."], ["Blood and metabolism", "High glucose and, without insulin, unchecked fat breakdown can produce ketoacidosis, a life-threatening acid buildup."], ["Eyes", "Over years, high glucose damages small retinal vessels (retinopathy), which can cause vision loss."], ["Kidneys", "Glucose injures the glomeruli, leading to protein in the urine and diabetic kidney disease."], ["Nerves", "High glucose damages nerves, causing numbness and pain in the feet and problems with digestion and blood pressure control."], ["Heart and vessels", "Long-term diabetes speeds up atherosclerosis, raising the risk of heart attack and stroke."]],
  symptoms: "When glucose rises above the kidney's ability to reabsorb it, sugar spills into urine and pulls water with it, causing frequent urination and bedwetting in children. Water loss triggers intense thirst. Because cells cannot use glucose, the body breaks down fat and muscle, causing weight loss despite eating more, along with fatigue. Blurry vision comes from fluid shifts in the lens. If ketones build up, people develop nausea, vomiting, belly pain, deep rapid breathing and a fruity breath odor. Onset in children is often fast, over days to weeks.",
  dx: "Diabetes is diagnosed by ADA criteria: fasting plasma glucose of 126 or higher, a 2-hour oral glucose tolerance test value of 200 or higher, an A1C of 6.5 percent or higher, or a random glucose of 200 or higher with classic symptoms (glucose in milligrams per deciliter). Type 1 is supported by islet autoantibodies (GAD65, IA-2, ZnT8, insulin) and a low C-peptide, which shows little insulin is being made. Many people are diagnosed during DKA. Relatives of people with type 1 can be screened for autoantibodies to find early-stage disease.",
  tx: "Treatment replaces insulin in a pattern that mimics the body. Basal (long-acting) insulin such as glargine, detemir or degludec covers the body's steady background needs, and bolus (rapid-acting) insulin such as lispro, aspart or glulisine covers meals and corrects highs; inhaled insulin is another mealtime option. Insulin pumps and continuous glucose monitors, including automated insulin delivery systems, adjust insulin in real time. Pramlintide, an amylin analog, slows stomach emptying and lowers glucagon after meals. Teplizumab, an anti-CD3 antibody, can delay the onset of stage 3 disease in people at stage 2. Glucagon (injectable or nasal) treats severe low blood sugar. Carbohydrate counting, exercise planning, diabetes education and regular eye, kidney, foot and heart checks are essential.",
  drugs: ["insulin-glargine", "insulin-degludec", "insulin-detemir", "insulin-lispro", "insulin-aspart", "insulin-glulisine", "regular-insulin", "insulin-nph", "inhaled-insulin", "pramlintide", "teplizumab", "glucagon", "glucagon-nasal", "dasiglucagon", "dextrose", "oral-glucose-gel"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Type 1 diabetes is linked to HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4 and features low C-peptide and islet autoantibodies such as GAD65."], ["Memory trick", "The 3 P's: polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia, plus weight loss."], ["Often confused with", "Type 2 diabetes has insulin resistance and usually normal or high C-peptide early on; type 1 has almost no insulin."], ["Key fact", "People with type 1 diabetes always need basal insulin, even when sick and not eating, to prevent DKA."]],
  red: "Vomiting, belly pain, deep fast breathing, fruity breath, confusion or extreme drowsiness can mean diabetic ketoacidosis and needs emergency care (call 911). Shakiness, sweating, confusion or a seizure can mean severe low blood sugar and also needs emergency care.",
  fun: "Leonard Thompson, the first person to receive insulin in 1922, lived about 13 more years at a time when type 1 diabetes was usually fatal within months.",
  src: ["ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes (2025)", "DCCT Research Group. N Engl J Med 1993", "Banting FG, Best CH. J Lab Clin Med 1922", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Herold KC et al. Teplizumab. N Engl J Med 2019"]
},
"type-2-diabetes": {
  name: "Type 2 diabetes",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["T2D", "adult-onset diabetes", "non-insulin-dependent diabetes", "prediabetes"],
  what: "Type 2 diabetes is a long-term condition in which the body does not respond well to insulin and the pancreas cannot make enough extra insulin to keep up. Blood sugar slowly rises and, over years, damages the heart, kidneys, eyes and nerves.",
  origin: "For centuries diabetes was seen as one disease. In 1936 British physician Harold Himsworth showed that some people with diabetes were sensitive to insulin while others were insulin-insensitive, an early description of insulin resistance. Around 1960 Rosalyn Yalow and Solomon Berson developed the radioimmunoassay, which showed that many people with adult-onset diabetes actually had normal or high insulin levels; Yalow later won the Nobel Prize. Sulfonylureas came into use in the 1950s. Metformin, a drug related to a compound in the French lilac plant, was introduced in France in 1957 by Jean Sterne and approved in the US in the mid-1990s. The UKPDS trial in 1998 showed that better control reduced complications, and the Diabetes Prevention Program in 2002 proved lifestyle change can prevent or delay the disease. GLP-1 receptor agonists, starting with exenatide in 2005, and SGLT2 inhibitors, which in 2015 were shown to lower cardiovascular deaths, changed treatment toward protecting the heart and kidneys.",
  timeline: [["1936", "Himsworth describes insulin-sensitive and insulin-insensitive diabetes."], ["1957", "Jean Sterne introduces metformin in France."], ["1998", "The UKPDS shows better glucose control lowers complications."], ["2002", "The Diabetes Prevention Program shows lifestyle change and metformin prevent diabetes."], ["2005", "Exenatide becomes the first GLP-1 receptor agonist approved in the US."], ["2015", "The EMPA-REG OUTCOME trial shows empagliflozin lowers cardiovascular death."]],
  cell: "Type 2 diabetes results from two problems together: insulin resistance and failing beta cells. Strong genetic risk (many genes, such as TCF7L2) combines with excess visceral fat, inactivity and aging. In insulin resistance, insulin binds its receptor normally, but signaling downstream is blunted. Fatty acids, diacylglycerol and inflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha activate kinases that put inhibitory phosphates on IRS-1, so less GLUT4 moves to the surface of muscle and fat cells, and the liver keeps making glucose. Beta cells first compensate by making more insulin, but over time high glucose and fat (glucotoxicity and lipotoxicity), oxidative and ER stress, and deposits of islet amyloid polypeptide (amylin) cause beta cell dysfunction and loss. Other players add to high glucose: too much glucagon from alpha cells, a weaker incretin (GLP-1 and GIP) effect from the gut, more glucose reabsorbed in the kidney by SGLT2, and increased fat breakdown. Because some insulin is still made, ketoacidosis is less common than in type 1.",
  body: [["Heart and vessels", "Insulin resistance and high glucose speed atherosclerosis and often come with high blood pressure and abnormal lipids, making heart disease the leading cause of death."], ["Kidneys", "High glucose and pressure damage the glomeruli, causing albumin in the urine and diabetic kidney disease, a top cause of dialysis."], ["Eyes", "Damage to retinal vessels causes retinopathy and macular edema, and cataracts and glaucoma become more common."], ["Nerves and feet", "Nerve damage causes numbness, burning pain and loss of protective sensation, so foot ulcers and infections can go unnoticed and lead to amputation."], ["Liver", "Excess fat in the liver (metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease) is common and can progress to scarring."], ["Brain", "Diabetes raises the risk of stroke and is linked to cognitive decline."]],
  symptoms: "Many people have no symptoms for years, which is why screening matters. When glucose is high, sugar spills into urine and pulls water with it, causing frequent urination and thirst. Fatigue, blurry vision from fluid shifts in the lens, slow wound healing and frequent yeast or skin infections come from high glucose impairing immune cells and blood flow. Dark velvety skin in body folds (acanthosis nigricans) reflects high insulin levels stimulating skin cells. Numbness or tingling in the feet signals nerve damage.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses ADA criteria: fasting plasma glucose of 126 or higher, a 2-hour oral glucose tolerance test value of 200 or higher, an A1C of 6.5 percent or higher, or a random glucose of 200 or higher with classic symptoms (glucose in milligrams per deciliter). Without symptoms, an abnormal result is confirmed by repeat testing. Prediabetes means values between normal and diabetes, such as an A1C of 5.7 to 6.4 percent. The ADA advises screening adults starting at age 35, or earlier with overweight and other risk factors. Kidney function, urine albumin, lipids, eye exams and foot exams look for complications.",
  tx: "Lifestyle change, including healthy eating, physical activity and weight loss, is the base of treatment. Metformin lowers liver glucose production, partly through AMP kinase. GLP-1 receptor agonists (semaglutide, liraglutide, dulaglutide) and the dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist tirzepatide boost glucose-dependent insulin release, lower glucagon, slow stomach emptying and cause weight loss; several lower cardiovascular risk. SGLT2 inhibitors (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin, canagliflozin) make the kidneys excrete glucose and protect the heart and kidneys. DPP-4 inhibitors prolong natural incretins. Sulfonylureas close beta cell potassium channels to release insulin but can cause low blood sugar. Pioglitazone activates PPAR-gamma to improve insulin sensitivity. Insulin is added when needed. Statins, blood pressure control with ACE inhibitors or ARBs, and regular eye, foot and kidney care reduce complications.",
  drugs: ["metformin", "semaglutide", "semaglutide-oral", "tirzepatide", "liraglutide", "dulaglutide", "exenatide", "empagliflozin", "dapagliflozin", "canagliflozin", "sitagliptin", "linagliptin", "glipizide", "glimepiride", "pioglitazone", "insulin-glargine", "insulin-degludec", "insulin-lispro", "acarbose", "repaglinide", "colesevelam", "atorvastatin", "lisinopril", "losartan"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Metformin is first-line in most people; it rarely causes low blood sugar but can cause lactic acidosis in severe kidney disease and lowers vitamin B12 over time."], ["Memory trick", "SGLT2 inhibitors make you pee sugar: think glucosuria, genital yeast infections and euglycemic DKA."], ["Often confused with", "Type 1 diabetes is autoimmune with absent insulin; type 2 is insulin resistance plus relative insulin deficiency."], ["Key fact", "In people with heart or kidney disease, GLP-1 receptor agonists and SGLT2 inhibitors are chosen for organ protection, not just glucose lowering."]],
  red: "Extreme thirst with confusion, severe drowsiness or very high glucose readings can mean hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state and needs emergency care (call 911). Chest pain, sudden weakness on one side or a foot wound that turns red, hot or black also needs urgent care.",
  fun: "Exenatide was developed from exendin-4, a hormone found in the saliva of the Gila monster lizard.",
  src: ["ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes (2025)", "UK Prospective Diabetes Study Group. Lancet 1998", "Diabetes Prevention Program Research Group. N Engl J Med 2002", "Zinman B et al. EMPA-REG OUTCOME. N Engl J Med 2015", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"diabetic-ketoacidosis": {
  name: "Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["DKA", "ketoacidosis", "euglycemic DKA"],
  what: "Diabetic ketoacidosis is a life-threatening emergency in which a lack of insulin makes the body burn fat so fast that acids called ketones build up in the blood. It causes severe dehydration, vomiting and fast deep breathing and needs hospital treatment.",
  origin: "Before insulin, most people with what is now called type 1 diabetes died in a diabetic coma. In 1874 German physician Adolf Kussmaul described the deep, labored breathing of these patients, now called Kussmaul respirations. Chemists in the late 1800s found acetone and acetoacetic acid in the urine of people in diabetic coma. The discovery of insulin in Toronto in 1921 and 1922 turned DKA from almost always fatal into a treatable condition. For decades large insulin doses were used, but in the 1970s researchers, including K. G. M. M. Alberti in the UK and Abbas Kitabchi in the US, showed that continuous low-dose insulin was safer and just as effective. Modern care centers on fluids, an insulin infusion and careful potassium replacement. Consensus statements from the ADA and partner groups, most recently in 2024, guide care. The use of SGLT2 inhibitors has made euglycemic DKA, ketoacidosis with near-normal glucose, more widely recognized.",
  timeline: [["1874", "Adolf Kussmaul describes deep, labored breathing in diabetic coma."], ["1922", "Insulin begins to save patients in diabetic coma."], ["1970s", "Low-dose insulin infusion becomes the standard treatment approach."], ["2015", "The FDA warns that SGLT2 inhibitors can cause ketoacidosis."], ["2024", "An international consensus updates definitions and care of hyperglycemic crises."]],
  cell: "DKA begins with a severe lack of insulin plus a rise in counter-regulatory hormones: glucagon, cortisol, catecholamines and growth hormone. Triggers include new type 1 diabetes, missed insulin, infection, heart attack, pancreatitis, certain drugs and SGLT2 inhibitors. Without insulin, hormone-sensitive lipase in fat cells breaks triglycerides into free fatty acids. In the liver, the high glucagon-to-insulin ratio lowers malonyl-CoA, which unblocks carnitine palmitoyltransferase 1, so fatty acids pour into mitochondria for beta-oxidation. The flood of acetyl-CoA is converted into the ketone bodies acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate. These are acids, and they use up bicarbonate, producing a high anion gap metabolic acidosis. At the same time, the liver makes glucose through gluconeogenesis and glycogen breakdown, and muscle cannot take it up. High glucose causes an osmotic diuresis, with loss of water, sodium, potassium and phosphate. Serum potassium is often normal or high at first because insulin lack and high osmolality shift potassium out of cells, even though total body potassium is low.",
  body: [["Blood chemistry", "Ketoacids cause a high anion gap metabolic acidosis, and high glucose raises blood osmolality."], ["Kidneys", "Glucose in the urine pulls out water and electrolytes, causing dehydration and sometimes acute kidney injury from low blood flow."], ["Lungs", "The brain senses acid and drives deep, rapid Kussmaul breathing to blow off carbon dioxide and buffer the blood."], ["Heart", "Potassium shifts and dehydration can cause dangerous rhythms, especially when potassium falls during treatment."], ["Gut", "Ketosis and acidosis cause nausea, vomiting and belly pain that can mimic a surgical emergency."], ["Brain", "Severe acidosis and high osmolality cause drowsiness and coma. Cerebral edema, mostly in children, can occur during treatment."]],
  symptoms: "DKA usually develops over hours to a day. High glucose causes heavy urination and thirst, then dehydration with dry mouth, fast heart rate and low blood pressure. Ketones cause nausea, vomiting and belly pain. Deep, fast breathing is the body's attempt to fix the acidosis by exhaling carbon dioxide. Acetone, a ketone, gives the breath a fruity smell. Confusion and drowsiness reflect acidosis, dehydration and high osmolality affecting the brain.",
  dx: "The 2024 consensus defines DKA by three parts: high glucose or known diabetes, raised ketones (blood beta-hydroxybutyrate is the preferred test) and metabolic acidosis (low pH or low bicarbonate). The anion gap is high. Labs also check potassium, sodium (corrected for high glucose), phosphate, kidney function and osmolality. An ECG looks for potassium-related changes. Clinicians search for a trigger with tests such as blood counts, cultures, lipase, troponin and a chest X-ray. Euglycemic DKA, often linked to SGLT2 inhibitors, pregnancy or starvation, has acidosis and ketones with near-normal glucose.",
  tx: "Treatment has four parts. First, IV fluids (normal saline or balanced fluids such as lactated Ringer's) restore volume and improve kidney flow. Second, insulin, usually a continuous IV infusion of regular insulin, stops fat breakdown and ketone production; milder cases may be treated with rapid-acting insulin under the skin. Insulin is delayed if potassium is low, because insulin drives potassium into cells. Third, potassium is replaced to prevent dangerous drops, and phosphate is replaced when very low. Dextrose is added to fluids as glucose falls so insulin can continue until ketoacidosis resolves. Sodium bicarbonate is reserved for very severe acidosis. When the person can eat and the acidosis has cleared, long-acting insulin is given under the skin before the infusion stops, to prevent rebound. The trigger is treated and diabetes education helps prevent repeat episodes.",
  drugs: ["regular-insulin", "normal-saline", "lactated-ringers", "plasma-lyte", "potassium-chloride", "potassium-phosphate", "dextrose", "sodium-bicarbonate", "insulin-glargine", "insulin-lispro", "insulin-aspart", "ondansetron"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Check potassium before starting insulin: insulin shifts potassium into cells and can cause life-threatening hypokalemia."], ["Memory trick", "Treatment order: Fluids, then Insulin, with Potassium watched closely (FIP)."], ["Key fact", "Anion gap closure, not glucose, signals that DKA has resolved; dextrose is added so insulin can keep clearing ketones."], ["Often confused with", "HHS has much higher glucose and osmolality with little or no ketoacidosis and is more common in older adults with type 2 diabetes."]],
  red: "Vomiting that will not stop, belly pain, deep or fast breathing, fruity breath, confusion or drowsiness in someone with diabetes needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Kussmaul breathing is named after Adolf Kussmaul, who also tried some of the earliest gastroscopies using a professional sword swallower.",
  src: ["Umpierrez GE et al. Hyperglycemic Crises in Adults With Diabetes: A Consensus Report. Diabetes Care 2024", "ISPAD Clinical Practice Consensus Guidelines (2022)", "Kitabchi AE et al. Diabetes Care 2009", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"hhs": {
  name: "Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS)",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["HHS", "hyperosmolar hyperglycemic nonketotic syndrome", "HHNS", "hyperosmolar coma", "HONK"],
  what: "Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state is an emergency in which blood sugar climbs extremely high over days, usually in type 2 diabetes. The body loses huge amounts of water in urine, and the blood becomes so concentrated that the brain is affected, causing confusion or coma.",
  origin: "After insulin made diabetic ketoacidosis treatable, doctors in the 1950s began to describe a different kind of diabetic coma. These patients, often older, had extremely high glucose and severe dehydration but little or no ketones in the blood. Reports in the late 1950s described this as diabetic stupor or coma without ketosis, and the condition was long called hyperosmolar nonketotic coma. Researchers learned that many patients were not in a coma at all, and that small amounts of ketones could be present, so the name was changed to hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state. Studies showed that it carries a higher death rate than DKA, mostly because patients are older, more dehydrated and often have a serious trigger such as infection, stroke or heart attack. Modern care, guided by consensus statements from the ADA and partner groups, most recently in 2024, focuses on careful fluid replacement and slow correction of blood concentration.",
  timeline: [["1950s", "Doctors describe diabetic coma with very high glucose but no ketosis."], ["2000s", "The name hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state replaces nonketotic coma."], ["2024", "An international consensus updates the diagnosis and management of HHS."]],
  cell: "HHS develops when insulin is reduced or not working well (relative insulin deficiency) and counter-regulatory hormones such as glucagon, cortisol and catecholamines rise, often because of infection, stroke, heart attack, missed medicines or drugs such as glucocorticoids, thiazides and some antipsychotics. The small amount of insulin still present is enough to suppress fat breakdown and ketone production in the liver, which needs less insulin, but not enough to drive glucose into muscle or stop liver glucose output. Glucose rises steadily over days. When it passes the kidney threshold, an osmotic diuresis pulls out large amounts of water along with sodium and potassium. If the person cannot drink enough, often because of age, a blunted thirst sense, disability or illness, water loss outpaces salt loss and serum osmolality rises sharply. Water moves out of brain cells along the osmotic gradient, so neurons shrink and function poorly, causing confusion, seizures or coma. Dehydration thickens the blood and raises the risk of clots.",
  body: [["Brain", "High osmolality draws water out of brain cells, causing confusion, focal weakness that can mimic stroke, seizures and coma."], ["Kidneys", "Massive glucose-driven urination causes severe dehydration and often acute kidney injury from low blood flow."], ["Heart and vessels", "Profound fluid loss causes low blood pressure and fast heart rate; thick blood raises the risk of clots, heart attack and stroke."], ["Electrolytes", "Total body potassium, phosphate and magnesium are depleted even when blood levels look normal at first."], ["Muscles", "Severe dehydration and high osmolality can cause muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis)."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms develop gradually over days to weeks. Heavy urination and thirst come from glucose in the urine pulling out water. Weakness, dry mouth, sunken eyes, fast heart rate and low blood pressure reflect severe dehydration. Confusion, drowsiness, seizures and coma happen because concentrated blood draws water out of brain cells, and the degree of confusion tracks with osmolality. Unlike DKA, there is usually little vomiting or deep breathing, because there is little ketoacidosis.",
  dx: "HHS is defined by very high glucose (often above 600 milligrams per deciliter), very high serum osmolality, and absence of significant ketoacidosis (near-normal pH and bicarbonate and low ketones). Effective osmolality is calculated from sodium and glucose. Sodium is corrected for the high glucose to judge true water loss. Labs check potassium, kidney function, CK and lactate. Clinicians search for the trigger with cultures, chest X-ray, ECG, troponin and brain imaging when needed. Some people have features of both HHS and DKA.",
  tx: "Fluid replacement is the most important treatment. IV normal saline restores circulation first, and later fluids are adjusted to replace free water as the corrected sodium is followed. Fluids alone lower glucose substantially by improving kidney excretion. An IV insulin infusion of regular insulin is started after fluids are underway, and potassium is replaced as needed because insulin moves potassium into cells. Dextrose is added when glucose falls to avoid dropping too quickly. Osmolality is lowered gradually because rapid shifts can cause brain swelling. Heparin or enoxaparin is commonly used to prevent clots. The trigger, such as pneumonia, urinary infection or stroke, is treated, and the diabetes plan is adjusted afterward.",
  drugs: ["normal-saline", "lactated-ringers", "regular-insulin", "potassium-chloride", "potassium-phosphate", "dextrose", "heparin", "enoxaparin", "insulin-glargine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "HHS has higher glucose and osmolality than DKA but little ketosis, because a small amount of insulin still blocks ketone production."], ["Memory trick", "HHS is the slow, dry, confused cousin of DKA: older patient, type 2 diabetes, days of dehydration."], ["Key fact", "Fluids come first and are the cornerstone; HHS carries a higher death rate than DKA."], ["Often confused with", "Stroke: HHS can cause one-sided weakness or seizures that improve as fluids and glucose are corrected."]],
  red: "Extreme thirst, very high glucose readings, confusion, severe drowsiness, seizures or new weakness in someone with diabetes needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Fluid replacement alone, before any insulin, can bring glucose down a long way in HHS because better kidney blood flow lets the kidneys flush out sugar.",
  src: ["Umpierrez GE et al. Hyperglycemic Crises in Adults With Diabetes: A Consensus Report. Diabetes Care 2024", "Kitabchi AE et al. Diabetes Care 2009", "Joint British Diabetes Societies guideline on the management of HHS in adults", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"hypoglycemia": {
  name: "Hypoglycemia",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["low blood sugar", "insulin reaction", "insulin shock", "hypo"],
  what: "Hypoglycemia means blood sugar has dropped too low for the brain and body to work properly. It most often happens in people treated with insulin or certain diabetes pills and causes shakiness, sweating and confusion, and severe cases can cause seizures or unconsciousness.",
  origin: "Low blood sugar became a common problem only after insulin was introduced in 1922, when early users experienced shaking, sweating and convulsions doctors called insulin reactions. In 1923 John Murlin and colleagues identified glucagon, a pancreatic hormone that raises blood sugar. In 1924 American physician Seale Harris described spontaneous low blood sugar in people without diabetes. In the 1930s surgeon Allen Whipple described the features of insulin-secreting tumors (insulinomas) and the diagnostic triad that bears his name. Injectable glucagon kits for emergency use became available in the 1960s. Later research by Philip Cryer and others explained hypoglycemia-associated autonomic failure, in which repeated lows dull the body's warning signs. A nasal glucagon spray was approved in 2019, making rescue easier for families and bystanders.",
  timeline: [["1922", "Insulin reactions are recognized soon after insulin enters use."], ["1923", "Glucagon is identified as a blood sugar-raising pancreatic hormone."], ["1930s", "Allen Whipple describes Whipple's triad in patients with insulinoma."], ["2019", "Nasal glucagon is approved for severe hypoglycemia."]],
  cell: "The brain uses glucose as its main fuel and cannot store much, so it depends on a steady supply through GLUT1 and GLUT3 transporters. When glucose falls, the body defends itself in steps. First, beta cells cut insulin release. Next, alpha cells release glucagon, which tells the liver to break down glycogen and make new glucose. Then the adrenal medulla and sympathetic nerves release epinephrine and norepinephrine, which raise glucose and cause warning symptoms; cortisol and growth hormone help over hours. In type 1 and long-standing type 2 diabetes, the glucagon response is lost, and repeated lows reset the brain's threshold so the adrenaline response and warning symptoms weaken (hypoglycemia unawareness). Most episodes come from insulin or insulin secretagogues (sulfonylureas, meglitinides) acting when food intake is low or activity is high. Other causes include alcohol, which blocks gluconeogenesis by raising NADH; kidney or liver failure; sepsis; adrenal insufficiency; and insulinoma, a tumor that makes insulin regardless of glucose. If glucose stays very low, neurons lose energy and can be injured or die.",
  body: [["Brain", "Low glucose starves neurons, causing confusion, odd behavior, slurred speech, seizures, coma and, if prolonged, permanent brain injury."], ["Autonomic nerves", "Epinephrine and acetylcholine release cause shakiness, a pounding heart, anxiety, sweating and hunger."], ["Heart", "The adrenaline surge raises heart rate and can trigger arrhythmias, especially in older adults with heart disease."], ["Liver", "The liver releases glucose from glycogen and makes new glucose; when liver function or glycogen stores fail, lows last longer."], ["Daily life", "Episodes can cause falls, car crashes and fear of future lows, which can make diabetes management harder."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms come in two groups. Neurogenic (autonomic) symptoms come from the stress response: tremor, palpitations and anxiety from adrenaline, and sweating, hunger and tingling from acetylcholine. Neuroglycopenic symptoms come from the brain running out of fuel: confusion, trouble concentrating, irritability, blurred vision, slurred speech, unusual behavior, seizures and loss of consciousness. Beta blockers can mask the tremor and fast heartbeat but not the sweating. Lows during sleep can cause nightmares, night sweats and morning headaches.",
  dx: "In diabetes, the ADA defines level 1 hypoglycemia as glucose below 70, level 2 as below 54 (glucose in milligrams per deciliter), and level 3 as a severe event with mental or physical changes that requires help from another person. A fingerstick or continuous glucose monitor confirms the low. In people without diabetes, hypoglycemia is diagnosed with Whipple's triad: symptoms, a low measured glucose, and relief when glucose is raised. Testing during a low measures insulin, C-peptide, proinsulin, beta-hydroxybutyrate and a sulfonylurea screen. High insulin with high C-peptide suggests insulinoma or sulfonylurea use, while high insulin with low C-peptide suggests injected insulin.",
  tx: "Treatment raises glucose quickly and then prevents a repeat drop. A person who is awake and can swallow is given fast-acting carbohydrate such as glucose tablets or gel, juice or regular soda, with glucose rechecked in about 15 minutes and a snack or meal afterward. When a person cannot safely swallow, glucagon (injection or nasal) or dasiglucagon triggers the liver to release stored glucose, and in medical settings IV dextrose acts within minutes. Glucagon works poorly when glycogen is depleted, such as in alcohol use or starvation, where IV dextrose and thiamine are used. Lows from sulfonylureas can recur for many hours, so observation and sometimes octreotide, which blocks insulin release, are used. Diazoxide reduces insulin release in some insulinoma cases, and surgery removes the tumor. Prevention includes adjusting medicines, CGM alarms and education.",
  drugs: ["dextrose", "oral-glucose-gel", "glucagon", "glucagon-nasal", "dasiglucagon", "octreotide", "diazoxide", "thiamine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Rule of 15: fast carbs, recheck in about 15 minutes, repeat if still low, then eat."], ["Most-tested fact", "In a confused or unresponsive person with diabetes, check glucose first; for an unconscious person, nothing is given by mouth."], ["Lab pattern", "High insulin with low C-peptide means insulin was injected; high insulin with high C-peptide means insulinoma or sulfonylurea."], ["Often confused with", "Hypoglycemia can look like a stroke, intoxication or a psychiatric emergency."]],
  red: "Confusion, unusual behavior, seizures, fainting or being unable to swallow in someone with diabetes needs emergency care (call 911). Low blood sugar from an accidental or intentional overdose of diabetes medicine also needs emergency care; Poison Control is 1-800-222-1222.",
  fun: "Glucagon was first noticed as a contaminant in early insulin extracts, because some batches briefly raised blood sugar before lowering it.",
  src: ["ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes (2025)", "Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Evaluation and Management of Adult Hypoglycemic Disorders (2009)", "Cryer PE. Hypoglycemia in Diabetes (American Diabetes Association)", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"hypothyroidism": {
  name: "Hypothyroidism",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["underactive thyroid", "Hashimoto's thyroiditis", "chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis", "myxedema"],
  what: "Hypothyroidism means the thyroid gland does not make enough thyroid hormone. Because this hormone sets the pace of metabolism in nearly every cell, the body slows down, causing tiredness, feeling cold, weight gain and constipation.",
  origin: "In 1874 English physician William Gull described a cretinoid state in adult women, and in 1878 William Ord named the condition myxedema after the thick, swollen skin he saw at autopsy. Surgeons noticed the same picture after removing the whole thyroid, which linked it to loss of the gland. In 1891 George Murray in England treated a woman with myxedema using injections of sheep thyroid extract, and she lived for decades, one of the first successful hormone replacements. In 1912 Japanese surgeon Hakaru Hashimoto described struma lymphomatosa, a thyroid filled with immune cells, now known as Hashimoto's thyroiditis, the most common cause in iodine-sufficient countries. In 1914 Edward Kendall at the Mayo Clinic isolated thyroxine, and Charles Harington worked out its structure and synthesized it in the late 1920s. T3 was identified in 1952 by Jack Gross and Rosalind Pitt-Rivers. Iodized salt, introduced in the US in 1924, greatly reduced deficiency-related goiter and hypothyroidism.",
  timeline: [["1878", "William Ord names the condition myxedema."], ["1891", "George Murray treats myxedema with thyroid extract."], ["1912", "Hakaru Hashimoto describes autoimmune (lymphocytic) thyroiditis."], ["1914", "Edward Kendall isolates thyroxine at the Mayo Clinic."], ["1924", "Iodized salt is introduced in the United States."], ["1952", "Gross and Pitt-Rivers identify triiodothyronine (T3)."]],
  cell: "Thyroid follicular cells trap iodide through the sodium-iodide symporter, and thyroid peroxidase (TPO) attaches iodine to tyrosines on thyroglobulin and couples them to form T4 and T3. TSH from the pituitary drives every step. In Hashimoto's thyroiditis, a T cell-mediated autoimmune attack, linked to HLA-DR3 and DR5 among other genes, targets TPO and thyroglobulin. CD8 T cells and cytokines kill follicular cells, and B cells form lymphoid follicles with germinal centers and make anti-TPO and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies. Surviving cells may become large and pink (Hurthle cells), and the gland gradually scars. As T4 output falls, the pituitary raises TSH (primary hypothyroidism). Other causes include iodine deficiency, thyroid removal or radioactive iodine, drugs (amiodarone, lithium, checkpoint inhibitors) and, rarely, pituitary or hypothalamic disease (central hypothyroidism with low TSH). T3 acts on nuclear thyroid hormone receptors to switch on genes for Na-K-ATPase, beta-1 adrenergic receptors, heat production and LDL receptors. When T3 is low, metabolism and heat production fall, LDL clearance drops, and glycosaminoglycans build up in the skin and tissues, causing nonpitting swelling.",
  body: [["Metabolism", "Fewer Na-K-ATPase pumps and less heat production slow the metabolic rate, causing cold intolerance, fatigue and modest weight gain."], ["Heart", "Fewer beta-1 receptors and slower contraction cause a slow heart rate and lower cardiac output; LDL cholesterol rises and pericardial fluid can collect."], ["Skin and hair", "Glycosaminoglycans in the dermis cause puffy, dry, cool skin, coarse hair, hair thinning and puffy eyelids."], ["Brain and nerves", "Low hormone causes slowed thinking, depression and slow-relaxing reflexes; fluid can compress the median nerve (carpal tunnel)."], ["Gut", "Slower gut movement causes constipation."], ["Reproduction", "Hypothyroidism causes heavy or irregular periods and fertility problems; high TRH can raise prolactin."]],
  symptoms: "Most symptoms come from a slowed metabolic rate: tiredness, sleepiness, feeling cold, weight gain, constipation and a slow pulse. Dry skin, brittle hair and swelling of the face and hands reflect glycosaminoglycan buildup. Muscle aches, cramps and a delayed relaxation phase of the ankle reflex reflect slowed muscle metabolism. A hoarse voice comes from swollen vocal cords. A goiter can form when high TSH stimulates the gland. Severe untreated cases can progress to myxedema coma, with low temperature, slow breathing, low sodium and confusion.",
  dx: "The key test is TSH, which is high in primary hypothyroidism, followed by free T4, which is low. A high TSH with normal free T4 is called subclinical hypothyroidism. Anti-TPO antibodies support Hashimoto's thyroiditis. In central hypothyroidism, TSH is low or inappropriately normal with low free T4, prompting pituitary evaluation. Labs may also show high LDL cholesterol, high CK and low sodium. Ultrasound is used if nodules are present. Newborns in the US are screened for congenital hypothyroidism.",
  tx: "Treatment replaces the missing hormone. Levothyroxine, synthetic T4, is the standard; the body converts it to active T3 as needed, and its long half-life gives stable levels. It is usually taken on an empty stomach and separated from calcium, iron and some other drugs that block absorption. TSH is rechecked after changes and adjusted toward the normal range; older adults and people with heart disease are started more gradually. Liothyronine (T3) is used in selected cases and in myxedema coma, along with IV levothyroxine, and stress-dose hydrocortisone is given until adrenal insufficiency is ruled out, because replacing thyroid hormone first can unmask an adrenal crisis. Desiccated thyroid is marketed but is not FDA approved. In pregnancy, needs often rise.",
  drugs: ["levothyroxine", "liothyronine", "thyroid-desiccated", "hydrocortisone"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Hashimoto's thyroiditis is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in the US and carries a higher risk of thyroid lymphoma."], ["Memory trick", "Hypothyroid means everything slows: slow heart, slow gut, slow thinking, slow reflexes."], ["Key fact", "In myxedema coma, steroids are given with thyroid hormone because treating the thyroid first can trigger adrenal crisis."], ["Often confused with", "Depression and normal aging; checking TSH helps tell them apart."]],
  red: "Extreme drowsiness, confusion, a very low body temperature, slow breathing or a very slow heart rate in someone with hypothyroidism can mean myxedema coma and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "George Murray's first patient treated with sheep thyroid extract in 1891 lived for about 28 more years.",
  src: ["ATA Guidelines for the Treatment of Hypothyroidism (2014)", "Murray GR. BMJ 1891", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"hyperthyroidism": {
  name: "Hyperthyroidism and Graves' disease",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["overactive thyroid", "thyrotoxicosis", "Graves' disease", "Basedow's disease", "toxic multinodular goiter", "toxic adenoma"],
  what: "Hyperthyroidism means the thyroid makes too much thyroid hormone, which speeds up the body. Graves' disease, the most common cause, is an autoimmune condition in which antibodies switch the thyroid on, sometimes also causing bulging eyes.",
  origin: "In 1786 English physician Caleb Parry described a woman with goiter, a racing heart and bulging eyes; his notes were published in 1825 after his death. In 1835 Irish physician Robert Graves described three women with palpitations and enlarged thyroids, and in 1840 German physician Karl von Basedow described the classic combination of goiter, bulging eyes and fast heartbeat, so the disease is called Graves' disease in English-speaking countries and Basedow's disease in much of Europe. Surgery was the main treatment until the 1940s. In 1941 Saul Hertz at Massachusetts General Hospital gave the first therapeutic radioactive iodine, and in 1943 Edwin Astwood introduced thiouracil, the first antithyroid drug, leading to methimazole and propylthiouracil. In 1956 Duncan Adams and Herbert Purves found a long-acting thyroid stimulator in patients' blood, later shown to be an antibody against the TSH receptor. In 2020 teprotumumab was approved for thyroid eye disease.",
  timeline: [["1835", "Robert Graves describes goiter with palpitations."], ["1840", "Karl von Basedow describes goiter, bulging eyes and fast heartbeat."], ["1941", "Saul Hertz treats the first patient with radioactive iodine."], ["1943", "Edwin Astwood introduces thiouracil, the first antithyroid drug."], ["1956", "Adams and Purves discover a long-acting thyroid stimulator in the blood."], ["2020", "Teprotumumab is approved for thyroid eye disease."]],
  cell: "In Graves' disease, B cells make IgG antibodies, called thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI or TRAb), that bind and activate the TSH receptor on thyroid follicular cells. This is a type II hypersensitivity in which the antibody stimulates rather than destroys. The receptor signals through Gs and cyclic AMP, so the gland grows (diffuse goiter), traps more iodine and makes excess T4 and T3 regardless of TSH, while pituitary TSH falls to near zero. Orbital fibroblasts and fat cells also carry the TSH receptor and IGF-1 receptor; T cells and antibodies activate them to make glycosaminoglycans and new fat, swelling the eye muscles and orbital tissue and pushing the eyes forward. Similar changes in the shin skin cause pretibial myxedema. Excess T3 acts on nuclear receptors to increase Na-K-ATPase, heat production and beta-adrenergic receptor numbers, which amplifies sympathetic effects. Other causes of hyperthyroidism include toxic multinodular goiter and toxic adenoma (often with activating TSH receptor mutations), and thyroiditis, in which a damaged gland leaks stored hormone.",
  body: [["Heart", "More beta-adrenergic receptors and higher metabolism cause a fast heart rate, palpitations, widened pulse pressure and atrial fibrillation, especially in older adults."], ["Eyes", "Graves' eye disease causes bulging eyes, grittiness, double vision and, rarely, optic nerve compression. Lid retraction and lid lag can occur with any cause."], ["Metabolism", "High metabolic rate causes weight loss despite a big appetite, heat intolerance and sweating."], ["Bones and muscles", "Thyroid hormone speeds bone turnover, causing bone loss, and causes weakness of the upper arms and thighs."], ["Brain and nerves", "Sympathetic overactivity causes anxiety, irritability, insomnia and a fine tremor; reflexes are brisk."], ["Skin and gut", "Skin is warm and moist; faster gut movement causes frequent stools. Pretibial myxedema is specific to Graves' disease."]],
  symptoms: "Excess thyroid hormone raises the metabolic rate and sensitizes the body to adrenaline. That explains weight loss with good appetite, heat intolerance, sweating, a racing or irregular heartbeat, tremor, anxiety, poor sleep and frequent bowel movements. Irregular or lighter periods are common. A smooth, enlarged thyroid, sometimes with a bruit from increased blood flow, suggests Graves' disease. Eye bulging, redness and double vision come from swollen orbital tissue and are specific to Graves' disease.",
  dx: "Blood tests show a suppressed TSH with high free T4 and/or T3. TSH receptor antibodies (TRAb or TSI) confirm Graves' disease. A radioactive iodine uptake scan helps find the cause: uptake is high and diffuse in Graves' disease, patchy in toxic multinodular goiter, focal in a toxic adenoma, and low in thyroiditis or when someone is taking extra thyroid hormone. Ultrasound with Doppler can show increased blood flow. Clinicians also check for atrial fibrillation and bone loss.",
  tx: "Beta blockers such as propranolol, atenolol or metoprolol quickly control tremor, palpitations and anxiety by blocking adrenaline's effects; propranolol at higher doses also reduces conversion of T4 to T3. Thionamides block thyroid peroxidase and lower hormone production. Methimazole is preferred for most people, while propylthiouracil is used in the first trimester of pregnancy and in thyroid storm because it also blocks T4 to T3 conversion; both can rarely cause agranulocytosis, and PTU carries a risk of severe liver injury. Radioactive iodine (I-131) is taken up by the thyroid and destroys overactive tissue, and thyroidectomy removes it; both usually lead to lifelong levothyroxine. Potassium iodide is used briefly before surgery to reduce gland activity and blood flow. Thyroid eye disease is treated with smoking cessation, eye protection, glucocorticoids and teprotumumab, an IGF-1 receptor blocking antibody. Thyroiditis usually needs only beta blockers.",
  drugs: ["methimazole", "propylthiouracil", "propranolol", "atenolol", "metoprolol", "sodium-iodide-i-131", "potassium-iodide", "teprotumumab", "prednisone", "levothyroxine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Graves' disease is a type II hypersensitivity: stimulating antibodies to the TSH receptor; exophthalmos and pretibial myxedema are specific to it."], ["Pregnancy rule", "PTU in the first trimester, methimazole after, because methimazole is linked to birth defects and PTU to liver injury."], ["Key fact", "Sore throat and fever on a thionamide can signal agranulocytosis and prompts a white blood cell count."], ["Often confused with", "Thyroiditis also causes thyrotoxicosis but has low radioactive iodine uptake and does not respond to thionamides."]],
  red: "Very high fever, a very fast or irregular heartbeat, confusion, agitation, vomiting or jaundice in someone with hyperthyroidism can mean thyroid storm and needs emergency care (call 911). Sudden vision loss or eye pain with Graves' disease also needs urgent care.",
  fun: "Saul Hertz began radioactive iodine therapy in 1941, making it one of the first targeted uses of nuclear medicine for disease.",
  src: ["ATA Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis (2016)", "Graves RJ. London Medical and Surgical Journal 1835", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"thyroid-storm": {
  name: "Thyroid storm",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["thyrotoxic crisis", "thyroid crisis"],
  what: "Thyroid storm is a rare, life-threatening flare of hyperthyroidism in which the body's metabolism and stress response go out of control. It causes high fever, a racing heart, agitation or confusion, and it needs intensive hospital care.",
  origin: "Surgeons in the late 1800s and early 1900s often saw patients with untreated Graves' disease develop high fever, delirium and heart failure after thyroid surgery, and many died. In 1923 Henry Plummer at the Mayo Clinic showed that giving iodine before surgery calmed the overactive gland, which sharply reduced deaths after thyroidectomy. Antithyroid drugs in the 1940s and beta blockers in the 1960s gave doctors tools to control the storm. Because there is no single lab test, Henry Burch and Leonard Wartofsky published a point scale in 1993 that scores fever, heart effects, brain effects and triggers to estimate the likelihood of storm. In 2012 the Japan Thyroid Association published its own criteria. Even with modern intensive care, thyroid storm still carries a significant death rate.",
  timeline: [["1923", "Henry Plummer introduces iodine before thyroid surgery, cutting postoperative storm."], ["1940s", "Thionamide antithyroid drugs come into use."], ["1993", "Burch and Wartofsky publish a point scale for diagnosing thyroid storm."], ["2012", "The Japan Thyroid Association publishes diagnostic criteria."]],
  cell: "Thyroid storm usually occurs in people with untreated or undertreated hyperthyroidism, most often Graves' disease, after a trigger such as infection, surgery, trauma, childbirth, DKA, stopping antithyroid drugs, radioactive iodine or a large iodine load such as contrast dye. Hormone levels are often not much higher than in ordinary hyperthyroidism, so the problem is the body's loss of ability to cope. Triggers can raise free hormone quickly by lowering binding proteins or releasing stored hormone. Excess T3 binds nuclear receptors and increases beta-adrenergic receptors, Na-K-ATPase activity and uncoupled heat production in mitochondria. The stress of the trigger releases catecholamines, which act on the sensitized receptors. The result is a hypermetabolic state with heat production that outpaces heat loss, very high oxygen demand, high-output heart strain progressing to heart failure and arrhythmias, and brain dysfunction. Liver injury and dehydration from fever, sweating, vomiting and diarrhea add to the danger.",
  body: [["Heart", "Very fast heart rates, atrial fibrillation and high-output heart failure develop as the heart cannot meet the body's demand."], ["Temperature", "Uncoupled heat production causes high fever and heavy sweating."], ["Brain", "Agitation, delirium, psychosis, seizures and coma reflect hormone and adrenaline effects on the brain."], ["Gut and liver", "Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea and belly pain are common; jaundice signals liver injury and a worse outlook."], ["Fluids", "Fever, sweating and diarrhea cause dehydration and low blood pressure."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms are an extreme version of hyperthyroidism. High fever and drenching sweats come from runaway heat production. A heart rate that is very fast or irregular, along with shortness of breath and swelling, comes from adrenaline sensitivity and heart strain. Agitation, confusion and delirium reflect the brain's response to excess hormone. Vomiting, diarrhea and jaundice come from gut and liver involvement. Older adults may instead present with apathy, weakness and heart failure.",
  dx: "Thyroid storm is a clinical diagnosis made at the bedside, because treatment cannot wait for test results. The Burch-Wartofsky Point Scale scores temperature, central nervous system effects, gut and liver effects, heart rate, heart failure, atrial fibrillation and the presence of a trigger; a high score strongly suggests storm. TSH is suppressed and free T4 and T3 are high, but the levels do not separate storm from uncomplicated hyperthyroidism. Labs also look for high glucose, high calcium, liver injury and infection.",
  tx: "Treatment attacks the problem at several steps at once, usually in an ICU. A beta blocker, often propranolol or esmolol, controls heart rate and adrenaline effects; propranolol also reduces T4 to T3 conversion. A thionamide blocks new hormone production: propylthiouracil is often preferred because it also blocks T4 to T3 conversion, though methimazole is also used. Iodine (such as potassium iodide) is given at least an hour after the thionamide to block release of stored hormone; giving iodine first could fuel more hormone production. Glucocorticoids such as hydrocortisone or dexamethasone reduce T4 to T3 conversion and cover possible adrenal insufficiency. Bile acid sequestrants such as cholestyramine can reduce hormone recycling through the gut. Cooling, acetaminophen, IV fluids with dextrose, and treatment of the trigger are essential. Aspirin is avoided because it can free hormone from binding proteins. Plasma exchange or urgent thyroidectomy is used when medicines fail.",
  drugs: ["propranolol", "esmolol", "propylthiouracil", "methimazole", "potassium-iodide", "hydrocortisone", "dexamethasone", "cholestyramine", "acetaminophen", "normal-saline", "dextrose"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Give the thionamide before iodine: PTU first, then iodine an hour later, so iodine does not become fuel for more hormone."], ["Memory trick", "The 5 Bs of storm: Block synthesis (PTU), Block release (iodine), Block conversion (PTU, steroids, propranolol), Beta block, and Bile acid sequestrant."], ["Key fact", "Acetaminophen, not aspirin, for fever: aspirin frees thyroid hormone from its carrier protein."], ["Often confused with", "Sepsis, sympathomimetic poisoning and serotonin syndrome can look similar; a known thyroid history and goiter point to storm."]],
  red: "High fever with a racing heart, confusion or agitation, vomiting or yellow skin in someone with hyperthyroidism needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Before Plummer's iodine method, operating on a thyroid in someone with active Graves' disease was among the riskiest surgeries of its day.",
  src: ["ATA Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis (2016)", "Burch HB, Wartofsky L. Endocrinol Metab Clin North Am 1993", "Akamizu T et al. Japan Thyroid Association criteria. Thyroid 2012", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"adrenal-insufficiency": {
  name: "Adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease)",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["Addison's disease", "primary adrenal insufficiency", "secondary adrenal insufficiency", "adrenal crisis", "hypoadrenalism"],
  what: "Adrenal insufficiency means the adrenal glands do not make enough cortisol, and in Addison's disease also not enough aldosterone. These hormones help the body handle stress and keep blood pressure, salt and sugar steady, so a lack causes fatigue, weight loss and, in a crisis, shock.",
  origin: "In 1855 English physician Thomas Addison, working at Guy's Hospital in London, described patients with weakness, anemia-like pallor, a weak heart, stomach upset and a bronze darkening of the skin, all linked at autopsy to diseased adrenal glands. At that time tuberculosis destroying the adrenals was the most common cause. Without treatment, the disease was fatal. In the 1930s and 1940s researchers including Edward Kendall in the US and Tadeus Reichstein in Switzerland isolated the adrenal steroids, and in 1948 Philip Hench showed cortisone's dramatic effects. Kendall, Hench and Reichstein shared the 1950 Nobel Prize. Cortisone, hydrocortisone and later fludrocortisone, which replaces aldosterone, turned Addison's disease into a manageable condition. Today autoimmune destruction of the adrenal cortex is the most common cause in developed countries, while secondary adrenal insufficiency from stopping long-term steroid medicines is the most common cause overall. US President John F. Kennedy had Addison's disease.",
  timeline: [["1855", "Thomas Addison describes the disease of the adrenal glands."], ["1948", "Philip Hench shows the effects of cortisone in patients."], ["1950", "Kendall, Hench and Reichstein receive the Nobel Prize for adrenal hormones."], ["1950s", "Fludrocortisone becomes available to replace aldosterone."], ["2016", "The Endocrine Society publishes guidelines on primary adrenal insufficiency."]],
  cell: "The adrenal cortex has three zones: the zona glomerulosa makes aldosterone, the zona fasciculata makes cortisol and the zona reticularis makes androgens. In primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease), the cortex itself is destroyed, most often by autoimmune attack with antibodies against 21-hydroxylase (CYP21A2), and less often by tuberculosis, fungal infection, cancer spread, bleeding into the glands (for example Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome in meningococcal sepsis or from anticoagulants), genetic conditions or drugs that block steroid production. Cortisol and aldosterone both fall. Low cortisol removes negative feedback, so the pituitary makes much more POMC, which is cut into ACTH and melanocyte-stimulating hormone; these stimulate MC1R on melanocytes and cause skin darkening. Low aldosterone means the kidney loses sodium and water and keeps potassium and hydrogen ions, causing low blood pressure, low sodium, high potassium and mild acidosis. In secondary insufficiency, from pituitary disease or suppression by long-term glucocorticoids, ACTH is low, so only cortisol is lacking: aldosterone is preserved by the renin-angiotensin system and there is no darkening or high potassium. In a crisis, the lack of cortisol leaves blood vessels unresponsive to catecholamines, causing shock.",
  body: [["Blood pressure", "Low cortisol reduces vascular response to adrenaline, and low aldosterone causes salt and water loss, leading to dizziness on standing and, in a crisis, shock."], ["Skin", "In primary disease, high ACTH and MSH darken the skin, especially skin creases, scars, gums and pressure points."], ["Kidneys and electrolytes", "Aldosterone loss causes salt wasting, low sodium, high potassium and salt craving."], ["Metabolism", "Cortisol normally supports glucose production, so low levels cause low blood sugar, weight loss and fatigue."], ["Gut", "Nausea, vomiting, belly pain and poor appetite are common and can be mistaken for a stomach illness."], ["Immune system", "Cortisol lack is linked to higher eosinophil counts and mild anemia."]],
  symptoms: "Fatigue, weakness, weight loss and poor appetite come from cortisol's role in energy and glucose balance. Lightheadedness on standing reflects low blood pressure from salt loss and weaker vessel tone. Salt craving is a direct result of aldosterone loss. Skin darkening, especially in palm creases and gums, comes from high ACTH and MSH in primary disease. Nausea, vomiting and belly pain are common. An adrenal crisis, often triggered by infection, surgery or stopping steroids, causes severe vomiting, confusion, very low blood pressure and shock.",
  dx: "A low early-morning cortisol suggests the diagnosis. The ACTH stimulation test uses cosyntropin, a synthetic ACTH, and a poor cortisol rise confirms adrenal insufficiency. Plasma ACTH then separates the types: high in primary disease, low or normal in secondary. Renin is high and aldosterone low in primary disease. Labs often show low sodium, high potassium and sometimes low glucose and high eosinophils. 21-hydroxylase antibodies support autoimmune disease, and CT can look for adrenal bleeding, infection or tumors. In a suspected crisis, treatment is never delayed for testing.",
  tx: "Treatment replaces the missing hormones. Hydrocortisone, which matches natural cortisol, is the usual daily glucocorticoid and is often split to mimic the body's morning peak; prednisone is an alternative. Children may use hydrocortisone granules. In primary disease, fludrocortisone replaces aldosterone by acting on mineralocorticoid receptors in the kidney, restoring salt balance and potassium. People are taught sick-day rules to increase glucocorticoid doses during illness, to carry an emergency injection kit and to wear medical alert identification. An adrenal crisis is treated with IV or IM hydrocortisone, large volumes of IV normal saline and dextrose for low blood sugar, along with treatment of the trigger.",
  drugs: ["hydrocortisone", "hydrocortisone-granules", "fludrocortisone", "prednisone", "dexamethasone", "normal-saline", "dextrose", "cosyntropin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Primary adrenal insufficiency has hyperpigmentation and high potassium; secondary does not, because ACTH is low and aldosterone is preserved."], ["Memory trick", "Addison's: Adds on pigment, drops sodium, raises potassium."], ["Key fact", "The most common cause of adrenal insufficiency overall is abruptly stopping long-term glucocorticoid medicines."], ["Often confused with", "Gastroenteritis or sepsis: a crisis with vomiting and shock that does not respond to fluids and vasopressors suggests adrenal crisis."]],
  red: "Severe vomiting, belly pain, confusion, fainting or very low blood pressure in someone with adrenal insufficiency can mean adrenal crisis and needs emergency care (call 911) and an emergency hydrocortisone injection.",
  fun: "President John F. Kennedy had Addison's disease, a fact kept largely private during his lifetime.",
  src: ["Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Diagnosis and Treatment of Primary Adrenal Insufficiency (2016)", "Addison T. On the Constitutional and Local Effects of Disease of the Supra-Renal Capsules (1855)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"cushings-syndrome": {
  name: "Cushing's syndrome",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["hypercortisolism", "Cushing's disease", "glucocorticoid excess", "ectopic ACTH syndrome"],
  what: "Cushing's syndrome is a condition caused by long-term high levels of cortisol, the body's main stress hormone. It causes weight gain in the face and trunk, thin skin, purple stretch marks, high blood pressure and diabetes, and it is most often caused by steroid medicines.",
  origin: "In 1912 American neurosurgeon Harvey Cushing described a young woman with rapid weight gain in the face and trunk, purple stretch marks, excess hair, high blood pressure and missed periods. In 1932 he linked this pattern to small basophil tumors of the pituitary gland, now called Cushing's disease. Doctors learned that the same features could come from adrenal tumors, from tumors elsewhere that make ACTH, and, once cortisone and other glucocorticoids were introduced in the 1950s, from steroid medicines, now the most common cause. In 1960 Grant Liddle described the dexamethasone suppression test, which helped tell the causes apart. Transsphenoidal pituitary surgery, refined with the operating microscope in the 1960s, became the main cure for Cushing's disease. In the 2010s and 2020s new medicines such as pasireotide, osilodrostat and levoketoconazole added options for people when surgery is not possible or does not cure.",
  timeline: [["1912", "Harvey Cushing describes the syndrome."], ["1932", "Cushing links the syndrome to pituitary basophil adenomas."], ["1960", "Grant Liddle describes the dexamethasone suppression test."], ["2012", "Pasireotide and mifepristone gain US approval for Cushing's-related uses."], ["2020", "Osilodrostat is approved for Cushing's disease."]],
  cell: "Cortisol is made in the adrenal zona fasciculata under the control of pituitary ACTH, with negative feedback to the pituitary and hypothalamus. Cushing's syndrome results from too much glucocorticoid over a long time. Exogenous steroids are the most common cause. Endogenous causes are ACTH-dependent, either a pituitary corticotroph adenoma (Cushing's disease, the most common endogenous cause) or ectopic ACTH from tumors such as small cell lung cancer or carcinoids, or ACTH-independent, from an adrenal adenoma, carcinoma or nodular hyperplasia. Excess cortisol binds glucocorticoid receptors in nearly every tissue and changes gene expression. It drives gluconeogenesis and causes insulin resistance, leading to high blood sugar. It breaks down protein in muscle, skin and bone, causing weakness, thin skin with purple striae and osteoporosis, and it inhibits osteoblasts and collagen synthesis. It redistributes fat to the face, upper back and abdomen. At high levels cortisol overwhelms 11-beta-HSD2 in the kidney and activates mineralocorticoid receptors, causing high blood pressure and low potassium, especially with ectopic ACTH. It suppresses lymphocytes and inflammation, raising infection risk.",
  body: [["Fat distribution", "Fat moves to the face (moon face), the back of the neck (buffalo hump) and the belly, while arms and legs stay thin."], ["Skin", "Protein breakdown thins the skin, causing easy bruising, slow healing and wide purple stretch marks on the belly and thighs."], ["Muscles and bones", "Muscle breakdown causes weakness of the hips and shoulders, and suppressed bone formation causes osteoporosis and fractures."], ["Metabolism", "Insulin resistance and increased glucose production cause prediabetes or diabetes."], ["Heart and vessels", "Hypertension, clotting tendency and atherosclerosis raise the risk of heart attack, stroke and blood clots."], ["Mind and hormones", "Depression, anxiety, insomnia and memory problems are common; menstrual changes, excess hair and low libido occur."]],
  symptoms: "Weight gain in the face and trunk with thin limbs reflects cortisol's fat redistribution and muscle breakdown. Purple stretch marks wider than a centimeter, easy bruising and thin skin are among the most specific signs because cortisol breaks down collagen. Proximal muscle weakness makes it hard to climb stairs or rise from a chair. High blood pressure, high blood sugar, kidney stones, frequent infections and mood changes reflect cortisol's effects on many organs. Excess hair and acne can come from adrenal androgens, especially in ACTH-dependent forms. Skin darkening suggests very high ACTH, as in ectopic ACTH syndrome.",
  dx: "Exogenous steroid use is ruled out first. Screening uses at least one of three tests: late-night salivary cortisol (which is normally lowest at night), 24-hour urine free cortisol, or the overnight low-dose dexamethasone suppression test, in which cortisol normally falls but does not in Cushing's syndrome. Once confirmed, plasma ACTH separates the causes: low ACTH points to an adrenal source and prompts adrenal CT, while normal or high ACTH points to the pituitary or an ectopic source. Pituitary MRI, high-dose dexamethasone testing, CRH or desmopressin stimulation and inferior petrosal sinus sampling help tell pituitary from ectopic sources. Imaging of the chest and abdomen looks for ectopic tumors.",
  tx: "Treatment removes the source of excess cortisol. Steroid medicines are tapered slowly when possible. Transsphenoidal surgery to remove a pituitary adenoma is first-line for Cushing's disease, an adrenal tumor is removed by adrenalectomy, and an ectopic ACTH tumor is removed or treated. Medicines are used when surgery is not possible or not curative. Osilodrostat blocks 11-beta-hydroxylase, levoketoconazole and ketoconazole (off-label) block several steroid enzymes, and metyrapone also blocks 11-beta-hydroxylase (used off-label for treatment in the US). Pasireotide acts on somatostatin receptors on pituitary tumors to lower ACTH, and cabergoline is used off-label. Mifepristone blocks glucocorticoid receptors and is used to control high blood sugar. Radiation and bilateral adrenalectomy are other options. After cure, the normal glands are suppressed, so hydrocortisone replacement is needed for months. Blood pressure, diabetes, bone loss and clot risk are treated too.",
  drugs: ["osilodrostat", "levoketoconazole", "ketoconazole", "metyrapone", "pasireotide", "mifepristone", "cabergoline", "hydrocortisone", "spironolactone", "dexamethasone-oral"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "The most common cause of Cushing's syndrome is exogenous glucocorticoids; the most common endogenous cause is a pituitary adenoma (Cushing's disease)."], ["Lab pattern", "High-dose dexamethasone suppresses a pituitary source but usually not an ectopic or adrenal one."], ["Name check", "Cushing's disease means a pituitary ACTH-secreting adenoma; Cushing's syndrome means high cortisol from any cause."], ["Often confused with", "Obesity and metabolic syndrome: wide purple striae, easy bruising and proximal weakness favor Cushing's."]],
  red: "Sudden severe weakness, chest pain, a swollen painful leg, signs of serious infection or very high blood sugar in someone with Cushing's syndrome needs emergency care (call 911). After treatment, vomiting, faintness or confusion can mean adrenal crisis.",
  fun: "Harvey Cushing is also considered a founder of modern neurosurgery and won a Pulitzer Prize in 1926 for his biography of physician William Osler.",
  src: ["Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Treatment of Cushing's Syndrome (2015)", "Nieman LK et al. The Diagnosis of Cushing's Syndrome. J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2008", "Cushing H. Bull Johns Hopkins Hosp 1932", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"hyperaldosteronism": {
  name: "Primary hyperaldosteronism",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["primary aldosteronism", "Conn syndrome", "aldosterone-producing adenoma", "bilateral adrenal hyperplasia"],
  what: "Primary hyperaldosteronism is a condition in which one or both adrenal glands make too much aldosterone on their own. Aldosterone makes the kidneys hold on to salt and lose potassium, causing high blood pressure that is often hard to control and sometimes low potassium.",
  origin: "Aldosterone was isolated in 1953 by Sylvia Simpson and James Tait in London, working with Tadeus Reichstein. Just a year later, American endocrinologist Jerome Conn at the University of Michigan studied a woman with high blood pressure, muscle weakness and very low potassium. He suspected an adrenal tumor making excess aldosterone, and surgeons removed an adrenal adenoma, which cured her. Conn reported the case in 1955, and the condition became known as Conn syndrome. For decades it was thought to be rare, found mainly in people with low potassium. Wider screening with the aldosterone-to-renin ratio from the 1980s and 1990s showed it is the most common curable cause of high blood pressure and that most patients have normal potassium. In 2011 researchers found mutations in the KCNJ5 potassium channel gene in many aldosterone-producing adenomas. Recent Endocrine Society guidance favors screening far more people with hypertension.",
  timeline: [["1953", "Simpson and Tait isolate aldosterone."], ["1955", "Jerome Conn reports an aldosterone-producing adrenal tumor."], ["1981", "The aldosterone-to-renin ratio is proposed as a screening test."], ["2011", "KCNJ5 mutations are found in aldosterone-producing adenomas."], ["2016", "The Endocrine Society publishes an updated guideline on primary aldosteronism."]],
  cell: "Aldosterone is normally made in the adrenal zona glomerulosa by aldosterone synthase (CYP11B2), driven mainly by angiotensin II and potassium. In primary aldosteronism, production becomes autonomous. The two main causes are an aldosterone-producing adenoma, usually on one side, and bilateral adrenal hyperplasia. Many adenomas carry somatic mutations in KCNJ5, which encodes a potassium channel; the mutant channel lets sodium in, depolarizing the cell and opening calcium channels, and calcium drives CYP11B2 and cell growth. Other mutations affect calcium channels and pumps (CACNA1D, ATP1A1, ATP2B3). Rare familial forms include glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism, in which a fused gene puts aldosterone synthase under ACTH control. Excess aldosterone binds mineralocorticoid receptors in kidney collecting duct principal cells, increasing ENaC channels and Na-K-ATPase, so sodium is absorbed and potassium and hydrogen ions are excreted. This causes volume expansion and hypertension, potassium loss and metabolic alkalosis. Renin is suppressed. Edema is uncommon because of aldosterone escape, in which pressure natriuresis and natriuretic peptides limit further sodium retention. Aldosterone also acts on the heart, vessels and kidneys to promote inflammation and fibrosis.",
  body: [["Blood pressure", "Salt and water retention raise blood pressure, often to levels that resist several medicines."], ["Kidneys", "Potassium and acid are lost in the urine; long-term exposure damages the kidneys and causes protein in the urine."], ["Heart", "Aldosterone promotes heart muscle thickening and scarring, raising the risk of atrial fibrillation, heart failure and heart attack beyond the effect of blood pressure alone."], ["Muscles", "Low potassium causes weakness, cramps and, rarely, paralysis."], ["Blood chemistry", "Low potassium, metabolic alkalosis and sometimes high-normal sodium; renin is suppressed."]],
  symptoms: "Many people have no symptoms other than high blood pressure. When potassium is low, muscle weakness, cramps, fatigue and palpitations occur because nerve and muscle cells depend on the potassium gradient. Frequent urination and thirst can come from low potassium impairing the kidney's ability to concentrate urine. Headaches can come from the high blood pressure. Swelling is uncommon because of aldosterone escape.",
  dx: "Screening uses the plasma aldosterone-to-renin ratio: aldosterone is high or inappropriately normal while renin is suppressed. Groups commonly screened include people with resistant hypertension, hypertension with low potassium (spontaneous or from diuretics), an adrenal nodule, sleep apnea, atrial fibrillation or a family history of early hypertension or stroke. A positive screen is often confirmed with a suppression test, such as saline infusion or oral salt loading, in which aldosterone fails to fall. Adrenal CT looks for a nodule, but small adenomas can be missed and nodules can be non-functioning, so adrenal vein sampling is used to tell one-sided from two-sided disease before surgery.",
  tx: "One-sided disease, usually an adenoma, is treated with laparoscopic removal of the affected adrenal gland, which normalizes potassium in nearly everyone and cures or improves blood pressure. Two-sided disease, or people who do not want or cannot have surgery, are treated with mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists. Spironolactone blocks aldosterone receptors but can cause breast enlargement and other hormone effects because it also blocks androgen receptors; eplerenone is more selective. Amiloride blocks ENaC channels directly. Potassium supplements correct low levels, and other blood pressure medicines are added as needed. Glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism responds to low-dose glucocorticoids that suppress ACTH. A low-salt diet helps.",
  drugs: ["spironolactone", "eplerenone", "amiloride", "potassium-chloride", "amlodipine", "dexamethasone-oral"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Primary aldosteronism: hypertension, low potassium, metabolic alkalosis, high aldosterone and low renin."], ["Memory trick", "Primary means the adrenal is the boss: renin is low. Secondary means the kidney is the boss: renin is high."], ["Key fact", "Most patients have normal potassium, so normal potassium does not rule it out."], ["Often confused with", "Renal artery stenosis also causes hypertension with high aldosterone, but renin is high."]],
  red: "Severe muscle weakness or paralysis, a very irregular heartbeat, chest pain or a very high blood pressure with headache, confusion or vision changes needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Jerome Conn's first patient was cured by removing an adrenal tumor only about a year after aldosterone itself was discovered.",
  src: ["Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Management of Primary Aldosteronism (2016)", "Conn JW. J Lab Clin Med 1955", "Choi M et al. KCNJ5 mutations. Science 2011", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"pheochromocytoma": {
  name: "Pheochromocytoma",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["pheo", "paraganglioma", "PPGL", "chromaffin cell tumor"],
  what: "A pheochromocytoma is a rare tumor of the adrenal gland's inner layer that makes too much adrenaline and related hormones. It causes spells of pounding headache, sweating and a racing heart, often with very high blood pressure.",
  origin: "In 1886 German physician Felix Frankel described an 18-year-old woman who had attacks of palpitations, headache and vomiting and died suddenly; autopsy showed tumors in both adrenal glands. In 1912 pathologist Ludwig Pick named the tumor pheochromocytoma, from Greek words meaning dusky color, because the tumor cells turn dark brown with chromium salts. The first successful removals were done by Cesar Roux in Switzerland in 1926 and Charles Mayo in the United States in 1927. Surgery was risky because handling the tumor released bursts of catecholamines; alpha blockers such as phenoxybenzamine, used before surgery from the 1950s on, made it much safer. Diagnosis improved from urine catecholamine tests to plasma free metanephrines, shown in a 2002 study to be highly sensitive. Genetic studies since the 1990s have shown that roughly a third or more of these tumors are inherited, linked to genes such as RET, VHL, NF1 and the SDH genes.",
  timeline: [["1886", "Felix Frankel describes bilateral adrenal tumors in a young woman."], ["1912", "Ludwig Pick names the tumor pheochromocytoma."], ["1926", "Cesar Roux performs the first successful removal."], ["1927", "Charles Mayo successfully removes a pheochromocytoma in the US."], ["2002", "Plasma free metanephrines are shown to be a top diagnostic test."], ["2014", "The Endocrine Society publishes its pheochromocytoma guideline."]],
  cell: "Pheochromocytomas arise from chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla, which are neural crest-derived cells; similar tumors outside the adrenal are paragangliomas. Chromaffin cells make catecholamines: tyrosine hydroxylase converts tyrosine to DOPA (the rate-limiting step), then DOPA becomes dopamine, then norepinephrine, and in the adrenal, PNMT, which is induced by cortisol from the surrounding cortex, converts norepinephrine to epinephrine. Tumor cells make catecholamines without normal nerve control and release them in bursts or continuously. Inside the tumor, the enzyme COMT steadily converts them to metanephrine and normetanephrine, which leak out constantly, which is why metanephrines are the best test. Released catecholamines act on alpha-1 receptors (vasoconstriction, high blood pressure), beta-1 receptors (fast, forceful heartbeat) and beta-2 and metabolic pathways (high glucose, sweating). Many tumors are hereditary: MEN2 (RET), von Hippel-Lindau (VHL), neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) and succinate dehydrogenase (SDHx) mutations, which cause pseudohypoxic signaling and a higher risk of spread.",
  body: [["Heart and vessels", "Catecholamine surges cause severe high blood pressure, fast heart rate and arrhythmias, and long exposure can cause a weakened heart (catecholamine cardiomyopathy)."], ["Brain", "Very high blood pressure causes pounding headaches and can cause stroke or brain bleeding."], ["Skin", "Sweating and pallor during attacks come from sympathetic activation and constricted skin vessels."], ["Metabolism", "Catecholamines raise glucose and metabolic rate, causing high blood sugar and weight loss."], ["Mind", "Attacks bring anxiety, tremor and a sense of doom that can be mistaken for panic attacks."], ["Gut", "Constipation and, rarely, bowel injury from vasoconstriction can occur."]],
  symptoms: "The classic triad is episodic headache, sweating and a racing heart (palpitations), caused by bursts of catecholamines. Blood pressure may be high only during spells or constantly. Spells can be triggered by exercise, pressure on the belly, surgery, childbirth or drugs such as some anesthetics, glucocorticoids, metoclopramide and tricyclic antidepressants. Pallor, tremor and anxiety reflect sympathetic overactivity. Some people have no symptoms, and the tumor is found by chance on a scan done for another reason.",
  dx: "The first test is plasma free metanephrines or 24-hour urine fractionated metanephrines, which are high because the tumor constantly converts catecholamines to metanephrines. Drugs such as tricyclic antidepressants can cause false positives. Once biochemistry is clearly positive, CT or MRI of the abdomen and pelvis finds the tumor; on MRI it often appears bright on T2 images. Functional imaging such as DOTATATE PET or MIBG scanning looks for spread or extra-adrenal tumors. Genetic testing is recommended for everyone with this tumor.",
  tx: "Surgery to remove the tumor, usually laparoscopic adrenalectomy, is the cure. Before surgery, alpha blockade for one to two weeks is essential: phenoxybenzamine (irreversible, nonselective) or selective alpha-1 blockers such as doxazosin or prazosin relax blood vessels and lower blood pressure. A high-salt diet and fluids refill the shrunken blood volume. A beta blocker is added only after alpha blockade is in place, to control fast heart rate; starting it first can cause dangerous high blood pressure from unopposed alpha stimulation. Calcium channel blockers and metyrosine, which blocks tyrosine hydroxylase and lowers catecholamine production, are added in some cases. During surgery, phentolamine, nicardipine or nitroprusside treat pressure spikes. After removal, low blood pressure and low blood sugar can occur. Metastatic disease is treated with surgery, radiation, targeted radiopharmaceuticals and chemotherapy, and family members are offered genetic testing.",
  drugs: ["phenoxybenzamine", "doxazosin", "prazosin", "metyrosine", "propranolol", "metoprolol", "phentolamine", "nicardipine", "nitroprusside", "amlodipine", "normal-saline"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Alpha blockade before beta blockade: a beta blocker alone leaves alpha receptors unopposed and can cause a hypertensive crisis."], ["Memory trick", "The 5 Ps: Pressure, Pain (headache), Perspiration, Palpitations, Pallor."], ["Genetics", "Linked to MEN2A and MEN2B (RET), von Hippel-Lindau, NF1 and SDH mutations; the old rule of 10s underestimates how often it is inherited."], ["Often confused with", "Panic attacks, hyperthyroidism and stimulant use can cause similar spells; metanephrine testing tells them apart."]],
  red: "A sudden severe headache with very high blood pressure, chest pain, a racing or irregular heartbeat, vision changes or weakness on one side needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The name pheochromocytoma describes a lab trick: the tumor cells turn dusky brown when exposed to chromium salts, called the chromaffin reaction.",
  src: ["Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Pheochromocytoma and Paraganglioma (2014)", "Lenders JWM et al. JAMA 2002", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"diabetes-insipidus": {
  name: "Diabetes insipidus",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["DI", "arginine vasopressin deficiency", "AVP-D", "arginine vasopressin resistance", "AVP-R", "central diabetes insipidus", "nephrogenic diabetes insipidus"],
  what: "Diabetes insipidus is a rare condition in which the body cannot hold on to water, so a person passes large amounts of very dilute urine and feels constantly thirsty. It is caused by a lack of the hormone vasopressin (ADH) or by kidneys that do not respond to it. It is unrelated to blood sugar.",
  origin: "Both kinds of diabetes cause heavy urination. In the 1600s Thomas Willis noted that some diabetic urine tasted sweet, and by the late 1700s physicians, including Johann Peter Frank, separated a form with tasteless urine, calling it diabetes insipidus, meaning without taste. In 1895 George Oliver and Edward Schafer found that extracts of the pituitary raised blood pressure. In 1913 physicians in Italy and Germany showed that posterior pituitary extracts reduced urine output in people with diabetes insipidus. The hormone, vasopressin, was synthesized by Vincent du Vigneaud in the 1950s, work honored with the 1955 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Desmopressin, a modified vasopressin that acts on the kidney without raising blood pressure, was developed in the late 1960s and became the main treatment. Nephrogenic forms were later traced to mutations in the vasopressin V2 receptor and aquaporin-2 genes. In 2022 international endocrine groups proposed renaming the condition arginine vasopressin deficiency and arginine vasopressin resistance to avoid confusion with diabetes mellitus.",
  timeline: [["1913", "Posterior pituitary extract is shown to reduce urine in diabetes insipidus."], ["1955", "Vincent du Vigneaud wins the Nobel Prize for synthesizing pituitary hormones."], ["1960s", "Desmopressin is developed as a kidney-selective vasopressin analog."], ["1992", "Mutations in the vasopressin V2 receptor are linked to nephrogenic diabetes insipidus."], ["2022", "New names, AVP deficiency and AVP resistance, are proposed."]],
  cell: "Vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone, ADH) is made by neurons in the hypothalamus (supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei) and released from the posterior pituitary when blood osmolality rises or blood volume falls. It binds V2 receptors on kidney collecting duct principal cells. The V2 receptor couples to Gs, raising cyclic AMP and activating protein kinase A, which inserts aquaporin-2 water channels into the cell membrane so water is reabsorbed and urine is concentrated. In central diabetes insipidus (AVP deficiency), these neurons are damaged by surgery, head trauma, tumors such as craniopharyngioma, infiltrative diseases such as Langerhans cell histiocytosis or sarcoidosis, autoimmune inflammation or genetic defects, or no cause is found. Symptoms usually appear when most vasopressin neurons are lost. In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (AVP resistance), vasopressin is present but the kidney does not respond: inherited mutations in the X-linked AVPR2 gene or the AQP2 gene, lithium (which enters principal cells through ENaC and reduces aquaporin-2), high calcium, low potassium and kidney disease. Either way, water is lost in urine. If thirst and water access are intact, people drink enough to keep sodium normal; if not, sodium rises and brain cells shrink.",
  body: [["Kidneys", "Without vasopressin action, the collecting ducts cannot reabsorb water, producing large volumes of dilute urine."], ["Brain", "High sodium and high osmolality draw water out of brain cells, causing irritability, confusion, seizures and, in severe cases, brain bleeding."], ["Thirst and fluid balance", "Intense thirst, often for ice-cold water, drives very high fluid intake; without water, dehydration develops fast."], ["Bladder", "Constant high urine flow can stretch the bladder and ureters over time."], ["Children", "Infants with nephrogenic DI may have fever, vomiting, poor growth and repeated dehydration."]],
  symptoms: "Large volumes of pale urine, day and night, come from the kidney's inability to concentrate urine. Waking at night to urinate and bedwetting are common. Intense thirst is the body's defense against water loss. If water intake cannot keep up, as in infants, older adults, after surgery or in someone who is unconscious, high sodium causes weakness, irritability, confusion, fever and seizures. Central DI after pituitary surgery or head injury can appear suddenly.",
  dx: "Testing first confirms a high volume of dilute urine (low urine osmolality) and rules out high glucose, which causes heavy urination for a different reason. Serum sodium and osmolality are normal or high. The traditional water deprivation test watches whether urine concentrates when fluids are stopped, and then whether it concentrates after desmopressin: a big response points to central DI, little response points to nephrogenic DI. Copeptin, a stable fragment made along with vasopressin, can be measured at baseline or after stimulation with hypertonic saline or arginine to help separate DI from primary polydipsia (drinking too much). Pituitary MRI looks for a cause in central DI.",
  tx: "Central DI is treated with desmopressin, a synthetic vasopressin analog that acts on V2 receptors to restore water reabsorption without raising blood pressure. It comes as tablets, nasal spray and injection. Because it holds water, the main risk is low sodium, so drinking to thirst rather than forcing fluids is emphasized and sodium is monitored. Nephrogenic DI is treated by fixing the cause, such as stopping lithium if possible or correcting calcium or potassium. Thiazide diuretics such as hydrochlorothiazide paradoxically reduce urine volume by causing mild volume loss, which increases water reabsorption earlier in the nephron. Amiloride blocks lithium entry into collecting duct cells, and NSAIDs such as indomethacin reduce prostaglandins that oppose water reabsorption. A low-salt, moderate-protein diet lowers the solute load and urine volume. Free access to water is essential.",
  drugs: ["desmopressin", "hydrochlorothiazide", "chlorthalidone", "amiloride", "indomethacin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Central DI responds to desmopressin; nephrogenic DI does not. Lithium is the classic drug cause of nephrogenic DI, treated with amiloride."], ["Memory trick", "DI makes you Dry Inside: lots of dilute urine, high sodium if water is not replaced."], ["Often confused with", "SIADH is the opposite: too much ADH, concentrated urine and low sodium. Primary polydipsia has low sodium and dilute urine."], ["Key fact", "Thiazides paradoxically reduce urine output in nephrogenic DI."]],
  red: "Confusion, extreme weakness, seizures or being unable to drink enough to keep up with urine in someone with diabetes insipidus needs emergency care (call 911). Headache, vomiting or confusion while taking desmopressin can mean dangerously low sodium.",
  fun: "Insipidus means tasteless, a reminder that early doctors told the two diabetes types apart by tasting urine.",
  src: ["Endocrine Society and partner societies. Changing the name of diabetes insipidus (2022)", "Fenske W et al. Copeptin in the diagnosis of polyuria. N Engl J Med 2018", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"siadh": {
  name: "SIADH",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone", "syndrome of inappropriate antidiuresis", "SIAD", "euvolemic hyponatremia"],
  what: "SIADH is a condition in which the body releases too much antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin), so the kidneys hold on to too much water. The extra water dilutes the blood, causing low sodium, which can lead to headache, confusion and seizures.",
  origin: "In 1957 William Schwartz, Frederic Bartter and colleagues described two men with lung cancer who had very low sodium and kept losing salt in their urine despite having no heart, liver or kidney failure. They proposed that the tumors were causing inappropriate release of antidiuretic hormone, and that fluid restriction corrected the sodium. In 1967 Bartter and Schwartz set out diagnostic criteria still used today. In 1959 Raymond Adams and colleagues described central pontine myelinolysis in malnourished patients and those with heavy alcohol use; in the 1980s Richard Sterns and others linked it to raising sodium too quickly, which led to today's careful correction limits. Measurement of vasopressin showed that many tumors, especially small cell lung cancer, and many drugs and brain and lung diseases could cause the syndrome. Vasopressin receptor antagonists, conivaptan in 2005 and tolvaptan in 2009, gave a targeted way to remove free water.",
  timeline: [["1957", "Schwartz and Bartter describe SIADH in two patients with lung cancer."], ["1959", "Adams and colleagues describe central pontine myelinolysis."], ["1967", "Bartter and Schwartz publish diagnostic criteria for SIADH."], ["1986", "Rapid correction of low sodium is linked to osmotic demyelination."], ["2009", "Tolvaptan is approved for hyponatremia including SIADH."]],
  cell: "Vasopressin normally rises only when blood is concentrated or volume is low. In SIADH it is released despite low osmolality. Causes include ectopic production by tumors (especially small cell lung cancer), brain disorders (stroke, bleeding, infection, trauma), lung disease (pneumonia, tuberculosis), drugs (SSRIs, carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, cyclophosphamide, vincristine, MDMA), pain, nausea and surgery. Vasopressin binds V2 receptors in the kidney collecting duct, activating the Gs-cAMP pathway and inserting aquaporin-2 channels, so free water is reabsorbed and urine stays concentrated. The retained water dilutes sodium. Volume expansion is mild because the body responds by releasing natriuretic peptides and suppressing renin and aldosterone, which increases sodium excretion. The result is euvolemic, hypotonic hyponatremia with inappropriately concentrated urine and high urine sodium. Low blood osmolality draws water into brain cells, causing swelling. Over about 48 hours, brain cells adapt by exporting electrolytes and organic osmolytes. Because of this adaptation, raising sodium too fast pulls water out of adapted cells and can injure myelin, causing osmotic demyelination syndrome.",
  body: [["Brain", "Water moves into brain cells, causing swelling, headache, confusion, seizures and, in severe cases, herniation; chronic mild low sodium causes falls and attention problems."], ["Kidneys", "The collecting ducts keep reabsorbing water, and urine stays concentrated while extra sodium is excreted."], ["Muscles and bones", "Chronic low sodium causes unsteady gait, falls and is linked to bone loss and fractures."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are common; nausea itself is a strong stimulus for more vasopressin."], ["Fluid balance", "Body water is slightly increased, but there is no edema and blood pressure is usually normal."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms depend on how low and how fast sodium falls. Mild, slow low sodium may cause no symptoms or subtle problems such as fatigue, poor concentration and unsteady walking, which raise the risk of falls. As brain swelling increases, people develop headache, nausea, vomiting, confusion and drowsiness. Severe or rapid drops can cause seizures, breathing arrest and coma. Edema is absent because the body excretes extra sodium.",
  dx: "Diagnosis rests on the Bartter-Schwartz criteria: low serum sodium with low serum osmolality, urine that is inappropriately concentrated, high urine sodium on normal salt intake, normal volume status (no dehydration or edema), and normal thyroid, adrenal and kidney function with no recent diuretic use. Serum osmolality rules out pseudohyponatremia and high glucose. Clinicians then look for the cause, such as a chest X-ray or CT for lung cancer or infection, brain imaging and a medication review. Uric acid and BUN are often low.",
  tx: "Treatment depends on symptoms and speed of onset. Severe symptoms such as seizures or coma are treated with small boluses of hypertonic (3 percent) saline to raise sodium quickly by a small amount and reduce brain swelling. In all cases, the total rise in sodium over each day is limited to avoid osmotic demyelination; if sodium rises too fast, clinicians may give desmopressin and water to slow it. For chronic SIADH, fluid restriction is first-line, along with treating the cause and stopping offending drugs. Increasing solute intake with salt tablets or oral urea helps the kidneys excrete more water, and loop diuretics such as furosemide reduce the kidney's ability to concentrate urine. Vasopressin receptor antagonists (vaptans) block V2 receptors and produce free water loss: conivaptan is IV for hospital use, and tolvaptan is oral, started in the hospital, with liver injury limiting long-term use.",
  drugs: ["hypertonic-saline-3-percent", "tolvaptan", "conivaptan", "furosemide", "desmopressin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "SIADH: euvolemic hyponatremia, low serum osmolality, concentrated urine and high urine sodium; small cell lung cancer is the classic tumor cause."], ["Memory trick", "SIADH: Soaked Inside, Always Diluted Hyponatremia; too much water, not too little salt."], ["Key fact", "Correcting chronic low sodium too fast can cause osmotic demyelination: low to high, the pons will die."], ["Often confused with", "Cerebral salt wasting also follows brain injury but causes volume loss and is treated with salt and fluids, not restriction."]],
  red: "Confusion, severe headache, vomiting, seizures or extreme drowsiness in someone with low sodium needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Normal saline does not reliably fix SIADH and can make sodium drop further, because the kidneys excrete the salt and keep the water.",
  src: ["Verbalis JG et al. Diagnosis, Evaluation, and Treatment of Hyponatremia: Expert Panel Recommendations. Am J Med 2013", "Schwartz WB, Bennett W, Curelop S, Bartter FC. Am J Med 1957", "Spasovski G et al. European Clinical Practice Guideline on Hyponatraemia (2014)", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"obesity": {
  name: "Obesity",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["excess adiposity", "overweight", "severe obesity", "adiposity-based chronic disease"],
  what: "Obesity is a chronic disease in which excess or poorly distributed body fat harms health. It is shaped by genes, biology, environment and life circumstances, not just willpower, and it raises the risk of diabetes, heart disease, sleep apnea, joint problems and some cancers.",
  origin: "Ancient physicians noticed links between body size and health; a Hippocratic writing observed that sudden death was more common in people who were naturally very heavy. In the 1830s Belgian scientist Adolphe Quetelet devised the weight-for-height index that American physiologist Ancel Keys named the body mass index (BMI) in 1972. In 1949 scientists at the Jackson Laboratory in Maine found a strain of extremely obese mice, and in 1994 Jeffrey Friedman's team at Rockefeller University showed the mice lacked leptin, a hormone made by fat cells that tells the brain how much energy is stored. This helped show that body weight is regulated biologically. Weight-loss surgery developed from the 1950s, and Edward Mason introduced gastric bypass in the 1960s. In 2013 the American Medical Association recognized obesity as a disease. GLP-1 based medicines, semaglutide in 2021 and tirzepatide in 2023, produced weight loss far larger than earlier drugs. In 2025 a Lancet commission proposed separating clinical obesity, with organ effects, from preclinical obesity.",
  timeline: [["1972", "Ancel Keys names the body mass index."], ["1994", "Jeffrey Friedman's lab discovers leptin."], ["2013", "The American Medical Association recognizes obesity as a disease."], ["2021", "Semaglutide is approved for chronic weight management."], ["2023", "Tirzepatide is approved for chronic weight management."], ["2025", "A Lancet commission proposes definitions of clinical and preclinical obesity."]],
  cell: "Body weight is regulated by the brain, especially the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus. POMC neurons release alpha-MSH, which activates melanocortin-4 receptors (MC4R) to reduce hunger and raise energy use, while AgRP and NPY neurons increase hunger. Leptin from fat cells signals long-term energy stores, and gut hormones such as ghrelin (hunger) and GLP-1, PYY and GIP (fullness) signal short-term status. In common obesity, many genes each add a small effect, and environments with abundant, energy-dense food, low activity, poor sleep, stress and some medicines push weight up. High leptin levels develop with reduced brain response (leptin resistance), and after weight loss the brain increases hunger and lowers energy use, which favors regain. Rare single-gene forms involve MC4R, leptin, the leptin receptor or POMC. As fat cells enlarge, they become stressed and hypoxic, attract inflammatory macrophages, release TNF-alpha and IL-6 and less adiponectin, and spill fatty acids into the liver, muscle and pancreas. This ectopic fat and inflammation cause insulin resistance, fatty liver, high triglycerides and blood vessel injury. Fat tissue also converts androgens to estrogen through aromatase.",
  body: [["Metabolism", "Insulin resistance from inflamed fat and ectopic fat raises the risk of prediabetes and type 2 diabetes."], ["Heart and vessels", "Obesity raises blood pressure, worsens cholesterol and triglycerides, enlarges the heart and increases risk of heart failure, atrial fibrillation and heart attack."], ["Lungs and sleep", "Fat around the neck and chest narrows the airway during sleep, causing obstructive sleep apnea, and can cause obesity hypoventilation."], ["Liver", "Fat buildup in the liver causes metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, which can progress to cirrhosis."], ["Joints", "Extra load and inflammation speed osteoarthritis of the knees and hips."], ["Hormones and reproduction", "Obesity is linked to irregular periods, PCOS, infertility, low testosterone and pregnancy complications."], ["Cancer risk", "Obesity is linked to higher rates of cancers including endometrial, breast after menopause, colon, kidney, liver and esophageal adenocarcinoma."]],
  symptoms: "Many effects of obesity are silent until complications appear. Shortness of breath and reduced exercise capacity come from higher energy demand and chest wall load. Snoring, daytime sleepiness and morning headaches suggest sleep apnea. Joint pain comes from mechanical load and inflammation. Heartburn reflects higher abdominal pressure. Dark velvety skin in folds (acanthosis nigricans) reflects high insulin. People with obesity also often face weight stigma, which can affect mental health and access to care.",
  dx: "In adults, BMI of 25 to under 30 is classed as overweight and 30 or higher as obesity, with lower cutoffs often used for people of Asian ancestry. Because BMI does not measure fat directly, clinicians add waist circumference, waist-to-height ratio or body composition, and look for obesity-related conditions. In children and teens, obesity is a BMI at or above the 95th percentile for age and sex. Evaluation includes blood pressure, A1C or glucose, lipids, liver tests, a sleep history and a review of medicines that cause weight gain. Genetic testing is considered for severe early-onset obesity.",
  tx: "Care is long-term and person-centered. Nutrition changes, physical activity, sleep and behavioral support are the foundation. Medicines target the biology of appetite. GLP-1 receptor agonists (semaglutide, liraglutide) and the dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist tirzepatide act on brain and gut receptors to increase fullness, reduce hunger and slow stomach emptying. Phentermine-topiramate combines a sympathomimetic appetite suppressant with topiramate. Naltrexone-bupropion acts on hypothalamic POMC neurons and reward pathways. Orlistat blocks gut lipase so less fat is absorbed. Phentermine alone is used short term. Setmelanotide, an MC4R agonist, treats specific genetic forms. Metabolic and bariatric surgery, such as sleeve gastrectomy and gastric bypass, produces the largest, most durable weight loss and often remission of type 2 diabetes. Treatment of related conditions and respectful, stigma-free care improve outcomes.",
  drugs: ["semaglutide", "tirzepatide", "liraglutide", "phentermine-topiramate", "naltrexone-bupropion", "orlistat", "phentermine", "setmelanotide", "metformin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Leptin is made by fat cells and signals satiety to the hypothalamus; ghrelin from the stomach signals hunger."], ["Memory trick", "Leptin keeps you lean; ghrelin makes your stomach growl."], ["Key fact", "MC4R mutations are the most common single-gene cause of severe obesity."], ["Language tip", "Person-first language, such as person with obesity, reduces stigma."]],
  red: "Sudden chest pain, severe shortness of breath, a swollen painful leg or loud snoring with pauses in breathing and extreme sleepiness needs prompt medical care; chest pain or severe breathing trouble needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The obese mouse strain that led to leptin's discovery appeared by chance at the Jackson Laboratory in 1949, decades before its gene was found.",
  src: ["Obesity Medicine Association and AACE Clinical Practice Guidelines", "Rubino F et al. Lancet Diabetes and Endocrinology Commission on Clinical Obesity (2025)", "Zhang Y, Friedman JM et al. Nature 1994", "AAP Clinical Practice Guideline for Pediatric Obesity (2023)", "CDC"]
},
"metabolic-syndrome": {
  name: "Metabolic syndrome",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["syndrome X", "insulin resistance syndrome", "cardiometabolic syndrome", "dysmetabolic syndrome"],
  what: "Metabolic syndrome is a cluster of risk factors that tend to occur together: a large waist, high blood pressure, high blood sugar, high triglycerides and low HDL (good) cholesterol. Having several of them sharply raises the risk of heart disease, stroke and type 2 diabetes.",
  origin: "In the 1920s Swedish physician Eskil Kylin described a clustering of high blood pressure, high blood sugar and gout. In 1947 French physician Jean Vague noted that people who carried fat around the upper body and belly (android obesity) were more likely to develop diabetes and heart disease than those who carried it on the hips. In his 1988 Banting Lecture, Stanford endocrinologist Gerald Reaven proposed that insulin resistance links these problems, calling the cluster Syndrome X. Formal definitions followed from the World Health Organization in 1998, the US National Cholesterol Education Program (ATP III) in 2001 and the International Diabetes Federation in 2005. In 2009 several organizations agreed on a harmonized definition requiring three of five risk factors. Some experts question whether it is a single syndrome, but it remains a practical way to spot people at high cardiometabolic risk.",
  timeline: [["1920s", "Eskil Kylin describes the clustering of hypertension, high glucose and gout."], ["1947", "Jean Vague links upper-body fat to diabetes and heart disease."], ["1988", "Gerald Reaven describes Syndrome X in his Banting Lecture."], ["2001", "NCEP ATP III publishes practical diagnostic criteria."], ["2009", "A joint statement harmonizes the definition of metabolic syndrome."]],
  cell: "The central problem is insulin resistance, driven largely by excess visceral (belly) fat and inactivity on a background of genetic risk. Enlarged visceral fat cells are inflamed and release free fatty acids directly into the portal vein, along with TNF-alpha and IL-6, while making less adiponectin. In the liver, extra fatty acids blunt insulin signaling, increase glucose output and boost production of triglyceride-rich VLDL particles. Through cholesteryl ester transfer protein (CETP) and hepatic lipase, these triglyceride-rich particles exchange lipids with HDL and LDL, leaving fewer HDL particles and more small, dense LDL particles that enter artery walls easily. In muscle, fat metabolites interfere with insulin receptor substrate signaling and GLUT4 movement, raising blood glucose. High insulin levels increase kidney sodium retention and sympathetic activity, which, together with endothelial dysfunction from less nitric oxide, raise blood pressure. Fat tissue and liver also make more PAI-1 and fibrinogen, creating a pro-clotting state. Together these drive atherosclerosis and push beta cells toward failure and type 2 diabetes.",
  body: [["Heart and vessels", "Atherogenic lipids, high blood pressure, inflammation and clotting tendency speed atherosclerosis, raising heart attack and stroke risk."], ["Pancreas and glucose", "Insulin resistance forces beta cells to work harder; over time many people develop prediabetes and type 2 diabetes."], ["Liver", "Fat accumulates in the liver, causing metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease."], ["Kidneys", "High insulin and blood pressure promote sodium retention and kidney damage, including albumin in the urine."], ["Other conditions", "Metabolic syndrome is linked to gout, PCOS, obstructive sleep apnea and some cancers."]],
  symptoms: "Metabolic syndrome usually causes no symptoms itself, which is why it is found through measurements. A larger waist reflects visceral fat. Dark velvety skin patches (acanthosis nigricans) and skin tags can reflect high insulin levels. Symptoms appear when complications develop, such as chest pain from coronary disease, thirst and frequent urination from diabetes, or snoring and daytime sleepiness from sleep apnea.",
  dx: "Under the 2009 harmonized definition, metabolic syndrome is present with any three of five findings: a large waist (with cutoffs that vary by population and sex), triglycerides of 150 or higher or treatment for them, HDL below 40 in men or below 50 in women or treatment for it, blood pressure of 130/85 or higher or treatment, and fasting glucose of 100 or higher or treatment (lipids and glucose in milligrams per deciliter). Tests include waist measurement, blood pressure, a fasting lipid panel and glucose or A1C.",
  tx: "Lifestyle change is the main treatment because it targets insulin resistance directly. Modest weight loss, regular aerobic and resistance exercise, and eating patterns such as the Mediterranean or DASH diet improve all five components. Each risk factor is then treated as needed. Statins such as atorvastatin and rosuvastatin block HMG-CoA reductase to lower LDL and cardiovascular risk. ACE inhibitors and ARBs lower blood pressure and protect the kidneys. Metformin can reduce progression from prediabetes to diabetes. GLP-1 receptor agonists and tirzepatide help with weight and glucose. For high triglycerides, fibrates, which activate PPAR-alpha, and icosapent ethyl are used in selected people. Stopping smoking, better sleep and treating sleep apnea also help.",
  drugs: ["atorvastatin", "rosuvastatin", "lisinopril", "losartan", "metformin", "semaglutide", "tirzepatide", "icosapent-ethyl", "fenofibrate", "ezetimibe"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Three of five: large waist, high triglycerides, low HDL, high blood pressure, high fasting glucose."], ["Memory trick", "Picture an apple shape: visceral fat around the middle drives the whole cluster."], ["Key fact", "Insulin resistance is the unifying mechanism; lifestyle change improves every component."], ["Often confused with", "Type 2 diabetes is one possible outcome of metabolic syndrome, not the same thing."]],
  red: "Chest pain, sudden weakness or numbness on one side, trouble speaking or sudden severe shortness of breath needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Gerald Reaven first called the cluster Syndrome X because the cause linking the factors was not yet fully understood.",
  src: ["Alberti KGMM et al. Harmonizing the Metabolic Syndrome. Circulation 2009", "NCEP Adult Treatment Panel III (2001)", "Reaven GM. Banting Lecture 1988. Diabetes 1988", "AHA/ACC Guideline on the Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Disease (2019)"]
},
"osteoporosis": {
  name: "Osteoporosis",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["porous bones", "low bone density", "osteopenia", "postmenopausal osteoporosis", "glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis"],
  what: "Osteoporosis is a condition in which bones lose strength and become thin and fragile, so they break easily, sometimes from a simple fall or even a cough. It often causes no symptoms until a fracture of the hip, spine or wrist.",
  origin: "In the early 1800s English surgeon Astley Cooper noted that hip fractures in older people came from thin, fragile bone, and French pathologist Jean Lobstein coined the word osteoporosis, meaning porous bone. In 1941 Fuller Albright at Massachusetts General Hospital linked osteoporosis in women to the drop in estrogen at menopause and tried estrogen as treatment. Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA) became available in the late 1980s and allowed precise bone density measurement. In 1994 the World Health Organization defined osteoporosis using the T-score. Alendronate, the first widely used bisphosphonate for osteoporosis, was approved in 1995. The Women's Health Initiative in 2002 confirmed hormone therapy reduced fractures but raised other risks, which shifted treatment toward other drugs. Teriparatide, the first bone-building drug, arrived in 2002, the FRAX fracture risk tool in 2008, denosumab in 2010 and romosozumab in 2019.",
  timeline: [["1941", "Fuller Albright links postmenopausal estrogen loss to osteoporosis."], ["1994", "The World Health Organization defines osteoporosis by bone density T-score."], ["1995", "Alendronate is approved for osteoporosis."], ["2002", "Teriparatide is approved as the first bone-building (anabolic) drug."], ["2010", "Denosumab, an anti-RANKL antibody, is approved."], ["2019", "Romosozumab, an anti-sclerostin antibody, is approved."]],
  cell: "Bone is constantly remodeled. Osteoclasts dissolve old bone and osteoblasts lay down new bone. Osteoblasts and osteocytes control osteoclasts through RANKL, which binds RANK on osteoclast precursors and drives their formation, and osteoprotegerin (OPG), a decoy receptor that blocks RANKL. Osteocytes also make sclerostin, which blocks the Wnt signaling that tells osteoblasts to build bone. Osteoporosis occurs when resorption outpaces formation. Peak bone mass, set by genetics, nutrition and activity by early adulthood, determines the starting point. Estrogen normally limits RANKL, raises OPG, dampens IL-1, IL-6 and TNF-alpha and shortens osteoclast life. After menopause, estrogen loss lets osteoclasts form more and live longer, causing rapid loss of spongy trabecular bone in the spine and wrist. With aging, osteoblasts become fewer and less active, and low calcium and vitamin D raise PTH, which increases resorption. Glucocorticoids directly suppress osteoblasts and promote their death. Other causes include low testosterone, hyperthyroidism, hyperparathyroidism, smoking, heavy alcohol use, immobility and drugs such as aromatase inhibitors. The result is thin cortices, broken trabecular connections and fragile bones, while blood calcium, phosphate and alkaline phosphatase stay normal.",
  body: [["Spine", "Weak vertebrae collapse (compression fractures), causing back pain, loss of height and a forward-curved upper back (kyphosis)."], ["Hips", "Hip fractures usually follow falls and often lead to surgery, loss of independence and a higher risk of death in the following year."], ["Wrists and other bones", "Wrist fractures from a fall on an outstretched hand are often an early warning; ribs, pelvis and upper arm can also break."], ["Lungs and gut", "Severe spinal curvature can compress the chest and belly, causing shortness of breath, early fullness and reflux."], ["Daily life", "Fear of falling, chronic pain and reduced mobility can lead to isolation and depression."]],
  symptoms: "Bone loss itself is painless, so osteoporosis is often called a silent disease. The first sign is often a fracture from minor trauma, such as a fall from standing height. Sudden back pain can signal a vertebral compression fracture, although many spinal fractures go unnoticed. Gradual height loss and a stooped posture come from multiple collapsed vertebrae.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses DXA bone density scanning of the hip and spine. A T-score compares bone density to a healthy young adult: minus 2.5 or lower is osteoporosis, and between minus 1 and minus 2.5 is low bone mass (osteopenia). A fragility fracture of the hip or spine diagnoses osteoporosis regardless of the T-score. The FRAX tool estimates 10-year fracture risk. Labs such as calcium, vitamin D, kidney function, thyroid tests, PTH and sometimes testosterone or celiac screening look for secondary causes. Vertebral imaging can find silent spine fractures. Screening is recommended for women aged 65 and older and for younger postmenopausal women at higher risk.",
  tx: "Calcium and vitamin D, weight-bearing and resistance exercise, fall prevention, stopping smoking and limiting alcohol support all treatment. Bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate, ibandronate and IV zoledronic acid) bind bone mineral and are taken up by osteoclasts, where nitrogen-containing types block farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase and cause osteoclast death. Oral forms are taken upright with water on an empty stomach to protect the esophagus. Denosumab is an antibody that blocks RANKL and stops osteoclast formation; stopping it without follow-up therapy can cause rapid bone loss and vertebral fractures. Teriparatide and abaloparatide, given intermittently, stimulate osteoblasts through the PTH receptor and build new bone. Romosozumab blocks sclerostin, building bone and reducing resorption. Raloxifene, a SERM, acts like estrogen on bone, and estrogen therapy is an option for some postmenopausal women. Rare complications of long-term antiresorptives include osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femur fractures.",
  drugs: ["alendronate", "risedronate", "ibandronate", "zoledronic-acid", "denosumab", "teriparatide", "abaloparatide", "romosozumab", "raloxifene", "estradiol", "calcitonin-salmon", "calcium-carbonate", "cholecalciferol"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "T-score of minus 2.5 or lower means osteoporosis; serum calcium, phosphate and alkaline phosphatase are normal."], ["Memory trick", "Bisphosphonate rules: upright, empty stomach, full glass of water, wait before eating."], ["Key fact", "Glucocorticoids are the most common drug cause of osteoporosis."], ["Often confused with", "Osteomalacia is soft, poorly mineralized bone from vitamin D deficiency, with low calcium or phosphate and high alkaline phosphatase."]],
  red: "Sudden severe back pain, inability to bear weight after a fall, or a deformed limb needs prompt medical care. A fall with a suspected hip fracture or head injury needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Bone is living tissue; the adult skeleton is remodeled continuously and is largely replaced over about a decade.",
  src: ["Bone Health and Osteoporosis Foundation Clinician's Guide (2022)", "Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Pharmacological Management of Osteoporosis in Postmenopausal Women (2019, updated 2020)", "USPSTF Osteoporosis Screening Recommendation (2025)", "Albright F et al. JAMA 1941", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"hyperparathyroidism": {
  name: "Hyperparathyroidism",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["primary hyperparathyroidism", "secondary hyperparathyroidism", "tertiary hyperparathyroidism", "parathyroid adenoma", "PHPT"],
  what: "Hyperparathyroidism means the parathyroid glands, four tiny glands in the neck, make too much parathyroid hormone (PTH). In the primary form this raises blood calcium, which can thin bones, cause kidney stones and affect mood, energy and digestion.",
  origin: "In 1880 Swedish medical student Ivar Sandstrom described the parathyroid glands, the last major organ found in humans. In 1891 German pathologist Friedrich von Recklinghausen described osteitis fibrosa cystica, a bone disease with cysts and brown tumors, without knowing its cause. In 1925 Felix Mandl in Vienna removed a parathyroid tumor from a tram conductor with severe bone disease, and the patient improved, the first parathyroidectomy for the disease. In the late 1920s and early 1930s, ship captain Charles Martell at Massachusetts General Hospital, studied by Fuller Albright and others, needed several operations before surgeons found his tumor in the chest. This taught surgeons about parathyroid glands in unusual places. When automated blood chemistry panels became routine in the 1970s, many people were found with mild high calcium, and the disease changed from a severe bone and kidney illness to a mostly mild one. Cinacalcet, which makes the calcium sensor more sensitive, was approved in 2004. International workshops, most recently in 2022, guide when to operate.",
  timeline: [["1880", "Ivar Sandstrom describes the parathyroid glands."], ["1891", "Von Recklinghausen describes osteitis fibrosa cystica."], ["1925", "Felix Mandl performs the first parathyroidectomy for bone disease."], ["1970s", "Routine calcium testing reveals many mild cases."], ["2004", "Cinacalcet is approved by the FDA."], ["2022", "The Fifth International Workshop updates guidance on primary hyperparathyroidism."]],
  cell: "Parathyroid chief cells sense blood calcium through the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR), a G protein-coupled receptor. When calcium falls, PTH release rises. PTH binds the PTH1 receptor in bone and kidney. In bone, it signals osteoblasts to make RANKL, which activates osteoclasts to release calcium and phosphate. In the kidney, PTH increases calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule, decreases phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule by removing NaPi-2a transporters, and switches on 1-alpha-hydroxylase to make calcitriol, which increases gut calcium absorption. In primary hyperparathyroidism, about 80 to 85 percent of cases come from a single benign adenoma, and most of the rest from hyperplasia of all four glands; carcinoma is rare. Tumor cells have a higher calcium set point, often with lower CaSR expression; some carry mutations in MEN1, cyclin D1 or CDC73, and familial forms occur in MEN1 and MEN2A. The result is high calcium, low phosphate and high urine calcium. In secondary hyperparathyroidism, usually from chronic kidney disease or vitamin D deficiency, low calcitriol, high phosphate and high FGF23 drive the glands to grow and make more PTH while calcium is low or normal. Tertiary hyperparathyroidism occurs when glands become autonomous after long-standing secondary disease.",
  body: [["Bones", "PTH-driven resorption thins cortical bone, causing osteoporosis and fractures; severe cases cause osteitis fibrosa cystica with bone pain, cysts and brown tumors."], ["Kidneys", "More filtered calcium spills into urine, causing kidney stones, calcium deposits in the kidney and reduced function; high calcium also causes excessive urination."], ["Gut", "High calcium slows gut movement and can cause constipation, nausea, peptic ulcers and pancreatitis."], ["Brain and mood", "High calcium causes fatigue, poor concentration, depression, anxiety and, when severe, confusion."], ["Heart", "Calcium shortens the QT interval and is linked to high blood pressure and valve and vessel calcification."], ["Muscles", "High calcium reduces nerve and muscle excitability, so weakness, easy fatigue and muscle aches are common."]],
  symptoms: "Many people have no symptoms, and the disease is found on routine blood tests. The classic rhyme stones, bones, abdominal groans, thrones and psychiatric overtones summarizes the effects. Kidney stones come from high urine calcium. Bone pain and fractures come from PTH-driven resorption. Constipation, nausea and belly pain come from slowed gut movement and, sometimes, pancreatitis. Thrones refers to frequent urination, because high calcium blocks the kidney's response to ADH. Fatigue, low mood and memory problems reflect calcium's effects on the nervous system.",
  dx: "Primary hyperparathyroidism is diagnosed with blood tests: high calcium with a PTH that is high or inappropriately normal. Phosphate is often low. Vitamin D, kidney function and a 24-hour urine calcium are checked; a very low urine calcium suggests familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia, a benign inherited CaSR condition that does not need surgery. DXA scanning, including the forearm, assesses bone loss, and kidney imaging looks for stones. Imaging such as ultrasound, sestamibi scans or 4D-CT is used only to locate a gland before surgery, not to make the diagnosis. In secondary hyperparathyroidism, PTH is high with low or normal calcium, often with high phosphate in kidney disease.",
  tx: "Surgery to remove the overactive gland or glands (parathyroidectomy) is the only cure for primary hyperparathyroidism and is advised for people with symptoms and for many without, such as those with calcium well above normal, osteoporosis or fractures, kidney stones, reduced kidney function or age under 50. Intraoperative PTH testing confirms success, and calcium can drop sharply afterward (hungry bone syndrome). People who are not operated on are monitored, keep hydrated and maintain vitamin D. Cinacalcet, a calcimimetic, binds the CaSR and makes it more sensitive, lowering PTH and calcium. Bisphosphonates such as alendronate protect bone. Severe high calcium is treated with IV fluids, calcitonin and IV bisphosphonates such as zoledronic acid. In secondary hyperparathyroidism of kidney disease, phosphate binders (calcium acetate, sevelamer), active vitamin D (calcitriol, paricalcitol) and calcimimetics (cinacalcet, etelcalcetide) lower PTH.",
  drugs: ["cinacalcet", "etelcalcetide", "calcitriol", "paricalcitol", "calcium-acetate", "sevelamer", "cholecalciferol", "alendronate", "zoledronic-acid", "calcitonin-salmon", "normal-saline"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Primary hyperparathyroidism: high calcium, high PTH, low phosphate; most often a single adenoma."], ["Memory trick", "Stones, bones, abdominal groans, thrones and psychiatric overtones."], ["Lab pattern", "Secondary (kidney disease): low or normal calcium, high phosphate, high PTH. Tertiary: high calcium and very high PTH after long kidney disease."], ["Often confused with", "Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia has mild high calcium and normal or high PTH but very low urine calcium; surgery does not help."]],
  red: "Confusion, extreme drowsiness, severe vomiting, severe belly pain, an irregular heartbeat or inability to keep fluids down in someone with high calcium needs emergency care (call 911). Numbness around the mouth or muscle cramps after parathyroid surgery can mean low calcium and need urgent care.",
  fun: "The parathyroid glands were the last major human organ to be discovered, described by a medical student in 1880.",
  src: ["Bilezikian JP et al. Fifth International Workshop on the Evaluation and Management of Primary Hyperparathyroidism. J Bone Miner Res 2022", "AAES Guidelines for Definitive Management of Primary Hyperparathyroidism (2016)", "KDIGO CKD-MBD Guideline (2017)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"pcos": {
  name: "Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["PCOS", "Stein-Leventhal syndrome", "polycystic ovarian syndrome"],
  what: "Polycystic ovary syndrome is a common hormone condition in which the ovaries make more androgens (male-type hormones) than usual and often do not release an egg each month. It causes irregular periods, excess hair growth, acne and trouble getting pregnant, and it is linked to insulin resistance.",
  origin: "In 1935 American gynecologists Irving Stein and Michael Leventhal in Chicago described seven women with absent periods, excess hair and enlarged ovaries covered in small cysts. They removed a wedge of each ovary, and many of the women began having periods and some became pregnant. For decades the condition was called Stein-Leventhal syndrome. In the following years researchers found high LH levels and high androgens, and in the 1980s insulin resistance was recognized as a key part of the condition. Clomiphene, approved in 1967, replaced wedge surgery for ovulation induction. Definitions evolved: an NIH conference in 1990, the Rotterdam criteria in 2003 and an international evidence-based guideline in 2018, updated in 2023. In 2014 a large trial by Richard Legro and colleagues showed that letrozole led to more live births than clomiphene in women with PCOS.",
  timeline: [["1935", "Stein and Leventhal describe the syndrome in seven women."], ["1967", "Clomiphene is approved for ovulation induction."], ["1990", "An NIH conference proposes the first formal diagnostic criteria."], ["2003", "The Rotterdam criteria are published."], ["2014", "A large trial shows letrozole beats clomiphene for live birth in PCOS."], ["2023", "The International Evidence-based Guideline for PCOS is updated."]],
  cell: "PCOS involves a cycle among the brain, ovaries, insulin and fat tissue, with strong genetic influence. The hypothalamus releases GnRH in faster pulses, which favors LH over FSH. LH stimulates ovarian theca cells to make androgens through enzymes such as CYP17. Granulosa cells normally convert androgens to estrogen with aromatase under FSH control, but relatively low FSH means many small follicles start to grow and then stall, so no dominant follicle matures and ovulation does not occur. These stalled follicles create the polycystic appearance, and they make high levels of anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH). Insulin resistance, present in many people with PCOS regardless of weight, leads to high insulin levels, which act with LH on theca cells to raise androgen production and lower liver production of sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG), increasing free testosterone. Androgens act on hair follicles and oil glands, causing excess hair, acne and scalp hair thinning. Without ovulation there is no corpus luteum and no progesterone, so the endometrium is exposed to unopposed estrogen and can overgrow.",
  body: [["Ovaries and fertility", "Irregular or absent ovulation causes irregular periods and difficulty conceiving; the ovaries may show many small follicles."], ["Uterus", "Unopposed estrogen without regular progesterone can thicken the uterine lining and raises the risk of endometrial hyperplasia and cancer."], ["Skin and hair", "Androgens cause excess hair on the face, chest and abdomen, acne and scalp hair thinning; acanthosis nigricans reflects high insulin."], ["Metabolism", "Insulin resistance raises the risk of prediabetes, type 2 diabetes, high triglycerides and fatty liver."], ["Heart and sleep", "PCOS is linked to higher cardiovascular risk factors and obstructive sleep apnea."], ["Mental health", "Depression, anxiety, body image distress and eating disorders are more common."]],
  symptoms: "Irregular, infrequent or absent periods happen because ovulation does not occur regularly. Excess hair growth, acne and scalp hair thinning come from high androgens acting on hair follicles and oil glands. Difficulty getting pregnant reflects lack of ovulation. Weight gain, especially around the belly, and dark velvety skin in folds reflect insulin resistance. Heavy bleeding after long gaps can occur when a thick, unopposed lining finally sheds. Symptoms often begin around the teen years.",
  dx: "In adults, the Rotterdam criteria, used in the 2023 international guideline, require two of three features: irregular cycles or lack of ovulation, clinical or lab evidence of high androgens, and polycystic ovarian appearance on ultrasound or a high AMH level. In adolescents, both irregular cycles and high androgens are needed, and ultrasound is not used within eight years of the first period. Other causes are ruled out, including thyroid disease, high prolactin, nonclassic congenital adrenal hyperplasia (17-hydroxyprogesterone), Cushing's syndrome and androgen-secreting tumors. Screening for diabetes, lipids, blood pressure, sleep apnea and mood is recommended.",
  tx: "Treatment is tailored to goals. Healthy eating, activity and support for emotional well-being help all features. Combined hormonal contraceptives are first-line for irregular periods and high androgen symptoms: the estrogen raises SHBG to bind testosterone, and the progestin suppresses LH and protects the uterine lining. Cyclic progestins such as medroxyprogesterone protect the endometrium in those not using combined contraceptives. Metformin improves insulin resistance and metabolic features (off-label for PCOS). Antiandrogens such as spironolactone block androgen receptors to reduce hair growth and acne and are used with reliable contraception because they can affect a developing fetus. Eflornithine cream slows facial hair growth. For fertility, letrozole, an aromatase inhibitor used off-label, lowers estrogen feedback so the pituitary releases more FSH; clomiphene, a SERM, is an alternative, followed by gonadotropins or IVF. GLP-1 receptor agonists can help with weight.",
  drugs: ["drospirenone-ethinyl-estradiol", "ethinyl-estradiol-norgestimate", "medroxyprogesterone", "metformin", "spironolactone", "eflornithine-cream", "letrozole", "clomiphene", "semaglutide", "liraglutide"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "PCOS: high LH-to-FSH ratio, high androgens, insulin resistance and an increased risk of endometrial cancer from unopposed estrogen."], ["Memory trick", "Rotterdam is two out of three: irregular cycles, high androgens, polycystic ovaries."], ["Fertility fact", "Letrozole is now the first-line ovulation induction drug in PCOS, ahead of clomiphene."], ["Often confused with", "Nonclassic congenital adrenal hyperplasia can look identical and is checked with 17-hydroxyprogesterone."]],
  red: "Very heavy bleeding with dizziness or fainting, or sudden severe pelvic pain, needs emergency care (call 911). Thoughts of self-harm need urgent help: call or text the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline.",
  fun: "PCOS affects roughly 1 in 10 women of reproductive age worldwide and is one of the most common hormone disorders in this group.",
  src: ["International Evidence-based Guideline for the Assessment and Management of PCOS (2023)", "Rotterdam ESHRE/ASRM-Sponsored PCOS Consensus Workshop Group (2003)", "Legro RS et al. Letrozole versus clomiphene for infertility in PCOS. N Engl J Med 2014", "Stein IF, Leventhal ML. Am J Obstet Gynecol 1935", "ACOG Practice Bulletin on PCOS"]
},
"gout": {
  name: "Gout",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["gouty arthritis", "podagra", "hyperuricemia", "tophaceous gout"],
  what: "Gout is a form of arthritis caused by too much uric acid in the blood, which forms needle-shaped crystals in joints. The crystals trigger sudden, very painful attacks of joint swelling, often in the big toe, and long-term buildup can damage joints and kidneys.",
  origin: "Gout is one of the oldest known diseases. Ancient Egyptian and Greek physicians described it, and Hippocrates called gout of the big toe podagra. It was long called the disease of kings because it was linked to rich food and wine. In 1679 Antonie van Leeuwenhoek saw crystals in material from a gouty lump under his microscope. In 1683 English physician Thomas Sydenham, who had gout himself, wrote a vivid description of an attack. Extracts of the autumn crocus were used for joint pain for centuries, and colchicine was isolated from it in 1820. In 1848 English physician Alfred Baring Garrod showed that people with gout had high uric acid in their blood. In 1961 Daniel McCarty and Joseph Hollander used polarized light microscopy to identify monosodium urate crystals in joint fluid. Allopurinol, developed by Gertrude Elion and George Hitchings, was approved in 1966, and Elion and Hitchings shared the 1988 Nobel Prize. Febuxostat (2009) and pegloticase (2010) followed.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Hippocrates describes podagra, gout of the big toe."], ["1679", "Van Leeuwenhoek sees crystals in material from a gouty lump."], ["1848", "Alfred Garrod shows high uric acid in the blood of people with gout."], ["1961", "McCarty and Hollander identify urate crystals in joint fluid."], ["1966", "Allopurinol is approved for gout."], ["2010", "Pegloticase is approved for refractory gout."]],
  cell: "Uric acid is the end product of purine breakdown in humans, made by xanthine oxidase, because humans lack the enzyme uricase. About 90 percent of people with gout under-excrete uric acid: the kidney transporters URAT1 and GLUT9 reabsorb too much, and the gut and kidney transporter ABCG2 secretes too little, often because of genetic variants, kidney disease or drugs such as thiazide and loop diuretics, low-dose aspirin and cyclosporine. Fewer people overproduce uric acid, as with high cell turnover, tumor lysis or rare enzyme defects such as HGPRT deficiency. When serum urate rises above its solubility limit, monosodium urate crystals form, especially in cooler joints such as the big toe and where the pH is lower. Macrophages engulf the crystals, which activate the NLRP3 inflammasome. This activates caspase-1, which cuts pro-IL-1 beta into active IL-1 beta, which recruits neutrophils and causes intense inflammation. Neutrophils then release extracellular traps that eventually help end the flare. Over years, crystal deposits grow into tophi, granulomas surrounded by macrophages and giant cells that erode bone and cartilage. Uric acid can also form kidney stones and deposit in the kidney.",
  body: [["Joints", "Crystals in joints cause sudden attacks of severe pain, redness and swelling, most often at the base of the big toe, and later chronic arthritis with joint erosion."], ["Skin and soft tissue", "Tophi, chalky lumps of urate crystals, form over joints, on the ear, elbows, fingers and Achilles tendons."], ["Kidneys", "Uric acid stones cause kidney colic; urate deposits and associated conditions can reduce kidney function."], ["Heart and metabolism", "Gout is linked to high blood pressure, metabolic syndrome, diabetes and higher cardiovascular risk."], ["Bones", "Tophi erode bone, producing punched-out lesions with overhanging edges on X-ray."]],
  symptoms: "A gout flare usually starts suddenly, often at night, with intense pain, redness, heat and swelling in one joint, most often the big toe, foot, ankle or knee. The pain comes from the burst of IL-1 beta-driven inflammation as neutrophils flood the joint. Even a bedsheet touching the toe can be unbearable. Mild fever can occur. Flares often follow triggers that raise urate or shift it quickly, such as alcohol (especially beer), red meat, seafood, sugary drinks, dehydration, surgery, illness or starting urate-lowering drugs. Untreated, flares become more frequent and tophi and chronic joint pain develop.",
  dx: "The gold standard is finding needle-shaped, negatively birefringent monosodium urate crystals in joint fluid under polarized light microscopy; the fluid is also tested for infection. Serum urate supports the diagnosis but can be normal during a flare. Ultrasound may show a double contour sign over cartilage, and dual-energy CT can show urate deposits. X-rays in long-standing gout show punched-out erosions with overhanging edges. When fluid cannot be obtained, clinical criteria such as the ACR/EULAR classification help. Kidney function is checked.",
  tx: "Flares are treated as early as possible with anti-inflammatory drugs. NSAIDs such as naproxen and indomethacin block COX enzymes and prostaglandins. Colchicine blocks tubulin polymerization, disrupting neutrophil movement and inflammasome activation. Glucocorticoids by mouth, injection or into the joint suppress inflammation, and IL-1 blockers such as canakinumab (and off-label anakinra) are used when others cannot be. Long-term, urate-lowering therapy aims for serum urate below 6 milligrams per deciliter so crystals dissolve. Allopurinol, a xanthine oxidase inhibitor, is first-line; testing for HLA-B*58:01 is advised in people of Southeast Asian and African American ancestry because of a risk of severe skin reactions. Febuxostat also blocks xanthine oxidase. Probenecid blocks URAT1 to increase urate excretion. Pegloticase, an infused enzyme that converts urate to allantoin, treats severe refractory gout. Low-dose anti-inflammatory prophylaxis is used for several months when starting therapy. Limiting alcohol and sugary drinks, weight management and reviewing diuretics help.",
  drugs: ["colchicine", "naproxen", "indomethacin", "ibuprofen", "celecoxib", "prednisone", "methylprednisolone", "triamcinolone-injection", "canakinumab", "anakinra", "allopurinol", "febuxostat", "probenecid", "pegloticase", "losartan"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Gout crystals are needle-shaped and negatively birefringent; pseudogout (CPPD) crystals are rhomboid and positively birefringent."], ["Memory trick", "Gout is yellow when parallel: negatively birefringent urate crystals look yellow when aligned with the compensator."], ["Key fact", "Allopurinol is not stopped during a flare, and the treatment goal is serum urate below 6."], ["Drug pearl", "Allopurinol interacts with azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine, which are also broken down by xanthine oxidase."]],
  red: "A hot, swollen joint with high fever or chills, or severe pain in a joint that has never had gout, needs urgent medical care because joint infection can look like gout. Severe flank pain with blood in the urine or inability to urinate needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Humans and other great apes lost the uricase enzyme millions of years ago, which is why people can develop gout while most other mammals cannot.",
  src: ["ACR Guideline for the Management of Gout (2020)", "ACR/EULAR Gout Classification Criteria (2015)", "McCarty DJ, Hollander JL. Ann Intern Med 1961", "Martinon F et al. Gout-associated uric acid crystals activate the NALP3 inflammasome. Nature 2006", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"hyperkalemia": {
  name: "Hyperkalemia",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["high potassium", "high blood potassium"],
  what: "Hyperkalemia means there is too much potassium in the blood. Because potassium controls the electrical charge of heart and muscle cells, high levels can cause weakness and dangerous heart rhythms, and it is most often linked to kidney disease or certain medicines.",
  origin: "In 1807 English chemist Humphry Davy first isolated potassium. In the early 1880s London physician Sydney Ringer found, partly by accident, that a frog heart kept beating only when the fluid around it contained the right balance of potassium and calcium, the basis of Ringer's solution and of the idea that calcium counteracts potassium in the heart. In the 1930s and 1940s researchers described how rising potassium changes the electrocardiogram, from peaked T waves to wide complexes and cardiac arrest. Willem Kolff's artificial kidney in the 1940s offered a way to remove potassium in kidney failure. The potassium binder sodium polystyrene sulfonate was approved in 1958. In the 2010s and later, wider use of ACE inhibitors, ARBs and mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists made hyperkalemia more common, and newer binders, patiromer in 2015 and sodium zirconium cyclosilicate in 2018, offered better-tolerated ways to control potassium so that these protective medicines could be continued.",
  timeline: [["1807", "Humphry Davy isolates potassium."], ["1882", "Sydney Ringer shows potassium and calcium balance controls the heartbeat."], ["1940s", "Willem Kolff builds an early artificial kidney for dialysis."], ["1958", "Sodium polystyrene sulfonate is approved as a potassium binder."], ["2015", "Patiromer is approved for hyperkalemia."], ["2018", "Sodium zirconium cyclosilicate is approved for hyperkalemia."]],
  cell: "About 98 percent of the body's potassium is inside cells, kept there by the Na-K-ATPase pump, which insulin and beta-2 adrenergic stimulation increase. The ratio of potassium inside to outside cells largely sets the resting membrane potential. The kidneys excrete most daily potassium through ROMK and BK channels in collecting duct principal cells, driven by aldosterone and by sodium delivery and urine flow. Hyperkalemia arises from reduced excretion (acute or chronic kidney disease, low aldosterone or type 4 renal tubular acidosis, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, mineralocorticoid antagonists, potassium-sparing diuretics, trimethoprim, NSAIDs, heparin, calcineurin inhibitors), from shifts out of cells (insulin deficiency, acidosis, beta blockers, digoxin toxicity, succinylcholine, high osmolality) or from release by damaged cells (rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, hemolysis, burns). High extracellular potassium makes the resting membrane potential less negative. At first this brings cells closer to threshold, but sustained depolarization inactivates voltage-gated sodium channels, slowing conduction and reducing excitability. Potassium also increases repolarizing potassium currents, producing peaked T waves. As levels climb, P waves flatten, the PR and QRS lengthen, and the rhythm can degenerate into a sine wave, ventricular fibrillation or asystole.",
  body: [["Heart", "Slowed conduction and altered repolarization cause peaked T waves, heart block, wide QRS complexes, slow rhythms and cardiac arrest."], ["Muscles", "Depolarized muscle cells become less excitable, causing weakness that can rise from the legs and, rarely, paralysis including breathing muscles."], ["Nerves", "Numbness and tingling can occur as nerve cells become less excitable."], ["Kidneys", "Kidney disease is both the main cause and a target, since potassium limits use of kidney-protective drugs."], ["Acid-base", "Hyperkalemia reduces kidney ammonia production, which can contribute to metabolic acidosis."]],
  symptoms: "Many people have no symptoms until levels are dangerous, which is why lab results and ECGs matter. When symptoms occur, muscle weakness, fatigue, tingling and, rarely, paralysis come from muscle and nerve cells losing excitability. Palpitations, a slow pulse, fainting or cardiac arrest come from slowed and disordered heart conduction. Symptoms correlate poorly with the exact level; a fast rise is more dangerous than a slow one.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is made with a serum potassium above the normal range (roughly 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L). Pseudohyperkalemia, a falsely high value from a hemolyzed sample, tight tourniquet, fist clenching or very high platelet or white cell counts, is ruled out, often with a repeat sample. An ECG is done right away to look for peaked T waves, flattened P waves, PR prolongation, wide QRS and sine waves. Labs check kidney function, glucose, acid-base status, CK and digoxin levels when relevant, and the medication list is reviewed. Urine potassium and aldosterone and renin levels help with chronic cases.",
  tx: "Emergency treatment works in three steps. First, IV calcium (calcium gluconate or calcium chloride) stabilizes the heart's cell membranes by restoring the gap between resting and threshold potential; it acts within minutes but does not lower potassium. Second, potassium is shifted into cells: IV regular insulin, given with dextrose to prevent low blood sugar, activates the Na-K-ATPase; inhaled albuterol, a beta-2 agonist, does the same; and sodium bicarbonate can help when there is metabolic acidosis. Third, potassium is removed from the body: loop diuretics such as furosemide increase urine potassium, gut binders (sodium zirconium cyclosilicate, patiromer, sodium polystyrene sulfonate) trap potassium in the bowel, and hemodialysis is used for severe cases or kidney failure. Offending drugs are stopped or adjusted. Fludrocortisone is used in some cases of low aldosterone. For chronic hyperkalemia, a low-potassium diet and newer binders often allow continued use of heart- and kidney-protective drugs.",
  drugs: ["calcium-gluconate", "calcium-chloride", "regular-insulin", "dextrose", "albuterol", "sodium-bicarbonate", "furosemide", "bumetanide", "sodium-zirconium-cyclosilicate", "patiromer", "sodium-polystyrene-sulfonate", "fludrocortisone"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Calcium first to protect the heart, then shift with insulin plus dextrose and albuterol, then remove with diuretics, binders or dialysis."], ["Memory trick", "C BIG K DROP: Calcium, Bicarbonate, Insulin, Glucose, Kayexalate (binder), Diuretics, Renal replacement (dialysis)."], ["ECG order", "Peaked T waves, then flat P waves and long PR, then wide QRS, then sine wave and arrest."], ["Often confused with", "Pseudohyperkalemia from a hemolyzed sample; repeat the sample when the ECG is normal and the result is unexpected."]],
  red: "Sudden muscle weakness, palpitations, a very slow or irregular heartbeat, fainting or trouble breathing in someone with kidney disease or on potassium-raising medicines needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Sydney Ringer's discovery reportedly began when his lab assistant made a heart-bathing solution with London tap water, which contained calcium, instead of distilled water.",
  src: ["UK Kidney Association Clinical Practice Guideline: Treatment of Acute Hyperkalaemia in Adults (2020)", "AHA Guidelines for CPR and Emergency Cardiovascular Care (2020)", "Brenner and Rector's The Kidney", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"hypokalemia": {
  name: "Hypokalemia",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["low potassium", "low blood potassium"],
  what: "Hypokalemia means there is too little potassium in the blood. It is most often caused by diuretics, vomiting or diarrhea, and because potassium is needed for normal nerve, muscle and heart function, low levels can cause weakness, cramps and dangerous heart rhythms.",
  origin: "Potassium was first isolated by Humphry Davy in 1807 by passing electricity through potash. For more than a century, measuring it in blood was slow and difficult, until flame photometry in the 1940s and 1950s made rapid potassium testing practical. Doctors then recognized low potassium as a common and dangerous problem, especially after thiazide diuretics such as chlorothiazide arrived in the late 1950s. Classic ECG findings of low potassium, flattened T waves and prominent U waves, became well described. Specific causes were identified: Jerome Conn linked low potassium and hypertension to aldosterone excess in 1955, Frederic Bartter described a salt-wasting syndrome with low potassium in 1962 and Hillel Gitelman described a related syndrome in 1966. Later genetic work traced these syndromes to mutations in kidney salt transporters. Today potassium replacement, magnesium correction and potassium-sparing diuretics are standard tools, and careful rules for IV potassium protect patients from accidental overdose.",
  timeline: [["1807", "Humphry Davy isolates potassium."], ["1950s", "Flame photometry makes rapid blood potassium testing routine."], ["1955", "Jerome Conn links aldosterone excess to low potassium."], ["1962", "Frederic Bartter describes Bartter syndrome."], ["1966", "Hillel Gitelman describes Gitelman syndrome."]],
  cell: "Most potassium is inside cells, held there by the Na-K-ATPase, and the gradient across the cell membrane sets the resting membrane potential. Hypokalemia develops from losses, shifts or, less often, low intake. Kidney losses come from diuretics, which deliver more sodium to the collecting duct and raise aldosterone; from primary or secondary aldosterone excess, Cushing's syndrome, licorice, Bartter syndrome (NKCC2 and related defects) and Gitelman syndrome (NCC defects); from renal tubular acidosis types 1 and 2; and from drugs such as amphotericin B. Low magnesium worsens losses because magnesium normally restrains ROMK channels, so potassium leaks out until magnesium is replaced. With vomiting, most potassium is lost in urine, because metabolic alkalosis and volume loss drive aldosterone and bicarbonate delivery. Diarrhea and laxatives lose potassium directly in stool. Shifts into cells come from insulin, beta-2 agonists, alkalosis, refeeding, thyrotoxic or familial periodic paralysis and rapid cell growth. In skeletal muscle, low extracellular potassium hyperpolarizes the membrane, making cells harder to excite and causing weakness. In the heart, low potassium paradoxically reduces some potassium channel currents, delaying repolarization, which produces U waves and a long QT and promotes arrhythmias, especially with digoxin, which competes with potassium at the Na-K-ATPase.",
  body: [["Heart", "Delayed repolarization causes flattened T waves, U waves and a long QT interval, raising the risk of premature beats, torsades de pointes and ventricular fibrillation."], ["Skeletal muscle", "Hyperpolarized muscle cells are harder to excite, causing weakness, cramps and, when severe, paralysis or muscle breakdown (rhabdomyolysis)."], ["Gut", "Smooth muscle weakness slows the bowel, causing constipation and, in severe cases, ileus."], ["Kidneys", "Chronic low potassium impairs urine concentration (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus), increases ammonia production and maintains metabolic alkalosis."], ["Metabolism", "Low potassium reduces insulin release from beta cells and can raise blood glucose."]],
  symptoms: "Mild hypokalemia often causes no symptoms. As levels fall, muscle weakness, fatigue and cramps appear because muscle cells are harder to excite. Constipation reflects slowed gut smooth muscle. Palpitations and skipped beats reflect delayed repolarization and irritability of heart cells. Severe hypokalemia can cause paralysis, trouble breathing from weak respiratory muscles, and muscle breakdown. Frequent urination and thirst can occur because low potassium impairs the kidney's response to ADH.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is a serum potassium below the normal range (below about 3.5 mEq/L). An ECG looks for flattened T waves, ST depression, U waves and a long QT. Magnesium is checked because low magnesium is common and must be fixed for potassium to recover. When the cause is unclear, urine potassium (a spot potassium-to-creatinine ratio) separates kidney losses from gut losses or shifts. Acid-base status helps: metabolic alkalosis suggests vomiting, diuretics or aldosterone excess, while acidosis suggests diarrhea or renal tubular acidosis. Blood pressure with renin and aldosterone levels helps identify aldosterone excess.",
  tx: "Treatment replaces potassium and corrects the cause. Oral potassium chloride is preferred when the person can take it by mouth, and the chloride form also corrects metabolic alkalosis. IV potassium chloride is used for severe or symptomatic hypokalemia or when the gut cannot be used, given by controlled infusion with ECG monitoring at faster rates. IV potassium is never given as a push, because a bolus can stop the heart. Magnesium sulfate or oral magnesium is given when magnesium is low. Potassium-sparing diuretics, including spironolactone, eplerenone, amiloride and triamterene, reduce ongoing kidney losses, for example in aldosterone excess or with chronic diuretic use. Potassium citrate is used in renal tubular acidosis. Initial IV fluids are often dextrose-free, since dextrose triggers insulin release, which shifts potassium into cells. In periodic paralysis, replacement is cautious to avoid rebound high potassium.",
  drugs: ["potassium-chloride", "potassium-citrate", "potassium-phosphate", "magnesium-sulfate", "magnesium-oxide", "spironolactone", "eplerenone", "amiloride", "triamterene"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Low potassium ECG: flattened T waves, U waves, ST depression and long QT; it increases digoxin toxicity."], ["Memory trick", "No pot, no tea, but a U: low potassium flattens the T and adds a U wave."], ["Key fact", "Refractory hypokalemia often means low magnesium; fix the magnesium to fix the potassium."], ["Safety", "IV potassium is never given by IV push; it is always diluted and infused at a controlled rate."]],
  red: "Severe muscle weakness, paralysis, trouble breathing, chest pain, fainting or a racing or irregular heartbeat needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Natural black licorice contains glycyrrhizin, which blocks the kidney enzyme that protects aldosterone receptors from cortisol, so eating large amounts can cause low potassium and high blood pressure.",
  src: ["Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "Gennari FJ. Hypokalemia. N Engl J Med 1998", "Brenner and Rector's The Kidney", "ISMP guidance on concentrated potassium chloride"]
}
});

;
DB.DIS = DB.DIS || {};
Object.assign(DB.DIS, {
"hyponatremia": {
  name: "Hyponatremia",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["low sodium", "low blood sodium", "SIADH (one cause)"],
  what: "Hyponatremia means the sodium level in the blood is too low. Usually the problem is too much water for the amount of salt, which makes body cells, especially brain cells, swell.",
  origin: "Doctors noticed confusion and seizures in people who drank huge amounts of water or lost salt long before they could measure sodium. Flame photometry made routine blood sodium tests possible in the mid 1900s. In 1957 William Schwartz, Frederic Bartter and colleagues described two patients with lung cancer who kept making antidiuretic hormone (ADH) even though their blood was diluted. They named the pattern the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH). In 1959 Raymond Adams and colleagues described central pontine myelinolysis, and in the 1980s researchers linked it to correcting chronic low sodium too quickly, which is why slow, careful correction became a core rule. Vasopressin receptor antagonists (vaptans) arrived in the 2000s as the first drugs to block ADH directly.",
  timeline: [["1957", "Schwartz and Bartter describe the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH)."], ["1959", "Adams, Victor and Mancall describe central pontine myelinolysis."], ["1980s", "Studies link rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia to osmotic demyelination."], ["2005", "Conivaptan becomes the first FDA-approved vasopressin receptor antagonist."], ["2009", "Oral tolvaptan is approved for hyponatremia."]],
  cell: "Sodium is the main particle that holds water outside cells, so serum sodium mostly reflects water balance. Water moves freely across cell membranes through aquaporins toward the side with more dissolved particles. When serum sodium falls, plasma osmolality falls, and water moves into cells. ADH (vasopressin) from the posterior pituitary binds V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells, raising cAMP and inserting aquaporin-2 channels so the kidney keeps free water. In SIADH, ADH is released without a proper trigger (lung cancer, brain disease, pain, nausea, drugs such as SSRIs, carbamazepine and thiazides), so water is kept and sodium is diluted. Low effective blood volume (heart failure, cirrhosis, vomiting) also releases ADH. Other causes are low solute intake (beer potomania, tea and toast diet), huge water intake and low cortisol or thyroid hormone. Brain cells swell, then over about 48 hours they push out potassium, chloride and organic osmolytes to shrink back. That adaptation protects the brain, but if sodium then rises too fast, water leaves the adapted cells too quickly and oligodendrocytes can be injured, causing osmotic demyelination.",
  body: [["Brain", "Water enters brain cells and the brain swells inside the rigid skull. This causes headache, confusion, seizures and in severe sudden cases brain herniation. Too-rapid correction can later cause osmotic demyelination, especially in the pons."], ["Kidneys", "In SIADH the kidneys make concentrated urine despite dilute blood because ADH keeps aquaporin-2 channels open. In salt loss or low volume they hold sodium and urine sodium is low."], ["Muscles", "Low sodium and cell swelling cause cramps, weakness and fatigue."], ["Gut", "Nausea and vomiting are early signs, and nausea itself is a strong trigger for more ADH release, which can worsen the problem."], ["Bones and balance", "Even mild chronic hyponatremia is linked to unsteady gait, falls and fractures in older adults."]],
  symptoms: "Most symptoms come from brain swelling. Mild or slow-onset cases may cause only fatigue, poor attention and unsteady walking because brain cells have time to adapt. A rapid fall gives no time to adapt, so headache, nausea, vomiting, confusion, seizures and coma can occur. Muscle cramps and weakness reflect altered nerve and muscle excitability. Signs of the cause also appear, such as swelling in heart failure or cirrhosis, or dry mouth and low blood pressure with fluid loss.",
  dx: "Diagnosis starts with a low serum sodium on a basic metabolic panel. Serum osmolality separates true low-osmolality hyponatremia from high glucose or lab artifacts. Clinicians then judge volume status (dry, normal or overloaded) and check urine osmolality and urine sodium. In SIADH the urine is inappropriately concentrated, urine sodium is high, the patient looks euvolemic, and thyroid and adrenal problems are ruled out. Low urine osmolality points to too much water intake or very low solute intake.",
  tx: "Treatment depends on how severe and how fast the sodium fell, and the rate of correction is limited to prevent osmotic demyelination. Severe symptoms such as seizures or coma are treated with small boluses of hypertonic (3%) saline to quickly shrink the brain a little. Low-volume hyponatremia is treated with isotonic saline, which switches off the volume trigger for ADH. In SIADH, fluid restriction is the first step, with salt tablets or oral urea to raise solute intake, loop diuretics to reduce urine concentrating ability, and vaptans such as tolvaptan or conivaptan, which block V2 receptors so the kidney releases free water. In heart failure and cirrhosis, the underlying disease is treated and diuretics are adjusted. Offending drugs such as thiazides and SSRIs are reviewed. If sodium rises too fast, clinicians may give free water or desmopressin to bring it back down.",
  drugs: ["hypertonic-saline-3-percent", "normal-saline", "tolvaptan", "conivaptan", "furosemide", "desmopressin", "hydrocortisone", "levothyroxine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "From low to high, your pons will die: correcting chronic hyponatremia too fast causes osmotic demyelination."], ["Most-tested fact", "SIADH is euvolemic hyponatremia with concentrated urine and high urine sodium; small cell lung cancer is the classic cause."], ["Often confused with", "Cerebral salt wasting also has high urine sodium but the patient is volume depleted, so it is treated with salt and fluid, not restriction."], ["EMS pearl", "Exercise-associated hyponatremia in marathon runners and very large water intake can cause seizures that look like heat illness."]],
  red: "Seizures, severe confusion, repeated vomiting, fainting or unresponsiveness in someone with known low sodium, heavy water intake or long endurance exercise needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Beer potomania gets its name because beer has lots of water but very little salt or protein, so heavy drinkers with poor diets cannot excrete enough free water.",
  src: ["Verbalis JG et al. Diagnosis, evaluation, and treatment of hyponatremia: expert panel recommendations. Am J Med 2013", "European Clinical Practice Guideline on Hyponatraemia (2014)", "Schwartz WB, Bartter FC et al. Am J Med 1957", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"hypercalcemia": {
  name: "Hypercalcemia",
  sys: "Hormones & Metabolism",
  aka: ["high calcium", "high blood calcium", "hypercalcemia of malignancy", "primary hyperparathyroidism (common cause)"],
  what: "Hypercalcemia means there is too much calcium in the blood. It is most often caused by overactive parathyroid glands or by cancer, and it can affect the kidneys, bones, gut and brain.",
  origin: "The parathyroid glands were first described in humans by Swedish medical student Ivar Sandstrom in 1880. In the 1920s researchers showed the glands control blood calcium, and in 1925 Felix Mandl in Vienna removed a parathyroid tumor from a man with severe bone disease, the first parathyroid surgery for hyperparathyroidism. In the 1930s Fuller Albright and colleagues in Boston described how the disease causes kidney stones and bone loss. Doctors later noticed that some cancers raise calcium without bone spread, and in 1987 researchers identified parathyroid hormone-related protein (PTHrP) as the cause in many of these cases. Bisphosphonates in the 1990s and 2000s, and later denosumab, gave clinicians strong tools to stop bone breakdown.",
  timeline: [["1880", "Ivar Sandstrom describes the human parathyroid glands."], ["1925", "Felix Mandl performs the first parathyroidectomy for hyperparathyroidism."], ["1930s", "Fuller Albright and colleagues define the clinical picture of hyperparathyroidism."], ["1987", "PTHrP is identified as a cause of humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy."], ["2001", "Zoledronic acid is approved in the US for hypercalcemia of malignancy."], ["2004", "Cinacalcet, the first calcimimetic, is approved."]],
  cell: "Blood calcium is controlled by parathyroid hormone (PTH), vitamin D and calcitonin. Parathyroid chief cells sense calcium through the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR), a G protein-coupled receptor; low calcium increases PTH release. PTH raises calcium by stimulating osteoblasts to express RANKL, which activates osteoclasts to resorb bone, by increasing calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule, and by inducing 1-alpha-hydroxylase to make calcitriol, which raises gut absorption. In primary hyperparathyroidism, usually a single adenoma, chief cells release PTH despite high calcium. In cancer, the main mechanisms are PTHrP secretion (squamous cell cancers, breast, kidney), local osteolysis by bone metastases or myeloma through RANKL and cytokines, and calcitriol made by lymphomas. Granulomas (sarcoidosis, TB) also make calcitriol. High calcium raises the threshold for nerve and muscle depolarization, blocks ADH action in the collecting duct (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus), and can deposit as calcium salts in the kidney.",
  body: [["Kidneys", "High calcium blocks the kidney's response to ADH, so large amounts of dilute urine are lost, causing dehydration. Calcium can form kidney stones or deposit in kidney tissue, and severe cases cause acute kidney injury."], ["Bones", "Excess PTH or tumor activity drives osteoclasts to break down bone, causing bone pain, osteoporosis and fractures. Severe long-standing hyperparathyroidism can cause osteitis fibrosa cystica."], ["Gut", "Calcium slows smooth muscle, causing constipation, nausea and loss of appetite. It also stimulates gastrin and is linked to peptic ulcers and pancreatitis."], ["Brain and mood", "Reduced nerve excitability causes fatigue, depression, poor concentration, confusion and in severe cases coma."], ["Heart", "Calcium shortens the QT interval and can cause slow heart rhythms or arrhythmias, and it adds to high blood pressure."], ["Muscles", "Weakness and easy tiring occur because nerve and muscle membranes are harder to excite."]],
  symptoms: "The classic phrase stones, bones, abdominal groans and psychiatric moans sums up the mechanism. Stones come from calcium deposits in the urine. Bone pain comes from osteoclast bone breakdown. Constipation, nausea and belly pain come from slowed gut muscle. Fatigue, low mood and confusion come from reduced nerve excitability. Thirst and frequent urination happen because calcium blocks ADH in the kidney, which also causes dehydration that pushes calcium even higher. Many people with mild primary hyperparathyroidism have no symptoms and are found on routine labs.",
  dx: "Diagnosis begins with a high serum calcium, corrected for low albumin or confirmed with ionized calcium. The key next test is PTH. High or inappropriately normal PTH points to primary hyperparathyroidism; low PTH points to cancer, vitamin D excess, granulomatous disease, or other causes, and leads to tests for PTHrP, calcitriol, calcidiol, serum protein electrophoresis and imaging. Urine calcium helps separate familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia from hyperparathyroidism. An ECG may show a short QT. Sestamibi scans or ultrasound locate a parathyroid adenoma before surgery.",
  tx: "Severe hypercalcemia is treated in steps. IV isotonic saline restores volume and helps the kidneys excrete calcium. Calcitonin acts on osteoclast receptors and increases kidney calcium loss, lowering calcium within hours, though the effect fades after a few days. Bisphosphonates such as zoledronic acid or pamidronate are taken up by osteoclasts and block farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase, shutting down bone resorption over several days. Denosumab blocks RANKL and is used when bisphosphonates fail or kidney function is poor. Loop diuretics are used only after volume is restored, mainly for fluid overload. Glucocorticoids lower calcitriol production in lymphoma and granulomatous disease. Dialysis is used for severe cases with kidney failure. Primary hyperparathyroidism is cured by parathyroidectomy; cinacalcet makes the CaSR more sensitive so PTH falls when surgery is not possible. Calcium-raising drugs such as thiazides, lithium and extra calcium or vitamin D are reviewed.",
  drugs: ["normal-saline", "calcitonin-salmon", "zoledronic-acid", "pamidronate", "denosumab", "furosemide", "prednisone", "hydrocortisone", "cinacalcet"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Stones, bones, abdominal groans, psychiatric moans (and thrones, for frequent urination)."], ["Most-tested fact", "Primary hyperparathyroidism is the most common cause in outpatients; cancer (often via PTHrP) is the most common cause in hospitalized patients."], ["Order of treatment", "Fluids first, then calcitonin for speed, then a bisphosphonate for lasting control."], ["Often confused with", "Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia has high calcium and normal or high PTH but low urine calcium, and surgery does not help."]],
  red: "Confusion, extreme drowsiness, severe weakness, fainting, an irregular heartbeat or inability to keep fluids down in someone with high calcium or cancer needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The parathyroid glands were among the last major organs discovered in humans, identified by a medical student in 1880.",
  src: ["Endocrine Society guidance on hypercalcemia of malignancy (2023)", "Fourth International Workshop on Asymptomatic Primary Hyperparathyroidism (2014)", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"gerd": {
  name: "GERD (acid reflux)",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["gastroesophageal reflux disease", "acid reflux", "heartburn", "reflux esophagitis", "GORD"],
  what: "GERD is a long-term condition where stomach acid and contents flow back up into the esophagus, the food pipe. It causes heartburn and regurgitation and can inflame or scar the esophagus over time.",
  origin: "Heartburn has been described since ancient times, but reflux was not clearly linked to esophageal injury until 1935, when Asher Winkelstein described peptic esophagitis caused by acid. In 1950 Norman Barrett described the lining change in the lower esophagus that now carries his name. In 1956 German surgeon Rudolph Nissen performed the wrap operation, fundoplication, that is still used today. The 1970s brought H2 blockers such as cimetidine, developed by James Black's team, and in 1989 omeprazole became the first proton pump inhibitor (PPI) approved in the US, which transformed treatment. In 2023 vonoprazan, a potassium-competitive acid blocker, was approved in the US for erosive esophagitis.",
  timeline: [["1935", "Asher Winkelstein describes peptic esophagitis caused by acid reflux."], ["1950", "Norman Barrett describes the changed lining now called Barrett esophagus."], ["1956", "Rudolph Nissen performs the first fundoplication."], ["1977", "Cimetidine, the first H2 blocker, is approved in the US."], ["1989", "Omeprazole becomes the first PPI approved in the US."], ["2023", "Vonoprazan is approved in the US for erosive esophagitis."]],
  cell: "The lower esophageal sphincter (LES) and the diaphragm crura form an antireflux barrier. GERD develops when this barrier fails, most often through transient LES relaxations triggered by stomach stretch through a vagal reflex, a low resting LES pressure, or a hiatal hernia that separates the LES from the diaphragm. Obesity and pregnancy raise abdominal pressure. Gastric acid is made by parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase (the proton pump) under histamine (H2), gastrin and acetylcholine signals. Refluxed acid and pepsin, and sometimes bile, damage squamous epithelium. Recent work suggests reflux triggers epithelial cells to release cytokines that recruit T cells, so injury begins in deeper layers and is partly immune-mediated. Repeated injury causes erosions, ulcers and peptic strictures from fibrosis. In some people, chronic injury leads squamous cells to be replaced by intestinal-type columnar cells with goblet cells (Barrett metaplasia), which can progress through dysplasia to adenocarcinoma.",
  body: [["Esophagus", "Acid and pepsin inflame the lining, causing erosive esophagitis, ulcers, narrowing (strictures) and in some people Barrett esophagus, a risk factor for esophageal adenocarcinoma."], ["Throat and voice box", "Reflux reaching the larynx can cause hoarseness, throat clearing, sore throat and a feeling of a lump in the throat."], ["Lungs and airways", "Microaspiration and a vagal reflex can cause chronic cough and worsen asthma."], ["Teeth", "Repeated acid exposure can erode tooth enamel."], ["Chest", "Heartburn and chest pain from the esophagus can mimic heart pain."]],
  symptoms: "Heartburn is a burning feeling behind the breastbone caused by acid stimulating sensory nerves in the inflamed esophageal lining. Regurgitation is stomach contents moving up into the mouth through a weak barrier. Symptoms are worse after big meals and when lying down because a full stomach triggers transient sphincter relaxations and gravity no longer helps. Trouble swallowing can mean a stricture or severe inflammation. Cough, hoarseness and sore throat happen when reflux reaches the airway.",
  dx: "Typical heartburn and regurgitation are often diagnosed clinically, and a response to a PPI trial supports it. Upper endoscopy is used for alarm features such as trouble swallowing, weight loss, bleeding or anemia, or for Barrett screening in people with risk factors; it can show erosive esophagitis graded by the Los Angeles classification, strictures or Barrett esophagus with biopsy. Ambulatory pH or pH-impedance monitoring measures acid exposure and links symptoms to reflux when the diagnosis is unclear. Esophageal manometry is done before surgery to exclude motility disorders.",
  tx: "Lifestyle measures include weight loss, raising the head of the bed, avoiding meals before bed and avoiding personal trigger foods. Antacids neutralize acid already in the stomach for quick relief. H2 receptor antagonists such as famotidine block histamine on parietal cells and lower acid, especially at night. PPIs such as omeprazole and pantoprazole irreversibly block the proton pump and are the most effective standard therapy for healing esophagitis; they work best taken before a meal. Vonoprazan blocks the pump by competing with potassium and does not need acid activation. Alginates form a raft on top of stomach contents. Surgical fundoplication or magnetic sphincter augmentation strengthen the barrier when medicines are not enough or not wanted. Barrett esophagus is followed with surveillance endoscopy.",
  drugs: ["omeprazole", "pantoprazole", "esomeprazole", "lansoprazole", "dexlansoprazole", "rabeprazole", "vonoprazan", "famotidine", "cimetidine", "nizatidine", "calcium-carbonate", "aluminum-hydroxide-magnesium-hydroxide", "sucralfate", "metoclopramide", "baclofen"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia (columnar cells with goblet cells) in the lower esophagus and raises the risk of adenocarcinoma, not squamous cell carcinoma."], ["Memory trick", "PPIs work best before breakfast because they block only pumps that are active, and pumps switch on with a meal."], ["Often confused with", "Heart attack: chest pain blamed on reflux, especially with exertion, sweating or shortness of breath, needs a cardiac workup first."]],
  red: "Chest pain with shortness of breath, sweating or arm or jaw pain, vomiting blood, black stools, food stuck in the throat or inability to swallow saliva needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The proton pump inhibitors were designed after scientists found the H+/K+-ATPase, the final common step of acid production, so blocking it works no matter which signal turned acid on.",
  src: ["ACG Clinical Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of GERD (2022)", "Lyon Consensus 2.0 (2023)", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"peptic-ulcer": {
  name: "Peptic ulcer disease and H. pylori",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["PUD", "stomach ulcer", "gastric ulcer", "duodenal ulcer", "Helicobacter pylori infection"],
  what: "A peptic ulcer is an open sore in the lining of the stomach or the first part of the small intestine (duodenum). Most are caused by the bacterium Helicobacter pylori or by NSAID pain relievers.",
  origin: "For most of the 1900s, ulcers were blamed on stress, spicy food and too much acid, and many patients had surgery to cut the vagus nerve or remove part of the stomach. Acid-blocking drugs arrived in the 1970s when James Black's team developed cimetidine, the first H2 blocker; Black later shared the 1988 Nobel Prize. In 1982 Australian pathologist Robin Warren and physician Barry Marshall in Perth grew a curved bacterium from stomach biopsies, now called Helicobacter pylori. Many doubted them, so in 1984 Marshall drank a culture and developed gastritis. Studies then showed that curing the infection usually cured the ulcer for good. Warren and Marshall won the 2005 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.",
  timeline: [["1955", "Zollinger and Ellison describe ulcers caused by gastrin-secreting tumors."], ["1977", "Cimetidine is approved in the US, the first H2 blocker."], ["1982", "Warren and Marshall culture Helicobacter pylori."], ["1984", "Barry Marshall drinks H. pylori culture and develops gastritis."], ["1994", "A US NIH consensus panel recommends antibiotics for ulcer patients with H. pylori."], ["2005", "Warren and Marshall receive the Nobel Prize."]],
  cell: "Ulcers form when injury factors beat the mucosal defenses (mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow and prostaglandins). H. pylori is a spiral, flagellated gram-negative rod that lives under the mucus layer. Its urease turns urea into ammonia, buffering acid around it. Virulence factors such as CagA, delivered into epithelial cells by a type IV secretion system, and VacA toxin trigger NF-kappaB, IL-8 release and neutrophil inflammation. Antrum-predominant infection destroys somatostatin-producing D cells, so gastrin and acid rise and duodenal ulcers form. Body-predominant infection causes atrophic gastritis with lower acid, gastric ulcers and a higher risk of gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma. NSAIDs and aspirin block COX-1, which lowers protective prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2), reducing mucus, bicarbonate and blood flow, and they also injure cells directly. Other causes are gastrinoma, smoking, and stress ulcers in critical illness.",
  body: [["Stomach", "Gastric ulcers and gastritis cause pain, bleeding and sometimes scarring that blocks the outlet. Chronic H. pylori raises the risk of gastric cancer and MALT lymphoma."], ["Duodenum", "Duodenal ulcers are the most common type and are strongly linked to H. pylori and high acid. A posterior ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery and bleed heavily."], ["Blood", "Slow bleeding causes iron deficiency anemia; fast bleeding causes vomiting blood, black stools and shock."], ["Belly lining", "An ulcer that goes through the wall causes perforation, spilling contents and air into the abdomen and causing peritonitis."], ["Pancreas", "A posterior ulcer can penetrate into the pancreas, causing back pain."]],
  symptoms: "Burning or gnawing upper belly pain comes from acid irritating exposed nerves in the ulcer crater. Duodenal ulcer pain often eases with food and returns hours later or at night, because food buffers acid briefly and then triggers more acid. Gastric ulcer pain often worsens with eating. Nausea, bloating and early fullness come from inflammation and slowed emptying. Black, tarry stools or vomiting blood mean the ulcer has eroded a blood vessel. Many NSAID ulcers cause no pain until they bleed, because NSAIDs also blunt pain.",
  dx: "Upper endoscopy shows the ulcer directly, allows biopsy of gastric ulcers to rule out cancer and can treat bleeding. H. pylori is detected by urea breath test, stool antigen test or biopsy-based tests; PPIs, bismuth and antibiotics can cause false negatives. Serology is less useful because it stays positive after cure. Testing to confirm cure is recommended after treatment. Very high fasting gastrin with multiple or unusual ulcers suggests Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.",
  tx: "Acid suppression with a PPI lets the ulcer heal, because peptic injury needs acid. H. pylori is cured with combinations of antibiotics and acid suppression, and US guidance now favors bismuth quadruple therapy (a PPI, bismuth, tetracycline and metronidazole) as first choice because clarithromycin resistance is common. Other options include rifabutin triple therapy and vonoprazan with amoxicillin. Acid suppression raises stomach pH, which helps antibiotics work and helps the organism become susceptible. Bismuth damages bacterial cell walls and has mild protective effects. NSAIDs are stopped when possible; if needed, a PPI or misoprostol, a prostaglandin E1 analog, protects the lining. Sucralfate forms a protective coat over the ulcer. Bleeding ulcers are treated with endoscopic therapy and IV PPI, and perforation usually needs surgery.",
  drugs: ["omeprazole", "pantoprazole", "esomeprazole", "lansoprazole", "bismuth-metronidazole-tetracycline", "bismuth-subsalicylate", "tetracycline", "metronidazole", "amoxicillin", "clarithromycin", "omeprazole-amoxicillin-rifabutin", "vonoprazan-amoxicillin", "vonoprazan", "lansoprazole-amoxicillin-clarithromycin", "levofloxacin", "famotidine", "sucralfate", "misoprostol"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "H. pylori makes urease, which is the basis of the urea breath test, and it is the most common cause of duodenal ulcers."], ["Memory trick", "Duodenal ulcer pain Decreases with food; Gastric ulcer pain Grows with food."], ["Often confused with", "Zollinger-Ellison syndrome: multiple or jejunal ulcers with diarrhea and very high gastrin from a gastrinoma."], ["Classic bleed", "A posterior duodenal ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery."]],
  red: "Vomiting blood or material like coffee grounds, black or bloody stools, fainting, or sudden severe belly pain with a rigid belly can mean bleeding or perforation and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Barry Marshall drank a broth of H. pylori to prove it caused stomach inflammation, and the experiment helped win him a Nobel Prize.",
  src: ["ACG Clinical Guideline: Treatment of Helicobacter pylori Infection (2024)", "Marshall BJ, Warren JR. Lancet 1984", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"gi-bleed": {
  name: "Gastrointestinal bleeding",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["GI bleed", "upper GI bleeding", "lower GI bleeding", "UGIB", "LGIB", "variceal bleeding"],
  what: "Gastrointestinal bleeding is bleeding anywhere in the digestive tract, from the esophagus to the anus. It can be slow and hidden or fast and life threatening.",
  origin: "Vomiting blood and passing black stools have been recognized since antiquity as signs of serious illness, but for centuries little could be done. Rigid and then flexible endoscopes in the 1900s let doctors see the source. In 1974 Forrest and colleagues published a way to grade bleeding ulcers by their endoscopic look, which still predicts rebleeding. In the 1980s endoscopic band ligation of esophageal varices was introduced, and TIPS, a stent connecting the portal and hepatic veins, was developed around the same era. In 2013 a large trial by Villanueva and colleagues showed that a restrictive blood transfusion strategy improved survival in upper GI bleeding. In 2020 the HALT-IT trial showed tranexamic acid did not reduce death from GI bleeding and raised clot risk.",
  timeline: [["1974", "Forrest classification of bleeding ulcers is published."], ["1980s", "Endoscopic band ligation of varices is introduced."], ["2000", "The Glasgow-Blatchford score is published to triage upper GI bleeding."], ["2013", "A trial shows restrictive transfusion improves outcomes in upper GI bleeding."], ["2020", "The HALT-IT trial finds tranexamic acid does not lower death from GI bleeding."]],
  cell: "Bleeding happens when the wall of a blood vessel in the gut is eroded or ruptures. Upper GI bleeding starts above the ligament of Treitz. Peptic ulcers erode arteries when acid and pepsin digest the vessel wall; acid also impairs platelet aggregation and dissolves clots, which is why acid suppression helps. Varices form when portal hypertension in cirrhosis opens collateral veins in the esophagus and stomach; thin-walled varices burst when pressure and wall tension rise. Mallory-Weiss tears are mucosal tears from forceful vomiting. Lower GI bleeding comes most often from diverticula, where a vasa recta artery stretches over the dome of the pouch and ruptures, and from angiodysplasia, hemorrhoids, colitis and cancer. Anticoagulants and antiplatelet drugs do not cause the lesion but make it bleed more. Blood loss lowers preload and cardiac output, triggering sympathetic activation, tachycardia and vasoconstriction, and eventually hemorrhagic shock. Digested blood protein raises BUN.",
  body: [["Circulation", "Rapid blood loss lowers blood volume, causing a fast heart rate, low blood pressure, fainting and hemorrhagic shock. Older patients and people on beta blockers may not show a fast heart rate."], ["Gut", "Blood in the upper gut is digested by acid, turning stool black and tarry (melena). Brisk bleeding passes red blood from the rectum (hematochezia)."], ["Heart", "Low blood oxygen delivery can trigger chest pain or a heart attack in people with coronary disease."], ["Kidneys", "Low blood flow can cause acute kidney injury. Digested blood is absorbed as protein, raising BUN."], ["Brain", "In cirrhosis, blood in the gut is a protein load that raises ammonia and can trigger hepatic encephalopathy."], ["Blood", "Slow chronic bleeding causes iron deficiency anemia with fatigue and pallor."]],
  symptoms: "Vomiting blood (hematemesis) or coffee-ground material means bleeding above the ligament of Treitz, with coffee-ground color from acid acting on hemoglobin. Black, sticky stool means blood has been in the gut long enough to be digested. Bright red blood from the rectum usually means a lower source, but a very fast upper bleed can do the same. Dizziness, weakness, pale skin, a fast pulse and fainting reflect low blood volume. Chronic slow bleeding causes tiredness and shortness of breath from anemia.",
  dx: "Assessment begins with vital signs, an exam including stool color, and labs: hemoglobin, platelet count, clotting tests, BUN and type and crossmatch. A high BUN to creatinine ratio suggests an upper source. Risk scores such as Glasgow-Blatchford help decide who needs urgent care. Upper endoscopy, usually within 24 hours, finds and treats most upper bleeding. Colonoscopy after bowel prep is used for lower bleeding. CT angiography can locate active bleeding, and capsule endoscopy finds small bowel sources.",
  tx: "Care starts with airway protection, two large IV lines and fluid resuscitation. Red cells are given with a restrictive threshold in most patients, because over-transfusion raises portal pressure and rebleeding. Anticoagulants are reversed when bleeding is severe: vitamin K and 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate for warfarin, idarucizumab for dabigatran, and 4F-PCC for factor Xa inhibitors. For ulcers, IV PPIs raise stomach pH so clots stay stable, and endoscopy treats the vessel with clips, heat or injection. For suspected variceal bleeding, octreotide constricts splanchnic vessels to lower portal pressure, antibiotics such as ceftriaxone prevent infection and improve survival, and endoscopic band ligation is performed; TIPS is used for uncontrolled bleeding. Interventional radiology embolization or surgery treats bleeding that endoscopy cannot stop. Tranexamic acid is not recommended routinely.",
  drugs: ["pantoprazole", "esomeprazole", "omeprazole", "octreotide", "ceftriaxone", "phytonadione", "prothrombin-complex-concentrate", "idarucizumab", "normal-saline", "lactated-ringers", "propranolol", "nadolol", "carvedilol", "erythromycin", "vasopressin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "The ligament of Treitz divides upper from lower GI bleeding; peptic ulcer is the most common upper cause and diverticulosis the most common lower cause in adults."], ["Variceal bundle", "Suspected variceal bleed: octreotide, antibiotics (ceftriaxone) and band ligation."], ["Memory trick", "Melena means the blood met acid: black stool usually means an upper GI source."], ["Pearl", "IV erythromycin before endoscopy speeds stomach emptying so the doctor can see better."]],
  red: "Vomiting blood or coffee-ground material, black tarry stools, large amounts of red blood from the rectum, dizziness, fainting, a fast heartbeat or confusion needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Black stool can also come from iron pills and bismuth, which is why doctors test the stool for blood before calling it melena.",
  src: ["ACG Clinical Guideline: Upper Gastrointestinal and Ulcer Bleeding (2021)", "ACG Clinical Guideline: Management of Patients With Acute Lower GI Bleeding (2023)", "Baveno VII consensus on portal hypertension (2022)", "Villanueva C et al. N Engl J Med 2013", "HALT-IT Trial Collaborators. Lancet 2020"]
},
"crohns-disease": {
  name: "Crohn's disease",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["Crohn disease", "regional enteritis", "regional ileitis", "IBD (inflammatory bowel disease)"],
  what: "Crohn's disease is a lifelong inflammatory bowel disease where the immune system attacks the digestive tract. It can affect any part from mouth to anus, most often the end of the small intestine, and it comes and goes in flares.",
  origin: "Scottish surgeon T. Kennedy Dalziel described patients with chronic inflammation of the intestine in 1913. In 1932 Burrill Crohn, Leon Ginzburg and Gordon Oppenheimer at Mount Sinai Hospital in New York published a paper describing regional ileitis, and the disease came to carry Crohn's name. Treatment for decades relied on steroids, which arrived in the 1950s, and surgery. Immune-modulating drugs such as azathioprine followed. A major turning point came in 1998, when infliximab, an antibody against TNF-alpha, was approved for Crohn's disease. In 2001 NOD2 became the first gene linked to Crohn's, and newer biologics targeting gut-homing white cells and the IL-12 and IL-23 pathways followed in the 2010s and 2020s.",
  timeline: [["1913", "T. Kennedy Dalziel describes chronic intestinal inflammation."], ["1932", "Crohn, Ginzburg and Oppenheimer describe regional ileitis."], ["1998", "Infliximab becomes the first biologic approved for Crohn's disease."], ["2001", "NOD2 is identified as the first Crohn's susceptibility gene."], ["2016", "Ustekinumab, targeting IL-12 and IL-23, is approved for Crohn's disease."]],
  cell: "Crohn's disease arises when a genetically susceptible person mounts an excessive immune response to gut bacteria. NOD2 normally senses bacterial muramyl dipeptide inside cells; loss-of-function variants weaken bacterial handling and Paneth cell antimicrobial defensins. Autophagy genes such as ATG16L1 and IRGM, and IL-23 receptor variants, also add risk. Bacteria cross a leaky barrier, dendritic cells and macrophages release IL-12 and IL-23, and these drive Th1 (interferon-gamma) and Th17 (IL-17) responses. Macrophages release TNF-alpha, which sustains inflammation. The inflammation is transmural, through the full thickness of the wall, and patchy, creating skip lesions, deep linear ulcers with cobblestone mucosa, and noncaseating granulomas. Transmural inflammation leads to fibrosis and strictures, and to fistulas when ulcers tunnel into other organs. Smoking raises risk. Gut-homing T cells use alpha4-beta7 integrin binding to MAdCAM-1 to enter the bowel wall, a target of vedolizumab.",
  body: [["Small intestine", "The terminal ileum is most often involved. Inflammation causes pain, diarrhea and narrowing that can block the bowel, and loss of ileal function reduces vitamin B12 and bile acid absorption."], ["Colon and anus", "Colitis causes diarrhea and bleeding. Perianal disease causes fissures, abscesses and fistulas."], ["Bowel wall", "Full-thickness inflammation creates fistulas to skin, bladder, vagina or other loops of bowel, and abscesses."], ["Nutrition and growth", "Poor absorption and inflammation cause weight loss, anemia and in children slow growth and delayed puberty."], ["Kidneys and gallbladder", "Fat malabsorption lets oxalate be absorbed, causing calcium oxalate kidney stones; bile acid loss causes gallstones."], ["Joints, skin and eyes", "Immune activity outside the gut causes arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, erythema nodosum, pyoderma gangrenosum and uveitis."], ["Bones", "Inflammation, steroids and poor vitamin D absorption raise osteoporosis risk."]],
  symptoms: "Crampy belly pain, often in the right lower area, comes from inflammation and narrowing of the terminal ileum. Diarrhea comes from inflamed, leaky mucosa and from unabsorbed bile acids. Blood in the stool is less common than in ulcerative colitis but occurs with colon disease. Weight loss, fatigue and fever reflect inflammation, cytokines and poor absorption. Perianal pain, drainage or recurrent abscesses come from fistulas. Symptoms flare and quiet down as immune activity changes.",
  dx: "Diagnosis combines symptoms with colonoscopy and ileoscopy showing patchy ulcers, skip lesions and cobblestoning, plus biopsies that may show noncaseating granulomas. CT or MR enterography shows small bowel thickening, strictures, fistulas and abscesses. Capsule endoscopy can find small bowel disease. Labs include CBC, CRP and fecal calprotectin, a marker of gut inflammation. Stool tests rule out infection, including C. difficile.",
  tx: "Treatment aims to calm inflammation, heal the lining and prevent complications. Corticosteroids such as prednisone, or budesonide that is mostly cleared by the liver, quickly reduce inflammation but are not used for maintenance. Anti-TNF biologics (infliximab, adalimumab, certolizumab) neutralize TNF-alpha. Vedolizumab blocks alpha4-beta7 integrin so immune cells cannot home to the gut. Ustekinumab, risankizumab, mirikizumab and guselkumab block IL-23 signaling. Upadacitinib, a JAK inhibitor, blocks cytokine signaling inside cells. Thiopurines (azathioprine, mercaptopurine) and methotrexate suppress lymphocytes and lower antibody formation against biologics. Antibiotics and drainage treat abscesses. Surgery removes strictures or diseased bowel but does not cure Crohn's. Stopping smoking, nutrition support, vitamin B12, iron and vaccines are key parts of care.",
  drugs: ["infliximab", "adalimumab", "certolizumab-pegol", "vedolizumab", "ustekinumab", "risankizumab", "mirikizumab", "guselkumab", "upadacitinib", "budesonide-oral", "prednisone", "methylprednisolone", "azathioprine", "mercaptopurine", "methotrexate", "metronidazole", "ciprofloxacin", "cyanocobalamin", "loperamide", "cholestyramine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Crohn's is CHRISTMAS: Cobblestones, High temperature, Reduced lumen (string sign), Intestinal fistulas, Skip lesions, Transmural, Malabsorption, Abdominal pain, Submucosal fibrosis."], ["Most-tested fact", "Transmural inflammation with skip lesions and noncaseating granulomas, most often in the terminal ileum."], ["Often confused with", "Ulcerative colitis, which is continuous, limited to the colon, starts in the rectum and involves only the mucosa."], ["Smoking", "Smoking worsens Crohn's disease, while it appears to lower the risk of ulcerative colitis."]],
  red: "Severe belly pain with a swollen belly and vomiting, high fever, heavy rectal bleeding, fainting or signs of dehydration in someone with Crohn's disease needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The 1932 paper listed its three authors in alphabetical order, which put Crohn's name first, and the disease came to carry his name.",
  src: ["AGA Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Medical Management of Moderate to Severe Luminal and Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease (2021)", "ACG Clinical Guideline: Management of Crohn's Disease in Adults", "Crohn BB, Ginzburg L, Oppenheimer GD. JAMA 1932", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"ulcerative-colitis": {
  name: "Ulcerative colitis",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["UC", "IBD (inflammatory bowel disease)", "ulcerative proctitis"],
  what: "Ulcerative colitis is a lifelong inflammatory bowel disease that causes inflammation and sores in the inner lining of the colon. It always starts at the rectum and spreads upward in one continuous stretch.",
  origin: "In 1859 English physician Samuel Wilks described a patient with ulceration of the colon, and the term ulcerative colitis came into use in the late 1800s. For decades the only effective treatment for severe disease was removing the colon. In the 1940s Swedish physician Nanna Svartz developed sulfasalazine, first meant for arthritis, and it helped colitis. In 1955 Sidney Truelove and Leslie Witts in Oxford showed in a controlled trial that cortisone helped ulcerative colitis. In 1977 researchers showed that 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA), part of sulfasalazine, was the active part, leading to mesalamine. Biologics such as infliximab (approved for UC in 2005), vedolizumab and newer oral drugs, including JAK inhibitors and S1P receptor modulators, followed.",
  timeline: [["1859", "Samuel Wilks describes ulceration of the colon."], ["1940s", "Nanna Svartz develops sulfasalazine."], ["1955", "Truelove and Witts show cortisone benefits ulcerative colitis."], ["1977", "5-ASA is shown to be the active part of sulfasalazine."], ["2005", "Infliximab is approved for ulcerative colitis."], ["2018", "Tofacitinib becomes the first oral JAK inhibitor approved for UC."]],
  cell: "Ulcerative colitis is an abnormal immune response to gut bacteria in a genetically susceptible person, limited to the colonic mucosa. Defects in the epithelial barrier and mucus layer let bacterial products contact the immune system. The immune response is often described as atypical Th2-like, with natural killer T cells making IL-13, which damages epithelial cells and tight junctions. Macrophages release TNF-alpha and IL-23 and IL-17 pathways also contribute. Neutrophils invade crypts, forming crypt abscesses, and the lining becomes ulcerated and bleeds easily. Inflammation is continuous from the rectum upward and does not involve the full wall, so fistulas and granulomas are not features. Chronic inflammation causes crypt distortion and pseudopolyps, and over years raises the risk of dysplasia and colorectal cancer. The disease is linked to primary sclerosing cholangitis and p-ANCA. Former smokers have a higher risk than current smokers.",
  body: [["Colon and rectum", "Continuous mucosal inflammation from the rectum upward causes bloody diarrhea, urgency and tenesmus. Long-standing disease raises colorectal cancer risk."], ["Colon wall (severe)", "Severe inflammation can paralyze the colon so it dilates, called toxic megacolon, which can perforate."], ["Liver and bile ducts", "Primary sclerosing cholangitis, scarring of the bile ducts, is strongly linked to UC and further raises cancer risk."], ["Blood", "Bleeding causes iron deficiency anemia, and inflammation raises the risk of blood clots."], ["Joints, skin and eyes", "Immune activity causes arthritis, erythema nodosum, pyoderma gangrenosum and eye inflammation."]],
  symptoms: "Bloody diarrhea with mucus is the hallmark, because the inflamed lining bleeds easily and cannot absorb water well. Urgency and tenesmus, the feeling of needing to pass stool even when empty, come from rectal inflammation. Crampy lower belly pain occurs before bowel movements. Fever, fast heart rate, weight loss and fatigue reflect more severe inflammation. Many patients wake at night to pass stool during flares.",
  dx: "Colonoscopy with biopsy shows continuous inflammation starting at the rectum with loss of the vascular pattern, friability and ulcers, and biopsies show crypt abscesses and chronic crypt distortion. Stool tests exclude infection, including C. difficile. Labs include CBC, CRP, albumin and fecal calprotectin. Severity is graded by stool frequency, bleeding and signs of systemic illness. In a severe flare, an abdominal X-ray can show toxic megacolon. Surveillance colonoscopy for dysplasia begins about 8 years after diagnosis.",
  tx: "Mesalamine and other 5-ASA drugs act on the colonic lining to reduce inflammatory mediators and are the base therapy for mild to moderate disease, given by mouth and rectally. Corticosteroids, including budesonide for milder cases and IV steroids for severe flares, rapidly suppress inflammation but are not used long term. Moderate to severe disease uses anti-TNF antibodies (infliximab, adalimumab, golimumab), vedolizumab to block gut-homing of immune cells, IL-23 or IL-12/23 blockers (ustekinumab, mirikizumab, guselkumab, risankizumab), oral JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib) that block cytokine signaling, and S1P receptor modulators (ozanimod, etrasimod) that trap lymphocytes in lymph nodes. Thiopurines help maintain remission. Severe flares are treated in the hospital with IV steroids, clot prevention, and rescue infliximab or cyclosporine. Removing the colon and rectum (proctocolectomy) cures the intestinal disease.",
  drugs: ["mesalamine", "sulfasalazine", "balsalazide", "olsalazine", "budesonide-oral", "prednisone", "methylprednisolone", "hydrocortisone", "infliximab", "adalimumab", "golimumab", "vedolizumab", "ustekinumab", "mirikizumab", "guselkumab", "risankizumab", "tofacitinib", "upadacitinib", "ozanimod", "etrasimod", "azathioprine", "mercaptopurine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "UC is continuous from the rectum: Up the Colon, never skipping."], ["Most-tested fact", "UC is mucosal only, starts in the rectum and is linked to primary sclerosing cholangitis and p-ANCA."], ["Often confused with", "Crohn's disease, which is transmural, patchy, can affect any part of the gut and causes fistulas and granulomas."], ["Danger", "Toxic megacolon: a dilated colon with fever and fast heart rate during a severe flare."]],
  red: "Heavy rectal bleeding, high fever, a swollen and very tender belly, a fast heartbeat, fainting or more than about six bloody stools a day can mean a severe flare or toxic megacolon and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Sulfasalazine was designed to treat rheumatoid arthritis by linking an antibiotic to an aspirin-like molecule, and its benefit in colitis was an unexpected bonus.",
  src: ["ACG Clinical Guideline: Ulcerative Colitis in Adults (2019)", "AGA Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Management of Moderate to Severe Ulcerative Colitis (2020)", "Truelove SC, Witts LJ. BMJ 1955", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"irritable-bowel": {
  name: "Irritable bowel syndrome",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["IBS", "IBS-C", "IBS-D", "IBS-M", "spastic colon (old term)", "disorder of gut-brain interaction"],
  what: "Irritable bowel syndrome is a common long-term condition with belly pain linked to changes in bowel habits, such as diarrhea, constipation or both. The gut looks normal on tests, but the way the gut and brain communicate is altered.",
  origin: "Doctors in the 1800s described patients with belly pain, mucus in the stool and no visible disease, using names such as mucous colitis and spastic colon. In 1978 Adrian Manning and colleagues published symptom criteria that helped separate IBS from other illnesses. An international group meeting in Rome began creating the Rome criteria in the late 1980s and 1990s, and Rome IV in 2016 renamed IBS a disorder of gut-brain interaction. Researchers also found that IBS can start after a bout of infectious gastroenteritis. In the 2000s a team at Monash University in Australia developed the low FODMAP diet. Drugs targeting gut receptors followed, including alosetron (2000, reintroduced with limits in 2002), linaclotide (2012), and rifaximin and eluxadoline for IBS with diarrhea (2015).",
  timeline: [["1978", "Manning criteria for IBS are published."], ["2000", "Alosetron is approved, withdrawn the same year, and returns with restrictions in 2002."], ["2000s", "Monash University researchers develop the low FODMAP diet."], ["2012", "Linaclotide is approved for IBS with constipation."], ["2015", "Rifaximin and eluxadoline are approved for IBS with diarrhea."], ["2016", "Rome IV criteria define IBS as a disorder of gut-brain interaction."]],
  cell: "IBS has no single lesion; several mechanisms combine. Visceral hypersensitivity means sensory nerves in the gut wall and spinal pathways send stronger pain signals in response to normal stretch, and the brain processes these signals with more attention and emotion. Gut motility is altered, with faster transit in IBS-D and slower transit in IBS-C. Serotonin (5-HT), about 90 percent of which is made by enterochromaffin cells in the gut lining, activates 5-HT3 and 5-HT4 receptors on enteric nerves, changing secretion, motility and pain; this is why 5-HT3 blockers help diarrhea. Low-grade immune activation, including mast cells near nerve endings, increased gut permeability, changes in the microbiome and bile acid malabsorption contribute in some people. Post-infectious IBS shows that an infection can leave lasting changes in nerves and immune cells. Poorly absorbed fermentable carbohydrates (FODMAPs) draw water into the bowel and produce gas, which stretches a sensitive gut. Stress acts through the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis and corticotropin-releasing factor to change motility and pain.",
  body: [["Large intestine", "Altered contractions and secretion cause diarrhea, constipation or alternation between them, with no structural damage, bleeding or inflammation."], ["Gut nerves", "Sensitized nerves make normal gas and stretch feel painful, causing cramping and bloating."], ["Brain", "The gut-brain axis links IBS with anxiety, depression and stress, which can intensify symptoms, and IBS symptoms in turn worsen mood."], ["Whole body", "IBS often overlaps with fibromyalgia, migraine, chronic fatigue and other pain conditions, suggesting shared pain processing changes."]],
  symptoms: "Belly pain related to bowel movements happens because a sensitive gut registers normal stretch as pain; the pain often changes after passing stool. Bloating comes from gas produced by fermentation, together with heightened sensation. Diarrhea comes from faster transit and more fluid secretion, while constipation comes from slow transit. Mucus in the stool can occur. Symptoms typically flare with stress or certain foods. Weight loss, bleeding, fever and night-time symptoms are not part of IBS and point to another cause.",
  dx: "IBS is a positive clinical diagnosis based on the Rome IV criteria: recurrent belly pain on average at least one day a week in the last three months, linked to defecation or a change in stool frequency or form, with onset at least six months earlier. Subtypes are set by the main stool form. A limited workup checks for other diseases: CBC, CRP or fecal calprotectin, celiac serology and, in diarrhea, tests for infection or bile acid diarrhea. Colonoscopy is done for alarm features or age-appropriate colon cancer screening.",
  tx: "Care starts with explaining the condition and building a trusting care plan. Diet changes include regular meals, soluble fiber such as psyllium, and a guided low FODMAP diet. For IBS-D, loperamide slows transit through opioid receptors, rifaximin changes gut bacteria, eluxadoline acts on gut opioid receptors, alosetron blocks 5-HT3 receptors in women with severe disease, and bile acid binders help bile acid diarrhea. For IBS-C, polyethylene glycol draws water into the stool, while linaclotide and plecanatide activate guanylate cyclase-C, lubiprostone opens chloride channels, and tenapanor reduces sodium absorption to increase fluid in the gut. For pain, antispasmodics such as dicyclomine relax smooth muscle, peppermint oil relaxes gut muscle, and low-dose tricyclic antidepressants such as amitriptyline dampen pain signaling. Gut-directed hypnotherapy and cognitive behavioral therapy have good evidence.",
  drugs: ["loperamide", "rifaximin", "eluxadoline", "alosetron", "cholestyramine", "linaclotide", "plecanatide", "lubiprostone", "tenapanor", "polyethylene-glycol", "dicyclomine", "hyoscyamine", "amitriptyline", "nortriptyline", "simethicone"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "IBS is diagnosed by Rome IV criteria: recurrent abdominal pain linked to defecation and a change in stool frequency or form."], ["Alarm features", "Weight loss, bleeding, anemia, fever, night-time symptoms or onset after age 50 are not IBS and need investigation."], ["Memory trick", "Linaclotide and plecanatide turn up cGMP to open the CFTR tap, so water flows into the gut."], ["Often confused with", "Celiac disease and inflammatory bowel disease, which is why celiac serology and calprotectin are commonly checked."]],
  red: "Rectal bleeding with dizziness, black stools, severe or worsening belly pain, high fever or repeated vomiting are not typical of IBS and need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Most of the body's serotonin is made in the gut, not the brain, which is part of why IBS drugs that target serotonin receptors work.",
  src: ["ACG Clinical Guideline: Management of Irritable Bowel Syndrome (2021)", "AGA Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Pharmacological Management of IBS-C and IBS-D (2022)", "Rome IV criteria (2016)", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"celiac-disease": {
  name: "Celiac disease",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["coeliac disease", "celiac sprue", "gluten-sensitive enteropathy", "gluten intolerance (informal)"],
  what: "Celiac disease is an autoimmune condition where eating gluten, a protein in wheat, barley and rye, makes the immune system damage the lining of the small intestine. This blocks absorption of nutrients.",
  origin: "In the 2nd century AD, the Greek physician Aretaeus of Cappadocia described a chronic diarrhea he called koiliakos, from the Greek word for belly. In 1888 English physician Samuel Gee published a classic description of the coeliac affection in children and suggested diet was key. In the 1940s Dutch pediatrician Willem Dicke noticed children improved when wheat was removed, an observation popularly tied to bread shortages in the Netherlands during World War II, and his 1950 thesis showed wheat was the trigger. In the 1950s new biopsy tubes let doctors see flattened villi in the small intestine. Links to the HLA-DQ2 gene were defined in 1989, and in 1997 a German team led by Detlef Schuppan identified tissue transglutaminase as the main autoantigen, leading to today's blood test.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Aretaeus of Cappadocia describes the koiliakos diarrhea in the 2nd century AD."], ["1888", "Samuel Gee publishes On the Coeliac Affection."], ["1950", "Willem Dicke's thesis shows wheat triggers celiac disease."], ["1989", "Celiac disease is tied to the HLA-DQ2 molecule."], ["1997", "Tissue transglutaminase is identified as the celiac autoantigen."]],
  cell: "Gluten contains gliadin and glutenin proteins rich in proline and glutamine, which makes some peptides, such as a 33-amino-acid gliadin fragment, resistant to digestion. These peptides cross the intestinal lining. In the lamina propria, tissue transglutaminase (tTG) deamidates glutamine to glutamate, adding negative charges that make the peptides bind tightly to HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8 molecules on antigen-presenting cells. Gluten-specific CD4 T cells then release interferon-gamma and other cytokines. Gliadin also drives epithelial cells to make IL-15, which activates intraepithelial CD8 cytotoxic lymphocytes (through NKG2D) that kill enterocytes. B cells make antibodies against tTG, endomysium and deamidated gliadin peptides. The result is villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes, worst in the duodenum and proximal jejunum where gluten exposure is greatest. Almost all patients carry HLA-DQ2 or DQ8, but most carriers never develop disease, so other factors such as infections and the microbiome play a role.",
  body: [["Small intestine", "Flattened villi drastically reduce the absorptive surface, causing diarrhea, fatty stools, bloating and malabsorption."], ["Blood", "Poor absorption of iron in the duodenum and of folate causes anemia, often iron deficiency anemia that does not improve with iron pills."], ["Bones", "Poor calcium and vitamin D absorption causes osteopenia and osteoporosis."], ["Skin", "Dermatitis herpetiformis, an intensely itchy blistering rash on elbows, knees and buttocks, is caused by IgA deposits in the skin."], ["Growth and development", "Children may have poor growth, short stature, delayed puberty and dental enamel defects."], ["Nervous system", "Some people develop peripheral neuropathy, ataxia or headaches."], ["Cancer risk", "Long-standing untreated disease raises the risk of enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma and small bowel cancer."]],
  symptoms: "Diarrhea, bloating and gas happen because damaged villi cannot absorb food, so sugars and fats reach the colon, where they draw water and are fermented. Fatty, foul-smelling stools reflect fat malabsorption. Weight loss and fatigue come from poor nutrition and anemia. Many adults have few gut symptoms and present with iron deficiency, bone loss, raised liver enzymes or the itchy rash of dermatitis herpetiformis. Symptoms improve once gluten is removed and the villi regrow.",
  dx: "Testing is done while the person is still eating gluten, because a gluten-free diet can make tests falsely normal. The first test is tissue transglutaminase IgA (tTG-IgA) with total IgA, since IgA deficiency is more common in celiac disease and causes false negatives; IgG-based tests are used if IgA is low. Endomysial antibodies are highly specific. Upper endoscopy with multiple duodenal biopsies shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia and intraepithelial lymphocytes, graded by the Marsh classification. HLA-DQ2 and DQ8 testing is mainly useful to rule celiac disease out.",
  tx: "The only effective treatment is a strict lifelong gluten-free diet, which removes the trigger so the immune reaction calms and the villi heal over months. A dietitian experienced in celiac disease is a key part of care, because hidden gluten and cross-contamination are common. Nutrient deficiencies are corrected with iron, folate, vitamin B12, calcium and vitamin D. Bone density is checked. Dermatitis herpetiformis responds to the gluten-free diet, and dapsone controls the rash faster while the diet takes effect. Refractory celiac disease is uncommon and may be treated with budesonide or other immunosuppression under specialist care. Pneumococcal vaccination is considered because some patients have reduced spleen function. No drug is FDA approved to replace the diet.",
  drugs: ["ferrous-sulfate", "folic-acid", "cyanocobalamin", "cholecalciferol", "calcium-carbonate", "dapsone", "budesonide-oral", "pneumococcal-21-valent-conjugate-vaccine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Avoid BRAW: Barley, Rye, Oats (if contaminated), Wheat."], ["Most-tested fact", "tTG-IgA with total IgA is the best first test, done while eating gluten; the duodenum is the most affected site."], ["Classic clue", "Iron deficiency anemia that does not respond to oral iron, or dermatitis herpetiformis, suggests celiac disease."], ["Genes", "HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8: a negative test makes celiac disease very unlikely."]],
  fun: "Pure oats are usually tolerated in celiac disease, but oats are often contaminated with wheat during growing and milling, which is why gluten-free diets use certified gluten-free oats.",
  src: ["ACG Clinical Guideline Update: Celiac Disease (2023)", "Gee S. On the Coeliac Affection. 1888", "Dieterich W et al. Nat Med 1997", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"cirrhosis": {
  name: "Cirrhosis",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["liver cirrhosis", "end-stage liver disease", "ESLD", "advanced liver fibrosis"],
  what: "Cirrhosis is late-stage scarring of the liver. Healthy liver tissue is replaced by scar and lumpy nodules, so the liver cannot work well and blood flow through it is blocked.",
  origin: "Ancient physicians noted hard, shrunken livers in people with swollen bellies. In 1819 French physician Rene Laennec, inventor of the stethoscope, named the disease cirrhosis from the Greek word for tawny, describing the orange-yellow color of the scarred liver. In the 1900s doctors linked cirrhosis to alcohol, viral hepatitis and later fatty liver disease. In 1964 Charles Child and Jeremiah Turcotte created a scoring system, later modified by Pugh in 1973. Thomas Starzl performed the first human liver transplant in 1963 and the first successful one in 1967. TIPS, a stent that lowers portal pressure, entered clinical use in the late 1980s. In 2002 the US began using the MELD score to rank patients for liver transplant.",
  timeline: [["1819", "Rene Laennec names cirrhosis."], ["1964", "The Child-Turcotte score is introduced, later modified by Pugh in 1973."], ["1967", "Thomas Starzl performs the first successful human liver transplant."], ["1980s", "TIPS is developed to lower portal pressure."], ["2002", "MELD score is adopted for US liver transplant allocation."]],
  cell: "Chronic injury from alcohol, viral hepatitis, metabolic fatty liver, autoimmune or genetic disease kills hepatocytes and triggers inflammation. Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes release TGF-beta, PDGF and other signals that activate hepatic stellate cells (Ito cells) in the space of Disse. Activated stellate cells lose their vitamin A stores, become contractile myofibroblasts and lay down type I and III collagen. Sinusoidal endothelial cells lose their fenestrations (capillarization), blocking exchange between blood and hepatocytes. Surviving hepatocytes form regenerative nodules surrounded by fibrous bands, which distort the architecture. Scar and contraction raise resistance to portal blood flow, and splanchnic vessels dilate from excess nitric oxide, raising portal flow. The result is portal hypertension. Low effective arterial volume activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, sympathetic nerves and ADH, causing sodium and water retention, ascites, hyponatremia and hepatorenal syndrome. Loss of hepatocyte mass reduces albumin and clotting factor synthesis and ammonia and bilirubin clearance. Ongoing cell turnover raises hepatocellular carcinoma risk.",
  body: [["Liver", "Scarred, nodular liver loses function, causing low albumin, poor clotting, jaundice and a high risk of liver cancer."], ["Portal circulation", "Portal hypertension opens collateral veins in the esophagus and stomach (varices) that can bleed massively, plus caput medusae and enlarged spleen."], ["Belly", "Fluid builds up as ascites from portal hypertension, low albumin and salt retention, and can become infected (spontaneous bacterial peritonitis)."], ["Brain", "Ammonia and other toxins bypass the liver and cause hepatic encephalopathy, ranging from poor attention to coma."], ["Kidneys", "Splanchnic vasodilation lowers kidney blood flow, causing hepatorenal syndrome, a functional kidney failure."], ["Blood", "An enlarged spleen traps platelets (thrombocytopenia), and the liver makes fewer clotting factors, raising the INR."], ["Hormones and skin", "Poor estrogen breakdown causes spider angiomas, palmar erythema, gynecomastia and testicular atrophy."], ["Lungs", "Some patients develop hepatopulmonary syndrome or portopulmonary hypertension."]],
  symptoms: "Early cirrhosis can cause no symptoms. Fatigue, weakness and muscle loss come from poor protein metabolism. Jaundice comes from failure to clear bilirubin, and itching from retained bile acids. Belly swelling is ascites, and leg swelling comes from low albumin and salt retention. Easy bruising reflects low clotting factors and platelets. Vomiting blood means bleeding varices. Confusion, sleep reversal and a flapping tremor (asterixis) reflect hepatic encephalopathy.",
  dx: "Diagnosis combines history, exam signs such as spider angiomas, ascites and splenomegaly, and labs: low albumin, high INR, high bilirubin, low platelets and liver enzymes that may be only mildly raised. Ultrasound shows a nodular, shrunken liver and ascites. Elastography (FibroScan) and blood scores such as FIB-4 estimate scarring without biopsy. Liver biopsy is used when the cause is unclear. Tests look for the cause (hepatitis B and C, alcohol, iron, copper, autoimmune markers). Endoscopy screens for varices. Child-Pugh and MELD scores grade severity.",
  tx: "Treating the cause can slow or partly reverse damage: stopping alcohol, curing hepatitis C, suppressing hepatitis B and weight loss for fatty liver. Nonselective beta blockers, with carvedilol often preferred, lower portal pressure and prevent variceal bleeding; band ligation is another option. Ascites is managed with sodium restriction and the diuretics spironolactone, which blocks aldosterone, and furosemide, plus large-volume paracentesis with IV albumin. Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis is treated with third-generation cephalosporins and albumin, and some patients receive preventive antibiotics. Hepatic encephalopathy is treated with lactulose and rifaximin. Hepatorenal syndrome is treated with terlipressin or other vasoconstrictors plus albumin. Ultrasound every six months screens for liver cancer. Hepatitis A and B vaccination, nutrition with adequate protein, and avoiding NSAIDs are part of care. Liver transplant is the definitive treatment for advanced disease.",
  drugs: ["spironolactone", "furosemide", "carvedilol", "propranolol", "nadolol", "albumin-human", "lactulose", "rifaximin", "terlipressin", "octreotide", "ceftriaxone", "cefotaxime", "ciprofloxacin", "trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole", "hepatitis-a-vaccine", "hepatitis-b-vaccine", "ursodiol", "phytonadione"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Hepatic stellate (Ito) cells are the main source of scar collagen in cirrhosis."], ["Memory trick", "Portal hypertension opens collateral veins in three classic places: the esophagus (varices), around the navel (caput medusae) and the rectum (rectal varices)."], ["Scoring", "MELD uses bilirubin, INR, creatinine and sodium to rank transplant urgency."], ["Pearl", "In cirrhosis a high INR does not mean the blood is thin; clotting and anticlotting factors both fall, so clot risk remains."]],
  red: "Vomiting blood, black stools, new confusion or extreme sleepiness, fever with belly pain or a swollen belly, or very little urine in someone with cirrhosis needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Laennec named cirrhosis after the tawny color of the diseased liver, not after its scarring.",
  src: ["AASLD Practice Guidance on Diagnosis, Evaluation, and Management of Ascites, SBP and Hepatorenal Syndrome (2021)", "Baveno VII consensus on portal hypertension (2022)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"hepatic-encephalopathy": {
  name: "Hepatic encephalopathy",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["HE", "portosystemic encephalopathy", "hepatic coma", "liver-related confusion"],
  what: "Hepatic encephalopathy is a change in brain function caused by liver failure or blood bypassing the liver. Toxins such as ammonia build up and cause confusion, sleepiness and in severe cases coma.",
  origin: "Ancient writers noted that people with liver disease could become confused or delirious. In 1893 researchers in Ivan Pavlov's laboratory in Russia, studying dogs with an Eck fistula that diverted portal blood around the liver, saw the animals become stuporous after meat meals, an early clue that gut-derived toxins were involved. In 1954 Sheila Sherlock and colleagues in London described portal-systemic encephalopathy in patients and linked it to protein and ammonia. In 1966 Johannes Bircher and colleagues reported that lactulose helped, and it became the backbone of treatment. In 2010 a trial led by Nathan Bass showed rifaximin lowered the risk of recurrent episodes, and it was approved that year.",
  timeline: [["1893", "Pavlov's laboratory reports meat intoxication in dogs with portal blood diverted around the liver."], ["1954", "Sherlock and colleagues describe portal-systemic encephalopathy."], ["1966", "Lactulose is first reported to treat hepatic encephalopathy."], ["2010", "Rifaximin is approved to reduce recurrence of overt hepatic encephalopathy."], ["2014", "AASLD and EASL publish a joint practice guideline."]],
  cell: "Gut bacteria break down protein and urea to ammonia, and enterocytes make ammonia from glutamine. A healthy liver removes ammonia through the urea cycle in periportal hepatocytes and glutamine synthesis in perivenous hepatocytes. In cirrhosis, lost hepatocyte function and portosystemic shunts let ammonia reach the brain. Astrocytes detoxify ammonia using glutamine synthetase to convert it and glutamate into glutamine. Glutamine builds up inside astrocytes and acts as an osmolyte, causing astrocyte swelling, and is carried into mitochondria where it releases ammonia, causing oxidative stress (the Trojan horse hypothesis). Astrocytes take on an Alzheimer type II appearance. Inflammation from infection worsens the effect of ammonia. Increased GABAergic tone from neurosteroids, altered neurotransmission and manganese deposits in the basal ganglia contribute. Alkalosis and low potassium raise ammonia, because hypokalemia increases kidney ammonia production and alkalosis favors uncharged NH3, which crosses the blood-brain barrier more easily. Common triggers are GI bleeding, infection, constipation, dehydration, electrolyte problems, sedatives and TIPS.",
  body: [["Brain", "Astrocyte swelling and altered neurotransmission cause confusion, poor attention, personality change, sleepiness and coma. In acute liver failure, brain edema can raise intracranial pressure and cause herniation."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Motor pathway dysfunction causes asterixis, a flapping tremor of the outstretched hands, slow speech and in severe cases increased tone and rigidity."], ["Gut", "Constipation, bleeding and a high protein load raise ammonia production by gut bacteria, which is why the gut is the target of treatment."], ["Kidneys", "Low potassium and alkalosis, often from diuretics, increase kidney ammonia production and worsen encephalopathy."]],
  symptoms: "Early or covert hepatic encephalopathy causes subtle problems with attention, reaction time and driving, plus reversal of the sleep-wake cycle. As ammonia and astrocyte swelling worsen, people become disoriented, drowsy and develop asterixis. Severe cases progress to stupor and coma. Breath may have a musty, sweet odor called fetor hepaticus, from sulfur compounds the liver cannot clear. Symptoms can appear suddenly after a trigger such as bleeding or infection.",
  dx: "It is a clinical diagnosis in a person with liver disease or a portosystemic shunt who has altered mental status after other causes are excluded. Severity is graded with the West Haven criteria from grade 1 (mild) to grade 4 (coma). Asterixis supports the diagnosis. Blood ammonia levels do not correlate well with severity and are not needed for diagnosis, though a normal level makes HE less likely. Workup looks for triggers: CBC, electrolytes, kidney function, glucose, infection screens including diagnostic paracentesis, and signs of GI bleeding. Brain imaging rules out bleeding or stroke when needed. Psychometric tests detect covert HE.",
  tx: "Treatment finds and fixes the trigger, such as infection, bleeding, constipation, dehydration or low potassium, and lowers ammonia. Lactulose is the first-line drug: colonic bacteria break it down into acids that turn ammonia (NH3) into ammonium (NH4+), which is trapped in the colon, and its laxative effect clears nitrogen-producing bacteria. It is given by mouth, through a nasogastric tube or as an enema. Rifaximin, a poorly absorbed antibiotic, reduces ammonia-producing gut bacteria and is added to prevent recurrence. Sedatives, especially benzodiazepines, and opioids are avoided because they deepen confusion. Protein is not restricted long term, since muscle helps clear ammonia; small frequent meals and a late evening snack are encouraged. Airway protection is needed for deep coma. Recurrent HE is an indication to consider liver transplant.",
  drugs: ["lactulose", "rifaximin", "potassium-chloride", "normal-saline", "zinc", "thiamine", "polyethylene-glycol"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Lactulose traps ammonia in the colon by acidifying it (NH3 to NH4+), and rifaximin is added to prevent recurrence."], ["Memory trick", "Triggers are the HEPATIC list: Hemorrhage, Electrolytes (low K), Protein load, Alkalosis, Toxins (sedatives), Infection, Constipation."], ["Classic sign", "Asterixis, a flapping tremor when the wrists are held extended."], ["Pearl", "Blood ammonia levels do not track severity well; the diagnosis is clinical."]],
  red: "New confusion, extreme sleepiness, inability to stay awake, vomiting blood or black stools, or fever in someone with liver disease needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Fetor hepaticus, the musty breath of liver failure, comes from dimethyl sulfide and related compounds that a failing liver cannot clear.",
  src: ["AASLD/EASL Practice Guideline: Hepatic Encephalopathy in Chronic Liver Disease (2014)", "Bass NM et al. Rifaximin treatment in hepatic encephalopathy. N Engl J Med 2010", "Sherlock S et al. Lancet 1954", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"hepatitis-b": {
  name: "Hepatitis B",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["HBV", "hepatitis B virus infection", "chronic hepatitis B", "serum hepatitis (old term)"],
  what: "Hepatitis B is a liver infection caused by the hepatitis B virus, spread through blood, sex and from mother to baby at birth. It can clear on its own or become chronic, leading to cirrhosis and liver cancer.",
  origin: "Outbreaks of jaundice after shared needles and blood products were described in the late 1800s and early 1900s, and the illness was called serum hepatitis. In the 1960s Baruch Blumberg, studying blood proteins around the world, found an antigen in the blood of an Aboriginal Australian man and named it the Australia antigen, published in 1965. It turned out to be the surface protein of the hepatitis B virus, and Blumberg shared the 1976 Nobel Prize. In 1970 David Dane saw the full virus particle under the electron microscope. A plasma-derived vaccine was approved in 1981, followed by a recombinant yeast-made vaccine in 1986. Hepatitis B vaccines later became the first vaccines shown to prevent a human cancer. Antiviral pills such as lamivudine (1998), entecavir (2005) and tenofovir (2008) now suppress the virus.",
  timeline: [["1965", "Baruch Blumberg reports the Australia antigen, later shown to be hepatitis B surface antigen."], ["1970", "David Dane describes the complete virus particle."], ["1976", "Blumberg shares the Nobel Prize for this discovery."], ["1981", "The first hepatitis B vaccine is approved in the US."], ["1986", "A recombinant hepatitis B vaccine is approved."], ["2005", "Entecavir is approved for chronic hepatitis B."]],
  cell: "Hepatitis B virus is a small, partially double-stranded DNA virus in the hepadnavirus family. It binds hepatocytes through the bile acid transporter NTCP and delivers its genome to the nucleus, where it is repaired into covalently closed circular DNA (cccDNA), a stable template that current drugs cannot remove. cccDNA is transcribed into RNAs, including pregenomic RNA, which the viral polymerase reverse transcribes into new DNA inside capsids. The virus itself is not very toxic to cells; liver injury comes mainly from cytotoxic CD8 T cells attacking infected hepatocytes. A strong immune response in adults usually clears infection, while newborns, whose immune systems are tolerant, become chronically infected in about 90 percent of cases. HBV DNA can integrate into the host genome, and the HBx protein affects cell signaling, so HBV can cause hepatocellular carcinoma even without cirrhosis. Immune complexes of viral antigen and antibody can deposit in vessels and kidneys. Hepatitis D virus needs HBsAg to replicate and makes disease more severe.",
  body: [["Liver", "Acute infection causes inflammation and jaundice and rarely acute liver failure. Chronic infection causes ongoing inflammation that leads to fibrosis, cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma."], ["Liver cancer", "HBV can cause liver cancer even in a non-cirrhotic liver because viral DNA integrates into the host genome."], ["Kidneys", "Immune complexes can cause membranous nephropathy and other glomerular diseases."], ["Blood vessels", "Immune complex deposits can cause polyarteritis nodosa, a medium-vessel vasculitis."], ["Joints and skin", "Early in acute infection, immune complexes can cause a serum sickness-like illness with rash and joint pain."]],
  symptoms: "Acute hepatitis B often begins with fatigue, nausea, poor appetite and aching joints, followed by jaundice, dark urine and pale stools as inflamed liver cells stop handling bilirubin. Right upper belly pain comes from a swollen liver stretching its capsule. Many infections, especially in children, cause no symptoms. Chronic hepatitis B is usually silent for years until cirrhosis or liver cancer develops, which is why screening matters.",
  dx: "Diagnosis relies on blood tests for viral antigens and antibodies. HBsAg means current infection; it lasting more than six months defines chronic infection. IgM anti-HBc marks recent acute infection. Anti-HBs alone means immunity from vaccine, while anti-HBs with anti-HBc means past infection that cleared. HBeAg and HBV DNA levels show how actively the virus is replicating. ALT, liver function tests and fibrosis assessment by elastography guide treatment. CDC recommends testing all adults at least once with a triple panel (HBsAg, anti-HBs and total anti-HBc).",
  tx: "Most acute infections in adults clear with supportive care. Chronic hepatitis B with active replication, inflammation, significant fibrosis or cirrhosis is treated with nucleoside or nucleotide analogs, mainly entecavir and tenofovir (TDF or TAF). These drugs block the viral polymerase so it cannot reverse transcribe new DNA, lowering virus levels and liver damage and cutting cancer risk, but they rarely remove cccDNA, so treatment is usually long term. Peginterferon alfa boosts immune responses for a set course in selected patients. Lamivudine is older and resistance is common. People with HBV who receive chemotherapy or B-cell depleting drugs such as rituximab get antiviral prophylaxis to prevent reactivation. Prevention uses hepatitis B vaccine, which the CDC recommends for all infants and adults up to 59 and for older adults at risk. Babies born to infected mothers receive vaccine and hepatitis B immune globulin soon after birth, and mothers with high viral loads may receive tenofovir late in pregnancy. Liver cancer surveillance continues in at-risk patients.",
  drugs: ["entecavir", "tenofovir", "peginterferon-alfa-2a", "lamivudine", "hepatitis-b-vaccine", "hepatitis-b-immune-globulin", "emtricitabine-tenofovir-disoproxil"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "HBsAg means current infection, anti-HBs means immunity, and IgM anti-HBc is the marker during the window period of acute infection."], ["Memory trick", "Age at infection decides chronicity: about 90 percent of infected newborns but under 5 percent of infected adults develop chronic infection."], ["Unique fact", "HBV is a DNA virus that uses reverse transcriptase, which is why some HIV drugs such as tenofovir also treat it."], ["Associations", "Polyarteritis nodosa and membranous nephropathy are classic extrahepatic links."]],
  red: "Deep jaundice, confusion, extreme sleepiness, easy bleeding or bruising, vomiting blood or a swollen belly in someone with hepatitis can mean liver failure and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The hepatitis B vaccine is often called the first anti-cancer vaccine, because preventing the infection prevents many cases of liver cancer.",
  src: ["AASLD Guidance: Update on Prevention, Diagnosis, and Treatment of Chronic Hepatitis B (2018)", "CDC Recommendations for Hepatitis B Screening and Testing (2023)", "Blumberg BS et al. JAMA 1965", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"hepatitis-c": {
  name: "Hepatitis C",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["HCV", "hepatitis C virus infection", "non-A, non-B hepatitis (old term)"],
  what: "Hepatitis C is a liver infection caused by the hepatitis C virus, spread mainly through blood, such as shared drug equipment or past transfusions. It is often silent for years but is now curable with pills in most people.",
  origin: "After tests for hepatitis A and B became available in the early 1970s, Harvey Alter and colleagues at the US National Institutes of Health found that many cases of hepatitis after blood transfusion were caused by neither. They called it non-A, non-B hepatitis and showed it could be passed to chimpanzees. In 1989 Michael Houghton and coworkers at the company Chiron, working with CDC scientist Daniel Bradley, cloned the virus's genetic material and named it hepatitis C. In 1997 Charles Rice showed that the cloned virus alone could cause disease. Alter, Houghton and Rice received the 2020 Nobel Prize. Treatment moved from interferon (1991) and ribavirin combinations to direct-acting antiviral pills starting with sofosbuvir in 2013, with cure rates above 95 percent.",
  timeline: [["1975", "Non-A, non-B hepatitis is described after blood transfusions."], ["1989", "The hepatitis C virus is identified and named."], ["1992", "Improved blood donor screening for hepatitis C begins in the US."], ["2013", "Sofosbuvir, a key direct-acting antiviral, is approved."], ["2020", "CDC recommends hepatitis C screening for all adults; Alter, Houghton and Rice win the Nobel Prize."]],
  cell: "Hepatitis C virus is a positive-sense, single-stranded RNA virus in the Flaviviridae family (genus Hepacivirus). It circulates attached to lipoproteins and enters hepatocytes using receptors including CD81, scavenger receptor B1, claudin-1 and occludin. Its RNA is translated into one polyprotein that is cut by host and viral proteases, including the NS3/4A protease. NS5A organizes the replication complex and NS5B is the RNA-dependent RNA polymerase. The polymerase has no proofreading, so the virus mutates quickly, especially in the envelope proteins E1 and E2, which helps it escape antibodies and has blocked vaccine development. The virus also blocks interferon signaling, so about 75 to 85 percent of infections become chronic. Liver damage comes mainly from immune attack by cytotoxic T cells, causing chronic inflammation, steatosis and stellate cell activation that leads to fibrosis and cirrhosis over decades, plus hepatocellular carcinoma. HCV also stimulates B cells, causing mixed cryoglobulinemia. Because the virus has no DNA stage, it can be eradicated, which is the basis for cure.",
  body: [["Liver", "Chronic inflammation slowly causes fibrosis, cirrhosis and liver cancer, usually over 20 to 30 years, faster with alcohol, HIV or fatty liver."], ["Blood vessels", "Mixed cryoglobulinemia causes vasculitis with purple skin spots, joint pain and nerve damage."], ["Kidneys", "Cryoglobulin deposits cause membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis."], ["Skin", "HCV is linked to porphyria cutanea tarda, with blisters on sun-exposed skin, and lichen planus."], ["Immune system", "Chronic B-cell stimulation raises the risk of non-Hodgkin lymphoma."], ["Metabolism", "HCV is linked to insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes."]],
  symptoms: "Most new infections cause no symptoms, so many people do not know they are infected. When acute symptoms occur, fatigue, nausea, dark urine and jaundice come from liver inflammation. Chronic infection often causes only fatigue for years, and symptoms appear later from cirrhosis, such as belly swelling, easy bruising and confusion. Rash, joint pain or kidney problems can appear from cryoglobulins.",
  dx: "Screening starts with an HCV antibody test. A positive antibody test is followed by an HCV RNA test, which confirms current infection, since about a quarter of people clear the virus but keep antibodies. Before treatment, clinicians assess liver fibrosis with FIB-4, elastography or other tests, check for hepatitis B and HIV, and review drug interactions. Genotype testing is less important with pan-genotypic regimens. Cure is defined as sustained virologic response, undetectable HCV RNA 12 weeks after finishing therapy (SVR12).",
  tx: "Direct-acting antivirals (DAAs) cure more than 95 percent of patients with a short course of pills. Combinations target different steps: NS5B polymerase inhibitors such as sofosbuvir act as chain terminators, NS5A inhibitors such as velpatasvir, ledipasvir and pibrentasvir block replication complex assembly, and NS3/4A protease inhibitors such as glecaprevir, grazoprevir and voxilaprevir stop polyprotein cutting. Pan-genotypic regimens such as sofosbuvir-velpatasvir and glecaprevir-pibrentasvir are standard first choices. Ribavirin is added in some cases, such as decompensated cirrhosis. Interferon is now largely historical. Hepatitis B can flare during DAA therapy, so patients are screened first. People with cirrhosis continue liver cancer surveillance after cure. Reinfection is possible, and harm reduction such as sterile injection equipment and treatment of substance use disorder (SAMHSA helpline 1-800-662-4357) are part of care.",
  drugs: ["sofosbuvir-velpatasvir", "glecaprevir-pibrentasvir", "ledipasvir-sofosbuvir", "sofosbuvir-velpatasvir-voxilaprevir", "elbasvir-grazoprevir", "sofosbuvir", "ribavirin", "peginterferon-alfa-2a"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "HCV is an RNA flavivirus whose envelope mutates quickly, so there is no vaccine, but DAAs cure more than 95 percent of infections."], ["Memory trick", "Drug names show their target: -previr = protease (NS3/4A), -asvir = NS5A, -buvir = NS5B polymerase."], ["Test order", "Antibody first, then HCV RNA to confirm current infection."], ["Associations", "Mixed cryoglobulinemia, membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis and porphyria cutanea tarda."]],
  red: "Deep jaundice, confusion, vomiting blood, black stools or a rapidly swelling belly in someone with hepatitis C or cirrhosis needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Hepatitis C was the first virus discovered purely by molecular cloning, before anyone had seen the virus itself.",
  src: ["AASLD-IDSA HCV Guidance: Recommendations for Testing, Managing, and Treating Hepatitis C", "CDC Recommendations for Hepatitis C Screening Among Adults (2020)", "Choo QL et al. Science 1989", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"fatty-liver": {
  name: "Fatty liver disease (MASLD)",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease", "MASLD", "MASH", "NAFLD (old term)", "NASH (old term)", "steatotic liver disease"],
  what: "MASLD is a buildup of fat in the liver linked to obesity, type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure or abnormal cholesterol. In some people it causes inflammation (MASH) and scarring that can lead to cirrhosis.",
  origin: "Pathologists long noticed fatty livers in people who did not drink heavily but looked like alcohol-related liver disease. In 1980 Jurgen Ludwig and colleagues at the Mayo Clinic described 20 such patients and coined the term nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH). As obesity and diabetes rose, it became the most common chronic liver disease worldwide. In 2008 researchers found that a variant of the PNPLA3 gene strongly raises risk. In 2023 international liver societies renamed the condition metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) and its inflammatory form MASH, to describe the cause and drop stigmatizing language. In 2024 resmetirom became the first FDA-approved drug for MASH with liver scarring, and in 2025 semaglutide was also approved for this use.",
  timeline: [["1980", "Ludwig and colleagues at the Mayo Clinic coin the term NASH."], ["2008", "A PNPLA3 gene variant is linked to fatty liver disease."], ["2023", "Liver societies adopt the names MASLD and MASH."], ["2024", "Resmetirom becomes the first FDA-approved drug for MASH with fibrosis."], ["2025", "Semaglutide receives FDA approval for MASH with moderate to advanced fibrosis."]],
  cell: "Insulin resistance is central. Resistant fat tissue keeps releasing free fatty acids through lipolysis, and high insulin drives de novo lipogenesis in hepatocytes through SREBP-1c, while dietary fructose drives it through ChREBP. When fat delivery and synthesis exceed fat burning and export as VLDL, triglycerides collect as large droplets (macrovesicular steatosis). Triglyceride itself is fairly harmless, but toxic lipids such as diacylglycerols, ceramides, saturated fatty acids and free cholesterol cause lipotoxicity: endoplasmic reticulum stress, mitochondrial dysfunction and reactive oxygen species. Injured hepatocytes balloon, form Mallory-Denk bodies and die, releasing danger signals that activate Kupffer cells and inflammasomes. Gut-derived bacterial products add to inflammation. Cytokines and TGF-beta activate hepatic stellate cells, which lay down collagen, first around central veins and sinusoids (chicken-wire fibrosis), then bridging toward cirrhosis. Genes such as PNPLA3 and TM6SF2 impair lipid handling and raise risk. Fibrosis stage, not fat amount, best predicts liver outcomes.",
  body: [["Liver", "Fat buildup can progress to MASH with inflammation and fibrosis, then cirrhosis, liver failure and hepatocellular carcinoma, which can occur even before cirrhosis."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death in MASLD, driven by shared insulin resistance, atherogenic lipids and inflammation."], ["Metabolism", "MASLD worsens insulin resistance and is strongly tied to type 2 diabetes, and having both raises the risk of advanced fibrosis."], ["Kidneys", "MASLD is linked to a higher risk of chronic kidney disease."]],
  symptoms: "Most people have no symptoms until late disease, because fat and early inflammation do not cause pain. Some feel fatigue or a vague ache in the right upper belly from a stretched liver capsule. The condition is often found when routine blood tests show raised ALT or imaging shows a bright, fatty liver. Advanced fibrosis and cirrhosis cause the signs of liver failure, such as jaundice, ascites and confusion.",
  dx: "MASLD is diagnosed when imaging or biopsy shows liver fat and the person has at least one cardiometabolic risk factor, such as overweight, high glucose, high blood pressure or abnormal lipids, without another main cause. Heavier alcohol use with metabolic risk is called MetALD. The key step is estimating fibrosis: the FIB-4 score from age, ALT, AST and platelets, followed by vibration-controlled transient elastography (FibroScan) or the ELF blood test when FIB-4 is not low. Liver biopsy is reserved for unclear cases and shows steatosis, ballooning, inflammation and fibrosis stage.",
  tx: "Weight loss is the most effective treatment: losing about 5 percent of body weight reduces liver fat, and about 7 to 10 percent can improve inflammation and fibrosis. A healthy diet such as a Mediterranean pattern, regular exercise, limiting alcohol and managing diabetes, blood pressure and lipids are core care. Resmetirom activates the thyroid hormone receptor-beta in the liver to increase fat burning and is approved for noncirrhotic MASH with moderate to advanced fibrosis. GLP-1 receptor agonists such as semaglutide lower weight and insulin resistance and improve MASH; semaglutide is approved for MASH with fibrosis. Pioglitazone improves insulin sensitivity through PPAR-gamma and helps MASH, especially with diabetes. Vitamin E, an antioxidant, is used in some adults without diabetes. Statins are safe and lower cardiovascular risk. Bariatric surgery helps people with severe obesity. Cirrhosis care includes liver cancer surveillance and transplant evaluation.",
  drugs: ["resmetirom", "semaglutide", "tirzepatide", "pioglitazone", "vitamin-e", "atorvastatin", "rosuvastatin", "metformin", "empagliflozin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Fibrosis stage, not the amount of fat, best predicts outcomes; cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death."], ["New names", "NAFLD is now MASLD, NASH is now MASH, and metabolic risk plus heavier drinking is MetALD."], ["Memory trick", "FIB-4 first, FibroScan next: use the simple score, then elastography if the score is not low."], ["Lab clue", "ALT is usually higher than AST in MASLD, while AST higher than ALT (often 2 to 1) suggests alcohol-related liver disease."]],
  red: "Vomiting blood, black stools, new confusion, yellow skin or eyes, or a rapidly swelling belly in someone with liver disease needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "MASLD affects roughly one in three adults worldwide, making it the most common chronic liver disease.",
  src: ["AASLD Practice Guidance on the Clinical Assessment and Management of NAFLD (2023)", "Rinella ME et al. Multisociety Delphi consensus statement on new fatty liver disease nomenclature (2023)", "Ludwig J et al. Mayo Clin Proc 1980", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"pancreatitis": {
  name: "Pancreatitis",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["acute pancreatitis", "chronic pancreatitis", "gallstone pancreatitis", "alcohol-associated pancreatitis"],
  what: "Pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas, the gland behind the stomach that makes digestive enzymes and insulin. In acute pancreatitis the enzymes switch on too early and start digesting the pancreas itself, causing severe belly pain.",
  origin: "In 1889 Boston pathologist Reginald Fitz published a detailed account of acute pancreatitis, describing its forms and suggesting that early surgery was harmful. Early 1900s work by Eugene Opie linked gallstones blocking the outlet of the pancreatic duct to the disease. In 1974 John Ranson published criteria to predict severity. Lipase and amylase blood tests and later CT scanning made diagnosis faster. The Atlanta classification of 1992, revised in 2012, gave a shared language for mild, moderately severe and severe disease. Care shifted from routine surgery and starvation to fluids, early feeding and a step-up approach to infected necrosis, and in 2022 the WATERFALL trial showed that moderate fluids were safer than aggressive fluids.",
  timeline: [["1889", "Reginald Fitz describes acute pancreatitis in detail."], ["1901", "Eugene Opie links gallstones to pancreatitis."], ["1974", "Ranson criteria for severity are published."], ["2012", "The revised Atlanta classification is published."], ["2022", "The WATERFALL trial favors moderate over aggressive fluid resuscitation."]],
  cell: "Pancreatic acinar cells make digestive enzymes as inactive precursors such as trypsinogen, stored in zymogen granules and normally activated only in the duodenum by enteropeptidase. Protection includes SPINK1, a trypsin inhibitor. In pancreatitis, a trigger such as gallstone obstruction of the ampulla, alcohol, very high triglycerides, high calcium, ERCP or certain drugs causes abnormal calcium signaling inside acinar cells. Zymogen granules fuse with lysosomes, and cathepsin B activates trypsinogen to trypsin inside the cell. Trypsin activates other enzymes: elastase damages blood vessels and causes bleeding, phospholipase A2 injures cell membranes, and lipase breaks down fat around the pancreas, releasing fatty acids that bind calcium (fat saponification). Injured cells activate NF-kappaB and release cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6 and TNF-alpha, recruiting neutrophils and macrophages. This can spread into a systemic inflammatory response with leaky capillaries, third spacing of fluid and organ failure. Genetic variants in PRSS1, SPINK1 and CFTR raise risk. Repeated injury activates pancreatic stellate cells, causing fibrosis, calcification and the loss of exocrine and endocrine function seen in chronic pancreatitis.",
  body: [["Pancreas", "Enzyme injury causes swelling (interstitial edematous pancreatitis) or tissue death (necrotizing pancreatitis), which can become infected. Fluid collections and pseudocysts can form."], ["Circulation", "Leaky capillaries shift large amounts of fluid out of the blood, causing low blood pressure, fast heart rate and shock."], ["Lungs", "Inflammatory mediators and phospholipase injure the lungs, causing pleural effusions and acute respiratory distress syndrome."], ["Kidneys", "Low blood volume and inflammation cause acute kidney injury."], ["Calcium and fat", "Fat necrosis binds calcium, causing low blood calcium; bleeding into tissues can cause bruising at the flanks or navel."], ["Digestion and blood sugar", "Chronic pancreatitis destroys acinar and islet cells, causing fatty stools, weight loss and diabetes."]],
  symptoms: "Severe, steady upper belly pain that often bores through to the back happens because the inflamed pancreas lies against the back wall of the abdomen and its nerves. Leaning forward may ease it. Nausea and vomiting come from inflammation and slowed bowel movement (ileus). Fever, fast heart rate and low blood pressure reflect systemic inflammation and fluid loss. Flank bruising (Grey Turner sign) or navel bruising (Cullen sign) points to bleeding in severe disease. In chronic pancreatitis, oily stools and weight loss come from enzyme loss, and diabetes from islet loss.",
  dx: "Acute pancreatitis is diagnosed when two of three features are present: typical upper belly pain, serum lipase (or amylase) at least three times the upper limit of normal, and imaging findings. Contrast CT or MRI is used when the diagnosis is unclear or the patient does not improve, not routinely at first. Ultrasound looks for gallstones. Triglycerides and calcium are checked. Severity is judged by organ failure lasting more than 48 hours, with tools such as BUN trends, hematocrit and the BISAP score. Chronic pancreatitis is shown by calcifications and duct changes on CT or MRCP and low fecal elastase.",
  tx: "Acute pancreatitis is treated with supportive care. Goal-directed IV fluids, often lactated Ringer's, restore blood volume while avoiding overload. Pain control uses opioids and other analgesics as needed. Early oral feeding as tolerated, rather than prolonged fasting, protects the gut barrier, with tube feeding if oral intake fails. Antiemetics such as ondansetron ease vomiting. Antibiotics are not given to prevent infection; they are used for infected necrosis or another infection, with carbapenems among the options. ERCP removes stones in patients with cholangitis. Cholecystectomy during the same admission prevents recurrence in mild gallstone pancreatitis. Infected necrosis uses a step-up approach with drainage and minimally invasive removal of dead tissue. Very high triglycerides are lowered with IV insulin or plasmapheresis, and alcohol cessation support helps prevent recurrence. Chronic pancreatitis is treated with pancreatic enzyme replacement (pancrelipase), pain management and diabetes care.",
  drugs: ["lactated-ringers", "normal-saline", "hydromorphone", "morphine", "fentanyl", "acetaminophen", "ondansetron", "meropenem", "imipenem-cilastatin", "regular-insulin", "pancrelipase", "fenofibrate", "indomethacin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Causes are I GET SMASHED: Idiopathic, Gallstones, Ethanol, Trauma, Steroids, Mumps, Autoimmune, Scorpion sting, Hypercalcemia or high triglycerides, ERCP, Drugs."], ["Most-tested fact", "Gallstones and alcohol are the two most common causes; diagnosis needs two of three: pain, lipase at least three times normal, imaging."], ["Pearl", "Rectal indomethacin is used to lower the risk of post-ERCP pancreatitis."], ["Often confused with", "A perforated ulcer can also raise lipase; free air on imaging points to perforation."]],
  red: "Severe belly pain spreading to the back with vomiting, fever, fainting, a fast heartbeat, trouble breathing or yellow skin needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Scorpion stings, notably from a species in Trinidad, can cause pancreatitis by overstimulating the nerves that drive enzyme secretion.",
  src: ["ACG Guideline: Management of Acute Pancreatitis (2024)", "Banks PA et al. Revised Atlanta Classification. Gut 2013", "de-Madaria E et al. WATERFALL trial. N Engl J Med 2022", "Fitz RH. Boston Med Surg J 1889"]
},
"cholecystitis": {
  name: "Gallstones and cholecystitis",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["cholelithiasis", "gallbladder attack", "biliary colic", "acute cholecystitis", "choledocholithiasis", "cholangitis"],
  what: "Gallstones are hard pieces that form in the gallbladder from bile. When a stone blocks the gallbladder's outlet, it causes pain called biliary colic, and if the blockage lasts, the gallbladder becomes inflamed and infected (cholecystitis).",
  origin: "Gallstones have been found in ancient Egyptian mummies, and physicians since antiquity described their pain. In 1877 French neurologist Jean-Martin Charcot described the triad of fever, jaundice and right upper belly pain in bile duct infection. In 1882 German surgeon Carl Langenbuch performed the first planned removal of the gallbladder (cholecystectomy) in Berlin. In 1959 Benedict Reynolds and Everett Dargan added confusion and shock to describe severe cholangitis. ERCP, first reported in 1968, allowed stones in the bile duct to be removed without open surgery. In 1985 Erich Muhe in Germany performed a laparoscopic cholecystectomy, and in 1987 Philippe Mouret in France helped make the video-guided keyhole operation the standard.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Gallstones are found in ancient Egyptian mummies."], ["1877", "Charcot describes the triad of biliary infection."], ["1882", "Carl Langenbuch performs the first cholecystectomy."], ["1968", "ERCP is first reported."], ["1985", "Erich Muhe performs an early laparoscopic cholecystectomy."], ["2018", "The Tokyo Guidelines are updated for acute cholecystitis and cholangitis."]],
  cell: "Bile is made of water, bile salts, phospholipids (lecithin), cholesterol and bilirubin. Cholesterol stays dissolved only in mixed micelles with bile salts and lecithin. Cholesterol stones, the most common type in the US, form when the liver secretes too much cholesterol (obesity, estrogen, pregnancy, rapid weight loss) or too few bile salts, so bile becomes supersaturated. Mucin and proteins promote nucleation of cholesterol crystals, and a sluggish gallbladder (pregnancy, fasting, total parenteral nutrition) lets crystals grow. Black pigment stones come from excess unconjugated bilirubin in chronic hemolysis or cirrhosis. Brown pigment stones form in infected ducts, where bacterial beta-glucuronidase deconjugates bilirubin. Acute cholecystitis begins when a stone blocks the cystic duct for hours. The gallbladder distends, phospholipase releases lysolecithin that injures the lining, prostaglandins drive inflammation, and wall pressure reduces blood flow. Bacteria such as E. coli, Klebsiella and Enterococcus often infect it secondarily, and severe cases cause gangrene or perforation. Acalculous cholecystitis occurs without stones in critically ill patients from stasis and ischemia.",
  body: [["Gallbladder", "Stone blockage causes colic, then inflammation, infection, gangrene, perforation or abscess if it persists."], ["Bile ducts", "A stone that passes into the common bile duct (choledocholithiasis) blocks bile flow, causing jaundice and, if infected, ascending cholangitis, which can lead to sepsis."], ["Pancreas", "A stone blocking the shared outlet at the ampulla can trigger gallstone pancreatitis."], ["Intestine", "A large stone can erode into the bowel through a fistula and block the small intestine (gallstone ileus)."], ["Whole body", "Infection can spread to the blood, causing fever, low blood pressure and confusion."]],
  symptoms: "Biliary colic is steady pain in the right upper or middle upper belly after a fatty meal, because fat triggers cholecystokinin, which contracts the gallbladder against a blocked outlet; it eases within a few hours when the stone falls back. Pain can spread to the right shoulder blade through shared nerve pathways. Cholecystitis causes pain lasting more than about six hours with fever and tenderness, because the wall is inflamed and touching the parietal peritoneum. Murphy sign is a catch in breathing when the inflamed gallbladder meets the examiner's hand. Jaundice, dark urine and pale stools mean a stone is blocking the common bile duct.",
  dx: "Ultrasound is the first test and shows stones, gallbladder wall thickening, fluid around the gallbladder and a sonographic Murphy sign. Labs include white blood cell count, liver tests (bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase rise with duct blockage) and lipase. A HIDA scan that does not fill the gallbladder confirms cystic duct obstruction when ultrasound is unclear. MRCP or endoscopic ultrasound looks for common duct stones, and ERCP both diagnoses and removes them. Tokyo Guidelines criteria grade the severity of cholecystitis and cholangitis.",
  tx: "Gallstones that cause no symptoms are usually left alone. Biliary colic is treated with pain control, often NSAIDs such as ketorolac, and planned laparoscopic cholecystectomy. Acute cholecystitis is treated with IV fluids, pain control, antibiotics covering gut bacteria (such as ceftriaxone with metronidazole, or piperacillin-tazobactam in severe cases) and early laparoscopic cholecystectomy, ideally within a few days. Patients too sick for surgery may have a drainage tube placed through the skin (percutaneous cholecystostomy). Cholangitis needs antibiotics and urgent biliary drainage by ERCP. Ursodiol lowers bile cholesterol and can dissolve small cholesterol stones in people who cannot have surgery, or prevent stones during rapid weight loss, but stones often return after stopping.",
  drugs: ["ketorolac", "ibuprofen", "morphine", "hydromorphone", "ceftriaxone", "metronidazole", "piperacillin-tazobactam", "ampicillin-sulbactam", "ertapenem", "ondansetron", "ursodiol", "lactated-ringers"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Risk factors are the 5 Fs: fat, female, forty, fertile and fair (the last is outdated), plus rapid weight loss."], ["Charcot vs Reynolds", "Charcot triad: fever, jaundice, right upper quadrant pain. Reynolds pentad adds confusion and low blood pressure."], ["Most-tested fact", "Ultrasound is the first test; a HIDA scan that does not show the gallbladder confirms acute cholecystitis."], ["Often confused with", "Biliary colic lasts less than about six hours with no fever; cholecystitis lasts longer with fever and inflammation."]],
  red: "Belly pain with fever and chills, yellow skin or eyes, confusion, fainting or pain that will not ease after several hours can mean cholecystitis or cholangitis and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Carl Langenbuch practiced the first gallbladder removal on human cadavers before he operated on a living patient, a man who had suffered gallstone attacks for years.",
  src: ["Tokyo Guidelines 2018 (TG18) for acute cholecystitis and cholangitis", "SAGES Guidelines for the Clinical Application of Laparoscopic Biliary Tract Surgery", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"appendicitis": {
  name: "Appendicitis",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["acute appendicitis", "inflamed appendix", "ruptured appendix", "perforated appendicitis"],
  what: "Appendicitis is inflammation of the appendix, a small finger-shaped pouch off the large intestine. It usually starts when the opening is blocked, and without treatment the appendix can burst and infect the belly.",
  origin: "For centuries, right-sided belly infections were blamed on the cecum and called perityphlitis. In 1735 surgeon Claudius Amyand in London removed an inflamed, perforated appendix found inside a hernia, often cited as the first recorded appendectomy. In 1886 Harvard pathologist Reginald Fitz showed that the appendix itself was the usual source of these infections, named the disease appendicitis and urged early surgery. In 1889 New York surgeon Charles McBurney described the point of maximum tenderness that bears his name. Appendectomy became one of the most common emergency operations. The Alvarado score was published in 1986, CT improved diagnosis in the 1990s, laparoscopic appendectomy became standard, and in 2020 the CODA trial showed that antibiotics alone are an option for some patients.",
  timeline: [["1735", "Claudius Amyand removes a perforated appendix found in a hernia."], ["1886", "Reginald Fitz names appendicitis and recommends early surgery."], ["1889", "Charles McBurney describes McBurney point."], ["1986", "The Alvarado score is published."], ["2020", "The CODA trial compares antibiotics with appendectomy."]],
  cell: "Most cases begin with blockage of the appendix lumen by a fecalith (appendicolith), swollen lymphoid tissue after infection (more common in children and young adults), or less often a tumor or parasite. The appendix lining keeps secreting mucus, so pressure rises in a closed space. Rising pressure first blocks lymph and venous outflow, causing swelling and congestion, then compromises arterial flow, causing ischemia of the wall. Bacteria trapped in the lumen, such as E. coli and Bacteroides fragilis, multiply and invade the injured wall, recruiting neutrophils (the key histologic finding is neutrophils in the muscularis propria). Inflammation spreads through the full wall to the serosa and parietal peritoneum. Without treatment the wall becomes gangrenous and can perforate, often within 24 to 72 hours of symptom onset, causing localized abscess or generalized peritonitis.",
  body: [["Appendix", "Blockage, swelling and infection lead to ischemia, gangrene and eventually rupture."], ["Peritoneum", "Inflammation reaching the belly lining causes sharp localized pain; perforation causes abscess or spreading peritonitis."], ["Whole body", "Infection triggers fever and a rise in white blood cells, and perforation can cause sepsis."], ["Gut", "Inflammation slows the bowel, causing loss of appetite, nausea and vomiting."], ["Pregnancy", "The growing uterus pushes the appendix upward, so pain can be higher in the belly and diagnosis is harder."]],
  symptoms: "Pain usually starts as a dull ache around the navel, because stretch of the appendix is carried by visceral nerves entering the spinal cord at about T10, which also serve the skin around the navel. Over hours the pain moves to the right lower belly and becomes sharp, because the inflamed appendix now irritates the parietal peritoneum, which has precise somatic nerves. Loss of appetite is common and usually comes before vomiting. Fever and pain with movement or coughing reflect peritoneal irritation. A retrocecal or pelvic appendix can cause atypical pain in the flank or pelvis.",
  dx: "Diagnosis combines the history and exam with labs and imaging. Signs include tenderness at McBurney point, rebound tenderness, Rovsing sign (pain on the right when the left lower belly is pressed), the psoas sign and the obturator sign. The white blood cell count and CRP are often raised. Scores such as Alvarado or the Appendicitis Inflammatory Response score estimate risk. CT is the most accurate test in adults. Ultrasound is preferred first in children and in pregnancy, with MRI in pregnancy when ultrasound is unclear. A pregnancy test is checked in people who could be pregnant.",
  tx: "The standard treatment is removing the appendix (appendectomy), usually by laparoscopy. Antibiotics covering gut gram-negative and anaerobic bacteria, such as cefoxitin, ceftriaxone with metronidazole, or piperacillin-tazobactam, are given before surgery and continued after perforation. Pain control, IV fluids and antiemetics support the patient. For uncomplicated appendicitis without an appendicolith, antibiotics alone are an option for selected adults, though some patients later need surgery. A perforated appendix with a walled-off abscess may be treated first with antibiotics and drainage through the skin, with surgery later.",
  drugs: ["cefoxitin", "ceftriaxone", "metronidazole", "piperacillin-tazobactam", "ertapenem", "ampicillin-sulbactam", "morphine", "ketorolac", "acetaminophen", "ondansetron", "lactated-ringers"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Pain migrates from the navel to McBurney point, about one third of the way from the right anterior superior iliac spine to the navel."], ["Most-tested fact", "Appendicitis is the most common surgical emergency of the abdomen, especially in children and young adults."], ["Signs", "Rovsing (left pressure, right pain), psoas (pain with hip extension) and obturator (pain with internal rotation of the hip)."], ["Often confused with", "Ectopic pregnancy, ovarian torsion and mesenteric adenitis can mimic appendicitis."]],
  red: "Belly pain that moves to the right lower side and worsens, especially with fever, vomiting, a hard belly or pain with walking or coughing, needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The appendix is not useless: it contains lymphoid tissue and may act as a safe house for helpful gut bacteria that can repopulate the colon after diarrhea.",
  src: ["WSES Jerusalem Guidelines on Diagnosis and Treatment of Acute Appendicitis (2020)", "CODA Collaborative. N Engl J Med 2020", "Fitz RH. Perforating inflammation of the vermiform appendix. 1886", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"diverticulitis": {
  name: "Diverticulitis",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["diverticular disease", "diverticulosis", "colonic diverticulitis", "left-sided appendicitis (nickname)"],
  what: "Diverticula are small pouches that bulge out through weak spots in the colon wall, which is very common with age (diverticulosis). Diverticulitis is when one of these pouches becomes inflamed or infected, usually causing left lower belly pain.",
  origin: "Pathologists in the 1800s described pouches in the colon wall, but they were thought rare. In the 1900s diverticular disease became common in Western countries. In 1971 Neil Painter and Denis Burkitt proposed that the disease was a result of low-fiber Western diets, which raise pressure inside the colon. In 1978 Edward Hinchey and colleagues published a system for grading perforated diverticulitis that is still used. CT scanning in the 1980s and 1990s became the main diagnostic test. Care then moved away from routine surgery after a second attack and, after trials such as AVOD (2012) and DIABOLO (2017), away from automatic antibiotics for mild uncomplicated disease.",
  timeline: [["1971", "Painter and Burkitt propose low dietary fiber as a cause of diverticular disease."], ["1978", "The Hinchey classification of perforated diverticulitis is published."], ["2008", "A large study finds nuts, corn and popcorn do not raise diverticulitis risk."], ["2012", "The AVOD trial shows antibiotics may not be needed in mild uncomplicated diverticulitis."], ["2017", "The DIABOLO trial supports observation without antibiotics for mild cases."]],
  cell: "Colonic diverticula are false diverticula: only the mucosa and submucosa push out, through weak points in the muscle layer where the vasa recta arteries pierce the wall. They form most often in the sigmoid colon in Western populations, where the bowel is narrowest and segmental contractions create high pressure. Low fiber intake, aging changes in collagen and elastin, altered motility and genetics contribute. Diverticulitis begins when the thin wall of a pouch is injured, often by trapped stool or raised pressure, leading to a microperforation. Bacteria cross into the surrounding fat, recruiting neutrophils and causing local inflammation (phlegmon). Larger perforations create abscesses, and inflammation can tunnel into neighboring organs, forming fistulas, most often to the bladder. Repeated inflammation causes fibrosis and narrowing. Chronic low-grade inflammation and changes in the microbiome are thought to explain some ongoing symptoms. Obesity, smoking, red meat, low activity and NSAIDs raise risk.",
  body: [["Colon", "Inflamed pouches cause pain and tenderness, usually in the sigmoid colon; repeated attacks cause scarring and strictures that can block the bowel."], ["Belly cavity", "Perforation causes an abscess or, if it ruptures freely, purulent or fecal peritonitis, a surgical emergency."], ["Bladder and vagina", "Fistulas cause air or stool in the urine (pneumaturia, fecaluria), recurrent urinary infections, or stool from the vagina."], ["Blood vessels", "Diverticular bleeding is a separate problem from diverticulitis, caused by rupture of a stretched vasa recta artery, and is a common cause of painless lower GI bleeding."], ["Whole body", "Infection causes fever and raised white blood cells, and severe cases cause sepsis."]],
  symptoms: "Left lower belly pain and tenderness come from inflammation of the sigmoid colon spreading to the peritoneum over it. Fever and a raised white cell count reflect infection. Nausea, constipation or diarrhea come from irritation of the bowel. Urinary urgency or pain occurs when the inflamed colon lies against the bladder. People of Asian descent more often have right-sided diverticula, so pain can mimic appendicitis. Most people with diverticulosis have no symptoms.",
  dx: "CT of the abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast is the main test, showing an inflamed segment with pouches, fat stranding, and complications such as abscess, free air, fistula or obstruction. It also grades severity. Labs include white blood cell count and CRP, and urinalysis checks for bladder involvement. After recovery from a first complicated episode, or when a recent colonoscopy has not been done, colonoscopy is performed to rule out colon cancer, which can look similar on CT. Colonoscopy is avoided during an acute attack because of perforation risk.",
  tx: "Mild uncomplicated diverticulitis in otherwise healthy people can be managed at home with pain control and a clear liquid diet advancing as tolerated, and antibiotics are used selectively rather than routinely. Antibiotics covering gram-negative and anaerobic gut bacteria, such as amoxicillin-clavulanate or ciprofloxacin or trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole with metronidazole, are used for people with complicated disease, immune suppression, frailty or systemic signs. Acetaminophen is preferred over NSAIDs for pain. Hospitalized patients receive IV antibiotics such as ceftriaxone with metronidazole or piperacillin-tazobactam. Larger abscesses are drained through the skin under imaging guidance. Free perforation with peritonitis needs emergency surgery. Elective removal of the affected colon is considered for recurrent attacks that affect quality of life, fistulas or strictures. A high-fiber diet, exercise, weight control and not smoking may lower recurrence; nuts and seeds do not need to be avoided.",
  drugs: ["amoxicillin-clavulanate", "ciprofloxacin", "metronidazole", "trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole", "ceftriaxone", "piperacillin-tazobactam", "ertapenem", "ampicillin-sulbactam", "acetaminophen", "morphine", "ondansetron"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Diverticulitis is often called left-sided appendicitis: left lower quadrant pain, fever and a high white count."], ["Most-tested fact", "Colonic diverticula are false diverticula at sites where vasa recta pierce the muscle; CT with contrast is the test of choice."], ["Bleeding vs itis", "Diverticulosis bleeds painlessly; diverticulitis is painful inflammation and rarely bleeds."], ["Fistula clue", "Air in the urine (pneumaturia) points to a colovesical fistula."]],
  red: "Severe belly pain with a rigid belly, high fever, repeated vomiting, inability to keep fluids down, fainting or confusion needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The old advice to avoid nuts, seeds and popcorn was disproved by a large 2008 study of more than 47,000 men that found no increased risk.",
  src: ["AGA Clinical Practice Update on Medical Management of Colonic Diverticulitis (2021)", "ASCRS Clinical Practice Guidelines for Left-Sided Colonic Diverticulitis (2020)", "ACP Guideline: Diagnosis and Management of Acute Left-Sided Colonic Diverticulitis (2022)", "Painter NS, Burkitt DP. BMJ 1971"]
},
"bowel-obstruction": {
  name: "Bowel obstruction",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["intestinal obstruction", "small bowel obstruction", "SBO", "large bowel obstruction", "LBO", "volvulus", "ileus (different but related)"],
  what: "A bowel obstruction is a blockage that stops food, fluid and gas from moving through the intestines. It causes cramping pain, swelling and vomiting, and can cut off blood supply to the bowel.",
  origin: "Ancient Greek physicians described the deadly illness of a blocked bowel and called it ileus, from a word meaning twisting. For centuries most patients died. In 1921 Abraham Levin introduced a thin rubber nasogastric tube, and in 1932 Owen Wangensteen in Minnesota showed that continuous suction through such a tube could decompress the bowel, sharply lowering death rates. Better anesthesia, fluids and antibiotics made surgery safer. In 1948 William Ogilvie described colonic pseudo-obstruction, a blockage-like condition without a physical block, and in 1999 a trial showed neostigmine could treat it. CT became the key test in the 1990s, and the Bologna guidelines, updated in 2017, guide care of adhesive small bowel obstruction, including water-soluble contrast.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Greek physicians describe ileus, meaning twisting, as a deadly bowel blockage."], ["1921", "Abraham Levin introduces the nasogastric tube."], ["1932", "Owen Wangensteen shows suction decompression lowers death from obstruction."], ["1948", "William Ogilvie describes colonic pseudo-obstruction."], ["1999", "A randomized trial shows neostigmine relieves acute colonic pseudo-obstruction."]],
  cell: "Mechanical obstruction is a physical block. In the small bowel the most common cause is adhesions, scar bands that form after abdominal surgery as injured peritoneum lays down fibrin that is organized by fibroblasts; hernias and tumors follow. In the large bowel the main causes are colon cancer, volvulus (twisting, often sigmoid or cecal) and diverticular strictures. Above the block, swallowed air and intestinal secretions collect, stretching the wall. Distension triggers more fluid secretion and reduces absorption, and the bowel wall becomes swollen and leaky, so large volumes of fluid shift out of the blood. Vomiting loses hydrogen, chloride and potassium, causing hypovolemia and hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. As wall tension rises, venous outflow is blocked first, then arterial inflow, causing ischemia (strangulation). Ischemic mucosa lets bacteria and toxins cross, and the wall can necrose and perforate. A closed-loop obstruction, blocked at two points, as in volvulus, strangulates quickly. A competent ileocecal valve in large bowel obstruction can cause a closed loop and cecal perforation. Ileus, by contrast, is a functional paralysis of the bowel from surgery, opioids, low potassium or inflammation.",
  body: [["Intestine", "The bowel above the block swells with gas and fluid; strangulation causes ischemia, gangrene and perforation."], ["Circulation", "Fluid loss into the bowel and from vomiting causes dehydration, low blood pressure and shock."], ["Kidneys", "Low blood volume leads to acute kidney injury and concentrated urine."], ["Electrolytes", "Vomiting causes low potassium, low chloride and metabolic alkalosis, while ischemia causes lactic acidosis."], ["Lungs", "A distended belly pushes up the diaphragm, making breathing harder, and vomiting raises aspiration risk."], ["Whole body", "Bacteria crossing a damaged wall cause sepsis and peritonitis."]],
  symptoms: "Crampy belly pain that comes in waves happens as the bowel contracts harder against the block. Constant, severe pain suggests strangulation. Vomiting comes early in high small bowel obstruction and can become feculent in low obstruction. The belly swells because gas and fluid collect, more so in lower blockages. Inability to pass stool or gas (obstipation) means a complete block. Thirst, dizziness and low urine output come from fluid loss. Fever, a fast heart rate and tenderness point to ischemia or perforation.",
  dx: "Exam looks for distension, surgical scars, hernias, and high-pitched bowel sounds early or silence late. Abdominal X-rays can show dilated loops and air-fluid levels. CT with IV contrast is the main test, showing the transition point, the cause, closed loops and signs of ischemia such as poor wall enhancement or free fluid. Labs include electrolytes, kidney function, CBC and lactate, which rises with ischemia. In adhesive small bowel obstruction, an oral water-soluble contrast study can show whether contrast reaches the colon, which predicts that surgery will not be needed.",
  tx: "Initial care is bowel rest, IV fluids to restore volume, correction of potassium and other electrolytes, and decompression with a nasogastric tube to remove gas and fluid and stop vomiting. Antiemetics and pain control are given, and opioids are used with care. Many adhesive small bowel obstructions resolve with this care; oral water-soluble contrast such as diatrizoate (Gastrografin) helps predict and may speed resolution by drawing water into the bowel. Strangulation, closed-loop obstruction, perforation or failure to improve needs surgery to release adhesions, repair hernias or remove dead bowel, with antibiotics. Sigmoid volvulus is often untwisted with a flexible scope, followed by planned surgery. Large bowel obstruction from cancer may be relieved with surgery or a stent. Colonic pseudo-obstruction is treated by stopping causes such as opioids and correcting electrolytes; neostigmine, an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that stimulates colonic contraction, is used with heart monitoring because it can slow the heart rate, and atropine is kept available.",
  drugs: ["normal-saline", "lactated-ringers", "potassium-chloride", "diatrizoate", "ondansetron", "morphine", "hydromorphone", "neostigmine", "atropine", "cefoxitin", "piperacillin-tazobactam", "metronidazole", "methylnaltrexone", "alvimopan"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Adhesions from past surgery are the most common cause of small bowel obstruction; colon cancer and volvulus are the top causes of large bowel obstruction."], ["Big three", "Small bowel obstruction in adults is caused mainly by adhesions, hernias and tumors, in roughly that order."], ["Classic X-ray", "Sigmoid volvulus shows a coffee bean sign, a bent inner tube of gas pointing from the pelvis."], ["Lab pattern", "Vomiting causes hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis; rising lactate warns of ischemia."]],
  red: "Severe or constant belly pain, a swollen hard belly, repeated vomiting (especially green or stool-like), no gas or stool, fever or fainting needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Owen Wangensteen's suction method is credited with cutting deaths from bowel obstruction dramatically, and the suction setup was widely called the Wangensteen apparatus.",
  src: ["Bologna Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Adhesive Small Bowel Obstruction (2017 update)", "Ponec RJ et al. Neostigmine for acute colonic pseudo-obstruction. N Engl J Med 1999", "Sabiston Textbook of Surgery", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"c-diff": {
  name: "Clostridioides difficile infection",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["C. diff", "C. difficile", "CDI", "Clostridium difficile (old name)", "pseudomembranous colitis", "antibiotic-associated colitis"],
  what: "C. difficile infection is a colon infection with toxin-making bacteria that causes watery diarrhea and colitis. It most often follows antibiotic use, which wipes out the healthy gut bacteria that normally keep it in check.",
  origin: "In 1935 Ivan Hall and Elizabeth O'Toole isolated a hard-to-grow bacterium from the stool of healthy newborns and named it Bacillus difficilis, for its difficulty. For decades it was not linked to disease. Pseudomembranous colitis was recognized after surgery and later after antibiotics, especially clindamycin in the 1970s. In 1978 John Bartlett and colleagues showed that toxin-producing C. difficile caused antibiotic-associated pseudomembranous colitis. Oral vancomycin and metronidazole became standard treatments. In the early 2000s a hypervirulent strain known as NAP1/BI/027 caused severe outbreaks in North America and Europe. Fidaxomicin was approved in 2011, and the bacterium was renamed Clostridioides difficile in 2016. Fecal microbiota transplantation, first described for colitis in 1958 by Ben Eiseman, led to FDA-approved microbiome products in 2022 and 2023.",
  timeline: [["1935", "Hall and O'Toole isolate Bacillus difficilis from newborn stool."], ["1958", "Ben Eiseman reports fecal enemas for pseudomembranous colitis."], ["1978", "Bartlett and colleagues show C. difficile toxin causes antibiotic-associated colitis."], ["2000s", "The hypervirulent NAP1/BI/027 strain spreads."], ["2011", "Fidaxomicin is approved by the FDA."], ["2022", "Rebyota becomes the first FDA-approved fecal microbiota product."]],
  cell: "C. difficile is a gram-positive, spore-forming anaerobic rod spread by the fecal-oral route. Spores survive for months on surfaces and are not killed by alcohol hand gels. A healthy microbiome resists colonization, partly because gut bacteria convert primary bile acids into secondary bile acids that inhibit C. difficile, while primary bile acids such as taurocholate promote spore germination. Antibiotics, especially clindamycin, fluoroquinolones and cephalosporins, disrupt this balance. Spores germinate into vegetative cells that release toxin A (TcdA) and toxin B (TcdB). These large toxins enter colonocytes and glucosylate Rho family GTPases, collapsing the actin cytoskeleton, opening tight junctions and killing cells. Epithelial cells release IL-8 and other cytokines, recruiting neutrophils. Fibrin, mucus, neutrophils and dead cells erupt from the damaged lining to form yellow-white pseudomembranes. Hypervirulent strains such as 027 make more toxin and also a binary toxin. Severe inflammation can paralyze and dilate the colon (toxic megacolon).",
  body: [["Colon", "Toxins damage the lining, causing watery diarrhea, colitis and pseudomembranes; severe cases cause toxic megacolon, perforation and the need for colectomy."], ["Circulation", "Diarrhea and leaky inflamed bowel cause dehydration, low blood pressure and in fulminant disease shock."], ["Kidneys", "Fluid loss and inflammation cause acute kidney injury, a marker of severe disease."], ["Blood", "The white blood cell count can rise very high, and low albumin reflects protein loss from the inflamed colon."], ["Gut microbiome", "Loss of normal bacteria allows recurrence, which happens in roughly one in five patients after a first episode."]],
  symptoms: "Watery diarrhea, often three or more unformed stools a day, comes from toxin damage to the colon lining and loss of fluid absorption. Crampy lower belly pain comes from colitis. Fever, nausea and loss of appetite reflect inflammation. The stool often has a distinct smell, though smell is not a reliable test. A high white count, belly swelling with little diarrhea, low blood pressure or confusion signal fulminant disease, because a paralyzed colon may stop passing stool.",
  dx: "Testing is done only on unformed stool in people with three or more unformed stools in 24 hours, ideally without laxatives. Tests include nucleic acid amplification (PCR) for the toxin genes, or multistep testing with glutamate dehydrogenase antigen plus a toxin immunoassay, which helps separate infection from harmless colonization. Severe disease is defined by a white count of 15,000 or more or a creatinine above 1.5. CT shows colon wall thickening and can reveal megacolon or perforation in fulminant cases. Colonoscopy can show pseudomembranes but is rarely needed. Test-of-cure is not recommended.",
  tx: "The causing antibiotic is stopped when possible. US guidelines prefer fidaxomicin, a narrow-spectrum macrocyclic antibiotic that blocks bacterial RNA polymerase and spares much of the normal flora, with oral vancomycin as an alternative; oral vancomycin is not absorbed and acts directly in the colon. Metronidazole is now used only when the others are unavailable for a mild first episode. Fulminant disease is treated with high-intensity oral or rectal vancomycin plus IV metronidazole and surgical evaluation for colectomy. Recurrence is treated with fidaxomicin or a tapered and pulsed vancomycin course. After repeated recurrences, fecal microbiota transplantation or approved microbiome products (Rebyota, Vowst) restore protective bacteria. Bezlotoxumab, an antibody against toxin B, lowered recurrence but was discontinued by its maker in 2025. Infection control uses soap and water handwashing, gloves and gowns, private rooms and sporicidal cleaners such as bleach. Antimotility drugs are avoided in severe disease.",
  drugs: ["fidaxomicin", "vancomycin", "metronidazole", "fecal-microbiota-live-jslm", "fecal-microbiota-spores-live-brpk", "bezlotoxumab", "normal-saline", "lactated-ringers"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Clindamycin, fluoroquinolones and cephalosporins are classic triggers; fidaxomicin or oral (not IV) vancomycin is first-line."], ["Memory trick", "Vancomycin must go by mouth for C. diff: IV vancomycin does not reach the colon."], ["Infection control", "Alcohol gel does not kill spores, so soap and water handwashing and bleach cleaning are used."], ["Risk factor", "Acid suppression with PPIs is linked to higher C. difficile risk."]],
  red: "Many watery stools with dizziness or fainting, a swollen or very painful belly, fever, blood in the stool, very little urine or confusion, especially after antibiotics, needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The species name difficile means difficult in Latin, because the bacterium was so hard to grow in the lab.",
  src: ["IDSA/SHEA Clinical Practice Guideline: Focused Update on Management of Clostridioides difficile Infection in Adults (2021)", "ACG Clinical Guidelines: Prevention, Diagnosis, and Treatment of C. difficile Infections (2021)", "Bartlett JG et al. N Engl J Med 1978", "CDC"]
},
"gastroenteritis": {
  name: "Gastroenteritis",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["stomach flu", "stomach bug", "infectious diarrhea", "acute diarrhea", "norovirus", "food poisoning (some types)", "travelers' diarrhea"],
  what: "Gastroenteritis is inflammation of the stomach and intestines, usually from a virus, bacteria or parasite. It causes diarrhea, vomiting and cramps, and the main danger is dehydration.",
  origin: "Diarrheal disease has been one of humanity's biggest killers since ancient times, and cholera pandemics in the 1800s led John Snow to trace an 1854 London outbreak to a water pump. In 1968 an outbreak at a school in Norwalk, Ohio, led to the discovery of the Norwalk virus, seen by Albert Kapikian with electron microscopy in 1972 and now called norovirus. In 1973 Ruth Bishop and colleagues in Melbourne discovered rotavirus, the leading cause of severe diarrhea in young children. A key breakthrough was oral rehydration solution: in 1960 Robert Crane described sodium-glucose cotransport in the gut, and trials in Bangladesh and India in the 1960s and 1970s showed a simple sugar-salt drink could replace IV fluids. The Lancet called it potentially the most important medical advance of the century in 1978. Rotavirus vaccines followed in 2006.",
  timeline: [["1854", "John Snow links a cholera outbreak in London to a contaminated water pump."], ["1960", "Robert Crane describes sodium-glucose cotransport in the intestine."], ["1972", "Norwalk virus (norovirus) is seen by electron microscopy."], ["1973", "Ruth Bishop's team discovers rotavirus."], ["1978", "The Lancet calls oral rehydration potentially the century's most important medical advance."], ["2006", "The RotaTeq rotavirus vaccine is approved in the US."]],
  cell: "Pathogens cause diarrhea in a few main ways. Norovirus, the most common cause in adults, binds histo-blood group antigens on gut cells and injures the small bowel lining, causing vomiting and diarrhea. Rotavirus infects mature enterocytes at villus tips; it kills them, reducing lactase and other enzymes so sugars stay in the gut and pull in water (osmotic diarrhea), and its NSP4 protein acts as an enterotoxin that raises intracellular calcium and activates enteric nerves to drive secretion. Toxin-making bacteria cause secretory diarrhea: cholera toxin and the heat-labile toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli ADP-ribosylate the Gs alpha protein, locking adenylate cyclase on, raising cAMP and opening CFTR chloride channels, so water follows chloride into the lumen. Invasive bacteria such as Shigella, Salmonella and Campylobacter invade the colon wall, causing inflammation and bloody stool (dysentery). Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (such as O157:H7) damages endothelium and can cause hemolytic uremic syndrome. Preformed toxins from Staphylococcus aureus and Bacillus cereus cause vomiting within hours. Importantly, SGLT1 sodium-glucose cotransport usually keeps working, which is why oral rehydration solutions work.",
  body: [["Gut", "Damaged or overstimulated gut lining secretes fluid and absorbs less, causing watery diarrhea, while invasive infections cause bloody diarrhea and cramps."], ["Circulation", "Large fluid and salt losses cause dehydration, low blood pressure and in severe cases shock, especially in infants and older adults."], ["Electrolytes", "Diarrhea loses bicarbonate and potassium, causing metabolic acidosis and low potassium; vomiting loses acid and chloride."], ["Kidneys", "Dehydration lowers kidney blood flow, causing acute kidney injury; Shiga toxin can cause hemolytic uremic syndrome."], ["Brain", "Severe dehydration and electrolyte problems cause lethargy, confusion and in children irritability or seizures."]],
  symptoms: "Watery diarrhea comes from secretion and poor absorption in the injured gut. Vomiting, prominent in norovirus and toxin-mediated food poisoning, happens when gut damage and toxins activate vagal nerves and the brainstem vomiting center. Cramps come from intestinal spasm. Fever and bloody stools suggest invasive bacteria. Thirst, dry mouth, dizziness, fewer wet diapers or little urine and sunken eyes signal dehydration. Most viral illness clears in one to three days as the lining regenerates.",
  dx: "Most cases are diagnosed clinically and need no tests. Dehydration is judged by exam: mental status, heart rate, mucous membranes, skin turgor, capillary refill and urine output. Stool testing, often a multiplex PCR panel, is used for bloody diarrhea, high fever, severe illness, symptoms longer than about a week, recent antibiotic use (to check for C. difficile), outbreaks, travel or a weak immune system. Electrolytes and kidney function are checked in moderate to severe dehydration. A CBC, smear and creatinine are checked when hemolytic uremic syndrome is a concern.",
  tx: "Rehydration is the core of treatment. Oral rehydration solution uses glucose and sodium together so that SGLT1 cotransport pulls salt and water back into the body, and it works for mild to moderate dehydration in children and adults. IV fluids such as lactated Ringer's or normal saline are used for severe dehydration, shock or inability to drink. Ondansetron, a 5-HT3 blocker, reduces vomiting in children so oral rehydration succeeds. Loperamide, an opioid agonist that acts in the gut, slows diarrhea in adults without fever or bloody stool. Bismuth subsalicylate reduces secretion. Antibiotics are not needed for most cases; azithromycin or a fluoroquinolone is used for selected severe bacterial or travelers' diarrhea, and antibiotics are avoided in suspected Shiga toxin E. coli because they may raise the risk of hemolytic uremic syndrome. Parasites such as Giardia are treated with metronidazole, tinidazole or nitazoxanide. Handwashing with soap, safe food and water, and rotavirus vaccination prevent spread.",
  drugs: ["electrolyte-oral-rehydration", "lactated-ringers", "normal-saline", "ondansetron", "loperamide", "bismuth-subsalicylate", "azithromycin", "ciprofloxacin", "rifaximin", "metronidazole", "tinidazole", "nitazoxanide", "zinc", "rotavirus-vaccine", "cholera-vaccine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Cholera toxin raises cAMP through Gs, opening CFTR so chloride and water pour into the gut; oral rehydration works because sodium-glucose cotransport stays intact."], ["Memory trick", "Fast vomiting within 1 to 6 hours of eating points to a preformed toxin: Staph aureus or Bacillus cereus (fried rice)."], ["Pitfall", "Antibiotics in E. coli O157:H7 may increase the risk of hemolytic uremic syndrome."], ["Infection control", "Norovirus resists alcohol hand gel, so soap and water handwashing is preferred."]],
  red: "Signs of severe dehydration (no urine for many hours, very dry mouth, dizziness or fainting, a sleepy or limp child), bloody diarrhea, high fever, severe belly pain or inability to keep down any fluids needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Norovirus is so contagious that a very small number of virus particles can cause infection, which is why it spreads so easily on cruise ships and in schools.",
  src: ["IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Infectious Diarrhea (2017)", "WHO: The Treatment of Diarrhoea (2005)", "CDC", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"constipation": {
  name: "Constipation",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["chronic idiopathic constipation", "functional constipation", "CIC", "opioid-induced constipation", "OIC", "fecal impaction"],
  what: "Constipation means having bowel movements that are infrequent, hard, difficult to pass or that feel incomplete. It is very common and usually harmless, but it can also be a sign of another condition or a medicine side effect.",
  origin: "Laxatives are among the oldest medicines; ancient Egyptian texts such as the Ebers papyrus mention castor oil, and senna was widely used by Arabic physicians in the Middle Ages. Around 1900 the theory of autointoxication claimed that retained stool poisoned the body, leading to unnecessary colon surgery and harsh purging before it was discredited. In 1997 Ken Heaton and Stephen Lewis in Bristol published the Bristol Stool Form Scale, which made stool consistency easy to describe. The Rome criteria defined functional constipation, with Rome IV in 2016. New drug classes arrived in the 2000s and 2010s, including peripherally acting opioid blockers such as methylnaltrexone (2008), the secretagogue linaclotide (2012) and the 5-HT4 agonist prucalopride (US 2018).",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Egyptian medical texts describe castor oil and other laxatives."], ["1997", "The Bristol Stool Form Scale is published."], ["2008", "Methylnaltrexone is approved for opioid-induced constipation."], ["2012", "Linaclotide is approved for chronic idiopathic constipation."], ["2016", "Rome IV criteria for functional constipation are published."], ["2018", "Prucalopride is approved in the US."]],
  cell: "Normal defecation depends on colonic motility, fluid balance and coordinated pelvic floor muscles. The enteric nervous system, with interstitial cells of Cajal as pacemakers, drives high-amplitude propagating contractions that move stool. Serotonin from enterochromaffin cells activates 5-HT4 receptors on enteric neurons to release acetylcholine and promote peristalsis. Epithelial chloride secretion through CFTR and ClC-2 channels draws water into stool. The longer stool stays in the colon, the more water is absorbed and the harder it gets. Primary constipation is grouped into normal-transit, slow-transit (reduced propagating contractions and fewer interstitial cells of Cajal) and defecatory disorders (dyssynergia), where the puborectalis and anal sphincter fail to relax during straining. Secondary causes include opioids, which activate mu receptors in the gut to reduce motility and secretion and increase sphincter tone; anticholinergics; iron and calcium supplements; calcium channel blockers; hypothyroidism; hypercalcemia; diabetes; Parkinson disease; pregnancy; and structural problems such as colon cancer or strictures.",
  body: [["Colon", "Slow transit lets the colon absorb too much water, producing hard stools. Severe retention causes fecal impaction, and very rarely a massively dilated colon."], ["Rectum and anus", "Straining with hard stool causes hemorrhoids, anal fissures and in some people rectal prolapse. Impaction can cause liquid stool to leak around it (overflow incontinence)."], ["Bladder", "A full rectum presses on the bladder, contributing to urinary retention and infections, especially in older adults and children."], ["Older adults", "Impaction can cause confusion, loss of appetite and vomiting in frail older people."]],
  symptoms: "Infrequent, hard or lumpy stools result from slow transit and extra water absorption. Straining and a feeling of incomplete emptying happen when the pelvic floor does not relax properly or the stool is too hard. Bloating and belly discomfort come from retained stool and gas. Sudden constipation with weight loss, blood in the stool or anemia can mean a structural cause such as colon cancer. In impaction, watery leakage can be mistaken for diarrhea.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is mostly clinical. Rome IV criteria for functional constipation include at least two of: straining, hard or lumpy stools, feeling of incomplete evacuation, feeling of blockage, needing manual maneuvers, or fewer than three spontaneous bowel movements a week, for three months. A careful drug review and a digital rectal exam to check for impaction and pelvic floor function are key. Blood tests may include CBC, calcium, glucose and thyroid function. Colonoscopy is done for alarm features or routine screening. Anorectal manometry with balloon expulsion and colonic transit studies are used for constipation that does not respond to treatment.",
  tx: "Care begins with more dietary fiber, adequate fluids, physical activity and a regular toilet routine, ideally after meals when the gastrocolic reflex is strongest. Bulk-forming fiber holds water in stool. Osmotic laxatives such as polyethylene glycol, lactulose and magnesium salts pull water into the colon; magnesium is used with caution in kidney disease. Stimulant laxatives such as senna and bisacodyl activate the enteric nerves and increase secretion. Docusate softens stool but has weak evidence. Secretagogues (linaclotide and plecanatide through guanylate cyclase-C, lubiprostone through chloride channels) add fluid to the gut. Prucalopride, a 5-HT4 agonist, increases propagating contractions. For opioid-induced constipation, PAMORAs such as methylnaltrexone, naloxegol and naldemedine block gut mu receptors without blocking pain relief in the brain. Dyssynergic defecation is treated with biofeedback therapy. Suppositories, enemas and manual disimpaction treat impaction.",
  drugs: ["polyethylene-glycol", "sennosides", "bisacodyl", "lactulose", "magnesium-hydroxide", "magnesium-citrate", "docusate", "linaclotide", "plecanatide", "lubiprostone", "prucalopride", "tenapanor", "methylnaltrexone", "naloxegol", "naldemedine", "sodium-phosphate"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Opioids cause constipation through gut mu receptors, and tolerance to this effect does not develop, so a bowel regimen is started with chronic opioids."], ["Memory trick", "PAMORA means Peripherally Acting Mu Opioid Receptor Antagonist: blocks the gut, spares the brain."], ["Pitfall", "Sodium phosphate enemas can cause dangerous phosphate levels in people with kidney disease and small children."], ["Alarm features", "New constipation after age 50, blood in stool, weight loss or anemia need evaluation for colon cancer."]],
  red: "Constipation with severe belly pain, a swollen belly, vomiting, no gas passing, blood in the stool, or fever needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The Bristol Stool Form Scale sorts stool into seven types from hard lumps (type 1) to watery (type 7), and types 3 and 4 are considered ideal.",
  src: ["AGA-ACG Clinical Practice Guideline on the Pharmacological Management of Chronic Idiopathic Constipation (2023)", "Rome IV criteria (2016)", "Lewis SJ, Heaton KW. Scand J Gastroenterol 1997", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"nausea-vomiting": {
  name: "Nausea and vomiting",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["emesis", "throwing up", "PONV", "chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting", "CINV", "motion sickness", "hyperemesis"],
  what: "Nausea is the queasy feeling of needing to throw up, and vomiting is the forceful emptying of the stomach through the mouth. They are symptoms with many causes, from infections and pregnancy to medicines, motion and serious illness.",
  origin: "Vomiting has been described since the earliest medical writings, and seasickness gave English the word nausea, from the Greek for ship. In the late 1940s and 1950s Herbert Borison and Shih-Chun Wang described a vomiting center in the brainstem and the chemoreceptor trigger zone in the area postrema, a region that samples the blood for toxins. Antihistamines such as dimenhydrinate were found to prevent motion sickness in 1949. The thalidomide tragedy of the late 1950s and early 1960s, when a drug sold for morning sickness caused severe birth defects, transformed drug safety rules worldwide. Serotonin research led to ondansetron, approved in 1991, which changed chemotherapy care, and aprepitant, the first NK1 receptor blocker, was approved in 2003.",
  timeline: [["1949", "Dimenhydrinate is found to prevent motion sickness."], ["1950s", "Borison and Wang describe the brainstem vomiting center and chemoreceptor trigger zone."], ["1961", "Thalidomide, used for morning sickness, is withdrawn after causing birth defects."], ["1991", "Ondansetron, a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist, is approved in the US."], ["2003", "Aprepitant becomes the first NK1 receptor antagonist approved."]],
  cell: "Vomiting is coordinated by a network in the dorsal medulla, centered on the nucleus tractus solitarius and nearby neurons, often called the vomiting center or central pattern generator. It receives four main inputs. The chemoreceptor trigger zone in the area postrema lies outside the blood-brain barrier and senses drugs, toxins and metabolic changes through dopamine D2, serotonin 5-HT3, neurokinin-1 (NK1, substance P) and opioid receptors. Vagal afferents from the gut carry signals when enterochromaffin cells release serotonin onto 5-HT3 receptors after chemotherapy, infection or distension. The vestibular system sends signals through histamine H1 and muscarinic M1 receptors in motion sickness. The cortex and limbic system trigger nausea from smells, pain, anxiety and anticipation. The output closes the airway, relaxes the lower esophageal sphincter, and contracts the diaphragm and abdominal muscles to expel stomach contents, along with salivation and sweating from autonomic activity. Each antiemetic class blocks a specific receptor in this network, which is why choosing the drug by cause works best.",
  body: [["Brain", "The brainstem vomiting network creates nausea and coordinates retching, and causes such as raised intracranial pressure act directly on it."], ["Esophagus", "Forceful retching can tear the lining at the gastroesophageal junction (Mallory-Weiss tear) or rarely rupture the esophagus (Boerhaave syndrome)."], ["Fluids and electrolytes", "Loss of stomach acid and fluid causes dehydration, low potassium, low chloride and metabolic alkalosis."], ["Kidneys", "Dehydration reduces kidney blood flow and can cause acute kidney injury."], ["Lungs", "Vomiting with reduced consciousness risks aspiration of stomach contents and pneumonia."], ["Teeth", "Repeated exposure to stomach acid erodes dental enamel over time."]],
  symptoms: "Nausea comes from activation of the brainstem network and is often accompanied by pallor, sweating and salivation from autonomic nerves. Retching and vomiting follow when the motor pathway fires. The timing points to the cause: vomiting within hours of food suggests a toxin, vomiting with headache and confusion suggests the brain, bilious vomiting with distension suggests bowel obstruction, and vomiting with chest pain can signal a heart attack. Weakness, thirst and dizziness mean dehydration. Cannabinoid hyperemesis syndrome causes cycles of severe vomiting in long-term heavy cannabis users that often ease with hot showers.",
  dx: "Diagnosis focuses on finding the cause through history and exam: timing, relation to meals, medicines, pregnancy, head injury, belly pain, and signs of dehydration. Common tests include a pregnancy test, electrolytes, kidney function, glucose, liver tests and lipase. An ECG is checked when a heart cause is possible and before some antiemetics because several prolong the QT interval. Imaging is chosen by suspected cause, such as abdominal CT for obstruction or head CT for neurologic signs. Gastric emptying studies diagnose gastroparesis.",
  tx: "Treatment matches the cause and the receptor involved, together with rehydration and correcting electrolytes. 5-HT3 antagonists such as ondansetron, granisetron and palonosetron block serotonin at gut vagal nerves and the trigger zone, and are mainstays for chemotherapy, postoperative and gastroenteritis vomiting. Dopamine D2 antagonists such as metoclopramide (which also speeds stomach emptying), prochlorperazine, promethazine, droperidol and haloperidol act on the trigger zone; they can cause restlessness, dystonia and QT prolongation. NK1 antagonists (aprepitant, fosaprepitant) block substance P and are added for highly emetogenic chemotherapy, along with dexamethasone and often olanzapine. Antihistamine and anticholinergic drugs (meclizine, dimenhydrinate, scopolamine patch) block vestibular signals in motion sickness. Doxylamine-pyridoxine is a first-line drug for nausea and vomiting in pregnancy. Cannabinoid hyperemesis improves with stopping cannabis, and haloperidol or topical capsaicin can help acute episodes.",
  drugs: ["ondansetron", "granisetron", "palonosetron", "metoclopramide", "prochlorperazine", "promethazine", "droperidol", "haloperidol", "amisulpride", "aprepitant", "fosaprepitant", "rolapitant", "netupitant-palonosetron", "dexamethasone", "olanzapine", "meclizine", "dimenhydrinate", "scopolamine-transdermal", "doxylamine-pyridoxine", "trimethobenzamide", "dronabinol", "capsaicin-topical", "lactated-ringers", "normal-saline"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Match the drug to the input: chemo and gut use 5-HT3 blockers; trigger zone uses D2 blockers; motion uses H1 and muscarinic blockers; delayed chemo vomiting adds NK1 blockers."], ["Most-tested fact", "The area postrema (chemoreceptor trigger zone) lies outside the blood-brain barrier, so it can sense toxins in the blood."], ["Side effect pearl", "Ondansetron, droperidol and haloperidol can prolong the QT; metoclopramide can cause dystonia and tardive dyskinesia."], ["Lab pattern", "Prolonged vomiting causes hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis."]],
  red: "Vomiting blood or coffee-ground material, green or stool-like vomit, severe belly or chest pain, a stiff neck or severe headache, confusion, signs of severe dehydration, or vomiting after a head injury needs emergency care (call 911). For a possible poisoning, call Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222.",
  fun: "The word nausea comes from the Greek word naus, meaning ship, a reminder that seasickness was one of the first kinds people named.",
  src: ["ASCO Antiemetics Guideline Update (2020)", "MASCC/ESMO Antiemetic Guidelines", "ACOG Practice Bulletin: Nausea and Vomiting of Pregnancy (2018)", "Fourth Consensus Guidelines for the Management of Postoperative Nausea and Vomiting (2020)"]
},
"colorectal-cancer": {
  name: "Colorectal cancer",
  sys: "Stomach & Digestion",
  aka: ["colon cancer", "rectal cancer", "bowel cancer", "CRC", "colorectal adenocarcinoma"],
  what: "Colorectal cancer is cancer that starts in the colon or rectum, usually growing slowly from a polyp over many years. Screening can find and remove polyps before they become cancer, and early cancers are often curable.",
  origin: "Surgeons in the 1800s and early 1900s developed operations to remove colon and rectal tumors. In 1913 pathologist Aldred Warthin described a family with many cancers, and in 1966 Henry Lynch described the inherited pattern now called Lynch syndrome. In 1957 Charles Heidelberger developed 5-fluorouracil, still the backbone of chemotherapy. In 1969 William Wolff and Hiromi Shinya in New York began removing polyps through a colonoscope, and the National Polyp Study in 1993 showed this lowered cancer rates. In 1990 Eric Fearon and Bert Vogelstein proposed a step-by-step genetic model of how a polyp becomes cancer. Oxaliplatin, irinotecan, targeted antibodies such as bevacizumab and cetuximab (2004) and immunotherapy for mismatch repair-deficient tumors (2020 first-line approval) followed. In 2021 USPSTF lowered the age to start screening to 45.",
  timeline: [["1957", "Charles Heidelberger develops 5-fluorouracil."], ["1966", "Henry Lynch describes hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer families."], ["1969", "Wolff and Shinya begin colonoscopic polypectomy."], ["1990", "Fearon and Vogelstein publish the genetic model of colorectal tumor development."], ["2004", "Bevacizumab and cetuximab are approved for metastatic colorectal cancer."], ["2021", "USPSTF recommends starting screening at age 45."]],
  cell: "Most colorectal cancers arise through the adenoma-carcinoma sequence. In the chromosomal instability pathway, the first hit is usually loss of the APC tumor suppressor gene. APC normally helps destroy beta-catenin; without it, beta-catenin builds up, enters the nucleus and switches on Wnt target genes such as MYC and cyclin D1, driving crypt cells to multiply into an adenoma. Activating KRAS mutations then drive growth signaling, loss of SMAD4 and other 18q genes removes TGF-beta growth control, and TP53 loss late allows cells with DNA damage to survive, completing the change to invasive carcinoma. About 15 percent of cancers instead follow the microsatellite instability pathway, where mismatch repair genes (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) are inactivated, either inherited in Lynch syndrome or by MLH1 promoter methylation in sporadic serrated polyps that often carry BRAF V600E mutations. These tumors accumulate many mutations, which makes them highly visible to the immune system and very responsive to PD-1 blockade. Inherited APC mutations cause familial adenomatous polyposis with hundreds of polyps. Risk factors include age, family history, inflammatory bowel disease, obesity, smoking, alcohol, and diets high in red and processed meat.",
  body: [["Colon and rectum", "The tumor grows through the bowel wall and can bleed, narrow the bowel and cause obstruction or perforation."], ["Blood", "Slow bleeding, especially from right-sided tumors, causes iron deficiency anemia."], ["Lymph nodes", "Spread to regional lymph nodes defines stage III disease and is the key reason for chemotherapy after surgery."], ["Liver", "Cancer cells reach the liver through the portal vein, making it the most common site of distant spread."], ["Lungs", "Lungs are the second most common site, and rectal cancers can reach them directly through systemic veins."], ["Whole body", "Advanced cancer causes weight loss, fatigue and loss of appetite from tumor-driven inflammation."]],
  symptoms: "Early cancer usually causes no symptoms, which is why screening matters. Right-sided colon cancers often bleed slowly and cause iron deficiency anemia, fatigue and shortness of breath, because the right colon is wide and stool is liquid there. Left-sided and rectal cancers more often cause a change in bowel habits, thinner stools, visible blood, cramping and obstruction, because the left colon is narrower and stool is solid. Rectal cancer can cause tenesmus. Weight loss and belly pain suggest more advanced disease.",
  dx: "Screening tests include colonoscopy, stool tests (fecal immunochemical test and multitarget stool DNA test), CT colonography and flexible sigmoidoscopy, starting at age 45 for average-risk adults per USPSTF. Diagnosis is confirmed by colonoscopy with biopsy. Staging uses CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis, and pelvic MRI for rectal cancer. CEA is measured as a baseline and to monitor for recurrence, not for screening. All tumors are tested for mismatch repair deficiency or microsatellite instability, and metastatic tumors for RAS, BRAF and HER2 changes, to guide treatment and identify Lynch syndrome.",
  tx: "Treatment depends on stage and tumor biology, with support for emotional health. Early colon cancer is cured by removing the segment and its lymph nodes. Stage III and some stage II cancers get FOLFOX or CAPOX: fluorouracil or oral capecitabine block thymidylate synthase (leucovorin boosts this) and oxaliplatin crosslinks DNA. Rectal cancer often gets chemotherapy and radiation before surgery, and mismatch repair-deficient rectal cancers can respond completely to the PD-1 antibody dostarlimab. Metastatic disease uses FOLFOX or FOLFIRI (irinotecan blocks topoisomerase I) with bevacizumab, which blocks VEGF, or with cetuximab or panitumumab, which block EGFR and work only in RAS wild-type tumors. MSI-high tumors get checkpoint inhibitors such as pembrolizumab or nivolumab with ipilimumab. BRAF V600E tumors get encorafenib with cetuximab, HER2-amplified tumors get HER2-targeted drugs, KRAS G12C tumors can get sotorasib or adagrasib with an anti-EGFR antibody; later options are regorafenib, trifluridine-tipiracil and fruquintinib. Some liver or lung metastases can be removed for cure.",
  drugs: ["fluorouracil", "capecitabine", "leucovorin", "oxaliplatin", "irinotecan", "bevacizumab", "cetuximab", "panitumumab", "pembrolizumab", "nivolumab", "ipilimumab", "dostarlimab", "encorafenib", "tucatinib", "trastuzumab", "sotorasib", "adagrasib", "regorafenib", "trifluridine-tipiracil", "fruquintinib", "ramucirumab", "aspirin", "ondansetron"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "The adenoma-carcinoma sequence: APC, then KRAS, then TP53 (A-K-P, like a key path to cancer)."], ["Right vs left", "Right-sided cancer bleeds (iron deficiency anemia); left-sided cancer blocks (change in stool caliber, obstruction)."], ["Pearl", "Anti-EGFR antibodies work only in RAS wild-type tumors; MSI-high tumors respond strongly to PD-1 blockade."], ["Association", "Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis) bacteremia or endocarditis calls for a colonoscopy to look for colon cancer."], ["Screening", "CEA monitors for recurrence but is not a screening test."]],
  red: "Heavy rectal bleeding, black stools with dizziness, severe belly pain with a swollen belly and vomiting, or inability to pass gas or stool can mean bleeding or blockage and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Colorectal cancer rates are falling in older adults thanks to screening but rising in people under 50, which is why the screening start age was lowered to 45.",
  src: ["USPSTF Recommendation: Screening for Colorectal Cancer (2021)", "NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Colon Cancer and Rectal Cancer", "Fearon ER, Vogelstein B. Cell 1990", "American Cancer Society"]
},
"acute-kidney-injury": {
  name: "Acute kidney injury",
  sys: "Kidneys & Urinary",
  aka: ["AKI", "acute renal failure", "ARF", "acute tubular necrosis (one type)", "ATN"],
  what: "Acute kidney injury is a sudden drop in kidney function over hours to days. Waste products and fluid build up, and potassium and acid levels can rise to dangerous levels.",
  origin: "Physicians long described sudden loss of urine output, once called ischuria. During the London Blitz in 1941, Eric Bywaters and Desmond Beall described people pulled from collapsed buildings who developed kidney failure days later, revealing crush syndrome and the role of muscle breakdown. In 1943 Dutch physician Willem Kolff built a working artificial kidney, and in 1945 a woman with acute kidney failure became the first patient to survive because of dialysis. Dialysis became routine for acute kidney failure in the Korean War era and after. For decades there were dozens of definitions, until the RIFLE criteria (2004), AKIN criteria (2007) and the KDIGO guideline (2012) created a shared definition and staging based on creatinine and urine output.",
  timeline: [["1941", "Bywaters and Beall describe crush syndrome with kidney failure."], ["1943", "Willem Kolff builds a working artificial kidney."], ["1945", "The first patient survives acute kidney failure because of dialysis."], ["2004", "The RIFLE criteria for acute kidney injury are published."], ["2012", "The KDIGO guideline unifies the definition and staging of AKI."]],
  cell: "AKI is grouped into three types. Prerenal AKI is reduced blood flow to the kidneys from dehydration, bleeding, heart failure, sepsis or cirrhosis; the kidney itself is intact, and angiotensin II and ADH make it hold sodium and water. Intrinsic AKI is damage inside the kidney, most often acute tubular necrosis. In ischemic ATN, the proximal tubule S3 segment and thick ascending limb in the outer medulla, which have high energy demand and low oxygen, lose ATP. Cells lose polarity, Na+/K+-ATPase moves off the basolateral membrane, the brush border is lost, and cells die and slough into the lumen, forming muddy brown granular casts that block tubules. Filtrate leaks back across damaged tubules, and more sodium reaching the macula densa triggers tubuloglomerular feedback that constricts afferent arterioles, lowering GFR further. Toxins (aminoglycosides, contrast dye, cisplatin, myoglobin in rhabdomyolysis) damage tubule cells directly. Other intrinsic causes include acute interstitial nephritis, a drug-triggered hypersensitivity reaction with white cell casts, and glomerulonephritis with red cell casts. Postrenal AKI is blockage of urine flow, as from an enlarged prostate or stones in both ureters, raising pressure backward into the kidney.",
  body: [["Kidneys", "Reduced filtration lets creatinine, urea and other wastes build up, and urine output often falls."], ["Heart", "Rising potassium can cause dangerous arrhythmias and cardiac arrest; uremia can cause pericarditis."], ["Lungs", "Fluid the kidneys cannot remove builds up as pulmonary edema."], ["Blood chemistry", "Failure to excrete acid causes metabolic acidosis, and phosphate rises while calcium may fall."], ["Brain", "Uremic toxins cause confusion, drowsiness and in severe cases seizures."], ["Blood", "Uremia impairs platelet function, raising bleeding risk."], ["Drug handling", "Many drugs cleared by the kidneys accumulate, so dosing must be adjusted."]],
  symptoms: "AKI is often found on blood tests before symptoms appear. Less urine comes from reduced filtration, though some patients keep making urine. Swelling and shortness of breath come from fluid retention. Nausea, poor appetite, tiredness and confusion come from uremic toxins. Weakness and palpitations can come from high potassium. Symptoms of the cause, such as dehydration, dark tea-colored urine in rhabdomyolysis, or trouble passing urine in obstruction, often dominate.",
  dx: "KDIGO defines AKI as a rise in serum creatinine of 0.3 milligrams per deciliter or more within 48 hours, a rise to 1.5 times baseline within 7 days, or urine output below 0.5 milliliters per kilogram per hour for 6 hours, and it stages severity from 1 to 3. Tests then sort the cause. Urinalysis and microscopy show muddy brown casts in ATN, white cell casts in interstitial nephritis and red cell casts in glomerulonephritis. A fractional excretion of sodium under 1 percent suggests a prerenal cause, and a BUN to creatinine ratio over 20 supports it. Bladder scan and kidney ultrasound look for obstruction. Potassium, bicarbonate and creatine kinase are checked.",
  tx: "Treatment fixes the cause and protects the patient while the kidneys recover. Prerenal AKI is treated by restoring blood flow with IV fluids, often balanced crystalloids, or by treating heart failure or sepsis. Nephrotoxic drugs such as NSAIDs, aminoglycosides and contrast are avoided, and ACE inhibitors, ARBs and diuretics may be paused. Obstruction is relieved with a bladder catheter or ureteral stents. Drugs causing interstitial nephritis are stopped, sometimes with steroids. Loop diuretics manage fluid overload but do not speed recovery. Dangerous hyperkalemia is treated in steps: IV calcium stabilizes heart muscle membranes, insulin with dextrose and inhaled albuterol shift potassium into cells, sodium bicarbonate helps when acidosis is present, and potassium binders such as sodium zirconium cyclosilicate or patiromer remove it through the gut. Dialysis is used for severe acidosis, high potassium, certain toxins, fluid overload or uremic symptoms that do not respond. Medication doses are adjusted to kidney function.",
  drugs: ["lactated-ringers", "normal-saline", "plasma-lyte", "furosemide", "bumetanide", "calcium-gluconate", "calcium-chloride", "regular-insulin", "dextrose", "albuterol", "sodium-bicarbonate", "sodium-zirconium-cyclosilicate", "patiromer", "sodium-polystyrene-sulfonate", "terlipressin", "albumin-human", "prednisone"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Dialysis indications are AEIOU: Acidosis, Electrolytes (high potassium), Ingestions (toxins), Overload of fluid, Uremia."], ["Most-tested fact", "Muddy brown granular casts mean acute tubular necrosis; FeNa under 1 percent suggests prerenal AKI."], ["Hyperkalemia order", "Calcium first to protect the heart, then shift (insulin, dextrose, albuterol), then remove (binders, diuretics, dialysis)."], ["Often confused with", "Chronic kidney disease: small kidneys on ultrasound and anemia suggest the problem has been present for months."]],
  red: "Very little or no urine, severe swelling or shortness of breath, chest pain, palpitations or a slow heartbeat, confusion, or dark cola-colored urine after a crush injury or extreme exercise needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Willem Kolff's first artificial kidney used sausage casing as the membrane, wrapped around a rotating drum in a tub of fluid.",
  src: ["KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for Acute Kidney Injury (2012)", "Bywaters EGL, Beall D. BMJ 1941", "Brenner and Rector's The Kidney", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"chronic-kidney-disease": {
  name: "Chronic kidney disease",
  sys: "Kidneys & Urinary",
  aka: ["CKD", "chronic renal failure", "end-stage kidney disease", "ESKD", "ESRD", "diabetic kidney disease"],
  what: "Chronic kidney disease is a slow, lasting loss of kidney function or kidney damage for at least three months. It often has no symptoms until it is advanced, and it raises the risk of heart disease and kidney failure.",
  origin: "In 1827 English physician Richard Bright described patients with swelling, protein in the urine and shrunken, scarred kidneys at autopsy, and chronic kidney disease was long called Bright's disease. For more than a century kidney failure was fatal. In 1954 Joseph Murray and colleagues in Boston performed the first successful kidney transplant between identical twins, later winning a Nobel Prize. In 1960 Belding Scribner in Seattle created a reusable arteriovenous shunt that made long-term dialysis possible, and in 1972 Congress extended Medicare to cover people with kidney failure, making dialysis widely available. Epoetin alfa for kidney anemia was approved in 1989. In 2002 the KDOQI guideline created CKD stages. In recent years SGLT2 inhibitors, finerenone and GLP-1 receptor agonists joined ACE inhibitors and ARBs as kidney-protecting drugs.",
  timeline: [["1827", "Richard Bright links swelling and protein in the urine to diseased kidneys."], ["1954", "Joseph Murray performs the first successful kidney transplant."], ["1960", "Belding Scribner's shunt makes long-term dialysis possible."], ["1972", "Congress extends Medicare coverage to people with kidney failure."], ["2002", "KDOQI defines CKD stages."], ["2020", "The DAPA-CKD trial shows an SGLT2 inhibitor protects kidneys with or without diabetes."]],
  cell: "The leading causes are diabetes and high blood pressure, followed by glomerulonephritis and polycystic kidney disease. Whatever the first injury, nephron loss sets off a common path. Remaining nephrons hyperfilter to compensate: angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole, and in diabetes increased SGLT2 sodium reabsorption in the proximal tubule reduces sodium delivery to the macula densa, so tubuloglomerular feedback dilates the afferent arteriole. The resulting glomerular hypertension stretches and injures podocytes, which detach, letting albumin leak into the urine. Filtered protein and high glucose stress tubule cells, which release inflammatory and fibrotic signals such as TGF-beta. Mineralocorticoid receptor overactivation adds inflammation and fibrosis. Fibroblasts and myofibroblasts deposit scar, causing glomerulosclerosis, tubular atrophy and interstitial fibrosis, which destroys more nephrons in a cycle. As function falls, the kidney makes less erythropoietin (from peritubular interstitial cells) and less calcitriol (1-alpha-hydroxylase), and it cannot excrete phosphate, potassium, acid and water. High phosphate raises FGF23 and PTH, leading to CKD-mineral and bone disorder and vascular calcification.",
  body: [["Kidneys", "Scarred kidneys shrink and lose filtration, progressing in some people to kidney failure needing dialysis or transplant."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death in CKD, driven by high blood pressure, fluid overload, vascular calcification and inflammation."], ["Blood", "Low erythropoietin and iron deficiency cause anemia, with fatigue and shortness of breath; uremia impairs platelet function."], ["Bones and minerals", "High phosphate, low calcitriol and high PTH cause renal osteodystrophy, bone pain, fractures and calcified vessels."], ["Electrolytes and acid", "Potassium and acid build up, causing hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis, which also weakens bones and muscles."], ["Brain and nerves", "Uremic toxins cause poor concentration, encephalopathy, restless legs and peripheral neuropathy."], ["Skin", "Uremia and mineral disorders cause severe itching and a sallow color."]],
  symptoms: "Early CKD is usually silent because the remaining nephrons compensate. As it advances, fatigue and shortness of breath come from anemia, and swelling and high blood pressure come from salt and water retention. Foamy urine reflects protein leaking through damaged glomeruli. Itching, nausea, poor appetite, a metallic taste and trouble thinking come from uremic toxins. Bone pain reflects mineral disorder. Waking at night to urinate happens because damaged kidneys cannot concentrate urine.",
  dx: "CKD is defined as an eGFR below 60 or markers of kidney damage, most often albuminuria, lasting more than three months. eGFR is calculated from serum creatinine, and sometimes cystatin C, using the race-free CKD-EPI 2021 equation. Albuminuria is measured with a urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio. KDIGO stages CKD by both GFR (G1 to G5) and albuminuria (A1 to A3), which together predict risk. Urinalysis, kidney ultrasound and tests for the cause (glucose, blood pressure, autoimmune and genetic tests, sometimes biopsy) complete the picture. Labs also track hemoglobin, potassium, bicarbonate, calcium, phosphate and PTH.",
  tx: "Treatment slows progression, lowers heart risk and manages complications. ACE inhibitors or ARBs lower glomerular pressure by dilating the efferent arteriole and reduce albuminuria. SGLT2 inhibitors restore tubuloglomerular feedback, lowering hyperfiltration, and protect kidneys and heart with or without diabetes. Finerenone, a nonsteroidal mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, reduces inflammation and fibrosis in diabetic kidney disease, and semaglutide, a GLP-1 receptor agonist, slows CKD in type 2 diabetes. Blood pressure and glucose control, statins and avoiding NSAIDs and other nephrotoxins are key. Anemia is treated with iron and erythropoiesis-stimulating agents, or HIF-PH inhibitors in dialysis patients. Mineral disorder is treated with phosphate binders, active vitamin D and calcimimetics. Sodium bicarbonate corrects acidosis, potassium binders treat hyperkalemia, and diuretics manage fluid. Drug doses are adjusted to kidney function. Kidney failure is treated with hemodialysis, peritoneal dialysis or transplant.",
  drugs: ["lisinopril", "losartan", "dapagliflozin", "empagliflozin", "finerenone", "semaglutide", "atorvastatin", "furosemide", "epoetin-alfa", "darbepoetin-alfa", "daprodustat", "vadadustat", "iron-sucrose", "ferrous-sulfate", "sevelamer", "calcium-acetate", "lanthanum-carbonate", "ferric-citrate", "sucroferric-oxyhydroxide", "calcitriol", "paricalcitol", "cinacalcet", "sodium-bicarbonate", "patiromer"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Diabetes is the leading cause of CKD and kidney failure in the US, followed by high blood pressure."], ["Memory trick", "Kidney protection pillars: ACE inhibitor or ARB, SGLT2 inhibitor, finerenone (in type 2 diabetes with albuminuria) and GLP-1 receptor agonist."], ["Lab pattern", "CKD-mineral bone disorder: high phosphate, high FGF23, low calcitriol, low or normal calcium and high PTH (secondary hyperparathyroidism)."], ["Pearl", "A small rise in creatinine after starting an ACE inhibitor or ARB is expected and reflects lower glomerular pressure."]],
  red: "Severe shortness of breath, chest pain, confusion, a very slow or irregular heartbeat, seizures, very little urine or vomiting that will not stop in someone with kidney disease needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The first successful kidney transplant worked because the donor and recipient were identical twins, so the immune system did not reject the new kidney.",
  src: ["KDIGO 2024 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Evaluation and Management of Chronic Kidney Disease", "KDIGO 2022 Clinical Practice Guideline for Diabetes Management in CKD", "Bright R. Reports of Medical Cases. 1827", "Heerspink HJL et al. DAPA-CKD. N Engl J Med 2020"]
}
});

;
DB.DIS = DB.DIS || {};
Object.assign(DB.DIS, {
"uti": {
  name: "Urinary tract infection",
  sys: "Kidneys & Urinary",
  aka: ["UTI", "bladder infection", "cystitis", "acute cystitis"],
  what: "A urinary tract infection happens when bacteria, usually from the gut, climb up the urethra and grow in the bladder. It causes burning with urination, frequent urges to go and lower belly discomfort.",
  origin: "Painful, cloudy urine has been described since ancient medicine, but the cause stayed unclear until the germ era. In 1885 German pediatrician Theodor Escherich described the gut bacterium now called Escherichia coli, which turned out to be the leading cause of bladder infections. In the 1950s Edward Kass at Harvard showed that counting bacteria in a clean urine sample could separate true infection from contamination, setting the classic threshold of 100,000 colony-forming units per milliliter. Sulfonamides in the 1930s and nitrofurantoin in the 1950s gave doctors reliable pills. Rising resistance, especially from ESBL-producing bacteria, has driven newer oral options in the 2020s such as sulopenem and gepotidacin.",
  timeline: [["1885", "Theodor Escherich describes the bacterium later named Escherichia coli."], ["1930s", "Sulfonamide drugs become the first widely used antibiotics for urinary infections."], ["1950s", "Edward Kass introduces quantitative urine culture to define significant bacteriuria."], ["1953", "Nitrofurantoin becomes available in the US for urinary infections."], ["2025", "Gepotidacin, a new oral antibiotic class, is approved for uncomplicated UTI."]],
  cell: "Most UTIs are caused by uropathogenic E. coli that live in the gut, colonize the skin around the urethra and then ascend into the bladder. Type 1 fimbriae carry the adhesin FimH, which binds mannose on uroplakin proteins that coat bladder lining cells (urothelium). Attached bacteria can invade these cells and form protected intracellular bacterial communities, which helps explain repeat infections. Bladder cells sense bacterial lipopolysaccharide through Toll-like receptor 4 and release IL-6 and IL-8, which call in neutrophils. Neutrophils and cytokines cause pus in the urine (pyuria), inflammation and the urgency and burning of cystitis. Other causes include Staphylococcus saprophyticus, Klebsiella, Enterococcus and Proteus. Proteus makes urease, which splits urea into ammonia, raising urine pH and promoting struvite stones. Risk rises with a short female urethra, sex, pregnancy, low estrogen after menopause, catheters, stones and anything that blocks urine flow.",
  body: [["Bladder", "Inflammation of the bladder lining causes urgency, frequency, burning with urination, lower belly pain and sometimes blood in the urine."], ["Urethra", "Irritated urethral tissue makes urination painful, a symptom called dysuria."], ["Kidneys", "If bacteria travel up the ureters, the infection can become pyelonephritis with fever and flank pain."], ["Bloodstream", "In older adults, people with catheters or blocked urine flow, bacteria can enter the blood and cause urosepsis."], ["Brain (older adults)", "Older adults with infection may show new confusion or falls, though cloudy or smelly urine alone without symptoms is not proof of infection."]],
  symptoms: "Burning with urination happens because inflamed urothelium and urethra are sensitive to the passage of urine. Urgency and frequency come from irritated bladder nerves that signal fullness even when little urine is present. Lower belly pressure reflects the inflamed bladder wall. Blood in the urine comes from fragile, inflamed lining. Fever, chills and flank pain suggest the infection has reached the kidneys rather than staying in the bladder.",
  dx: "Uncomplicated cystitis in a healthy woman is often diagnosed from typical symptoms alone. Urinalysis may show leukocyte esterase (from white blood cells) and nitrites, which form when gram-negative bacteria such as E. coli convert dietary nitrate. Microscopy shows pyuria and bacteria. A urine culture identifies the organism and its antibiotic sensitivities and is used for men, pregnancy, recurrent or complicated cases. Bacteria in the urine without symptoms (asymptomatic bacteriuria) usually is not treated, except in pregnancy and before certain urologic procedures.",
  tx: "Antibiotics kill the bacteria, and the choice depends on local resistance and whether the infection is uncomplicated. Nitrofurantoin damages bacterial DNA and proteins and concentrates in the bladder. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole blocks two steps of bacterial folate synthesis. Fosfomycin blocks the first step of cell wall building. Oral beta-lactams such as cephalexin or cefpodoxime are alternatives, and fluoroquinolones are generally saved for more serious infections because of side effects. Newer agents such as gepotidacin and sulopenem are options when resistance limits choices. Phenazopyridine numbs the urinary lining for short-term relief but does not cure infection. For recurrent UTIs, clinicians may use methenamine, vaginal estrogen after menopause or preventive antibiotics.",
  drugs: ["nitrofurantoin", "trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole", "fosfomycin", "cephalexin", "cefpodoxime", "cefdinir", "cefixime", "amoxicillin-clavulanate", "ciprofloxacin", "levofloxacin", "gepotidacin", "sulopenem", "phenazopyridine", "methenamine", "estradiol-vaginal"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "SEEKS PP lists urinary pathogens: Serratia, E. coli, Enterobacter, Klebsiella, S. saprophyticus, Proteus, Pseudomonas. E. coli is by far the most common."], ["Most-tested fact", "A positive nitrite test points to gram-negative bacteria such as E. coli; leukocyte esterase means white blood cells are present."], ["Often confused with", "Pyelonephritis adds fever, flank pain and costovertebral angle tenderness. Nitrofurantoin and fosfomycin do not reach kidney tissue well, so they are not used for it."]],
  red: "Fever with shaking chills, flank pain, vomiting, confusion, low blood pressure or a UTI in pregnancy with these signs can mean kidney infection or urosepsis and needs emergency care (call 911 for severe illness).",
  fun: "Phenazopyridine, a urinary pain reliever, turns urine bright orange and can permanently stain contact lenses.",
  src: ["IDSA International Clinical Practice Guidelines for Acute Uncomplicated Cystitis and Pyelonephritis in Women (2010)", "IDSA Guideline on Complicated Urinary Tract Infections (2025)", "Kass EH. Trans Assoc Am Physicians 1956", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"pyelonephritis": {
  name: "Pyelonephritis",
  sys: "Kidneys & Urinary",
  aka: ["kidney infection", "upper urinary tract infection", "acute pyelonephritis"],
  what: "Pyelonephritis is a bacterial infection of the kidney. It usually starts as a bladder infection that climbs up to the kidney and causes fever, flank pain and feeling very sick.",
  origin: "The name comes from the Greek words for basin (pyelos, the kidney's collecting pelvis) and kidney (nephros). Physicians in the 1800s recognized kidney abscesses and scarring at autopsy, and the arrival of bacteriology linked these to gut bacteria such as E. coli. In the 1950s and 1960s Edward Kass showed that pregnant women with bacteria in their urine, even without symptoms, often went on to develop kidney infection, and that treating the bacteriuria prevented many cases. This is why pregnant women are still screened today. Antibiotics turned a disease that once caused kidney failure and death into one that most people recover from at home, although resistant bacteria have made the choice of drug harder.",
  timeline: [["1885", "Theodor Escherich describes E. coli, the main cause of kidney infection."], ["1960", "Edward Kass reports that treating bacteriuria in pregnancy prevents many kidney infections."], ["1980s", "Oral fluoroquinolones allow many adults with pyelonephritis to be treated outside the hospital."], ["2000s", "ESBL-producing gram-negative bacteria spread and make carbapenems more often necessary."]],
  cell: "Most cases are ascending infections. Uropathogenic E. coli colonizes the bladder, then reaches the kidney through the ureter, helped by vesicoureteral reflux, pregnancy hormones that relax the ureter, or obstruction. P fimbriae (pyelonephritis-associated pili) bind Gal-alpha(1-4)Gal glycolipids on kidney tubular cells and are a key virulence factor. Bacteria trigger Toll-like receptor 4 signaling in tubular cells, which releases chemokines and draws neutrophils into the interstitium and tubules. Neutrophils packed in the tubules are molded into white blood cell casts, the classic urine finding. Inflammation, abscesses and swelling of the kidney cause pain and fever. Repeated or obstructed infections can scar the kidney (chronic pyelonephritis), with blunted calyces and thyroidization of tubules. Less often, bacteria seed the kidney from the bloodstream, as with Staphylococcus aureus.",
  body: [["Kidneys", "Neutrophils invade the tubules and interstitium, causing swelling, pain and sometimes abscesses or permanent scarring."], ["Urinary tract", "A blockage such as a stone or enlarged prostate traps infected urine and can lead to a pus-filled kidney that needs drainage."], ["Bloodstream", "Bacteria can spill into the blood, causing bacteremia and urosepsis, especially in older adults and people with diabetes."], ["Whole body", "Cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6 and TNF-alpha cause high fever, chills, aches and nausea."], ["Pregnancy", "Kidney infection in pregnancy raises the risk of preterm labor and maternal sepsis."]],
  symptoms: "Fever and shaking chills come from cytokines released as the immune system fights bacteria in the kidney. Flank pain and costovertebral angle tenderness happen because the inflamed kidney swells and stretches its pain-sensitive capsule. Nausea and vomiting are common with kidney inflammation. Bladder symptoms such as burning and urgency are often present because the infection started in the bladder. Confusion or low blood pressure suggests spread to the blood.",
  dx: "Diagnosis rests on fever, flank pain and costovertebral angle tenderness plus an abnormal urinalysis showing pyuria, bacteria and sometimes white blood cell casts. A urine culture is always sent to guide antibiotic choice, and blood cultures are drawn in sicker patients. Imaging such as CT or ultrasound is used when the patient does not improve within a few days, has severe illness, a possible stone or obstruction, or an abscess is suspected. Emphysematous pyelonephritis, a gas-forming infection seen mainly in diabetes, is identified on CT.",
  tx: "Treatment uses antibiotics that reach high levels in kidney tissue. Fluoroquinolones such as ciprofloxacin or levofloxacin block bacterial DNA gyrase and are common oral choices when local resistance is low. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is used if the organism is known to be susceptible. Many clinicians give a first IV dose of a long-acting drug such as ceftriaxone. Hospitalized patients receive IV beta-lactams such as ceftriaxone, cefepime or piperacillin-tazobactam, and a carbapenem such as ertapenem or meropenem for ESBL-producing bacteria. Nitrofurantoin and fosfomycin are avoided because they do not reach the kidney well. Fluids, fever control and relief of any obstruction, sometimes by a stent or drain, are key parts of care.",
  drugs: ["ciprofloxacin", "levofloxacin", "trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole", "ceftriaxone", "cefepime", "piperacillin-tazobactam", "ertapenem", "meropenem", "gentamicin", "cefpodoxime", "amoxicillin-clavulanate", "ampicillin", "acetaminophen", "ondansetron", "normal-saline"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "White blood cell casts in the urine localize infection to the kidney rather than the bladder."], ["Memory trick", "P fimbriae for Pyelonephritis: the P pili help E. coli stick to kidney cells."], ["Often confused with", "Kidney stones cause flank pain and blood in the urine but usually no fever unless an infection is trapped behind the stone, which is an emergency."]],
  red: "High fever with flank pain plus vomiting that prevents keeping fluids down, confusion, fast breathing, low blood pressure or kidney infection with a known stone needs emergency care (call 911 for severe illness).",
  fun: "Kidney infection in pregnancy was common enough that Edward Kass's bacteriuria work made urine screening a routine part of prenatal care worldwide.",
  src: ["IDSA International Clinical Practice Guidelines for Acute Uncomplicated Cystitis and Pyelonephritis in Women (2010)", "IDSA Guideline on Complicated Urinary Tract Infections (2025)", "Kass EH. Bacteriuria and pyelonephritis of pregnancy. Arch Intern Med 1960", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"kidney-stones": {
  name: "Kidney stones",
  sys: "Kidneys & Urinary",
  aka: ["nephrolithiasis", "urolithiasis", "renal calculi", "renal colic"],
  what: "Kidney stones are hard crystals that form from minerals in urine. When a stone moves into the ureter it can block urine flow and cause severe, wave-like flank pain.",
  origin: "Stones are among the oldest known diseases: a bladder stone was found with the remains of an ancient Egyptian buried thousands of years ago. The Hippocratic Oath told physicians not to cut for stone, leaving the dangerous operation of lithotomy to specialists. For centuries traveling stone cutters removed bladder stones without anesthesia. Modern chemistry in the 1800s and 1900s identified stone types such as calcium oxalate, uric acid, struvite and cystine. A major breakthrough came in 1980 in Munich, where shock wave lithotripsy was first used to break kidney stones in a patient without surgery. Flexible ureteroscopes and lasers followed, and today most stones are treated through natural openings or tiny incisions.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Bladder stones are found in ancient Egyptian remains and described in Greek medicine."], ["1980", "Extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy is first used on a human kidney stone in Munich."], ["1980s", "Ureteroscopy and percutaneous nephrolithotomy become common stone treatments."], ["2000s", "Medical expulsive therapy with alpha blockers is widely adopted for distal ureteral stones."]],
  cell: "Stones form when urine becomes supersaturated with a salt, so crystals nucleate, grow and stick together. About 80 percent are calcium stones, mostly calcium oxalate. Risks include high urine calcium (idiopathic hypercalciuria, hyperparathyroidism), high urine oxalate (high oxalate diet, or fat malabsorption in Crohn disease where free fatty acids bind calcium so more oxalate is absorbed), low urine citrate (citrate normally binds calcium) and low urine volume. Calcium oxalate crystals often begin on Randall plaques, deposits of calcium phosphate at the tips of kidney papillae. Struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate) stones form when urease-producing bacteria such as Proteus split urea into ammonia, alkalinizing urine and building large staghorn stones. Uric acid stones form in acidic urine, as in gout, metabolic syndrome and tumor lysis. Cystine stones come from cystinuria, an inherited defect in tubular reabsorption of cystine, ornithine, lysine and arginine.",
  body: [["Ureter", "A stone lodged in the narrow ureter blocks urine and triggers spasm, causing intense colicky pain and blood in the urine."], ["Kidney", "Blocked flow causes hydronephrosis (swelling of the collecting system); long-standing blockage can damage kidney function."], ["Urinary tract infection", "Infected urine trapped behind a stone can rapidly cause sepsis, a surgical emergency needing drainage."], ["Gut", "Shared nerve pathways with the bowel cause nausea and vomiting during renal colic."], ["Bladder and urethra", "As the stone nears the bladder, pain moves toward the groin and urinary urgency and frequency appear."]],
  symptoms: "Severe flank pain that comes in waves happens because the ureter contracts against the blockage and the kidney capsule stretches as urine backs up. Pain spreads to the groin as the stone moves down, following the nerve supply of the ureter. People often cannot lie still, unlike with peritonitis. Blood in the urine comes from the stone scraping the lining. Nausea and vomiting come from shared visceral nerves. Fever suggests infection behind the stone.",
  dx: "Noncontrast CT of the abdomen and pelvis is the most sensitive test and shows nearly all stones, including uric acid stones, which do not show on plain X-ray. Ultrasound is preferred in pregnancy and children and shows hydronephrosis. Urinalysis often shows blood. Labs check kidney function and signs of infection. After a stone passes, stone analysis and a 24-hour urine collection for volume, calcium, oxalate, citrate, uric acid and sodium guide prevention. Crystal shapes on microscopy are classic: envelope-shaped calcium oxalate, coffin-lid struvite and hexagonal cystine.",
  tx: "Pain control is first. NSAIDs such as ketorolac block prostaglandins that increase kidney blood flow and ureteral spasm and are first-line; opioids are added if needed. Antiemetics such as ondansetron treat nausea. Alpha-1 blockers such as tamsulosin relax ureteral smooth muscle and help many distal stones pass. Large or stuck stones are treated with shock wave lithotripsy, ureteroscopy with laser, or percutaneous nephrolithotomy. A blocked, infected kidney needs urgent drainage with a stent or nephrostomy plus antibiotics. Prevention includes high fluid intake, less salt and animal protein, and normal dietary calcium. Thiazide diuretics lower urine calcium, potassium citrate raises urine citrate and pH, allopurinol lowers uric acid, tiopronin treats cystinuria, and acetohydroxamic acid blocks urease in struvite disease.",
  drugs: ["ketorolac", "ibuprofen", "morphine", "hydromorphone", "ondansetron", "tamsulosin", "hydrochlorothiazide", "chlorthalidone", "indapamide", "potassium-citrate", "allopurinol", "tiopronin", "acetohydroxamic-acid", "ceftriaxone", "ciprofloxacin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Struvite stones grow in alkaline urine made by urease-positive bugs like Proteus, often forming staghorn stones."], ["Most-tested fact", "Most kidney stones are calcium oxalate; uric acid stones are radiolucent on plain X-ray but visible on CT."], ["Often confused with", "Abdominal aortic aneurysm can mimic renal colic in older adults and must not be missed."], ["Classic crystals", "Envelope for calcium oxalate, coffin lid for struvite, hexagon for cystine."]],
  red: "Stone pain with fever or chills, inability to urinate, a single kidney, or pain that cannot be controlled needs emergency care. An infected obstructed kidney can cause sepsis quickly (call 911 for severe illness).",
  fun: "Shock wave lithotripsy grew out of research on damage that raindrops and dust caused to supersonic aircraft, which showed how shock waves can shatter solid objects.",
  src: ["AUA/Endourological Society Surgical Management of Stones Guideline (2016)", "AUA Medical Management of Kidney Stones Guideline (2014)", "Chaussy C et al. Lancet 1980", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"nephrotic-syndrome": {
  name: "Nephrotic syndrome",
  sys: "Kidneys & Urinary",
  aka: ["nephrosis", "minimal change disease", "FSGS", "membranous nephropathy"],
  what: "Nephrotic syndrome is a kidney disorder where the kidney filters leak large amounts of protein into the urine. Low blood protein causes swelling, especially around the eyes, legs and belly.",
  origin: "In 1827 London physician Richard Bright linked swelling (dropsy) and protein in the urine to diseased kidneys found at autopsy, and for a century kidney disease was simply called Bright disease. In the early 1900s physicians separated the inflammatory nephritis from a noninflammatory condition called nephrosis, later named nephrotic syndrome. Before steroids, many children with nephrotic syndrome died of infections. In the 1950s corticosteroids were found to induce remission in most children, which defined minimal change disease. Electron microscopy then showed effacement of the podocyte foot processes. In 1998 nephrin, a key slit diaphragm protein, was identified as the gene behind congenital nephrotic syndrome of the Finnish type, and in 2009 antibodies against the PLA2R receptor were found to cause most primary membranous nephropathy.",
  timeline: [["1827", "Richard Bright links edema and albuminuria to kidney disease."], ["1950s", "Corticosteroids are shown to induce remission in childhood nephrotic syndrome."], ["1998", "The nephrin gene is identified as the cause of congenital nephrotic syndrome of the Finnish type."], ["2009", "Anti-PLA2R antibodies are discovered in primary membranous nephropathy."]],
  cell: "The glomerular filter has three layers: fenestrated endothelium, the glomerular basement membrane and podocytes, whose interlocking foot processes are bridged by the slit diaphragm (nephrin, podocin). Injury to podocytes is the central event. In minimal change disease, likely immune signals (and in some cases anti-nephrin antibodies) cause diffuse foot process effacement with normal light microscopy. In focal segmental glomerulosclerosis, podocyte loss leads to scarring of parts of some glomeruli; causes include APOL1 risk variants, HIV, obesity, heroin and genetic podocyte defects. In membranous nephropathy, antibodies against PLA2R on podocytes form subepithelial immune deposits that activate complement and damage the podocyte. When the barrier fails, albumin floods the urine. Low blood albumin lowers oncotic pressure and the kidney retains sodium, causing edema. The liver makes more lipoproteins, causing hyperlipidemia. Loss of antithrombin III and other proteins causes clotting, and loss of immunoglobulins raises infection risk.",
  body: [["Kidneys", "Leaky glomeruli lose large amounts of protein, and some forms scar over time and progress to chronic kidney disease."], ["Fluid balance", "Low albumin and sodium retention cause soft swelling around the eyes, legs, scrotum or labia, and fluid in the belly or chest."], ["Blood vessels and clotting", "Loss of anticoagulant proteins such as antithrombin III makes blood clot easily, causing renal vein thrombosis, DVT or pulmonary embolism."], ["Lipids", "The liver overproduces cholesterol-rich lipoproteins, causing high cholesterol and fatty casts (oval fat bodies) in urine."], ["Immune defense", "Loss of immunoglobulins and complement raises the risk of infections, including spontaneous bacterial peritonitis in children."]],
  symptoms: "Swelling, often first noticed as puffy eyelids in the morning, happens because low albumin lets fluid leave the blood vessels and the kidney holds on to salt. Foamy urine comes from the protein in it. Weight gain is from retained fluid. Fatigue and poor appetite are common. Clots in the legs or kidney veins and repeated infections can be the first sign of the protein losses.",
  dx: "Nephrotic syndrome is defined by heavy proteinuria (more than 3.5 grams per day in adults, or a high urine protein-to-creatinine ratio), low serum albumin, edema and usually high lipids, often with little or no blood in the urine. A urine protein-to-creatinine ratio or 24-hour collection measures the protein loss. Blood tests look for causes such as diabetes, lupus, hepatitis B or C, HIV and anti-PLA2R antibodies. A kidney biopsy is usually done in adults to identify minimal change disease, FSGS or membranous nephropathy. In children, a typical case is usually treated with steroids first without biopsy.",
  tx: "Treatment targets the cause and the complications. Corticosteroids such as prednisone suppress the immune signals that injure podocytes and induce remission in most children with minimal change disease. Calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, cyclosporine), rituximab (anti-CD20 B-cell depletion), cyclophosphamide and mycophenolate are used for steroid-resistant or relapsing disease and for membranous nephropathy. ACE inhibitors or ARBs lower pressure inside the glomerulus and reduce protein loss; SGLT2 inhibitors are also used in proteinuric kidney disease. Loop diuretics remove fluid, sometimes with albumin in severe cases. Statins treat high cholesterol, and anticoagulants are used for clots or high clot risk. A low-salt diet and vaccines against pneumococcus and other infections are important.",
  drugs: ["prednisone", "prednisolone", "tacrolimus", "cyclosporine", "rituximab", "cyclophosphamide", "mycophenolate", "lisinopril", "losartan", "furosemide", "metolazone", "spironolactone", "albumin-human", "dapagliflozin", "atorvastatin", "enoxaparin", "heparin", "apixaban", "warfarin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Nephrotic is all about prOteinuria (lots of protein); nephrItic is about Inflammation with blood in the urine."], ["Most-tested fact", "Minimal change disease is the most common cause in children, shows foot process effacement only on electron microscopy and responds to steroids."], ["Associations", "Membranous nephropathy: anti-PLA2R and spike-and-dome deposits. FSGS: APOL1, HIV, heroin, obesity. Minimal change: Hodgkin lymphoma and NSAIDs."], ["Often confused with", "Heart failure and liver cirrhosis also cause edema and low albumin, but they do not cause heavy proteinuria."]],
  red: "Sudden shortness of breath or chest pain (possible pulmonary embolism), a swollen painful leg, belly pain with fever, very little urine or rapid swelling with breathing trouble needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Before steroids and antibiotics, childhood nephrotic syndrome was often fatal from infections; a measles infection sometimes caused unexplained remissions, a clue that the immune system drives the disease.",
  src: ["KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Glomerular Diseases (2021)", "Bright R. Reports of Medical Cases (1827)", "Beck LH et al. N Engl J Med 2009", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"bph": {
  name: "Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)",
  sys: "Kidneys & Urinary",
  aka: ["BPH", "enlarged prostate", "benign prostatic enlargement", "lower urinary tract symptoms", "LUTS"],
  what: "Benign prostatic hyperplasia is a noncancerous enlargement of the prostate that is very common as men age. The growing gland squeezes the urethra, making urine flow weak and causing frequent urination, especially at night.",
  origin: "Physicians have long described old men who could not empty the bladder, and for centuries the main treatment was passing a catheter. In the late 1700s John Hunter observed that castration shrinks the prostate, an early clue that male hormones drive its growth. Open prostatectomy developed in the late 1800s and early 1900s. In the 1920s and 1930s the resectoscope made transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP) possible, which became the standard surgery for decades. Drug therapy arrived later: alpha blockers were adopted for urinary symptoms in the 1980s and 1990s, and finasteride, the first 5-alpha reductase inhibitor, was approved in 1992. Its development was inspired by research on boys in the Dominican Republic with inherited 5-alpha reductase deficiency, who had small prostates.",
  timeline: [["1780s", "John Hunter notes that castration causes the prostate to shrink."], ["1930s", "The modern resectoscope makes transurethral resection of the prostate practical."], ["1992", "Finasteride is approved in the US as the first 5-alpha reductase inhibitor for BPH."], ["1997", "Tamsulosin, a uroselective alpha-1 blocker, is approved in the US."], ["2011", "Tadalafil is approved for BPH symptoms."]],
  cell: "BPH is a true hyperplasia (more cells, not cancer) of both stromal smooth muscle cells and glandular epithelium, mainly in the transition zone around the urethra. The key driver is dihydrotestosterone (DHT). Testosterone is converted to DHT by 5-alpha reductase type 2 in prostate stromal cells. DHT binds androgen receptors more strongly than testosterone and turns on growth factors such as FGF, which stimulate stromal and epithelial growth and reduce cell death. Over years, nodules form and compress the urethra (the static part of the obstruction). The prostate stroma and capsule also contain smooth muscle with many alpha-1A adrenergic receptors, and sympathetic tone tightens this muscle (the dynamic part). The bladder works harder against the blockage, its detrusor muscle thickens and becomes overactive, and later it may weaken. Unlike prostate cancer, BPH arises centrally and is not a premalignant lesion.",
  body: [["Prostate", "Nodules of stromal and glandular tissue enlarge the transition zone and compress the urethra."], ["Bladder", "The detrusor muscle thickens (trabeculation), becomes irritable and may form outpouchings (diverticula); late in disease it can fail to empty."], ["Kidneys", "Chronic retention can back pressure up the ureters, causing hydronephrosis and kidney injury."], ["Urinary tract infection", "Leftover urine in the bladder lets bacteria grow and raises the risk of infections and bladder stones."], ["Sleep and quality of life", "Waking at night to urinate disturbs sleep and raises fall risk in older men."]],
  symptoms: "A weak stream, hesitancy, straining and dribbling happen because the enlarged gland narrows the urethra. The feeling of incomplete emptying comes from urine left behind. Frequency, urgency and waking at night to urinate (nocturia) come from an irritable, thickened bladder and a smaller usable bladder volume. Sudden inability to urinate (acute urinary retention) can be triggered by cold medicines with decongestants or anticholinergics, alcohol or anesthesia, which tighten the prostate or weaken bladder contraction.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is based on symptoms, often scored with the International Prostate Symptom Score (IPSS). Digital rectal exam shows a smooth, firm, rubbery, symmetrically enlarged prostate, unlike the hard nodule that raises concern for cancer. Urinalysis checks for infection and blood. PSA may be mildly raised by BPH and is checked when cancer screening is appropriate. Post-void residual by bladder ultrasound and uroflowmetry measure emptying and flow. Kidney function is checked if retention is suspected.",
  tx: "Treatment depends on how bothersome symptoms are. Alpha-1 blockers such as tamsulosin, alfuzosin, silodosin, doxazosin and terazosin relax smooth muscle in the prostate and bladder neck and improve flow within days. 5-alpha reductase inhibitors (finasteride, dutasteride) block DHT production and shrink the gland over months, lowering the risk of retention and surgery; they also lower PSA by about half. Combination therapy works best for large prostates. Tadalafil, a PDE-5 inhibitor, relaxes prostate and bladder smooth muscle. Antimuscarinics or mirabegron may be added for storage symptoms when emptying is adequate. Procedures include TURP, laser enucleation and minimally invasive options such as prostatic urethral lift and water vapor therapy. Limiting evening fluids, caffeine and alcohol helps.",
  drugs: ["tamsulosin", "alfuzosin", "silodosin", "doxazosin", "terazosin", "finasteride", "dutasteride", "dutasteride-tamsulosin", "tadalafil", "mirabegron", "solifenacin", "tolterodine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "BPH arises in the transition (periurethral) zone; prostate cancer usually arises in the peripheral zone, which is why cancer can be felt on rectal exam."], ["Memory trick", "Alpha blockers act fast (relax), 5-ARIs act slow (shrink)."], ["Watch out", "First-dose orthostatic hypotension is a classic effect of nonselective alpha blockers like terazosin and doxazosin; floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery is linked to tamsulosin."], ["Key fact", "Finasteride and dutasteride cut PSA roughly in half, so PSA values are interpreted with that in mind."]],
  red: "Being unable to urinate at all with a painful, full bladder, urinary symptoms with fever and chills, or very little urine output needs emergency care.",
  fun: "Finasteride was developed after scientists studied a community in the Dominican Republic where boys with inherited 5-alpha reductase deficiency had small prostates and never went bald.",
  src: ["AUA Guideline: Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to BPH (2021, amended 2023)", "Imperato-McGinley J et al. Science 1974", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology"]
},
"overactive-bladder": {
  name: "Overactive bladder",
  sys: "Kidneys & Urinary",
  aka: ["OAB", "urge incontinence", "urgency urinary incontinence", "detrusor overactivity"],
  what: "Overactive bladder is a condition where the bladder sends sudden, hard-to-delay urges to urinate. It causes frequent bathroom trips, waking at night to urinate and sometimes leaking before reaching the toilet.",
  origin: "Urinary urgency and leakage have been described for centuries, but they were often seen as an unavoidable part of aging. In the mid 1900s urodynamic testing let doctors measure bladder pressure and see involuntary contractions of the detrusor muscle. Antimuscarinic drugs such as oxybutynin became available in the 1970s, followed by more bladder-focused agents in the late 1990s and 2000s. The International Continence Society standardized the term overactive bladder in 2002 as urgency, usually with frequency and nocturia, with or without leakage. A new drug class, beta-3 agonists, arrived with mirabegron in 2012. Onabotulinumtoxin A injections into the bladder were approved for overactive bladder in 2013, and nerve stimulation therapies offered further options.",
  timeline: [["1970s", "Oxybutynin becomes available as an antimuscarinic bladder relaxant."], ["2002", "The International Continence Society standardizes the definition of overactive bladder."], ["2012", "Mirabegron becomes the first beta-3 agonist approved for overactive bladder."], ["2013", "Onabotulinumtoxin A is approved for overactive bladder in adults."], ["2020", "Vibegron, a second beta-3 agonist, is approved."]],
  cell: "Normal bladder filling relies on sympathetic signals: norepinephrine acts on beta-3 adrenergic receptors in the detrusor muscle to relax it. Emptying relies on parasympathetic pelvic nerves releasing acetylcholine onto M3 muscarinic receptors (with M2 also present), which raises intracellular calcium and contracts the detrusor. In overactive bladder, the detrusor contracts involuntarily during filling. Proposed causes include changes in detrusor smooth muscle cells that spread contractions (myogenic theory), increased sensory signaling from the urothelium, which releases ATP and acetylcholine that excite afferent nerves (urothelial theory), and reduced inhibition from the brain and spinal cord (neurogenic theory), as in stroke, Parkinson disease, multiple sclerosis and spinal cord injury. Aging, obesity, low estrogen and bladder outlet obstruction from an enlarged prostate also contribute. The result is urgency at low volumes, frequent small voids and sometimes leakage.",
  body: [["Bladder", "The detrusor muscle contracts before the bladder is full, creating sudden urgency and small, frequent voids."], ["Sleep", "Waking two or more times a night to urinate fragments sleep and causes daytime tiredness."], ["Skin", "Repeated leakage can irritate the skin around the genitals and raise infection risk."], ["Bones and falls", "Rushing to the bathroom, especially at night, raises the risk of falls and hip fractures in older adults."], ["Mental health and social life", "Fear of leaking can lead to limiting travel, exercise and social activity, and is linked with anxiety and depression."]],
  symptoms: "Urgency, the main symptom, happens because sensory nerves signal fullness too early and the detrusor contracts on its own. Frequency follows because each void empties a small volume. Nocturia occurs when these urges interrupt sleep. Urge incontinence happens when the detrusor contraction overpowers the urethral sphincter before the person reaches a toilet. It differs from stress incontinence, which is leakage with coughing or sneezing from a weak sphincter or pelvic floor.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical and based on symptoms of urgency with frequency and nocturia. A bladder diary records fluid intake, voids and leaks. Urinalysis rules out infection and blood. Post-void residual measurement checks for incomplete emptying, which would point toward obstruction or weak bladder contraction instead. Medications such as diuretics and conditions such as diabetes, which increase urine output, are reviewed. Urodynamic testing is used in complex cases or before invasive treatments and may show involuntary detrusor contractions.",
  tx: "Behavioral therapy is first-line: bladder training to stretch intervals between voids, urge suppression, pelvic floor muscle exercises, limiting caffeine and alcohol, timing fluids and weight loss. Antimuscarinics (oxybutynin, tolterodine, solifenacin, darifenacin, trospium, fesoterodine) block M3 receptors to reduce involuntary contractions; they can cause dry mouth and constipation, and in older adults strong anticholinergic burden is linked to confusion, so many clinicians prefer other options. Beta-3 agonists (mirabegron, vibegron) relax the detrusor during filling without anticholinergic effects. Vaginal estrogen can help after menopause. For refractory cases, onabotulinumtoxin A injected into the bladder wall blocks acetylcholine release, and sacral or tibial nerve stimulation modulates bladder reflexes.",
  drugs: ["mirabegron", "vibegron", "oxybutynin", "oxybutynin-transdermal", "solifenacin", "tolterodine", "fesoterodine", "darifenacin", "trospium", "onabotulinumtoxina", "estradiol-vaginal"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Detrusor contraction is driven by acetylcholine on M3 receptors; relaxation during filling is helped by beta-3 receptors."], ["Memory trick", "Urge leak: Detrusor is too eager. Stress leak: Sphincter is too weak."], ["Watch out", "Antimuscarinics are avoided in narrow-angle glaucoma and urinary retention, and the Beers Criteria flag strong anticholinergics in older adults."], ["Key fact", "Trospium is a quaternary amine that crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, so it causes less confusion."]],
  red: "Sudden loss of bladder control with leg weakness, numbness in the groin or saddle area, or back pain can mean spinal cord or cauda equina compression and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Botulinum toxin, one of the most potent toxins known, is used in tiny amounts to quiet overactive bladders for many months per treatment.",
  src: ["AUA/SUFU Guideline on the Diagnosis and Treatment of Idiopathic Overactive Bladder (2024)", "Abrams P et al. International Continence Society Standardisation of Terminology. Neurourol Urodyn 2002", "American Geriatrics Society Beers Criteria (2023)", "Campbell-Walsh-Wein Urology"]
},
"erectile-dysfunction": {
  name: "Erectile dysfunction",
  sys: "Kidneys & Urinary",
  aka: ["ED", "impotence"],
  what: "Erectile dysfunction is trouble getting or keeping an erection firm enough for sex. It is common with age and is often an early sign of blood vessel disease, diabetes or other health conditions.",
  origin: "Erectile problems appear in ancient Egyptian, Greek and Indian writings, and remedies ranged from herbs to charms. For much of the 1900s ED was thought to be mostly psychological. In the 1970s penile implants offered a surgical solution. In 1983 British physiologist Giles Brindley famously showed at a urology meeting that injecting a smooth muscle relaxant into the penis produced an erection, proving that the problem was often vascular. In the 1980s and 1990s researchers including Robert Furchgott, Louis Ignarro and Ferid Murad showed that nitric oxide is a signaling molecule that relaxes blood vessels, earning the 1998 Nobel Prize. That same year sildenafil, a PDE-5 inhibitor first studied for angina, was approved for ED and transformed care. Tadalafil and vardenafil followed in 2003.",
  timeline: [["1970s", "Inflatable penile prostheses are introduced."], ["1983", "Giles Brindley demonstrates drug-induced erection by penile injection."], ["1995", "Alprostadil penile injection is approved in the US for ED."], ["1998", "Sildenafil is approved, and the Nobel Prize honors the discovery of nitric oxide signaling."], ["2003", "Tadalafil and vardenafil are approved in the US."]],
  cell: "An erection is a vascular event controlled by nerves. Sexual stimulation activates parasympathetic pelvic nerves (cavernous nerves), whose neurons release nitric oxide (NO) from neuronal NO synthase; endothelial cells add more NO through endothelial NO synthase. NO activates soluble guanylate cyclase in smooth muscle of the corpora cavernosa, raising cyclic GMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G, which lowers intracellular calcium and relaxes the smooth muscle. Arterial inflow fills the sinusoidal spaces, which press the outflow veins against the tough tunica albuginea and trap blood (veno-occlusion). PDE-5 breaks down cGMP and ends the erection. ED results when any step fails: endothelial dysfunction from atherosclerosis, diabetes, smoking and high blood pressure lowers NO; diabetic or surgical nerve damage (for example after prostatectomy) blocks signaling; low testosterone reduces desire and NO synthase activity; fibrosis of cavernosal tissue causes venous leak; and sympathetic overdrive from anxiety tightens smooth muscle.",
  body: [["Penis", "Smooth muscle in the corpora cavernosa fails to relax or blood leaks out through veins, so the erection is weak or does not last."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Penile arteries are small, so ED often appears years before a heart attack or stroke and is a warning sign of atherosclerosis."], ["Nerves", "Diabetes, spinal cord injury, multiple sclerosis or pelvic surgery can damage the nerves that start an erection."], ["Hormones", "Low testosterone, high prolactin or thyroid disease can lower desire and contribute to ED."], ["Mental health", "Performance anxiety, depression and relationship stress both cause ED and result from it."]],
  symptoms: "Difficulty getting an erection reflects too little NO and smooth muscle relaxation, often from narrowed arteries or damaged nerves. Losing an erection early suggests venous leak. A gradual onset with lost morning erections points to a physical (organic) cause, while sudden onset with normal morning erections points more to psychological factors. Low desire suggests a hormonal cause such as low testosterone.",
  dx: "Diagnosis rests on the sexual, medical and medication history, often with a questionnaire such as the International Index of Erectile Function. Common drug causes include some antihypertensives, SSRIs, antiandrogens and opioids. The exam checks blood pressure, pulses, testicles and signs of low testosterone or Peyronie disease. Labs typically include glucose or A1c, lipids and morning testosterone. Because ED often signals vascular disease, cardiovascular risk is assessed. Penile duplex ultrasound is used in selected cases.",
  tx: "Treating the cause is part of care: improving diabetes control, stopping smoking, exercise, weight loss, reviewing medicines and counseling. PDE-5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil, vardenafil, avanafil) block cGMP breakdown, so they strengthen the body's own NO signal and need sexual stimulation to work. They must not be combined with nitrates or riociguat because the combined rise in cGMP can cause dangerous low blood pressure. Testosterone therapy is used when true hypogonadism is confirmed. Alprostadil, a prostaglandin E1 analog, raises cAMP to relax smooth muscle and is given by penile injection or urethral pellet. Vacuum devices draw blood into the penis. Penile implants are a reliable surgical option. Sex therapy helps when psychological factors play a role.",
  drugs: ["sildenafil", "tadalafil", "vardenafil", "avanafil", "alprostadil", "testosterone", "testosterone-cypionate"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "PDE-5 inhibitors plus nitrates can cause severe hypotension; this combination is contraindicated."], ["Memory trick", "Point and Shoot: Parasympathetic for erection (Point), Sympathetic for ejaculation (Shoot)."], ["Key fact", "ED can be an early warning of coronary artery disease because penile arteries are smaller and narrow first."], ["Side effect clue", "Sildenafil can cause a blue tint to vision by also blocking PDE-6 in the retina; tadalafil is linked to back pain."]],
  red: "An erection lasting more than four hours (priapism), chest pain during sex, or sudden vision or hearing loss after a PDE-5 inhibitor needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Sildenafil was originally tested by Pfizer researchers in the UK as a treatment for angina; its effect on erections showed up as a side effect in trial volunteers.",
  src: ["AUA Guideline on Erectile Dysfunction (2018)", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1998 (Furchgott, Ignarro, Murad)", "Goldstein I et al. Oral sildenafil in the treatment of erectile dysfunction. N Engl J Med 1998", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"prostate-cancer": {
  name: "Prostate cancer",
  sys: "Kidneys & Urinary",
  aka: ["prostate adenocarcinoma", "CRPC", "castration-resistant prostate cancer"],
  what: "Prostate cancer is a cancer that starts in the prostate gland, which helps make semen. Many prostate cancers grow slowly and may never cause harm, while some grow fast and spread, most often to bone.",
  origin: "Prostate cancer was considered rare in the 1800s, partly because few men lived long enough to develop it. In 1941 Charles Huggins and Clarence Hodges at the University of Chicago showed that removing the testicles or giving estrogen made metastatic prostate cancer shrink, the first proof that a cancer could be controlled with hormones. Huggins shared the 1966 Nobel Prize. In 1966 pathologist Donald Gleason created the grading system that still bears his name. Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) was purified in the late 1970s and became widely used for monitoring and then screening in the late 1980s and 1990s. GnRH agonists such as leuprolide replaced surgical castration for many men. Since 2010 a wave of new treatments has arrived: sipuleucel-T, abiraterone, enzalutamide, radium-223, PARP inhibitors and PSMA-targeted radioligand therapy.",
  timeline: [["1941", "Huggins and Hodges show that lowering androgens makes prostate cancer regress."], ["1966", "Donald Gleason publishes his grading system; Huggins receives the Nobel Prize."], ["1986", "The FDA approves the PSA test for monitoring prostate cancer."], ["2011", "Abiraterone is approved for metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer."], ["2022", "Lutetium-177 PSMA-617 radioligand therapy is approved."]],
  cell: "Almost all prostate cancers are adenocarcinomas from glandular cells, usually in the peripheral zone. Growth depends on the androgen receptor (AR): testosterone and DHT bind AR, which moves to the nucleus and switches on genes for growth and survival, including PSA (KLK3). Precursor lesions (high-grade prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia) acquire changes such as the TMPRSS2-ERG gene fusion (in about half of cases), which places the ERG oncogene under androgen control, loss of the PTEN tumor suppressor that activates PI3K-AKT signaling, and NKX3.1 loss. Inherited mutations in BRCA2, BRCA1, other DNA repair genes, HOXB13 and Lynch syndrome genes raise risk. Androgen deprivation works at first, but cancers become castration resistant by amplifying AR, mutating it, making splice variants such as AR-V7 that lack the hormone-binding domain, or making androgens inside the tumor. Cancer cells spread to bone, where they stimulate osteoblasts, forming osteoblastic (bone-building) metastases.",
  body: [["Prostate", "Early cancer usually causes no symptoms; larger tumors can press on the urethra or invade the seminal vesicles and bladder base."], ["Bones", "Spread to the spine, pelvis and ribs causes pain, fractures and osteoblastic lesions that raise alkaline phosphatase."], ["Spinal cord", "Spine metastases can compress the spinal cord, causing back pain, leg weakness and loss of bladder control."], ["Lymph nodes", "Pelvic lymph nodes are a common early site of spread and can cause leg swelling."], ["Kidneys and urinary tract", "Tumor can block the ureters, causing hydronephrosis and kidney failure."], ["Whole body", "Advanced disease causes fatigue, weight loss and anemia; hormone therapy itself causes hot flashes, bone loss and muscle loss."]],
  symptoms: "Most early prostate cancers cause no symptoms because they grow in the outer zone away from the urethra, which is why they are often found by PSA testing. Urinary symptoms are more often from BPH, but can occur when a tumor presses on the urethra. Bone pain, especially in the back and hips, happens when cancer spreads to bone. Leg weakness or numbness can signal spinal cord compression. Blood in the semen or urine is less common.",
  dx: "PSA testing is used for screening after shared decision-making, typically for men aged 55 to 69, with earlier discussion for Black men and those with a strong family history or BRCA mutations. A digital rectal exam may feel a hard, irregular nodule. Prostate MRI helps target biopsy. Diagnosis requires a biopsy, and the tumor is graded by the Gleason score and Grade Group, which strongly predict behavior. Staging may use bone scan, CT or PSMA PET imaging. Germline genetic testing is recommended for many men with high-risk or metastatic disease.",
  tx: "Treatment matches risk and the person's goals. Low-risk cancer is often watched with active surveillance using PSA, MRI and repeat biopsies. Localized disease can be treated with radical prostatectomy or radiation, sometimes with short-term hormone therapy. Androgen deprivation therapy is the backbone for advanced disease: GnRH agonists (leuprolide, goserelin) cause an initial testosterone flare before shutting the pituitary signal down, while GnRH antagonists (degarelix, relugolix) lower testosterone quickly without a flare. Androgen receptor pathway inhibitors add power: abiraterone blocks CYP17 androgen synthesis (given with prednisone), while enzalutamide, apalutamide and darolutamide block the receptor. Docetaxel and cabazitaxel are taxane chemotherapy. Radium-223 targets bone metastases, lutetium-177 PSMA-617 delivers radiation to PSMA-positive cells, PARP inhibitors treat BRCA-mutated disease, and sipuleucel-T is a cell-based vaccine. Denosumab or zoledronic acid protect bone.",
  drugs: ["leuprolide", "goserelin", "triptorelin", "degarelix", "relugolix", "abiraterone", "enzalutamide", "apalutamide", "darolutamide", "bicalutamide", "docetaxel", "cabazitaxel", "radium-ra-223-dichloride", "lutetium-lu-177-vipivotide-tetraxetan", "olaparib", "rucaparib", "sipuleucel-t", "prednisone", "denosumab", "zoledronic-acid"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Prostate cancer arises mostly in the peripheral zone and spreads to bone as osteoblastic metastases with raised alkaline phosphatase."], ["Memory trick", "GnRH agonists (leuprolide) Flare first, then Fall; antagonists (degarelix, relugolix) drop testosterone right away."], ["Key fact", "Huggins proved in 1941 that prostate cancer depends on androgens, which is why androgen deprivation remains the backbone of advanced treatment."], ["Often confused with", "BPH causes a smooth, rubbery, enlarged prostate in the transition zone; cancer feels hard and nodular."]],
  red: "New back pain with leg weakness, numbness or loss of bladder or bowel control in someone with prostate cancer can mean spinal cord compression and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Prostate cancer is found in a large share of older men at autopsy who never knew they had it, which is a big reason active surveillance is safe for many low-risk cancers.",
  src: ["NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Prostate Cancer", "AUA/ASTRO Clinically Localized Prostate Cancer Guideline (2022)", "USPSTF Prostate Cancer Screening Recommendation (2018)", "Huggins C, Hodges CV. Cancer Res 1941", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"rhabdomyolysis": {
  name: "Rhabdomyolysis",
  sys: "Kidneys & Urinary",
  aka: ["rhabdo", "crush syndrome", "myoglobinuria"],
  what: "Rhabdomyolysis is the rapid breakdown of skeletal muscle. Damaged muscle releases its contents into the blood, including myoglobin and potassium, which can injure the kidneys and disturb the heart rhythm.",
  origin: "Dark urine after severe muscle injury was noted in the early 1900s, including after earthquakes and wartime injuries. The modern understanding began in 1941, when Eric Bywaters and Desmond Beall in London described people pulled from bombed buildings during the Blitz who seemed well at first but then developed kidney failure and died. They found pigmented casts in the kidney tubules and linked them to substances released from crushed muscle, later identified as myoglobin. This crush syndrome shaped disaster medicine, leading to early aggressive fluids for trapped victims, sometimes even before they are freed. Later, doctors recognized many nontraumatic causes, including extreme exercise, heat illness, seizures, drugs, alcohol, statins and inherited muscle disorders.",
  timeline: [["1941", "Bywaters and Beall describe crush syndrome and kidney failure in Blitz victims."], ["1980s", "Studies of crush victims support starting IV fluids early, even before the person is freed."], ["2000s", "Statin-associated rhabdomyolysis, especially with certain drug combinations, becomes widely recognized."]],
  cell: "Muscle injury from crushing, ischemia, extreme exertion, heat, toxins or genetic defects depletes ATP inside muscle cells. Without ATP, the sodium-potassium ATPase and calcium ATPase pumps fail. Sodium and calcium flood into the cell, and the high calcium activates proteases (calpains) and phospholipase A2, damages mitochondria and creates reactive oxygen species. The cell membrane breaks down and releases potassium, phosphate, urate, creatine kinase (CK) and myoglobin. In the kidney, myoglobin is filtered and harms the tubules in three ways: it causes vasoconstriction, it precipitates with Tamm-Horsfall protein to form obstructing casts (worse in acidic urine and low volume), and its heme iron drives oxidative injury and lipid peroxidation of tubular cells. This leads to acute tubular necrosis. Calcium deposits into injured muscle, causing early low calcium, and can be released later in recovery. Swollen muscle inside tight fascia can cause compartment syndrome, and tissue factor release can trigger DIC.",
  body: [["Muscles", "Injured muscle swells, aches and weakens; swelling inside fascial compartments can cut off blood flow (compartment syndrome)."], ["Kidneys", "Myoglobin forms tubular casts and causes oxidative tubular injury, leading to acute kidney injury that may need dialysis."], ["Heart", "Potassium released from muscle can cause hyperkalemia and life-threatening arrhythmias; low calcium adds to the risk."], ["Blood", "Released tissue factor can trigger disseminated intravascular coagulation, and fluid shifts into damaged muscle can cause low blood volume."], ["Liver", "Released proteases can raise liver enzymes, which are often mildly elevated."]],
  symptoms: "Muscle pain, tenderness and weakness come from the injured, swollen muscle fibers, although many patients, especially after drug use or a prolonged fall, have few muscle symptoms. Dark, tea- or cola-colored urine comes from filtered myoglobin. Low urine output signals kidney injury. Palpitations or cardiac arrest can come from high potassium. Swollen, tense limbs with severe pain suggest compartment syndrome.",
  dx: "The key lab is a very high creatine kinase, usually at least five times the upper limit of normal and often far higher. Urine dipstick is positive for blood because the test reacts with heme in myoglobin, but microscopy shows few or no red blood cells, a classic clue. Labs also show high potassium, high phosphate, low calcium early, high uric acid and rising creatinine. An ECG looks for signs of hyperkalemia. Testing searches for the cause, such as drug screens, thyroid tests and, in recurrent cases, genetic muscle disorders.",
  tx: "The core treatment is early, aggressive IV fluids with crystalloids such as normal saline or lactated Ringer's to keep urine flowing and wash out myoglobin, with careful monitoring for fluid overload. Bicarbonate to alkalinize urine and mannitol are sometimes used, but evidence for them is limited. Hyperkalemia is treated urgently: IV calcium stabilizes the heart membrane, insulin with dextrose and albuterol shift potassium into cells, and binders such as sodium zirconium cyclosilicate or patiromer remove it. Dialysis is used for severe kidney injury, dangerous potassium or fluid overload. Compartment syndrome needs surgical fasciotomy. The cause is addressed, for example cooling for heat stroke, stopping statins, and dantrolene for malignant hyperthermia. In crush injuries, fluids may begin before the victim is freed.",
  drugs: ["normal-saline", "lactated-ringers", "plasma-lyte", "sodium-bicarbonate", "mannitol", "calcium-gluconate", "calcium-chloride", "regular-insulin", "dextrose", "albuterol", "sodium-zirconium-cyclosilicate", "patiromer", "sodium-polystyrene-sulfonate", "dantrolene"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Urine dipstick positive for blood with no red blood cells on microscopy suggests myoglobinuria (or hemoglobinuria)."], ["Memory trick", "Rhabdo spills the 3 Ps from muscle: Potassium, Phosphate and Pigment (myoglobin), plus CK."], ["Classic causes", "Crush injury, long immobilization after a fall, extreme exercise, heat stroke, seizures, cocaine and other stimulants, alcohol, statins (especially with gemfibrozil) and hypokalemia."], ["EMS fact", "In crush injuries, sudden release of a trapped limb can flood the circulation with potassium and acid, so fluids and cardiac monitoring are started before extrication when possible."]],
  red: "Dark brown urine with muscle pain after intense exercise, heat, a long time on the floor, a crush injury or drug use, little or no urine, a tense swollen limb, or palpitations needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Military recruits and athletes can develop rhabdomyolysis from extreme workouts, which is why some high-intensity training programs warn about doing too much too soon.",
  src: ["Bywaters EGL, Beall D. Crush injuries with impairment of renal function. BMJ 1941", "Bosch X, Poch E, Grau JM. Rhabdomyolysis and acute kidney injury. N Engl J Med 2009", "KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for Acute Kidney Injury (2012)", "Tintinalli's Emergency Medicine"]
},
"sepsis": {
  name: "Sepsis and septic shock",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["septicemia", "blood poisoning", "septic shock", "severe sepsis", "urosepsis"],
  what: "Sepsis is a life-threatening reaction where the body's response to an infection injures its own organs. In septic shock, blood pressure falls so low that vital organs do not get enough blood.",
  origin: "Hippocrates used the word sepsis to describe rotting of flesh. In the 1840s Ignaz Semmelweis in Vienna showed that handwashing cut deaths from childbed fever, and in the 1860s Joseph Lister introduced antiseptic surgery, both before germs were fully understood. Louis Pasteur and Robert Koch then proved that bacteria cause infection. For much of the 1900s sepsis was thought of as bacteria poisoning the blood, but research showed that much of the damage comes from the host's own immune response. In 1991 a consensus conference defined sepsis using SIRS criteria. In 2001 the Rivers trial popularized early goal-directed therapy, and the Surviving Sepsis Campaign launched guidelines. In 2016 the Sepsis-3 definitions redefined sepsis as life-threatening organ dysfunction from a dysregulated host response to infection.",
  timeline: [["1847", "Ignaz Semmelweis shows that handwashing reduces deaths from childbed fever."], ["1867", "Joseph Lister publishes on antiseptic surgery."], ["1991", "The ACCP/SCCM consensus conference defines sepsis using SIRS criteria."], ["2001", "The Rivers trial of early goal-directed therapy changes emergency sepsis care."], ["2016", "Sepsis-3 redefines sepsis and septic shock around organ dysfunction."], ["2021", "Updated Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines are published."]],
  cell: "Sepsis begins when immune cells detect microbial molecules (PAMPs) such as lipopolysaccharide from gram-negative bacteria, peptidoglycan and lipoteichoic acid from gram-positive bacteria, and danger signals from injured tissue (DAMPs). LPS binds LPS-binding protein, CD14 and the TLR4-MD-2 complex on macrophages, activating NF-kappaB and releasing TNF-alpha, IL-1 and IL-6. These cytokines activate endothelium, shed the protective glycocalyx and cause capillary leak. Inducible nitric oxide synthase floods vessels with nitric oxide, causing profound vasodilation and low blood pressure that respond poorly to catecholamines. Monocytes and endothelium express tissue factor, triggering clotting, consuming platelets and clotting factors (DIC) and forming microthrombi, while natural anticoagulants such as protein C fall. Mitochondrial dysfunction keeps cells from using oxygen. Together, these cause tissue hypoperfusion, lactate rise and multiorgan failure. Many patients later enter an immunosuppressed phase with lymphocyte death, which raises the risk of secondary infections.",
  body: [["Blood vessels", "Widespread vasodilation and capillary leak cause low blood pressure, warm or mottled skin and swelling."], ["Lungs", "Inflamed capillaries leak fluid into the air sacs, causing acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) and low oxygen."], ["Kidneys", "Low perfusion and inflammation cause acute kidney injury and low urine output."], ["Brain", "Sepsis-associated encephalopathy causes confusion, agitation or drowsiness, and is often the first sign in older adults."], ["Heart", "Septic cardiomyopathy lowers pumping strength, and a fast heart rate tries to keep up with dilated vessels."], ["Blood and clotting", "DIC causes low platelets, bleeding and small clots; lactate rises as tissues lack oxygen."], ["Liver and gut", "Poor blood flow causes jaundice and raised liver tests, and a leaky gut may let more bacteria into the blood."]],
  symptoms: "Fever or a low temperature reflects cytokines acting on the hypothalamus. A fast heart rate and fast breathing are compensations for low blood pressure and rising acid (lactate). Confusion comes from poor brain perfusion and inflammation. Low urine output reflects kidney hypoperfusion. Cold, mottled skin or slow capillary refill shows the body shunting blood to vital organs. Shortness of breath may come from pneumonia or ARDS.",
  dx: "Sepsis is suspected when infection is accompanied by organ dysfunction, often shown by an acute rise of two or more points in the SOFA score. The bedside qSOFA (breathing rate of 22 or more, altered mentation, systolic blood pressure of 100 or less) flags higher risk, although current guidelines advise against using it alone as a screening tool. Serum lactate measures tissue hypoperfusion. Blood cultures are drawn, ideally before antibiotics, along with cultures from the likely source, CBC, chemistry, liver tests and coagulation studies. Imaging looks for a source such as pneumonia, abscess or urinary obstruction. Septic shock is defined as sepsis needing vasopressors to keep a mean arterial pressure of 65 mm Hg or more and a lactate above 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluids.",
  tx: "Treatment is time critical. Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics are started quickly, ideally within one hour for probable septic shock, and later narrowed by culture results. Common regimens combine an anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam such as piperacillin-tazobactam, cefepime or meropenem with vancomycin when MRSA is a concern. IV crystalloids, preferably balanced solutions such as lactated Ringer's, restore circulating volume. Norepinephrine, an alpha-1 agonist that tightens vessels, is the first-line vasopressor; vasopressin is added to spare catecholamines, and epinephrine, phenylephrine or angiotensin II are other options. Hydrocortisone is used in shock that needs ongoing vasopressors. Dobutamine may help when the heart pumps poorly. Source control, such as draining an abscess or removing an infected catheter, is essential. Supportive care includes oxygen, ventilation, dialysis and clot prevention.",
  drugs: ["piperacillin-tazobactam", "vancomycin", "cefepime", "meropenem", "ceftriaxone", "linezolid", "lactated-ringers", "normal-saline", "plasma-lyte", "albumin-human", "norepinephrine", "vasopressin", "epinephrine", "phenylephrine", "angiotensin-ii", "dopamine", "dobutamine", "hydrocortisone"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Septic shock is distributive shock: low systemic vascular resistance, often with high cardiac output early (warm shock)."], ["Memory trick", "qSOFA is HAT: Hypotension (systolic 100 or less), Altered mentation, Tachypnea (22 or more)."], ["Key fact", "Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor; vasopressin is the usual second agent."], ["NREMT/NCLEX fact", "Early recognition and early antibiotics save lives; each hour of delay in septic shock raises the risk of death."]],
  red: "Confusion, very fast breathing, a racing heart, cold or mottled skin, very low urine output or feeling like dying, in someone with an infection, can be sepsis and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Sepsis is one of the most common causes of death in US hospitals, yet Semmelweis, whose handwashing work helped prevent it, was largely ignored in his lifetime.",
  src: ["Singer M et al. The Third International Consensus Definitions for Sepsis and Septic Shock (Sepsis-3). JAMA 2016", "Surviving Sepsis Campaign International Guidelines (2021)", "Rivers E et al. N Engl J Med 2001", "CDC Hospital Sepsis Program Core Elements", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"hiv": {
  name: "HIV and AIDS",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["human immunodeficiency virus", "acquired immunodeficiency syndrome", "AIDS", "HIV-1"],
  what: "HIV is a virus that infects and destroys immune cells called CD4 T cells. Without treatment it slowly weakens the immune system until AIDS develops, but modern daily or long-acting medicines keep the virus suppressed so people can live long, healthy lives.",
  origin: "On June 5, 1981, the CDC reported unusual Pneumocystis pneumonia in five young gay men in Los Angeles, the first official description of what became AIDS. Cases soon appeared in people who received blood products, people who injected drugs, infants and heterosexual partners, showing a transmissible cause. In 1983 Francoise Barre-Sinoussi and Luc Montagnier at the Pasteur Institute isolated the virus, and Robert Gallo's lab confirmed it as the cause in 1984; it was named HIV in 1986. Barre-Sinoussi and Montagnier received the 2008 Nobel Prize. The first drug, zidovudine (AZT), was approved in 1987. In 1996 combination antiretroviral therapy with protease inhibitors dramatically cut deaths. Later milestones included single-pill regimens, proof that people with an undetectable viral load do not transmit HIV sexually, oral PrEP in 2012 and long-acting injectable prevention and treatment.",
  timeline: [["1981", "The CDC reports the first cases of what is later called AIDS."], ["1983", "Barre-Sinoussi and Montagnier isolate the virus at the Pasteur Institute."], ["1987", "Zidovudine (AZT) becomes the first approved HIV drug."], ["1996", "Combination antiretroviral therapy sharply lowers AIDS deaths."], ["2012", "Oral emtricitabine-tenofovir is approved for HIV pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP)."], ["2025", "Twice-yearly injectable lenacapavir is approved for PrEP."]],
  cell: "HIV is a retrovirus spread through blood, sexual fluids and from parent to child. Its envelope protein gp120 binds CD4 on helper T cells, macrophages and dendritic cells, then a coreceptor, CCR5 (early infection) or CXCR4 (often later). gp41 then fuses the viral and cell membranes. Reverse transcriptase copies the RNA genome into DNA, making many errors that drive rapid mutation and drug resistance. Integrase inserts this DNA into the host chromosome, creating a permanent provirus. The host cell makes viral proteins, and HIV protease cuts them into mature parts for new virions. Within weeks, HIV destroys most CD4 T cells in gut-associated lymphoid tissue. Persistent immune activation, direct killing and pyroptosis of bystander cells slowly deplete CD4 cells over years. A latent reservoir of resting memory T cells with silent provirus is why therapy must continue for life. People homozygous for the CCR5-delta32 deletion are highly resistant to CCR5-using strains. When CD4 counts fall below 200 cells per microliter, opportunistic infections and cancers appear (AIDS).",
  body: [["Immune system", "Loss of CD4 helper T cells weakens nearly all immune responses, allowing opportunistic infections such as Pneumocystis pneumonia, toxoplasmosis, cryptococcal meningitis, CMV and tuberculosis."], ["Lymph nodes and gut", "Early infection wipes out CD4 cells in gut lymphoid tissue; swollen lymph nodes are common."], ["Brain and nerves", "HIV in microglia and macrophages can cause HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder and peripheral neuropathy."], ["Skin and mouth", "Oral thrush, oral hairy leukoplakia and Kaposi sarcoma (caused by HHV-8) are classic signs of immune decline."], ["Heart and blood vessels", "Chronic inflammation raises the risk of heart disease even when the virus is controlled."], ["Whole body", "Advanced disease causes wasting, chronic diarrhea and fever; lymphomas and cervical and anal cancers are more common."]],
  symptoms: "Two to four weeks after infection, many people have acute retroviral syndrome with fever, sore throat, rash, swollen glands and aches, caused by very high viral levels and the immune response. A long quiet phase follows, often without symptoms, while CD4 cells slowly fall. As immunity fails, infections and cancers that a healthy immune system would control appear, causing weight loss, fevers, night sweats, cough, thrush and diarrhea.",
  dx: "Screening uses a fourth-generation HIV-1/2 antigen-antibody test, which detects the p24 antigen within a few weeks of infection as well as antibodies. A positive result is confirmed with an HIV-1/HIV-2 antibody differentiation test, and an HIV RNA (viral load) test is used if results disagree or acute infection is suspected. After diagnosis, the CD4 count, viral load, genotype resistance testing, HLA-B*5701 (before abacavir) and tests for hepatitis B and C, tuberculosis and STIs are done. The CDC recommends HIV testing at least once for everyone aged 13 to 64.",
  tx: "Antiretroviral therapy (ART) is recommended for everyone with HIV, started as soon as possible. Regimens usually combine drugs from different classes. Integrase strand transfer inhibitors (bictegravir, dolutegravir, raltegravir, cabotegravir) block insertion of viral DNA. Nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (tenofovir, emtricitabine, lamivudine, abacavir, zidovudine) act as chain terminators. Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (rilpivirine, doravirine, efavirenz) bind the enzyme directly. Protease inhibitors (darunavir), boosted by ritonavir or cobicistat, block virion maturation. Newer agents target the capsid (lenacapavir), CCR5 (maraviroc), attachment (fostemsavir) or CD4 (ibalizumab). An undetectable viral load means the virus is not passed on sexually (Undetectable = Untransmittable). Opportunistic infections are prevented with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole and other drugs when CD4 counts are low. PrEP and post-exposure prophylaxis prevent infection in people without HIV.",
  drugs: ["bictegravir-emtricitabine-tenofovir-alafenamide", "dolutegravir", "dolutegravir-lamivudine", "dolutegravir-abacavir-lamivudine", "darunavir-cobicistat-emtricitabine-tenofovir-alafenamide", "cabotegravir-rilpivirine", "emtricitabine-tenofovir-disoproxil", "cabotegravir", "lenacapavir", "raltegravir", "darunavir", "ritonavir", "doravirine-lamivudine-tenofovir", "rilpivirine", "efavirenz-emtricitabine-tenofovir", "abacavir", "lamivudine", "zidovudine", "maraviroc", "fostemsavir", "ibalizumab", "trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole", "azithromycin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "AIDS is defined by a CD4 count below 200 cells per microliter or an AIDS-defining illness."], ["Memory trick", "U = U: Undetectable equals Untransmittable through sex."], ["Key fact", "Test for HLA-B*5701 before abacavir because carriers are at high risk of a severe hypersensitivity reaction."], ["Prophylaxis ladder", "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for Pneumocystis at CD4 below 200, and for toxoplasmosis at CD4 below 100 with a positive Toxoplasma test."], ["Often confused with", "Acute HIV looks like mononucleosis or flu; a fourth-generation test or viral load is needed because antibodies may not be present yet."]],
  red: "Shortness of breath with fever, a severe headache with stiff neck, confusion, seizures, vision changes or high fever in someone with advanced HIV needs emergency care (call 911). After a possible exposure, post-exposure prophylaxis works best when started as soon as possible and within 72 hours.",
  fun: "Timothy Ray Brown, known as the Berlin patient, was the first person considered cured of HIV after a stem cell transplant in 2007 from a donor with two copies of the CCR5-delta32 mutation.",
  src: ["DHHS Guidelines for the Use of Antiretroviral Agents in Adults and Adolescents with HIV", "CDC MMWR, Pneumocystis Pneumonia, Los Angeles (June 5, 1981)", "Barre-Sinoussi F et al. Science 1983", "IAS-USA Antiretroviral Drug Treatment Recommendations (2024)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"cellulitis": {
  name: "Cellulitis and skin abscess",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["cellulitis", "erysipelas", "skin abscess", "boil", "furuncle", "carbuncle", "SSTI"],
  what: "Cellulitis is a bacterial infection of the deeper skin and fat that causes a spreading area of red, warm, swollen and painful skin. A skin abscess is a pocket of pus under the skin, often from staph bacteria.",
  origin: "Spreading red skin infections were described by ancient physicians, and erysipelas was a feared complication of wounds in hospitals before antisepsis. In 1880 to 1882 Scottish surgeon Alexander Ogston studied pus from abscesses and named the grape-like clusters of bacteria Staphylococcus. In the early 1880s German physician Friedrich Fehleisen grew streptococci from erysipelas and showed they could reproduce it. Before antibiotics, spreading infections often led to amputation or death. Penicillin in the 1940s changed outcomes dramatically. In the late 1990s and 2000s community-associated MRSA, especially the USA300 strain, caused a surge in skin abscesses, and guidelines emphasized that draining the abscess is the key treatment.",
  timeline: [["1880s", "Alexander Ogston names Staphylococcus from abscess pus, and Friedrich Fehleisen links streptococci to erysipelas."], ["1940s", "Penicillin becomes widely available for skin infections."], ["2000s", "Community-associated MRSA drives a large rise in skin abscesses in the US."], ["2014", "IDSA publishes guidelines on skin and soft tissue infections."]],
  cell: "Bacteria enter through a break in the skin barrier, such as a cut, bite, ulcer, athlete's foot cracks or eczema. Nonpurulent cellulitis and erysipelas are mostly caused by beta-hemolytic streptococci, especially Streptococcus pyogenes. Strep spread through tissue using hyaluronidase, streptokinase and DNases, while M protein resists phagocytosis. The immune response releases cytokines, prostaglandins and bradykinin, causing redness, heat, swelling and pain. Erysipelas involves the upper dermis and superficial lymphatics, so it has a raised, sharp border. Abscesses are usually caused by Staphylococcus aureus. Coagulase converts fibrinogen to fibrin, walling off the infection, and neutrophils fill the space and die, forming pus. Community MRSA strains often carry Panton-Valentine leukocidin, a toxin that kills white blood cells and causes necrotic lesions. Lymphedema, venous insufficiency, obesity, diabetes and prior cellulitis raise risk. Deep, fast-spreading necrotizing fasciitis is a separate, surgical emergency.",
  body: [["Skin and fat", "Inflamed dermis and subcutaneous fat become red, warm, swollen and tender, with an edge that spreads over hours to days."], ["Lymphatics", "Infection can travel along lymph channels, causing red streaks (lymphangitis) and tender lymph nodes."], ["Abscess cavity", "Pus collects in a walled-off pocket that feels soft and fluctuant and often needs drainage."], ["Bloodstream", "Bacteria can enter the blood and cause bacteremia, sepsis or seeding of joints, bones or heart valves."], ["Deep tissues", "Rarely, infection spreads along fascia (necrotizing fasciitis), destroying tissue and needing emergency surgery."]],
  symptoms: "Redness, warmth and swelling come from widened blood vessels and fluid leaking into inflamed tissue. Pain and tenderness come from inflammatory mediators acting on nerve endings. Fever and chills reflect cytokines in the blood. An abscess feels like a firm, then soft lump because pus collects under pressure. Cellulitis is usually on one leg; redness on both legs more often points to stasis dermatitis rather than infection.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is mostly clinical, based on the appearance and spread of redness and warmth. Marking the border helps track spread. Bedside ultrasound helps find hidden abscesses. Blood cultures and labs are done for severe illness, immune suppression or animal bites. Cultures of abscess pus guide antibiotic choice, especially for MRSA. Severe pain out of proportion to the skin appearance, rapid spread, skin blisters, crepitus or a very ill patient raise concern for necrotizing infection, which may need imaging and surgical exploration.",
  tx: "Treatment depends on whether pus is present. Nonpurulent cellulitis is treated with antibiotics active against streptococci, such as cephalexin, dicloxacillin or penicillin, or IV cefazolin when more severe; these beta-lactams block bacterial cell wall building. Abscesses are treated mainly by incision and drainage, which removes the pus that antibiotics cannot penetrate well. Antibiotics against MRSA, such as trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline or clindamycin, are added for larger abscesses, systemic signs or other risk factors. Severe infections are treated with IV vancomycin, daptomycin, linezolid or ceftaroline, and long-acting dalbavancin or oritavancin are options. Elevating the limb reduces swelling, and treating athlete's foot, edema and dry skin lowers recurrence.",
  drugs: ["cephalexin", "cefadroxil", "dicloxacillin", "penicillin-v", "cefazolin", "nafcillin", "trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole", "doxycycline", "minocycline", "clindamycin", "amoxicillin-clavulanate", "vancomycin", "daptomycin", "linezolid", "tedizolid", "ceftaroline", "dalbavancin", "oritavancin", "mupirocin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Purulent means Staph (Pus); nonpurulent means Strep."], ["Most-tested fact", "For an abscess, incision and drainage is the key treatment; antibiotics are added in selected cases."], ["Often confused with", "Erysipelas has a raised, sharply demarcated border; stasis dermatitis is usually on both legs and not very tender."], ["Bites", "Cat and dog bites can carry Pasteurella multocida, so amoxicillin-clavulanate is a standard choice."]],
  red: "Rapidly spreading redness, severe pain out of proportion to the skin, dusky or blistering skin, crackling under the skin, high fever, confusion or infection around the eye needs emergency care (call 911 for severe illness).",
  fun: "Alexander Ogston named Staphylococcus from Greek words meaning bunch of grapes, after how the bacteria clustered under his microscope.",
  src: ["IDSA Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Skin and Soft Tissue Infections (2014)", "Ogston A. Micrococcus poisoning. J Anat Physiol 1882", "Talan DA et al. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for uncomplicated skin abscess. N Engl J Med 2016", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"mrsa": {
  name: "MRSA infections",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus", "MRSA", "CA-MRSA", "HA-MRSA"],
  what: "MRSA is a type of staph bacteria that resists methicillin and most other beta-lactam antibiotics. It causes skin infections and can also cause serious pneumonia, bloodstream, bone and heart valve infections.",
  origin: "Staphylococcus aureus was a major killer before antibiotics. Penicillin worked at first, but by the 1950s most hospital staph made penicillinase, an enzyme that destroys penicillin. Methicillin, a penicillinase-resistant penicillin, was introduced in 1959 to solve this. Within about two years, in 1961, Patricia Jevons in England reported methicillin-resistant strains. MRSA spread through hospitals worldwide in the 1970s and 1980s. In the late 1990s, deaths of previously healthy children in Minnesota and North Dakota revealed community-associated MRSA spreading outside hospitals, and the USA300 strain became a leading cause of skin infections in the US. Vancomycin became the main treatment, but strains with reduced vancomycin susceptibility were reported in Japan in 1997 and fully resistant strains in the US in 2002. Newer drugs such as linezolid, daptomycin and ceftaroline expanded options.",
  timeline: [["1959", "Methicillin is introduced to treat penicillinase-producing staph."], ["1961", "Patricia Jevons reports the first methicillin-resistant S. aureus in England."], ["1999", "The CDC reports MRSA deaths in healthy children, marking community-associated MRSA."], ["2002", "The first vancomycin-resistant S. aureus is reported in the US."], ["2010", "Ceftaroline, a cephalosporin active against MRSA, is approved."]],
  cell: "S. aureus resists beta-lactams in two ways. Penicillinase (beta-lactamase) breaks penicillin, which methicillin, nafcillin and oxacillin were designed to resist. MRSA instead carries the mecA gene on a mobile element called SCCmec. mecA encodes penicillin-binding protein 2a (PBP2a), a cell wall transpeptidase with very low affinity for nearly all beta-lactams, so the bacterium keeps building peptidoglycan while the drug is present. Ceftaroline is one of the few beta-lactams that binds PBP2a. Virulence comes from surface adhesins (MSCRAMMs) that bind collagen, fibronectin and fibrinogen, protein A that binds antibodies backwards to block opsonization, coagulase that walls off abscesses, biofilm on catheters and implants, and toxins. Community strains such as USA300 often carry Panton-Valentine leukocidin, which forms pores in neutrophils and is linked to necrotic skin lesions and necrotizing pneumonia. About a third of people carry S. aureus in the nose, and a small percentage carry MRSA, which serves as a reservoir for infection.",
  body: [["Skin and soft tissue", "MRSA commonly causes painful abscesses, boils and infected wounds, often mistaken for spider bites."], ["Lungs", "MRSA can cause severe necrotizing pneumonia, especially after influenza, and hospital or ventilator pneumonia."], ["Bloodstream and heart", "Bacteremia can seed heart valves (endocarditis), especially in people who inject drugs or have devices."], ["Bones and joints", "Blood-borne MRSA can infect bones, the spine and joints, and prosthetic joints."], ["Devices", "Biofilm on catheters, pacemakers and implants protects bacteria from antibiotics and the immune system."]],
  symptoms: "Skin infections cause a red, painful, swollen bump that fills with pus because neutrophils gather and coagulase walls off the area. Fever and chills come from cytokines released when bacteria enter the blood. Pneumonia causes cough, sometimes with blood, and breathing difficulty because toxins destroy lung tissue. Back pain with fever can signal spinal infection, and a new murmur can signal endocarditis.",
  dx: "Diagnosis relies on culture of pus, blood, sputum or tissue, which identifies S. aureus and tests for methicillin (oxacillin or cefoxitin) resistance. Rapid PCR tests detect mecA or S. aureus DNA from blood cultures and nasal swabs. A negative nasal MRSA PCR has a high negative predictive value for MRSA pneumonia and helps stop empiric vancomycin. Anyone with S. aureus bacteremia typically has repeat blood cultures and an echocardiogram to look for endocarditis. Vancomycin MIC results guide therapy.",
  tx: "Skin abscesses are drained, and oral antibiotics such as trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline, minocycline, clindamycin or linezolid are used when needed. Serious infections are treated with IV vancomycin, a glycopeptide that binds D-Ala-D-Ala to block cell wall building, monitored with blood levels to balance effect and kidney toxicity. Daptomycin disrupts the bacterial membrane and is used for bacteremia and endocarditis, but not pneumonia because lung surfactant inactivates it. Linezolid and tedizolid block the 50S ribosome and work well in lung tissue. Ceftaroline binds PBP2a. Long-acting lipoglycopeptides (dalbavancin, oritavancin) are used for skin and some deep infections. Removing infected devices and draining collections are essential. Infection control includes hand hygiene, contact precautions, and decolonization with nasal mupirocin and chlorhexidine bathing in selected patients.",
  drugs: ["vancomycin", "daptomycin", "linezolid", "tedizolid", "ceftaroline", "trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole", "doxycycline", "minocycline", "clindamycin", "dalbavancin", "oritavancin", "telavancin", "rifampin", "mupirocin-nasal", "mupirocin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "MRSA resistance comes from mecA, which encodes PBP2a, a penicillin-binding protein with low affinity for beta-lactams."], ["Memory trick", "Daptomycin does not work in the lungs: Dapto Doesn't Do pneumonia, because surfactant inactivates it."], ["Key fact", "Vancomycin infusion reaction (formerly called red man syndrome) comes from histamine release when the drug is infused too fast; it is not a true allergy."], ["Often confused with", "A spider bite: many lesions people call spider bites are actually MRSA abscesses."]],
  red: "High fever with a spreading skin infection, a boil on the face with fever, cough with bloody sputum after the flu, chest pain, confusion or signs of sepsis needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Resistance to methicillin was reported only about two years after the drug entered use, one of the fastest examples of antibiotic resistance in medical history.",
  src: ["IDSA Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Treatment of MRSA Infections (2011)", "Jevons MP. Celbenin-resistant staphylococci. BMJ 1961", "CDC MMWR, Four Pediatric Deaths from Community-Acquired MRSA (1999)", "ASHP/IDSA/PIDS/SIDP Vancomycin Therapeutic Monitoring Guideline (2020)"]
},
"osteomyelitis": {
  name: "Osteomyelitis",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["bone infection", "vertebral osteomyelitis", "diabetic foot osteomyelitis", "Brodie abscess"],
  what: "Osteomyelitis is an infection of bone, most often caused by Staphylococcus aureus. It can come from bacteria in the blood, a nearby wound or ulcer, or surgery and injury, and it often needs weeks of antibiotics and sometimes surgery.",
  origin: "Bone infection is ancient, and signs of it appear in prehistoric skeletons and Egyptian mummies. Before antibiotics, chronic draining bone infections were common, and surgeons removed dead bone, opened sinuses or amputated limbs. In 1832 Benjamin Brodie described a localized bone abscess now called a Brodie abscess. In the 1880s Louis Pasteur and others linked staphylococci in pus to bone infection. During World War I, the Spanish Civil War and World War II, surgeons learned that debridement of dead tissue and immobilization reduced deaths from open fractures. Penicillin in the 1940s greatly lowered death rates, especially from childhood hematogenous osteomyelitis. More recently, the OVIVA trial published in 2019 showed that oral antibiotics can be as effective as IV therapy for many bone and joint infections.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Bone changes from chronic infection are found in prehistoric and ancient skeletons."], ["1832", "Benjamin Brodie describes a chronic localized bone abscess."], ["1940s", "Penicillin dramatically lowers deaths from bone infection."], ["2019", "The OVIVA trial shows oral antibiotics can match IV therapy for many bone and joint infections."]],
  cell: "Bacteria reach bone in three ways. Hematogenous spread is most common in children, where blood flow slows in the looping capillaries of the metaphysis near growth plates, letting bacteria settle; in adults it often affects the vertebrae. Contiguous spread comes from nearby ulcers, as in diabetic foot ulcers or pressure injuries. Direct inoculation follows open fractures, surgery or puncture wounds. S. aureus uses adhesins (MSCRAMMs) to bind collagen, fibronectin and bone matrix, forms biofilm and can even survive inside osteoblasts. Neutrophils and cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6 and TNF-alpha activate osteoclasts through RANKL, so bone is resorbed. Pus under pressure inside rigid bone compresses blood vessels, causing bone death. The dead fragment, called a sequestrum, is a haven for bacteria that antibiotics cannot reach. New bone formed around it is the involucrum. Chronic infection may drain through sinus tracts and rarely leads to squamous cell carcinoma (Marjolin ulcer).",
  body: [["Bone", "Infection destroys bone, causes dead fragments (sequestra) and can lead to fractures or deformity."], ["Spine", "Vertebral osteomyelitis and discitis cause back pain and can spread to an epidural abscess that compresses the spinal cord."], ["Feet in diabetes", "Infection under a foot ulcer often reaches bone and is a major cause of amputation."], ["Joints", "Infection near a joint can spread into it, causing septic arthritis, especially in young children."], ["Growth plates", "In children, infection can damage the growth plate and cause limb length differences."], ["Bloodstream", "Bacteremia can be the source of or result from bone infection and can seed heart valves."]],
  symptoms: "Bone pain and tenderness come from inflammation and pressure inside rigid bone. Fever and chills reflect bacteria and cytokines in the blood, mainly in acute hematogenous cases; chronic infection may cause little fever. Children may limp or refuse to use a limb. Swelling, warmth and redness over the bone, or a draining sinus, suggest spread to the soft tissue. Back pain that worsens at night with fever suggests vertebral infection.",
  dx: "ESR and CRP are usually raised and are used to follow response. Plain X-rays may be normal for about two weeks, then show bone loss, periosteal reaction or sequestra. MRI is the most sensitive and specific imaging test, showing marrow edema and abscesses. Blood cultures are often positive in hematogenous cases. Bone biopsy with culture and pathology is the gold standard and guides antibiotic choice. In diabetic foot ulcers, being able to touch bone with a sterile probe (probe-to-bone test) strongly suggests osteomyelitis.",
  tx: "Treatment combines prolonged antibiotics, often for about six weeks, with surgery when needed. For methicillin-susceptible S. aureus, anti-staphylococcal beta-lactams such as cefazolin, nafcillin or oxacillin are used; MRSA is treated with vancomycin or daptomycin, with linezolid or trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole as oral options. Gram-negative infections, such as Pseudomonas after a foot puncture through a sneaker, are treated with agents such as cefepime or fluoroquinolones. Rifampin is sometimes added for biofilm infections involving hardware but never used alone because resistance develops quickly. Surgical debridement removes dead bone and infected tissue, and infected hardware may be removed. Good blood sugar control, wound care, offloading the foot and improving blood flow help diabetic foot infections heal.",
  drugs: ["cefazolin", "nafcillin", "oxacillin", "vancomycin", "daptomycin", "linezolid", "trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole", "ceftriaxone", "cefepime", "piperacillin-tazobactam", "ciprofloxacin", "levofloxacin", "rifampin", "clindamycin", "dalbavancin", "ertapenem"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "S. aureus is the most common cause overall; Salmonella is a classic cause in sickle cell disease, and Pseudomonas after a nail puncture through a sneaker."], ["Memory trick", "Sequestrum is the Sad dead bone; involucrum is the new bone that Involves (wraps) it."], ["Key fact", "MRI is the best imaging test; X-ray findings lag about two weeks behind infection."], ["Associations", "Pott disease is tuberculosis of the spine; Pasteurella osteomyelitis follows cat and dog bites."]],
  red: "Back pain with fever plus leg weakness, numbness or loss of bladder control can mean a spinal epidural abscess and needs emergency care (call 911). A child with fever who refuses to bear weight, or a diabetic foot ulcer with spreading redness or fever, also needs urgent evaluation.",
  fun: "Before antibiotics, some surgeons treated chronic bone infection with sterile maggots, which eat dead tissue; medical maggot therapy is still FDA-cleared for wound debridement today.",
  src: ["IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for Native Vertebral Osteomyelitis (2015)", "IWGDF/IDSA Guidelines on the Diagnosis and Treatment of Diabetes-related Foot Infections (2023)", "Li HK et al. OVIVA. N Engl J Med 2019", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"malaria": {
  name: "Malaria",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["Plasmodium infection", "falciparum malaria", "vivax malaria", "severe malaria"],
  what: "Malaria is an infection with Plasmodium parasites spread by the bite of infected Anopheles mosquitoes. It causes cycles of fever and chills, and the falciparum type can quickly become life-threatening.",
  origin: "Periodic fevers were described by Hippocrates, and the disease was named from the Italian mala aria, bad air, because it was linked to swamps. In the 1600s bark of the South American cinchona tree came to Europe as a fever remedy, and in 1820 Pierre Joseph Pelletier and Joseph Caventou isolated quinine from it. In 1880 French army physician Alphonse Laveran saw parasites in the blood of patients in Algeria. In 1897 Ronald Ross, working in India, found malaria parasites in mosquitoes and later showed transmission in birds, and Giovanni Battista Grassi's team proved Anopheles mosquitoes transmit human malaria. Ross and Laveran won Nobel Prizes. Chloroquine became the main drug in the 1940s, but resistance spread. In 1972 Tu Youyou's team in China isolated artemisinin from sweet wormwood, earning a 2015 Nobel Prize. The CDC itself grew out of a wartime malaria control program, and the US eliminated local malaria around 1951. WHO recommended the first malaria vaccine in 2021.",
  timeline: [["1820", "Pelletier and Caventou isolate quinine from cinchona bark."], ["1880", "Alphonse Laveran sees malaria parasites in human blood."], ["1897", "Ronald Ross finds malaria parasites in mosquitoes."], ["1940s", "Chloroquine becomes the main antimalarial drug."], ["1972", "Tu Youyou's team isolates artemisinin."], ["2021", "WHO recommends the RTS,S malaria vaccine for children in high-risk areas."]],
  cell: "An infected female Anopheles mosquito injects sporozoites, which travel to the liver and invade hepatocytes. There they multiply into thousands of merozoites. P. vivax and P. ovale can also form dormant liver hypnozoites that cause relapses months later. Merozoites burst out and invade red blood cells, where they digest hemoglobin and convert toxic heme into hemozoin crystals. They grow through ring, trophozoite and schizont stages, then rupture the cell to release more merozoites. Rupture releases parasite products and hemozoin that trigger TNF-alpha and IL-1, causing fever spikes that can become cyclic (about every 48 hours for vivax and ovale, 72 hours for malariae). P. falciparum infects red cells of all ages, reaching very high parasite levels, and places PfEMP1 on knobs on the red cell surface. PfEMP1 binds endothelial receptors such as ICAM-1 and EPCR, so infected cells stick in small vessels of the brain, kidneys and placenta (sequestration), causing cerebral malaria and organ failure. P. vivax needs the Duffy antigen to enter red cells. Sickle cell trait, thalassemia and G6PD deficiency offer partial protection.",
  body: [["Blood", "Red cell destruction causes anemia and jaundice; low platelets are common, and very high parasite levels are a sign of severe disease."], ["Brain", "Sequestered falciparum-infected cells in brain vessels cause cerebral malaria with confusion, seizures and coma."], ["Spleen", "The spleen enlarges as it filters infected cells and can rupture; repeated infection causes chronic enlargement."], ["Kidneys", "Sequestration and hemoglobin release can cause acute kidney injury and dark urine (blackwater fever)."], ["Lungs", "Severe malaria can cause acute respiratory distress syndrome."], ["Metabolism", "Low blood sugar and lactic acidosis occur, especially in children and pregnancy and with quinine treatment."], ["Pregnancy", "Parasites sequester in the placenta, raising the risk of maternal anemia, low birth weight and stillbirth."]],
  symptoms: "Fever, shaking chills and sweats happen when red cells rupture together and release parasite material that triggers cytokines. Headache, muscle aches and fatigue are common and can look like flu. Anemia and jaundice come from red cell destruction. Confusion, seizures, trouble breathing or dark urine signal severe disease from sequestration, mainly with P. falciparum. Symptoms usually start one to four weeks after a bite, but vivax and ovale can relapse months later.",
  dx: "Any fever in someone who has been in a malaria area is considered possible malaria until tested. Thick and thin Giemsa-stained blood smears are the standard: thick smears detect parasites and thin smears identify the species and percent of infected cells. Rapid diagnostic tests detect parasite antigens such as HRP2. PCR confirms species. Smears are repeated every 12 to 24 hours if the first is negative. Severe malaria is identified by impaired consciousness, seizures, severe anemia, kidney injury, acidosis, low blood sugar, shock or high parasitemia. G6PD testing is done before primaquine or tafenoquine.",
  tx: "Treatment depends on species, severity and where the infection was acquired. Uncomplicated falciparum malaria is treated with artemisinin-based combination therapy such as artemether-lumefantrine, or with atovaquone-proguanil. Artemisinins are activated by heme iron to release free radicals that kill parasites rapidly, while the partner drug clears remaining parasites. Chloroquine, which blocks heme detoxification, still works for infections from chloroquine-sensitive areas. Severe malaria is treated with IV artesunate, followed by a full oral course. For P. vivax and P. ovale, primaquine or tafenoquine kills liver hypnozoites to prevent relapse, after G6PD testing because these drugs can cause hemolysis in G6PD deficiency. Prevention includes insect repellent, bed nets treated with insecticide, and preventive drugs for travelers such as atovaquone-proguanil, doxycycline, mefloquine, tafenoquine or chloroquine.",
  drugs: ["artemether-lumefantrine", "atovaquone-proguanil", "artesunate", "chloroquine", "hydroxychloroquine", "primaquine", "tafenoquine", "mefloquine", "quinine", "doxycycline", "clindamycin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "P. vivax and P. ovale form dormant liver hypnozoites, so primaquine or tafenoquine is added to prevent relapse after G6PD testing."], ["Memory trick", "Falciparum is Fatal: it infects red cells of all ages and sticks them to small vessels in the brain."], ["Fever cycles", "Tertian (every 48 hours) for vivax and ovale; quartan (every 72 hours) for malariae."], ["Key fact", "Sickle cell trait, G6PD deficiency and lack of the Duffy antigen protect against some forms of malaria."]],
  red: "Fever during or after travel to a malaria area, especially with confusion, seizures, trouble breathing, dark urine, yellow skin or severe weakness, needs emergency care (call 911). Malaria can become deadly within a day or two.",
  fun: "Tonic water was originally a way to take quinine; British officials in India reportedly mixed it with gin, and modern tonic still contains a small amount of quinine for flavor.",
  src: ["CDC Treatment of Malaria: Guidelines for Clinicians (United States)", "WHO Guidelines for Malaria", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1902 (Ross), 1907 (Laveran), 2015 (Tu Youyou)", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"lyme-disease": {
  name: "Lyme disease",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["Lyme borreliosis", "Borrelia burgdorferi infection", "erythema migrans"],
  what: "Lyme disease is a bacterial infection spread by the bite of infected blacklegged (deer) ticks. It often starts with an expanding red rash and, if untreated, can spread to the joints, heart and nerves.",
  origin: "In Europe, a spreading ring rash after tick bites was described by Swedish dermatologist Arvid Afzelius in the early 1900s. The disease got its American name in the 1970s, when mothers in Lyme, Connecticut, including Polly Murray and Judith Mensch, reported an unusual cluster of children diagnosed with arthritis. Rheumatologist Allen Steere and colleagues at Yale investigated and described Lyme arthritis in 1977, linking it to tick bites and a preceding rash. In 1981 to 1982 Willy Burgdorfer at the Rocky Mountain Laboratories found a spirochete in Ixodes ticks, which was named Borrelia burgdorferi. Antibiotics such as doxycycline and amoxicillin proved effective. A human vaccine (LYMErix) was approved in 1998 but withdrawn in 2002 due to low sales. Cases have grown as tick ranges spread, and new vaccines are in development.",
  timeline: [["1909", "Arvid Afzelius describes the expanding rash later called erythema migrans."], ["1977", "Allen Steere and colleagues describe Lyme arthritis in Connecticut."], ["1982", "Willy Burgdorfer reports the spirochete in Ixodes ticks, later named Borrelia burgdorferi."], ["1998", "The LYMErix vaccine is approved; it is withdrawn in 2002."], ["2020", "IDSA, AAN and ACR publish joint Lyme disease guidelines."]],
  cell: "Borrelia burgdorferi is a spirochete carried by Ixodes ticks (Ixodes scapularis in the Northeast and Upper Midwest, Ixodes pacificus on the West Coast), with mice and other small mammals as reservoirs and deer supporting adult ticks. Nymphs, which are tiny and easily missed, cause most infections. In the unfed tick gut the bacteria express outer surface protein A (OspA). When the tick feeds, they switch to OspC and move to the salivary glands, which usually takes more than 36 hours of attachment. In skin, the bacteria spread outward, causing erythema migrans. Their lipoproteins activate TLR2 rather than classic lipopolysaccharide pathways. Borrelia binds host plasminogen to move through tissue, evades complement and changes its VlsE surface protein to dodge antibodies. Bloodstream spread seeds joints, heart and nerves. Lyme arthritis in some people persists after the bacteria are cleared, driven by immune responses, including in people with certain HLA-DR alleles.",
  body: [["Skin", "Erythema migrans is an expanding red rash at the bite site, sometimes with central clearing (bull's-eye); multiple rashes mean spread through the blood."], ["Nerves", "Early spread can cause facial nerve palsy (sometimes on both sides), meningitis and painful nerve root inflammation (radiculopathy)."], ["Heart", "Lyme carditis can cause atrioventricular heart block, which can change from day to day."], ["Joints", "Months later, untreated infection causes episodes of swelling in large joints, especially the knee."], ["Whole body", "Fever, fatigue, headache and muscle aches are common early and reflect the immune response to spreading bacteria."]],
  symptoms: "The rash appears days to weeks after a bite because bacteria spread outward through the skin. Flu-like symptoms come from the immune response. Facial palsy and nerve pain happen when spirochetes and inflammation reach cranial and spinal nerve roots. Lightheadedness or fainting can come from heart block. Swollen, sometimes painless knees months later come from immune inflammation in the joint. Many people never noticed the tick bite.",
  dx: "Erythema migrans in someone who could have been exposed to ticks is diagnosed clinically, without blood tests, because antibodies may not have formed yet. For later stages, two-tier serologic testing is used: an enzyme immunoassay followed by either a Western blot or a second immunoassay (modified two-tier testing). Joint fluid PCR can help in Lyme arthritis. An ECG is done for symptoms suggesting heart involvement. Lumbar puncture may be done for suspected meningitis. Testing is not done after a tick bite without symptoms.",
  tx: "Doxycycline, which blocks the bacterial 30S ribosome, is the preferred treatment for early Lyme disease in most adults and children, and it also covers other tick-borne infections such as anaplasmosis. Amoxicillin and cefuroxime are alternatives, for example in pregnancy. Azithromycin is a less effective backup. Lyme meningitis and facial palsy can often be treated with oral doxycycline, while IV ceftriaxone is used for severe carditis needing hospital care and some neurologic or refractory joint cases. A temporary pacemaker is sometimes needed for complete heart block. A single preventive dose of doxycycline may be given after a high-risk tick bite in endemic areas. Prevention includes repellents, permethrin-treated clothing, tick checks and removing ticks quickly with tweezers.",
  drugs: ["doxycycline", "amoxicillin", "cefuroxime", "ceftriaxone", "azithromycin", "penicillin-g"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Bilateral facial nerve palsy in a person from an endemic area strongly suggests Lyme disease."], ["Memory trick", "FAKE a Key Lyme pie: Facial palsy, Arthritis, Kardiac block, Erythema migrans."], ["Key fact", "Ticks usually must be attached more than 36 hours to pass the infection, so prompt tick removal lowers risk."], ["Often confused with", "Southern tick-associated rash illness (STARI) from lone star ticks looks like erythema migrans but is not Lyme disease."]],
  red: "Fainting, chest pain, shortness of breath or a slow or irregular heartbeat after a tick bite or Lyme rash may mean Lyme carditis and needs emergency care (call 911). A severe headache with stiff neck also needs urgent care.",
  fun: "Borrelia burgdorferi is unusual because it does not depend on iron like most bacteria; it uses manganese instead, which helps it survive when the body hides iron during infection.",
  src: ["IDSA/AAN/ACR Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Prevention, Diagnosis and Treatment of Lyme Disease (2020)", "Steere AC et al. Lyme arthritis. Arthritis Rheum 1977", "Burgdorfer W et al. Science 1982", "CDC Lyme Disease Clinical Resources"]
},
"syphilis": {
  name: "Syphilis",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["Treponema pallidum infection", "lues", "neurosyphilis", "congenital syphilis"],
  what: "Syphilis is a sexually transmitted infection caused by the spiral bacterium Treponema pallidum. It moves through stages, from a painless sore to a body rash to years of hidden infection, and can damage the brain, heart and a developing baby if untreated.",
  origin: "A severe epidemic swept Europe in 1495, starting among soldiers during the French invasion of Naples, and each country blamed its neighbors for the disease. In 1530 Italian physician Girolamo Fracastoro wrote a poem about a shepherd named Syphilus, which gave the disease its name. Mercury treatments were used for centuries despite serious toxicity. In 1905 Fritz Schaudinn and Erich Hoffmann in Berlin identified the spiral bacterium in syphilis sores. In 1906 August von Wassermann developed the first blood test, and in 1910 Paul Ehrlich introduced arsphenamine (Salvarsan), one of the first modern targeted drugs. In 1943 John Mahoney showed that penicillin cured syphilis, and it remains the treatment today. The US Public Health Service Tuskegee study, which withheld treatment from Black men from 1932 to 1972, is a lasting lesson in research ethics. Cases, including congenital syphilis, have risen sharply in the US since the early 2000s.",
  timeline: [["1495", "A syphilis epidemic spreads through Europe after the siege of Naples."], ["1905", "Schaudinn and Hoffmann identify Treponema pallidum."], ["1906", "The Wassermann blood test for syphilis is introduced."], ["1910", "Paul Ehrlich introduces arsphenamine (Salvarsan)."], ["1943", "John Mahoney shows that penicillin cures syphilis."], ["1972", "The Tuskegee syphilis study is exposed and ended."]],
  cell: "Treponema pallidum is a thin, corkscrew-shaped spirochete that cannot be grown on standard culture media. It enters through tiny breaks in mucous membranes or skin during sexual contact, or crosses the placenta. Its outer membrane has very few surface proteins, which helps it hide from antibodies, and it moves through tissue with internal flagella. At the entry site, the immune response with lymphocytes, macrophages and plasma cells causes a painless ulcer (chancre). Within hours to days the organism spreads through lymphatics and blood. The hallmark lesion is endarteritis obliterans: inflammation of small arteries with swollen endothelium and perivascular plasma cells, which narrows vessels and causes tissue damage. Secondary syphilis reflects widespread infection with rash and mucosal lesions. Later, the infection becomes latent. Tertiary disease, years later, includes gummas (granulomas with central necrosis), aortitis from endarteritis of the vasa vasorum (tree-bark aorta and aneurysm), and neurosyphilis, including tabes dorsalis (dorsal column damage) and general paresis. Neurosyphilis can occur at any stage.",
  body: [["Genitals, mouth or anus", "Primary syphilis causes a painless, firm, clean-based ulcer (chancre) at the site of entry with swollen nearby lymph nodes."], ["Skin and mucous membranes", "Secondary syphilis causes a rash that often involves the palms and soles, moist wart-like lesions (condylomata lata) and patchy hair loss."], ["Brain and nerves", "Neurosyphilis can cause meningitis, stroke, dementia, personality change, loss of position sense (tabes dorsalis) and Argyll Robertson pupils."], ["Eyes and ears", "Ocular and otic syphilis can cause vision loss and hearing loss at any stage and are treated like neurosyphilis."], ["Aorta and heart", "Tertiary syphilis inflames the aortic wall, causing aneurysm of the ascending aorta and aortic regurgitation."], ["Developing baby", "Infection in pregnancy can cause miscarriage, stillbirth or congenital syphilis with bone, skin, liver and dental problems."]],
  symptoms: "The chancre is painless because the immune reaction is mostly plasma cells and lymphocytes rather than neutrophils, so many people miss it. The secondary rash, fever, sore throat and swollen glands appear weeks later as the bacteria spread throughout the body. Without treatment these go away, and the infection becomes latent without symptoms. Years later, damaged blood vessels and nerves cause problems such as unsteady walking, shooting pains, memory loss and chest pain from aortic disease.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses two kinds of blood tests. Nontreponemal tests (RPR, VDRL) measure antibodies to cardiolipin, give titers that track disease activity and fall after treatment, but can be falsely positive in pregnancy, lupus and other conditions. Treponemal tests (FTA-ABS, TP-PA, enzyme or chemiluminescence immunoassays) detect antibodies to the organism and usually stay positive for life. Many labs use a reverse sequence algorithm that starts with a treponemal test. Darkfield microscopy or PCR of a chancre can confirm early disease. Cerebrospinal fluid testing with CSF VDRL is used when neurosyphilis is suspected. Screening is recommended in pregnancy, often more than once.",
  tx: "Penicillin G is the treatment for all stages. Benzathine penicillin G, a long-acting intramuscular form, keeps low levels of penicillin in the blood for weeks, which is needed because the organism divides slowly; it blocks cell wall cross-linking. Early syphilis is treated with a single injection, while late latent disease uses a series of weekly injections. Neurosyphilis, ocular and otic syphilis are treated with IV aqueous penicillin G, which reaches the nervous system. Doxycycline is an alternative for nonpregnant people with penicillin allergy. In pregnancy, people with penicillin allergy are desensitized and treated with penicillin, the only proven way to prevent congenital syphilis. The Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, a fever with aches within a day of treatment, comes from the release of bacterial products as the organisms die. Partners are notified and treated. Doxycycline taken after sex (doxy-PEP) reduces syphilis in some groups at high risk.",
  drugs: ["penicillin-g-benzathine", "penicillin-g", "penicillin-g-procaine", "doxycycline", "ceftriaxone"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "The chancre of primary syphilis is painless; chancroid (Haemophilus ducreyi) is painful."], ["Memory trick", "Argyll Robertson pupils are small pupils that Accommodate (constrict for near focus) but do not React to light, a classic sign of neurosyphilis."], ["Key fact", "RPR and VDRL titers track treatment response; treponemal tests usually stay positive for life."], ["Rash clue", "A rash on the palms and soles suggests secondary syphilis, Rocky Mountain spotted fever or hand, foot and mouth disease."]],
  red: "Sudden vision changes, hearing loss, severe headache, confusion, weakness on one side or a stiff neck in someone with syphilis or a possible exposure needs urgent or emergency care (call 911 for stroke-like symptoms).",
  fun: "Paul Ehrlich called Salvarsan a magic bullet; it was compound number 606 in his lab's systematic search for a drug that would kill the organism but spare the patient.",
  src: ["CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines (2021)", "CDC Clinical Guidelines on the Use of Doxycycline Postexposure Prophylaxis (2024)", "Schaudinn F, Hoffmann E. 1905", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"gonorrhea-chlamydia": {
  name: "Gonorrhea and chlamydia",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["gonorrhea", "chlamydia", "Neisseria gonorrhoeae", "Chlamydia trachomatis", "the clap", "urethritis", "cervicitis"],
  what: "Gonorrhea and chlamydia are common bacterial sexually transmitted infections of the genitals, throat or rectum. Many people have no symptoms, but untreated infection can cause pelvic inflammatory disease, infertility and ectopic pregnancy.",
  origin: "Genital discharge has been described since ancient times, and gonorrhea gets its name from Greek words for flow of seed, a misunderstanding of the discharge. In 1879 Albert Neisser in Germany identified the diplococcus that causes gonorrhea, later named Neisseria gonorrhoeae. Chlamydia was first seen in 1907, when Ludwig Halberstaedter and Stanislaus von Prowazek described inclusion bodies in cells from patients with trachoma, an eye disease caused by related strains. Chlamydia was grown in culture in the 1950s and was eventually recognized as the most commonly reported bacterial STI in the US. Gonorrhea has repeatedly outpaced antibiotics: sulfonamides, penicillin, tetracycline, fluoroquinolones and oral cefixime each lost their place as first-line treatment, and in 2020 the CDC recommended ceftriaxone alone. Nucleic acid amplification tests (NAATs) in the 1990s and 2000s made testing from urine or self-collected swabs easy.",
  timeline: [["1879", "Albert Neisser identifies the gonococcus."], ["1907", "Halberstaedter and von Prowazek describe chlamydial inclusion bodies."], ["1940s", "Penicillin becomes the main treatment for gonorrhea."], ["2007", "The CDC stops recommending fluoroquinolones for gonorrhea because of resistance."], ["2020", "The CDC recommends ceftriaxone monotherapy for uncomplicated gonorrhea."], ["2021", "Doxycycline becomes the preferred treatment for chlamydia in CDC guidelines."]],
  cell: "Neisseria gonorrhoeae is a gram-negative diplococcus that infects columnar epithelium of the urethra, cervix, rectum, throat and eye. Type IV pili attach it to cells, and the pilin gene recombines constantly (antigenic variation), so infection does not create lasting immunity and vaccines have been hard to make. Opa proteins promote tight binding and entry into cells, IgA1 protease cleaves mucosal antibodies, and lipooligosaccharide triggers a strong neutrophil response, producing purulent discharge. Chlamydia trachomatis is an obligate intracellular bacterium with a two-form life cycle: the small, hardy elementary body infects cells, then turns into a larger reticulate body that divides inside a cytoplasmic inclusion and makes new elementary bodies that are released. Chlamydia has little peptidoglycan, so beta-lactams work poorly. Its infections trigger milder, often silent inflammation, but repeated infection drives scarring of the fallopian tubes through cytokine and heat shock protein responses. Serovars D through K cause genital infection, L1 to L3 cause lymphogranuloma venereum, and A through C cause trachoma.",
  body: [["Urethra and penis", "Infection causes urethritis with discharge and burning, and can spread to the epididymis, causing testicular pain and swelling."], ["Cervix, uterus and tubes", "Cervicitis can ascend to pelvic inflammatory disease, scarring the tubes and causing infertility, ectopic pregnancy and chronic pelvic pain."], ["Liver capsule", "Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome is inflammation of the liver capsule from PID, causing right upper belly pain."], ["Rectum and throat", "Infection from receptive anal or oral sex is often silent but can cause proctitis or sore throat."], ["Joints and skin", "Disseminated gonococcal infection causes septic arthritis, tenosynovitis and pustular skin lesions; chlamydia can trigger reactive arthritis."], ["Newborn eyes and lungs", "Babies born through an infected birth canal can develop conjunctivitis, and chlamydia can cause infant pneumonia."]],
  symptoms: "Burning with urination and discharge come from neutrophils drawn into the inflamed urethra or cervix. Gonorrhea tends to cause thicker, purulent discharge because of its strong neutrophil response, while chlamydia often causes mild or no symptoms, which is why screening matters. Pelvic pain, fever and pain with sex suggest PID. Joint pain with skin pustules suggests disseminated gonorrhea.",
  dx: "NAAT is the test of choice for both infections and can be done on first-catch urine, vaginal swabs (including self-collected), and rectal and throat swabs. Culture is used when gonorrhea treatment fails, to test antibiotic resistance. A Gram stain of male urethral discharge showing gram-negative diplococci inside neutrophils supports gonorrhea. The CDC recommends yearly chlamydia and gonorrhea screening for sexually active women under 25 and older women at higher risk, and testing based on exposure sites for men who have sex with men. Testing for HIV and syphilis is done at the same time.",
  tx: "Uncomplicated gonorrhea is treated with a single intramuscular injection of ceftriaxone, a cephalosporin that blocks cell wall cross-linking. If chlamydia has not been ruled out, doxycycline is added. Gentamicin plus azithromycin, or cefixime, are alternatives when ceftriaxone cannot be used, and newer oral agents such as gepotidacin are options in limited situations. Chlamydia is treated with doxycycline, which blocks the 30S ribosome, with azithromycin as an alternative, especially in pregnancy. Pelvic inflammatory disease uses combination regimens such as ceftriaxone, doxycycline and metronidazole. Sex partners are treated, sometimes through expedited partner therapy, and people are retested about three months later because reinfection is common. Newborns receive erythromycin eye ointment to prevent gonococcal eye infection.",
  drugs: ["ceftriaxone", "doxycycline", "azithromycin", "gentamicin", "cefixime", "gepotidacin", "levofloxacin", "metronidazole", "cefoxitin", "erythromycin-ophthalmic"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Chlamydia is the most commonly reported bacterial STI in the US and is often asymptomatic, especially in women."], ["Memory trick", "Chlamydia is a Cell parasite: obligate intracellular, so it hides inside cells where beta-lactams do not help."], ["Key fact", "Gram-negative diplococci inside neutrophils on a urethral smear point to gonorrhea."], ["Often confused with", "Trichomonas also causes discharge but is a protozoan treated with metronidazole."], ["Reactive arthritis", "Can't see, can't pee, can't climb a tree: conjunctivitis, urethritis and arthritis after chlamydia."]],
  red: "Severe lower belly pain, fever with vomiting, sudden testicular pain or swelling, a hot swollen joint with fever, or pain and bleeding in early pregnancy (possible ectopic pregnancy) needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Gonorrhea is so good at gaining resistance that the WHO lists it as a high-priority pathogen; every class of drug once used as first-line treatment has eventually failed.",
  src: ["CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines (2021)", "St Cyr S et al. Update to CDC's Treatment Guidelines for Gonococcal Infection. MMWR 2020", "Neisser A. 1879", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"herpes-simplex": {
  name: "Herpes simplex",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["HSV-1", "HSV-2", "cold sores", "fever blisters", "genital herpes", "herpes labialis"],
  what: "Herpes simplex is a common lifelong virus infection that causes painful clusters of blisters, usually on the lips (cold sores) or genitals. The virus hides in nerve cells between outbreaks and can come back, especially with stress, illness or sun.",
  origin: "The word herpes comes from the Greek for to creep, and ancient physicians including Hippocrates described creeping skin sores. Cold sores with fever were noted by Roman writers, and genital herpes was described by physicians in the 1700s. In the early 1900s researchers showed that material from herpes blisters could transmit infection to the rabbit cornea, proving a transmissible agent. In the 1960s scientists distinguished two types, HSV-1 and HSV-2, which tend to favor the mouth and genitals. In the 1970s Gertrude Elion and colleagues at Burroughs Wellcome developed acyclovir, the first highly selective antiviral drug, approved in the early 1980s. Elion, George Hitchings and James Black shared the 1988 Nobel Prize. Valacyclovir and famciclovir later allowed easier dosing and daily suppression.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Hippocrates and other Greek writers describe creeping skin sores called herpes."], ["1960s", "HSV-1 and HSV-2 are distinguished as separate virus types."], ["1982", "Acyclovir is approved in the US, the first selective antiviral against herpes."], ["1988", "Gertrude Elion shares the Nobel Prize for drug discoveries including acyclovir."], ["1995", "Valacyclovir is approved, improving oral absorption."]],
  cell: "HSV-1 and HSV-2 are enveloped double-stranded DNA herpesviruses. They enter epithelial cells at mucosa or broken skin by binding glycoproteins (gB, gD and others) to receptors such as nectin-1 and HVEM. The virus replicates in the epithelium and kills cells, forming multinucleated giant cells and intranuclear Cowdry A inclusions, which create vesicles and ulcers. The virus then enters sensory nerve endings and travels by retrograde axonal transport to the nucleus of sensory neurons, usually in the trigeminal ganglion (HSV-1) or sacral dorsal root ganglia (HSV-2). There it becomes latent, with only latency-associated transcripts (LATs) expressed and no active virus. Triggers such as stress, fever, UV light, menstruation or immune suppression reactivate it, and the virus travels back down the nerve to the skin. Asymptomatic shedding allows spread even without sores. HSV-1 is the most common cause of sporadic viral encephalitis, favoring the temporal lobes. Antivirals such as acyclovir are activated by viral thymidine kinase and then block viral DNA polymerase.",
  body: [["Lips and mouth", "HSV-1 causes cold sores and, in first infections in children, painful gingivostomatitis with many mouth ulcers."], ["Genitals", "HSV-2 and increasingly HSV-1 cause painful genital ulcers, sometimes with fever, swollen nodes and painful urination."], ["Eyes", "Herpes keratitis causes painful red eyes and branching (dendritic) corneal ulcers and is a leading infectious cause of corneal blindness in high-income countries."], ["Brain", "HSV encephalitis inflames the temporal lobes, causing fever, confusion, personality change and seizures."], ["Skin", "Herpetic whitlow infects fingers, and eczema herpeticum spreads widely over skin with atopic dermatitis."], ["Newborns", "Babies infected at birth can develop severe skin, brain or disseminated infection."]],
  symptoms: "Tingling, burning or itching before an outbreak (prodrome) happens as the reactivated virus travels down the sensory nerve. Grouped blisters on a red base form as the virus kills epithelial cells and fluid fills the space. The blisters break into painful ulcers, then crust and heal. First infections are usually worse, with fever and swollen glands, because the immune system has not yet seen the virus. Recurrences are milder and shorter.",
  dx: "Typical sores are often recognized clinically. PCR of fluid from an active lesion is the most sensitive test and identifies HSV-1 versus HSV-2. Viral culture is less sensitive. A Tzanck smear showing multinucleated giant cells is a classic but outdated test that cannot separate HSV from varicella-zoster. Type-specific blood tests for HSV-2 antibodies help in selected situations. HSV encephalitis is diagnosed by PCR of cerebrospinal fluid, often with MRI showing temporal lobe changes.",
  tx: "Antiviral drugs shorten outbreaks but do not remove latent virus. Acyclovir, valacyclovir (a prodrug of acyclovir) and famciclovir (a prodrug of penciclovir) are phosphorylated first by viral thymidine kinase, so they become active mainly in infected cells, then block viral DNA polymerase and terminate DNA chains. They are used for first episodes, recurrences and daily suppression, which reduces outbreaks and lowers transmission to partners. IV acyclovir is started urgently for suspected encephalitis and used for neonatal and severe disease. Foscarnet, which directly blocks the polymerase without needing thymidine kinase, is used for resistant virus. Topical docosanol or penciclovir can modestly shorten cold sores. Herpes keratitis is managed with topical or oral antivirals by eye specialists. Condoms and avoiding contact during outbreaks reduce spread.",
  drugs: ["valacyclovir", "acyclovir", "famciclovir", "foscarnet", "penciclovir", "docosanol", "trifluridine-ophthalmic", "lidocaine", "acetaminophen", "ibuprofen"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "HSV-1 is the most common cause of sporadic viral encephalitis and favors the temporal lobes; start IV acyclovir as soon as it is suspected."], ["Memory trick", "HSV-1 is above the waist, HSV-2 below, but either type can infect either site."], ["Key fact", "HSV becomes latent in sensory ganglia: trigeminal for oral herpes, sacral for genital herpes."], ["Resistance clue", "Acyclovir resistance usually comes from loss of viral thymidine kinase, so foscarnet, which does not need activation, is used."]],
  red: "Fever with confusion, unusual behavior, severe headache or seizures, a painful red eye with vision changes, or blisters in a newborn needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Gertrude Elion never completed a PhD, yet her rational drug design approach led to acyclovir and several other lifesaving drugs and a Nobel Prize.",
  src: ["CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines (2021)", "Elion GB et al. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1977", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1988 (Black, Elion, Hitchings)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"shingles": {
  name: "Shingles (herpes zoster) and chickenpox",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["herpes zoster", "zoster", "chickenpox", "varicella", "VZV", "postherpetic neuralgia"],
  what: "Chickenpox and shingles are caused by the same virus, varicella-zoster virus. Chickenpox is the first infection, with an itchy blistering rash all over; the virus then sleeps in nerves and can wake up years later as shingles, a painful band of blisters on one side of the body.",
  origin: "Chickenpox was long confused with mild smallpox until English physician William Heberden described clear differences in 1767. In 1888 Hungarian pediatrician Janos von Bokay noticed that children often developed chickenpox after contact with adults with shingles, suggesting a shared cause. In the 1950s Thomas Weller grew the virus in cell culture and showed that chickenpox and shingles viruses were the same. In 1965 British general practitioner Edgar Hope-Simpson proposed that shingles is reactivation of latent virus when immunity wanes. Japanese researcher Michiaki Takahashi developed the live attenuated Oka strain vaccine in 1974, and the US began routine childhood chickenpox vaccination in 1995. A live shingles vaccine was approved in 2006, followed in 2017 by the much more effective recombinant vaccine (Shingrix).",
  timeline: [["1767", "William Heberden distinguishes chickenpox from smallpox."], ["1888", "Janos von Bokay links chickenpox in children to contact with shingles."], ["1950s", "Thomas Weller shows chickenpox and shingles are caused by the same virus."], ["1974", "Michiaki Takahashi develops the Oka strain chickenpox vaccine."], ["1995", "The varicella vaccine is licensed in the US."], ["2017", "The recombinant zoster vaccine is approved in the US."]],
  cell: "Varicella-zoster virus (VZV), human herpesvirus 3, is a double-stranded DNA alphaherpesvirus. In chickenpox, it enters through the respiratory tract and infects tonsillar T cells, which carry it to the skin and other organs. In the skin it spreads cell to cell, forming multinucleated giant cells and intranuclear inclusions, creating crops of vesicles at different stages. During infection the virus enters sensory nerve endings and travels to dorsal root ganglia and cranial nerve ganglia, where it becomes latent. VZV-specific cell-mediated (T cell) immunity keeps it in check. When this immunity declines with age, cancer, HIV, transplant drugs or steroids, the virus reactivates in one ganglion and travels down the sensory nerve, causing a rash in that nerve's dermatome. Inflammation and damage in the ganglion and nerve cause the pain, and lasting nerve injury with abnormal firing and loss of inhibitory signaling causes postherpetic neuralgia. The recombinant vaccine uses glycoprotein E with a strong adjuvant to boost this T cell immunity.",
  body: [["Skin", "Chickenpox causes itchy crops of spots, blisters and crusts over the whole body; shingles causes a band of painful blisters in one dermatome."], ["Nerves", "Shingles inflames the sensory ganglion and nerve; postherpetic neuralgia is nerve pain lasting months after the rash heals."], ["Eyes", "Herpes zoster ophthalmicus affects the first branch of the trigeminal nerve and can damage the eye; a rash on the tip of the nose is a warning sign."], ["Face and ears", "Ramsay Hunt syndrome involves the geniculate ganglion, causing facial paralysis, ear pain and blisters in the ear."], ["Lungs and brain", "Varicella pneumonia (especially in adults and pregnancy), encephalitis and cerebellar ataxia in children are serious complications; zoster can raise stroke risk."], ["Skin infection", "Scratched chickenpox lesions can become infected with strep or staph, including necrotizing fasciitis."]],
  symptoms: "In chickenpox, fever and fatigue are followed by an itchy rash that moves from spots to blisters to crusts, with lesions in different stages at once, because new crops form as the virus spreads. In shingles, burning or tingling pain often comes days before the rash because the virus is first inflaming the nerve. The rash stays on one side and does not cross the midline because it follows a single nerve. Pain after healing comes from damaged, overactive nerve fibers.",
  dx: "Both are usually diagnosed by the typical rash. PCR of fluid from a blister is the most sensitive confirmation and separates VZV from HSV. Direct fluorescent antibody testing is an older option. Blood tests for VZV antibodies show past infection or vaccination. An eye exam with a slit lamp is done for zoster near the eye, and cerebrospinal fluid PCR is used if encephalitis or meningitis is suspected.",
  tx: "Antivirals (valacyclovir, acyclovir, famciclovir) are activated by viral thymidine kinase and block viral DNA polymerase. For shingles they work best started within 72 hours of the rash and speed healing and reduce acute pain. IV acyclovir is used for disseminated disease, immune suppression and serious complications. For chickenpox, antivirals are used for adolescents, adults and people at high risk. Pain is treated with acetaminophen, NSAIDs and sometimes opioids. Postherpetic neuralgia is treated with gabapentin or pregabalin (which reduce calcium channel signaling in pain nerves), lidocaine patches, capsaicin patches or tricyclic antidepressants. Aspirin is avoided in children with chickenpox because of Reye syndrome. Prevention includes the live varicella vaccine for children, the recombinant zoster vaccine for adults aged 50 and older and some immunocompromised adults, and varicella-zoster immune globulin for exposed high-risk people.",
  drugs: ["valacyclovir", "acyclovir", "famciclovir", "foscarnet", "varicella-vaccine", "zoster-recombinant-vaccine", "varicella-zoster-immune-globulin", "gabapentin", "pregabalin", "lidocaine-patch", "capsaicin-topical", "nortriptyline", "amitriptyline", "acetaminophen", "ibuprofen", "diphenhydramine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Shingles follows a dermatome and does not cross the midline; the virus reactivates from a dorsal root or cranial nerve ganglion."], ["Memory trick", "Hutchinson sign: a rash on the tip of the nose means the nasociliary nerve is involved and the eye is at risk."], ["Key fact", "Aspirin is avoided in children with chickenpox because of the risk of Reye syndrome (liver failure and brain swelling)."], ["Chickenpox clue", "Lesions in different stages at the same time (dew drop on a rose petal) suggest chickenpox, unlike smallpox where lesions are all at the same stage."]],
  red: "Shingles near the eye or on the tip of the nose, facial weakness, confusion, stiff neck, shortness of breath with chickenpox, a widespread rash in someone with a weak immune system, or a red, very painful spreading area around chickenpox sores needs urgent or emergency care (call 911 for severe illness).",
  fun: "About one in three people in the US will develop shingles in their lifetime, and the recombinant vaccine is more than 90 percent effective at preventing it in older adults.",
  src: ["CDC/ACIP Recommendations for Use of Recombinant Zoster Vaccine (2018, updated 2022)", "CDC Pink Book: Varicella", "Hope-Simpson RE. Proc R Soc Med 1965", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"strep-pharyngitis": {
  name: "Strep throat",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["streptococcal pharyngitis", "group A strep", "GAS pharyngitis", "strep tonsillitis", "scarlet fever"],
  what: "Strep throat is a throat and tonsil infection caused by group A Streptococcus bacteria. It causes a sudden sore throat and fever, and antibiotics help prevent complications such as rheumatic fever.",
  origin: "Sore throats with rash, called scarlet fever, were described in the 1600s and were a major killer of children in the 1800s. In 1874 Theodor Billroth saw chains of round bacteria in infected wounds, and in 1884 Friedrich Rosenbach named Streptococcus pyogenes. In the late 1920s and 1930s Rebecca Lancefield at the Rockefeller Institute classified streptococci into groups by their cell wall carbohydrates, establishing group A as the main human pathogen. The link between strep throat and rheumatic fever was confirmed in the mid 1900s. In studies at Warren Air Force Base in Wyoming around 1950, Floyd Denny, Charles Rammelkamp, Lewis Wannamaker and colleagues showed that penicillin treatment of strep throat prevents rheumatic fever. Rapid antigen tests in the 1980s made diagnosis faster.",
  timeline: [["1884", "Friedrich Rosenbach names Streptococcus pyogenes."], ["1930s", "Rebecca Lancefield classifies streptococci into serologic groups."], ["1950", "Studies at Warren Air Force Base show penicillin prevents rheumatic fever after strep throat."], ["1980s", "Rapid antigen detection tests for group A strep come into use."], ["2012", "IDSA publishes updated guidelines on group A strep pharyngitis."]],
  cell: "Streptococcus pyogenes is a gram-positive coccus in chains that is beta-hemolytic and bacitracin sensitive. It spreads by respiratory droplets and attaches to throat epithelium using lipoteichoic acid, fibronectin-binding proteins and M protein. M protein is the main virulence factor: it blocks complement deposition and phagocytosis. The hyaluronic acid capsule mimics human tissue, and C5a peptidase blocks neutrophil recruitment. Streptolysin O and S damage cell membranes, and enzymes such as streptokinase, DNase and hyaluronidase help spread. Strains carrying pyrogenic exotoxins (superantigens) cause the rash of scarlet fever and can cause streptococcal toxic shock. Complications come from immune responses: in acute rheumatic fever, antibodies against M protein cross-react with cardiac myosin and other heart, joint and brain proteins (molecular mimicry), a type II hypersensitivity. Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis comes from immune complex deposits in the glomeruli (type III hypersensitivity) and can follow throat or skin infection.",
  body: [["Throat and tonsils", "Inflamed, swollen tonsils with white or yellow exudate, red throat and tiny red spots on the palate (petechiae)."], ["Neck lymph nodes", "Front neck nodes swell and become tender as they filter bacteria."], ["Skin", "Scarlet fever causes a fine, sandpaper-like red rash and a strawberry tongue from exotoxins."], ["Heart", "Rheumatic fever can cause carditis and, over time, scarred heart valves (mitral stenosis is classic)."], ["Kidneys", "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis causes cola-colored urine, swelling and high blood pressure one to three weeks after infection."], ["Neck spaces", "Infection can spread into a peritonsillar abscess, causing a muffled voice, trismus and a deviated uvula."]],
  symptoms: "A sudden sore throat, pain on swallowing and fever come from bacterial invasion and the neutrophil response in the tonsils. Tender swollen neck nodes reflect local immune activity. Headache, belly pain and vomiting are common in children. Cough, runny nose and hoarseness are unusual and suggest a virus instead. Scarlet fever's rash comes from circulating toxin, not from bacteria in the skin.",
  dx: "Clinical scores such as the Centor or McIsaac criteria (fever, tonsillar exudate, tender anterior neck nodes, absence of cough, and age) estimate the likelihood of strep. Because symptoms overlap with viral infections, testing is used to confirm: a rapid antigen detection test from a throat swab, with a backup throat culture in children and teens if negative, since missing strep in children carries a risk of rheumatic fever. Testing is usually not needed with clear viral features or in children under 3. ASO and anti-DNase B antibodies help diagnose rheumatic fever or glomerulonephritis, not acute strep throat.",
  tx: "Penicillin V or amoxicillin is first-line, and group A strep has never developed resistance to penicillin. These beta-lactams block cell wall cross-linking. A single intramuscular injection of benzathine penicillin G is an option when taking pills for the full course is unlikely. For penicillin allergy, first-generation cephalosporins (cephalexin, cefadroxil) are used if the allergy is not severe; clindamycin, azithromycin or clarithromycin are used otherwise, though macrolide resistance occurs. Finishing the full course matters for preventing rheumatic fever. Acetaminophen or ibuprofen relieves pain and fever. People are usually considered no longer contagious after about 12 hours of effective antibiotics. Peritonsillar abscess is drained.",
  drugs: ["penicillin-v", "amoxicillin", "penicillin-g-benzathine", "cephalexin", "cefadroxil", "cefuroxime", "cefdinir", "clindamycin", "azithromycin", "clarithromycin", "acetaminophen", "ibuprofen"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Treating strep throat prevents rheumatic fever but does not reliably prevent post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis."], ["Memory trick", "Rheumatic fever is JONES: Joints, O (heart, carditis), Nodules, Erythema marginatum, Sydenham chorea."], ["Key fact", "Group A strep remains universally sensitive to penicillin."], ["Often confused with", "Infectious mononucleosis (EBV) causes exudative pharyngitis with posterior neck nodes and fatigue; amoxicillin often causes a rash in mono."]],
  red: "Trouble breathing, drooling or inability to swallow, a muffled hot-potato voice, inability to open the mouth, a stiff or swollen neck, or signs of dehydration needs emergency care (call 911 for breathing trouble).",
  fun: "Rebecca Lancefield's grouping system was so useful that streptococci are still named by her letters, group A, group B and so on, nearly a century later.",
  src: ["IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for Group A Streptococcal Pharyngitis (2012)", "AHA Scientific Statement on Prevention of Rheumatic Fever (2009)", "Denny FW et al. JAMA 1950", "Lancefield RC. J Exp Med 1933"]
},
"otitis-media": {
  name: "Ear infection (otitis media)",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["acute otitis media", "AOM", "middle ear infection", "otitis media with effusion", "glue ear"],
  what: "Otitis media is an infection or inflammation of the middle ear, the air space behind the eardrum. It is very common in young children, often follows a cold and causes ear pain, fever and fussiness.",
  origin: "Ear pain and draining ears were described in ancient Egyptian, Greek and Roman medicine. In the 1800s Adam Politzer in Vienna and other otologists improved otoscopy and described how the eustachian tube links the middle ear to the throat. Before antibiotics, ear infections often spread to the mastoid bone and brain, and mastoidectomy was a common operation. Antibiotics in the 1940s made such complications much rarer. Ventilation (tympanostomy) tubes were popularized in the 1950s for persistent fluid. Conjugate vaccines against Haemophilus influenzae type b (late 1980s) and Streptococcus pneumoniae (2000) changed which bacteria cause ear infections. In 2004 and 2013, American Academy of Pediatrics guidelines set strict criteria for diagnosis and endorsed watchful waiting for some children to reduce unnecessary antibiotics.",
  timeline: [["1940s", "Antibiotics make mastoiditis and brain complications of ear infections much less common."], ["1950s", "Tympanostomy tubes are popularized for persistent middle ear fluid."], ["2000", "The first pneumococcal conjugate vaccine is licensed in the US for infants."], ["2013", "The AAP updates its acute otitis media guideline, including observation options."]],
  cell: "The eustachian tube normally ventilates and drains the middle ear. In young children it is shorter, more horizontal and floppier, and adenoids may block it. A viral upper respiratory infection inflames the nasopharynx and eustachian tube, which then fails to open. The middle ear absorbs its air, creating negative pressure that draws in fluid and bacteria from the nasopharynx. The main bacteria are Streptococcus pneumoniae, nontypeable Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis; many H. influenzae and nearly all Moraxella make beta-lactamase. Bacteria trigger cytokines and neutrophil influx, filling the space with pus that bulges the eardrum. Daycare, bottle feeding while lying down, secondhand smoke, cleft palate and Down syndrome raise risk, while breastfeeding and vaccines lower it. Fluid can persist for weeks after infection (otitis media with effusion), causing temporary hearing loss. Rarely, infection spreads to the mastoid air cells, inner ear or brain.",
  body: [["Middle ear", "Pus and fluid fill the space behind the eardrum, which becomes red and bulging and moves poorly."], ["Eardrum", "High pressure can rupture the eardrum, causing sudden drainage and often pain relief; most perforations heal."], ["Hearing", "Fluid dampens eardrum movement, causing temporary conductive hearing loss that can affect speech and learning if long-lasting."], ["Mastoid bone", "Spread to the mastoid causes mastoiditis with swelling and redness behind the ear and a pushed-forward ear."], ["Brain and inner ear", "Rare spread can cause meningitis, brain abscess, venous sinus thrombosis or labyrinthitis with vertigo."]],
  symptoms: "Ear pain comes from pressure and inflammation stretching the sensitive eardrum, which is why infants pull at ears, cry and sleep poorly, especially when lying down. Fever reflects cytokines. Muffled hearing is due to fluid behind the drum. Drainage from the ear means the drum has perforated. Cold symptoms often come first because a virus starts the process.",
  dx: "Diagnosis relies on otoscopy. The AAP criteria include a moderately to severely bulging eardrum, or new ear drainage not caused by outer ear infection, or a mildly bulging drum with recent ear pain or intense redness. Pneumatic otoscopy shows reduced eardrum movement from fluid. Tympanometry can confirm fluid. Otitis media with effusion shows fluid without signs of acute infection. A red eardrum alone, which can come from crying or fever, is not enough to diagnose infection.",
  tx: "Pain control with acetaminophen or ibuprofen is a core part of care. For many children over 6 months with mild, one-sided symptoms, clinicians may observe for 48 to 72 hours, because many infections clear on their own. Amoxicillin, a beta-lactam that blocks cell wall building, is the first-choice antibiotic. Amoxicillin-clavulanate adds a beta-lactamase inhibitor and is used when amoxicillin was given recently, with conjunctivitis (which suggests H. influenzae) or after treatment failure. Cephalosporins such as cefdinir, cefuroxime or cefpodoxime are used with non-severe penicillin allergy, and intramuscular ceftriaxone when oral therapy is not possible. Children with ear tubes and drainage are often treated with antibiotic ear drops such as ofloxacin. Tympanostomy tubes are considered for recurrent infections or long-lasting fluid with hearing loss. Pneumococcal and flu vaccines, breastfeeding and avoiding smoke exposure help prevention.",
  drugs: ["amoxicillin", "amoxicillin-clavulanate", "cefdinir", "cefuroxime", "cefpodoxime", "ceftriaxone", "azithromycin", "clindamycin", "ofloxacin", "acetaminophen", "ibuprofen"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "The three main bacteria are S. pneumoniae, nontypeable H. influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis, the same ones that cause sinusitis."], ["Memory trick", "Otitis-conjunctivitis syndrome points to H. influenzae, so choose amoxicillin-clavulanate."], ["Key fact", "A bulging tympanic membrane is the most specific sign of acute otitis media; redness alone is not enough."], ["Often confused with", "Otitis externa (swimmer's ear) is an outer ear canal infection, often with Pseudomonas, with pain on pulling the ear."]],
  red: "Swelling, redness or tenderness behind the ear, an ear pushed forward, severe headache, stiff neck, confusion, facial weakness, high fever or a very sleepy or hard-to-wake child needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "By age 3, most children in the US have had at least one ear infection, which makes it one of the most common reasons children receive antibiotics.",
  src: ["AAP Clinical Practice Guideline: The Diagnosis and Management of Acute Otitis Media (2013)", "AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Otitis Media with Effusion (2016)", "AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Tympanostomy Tubes in Children (2022)", "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"]
},
"sinusitis": {
  name: "Sinusitis",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["rhinosinusitis", "acute bacterial rhinosinusitis", "chronic rhinosinusitis", "sinus infection", "CRSwNP"],
  what: "Sinusitis is inflammation of the air-filled spaces around the nose. Most cases follow a cold and are viral, causing stuffiness, thick nasal mucus and facial pressure; a small share become bacterial or chronic.",
  origin: "The paranasal sinuses were described by anatomists of the Renaissance, and in 1651 English anatomist Nathaniel Highmore described the maxillary sinus, still sometimes called the antrum of Highmore. In the late 1800s surgeons including George Caldwell and Henri Luc developed an operation to open and clean the maxillary sinus through the mouth. In the 1970s and 1980s Walter Messerklinger and Heinz Stammberger in Austria showed that blockage at a narrow drainage pathway, the ostiomeatal complex, drives most sinus disease, and David Kennedy brought functional endoscopic sinus surgery to the US in 1985. Guidelines from IDSA (2012) and the American Academy of Otolaryngology (2015) aimed to limit antibiotics to likely bacterial cases. Biologic drugs targeting type 2 inflammation, starting with dupilumab in 2019, opened a new approach to chronic sinusitis with nasal polyps.",
  timeline: [["1651", "Nathaniel Highmore describes the maxillary sinus."], ["1890s", "The Caldwell-Luc operation is developed for maxillary sinus disease."], ["1985", "Functional endoscopic sinus surgery is introduced in the US."], ["2012", "IDSA publishes guidelines for acute bacterial rhinosinusitis."], ["2019", "Dupilumab is approved for chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps."]],
  cell: "The sinuses are lined with ciliated respiratory epithelium that sweeps mucus toward small openings (ostia) that drain into the nose, mainly through the ostiomeatal complex. A cold virus, such as rhinovirus, inflames the lining, causing swelling that narrows the ostia and slows ciliary beating. Mucus builds up, oxygen falls and pressure changes, giving a good setting for bacteria. In a minority of cases, nasal bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella catarrhalis invade, and neutrophils produce purulent mucus. Allergies, dental infections (for the maxillary sinus), cystic fibrosis, ciliary dyskinesia and anatomic blockage raise risk. Chronic rhinosinusitis lasts 12 weeks or more and is mostly an inflammatory disease rather than an infection. In chronic sinusitis with nasal polyps, type 2 inflammation with Th2 cells, ILC2s, IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, eosinophils and IgE causes swollen tissue that forms polyps. Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease combines polyps, asthma and reactions to NSAIDs due to altered arachidonic acid metabolism. In uncontrolled diabetes or neutropenia, invasive fungi such as Mucor can cause rapidly destructive sinus infection.",
  body: [["Sinuses", "Swollen lining and trapped mucus cause pressure and pain over the cheeks, forehead or between the eyes."], ["Nose", "Congestion, thick discolored drainage and reduced sense of smell come from inflamed nasal tissue and polyps."], ["Throat and lungs", "Postnasal drip causes cough and sore throat; chronic sinus disease often travels with asthma."], ["Eyes and orbit", "Ethmoid sinusitis can spread into the orbit, causing orbital cellulitis with eye swelling, pain with eye movement and vision changes."], ["Brain", "Rare spread causes meningitis, brain abscess or cavernous sinus thrombosis; frontal sinusitis can cause a forehead abscess (Pott puffy tumor)."], ["Teeth", "Upper tooth pain can come from maxillary sinus inflammation, and dental infections can cause sinusitis."]],
  symptoms: "Nasal blockage and discharge happen because the swollen lining makes extra mucus and blocks drainage. Facial pain and pressure, worse when bending forward, reflect trapped mucus and swelling. Thick yellow or green mucus reflects inflammatory cells and does not alone prove bacterial infection. Loss of smell occurs when swelling or polyps block air from reaching the smell nerves. Fever, a high fever at onset or worsening after initial improvement suggests bacterial infection.",
  dx: "Acute rhinosinusitis is diagnosed clinically. Bacterial infection is suspected when symptoms last 10 days or more without improvement, when severe symptoms with high fever and purulent discharge last 3 to 4 days at onset, or when symptoms worsen again after starting to improve (double worsening). Imaging is not needed for routine cases. CT of the sinuses is used for chronic sinusitis, before surgery or when complications are suspected, and MRI when spread to the eye or brain is a concern. Chronic rhinosinusitis requires 12 weeks of symptoms plus objective evidence on nasal endoscopy or CT.",
  tx: "Most acute sinusitis is viral and improves on its own. Saline irrigation thins and clears mucus, and intranasal corticosteroids such as fluticasone or mometasone reduce lining swelling. Analgesics relieve pain, and short-term decongestants such as oxymetazoline (a few days only, to avoid rebound congestion) or pseudoephedrine shrink swollen vessels. When bacterial infection is likely, amoxicillin or amoxicillin-clavulanate is first-line; doxycycline or a respiratory fluoroquinolone such as levofloxacin is used for penicillin allergy. Watchful waiting is an option for uncomplicated cases. Chronic rhinosinusitis is treated with daily saline and nasal steroids, short oral steroid courses for polyps, steroid-releasing sinus implants, endoscopic sinus surgery, and biologics for polyps: dupilumab (blocks IL-4 and IL-13 signaling), mepolizumab (blocks IL-5) and omalizumab (blocks IgE). Complications such as orbital cellulitis are treated with IV antibiotics and sometimes surgery.",
  drugs: ["amoxicillin-clavulanate", "amoxicillin", "doxycycline", "levofloxacin", "cefpodoxime", "cefdinir", "fluticasone-nasal", "mometasone", "budesonide-nasal", "saline-nasal-spray", "oxymetazoline", "pseudoephedrine", "azelastine-fluticasone", "ipratropium-nasal", "prednisone", "mometasone-sinus-implant", "dupilumab", "mepolizumab", "omalizumab", "acetaminophen", "ibuprofen"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Most acute sinusitis is viral; think bacterial only with symptoms lasting 10 or more days, severe onset or double worsening."], ["Memory trick", "Ethmoid sinusitis threatens the Eye: orbital cellulitis is its classic complication, especially in children."], ["Key fact", "Black necrotic tissue in the nose of a person with diabetic ketoacidosis suggests mucormycosis, an emergency."], ["Samter triad", "Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease: nasal polyps, asthma and reactions to aspirin or NSAIDs."]],
  red: "Swelling or redness around the eye, double vision or vision changes, pain with eye movement, severe headache, a stiff neck, confusion, forehead swelling or high fever with sinus symptoms needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Babies are born with only tiny maxillary and ethmoid sinuses; the frontal sinuses do not develop until about school age, so frontal sinusitis is rare in young children.",
  src: ["IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for Acute Bacterial Rhinosinusitis in Children and Adults (2012)", "AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Adult Sinusitis Update (2015)", "European Position Paper on Rhinosinusitis and Nasal Polyps (EPOS 2020)", "Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery"]
},
"measles": {
  name: "Measles",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["rubeola", "morbilli", "measles virus infection"],
  what: "Measles is a very contagious viral infection that causes high fever, cough, runny nose, red eyes and a spreading rash. It can cause pneumonia, brain swelling and death, and it is preventable with the MMR vaccine.",
  origin: "Around the year 900, Persian physician Abu Bakr al-Razi (Rhazes) wrote one of the first clear accounts separating measles from smallpox. In 1846 Danish physician Peter Panum studied an outbreak on the isolated Faroe Islands and showed that measles has an incubation period of about two weeks and gives lifelong immunity. In 1954 John Enders and Thomas Peebles isolated the virus from a sick boy named David Edmonston, and the Edmonston strain became the basis for the first vaccine, licensed in the US in 1963. Maurice Hilleman developed the improved vaccine used in the combined MMR vaccine, licensed in 1971. A second dose was recommended in 1989 after outbreaks. Measles was declared eliminated from the US in 2000, meaning no continuous spread for 12 months. Falling vaccination rates have since brought large outbreaks, including in 2019 and 2025, when the US saw its highest case counts in decades and child deaths.",
  timeline: [["1846", "Peter Panum studies the Faroe Islands outbreak and describes incubation and lasting immunity."], ["1954", "John Enders and Thomas Peebles isolate the measles virus."], ["1963", "The first measles vaccine is licensed in the US."], ["1971", "The combined MMR vaccine is licensed."], ["2000", "Measles is declared eliminated from the United States."], ["2025", "Large US outbreaks cause the most cases in more than 30 years."]],
  cell: "Measles virus is an enveloped, single-stranded negative-sense RNA virus in the paramyxovirus family (genus Morbillivirus). It spreads through the air and can linger in a room for up to two hours. Its hemagglutinin (H) protein binds SLAM (CD150) on activated immune cells, including memory T and B cells, dendritic cells and macrophages in the airway, and the fusion (F) protein lets it enter. Infected immune cells carry the virus to lymph nodes and then throughout the body. Late in infection, the virus binds nectin-4 on the airway epithelium, which lets it exit into respiratory secretions, making people contagious from about four days before to four days after the rash. Infected cells fuse into multinucleated giant cells (Warthin-Finkeldey cells). The rash and Koplik spots reflect T cell attack on infected endothelium and epithelium. Measles kills many memory lymphocytes, causing immune amnesia that erases protection against other infections for months to years. Vitamin A deficiency worsens disease. Rarely, a defective virus persists in the brain and causes subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE) years later.",
  body: [["Skin and mouth", "A red, blotchy rash starts at the hairline and face and spreads down; tiny white Koplik spots on the inner cheeks appear before the rash."], ["Lungs", "Measles pneumonia, from the virus or secondary bacteria, is the most common cause of measles death in children."], ["Immune system", "Destruction of memory immune cells causes immune amnesia, raising the risk of other infections for years."], ["Brain", "Acute encephalitis can cause seizures, deafness or intellectual disability, and SSPE is a fatal brain disease years later."], ["Eyes", "Conjunctivitis is common, and in vitamin A deficiency measles can damage the cornea and cause blindness."], ["Gut and ears", "Diarrhea and ear infections are common complications, especially in young children."], ["Pregnancy", "Measles in pregnancy raises the risk of miscarriage, preterm birth and low birth weight."]],
  symptoms: "The illness starts with high fever, cough, runny nose and red eyes (the three Cs) as the virus spreads through the airways and immune cells. Koplik spots appear a day or two before the rash. The rash appears around day 3 to 5 of illness, starting at the face and spreading downward, as T cells attack infected cells in the skin's blood vessels. Fever often peaks when the rash appears. Fast breathing, trouble breathing or confusion signal complications.",
  dx: "Measles is suspected with fever, rash and the three Cs, especially with known exposure, travel or lack of vaccination. Confirmation uses RT-PCR on a throat or nasopharyngeal swab (and urine), and measles IgM antibodies in blood. Suspected cases are reported immediately to public health, often before results return, so contacts can be found. Patients are placed in airborne isolation, ideally in a negative-pressure room.",
  tx: "There is no specific antiviral approved for measles, so care is supportive with fluids, fever control and treatment of complications such as bacterial pneumonia or ear infections. Vitamin A is recommended for children with measles, as it lowers death and complications, especially in children with low vitamin A levels. Prevention after exposure includes the MMR vaccine within 72 hours, or immune globulin within six days for infants, pregnant people without immunity and people with weakened immune systems, who cannot receive live vaccines. Two doses of MMR are about 97 percent effective at preventing measles. Isolation until four days after the rash appears limits spread.",
  drugs: ["mmr-vaccine", "vitamin-a", "intravenous-immune-globulin", "acetaminophen", "ibuprofen"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Koplik spots, small white spots on the buccal mucosa, appear before the rash and are specific for measles."], ["Memory trick", "The three Cs of measles: Cough, Coryza (runny nose), Conjunctivitis, plus fever and a head-to-toe rash."], ["Key fact", "Measles is one of the most contagious diseases known; each case can infect 12 to 18 unvaccinated people."], ["Often confused with", "Rubella (German measles) is milder with posterior ear and neck lymph nodes; roseola gives a rash after the fever breaks."]],
  red: "Trouble breathing, fast breathing, blue lips, seizures, confusion, extreme sleepiness, signs of dehydration or a very high fever with measles needs emergency care (call 911). Calling ahead before arriving helps the clinic or hospital prevent spreading measles.",
  fun: "The same virus that inspired the measles vaccine was taken from a single boy, David Edmonston, in 1954; versions of his strain are still in the MMR vaccine today.",
  src: ["CDC Pink Book: Measles", "CDC Measles Clinical Overview and Post-exposure Prophylaxis Guidance", "AAP Red Book: Measles", "Enders JF, Peebles TC. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1954", "Mina MJ et al. Science 2019 (immune amnesia)"]
},
"tetanus": {
  name: "Tetanus",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["lockjaw", "Clostridium tetani infection", "neonatal tetanus"],
  what: "Tetanus is a nervous system illness caused by a toxin from Clostridium tetani bacteria that enter through a wound. The toxin causes painful muscle stiffness and spasms, often starting with a locked jaw, and is preventable with vaccination.",
  origin: "Hippocrates described the painful spasms of tetanus after wounds, and the disease was a common killer of soldiers and newborns for centuries. In 1884 Antonio Carle and Giorgio Rattone in Italy transmitted tetanus to animals, and the same year Arthur Nicolaier in Germany produced it by injecting soil and saw the slender bacteria. In 1889 Shibasaburo Kitasato, working in Robert Koch's lab, grew C. tetani in pure culture and showed it causes disease through a toxin. In 1890 Kitasato and Emil von Behring showed that serum from immunized animals (antitoxin) could neutralize the toxin. In the 1920s Gaston Ramon and others developed tetanus toxoid, a toxin made harmless with formaldehyde. Its use in soldiers during World War II nearly eliminated wartime tetanus, and routine childhood vaccination later made the disease rare in the US. Human tetanus immune globulin later replaced horse serum antitoxin in the US.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Hippocrates describes tetanus after wounds."], ["1884", "Carle and Rattone transmit tetanus, and Nicolaier links it to soil bacteria."], ["1889", "Shibasaburo Kitasato grows Clostridium tetani in pure culture."], ["1890", "Behring and Kitasato describe tetanus antitoxin."], ["1920s", "Tetanus toxoid vaccine is developed."], ["1940s", "Tetanus toxoid vaccination of soldiers in World War II nearly eliminates combat tetanus."]],
  cell: "Clostridium tetani is a gram-positive, spore-forming, strictly anaerobic rod with a drumstick or tennis racket shape from its end spore. Spores live in soil and animal feces and survive for years. They enter through wounds, especially deep, dirty or crush wounds, burns, injection sites or an infected umbilical stump. In the low-oxygen wound they germinate and release tetanospasmin, a plasmid-encoded zinc metalloprotease, one of the most potent toxins known. The toxin binds gangliosides on motor nerve terminals, is taken up and travels by retrograde axonal transport to the spinal cord and brainstem. There it crosses into inhibitory interneurons, including Renshaw cells, and cleaves synaptobrevin (VAMP), a SNARE protein needed for vesicle release. Release of the inhibitory neurotransmitters GABA and glycine stops, so motor neurons fire without restraint, causing rigidity and spasms. Loss of inhibition in autonomic pathways causes sympathetic overactivity. Toxin binding is irreversible, so recovery requires growth of new nerve terminals over weeks. Botulinum toxin, by contrast, blocks acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction and causes flaccid paralysis.",
  body: [["Jaw and face", "Masseter spasm causes lockjaw (trismus), and facial muscle spasm causes a fixed grin (risus sardonicus)."], ["Back and trunk", "Strong spasms of back muscles arch the body (opisthotonus) and can cause fractures."], ["Airway and breathing", "Spasms of the larynx and chest wall can block breathing and are a main cause of death without ventilator support."], ["Autonomic nervous system", "Sympathetic overactivity causes swings in blood pressure, fast heart rate, sweating, fever and arrhythmias."], ["Muscles", "Constant contraction can cause rhabdomyolysis and severe pain."], ["Newborns", "Neonatal tetanus from an unclean umbilical cord causes poor feeding and spasms and remains a cause of infant death in some countries."]],
  symptoms: "Jaw stiffness is often first because the cranial nerve pathways are short, so the toxin reaches them early. Stiff neck, trouble swallowing and abdominal rigidity follow. Painful spasms can be set off by noise, light or touch because motor neurons have lost inhibition. People usually stay fully awake during spasms, which makes them very painful. Sweating, fast heart rate and swinging blood pressure reflect autonomic instability.",
  dx: "Tetanus is a clinical diagnosis based on typical muscle stiffness and spasms, often with a recent wound and incomplete vaccination, although some patients have no recalled wound. No lab test confirms it, and wound cultures are often negative. The spatula test, in which touching the back of the throat causes the jaw to bite down instead of gagging, is a classic bedside sign. Other causes of spasms, such as strychnine poisoning, dystonic drug reactions, low calcium and meningitis, are ruled out.",
  tx: "Treatment has several goals. Human tetanus immune globulin neutralizes toxin that has not yet entered nerves, and the wound is cleaned and debrided to remove the toxin source. Antibiotics, with metronidazole preferred and penicillin as an alternative, kill the bacteria. Benzodiazepines such as diazepam, lorazepam or midazolam enhance GABA-A signaling to control rigidity and spasms. Magnesium sulfate helps control spasms and autonomic swings. Severe cases need intubation, sedation and neuromuscular blockade with drugs such as vecuronium in an ICU, sometimes for weeks, and intrathecal baclofen is used in some centers. Patients are kept in a quiet, dark room to reduce triggers. Because the disease does not cause immunity, vaccination with tetanus toxoid is started during recovery. Prevention includes the DTaP series for children, Tdap for adolescents and adults, boosters every 10 years, and tetanus immune globulin for dirty wounds in people without full vaccination.",
  drugs: ["tetanus-immune-globulin", "tdap-vaccine", "td-vaccine", "dtap-vaccine", "metronidazole", "penicillin-g", "diazepam", "lorazepam", "midazolam", "magnesium-sulfate", "baclofen", "vecuronium", "rocuronium", "propofol", "labetalol"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Tetanus toxin blocks release of GABA and glycine from inhibitory interneurons (Renshaw cells) by cleaving synaptobrevin, causing spastic paralysis."], ["Memory trick", "Tetanus means Tense muscles (spastic); Botulinum means Barely moving muscles (flaccid)."], ["Key fact", "Surviving tetanus does not create immunity, so patients still need the vaccine series."], ["Wound care rule", "Tetanus immune globulin plus vaccine is used for dirty wounds when a person has had fewer than three tetanus doses or an unknown history."]],
  red: "Jaw stiffness, trouble swallowing, neck stiffness or muscle spasms after a wound, puncture or burn needs emergency care (call 911). A dirty or deep wound in someone not up to date on tetanus shots needs prompt medical evaluation.",
  fun: "Stepping on a rusty nail is linked with tetanus not because of rust, but because nails outdoors are often covered in soil and make deep, low-oxygen puncture wounds where spores thrive.",
  src: ["CDC Pink Book: Tetanus", "CDC/ACIP Tdap and Td Vaccine Recommendations (2020)", "Kitasato S. 1889; Behring E, Kitasato S. 1890", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"rabies": {
  name: "Rabies",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["hydrophobia", "lyssavirus infection"],
  what: "Rabies is a viral infection of the brain spread through the bite or scratch of an infected animal. Once symptoms start it is almost always fatal, but prompt wound washing and post-exposure shots prevent it nearly every time.",
  origin: "Rabies is one of the oldest recognized diseases: the Mesopotamian Laws of Eshnunna, nearly 4,000 years old, set penalties for owners of mad dogs that bit people. Ancient Greek and Roman writers linked the disease to dog bites and described hydrophobia. In 1804 Georg Gottfried Zinke showed that saliva from a rabid dog could transmit the disease. In 1885 Louis Pasteur and Emile Roux, using a vaccine made from dried infected rabbit spinal cord, treated nine-year-old Joseph Meister after he was bitten, and he survived, a landmark in vaccine history. In 1903 Adelchi Negri described the inclusion bodies in nerve cells now called Negri bodies. Safer vaccines grown in human diploid cells replaced older nerve tissue vaccines in the 1970s and 1980s. In 2004 Jeanna Giese in Wisconsin became one of the very few people to survive rabies without vaccination, after an experimental coma treatment, although later attempts with the same approach have mostly failed.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "The Mesopotamian Laws of Eshnunna mention penalties for bites from mad dogs."], ["1804", "Georg Gottfried Zinke shows rabies can be transmitted through saliva."], ["1885", "Louis Pasteur's rabies vaccine is given to Joseph Meister, who survives."], ["1903", "Adelchi Negri describes Negri bodies in infected neurons."], ["1980", "A human diploid cell rabies vaccine is licensed in the US."], ["2004", "Jeanna Giese survives rabies after symptoms began, without prior vaccination."]],
  cell: "Rabies virus is a bullet-shaped, enveloped, single-stranded negative-sense RNA virus (rhabdovirus, genus Lyssavirus). It enters through a bite or scratch or contact of saliva with mucous membranes. The virus may replicate briefly in muscle, then its glycoprotein (G) binds receptors such as the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor at the neuromuscular junction, NCAM and p75 neurotrophin receptor. It enters peripheral nerves and travels toward the central nervous system by retrograde axonal transport using dynein, at only a few centimeters per day, which explains the incubation period of weeks to months, shorter with bites on the face or hands. During this time it is hidden from the immune system, which is why vaccine plus immune globulin given early can stop it. In the brain the virus spreads from neuron to neuron, especially in the brainstem, limbic system, hippocampus and cerebellum, forming Negri bodies (cytoplasmic inclusions), with surprisingly little cell death. It disrupts neuron function and neurotransmission. The virus then spreads outward along nerves to the salivary glands and other organs, ready to be passed on in saliva.",
  body: [["Brain", "Encephalitis causes agitation, confusion, hallucinations, seizures and then coma; furious rabies affects the brainstem and limbic system."], ["Throat and breathing muscles", "Painful spasms of swallowing and breathing muscles when trying to drink cause the classic fear of water (hydrophobia) and fear of drafts (aerophobia)."], ["Spinal cord and nerves", "Paralytic (dumb) rabies causes ascending weakness that can look like Guillain-Barre syndrome."], ["Bite site", "Tingling, itching or pain at the healed bite site is an early sign as the virus inflames the local nerve."], ["Autonomic nervous system", "Excess salivation, sweating, fever and unstable heart rhythm and blood pressure occur."], ["Salivary glands", "The virus multiplies in the salivary glands, which allows spread through bites."]],
  symptoms: "Early symptoms are fever, headache and unusual tingling or pain at the bite site as the virus inflames the sensory nerve and its ganglion. As it reaches the brain, anxiety, confusion, agitation and hallucinations appear. Brainstem involvement causes violent throat spasms when trying to swallow, so people fear water, and drooling occurs because saliva cannot be swallowed. In the paralytic form, weakness spreads from the bitten limb. Coma and death usually follow within days to a couple of weeks.",
  dx: "Diagnosis in a living person uses several tests because no single one is perfect: RT-PCR of saliva, a skin biopsy from the nape of the neck tested for viral antigen around hair follicle nerves, and rabies antibodies in serum and cerebrospinal fluid. These tests are coordinated with public health and the CDC. After death, brain tissue is tested by direct fluorescent antibody. A suspected animal can be observed (healthy dogs, cats and ferrets for 10 days) or tested. Diagnosis is often delayed because many people never noticed an exposure, especially to bats.",
  tx: "Post-exposure prophylaxis is extremely effective when given before symptoms. It starts with thorough washing of the wound with soap and water for several minutes, which removes much of the virus. For people never vaccinated, human rabies immune globulin is infiltrated into and around the wound to neutralize virus locally, and rabies vaccine is given as a series of shots over two weeks so the body builds its own antibodies. People with prior rabies vaccination need only vaccine boosters. Tetanus status is also updated. Once symptoms begin, there is no proven treatment, and care is usually focused on comfort. Prevention includes vaccinating pets, avoiding contact with wild animals, and pre-exposure vaccination for veterinarians, lab workers and some travelers. In the US most human cases come from bats, whose bites can be tiny and unnoticed.",
  drugs: ["rabies-immune-globulin", "rabies-vaccine", "tdap-vaccine", "tetanus-immune-globulin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Negri bodies are cytoplasmic inclusions in neurons, classically in the hippocampus and cerebellar Purkinje cells."], ["Memory trick", "Rabies Rides Retrograde: the virus travels backward up nerves to the brain using dynein."], ["Key fact", "In the US, bats are the most common source of human rabies; waking up with a bat in the room can count as an exposure."], ["Timing fact", "Rabies post-exposure prophylaxis is an urgent priority; it works best started as soon as possible but is still given weeks later if symptoms have not begun."]],
  red: "Any bite or scratch from a wild animal such as a bat, raccoon, skunk or fox, or from a dog or cat that may be rabid, or waking to find a bat in the room, needs prompt medical care, and post-exposure shots are best started as soon as possible. Fever, confusion, agitation or trouble swallowing after an animal exposure needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Joseph Meister, the first person to receive Pasteur's rabies vaccine, later worked as a caretaker at the Pasteur Institute in Paris.",
  src: ["CDC/ACIP Use of a Reduced (4-Dose) Vaccine Schedule for Postexposure Prophylaxis to Prevent Human Rabies (2010)", "CDC/ACIP Human Rabies Prevention Recommendations (2008, updated 2022)", "WHO Expert Consultation on Rabies (2018)", "Willoughby RE et al. N Engl J Med 2005"]
}
});

;
DB.DIS = DB.DIS || {};
Object.assign(DB.DIS, {
"candidiasis": {
  name: "Candidiasis (yeast infection)",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["yeast infection", "thrush", "moniliasis", "vulvovaginal candidiasis", "VVC", "invasive candidiasis", "candidemia"],
  what: "Candidiasis is an infection caused by Candida, a yeast that normally lives in small amounts in the mouth, gut and vagina. When the body's balance is upset, it overgrows and causes thrush, vaginal yeast infections, skin rashes or, rarely, dangerous bloodstream infections.",
  origin: "Ancient Greek physicians, including the Hippocratic writers, described white patches in the mouths of sick infants that we now call thrush. In the 1800s microscopes showed a fungus growing in these patches, and in 1853 the French scientist Charles Robin named it Oidium albicans. In 1923 the Dutch mycologist Christine Marie Berkhout created the genus name Candida, from the Latin for white. The first antifungal that worked well against it, nystatin, was discovered around 1950 by Elizabeth Hazen and Rachel Fuller Brown at the New York State Department of Health; they named it after New York State. Amphotericin B followed in the 1950s for deep infections. Azole pills such as fluconazole (US approval 1990) made treatment far easier, and echinocandins such as caspofungin (2001) became the main drugs for bloodstream infections. In 2009 a new, often drug-resistant species, Candida auris, was described in Japan and has since spread worldwide.",
  timeline: [["1853", "Charles Robin names the thrush fungus Oidium albicans."], ["1923", "Christine Marie Berkhout proposes the genus name Candida."], ["1950", "Hazen and Brown report nystatin, the first widely used antifungal antibiotic."], ["1990", "Fluconazole is approved in the US, offering a simple oral treatment."], ["2001", "Caspofungin becomes the first echinocandin approved in the US."], ["2009", "Candida auris is first described from a patient's ear in Japan."]],
  cell: "Candida albicans is a dimorphic fungus: it lives as round budding yeast but can switch to long hyphae and pseudohyphae, which invade tissue. Normally, healthy bacteria compete with it for space and nutrients, and the immune system holds it in check. Th17 cells release IL-17 and IL-22, which recruit neutrophils and make epithelial cells produce antimicrobial peptides; neutrophils then kill the fungus. When this balance breaks, Candida overgrows. Common triggers are broad-spectrum antibiotics (which kill competing bacteria), high blood sugar, estrogen changes, inhaled or systemic steroids, low CD4 T cells in HIV, and neutropenia. Hyphae express adhesins (such as Als3) and secrete aspartyl proteases and a peptide toxin called candidalysin that damage the epithelium. In invasive disease, Candida enters the blood through central lines or a damaged gut wall and seeds organs such as the eyes, kidneys, liver, spleen and heart valves. Antifungals target what is unique to fungi: azoles block ergosterol synthesis (CYP51), polyenes bind ergosterol and punch holes in the membrane, and echinocandins block beta-1,3-glucan synthase in the cell wall.",
  body: [["Mouth and throat", "Oral thrush forms creamy white plaques on the tongue and cheeks that scrape off to leave red, sore tissue. Spread into the esophagus causes painful swallowing and is an AIDS-defining illness."], ["Vagina and vulva", "Vulvovaginal candidiasis causes itching, burning, redness and a thick white discharge, often after antibiotics, in pregnancy or with diabetes."], ["Skin and nails", "Warm, moist skin folds, the diaper area and around the nails can develop bright red, moist rashes with small satellite spots at the edges."], ["Blood", "Candidemia, often linked to central lines, causes fever that does not respond to antibiotics and can lead to septic shock."], ["Eyes", "Candida spread through the blood can seed the retina (endophthalmitis) and threaten vision, which is why eye exams are done in candidemia."], ["Heart, kidneys and organs", "Deep-seated infection can infect heart valves (endocarditis), kidneys, liver and spleen, forming abscesses that are hard to clear."]],
  symptoms: "White plaques in thrush are mats of yeast, dead skin cells and debris; scraping them exposes inflamed tissue underneath. Itching and burning come from fungal invasion of the outer layers and the inflammatory response, with neutrophils and cytokines irritating nerve endings. The thick white discharge is shed epithelial cells mixed with yeast. Satellite spots on the skin are small new colonies at the edge of the main rash. Fever and low blood pressure in candidemia come from the body's whole-system inflammatory response to fungal cell wall parts.",
  dx: "Mucosal and skin infections are often recognized by their look. A wet mount or KOH prep under the microscope shows budding yeast and pseudohyphae, and vaginal pH is usually normal. Culture identifies the species, which matters because Candida glabrata, Candida krusei and Candida auris may resist fluconazole. Invasive disease is diagnosed by blood cultures, with beta-D-glucan or T2Candida tests as add-on tools. Patients with candidemia usually get an eye exam and line review.",
  tx: "Treatment depends on where the infection is. Thrush and skin infections are treated with topical agents such as nystatin (a polyene that binds ergosterol), clotrimazole or miconazole (azoles), or oral fluconazole, which blocks fungal ergosterol synthesis. Vaginal infections respond to topical azoles or oral fluconazole; ibrexafungerp (a glucan synthase inhibitor) and oteseconazole are options for recurrent cases. For candidemia, IDSA guidelines favor an echinocandin (micafungin, caspofungin, anidulafungin or rezafungin) first, which blocks cell wall glucan synthesis, then stepping down to fluconazole when the species is sensitive. Amphotericin B is used for resistant or severe infections. Removing infected central lines, controlling blood sugar and limiting unnecessary antibiotics are key parts of care.",
  drugs: ["fluconazole", "nystatin", "clotrimazole", "clotrimazole-vaginal", "miconazole", "terconazole", "tioconazole", "ibrexafungerp", "oteseconazole", "micafungin", "caspofungin", "anidulafungin", "rezafungin", "amphotericin-b-liposomal", "amphotericin-b", "voriconazole", "posaconazole", "isavuconazonium", "itraconazole", "flucytosine", "nystatin-topical"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Echinocandins are for the blood: think echino attacks the wall (glucan), azoles attack the membrane factory, polyenes punch holes in the membrane."], ["Most-tested fact", "Esophageal candidiasis in an adult suggests a weak immune system, such as HIV with a low CD4 count, and is an AIDS-defining illness."], ["Often confused with", "Bacterial vaginosis (thin gray fishy discharge, pH above 4.5, clue cells) and trichomoniasis (frothy discharge, motile protozoa); yeast has normal pH and thick white discharge."], ["Classic clue", "Thrush plaques scrape off; oral hairy leukoplakia (from Epstein-Barr virus) does not."]],
  red: "Fever or chills that will not go away in someone who is very ill, has a central line, is on chemotherapy or has a weak immune system can signal a bloodstream infection and needs emergency care (call 911). New vision changes during a Candida infection also need urgent care.",
  fun: "Nystatin was named for the New York State Department of Health, and its discoverers donated their royalties to a fund that supported science research.",
  src: ["IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Candidiasis (2016)", "CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines (2021)", "CDC Candida auris resources", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"mononucleosis": {
  name: "Infectious mononucleosis",
  sys: "Infections",
  aka: ["mono", "glandular fever", "kissing disease", "EBV infection"],
  what: "Infectious mononucleosis is a viral illness, usually caused by the Epstein-Barr virus, that brings fever, a very sore throat, swollen neck glands and deep tiredness. It is most common in teens and young adults and spreads mainly through saliva.",
  origin: "In 1889 the German doctor Emil Pfeiffer described children with fever and swollen glands and called it glandular fever. In 1920 Thomas Sprunt and Frank Evans at Johns Hopkins named it infectious mononucleosis after the many unusual mononuclear white cells in patients' blood. In 1932 John Paul and Walls Bunnell found that patients' blood contained heterophile antibodies that clump sheep red cells, the basis of the monospot test. The cause stayed a mystery until 1964, when Anthony Epstein, Yvonne Barr and Bert Achong found a new herpes virus in Burkitt lymphoma cells from Africa. In 1968 Gertrude and Werner Henle linked this Epstein-Barr virus to mono after a lab worker in their lab developed the illness and new antibodies to the virus. There is still no vaccine or specific antiviral, so care focuses on rest and safety.",
  timeline: [["1889", "Emil Pfeiffer describes glandular fever in children."], ["1920", "Sprunt and Evans name the illness infectious mononucleosis."], ["1932", "Paul and Bunnell discover the heterophile antibody, leading to the monospot test."], ["1964", "Epstein, Achong and Barr discover the Epstein-Barr virus in Burkitt lymphoma cells."], ["1968", "The Henles link Epstein-Barr virus to infectious mononucleosis."]],
  cell: "Epstein-Barr virus (EBV, human herpesvirus 4) is a double-stranded DNA virus spread in saliva. It first infects epithelial cells in the throat and then B lymphocytes in the tonsils. The viral envelope protein gp350 binds CD21 (complement receptor 2) on B cells, and HLA class II acts as a co-receptor. Inside the B cell, viral proteins such as EBNA and LMP1 drive the cell to grow and divide; LMP1 mimics an active CD40 signal. Many B cells become infected and multiply. The body answers with a large wave of cytotoxic CD8 T cells that kill infected B cells. These activated T cells are the atypical lymphocytes seen on the blood smear, and their expansion swells the tonsils, lymph nodes, liver and spleen. Infected B cells also make nonspecific heterophile antibodies. EBV is never fully cleared; it hides in memory B cells for life (latency) and can rarely drive cancers such as Burkitt lymphoma, Hodgkin lymphoma and nasopharyngeal carcinoma, especially when T-cell immunity is weak.",
  body: [["Throat and tonsils", "Tonsils swell, often with a white or gray coating, and the throat becomes very painful. In rare cases swelling narrows the airway."], ["Lymph nodes", "Nodes in the back of the neck (posterior cervical) and elsewhere enlarge as infected B cells and reacting T cells crowd them."], ["Spleen", "The spleen enlarges in about half of patients and its capsule stretches thin, so a blow to the belly can rupture it and cause internal bleeding."], ["Liver", "Mild hepatitis with raised liver enzymes is common; a few people develop jaundice."], ["Blood", "Lymphocytes rise with many atypical forms; mild drops in platelets or red cells can occur from immune destruction."], ["Whole body", "Cytokines from the large immune response cause fever, deep fatigue and body aches that can last weeks."]],
  symptoms: "The classic triad is fever, sore throat and swollen lymph nodes. Each comes from the immune fight: cytokines raise the body temperature, swollen lymphoid tissue in the tonsils causes pain, and expanding T and B cells enlarge the nodes. Fatigue comes from the same cytokine storm and can linger. Left upper belly fullness reflects a big spleen. Many patients given amoxicillin or ampicillin develop an itchy widespread rash, thought to be an immune reaction during the infection rather than a true lasting allergy.",
  dx: "Diagnosis rests on symptoms plus blood tests. A complete blood count shows more lymphocytes with atypical lymphocytes on the smear. The heterophile antibody (monospot) test is usually positive but can be falsely negative early in the illness or in young children. EBV-specific antibodies help in those cases: IgM to viral capsid antigen (VCA) means recent infection, while antibodies to EBNA appear later. Liver enzymes are often mildly high. A strep test is often done too, since strep throat can coexist.",
  tx: "There is no specific cure, and treatment is supportive while the immune system controls the virus. Rest, fluids, and acetaminophen or NSAIDs such as ibuprofen ease fever and throat pain. Avoiding contact sports and heavy lifting for several weeks protects an enlarged spleen from rupture. Antiviral drugs such as acyclovir are not routinely used because they do not shorten the illness. Corticosteroids are reserved for complications such as tonsils swelling enough to threaten the airway, severe hemolytic anemia or very low platelets, because they calm the immune response. Antibiotics are used only if a bacterial infection such as strep is also present.",
  drugs: ["acetaminophen", "ibuprofen", "naproxen", "prednisone", "dexamethasone"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "EBV docks on CD21 (complement receptor 2) on B cells, and the kissing disease spreads by saliva."], ["Most-tested fact", "An itchy rash after amoxicillin or ampicillin in a teen with sore throat points to mono. Atypical lymphocytes are reactive CD8 T cells, not infected B cells."], ["Safety point", "Contact sports are avoided for weeks because the enlarged spleen can rupture."], ["Often confused with", "Strep throat (no big spleen, no atypical lymphocytes) and CMV mononucleosis, which is heterophile-negative."]],
  red: "Sudden sharp pain in the left upper belly or left shoulder, dizziness or fainting can mean a ruptured spleen. Trouble breathing, drooling or being unable to swallow saliva can mean airway blockage. Both need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The Epstein-Barr virus was first spotted with an electron microscope in tumor cells sent from Uganda by surgeon Denis Burkitt, and it became the first virus shown to be linked to a human cancer.",
  src: ["CDC: About Epstein-Barr Virus and Infectious Mononucleosis", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "Henle G, Henle W, Diehl V. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 1968", "Epstein MA, Achong BG, Barr YM. Lancet 1964"]
},
"anemia-iron": {
  name: "Iron deficiency anemia",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["IDA", "microcytic anemia", "chlorosis (historical)"],
  what: "Iron deficiency anemia happens when the body does not have enough iron to make hemoglobin, the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen. Red cells become small and pale, and people feel tired, weak and short of breath. It is the most common anemia in the world.",
  origin: "In the 1500s European doctors described chlorosis, or green sickness, a pale, weak state in young women; the German physician Johannes Lange wrote about it in 1554. In the 1600s the English physician Thomas Sydenham treated similar patients with iron steeped in wine. In 1832 the French doctor Pierre Blaud introduced Blaud's pills, a mix of iron sulfate and potassium carbonate, which reliably cured chlorosis and were used for about a century. In the 1900s scientists learned that iron is part of hemoglobin and that the body cannot actively excrete iron, so loss through bleeding is the main cause in adults. Injectable iron followed in the mid 1900s, and newer IV forms such as ferric carboxymaltose, approved in the US in 2013, made fast replacement safer. Around 2000 researchers discovered hepcidin, the liver hormone that controls iron absorption, which explained why inflammation can block iron use.",
  timeline: [["1554", "Johannes Lange describes chlorosis, the green sickness of young women."], ["1832", "Pierre Blaud introduces iron sulfate pills that cure chlorosis."], ["2000s", "Hepcidin is discovered as the master hormone of iron balance."], ["2013", "Ferric carboxymaltose is approved in the US as an IV iron that can be given in fewer visits."]],
  cell: "Iron from food is absorbed mainly in the duodenum. Ferric iron is reduced to ferrous iron by duodenal cytochrome b, enters gut cells through DMT1, and leaves through ferroportin into the blood, where transferrin carries it to the bone marrow. Hepcidin from the liver controls this exit by degrading ferroportin. Excess iron is stored as ferritin. When iron is lost faster than it is absorbed, usually by blood loss, stores drain first (low ferritin), then transferrin saturation falls, and finally developing red cells (erythroblasts) cannot make enough heme. In heme synthesis, iron is inserted into protoporphyrin IX by ferrochelatase; without iron, protoporphyrin builds up. Because hemoglobin is low, erythroblasts keep dividing to reach a target hemoglobin level, producing extra divisions and small (microcytic), pale (hypochromic) cells. The marrow also increases transferrin receptors. Iron is also needed in muscle myoglobin and in enzymes such as cytochromes, which helps explain fatigue, poor exercise tolerance and changes in skin, nails and tongue.",
  body: [["Blood", "Red cells become small and pale with varied sizes (high RDW). Hemoglobin falls, lowering the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity; platelets are often raised."], ["Heart", "To deliver enough oxygen, the heart beats faster and pumps more blood, which can cause palpitations and, in severe cases, heart failure."], ["Muscles and brain", "Less oxygen and fewer iron-containing enzymes cause fatigue, weakness, poor concentration and, in children, delayed development."], ["Mouth, nails and skin", "Iron-poor tissues heal and renew slowly, causing a smooth sore tongue, cracks at the mouth corners, pale skin and spoon-shaped nails (koilonychia)."], ["Gut", "Often the gut is the source: ulcers, colon cancer, celiac disease or heavy NSAID use can cause slow blood loss or poor absorption."], ["Pregnancy", "The fetus and larger blood volume raise iron needs; deficiency is linked to preterm birth and low birth weight."]],
  symptoms: "Fatigue, weakness and shortness of breath with effort happen because less hemoglobin means less oxygen reaches tissues. A fast heartbeat is the heart compensating. Pale skin and pale inner eyelids come from less hemoglobin in the blood. Pica, especially craving ice (pagophagia), is a classic and still not fully explained sign. Restless legs are linked to low iron in the brain, where iron helps make dopamine. Hair loss and brittle nails reflect iron-hungry growing tissues.",
  dx: "A complete blood count shows low hemoglobin with a low MCV (microcytic), low MCH and high RDW. Iron studies show low ferritin (the most specific test), low serum iron, high total iron binding capacity and low transferrin saturation. Ferritin can be falsely normal when inflammation is present, because it rises as an acute phase protein. The smear shows small, pale cells and pencil cells. In adult men and postmenopausal women, a source of gut blood loss is usually looked for with colonoscopy and upper endoscopy.",
  tx: "Treatment replaces iron and fixes the cause. Oral iron such as ferrous sulfate is absorbed in the duodenum; taking it every other day may improve absorption because each dose raises hepcidin briefly, and vitamin C can help absorption. Common side effects are constipation, nausea and dark stools. IV iron (iron sucrose, ferric carboxymaltose, ferumoxytol, ferric derisomaltose, low molecular weight iron dextran, sodium ferric gluconate) is used when pills fail, are not tolerated, cannot be absorbed, or iron is needed quickly, as in late pregnancy or chronic kidney disease. A reticulocyte rise within about a week is an early sign of response. Blood transfusion is reserved for severe symptoms or active bleeding. Finding and treating the source, such as heavy periods, ulcers or colon cancer, is essential.",
  drugs: ["ferrous-sulfate", "ferric-maltol", "iron-sucrose", "ferric-carboxymaltose", "ferumoxytol", "ferric-derisomaltose", "iron-dextran", "sodium-ferric-gluconate", "ferric-citrate", "ascorbic-acid"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Iron deficiency is the FIRST thing: Ferritin falls first, and it is the most common anemia worldwide."], ["Most-tested fact", "Low ferritin is the most specific single test. Iron deficiency has high TIBC; anemia of chronic disease has low TIBC and normal or high ferritin."], ["Classic board clue", "Iron deficiency anemia in an older adult is colon cancer until proven otherwise."], ["Often confused with", "Thalassemia trait, which is also microcytic but with normal ferritin, a high red cell count and a low Mentzer index."]],
  red: "Chest pain, fainting, severe shortness of breath or signs of active bleeding such as black tarry stools or vomiting blood need emergency care (call 911). A toddler who swallows adult iron pills needs Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) or 911 right away, because iron overdose is dangerous.",
  fun: "Ice craving, called pagophagia, is so linked to iron deficiency that it often fades within days of starting iron, even before hemoglobin rises.",
  src: ["Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "AGA Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Gastrointestinal Evaluation of Iron Deficiency Anemia (2020)", "CDC Recommendations to Prevent and Control Iron Deficiency"]
},
"anemia-b12": {
  name: "Vitamin B12 and folate deficiency anemia",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["megaloblastic anemia", "macrocytic anemia", "pernicious anemia", "folate deficiency"],
  what: "This anemia happens when the body lacks vitamin B12 or folate, two vitamins needed to copy DNA. Red blood cells grow too large but too few, causing tiredness. Low B12 can also damage nerves, causing numbness and trouble walking.",
  origin: "In 1849 the English physician Thomas Addison described a fatal anemia, and in 1872 the German doctor Anton Biermer named it progressive pernicious anemia, because it always killed. In 1926 George Minot and William Murphy in Boston showed that eating large amounts of raw liver cured it, building on George Whipple's work in dogs; the three shared the 1934 Nobel Prize. In 1929 William Castle showed that the stomach makes an intrinsic factor needed to absorb an extrinsic factor in food. In 1948 that factor, vitamin B12, was isolated by teams in the US and UK, and Dorothy Hodgkin solved its complex structure in 1956. Folate's story began in 1931, when Lucy Wills found that a yeast extract cured a macrocytic anemia in pregnant women in Bombay. Folic acid was later made in the lab, and in 1998 the US began adding it to enriched grain products to prevent neural tube defects.",
  timeline: [["1849", "Thomas Addison describes a fatal anemia later called pernicious anemia."], ["1926", "Minot and Murphy show that a liver diet cures pernicious anemia."], ["1929", "William Castle describes intrinsic factor from the stomach."], ["1931", "Lucy Wills shows yeast extract cures anemia in pregnant women, pointing to folate."], ["1948", "Vitamin B12 is isolated."], ["1998", "US enriched grain products begin to carry added folic acid."]],
  cell: "Both vitamins are needed to make thymidine for DNA. Folate, as methylene-tetrahydrofolate, gives a carbon to thymidylate synthase, which turns dUMP into dTMP. B12 (as methylcobalamin) is the cofactor for methionine synthase, which moves a methyl group from methyl-tetrahydrofolate to homocysteine, making methionine and freeing tetrahydrofolate. Without B12, folate gets stuck as methyl-THF (the folate trap), so B12 lack causes functional folate lack. Short on thymidine, dividing cells slow DNA synthesis while RNA and cytoplasm keep growing, so the nucleus lags behind (nuclear-cytoplasmic asynchrony). Marrow precursors become large megaloblasts, and many die inside the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis), raising LDH and bilirubin. Neutrophils become hypersegmented. B12 is also the cofactor for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase; when it fails, methylmalonic acid builds up and abnormal fatty acids form in myelin, which along with low methionine for methylation reactions damages the spinal cord dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts (subacute combined degeneration). Pernicious anemia is autoimmune: antibodies and T cells attack gastric parietal cells and intrinsic factor, so B12 cannot be absorbed in the ileum.",
  body: [["Blood and bone marrow", "Red cells are large (macrocytic) and fewer; white cells and platelets can also fall. Hypersegmented neutrophils are a classic sign."], ["Spinal cord (B12 only)", "Damage to the dorsal columns causes loss of vibration and position sense; corticospinal damage causes weakness and brisk reflexes."], ["Nerves and brain (B12 only)", "Peripheral nerves cause numbness and tingling, and the brain can show memory loss, depression or psychosis, sometimes before anemia appears."], ["Mouth and gut", "Fast-dividing cells of the tongue and gut lining suffer, causing a smooth, red, sore tongue (glossitis) and sometimes diarrhea."], ["Stomach", "In pernicious anemia the stomach lining thins (atrophic gastritis), which raises the risk of gastric cancer and carcinoid tumors."], ["Pregnancy and fetus", "Low folate early in pregnancy raises the risk of neural tube defects such as spina bifida."]],
  symptoms: "Tiredness, pale skin and shortness of breath come from fewer red cells. Mild jaundice can occur because many fragile megaloblasts die in the marrow and release bilirubin. Numbness, tingling, poor balance and a positive Romberg sign happen with B12 lack because myelin in the spinal cord and nerves breaks down. Folate deficiency does not cause these nerve problems, which is a key difference. A sore tongue reflects slow renewal of mouth lining cells.",
  dx: "A complete blood count shows anemia with a high MCV, and the smear shows large oval red cells and hypersegmented neutrophils. Low serum B12 or folate confirms the deficiency. When levels are borderline, methylmalonic acid and homocysteine help: both are high in B12 deficiency, while only homocysteine is high in folate deficiency. LDH and indirect bilirubin are often high from ineffective red cell production. Anti-intrinsic factor and anti-parietal cell antibodies support pernicious anemia.",
  tx: "Treatment replaces the missing vitamin and treats the cause. Vitamin B12 is replaced as cyanocobalamin or hydroxocobalamin by injection, or by high-dose oral B12 in people who can still absorb some by passive diffusion. Pernicious anemia and ileal disease need lifelong replacement. Folate deficiency is treated with oral folic acid. Giving folic acid alone to someone with B12 deficiency can improve the blood counts but let nerve damage progress, so B12 status is checked first. A brisk rise in reticulocytes within about a week shows response; potassium can fall early as new cells form. Leucovorin (folinic acid) is used when drugs such as methotrexate block folate recycling. Causes such as diet, alcohol use, metformin, acid-blocking drugs, gastric surgery and nitrous oxide misuse are also addressed.",
  drugs: ["cyanocobalamin", "hydroxocobalamin", "folic-acid", "leucovorin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "B12 hits blood and brain; folate hits blood only. MMA (methylmalonic acid) is the B12-only marker."], ["Most-tested fact", "Both deficiencies raise homocysteine; only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid."], ["Classic board clue", "Subacute combined degeneration: loss of vibration and position sense with spastic weakness in an older adult with macrocytic anemia, or in someone who misuses nitrous oxide."], ["Often confused with", "Other causes of a high MCV without megaloblasts, such as alcohol use, liver disease, hypothyroidism and reticulocytosis."]],
  red: "New trouble walking, falling, severe confusion, chest pain or fainting with anemia needs urgent medical care, and severe symptoms need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Dorothy Hodgkin's X-ray work on vitamin B12, the largest and most complex vitamin molecule, helped win her the 1964 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.",
  src: ["Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine", "Minot GR, Murphy WP. JAMA 1926", "Wills L. Br Med J 1931", "CDC Folic Acid Recommendations"]
},
"sickle-cell": {
  name: "Sickle cell disease",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["SCD", "sickle cell anemia", "HbSS", "HbSC disease", "sickle beta-thalassemia"],
  what: "Sickle cell disease is an inherited blood disorder in which red blood cells contain an abnormal hemoglobin that makes them stiff and curved like a sickle. These cells break down early and get stuck in small blood vessels, causing anemia, severe pain episodes and organ damage.",
  origin: "West African healers recognized the disease long before Western medicine, giving it local names that described pain and cycles of illness. In 1910 Chicago physician James Herrick published the first Western description of peculiar elongated, sickle-shaped red cells in a dental student from Grenada, Walter Clement Noel. In 1949 Linus Pauling, Harvey Itano and colleagues showed that sickle hemoglobin moves differently in an electric field, calling it the first molecular disease. In 1956 Vernon Ingram found the exact change: one amino acid swap in the beta-globin chain. A 1986 trial showed that daily penicillin in young children prevents deadly pneumococcal infections, leading to newborn screening. In 1995 a large trial showed hydroxyurea cuts pain crises, and it was approved in 1998. L-glutamine (2017) and crizanlizumab (2019) followed. In December 2023 the FDA approved the first gene therapies, including the first CRISPR-based medicine, exagamglogene autotemcel.",
  timeline: [["1910", "James Herrick describes sickle-shaped red cells."], ["1949", "Pauling and colleagues call sickle cell anemia a molecular disease."], ["1956", "Vernon Ingram identifies the single amino acid change in sickle hemoglobin."], ["1986", "The PROPS trial shows penicillin prevents deadly infections in young children."], ["1998", "Hydroxyurea is approved for adults with sickle cell anemia."], ["2023", "The FDA approves the first gene therapies for sickle cell disease."]],
  cell: "A point mutation in the HBB gene (codon 6) replaces glutamic acid with valine, creating hemoglobin S. Inheritance is autosomal recessive; sickle cell trait (HbAS) is usually silent. When HbS releases oxygen, the hydrophobic valine sticks to a pocket on a neighboring hemoglobin, and deoxygenated HbS molecules polymerize into long rigid fibers. These fibers bend the cell into a sickle. Dehydration, acidosis, low oxygen and cold speed polymerization. Repeated sickling damages the membrane, so cells lose water and potassium and become dense and rigid; they are destroyed in the spleen and also burst inside vessels (extravascular and intravascular hemolysis). Free hemoglobin scavenges nitric oxide, causing vasoconstriction. Sickled cells, white cells and platelets stick to an activated endothelium through adhesion molecules such as P-selectin, plugging small vessels (vaso-occlusion) and causing ischemia, infarction and reperfusion injury. Fetal hemoglobin (HbF) does not join the polymers, which is why infants are protected for the first months and why drugs or gene therapies that raise HbF help.",
  body: [["Bones and joints", "Blocked vessels in the marrow cause severe pain crises, hand and foot swelling in babies (dactylitis), and avascular necrosis of the hip."], ["Spleen", "Repeated infarcts shrink and scar the spleen (autosplenectomy) by early childhood, raising the risk of infection with encapsulated bacteria. In young children, sudden splenic sequestration can trap blood."], ["Lungs", "Acute chest syndrome, from infection, fat emboli or vaso-occlusion in the lungs, causes chest pain, fever and low oxygen and is a leading cause of death."], ["Brain", "Damage to large brain arteries raises stroke risk in children; transcranial Doppler screening finds those at risk."], ["Kidneys", "The salty, low-oxygen kidney medulla promotes sickling, causing inability to concentrate urine, blood in the urine, papillary necrosis and chronic kidney disease."], ["Penis", "Sickling in the penis can cause priapism, a painful prolonged erection that can damage tissue."], ["Eyes and skin", "Blocked retinal vessels can cause retinopathy, and poor blood flow causes leg ulcers."]],
  symptoms: "Pain crises happen when sickled cells block small vessels and starve tissue of oxygen, especially in bone marrow. Fatigue and pallor come from chronic hemolytic anemia, since sickle cells live only a fraction of a normal red cell lifespan. Yellow eyes and gallstones come from bilirubin released by broken cells. Fever is dangerous because a damaged spleen cannot filter bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae.",
  dx: "All US newborns are screened, using hemoglobin electrophoresis or HPLC, which separates HbS from normal HbA and HbF. In HbSS there is no HbA. A blood count shows anemia with a high reticulocyte count, and the smear shows sickle cells and Howell-Jolly bodies (from a nonfunctioning spleen). Bilirubin and LDH are high from hemolysis. Children have yearly transcranial Doppler studies to look for stroke risk.",
  tx: "Care combines prevention, crisis treatment and disease-modifying therapy. Penicillin prophylaxis in young children and vaccines guard against infections. Hydroxyurea is the backbone: it inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and raises fetal hemoglobin, which blocks polymer formation and lowers pain crises and acute chest syndrome. L-glutamine reduces oxidative stress, and crizanlizumab blocks P-selectin to reduce cell sticking. Voxelotor was withdrawn in 2024. Pain crises are treated quickly with IV fluids and opioids such as morphine or hydromorphone plus NSAIDs. Transfusions treat acute chest syndrome and stroke and prevent stroke in high-risk children, and iron chelation removes excess iron. Stem cell transplant and gene therapies (exagamglogene autotemcel, which raises HbF via BCL11A editing, and lovotibeglogene autotemcel, which adds an anti-sickling gene) can be curative.",
  drugs: ["hydroxyurea", "l-glutamine", "crizanlizumab", "exagamglogene-autotemcel", "lovotibeglogene-autotemcel", "morphine", "hydromorphone", "ketorolac", "ibuprofen", "acetaminophen", "penicillin-v", "folic-acid", "deferasirox", "deferoxamine", "deferiprone"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Glutamic acid to valine at position 6: sickle cells are a big pain in the six."], ["Most-tested fact", "Autosplenectomy leaves patients at risk from encapsulated bacteria (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Salmonella osteomyelitis)."], ["Classic board clue", "Parvovirus B19 can cause a sudden aplastic crisis with a very low reticulocyte count."], ["Pain care point", "Pain in a crisis is real tissue ischemia. Fast, adequate pain control is the standard of care and stigma delays treatment."]],
  red: "Fever, chest pain or trouble breathing, sudden weakness or trouble speaking, a painful erection lasting a few hours, sudden belly swelling or severe pain not controlled at home need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Sickle cell trait protects against severe malaria, which is why the gene became common in parts of Africa, the Middle East and India where malaria was widespread.",
  src: ["NHLBI Evidence-Based Management of Sickle Cell Disease (2014)", "ASH Guidelines for Sickle Cell Disease (2019-2021)", "Herrick JB. Arch Intern Med 1910", "Pauling L et al. Science 1949", "Charache S et al. Multicenter Study of Hydroxyurea. N Engl J Med 1995"]
},
"thalassemia": {
  name: "Thalassemia",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["beta-thalassemia", "alpha-thalassemia", "Cooley's anemia", "thalassemia major", "thalassemia trait", "HbH disease"],
  what: "Thalassemia is a group of inherited blood disorders in which the body makes too little of one of the protein chains of hemoglobin. Red cells are small and break easily, causing anything from no symptoms to severe lifelong anemia needing regular transfusions.",
  origin: "In 1925 Detroit pediatricians Thomas Cooley and Pearl Lee described five young children of Mediterranean families with severe anemia, large spleens and bone changes; the illness became known as Cooley's anemia. In 1932 George Whipple and William Bradford coined the name thalassemia, from the Greek word for sea, because many patients had roots around the Mediterranean. In the 1960s researchers showed it was caused by reduced production of alpha or beta globin chains, and in the 1970s and 1980s it became one of the first diseases understood at the gene level. Regular transfusions kept children alive, but iron buildup killed many in their teens until iron chelation with deferoxamine arrived in the 1960s and 1970s. Oral chelators deferasirox (2005) and deferiprone followed. Luspatercept (2019) reduced transfusion needs, and gene therapies such as betibeglogene autotemcel (2022) and exagamglogene autotemcel (2024) offered a chance to stop transfusions entirely.",
  timeline: [["1925", "Thomas Cooley and Pearl Lee describe severe anemia in Mediterranean children."], ["1932", "Whipple and Bradford introduce the name thalassemia."], ["1960s", "Deferoxamine iron chelation is developed to treat transfusional iron overload."], ["2005", "Deferasirox becomes the first once-daily oral iron chelator approved in the US."], ["2019", "Luspatercept is approved to reduce transfusions in beta-thalassemia."], ["2022", "The first gene therapy for transfusion-dependent beta-thalassemia is approved in the US."]],
  cell: "Adult hemoglobin A is made of two alpha and two beta globin chains. In beta-thalassemia, mutations in the HBB gene (often splice-site or promoter mutations) lower (beta-plus) or abolish (beta-zero) beta chain production. Extra alpha chains have no partner; they precipitate inside red cell precursors, damage membranes through oxidation and cause the precursors to die in the marrow (ineffective erythropoiesis). Surviving cells are small and pale and are removed by the spleen. The anemia drives erythropoietin up, so the marrow expands massively, deforming bones, and production also moves to the liver and spleen. Ineffective erythropoiesis releases erythroferrone, which suppresses hepcidin, so the gut absorbs too much iron even without transfusions. In alpha-thalassemia, deletions of the four alpha-globin genes (HBA1, HBA2) reduce alpha chains. Losing one or two genes causes silent carrier or trait states; three causes HbH disease, in which extra beta chains form beta-4 tetramers (HbH); four causes hemoglobin Barts (gamma-4) and hydrops fetalis, because these tetramers hold oxygen too tightly to release it to tissues.",
  body: [["Blood and marrow", "Red cells are small, pale and short-lived. In thalassemia major the anemia is severe and begins around 6 months of age as fetal hemoglobin falls."], ["Bones", "The marrow expands to make more red cells, thinning bones and widening the skull and cheekbones (chipmunk face, crew-cut skull X-ray) and raising fracture risk."], ["Spleen and liver", "Both enlarge from clearing damaged cells and making blood outside the marrow (extramedullary hematopoiesis)."], ["Heart", "Iron from transfusions and extra absorption deposits in heart muscle, causing heart failure and arrhythmias, historically the main cause of death."], ["Endocrine glands", "Iron damages the pituitary, pancreas, thyroid and gonads, causing delayed puberty, diabetes, low thyroid and short stature."], ["Fetus (alpha)", "Loss of all four alpha genes causes hydrops fetalis, severe swelling and heart failure before birth, usually fatal without in-utero transfusion."]],
  symptoms: "Tiredness and pale skin come from anemia. Yellow skin and gallstones come from bilirubin released by broken cells. A swollen belly reflects a large spleen and liver. Bone pain and face shape changes come from marrow expansion. People with trait usually feel well and only have small red cells on blood tests. In major disease, poor growth and iron overload symptoms appear over time.",
  dx: "The blood count shows a low MCV with a normal or high red cell count, and the smear shows target cells and, in more severe forms, nucleated red cells. Iron studies are normal or high, which separates it from iron deficiency. Hemoglobin electrophoresis or HPLC in beta-thalassemia trait shows raised HbA2 and sometimes HbF; in major disease HbA is low or absent. Alpha-thalassemia trait often has normal electrophoresis and is confirmed by DNA testing. Ferritin and cardiac and liver MRI track iron overload.",
  tx: "Thalassemia trait needs no treatment, though genetic counseling helps families. Transfusion-dependent thalassemia is managed with regular red cell transfusions that correct anemia and suppress the overactive marrow. Because the body cannot excrete iron, chelators are essential: deferoxamine (an injected chelator), deferasirox and deferiprone (oral) bind iron so it leaves in urine or stool. Luspatercept traps TGF-beta family signals that block late red cell maturation, reducing transfusion needs. Mitapivat, a pyruvate kinase activator, is approved for anemia in adults with alpha- or beta-thalassemia. Folic acid supports high red cell turnover. Splenectomy is sometimes used. Allogeneic stem cell transplant and gene therapies can be curative. Iron supplements are avoided unless iron deficiency is proven.",
  drugs: ["deferasirox", "deferoxamine", "deferiprone", "luspatercept", "mitapivat", "exagamglogene-autotemcel", "folic-acid", "hydroxyurea"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Thalassa means sea: think Mediterranean for beta and Southeast Asia and Africa for alpha."], ["Most-tested fact", "Beta-thalassemia trait has a raised HbA2. Microcytic anemia with normal ferritin and a high red cell count suggests thalassemia trait, not iron deficiency."], ["Gene counting", "Alpha: 1 gene lost silent, 2 trait, 3 HbH disease, 4 Barts hydrops fetalis."], ["Often confused with", "Iron deficiency anemia; giving iron to a thalassemia carrier does not help and can add to iron load."]],
  red: "Fever after splenectomy, chest pain, palpitations or fainting, severe shortness of breath, or sudden yellowing with dark urine need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Carriers of thalassemia genes are partly protected from severe malaria, which explains why the genes are so common in the Mediterranean, Africa, the Middle East and Southeast Asia.",
  src: ["Thalassaemia International Federation Guidelines for the Management of Transfusion Dependent Thalassaemia (2021)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Cooley TB, Lee P. Trans Am Pediatr Soc 1925", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"hemophilia": {
  name: "Hemophilia",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["hemophilia A", "hemophilia B", "factor VIII deficiency", "factor IX deficiency", "Christmas disease", "the royal disease"],
  what: "Hemophilia is an inherited bleeding disorder in which the blood lacks enough of a clotting protein, factor VIII (hemophilia A) or factor IX (hemophilia B). Bleeding lasts longer, often deep inside joints and muscles. It mostly affects males.",
  origin: "Ancient Jewish writings in the Talmud excused baby boys from circumcision if two older brothers had bled to death after it, an early sign that the disorder ran in families. In 1803 Philadelphia doctor John Conrad Otto described bleeders in families, noting that men bled and women passed it on. The word haemophilia appeared in 1828 in a thesis by Friedrich Hopff. Queen Victoria carried the gene and passed it through her daughters to royal families of Spain, Germany and Russia; DNA tests in 2009 showed the Russian royal family had hemophilia B. In 1937 Harvard scientists described antihemophilic globulin, and in 1952 hemophilia B was separated after study of a boy named Stephen Christmas. In 1964 Judith Graham Pool discovered cryoprecipitate, a factor VIII-rich plasma product. Concentrates in the 1970s allowed home treatment, but in the 1980s many patients got HIV and hepatitis C from contaminated plasma. Recombinant factor VIII arrived in 1992, emicizumab in 2017, and gene therapies in 2022 and 2023.",
  timeline: [["1803", "John Conrad Otto describes inherited bleeding in males."], ["1952", "Hemophilia B (Christmas disease) is identified as a separate disorder."], ["1964", "Judith Graham Pool discovers cryoprecipitate."], ["1992", "The first recombinant factor VIII is approved in the US."], ["2017", "Emicizumab, a factor VIII mimicking antibody, is approved."], ["2022", "The first gene therapy for hemophilia B is approved in the US."]],
  cell: "Hemophilia A and B are X-linked recessive. Males with one affected X have the disease; female carriers can have mild bleeding. About a third of cases arise from new mutations. In hemophilia A, a common severe mutation is an inversion of intron 22 in the F8 gene. In secondary hemostasis, factor IXa uses factor VIIIa as a cofactor on activated platelet surfaces (the tenase complex) to activate factor X. This step produces most of the thrombin burst needed to turn fibrinogen into a strong fibrin net. Without factor VIII or IX, the initial platelet plug forms normally (primary hemostasis is fine), but the fibrin clot is too weak and late, so bleeding restarts hours later or continues deep in tissue. Severity tracks factor activity: severe disease has less than 1 percent of normal activity and causes bleeding without injury. Repeated blood in a joint releases iron, which inflames the synovium, creating a target joint and progressive hemophilic arthropathy. Some patients make neutralizing antibodies (inhibitors) against infused factor.",
  body: [["Joints", "Bleeding into knees, elbows and ankles (hemarthrosis) causes pain, swelling and, after repeated bleeds, permanent joint damage."], ["Muscles", "Deep muscle bleeds, such as in the iliopsoas or forearm, can press on nerves and cause compartment syndrome."], ["Brain", "Intracranial bleeding, even after minor head bumps, is the most dangerous complication and a leading cause of death."], ["Mouth and gut", "Bleeding after dental work or tongue bites can be prolonged; gut bleeding can occur."], ["Skin", "Large bruises form easily, but tiny pinpoint petechiae are uncommon because platelets work normally."], ["Newborns", "Bleeding after circumcision or heel sticks may be the first sign; a head bleed can occur during birth."]],
  symptoms: "Bleeding into joints and muscles is the hallmark because these are sites where only a strong fibrin clot can stop bleeding under movement. The joint feels warm, tight and painful, and many patients feel a tingling aura before swelling. Bleeding that stops and then starts again happens because the platelet plug forms but is not reinforced by fibrin. Easy deep bruising comes from the same weak clot.",
  dx: "Lab tests show a prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) with a normal prothrombin time (PT), normal platelet count and normal bleeding time. A mixing study with normal plasma corrects the aPTT unless an inhibitor is present. Specific factor VIII or factor IX activity assays confirm the type and severity. Genetic testing identifies carriers and helps family planning, and inhibitor tests (Bethesda assay) look for antibodies.",
  tx: "Treatment replaces or bypasses the missing factor. Factor VIII or factor IX concentrates, recombinant or plasma-derived, are given for bleeds and as regular prophylaxis to prevent joint damage. Emicizumab is a bispecific antibody that bridges factor IXa and factor X, mimicking factor VIII, and is given by injection under the skin for hemophilia A with or without inhibitors. Newer agents rebalance clotting: concizumab and marstacimab block tissue factor pathway inhibitor, and fitusiran lowers antithrombin. Bypassing agents such as recombinant factor VIIa treat bleeds when inhibitors are present. Desmopressin releases stored factor VIII and helps mild hemophilia A. Antifibrinolytics such as tranexamic acid protect clots in the mouth. Gene therapies such as etranacogene dezaparvovec (B) and valoctocogene roxaparvovec (A) deliver a working gene to the liver. NSAIDs and IM injections are avoided because they add bleeding risk.",
  drugs: ["factor-viii-recombinant", "factor-ix-recombinant", "factor-ix", "emicizumab", "concizumab", "marstacimab", "fitusiran", "factor-viia-recombinant", "desmopressin", "tranexamic-acid", "aminocaproic-acid", "etranacogene-dezaparvovec", "valoctocogene-roxaparvovec", "acetaminophen"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "A comes before B and 8 comes before 9: hemophilia A lacks factor VIII, hemophilia B lacks factor IX. Both are X-linked and both prolong only the aPTT."], ["Most-tested fact", "Isolated prolonged aPTT that corrects on a mixing study, with normal platelets and PT, suggests hemophilia."], ["Classic board clue", "Bleeding into joints (hemarthrosis) points to a clotting factor problem; mucosal bleeding and petechiae point to a platelet problem."], ["Often confused with", "Von Willebrand disease, which affects both sexes and causes nosebleeds and heavy periods."]],
  red: "Any head injury, severe headache, vomiting or confusion, bleeding in the neck or throat, a swollen painful muscle with numbness, or bleeding that will not stop needs emergency care (call 911) and quick factor treatment.",
  fun: "Judith Graham Pool noticed that the sludge left at the bottom of thawing plasma was rich in factor VIII. Her cryoprecipitate let patients have surgery and treat bleeds far more easily.",
  src: ["World Federation of Hemophilia Guidelines for the Management of Hemophilia (2020)", "National Bleeding Disorders Foundation MASAC recommendations", "CDC Hemophilia resources", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"von-willebrand": {
  name: "Von Willebrand disease",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["VWD", "vWD", "von Willebrand factor deficiency"],
  what: "Von Willebrand disease is the most common inherited bleeding disorder. The blood has too little, or a poorly working form, of von Willebrand factor, a protein that helps platelets stick at a cut. It causes nosebleeds, easy bruising, and heavy periods or bleeding after surgery.",
  origin: "In 1926 the Finnish doctor Erik von Willebrand studied a family on the Aland Islands in the Baltic Sea. A young girl named Hjordis had severe bleeding, and several family members had died from it. Unlike hemophilia, it affected both girls and boys and caused long bleeding times, so he called it hereditary pseudohemophilia. For decades its cause was confused with factor VIII. In 1971 researchers including Theodore Zimmerman and Oscar Ratnoff showed that von Willebrand factor is a separate protein that carries factor VIII. In 1977 the Italian hematologist Pier Mannuccio Mannucci showed that desmopressin could raise factor levels, sparing many patients from blood products. A recombinant von Willebrand factor was approved in the US in 2015, and in 2021 joint international guidelines updated how the disease is diagnosed and managed.",
  timeline: [["1926", "Erik von Willebrand describes a bleeding disorder in a family on the Aland Islands."], ["1971", "Von Willebrand factor is shown to be distinct from factor VIII."], ["1977", "Desmopressin is reported to raise von Willebrand factor and factor VIII levels."], ["2015", "Recombinant von Willebrand factor is approved in the US."], ["2021", "ASH, ISTH, NHF and WFH publish joint VWD guidelines."]],
  cell: "Von Willebrand factor (VWF) is a large multimeric glycoprotein made by endothelial cells (stored in Weibel-Palade bodies) and megakaryocytes (stored in platelet alpha granules). It has two key jobs. First, at an injury, VWF binds exposed subendothelial collagen and grabs platelets through the platelet receptor glycoprotein Ib, which is essential for platelet adhesion under high shear flow in small vessels. Second, it binds and protects factor VIII in plasma, preventing its rapid breakdown. Larger multimers are more active, and the enzyme ADAMTS13 trims them to a normal size. In type 1 VWD (most common, usually autosomal dominant) VWF is normal but reduced. In type 2, VWF is abnormal: 2A lacks large multimers, 2B binds platelets too eagerly so they are cleared (causing low platelets), 2M has weak platelet binding, and 2N cannot bind factor VIII, mimicking hemophilia A. Type 3 is a near-total absence and is recessive. Less VWF means weak platelet plugs, producing mucosal bleeding, and in severe forms low factor VIII adds joint bleeding.",
  body: [["Nose and mouth", "Weak platelet plugs in mucous membranes cause frequent, long nosebleeds and gum bleeding, especially after dental work."], ["Uterus", "Heavy menstrual bleeding is very common and can cause iron deficiency; bleeding after childbirth is a risk."], ["Skin", "Easy bruising and prolonged bleeding from small cuts reflect poor platelet adhesion."], ["Gut", "Bleeding from the stomach or bowel can occur, sometimes from small abnormal blood vessels."], ["Joints and muscles", "In type 3 and type 2N, low factor VIII leads to hemophilia-like joint and muscle bleeds."]],
  symptoms: "Mucosal bleeding, such as nosebleeds, bleeding gums and heavy periods, is the signature because these surfaces depend on fast platelet plug formation, which needs VWF. Bleeding after surgery, dental extraction or childbirth happens for the same reason. Many people with mild type 1 do not know they have it until a procedure. Fatigue may come from iron deficiency caused by ongoing blood loss.",
  dx: "Screening tests may show a normal platelet count, a normal PT and a normal or mildly prolonged aPTT (from low factor VIII). Specific tests measure VWF antigen, VWF platelet-binding activity (such as VWF:GPIbM or ristocetin cofactor) and factor VIII activity. A low ratio of activity to antigen suggests type 2. Multimer analysis and ristocetin-induced platelet aggregation help classify subtypes. VWF levels rise with stress, pregnancy, estrogen and inflammation, so tests are sometimes repeated, and type O blood normally has lower levels.",
  tx: "Treatment raises VWF, protects clots or controls hormones. Desmopressin (DDAVP), a vasopressin V2 agonist, releases stored VWF and factor VIII from endothelial cells and works well in most type 1; a trial is often done to confirm response, and fluid intake is limited afterward because it can cause low sodium. It is generally avoided in type 2B because it can worsen low platelets. VWF concentrates, either plasma-derived (with factor VIII) or recombinant, are used for type 3, severe bleeding, surgery and those who do not respond to desmopressin. Antifibrinolytics such as tranexamic acid and aminocaproic acid block plasmin from breaking down clots and help with mouth bleeding and heavy periods. Hormonal therapy can reduce menstrual bleeding. Aspirin and NSAIDs are avoided because they weaken platelets.",
  drugs: ["desmopressin", "von-willebrand-factor", "tranexamic-acid", "aminocaproic-acid", "factor-viii-recombinant"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Von Willebrand is the glue: VWF glues platelets to the wall (GPIb) and babysits factor VIII."], ["Most-tested fact", "VWD is the most common inherited bleeding disorder; type 1 (quantitative) is the most common type."], ["Classic board clue", "A woman with heavy periods since her first period and frequent nosebleeds, with normal platelets, suggests VWD."], ["Often confused with", "Hemophilia A; type 2N VWD mimics it because factor VIII is low, but it can affect females."]],
  red: "Bleeding that will not stop with pressure, soaking through pads every hour, vomiting blood or black stools, or any head injury in someone with a bleeding disorder needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Von Willebrand disease is thought to affect about 1 in 100 people, yet most never know because their bleeding is mild.",
  src: ["ASH ISTH NHF WFH 2021 Guidelines on the Diagnosis and Management of von Willebrand Disease", "NHLBI The Diagnosis, Evaluation, and Management of von Willebrand Disease (2007)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "CDC Von Willebrand Disease resources"]
},
"dic": {
  name: "Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["DIC", "consumptive coagulopathy", "consumption coagulopathy", "defibrination syndrome"],
  what: "DIC is a dangerous condition in which a severe illness, such as sepsis, trauma or a complication of childbirth, switches on blood clotting throughout the body. Tiny clots form everywhere and use up clotting factors and platelets, so the person can clot and bleed at the same time.",
  origin: "In the 1800s scientists noticed that injecting tissue extracts into animals caused sudden, widespread clotting. In the 1900s obstetricians described women who bled uncontrollably after placental abruption or amniotic fluid embolism and whose blood had lost its fibrinogen; these cases showed that clotting proteins could be consumed. In the 1950s and 1960s researchers in Europe and the US described consumption coagulopathy, and in 1965 the American pathologist Donald McKay published a book that established DIC as an intermediary mechanism of disease rather than a disease in itself. Later work showed that tissue factor, released or expressed in sepsis, cancer and trauma, drives the process. In 2001 the International Society on Thrombosis and Haemostasis (ISTH) published a scoring system to standardize the diagnosis of overt DIC. Treatment has remained focused on the cause, with blood products to support the patient.",
  timeline: [["1950s", "Obstetric cases show that blood can lose fibrinogen through consumption after placental problems."], ["1965", "Donald McKay publishes a book defining DIC as an intermediary mechanism of disease."], ["2001", "The ISTH publishes a scoring system for overt DIC."]],
  cell: "DIC always has a trigger. Sepsis (especially gram-negative endotoxin), major trauma, burns, pancreatitis, obstetric emergencies (placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism, retained dead fetus), acute promyelocytic leukemia and adenocarcinomas all expose tissue factor to blood. Tissue factor binds factor VIIa and starts the extrinsic pathway, producing a flood of thrombin. Inflammatory cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha) make monocytes and endothelium express more tissue factor, while natural anticoagulants (antithrombin, protein C and tissue factor pathway inhibitor) are consumed and down-regulated. Fibrinolysis is first activated, then suppressed by PAI-1 in sepsis. Thrombin turns fibrinogen into fibrin, and microthrombi lodge in small vessels of the kidneys, lungs, brain and skin, causing ischemia and organ failure. Red cells are sheared on fibrin strands (schistocytes). Platelets and factors such as fibrinogen, V and VIII are used up faster than the liver and marrow can replace them. Plasmin breaks fibrin into fragments such as D-dimer, which also impair clotting. The result is simultaneous thrombosis and bleeding.",
  body: [["Skin", "Petechiae, bruises and oozing from IV sites and wounds come from low platelets and clotting factors; purpura fulminans can cause skin death."], ["Kidneys", "Microthrombi in glomeruli and low blood pressure cause acute kidney injury."], ["Lungs", "Clots and leaky vessels in the lungs contribute to acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) and low oxygen."], ["Brain", "Small clots and bleeds cause confusion, seizures or stroke."], ["Gut and other mucosa", "Bleeding from the gut, gums, nose and urinary tract reflects consumed clotting factors."], ["Adrenal glands", "Adrenal hemorrhage, as in Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome with meningococcal sepsis, causes acute adrenal failure."]],
  symptoms: "Oozing from IV lines, wounds and gums is a classic sign because platelets and fibrinogen are used up, so small vessel injuries cannot seal. Petechiae and bruising appear for the same reason. At the same time, cold, blue or dead fingers and toes and organ failure come from microscopic clots blocking blood flow. Symptoms of the trigger, such as fever and low blood pressure in sepsis, are usually present too.",
  dx: "DIC is diagnosed from the setting plus lab patterns, which are repeated over time. Typical findings are a falling platelet count, prolonged PT and aPTT, low or falling fibrinogen and high D-dimer or fibrin degradation products. The blood smear may show schistocytes. The ISTH score combines platelet count, D-dimer, PT prolongation and fibrinogen into a total that suggests overt DIC. Chronic DIC in cancer may show mainly high D-dimer with clotting events.",
  tx: "The most important step is treating the cause: antibiotics and source control for sepsis, delivery of the baby and placenta in obstetric cases, and treatment of the leukemia, for example tretinoin and arsenic trioxide in APL. Supportive care replaces what has been consumed in patients who are bleeding or need procedures: platelet transfusion, fresh frozen plasma for clotting factors, and cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate to raise fibrinogen. Vitamin K (phytonadione) may be given if deficiency also exists. Heparin is used in selected cases where clotting dominates, such as large vessel clots or some cancer-related DIC, because it boosts antithrombin and limits further thrombin formation. Antifibrinolytics are generally avoided because they can worsen clotting, except in specific situations such as APL or trauma under expert guidance.",
  drugs: ["heparin", "fibrinogen-concentrate", "phytonadione", "enoxaparin", "tretinoin", "arsenic-trioxide"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "DIC can also stand for Death Is Coming: clotting and bleeding at the same time. Causes STOP Making New Thrombi: Sepsis, Trauma, Obstetric, Pancreatitis, Malignancy, Nephrotic, Transfusion."], ["Most-tested fact", "DIC shows low platelets, prolonged PT and aPTT, low fibrinogen and high D-dimer, unlike TTP, where PT and aPTT are normal."], ["Classic board clue", "Acute promyelocytic leukemia (M3) classically presents with DIC."], ["Often confused with", "TTP and HUS, which also cause schistocytes and low platelets but do not consume clotting factors."]],
  red: "Bleeding from many sites, purple spots spreading over the skin, confusion or low blood pressure in someone with sepsis, trauma or a pregnancy complication is life-threatening and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "In meningococcal sepsis, DIC can cause bleeding into both adrenal glands, a combination named after two physicians, Waterhouse and Friderichsen.",
  src: ["ISTH guidance: Taylor FB et al. Thromb Haemost 2001", "Levi M et al. Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of DIC. Br J Haematol 2009", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"itp": {
  name: "Immune thrombocytopenia (ITP)",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["ITP", "idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura", "immune thrombocytopenic purpura", "Werlhof disease"],
  what: "ITP is an autoimmune condition in which the immune system destroys platelets, the small blood cells that help stop bleeding. Platelet counts fall, causing easy bruising, tiny red skin spots and bleeding gums. In children it often follows a virus and goes away on its own.",
  origin: "In 1735 the German physician Paul Gottlieb Werlhof described a young woman with purple skin spots and bleeding, and the illness was long called Werlhof disease. Later, scientists found that patients had low platelets but plenty of platelet-making cells in their marrow. In 1916 Paul Kaznelson, then a medical student in Prague, suggested removing the spleen, and splenectomy became the first effective treatment. In 1951 hematologists William Harrington and James Hollingsworth tested whether a factor in the blood destroyed platelets: Harrington infused himself with plasma from a patient with ITP, his own platelets dropped sharply, and he developed bruising. This proved an antibody-like factor was to blame. Corticosteroids came into use in the 1950s, IV immune globulin in the early 1980s, and rituximab later. Thrombopoietin receptor agonists romiplostim and eltrombopag were approved in 2008, and fostamatinib, a SYK inhibitor, in 2018.",
  timeline: [["1735", "Paul Werlhof describes a bleeding disorder with purple skin spots."], ["1916", "Splenectomy is proposed and becomes the first effective ITP treatment."], ["1951", "The Harrington-Hollingsworth experiment shows a blood factor destroys platelets."], ["1981", "IV immune globulin is reported to raise platelet counts in children with ITP."], ["2008", "Romiplostim and eltrombopag are approved in the US for chronic ITP."], ["2018", "Fostamatinib is approved as a new type of ITP treatment."]],
  cell: "ITP is a loss of tolerance to platelet surface proteins, most often glycoprotein IIb/IIIa (integrin alpha-IIb beta-3) and glycoprotein Ib-IX. B cells make IgG autoantibodies that coat platelets. Macrophages in the spleen, and to a lesser degree the liver, recognize the antibody Fc portion through Fc-gamma receptors and engulf the platelets; this depends partly on spleen tyrosine kinase (SYK) signaling. Cytotoxic CD8 T cells can also kill platelets directly, and regulatory T cells are reduced. The spleen is both the main site of platelet destruction and a major site of autoantibody production. The same antibodies bind megakaryocytes in the marrow and impair platelet production, while thrombopoietin levels stay inappropriately normal, so the marrow does not fully compensate. Primary ITP has no other cause; secondary ITP occurs with lupus, HIV, hepatitis C, H. pylori, CLL and some drugs. In children, an antecedent viral infection often triggers a short-lived immune response through molecular mimicry, explaining why most childhood cases resolve within months.",
  body: [["Skin", "Low platelets let red cells leak from tiny capillaries, causing petechiae (pinpoint red spots that do not blanch), purpura and easy bruising."], ["Mouth and nose", "Mucosal surfaces bleed easily, causing gum bleeding, blood blisters inside the mouth (wet purpura) and nosebleeds."], ["Uterus", "Heavy menstrual bleeding is common in women with ITP."], ["Brain", "Intracranial hemorrhage is rare but is the most feared complication, mostly with very low counts or added risks such as head injury."], ["Spleen", "The spleen is where most platelets are destroyed, but it is usually not enlarged; a large spleen suggests another diagnosis."]],
  symptoms: "Platelets plug tiny breaks in small vessels every day. When they are low, blood leaks out as petechiae and bruises, typically on the legs where pressure is highest. Mucosal bleeding such as nosebleeds and gum bleeding happens for the same reason. Many people with mild ITP have no symptoms and are found on a routine blood count. Fatigue is common, possibly linked to the immune activity itself.",
  dx: "ITP is a diagnosis of exclusion. The blood count shows isolated low platelets with normal red and white cells. The smear confirms true low platelets (ruling out clumping) and may show large young platelets, but no schistocytes. Tests for HIV, hepatitis C and sometimes H. pylori look for secondary causes. PT and aPTT are normal. Bone marrow biopsy is not routinely needed but is considered in older adults or atypical cases to look for other marrow diseases.",
  tx: "Treatment aims to prevent serious bleeding, not to reach a normal count. Many children and adults with mild ITP are watched without drugs. First-line therapy uses corticosteroids (prednisone or dexamethasone), which reduce antibody production and slow macrophage destruction. IV immune globulin saturates Fc receptors on macrophages, raising counts quickly, and anti-D immune globulin coats red cells to do the same in Rh-positive patients with a spleen. For chronic ITP, thrombopoietin receptor agonists (romiplostim, eltrombopag, avatrombopag) stimulate megakaryocytes to make more platelets. Rituximab depletes B cells, fostamatinib blocks SYK in macrophages, and splenectomy removes the main site of destruction. Severe bleeding is treated with platelet transfusion combined with IVIG and steroids, and antifibrinolytics may help. NSAIDs and aspirin are avoided.",
  drugs: ["prednisone", "dexamethasone", "methylprednisolone", "intravenous-immune-globulin", "rho-d-immune-globulin", "romiplostim", "eltrombopag", "avatrombopag", "rituximab", "fostamatinib", "tranexamic-acid", "aminocaproic-acid"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "ITP: Isolated Thrombocytopenia, Period. Everything else in the blood count is normal."], ["Most-tested fact", "Antibodies target GPIIb/IIIa; platelets are destroyed by splenic macrophages. A child with petechiae a few weeks after a viral illness and otherwise normal counts has classic acute ITP."], ["Classic board clue", "Normal spleen size, normal PT and aPTT, and no schistocytes separate ITP from DIC and TTP."], ["Often confused with", "Pseudothrombocytopenia from platelet clumping in EDTA tubes, and heparin-induced thrombocytopenia."]],
  red: "Severe headache, confusion, vomiting, vision changes, blood in vomit, black stools, or heavy bleeding that will not stop with a very low platelet count can mean serious bleeding and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "William Harrington infused himself with an ITP patient's blood to test his idea. His platelet count crashed within hours, proving the cause was in the blood.",
  src: ["ASH 2019 Guidelines for Immune Thrombocytopenia", "International Consensus Report on ITP (2019)", "Harrington WJ et al. J Lab Clin Med 1951", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"hit": {
  name: "Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT)",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["HIT", "HITT", "heparin-induced thrombocytopenia and thrombosis", "white clot syndrome", "HIT type II"],
  what: "HIT is a dangerous immune reaction to the blood thinner heparin. The body makes antibodies that activate platelets, so platelet counts drop and, surprisingly, new blood clots form in veins and arteries. It usually starts 5 to 10 days after heparin is begun.",
  origin: "Heparin came into clinical use in the late 1930s. In 1958 surgeons Rodger Weismann and Richard Tobin described patients on heparin who developed sudden arterial blockages from pale, platelet-rich clots, later called the white clot syndrome. In the 1970s researchers, including George Rhodes and colleagues, showed that the low platelet count was immune-mediated and caused by an antibody in patients' blood. In 1992 Jean Amiral and colleagues in France identified the target: a complex of heparin and platelet factor 4 (PF4). Through the 1990s Theodore Warkentin and John Kelton in Canada showed how often clotting followed and that low molecular weight heparin carried less risk. Direct thrombin inhibitors became the treatment; argatroban was approved for HIT in the US in 2000. Later, the 4Ts score helped clinicians estimate the chance of HIT at the bedside, and direct oral anticoagulants became options.",
  timeline: [["1958", "Weismann and Tobin describe arterial clots in patients on heparin."], ["1970s", "HIT is shown to be caused by an antibody in the blood."], ["1992", "The heparin-platelet factor 4 complex is identified as the antibody target."], ["2000", "Argatroban is approved in the US for prevention and treatment of thrombosis in HIT."], ["2018", "ASH publishes its first guideline on the management of HIT."]],
  cell: "HIT is a type II hypersensitivity reaction. Platelet factor 4 (PF4) is a positively charged protein released from platelet alpha granules. Heparin, which is negatively charged, binds PF4 and forms large complexes that expose new shapes (neoepitopes). The immune system makes IgG antibodies against the heparin-PF4 complex. These antibodies bind the complexes on platelet surfaces, and their Fc portion cross-links platelet Fc-gamma RIIa receptors. This strongly activates platelets, which release more PF4 (feeding the cycle), shed procoagulant microparticles and are cleared by the spleen, so counts fall. Antibody complexes also activate monocytes and endothelial cells to express tissue factor, driving thrombin generation. The result is a hypercoagulable state with low platelets: platelets are low because they are being consumed in clots, not because of bleeding. Unfractionated heparin causes HIT more often than low molecular weight heparin because its longer chains form complexes more easily, and fondaparinux rarely does. A benign, non-immune drop (type I) can occur in the first days and needs no treatment.",
  body: [["Veins", "Deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism are the most common clots in HIT."], ["Arteries", "Arterial clots can block limb arteries, cause stroke or heart attack, and can lead to limb loss."], ["Skin", "Painful red or dark skin lesions can form at heparin injection sites, sometimes progressing to skin necrosis."], ["Adrenal glands", "Clots in adrenal veins can cause adrenal hemorrhage and adrenal failure."], ["Blood", "Platelets typically fall by more than half; bleeding is uncommon despite the low count."], ["Immune reaction", "An IV heparin bolus in someone with antibodies can cause sudden fever, chills, shortness of breath or cardiac arrest within minutes."]],
  symptoms: "Many patients first show only a falling platelet count on routine labs. Swelling and pain in a leg reflect a new DVT, and sudden shortness of breath may mean a pulmonary embolism, both from the platelet-activating antibodies. A cold, pale or painful limb signals an arterial clot. Skin lesions at injection sites come from local platelet activation and clotting in small vessels. Bleeding is unusual because the problem is overactive clotting.",
  dx: "HIT is suspected when platelets fall by more than half, typically 5 to 10 days after heparin starts, or sooner if heparin was given recently. The 4Ts score rates Thrombocytopenia, Timing, Thrombosis and oTher causes to estimate pretest probability. An immunoassay (ELISA) for anti-PF4 heparin antibodies is sensitive but can be positive without disease. A functional test, such as the serotonin release assay, confirms platelet-activating antibodies. Ultrasound of the legs often finds hidden clots.",
  tx: "When HIT is suspected, all heparin is stopped, including heparin flushes and heparin-coated catheters. Because clot risk is very high, a non-heparin anticoagulant is started right away. Options include argatroban and bivalirudin, direct thrombin inhibitors that block thrombin without involving PF4, fondaparinux, a factor Xa inhibitor that rarely cross-reacts, and direct oral anticoagulants such as rivaroxaban and apixaban in stable patients. Warfarin is not started until platelets recover, because early warfarin lowers protein C and can cause venous limb gangrene; vitamin K is given if warfarin was already started. Platelet transfusions are generally avoided unless bleeding is serious. Heparin allergy is recorded in the chart, and IVIG may be used for severe, persistent cases.",
  drugs: ["argatroban", "bivalirudin", "fondaparinux", "rivaroxaban", "apixaban", "dabigatran", "phytonadione", "intravenous-immune-globulin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "HIT makes platelets HIT the gas: low platelets but more clots. Think 4Ts: Thrombocytopenia, Timing, Thrombosis, oTher causes."], ["Most-tested fact", "Antibodies target the heparin-PF4 complex. Treatment is stopping all heparin and starting a direct thrombin inhibitor such as argatroban."], ["Classic board clue", "A platelet drop of more than half, 5 to 10 days after starting heparin, with a new clot."], ["Do not", "Do not start warfarin alone during acute HIT; it can cause venous limb gangrene through protein C depletion."]],
  red: "Sudden chest pain, shortness of breath, a cold or painful limb, new weakness or trouble speaking, or a swollen painful leg in someone recently given heparin needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "HIT is one of the few conditions in which a low platelet count causes clotting instead of bleeding. A similar PF4 antibody reaction explained the rare clotting syndrome seen after some adenovirus-vector COVID-19 vaccines.",
  src: ["ASH 2018 Guidelines for Management of Venous Thromboembolism: Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia", "ACCP Antithrombotic Therapy Guidelines", "Warkentin TE et al. N Engl J Med 1995", "Amiral J et al. Thromb Haemost 1992"]
},
"ttp": {
  name: "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["TTP", "immune TTP", "iTTP", "acquired TTP", "congenital TTP", "Moschcowitz syndrome", "Upshaw-Schulman syndrome"],
  what: "TTP is a rare, life-threatening blood disorder in which tiny clots form in small blood vessels all over the body. The clots use up platelets and shred red blood cells, and can damage the brain, heart and kidneys. Without fast treatment it is often fatal.",
  origin: "In 1924 New York physician Eli Moschcowitz described a 16-year-old girl who died suddenly with fever, anemia, weakness and paralysis; her small vessels were filled with clots made mostly of platelets. In 1966 Edmond Amorosi and John Ultmann reviewed many cases and described a classic pentad of fever, low platelets, red cell breakdown, neurologic changes and kidney problems, and noted that most patients died. In 1982 Joel Moake found unusually large von Willebrand factor multimers in patients' blood. A major turning point came in 1991, when the Canadian Apheresis Group showed that plasma exchange dramatically improved survival. In 1998 two groups, led by Miha Furlan and Han-Mou Tsai, independently showed that patients lacked a von Willebrand factor cleaving protease, often because of an autoantibody. That enzyme, ADAMTS13, was identified in 2001. Caplacizumab was approved in 2019, and a recombinant ADAMTS13 was approved for congenital TTP in 2023.",
  timeline: [["1924", "Eli Moschcowitz describes the first case of TTP."], ["1966", "Amorosi and Ultmann describe the classic pentad."], ["1991", "A Canadian trial shows plasma exchange greatly improves survival."], ["1998", "Two groups show severe deficiency of a VWF-cleaving protease in TTP."], ["2001", "The protease is identified as ADAMTS13."], ["2019", "Caplacizumab is approved in the US for acquired TTP."]],
  cell: "Endothelial cells release von Willebrand factor (VWF) as ultralarge multimers. Normally the plasma metalloprotease ADAMTS13 cuts these long strings into smaller pieces as they unfold under shear stress. In TTP, ADAMTS13 activity is severely deficient. In immune TTP, the most common form, IgG autoantibodies inhibit or clear ADAMTS13; in congenital TTP (Upshaw-Schulman syndrome), the ADAMTS13 gene is mutated. Uncleaved ultralarge VWF strings stay anchored to endothelium and grab platelets through glycoprotein Ib, causing spontaneous platelet aggregation in the high-shear microcirculation. Platelet-rich microthrombi (with little fibrin) block arterioles and capillaries, causing ischemia in the brain, heart, kidneys and gut. Platelets are consumed, causing thrombocytopenia. Red cells passing through partly blocked vessels are sheared into fragments (schistocytes), causing microangiopathic hemolytic anemia with high LDH. Because the coagulation cascade is not primarily activated, PT and aPTT are normal, which separates TTP from DIC. Triggers include pregnancy, infection, autoimmune disease and some drugs (such as ticlopidine, clopidogrel and quinine).",
  body: [["Brain", "Microclots cause headache, confusion, seizures, stroke-like weakness or coma, which can come and go."], ["Heart", "Clots in small coronary vessels can cause heart attacks, heart failure and arrhythmias, an important cause of death."], ["Kidneys", "Mild kidney injury with blood or protein in the urine is common; severe kidney failure suggests HUS instead."], ["Blood", "Red cells are fragmented (schistocytes), causing anemia, jaundice and dark urine; platelets drop very low."], ["Gut", "Clots in gut vessels can cause belly pain, nausea and vomiting."], ["Skin", "Low platelets cause petechiae and purpura, which gives the condition its name."]],
  symptoms: "Neurologic signs such as confusion, headache and stroke-like symptoms come from microclots in brain vessels, and they often fluctuate as clots form and dissolve. Fatigue and shortness of breath come from hemolytic anemia. Yellowing of the skin and dark urine come from bilirubin and hemoglobin released by broken red cells. Purple spots and bruising appear because platelets are consumed. Fever may occur. The full pentad is now seen in only a minority, because treatment starts earlier.",
  dx: "TTP is suspected with microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (schistocytes on the smear, high LDH, low haptoglobin, high indirect bilirubin, high reticulocytes, negative direct Coombs test) and low platelets. PT and aPTT are normal. Severe deficiency of ADAMTS13 activity confirms the diagnosis, and an inhibitor or antibody test identifies immune TTP. Because results take time, the PLASMIC score helps estimate likelihood so treatment can start immediately. Troponin and brain imaging check for organ damage.",
  tx: "Immune TTP is a medical emergency. Therapeutic plasma exchange removes the anti-ADAMTS13 antibodies and ultralarge VWF and replaces ADAMTS13 with donor plasma; plasma infusion is used if exchange is delayed. Corticosteroids such as methylprednisolone or prednisone suppress the autoimmune response. Caplacizumab, a nanobody that blocks the VWF A1 domain from binding platelets, stops new microclots while immunosuppression takes effect. Rituximab depletes the B cells that make the antibody and lowers relapse risk. Congenital TTP is treated with plasma or recombinant ADAMTS13 replacement. Platelet transfusions are generally avoided unless bleeding is life-threatening, because they may feed new clots. Patients are monitored long-term because relapses are common.",
  drugs: ["caplacizumab", "methylprednisolone", "prednisone", "rituximab"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Pentad FAT RN: Fever, Anemia (microangiopathic hemolytic), Thrombocytopenia, Renal problems, Neurologic changes."], ["Most-tested fact", "TTP is caused by low ADAMTS13 activity, leaving ultralarge VWF multimers that clump platelets. PT and aPTT are normal."], ["Often confused with", "HUS (mainly kidney failure, often after Shiga toxin E. coli in children) and DIC (prolonged PT and aPTT, low fibrinogen)."], ["Classic board clue", "Low platelets with schistocytes and confusion: start plasma exchange without waiting for ADAMTS13 results."]],
  red: "Confusion, sudden weakness or trouble speaking, seizure, chest pain, unusual bruising or purple spots with fatigue, or yellowing of the skin needs emergency care (call 911). TTP can be fatal within days without treatment.",
  fun: "Before plasma exchange, about 90 percent of people with TTP died. Today, with fast treatment, about 90 percent survive.",
  src: ["ISTH Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of TTP (2020)", "Rock GA et al. Canadian Apheresis Study Group. N Engl J Med 1991", "Furlan M et al. and Tsai HM, Lian EC. N Engl J Med 1998", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"leukemia": {
  name: "Leukemia",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["blood cancer", "acute lymphoblastic leukemia", "ALL", "acute myeloid leukemia", "AML", "chronic lymphocytic leukemia", "CLL", "chronic myeloid leukemia", "CML"],
  what: "Leukemia is a group of cancers of the blood-forming cells in the bone marrow. Abnormal white blood cells grow out of control and crowd out normal cells, causing anemia, infections and bleeding. It can be fast-growing (acute) or slow (chronic).",
  origin: "In 1845 John Hughes Bennett in Edinburgh and Rudolf Virchow in Berlin each described patients whose blood was thick with white cells. Virchow called it weisses Blut, white blood, and in 1847 coined the word leukemia. For a century it was nearly always fatal. In 1948 Sidney Farber in Boston reported temporary remissions in children with acute leukemia using aminopterin, a folate blocker, launching modern chemotherapy. Combination chemotherapy in the 1960s and 1970s turned childhood ALL into a mostly curable cancer. In 1960 Peter Nowell and David Hungerford found a small chromosome, the Philadelphia chromosome, in CML cells, and in 1973 Janet Rowley showed it came from a swap between chromosomes 9 and 22. This led to imatinib, approved in 2001, the first drug designed to block a cancer's driving protein. Tretinoin and arsenic trioxide made acute promyelocytic leukemia highly curable, and in 2017 the first CAR T-cell therapy was approved for childhood ALL.",
  timeline: [["1845", "Bennett and Virchow describe leukemia; Virchow names it soon after."], ["1948", "Sidney Farber reports remissions in childhood leukemia with aminopterin."], ["1960", "Nowell and Hungerford discover the Philadelphia chromosome in CML."], ["1973", "Janet Rowley shows the Philadelphia chromosome is a 9;22 translocation."], ["2001", "Imatinib is approved for CML, the first major targeted cancer pill."], ["2017", "Tisagenlecleucel becomes the first approved CAR T-cell therapy, for ALL."]],
  cell: "Leukemia starts when a hematopoietic stem or progenitor cell gains mutations that let it multiply without control and, in acute leukemia, block maturation. In acute leukemias (ALL, AML), immature blasts fill the marrow and suppress normal blood production. Common AML drivers include FLT3 and NPM1 mutations, IDH1/IDH2 and core binding factor fusions; t(15;17) creates PML-RARA in acute promyelocytic leukemia, blocking differentiation at the promyelocyte stage and causing DIC. ALL arises from B or T lymphoid precursors and may carry BCR-ABL1 or ETV6-RUNX1 fusions. In chronic leukemias, cells mature more but accumulate. CML is driven by the BCR-ABL1 fusion from t(9;22), a constantly active tyrosine kinase that drives myeloid growth. CLL is a buildup of mature-looking CD5+ CD20+ B cells that resist apoptosis, often through BCL-2 overexpression and constant B-cell receptor signaling through BTK. Marrow crowding causes anemia, neutropenia and thrombocytopenia; blasts can spread to the liver, spleen, lymph nodes, gums, skin and the central nervous system.",
  body: [["Bone marrow", "Leukemic cells crowd out normal production, causing anemia, low working white cells and low platelets; marrow expansion causes bone pain, especially in children."], ["Blood", "White counts may be very high or low. Extremely high blast counts (leukostasis) thicken the blood and block small vessels in the brain and lungs."], ["Spleen, liver and lymph nodes", "Leukemic cells infiltrate these organs, causing enlargement, especially in CML (massive spleen) and CLL (nodes)."], ["Brain and spinal fluid", "ALL can spread to the central nervous system, so treatment includes medicine delivered into the spinal fluid."], ["Gums and skin", "Monocytic AML can infiltrate gums (swelling) and skin (leukemia cutis)."], ["Clotting system", "APL releases procoagulant substances that trigger DIC with serious bleeding."]],
  symptoms: "Tiredness and paleness come from anemia, infections and fever from too few working neutrophils, and bruising, petechiae and nosebleeds from low platelets. Bone pain comes from the expanding marrow. Night sweats, weight loss and fever reflect high cell turnover and cytokines. A full belly reflects a big spleen. Chronic leukemias may cause no symptoms for years and are often found on routine blood tests.",
  dx: "A complete blood count and peripheral smear are the first tests and may show blasts, Auer rods (AML) or many small mature lymphocytes with smudge cells (CLL). Bone marrow biopsy confirms acute leukemia, often with at least 20 percent blasts. Flow cytometry identifies cell lineage by surface markers (for example CD19 in B-ALL, CD33 in AML). Cytogenetics and molecular tests look for BCR-ABL1, FLT3, NPM1, PML-RARA and TP53, which guide treatment and prognosis. A lumbar puncture checks for CNS spread in ALL.",
  tx: "Treatment depends on the type. Acute leukemias are treated with intensive combination chemotherapy: cytarabine with an anthracycline such as daunorubicin or idarubicin in AML, and multi-drug regimens with vincristine, steroids, asparaginase, methotrexate and mercaptopurine in ALL. Targeted drugs add precision: FLT3 inhibitors (midostaurin, gilteritinib), IDH inhibitors, venetoclax with azacitidine in older adults, and tyrosine kinase inhibitors for BCR-ABL1. APL is treated with tretinoin and arsenic trioxide, which force the cells to mature. CML is controlled with tyrosine kinase inhibitors such as imatinib, dasatinib and nilotinib. CLL is often watched early, then treated with BTK inhibitors (ibrutinib, acalabrutinib, zanubrutinib) or venetoclax with an anti-CD20 antibody. Immunotherapies include blinatumomab, inotuzumab and CAR T cells. Stem cell transplant can cure some patients. Supportive care includes transfusions, infection prevention, and allopurinol or rasburicase to prevent tumor lysis syndrome.",
  drugs: ["imatinib", "dasatinib", "nilotinib", "bosutinib", "ponatinib", "asciminib", "cytarabine", "daunorubicin", "idarubicin", "vincristine", "methotrexate", "mercaptopurine", "pegaspargase", "tretinoin", "arsenic-trioxide", "venetoclax", "azacitidine", "midostaurin", "gilteritinib", "ibrutinib", "acalabrutinib", "blinatumomab", "tisagenlecleucel", "rasburicase"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Age guide: ALL in kids, AML in adults over 60, CLL in older adults, CML from middle age. Children ALL, Middle CML, Older CLL."], ["Most-tested fact", "CML has t(9;22) BCR-ABL1, treated with imatinib. APL has t(15;17) PML-RARA, causes DIC and is treated with tretinoin."], ["Classic smear clues", "Auer rods mean AML; smudge cells mean CLL."], ["Emergency link", "Starting treatment can cause tumor lysis syndrome: high potassium, phosphate and uric acid and low calcium."]],
  red: "Fever in someone with leukemia or on chemotherapy, bleeding that will not stop, severe headache, confusion, trouble breathing, or very low urine output after starting treatment needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "In the early 1960s childhood ALL was nearly always fatal. Today about 90 percent of children with ALL in high-income countries are cured.",
  src: ["NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: AML, ALL, CML, CLL", "ELN 2022 recommendations for AML", "Farber S et al. N Engl J Med 1948", "Rowley JD. Nature 1973", "National Cancer Institute"]
},
"lymphoma": {
  name: "Lymphoma",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["Hodgkin lymphoma", "non-Hodgkin lymphoma", "NHL", "diffuse large B-cell lymphoma", "DLBCL", "follicular lymphoma", "Burkitt lymphoma"],
  what: "Lymphoma is a cancer of lymphocytes, the immune cells that live in lymph nodes, the spleen and other tissues. It usually shows up as painless swollen lymph nodes, sometimes with fevers, night sweats and weight loss. The two main groups are Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma.",
  origin: "In 1832 the London physician Thomas Hodgkin described seven patients with enlarged lymph nodes and spleen. In 1865 Samuel Wilks rediscovered the paper and named the disease after him. Carl Sternberg in 1898 and Dorothy Reed in 1902 described the large, distinctive cells now called Reed-Sternberg cells. In 1958 surgeon Denis Burkitt described a fast-growing jaw tumor in African children, which led to the discovery of the Epstein-Barr virus. In the 1960s, at the US National Cancer Institute, Vincent DeVita and colleagues developed MOPP, the first combination chemotherapy to cure advanced Hodgkin lymphoma, and in 1975 Gianni Bonadonna in Milan introduced ABVD. CHOP chemotherapy became standard for aggressive non-Hodgkin lymphoma in the 1970s. In 1997 rituximab became the first monoclonal antibody approved to treat cancer, and in 2017 CAR T-cell therapy was approved for relapsed large B-cell lymphoma.",
  timeline: [["1832", "Thomas Hodgkin describes the disease later named after him."], ["1958", "Denis Burkitt describes a jaw lymphoma in African children."], ["1960s", "MOPP combination chemotherapy is shown to cure advanced Hodgkin lymphoma."], ["1975", "ABVD chemotherapy is introduced for Hodgkin lymphoma."], ["1997", "Rituximab becomes the first monoclonal antibody approved for cancer."], ["2017", "Axicabtagene ciloleucel is approved for large B-cell lymphoma."]],
  cell: "Lymphomas arise when a B or T lymphocyte at a particular stage of development gains genetic changes that block apoptosis or drive proliferation. Most non-Hodgkin lymphomas are B-cell tumors. Follicular lymphoma carries t(14;18), which places BCL2 next to the immunoglobulin heavy chain gene, so cells overproduce the anti-apoptotic BCL-2 protein and accumulate slowly. Burkitt lymphoma carries t(8;14), placing MYC next to the heavy chain gene and driving extremely fast growth (a starry sky pattern). Mantle cell lymphoma has t(11;14) with cyclin D1 overexpression. Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, the most common type, has varied mutations, often in NF-kB and B-cell receptor pathways. In Hodgkin lymphoma, the malignant Reed-Sternberg cells (CD15+ CD30+) are rare and are surrounded by a large reactive crowd of T cells, eosinophils and other cells drawn in by cytokines; Reed-Sternberg cells often overexpress PD-L1, helping them evade T cells. Risk rises with immune suppression, HIV, EBV, H. pylori (gastric MALT lymphoma) and autoimmune disease.",
  body: [["Lymph nodes", "Nodes in the neck, armpit, chest or groin enlarge painlessly and feel rubbery. Hodgkin lymphoma often spreads node to node in order."], ["Chest", "A mass in the mediastinum can press on airways or the superior vena cava, causing cough, shortness of breath or face swelling."], ["Spleen and liver", "Infiltration enlarges these organs, causing fullness and early satiety."], ["Bone marrow", "Spread to the marrow lowers normal blood counts, causing anemia, infections and bleeding."], ["Gut and other sites", "Non-Hodgkin lymphoma often appears outside nodes, such as the stomach, skin, brain or testes."], ["Immune system", "Lymphoma disturbs normal immunity, raising infection risk and sometimes causing autoimmune anemia."]],
  symptoms: "Painless swollen nodes come from the buildup of lymphoma cells. B symptoms, which are fever, drenching night sweats and weight loss, come from cytokines such as IL-6 and TNF released by the tumor and surrounding cells. Itching is common in Hodgkin lymphoma, likely from cytokines and eosinophils. Pain in the nodes after drinking alcohol is a rare but classic Hodgkin clue. Pressure symptoms depend on where masses grow.",
  dx: "Diagnosis needs a tissue biopsy, ideally removing a whole lymph node so the architecture can be studied. Immunohistochemistry and flow cytometry identify cell type and markers (for example CD20 for B cells, CD15 and CD30 for Reed-Sternberg cells). Genetic tests look for translocations such as MYC, BCL2 and cyclin D1. PET-CT scans stage the disease using the Lugano classification. Blood tests include a blood count, LDH, liver and kidney tests, and HIV and hepatitis B screening.",
  tx: "Treatment depends on the type and stage. Classic Hodgkin lymphoma is treated with combination chemotherapy such as ABVD (doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, dacarbazine), sometimes with radiation; brentuximab vedotin, an anti-CD30 antibody-drug conjugate, and PD-1 inhibitors such as nivolumab and pembrolizumab are used in advanced or relapsed disease. Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma is treated with R-CHOP (rituximab plus cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine and prednisone) or pola-R-CHP with polatuzumab vedotin. Rituximab binds CD20 and marks B cells for destruction. Slow-growing lymphomas may be watched or treated with rituximab and bendamustine. Relapsed disease may be treated with high-dose chemotherapy and stem cell transplant, CAR T cells, or bispecific antibodies such as glofitamab and epcoritamab that bring T cells to lymphoma cells. BTK inhibitors are used for some types, and H. pylori eradication can cure early gastric MALT lymphoma.",
  drugs: ["rituximab", "cyclophosphamide", "doxorubicin", "vincristine", "prednisone", "polatuzumab-vedotin", "bendamustine", "brentuximab-vedotin", "bleomycin", "vinblastine", "dacarbazine", "nivolumab", "pembrolizumab", "axicabtagene-ciloleucel", "lisocabtagene-maraleucel", "tisagenlecleucel", "glofitamab", "epcoritamab", "mosunetuzumab", "obinutuzumab", "ibrutinib", "zanubrutinib", "allopurinol", "rasburicase"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Hodgkin: owl-eye Reed-Sternberg cells, CD15 and CD30, contiguous spread, young adults and older adults (two peaks)."], ["Most-tested facts", "Follicular is t(14;18) BCL2, Burkitt is t(8;14) MYC with starry sky, mantle cell is t(11;14) cyclin D1."], ["Classic board clue", "A young adult with a neck or chest mass, night sweats, itching and pain in nodes after alcohol suggests Hodgkin lymphoma."], ["Emergency link", "Burkitt lymphoma grows so fast that tumor lysis syndrome can occur even before treatment."]],
  red: "Sudden swelling of the face and neck with trouble breathing, high fever during chemotherapy, sudden weakness in the legs or loss of bladder control, or confusion needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Thomas Hodgkin's original specimens are still kept at Guy's Hospital in London, and modern tests on them confirmed that some of his patients did have the lymphoma that bears his name.",
  src: ["NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: B-Cell Lymphomas and Hodgkin Lymphoma", "WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours, 5th edition (2022)", "Lugano Classification (2014)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "National Cancer Institute"]
},
"multiple-myeloma": {
  name: "Multiple myeloma",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["myeloma", "plasma cell myeloma", "Kahler disease", "MGUS (precursor)", "smoldering myeloma"],
  what: "Multiple myeloma is a cancer of plasma cells, the bone marrow cells that make antibodies. The cancer cells crowd the marrow, make large amounts of a single abnormal antibody, and damage bones and kidneys. It is most common in older adults.",
  origin: "In the 1840s the London physician Henry Bence Jones studied the urine of a patient with softened, broken bones and found an unusual protein that appeared when urine was heated, vanished on boiling and returned on cooling. These Bence Jones proteins are now known to be free antibody light chains. In 1889 the Austrian physician Otto Kahler described a patient in detail, and the disease became known as Kahler disease in parts of Europe. In the 1900s scientists recognized plasma cells as the source, and serum protein electrophoresis in the 1930s and later revealed the monoclonal M spike. Melphalan with prednisone became the main treatment in the 1960s. A new era began in 1999, when a study showed thalidomide worked in relapsed myeloma. Bortezomib (2003), lenalidomide (2006) and daratumumab (2015) transformed outcomes, and the first CAR T-cell therapy for myeloma was approved in 2021.",
  timeline: [["1840s", "Henry Bence Jones describes an unusual urine protein in a patient with bone disease."], ["1889", "Otto Kahler publishes a detailed description of the disease."], ["1960s", "Melphalan with prednisone becomes standard treatment."], ["2003", "Bortezomib becomes the first proteasome inhibitor approved for myeloma."], ["2015", "Daratumumab, an anti-CD38 antibody, is approved."], ["2021", "Idecabtagene vicleucel becomes the first CAR T-cell therapy approved for myeloma."]],
  cell: "Myeloma starts from a single plasma cell clone, usually after a long precursor stage called MGUS (monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance). Early events include translocations involving the immunoglobulin heavy chain gene on chromosome 14 (such as t(11;14) with cyclin D1 or t(4;14)) or extra copies of odd-numbered chromosomes; later events such as MYC, RAS and TP53 changes drive progression. Myeloma cells depend on the bone marrow niche: IL-6 from stromal cells promotes their survival. They make huge amounts of one immunoglobulin or light chain, which requires the proteasome to clear misfolded proteins, explaining why proteasome inhibitors work. Myeloma cells increase RANKL and reduce osteoprotegerin, activating osteoclasts, and they secrete DKK1 to block osteoblasts, so bone is destroyed without repair, causing lytic lesions and high calcium. Free light chains are filtered by the kidneys and combine with Tamm-Horsfall protein to form casts that block tubules (myeloma cast nephropathy). Normal antibody production falls, raising infection risk. Light chains can also deposit as AL amyloid.",
  body: [["Bones", "Osteoclast activation punches out lytic holes in the skull, spine, ribs and pelvis, causing bone pain and fractures, especially of the vertebrae."], ["Kidneys", "Light chain casts, high calcium and dehydration injure the kidneys, often causing kidney failure."], ["Blood", "Marrow crowding causes anemia; high protein makes red cells stack like coins (rouleaux) and can thicken the blood."], ["Calcium balance", "Bone breakdown releases calcium, causing confusion, constipation, thirst and kidney injury."], ["Immune system", "Low normal antibodies cause frequent infections, especially pneumonia from encapsulated bacteria."], ["Nerves", "Collapsed vertebrae can compress the spinal cord, and amyloid or drugs can cause neuropathy."]],
  symptoms: "Bone pain, especially in the back, comes from lytic lesions and fractures. Tiredness comes from anemia. Thirst, confusion and constipation come from high calcium. Foamy urine or reduced urine reflects kidney damage. Repeated infections reflect low normal antibodies. These features are summed up as CRAB: Calcium high, Renal failure, Anemia, Bone lesions.",
  dx: "Serum protein electrophoresis shows a monoclonal M spike, and immunofixation identifies the type, most often IgG. Serum free light chain testing and urine studies detect light chains (Bence Jones protein). Bone marrow biopsy shows clonal plasma cells. Imaging with whole-body low-dose CT, PET-CT or MRI finds bone lesions. The International Myeloma Working Group criteria define active myeloma by clonal plasma cells plus CRAB features or specific biomarkers. Cytogenetics help predict risk.",
  tx: "Treatment combines several drug classes to control the disease, which usually relapses over time. Proteasome inhibitors (bortezomib, carfilzomib, ixazomib) cause toxic protein buildup in myeloma cells. Immunomodulatory drugs (lenalidomide, pomalidomide, thalidomide) bind cereblon, leading to breakdown of transcription factors the cells need and boosting T and NK cells. Anti-CD38 antibodies (daratumumab, isatuximab) mark plasma cells for destruction. Dexamethasone kills myeloma cells directly. Many fit patients receive high-dose melphalan with autologous stem cell transplant, followed by lenalidomide maintenance. Relapsed disease may be treated with CAR T cells (idecabtagene vicleucel, ciltacabtagene autoleucel) or bispecific antibodies that target BCMA or GPRC5D (teclistamab, elranatamab, talquetamab). Bone-protecting drugs (zoledronic acid, denosumab) reduce fractures, and care includes hydration, vaccines and treatment of high calcium.",
  drugs: ["bortezomib", "carfilzomib", "ixazomib", "lenalidomide", "pomalidomide", "thalidomide", "daratumumab", "isatuximab", "elotuzumab", "dexamethasone", "melphalan", "cyclophosphamide", "selinexor", "idecabtagene-vicleucel", "ciltacabtagene-autoleucel", "teclistamab", "elranatamab", "talquetamab", "zoledronic-acid", "denosumab", "pamidronate", "acyclovir"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "CRAB: Calcium high, Renal failure, Anemia, Bone lytic lesions."], ["Most-tested fact", "An M spike on serum protein electrophoresis with Bence Jones proteinuria and punched-out lytic skull lesions; rouleaux on the smear."], ["Classic board clue", "An older adult with back pain, anemia, high calcium and kidney injury, with a large gap between total protein and albumin."], ["Often confused with", "MGUS, which has a small M protein and no end-organ damage, and Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia (IgM, no lytic lesions)."]],
  red: "Sudden back pain with leg weakness, numbness or loss of bladder or bowel control can mean spinal cord compression and needs emergency care (call 911). Severe confusion, very low urine output or high fever also need urgent care.",
  fun: "Bence Jones protein was described more than a century before scientists understood it was a piece of an antibody, making it one of the first tumor markers in medicine.",
  src: ["International Myeloma Working Group Criteria (2014)", "NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Multiple Myeloma", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "National Cancer Institute"]
},
"polycythemia-vera": {
  name: "Polycythemia vera",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["PV", "primary polycythemia", "Vaquez disease", "Osler-Vaquez disease", "myeloproliferative neoplasm"],
  what: "Polycythemia vera is a slow-growing blood cancer in which the bone marrow makes too many red blood cells, and often too many white cells and platelets. The blood becomes thick, raising the risk of clots, strokes and heart attacks.",
  origin: "In 1892 the French physician Louis Henri Vaquez described a patient with a very high red cell count, a large spleen and a ruddy face. In 1903 William Osler in Baltimore described more cases, and the disease became known as Vaquez disease or Osler-Vaquez disease. Treatment for decades was bloodletting, an ancient practice that now had a clear reason. In 1951 the American hematologist William Dameshek grouped polycythemia vera with related marrow disorders as myeloproliferative disorders, predicting they shared a common cause. That cause was found in 2005, when several research groups independently discovered the JAK2 V617F mutation in most patients. This led to ruxolitinib, a JAK inhibitor, approved for PV in 2014. In 2013 the CYTO-PV trial showed that keeping the hematocrit below 45 percent lowered cardiovascular events. Ropeginterferon alfa-2b was approved in the US in 2021.",
  timeline: [["1892", "Louis Henri Vaquez describes the disease."], ["1903", "William Osler publishes further cases."], ["1951", "William Dameshek groups PV with other myeloproliferative disorders."], ["2005", "The JAK2 V617F mutation is discovered in most patients with PV."], ["2014", "Ruxolitinib is approved for PV after hydroxyurea."], ["2021", "Ropeginterferon alfa-2b is approved in the US for PV."]],
  cell: "PV is a myeloproliferative neoplasm arising from a hematopoietic stem cell. About 95 percent of patients have the JAK2 V617F mutation, and most others have JAK2 exon 12 mutations. JAK2 is a tyrosine kinase that normally relays signals from the erythropoietin receptor, thrombopoietin receptor and G-CSF receptor. The V617F change disrupts the JH2 pseudokinase domain, which normally keeps JAK2 switched off, so JAK2 is always on and constantly activates STAT5, PI3K and MAPK pathways. Red cell precursors grow even without erythropoietin, so EPO levels in the blood are low. Red cell mass expands, raising blood viscosity and slowing flow; platelets and white cells are also activated, promoting thrombosis. Iron is used up by the expanding red cell mass. Over time, some patients progress to myelofibrosis, in which the marrow scars, or to acute myeloid leukemia. Secondary polycythemia, in contrast, is driven by high EPO from low oxygen (lung disease, high altitude, smoking) or EPO-producing tumors.",
  body: [["Blood", "Thick blood with high hematocrit flows slowly, raising the risk of venous and arterial clots, including unusual sites such as the hepatic veins (Budd-Chiari syndrome)."], ["Brain", "Sluggish blood flow causes headaches, dizziness, vision changes and raises stroke risk."], ["Heart", "Higher viscosity and clots raise the risk of heart attack and add work to the heart."], ["Spleen", "The spleen enlarges from extra blood cell production and pooling, causing left-sided fullness."], ["Skin", "A red, ruddy face (plethora), burning red hands and feet (erythromelalgia) and itching after warm showers are common."], ["Gut and joints", "High cell turnover raises uric acid, causing gout; stomach ulcers are more common."]],
  symptoms: "Headache, dizziness and blurred vision come from thick blood slowing flow in small brain and eye vessels. A red face comes from more red cells in skin vessels. Itching after a warm bath (aquagenic pruritus) is thought to come from histamine released by increased mast cells and basophils. Burning pain in hands and feet comes from platelet-driven blockage of small vessels. Fullness in the belly reflects a large spleen. Many people are diagnosed on a routine blood test before symptoms.",
  dx: "The WHO criteria combine a high hemoglobin or hematocrit (or increased red cell mass), a bone marrow biopsy showing increased activity of all three cell lines, and a JAK2 V617F or exon 12 mutation. A low serum erythropoietin level is a minor criterion and helps separate PV from secondary polycythemia, which has normal or high EPO. Blood counts often also show high platelets and white cells. Ferritin may be low.",
  tx: "The main goals are to prevent clots and relieve symptoms. Phlebotomy removes blood to keep the hematocrit below 45 percent, reducing viscosity; it also causes mild iron deficiency that slows red cell production. Low-dose aspirin reduces platelet-driven clots and erythromelalgia unless bleeding risk is high. Higher-risk patients (age 60 or older, or a prior clot) receive cytoreductive therapy: hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase to slow cell production, and interferon alfa, such as ropeginterferon alfa-2b or off-label peginterferon alfa-2a, can reduce the mutant clone. Ruxolitinib, a JAK1 and JAK2 inhibitor, is used when hydroxyurea is not tolerated or does not work, and it helps itching and spleen size. Managing blood pressure, cholesterol and smoking lowers vascular risk, and antihistamines or allopurinol may help itching or high uric acid.",
  drugs: ["hydroxyurea", "aspirin", "ruxolitinib", "peginterferon-alfa-2a", "allopurinol", "anagrelide"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "PV: Plethoric, Very thick blood. JAK2 V617F is found in about 95 percent."], ["Most-tested fact", "PV has a high red cell mass with LOW erythropoietin; secondary polycythemia has high EPO."], ["Classic board clue", "Itching after a hot shower, a ruddy face and a big spleen in an older adult, sometimes presenting with Budd-Chiari syndrome."], ["Often confused with", "Relative polycythemia from dehydration (normal red cell mass) and secondary polycythemia from lung disease, sleep apnea or testosterone use."]],
  red: "Sudden weakness, trouble speaking, chest pain, severe belly pain with swelling, a swollen painful leg or sudden vision loss can mean a blood clot and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Bloodletting, one of the oldest and most criticized medical practices, is still the first-line treatment for polycythemia vera, one of the few diseases where it truly helps.",
  src: ["NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Myeloproliferative Neoplasms", "WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours, 5th edition (2022)", "Marchioli R et al. CYTO-PV. N Engl J Med 2013", "James C et al. Nature 2005", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"lupus": {
  name: "Systemic lupus erythematosus",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["SLE", "lupus", "lupus nephritis", "drug-induced lupus"],
  what: "Lupus is a chronic autoimmune disease in which the immune system attacks the body's own tissues. It can inflame the skin, joints, kidneys, blood cells, heart, lungs and brain, with flares and calmer periods. It most often affects women in their childbearing years.",
  origin: "The word lupus, Latin for wolf, was used in the Middle Ages for skin sores thought to look like wolf bites. In the 1800s the Austrian dermatologist Ferdinand von Hebra described the butterfly rash, and in 1872 his student and son-in-law Moriz Kaposi described lupus as a disease that could affect the whole body. William Osler in the 1890s and early 1900s described its effects on internal organs. In 1948 Malcolm Hargraves at the Mayo Clinic discovered the LE cell, an immune cell that had swallowed nuclear material, showing that autoantibodies against the nucleus were involved. Antinuclear antibody tests followed in the 1950s. Corticosteroids, introduced around 1950, and antimalarials such as hydroxychloroquine improved survival. Cyclophosphamide and later mycophenolate protected kidneys. In 2011 belimumab became the first new lupus drug approved in about 50 years, followed by anifrolumab and voclosporin in 2021.",
  timeline: [["1872", "Moriz Kaposi describes lupus as a systemic disease."], ["1948", "Malcolm Hargraves discovers the LE cell at the Mayo Clinic."], ["1950s", "Antinuclear antibody testing and corticosteroids change diagnosis and care."], ["2011", "Belimumab is approved, the first new lupus drug in decades."], ["2019", "EULAR and ACR publish new SLE classification criteria."], ["2021", "Anifrolumab and voclosporin are approved in the US."]],
  cell: "Lupus results from genes, hormones and environment acting together. Defects in clearing apoptotic cells (for example complement C1q, C2 or C4 deficiency) leave nuclear material such as DNA, histones and ribonucleoproteins exposed. Plasmacytoid dendritic cells take up these nucleic acids through Toll-like receptors 7 and 9 and release large amounts of type I interferon (the interferon signature). Interferon and BAFF (B-cell activating factor, also called BLyS) support autoreactive B cells, which, with help from T follicular helper cells, make autoantibodies such as anti-dsDNA, anti-Smith and anti-Ro. These antibodies form immune complexes that deposit in glomeruli, skin, joints and vessel walls and activate complement (type III hypersensitivity), drawing in neutrophils and causing inflammation; complement levels fall. Some antibodies act directly on cells (type II hypersensitivity), causing hemolytic anemia and low platelets. Estrogen and X-chromosome genes such as TLR7 may explain why most patients are female. UV light increases skin cell apoptosis and triggers flares. Some drugs (hydralazine, procainamide, isoniazid) cause drug-induced lupus with anti-histone antibodies.",
  body: [["Skin", "A butterfly-shaped malar rash over the cheeks and nose that spares the nasolabial folds, photosensitivity, discoid scarring lesions and oral ulcers are common."], ["Joints", "Painful, swollen joints without erosions; deformities, if any, are reducible (Jaccoud arthropathy)."], ["Kidneys", "Immune complexes in glomeruli cause lupus nephritis, with protein or blood in the urine, high blood pressure and possible kidney failure."], ["Blood", "Antibodies destroy red cells, white cells or platelets; antiphospholipid antibodies can cause clots and pregnancy loss."], ["Heart and lungs", "Inflammation of the lining around the heart and lungs (pericarditis, pleuritis) causes chest pain. Libman-Sacks endocarditis and early atherosclerosis can occur."], ["Brain", "Neuropsychiatric lupus can cause seizures, psychosis, stroke, headaches and memory problems."], ["Pregnancy", "Lupus raises risks of preeclampsia and loss; anti-Ro antibodies can cause neonatal heart block."]],
  symptoms: "Fatigue and fever come from type I interferon and other cytokines. Joint pain and swelling come from immune complex inflammation in the synovium. The rash worsens in the sun because UV light makes skin cells die and expose their nuclear contents. Chest pain with breathing comes from inflamed pleura or pericardium. Swelling of the legs and foamy urine come from kidney involvement. Symptoms flare and calm because immune activity rises and falls.",
  dx: "Diagnosis combines clinical features and lab tests. The ANA test is positive in nearly all patients and is a sensitive screen; the 2019 EULAR/ACR classification criteria require a positive ANA before scoring other features. Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith antibodies are much more specific, and anti-dsDNA levels often rise with kidney flares. Low complement (C3, C4) suggests active disease. A blood count, urinalysis, urine protein measurement and kidney biopsy for suspected nephritis guide treatment. Antiphospholipid antibodies are checked.",
  tx: "Treatment calms the immune system while limiting side effects. Hydroxychloroquine is recommended for nearly all patients; it raises lysosomal pH and dampens Toll-like receptor signaling, reduces flares and improves survival, and patients have regular eye exams for retinal toxicity. Corticosteroids rapidly control flares and are tapered to the lowest effective dose. Steroid-sparing drugs include methotrexate, azathioprine and mycophenolate. Belimumab blocks BAFF to reduce autoreactive B cells, and anifrolumab blocks the type I interferon receptor. Lupus nephritis is treated with mycophenolate or cyclophosphamide, often with belimumab, voclosporin (a calcineurin inhibitor) or obinutuzumab. Rituximab is used in refractory cases. Sun protection, blood pressure control, vaccines and careful pregnancy planning are key.",
  drugs: ["hydroxychloroquine", "prednisone", "methylprednisolone", "mycophenolate", "azathioprine", "methotrexate", "cyclophosphamide", "belimumab", "anifrolumab", "voclosporin", "obinutuzumab", "rituximab", "tacrolimus"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "SOAP BRAIN MD: Serositis, Oral ulcers, Arthritis, Photosensitivity, Blood disorders, Renal, ANA, Immunologic, Neurologic, Malar rash, Discoid rash."], ["Most-tested fact", "ANA is the most sensitive test; anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith are the most specific. Anti-histone suggests drug-induced lupus."], ["Classic board clue", "A young woman with joint pain, malar rash, low complement and protein in the urine."], ["Often confused with", "Rheumatoid arthritis (erosive joints, anti-CCP) and dermatomyositis (heliotrope rash, muscle weakness)."]],
  red: "Chest pain, shortness of breath, seizures, sudden confusion, severe headache, leg swelling with foamy urine, or high fever while on immune-suppressing drugs needs urgent care, and severe symptoms need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The lupus butterfly rash usually spares the creases between the nose and the corners of the mouth, a detail that helps separate it from rosacea.",
  src: ["2019 EULAR/ACR Classification Criteria for SLE", "EULAR Recommendations for the Management of SLE (2023)", "KDIGO 2024 Clinical Practice Guideline for Lupus Nephritis", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"neutropenic-fever": {
  name: "Neutropenic fever",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["febrile neutropenia", "FN", "fever and neutropenia"],
  what: "Neutropenic fever is a fever in someone whose neutrophils, the white blood cells that fight bacteria, are very low, usually after chemotherapy. Because the body cannot fight infection well, it is treated as a medical emergency.",
  origin: "In 1966 Gerald Bodey and colleagues at the US National Cancer Institute showed that the risk of infection in leukemia rose sharply as the neutrophil count fell, linking low neutrophils to deadly infections. In the 1960s many patients with gram-negative bacterial infections died within hours or days. In 1971 Stephen Schimpff and colleagues reported that starting broad-spectrum antibiotics immediately when fever appeared, without waiting for culture results, saved lives; this empiric approach remains the foundation of care. Over the following decades, single broad antibiotics with activity against Pseudomonas replaced combinations, and colony-stimulating factors such as filgrastim, approved in 1991, shortened periods of neutropenia. In 2000 the MASCC risk index helped identify lower-risk patients, and IDSA and ASCO guidelines later supported outpatient oral treatment for carefully selected people.",
  timeline: [["1966", "Bodey and colleagues link low neutrophil counts to infection risk."], ["1971", "Early empiric antibiotics are shown to save lives in neutropenic fever."], ["1991", "Filgrastim is approved to shorten neutropenia after chemotherapy."], ["2000", "The MASCC risk index is published to identify lower-risk patients."], ["2010", "IDSA publishes its guideline on fever and neutropenia in cancer patients."]],
  cell: "Neutrophils are made in the bone marrow from myeloid precursors under the control of G-CSF and live only hours to days in the blood. Chemotherapy kills rapidly dividing marrow cells, so neutrophil counts fall to their lowest point (nadir) typically 7 to 14 days after treatment. Neutrophils normally kill bacteria and fungi by phagocytosis, reactive oxygen species (NADPH oxidase), granule enzymes such as myeloperoxidase and neutrophil extracellular traps. Without them, microbes from the patient's own gut, skin and mouth invade. Chemotherapy also damages the gut and mouth lining (mucositis), letting gram-negative bacteria such as E. coli, Klebsiella and Pseudomonas aeruginosa into the blood. Central lines allow gram-positive bacteria such as Staphylococcus to enter. Prolonged neutropenia, often over a week, adds risk of invasive fungi such as Candida and Aspergillus. Because neutrophils make pus and much of the local inflammation, the usual signs of infection are blunted: pneumonia may show little on X-ray and urine may show no white cells. Fever may be the only sign, and sepsis can develop within hours.",
  body: [["Blood", "Bacteria can enter the bloodstream quickly, causing bacteremia and septic shock, the main cause of death."], ["Mouth and gut", "Chemotherapy injures the lining, causing painful mucositis and letting gut bacteria enter the blood; neutropenic enterocolitis (typhlitis) can affect the cecum."], ["Lungs", "Pneumonia from bacteria or molds such as Aspergillus can develop with few early signs."], ["Skin and lines", "Central line sites and skin breaks can become infected with little redness or pus."], ["Circulation", "Infection-driven inflammation dilates vessels and causes low blood pressure, low urine output and organ failure."]],
  symptoms: "Fever is the key sign because neutrophils normally create the redness, swelling and pus that would point to a source, so fever may be the only clue. Chills, a fast heart rate and low blood pressure suggest bacteria in the blood. Mouth sores, belly pain or diarrhea point to injured gut lining or typhlitis. Cough or shortness of breath can signal pneumonia even when an X-ray looks normal.",
  dx: "IDSA defines neutropenic fever as a single oral temperature of at least 38.3 C (100.9 F), or at least 38.0 C (100.4 F) sustained over an hour, in a person with an absolute neutrophil count below 500 cells per microliter or expected to fall below that soon. Evaluation includes at least two sets of blood cultures (including from central lines), a blood count, kidney and liver tests, lactate, urinalysis and culture, and a chest X-ray when breathing symptoms are present. The MASCC score or CISNE score helps sort risk.",
  tx: "Empiric broad-spectrum IV antibiotics are started as fast as possible, ideally within an hour, without waiting for culture results. Single agents with activity against Pseudomonas, such as cefepime, piperacillin-tazobactam, meropenem or imipenem-cilastatin, are the standard; they kill gram-negative bacteria by blocking cell wall synthesis. Vancomycin is added for suspected line infection, skin infection, pneumonia, MRSA colonization or unstable patients. Low-risk adults may receive oral therapy such as ciprofloxacin or levofloxacin with amoxicillin-clavulanate under close follow-up. Persistent fever after several days of antibiotics leads to a search for fungal infection and empiric antifungals such as an echinocandin, voriconazole or liposomal amphotericin B. Septic shock is treated with fluids and vasopressors. Growth factors (filgrastim, pegfilgrastim) stimulate neutrophil production and are mainly used to prevent neutropenia in high-risk chemotherapy. Hand hygiene and patient education about checking temperatures are key.",
  drugs: ["cefepime", "piperacillin-tazobactam", "meropenem", "imipenem-cilastatin", "ceftazidime", "vancomycin", "linezolid", "daptomycin", "ciprofloxacin", "levofloxacin", "amoxicillin-clavulanate", "aztreonam", "filgrastim", "pegfilgrastim", "micafungin", "caspofungin", "voriconazole", "posaconazole", "amphotericin-b-liposomal", "acyclovir", "norepinephrine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Fever plus neutropenia equals antibiotics within the hour. Treat first, find the bug later."], ["Most-tested fact", "Empiric monotherapy with an anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam (cefepime, piperacillin-tazobactam or a carbapenem) is first-line; vancomycin is not routinely added."], ["Classic board clue", "A patient about 10 days after chemotherapy with fever and few other signs; rectal temperatures and exams are avoided."], ["Nursing point", "Neutropenic precautions focus on hand hygiene; fever is reported immediately, and acetaminophen is not used to mask it before evaluation."]],
  red: "Any fever, chills, shaking, confusion, dizziness or low urine output in someone who recently had chemotherapy needs emergency care right away (call 911 if very ill). The cancer team or emergency department needs to be told about recent chemotherapy.",
  fun: "Studies show that each hour of delay in giving antibiotics for neutropenic fever is linked to higher death rates, which is why many hospitals have a door-to-antibiotic time goal.",
  src: ["IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline for the Use of Antimicrobial Agents in Neutropenic Patients with Cancer (2010)", "ASCO/IDSA Outpatient Management of Fever and Neutropenia in Adults (2018)", "NCCN Guidelines: Prevention and Treatment of Cancer-Related Infections", "Bodey GP et al. Ann Intern Med 1966"]
},
"transplant-rejection": {
  name: "Organ transplant rejection",
  sys: "Blood & Immune",
  aka: ["graft rejection", "allograft rejection", "acute cellular rejection", "antibody-mediated rejection", "AMR", "chronic allograft dysfunction"],
  what: "Transplant rejection happens when the immune system recognizes a donated organ as foreign and attacks it. It can happen within minutes, weeks or years after a transplant. Lifelong anti-rejection medicines are used to prevent it.",
  origin: "In the early 1900s the French surgeon Alexis Carrel developed ways to sew blood vessels together, making organ transplants technically possible, and won the 1912 Nobel Prize. But transplanted organs failed. During World War II the British biologist Peter Medawar studied skin grafts for burned airmen and showed that rejection was an immune response, with a second graft from the same donor rejected faster; he shared the 1960 Nobel Prize with Frank Macfarlane Burnet. In 1954 Joseph Murray in Boston performed the first long-term successful kidney transplant between identical twins, Ronald and Richard Herrick, avoiding rejection because their tissues matched. In the 1960s azathioprine, developed from the work of Gertrude Elion and George Hitchings, plus steroids allowed transplants between non-twins. Cyclosporine, discovered from a fungus at Sandoz and approved in the US in 1983, made heart, liver and lung transplants far more successful. Tacrolimus followed in 1994.",
  timeline: [["1912", "Alexis Carrel wins the Nobel Prize for blood vessel suturing and transplant work."], ["1940s", "Peter Medawar shows that graft rejection is an immune response."], ["1954", "Joseph Murray performs the first successful kidney transplant between identical twins."], ["1960s", "Azathioprine and steroids allow transplants between unrelated people."], ["1983", "Cyclosporine is approved in the US and transforms transplant survival."], ["1994", "Tacrolimus is approved in the US."]],
  cell: "Rejection is driven mainly by differences in HLA (MHC) molecules between donor and recipient. Hyperacute rejection occurs within minutes when the recipient already has antibodies against donor ABO or HLA antigens; the antibodies bind the graft endothelium, activate complement and cause immediate thrombosis (type II hypersensitivity). Pre-transplant crossmatch testing has made it rare. Acute cellular rejection, usually in the first months, is T-cell mediated. Recipient T cells recognize donor MHC directly on donor antigen-presenting cells or indirectly as peptides on their own cells. With signal 1 (T-cell receptor binding MHC) and signal 2 (CD28 binding B7 [CD80/86]), T cells activate calcineurin, which dephosphorylates NFAT, leading to IL-2 production and proliferation through mTOR. CD8 cytotoxic T cells then attack graft cells and CD4 cells recruit macrophages. Antibody-mediated rejection occurs when donor-specific antibodies bind endothelium, deposit C4d and cause capillary inflammation. Chronic rejection develops over months to years through repeated immune and non-immune injury, causing fibrosis and vascular narrowing (graft arteriosclerosis), such as bronchiolitis obliterans in lung transplants.",
  body: [["Kidney", "Rejection raises creatinine, lowers urine output and can cause fluid retention and high blood pressure; chronic rejection causes interstitial fibrosis and tubular atrophy."], ["Heart", "Rejection causes heart failure, arrhythmias and, chronically, cardiac allograft vasculopathy, a diffuse narrowing of the coronary arteries."], ["Liver", "Rejection raises liver enzymes and bilirubin; chronic rejection damages bile ducts (vanishing bile duct syndrome)."], ["Lungs", "Acute rejection lowers oxygen and lung function; chronic rejection causes bronchiolitis obliterans syndrome, the main limit to long-term survival."], ["Whole body", "Fever, fatigue and tenderness over the graft can occur, though modern drugs often blunt symptoms."], ["Effects of anti-rejection drugs", "Immune suppression raises the risk of infections such as CMV, BK virus and Pneumocystis, and of skin cancer and lymphoma."]],
  symptoms: "Many rejection episodes cause no symptoms and are found on routine blood tests or biopsies, which is why close monitoring matters. When symptoms occur, they reflect the failing organ: less urine and swelling with a kidney, shortness of breath with a heart or lung, and jaundice with a liver. Fever and pain over the graft come from immune inflammation. Chronic rejection causes slow, steady loss of organ function.",
  dx: "Diagnosis combines organ function tests (creatinine for kidney, liver enzymes and bilirubin for liver, spirometry for lung, echocardiogram for heart), drug levels of anti-rejection medicines, and testing for donor-specific antibodies. A biopsy of the graft is the gold standard and is graded by the Banff classification for kidney and similar systems for other organs; C4d staining supports antibody-mediated rejection. Newer blood tests measure donor-derived cell-free DNA. Infections and drug toxicity are ruled out.",
  tx: "Prevention uses combined immunosuppression. Induction at the time of transplant uses antithymocyte globulin (depletes T cells) or basiliximab (blocks the IL-2 receptor). Maintenance usually combines a calcineurin inhibitor (tacrolimus or cyclosporine), which blocks NFAT-driven IL-2 production, an antimetabolite (mycophenolate, which blocks IMP dehydrogenase in lymphocytes, or azathioprine), and corticosteroids. mTOR inhibitors (sirolimus, everolimus) block IL-2 signaling, and belatacept blocks CD28 costimulation in kidney transplants. Acute cellular rejection is treated with high-dose IV methylprednisolone, and antithymocyte globulin for severe cases. Antibody-mediated rejection is treated with plasmapheresis, IVIG and sometimes rituximab. Prophylaxis against infection, such as trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for Pneumocystis and valganciclovir or letermovir for CMV, is part of care. Taking every dose on schedule is critical, as missed doses are a common cause of rejection.",
  drugs: ["tacrolimus", "cyclosporine", "mycophenolate", "azathioprine", "prednisone", "methylprednisolone", "sirolimus", "everolimus", "belatacept", "basiliximab", "antithymocyte-globulin", "intravenous-immune-globulin", "rituximab", "trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole", "valganciclovir", "letermovir"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Hyperacute is minutes (pre-formed antibodies), acute is weeks to months (T cells or new antibodies), chronic is months to years (fibrosis and vessel narrowing)."], ["Most-tested fact", "Calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, cyclosporine) block NFAT and IL-2 transcription; both are nephrotoxic, and tacrolimus can cause diabetes and tremor."], ["Often confused with", "Graft-versus-host disease, in which donor immune cells (from a bone marrow or liver transplant) attack the recipient's skin, liver and gut."], ["Drug interaction point", "Many drugs that inhibit CYP3A4, such as azole antifungals and macrolides, raise tacrolimus and cyclosporine levels."]],
  red: "Fever, sharply reduced urine, sudden shortness of breath, chest pain, yellowing skin or pain over the transplanted organ in a transplant recipient needs urgent care, and severe symptoms need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Joseph Murray, who performed the first successful kidney transplant, shared the 1990 Nobel Prize, and the donor twin Ronald Herrick lived more than 50 years after giving his kidney.",
  src: ["KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for the Care of Kidney Transplant Recipients (2009)", "Banff Classification of Allograft Pathology", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Abbas, Lichtman and Pillai. Cellular and Molecular Immunology"]
},
"rheumatoid-arthritis": {
  name: "Rheumatoid arthritis",
  sys: "Bones, Joints & Muscles",
  aka: ["RA", "inflammatory arthritis", "rheumatoid disease"],
  what: "Rheumatoid arthritis is a long-term autoimmune disease in which the immune system attacks the lining of the joints. It causes painful, swollen, stiff joints, usually in the hands and feet on both sides of the body, and can damage joints and other organs if untreated.",
  origin: "Signs that may represent rheumatoid arthritis appear in some old paintings and skeletons, but the first clear medical description came in 1800 from the French doctor Augustin Jacob Landre-Beauvais, who called it primary asthenic gout. In 1859 the English physician Alfred Baring Garrod named it rheumatoid arthritis. In 1940 Erik Waaler found rheumatoid factor in patients' blood. In 1948 and 1949 Philip Hench at the Mayo Clinic gave the newly made hormone cortisone to a woman with severe RA, and she walked again within days; Hench, Edward Kendall and Tadeus Reichstein won the 1950 Nobel Prize. Gold salts and later methotrexate, approved for RA in 1988, became mainstays. In 1998 etanercept became the first TNF inhibitor approved for RA, starting the era of biologic drugs. Anti-CCP antibody tests in the 2000s and the 2010 ACR/EULAR criteria made earlier diagnosis possible.",
  timeline: [["1800", "Landre-Beauvais gives the first clear medical description."], ["1859", "Alfred Baring Garrod names the disease rheumatoid arthritis."], ["1949", "Philip Hench shows cortisone dramatically improves RA."], ["1988", "Methotrexate is approved in the US for rheumatoid arthritis."], ["1998", "Etanercept becomes the first TNF inhibitor approved for RA."], ["2010", "ACR and EULAR publish new classification criteria for early RA."]],
  cell: "RA develops in genetically susceptible people, especially those with HLA-DRB1 shared epitope alleles, after environmental triggers such as smoking, which promotes citrullination of proteins in the lungs by peptidyl arginine deiminase (PAD) enzymes. The immune system loses tolerance to citrullinated proteins and makes anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (anti-CCP) antibodies and rheumatoid factor (IgM against the Fc portion of IgG), often years before symptoms. In the joint, CD4 T cells (Th1 and Th17), B cells and macrophages invade the synovium. Macrophages and synovial fibroblasts produce TNF-alpha, IL-1 and IL-6, which drive inflammation through JAK-STAT and NF-kB signaling. The synovium thickens into an invasive pannus that erodes cartilage and bone. Fibroblasts release metalloproteinases that digest cartilage, and RANKL from T cells and fibroblasts activates osteoclasts, causing bony erosions near the joint margins. IL-6 drives the liver to make CRP and hepcidin, causing anemia of chronic disease. Systemic inflammation also accelerates atherosclerosis.",
  body: [["Joints", "Small joints of the hands (MCP and PIP joints, sparing DIP joints), wrists and feet become swollen, warm and tender on both sides, with erosions and deformities such as ulnar deviation, swan neck and boutonniere."], ["Neck", "Inflammation at C1-C2 can cause atlantoaxial instability, a risk during intubation."], ["Lungs", "Interstitial lung disease, pleural effusions and rheumatoid nodules in the lungs can occur, especially in smokers."], ["Heart and vessels", "Chronic inflammation roughly doubles cardiovascular risk and can inflame the pericardium."], ["Skin", "Firm rheumatoid nodules form over pressure points such as the elbows."], ["Blood", "Anemia of chronic disease is common; Felty syndrome combines RA, a large spleen and low neutrophils."], ["Eyes", "Dry eyes (secondary Sjogren syndrome) and scleritis can occur."]],
  symptoms: "Joint pain and swelling come from inflamed, thickened synovium and excess joint fluid. Morning stiffness lasting more than an hour is typical because inflammatory fluid builds up overnight while joints are still. Fatigue and low-grade fever come from cytokines such as IL-6 and TNF. Deformities come from pannus eroding bone and stretching ligaments and tendons. Symptoms are usually symmetric because the process is systemic.",
  dx: "Diagnosis combines the pattern of joint swelling with tests. Anti-CCP antibodies are highly specific; rheumatoid factor is common but less specific. ESR and CRP measure inflammation. X-rays may show joint space narrowing and erosions at the joint margins, while ultrasound or MRI detect earlier synovitis and erosions. The 2010 ACR/EULAR criteria score joint involvement, serology, acute phase reactants and symptom duration.",
  tx: "The goal is early, aggressive control (treat to target) to prevent joint damage. Methotrexate is the anchor disease-modifying drug; it increases adenosine and inhibits enzymes in folate metabolism, calming immune cells, and folic acid reduces side effects. Other conventional DMARDs are hydroxychloroquine, sulfasalazine and leflunomide (blocks pyrimidine synthesis in lymphocytes). When these are not enough, biologics are added: TNF inhibitors (etanercept, adalimumab, infliximab, certolizumab, golimumab), abatacept (blocks T-cell costimulation), rituximab (depletes B cells), and IL-6 receptor blockers (tocilizumab, sarilumab). JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, baricitinib, upadacitinib) block cytokine signaling inside cells. NSAIDs and short courses of glucocorticoids ease symptoms but do not change the course on their own. Screening for TB and hepatitis B before biologics, vaccines, exercise, joint protection and smoking cessation are important.",
  drugs: ["methotrexate", "folic-acid", "hydroxychloroquine", "sulfasalazine", "leflunomide", "etanercept", "adalimumab", "infliximab", "certolizumab-pegol", "golimumab", "abatacept", "rituximab", "tocilizumab", "sarilumab", "anakinra", "tofacitinib", "baricitinib", "upadacitinib", "prednisone", "naproxen", "ibuprofen", "celecoxib", "meloxicam"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "RA likes the knuckles and spares the DIPs; OA loves the DIPs (Heberden nodes)."], ["Most-tested fact", "Anti-CCP is the most specific test. Methotrexate is the first-line DMARD, and TB screening is required before TNF inhibitors."], ["Classic board clue", "A woman with symmetric swelling of the MCP joints and wrists and more than an hour of morning stiffness."], ["Often confused with", "Osteoarthritis (brief morning stiffness, DIP joints, no systemic signs) and lupus arthritis (non-erosive)."]],
  red: "Fever with a red, hot, swollen joint can mean an infected joint, especially on immune-suppressing drugs. New neck pain with arm tingling or weakness, or sudden shortness of breath, also needs urgent care, and severe symptoms need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Philip Hench's first cortisone patient in 1948, a woman with severe RA, was able to walk out of the hospital and go shopping within days, an event that made headlines around the world.",
  src: ["ACR Guideline for the Treatment of Rheumatoid Arthritis (2021)", "2010 ACR/EULAR Rheumatoid Arthritis Classification Criteria", "EULAR Recommendations for the Management of RA (2022 update)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"osteoarthritis": {
  name: "Osteoarthritis",
  sys: "Bones, Joints & Muscles",
  aka: ["OA", "degenerative joint disease", "DJD", "wear-and-tear arthritis"],
  what: "Osteoarthritis is the most common type of arthritis. The smooth cartilage that cushions the ends of bones slowly breaks down, so joints become painful and stiff. It most often affects knees, hips, hands and the spine, and becomes more common with age.",
  origin: "Osteoarthritis is ancient: it has been found in dinosaur bones, Egyptian mummies and prehistoric human skeletons. In 1802 the English physician William Heberden described small hard knobs on the finger joints, now called Heberden nodes, and the French doctor Charles Bouchard later described similar nodes on the middle finger joints. For a long time OA was seen as simple wear and tear. In 1957 Jonas Kellgren and John Lawrence published an X-ray grading system still used today. In 1962 the British surgeon John Charnley developed the low-friction total hip replacement, one of the most successful operations in medicine. Over recent decades, research has shown OA is an active disease of the whole joint, with inflammation and changes in bone, ligaments and synovium, not just thinning cartilage. Exercise and weight loss are now known to be among the most effective treatments.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Osteoarthritis is found in Egyptian mummies and ancient skeletons."], ["1802", "William Heberden describes the finger nodes that bear his name."], ["1957", "Kellgren and Lawrence publish an X-ray grading scale for OA."], ["1962", "John Charnley develops the low-friction total hip replacement."], ["2019", "The ACR and Arthritis Foundation publish an updated OA treatment guideline."]],
  cell: "Articular cartilage is made of chondrocytes embedded in a matrix of type II collagen and aggrecan, a proteoglycan that holds water and resists compression. In OA, mechanical stress (from obesity, injury, joint misalignment), aging and genetics push chondrocytes into a damaging state. They release matrix-degrading enzymes, especially matrix metalloproteinase 13 (MMP-13) and ADAMTS-4 and ADAMTS-5 aggrecanases, along with inflammatory mediators such as IL-1 beta, TNF and prostaglandins. Proteoglycans are lost first, so cartilage softens and fibrillates (cracks), then wears away, exposing bone. Chondrocytes try to repair by clustering and dividing, but cannot keep up. The bone under the cartilage thickens and becomes polished (eburnation), develops cysts, and grows bony spurs (osteophytes) at the joint edges through endochondral ossification. The synovium becomes mildly inflamed from cartilage debris. Pain arises from bone, synovium, joint capsule and ligaments, since cartilage has no nerves, and nervous system sensitization can amplify it. Obesity contributes both by load and by adipokines such as leptin.",
  body: [["Knees", "Cartilage loss, often on the inner side, causes pain with walking and stairs, crepitus, and bow-leg deformity over time."], ["Hips", "Hip OA causes groin pain, limping and loss of internal rotation."], ["Hands", "Bony enlargement of the DIP joints (Heberden nodes) and PIP joints (Bouchard nodes), and base of thumb arthritis, weaken grip."], ["Spine", "Disc and facet joint degeneration with osteophytes can narrow the spinal canal (spinal stenosis) and pinch nerve roots."], ["Muscles", "Pain leads to less use, weakening muscles such as the quadriceps, which worsens joint loading."], ["Mood and sleep", "Chronic pain commonly disrupts sleep and mood and reduces activity."]],
  symptoms: "Pain that worsens with activity and improves with rest happens because loading irritates bone, synovium and capsule. Morning stiffness is brief, usually under 30 minutes, unlike inflammatory arthritis. Crepitus is the grating of rough joint surfaces. Bony swelling comes from osteophytes, and reduced range of motion from spurs and capsule tightening. The joint may feel unstable or lock if loose pieces of cartilage get caught.",
  dx: "OA is usually diagnosed clinically in adults over 45 with activity-related joint pain and short morning stiffness. X-rays show joint space narrowing (often uneven), osteophytes, subchondral sclerosis and cysts. Blood tests are normal, with no raised ESR, CRP or autoantibodies, and are used mainly to rule out other arthritis. Joint fluid, if tested, has few white cells (noninflammatory). MRI is rarely needed.",
  tx: "Treatment reduces pain and keeps people moving. The foundation is non-drug care: exercise, strengthening, physical therapy, weight loss for knee and hip OA, tai chi, walking aids and braces. Topical NSAIDs such as diclofenac gel are recommended for knee and hand OA and have fewer system-wide side effects. Oral NSAIDs (ibuprofen, naproxen, meloxicam, celecoxib) block COX enzymes and prostaglandin formation, with stomach, kidney and heart risks in older adults. Acetaminophen has modest benefit. Duloxetine, an SNRI, strengthens pain-dampening pathways and helps some people. Corticosteroid injections (such as triamcinolone) give short-term relief of flares. Tramadol is conditionally used; other opioids are generally avoided. Topical capsaicin helps some with knee OA. Total joint replacement is effective for severe disease that limits daily life.",
  drugs: ["diclofenac-topical", "ibuprofen", "naproxen", "meloxicam", "celecoxib", "diclofenac", "acetaminophen", "duloxetine", "triamcinolone", "capsaicin-topical", "tramadol"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "OA: Old Age, One side more than the other, Osteophytes. Pain is worse at the end of the day."], ["Most-tested fact", "Heberden nodes are at the DIP joints, Bouchard nodes at the PIP joints. X-ray shows uneven joint space narrowing, osteophytes, sclerosis and cysts."], ["Often confused with", "Rheumatoid arthritis (symmetric MCP and wrist swelling, long morning stiffness, high CRP, anti-CCP) and gout or pseudogout (sudden hot joint, crystals)."]],
  red: "A suddenly hot, red, very swollen joint with fever can mean a joint infection, and new back pain with leg weakness or loss of bladder control can mean nerve compression. Both need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Charnley's hip replacement used a small metal ball in a plastic socket, inspired partly by studying how low-friction materials worked in engineering, and versions of his design are still used today.",
  src: ["2019 ACR/Arthritis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Osteoarthritis of the Hand, Hip, and Knee", "OARSI Guidelines for Non-Surgical Management of Knee, Hip and Polyarticular OA (2019)", "Kellgren JH, Lawrence JS. Ann Rheum Dis 1957", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"ankylosing-spondylitis": {
  name: "Ankylosing spondylitis",
  sys: "Bones, Joints & Muscles",
  aka: ["AS", "axial spondyloarthritis", "axSpA", "radiographic axial spondyloarthritis", "Bechterew disease", "Marie-Strumpell disease"],
  what: "Ankylosing spondylitis is a long-term inflammatory arthritis that mainly affects the spine and the joints where the spine meets the pelvis. It causes back pain and stiffness that improve with movement, and over time some of the spine bones can fuse together.",
  origin: "Spinal fusion consistent with AS has been found in ancient skeletons. In the 1690s the Irish physician Bernard Connor described a skeleton whose spine and pelvis had fused into a single bone. In the 1890s three doctors described the disease in living patients: Vladimir Bekhterev in Russia, Adolf Strumpell in Germany and Pierre Marie in France, which is why it carries their names. For decades it was treated mainly with exercise and anti-inflammatory drugs. In 1973 two research groups, in London and Los Angeles, discovered that most patients carry the gene HLA-B27, one of the strongest links between a gene and a disease. The modified New York criteria (1984) standardized diagnosis using X-rays. In 2003 the first TNF inhibitor was approved in the US for AS, dramatically reducing pain and inflammation. Later, IL-17 blockers and JAK inhibitors were added, and the 2009 ASAS criteria recognized earlier, non-radiographic disease.",
  timeline: [["1690s", "Bernard Connor describes a fused human skeleton."], ["1890s", "Bekhterev, Strumpell and Marie describe the disease in patients."], ["1973", "HLA-B27 is linked to ankylosing spondylitis."], ["1984", "The modified New York criteria for AS are published."], ["2003", "The first TNF inhibitor is approved in the US for ankylosing spondylitis."], ["2009", "ASAS criteria define axial spondyloarthritis, including non-radiographic disease."]],
  cell: "AS is a seronegative spondyloarthritis: rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP are absent. Over 85 to 90 percent of patients of European ancestry carry HLA-B27, though most HLA-B27 carriers never develop AS. Leading theories include HLA-B27 misfolding in the endoplasmic reticulum, which triggers an unfolded protein response and IL-23 release, and HLA-B27 heavy chain dimers that activate immune cells. Variants in ERAP1 and the IL-23 receptor also raise risk, and gut inflammation may play a role. The primary target is the enthesis, where tendons and ligaments attach to bone, and the sacroiliac joints. Resident cells there respond to mechanical stress and IL-23 by producing IL-17 and TNF, which drive inflammation and bone erosion (osteitis). As inflammation settles, new bone forms through Wnt and BMP signaling, creating syndesmophytes that bridge vertebrae. Eventually the spine may fuse (ankylosis), producing a bamboo spine, while the vertebral bodies themselves become osteoporotic and fragile.",
  body: [["Spine and sacroiliac joints", "Inflammation causes chronic low back and buttock pain; over years, bony bridges fuse vertebrae, causing loss of motion and a stooped posture."], ["Entheses", "Tendon attachments, such as the Achilles tendon and plantar fascia, become inflamed and painful (enthesitis)."], ["Eyes", "Acute anterior uveitis causes a painful red eye with light sensitivity in many patients."], ["Chest", "Inflammation and fusion of rib-spine joints restrict chest expansion and breathing."], ["Heart", "Aortitis can cause aortic regurgitation, and conduction blocks may occur."], ["Bones", "Osteoporosis and a rigid spine raise the risk of spinal fractures, even after minor falls."], ["Gut", "Many patients have subclinical bowel inflammation, and some develop inflammatory bowel disease."]],
  symptoms: "Inflammatory back pain usually begins before age 45, develops gradually, wakes people in the second half of the night, and improves with exercise but not rest. This pattern comes from inflammation that builds while still and eases with movement. Morning stiffness lasts more than 30 minutes. Alternating buttock pain reflects sacroiliac inflammation. Heel pain comes from enthesitis, and a painful red eye from uveitis. Fatigue comes from systemic inflammation.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is based on inflammatory back pain plus imaging and tests. X-rays of the pelvis show sacroiliitis (erosions, sclerosis and later fusion); MRI detects earlier bone marrow edema before X-ray changes appear. Spine X-rays may show squaring of vertebrae and syndesmophytes. HLA-B27 testing supports the diagnosis, and CRP and ESR may be raised. Reduced lumbar flexion on the Schober test and reduced chest expansion are exam findings. The ASAS criteria classify axial spondyloarthritis.",
  tx: "NSAIDs are first-line; they block COX enzymes and reduce pain and stiffness, and they help many patients. Regular exercise and physical therapy are essential to keep posture and mobility. If NSAIDs are not enough, TNF inhibitors (adalimumab, etanercept, infliximab, certolizumab, golimumab) or IL-17 inhibitors (secukinumab, ixekizumab, bimekizumab) are used to block the key cytokines; TNF inhibitors are preferred when uveitis or inflammatory bowel disease is present. JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib) are another option. Unlike in RA, conventional DMARDs such as methotrexate do not help spinal disease, though sulfasalazine may help peripheral joints. Long-term systemic steroids are not recommended for axial disease. Uveitis is treated with steroid eye drops. Smoking cessation, fall prevention and bone health care matter.",
  drugs: ["naproxen", "ibuprofen", "celecoxib", "meloxicam", "indomethacin", "diclofenac", "adalimumab", "etanercept", "infliximab", "certolizumab-pegol", "golimumab", "secukinumab", "ixekizumab", "bimekizumab", "tofacitinib", "upadacitinib", "sulfasalazine"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "PAIR for HLA-B27 diseases: Psoriatic arthritis, Ankylosing spondylitis, Inflammatory bowel disease arthritis, Reactive arthritis."], ["Most-tested fact", "A young man with back pain that improves with exercise, worse in the morning, with sacroiliitis on imaging and HLA-B27; bamboo spine on X-ray in late disease."], ["Extra-articular clue", "Anterior uveitis and aortic regurgitation are classic associations."], ["Often confused with", "Mechanical low back pain, which worsens with activity and improves with rest."]],
  red: "Neck or back pain after even a minor fall in someone with a fused spine can mean a spinal fracture and needs emergency care (call 911). A sudden painful red eye with vision changes also needs urgent eye care.",
  fun: "HLA-B27 was one of the first gene-disease associations found in human medicine, yet only a small fraction of people who carry it ever develop ankylosing spondylitis.",
  src: ["ACR/SAA/SPARTAN Recommendations for the Treatment of Ankylosing Spondylitis and Nonradiographic Axial Spondyloarthritis (2019)", "ASAS-EULAR Recommendations for the Management of Axial Spondyloarthritis (2022)", "Brewerton DA et al. Lancet 1973", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"psoriatic-arthritis": {
  name: "Psoriatic arthritis",
  sys: "Bones, Joints & Muscles",
  aka: ["PsA", "psoriatic arthropathy", "spondyloarthritis"],
  what: "Psoriatic arthritis is an inflammatory arthritis that affects some people with the skin disease psoriasis. It causes painful, swollen joints, swollen sausage-like fingers or toes, pain where tendons attach to bone, and changes in the nails.",
  origin: "In 1818 the French dermatologist Jean-Louis Alibert first noted a link between psoriasis and arthritis. In 1860 Pierre Bazin used the term psoriasis arthritique. For many years doctors debated whether it was simply rheumatoid arthritis in people who happened to have psoriasis. In 1973 the British rheumatologists Verna Wright and John Moll defined psoriatic arthritis as a distinct disease, usually negative for rheumatoid factor, and described five clinical patterns. In 2006 the CASPAR criteria gave researchers a reliable way to classify it. Treatment changed greatly in the 2000s: etanercept became the first biologic approved for PsA in the US in 2002. Apremilast (2014) added an oral option, and IL-17 and IL-23 inhibitors arrived from the mid 2010s onward, reflecting discoveries that the IL-23 and IL-17 pathway drives both the skin and joint disease.",
  timeline: [["1818", "Jean-Louis Alibert links psoriasis and arthritis."], ["1973", "Moll and Wright define psoriatic arthritis as a distinct disease."], ["2002", "Etanercept becomes the first biologic approved for PsA in the US."], ["2006", "The CASPAR classification criteria are published."], ["2014", "Apremilast is approved as an oral option for PsA."]],
  cell: "PsA is a seronegative spondyloarthritis that develops in roughly a quarter to a third of people with psoriasis, usually years after skin disease starts. Genetic risk includes HLA-B27 (especially with spine involvement), HLA-C*06 and variants in the IL-23 receptor and IL-12B genes. Mechanical stress at entheses (tendon and ligament attachments) and possibly skin or gut microbes trigger local innate immune responses. Dendritic cells and macrophages release IL-23, which drives Th17 cells, gamma-delta T cells and innate lymphoid cells to make IL-17A, IL-17F and IL-22, along with TNF. These cytokines inflame the enthesis, synovium and nearby bone. Enthesitis spreading into joints and tendon sheaths causes dactylitis. Osteoclasts activated by RANKL erode bone, creating pencil-in-cup deformities, while at the same time new bone forms (periostitis, syndesmophytes), a mix typical of PsA. The nail bed is anatomically connected to the DIP joint enthesis, explaining why nail changes predict DIP arthritis. In the skin, the same cytokines make keratinocytes overgrow, forming plaques.",
  body: [["Joints", "Arthritis can affect a few joints asymmetrically, many joints symmetrically, DIP joints mainly, or, rarely, destroy joints (arthritis mutilans)."], ["Fingers and toes", "Dactylitis makes a whole finger or toe swell like a sausage because tendons, joints and soft tissue inflame together."], ["Entheses", "Inflammation where tendons attach, such as the Achilles tendon and plantar fascia, causes heel pain."], ["Spine", "Some patients develop inflammatory back pain and sacroiliitis, often asymmetric."], ["Nails and skin", "Nail pitting, lifting (onycholysis) and thickening are common; psoriasis plaques usually precede the arthritis."], ["Eyes and heart", "Uveitis can occur, and cardiovascular and metabolic risks are higher due to chronic inflammation."]],
  symptoms: "Joint pain, stiffness and swelling come from cytokine-driven inflammation of the synovium and entheses. Stiffness is worse in the morning or after rest. Sausage digits happen because the entire digit inflames, not just the joint. Heel and foot pain reflects enthesitis. Nail changes come from inflammation of the nail bed linked to the DIP joint. Fatigue reflects systemic inflammation. Skin and joint flares do not always happen together.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical, supported by imaging and labs. The CASPAR criteria include inflammatory joint, spine or enthesis disease plus points for current or past psoriasis (or family history), nail changes, dactylitis, a negative rheumatoid factor and new bone formation near joints on X-ray. X-rays may show pencil-in-cup deformity, erosions with new bone formation, and DIP involvement. Ultrasound and MRI detect enthesitis and synovitis earlier. CRP and ESR may be normal or raised.",
  tx: "Treatment is chosen by which domains are affected: joints, skin, nails, entheses, dactylitis or spine. NSAIDs relieve mild symptoms, and joint steroid injections help single inflamed joints. Methotrexate helps peripheral arthritis and skin, and sulfasalazine or leflunomide are alternatives. Biologics include TNF inhibitors (adalimumab, etanercept, infliximab, certolizumab, golimumab), IL-17 inhibitors (secukinumab, ixekizumab, bimekizumab), IL-12/23 or IL-23 inhibitors (ustekinumab, guselkumab, risankizumab) and abatacept. JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib) and apremilast, a PDE4 inhibitor that lowers inflammatory cytokines, are oral options. IL-23 inhibitors are not effective for spinal disease. Long-term oral steroids are avoided because tapering can flare psoriasis. Exercise, weight management and cardiovascular risk care are important.",
  drugs: ["methotrexate", "adalimumab", "etanercept", "infliximab", "certolizumab-pegol", "golimumab", "secukinumab", "ixekizumab", "bimekizumab", "ustekinumab", "guselkumab", "risankizumab", "abatacept", "tofacitinib", "upadacitinib", "apremilast", "sulfasalazine", "leflunomide", "naproxen", "ibuprofen", "celecoxib", "triamcinolone"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "PsA hits the tips: DIP joints, nail pitting and pencil-in-cup changes on X-ray."], ["Most-tested fact", "Dactylitis (sausage digits), nail pitting, DIP involvement and a pencil-in-cup deformity in a rheumatoid factor-negative patient with psoriasis."], ["Often confused with", "Rheumatoid arthritis (spares DIPs, anti-CCP positive), osteoarthritis with Heberden nodes, and gout."], ["Key association", "PsA belongs to the HLA-B27-linked spondyloarthritis family."]],
  red: "A single hot, red, very swollen joint with fever, especially in someone on biologic medicines, can mean infection and needs urgent care. A sudden painful red eye with vision loss also needs urgent eye care; severe symptoms need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Nail changes appear in most people with psoriatic arthritis, and the nail is literally attached to the same tissue as the end finger joint, which helps explain the link.",
  src: ["GRAPPA Treatment Recommendations for Psoriatic Arthritis (2021)", "ACR/NPF Guideline for the Treatment of Psoriatic Arthritis (2018)", "Taylor W et al. CASPAR criteria. Arthritis Rheum 2006", "Moll JM, Wright V. Semin Arthritis Rheum 1973"]
},
"fibromyalgia": {
  name: "Fibromyalgia",
  sys: "Bones, Joints & Muscles",
  aka: ["fibromyalgia syndrome", "FMS", "fibrositis (historical)", "nociplastic pain"],
  what: "Fibromyalgia is a long-term condition that causes widespread pain throughout the body, along with tiredness, poor sleep and trouble with memory and focus. Joints and muscles are not damaged; instead, the nervous system processes pain signals more strongly than normal.",
  origin: "In 1904 the British neurologist William Gowers coined the term fibrositis, thinking muscle tissue was inflamed. Later studies found no such inflammation. In the 1970s Hugh Smythe and Harvey Moldofsky in Toronto described tender points and showed that disrupting deep sleep in healthy volunteers could produce similar muscle pain and tenderness. The name fibromyalgia came into use in the 1970s and 1980s, and in 1990 the American College of Rheumatology published its first classification criteria, based on widespread pain and tender points. Research in the 1990s and 2000s using brain imaging showed amplified pain processing in the central nervous system. In 2010 the ACR introduced criteria based on widespread pain and symptom severity rather than tender points, revised in 2016. The FDA approved the first drug for fibromyalgia, pregabalin, in 2007, followed by duloxetine (2008) and milnacipran (2009).",
  timeline: [["1904", "William Gowers coins the term fibrositis."], ["1970s", "Smythe and Moldofsky link tender points to disrupted deep sleep."], ["1990", "The ACR publishes the first fibromyalgia classification criteria."], ["2007", "Pregabalin becomes the first FDA-approved drug for fibromyalgia."], ["2010", "New ACR criteria replace the tender point exam with symptom scales."]],
  cell: "Fibromyalgia is considered the classic example of nociplastic pain, meaning pain from altered nervous system processing without clear tissue or nerve damage. In central sensitization, neurons in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord and pain-processing brain regions become hyperexcitable. Levels of pronociceptive neurotransmitters, such as substance P (raised in cerebrospinal fluid) and glutamate (raised in the insula on imaging), increase. Descending inhibitory pathways from the brainstem, which use serotonin and norepinephrine to dampen pain, are weakened. Functional MRI studies show that people with fibromyalgia activate pain regions with smaller stimuli. Endogenous opioid systems appear already highly active, which may explain why opioids work poorly. Disrupted slow-wave sleep and changes in the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis and autonomic nervous system add to pain and fatigue. Some patients show small fiber neuropathy on skin biopsy. Genetic factors affecting serotonin, catecholamine and dopamine pathways, along with physical or emotional trauma and infections, likely contribute.",
  body: [["Nervous system", "Pain signals are amplified, so normal touch can feel painful (allodynia) and painful stimuli feel more intense (hyperalgesia)."], ["Muscles and soft tissue", "Widespread aching and tenderness occur without inflammation or damage on tests."], ["Sleep", "Sleep is unrefreshing, with disrupted deep sleep, which worsens pain and fatigue."], ["Brain and mood", "Trouble with memory and concentration (fibro fog) is common, and anxiety and depression often occur alongside it."], ["Gut and bladder", "Irritable bowel syndrome and painful bladder syndrome often coexist, reflecting shared sensitization."], ["Head", "Tension-type headaches, migraines and jaw pain (TMJ disorder) are common."]],
  symptoms: "Widespread pain on both sides of the body, above and below the waist, comes from the nervous system turning up the volume on pain signals. Fatigue and waking unrefreshed come from disrupted deep sleep. Fibro fog reflects altered brain network activity and poor sleep. Sensitivity to light, noise and smells suggests broad sensory amplification. Symptoms often worsen with stress, poor sleep, weather changes and overexertion.",
  dx: "There is no blood test or scan for fibromyalgia. The 2016 ACR criteria use a widespread pain index and a symptom severity scale (fatigue, waking unrefreshed, cognitive symptoms and other symptoms), with symptoms present for at least 3 months. Blood tests such as a complete blood count, ESR or CRP and thyroid tests are often done to look for other conditions, such as hypothyroidism, polymyalgia rheumatica or inflammatory arthritis. Fibromyalgia can coexist with other conditions.",
  tx: "Treatment combines education, activity and medicines, with non-drug care first. EULAR recommends graded aerobic exercise most strongly, since it improves pain and function. Cognitive behavioral therapy, sleep hygiene, tai chi, yoga and stress management also help. Medicines that calm pain signaling include duloxetine and milnacipran (SNRIs that strengthen descending inhibitory pathways), pregabalin (binds the alpha-2-delta subunit of calcium channels to reduce release of excitatory neurotransmitters), and low-dose amitriptyline or cyclobenzaprine at bedtime to improve sleep. Gabapentin is sometimes used. NSAIDs and opioids generally work poorly because the pain is not inflammatory or tissue-based; opioids are not recommended. Treating coexisting depression, anxiety and sleep problems is important.",
  drugs: ["duloxetine", "milnacipran", "pregabalin", "amitriptyline", "cyclobenzaprine", "gabapentin", "nortriptyline"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Fibromyalgia is a volume knob problem, not a tissue problem: central sensitization turns pain up."], ["Most-tested fact", "Normal labs and exam aside from tenderness; the FDA-approved drugs are pregabalin, duloxetine and milnacipran, and exercise is the most effective treatment."], ["Often confused with", "Polymyalgia rheumatica (older adults, raised ESR, rapid steroid response), hypothyroidism and inflammatory myopathy (true weakness, high CK)."]],
  red: "Chest pain, new weakness, fever with severe pain, or thoughts of suicide need urgent help; the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline (call or text 988) is available any time, and an emergency needs 911.",
  fun: "Some of the earliest sleep studies on fibromyalgia found that disturbing healthy people's deep sleep for a few nights produced muscle tenderness similar to fibromyalgia.",
  src: ["EULAR Revised Recommendations for the Management of Fibromyalgia (2016)", "Wolfe F et al. 2016 Revisions to the 2010/2011 Fibromyalgia Diagnostic Criteria", "Clauw DJ. Fibromyalgia: a clinical review. JAMA 2014", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"low-back-pain": {
  name: "Low back pain",
  sys: "Bones, Joints & Muscles",
  aka: ["lumbago", "lumbar strain", "sciatica", "lumbar radiculopathy", "herniated disc", "mechanical back pain"],
  what: "Low back pain is pain between the bottom of the ribs and the buttocks. It is one of the most common reasons people see a doctor. Most cases come from muscles, ligaments, discs or joints, and most improve within weeks.",
  origin: "Back pain is one of the oldest recorded health problems. The Edwin Smith Papyrus from ancient Egypt describes examining a patient with a sprained back by having them stretch their legs. In 1880 the Serbian physician Laza Lazarevic described the straight leg raise test, often credited to the French physician Charles Lasegue, whose student published it in 1881. For a long time sciatica was blamed on nerve inflammation. In 1934 William Mixter and Joseph Barr in Boston showed that a ruptured intervertebral disc pressing on a nerve root could cause sciatica, and that surgery could relieve it; this led to many disc operations. Later research showed that most back pain improves without surgery and that bed rest slows recovery. Modern guidelines, such as the American College of Physicians guideline of 2017, recommend staying active and using non-drug treatments first.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "The Edwin Smith Papyrus describes an exam for a sprained back."], ["1880", "Laza Lazarevic describes the straight leg raise test."], ["1934", "Mixter and Barr show that a herniated disc can cause sciatica."], ["2017", "The American College of Physicians recommends non-drug treatments first for back pain."]],
  cell: "Most low back pain is nonspecific or mechanical, meaning no single structure can be blamed. Sources include muscles and ligaments, facet joints, the sacroiliac joints and intervertebral discs. A disc has a tough outer annulus fibrosus and a gel-like nucleus pulposus rich in proteoglycans and water. With age and load, the nucleus loses proteoglycans and water, so the disc flattens and the annulus develops tears. Nerve fibers can grow into torn annulus, causing discogenic pain. If the nucleus pushes through the annulus (herniation), usually posterolaterally at L4-L5 or L5-S1, it can compress a nerve root and also release inflammatory chemicals such as TNF-alpha, IL-1 and phospholipase A2, which sensitize the nerve and cause radicular pain (sciatica). Over time, degeneration leads to facet arthritis, osteophytes and thickened ligaments that narrow the spinal canal (lumbar stenosis). Chronic back pain often involves central sensitization and psychological factors, such as fear of movement, which maintain pain after tissue heals. Less common specific causes include fractures, infection, cancer, cauda equina syndrome and inflammatory spondyloarthritis.",
  body: [["Muscles and ligaments", "Strains and sprains cause local pain and spasm, often after lifting or twisting, and usually improve within weeks."], ["Discs", "Degenerated or herniated discs can cause back pain, and herniation can cause radiating leg pain."], ["Nerve roots", "Compression causes sciatica: shooting leg pain, numbness, tingling or weakness in the pattern of the affected root."], ["Spinal canal", "Stenosis causes leg pain and heaviness when standing or walking that eases with sitting or bending forward (neurogenic claudication)."], ["Cauda equina", "Severe compression of the nerve bundle at the bottom of the spinal canal causes saddle numbness, bladder or bowel dysfunction and leg weakness."], ["Mood and function", "Chronic pain can lead to poor sleep, depression, reduced activity and work disability."]],
  symptoms: "Aching pain and muscle spasm come from strained tissues and protective muscle tightening. Pain worse with bending, sitting or lifting points to mechanical load on discs and joints. Sharp pain running below the knee comes from an irritated nerve root, since inflamed nerve tissue sends signals along the whole nerve path. Numbness and weakness reflect compression of sensory and motor fibers. Morning stiffness that improves with exercise suggests inflammatory back pain instead.",
  dx: "Most cases are diagnosed by history and exam, with attention to red flags. The straight leg raise test suggests nerve root irritation, and strength, reflex and sensation testing localizes the root (for example, L5 affects big toe extension and S1 affects ankle reflex). Imaging is not needed for most acute back pain, since disc changes are common in people without pain. MRI is used for red flags such as suspected cauda equina, infection, cancer or progressive neurologic deficits, or before surgery or injections. Blood tests such as ESR and CRP help when infection or cancer is suspected.",
  tx: "For acute and subacute pain, guidelines recommend staying active and using non-drug treatments first: superficial heat, massage, acupuncture or spinal manipulation. Bed rest is discouraged. If medicines are needed, NSAIDs (ibuprofen, naproxen) block COX enzymes and prostaglandin formation, and skeletal muscle relaxants (cyclobenzaprine, methocarbamol, tizanidine, metaxalone) act in the brainstem or spinal cord to reduce spasm but cause drowsiness. Acetaminophen shows little benefit for back pain. For chronic pain, exercise therapy, physical therapy, cognitive behavioral therapy, yoga, tai chi and mindfulness are recommended; duloxetine or tramadol may be considered, and opioids are a last option because of limited benefit and risks. Systemic steroids do not clearly help. Epidural steroid injections may give short-term relief of sciatica, and surgery is considered for persistent radiculopathy, stenosis or cauda equina syndrome.",
  drugs: ["ibuprofen", "naproxen", "celecoxib", "meloxicam", "diclofenac", "cyclobenzaprine", "methocarbamol", "tizanidine", "metaxalone", "duloxetine", "tramadol", "acetaminophen", "lidocaine-patch", "gabapentin", "pregabalin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Red flags TUNA FISH: Trauma, Unexplained weight loss, Neurologic deficits, Age over 50, Fever, IV drug use, Steroid use, History of cancer."], ["Most-tested fact", "Most acute low back pain resolves within weeks; routine imaging is not recommended without red flags, and staying active beats bed rest."], ["Emergency clue", "Saddle anesthesia, urinary retention and leg weakness suggest cauda equina syndrome and need emergency MRI and surgery."], ["Often confused with", "Kidney stones, pyelonephritis, abdominal aortic aneurysm and pancreatitis can all cause pain felt in the back."]],
  red: "Numbness in the groin or inner thighs, new trouble controlling bladder or bowels, rapidly worsening leg weakness, back pain with fever, or sudden severe back or belly pain in an older adult needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Many people with no back pain at all have bulging or degenerated discs on MRI, which is one reason scans often do not explain who hurts and who does not.",
  src: ["ACP Clinical Practice Guideline: Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain (2017)", "NICE Guideline NG59: Low back pain and sciatica (2016, updated)", "Mixter WJ, Barr JS. N Engl J Med 1934", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"polymyalgia-rheumatica": {
  name: "Polymyalgia rheumatica and giant cell arteritis",
  sys: "Bones, Joints & Muscles",
  aka: ["PMR", "GCA", "temporal arteritis", "cranial arteritis", "Horton disease"],
  what: "Polymyalgia rheumatica (PMR) is an inflammatory condition of older adults that causes pain and stiffness in the shoulders and hips. Giant cell arteritis (GCA) is a related inflammation of large arteries, often in the head, that can cause sudden blindness. The two often occur together.",
  origin: "In 1888 the Scottish physician William Bruce described older patients with aching shoulders and hips, which he called senile rheumatic gout. In 1890 Jonathan Hutchinson in London described an elderly man with red, swollen, painful arteries on his temples that kept him from wearing his hat. In 1932 Bayard Horton and colleagues at the Mayo Clinic described the granulomatous inflammation of the temporal artery under the microscope, and the disease was sometimes called Horton disease. In 1957 Stuart Barber in England gave PMR its modern name. In the early 1950s doctors found that corticosteroids rapidly relieved both conditions and prevented vision loss in GCA, and steroids remain the mainstay. More recently, IL-6 blockers changed care: tocilizumab was approved for GCA in 2017 after the GiACTA trial, sarilumab for PMR in 2023, and the JAK inhibitor upadacitinib for GCA in 2025.",
  timeline: [["1888", "William Bruce describes senile rheumatic gout, an early account of PMR."], ["1890", "Jonathan Hutchinson describes painful inflamed temporal arteries."], ["1932", "Horton and colleagues describe the pathology of temporal arteritis."], ["1957", "Stuart Barber names polymyalgia rheumatica."], ["2017", "Tocilizumab becomes the first drug approved in the US for GCA."], ["2023", "Sarilumab is approved in the US for PMR."]],
  cell: "Both conditions mostly affect people over 50, peaking in their 70s, and are more common in people of Northern European ancestry and with HLA-DRB1*04. In GCA, dendritic cells in the adventitia (outer layer) of large arteries become activated, possibly by an unknown trigger, through Toll-like receptors. They recruit CD4 T cells, which differentiate into Th1 cells (making interferon-gamma) and Th17 cells (making IL-17). Interferon-gamma drives macrophages to form granulomas with multinucleated giant cells, which release metalloproteinases and reactive oxygen species that fragment the internal elastic lamina. Platelet-derived and vascular endothelial growth factors cause the intima to thicken, narrowing the lumen and causing ischemia. Branches of the external carotid (temporal artery) and ophthalmic artery are commonly affected, as are the aorta and its branches. IL-6 drives the systemic inflammatory response, high CRP and ESR, fever and anemia. In PMR, similar innate immune activation and IL-6 produce inflammation of bursae and tendon sheaths around the shoulders and hips (bursitis, tenosynovitis) rather than the arteries. About 15 to 20 percent of PMR patients have GCA, and about half of GCA patients have PMR symptoms.",
  body: [["Shoulders and hips (PMR)", "Inflamed bursae and tendon sheaths cause aching and severe morning stiffness, making it hard to get out of bed, comb hair or rise from a chair."], ["Temporal arteries (GCA)", "Inflamed scalp arteries cause a new headache and scalp tenderness; the artery may feel hard, tender or pulseless."], ["Eyes (GCA)", "Narrowing of the arteries that feed the optic nerve causes sudden, painless vision loss (arteritic ischemic optic neuropathy), which can be permanent and can affect both eyes."], ["Jaw and tongue (GCA)", "Reduced blood flow to chewing muscles causes jaw pain with chewing (jaw claudication), a strong clue to GCA."], ["Aorta and large arteries (GCA)", "Inflammation can cause aortic aneurysm or dissection years later, and narrowed arm arteries cause arm claudication and unequal blood pressures."], ["Whole body", "IL-6 drives fever, fatigue, weight loss, low mood and anemia in both conditions."]],
  symptoms: "Morning stiffness and aching in the shoulders and hips in PMR come from inflammation in bursae and tendon sheaths, which worsens after inactivity. Muscle strength is normal; pain limits movement. In GCA, headache and scalp tenderness come from inflamed temporal arteries, and jaw claudication from poor blood flow to chewing muscles. Vision symptoms come from ischemia of the optic nerve. Fever, weight loss and fatigue come from IL-6 and other cytokines.",
  dx: "PMR is diagnosed clinically in adults over 50 with bilateral shoulder and hip girdle pain and stiffness and a raised ESR or CRP, after ruling out other causes; ultrasound may show bursitis, and a rapid response to low-dose steroids supports it. GCA is suspected with a new headache, jaw claudication, vision changes or scalp tenderness and very high ESR or CRP. Temporal artery biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation with giant cells and fragmentation of the internal elastic lamina, though skip lesions can cause false negatives. Temporal artery ultrasound (halo sign), and CT, MRI or PET of large vessels, also help.",
  tx: "Glucocorticoids are the mainstay for both because they rapidly suppress cytokine production. PMR responds to relatively low doses of prednisone with a slow taper over a year or more. In GCA, high-dose steroids, sometimes IV methylprednisolone if vision is threatened, are started right away, even before biopsy results, to prevent blindness. Because long steroid courses cause bone loss, diabetes, weight gain and infections, steroid-sparing drugs are used. Tocilizumab blocks the IL-6 receptor and reduces relapses in GCA, sarilumab is approved for PMR when steroids cannot be tapered, and upadacitinib (a JAK1 inhibitor) is approved for GCA. Methotrexate is a steroid-sparing option for both. Bone protection with calcium, vitamin D and bisphosphonates, and monitoring for aortic aneurysm in GCA are part of long-term care.",
  drugs: ["prednisone", "methylprednisolone", "tocilizumab", "sarilumab", "upadacitinib", "methotrexate", "alendronate", "calcium-carbonate", "cholecalciferol"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "PMR: Pain in shoulders and hips, Morning stiffness, Rapid response to steroids. GCA: Giant cells, Can blind, Always treat at once."], ["Most-tested fact", "In suspected GCA with vision symptoms, high-dose steroids are started immediately; biopsy results stay valid for days to weeks after starting."], ["Classic board clue", "An adult over 50 with new headache, jaw claudication and a very high ESR; the same patient may have shoulder and hip stiffness."], ["Often confused with", "Polymyositis (true muscle weakness with high CK) and rheumatoid arthritis in older adults."]],
  red: "Sudden vision loss, double vision or a dark curtain over one eye, a new severe headache with jaw pain on chewing, or stroke-like symptoms in someone over 50 needs emergency care (call 911), since GCA can cause permanent blindness within hours to days.",
  fun: "Jonathan Hutchinson's 1890 patient could not wear his hat because his inflamed temple arteries were so tender, one of the most memorable early clues in the history of medicine.",
  src: ["2021 ACR/Vasculitis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Giant Cell Arteritis and Takayasu Arteritis", "2015 EULAR/ACR Recommendations for the Management of Polymyalgia Rheumatica", "Stone JH et al. GiACTA. N Engl J Med 2017", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
}
});

;
DB.DIS = DB.DIS || {};
Object.assign(DB.DIS, {
"muscular-dystrophy": {
  name: "Duchenne muscular dystrophy",
  sys: "Bones, Joints & Muscles",
  aka: ["DMD", "Duchenne", "pseudohypertrophic muscular dystrophy"],
  what: "Duchenne muscular dystrophy is an inherited disease, mostly in boys, in which muscles slowly break down because they lack a protein called dystrophin. Weakness starts in early childhood and later affects the heart and breathing muscles.",
  origin: "English physician Edward Meryon described boys with wasting muscles in the early 1850s, and French neurologist Guillaume-Benjamin Duchenne gave a detailed account in the 1860s, using early photographs and muscle biopsies taken with a needle tool he designed. In 1879 William Gowers described the classic way affected boys push up on their own legs to stand, now called the Gowers sign. The cause stayed hidden until 1986, when Louis Kunkel's team in Boston found the gene on the X chromosome, and in 1987 the missing protein was named dystrophin. Steroids became standard care in the 1990s and 2000s after trials showed they slow the loss of strength. Since 2016 the FDA has approved exon-skipping drugs for certain mutations, newer steroid-like drugs, a histone deacetylase inhibitor and a gene therapy.",
  timeline: [["1860s", "Guillaume-Benjamin Duchenne publishes detailed descriptions of the disease."], ["1879", "William Gowers describes the Gowers sign."], ["1986", "Louis Kunkel's team identifies the DMD gene on the X chromosome."], ["1987", "The missing protein is identified and named dystrophin."], ["2016", "Eteplirsen becomes the first exon-skipping drug approved in the US."], ["2023", "The FDA grants accelerated approval to the first gene therapy for Duchenne."]],
  cell: "The DMD gene on the X chromosome is one of the largest human genes and codes for dystrophin. Dystrophin sits under the muscle cell membrane (sarcolemma) and links the actin cytoskeleton to the dystroglycan complex, which binds laminin in the extracellular matrix. It works like a shock absorber during contraction. In Duchenne, most mutations are deletions that shift the reading frame, so almost no dystrophin is made. Without it, the membrane tears with each contraction. Calcium floods in, calcium-activated proteases (calpains) digest the cell, and fibers die. Satellite cells repair the damage at first, but over years regeneration fails, and muscle is replaced by fat and scar tissue. Becker muscular dystrophy comes from in-frame mutations that leave a shorter, partly working dystrophin, so it is milder. Inheritance is X-linked recessive, and about one third of cases arise from a new mutation.",
  body: [["Skeletal muscle", "Hip and thigh muscles weaken first, then shoulders and arms. Most boys lose the ability to walk in the early teens without modern care, and joint contractures and scoliosis follow."], ["Calves", "Calf muscles often look large because dead muscle is replaced by fat and connective tissue (pseudohypertrophy), even though they are weak."], ["Heart", "Dystrophin is also needed in heart muscle, so dilated cardiomyopathy and rhythm problems develop, usually in the teen years, and are a leading cause of death."], ["Lungs", "Weak diaphragm and chest muscles cause a weak cough, poor sleep breathing and later respiratory failure that may need ventilation support."], ["Bones", "Low activity and long-term steroid use thin the bones, raising the risk of fractures, including spine fractures."], ["Brain", "Dystrophin forms are found in the brain, and some children have learning differences, attention problems or autism traits."]],
  symptoms: "Boys often walk late, fall often, waddle and walk on their toes because the hip muscles cannot hold the pelvis steady. To stand from the floor they push on their thighs with their hands (Gowers sign) because the hip extensors are weak. Large calves come from fat and scar replacing muscle. Fatigue, breathlessness and swelling in later years reflect heart and breathing muscle involvement.",
  dx: "The creatine kinase (CK) level in blood is extremely high, often many times normal, because the enzyme leaks from damaged muscle. Genetic testing confirms the diagnosis, starting with tests for deletions and duplications and then gene sequencing. Muscle biopsy, now used less often, shows absent dystrophin staining. Heart MRI or echocardiogram, lung function tests and bone checks track complications. Newborn screening programs for DMD are being added in some US states.",
  tx: "Glucocorticoids such as prednisone, deflazacort and vamorolone reduce muscle inflammation and slow loss of strength. Exon-skipping antisense drugs (eteplirsen for exon 51, golodirsen and viltolarsen for exon 53, casimersen for exon 45) help the cell skip a faulty piece of the gene so a shorter dystrophin can be made. Delandistrogene moxeparvovec is a one-time gene therapy that delivers a shortened micro-dystrophin gene. Givinostat blocks histone deacetylases to reduce inflammation and scarring. Heart care uses ACE inhibitors or ARBs, beta blockers and mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists. Physical therapy, bracing, breathing support, spine surgery and bone health care are key. Succinylcholine and volatile anesthetics are avoided because they can cause dangerous muscle breakdown and high potassium.",
  drugs: ["prednisone", "deflazacort", "vamorolone", "givinostat", "eteplirsen", "golodirsen", "viltolarsen", "casimersen", "delandistrogene-moxeparvovec", "lisinopril", "losartan", "metoprolol", "carvedilol", "eplerenone", "spironolactone", "cholecalciferol", "zoledronic-acid"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Duchenne = Deleted reading frame (no dystrophin). Becker = Better (in-frame, some dystrophin)."], ["Most-tested fact", "X-linked recessive frameshift deletion in the dystrophin gene, Gowers sign, calf pseudohypertrophy and very high CK."], ["Often confused with", "Becker muscular dystrophy, which starts later and progresses more slowly, and spinal muscular atrophy, which is a motor neuron disease with normal or mildly raised CK."]],
  red: "Sudden trouble breathing, a weak cough with a chest infection, fainting, chest pain or a very fast or irregular heartbeat in someone with Duchenne needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The DMD gene spans about 2.2 million DNA base pairs, making it one of the largest genes known in humans, which is one reason new mutations are common.",
  src: ["Birnkrant DJ et al. DMD Care Considerations. Lancet Neurol 2018", "Hoffman EP, Brown RH, Kunkel LM. Cell 1987", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"malignant-hyperthermia": {
  name: "Malignant hyperthermia",
  sys: "Bones, Joints & Muscles",
  aka: ["MH", "malignant hyperpyrexia", "MH susceptibility"],
  what: "Malignant hyperthermia is a rare, inherited, life-threatening reaction to certain anesthesia gases and the muscle relaxant succinylcholine. Muscles go into a runaway state that burns energy, makes heat and acid, and can quickly become fatal without treatment.",
  origin: "In 1960 Michael Denborough and George Lovell in Melbourne, Australia, reported a young man who was terrified of anesthesia because many relatives had died during it. He survived, and their letter in The Lancet made doctors recognize an inherited anesthesia reaction. In the 1960s and 1970s similar reactions were found in pigs (porcine stress syndrome), which gave researchers a model. In 1975 South African anesthesiologist Gaisford Harrison showed that dantrolene reversed the reaction in pigs, and IV dantrolene was approved in the US in 1979. Death rates, once around 70 to 80 percent, fell dramatically with dantrolene and better monitoring. In 1991 mutations in the ryanodine receptor gene (RYR1) were linked to MH. The Malignant Hyperthermia Association of the United States (MHAUS) runs a 24-hour hotline for clinicians.",
  timeline: [["1960", "Denborough and Lovell describe a family with deaths during anesthesia."], ["1975", "Gaisford Harrison shows dantrolene reverses MH in pigs."], ["1979", "IV dantrolene is approved in the US for MH."], ["1991", "RYR1 mutations are linked to MH susceptibility."], ["2014", "A concentrated dantrolene formulation that is faster to prepare is approved."]],
  cell: "In skeletal muscle, an electrical signal reaches the dihydropyridine receptor (a voltage sensor, CACNA1S), which opens the ryanodine receptor RyR1 on the sarcoplasmic reticulum to release calcium for contraction. In MH-susceptible people, most often with an autosomal dominant RYR1 mutation, volatile anesthetics (such as sevoflurane, desflurane and isoflurane) or succinylcholine make RyR1 stay open. Calcium pours into the cytoplasm uncontrolled. The muscle contracts continuously, and pumps such as SERCA burn huge amounts of ATP trying to clear calcium. This hypermetabolism produces carbon dioxide, heat and lactic acid and uses up oxygen. As ATP runs out, muscle cells break down (rhabdomyolysis), releasing potassium, myoglobin and creatine kinase. Dantrolene works by binding RyR1 and stopping the calcium leak.",
  body: [["Skeletal muscle", "Sustained contraction causes rigidity, often first seen as jaw stiffness (masseter spasm), then muscle breakdown with very high CK and dark urine."], ["Lungs and gas exchange", "Hypermetabolism produces carbon dioxide faster than it can be breathed off, so end-tidal CO2 rises sharply even with increased ventilation."], ["Heart", "High potassium, acidosis and catecholamines cause a fast heart rate and dangerous arrhythmias, including cardiac arrest."], ["Body temperature", "Heat from runaway muscle work raises temperature, often rapidly, though fever can be a later sign."], ["Kidneys", "Myoglobin from broken-down muscle can block and injure the kidney tubules, causing acute kidney injury."], ["Blood", "Severe cases can trigger disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), with both clotting and bleeding."]],
  symptoms: "The earliest clue is usually an unexplained rise in end-tidal carbon dioxide, because muscles are producing CO2 faster than normal. A fast heart rate follows from acidosis and sympathetic activation. Muscle rigidity and jaw stiffness come from continuous contraction. Rising temperature, sweating and mottled skin reflect heat production. Dark cola-colored urine signals myoglobin from muscle breakdown.",
  dx: "During an episode, diagnosis is clinical, based on rising end-tidal CO2, tachycardia, rigidity and rising temperature after a trigger. Blood gases show combined respiratory and metabolic acidosis. Labs show high potassium, very high CK and myoglobin in the urine. Afterward, susceptibility can be tested with a caffeine-halothane contracture test on a fresh muscle biopsy, or with genetic testing for RYR1 and CACNA1S variants, though a negative gene test does not rule it out.",
  tx: "The anesthesia team stops all trigger agents immediately and hyperventilates with high-flow oxygen. Dantrolene is the antidote: it binds RyR1 and blocks calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, stopping the hypermetabolic state. Cooling with cold IV fluids, ice packs and other methods treats high temperature. High potassium is treated with calcium to protect the heart, insulin with dextrose to shift potassium into cells and sodium bicarbonate for acidosis. Fluids and diuretics maintain urine output to protect the kidneys from myoglobin. Calcium channel blockers are avoided with dantrolene because the combination can cause dangerous high potassium and heart depression. Patients are monitored in an ICU because the reaction can recur. Future anesthetics use non-triggering agents such as propofol and nondepolarizing muscle relaxants.",
  drugs: ["dantrolene", "sodium-bicarbonate", "calcium-chloride", "calcium-gluconate", "regular-insulin", "dextrose", "normal-saline", "furosemide", "amiodarone", "propofol", "rocuronium"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Earliest sign is a rise in end-tidal CO2; treatment is dantrolene, which blocks the RyR1 calcium channel."], ["Memory trick", "Triggers are the gases and succinylcholine (S for sux, S for sevoflurane). Propofol and rocuronium are safe."], ["Often confused with", "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, which follows dopamine-blocking drugs and develops over days, and serotonin syndrome, which causes clonus and hyperreflexia."], ["NCLEX focus", "Know where the MH cart and dantrolene are stored, and that MH can start in the recovery unit, not only in the operating room."]],
  red: "High fever, muscle rigidity, a racing heart, fast breathing or dark urine during or soon after anesthesia is an emergency and needs immediate care (call 911 if outside a hospital).",
  fun: "Pigs with the same type of ryanodine receptor mutation can have an MH-like crisis from stress alone, which helped researchers study the disease.",
  src: ["Malignant Hyperthermia Association of the United States (MHAUS) guidance", "Denborough MA, Lovell RRH. Lancet 1960", "Harrison GG. Br J Anaesth 1975", "Miller's Anesthesia"]
},
"compartment-syndrome": {
  name: "Compartment syndrome",
  sys: "Bones, Joints & Muscles",
  aka: ["acute compartment syndrome", "ACS", "Volkmann ischemic contracture (late result)", "chronic exertional compartment syndrome"],
  what: "Compartment syndrome happens when pressure builds inside a closed muscle compartment, usually in the leg or forearm, until blood can no longer flow in. Without fast surgery, muscles and nerves can die within hours.",
  origin: "In 1881 German surgeon Richard von Volkmann described stiff, clawed hands that developed after tight bandages on forearm injuries, and he blamed lack of blood flow. This crippling result is still called Volkmann ischemic contracture. In the early 1900s surgeons began to suspect that pressure inside the muscle compartment was the real problem, and cutting the tough fascia (fasciotomy) to relieve it became the treatment. In the 1970s researchers such as Thomas Whitesides developed simple needle methods to measure compartment pressure, and in the 1990s surgeons in Edinburgh popularized the delta pressure, comparing diastolic blood pressure with compartment pressure, to decide when fasciotomy is needed.",
  timeline: [["1881", "Richard von Volkmann describes ischemic contracture after tight bandaging."], ["1975", "Whitesides and colleagues describe a needle method to measure compartment pressure."], ["1996", "Edinburgh researchers report the delta pressure threshold for fasciotomy."]],
  cell: "Muscles are wrapped in fascia that does not stretch. After a fracture, crush injury, burn, reperfusion of an ischemic limb or a tight cast, bleeding or swelling increases the volume inside the compartment. Pressure rises and first collapses the thin-walled veins, so blood cannot leave. Capillary pressure then rises, more fluid leaks out, and the arteriovenous pressure gradient falls until capillary blood flow stops. Muscle and nerve cells become hypoxic, switch to anaerobic metabolism and lose ATP. Sodium-potassium pumps fail, cells swell, and membrane damage releases more fluid, making a vicious cycle. Nerves lose function within an hour or so and muscle starts dying after about 4 to 6 hours. When flow returns, oxygen free radicals and neutrophils add reperfusion injury. Dead muscle releases potassium, myoglobin and CK. Importantly, large arteries can keep a pulse until very late.",
  body: [["Muscle", "Ischemic muscle dies and is replaced by scar, which shortens and stiffens, causing contractures such as Volkmann claw hand."], ["Nerves", "Nerves passing through the compartment lose blood supply, causing numbness, tingling and weakness that can become permanent, such as foot drop after anterior leg compartment syndrome."], ["Kidneys", "Myoglobin from dead muscle can cause acute kidney injury (rhabdomyolysis)."], ["Heart", "Potassium released from dead muscle, especially after pressure is relieved, can cause dangerous arrhythmias."], ["Limb", "Without treatment the limb may become infected or nonfunctional, sometimes requiring amputation."]],
  symptoms: "Pain out of proportion to the injury is the earliest sign, because ischemic muscle is extremely painful. Pain with passive stretching of the muscles is a classic sign, since stretching squeezes the swollen ischemic tissue. The compartment feels tense and firm. Tingling and numbness follow as nerves become ischemic. Paralysis, pallor and loss of pulse are late findings, which is why a normal pulse does not rule it out.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is mainly clinical, based on pain out of proportion, pain with passive stretch and a tense compartment. When the exam is unreliable, such as in a sedated patient, compartment pressure is measured with a needle device. A difference between diastolic blood pressure and compartment pressure (delta pressure) of 30 mmHg or less supports the diagnosis. CK, potassium, kidney function and urine myoglobin check for muscle breakdown.",
  tx: "Acute compartment syndrome is a surgical emergency. Fasciotomy, cutting the fascia along the compartment, relieves pressure and restores blood flow. The wounds are left open and closed or skin grafted later. Before surgery, tight casts, splints and dressings are loosened or removed, and the limb is kept at heart level, because raising it lowers arterial inflow. Supplemental oxygen and correcting low blood pressure help perfusion. Opioid pain relief is given, while watching that rising need for pain medicine may itself be a warning sign. IV fluids protect the kidneys from myoglobin, and potassium is monitored. In burns, escharotomy releases the stiff burned skin. Chronic exertional compartment syndrome in athletes is managed with activity changes or elective fasciotomy.",
  drugs: ["morphine", "fentanyl", "hydromorphone", "normal-saline", "lactated-ringers", "calcium-gluconate", "sodium-bicarbonate", "cefazolin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Earliest sign is pain out of proportion and pain with passive stretch; loss of pulse is a late sign."], ["Memory trick", "The 6 Ps: pain, pressure, paresthesia, paralysis, pallor, pulselessness. Pain comes first, pulselessness comes last."], ["Classic setting", "Tibial shaft fracture is the most common cause; supracondylar elbow fractures in children can cause forearm compartment syndrome."], ["NCLEX focus", "Do not elevate the limb above the heart; keep it at heart level and loosen tight dressings."]],
  red: "Severe, worsening pain after an injury or cast, especially pain that is not eased by pain medicine, with a tight swollen limb, numbness or weakness, needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "A pulse can still be felt in a limb with severe compartment syndrome because compartment pressure is usually far below arterial pressure, even when capillary flow has stopped.",
  src: ["Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults", "McQueen MM, Court-Brown CM. J Bone Joint Surg Br 1996", "von Volkmann R. Zentralbl Chir 1881"]
},
"fractures": {
  name: "Bone fractures",
  sys: "Bones, Joints & Muscles",
  aka: ["broken bone", "fracture", "open fracture", "compound fracture", "fragility fracture"],
  what: "A fracture is a break in a bone. It can be a small crack or a complete break, and the bone may stay in place or shift. Bones heal on their own when the pieces are lined up and held still.",
  origin: "Setting broken bones is one of the oldest parts of medicine. Ancient Egyptian splints have been found on mummies, and the Edwin Smith Papyrus describes how to treat fractures. Hippocrates wrote about traction to pull bones back into line. In 1852 Dutch army surgeon Antonius Mathijsen introduced bandages soaked in plaster of Paris, creating the modern cast. In 1895 Wilhelm Roentgen discovered X-rays, which let doctors see breaks for the first time. In the early 1900s surgeons such as Albin Lambotte and William Arbuthnot Lane used plates and screws, and in 1939 Gerhard Kuntscher introduced the intramedullary nail. In 1958 a Swiss group founded the AO Foundation, which set modern principles of internal fixation.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Egyptians use wooden splints, and the Edwin Smith Papyrus describes fracture care."], ["1852", "Antonius Mathijsen introduces plaster of Paris bandages for casts."], ["1895", "Wilhelm Roentgen discovers X-rays."], ["1939", "Gerhard Kuntscher introduces the intramedullary nail."], ["1958", "The AO Foundation is founded in Switzerland to study internal fixation."]],
  cell: "Bone breaks when force exceeds its strength, from high-energy trauma, repeated stress or weakened bone (osteoporosis, tumors). Healing follows stages. First, torn vessels form a hematoma, and platelets and macrophages release cytokines and growth factors such as PDGF, TGF-beta, VEGF and bone morphogenetic proteins (BMPs). In the inflammatory phase, mesenchymal stem cells from the periosteum and marrow are recruited. They become chondrocytes, making a soft callus of fibrocartilage, and osteoblasts. In secondary healing the cartilage calcifies and is replaced by woven bone (hard callus) through endochondral ossification. Finally osteoclasts and osteoblasts remodel woven bone into strong lamellar bone along lines of stress (Wolff law). When fragments are rigidly fixed with plates, primary healing occurs directly by cutting cones of osteoclasts and osteoblasts without callus. Poor blood supply, motion, smoking, infection and diabetes slow healing.",
  body: [["Bone", "The break causes pain, deformity and loss of function. Healing may fail (nonunion) or heal crooked (malunion), and some bones such as the scaphoid and femoral neck have fragile blood supply and risk avascular necrosis."], ["Blood and circulation", "Pelvic and femur fractures can bleed heavily into the tissues and cause hemorrhagic shock."], ["Nerves and vessels", "Sharp fragments can injure nearby structures, such as the radial nerve in midshaft humerus fractures or the brachial artery in supracondylar elbow fractures in children."], ["Muscle compartments", "Swelling after fractures, especially of the tibia, can cause compartment syndrome."], ["Lungs", "Fat from the marrow of long bones can enter the blood and cause fat embolism syndrome, and immobility raises the risk of blood clots and pulmonary embolism."], ["Skin and infection", "In open fractures the bone breaks through the skin, letting bacteria in and raising the risk of bone infection (osteomyelitis)."]],
  symptoms: "Pain comes from torn periosteum, which is rich in nerves, and from muscle spasm. Swelling and bruising come from bleeding into the tissues. Deformity, abnormal movement and a grating feeling (crepitus) happen when bone ends shift. People cannot bear weight or use the limb because moving the fragments hurts. Point tenderness over the bone is a key sign.",
  dx: "X-rays in at least two views confirm most fractures. CT shows complex fractures, joint involvement and the spine and pelvis. MRI finds hidden fractures, such as scaphoid or hip fractures and stress fractures, that X-rays can miss. Clinical rules such as the Ottawa ankle rules help decide who needs imaging. Exam checks pulses, sensation and skin. A low-trauma fracture in an older adult prompts a bone density (DXA) scan for osteoporosis.",
  tx: "Fractures are reduced (lined up) when needed and then held still with a splint, cast, brace or surgery. Surgery uses plates and screws, intramedullary nails or external fixators. Open fractures get early IV antibiotics such as cefazolin, tetanus protection and surgical cleaning. Pain is treated with acetaminophen, NSAIDs, opioids, ketamine or nerve blocks. In EMS, splinting includes the joints above and below the break, and pulses and sensation are checked before and after. Blood clot prevention with drugs such as enoxaparin is used after major leg and hip fractures. After a fragility fracture, osteoporosis treatment with bisphosphonates, denosumab or bone-building drugs (teriparatide, romosozumab), plus calcium and vitamin D, lowers the risk of the next fracture. Physical therapy restores strength and motion.",
  drugs: ["acetaminophen", "ibuprofen", "ketorolac", "morphine", "fentanyl", "hydromorphone", "ketamine", "oxycodone", "cefazolin", "tdap-vaccine", "td-vaccine", "tetanus-immune-globulin", "enoxaparin", "alendronate", "zoledronic-acid", "denosumab", "teriparatide", "romosozumab", "calcium-carbonate", "cholecalciferol"],
  mem: [["NREMT focus", "Splint the joint above and below the fracture, and check pulse, motor and sensation before and after splinting."], ["Most-tested fact", "Fat embolism syndrome after long bone fractures: low oxygen, confusion and a petechial rash, usually 1 to 3 days after injury."], ["Classic pairing", "Snuffbox tenderness suggests a scaphoid fracture, which is splinted even if the first X-ray is normal because of avascular necrosis risk."], ["Memory trick", "Healing stages: Hematoma, Inflammation, Soft callus, Hard callus, Remodeling."]],
  red: "A bone poking through the skin, a cold, pale or numb limb, heavy bleeding, a suspected hip, pelvis, spine or femur fracture, or a fracture with trouble breathing needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Healed bone can end up just as strong as before, and in children a crooked break can partly straighten itself as the bone remodels while growing.",
  src: ["Rockwood and Green's Fractures in Adults", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Bone Health and Osteoporosis Foundation Clinician's Guide (2022)", "AAOS Clinical Practice Guidelines"]
},
"psoriasis": {
  name: "Psoriasis",
  sys: "Skin",
  aka: ["plaque psoriasis", "psoriasis vulgaris", "psoriatic disease", "guttate psoriasis", "pustular psoriasis"],
  what: "Psoriasis is a long-term immune disease that makes skin cells grow too fast, forming thick, red, scaly patches, often on the elbows, knees and scalp. It is not contagious and can also affect the nails and joints.",
  origin: "Ancient writers described scaly skin diseases, but psoriasis was long confused with leprosy, and people with it were sometimes shunned. In 1808 English dermatologist Robert Willan gave a clear clinical description, and in the 1840s Vienna dermatologist Ferdinand von Hebra separated it firmly from leprosy. In 1925 William Goeckerman at the Mayo Clinic combined coal tar with ultraviolet light, a treatment used for decades. In the 1970s PUVA (psoralen plus UVA) and methotrexate became standard, and cyclosporine's benefit, noticed in 1979, showed that psoriasis is driven by T cells, not just the skin. That insight led to biologic drugs in the 2000s, first TNF blockers and then drugs targeting IL-12/23, IL-17 and IL-23, which can clear skin in many patients.",
  timeline: [["1808", "Robert Willan publishes a clear description of psoriasis."], ["1925", "William Goeckerman introduces coal tar plus ultraviolet light."], ["1974", "PUVA therapy is reported to clear psoriasis."], ["1979", "Cyclosporine's benefit points to T cells as a driver."], ["2009", "Ustekinumab, an IL-12/23 blocker, is approved."], ["2015", "Secukinumab becomes the first approved IL-17A blocker for psoriasis."]],
  cell: "Psoriasis develops in genetically prone people (for example with HLA-C*06:02) after triggers such as strep infection, skin injury, stress or some drugs. Stressed keratinocytes release antimicrobial peptides such as LL-37, which form complexes with self DNA and RNA and activate plasmacytoid dendritic cells to make interferon-alpha. Myeloid dendritic cells then produce TNF-alpha, IL-12 and especially IL-23. IL-23 expands and sustains Th17 and Tc17 cells, which release IL-17A, IL-17F and IL-22. These cytokines make keratinocytes proliferate rapidly, so the epidermis turns over in days instead of about a month, and the cells make more chemokines and antimicrobial peptides, creating a self-sustaining loop. Histology shows acanthosis (thick epidermis), parakeratosis (nuclei kept in the scale), thinning of the granular layer, dilated capillaries in dermal papillae and neutrophil collections (Munro microabscesses).",
  body: [["Skin", "Well-defined red plaques with silvery scale form on extensor surfaces, scalp, lower back and folds. Lesions can appear at sites of injury (Koebner phenomenon)."], ["Nails", "Nail matrix inflammation causes pitting, oil-drop spots, lifting of the nail (onycholysis) and thickening, and nail disease is linked to joint disease."], ["Joints", "Up to about a third of people develop psoriatic arthritis, with swollen fingers (dactylitis), inflamed tendon insertions (enthesitis) and possible joint damage."], ["Heart and metabolism", "Chronic systemic inflammation raises the risk of obesity, diabetes, fatty liver and cardiovascular disease, especially in severe psoriasis."], ["Eyes and gut", "Psoriasis is associated with uveitis and inflammatory bowel disease, which share IL-23 and IL-17 pathways."], ["Mental health", "Visible skin disease and itch are linked with depression, anxiety and social stigma."]],
  symptoms: "Plaques are raised because the epidermis is thick from rapid cell growth. The scale is silvery because cells are shed before they mature. Redness comes from dilated, twisted capillaries close to the surface, which also bleed in pinpoints when scale is removed (Auspitz sign). Itch and burning come from inflammatory cytokines acting on skin nerves. Joint pain and stiffness reflect psoriatic arthritis.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is usually clinical, based on the look and location of plaques, nail changes and family history. A skin biopsy can confirm unclear cases, showing acanthosis, parakeratosis and Munro microabscesses. Severity is measured by body surface area and scores such as PASI. Joint symptoms are screened for psoriatic arthritis. Before systemic or biologic drugs, tests usually include TB screening, hepatitis tests, blood counts and liver function.",
  tx: "Mild disease is treated with topical corticosteroids, vitamin D analogs such as calcipotriene (which slow keratinocyte growth), topical retinoids, the aryl hydrocarbon receptor agonist tapinarof and the PDE4 inhibitor roflumilast cream. Narrowband UVB phototherapy calms skin immune cells. Moderate to severe disease uses oral drugs: methotrexate, cyclosporine (blocks calcineurin in T cells), acitretin (a retinoid), apremilast (PDE4 inhibitor) and deucravacitinib (TYK2 inhibitor). Biologics block key cytokines: TNF-alpha (adalimumab, etanercept, infliximab, certolizumab), IL-12/23 (ustekinumab), IL-17 (secukinumab, ixekizumab, brodalumab, bimekizumab) and IL-23 (guselkumab, risankizumab, tildrakizumab). Spesolimab, an IL-36 receptor blocker, treats generalized pustular psoriasis flares. Systemic steroids are generally avoided because stopping them can trigger a severe flare.",
  drugs: ["calcipotriene", "calcipotriene-betamethasone", "clobetasol", "triamcinolone-topical", "tapinarof", "roflumilast-topical", "tazarotene", "methotrexate", "cyclosporine", "acitretin", "apremilast", "deucravacitinib", "adalimumab", "etanercept", "infliximab", "certolizumab-pegol", "ustekinumab", "secukinumab", "ixekizumab", "brodalumab", "bimekizumab", "guselkumab", "risankizumab", "spesolimab"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Psoriasis is on the outside of joints (extensor surfaces), eczema in adults hides in the bends (flexures)."], ["Most-tested fact", "IL-23 drives Th17 cells that make IL-17; histology shows parakeratosis and Munro microabscesses; Auspitz sign is pinpoint bleeding when scale is lifted."], ["Often confused with", "Seborrheic dermatitis, tinea and eczema. Guttate psoriasis, small drop-like spots, often follows strep throat."], ["Watch out", "Biologics and other immune suppressants require TB screening before starting."]],
  red: "Sudden widespread redness of nearly all the skin, sheets of pus bumps, fever, chills or feeling very unwell can mean erythrodermic or generalized pustular psoriasis and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Cyclosporine was used for arthritis when doctors noticed that patients' psoriasis cleared, a clue that changed psoriasis from a skin disease to an immune disease.",
  src: ["AAD-NPF Joint Guidelines for the Management of Psoriasis (2019-2021)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Fitzpatrick's Dermatology", "Griffiths CEM et al. Lancet 2021"]
},
"atopic-dermatitis": {
  name: "Eczema (atopic dermatitis)",
  sys: "Skin",
  aka: ["atopic dermatitis", "AD", "atopic eczema", "eczema"],
  what: "Atopic dermatitis is a common, long-term itchy rash caused by a weak skin barrier and an overactive allergic-type immune response. It often starts in babies, comes and goes in flares, and is linked with asthma and hay fever.",
  origin: "The word eczema comes from a Greek word meaning to boil over. In 1892 French dermatologist Ernest Besnier described an itchy rash in people who also had asthma and hay fever. In 1923 Arthur Coca and Robert Cooke coined the word atopy for this inherited tendency to allergy, and in 1933 Fred Wise and Marion Sulzberger named atopic dermatitis. In 1952 Sulzberger and Victor Witten reported that topical hydrocortisone calmed the rash, the start of modern treatment. In 2006 researchers led by Irwin McLean found that loss-of-function mutations in the filaggrin gene weaken the skin barrier and strongly raise the risk of eczema. Since 2017, targeted drugs such as dupilumab and JAK inhibitors have given new options for severe disease.",
  timeline: [["1892", "Ernest Besnier links an itchy rash with asthma and hay fever."], ["1933", "Wise and Sulzberger name the condition atopic dermatitis."], ["1952", "Topical hydrocortisone is reported to treat eczema."], ["2006", "Filaggrin gene mutations are linked to eczema."], ["2017", "Dupilumab is approved as the first biologic for atopic dermatitis."]],
  cell: "Two problems feed each other. First, the skin barrier is defective. Filaggrin, a protein that bundles keratin and breaks down into natural moisturizing factors, is low from gene mutations or from inflammation itself. Ceramides and tight junctions are reduced, so water escapes and allergens, irritants and microbes get in. Second, the immune system leans toward type 2 inflammation. Damaged keratinocytes release alarmins (TSLP, IL-33, IL-25), which activate group 2 innate lymphoid cells and dendritic cells that push T cells toward Th2. Th2 cells release IL-4 and IL-13, which further lower filaggrin and barrier lipids, drive IgE production, and sensitize itch nerves. IL-31 is a key itch cytokine. Scratching damages the barrier more, creating the itch-scratch cycle. Staphylococcus aureus colonizes the skin and its toxins worsen inflammation. Chronic lesions add Th22 and Th1 signals, causing thick skin.",
  body: [["Skin", "Dry, red, itchy patches appear on the cheeks and outer limbs in babies and in the elbow and knee creases in older children and adults. Long scratching thickens the skin (lichenification)."], ["Skin infections", "Barrier damage allows Staphylococcus aureus infection (impetigo) and widespread herpes simplex infection (eczema herpeticum)."], ["Airways", "Many children follow the atopic march, developing food allergy, asthma and allergic rhinitis after eczema."], ["Eyes", "Eczema is linked to eyelid dermatitis, allergic conjunctivitis, keratoconus and early cataracts."], ["Sleep and mental health", "Night itch disrupts sleep for the child and family and is associated with attention problems, anxiety and depression."]],
  symptoms: "Itch is the main symptom, driven by IL-31, IL-4 and IL-13 acting on sensory nerves and made worse by dry skin. Dryness comes from water loss through a leaky barrier. Redness and oozing reflect active inflammation. Thick, leathery skin comes from repeated scratching. Flares follow triggers such as soaps, sweat, wool, stress, dry weather and infection, which further break the barrier.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical, based on itch, typical rash pattern for age, a chronic relapsing course, and a personal or family history of atopy (Hanifin and Rajka or similar criteria). Blood IgE is often high but is not needed to diagnose. Patch testing checks for contact allergy when the rash does not behave as expected. Swabs or viral tests are done for suspected infection. Allergy testing is used selectively, because many positive tests do not mean a true allergy.",
  tx: "Daily moisturizers and gentle skin care repair the barrier and are the base of treatment. Topical corticosteroids calm inflammation during flares. Topical calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, pimecrolimus) block T-cell activation without thinning the skin. Crisaborole and roflumilast cream inhibit PDE4, ruxolitinib cream blocks JAK1 and JAK2, and tapinarof activates the aryl hydrocarbon receptor. For moderate to severe disease, biologics block type 2 signals: dupilumab blocks the IL-4 receptor alpha, tralokinumab and lebrikizumab block IL-13, and nemolizumab blocks the IL-31 receptor. Oral JAK inhibitors (upadacitinib, abrocitinib) block cytokine signaling. Phototherapy, cyclosporine and methotrexate are other options. Dilute bleach baths can reduce staph, antihistamines that cause drowsiness can help sleep, and eczema herpeticum is treated urgently with antivirals.",
  drugs: ["hydrocortisone-cream", "triamcinolone-topical", "desonide", "mometasone-topical", "betamethasone-valerate-topical", "clobetasol", "tacrolimus-topical", "pimecrolimus", "crisaborole", "roflumilast-topical", "ruxolitinib-cream", "tapinarof", "dupilumab", "tralokinumab", "lebrikizumab", "nemolizumab", "upadacitinib", "abrocitinib", "cyclosporine", "methotrexate", "hydroxyzine", "acyclovir", "valacyclovir", "mupirocin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Eczema is the itch that rashes: itch comes first, and scratching makes the rash."], ["Most-tested fact", "Filaggrin mutations weaken the barrier; Th2 cytokines IL-4 and IL-13 drive inflammation, and dupilumab blocks their shared receptor."], ["Atopic triad", "Atopic dermatitis, asthma and allergic rhinitis often occur together, sometimes in sequence (the atopic march)."], ["Often confused with", "Contact dermatitis, seborrheic dermatitis, scabies and psoriasis, which favors the outer elbows and knees."]],
  red: "Sudden painful clusters of punched-out sores or blisters on eczema with fever (possible eczema herpeticum), spreading redness, or a baby with eczema who becomes very ill needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Babies who have their skin moisturized daily from birth were studied as a way to prevent eczema, but larger trials found it did not reliably prevent it.",
  src: ["AAD Guidelines of Care for the Management of Atopic Dermatitis (2023)", "AAAAI/ACAAI Atopic Dermatitis Guideline (2023)", "Palmer CN et al. Nat Genet 2006", "Fitzpatrick's Dermatology"]
},
"acne": {
  name: "Acne",
  sys: "Skin",
  aka: ["acne vulgaris", "pimples", "zits", "blackheads", "whiteheads"],
  what: "Acne is a very common skin condition in which hair follicles get clogged with oil and dead skin cells, forming blackheads, whiteheads, pimples and sometimes deep cysts. It usually starts in the teen years when hormones rise.",
  origin: "Acne has been noted since ancient times, and ancient Greek writers used a word that later became acne. For centuries it was blamed on diet or hygiene. In the 1900s scientists linked it to oil glands and male hormones (androgens). Tetracycline antibiotics, introduced in the 1950s, were found to help inflammatory acne. Topical benzoyl peroxide came into wide use in the 1960s. In 1969 Albert Kligman and colleagues reported that topical tretinoin, a vitamin A derivative, unclogs follicles, and it was approved in the US in 1971. In 1982 isotretinoin was approved for severe nodular acne and could produce long remissions, but its risk of birth defects later led to strict programs, including iPLEDGE in 2006. Clascoterone, a topical androgen receptor blocker, followed in 2020.",
  timeline: [["1950s", "Tetracycline antibiotics are found to help inflammatory acne."], ["1971", "Topical tretinoin is approved in the US."], ["1982", "Oral isotretinoin is approved for severe nodular acne."], ["2006", "The iPLEDGE program begins for isotretinoin."], ["2020", "Clascoterone becomes the first topical androgen receptor inhibitor for acne."]],
  cell: "Acne starts in the pilosebaceous unit (a hair follicle with its oil gland). Four steps work together. Androgens at puberty bind receptors in sebocytes, enlarging the glands and increasing sebum. Keratinocytes lining the follicle opening stick together and shed abnormally (follicular hyperkeratinization), forming a plug called a microcomedone. Cutibacterium acnes (formerly Propionibacterium acnes) grows in the oily, low-oxygen plug and activates toll-like receptor 2 on immune cells, releasing IL-1, IL-8 and other cytokines that recruit neutrophils. Bacterial enzymes break down sebum into irritating fatty acids. If the follicle wall ruptures, contents spill into the dermis, causing deep inflammation, nodules and scarring. Insulin and IGF-1 can also boost oil production.",
  body: [["Face and skin", "Comedones, red papules and pustules appear where oil glands are dense: the face, chest and back. Deep nodules and cysts can leave pitted or raised scars."], ["Skin color", "Inflammation can leave dark spots (post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation), especially in darker skin tones."], ["Hormones", "Acne with irregular periods, extra hair growth or sudden severe onset can be a sign of high androgens, such as in polycystic ovary syndrome."], ["Mental health", "Acne is linked to lower self-esteem, social anxiety and depression, even when it is not severe."]],
  symptoms: "Whiteheads are closed plugs under the skin. Blackheads are open plugs that look dark because melanin and oxidized lipids in the plug darken on exposure to air, not because of dirt. Red, tender pimples and pustules form when bacteria trigger neutrophil inflammation. Painful nodules and cysts happen when the follicle wall ruptures deep in the skin. Scars form when deep inflammation destroys collagen.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical, based on comedones, papules, pustules and nodules on the face, chest or back. Severity is graded by lesion type and number and by scarring. Hormone tests (such as testosterone and DHEA-S) are done when signs suggest excess androgens. Drug-induced acne from steroids, lithium or some anticonvulsants, and look-alikes such as rosacea or folliculitis, are considered.",
  tx: "Topical retinoids (tretinoin, adapalene, tazarotene) normalize follicle shedding and unclog pores. Benzoyl peroxide kills C. acnes through oxidation without causing resistance. Topical antibiotics such as clindamycin are paired with benzoyl peroxide to prevent resistance. Azelaic acid and topical dapsone reduce inflammation. Oral tetracyclines (doxycycline, minocycline, sarecycline) reduce bacteria and inflammation for moderate acne, used for limited periods. Hormonal options for females include combined oral contraceptives, which lower free androgens, and spironolactone, which blocks androgen receptors. Clascoterone blocks androgen receptors in the skin. Isotretinoin shrinks oil glands and treats all four causes; it causes severe birth defects, so pregnancy prevention and monitoring are required. Procedures such as chemical peels and lasers treat scars.",
  drugs: ["tretinoin-topical", "adapalene", "tazarotene", "benzoyl-peroxide", "adapalene-benzoyl-peroxide", "clindamycin-benzoyl-peroxide", "clindamycin-topical", "clindamycin-tretinoin", "azelaic-acid", "dapsone-topical", "clascoterone", "minocycline-topical-foam", "doxycycline", "minocycline", "sarecycline", "spironolactone", "ethinyl-estradiol-norgestimate", "drospirenone-ethinyl-estradiol", "isotretinoin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Isotretinoin is highly teratogenic and requires iPLEDGE registration and pregnancy testing; it also requires monitoring of lipids and liver tests."], ["Memory trick", "Four steps: Oil, Plug, Bug, Inflammation."], ["Myth buster", "Blackheads are dark from oxidized material in the open pore, not from dirt."], ["Often confused with", "Rosacea, which has redness and pimples but no comedones."]],
  fun: "Albert Kligman, who helped develop tretinoin for acne, noticed that it also made skin look smoother, which led to its later use for sun-damaged skin.",
  src: ["AAD Guidelines of Care for the Management of Acne Vulgaris (2024)", "Fitzpatrick's Dermatology", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Kligman AM et al. Arch Dermatol 1969"]
},
"rosacea": {
  name: "Rosacea",
  sys: "Skin",
  aka: ["acne rosacea", "ocular rosacea", "rhinophyma"],
  what: "Rosacea is a long-term skin condition of the central face that causes flushing, lasting redness, visible small blood vessels and sometimes pimple-like bumps. It can also irritate the eyes and, in some people, thicken the skin of the nose.",
  origin: "Red, bumpy faces appear in medieval medical writing and art, and French surgeon Guy de Chauliac described a facial redness in the 1300s. Rosacea was long called acne rosacea and was wrongly blamed on heavy drinking. In the 1800s and 1900s dermatologists separated it from acne, since rosacea has no blackheads. Researchers noticed tiny Demodex mites in higher numbers on rosacea skin, and in 2007 Kenshi Yamasaki and Richard Gallo showed that overactive innate immunity, with too much of the peptide cathelicidin, can cause rosacea-like inflammation. Treatments grew from topical metronidazole in the late 1980s to azelaic acid, low-dose doxycycline, brimonidine gel for redness in 2013, ivermectin cream in 2014 and oxymetazoline cream in 2017. In 2017 the National Rosacea Society moved from subtypes to a phenotype-based system.",
  timeline: [["1980s", "Topical metronidazole becomes a standard rosacea treatment."], ["2007", "Research links excess cathelicidin to rosacea inflammation."], ["2013", "Brimonidine gel is approved for facial redness of rosacea."], ["2014", "Ivermectin cream is approved for rosacea bumps."], ["2017", "The National Rosacea Society adopts a phenotype-based classification."]],
  cell: "Rosacea combines innate immune overactivity and blood vessel and nerve dysregulation in genetically prone skin. Triggers such as heat, sunlight, alcohol, spicy food and Demodex mites activate toll-like receptor 2 on keratinocytes. This increases the serine protease kallikrein 5, which cleaves cathelicidin into the active peptide LL-37. In rosacea, LL-37 is abnormally high and drives inflammation, leukocyte recruitment and new blood vessel growth. Mast cells release more mediators. Heat and spice sensors on nerves (TRPV1 and related channels) release neuropeptides such as substance P and CGRP, which dilate vessels and cause flushing. Repeated dilation leads to lasting redness and visible telangiectasias. In the phymatous type, chronic inflammation causes sebaceous gland enlargement and fibrosis, thickening the nose.",
  body: [["Central face", "Cheeks, nose, chin and forehead show flushing, persistent redness, small visible vessels, burning or stinging, and sometimes papules and pustules."], ["Nose", "Phymatous rosacea causes thick, bumpy skin, most often on the nose (rhinophyma), more often in men."], ["Eyes", "Ocular rosacea causes dry, gritty, red eyes, eyelid inflammation (blepharitis), styes and rarely corneal damage that threatens vision."], ["Mental health", "Visible facial redness can affect self-esteem and social life."]],
  symptoms: "Flushing happens because nerves release vasodilating neuropeptides in response to triggers. Persistent redness and small visible vessels come from chronic vessel dilation and new vessel growth driven by LL-37 and VEGF. Burning and stinging reflect sensitive nerve endings. Bumps and pustules come from innate immune inflammation, and unlike acne there are no blackheads or whiteheads. Gritty eyes come from inflamed eyelid oil glands.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical. One diagnostic feature is enough, such as persistent central facial redness that flares, or phymatous changes. Major features include flushing, papules and pustules, telangiectasias and eye findings. Doctors distinguish it from acne (comedones), lupus (a malar rash that classically spares the nasolabial folds, with other body-wide signs), seborrheic dermatitis and steroid-induced rash. Biopsy is rarely needed.",
  tx: "Treatment is matched to each feature. Gentle skin care, daily sunscreen and avoiding personal triggers help everyone. For bumps and pustules, topical ivermectin kills Demodex and calms inflammation, topical metronidazole and azelaic acid reduce inflammation, and oral doxycycline at anti-inflammatory strength reduces inflammation without acting mainly as an antibiotic. Persistent redness is reduced for hours by topical alpha-agonist vasoconstrictors, brimonidine and oxymetazoline. Lasers and intense pulsed light shrink visible vessels. Low-dose isotretinoin is used in severe, resistant cases. Ocular rosacea is treated with warm compresses, lid hygiene, oral doxycycline and sometimes cyclosporine eye drops. Rhinophyma is treated with surgery or laser resurfacing.",
  drugs: ["ivermectin-topical", "metronidazole-topical", "azelaic-acid", "brimonidine-topical", "oxymetazoline-topical", "doxycycline-low-dose-rosacea", "doxycycline", "minocycline", "isotretinoin", "cyclosporine-ophthalmic"],
  mem: [["Often confused with", "Acne: rosacea has no comedones (blackheads or whiteheads) and usually starts after age 30."], ["Most-tested fact", "Excess cathelicidin (LL-37) and Demodex mites are linked to rosacea; rhinophyma is the phymatous form on the nose."], ["Memory trick", "Rosacea is Rosy, Recurrent flushing, and Red in the middle of the face."]],
  fun: "Rhinophyma was once mocked as a drinker's nose, but it develops in people who never drink alcohol.",
  src: ["National Rosacea Society Expert Committee. J Am Acad Dermatol 2018 (2017 classification update)", "Yamasaki K et al. Nat Med 2007", "Fitzpatrick's Dermatology"]
},
"urticaria": {
  name: "Hives (urticaria)",
  sys: "Skin",
  aka: ["urticaria", "hives", "wheals", "chronic spontaneous urticaria", "CSU", "nettle rash"],
  what: "Hives are itchy, raised welts on the skin that come and go, each one usually lasting less than a day. They happen when mast cells in the skin release histamine. Some people also get deeper swelling of the lips, eyes or hands, called angioedema.",
  origin: "Hippocrates described itchy skin bumps, and the name comes from Urtica, the Latin name for stinging nettle, because hives look like nettle stings. The term urticaria was popularized by Scottish physician William Cullen in the 1700s. In 1910 Henry Dale and Patrick Laidlaw described the effects of histamine, and Paul Ehrlich had earlier described mast cells in 1878. The first antihistamines were developed in France in the 1930s and 1940s, and diphenhydramine came into use in the US in the 1940s. Less-sedating second-generation antihistamines arrived in the 1980s. Research in the 1990s showed that many people with chronic hives have autoantibodies that activate mast cells. In 2014 omalizumab, an anti-IgE antibody, was approved for chronic hives, and dupilumab was approved for chronic spontaneous urticaria in 2025.",
  timeline: [["1878", "Paul Ehrlich describes mast cells."], ["1910", "Dale and Laidlaw describe the effects of histamine."], ["1940s", "First-generation antihistamines such as diphenhydramine come into use."], ["2014", "Omalizumab is approved for chronic idiopathic urticaria."], ["2025", "Dupilumab is approved for chronic spontaneous urticaria."]],
  cell: "Mast cells in the dermis store histamine and other mediators in granules. In acute allergic hives, an allergen crosslinks IgE bound to the high-affinity receptor FcepsilonRI, triggering degranulation. In chronic spontaneous urticaria, IgG autoantibodies against FcepsilonRI or IgE (type IIb autoimmunity) or IgE against self antigens (such as thyroid peroxidase, type I autoallergy) activate mast cells without an outside allergen. Some drugs (opioids, vancomycin, contrast dye) trigger mast cells directly, and NSAIDs can worsen hives by shifting arachidonic acid toward leukotrienes. Released histamine acts on H1 receptors on small venules, opening gaps between endothelial cells so fluid leaks into the upper dermis, forming a wheal. Histamine also stimulates sensory nerves, causing itch and an axon reflex that dilates nearby vessels (flare). Leukotrienes, prostaglandin D2 and platelet-activating factor add to the swelling. When the leak is in the deep dermis and subcutis, angioedema results.",
  body: [["Skin", "Raised, pale or red itchy wheals with a red flare appear anywhere, move around and fade within 24 hours without leaving marks."], ["Deeper tissues", "Angioedema causes swelling of the lips, eyelids, tongue, hands, feet or genitals that lasts up to a few days and is more painful than itchy."], ["Airway", "In anaphylaxis, swelling of the throat and larynx can block breathing, and bronchospasm causes wheezing."], ["Circulation", "Widespread mediator release in anaphylaxis dilates vessels and leaks fluid, causing low blood pressure and shock."], ["Daily life", "Chronic hives disrupt sleep and work and are linked with anxiety and depression."]],
  symptoms: "Itch comes from histamine stimulating skin nerves. The raised welt comes from fluid leaking out of venules into the skin. The red flare around it is from nerve-driven vessel dilation. Welts move and fade within hours because the fluid is reabsorbed. Lip and eye swelling happen when the same leak occurs deeper in the skin. Hives that last longer than a day, burn more than itch or leave bruises suggest urticarial vasculitis instead.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical, based on wheals that each last under 24 hours. Acute urticaria lasts less than 6 weeks and often follows infections, foods, drugs or insect stings. Chronic urticaria lasts 6 weeks or longer and is usually spontaneous, though physical triggers (pressure, cold, heat, sun, scratching) can be tested. For chronic hives, limited tests such as a blood count and CRP or ESR are usual, with thyroid antibodies in some cases. Extensive allergy testing is generally not helpful in chronic spontaneous urticaria. Angioedema without hives prompts testing for C1 inhibitor problems and a review of ACE inhibitors.",
  tx: "Clinicians identify and avoid triggers where possible. Second-generation H1 antihistamines (cetirizine, levocetirizine, fexofenadine, loratadine, desloratadine) are first-line because they block histamine with less sedation, and they are often increased above the usual amount under clinician guidance in chronic hives. First-generation antihistamines such as diphenhydramine and hydroxyzine work but cause drowsiness. H2 blockers and leukotriene blockers are sometimes added. Short courses of oral corticosteroids are used for severe flares. For chronic hives not controlled by antihistamines, omalizumab binds free IgE and lowers mast cell receptor numbers, and dupilumab blocks IL-4 and IL-13 signaling. Cyclosporine suppresses mast cell and T-cell activation in resistant cases. Anaphylaxis is treated with intramuscular epinephrine, which tightens vessels and opens airways.",
  drugs: ["cetirizine", "levocetirizine", "fexofenadine", "loratadine", "desloratadine", "diphenhydramine", "hydroxyzine", "famotidine", "montelukast", "prednisone", "omalizumab", "dupilumab", "cyclosporine", "epinephrine", "epinephrine-auto-injector"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Each hive lasts less than 24 hours; chronic urticaria means hives for 6 weeks or more."], ["Often confused with", "Hereditary angioedema and ACE inhibitor angioedema, which are bradykinin-driven, cause swelling without hives and do not respond well to antihistamines."], ["Memory trick", "Hives = Histamine: H1 blockers first."], ["NREMT focus", "Hives with breathing trouble, throat swelling, vomiting or low blood pressure is anaphylaxis: epinephrine first."]],
  red: "Hives with trouble breathing, throat tightness, swelling of the tongue or lips, wheezing, fainting, vomiting or dizziness can mean anaphylaxis and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "In dermatographism, a common physical urticaria, firmly stroking the skin raises a welt in the exact shape of the line, so people can write on their skin.",
  src: ["EAACI/GA2LEN/EuroGuiDerm/APAAACI Guideline for Urticaria (2022)", "AAAAI/ACAAI Practice Parameter: Diagnosis and Management of Acute and Chronic Urticaria (2014)", "Fitzpatrick's Dermatology", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"melanoma": {
  name: "Melanoma and skin cancer",
  sys: "Skin",
  aka: ["melanoma", "basal cell carcinoma", "BCC", "squamous cell carcinoma", "cSCC", "nonmelanoma skin cancer", "keratinocyte carcinoma"],
  what: "Skin cancer is the most common cancer in the US. Basal cell and squamous cell carcinomas grow from skin surface cells and are usually curable. Melanoma grows from pigment cells and is less common but more likely to spread if not found early.",
  origin: "In 1787 Scottish surgeon John Hunter removed a tumor later recognized as melanoma, and French physician Rene Laennec described melanosis in the early 1800s. In 1820 English doctor William Norris described melanoma and noted that it could run in families. In 1827 Arthur Jacob described the basal cell carcinoma known as a rodent ulcer. In 1969 Wallace Clark described levels of invasion, and in 1970 Alexander Breslow showed that tumor thickness in millimeters predicts survival, still the key prognostic measure. For decades advanced melanoma had few treatments. That changed when the BRAF V600E mutation was found in 2002, leading to BRAF inhibitors in 2011, and when the immune checkpoint antibody ipilimumab was approved in 2011, followed by PD-1 blockers in 2014. These drugs turned metastatic melanoma into a disease that some people survive long term.",
  timeline: [["1820", "William Norris describes melanoma and notes it can run in families."], ["1970", "Alexander Breslow shows tumor thickness predicts melanoma outcome."], ["2002", "BRAF mutations are found in about half of melanomas."], ["2011", "Ipilimumab and vemurafenib are approved for advanced melanoma."], ["2012", "Vismodegib is approved for advanced basal cell carcinoma."], ["2014", "PD-1 blockers pembrolizumab and nivolumab are approved for melanoma."]],
  cell: "Ultraviolet light is the main cause. UVB is absorbed by DNA and forms pyrimidine dimers, leaving C-to-T signature mutations; UVA adds oxidative damage. Melanoma arises from melanocytes. About half have activating BRAF V600E mutations, and others have NRAS or NF1 changes, all overactivating the MAPK pathway (RAS, RAF, MEK, ERK) and driving growth. Loss of the CDKN2A tumor suppressor (p16), a cause of familial melanoma, removes a brake on the cell cycle, and TERT promoter mutations let cells divide indefinitely. Melanoma first grows sideways in the epidermis (radial phase) and then down into the dermis (vertical phase), where it can reach lymph vessels and spread. Basal cell carcinoma is driven by the hedgehog pathway, usually through PTCH1 loss that frees Smoothened. Squamous cell carcinoma often begins with TP53 mutations in keratinocytes, starting as actinic keratosis. Tumors hide from T cells by using checkpoints such as PD-L1 and CTLA-4.",
  body: [["Skin", "Melanoma appears as a changing, irregular dark spot. Basal cell carcinoma is a pearly bump with rolled edges and fine vessels, and squamous cell carcinoma is a scaly, crusted or sore-like bump on sun-exposed skin."], ["Lymph nodes", "Melanoma and some squamous cell cancers spread first to nearby lymph nodes, which is why sentinel lymph node biopsy is used for staging."], ["Lungs and liver", "Metastatic melanoma commonly spreads to the lungs, liver and bone."], ["Brain", "Melanoma has a strong tendency to spread to the brain, causing headaches, seizures or weakness."], ["Local tissue", "Basal cell carcinoma rarely spreads but can invade and destroy nearby skin, cartilage and bone if left untreated."]],
  symptoms: "Many skin cancers cause no symptoms early, which is why changes in appearance matter. Melanoma looks irregular because cancer cells grow unevenly and make pigment unevenly. Itching or bleeding of a mole can mean the tumor is invading and breaking the skin surface. Nonhealing sores happen when cancer cells replace normal skin repair. Symptoms in other organs, such as cough or headaches, reflect spread.",
  dx: "The ABCDE features (Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variation, Diameter usually over 6 millimeters, Evolving) and the ugly duckling sign (a mole that looks different from the others) raise concern. Dermoscopy improves accuracy. Diagnosis requires biopsy, ideally a full excision for suspected melanoma. The pathology report gives Breslow thickness, ulceration and mitotic rate, which guide AJCC staging. Sentinel lymph node biopsy checks for spread in thicker melanomas. Advanced disease uses CT or PET scans and brain MRI, and tumors are tested for BRAF mutations.",
  tx: "Surgery is the main treatment. Melanoma is removed with a wide margin of normal skin. Basal and squamous cell cancers are excised, and Mohs surgery removes them layer by layer with immediate margin checks on the face and other sensitive areas. Superficial cancers and precancerous actinic keratoses can be treated with topical fluorouracil (blocks DNA synthesis), imiquimod (activates toll-like receptor 7 immune responses), tirbanibulin, freezing or photodynamic therapy. For advanced or high-risk melanoma, checkpoint inhibitors release brakes on T cells: PD-1 blockers (pembrolizumab, nivolumab), the CTLA-4 blocker ipilimumab and the LAG-3 blocker relatlimab. BRAF-mutant melanoma can be treated with BRAF plus MEK inhibitors (dabrafenib with trametinib, vemurafenib with cobimetinib, encorafenib with binimetinib). Cemiplimab treats advanced squamous and basal cell carcinoma, and hedgehog inhibitors (vismodegib, sonidegib) treat advanced basal cell carcinoma. Radiation helps some cases. Sun protection and regular skin checks help prevent new cancers.",
  drugs: ["pembrolizumab", "nivolumab", "ipilimumab", "nivolumab-relatlimab", "dabrafenib", "trametinib", "vemurafenib", "cobimetinib", "encorafenib", "binimetinib", "aldesleukin", "cemiplimab", "vismodegib", "sonidegib", "fluorouracil-topical", "imiquimod", "tirbanibulin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "ABCDE for melanoma: Asymmetry, Border, Color, Diameter, Evolving."], ["Most-tested fact", "Breslow thickness (depth of invasion) is the most important prognostic factor in localized melanoma; BRAF V600E is the most common driver mutation."], ["Classic pairing", "Basal cell carcinoma: pearly papule with rolled borders and telangiectasias, hedgehog pathway (PTCH1). Squamous cell carcinoma: arises from actinic keratosis, TP53."], ["Often confused with", "Seborrheic keratosis, a harmless stuck-on waxy growth that can look dark."]],
  fun: "Acral lentiginous melanoma, the most common type in people with darker skin, appears on palms, soles and under nails and is not strongly linked to sun exposure. Reggae musician Bob Marley died from melanoma that began under a toenail.",
  src: ["NCCN Guidelines: Melanoma: Cutaneous", "AJCC Cancer Staging Manual, 8th edition", "AAD Guidelines of Care for Primary Cutaneous Melanoma (2019)", "Breslow A. Ann Surg 1970", "Davies H et al. Nature 2002"]
},
"burns": {
  name: "Burns",
  sys: "Skin",
  aka: ["thermal burn", "scald", "chemical burn", "electrical burn", "inhalation injury", "burn shock"],
  what: "A burn is damage to the skin and deeper tissues from heat, chemicals, electricity, radiation or friction. Small burns heal on their own, but large burns affect the whole body, causing fluid loss, infection risk and breathing problems.",
  origin: "Burn remedies appear in the ancient Egyptian Ebers Papyrus. In 1832 French surgeon Guillaume Dupuytren classified burns by depth. The Cocoanut Grove nightclub fire in Boston in 1942 killed nearly 500 people, and doctors such as Oliver Cope and Francis Moore treating survivors advanced the understanding of burn shock, fluid needs and lung injury. In the 1940s and 1950s the rule of nines made it easier to estimate burn size. In 1953 Douglas Jackson described the zones of a burn wound. In 1968 Charles Baxter at Parkland Hospital in Dallas published the Parkland formula for fluid resuscitation, and silver sulfadiazine cream was introduced to fight infection. In the 1970s early excision of burned tissue and skin grafting, championed by Zora Janzekovic, greatly improved survival. Specialized burn centers now treat the most serious injuries.",
  timeline: [["1832", "Guillaume Dupuytren classifies burns by depth."], ["1942", "The Cocoanut Grove fire leads to major advances in burn care."], ["1953", "Douglas Jackson describes the three zones of a burn wound."], ["1968", "The Parkland formula and silver sulfadiazine are introduced."], ["1970s", "Early excision and grafting improve burn survival."]],
  cell: "Heat denatures proteins and kills cells in the center of the burn (zone of coagulation). Around it is the zone of stasis, where cells are injured but can survive if blood flow is kept up; poor resuscitation or infection can turn it into dead tissue. The outer zone of hyperemia has inflammation and usually recovers. Injured tissue releases histamine, prostaglandins, bradykinin, oxygen free radicals and cytokines such as IL-6 and TNF-alpha. In large burns (roughly over 20 percent of body surface), these mediators circulate and make capillaries leaky throughout the body, so plasma moves into tissues and blood volume falls (burn shock). Catecholamines, cortisol and glucagon then drive a long hypermetabolic state with muscle breakdown and insulin resistance. Immune function is suppressed. In smoke inhalation, carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin much more tightly than oxygen, and cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase in mitochondria.",
  body: [["Skin", "Superficial burns are red and painful; partial-thickness burns blister; full-thickness burns are leathery, white or charred and painless because nerve endings are destroyed. The skin's barrier, temperature control and infection protection are lost."], ["Circulation", "Capillary leak and fluid loss cause low blood volume, low blood pressure and shock in large burns."], ["Lungs", "Inhaled hot gases and smoke injure the airway, causing swelling that can close it, plus chemical injury, pneumonia and ARDS."], ["Blood and oxygen", "Carbon monoxide and cyanide from smoke prevent oxygen delivery and use, causing confusion, headache and cardiac arrest."], ["Kidneys", "Low blood volume and myoglobin from deep or electrical burns can cause acute kidney injury."], ["Limbs and chest", "Stiff full-thickness burn skin (eschar) that circles a limb or chest can cut off blood flow or limit breathing."], ["Metabolism and immunity", "Severe burns raise metabolic rate for months, causing muscle wasting, and weaken immunity, making infection and sepsis the leading late causes of death."]],
  symptoms: "Pain is intense in superficial and partial-thickness burns because nerve endings are exposed and inflamed, while full-thickness burns may feel numb. Blisters form when fluid collects between the damaged epidermis and dermis. Thirst, a fast heart rate and low urine output come from fluid loss. Hoarseness, singed nose hairs, soot in the mouth and stridor signal airway injury. Headache, nausea and confusion after a fire can come from carbon monoxide.",
  dx: "Burn size is estimated as a percentage of total body surface area using the rule of nines in adults, the Lund-Browder chart (more accurate in children) or the patient's palm, which is about 1 percent. Superficial (first-degree) burns are not counted. Depth is assessed by appearance, blanching and sensation. Inhalation injury is suspected from history and exam and confirmed by bronchoscopy. Carboxyhemoglobin is measured with co-oximetry because standard pulse oximetry can read falsely normal. High lactate suggests cyanide poisoning. Labs check electrolytes, kidney function, CK and myoglobin.",
  tx: "First aid stops the burning, removes clothing and jewelry, and cools the burn with cool running water (not ice) for about 20 minutes, then covers it with clean dressings and keeps the person warm. Airway protection with early intubation is used when swelling threatens it. Large burns get IV crystalloid, usually lactated Ringer's, started with a formula such as Parkland and adjusted to urine output, since too much fluid also harms. Pain is treated with opioids and ketamine. High-flow oxygen treats carbon monoxide, and hydroxocobalamin binds cyanide. Escharotomy releases tight eschar. Wounds are cleaned and dressed with topical antimicrobials such as silver sulfadiazine or bacitracin, or silver dressings. Deep burns need early excision and skin grafting. Tetanus protection is updated. Nutrition, early movement, propranolol to blunt hypermetabolism in some patients, and rehabilitation for scars and contractures support recovery. Serious burns go to a burn center.",
  drugs: ["lactated-ringers", "normal-saline", "albumin-human", "morphine", "fentanyl", "hydromorphone", "ketamine", "acetaminophen", "silver-sulfadiazine", "bacitracin", "triple-antibiotic-ointment", "hydroxocobalamin", "tdap-vaccine", "td-vaccine", "tetanus-immune-globulin", "propranolol", "lidocaine-topical"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Rule of nines for adults: head and each arm 9 percent, front and back of trunk 18 percent each, each leg 18 percent, groin 1 percent."], ["NREMT focus", "Stop the burning, protect the airway early in suspected inhalation injury, and cover the burn to prevent heat loss; do not use ice."], ["Most-tested fact", "Pulse oximetry can be falsely normal in carbon monoxide poisoning; carboxyhemoglobin must be measured with co-oximetry."], ["Classic pairing", "Electrical burns can look small on the skin but cause deep muscle injury, rhabdomyolysis and arrhythmias."]],
  red: "Burns that are large, deep, or involve the face, hands, feet, genitals or major joints, any burn with smoke exposure, hoarseness or trouble breathing, and electrical or chemical burns need emergency care (call 911). For chemical exposures, Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) can give guidance.",
  fun: "Studies of Cocoanut Grove survivors and families also produced one of the first major medical papers on grief, by psychiatrist Erich Lindemann in 1944.",
  src: ["American Burn Association Guidelines and Burn Center Referral Criteria", "Advanced Burn Life Support (ABLS) Provider Manual", "Jackson DM. Br J Surg 1953", "Baxter CR, Shires T. Ann N Y Acad Sci 1968", "Total Burn Care (Herndon)"]
},
"pressure-injuries": {
  name: "Pressure injuries",
  sys: "Skin",
  aka: ["pressure ulcers", "bedsores", "decubitus ulcers", "pressure sores", "deep tissue pressure injury"],
  what: "A pressure injury is damage to the skin and the tissue under it caused by pressure, often over a bony area, that cuts off blood flow for too long. It happens mostly in people who cannot move easily, such as those who are bedbound, in wheelchairs or very ill.",
  origin: "Pressure sores have been found on ancient Egyptian mummies. In the 1800s French neurologist Jean-Martin Charcot thought they were caused by nerve damage, while others pointed to pressure. Florence Nightingale wrote in 1859 that bedsores usually reflect nursing care rather than the disease itself. In 1930 Eugene Landis measured capillary pressure, giving a basis for the idea that sustained pressure stops blood flow. In 1962 Doreen Norton created the first risk scale, and in 1987 nurses Barbara Braden and Nancy Bergstrom published the Braden Scale, still widely used. The US National Pressure Ulcer Advisory Panel, founded in 1987, created staging systems, and in 2016 it changed the term from pressure ulcer to pressure injury, since early stages have intact skin.",
  timeline: [["1859", "Florence Nightingale links bedsores to nursing care in Notes on Nursing."], ["1962", "Doreen Norton publishes the first pressure risk scale."], ["1987", "The Braden Scale is published, and the National Pressure Ulcer Advisory Panel is founded."], ["2016", "The staging system adopts the term pressure injury."]],
  cell: "When skin and soft tissue are squeezed between a bone and a surface, pressure higher than capillary pressure closes small vessels. Cells become hypoxic, switch to anaerobic metabolism and build up lactic acid and waste. Direct deformation of cells, especially muscle, can damage cell membranes and the cytoskeleton within hours, even before ischemia is severe. Muscle is more sensitive than skin, so damage often starts deep at the bone-muscle interface and spreads outward, a cone-shaped pattern seen in deep tissue injury. When pressure is relieved, reperfusion releases oxygen free radicals and recruits neutrophils, adding injury. Shear (layers of tissue sliding when a person slides down in bed) stretches and kinks vessels, friction strips the epidermis, and moisture from sweat or incontinence softens skin. Poor nutrition, anemia, low blood pressure, diabetes and reduced sensation all weaken tissue tolerance.",
  body: [["Skin", "Damage ranges from persistent redness that does not blanch to full skin loss, often over the sacrum, heels, hips, ischial bones and back of the head."], ["Fat and muscle", "Deep injuries destroy fat and muscle, forming craters and tunnels under the skin."], ["Bone", "Stage 4 injuries expose bone and can lead to osteomyelitis, which is hard to clear."], ["Whole body", "Infected pressure injuries can cause cellulitis, sepsis and death, and large wounds lose protein and fluid."], ["Quality of life", "Pressure injuries cause pain, longer hospital stays and loss of independence."]],
  symptoms: "Early on, skin stays red when pressed because blood has pooled in damaged vessels. Pain, warmth, firmness or a boggy feeling reflect inflammation and deep tissue damage. A purple or maroon area suggests deep tissue injury that began near the bone. Open wounds, drainage and odor appear as tissue dies. People with spinal cord injury or nerve damage may feel no pain at all, which is part of why the injuries form.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is by skin inspection, especially of bony areas, and staging using the NPIAP system. Stage 1 is intact skin with nonblanchable redness. Stage 2 is partial-thickness skin loss with exposed dermis. Stage 3 is full-thickness skin loss into fat. Stage 4 exposes muscle, tendon or bone. Unstageable injuries are covered by slough or eschar, and deep tissue pressure injury is persistent purple or maroon discoloration. Risk is scored with tools such as the Braden Scale. Wound cultures, blood tests and MRI or bone biopsy are used when infection or osteomyelitis is suspected.",
  tx: "The key treatment is removing pressure: regular repositioning, pressure-redistributing mattresses and cushions, floating the heels, and keeping the head of the bed low when safe to reduce shear. Skin is kept clean and dry, with barrier creams such as zinc oxide for incontinence. Nutrition with enough protein, calories and fluids supports healing. Wounds are kept moist with appropriate dressings. Dead tissue is removed by sharp, enzymatic or autolytic debridement, except stable dry heel eschar, which is often left in place. Negative pressure wound therapy helps some deep wounds. Antibiotics treat cellulitis, osteomyelitis or sepsis, and topical metronidazole can reduce odor. Pain is treated, including before dressing changes. Stage 3 and 4 injuries may need surgical flap repair.",
  drugs: ["zinc-oxide", "silver-sulfadiazine", "metronidazole-topical", "acetaminophen", "lidocaine-topical", "cephalexin", "vancomycin", "piperacillin-tazobactam"],
  mem: [["NCLEX focus", "Reposition bedbound patients regularly, keep the head of the bed at or below 30 degrees when allowed to reduce shear, and never massage reddened bony areas."], ["Most-tested fact", "Stage 2 is partial thickness with exposed dermis; stage 4 exposes bone, tendon or muscle; injuries covered with slough or eschar are unstageable."], ["Memory trick", "Braden Scale scores six areas: sensory perception, moisture, activity, mobility, nutrition, friction and shear. Lower score means higher risk."], ["Often confused with", "Incontinence-associated dermatitis, which is shallow, diffuse and caused by moisture rather than pressure."]],
  red: "A pressure wound with spreading redness, foul drainage, fever, chills, confusion or fast heart rate can mean serious infection or sepsis and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Deep tissue pressure injury can begin within hours of lying on a hard surface, so injuries are sometimes first seen days after a long surgery or a fall with a long lie on the floor.",
  src: ["NPIAP/EPUAP/PPPIA International Pressure Injury Guideline (2019)", "Edsberg LE et al. Revised NPUAP Pressure Injury Staging System. J Wound Ostomy Continence Nurs 2016", "Bergstrom N, Braden BJ et al. Nurs Res 1987", "Nightingale F. Notes on Nursing (1859)"]
},
"stevens-johnson": {
  name: "Stevens-Johnson syndrome and TEN",
  sys: "Skin",
  aka: ["SJS", "toxic epidermal necrolysis", "TEN", "Lyell syndrome", "SJS/TEN overlap", "epidermal necrolysis"],
  what: "Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) and toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) are rare, severe reactions, usually to a medicine, in which the top layer of skin and the linings of the mouth, eyes and genitals die and peel off. They are medical emergencies, like a severe burn from the inside.",
  origin: "In 1922 two American pediatricians, Albert Mason Stevens and Frank Chambliss Johnson, described two boys with fever, severe mouth and eye sores and a widespread rash. In 1956 Scottish dermatologist Alan Lyell described toxic epidermal necrolysis, where skin peeled as if scalded. Doctors later realized SJS and TEN are one disease of different severity, mostly triggered by drugs. In 2000 the SCORTEN score was published to predict death risk. In 2004 researchers in Taiwan led by Wen-Hung Chung and Yuan-Tsong Chen found that the gene variant HLA-B*15:02 strongly predicts carbamazepine-induced SJS/TEN in Han Chinese people, and in 2005 HLA-B*58:01 was linked to allopurinol. In 2007 the FDA advised genetic screening for HLA-B*15:02 in at-risk ancestry groups before starting carbamazepine.",
  timeline: [["1922", "Stevens and Johnson describe two children with the syndrome."], ["1956", "Alan Lyell describes toxic epidermal necrolysis."], ["2000", "The SCORTEN severity score is published."], ["2004", "HLA-B*15:02 is linked to carbamazepine-induced SJS/TEN."], ["2007", "The FDA recommends HLA-B*15:02 screening before carbamazepine in at-risk groups."]],
  cell: "SJS/TEN is a type IV (delayed, T cell-mediated) hypersensitivity reaction. A drug or its metabolite binds directly to a specific HLA class I molecule or to the T-cell receptor (the p-i concept), or alters the peptides presented, so the drug-HLA complex looks foreign. Drug-specific CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and natural killer cells expand and enter the skin and mucosa. They kill keratinocytes through granulysin (the main cytotoxic protein, found at high levels in blister fluid), perforin and granzyme B, and Fas-Fas ligand signaling. Keratinocytes die by apoptosis and necroptosis across the full thickness of the epidermis, which then separates from the dermis. Histology shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis with only sparse inflammation. Common culprits include allopurinol, aromatic anticonvulsants (carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, lamotrigine), sulfonamide antibiotics, nevirapine and oxicam NSAIDs. Mycoplasma pneumoniae is a cause in children. Onset is usually 4 days to 4 weeks after starting the drug.",
  body: [["Skin", "Painful dusky red or purple spots and atypical targets merge into blisters, and the dead epidermis peels off in sheets. SJS involves under 10 percent of body surface, overlap 10 to 30 percent and TEN over 30 percent."], ["Mucous membranes", "Painful erosions affect at least two sites, such as the mouth and lips, eyes and genitals, making eating, drinking and urinating hard."], ["Eyes", "Conjunctival inflammation and scarring can cause dry eye, eyelid problems, corneal damage and permanent vision loss."], ["Fluid and temperature", "Like a large burn, lost skin leaks fluid and protein and cannot hold heat, causing dehydration, electrolyte problems and low body temperature."], ["Infection", "The open skin lets bacteria enter, and sepsis is a leading cause of death."], ["Lungs and gut", "The lining of the airways and digestive tract can also slough, causing breathing failure or GI bleeding."]],
  symptoms: "A flu-like phase of fever, sore throat, burning eyes and body aches often comes first, as cytotoxic T cells activate. Skin pain, often out of proportion to how the skin looks, comes from keratinocyte death. Blisters form when the dead epidermis lifts off the dermis, and the skin shears off with gentle pressure (Nikolsky sign). Mouth, eye and genital sores come from the same attack on mucosal cells.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical, based on rapid painful skin detachment with mucosal involvement after a new drug, with body surface area of detached skin defining SJS, overlap or TEN. A skin biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis and helps rule out look-alikes such as staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome (a split higher in the epidermis), pemphigus and erythema multiforme. The ALDEN algorithm helps identify the likely culprit drug, and SCORTEN estimates mortality. Labs check electrolytes, kidney function, blood counts and infection.",
  tx: "The most important step is stopping the suspected drug as early as possible. Patients are moved to a burn unit or ICU for supportive care: warmed fluids, electrolyte replacement, nutrition, pain control, careful nonstick wound dressings without removing attached skin, infection watch, and a warm room. Ophthalmology sees patients early, using lubricants, steroid and antibiotic drops and sometimes amniotic membrane grafts to protect vision. Mouth care and urologic or gynecologic care prevent scarring. Immune-modulating treatments are debated: cyclosporine (blocks T-cell activation), TNF-alpha blockers such as etanercept, IVIG (thought to block Fas ligand) and corticosteroids have been used, with mixed evidence. The culprit drug and related drugs are avoided for life, and HLA screening before certain drugs helps prevent cases.",
  drugs: ["cyclosporine", "etanercept", "intravenous-immune-globulin", "methylprednisolone", "lactated-ringers", "morphine", "fentanyl", "prednisolone-acetate-ophthalmic", "moxifloxacin-ophthalmic", "erythromycin-ophthalmic"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Top culprits are the ABC-S drugs: Allopurinol, Barbiturates and other anticonvulsants (Carbamazepine, lamotrigine, phenytoin), and Sulfa drugs, plus oxicam NSAIDs and nevirapine."], ["Most-tested fact", "HLA-B*15:02 screening before carbamazepine in people of Asian ancestry; HLA-B*58:01 links allopurinol to SJS/TEN."], ["Severity cutoffs", "SJS under 10 percent body surface detachment, overlap 10 to 30 percent, TEN over 30 percent."], ["Often confused with", "Erythema multiforme, usually triggered by herpes simplex, with classic target lesions on the limbs and little skin detachment."]],
  red: "Fever or flu-like symptoms followed by a painful spreading rash, blisters, peeling skin, or sores in the mouth, eyes or genitals, especially within weeks of starting a new medicine, needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Granulysin, the protein that does much of the killing in SJS/TEN, is also used by immune cells to kill bacteria such as tuberculosis.",
  src: ["Chung WH et al. Nature 2004", "Bastuji-Garin S et al. SCORTEN. J Invest Dermatol 2000", "Stevens AM, Johnson FC. Am J Dis Child 1922", "Lyell A. Br J Dermatol 1956", "Fitzpatrick's Dermatology"]
},
"scabies-lice": {
  name: "Scabies and lice",
  sys: "Skin",
  aka: ["scabies", "crusted scabies", "Norwegian scabies", "head lice", "pediculosis", "body lice", "pubic lice", "crabs"],
  what: "Scabies is an itchy rash caused by tiny mites that burrow into the skin. Lice are small insects that live on the scalp, body or pubic hair and feed on blood. Both spread by close contact and are treated with medicines that kill the parasites.",
  origin: "Lice have lived with humans since ancient times, and lice and nit combs have been found with Egyptian mummies. Scabies was also known in antiquity, but its cause was argued about for centuries. In 1687 Italian scientists Giovanni Cosimo Bonomo and Diacinto Cestoni used a microscope to show that a tiny mite caused scabies, one of the first times a specific living cause was proven for any disease. In 1909 French scientist Charles Nicolle showed that body lice spread epidemic typhus, work that earned a Nobel Prize in 1928. In the 1980s permethrin, a synthetic form of a flower-derived insecticide, became a main treatment for lice and scabies. Oral ivermectin, developed for parasitic worms, was found to cure scabies. Newer lice treatments include spinosad, ivermectin lotion and abametapir.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Lice and nit combs are found with ancient Egyptian remains."], ["1687", "Bonomo and Cestoni identify the scabies mite as the cause."], ["1909", "Charles Nicolle shows that body lice transmit typhus."], ["1980s", "Permethrin products are approved for head lice and scabies in the US."], ["2020", "Abametapir lotion is approved for head lice."]],
  cell: "Scabies is caused by Sarcoptes scabiei var. hominis. The fertilized female mite digs a burrow in the stratum corneum, lays eggs and leaves feces (scybala). Larvae hatch, mature and spread. In a first infestation, symptoms start after about 2 to 6 weeks because the itchy rash is a delayed type IV hypersensitivity to mite proteins, eggs and feces, so people can spread mites before they itch. With reinfestation, sensitized people itch within days. Most infestations involve only a few mites, but in crusted scabies, seen in people with weak immunity or who cannot scratch, thousands to millions of mites thrive in thick crusts. Lice are wingless insects. Head lice (Pediculus humanus capitis) and pubic lice (Pthirus pubis) glue eggs (nits) to hair shafts, and body lice (Pediculus humanus corporis) live in clothing seams. Lice bite to feed, and their saliva triggers an itchy allergic reaction.",
  body: [["Skin (scabies)", "Itchy bumps and thin wavy burrows appear on finger webs, wrists, elbows, armpits, waist, buttocks, nipples and genitals. Infants can have lesions on the palms, soles, face and scalp."], ["Skin (crusted scabies)", "Thick, gray, scaly crusts form on the hands, feet and other areas, sometimes with little itch, and are highly contagious."], ["Scalp and hair (head lice)", "Lice and nits sit close to the scalp, especially behind the ears and at the nape of the neck, causing itch."], ["Body and blood (body lice)", "Body lice can spread typhus, trench fever and relapsing fever, mostly in crowded settings without access to laundry."], ["Skin infection", "Scratching breaks the skin and allows strep or staph infection, and in some regions scabies-related strep infection leads to kidney and heart complications."]],
  symptoms: "Scabies itch is worse at night and comes from the immune reaction to mites, eggs and feces, not the burrowing itself. Burrows look like short gray lines because the mite tunnels just under the surface. Itch can last for weeks after successful treatment because dead mites and allergens remain in the skin. Lice cause itch from an allergic reaction to their saliva, and some people feel a tickling sensation of movement.",
  dx: "Scabies is often diagnosed clinically from intense itch, typical locations and itchy close contacts. Confirmation comes from a skin scraping of a burrow examined under the microscope, showing mites, eggs or feces, or from dermoscopy showing the mite as a small dark triangle (delta wing sign). Lice are diagnosed by finding a live louse. Nits close to the scalp suggest active infestation, while nits far down the hair shaft may be old empty cases. Fine-toothed combing of wet hair improves detection.",
  tx: "Scabies is treated with permethrin cream, which blocks sodium channels in mite nerves, applied to the whole body from the neck down, or with oral ivermectin, which opens glutamate-gated chloride channels in the mite and paralyzes it. A second treatment is often used a week or two later. Close contacts are treated at the same time to prevent reinfection. Clothing and bedding are washed in hot water and dried on high heat, or sealed in bags for several days. Antihistamines and topical steroids ease the lingering itch. Crusted scabies needs combined oral ivermectin and topical treatment plus infection control. Head lice are treated with permethrin or pyrethrins, ivermectin lotion, spinosad, abametapir or malathion, which act on louse nerves or egg development, along with careful nit combing. Body lice are treated mainly by washing clothes and bedding and bathing. Pubic lice are treated like head lice, and partners are evaluated.",
  drugs: ["permethrin", "ivermectin", "ivermectin-topical", "spinosad", "abametapir", "malathion", "hydroxyzine", "cetirizine", "diphenhydramine", "triamcinolone-topical"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Scabies: intense night itch with burrows in the finger webs and wrists; treat with permethrin and treat all close contacts at once."], ["Memory trick", "Itch persists after treatment because it is an allergy to dead mites, not proof of treatment failure."], ["Classic pairing", "Body lice spread epidemic typhus (Rickettsia prowazekii), trench fever (Bartonella quintana) and louse-borne relapsing fever."], ["Myth buster", "Head lice do not mean poor hygiene, and they do not jump or fly; they crawl from head to head."]],
  fun: "The scabies mite is about the size of a period at the end of a sentence, barely visible to the naked eye.",
  src: ["CDC Parasites: Scabies and Lice (Pediculosis) Clinical Guidance", "AAP Clinical Report: Head Lice (2022)", "Bonomo GC. Osservazioni intorno a pellicelli del corpo umano (1687)", "Fitzpatrick's Dermatology"]
},
"alopecia": {
  name: "Hair loss (alopecia)",
  sys: "Skin",
  aka: ["alopecia", "androgenetic alopecia", "male pattern baldness", "female pattern hair loss", "alopecia areata", "telogen effluvium"],
  what: "Alopecia means hair loss. The most common form is pattern hair loss from hormones and genes. Other forms include alopecia areata, an autoimmune patchy loss, and telogen effluvium, sudden shedding after stress or illness.",
  origin: "Ancient Egyptian papyri list remedies for baldness, and Hippocrates noted that eunuchs did not go bald. In 1942 American anatomist James Hamilton showed that men castrated before puberty did not develop pattern baldness unless given testosterone, proving the role of androgens. He made a classification scale, later updated by O'Tar Norwood in 1975, and Erich Ludwig described female pattern hair loss in 1977. In 1961 Albert Kligman described telogen effluvium. Minoxidil, developed as a blood pressure pill, caused unexpected hair growth, and topical minoxidil was approved for hair loss in 1988. Finasteride, which blocks the hormone DHT, was approved for male hair loss in 1997. In 2022 baricitinib became the first systemic drug approved for severe alopecia areata, followed by ritlecitinib in 2023 and deuruxolitinib in 2024.",
  timeline: [["1942", "James Hamilton shows androgens are required for male pattern baldness."], ["1961", "Albert Kligman describes telogen effluvium."], ["1988", "Topical minoxidil is approved for hair loss."], ["1997", "Finasteride is approved for male pattern hair loss."], ["2022", "Baricitinib becomes the first systemic drug approved for severe alopecia areata."]],
  cell: "Hair follicles cycle through anagen (growth, lasting years on the scalp), catagen (regression) and telogen (rest and shedding). In androgenetic alopecia, testosterone is converted by 5-alpha reductase (mainly type 2) into dihydrotestosterone (DHT), which binds androgen receptors in dermal papilla cells of genetically susceptible scalp follicles. This shortens anagen and makes follicles shrink (miniaturization), turning thick terminal hairs into fine vellus hairs. In alopecia areata, follicles lose their normal immune privilege. CD8+ NKG2D+ T cells attack the hair bulb, driven by interferon-gamma and IL-15 signaling through the JAK-STAT pathway, which pushes follicles out of anagen. Stem cells are spared, so regrowth is possible. In telogen effluvium, a shock such as fever, childbirth, surgery or crash dieting pushes many follicles into telogen at once, and shedding follows about 2 to 3 months later. Scarring alopecias destroy the follicle stem cells and cause permanent loss.",
  body: [["Scalp", "Pattern loss thins the crown and temples in men and widens the central part in women. Alopecia areata causes round smooth patches, and telogen effluvium causes diffuse thinning."], ["Body hair", "Alopecia areata can affect eyebrows, eyelashes and beard, and can progress to total scalp loss (totalis) or loss of all body hair (universalis)."], ["Nails", "Alopecia areata is often accompanied by nail pitting or rough nails."], ["Hormones and blood", "Hair loss can reflect thyroid disease, iron deficiency, high androgens or other conditions."], ["Mental health", "Hair loss can strongly affect self-image and is linked with anxiety and depression, especially in alopecia areata."]],
  symptoms: "Pattern thinning appears slowly because miniaturized follicles make finer, shorter hairs over years. Sudden round bald patches with short broken hairs that taper toward the scalp (exclamation point hairs) reflect the immune attack in alopecia areata. Heavy shedding of hairs with a white bulb at the root, months after a stressful event, reflects telogen effluvium. Itching, scaling or scarring suggests an inflammatory or fungal cause.",
  dx: "Diagnosis rests on the pattern of loss, history of stress, illness, drugs and family history, and scalp exam. A gentle pull test shows active shedding. Trichoscopy (dermoscopy of the scalp) shows miniaturized hairs in pattern loss and exclamation point hairs and yellow dots in alopecia areata. Labs such as thyroid tests, iron studies and androgen levels are used when signs suggest a cause. A fungal culture is done for scaly patches in children (tinea capitis). A scalp biopsy is used for scarring or unclear cases.",
  tx: "Topical minoxidil opens potassium channels in follicles, extends the growth phase and enlarges follicles, and low-dose oral minoxidil is also used off label. Finasteride and dutasteride block 5-alpha reductase, lowering DHT, and are used in men. Spironolactone blocks androgen receptors and is used in women. Platelet-rich plasma, low-level light therapy and hair transplantation are other options. Alopecia areata is treated with steroid injections into the patches or strong topical steroids, and severe cases with oral JAK inhibitors (baricitinib, ritlecitinib, deuruxolitinib), which block the cytokine signals behind the immune attack. Telogen effluvium usually recovers on its own once the trigger is gone. Tinea capitis needs oral antifungals such as griseofulvin or terbinafine. Wigs, support groups and counseling are part of care.",
  drugs: ["minoxidil-topical", "minoxidil", "finasteride", "dutasteride", "spironolactone", "baricitinib", "ritlecitinib", "deuruxolitinib", "triamcinolone-injection", "clobetasol", "griseofulvin", "terbinafine", "ketoconazole-topical"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Androgenetic alopecia is driven by DHT made by 5-alpha reductase; alopecia areata shows exclamation point hairs and is autoimmune."], ["Memory trick", "Telogen effluvium hair loss Trails the trigger by about Three months."], ["Often confused with", "Tinea capitis (scaly patches, often in children, needs oral antifungals) and trichotillomania (hairs broken at different lengths from pulling)."], ["Drug safety", "Finasteride and dutasteride can cause birth defects in a male fetus, so pregnant people avoid handling crushed tablets."]],
  fun: "Minoxidil was first developed as a blood pressure pill, and its hair-growth effect was discovered as a side effect.",
  src: ["Hamilton JB. Am J Anat 1942", "AAD Clinical Guidance on Hair Loss", "King B et al. Baricitinib for Alopecia Areata. N Engl J Med 2022", "Fitzpatrick's Dermatology"]
},
"glaucoma": {
  name: "Glaucoma",
  sys: "Eyes, Ears, Nose & Throat",
  aka: ["primary open-angle glaucoma", "POAG", "angle-closure glaucoma", "acute angle closure", "normal-tension glaucoma"],
  what: "Glaucoma is a group of eye diseases that damage the optic nerve, usually linked to high pressure inside the eye. It slowly steals side vision without pain and is a leading cause of permanent blindness, but treatment can slow or stop the damage.",
  origin: "The word glaucoma comes from a Greek word for a bluish-gray color, used by ancient writers for eyes that had gone blind. Its link to eye pressure became clear in the 1800s. After Hermann von Helmholtz invented the ophthalmoscope in 1851, doctors could see the cupped optic nerve of glaucoma. In 1857 German ophthalmologist Albrecht von Graefe showed that removing a piece of iris (iridectomy) relieved acute glaucoma. In the 1870s eye drops made from plants, physostigmine and then pilocarpine, were found to lower pressure. Accurate pressure measurement came with the Schiotz tonometer in 1905 and the Goldmann applanation tonometer in the 1950s. Beta-blocker drops (timolol) were approved in 1978, laser trabeculoplasty was introduced in 1979, and prostaglandin analog drops (latanoprost, 1996) became the most common first treatment.",
  timeline: [["1851", "Helmholtz invents the ophthalmoscope, allowing views of the optic nerve."], ["1857", "Albrecht von Graefe introduces iridectomy for acute glaucoma."], ["1870s", "Physostigmine and pilocarpine eye drops are used to lower eye pressure."], ["1978", "Timolol eye drops are approved."], ["1996", "Latanoprost, the first prostaglandin analog for glaucoma, is approved in the US."]],
  cell: "Aqueous humor is made by the ciliary body epithelium, a process driven by carbonic anhydrase and Na-K ATPase and boosted by beta-2 adrenergic stimulation. It flows through the pupil and drains mainly through the trabecular meshwork into Schlemm canal (conventional outflow), with a smaller amount through the ciliary muscle (uveoscleral outflow). In primary open-angle glaucoma, the angle is open but the trabecular meshwork resists outflow because of extracellular matrix buildup, cell loss and stiffening, so pressure rises. In angle-closure glaucoma, the iris blocks the drainage angle, often from pupillary block in eyes with a shallow front chamber, and pressure can spike suddenly. Raised pressure strains the lamina cribrosa, where retinal ganglion cell axons leave the eye. Axonal transport is blocked, neurotrophic support is lost, and ganglion cells die by apoptosis. Vascular dysregulation and genetic factors also contribute, which explains damage at normal pressures.",
  body: [["Optic nerve", "Loss of ganglion cell axons enlarges the cup of the optic disc (cupping) and thins the nerve rim, which cannot be restored."], ["Retina", "The retinal nerve fiber layer thins, which can be measured on OCT before vision loss is noticed."], ["Visual field", "Side vision is lost first in arc-shaped patterns, and central vision goes last, leading to tunnel vision and then blindness."], ["Front of the eye (acute angle closure)", "A sudden pressure spike makes the cornea swell and cloudy, the eye red and the pupil fixed in mid-dilation."], ["Whole body (acute angle closure)", "Severe pain triggers headache, nausea and vomiting, which can be mistaken for a migraine or stomach illness."]],
  symptoms: "Open-angle glaucoma causes no pain or early symptoms because pressure rises slowly and the brain fills in gaps in side vision. By the time people notice, much of the nerve is damaged. Acute angle closure causes sudden severe eye pain, blurred vision and halos around lights because high pressure pushes water into the cornea, plus a red eye, headache, nausea and vomiting. Dim light or drugs that dilate the pupil can trigger an attack.",
  dx: "Eye exams measure intraocular pressure by tonometry and examine the optic nerve for cupping. Gonioscopy, using a mirrored lens, shows whether the drainage angle is open or closed. Visual field testing maps lost areas of vision. OCT measures the thickness of the nerve fiber layer and ganglion cell layer. Corneal thickness (pachymetry) affects pressure readings. Diagnosis is based on the pattern of optic nerve damage and matching field loss, not pressure alone.",
  tx: "Lowering eye pressure is the only proven treatment. Prostaglandin analogs (latanoprost, bimatoprost, travoprost, tafluprost) increase uveoscleral outflow and are usually first. Latanoprostene bunod also releases nitric oxide to relax the trabecular meshwork. Beta blockers (timolol, betaxolol) reduce aqueous production. Alpha-2 agonists (brimonidine) reduce production and increase outflow. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (dorzolamide, brinzolamide drops, oral acetazolamide) reduce production. The rho kinase inhibitor netarsudil relaxes the trabecular meshwork. Pilocarpine contracts the ciliary muscle to open the meshwork. Selective laser trabeculoplasty improves outflow, and surgeries such as trabeculectomy, tube shunts and minimally invasive procedures create new drainage paths. Acute angle closure is an emergency treated with pressure-lowering drops, oral or IV acetazolamide, IV mannitol and then laser peripheral iridotomy, a small hole in the iris that lets fluid bypass the block.",
  drugs: ["latanoprost", "bimatoprost", "travoprost", "tafluprost", "latanoprostene-bunod", "timolol-ophthalmic", "betaxolol", "brimonidine", "dorzolamide", "brinzolamide", "timolol-dorzolamide", "brimonidine-timolol", "brinzolamide-brimonidine", "netarsudil", "netarsudil-latanoprost", "pilocarpine-ophthalmic", "apraclonidine", "acetazolamide", "methazolamide", "mannitol", "bimatoprost-implant", "travoprost-implant"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Open-angle glaucoma is painless with peripheral vision loss and optic disc cupping; acute angle closure is a painful red eye with a fixed mid-dilated pupil and halos."], ["Drug side effects", "Prostaglandin analogs can darken the iris and lengthen eyelashes; timolol drops can cause bradycardia and bronchospasm."], ["Watch out", "Drugs that dilate the pupil, such as anticholinergics and some cold medicines, can trigger angle closure in people with narrow angles."], ["Memory trick", "Glaucoma drugs either cut production (beta blockers, CAIs, alpha-2 agonists) or open the drain (prostaglandins, pilocarpine, rho kinase inhibitors)."]],
  red: "Sudden severe eye pain with blurred vision, halos around lights, a red eye, headache and nausea or vomiting can mean acute angle-closure glaucoma and needs emergency care (call 911 or go to an emergency department).",
  fun: "The eyelash-lengthening side effect of bimatoprost led to a separate cosmetic product for growing longer lashes.",
  src: ["American Academy of Ophthalmology Preferred Practice Pattern: Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma (2020)", "Weinreb RN et al. JAMA 2014", "Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"macular-degeneration": {
  name: "Macular degeneration",
  sys: "Eyes, Ears, Nose & Throat",
  aka: ["age-related macular degeneration", "AMD", "ARMD", "dry AMD", "wet AMD", "neovascular AMD", "geographic atrophy"],
  what: "Age-related macular degeneration is damage to the macula, the small center of the retina used for reading and seeing faces. It blurs central vision in older adults while side vision stays. It comes in a slow dry form and a faster wet form with leaking blood vessels.",
  origin: "Eye doctors in the late 1800s described degeneration of the macula in older people, including Swiss ophthalmologist Otto Haab in the 1880s. Yellow deposits under the retina, called drusen, were recognized as an early sign. For most of the 1900s there was no effective treatment. Laser treatment of leaking vessels in the 1980s helped some patients but left blind spots. In 2000 photodynamic therapy with verteporfin was approved. In 2001 the Age-Related Eye Disease Study (AREDS) showed that a high-dose antioxidant and zinc supplement slowed progression in people at high risk. In 2005 three research groups linked a variant of the complement factor H gene to AMD. Anti-VEGF eye injections changed wet AMD care: pegaptanib in 2004, ranibizumab in 2006 and later aflibercept and faricimab, often preserving vision. In 2023 the first drugs for geographic atrophy, pegcetacoplan and avacincaptad pegol, were approved.",
  timeline: [["1880s", "Otto Haab and others describe macular degeneration in older adults."], ["2001", "AREDS shows antioxidant and zinc supplements slow progression."], ["2005", "A complement factor H gene variant is linked to AMD."], ["2006", "Ranibizumab, an anti-VEGF injection, is approved for wet AMD."], ["2023", "Pegcetacoplan and avacincaptad pegol are approved for geographic atrophy."]],
  cell: "The retinal pigment epithelium (RPE) supports photoreceptors by recycling visual pigment and digesting shed outer segments. With age, oxidative stress from light and high oxygen use, and smoking, damage the RPE. Waste such as lipofuscin builds up inside RPE cells, and lipids, proteins and complement components collect as drusen between the RPE and Bruch membrane. Overactive complement, especially with the CFH Y402H variant or ARMS2/HTRA1 risk alleles, promotes chronic local inflammation. In dry AMD this leads to death of RPE cells and the photoreceptors that depend on them, creating patches of geographic atrophy. In wet (neovascular) AMD, stressed and hypoxic RPE releases vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF-A). New fragile vessels grow from the choroid through breaks in Bruch membrane under the RPE or retina (choroidal neovascularization). They leak fluid and blood, separating retinal layers, and eventually form a fibrous disciform scar that destroys central vision.",
  body: [["Macula", "Drusen, pigment changes and atrophy in dry AMD, or fluid, bleeding and scarring in wet AMD, damage the cone-rich center of the retina."], ["Central vision", "Reading, driving, and recognizing faces become difficult, and a blind or dark spot can form in the center of vision."], ["Visual perception", "Some people with vision loss see vivid, nonthreatening visual hallucinations (Charles Bonnet syndrome) as the brain fills in missing input."], ["Daily life and mood", "Loss of central vision raises the risk of falls, loss of independence and depression."]],
  symptoms: "Straight lines look wavy or bent (metamorphopsia) because fluid or drusen lift and distort the photoreceptor layer. Central blur and a dark spot happen when macular cells die or are covered by blood and scar. Colors look faded and it is harder to adjust to dim light because cones and RPE are failing. Wet AMD can change vision over days because vessels leak suddenly, while dry AMD changes over years.",
  dx: "A dilated eye exam shows drusen, pigment changes, atrophy or bleeding. An Amsler grid can reveal distortion. Optical coherence tomography (OCT) shows drusen, retinal thinning and fluid in or under the retina and is used to guide treatment. Fluorescein angiography or OCT angiography shows abnormal leaking vessels in wet AMD. Fundus autofluorescence maps geographic atrophy.",
  tx: "Wet AMD is treated with repeated anti-VEGF injections into the eye (ranibizumab, aflibercept, bevacizumab, brolucizumab, faricimab, which also blocks angiopoietin-2), which shrink and dry up leaking vessels. Photodynamic therapy with verteporfin, activated by a laser, closes abnormal vessels in selected cases. For geographic atrophy, complement inhibitors given by eye injection slow lesion growth: pegcetacoplan blocks C3 and avacincaptad pegol blocks C5. They do not restore vision. For intermediate AMD, the AREDS2 supplement formula (vitamins C and E, zinc, copper, lutein and zeaxanthin) lowers the risk of progression. Stopping smoking, a diet rich in leafy greens and fish, home vision monitoring and low-vision rehabilitation with magnifiers and lighting are important.",
  drugs: ["aflibercept", "ranibizumab", "faricimab", "bevacizumab", "brolucizumab", "verteporfin", "pegcetacoplan", "avacincaptad-pegol"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Wet AMD is driven by VEGF and treated with anti-VEGF injections; dry AMD shows drusen and geographic atrophy."], ["Memory trick", "AMD takes the center; glaucoma takes the sides."], ["Risk factors", "Age, smoking (the strongest modifiable risk), family history, CFH gene variants and light-colored eyes."], ["Supplement note", "AREDS2 replaced beta-carotene with lutein and zeaxanthin because beta-carotene raised lung cancer risk in smokers."]],
  red: "Sudden new distortion, a new dark spot or a sudden drop in central vision can mean wet AMD or bleeding and needs urgent same-day eye care.",
  fun: "Charles Bonnet syndrome is named after an 18th century Swiss naturalist who described the vivid visions of his nearly blind grandfather.",
  src: ["American Academy of Ophthalmology Preferred Practice Pattern: Age-Related Macular Degeneration (2019)", "AREDS Research Group. Arch Ophthalmol 2001", "AREDS2 Research Group. JAMA 2013", "Klein RJ et al. Science 2005"]
},
"cataracts": {
  name: "Cataracts",
  sys: "Eyes, Ears, Nose & Throat",
  aka: ["cataract", "age-related cataract", "nuclear sclerosis", "congenital cataract", "posterior subcapsular cataract"],
  what: "A cataract is clouding of the eye's natural lens, which sits behind the pupil. It makes vision blurry, dim and glary, usually with age. Surgery to replace the cloudy lens with a clear artificial one is one of the most common and successful operations.",
  origin: "Cataracts are among the oldest known eye diseases. In ancient India, the surgeon Sushruta described couching, in which a needle pushes the cloudy lens out of the line of sight, a method used for many centuries worldwide. In 1747 French surgeon Jacques Daviel performed a planned removal of the lens through a cut in the cornea, the start of modern cataract extraction. In 1949 British ophthalmologist Harold Ridley implanted the first intraocular lens, inspired by noticing that fragments of acrylic plastic from fighter plane canopies did not inflame pilots' eyes. In 1967 American ophthalmologist Charles Kelman introduced phacoemulsification, using ultrasound to break up the lens through a tiny incision. Today surgery is usually done as an outpatient procedure with numbing drops.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Surgeons in ancient India and elsewhere treat cataracts by couching."], ["1747", "Jacques Daviel performs planned cataract extraction."], ["1949", "Harold Ridley implants the first intraocular lens."], ["1967", "Charles Kelman introduces phacoemulsification."]],
  cell: "The lens is made of tightly packed fiber cells filled with crystallin proteins arranged so that light passes straight through. Lens fibers lose their nuclei and organelles and cannot replace their proteins, so damage builds up over a lifetime. Oxidative stress from UV light, smoking and aging depletes glutathione, the lens's main antioxidant. Crystallins become oxidized, cross-linked, glycated and misfolded. Alpha-crystallin, a chaperone that normally holds damaged proteins in solution, becomes overwhelmed, and proteins clump into large aggregates that scatter light. In nuclear cataracts the central lens yellows and hardens. In diabetes, aldose reductase converts glucose to sorbitol, which draws water into lens cells and adds to damage. Steroids, radiation and inflammation favor posterior subcapsular cataracts. In infants, galactosemia (galactitol buildup), congenital rubella and genetic crystallin mutations can cause cataracts.",
  body: [["Lens", "Cloudy, yellowed or opaque areas form in the center (nuclear), outer edges (cortical) or back surface (posterior subcapsular) of the lens."], ["Vision", "Light scattering blurs vision, dims colors, causes glare and halos and reduces contrast, especially at night."], ["Daily safety", "Poor vision raises the risk of falls, hip fractures and car crashes in older adults."], ["Children's brain development", "Dense cataracts at birth block clear images, and without early surgery the brain's visual pathways do not develop (amblyopia)."]],
  symptoms: "Blurry or cloudy vision happens because aggregated proteins scatter light before it reaches the retina. Glare and halos around lights come from light scattering, especially with posterior subcapsular cataracts. Colors look faded or yellow because the lens yellows. Nuclear cataracts can temporarily improve reading vision (second sight) because the hardening lens becomes more nearsighted. In babies, a white pupil (leukocoria) can be the first sign.",
  dx: "A slit-lamp exam shows the type and density of the clouding. Visual acuity and glare testing measure how much it affects vision. A dilated exam checks the retina and optic nerve for other causes of vision loss. In newborns, the red reflex test with an ophthalmoscope detects a missing or white reflex. Before surgery, measurements of eye length and corneal curvature (biometry) are used to choose the artificial lens power.",
  tx: "No medicine reverses a cataract. Early on, stronger glasses, brighter lighting and anti-glare lenses help. When vision limits daily life, surgery removes the cloudy lens, usually by phacoemulsification, and replaces it with an intraocular lens. Around surgery, drops dilate the pupil (tropicamide, phenylephrine, cyclopentolate), numb the eye (proparacaine, tetracaine, lidocaine gel), prevent infection (fluoroquinolones such as moxifloxacin) and control inflammation (steroids such as prednisolone acetate and difluprednate and NSAIDs such as ketorolac, bromfenac and nepafenac, which also help prevent macular swelling). Months to years later, the back of the lens capsule can cloud (posterior capsule opacification), which is treated with a quick YAG laser opening. Babies with dense cataracts need early surgery and vision rehabilitation.",
  drugs: ["tropicamide", "phenylephrine-ketorolac-intraocular", "cyclopentolate", "proparacaine", "tetracaine", "lidocaine-ophthalmic-gel", "moxifloxacin-ophthalmic", "gatifloxacin-ophthalmic", "ofloxacin-ophthalmic", "prednisolone-acetate-ophthalmic", "difluprednate", "loteprednol", "dexamethasone-ophthalmic", "ketorolac-ophthalmic", "bromfenac", "nepafenac"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Leading cause of reversible blindness worldwide; treated with surgery and lens implant."], ["Classic pairing", "Steroids cause posterior subcapsular cataracts; galactosemia causes oil-droplet cataracts in infants; diabetes causes early cataracts via sorbitol."], ["Memory trick", "Second sight: an older person suddenly reading without glasses may be developing a nuclear cataract."], ["Often confused with", "Glaucoma and macular degeneration, which damage the nerve or retina rather than clouding the lens."]],
  red: "Sudden eye pain, redness, worsening vision or flashes and new floaters in the days after cataract surgery can mean infection or retinal detachment and needs emergency eye care.",
  fun: "Harold Ridley's idea for the lens implant came from treating World War II pilots whose eyes tolerated plastic splinters from cockpit canopies.",
  src: ["American Academy of Ophthalmology Preferred Practice Pattern: Cataract in the Adult Eye (2021)", "Kanski's Clinical Ophthalmology", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "World Health Organization World Report on Vision (2019)"]
},
"conjunctivitis": {
  name: "Conjunctivitis (pink eye)",
  sys: "Eyes, Ears, Nose & Throat",
  aka: ["pink eye", "viral conjunctivitis", "bacterial conjunctivitis", "allergic conjunctivitis", "ophthalmia neonatorum"],
  what: "Conjunctivitis is inflammation of the conjunctiva, the thin clear layer covering the white of the eye and the inside of the eyelids. It makes the eye pink, irritated and watery or sticky. Viruses, bacteria and allergies are the main causes.",
  origin: "Eye infections were described in ancient Egypt and Greece, and trachoma, a chronic chlamydial conjunctivitis, has caused blindness since antiquity. In 1881 German obstetrician Carl Crede showed that putting silver nitrate drops in newborns' eyes prevented gonorrhea eye infection (ophthalmia neonatorum), then a major cause of childhood blindness, and the practice spread worldwide. In 1907 Ludwig Halberstaedter and Stanislaus von Prowazek found inclusion bodies of Chlamydia in trachoma. In 1953 researchers including Wallace Rowe discovered adenoviruses, later found to be the main cause of viral pink eye and epidemic keratoconjunctivitis. Antibiotic eye drops and ointments, then mast cell stabilizer and antihistamine drops for allergy, changed treatment through the 1900s. Newborn prophylaxis in the US now uses erythromycin ointment.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Egyptian and Greek writers describe eye inflammation and trachoma."], ["1881", "Carl Crede introduces silver nitrate drops to prevent newborn gonococcal eye infection."], ["1907", "Chlamydial inclusion bodies are found in trachoma."], ["1953", "Adenoviruses are discovered."]],
  cell: "Viral conjunctivitis, most often from adenovirus, infects conjunctival epithelial cells, triggering interferon and lymphocyte responses that form follicles (lymphoid bumps) and often a swollen lymph node in front of the ear. Adenovirus spreads very easily by hands and surfaces. Bacterial conjunctivitis, from Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae and Moraxella, recruits neutrophils, producing pus. Neisseria gonorrhoeae causes a fast, severe infection that can perforate the cornea. Chlamydia trachomatis infects epithelial cells as an obligate intracellular bacterium, causing chronic follicular inflammation and, in trachoma, scarring of the eyelids. Allergic conjunctivitis is a type I hypersensitivity: allergens crosslink IgE on conjunctival mast cells, which release histamine, leukotrienes and prostaglandins, causing itch, vessel dilation and swelling, followed by eosinophil-rich late-phase inflammation.",
  body: [["Conjunctiva", "Dilated surface vessels make the white of the eye pink or red, and swelling (chemosis) can make it puffy."], ["Eyelids", "Lids can stick together with discharge, swell and show follicles or bumps on their inner surface."], ["Cornea", "Adenovirus can cause small corneal spots with blurry vision and light sensitivity, and gonorrhea can rapidly ulcerate and perforate the cornea."], ["Lymph nodes", "Viral and chlamydial conjunctivitis often cause a tender lymph node in front of the ear."], ["Nose and throat", "Viral conjunctivitis often comes with a cold or sore throat, and allergic conjunctivitis with sneezing and a runny nose."]],
  symptoms: "Redness comes from dilated conjunctival vessels. Watery discharge is typical of viruses and allergy, while thick yellow or green discharge reflects neutrophils in bacterial infection. Itching is the hallmark of allergy because histamine stimulates nerve endings. A gritty or burning feeling comes from inflamed surface cells. Vision usually stays normal, so pain, light sensitivity or blurred vision suggest a more serious problem.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is usually clinical, based on the type of discharge, itch, recent colds or contacts, and whether one or both eyes are involved. Rapid adenovirus tests are available. Cultures and gram stain are done for severe or hyperacute discharge, newborns, contact lens wearers and cases that do not improve. Nucleic acid tests detect gonorrhea and chlamydia. Fluorescein staining looks for corneal damage, including the branching (dendritic) ulcer of herpes simplex.",
  tx: "Viral conjunctivitis usually clears on its own in one to three weeks. Cool compresses and artificial tears ease symptoms, and handwashing and not sharing towels limit spread. Bacterial conjunctivitis often improves on its own, and antibiotic drops or ointment (such as polymyxin B with trimethoprim, erythromycin or fluoroquinolones) shorten it. Contact lens wearers get drops that cover Pseudomonas and stop wearing lenses. Gonococcal conjunctivitis needs systemic ceftriaxone, and chlamydial conjunctivitis needs oral antibiotics such as azithromycin or doxycycline, with partners treated. Herpes simplex eye infection is treated with antivirals. Allergic conjunctivitis uses dual antihistamine and mast cell stabilizer drops (olopatadine, ketotifen), mast cell stabilizers (cromolyn) and allergen avoidance. Steroid drops are avoided unless an eye specialist directs them, since they can worsen herpes and raise eye pressure.",
  drugs: ["polymyxin-b-trimethoprim", "erythromycin-ophthalmic", "moxifloxacin-ophthalmic", "gatifloxacin-ophthalmic", "ofloxacin-ophthalmic", "ciprofloxacin-ophthalmic", "tobramycin-ophthalmic", "ceftriaxone", "azithromycin", "doxycycline", "olopatadine", "ketotifen", "cromolyn", "trifluridine-ophthalmic", "ganciclovir-ophthalmic", "valacyclovir", "acyclovir"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Itchy = allergic, watery with a cold = viral, goopy and glued shut = bacterial."], ["Most-tested fact", "Newborn conjunctivitis timing: chemical in the first day, gonococcal at 2 to 5 days, chlamydial at 5 to 14 days."], ["Watch out", "Contact lens wearers with a red painful eye are evaluated for Pseudomonas keratitis, a vision-threatening corneal infection."], ["Often confused with", "Acute angle-closure glaucoma, iritis and keratitis, which cause pain, light sensitivity and reduced vision."]],
  red: "Eye pain, sensitivity to light, decreased vision, a very red eye after injury or chemical splash, or a newborn with eye discharge needs urgent care (call 911 for chemical injuries; Poison Control is 1-800-222-1222).",
  fun: "Adenovirus can survive on surfaces such as doorknobs and eye equipment for weeks, which is why outbreaks of epidemic keratoconjunctivitis can spread through clinics.",
  src: ["American Academy of Ophthalmology Preferred Practice Pattern: Conjunctivitis (2023)", "CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines (2021)", "Azari AA, Barney NP. JAMA 2013", "Crede CSF. Arch Gynakol 1881"]
},
"diabetic-retinopathy": {
  name: "Diabetic retinopathy",
  sys: "Eyes, Ears, Nose & Throat",
  aka: ["DR", "diabetic eye disease", "diabetic macular edema", "DME", "proliferative diabetic retinopathy", "PDR"],
  what: "Diabetic retinopathy is damage to the small blood vessels of the retina caused by years of high blood sugar. It often has no symptoms early but can lead to bleeding, swelling and scarring that cause blindness. Regular eye exams and treatment can prevent most severe vision loss.",
  origin: "In the 1850s, soon after the ophthalmoscope was invented, Austrian ophthalmologist Eduard Jaeger described changes in the retinas of people with diabetes, though some doubted diabetes was the cause. Once insulin (1922) let people live for decades with diabetes, retinopathy became a leading cause of blindness. In the 1950s German ophthalmologist Gerd Meyer-Schwickerath pioneered light coagulation of the retina. The Diabetic Retinopathy Study (1976) showed that panretinal laser photocoagulation cut severe vision loss from proliferative disease by more than half, and the Early Treatment Diabetic Retinopathy Study (1985) showed focal laser helped macular edema. The DCCT (1993) and UKPDS (1998) showed that better glucose and blood pressure control slow retinopathy. In 2012 ranibizumab was approved for diabetic macular edema, and anti-VEGF injections became first-line for it.",
  timeline: [["1850s", "Eduard Jaeger describes diabetic changes in the retina."], ["1976", "The Diabetic Retinopathy Study shows laser photocoagulation prevents severe vision loss."], ["1985", "ETDRS shows focal laser treatment helps diabetic macular edema."], ["1993", "The DCCT shows tight glucose control slows retinopathy in type 1 diabetes."], ["2012", "Ranibizumab is approved for diabetic macular edema."]],
  cell: "Chronic hyperglycemia harms retinal capillaries through several pathways: the polyol pathway (aldose reductase makes sorbitol, causing osmotic and oxidative stress), advanced glycation end products binding their receptor (RAGE), protein kinase C activation and excess reactive oxygen species. Pericytes, which support capillaries, die early, and the basement membrane thickens. Weakened capillary walls bulge into microaneurysms, and leaky vessels let plasma, lipids and blood into the retina, forming hemorrhages and hard exudates. Leukocytes stick to endothelium and block capillaries, causing areas of nonperfusion. Ischemic retina, seen as cotton wool spots (nerve fiber infarcts), releases VEGF and other growth factors. VEGF raises vessel permeability, causing diabetic macular edema, and drives fragile new vessels to grow on the retina and optic disc (proliferative retinopathy). These vessels bleed into the vitreous and pull on the retina as they scar, causing tractional detachment. VEGF reaching the iris can block drainage and cause neovascular glaucoma.",
  body: [["Retina", "Microaneurysms, dot and blot hemorrhages, hard exudates, cotton wool spots and venous beading mark nonproliferative disease."], ["Macula", "Leaking fluid thickens the macula (diabetic macular edema), the most common cause of vision loss in diabetes."], ["Vitreous", "New vessels bleed into the gel filling the eye, causing sudden floaters or a curtain of lost vision."], ["Retinal attachment", "Scar tissue from new vessels can pull the retina off the back of the eye (tractional retinal detachment)."], ["Eye pressure", "New vessels on the iris and drainage angle cause neovascular glaucoma, a painful, hard-to-treat high pressure."], ["Other organs", "Retinopathy signals similar small-vessel damage in the kidneys and nerves."]],
  symptoms: "Early retinopathy causes no symptoms because the central macula is spared. Blurred or fluctuating vision comes from fluid swelling the macula or from glucose swings changing the lens. Sudden floaters, dark streaks or loss of vision come from bleeding into the vitreous. A shadow or curtain over vision can mean retinal detachment. Poor night vision can follow extensive laser treatment.",
  dx: "A dilated eye exam or retinal photographs show characteristic lesions, and severity is graded from mild nonproliferative to proliferative disease. OCT measures macular thickness and detects diabetic macular edema. Fluorescein angiography shows leakage, nonperfusion and new vessels. The ADA recommends a first eye exam within 5 years of diagnosing type 1 diabetes and at the time of diagnosis of type 2 diabetes, then repeated regularly. Pregnancy calls for closer eye checks.",
  tx: "Controlling blood sugar, blood pressure and lipids slows progression. Very rapid lowering of glucose can briefly worsen retinopathy, so eye checks are closer when control improves quickly. Anti-VEGF injections into the eye (ranibizumab, aflibercept, bevacizumab, faricimab, brolucizumab) block VEGF, reducing leakage in macular edema and shrinking new vessels in proliferative disease. Steroid implants in the eye (dexamethasone, fluocinolone) reduce inflammation and leakage when anti-VEGF is not enough, but can raise eye pressure and cause cataracts. Panretinal laser photocoagulation destroys ischemic peripheral retina, lowering VEGF production and causing new vessels to regress. Vitrectomy surgery removes blood and scar tissue and repairs detachment. Fenofibrate has been shown in trials to slow retinopathy progression in some people with type 2 diabetes.",
  drugs: ["aflibercept", "ranibizumab", "faricimab", "bevacizumab", "brolucizumab", "dexamethasone-intravitreal-implant", "fluocinolone-intravitreal-implant", "insulin-glargine", "metformin", "lisinopril"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Proliferative diabetic retinopathy is defined by new vessel growth driven by VEGF; macular edema is the most common cause of vision loss."], ["Memory trick", "Nonproliferative: leaks and bleeds. Proliferative: new vessels grow, bleed and pull."], ["Screening", "Type 1: eye exam within 5 years of diagnosis. Type 2: eye exam at diagnosis, because it may have been present for years."], ["Often confused with", "Hypertensive retinopathy, which shows arteriovenous nicking, flame hemorrhages and, in severe cases, optic disc swelling."]],
  red: "Sudden vision loss, a shower of new floaters, flashes of light or a curtain over vision in someone with diabetes needs emergency eye care.",
  fun: "A retinal photo can be taken without dilating drops in many primary care offices, and this simple screening has greatly reduced blindness from diabetes in countries with national programs.",
  src: ["ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes (2025): Retinopathy", "American Academy of Ophthalmology Preferred Practice Pattern: Diabetic Retinopathy (2019)", "Diabetic Retinopathy Study Research Group. Am J Ophthalmol 1976", "DCCT Research Group. N Engl J Med 1993"]
},
"allergic-rhinitis": {
  name: "Allergic rhinitis (hay fever)",
  sys: "Eyes, Ears, Nose & Throat",
  aka: ["hay fever", "seasonal allergies", "nasal allergies", "perennial allergic rhinitis", "AR"],
  what: "Allergic rhinitis is inflammation of the inside of the nose caused by an allergic reaction to things in the air, such as pollen, dust mites, mold or pet dander. It causes sneezing, a runny or stuffy nose and itchy eyes.",
  origin: "In 1819 English physician John Bostock described his own summer catarrh to a London medical society, the first clear medical account of hay fever. People blamed the smell of hay, but in 1873 Manchester physician Charles Blackley showed through careful experiments on himself, including rubbing pollen on scratched skin, that pollen was the cause. In 1911 Leonard Noon and John Freeman in London reported that injections of pollen extract could reduce symptoms, the beginning of allergen immunotherapy. In the late 1960s Kimishige and Teruko Ishizaka in the US and Gunnar Johansson and Hans Bennich in Sweden discovered IgE, the antibody behind allergy. Antihistamines arrived in the 1940s, nasal corticosteroid sprays in the 1970s and less-sedating antihistamines in the 1980s. Several nasal steroid sprays became available over the counter in the US starting in 2014, the same year sublingual allergy tablets were approved.",
  timeline: [["1819", "John Bostock describes hay fever."], ["1873", "Charles Blackley shows pollen causes hay fever."], ["1911", "Noon and Freeman report pollen injection immunotherapy."], ["1960s", "IgE is discovered as the allergy antibody."], ["2014", "Sublingual allergy tablets are approved and nasal steroid sprays start moving over the counter."]],
  cell: "In sensitization, inhaled allergens cross the nasal lining and are taken up by dendritic cells, which present them to naive T cells that become Th2 cells. Epithelial alarmins (TSLP, IL-33, IL-25) push this response. Th2 cells release IL-4 and IL-13, which make B cells switch to IgE, and IgE binds high-affinity FcepsilonRI receptors on mast cells and basophils. On re-exposure, allergen crosslinks the IgE, and within minutes mast cells release histamine, leukotrienes, prostaglandin D2 and tryptase (early phase). Histamine stimulates sensory nerves (itch, sneezing) and opens leaky vessels; leukotrienes and prostaglandins add congestion; reflexes make glands secrete mucus. Hours later, the late phase brings eosinophils (recruited by IL-5 and chemokines), basophils and T cells, causing ongoing congestion. Repeated exposure makes the lining more reactive (priming), so smaller allergen amounts trigger symptoms.",
  body: [["Nose", "Swollen, pale or bluish turbinates block airflow, and glands produce clear watery mucus."], ["Eyes", "Allergic conjunctivitis causes itchy, red, watery eyes and dark circles under the eyes (allergic shiners) from venous congestion."], ["Sinuses and ears", "Swelling can block sinus openings and the eustachian tube, causing sinus pressure and ear fullness or fluid."], ["Lungs", "Allergic rhinitis and asthma often occur together as one airway disease, and uncontrolled rhinitis can worsen asthma."], ["Sleep and mind", "Congestion disturbs sleep, leading to daytime tiredness, poor concentration and lower school or work performance."]],
  symptoms: "Sneezing and itching come from histamine stimulating nerve endings. A clear runny nose comes from leaky vessels and reflex gland secretion. Congestion is due to swelling of blood-filled tissue in the turbinates from histamine, leukotrienes and late-phase inflammation. Children who often rub their nose upward (allergic salute) can develop a crease across the nose. Symptoms follow seasons for pollens or last all year for dust mites, pets and mold.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is mostly clinical, based on typical symptoms, timing and triggers, plus findings such as pale swollen turbinates. Allergy skin prick testing or blood tests for allergen-specific IgE identify which allergens are involved, which helps with avoidance and immunotherapy. Nasal endoscopy or imaging is used only for unusual features such as one-sided symptoms, polyps or bleeding.",
  tx: "Avoiding allergens, such as using dust mite covers, keeping windows closed in pollen season and saline rinses, reduces exposure. Intranasal corticosteroids (fluticasone, budesonide, mometasone, triamcinolone) reduce inflammation and are the most effective single treatment for congestion. Intranasal antihistamines (azelastine, olopatadine) work quickly, and combined steroid-antihistamine sprays work better than either alone. Oral second-generation antihistamines block H1 receptors for sneezing, itch and runny nose. Montelukast blocks leukotriene receptors but carries a boxed warning for neuropsychiatric effects, so it is not a first choice. Cromolyn stabilizes mast cells before exposure. Ipratropium nasal spray dries a runny nose. Oral decongestants and short courses of decongestant sprays shrink vessels, but sprays used for more than a few days cause rebound congestion. Allergen immunotherapy, as shots or tablets under the tongue, retrains the immune system toward tolerance.",
  drugs: ["fluticasone-nasal", "budesonide-nasal", "mometasone", "triamcinolone", "ciclesonide-nasal", "fluticasone-exhalation-nasal", "azelastine", "olopatadine-nasal", "azelastine-fluticasone", "olopatadine-mometasone", "cetirizine", "levocetirizine", "fexofenadine", "loratadine", "desloratadine", "diphenhydramine", "montelukast", "cromolyn", "ipratropium-nasal", "pseudoephedrine", "phenylephrine-oral", "oxymetazoline", "saline-nasal-spray", "olopatadine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Intranasal corticosteroids are the most effective single treatment for allergic rhinitis."], ["Watch out", "Topical decongestant sprays used for more than about 3 days cause rebound congestion (rhinitis medicamentosa)."], ["Memory trick", "Allergic signs on the face: shiners under the eyes, a crease across the nose and pale, boggy turbinates."], ["Often confused with", "The common cold, which brings fever and aches and lasts about a week, and nonallergic rhinitis, triggered by irritants, temperature or food."]],
  fun: "Charles Blackley built kites to carry sticky glass slides high into the air and proved that pollen counts were even higher above the ground than at nose level.",
  src: ["AAAAI/ACAAI Joint Task Force Rhinitis Practice Parameter (2020)", "AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Allergic Rhinitis (2015)", "Bostock J. Med Chir Trans 1819", "Blackley CH. Experimental Researches on the Causes and Nature of Catarrhus Aestivus (1873)"]
},
"vertigo": {
  name: "Vertigo and Meniere's disease",
  sys: "Eyes, Ears, Nose & Throat",
  aka: ["vertigo", "BPPV", "benign paroxysmal positional vertigo", "Meniere disease", "vestibular neuritis", "labyrinthitis", "endolymphatic hydrops"],
  what: "Vertigo is a false feeling that the body or the room is spinning. It usually comes from the inner ear's balance system. Common causes include loose crystals in the ear (BPPV), inflammation of the balance nerve and Meniere's disease, which also affects hearing. Sometimes it comes from the brain, such as a stroke.",
  origin: "For centuries, sudden vertigo was blamed on the brain. In 1861 French physician Prosper Meniere told the French Academy of Medicine that attacks of vertigo, hearing loss and ringing in the ear could come from the inner ear itself. In 1914 Austrian otologist Robert Barany won a Nobel Prize for his work on the vestibular system, and he described positional vertigo in 1921. In 1938 Charles Hallpike and Hugh Cairns in England, and Kyoshiro Yamakawa in Japan, found swelling of the inner ear fluid spaces (endolymphatic hydrops) in Meniere's disease. In 1952 Margaret Dix and Hallpike described the head-positioning test still used to diagnose BPPV. In 1969 Harold Schuknecht proposed that loose crystal debris causes BPPV, and in 1980 John Epley introduced a repositioning maneuver that cures most cases in minutes.",
  timeline: [["1861", "Prosper Meniere shows vertigo can arise from the inner ear."], ["1914", "Robert Barany receives a Nobel Prize for vestibular research."], ["1938", "Endolymphatic hydrops is found in Meniere's disease."], ["1952", "Dix and Hallpike describe the positional test for BPPV."], ["1980", "John Epley introduces the canalith repositioning maneuver."]],
  cell: "The inner ear has three semicircular canals that sense head rotation and two otolith organs (utricle and saccule) that sense gravity and straight-line motion. Hair cells in each canal's cupula bend when fluid (endolymph) moves, changing signals in the vestibular nerve. In BPPV, calcium carbonate crystals (otoconia) break loose from the utricle and drift into a canal, usually the posterior canal. When the head moves, the crystals keep sliding, dragging endolymph and bending the cupula after the head has stopped, causing brief vertigo and nystagmus. In Meniere's disease, too much endolymph builds up (hydrops), possibly from poor absorption by the endolymphatic sac. The membranes stretch and may leak, so potassium-rich endolymph mixes with perilymph and temporarily paralyzes hair cells and nerve fibers, causing attacks. Repeated attacks damage cochlear hair cells, first in the low-frequency region. In vestibular neuritis, viral inflammation (often thought to be reactivated herpes simplex) knocks out one vestibular nerve, creating a sudden imbalance between the two sides.",
  body: [["Inner ear balance organs", "Abnormal signals from one side make the brain think the head is spinning, causing vertigo and imbalance."], ["Eyes", "The vestibulo-ocular reflex drives nystagmus, rhythmic eye jerks that match the direction of the false motion signal."], ["Hearing (Meniere's)", "Attacks cause fluctuating low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss, ringing (tinnitus) and ear fullness, and over years hearing loss can become permanent."], ["Gut", "Vestibular signals connect to brainstem vomiting centers, causing nausea and vomiting."], ["Brain (central causes)", "Strokes or bleeding in the cerebellum or brainstem can mimic inner ear vertigo and are life-threatening."], ["Daily life", "Unpredictable attacks raise fall risk, especially in older adults, and cause anxiety."]],
  symptoms: "BPPV causes brief spinning, under a minute, triggered by rolling over, looking up or bending down, because crystals move only with head position changes. Vestibular neuritis causes sudden continuous vertigo for days with nausea, but normal hearing, because only the balance nerve is inflamed. Labyrinthitis adds hearing loss. Meniere's attacks last 20 minutes to 12 hours with hearing loss, tinnitus and fullness because hydrops affects both balance and hearing parts of the inner ear. Nausea and vomiting come from vestibular connections to the brainstem.",
  dx: "Diagnosis starts with timing and triggers. The Dix-Hallpike test brings on vertigo and upbeating torsional nystagmus in posterior canal BPPV, and a supine roll test checks the horizontal canal. In continuous vertigo, the HINTS exam (head impulse test, nystagmus type, test of skew) helps tell inner ear causes from stroke when done by trained clinicians. Meniere's disease is diagnosed by criteria: at least two spontaneous episodes of vertigo lasting 20 minutes to 12 hours, low- to mid-frequency hearing loss on audiometry in the affected ear, and fluctuating ear symptoms. MRI is used when stroke, a tumor such as vestibular schwannoma or other central cause is suspected.",
  tx: "BPPV is treated with canalith repositioning maneuvers, such as the Epley maneuver, which move the crystals out of the canal back into the utricle; medicines are not needed for BPPV itself. Vestibular suppressants and antiemetics, such as antihistamines (meclizine, dimenhydrinate, promethazine), benzodiazepines and ondansetron, ease severe acute vertigo and nausea but are used briefly because they slow the brain's adaptation. Vestibular neuritis may be treated with corticosteroids early, plus vestibular rehabilitation exercises. Meniere's disease is managed with a low-salt diet, limiting caffeine and alcohol, and diuretics such as hydrochlorothiazide with triamterene or acetazolamide to reduce endolymph. Intratympanic steroid injections can reduce attacks, and intratympanic gentamicin destroys vestibular hair cells to stop severe attacks, with some risk to hearing. Surgery is reserved for resistant cases.",
  drugs: ["meclizine", "dimenhydrinate", "promethazine", "diazepam", "lorazepam", "ondansetron", "prochlorperazine", "scopolamine-transdermal", "hydrochlorothiazide", "triamterene-hydrochlorothiazide", "acetazolamide", "prednisone", "methylprednisolone", "dexamethasone", "gentamicin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Timing tells the cause: seconds with head movement = BPPV; minutes to hours with hearing loss = Meniere's; days without hearing loss = vestibular neuritis."], ["Most-tested fact", "Dix-Hallpike diagnoses posterior canal BPPV, and the Epley maneuver treats it."], ["Meniere's triad", "Episodic vertigo, low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus, often with ear fullness."], ["Watch out", "Vertigo with new trouble speaking, double vision, weakness, numbness or inability to walk suggests a posterior circulation stroke."]],
  red: "Vertigo with a sudden severe headache, slurred speech, double vision, facial drooping, weakness or numbness, trouble walking, or new sudden hearing loss can mean a stroke and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The inner ear crystals that cause BPPV are tiny calcium carbonate particles, the same mineral found in chalk and seashells.",
  src: ["AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (2017)", "AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Meniere's Disease (2020)", "Lopez-Escamez JA et al. Barany Society diagnostic criteria for Meniere's disease (2015)", "Dix MR, Hallpike CS. Proc R Soc Med 1952"]
},
"epistaxis": {
  name: "Nosebleeds (epistaxis)",
  sys: "Eyes, Ears, Nose & Throat",
  aka: ["epistaxis", "nosebleed", "anterior epistaxis", "posterior epistaxis"],
  what: "A nosebleed is bleeding from the blood vessels lining the inside of the nose. Most come from the front of the nose and stop with simple pressure. Less often, bleeding from the back of the nose is heavy and needs emergency treatment.",
  origin: "Nosebleeds were described in ancient medicine, and Hippocrates wrote about pressing on the nose to stop them. In 1879 American surgeon James Little described a bleeding spot on the front of the nasal septum, and in 1884 German physician Wilhelm Kiesselbach described the network of vessels there, now called Kiesselbach plexus or Little area. Nasal packing with gauze was the main treatment for centuries. In the 1900s surgeons learned to tie off the arteries feeding the nose, and in the 1970s radiologists began blocking these vessels with catheter embolization. Endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation, developed in the 1990s, gave a less invasive option for severe posterior bleeds. In 2020 the American Academy of Otolaryngology published its first clinical practice guideline on nosebleeds.",
  timeline: [["1879", "James Little describes the anterior septal bleeding area."], ["1884", "Wilhelm Kiesselbach describes the septal vascular plexus."], ["1970s", "Catheter embolization is used for severe nosebleeds."], ["1990s", "Endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation is developed."], ["2020", "AAO-HNS publishes a clinical practice guideline on nosebleeds."]],
  cell: "The nasal lining is thin and packed with vessels that warm and humidify air. About 90 percent of nosebleeds start in Kiesselbach plexus on the front of the septum, where branches of the anterior ethmoidal, sphenopalatine, greater palatine and superior labial arteries meet. Dry air cracks the mucosa, and nose picking, colds, allergies, nasal sprays, oxygen tubing and minor trauma break the small vessels. Posterior bleeds come from branches of the sphenopalatine artery and are more common in older adults with atherosclerosis. Bleeding is prolonged by anticoagulants, antiplatelet drugs, liver disease, thrombocytopenia and inherited bleeding disorders such as von Willebrand disease. Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (HHT), caused by mutations in ENG, ACVRL1 or SMAD4 that disrupt TGF-beta and BMP signaling, creates fragile direct artery-to-vein connections (telangiectasias) that bleed repeatedly. Tumors are rare causes, such as juvenile nasopharyngeal angiofibroma in adolescent males.",
  body: [["Nose", "Blood flows from one nostril, often after a crust breaks, and recurrent bleeds can leave crusting and visible vessels."], ["Throat and stomach", "Posterior bleeding runs down the throat, so it can be swallowed, causing nausea, vomiting blood or dark stools."], ["Airway", "Heavy bleeding, especially in a person who is drowsy or lying flat, can be inhaled, threatening the airway."], ["Circulation", "Large or prolonged bleeds, especially in older adults on blood thinners, can cause anemia, low blood pressure and shock."]],
  symptoms: "Bleeding from one nostril usually means an anterior source on the septum. Blood running down the back of the throat, bleeding from both sides or bleeding that does not stop with pressure suggests a posterior source. Swallowed blood irritates the stomach and causes nausea. Dizziness, pallor and a fast heart rate come from blood loss. Repeated nosebleeds with small red spots on the lips, tongue or fingers suggest HHT.",
  dx: "Clinicians look inside the nose after clearing clots, often with a light and a nasal speculum, to find the bleeding point. Nasal endoscopy helps locate posterior or recurrent bleeding sites. Vital signs and a blood count assess blood loss. Clotting tests such as INR are checked in people on warfarin or with suspected bleeding disorders. Imaging or angiography is used when a tumor or a vessel for embolization is suspected.",
  tx: "First aid is sitting up, leaning forward so blood does not run down the throat, and firm continuous pressure on the soft part of the nose for at least 10 to 15 minutes. Topical vasoconstrictors such as oxymetazoline shrink vessels and slow bleeding. Once a spot is seen, it can be cauterized chemically with silver nitrate or electrically, usually on one side of the septum at a time. Persistent bleeding is treated with nasal packing or inflatable balloon devices, sometimes with topical tranexamic acid, which blocks plasmin from breaking down clots. Posterior bleeding may need posterior packing with hospital monitoring, endoscopic artery ligation or embolization. Clinicians decide whether to reverse anticoagulants, for example with vitamin K and 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (4F-PCC) for warfarin or idarucizumab for dabigatran, balancing clot risk. Prevention includes saline sprays, humidifiers and petroleum-based or antibiotic ointments. HHT may be treated with laser, surgery or anti-angiogenic therapy such as bevacizumab.",
  drugs: ["oxymetazoline", "silver-nitrate", "tranexamic-acid", "lidocaine-topical", "saline-nasal-spray", "mupirocin-nasal", "phytonadione", "prothrombin-complex-concentrate", "idarucizumab", "desmopressin", "bevacizumab"],
  mem: [["NREMT focus", "Have the patient sit up and lean forward, then apply firm pinching pressure to the soft part of the nose; do not tilt the head back."], ["Most-tested fact", "Most nosebleeds arise from Kiesselbach plexus on the anterior septum; posterior bleeds come from the sphenopalatine artery."], ["Memory trick", "Kiesselbach is where the Kids bleed: the front of the septum in children who pick their noses."], ["Often confused with", "Bleeding from the lungs (hemoptysis) or stomach (hematemesis), since swallowed nasal blood can be coughed or vomited up."]],
  red: "A nosebleed that does not stop after 15 to 20 minutes of firm pressure, heavy bleeding with dizziness, fainting or trouble breathing, bleeding after a head injury, or bleeding in someone on blood thinners needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Nosebleeds are most common in winter, when indoor heating dries the air and the nasal lining cracks more easily.",
  src: ["AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline: Nosebleed (Epistaxis) (2020)", "Cummings Otolaryngology: Head and Neck Surgery", "Kiesselbach W. Berl Klin Wochenschr 1884", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"preeclampsia": {
  name: "Preeclampsia and eclampsia",
  sys: "Pregnancy & Reproductive",
  aka: ["preeclampsia", "pre-eclampsia", "eclampsia", "toxemia of pregnancy", "HELLP syndrome", "hypertensive disorders of pregnancy"],
  what: "Preeclampsia is a serious pregnancy condition with new high blood pressure after 20 weeks, plus signs of damage to organs such as the kidneys, liver, brain or blood. Eclampsia is when seizures occur. It is caused by problems with the placenta and is cured by delivery, though it can also start after birth.",
  origin: "Seizures in pregnancy were described by ancient Greek, Egyptian and Indian writers, and the word eclampsia comes from a Greek word for a sudden flash. In the 1840s doctors such as John Lever in London found protein in the urine of women with eclampsia, and after the blood pressure cuff came into use around 1900, doctors saw that high blood pressure came before the seizures, so preeclampsia could be detected early. Magnesium sulfate was used for eclampsia in the early 1900s and was standardized in the US by Jack Pritchard in 1955. In 1995 the Collaborative Eclampsia Trial proved magnesium sulfate was better than diazepam or phenytoin, and the Magpie Trial (2002) showed it halves the risk of eclampsia in preeclampsia. In 2003 S. Ananth Karumanchi's group showed that the placenta releases sFlt-1, a protein that blocks blood vessel growth factors and drives the disease. Low-dose aspirin is now recommended for prevention in people at high risk.",
  timeline: [["1840s", "Protein in the urine is linked to eclampsia."], ["1955", "Jack Pritchard publishes a standardized magnesium sulfate regimen."], ["1995", "The Collaborative Eclampsia Trial shows magnesium sulfate prevents repeat seizures best."], ["2002", "The Magpie Trial shows magnesium sulfate halves eclampsia risk in preeclampsia."], ["2003", "Placental sFlt-1 is identified as a key cause of maternal symptoms."]],
  cell: "In normal pregnancy, invasive cytotrophoblasts remodel the mother's spiral arteries into wide, low-resistance vessels, replacing their muscular walls. In preeclampsia this remodeling is shallow, possibly because of abnormal interaction between maternal uterine natural killer cells and fetal trophoblast HLA molecules. The spiral arteries stay narrow, so the placenta becomes poorly perfused and oxidatively stressed. The stressed placenta releases antiangiogenic factors into the mother's blood: soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1 (sFlt-1) binds and inactivates VEGF and placental growth factor (PlGF), and soluble endoglin blocks TGF-beta signaling. This causes widespread maternal endothelial dysfunction, with less nitric oxide and prostacyclin, more thromboxane and endothelin, and greater sensitivity to angiotensin II. The results are vasoconstriction (hypertension), glomerular endotheliosis (proteinuria), capillary leak, platelet activation and microthrombi. In the brain, loss of autoregulation causes vasogenic edema (posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome) and seizures.",
  body: [["Blood vessels", "Endothelial damage and vasoconstriction raise blood pressure and make vessels leaky, causing swelling and, at severe levels, stroke risk."], ["Kidneys", "Swollen glomerular endothelial cells (glomerular endotheliosis) leak protein, and kidney function can fall."], ["Liver", "Microvascular injury in the liver raises liver enzymes and causes right upper belly pain, and the liver capsule can bleed or rupture in HELLP syndrome."], ["Blood", "Platelet consumption and red cell breakage cause low platelets and hemolysis (HELLP: hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelets)."], ["Brain and eyes", "Cerebral edema causes severe headache, visual changes and seizures (eclampsia); stroke is a leading cause of death."], ["Lungs", "Capillary leak and heart strain can cause pulmonary edema."], ["Placenta and baby", "Poor placental blood flow causes fetal growth restriction and low amniotic fluid and raises the risk of placental abruption and preterm birth."]],
  symptoms: "Many people feel well at first, which is why blood pressure checks matter. Severe headache comes from high pressure and brain edema. Blurred vision, flashing lights or spots come from swelling in the visual parts of the brain or retina. Pain in the upper right belly or under the breastbone comes from a swollen liver stretching its capsule. Sudden swelling of the face and hands and rapid weight gain reflect capillary leak. Shortness of breath may mean fluid in the lungs.",
  dx: "Preeclampsia is diagnosed after 20 weeks of pregnancy by blood pressure of 140/90 mmHg or higher on two readings at least 4 hours apart, plus protein in the urine (by 24-hour collection or protein-to-creatinine ratio) or signs of organ damage. Severe features include blood pressure of 160/110 mmHg or higher, low platelets, raised liver enzymes or right upper quadrant pain, rising creatinine, pulmonary edema, and new headache or visual symptoms. Labs include a blood count, liver enzymes, creatinine and sometimes LDH. Fetal growth and well-being are checked with ultrasound and monitoring. A blood test measuring the sFlt-1 to PlGF ratio can help predict progression to severe disease in hospitalized patients.",
  tx: "Delivery of the placenta is the only cure. Timing balances the risks to the mother and baby: without severe features, delivery is usually at 37 weeks, and with severe features it is usually at 34 weeks or as soon as the mother is stable if she is unstable. Severe high blood pressure is treated quickly with IV labetalol, IV hydralazine or oral immediate-release nifedipine to prevent stroke. Magnesium sulfate prevents and treats eclamptic seizures by blocking calcium channels and NMDA receptors in the brain and relaxing cerebral vessels. Patients on magnesium are watched for toxicity, such as loss of reflexes, slow breathing and low urine output, and calcium gluconate reverses it. Antenatal corticosteroids (betamethasone or dexamethasone) speed the baby's lung maturity before preterm delivery. For prevention, low-dose aspirin started late in the first trimester or early in the second lowers risk in people at high risk. Blood pressure is monitored closely after birth because preeclampsia can start or worsen up to 6 weeks postpartum.",
  drugs: ["magnesium-sulfate", "labetalol", "hydralazine", "nifedipine", "calcium-gluconate", "betamethasone", "dexamethasone", "aspirin", "methyldopa", "furosemide"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Magnesium sulfate prevents eclamptic seizures; signs of toxicity are loss of deep tendon reflexes and respiratory depression, and the antidote is calcium gluconate."], ["Memory trick", "HELLP = Hemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets."], ["Key mechanism", "Abnormal spiral artery remodeling leads to placental release of sFlt-1, which blocks VEGF and PlGF and damages the mother's endothelium."], ["NCLEX focus", "Check reflexes, breathing rate and urine output often during magnesium infusion, and keep calcium gluconate at the bedside."]],
  red: "During pregnancy or in the weeks after birth, a severe headache that does not go away, vision changes, pain in the upper belly, trouble breathing, sudden swelling of the face or hands, or a seizure needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Preeclampsia can begin after delivery, even in someone whose pregnancy blood pressure was normal, which is why postpartum warning signs are taught to every new parent.",
  src: ["ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 222: Gestational Hypertension and Preeclampsia (2020)", "USPSTF Aspirin Use to Prevent Preeclampsia (2021)", "Maynard SE et al. J Clin Invest 2003", "Magpie Trial Collaborative Group. Lancet 2002", "Eclampsia Trial Collaborative Group. Lancet 1995"]
},
"postpartum-hemorrhage": {
  name: "Postpartum hemorrhage",
  sys: "Pregnancy & Reproductive",
  aka: ["PPH", "obstetric hemorrhage", "uterine atony", "postpartum bleeding"],
  what: "Postpartum hemorrhage is heavy bleeding after giving birth, most often because the uterus does not tighten enough after the placenta comes out. It can happen quickly and is a leading cause of maternal death worldwide, but fast treatment saves most lives.",
  origin: "For most of history, bleeding after birth was a common cause of death for mothers. Midwives used ergot, a fungus on rye, to tighten the uterus, and in 1808 American physician John Stearns described its use in labor. In 1906 Henry Dale found that an extract of the pituitary gland made the uterus contract, and in 1935 John Chassar Moir and Harold Dudley isolated ergometrine (ergonovine) from ergot. In 1953 Vincent du Vigneaud synthesized oxytocin, the first peptide hormone made in a lab, earning a Nobel Prize in 1955. Giving oxytocin right after birth as part of active management of the third stage of labor became standard prevention. Prostaglandins such as carboprost and misoprostol added more options. In 1997 Christopher B-Lynch described a compression stitch around the uterus. In 2017 the WOMAN trial showed that tranexamic acid given within 3 hours of birth lowers death from bleeding.",
  timeline: [["1808", "John Stearns describes the use of ergot in childbirth."], ["1935", "Ergometrine is isolated from ergot."], ["1953", "Vincent du Vigneaud synthesizes oxytocin."], ["1997", "The B-Lynch uterine compression suture is described."], ["2017", "The WOMAN trial shows tranexamic acid reduces deaths from postpartum bleeding."]],
  cell: "At term, the uterus receives a large share of the mother's blood flow through widened spiral arteries. After the placenta separates, these vessels are left open at the placental site. The main way bleeding stops is mechanical: the interlacing myometrial fibers contract and kink the vessels shut (often called living ligatures). Oxytocin, acting on G protein-coupled receptors that raise intracellular calcium, and prostaglandins drive this contraction. Clotting is the second defense. The causes are summarized as the four Ts. Tone: uterine atony, the most common cause, from an overstretched uterus (twins, polyhydramnios, large baby), long or very fast labor, infection, magnesium or prolonged oxytocin exposure, which downregulates oxytocin receptors. Trauma: tears, uterine rupture or inversion. Tissue: retained placenta or placenta accreta spectrum, where the placenta grows into the wall and cannot separate. Thrombin: clotting problems such as DIC from abruption or amniotic fluid embolism, and dilution of clotting factors. Low fibrinogen strongly predicts severe bleeding.",
  body: [["Uterus", "A soft, boggy uterus that does not contract keeps bleeding at the placental site, and tears or retained tissue add to blood loss."], ["Circulation", "Rapid blood loss leads to hemorrhagic shock. Healthy pregnant people can compensate well, so blood pressure may stay normal until a large volume is lost."], ["Blood clotting", "Loss and consumption of clotting factors, especially fibrinogen, can cause DIC, which makes bleeding worse."], ["Kidneys", "Low blood flow can cause acute kidney injury."], ["Pituitary", "Severe shock can cause death of the enlarged pituitary gland (Sheehan syndrome), leading to inability to breastfeed and hormone deficiencies."], ["Heart and lungs", "Massive transfusion can cause fluid overload and lung injury, and severe anemia strains the heart."]],
  symptoms: "Heavy bright red bleeding or large clots come from open vessels at the placental site or from tears. A soft, enlarged uterus that feels boggy means atony. A fast heart rate is often the first sign of shock, followed by dizziness, pale clammy skin, fast breathing, confusion and low blood pressure as blood volume drops. Severe pain may suggest a hematoma, uterine rupture or inversion.",
  dx: "Postpartum hemorrhage is defined by ACOG as cumulative blood loss of about a liter or more, or any blood loss with signs of low blood volume, within 24 hours of birth. Measuring blood loss by weighing pads and using collection drapes is more accurate than visual estimates. Rising heart rate and the shock index (heart rate divided by systolic pressure) help detect hidden loss. The uterus, cervix, vagina and perineum are examined for tone and tears, and ultrasound checks for retained tissue. Labs include a blood count, fibrinogen, clotting tests and type and crossmatch. Bleeding after 24 hours and up to 12 weeks is secondary postpartum hemorrhage.",
  tx: "Care follows the four Ts, often using a hospital hemorrhage bundle. Oxytocin is given to every birthing person as prevention and is first-line treatment, along with uterine massage and emptying the bladder. Additional uterotonics include methylergonovine (an ergot that causes sustained contraction, avoided in high blood pressure and preeclampsia), carboprost (prostaglandin F2-alpha, avoided in asthma because it constricts airways) and misoprostol (prostaglandin E1). Tranexamic acid blocks plasminogen activation to stabilize clots and works best given within 3 hours of birth. Tears are repaired and retained tissue is removed. Balloon tamponade or uterine vacuum devices compress or collapse the uterus. Blood loss is replaced with blood products in balanced ratios under a massive transfusion protocol, with fibrinogen replacement and warming. When bleeding continues, uterine artery embolization, compression sutures or hysterectomy may be lifesaving. EMS care focuses on fundal massage, oxygen, IV access, keeping the patient warm and rapid transport.",
  drugs: ["oxytocin", "methylergonovine", "carboprost", "misoprostol", "tranexamic-acid", "lactated-ringers", "normal-saline", "fibrinogen-concentrate", "calcium-chloride", "calcium-gluconate"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "The four Ts: Tone (most common, about 70 percent), Trauma, Tissue, Thrombin."], ["Drug contraindications", "Methylergonovine is avoided in hypertension and preeclampsia; carboprost is avoided in asthma."], ["NREMT and NCLEX focus", "Massage the fundus firmly until it is firm, and watch for a rising heart rate, which is an early sign of shock."], ["Most-tested fact", "Uterine atony is the most common cause of postpartum hemorrhage, and oxytocin is first-line."]],
  red: "After giving birth, bleeding that soaks a pad in an hour or less, passing large clots, dizziness, fainting, a racing heart, blurred vision, chills or clammy skin needs emergency care (call 911). This can happen in the days and weeks after going home.",
  fun: "Vincent du Vigneaud's synthesis of oxytocin was the first time scientists built a working peptide hormone in a laboratory.",
  src: ["ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 183: Postpartum Hemorrhage (2017)", "WOMAN Trial Collaborators. Lancet 2017", "WHO Recommendations for the Prevention and Treatment of Postpartum Haemorrhage (2012, updated)", "California Maternal Quality Care Collaborative Obstetric Hemorrhage Toolkit"]
},
"gestational-diabetes": {
  name: "Gestational diabetes",
  sys: "Pregnancy & Reproductive",
  aka: ["GDM", "gestational diabetes mellitus", "diabetes in pregnancy"],
  what: "Gestational diabetes is high blood sugar that first develops during pregnancy, usually in the second half, when pregnancy hormones make the body resist insulin. It often goes away after birth but needs treatment to protect the baby and raises the parent's future risk of type 2 diabetes.",
  origin: "In 1824 German physician Heinrich Bennewitz described a pregnant woman with sugar in her urine and great thirst that went away after birth, often cited as the first report of gestational diabetes. Before insulin was discovered in 1921 and 1922, diabetes in pregnancy was often fatal for babies. In 1952 Danish physician Jorgen Pedersen proposed that high maternal sugar crosses the placenta and makes the baby produce extra insulin, which explains large babies. In 1964 John O'Sullivan and Claire Mahan set the first criteria for an oral glucose tolerance test in pregnancy and showed that women with gestational diabetes often later develop diabetes. In 2008 the HAPO study showed that the baby's risks rise steadily with the mother's glucose, even below old cutoffs, and in 2010 the IADPSG proposed new one-step criteria. Today ACOG and the ADA recommend universal screening at 24 to 28 weeks.",
  timeline: [["1824", "Heinrich Bennewitz describes diabetes that resolves after pregnancy."], ["1952", "Jorgen Pedersen proposes the fetal hyperinsulinism hypothesis."], ["1964", "O'Sullivan and Mahan publish criteria for glucose tolerance testing in pregnancy."], ["2008", "The HAPO study links rising maternal glucose to infant risks."], ["2010", "IADPSG proposes one-step diagnostic criteria."]],
  cell: "As pregnancy progresses, the placenta makes hormones that oppose insulin, including human placental lactogen, placental growth hormone, progesterone, estrogen and cortisol, along with inflammatory signals such as TNF-alpha. These reduce insulin signaling in muscle and fat (impaired insulin receptor substrate-1 and GLUT4 movement), so insulin sensitivity falls by about half by the third trimester. This keeps more glucose available for the fetus. Normally, pancreatic beta cells enlarge and multiply to make much more insulin. Gestational diabetes develops when beta cells cannot keep up, often because of pre-existing insulin resistance (obesity, family history, PCOS) or limited beta-cell reserve. Glucose crosses the placenta by facilitated diffusion through GLUT1, but maternal insulin does not. The fetal pancreas responds with high insulin (fetal hyperinsulinemia). Insulin acts as a growth factor, causing macrosomia with extra fat on the shoulders and abdomen, and it delays surfactant production in fetal lungs. After birth, the baby's high insulin persists while the glucose supply stops, causing neonatal hypoglycemia.",
  body: [["Baby's growth", "Fetal hyperinsulinemia causes large-for-gestational-age babies with extra fat on the trunk and shoulders, raising the risk of shoulder dystocia and birth injury."], ["Newborn metabolism", "Babies can have low blood sugar after birth, plus low calcium, high bilirubin (jaundice) and high red cell counts (polycythemia)."], ["Baby's lungs", "High insulin slows surfactant production, raising the risk of breathing problems after birth."], ["Pregnancy", "Gestational diabetes increases the risk of preeclampsia, too much amniotic fluid, cesarean birth and stillbirth if untreated."], ["Parent's long-term health", "Many people with gestational diabetes develop type 2 diabetes in later years and have higher cardiovascular risk."], ["Child's long-term health", "Children exposed to high glucose in the womb have higher risks of obesity and abnormal glucose later in life."]],
  symptoms: "Most people have no symptoms, which is why screening is routine. When glucose is very high, increased thirst and urination happen because glucose spills into the urine and pulls water with it. Fatigue is common but also typical of pregnancy. A measurement of a larger than expected uterus or extra amniotic fluid can be a clue, because fetal hyperinsulinemia drives growth and fetal urine output.",
  dx: "Screening is done at 24 to 28 weeks of pregnancy, and earlier for people with risk factors to look for diabetes that existed before pregnancy. The two-step approach starts with a non-fasting glucose challenge drink and a blood test one hour later, followed for abnormal results by a longer 3-hour fasting oral glucose tolerance test with several blood draws, judged by Carpenter-Coustan or similar criteria. The one-step approach uses a single 2-hour fasting oral glucose tolerance test with IADPSG cutoffs, where one abnormal value is enough. An A1C or glucose that meets diabetes thresholds in early pregnancy suggests pre-existing diabetes rather than gestational diabetes.",
  tx: "Medical nutrition therapy, with balanced meals that spread carbohydrates through the day, and regular physical activity are first-line and control glucose for many people. Blood glucose is checked several times a day with a meter or continuous monitor. When targets are not met, insulin is the preferred medicine because it does not cross the placenta in meaningful amounts; basal insulins (NPH, detemir) and rapid-acting mealtime insulins (aspart, lispro) are used. Metformin and glyburide cross the placenta and are considered second-line by ACOG and the ADA, with metformin used in some cases. Fetal growth and well-being are monitored, and delivery timing is planned. Newborns have their glucose checked after birth. Breastfeeding is encouraged. Because of future risk, a glucose tolerance test is done 4 to 12 weeks after birth, then diabetes screening every 1 to 3 years for life.",
  drugs: ["insulin-nph", "insulin-detemir", "insulin-aspart", "insulin-lispro", "regular-insulin", "insulin-glargine", "metformin", "glyburide", "dextrose"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Insulin is the preferred drug for gestational diabetes because it does not cross the placenta; screening is at 24 to 28 weeks."], ["Pedersen hypothesis", "Maternal glucose crosses the placenta, insulin does not; the fetus makes extra insulin, grows large and becomes hypoglycemic after birth."], ["NCLEX focus", "Check the newborn's blood glucose soon after birth and watch for jitteriness, poor feeding and lethargy."], ["Often confused with", "Pre-existing type 2 diabetes first found in pregnancy, which is suspected when glucose is high early in pregnancy and carries a higher risk of birth defects."]],
  red: "Signs of low blood sugar in someone using insulin, such as shaking, sweating and confusion that do not improve, fainting, or warning signs of preeclampsia such as a severe headache or vision changes, need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Gestational diabetes is sometimes called a stress test for the future, since it can reveal a tendency to type 2 diabetes decades before it would otherwise appear.",
  src: ["ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 190: Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (2018)", "ADA Standards of Care in Diabetes (2025): Management of Diabetes in Pregnancy", "HAPO Study Cooperative Research Group. N Engl J Med 2008", "O'Sullivan JB, Mahan CM. Diabetes 1964"]
}
});

;
DB.DIS = DB.DIS || {};
Object.assign(DB.DIS, {
"preterm-labor": {
  name: "Preterm labor",
  sys: "Pregnancy & Reproductive",
  aka: ["premature labor", "preterm birth", "PTL", "PTB"],
  what: "Preterm labor is labor that starts before 37 weeks of pregnancy, with regular contractions that open the cervix. Babies born early may have trouble breathing, feeding and staying warm because their organs are not yet mature.",
  origin: "Babies born too early have always been at risk, but for most of history little could be done. A turning point came in 1969 when New Zealand obstetrician Graham Liggins gave steroids to pregnant sheep to study labor and noticed that the premature lambs breathed far better than expected. With pediatrician Ross Howie he ran a landmark trial in Auckland, published in 1972, showing that steroids given to the mother before preterm birth reduced breathing problems and deaths in newborns. Over the following decades drugs that relax the uterus (tocolytics) were tried to buy time for those steroids to work. In the 2000s, trials showed that magnesium sulfate given before very early birth lowers the risk of cerebral palsy, and that vaginal progesterone helps prevent preterm birth in people with a short cervix. Hydroxyprogesterone caproate injections were approved in 2011 but were withdrawn from the US market in 2023 after a larger trial showed no benefit.",
  timeline: [["1969", "Graham Liggins notices that steroid-exposed premature lambs breathe unusually well."], ["1972", "Liggins and Howie publish their trial showing antenatal steroids reduce newborn breathing problems and death."], ["2008", "The BEAM trial links magnesium sulfate before very early birth to less cerebral palsy."], ["2011", "The FDA adds a boxed warning against prolonged use of injectable terbutaline to stop labor."], ["2023", "Hydroxyprogesterone caproate is withdrawn from the US market after it failed to show benefit."]],
  cell: "Preterm labor is a syndrome with several pathways that end in the same place: early activation of the uterus, cervix and fetal membranes. Infection or inflammation (often bacteria rising from the vagina) triggers immune cells in the decidua and membranes to release IL-1, IL-6, IL-8 and TNF-alpha. These cytokines switch on COX-2, raising prostaglandins E2 and F2-alpha. Other triggers include bleeding in the decidua (thrombin is a strong uterine stimulant), overstretching of the uterus by twins or extra fluid, and maternal or fetal stress that raises placental corticotropin-releasing hormone. Prostaglandins and stretch increase oxytocin receptors and gap junctions (connexin-43) in the muscle so that contractions become coordinated. Matrix metalloproteinases break down collagen in the cervix and membranes, causing cervical ripening and sometimes early rupture of membranes. A functional drop in progesterone action removes the brake that normally keeps the uterus quiet.",
  body: [["Uterus", "Muscle cells gain oxytocin receptors and gap junctions, so weak irregular tightening turns into regular, coordinated contractions."], ["Cervix", "Collagen breakdown and water uptake soften, shorten and open the cervix, which is the change that defines true labor."], ["Fetal membranes", "Inflammation and enzymes weaken the membranes, which can rupture early (preterm premature rupture of membranes) and let infection in."], ["Baby's lungs", "Before about 34 weeks the lungs make too little surfactant, so the baby is at risk of respiratory distress syndrome."], ["Baby's brain", "Fragile blood vessels near the ventricles can bleed (intraventricular hemorrhage), and early birth raises the risk of cerebral palsy."], ["Baby's gut and eyes", "Immature intestines are prone to necrotizing enterocolitis, and immature retinal vessels can grow abnormally (retinopathy of prematurity)."]],
  symptoms: "Regular tightening or cramping of the belly happens because the uterine muscle is contracting in a coordinated way. Low back ache and pelvic pressure come from the baby moving down and the cervix stretching. A change in vaginal discharge, spotting or a gush of fluid can mean the cervix is opening or the membranes have broken. Some people feel only mild period-like cramps, which is why early preterm labor is easy to miss.",
  dx: "Preterm labor is diagnosed when regular contractions occur before 37 weeks along with a change in the cervix seen on exam, or a cervix that is already well opened. Transvaginal ultrasound measures cervical length; a long cervix makes birth in the next week unlikely. A fetal fibronectin swab is mainly useful when negative, because a negative result makes delivery within about two weeks unlikely. Testing looks for ruptured membranes, group B strep, urinary and other infections, and the baby's well-being on fetal monitoring.",
  tx: "Care aims to give the baby the best start rather than to stop labor forever. Antenatal corticosteroids such as betamethasone cross the placenta and switch on genes in fetal lung cells that make surfactant, lowering breathing problems and death. Magnesium sulfate given before very early birth protects the baby's brain and lowers cerebral palsy risk. Tocolytics may delay delivery for about 48 hours so steroids can work or the mother can be moved to a hospital with a NICU: nifedipine blocks L-type calcium channels in uterine muscle, indomethacin blocks prostaglandin production (used before 32 weeks because it can narrow the fetal ductus arteriosus and reduce amniotic fluid), and terbutaline is a beta-2 agonist used only short term. Antibiotics such as penicillin G or ampicillin prevent group B strep infection in the newborn. For prevention, vaginal progesterone helps people with a short cervix, and a cervical stitch (cerclage) is used in selected cases.",
  drugs: ["betamethasone", "dexamethasone", "magnesium-sulfate", "nifedipine", "indomethacin", "terbutaline", "progesterone", "penicillin-g", "ampicillin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Antenatal corticosteroids (betamethasone) are the single most helpful drug for a baby about to be born early; they speed up fetal lung surfactant."], ["Memory trick", "Steroids for the lungs, magnesium for the brain, tocolytics to buy time."], ["Indomethacin rule", "Indomethacin is avoided after 32 weeks because it can close the fetal ductus arteriosus early and lower amniotic fluid."], ["Definition", "Preterm means before 37 weeks; viability is generally considered around 22 to 24 weeks."]],
  red: "Regular contractions, a gush or steady leak of fluid, vaginal bleeding, or strong pelvic pressure before 37 weeks needs urgent evaluation at a hospital labor unit, and heavy bleeding or a baby being born needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The idea of giving steroids to speed up a baby's lungs came from an unexpected result in lambs, in a study that was actually trying to learn what starts labor.",
  src: ["ACOG Practice Bulletin: Management of Preterm Labor", "ACOG Committee Opinion: Antenatal Corticosteroid Therapy for Fetal Maturation", "Liggins GC, Howie RN. Pediatrics 1972", "Rouse DJ et al. BEAM trial. N Engl J Med 2008", "Williams Obstetrics"]
},
"ectopic-pregnancy": {
  name: "Ectopic pregnancy",
  sys: "Pregnancy & Reproductive",
  aka: ["tubal pregnancy", "extrauterine pregnancy"],
  what: "An ectopic pregnancy is a pregnancy that implants outside the main cavity of the uterus, most often in a fallopian tube. It cannot grow into a healthy baby and can rupture and cause dangerous internal bleeding.",
  origin: "Pregnancies outside the womb were described by early physicians and anatomists, and for centuries rupture was almost always fatal because there was no safe surgery. In 1883 British surgeon Lawson Tait performed one of the first successful operations to remove a ruptured tubal pregnancy and stop the bleeding, and surgery soon became the standard rescue. In the 20th century blood transfusion and antibiotics made that surgery far safer. The 1970s and 1980s brought sensitive hCG blood tests and transvaginal ultrasound, so ectopic pregnancies could be found before they ruptured. In the 1980s doctors began treating early, unruptured ectopic pregnancies with methotrexate, a drug already used for placental tumors, and laparoscopic surgery replaced large open incisions for many patients. Today most ectopic pregnancies in high-resource settings are diagnosed early, but rupture is still a leading cause of pregnancy-related death in the first trimester.",
  timeline: [["1883", "Lawson Tait reports successful surgery for ruptured tubal pregnancy."], ["1970s", "Sensitive hCG tests and better ultrasound allow earlier diagnosis."], ["1980s", "Methotrexate is introduced as a medical treatment for early ectopic pregnancy."], ["1980s", "Laparoscopic surgery becomes common for removing tubal pregnancies."]],
  cell: "After fertilization in the ampulla of the fallopian tube, the embryo is normally moved toward the uterus over several days by cilia on tubal lining cells and by waves of tubal smooth muscle. When the tube is damaged, most often by past pelvic inflammatory disease from Chlamydia trachomatis or gonorrhea, prior tubal surgery or a previous ectopic, the cilia are fewer and the lining is scarred, so the embryo stalls. Smoking also reduces ciliary beating. The embryo then implants in the tubal wall, which lacks the thick decidua of the uterus. Trophoblast cells invade the thin wall and its blood vessels, while hCG keeps the corpus luteum making progesterone. The tube cannot stretch enough, so as the pregnancy grows (often around 6 to 8 weeks) the wall thins and ruptures, bleeding from tubal and ovarian artery branches into the abdomen. The most common site is the ampulla.",
  body: [["Fallopian tube", "The pregnancy stretches and erodes the tube wall, causing pain and eventually rupture with bleeding."], ["Abdomen and pelvis", "Blood in the peritoneal cavity irritates the lining, causing sharp pain, tenderness and sometimes pain felt in the shoulder from diaphragm irritation."], ["Circulation", "Rapid internal bleeding leads to fast heart rate, low blood pressure and hemorrhagic shock."], ["Uterus", "The uterine lining responds to hormones but has no pregnancy inside; falling hormone support can cause vaginal bleeding."], ["Future fertility", "Tube damage or removal can lower future fertility and raises the chance of another ectopic pregnancy."]],
  symptoms: "The classic picture is a missed period, then abdominal or pelvic pain and vaginal bleeding. The missed period and positive pregnancy test come from hCG made by the trophoblast. Pain on one side comes from the tube being stretched. Bleeding happens because hormone levels are often lower and unstable compared with a normal pregnancy, so the uterine lining sheds. Shoulder tip pain, dizziness or fainting suggest blood in the abdomen irritating the diaphragm and lowering blood volume.",
  dx: "Diagnosis relies on a positive pregnancy test plus transvaginal ultrasound. Finding no pregnancy inside the uterus when the hCG level is above the range where one should be visible strongly suggests an ectopic pregnancy, and a mass or free fluid next to the uterus supports it. Serial hCG levels that rise more slowly than expected or plateau also point to an abnormal pregnancy. Blood type and Rh status, a blood count and kidney and liver tests are checked, especially before methotrexate.",
  tx: "Treatment removes the pregnancy before it ruptures or stops the bleeding if it has. Methotrexate, a folate antagonist that blocks dihydrofolate reductase, stops the rapidly dividing trophoblast cells from making DNA, so the pregnancy tissue dies and is absorbed; it is used for stable patients with an unruptured ectopic and follow-up hCG checks until levels fall to zero. Surgery, usually by laparoscopy, either opens the tube to remove the pregnancy (salpingostomy) or removes the tube (salpingectomy). A ruptured ectopic with shock needs emergency surgery, IV fluids and blood transfusion. Very early cases with falling hCG may be watched closely. Rh-negative patients may receive Rho(D) immune globulin to prevent Rh sensitization. Emotional support matters, since this is a pregnancy loss.",
  drugs: ["methotrexate", "rho-d-immune-globulin", "lactated-ringers", "normal-saline"],
  mem: [["Classic triad", "Amenorrhea, abdominal pain and vaginal bleeding in a person of reproductive age means ectopic pregnancy until proven otherwise."], ["Most-tested fact", "The most common site is the ampulla of the fallopian tube, and the biggest risk factor is prior tubal damage such as pelvic inflammatory disease."], ["Memory trick", "Methotrexate stops Multiplying trophoblast by blocking folate."], ["Often confused with", "Miscarriage, ovarian cyst rupture or torsion, and appendicitis can look similar; ultrasound and hCG sort them out."]],
  red: "Severe belly or pelvic pain, shoulder tip pain, fainting, dizziness or heavy bleeding in early pregnancy can mean a ruptured ectopic pregnancy and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Rarely, an ectopic pregnancy can implant on the ovary, the cervix, a cesarean scar or even elsewhere in the abdomen.",
  src: ["ACOG Practice Bulletin: Tubal Ectopic Pregnancy (2018)", "Williams Obstetrics", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Tait L. Lectures on Ectopic Pregnancy (1888)"]
},
"endometriosis": {
  name: "Endometriosis",
  sys: "Pregnancy & Reproductive",
  aka: ["endo", "endometrioma", "chocolate cyst"],
  what: "Endometriosis is a long-term condition where tissue similar to the lining of the uterus grows outside the uterus, such as on the ovaries and pelvic lining. It causes inflammation, painful periods, pelvic pain and sometimes trouble getting pregnant.",
  origin: "Austrian pathologist Karl von Rokitansky described lining-like glands outside their usual place in 1860. In the 1920s American gynecologist John Sampson studied the condition in detail, popularized the name endometriosis and proposed that menstrual blood flowing backward through the fallopian tubes carries cells into the pelvis, the retrograde menstruation theory still central today. Because it was hard to see without surgery, many people waited years for a diagnosis. Laparoscopy, widely used from the 1970s, made the lesions visible. Hormonal treatments followed: progestins and birth control pills, danazol in the 1970s, and GnRH agonists such as leuprolide in the late 1980s and 1990s. In 2018 the FDA approved elagolix, the first oral GnRH antagonist for endometriosis pain, and relugolix combination therapy followed in 2022. Guidelines now accept making a clinical diagnosis without surgery so treatment can start sooner.",
  timeline: [["1860", "Karl von Rokitansky describes endometrial-like glands outside the uterus."], ["1920s", "John Sampson proposes the retrograde menstruation theory."], ["1970s", "Diagnostic laparoscopy becomes widely used to see lesions."], ["2018", "Elagolix becomes the first oral GnRH antagonist approved for endometriosis pain."], ["2022", "Relugolix combination therapy is approved for endometriosis pain."]],
  cell: "Most cases are thought to start with retrograde menstruation: endometrial cells flow backward through the tubes and land on the peritoneum and ovaries. Most people have some backflow, so disease depends on cells surviving and immune cells failing to clear them. Implanted cells attach to the lining, recruit new blood vessels (VEGF) and grow nerve fibers. Lesions are estrogen dependent and also make their own estrogen because they express aromatase, which is absent in normal endometrium, and a defect in steroidogenic factor-1 regulation keeps that loop on. They show progesterone resistance, so progesterone fails to calm them. Activated peritoneal macrophages release IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha and prostaglandin E2, which further boosts aromatase and causes pain. Each cycle the implants bleed and inflame, leading to fibrosis and adhesions. Other theories include coelomic metaplasia and spread through blood or lymph vessels, which explain rare distant lesions.",
  body: [["Pelvic lining", "Implants on the peritoneum bleed and inflame each cycle, causing pain, scarring and powder-burn or red, white and dark lesions."], ["Ovaries", "Blood-filled cysts called endometriomas (chocolate cysts) form and can lower ovarian reserve."], ["Fallopian tubes", "Adhesions can kink or block the tubes and distort pelvic anatomy, reducing fertility."], ["Uterus", "A related condition, adenomyosis, where lining tissue grows into the muscle, often occurs alongside it."], ["Bowel and bladder", "Deep implants can cause painful bowel movements or painful urination, especially during periods."], ["Nervous system", "Long-term inflammation sensitizes pain pathways, so chronic pelvic pain can persist even after lesions are removed."]],
  symptoms: "Painful periods (dysmenorrhea) happen because implants bleed and release prostaglandins and cytokines each cycle. Pain during sex (dyspareunia) comes from implants and scarring behind the uterus and on the uterosacral ligaments being pressed. Pain with bowel movements or urination comes from deep lesions on those organs. Infertility results from scarred tubes, ovarian cysts and an inflamed pelvic environment that harms eggs and implantation. Pain severity does not match the amount of disease seen.",
  dx: "Diagnosis often starts from the history of cyclic pelvic pain, painful periods and painful sex, with tenderness or nodules behind the uterus on exam. Transvaginal ultrasound can show endometriomas and deep disease, and MRI helps map deep lesions. Laparoscopy with biopsy shows the implants directly and was long the gold standard, but current guidelines allow a clinical diagnosis to start treatment. Diagnosis is often delayed by many years.",
  tx: "Treatment lowers estrogen stimulation and inflammation, or removes the lesions. NSAIDs block COX enzymes and reduce prostaglandin-driven pain. Combined hormonal contraceptives and progestins (norethindrone acetate, medroxyprogesterone, a levonorgestrel IUD) suppress ovulation and make implants quiet and thin. GnRH antagonists such as elagolix and relugolix combination block pituitary GnRH receptors right away, lowering estrogen. GnRH agonists such as leuprolide and goserelin first surge then desensitize the pituitary; add-back hormone therapy protects bone. Aromatase inhibitors are sometimes used off-label for resistant pain. Surgery excises or destroys lesions, removes endometriomas and frees adhesions; hysterectomy is reserved for selected cases. Fertility treatment, pelvic floor therapy and pain care are part of long-term care.",
  drugs: ["ibuprofen", "naproxen", "ethinyl-estradiol-norethindrone", "norethindrone-acetate", "medroxyprogesterone", "levonorgestrel-iud", "elagolix", "elagolix-estradiol-norethindrone", "relugolix-combination", "leuprolide", "goserelin"],
  mem: [["Classic triad", "Dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia and infertility, often with a fixed retroverted uterus or uterosacral nodules."], ["Most-tested fact", "Ovarian endometriomas are called chocolate cysts because they fill with old dark blood."], ["Mechanism key", "Lesions make their own estrogen through aromatase and resist progesterone."], ["Often confused with", "Adenomyosis is lining tissue inside the uterine muscle, giving a large, boggy, tender uterus."]],
  red: "Sudden severe pelvic pain with fever, vomiting or fainting can mean a ruptured or twisted ovarian cyst and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Endometriosis lesions have been found in rare cases far from the pelvis, including the chest, where they can cause a collapsed lung that recurs with periods (catamenial pneumothorax).",
  src: ["ACOG Practice Bulletin: Management of Endometriosis", "ESHRE Guideline: Endometriosis (2022)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "Sampson JA. Am J Obstet Gynecol 1927"]
},
"menopause": {
  name: "Menopause",
  sys: "Pregnancy & Reproductive",
  aka: ["perimenopause", "climacteric", "vasomotor symptoms", "genitourinary syndrome of menopause"],
  what: "Menopause is the natural end of menstrual periods, confirmed after 12 months without a period, usually around age 51 in the US. The ovaries make much less estrogen, which can cause hot flashes, sleep changes, vaginal dryness and bone loss.",
  origin: "The end of fertility has been noted since antiquity, but the word menopause was coined in 1821 by French physician Charles de Gardanne. In the 20th century scientists isolated estrogens, and in 1942 the FDA approved conjugated estrogens made from pregnant mares' urine, which became one of the most prescribed drugs in the US. Estrogen alone was later linked to endometrial cancer in the 1970s, so a progestogen was added for people with a uterus. In 2002 the Women's Health Initiative trial reported more breast cancer, stroke and blood clots with combined hormone therapy in older women, and hormone use fell sharply. Later analyses showed that timing matters: for healthy people under 60 or within 10 years of menopause, benefits often outweigh risks. Nonhormonal options grew too, with low-dose paroxetine approved in 2013 and fezolinetant, a neurokinin 3 receptor blocker, approved in 2023.",
  timeline: [["1821", "Charles de Gardanne coins the term menopause."], ["1942", "Conjugated estrogens are approved in the US."], ["2002", "The Women's Health Initiative reports risks of combined hormone therapy."], ["2013", "Low-dose paroxetine becomes the first approved nonhormonal treatment for hot flashes."], ["2023", "Fezolinetant is approved for hot flashes."]],
  cell: "Ovarian follicles are lost steadily from before birth. As they run low in the 40s, granulosa cells make less inhibin B and anti-Mullerian hormone, so the pituitary releases more FSH. Estradiol first swings widely in perimenopause and then falls once follicles are gone. Low estrogen removes negative feedback on KNDy neurons (kisspeptin, neurokinin B, dynorphin) in the hypothalamic arcuate nucleus. These neurons enlarge and signal through neurokinin 3 receptors to the nearby thermoregulatory center, narrowing the comfort zone, so small rises in core temperature trigger heat-losing flushes and sweating. In bone, estrogen loss raises RANKL and lowers osteoprotegerin, so osteoclasts resorb bone faster than osteoblasts rebuild it. In the vagina and urethra, estrogen receptor-rich epithelium thins, loses glycogen and lactobacilli, and pH rises. Lipids shift toward higher LDL.",
  body: [["Brain and temperature control", "Overactive KNDy neuron signaling triggers hot flashes and night sweats that disrupt sleep."], ["Bones", "Faster bone resorption causes rapid bone loss in the first years after menopause and raises osteoporosis and fracture risk."], ["Vagina and urinary tract", "Thinner, drier tissue causes dryness, painful sex, urgency and more urinary infections (genitourinary syndrome of menopause)."], ["Heart and vessels", "Loss of estrogen's effects on lipids and vessel walls is linked to rising cardiovascular risk after menopause."], ["Mood and sleep", "Hormone swings and night sweats can worsen sleep, mood and concentration, especially in perimenopause."], ["Skin", "Lower collagen production makes skin thinner and drier."]],
  symptoms: "Hot flashes are sudden waves of heat, flushing and sweating caused by the hypothalamus misreading body temperature as too high. Night sweats are the same event during sleep and cause tiredness. Irregular periods in perimenopause reflect erratic ovulation and swinging estrogen. Vaginal dryness and painful sex come from estrogen-dependent tissue thinning. Joint aches, mood changes and brain fog are common and partly tied to poor sleep and hormone shifts.",
  dx: "Menopause is a clinical diagnosis: 12 months in a row without a period in someone over about 45, with no other cause. Blood tests are usually not needed, though a high FSH can support the diagnosis in younger people or after hysterectomy. Menopause before 40 is called primary ovarian insufficiency and is evaluated further. A bone density scan (DXA) screens for osteoporosis.",
  tx: "Treatment eases symptoms and protects bone, matched to each person's risks. Systemic estrogen (estradiol or conjugated estrogens as pills, patches or gels) is the most effective treatment for hot flashes and also slows bone loss; people with a uterus also take a progestogen such as progesterone or medroxyprogesterone to prevent endometrial overgrowth. Low-dose vaginal estrogen treats dryness and urinary symptoms with little absorption. Nonhormonal options include fezolinetant, which blocks neurokinin 3 receptors on KNDy neurons, SSRIs and SNRIs such as paroxetine and venlafaxine, and gabapentin. Ospemifene, a selective estrogen receptor modulator, treats painful sex. Bone protection uses calcium, vitamin D, exercise and drugs like alendronate or raloxifene when needed. Layered clothing, cooler rooms and sleep habits help too.",
  drugs: ["estradiol", "conjugated-estrogens", "estradiol-progesterone", "progesterone", "medroxyprogesterone", "estradiol-vaginal", "conjugated-estrogens-vaginal", "fezolinetant", "paroxetine", "venlafaxine", "gabapentin", "ospemifene", "alendronate", "raloxifene"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Menopause shows high FSH and LH with low estradiol, because the ovaries no longer respond and feedback is lost."], ["Key safety rule", "A person with a uterus who takes estrogen also needs a progestogen to protect against endometrial cancer."], ["Timing hypothesis", "Hormone therapy started under 60 or within 10 years of menopause has a more favorable risk profile."], ["Often confused with", "Thyroid disease and pregnancy can also cause irregular periods and flushing and are ruled out when unclear."]],
  red: "Any vaginal bleeding after menopause needs prompt medical evaluation, and sudden chest pain, leg swelling with shortness of breath, or stroke signs in someone on hormone therapy needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Humans are one of very few species, along with some toothed whales such as orcas, where females regularly live for decades after their fertile years end.",
  src: ["The Menopause Society (NAMS) Hormone Therapy Position Statement (2022)", "Women's Health Initiative. JAMA 2002", "ACOG Clinical Consensus: Management of Menopausal Symptoms", "Williams Gynecology"]
},
"infertility": {
  name: "Infertility",
  sys: "Pregnancy & Reproductive",
  aka: ["subfertility", "female infertility", "male infertility"],
  what: "Infertility means not becoming pregnant after 12 months of regular unprotected sex, or after 6 months when the woman is 35 or older. It can come from either partner, both, or no clear cause, and many couples are helped by treatment.",
  origin: "Childlessness is described in the oldest written records, but real understanding came with 20th century reproductive endocrinology. In the 1960s gonadotropins extracted from the urine of postmenopausal women were first used to stimulate ovulation, and clomiphene citrate was approved in the US in 1967 as an oral ovulation drug. In 1978 Louise Brown, the first baby conceived by in vitro fertilization (IVF), was born in England after the work of physiologist Robert Edwards and gynecologist Patrick Steptoe; Edwards later received the 2010 Nobel Prize. In 1992 Belgian researchers reported intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI), injecting one sperm into an egg, which transformed treatment of male infertility. Embryo freezing, recombinant hormones and genetic testing of embryos have since improved success, and millions of babies worldwide have been born through IVF.",
  timeline: [["1967", "Clomiphene citrate is approved in the US to induce ovulation."], ["1978", "Louise Brown, the first IVF baby, is born in England."], ["1992", "ICSI is first reported, revolutionizing male factor treatment."], ["2010", "Robert Edwards receives the Nobel Prize for developing IVF."]],
  cell: "Pregnancy needs a working hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis, healthy eggs and sperm, open tubes and a receptive uterus. Pulsatile GnRH drives FSH and LH; FSH grows follicles and LH triggers ovulation. Ovulatory disorders are the most common female cause. In polycystic ovary syndrome, high LH and insulin resistance push theca cells to make excess androgens, follicles stall and no egg is released. Hypothalamic suppression from low energy, stress or heavy exercise slows GnRH pulses, and high prolactin inhibits GnRH. With age, the number and quality of eggs fall and meiotic errors (aneuploidy) rise. Tubal factor follows scarring from Chlamydia or endometriosis. Uterine causes include fibroids, polyps and scarring. In men, spermatogenesis needs FSH acting on Sertoli cells and high testicular testosterone made by Leydig cells under LH; problems include varicocele, genetic causes such as Klinefelter syndrome or Y chromosome microdeletions, blocked ducts and outside testosterone, which suppresses LH and FSH and shuts down sperm production.",
  body: [["Ovaries", "Lack of ovulation, low egg reserve or poor egg quality reduce the chance of conception."], ["Fallopian tubes", "Scarred or blocked tubes stop the egg and sperm from meeting and raise the risk of ectopic pregnancy."], ["Uterus", "Fibroids, polyps or scarring can interfere with implantation or raise miscarriage risk."], ["Testes", "Low sperm count, poor motility or abnormal shape lower the chance that sperm can fertilize an egg."], ["Hormone axis", "Thyroid disease, high prolactin or hypothalamic suppression disrupt the hormone signals that control ovulation and sperm production."], ["Emotional health", "Infertility often causes grief, stress and relationship strain, which are a recognized part of care."]],
  symptoms: "Often the only sign is not becoming pregnant. Irregular or absent periods point to ovulation problems because without ovulation the cycle loses its regular progesterone phase. Excess hair growth and acne suggest the high androgens of PCOS. Painful periods and painful sex point to endometriosis. Milky nipple discharge suggests high prolactin. In men, small testes or reduced body hair can hint at low testosterone or a genetic cause.",
  dx: "Both partners are evaluated together. Semen analysis measures count, motility and shape. Ovulation is checked by cycle history and a mid-luteal progesterone level. Ovarian reserve is estimated with anti-Mullerian hormone, day 3 FSH and an ultrasound antral follicle count. Hysterosalpingography or saline sonography shows whether the tubes are open and the uterine cavity is normal. TSH and prolactin are checked when cycles are irregular. Unexplained infertility is diagnosed when these are normal.",
  tx: "Treatment targets the cause. Clomiphene blocks estrogen receptors in the hypothalamus so the brain releases more FSH and LH to induce ovulation. Letrozole, an aromatase inhibitor, is used off-label for the same goal and is often preferred in PCOS. Gonadotropins (follitropin, menotropins) directly stimulate follicle growth, and an hCG trigger (choriogonadotropin alfa) acts like the LH surge to finish egg maturation. In IVF, GnRH antagonists such as ganirelix or cetrorelix prevent early ovulation, eggs are retrieved and fertilized (with ICSI when needed), and progesterone supports the uterine lining. Dopamine agonists (cabergoline, bromocriptine) lower high prolactin. Metformin can help insulin resistance in PCOS. Surgery treats blocked tubes, fibroids or varicocele. Weight, smoking and alcohol changes improve chances, and counseling supports emotional health.",
  drugs: ["clomiphene", "letrozole", "follitropin", "menotropins", "choriogonadotropin-alfa", "ganirelix", "cetrorelix", "leuprolide", "progesterone", "cabergoline", "bromocriptine", "metformin", "folic-acid"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Clomiphene is a SERM that blocks hypothalamic estrogen feedback, raising FSH and LH to trigger ovulation."], ["Male factor trap", "Testosterone therapy suppresses LH and FSH and can stop sperm production, so it causes rather than treats male infertility."], ["Memory trick", "The causes in order: Ovulation, Tubes, Uterus, Sperm. Think of the path the egg and sperm travel."], ["Definition", "12 months of trying under 35, or 6 months at 35 or older, before an evaluation is recommended."]],
  red: "Sudden severe belly swelling, rapid weight gain, shortness of breath or very little urine during fertility treatment can mean severe ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome and needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Millions of babies around the world have been born through IVF since 1978, and Louise Brown herself later had children conceived naturally.",
  src: ["ASRM Committee Opinion: Fertility Evaluation of Infertile Women (2021)", "AUA/ASRM Guideline: Diagnosis and Treatment of Infertility in Men (2020)", "Steptoe PC, Edwards RG. Lancet 1978", "Speroff's Clinical Gynecologic Endocrinology and Infertility"]
},
"breast-cancer": {
  name: "Breast cancer",
  sys: "Pregnancy & Reproductive",
  aka: ["invasive ductal carcinoma", "invasive lobular carcinoma", "DCIS", "triple-negative breast cancer", "HER2-positive breast cancer"],
  what: "Breast cancer is a cancer that starts in the cells lining the milk ducts or lobules of the breast. It is the most common cancer in women in the US, and it can also occur in men. Many cases are found early by screening and are highly treatable.",
  origin: "The ancient Egyptian Edwin Smith papyrus describes bulging tumors of the breast and notes there was no treatment. For centuries surgery was crude and dangerous. In the 1890s American surgeon William Halsted popularized the radical mastectomy, removing the breast, chest muscles and armpit lymph nodes, which became standard for decades. In 1896 Scottish surgeon George Beatson showed that removing the ovaries could shrink advanced breast cancer, the first clue that hormones drive many tumors. Later trials led by Bernard Fisher showed that lumpectomy with radiation works as well as mastectomy for many patients. Tamoxifen, which blocks estrogen receptors, was approved in 1977. Mary-Claire King mapped the BRCA1 gene in 1990, and it was cloned in 1994. In 1998 trastuzumab became one of the first targeted antibody drugs, transforming HER2-positive disease, and CDK4/6 inhibitors, PARP inhibitors, antibody-drug conjugates and immunotherapy followed.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "The Edwin Smith papyrus describes breast tumors with no known treatment."], ["1896", "George Beatson shows that removing the ovaries can shrink breast cancer."], ["1977", "Tamoxifen is approved in the US for breast cancer."], ["1990", "Mary-Claire King's team maps BRCA1 to chromosome 17."], ["1998", "Trastuzumab is approved for HER2-positive breast cancer."], ["2015", "The first CDK4/6 inhibitor, palbociclib, is approved."]],
  cell: "Breast cancer arises from epithelial cells of the terminal duct-lobular unit after a series of DNA changes. In hormone receptor-positive cancer (most cases), estrogen binds estrogen receptor alpha, which drives cyclin D1 and the CDK4/6 pathway, phosphorylating Rb and pushing cells through the G1 checkpoint. In HER2-positive cancer, amplification of the ERBB2 gene floods the cell with HER2 receptor tyrosine kinases that signal through PI3K-AKT-mTOR and RAS-MAPK, driving growth and survival. Triple-negative cancer lacks ER, PR and HER2 and is often linked to BRCA1. Inherited BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutations break homologous recombination repair of double-strand DNA breaks, so errors pile up. PIK3CA and TP53 mutations are also common. Invasive lobular carcinoma typically loses E-cadherin (CDH1), so cells grow in single-file lines. Cells first stay inside ducts (ductal carcinoma in situ), then break through the basement membrane, invade lymphatics to axillary lymph nodes and can spread to bone, liver, lung and brain.",
  body: [["Breast", "A growing mass distorts tissue, can pull on ligaments to dimple the skin, retract the nipple or block lymph drainage causing orange-peel skin (peau d'orange)."], ["Lymph nodes", "Spread to armpit nodes is the strongest predictor of prognosis and can cause a firm lump under the arm."], ["Bones", "Bone is the most common distant site; tumor cells activate osteoclasts, causing pain, fractures and high calcium."], ["Liver and lungs", "Metastases can cause jaundice, abdominal pain, cough or shortness of breath."], ["Brain", "Spread to the brain, more common in HER2-positive and triple-negative disease, can cause headaches, seizures or weakness."], ["Whole body", "Advanced disease and treatment can cause fatigue, weight loss and higher blood clot risk."]],
  symptoms: "A painless, firm, irregular lump is the most common sign because tumor cells grow and stiffen surrounding tissue. Skin dimpling or nipple pulling inward happens when the tumor tethers the suspensory ligaments. Bloody nipple discharge can come from tumor in a duct. Redness, warmth and swelling of much of the breast can signal inflammatory breast cancer, where tumor cells block skin lymphatics. Many early cancers cause no symptoms and are found by mammography.",
  dx: "Screening mammography finds many cancers before they can be felt; the USPSTF (2024) recommends screening every two years from ages 40 to 74 for average-risk women. A suspicious lump or screening finding leads to diagnostic mammography and ultrasound, sometimes MRI. Core needle biopsy confirms cancer and tests estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor and HER2 status, which guides treatment. Staging uses tumor size, lymph node involvement (often with sentinel node biopsy) and imaging for spread. Genomic tests help decide on chemotherapy in some hormone-positive cancers, and genetic testing looks for BRCA and other mutations.",
  tx: "Treatment combines local and whole-body therapy based on stage and subtype. Surgery is lumpectomy or mastectomy with sentinel node biopsy, and radiation lowers local recurrence. Endocrine therapy treats hormone-positive disease: tamoxifen blocks estrogen receptors in breast tissue, aromatase inhibitors (anastrozole, letrozole, exemestane) block estrogen production after menopause, fulvestrant degrades the receptor, and goserelin or leuprolide suppress the ovaries. CDK4/6 inhibitors (palbociclib, ribociclib, abemaciclib) block the cell cycle step estrogen drives. HER2-targeted drugs include trastuzumab and pertuzumab, which bind the receptor, the antibody-drug conjugates ado-trastuzumab emtansine and trastuzumab deruxtecan, and the kinase inhibitor tucatinib. Chemotherapy uses anthracyclines, cyclophosphamide, taxanes and capecitabine. PARP inhibitors such as olaparib exploit BRCA repair defects. Pembrolizumab and sacituzumab govitecan help triple-negative disease. Denosumab or zoledronic acid protect bone.",
  drugs: ["tamoxifen", "anastrozole", "letrozole", "exemestane", "fulvestrant", "palbociclib", "ribociclib", "abemaciclib", "trastuzumab", "pertuzumab", "ado-trastuzumab-emtansine", "trastuzumab-deruxtecan", "tucatinib", "doxorubicin", "cyclophosphamide", "paclitaxel", "docetaxel", "capecitabine", "olaparib", "pembrolizumab", "sacituzumab-govitecan", "goserelin", "zoledronic-acid", "denosumab"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "The most common type is invasive ductal carcinoma; invasive lobular carcinoma loses E-cadherin and grows in single-file lines."], ["Drug match", "Trastuzumab targets HER2 and can weaken the heart; tamoxifen raises the risk of endometrial cancer and blood clots."], ["Memory trick", "Aromatase inhibitors are for After menopause, because before menopause the ovaries make most of the estrogen."], ["Often confused with", "Fibroadenoma is a smooth, rubbery, mobile benign lump in younger women."]],
  red: "During chemotherapy, a fever, shaking chills or feeling very unwell can mean a dangerous infection from low white cells and needs emergency care (call 911 if severe). Sudden weakness, confusion or severe back pain with leg weakness in someone with known cancer also needs emergency care.",
  fun: "Breast cancer occurs in men too; about 1 in 100 US breast cancers is in a man.",
  src: ["NCCN Guidelines: Breast Cancer", "USPSTF Breast Cancer Screening Recommendation (2024)", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease", "American Cancer Society", "Slamon DJ et al. N Engl J Med 2001"]
},
"cervical-cancer": {
  name: "Cervical cancer and HPV",
  sys: "Pregnancy & Reproductive",
  aka: ["human papillomavirus", "HPV", "CIN", "cervical dysplasia", "cervical intraepithelial neoplasia"],
  what: "Cervical cancer is a cancer of the cervix, the lower part of the uterus, and almost all cases are caused by long-lasting infection with high-risk types of human papillomavirus (HPV). Screening finds precancer early and the HPV vaccine prevents most cases.",
  origin: "Cervical cancer was once one of the leading cancer killers of women in the US. In the 1920s Greek-American physician George Papanicolaou noticed cancer cells in vaginal smears, and his 1943 monograph with Herbert Traut launched the Pap test, which cut US cervical cancer deaths dramatically as screening spread. In 1951, cells taken from Henrietta Lacks's cervical tumor without her knowledge became the HeLa line, the first immortal human cell line, later found to contain HPV 18. In 1983 and 1984 German virologist Harald zur Hausen and colleagues identified HPV 16 and 18 in cervical cancers, work that won the 2008 Nobel Prize. The first HPV vaccine was approved in 2006 and the nine-type vaccine in 2014. Countries with high vaccination, such as Australia and Scotland, have seen steep drops in precancer and cancer in vaccinated groups. The WHO launched a global plan in 2020 to eliminate cervical cancer as a public health problem.",
  timeline: [["1943", "Papanicolaou and Traut publish the Pap smear method."], ["1951", "HeLa cells are grown from Henrietta Lacks's cervical cancer."], ["1983", "Harald zur Hausen's team identifies HPV 16 in cervical cancer."], ["2006", "The first HPV vaccine is approved in the US."], ["2008", "Zur Hausen receives the Nobel Prize for linking HPV to cervical cancer."], ["2020", "The WHO launches its strategy to eliminate cervical cancer."]],
  cell: "HPV is a small DNA virus spread by skin-to-skin sexual contact. It reaches basal epithelial cells through tiny breaks, especially at the transformation zone where columnar cells change to squamous cells. Most infections are cleared by the immune system within two years. In persistent infection with high-risk types (mostly 16 and 18), the viral genome can integrate into host DNA, breaking the E2 gene that normally restrains the E6 and E7 oncogenes. E6 binds p53 and, with the cell ligase E6AP, tags it for destruction, so damaged cells avoid apoptosis. E7 binds and inactivates Rb, releasing E2F and forcing cells into S phase. Cells pile up mutations and progress from low-grade to high-grade cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN 1 to CIN 3), with p16 overexpression as a marker. Over years, cells break through the basement membrane to become invasive squamous cell carcinoma (most common) or adenocarcinoma. Low-risk types 6 and 11 cause genital warts. HPV also causes anal, oropharyngeal, vulvar, vaginal and penile cancers.",
  body: [["Cervix", "Precancerous changes cause no symptoms; invasive cancer forms a fragile mass that bleeds easily."], ["Vagina and pelvis", "Tumor spreads directly into the vagina, parametrial tissue and pelvic side walls."], ["Ureters and kidneys", "Pelvic spread can block the ureters, causing hydronephrosis and kidney failure, a common cause of death in advanced disease."], ["Lymph nodes", "Cancer spreads to pelvic and para-aortic lymph nodes, which affects staging and treatment."], ["Bladder and rectum", "Advanced tumor can invade these organs, causing blood in urine or stool and abnormal connections (fistulas)."], ["Throat and anus", "The same high-risk HPV types cause oropharyngeal and anal cancers in all sexes."]],
  symptoms: "Early HPV infection and precancer usually cause no symptoms, which is why screening matters. As cancer invades, bleeding after sex or between periods happens because the tumor surface is fragile and full of new vessels. Watery or foul-smelling discharge comes from tumor tissue breaking down. Pelvic or back pain, leg swelling and kidney problems appear when the tumor presses on nerves, veins or ureters.",
  dx: "Screening in the US uses primary HPV testing, HPV and Pap co-testing, or Pap cytology alone, depending on age, starting at 21 or 25 per current guidelines. Abnormal results lead to colposcopy, where a magnified view after applying acetic acid shows abnormal white areas that are biopsied. Biopsy grades the lesion as CIN or invasive cancer. Cancer is staged with the FIGO system using exam, MRI and PET-CT to look for spread.",
  tx: "Prevention is the most powerful tool: the HPV vaccine uses virus-like particles from the L1 shell protein to create neutralizing antibodies, and works best before any exposure. High-grade precancer is removed by LEEP or cone biopsy, or destroyed by cryotherapy or ablation. Early invasive cancer is treated with surgery such as radical hysterectomy, or fertility-sparing surgery in selected cases. Locally advanced cancer is treated with radiation plus cisplatin, which cross-links DNA and makes tumor cells more sensitive to radiation, and pembrolizumab may be added for higher-risk stages. Advanced or recurrent disease uses platinum chemotherapy with paclitaxel, bevacizumab (which blocks VEGF to cut off tumor blood vessels), pembrolizumab (a PD-1 blocker for PD-L1-positive tumors) and tisotumab vedotin, an antibody-drug conjugate that targets tissue factor.",
  drugs: ["hpv-vaccine", "cisplatin", "carboplatin", "paclitaxel", "bevacizumab", "pembrolizumab", "tisotumab-vedotin", "topotecan"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "HPV E6 tags p53 for destruction and E7 inactivates Rb, removing two key brakes on the cell cycle."], ["Memory trick", "HPV 16 and 18 cause cancer; HPV 6 and 11 cause warts."], ["Location", "Lesions start in the transformation zone, the area the Pap and HPV test sample."], ["Board fact", "Death in advanced cervical cancer is often from kidney failure due to ureteral obstruction."]],
  red: "Heavy vaginal bleeding with dizziness, or very little urine with swelling in someone with known cervical cancer, needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "HeLa cells have been used in research on the polio vaccine, cancer and many other topics, and in 2013 the Lacks family reached an agreement with the NIH on how her genome data is used.",
  src: ["ACOG and ASCCP Cervical Cancer Screening and Management Guidelines (2019)", "American Cancer Society Cervical Cancer Screening Guideline (2020)", "CDC: HPV Vaccination", "NCCN Guidelines: Cervical Cancer", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"bacterial-vaginosis": {
  name: "Bacterial vaginosis",
  sys: "Pregnancy & Reproductive",
  aka: ["BV", "Gardnerella vaginalis", "nonspecific vaginitis"],
  what: "Bacterial vaginosis is the most common cause of abnormal vaginal discharge in people of reproductive age. It happens when the normal protective lactobacilli drop and other bacteria overgrow, causing a thin gray discharge with a fishy odor.",
  origin: "Doctors long noted a common, irritating discharge they called nonspecific vaginitis because no single cause was found. In 1955 American physicians Herman Gardner and Charles Dukes described a bacterium they believed caused it, first called Haemophilus vaginalis and renamed Gardnerella vaginalis in 1980 in Gardner's honor. Researchers later realized BV is not one infection but a shift in the whole vaginal community, with many anaerobic bacteria replacing lactobacilli, so the name changed to vaginosis to show there is little inflammation. In 1983 Richard Amsel and colleagues published simple bedside criteria, and in 1991 Robert Nugent described a Gram stain score still used in research. Metronidazole, first developed for trichomoniasis around 1960, became the main treatment. Because BV often returns, research has turned to the role of sexual partners; a 2025 trial found that treating male partners at the same time lowered recurrence.",
  timeline: [["1955", "Gardner and Dukes describe the organism later named Gardnerella vaginalis."], ["1983", "Amsel criteria for diagnosis are published."], ["1991", "Nugent publishes the Gram stain scoring system."], ["2025", "A randomized trial shows treating male partners reduces BV recurrence."]],
  cell: "A healthy vagina is usually dominated by Lactobacillus species, especially L. crispatus, which ferment glycogen from estrogen-primed epithelial cells into lactic acid, keeping the pH low, and some make hydrogen peroxide and bacteriocins. In BV, lactobacilli fall and a mixed group of anaerobes overgrows, including Gardnerella, Prevotella, Fannyhessea (formerly Atopobium) vaginae and Mobiluncus. Gardnerella attaches to epithelial cells and forms a dense polymicrobial biofilm that protects the bacteria from antibiotics and the immune system, which helps explain frequent relapse. Anaerobes make amines (trimethylamine, putrescine, cadaverine) that raise the pH above 4.5 and cause odor. Their enzymes, such as sialidases, break down the protective mucus layer. Epithelial cells coated with bacteria shed as clue cells. Because few neutrophils are drawn in, there is little inflammation. The disturbed barrier raises the risk of HIV and other STIs, pelvic inflammatory disease and preterm birth.",
  body: [["Vagina", "Loss of lactobacilli raises the pH, and an anaerobic biofilm forms on the lining, causing discharge and odor with little redness."], ["Upper genital tract", "BV bacteria can ascend, raising the risk of pelvic inflammatory disease and infection after gynecologic surgery."], ["Pregnancy", "BV is linked to preterm birth, early rupture of membranes and infection of the uterine lining after delivery."], ["Immune defenses", "A damaged mucosal barrier increases the chance of acquiring HIV, gonorrhea, chlamydia, trichomonas and herpes."]],
  symptoms: "The thin, gray-white discharge that coats the vaginal walls comes from shed epithelial cells and bacterial overgrowth. The fishy odor comes from volatile amines and is often stronger after sex or during periods, because alkaline semen or blood releases more amines. Itching and redness are usually mild or absent because there is little inflammation, which helps tell BV apart from yeast and trichomonas. Many people have no symptoms.",
  dx: "Diagnosis often uses Amsel criteria, with at least three of four: thin homogeneous gray discharge, vaginal pH above 4.5, a fishy odor when potassium hydroxide is added (positive whiff test) and clue cells on microscopy (epithelial cells covered with bacteria so their edges look fuzzy). The Nugent Gram stain score is the lab gold standard. Molecular (NAAT) tests are also available. Culture for Gardnerella is not useful because it is found in many healthy people.",
  tx: "Treatment kills the anaerobes so lactobacilli can return. Metronidazole, taken by mouth or as a vaginal gel, is a nitroimidazole that is activated inside anaerobic bacteria and damages their DNA. Clindamycin cream or pills block the bacterial 50S ribosome. Tinidazole and single-dose secnidazole are related nitroimidazoles. Treatment is recommended for symptomatic people, including during pregnancy. Recurrence is common, and clinicians may use longer suppressive courses of metronidazole gel, sometimes after boric acid, which helps break up biofilm. Avoiding douching helps, and condoms reduce recurrence. Recent evidence supports treating regular male partners at the same time in selected cases.",
  drugs: ["metronidazole", "metronidazole-vaginal", "clindamycin-vaginal", "clindamycin", "tinidazole", "secnidazole", "boric-acid-vaginal"],
  mem: [["Amsel criteria", "Three of four: thin gray discharge, pH above 4.5, positive whiff test, clue cells."], ["Most-tested fact", "Clue cells are vaginal epithelial cells covered with bacteria; BV has few white blood cells."], ["Memory trick", "Compare the three: BV is gray and fishy with pH over 4.5; yeast is white, clumpy and itchy with normal pH; trichomonas is frothy green-yellow with a strawberry cervix and pH over 4.5."], ["Common mistake", "BV is a change in the vaginal flora, not a classic STI, though it is linked to sexual activity."]],
  red: "Pelvic pain with fever, or fluid leaking or bleeding during pregnancy, needs urgent medical care, and severe pain with fainting needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Gardnerella vaginalis is named after Herman Gardner, one of the two doctors who first described it.",
  src: ["CDC Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines (2021)", "Amsel R et al. Am J Med 1983", "Nugent RP et al. J Clin Microbiol 1991", "Vodstrcil LA et al. N Engl J Med 2025"]
},
"hyperemesis-gravidarum": {
  name: "Hyperemesis gravidarum",
  sys: "Pregnancy & Reproductive",
  aka: ["HG", "severe nausea and vomiting of pregnancy", "severe morning sickness"],
  what: "Hyperemesis gravidarum is extreme, persistent nausea and vomiting in pregnancy that leads to dehydration, weight loss and electrolyte problems. It is far more severe than ordinary morning sickness and often needs medical treatment.",
  origin: "Severe vomiting in pregnancy has been described since ancient times, and before IV fluids it could be fatal. Writer Charlotte Bronte died in 1855 during early pregnancy, and many historians believe hyperemesis played a role. In the late 1950s thalidomide was marketed in many countries for morning sickness and caused thousands of severe birth defects, a tragedy that reshaped drug safety rules worldwide. A combination of doxylamine, pyridoxine and dicyclomine, sold as Bendectin from 1956, was taken off the US market in 1983 because of lawsuits, even though studies did not show harm; the doxylamine-pyridoxine combination returned with FDA approval in 2013. For decades the cause was blamed on hCG or even on emotions. In 2023 a study led by geneticist Marlena Fejzo, who herself had severe HG, and colleagues showed that the hormone GDF15 made by the placenta, together with how sensitive the mother is to it, is a major cause.",
  timeline: [["1855", "Charlotte Bronte dies in early pregnancy, widely attributed to severe vomiting."], ["1950s", "Thalidomide is given for morning sickness, causing a wave of birth defects."], ["1983", "Bendectin is withdrawn from the US market because of litigation."], ["2013", "Doxylamine-pyridoxine is approved by the FDA for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy."], ["2023", "Research identifies the placental hormone GDF15 as a key cause of HG."]],
  cell: "Nausea and vomiting peak around 9 to 12 weeks, when placental hormones are highest. The placental hormone GDF15 acts on its receptor GFRAL, found in the area postrema and nucleus of the solitary tract in the brainstem, the brain's vomiting center. High GDF15 levels, plus genetic variants that make the mother more sensitive or that lower her own GDF15 before pregnancy (so she has little tolerance), strongly predict HG. Human chorionic gonadotropin also rises at this time; it shares a subunit with TSH and can stimulate the TSH receptor, causing transient gestational thyrotoxicosis with low TSH. Conditions with more placental tissue, such as twins or molar pregnancy, raise the risk. Repeated vomiting loses stomach acid, sodium, potassium and chloride, causing hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis and hypokalemia. Starvation leads to ketosis. Low intake of thiamine (vitamin B1), a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and transketolase, can cause Wernicke encephalopathy, especially if glucose is given without thiamine.",
  body: [["Brainstem", "GDF15 activates the vomiting center, causing relentless nausea and vomiting."], ["Fluids and kidneys", "Fluid loss causes dehydration, low urine output and acute kidney injury in severe cases."], ["Electrolytes", "Loss of stomach acid and potassium causes metabolic alkalosis and hypokalemia, which can lead to weakness and heart rhythm problems."], ["Thyroid", "High hCG stimulates the thyroid, lowering TSH, usually without needing treatment."], ["Liver", "Mild rises in liver enzymes are common and improve as vomiting settles."], ["Nervous system", "Thiamine deficiency can cause Wernicke encephalopathy with confusion, eye movement problems and unsteady walking."]],
  symptoms: "Constant nausea and vomiting many times a day happen because the brainstem vomiting center is being driven by placental GDF15. Weight loss of more than 5 percent of pre-pregnancy weight reflects poor intake and fluid loss. Dizziness, dark urine and a fast pulse are signs of dehydration. Excess saliva and sensitivity to smells are common. Weakness and fatigue come from low potassium and starvation. Many people also experience anxiety and depression from the long, exhausting illness.",
  dx: "HG is a clinical diagnosis of persistent vomiting in early pregnancy with weight loss, dehydration and often ketones in the urine, after other causes are excluded. Labs look for low potassium and chloride, metabolic alkalosis, high urea and creatinine, mildly raised liver enzymes and low TSH. Ultrasound checks for twins or a molar pregnancy. The PUQE score rates severity. Vomiting that starts after 10 weeks, or with fever, belly pain or headache, suggests another cause.",
  tx: "Treatment restores fluids and nutrition and calms the vomiting center. IV fluids correct dehydration, and potassium and other electrolytes are replaced. Thiamine is given before or with glucose-containing fluids to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy. Doxylamine-pyridoxine is first-line: doxylamine blocks H1 and muscarinic receptors in vomiting pathways and pyridoxine adds an antiemetic effect. Other antihistamines such as diphenhydramine and promethazine are used, along with dopamine D2 blockers (metoclopramide, prochlorperazine) that act on the chemoreceptor trigger zone and ondansetron, a 5-HT3 blocker. Acid suppression helps heartburn. For refractory cases, corticosteroids such as methylprednisolone may be used, usually avoided before 10 weeks because of a possible small risk of oral clefts. Small frequent bland meals help milder cases, and tube or IV nutrition is used when weight loss continues. Emotional support is important.",
  drugs: ["doxylamine-pyridoxine", "pyridoxine", "doxylamine", "ondansetron", "metoclopramide", "promethazine", "prochlorperazine", "diphenhydramine", "thiamine", "methylprednisolone", "lactated-ringers", "normal-saline", "potassium-chloride", "famotidine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Give thiamine before glucose to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy in prolonged vomiting."], ["Lab pattern", "Hypokalemic, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with ketonuria."], ["Memory trick", "HG means Huge Gestational hormones: think twins and molar pregnancy, which make more placenta."], ["Often confused with", "Ordinary morning sickness does not cause weight loss, dehydration or electrolyte problems."]],
  red: "Being unable to keep down any fluids for a day, very little urine, fainting, confusion or vomiting blood in pregnancy needs urgent medical care, and severe symptoms need emergency care (call 911). Thoughts of self-harm can be reached through the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline.",
  fun: "Catherine, Princess of Wales, had hyperemesis gravidarum in all three of her pregnancies, which brought the condition wide public attention.",
  src: ["ACOG Practice Bulletin: Nausea and Vomiting of Pregnancy (2018)", "Fejzo M et al. Nature 2024", "Williams Obstetrics", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"neonatal-jaundice": {
  name: "Neonatal jaundice",
  sys: "Children",
  aka: ["newborn jaundice", "neonatal hyperbilirubinemia", "physiologic jaundice", "kernicterus"],
  what: "Neonatal jaundice is yellowing of a newborn's skin and eyes from a buildup of bilirubin, a pigment made when red blood cells break down. It is very common and usually harmless, but very high levels can damage the brain if not treated.",
  origin: "Yellow newborns have been noted for centuries. In the 1870s and early 1900s pathologists described yellow staining of the deep brain nuclei in babies who died with severe jaundice, and German pathologist Christian Schmorl named it kernicterus in 1903. In the 1940s doctors linked severe jaundice to Rh blood group incompatibility, and Louis Diamond and colleagues developed exchange transfusion to remove bilirubin and antibody-coated cells. Phototherapy was discovered by chance in the 1950s at Rochford General Hospital in England, where nursing sister Jean Ward noticed that sunlight faded jaundice on babies' skin; pediatrician Richard Cremer and colleagues reported it in 1958. Rh immune globulin, introduced in 1968, prevented most Rh disease. The American Academy of Pediatrics issued guidelines in 2004 and updated them in 2022 with thresholds based on gestational age and risk factors.",
  timeline: [["1903", "Christian Schmorl names kernicterus."], ["1940s", "Exchange transfusion is developed for severe newborn jaundice."], ["1958", "Cremer and colleagues report phototherapy after a nurse's observation about sunlight."], ["1968", "Rh immune globulin is approved, preventing most Rh hemolytic disease."], ["2022", "The AAP updates its guideline for managing newborn hyperbilirubinemia."]],
  cell: "Heme from broken-down red cells is converted by heme oxygenase to biliverdin and then to unconjugated bilirubin, which is fat-soluble and travels bound to albumin. In the liver, the enzyme UGT1A1 attaches glucuronic acid to make conjugated, water-soluble bilirubin that is excreted in bile. Newborns make more bilirubin because they have a high red cell mass and their red cells live a shorter time. Their UGT1A1 activity is low for the first days, and a sterile gut plus intestinal beta-glucuronidase deconjugates bilirubin so it is reabsorbed (enterohepatic circulation). Together these cause physiologic jaundice peaking around day 3 to 5. Pathologic causes add more load, such as hemolysis from ABO or Rh incompatibility, G6PD deficiency or spherocytosis, or poor feeding that increases reabsorption. When unconjugated bilirubin exceeds albumin binding, free bilirubin crosses the blood-brain barrier and injures neurons in the globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus, hippocampus, brainstem auditory nuclei and cerebellum, causing acute bilirubin encephalopathy and kernicterus.",
  body: [["Skin and eyes", "Bilirubin deposits in skin and the whites of the eyes, spreading from head to toe as levels rise."], ["Brain", "Very high free bilirubin damages the basal ganglia and brainstem, causing kernicterus with movement disorders and cerebral palsy."], ["Hearing", "Brainstem auditory nuclei are sensitive to bilirubin, so hearing loss can follow severe jaundice."], ["Blood", "Hemolytic causes destroy red cells, causing anemia and faster bilirubin rise."], ["Liver and gut", "Immature conjugation and gut reabsorption of bilirubin keep levels high in the first week."]],
  symptoms: "Yellow skin and eyes appear as bilirubin builds up, starting on the face and moving down the body. Jaundice in the first 24 hours is never normal and usually means hemolysis. Breastfeeding jaundice in the first week comes from low intake, which slows stooling and increases bilirubin reabsorption. Breast milk jaundice is a later, harmless rise caused by milk factors. Sleepiness, poor feeding, high-pitched cry and arching of the back or neck are signs bilirubin is affecting the brain.",
  dx: "Babies are checked before going home using a transcutaneous meter or a total serum bilirubin level, which is plotted on an hour-specific chart against thresholds that depend on gestational age and neurotoxicity risk factors (AAP 2022). Testing looks for causes: mother and baby blood types, a direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test, blood count, reticulocytes and G6PD testing. A conjugated (direct) bilirubin is measured if jaundice lasts more than about two weeks or stools are pale, because conjugated hyperbilirubinemia suggests liver disease such as biliary atresia.",
  tx: "Treatment lowers bilirubin before it can harm the brain. Frequent feeding helps babies pass stool and remove bilirubin. Phototherapy shines blue-green light on the skin, which changes bilirubin into water-soluble isomers, especially lumirubin, that are excreted in bile and urine without conjugation. When levels approach dangerous thresholds or signs of brain injury appear, exchange transfusion replaces the baby's blood to remove bilirubin and antibody-coated cells. For immune hemolysis from ABO or Rh incompatibility, IV immune globulin blocks Fc receptors on macrophages and slows red cell destruction. Rh immune globulin given to Rh-negative mothers prevents future Rh disease. Sunlight through a window is not a safe substitute for phototherapy.",
  drugs: ["intravenous-immune-globulin", "rho-d-immune-globulin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Jaundice in the first 24 hours of life is always pathologic, most often hemolysis."], ["Memory trick", "Breastfeeding jaundice is from not enough milk (first week); breast milk jaundice is from the milk itself (later, harmless)."], ["Danger zone", "Unconjugated bilirubin causes kernicterus; conjugated bilirubin in a newborn points to biliary atresia."], ["Phototherapy fact", "Blue-green light makes lumirubin, which leaves without needing the liver to conjugate it."]],
  red: "A jaundiced baby who is very sleepy, hard to wake, feeding poorly, has a high-pitched cry, arching or fever, or jaundice in the first day of life needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Phototherapy was discovered because a nurse noticed that the parts of babies' skin exposed to sunlight in a hospital courtyard were less yellow than the parts covered by blankets.",
  src: ["AAP Clinical Practice Guideline: Management of Hyperbilirubinemia in the Newborn Infant 35 or More Weeks of Gestation (2022)", "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics", "Cremer RJ, Perryman PW, Richards DH. Lancet 1958", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"kawasaki-disease": {
  name: "Kawasaki disease",
  sys: "Children",
  aka: ["Kawasaki syndrome", "mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome", "KD"],
  what: "Kawasaki disease is an inflammation of medium-sized blood vessels that mainly affects children under 5. It causes several days of high fever, rash, red eyes and swollen hands, and without treatment it can damage the coronary arteries of the heart.",
  origin: "In 1961 Japanese pediatrician Tomisaku Kawasaki saw a four-year-old boy with fever, rash, red eyes, swollen lymph nodes and peeling skin that did not fit any known illness. After collecting 50 similar cases he published a detailed report in 1967, calling it mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome. At first it was thought to be harmless, but autopsies soon showed that some children died from coronary artery aneurysms and clots. Cases were reported in Hawaii and elsewhere in the 1970s, showing it occurs worldwide. In 1984 Japanese researchers reported that IV immune globulin reduced coronary damage, and a 1986 US multicenter trial led by Jane Newburger confirmed that IVIG plus aspirin greatly lowered aneurysm rates. Its cause is still unknown, but it is now the leading cause of acquired heart disease in children in developed countries. During the COVID-19 pandemic a related condition, MIS-C, drew new attention to it.",
  timeline: [["1961", "Tomisaku Kawasaki sees his first patient with the illness."], ["1967", "Kawasaki publishes his report of 50 cases."], ["1984", "IVIG is reported to reduce coronary artery damage."], ["1986", "A US trial confirms IVIG plus aspirin prevents aneurysms."], ["2017", "The American Heart Association publishes an updated scientific statement on diagnosis and treatment."]],
  cell: "The leading theory is that an unknown trigger, probably an infectious agent entering through the respiratory tract, sets off a strong immune response in genetically susceptible children. Variants in genes such as ITPKC and CASP3, which normally restrain T cell activation, raise risk, and the disease is more common in children of Asian ancestry. The response activates neutrophils first, then CD8 T cells, IgA-producing plasma cells and macrophages that infiltrate the walls of medium-sized arteries, especially the coronary arteries. Cytokines such as TNF-alpha, IL-1 and IL-6 drive fever and inflammation. Matrix metalloproteinases and inflammatory cells destroy the internal elastic lamina and smooth muscle of the media, so the weakened wall balloons into an aneurysm. Later, myofibroblasts proliferate, which can narrow the artery (stenosis). Slow, turbulent blood flow in aneurysms plus a high platelet count promotes clots that can block the artery and cause a heart attack.",
  body: [["Coronary arteries", "Inflammation weakens the vessel walls, causing dilation or aneurysms in up to about a quarter of untreated children, which can clot or narrow later."], ["Heart muscle and valves", "Myocarditis, pericardial fluid and valve leakage can occur in the acute phase."], ["Skin and mucous membranes", "Vessel inflammation causes a rash, red cracked lips, strawberry tongue and red eyes without discharge."], ["Hands and feet", "Swelling and redness occur early, then the skin peels around the fingertips and toes in the second to third week."], ["Lymph nodes", "A single large neck lymph node is often present."], ["Other organs", "Inflammation can cause irritability, mild hepatitis, gallbladder swelling, joint pain and sterile pyuria."]],
  symptoms: "High fever for five days or more that does not respond well to fever medicine comes from cytokines released by the vasculitis. Red eyes without pus (bulbar conjunctival injection that spares the area around the iris) come from dilated small vessels. Red, cracked lips and a strawberry tongue reflect mucosal inflammation. A rash of many types and swollen red hands and feet come from inflamed small vessels in the skin. Children are often extremely irritable. Peeling of fingers and toes appears later, as inflammation settles.",
  dx: "Classic Kawasaki disease is diagnosed with fever for at least five days plus at least four of five features: bilateral conjunctival injection without discharge, changes of lips and mouth, a rash, changes of the hands and feet, and a neck lymph node of at least 1.5 cm. Children with fewer features can have incomplete Kawasaki disease, which uses lab and echo criteria. Labs often show high CRP and ESR, high white count, anemia, low albumin, raised liver enzymes, sterile pyuria and a high platelet count after the first week. An echocardiogram looks for coronary artery dilation and aneurysms.",
  tx: "Treatment aims to stop inflammation quickly and prevent coronary damage, ideally within the first 10 days of fever. IV immune globulin (IVIG) is the main treatment; it modulates the immune response by blocking Fc receptors, neutralizing cytokines and toxins, and calming T cell activation. Aspirin is given at anti-inflammatory levels during the acute phase and then at a low antiplatelet level until the heart is shown to be normal. Children who keep having fever after IVIG may receive a second IVIG course, corticosteroids, the TNF blocker infliximab, the IL-1 blocker anakinra or cyclosporine. Children with large aneurysms need long-term antiplatelet drugs plus anticoagulation such as warfarin or enoxaparin, and regular cardiology follow-up. Because aspirin with influenza or chickenpox raises the risk of Reye syndrome, the inactivated flu vaccine is given, and live vaccines are delayed for months after IVIG.",
  drugs: ["intravenous-immune-globulin", "aspirin", "methylprednisolone", "prednisone", "infliximab", "anakinra", "cyclosporine", "clopidogrel", "warfarin", "enoxaparin", "heparin"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "CRASH and BURN: Conjunctivitis, Rash, Adenopathy, Strawberry tongue (mouth changes), Hands and feet changes, with BURN for fever at least five days."], ["Most-tested fact", "Kawasaki disease is one of the few times aspirin is used in children; the main danger is coronary artery aneurysm."], ["Treatment pair", "IVIG plus aspirin, ideally started within 10 days of fever onset."], ["Often confused with", "Scarlet fever, measles, toxic shock syndrome and MIS-C after COVID-19 can look similar."]],
  red: "A child with Kawasaki disease or recent Kawasaki disease who has chest pain, trouble breathing, pale or cold skin, fainting or unusual sleepiness needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Tomisaku Kawasaki kept working on the disease that bears his name for decades and lived to the age of 95.",
  src: ["AHA Scientific Statement: Diagnosis, Treatment, and Long-Term Management of Kawasaki Disease (2017)", "Kawasaki T. Japanese Journal of Allergology 1967", "Newburger JW et al. N Engl J Med 1986", "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"]
},
"febrile-seizures": {
  name: "Febrile seizures",
  sys: "Children",
  aka: ["febrile convulsions", "fever fits", "simple febrile seizure", "complex febrile seizure"],
  what: "A febrile seizure is a seizure in a young child, usually between 6 months and 5 years old, that happens with a fever and without an infection of the brain. They are frightening to watch but most are brief and do not cause lasting harm.",
  origin: "Convulsions in feverish young children have been described since ancient times, and for centuries families feared they meant permanent brain damage or epilepsy. In the 20th century, large population studies in the US and Britain followed thousands of children and showed that most febrile seizures are benign, that about a third of children have another one, and that the risk of later epilepsy is only slightly higher than in other children. A 1980 NIH consensus conference defined febrile seizures and advised against routine long-term seizure medicine. The American Academy of Pediatrics published guidance on long-term management in 2008, concluding that the small benefits of daily drugs did not outweigh their side effects, and guidance on evaluating simple febrile seizures in 2011. Genetic studies later found families with ion channel mutations that make fever-triggered seizures more likely.",
  timeline: [["1980", "An NIH consensus conference defines febrile seizures and their management."], ["2008", "The AAP advises against continuous or intermittent drug prophylaxis for simple febrile seizures."], ["2011", "The AAP publishes guidance on evaluating a first simple febrile seizure."]],
  cell: "The young brain is naturally more excitable because excitatory glutamate circuits mature before inhibitory GABA circuits, and in early development GABA can even be less inhibitory because of chloride transporter patterns. A rapid rise in body temperature increases neuronal firing and affects temperature-sensitive ion channels, including sodium channels and HCN channels. Fever also releases cytokines such as IL-1 beta, which increases neuronal excitability through glutamate receptors. Hyperventilation from fever can cause alkalosis that further raises excitability. Genetics play a large role: febrile seizures run in families, and mutations in genes such as SCN1A (a sodium channel subunit) cause syndromes ranging from genetic epilepsy with febrile seizures plus (GEFS+) to Dravet syndrome. Common triggers are viral illnesses, especially human herpesvirus 6 (roseola) and influenza, and sometimes fever after vaccines. Very long febrile seizures (febrile status epilepticus) can injure the hippocampus and are linked to later mesial temporal sclerosis.",
  body: [["Brain", "Fever lowers the seizure threshold in the immature brain, triggering a brief burst of synchronized electrical activity."], ["Muscles", "Generalized seizures cause stiffening and rhythmic jerking of the arms and legs."], ["Breathing", "Breathing may pause or become irregular during the seizure, and the child may look blue briefly."], ["Recovery", "After the seizure, children are often sleepy or confused for a short time (postictal state)."]],
  symptoms: "The child may suddenly stiffen, jerk rhythmically, roll the eyes back and stop responding because neurons across the brain fire together. Seizures often happen on the first day of illness, sometimes before anyone notices the fever, because a fast temperature rise is a strong trigger. Drowsiness afterward reflects brain recovery. Simple febrile seizures are generalized, last less than 15 minutes and happen once in 24 hours. Complex seizures are focal, last longer than 15 minutes or recur within 24 hours.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical, based on a seizure with a temperature of at least 38 C (100.4 F) in a child of the typical age, with no brain infection or prior afebrile seizures. Evaluation looks for the source of fever. A lumbar puncture is done if there are signs of meningitis and may be considered in infants who are not fully vaccinated or who already received antibiotics. For simple febrile seizures, routine blood tests, EEG and brain imaging are not needed. Complex seizures may lead to further testing.",
  tx: "Most febrile seizures stop by themselves within a few minutes, and care focuses on safety: placing the child on their side, keeping the area clear and not putting anything in the mouth. Seizures lasting 5 minutes or more are treated with benzodiazepines, which enhance GABA-A receptor inhibition; clinicians use rectal or nasal diazepam, nasal or IM midazolam, or IV lorazepam. Longer seizures may need second-line drugs such as levetiracetam, fosphenytoin or phenobarbital. Fever reducers such as acetaminophen and ibuprofen make the child comfortable but do not prevent febrile seizures. Daily seizure medicines are not recommended for simple febrile seizures because the risks outweigh the benefits. Families of children with recurrent or prolonged seizures may be given a rescue medicine plan.",
  drugs: ["diazepam", "diazepam-nasal", "midazolam", "midazolam-nasal", "lorazepam", "levetiracetam", "fosphenytoin", "phenobarbital", "acetaminophen", "ibuprofen"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Antipyretics do not prevent febrile seizures, and simple febrile seizures do not need EEG or imaging."], ["Simple vs complex", "Simple: generalized, under 15 minutes, once in 24 hours. Complex: focal, over 15 minutes, or more than once in 24 hours."], ["Memory trick", "6 months to 6 years (often taught as 6 to 60 months)."], ["Recurrence", "About one third of children have another febrile seizure, more often if the first was before age 18 months."]],
  red: "A seizure lasting 5 minutes or more, a child who does not wake up normally afterward, trouble breathing, a stiff neck, a purple rash or repeated seizures needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Roseola, caused by human herpesvirus 6, is one of the most common triggers, because its high fever often comes before the rash appears.",
  src: ["AAP Clinical Practice Guideline: Febrile Seizures, Long-term Management (2008)", "AAP Clinical Practice Guideline: Neurodiagnostic Evaluation of the Child With a Simple Febrile Seizure (2011)", "NIH Consensus Statement on Febrile Seizures (1980)", "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"]
},
"pyloric-stenosis": {
  name: "Pyloric stenosis",
  sys: "Children",
  aka: ["infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis", "IHPS", "hypertrophic pyloric stenosis"],
  what: "Pyloric stenosis is a thickening of the muscle at the outlet of the stomach in young babies, which blocks food from passing into the intestine. Babies, usually between 2 and 8 weeks old, have forceful projectile vomiting after feeds and are hungry again right away.",
  origin: "Cases of babies vomiting and wasting were recorded in the 1700s, but Danish pediatrician Harald Hirschsprung gave the classic description of congenital pyloric stenosis in 1888. Early treatment was risky, and many babies died. Surgeons tried several operations, and in 1912 German surgeon Conrad Ramstedt performed a simple procedure that splits the thick muscle down to the lining without opening it, the pyloromyotomy that still bears his name. As anesthesia, fluid and electrolyte care improved, deaths fell to near zero. In 1977 ultrasound was shown to diagnose the condition reliably, replacing barium studies for most babies. In 1999 an outbreak study in Tennessee linked erythromycin given to newborns after pertussis exposure to a higher risk, and laparoscopic pyloromyotomy has become common since the 1990s.",
  timeline: [["1888", "Harald Hirschsprung describes congenital pyloric stenosis."], ["1912", "Conrad Ramstedt performs the pyloromyotomy that becomes standard surgery."], ["1977", "Ultrasound is shown to diagnose pyloric stenosis reliably."], ["1990s", "Laparoscopic pyloromyotomy is introduced."], ["1999", "Erythromycin given to newborns is linked to pyloric stenosis."]],
  cell: "The pylorus is a ring of circular smooth muscle that opens to let stomach contents into the duodenum. In pyloric stenosis, these muscle cells undergo hypertrophy and hyperplasia over the first weeks of life until the canal is long, thick and narrow. The cause involves genetics and environment. One leading mechanism is poor relaxation of the muscle: affected muscle has reduced neuronal nitric oxide synthase (nNOS), so less nitric oxide is available to relax it, and there are fewer interstitial cells of Cajal and nerve terminals. Persistent contraction may itself drive muscle growth. Macrolide antibiotics (erythromycin, azithromycin) given in early weeks act as motilin receptor agonists, causing strong pyloric contractions, and raise the risk. It is more common in boys (about 4 to 1), first-born children and families with a history. The blockage means the baby vomits stomach acid (HCl), losing hydrogen and chloride ions, which causes metabolic alkalosis. Dehydration activates aldosterone, which makes the kidneys save sodium and waste potassium and hydrogen, producing hypokalemia and a paradoxical acidic urine.",
  body: [["Stomach outlet", "The thickened pyloric muscle blocks emptying, so milk stays in the stomach and is vomited."], ["Fluids", "Repeated vomiting causes dehydration with sunken fontanelle, fewer wet diapers and lethargy."], ["Electrolytes and acid-base", "Loss of stomach acid causes hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis."], ["Kidneys", "Aldosterone-driven sodium saving leads to loss of potassium and hydrogen in the urine (paradoxical aciduria)."], ["Growth", "Poor nutrition leads to weight loss or failure to gain weight."], ["Skin", "Some babies develop mild unconjugated jaundice linked to reduced liver UGT activity during starvation."]],
  symptoms: "Projectile, non-bilious vomiting soon after feeds happens because the blockage is above the opening of the bile duct, so vomit contains only milk and stomach juices. The baby is hungry right after vomiting because they are not absorbing food. A small, firm, olive-shaped mass in the upper belly is the thickened pylorus. Waves of stomach muscle moving across the belly (visible peristalsis) show the stomach trying to push past the blockage. Weight loss and dehydration follow.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is suggested by a baby of the typical age with projectile non-bilious vomiting and sometimes a palpable olive. Ultrasound is the test of choice and shows a thickened, elongated pyloric muscle and a narrow channel that does not open. An upper GI contrast study, used less often, shows a thin string of contrast through the canal (string sign). Blood tests show low chloride, low potassium and high bicarbonate, which are corrected before surgery.",
  tx: "Pyloric stenosis is a medical urgency but not a surgical emergency. First, IV fluids correct dehydration and the electrolyte and acid-base problems, usually starting with saline boluses and then fluid with dextrose, adding potassium once the baby is passing urine. Correcting the alkalosis matters because anesthesia with alkalosis raises the risk of breathing pauses after surgery. Then a pyloromyotomy, done open or by laparoscopy, splits the thick muscle lengthwise down to the mucosa so the canal opens. Feeding usually starts again within hours, and the long-term outcome is excellent. Avoiding macrolide antibiotics in early infancy when alternatives exist lowers the risk.",
  drugs: ["normal-saline", "dextrose", "potassium-chloride"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Projectile non-bilious vomiting at 2 to 8 weeks with an olive mass and hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis."], ["Memory trick", "Pyloric stenosis: first-born boy, olive, hungry vomiter."], ["Drug link", "Erythromycin and azithromycin in early infancy raise the risk because they stimulate motilin receptors."], ["Often confused with", "Bilious (green) vomiting points to a blockage below the bile duct, such as malrotation with volvulus, which is a surgical emergency."]],
  red: "Green vomit, vomiting with a swollen belly, blood in vomit or stool, a very sleepy or limp baby, or no wet diapers for many hours needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Ramstedt's operation is so effective that it has remained almost unchanged for more than 100 years.",
  src: ["Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics", "Hirschsprung H. Jahrbuch fur Kinderheilkunde 1888", "Honein MA et al. Lancet 1999", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"neonatal-respiratory-distress": {
  name: "Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome",
  sys: "Children",
  aka: ["RDS", "hyaline membrane disease", "HMD", "surfactant deficiency disorder"],
  what: "Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome is a breathing problem in premature babies whose lungs do not yet make enough surfactant, the slippery substance that keeps the tiny air sacs open. Without it the air sacs collapse and the baby works very hard to breathe.",
  origin: "For much of the 20th century, hyaline membrane disease was the leading cause of death in premature babies, and its cause was a mystery. In 1959 Mary Ellen Avery and Jere Mead at Harvard showed that the lungs of babies who died of the disease lacked surfactant, building on earlier work by John Clements and others describing lung surfactant. In 1963 Patrick Bouvier Kennedy, the newborn son of President John F. Kennedy, died of the disease, drawing national attention and research funding. In 1971 George Gregory and colleagues showed that continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) could keep the lungs open. In 1972 Graham Liggins and Ross Howie reported that steroids given to mothers before preterm birth prevent the disease. In 1980 Japanese pediatrician Tetsuro Fujiwara reported successful surfactant replacement, and the FDA approved surfactants in 1990 and 1991, sharply lowering deaths.",
  timeline: [["1959", "Avery and Mead show that surfactant is missing in hyaline membrane disease."], ["1963", "Patrick Bouvier Kennedy dies of the disease, spurring research."], ["1971", "CPAP is shown to help babies with RDS."], ["1972", "Liggins and Howie show antenatal steroids prevent RDS."], ["1980", "Fujiwara reports successful surfactant replacement."], ["1990", "The first surfactant is approved in the US."]],
  cell: "Surfactant is made by type II pneumocytes, which begin producing it around 24 weeks of gestation, with adequate amounts usually by about 35 weeks. It is mostly phospholipid, especially dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (lecithin), plus surfactant proteins SP-A, SP-B, SP-C and SP-D, and is stored in lamellar bodies. It lowers surface tension at the air-liquid interface. By the law of Laplace (pressure equals 2 times tension divided by radius), small alveoli with high surface tension tend to collapse into larger ones. Without surfactant, alveoli collapse at the end of each breath (atelectasis), lung compliance falls and each breath needs huge pressure. Collapsed but perfused alveoli cause right-to-left shunting, hypoxemia and hypercapnia. Damaged epithelium and capillaries leak protein-rich fluid, and fibrin and cell debris form the pink hyaline membranes lining the airspaces. Risk factors include prematurity, male sex, maternal diabetes (high fetal insulin suppresses surfactant production), cesarean delivery without labor and multiple births. Glucocorticoids stimulate surfactant production.",
  body: [["Lungs", "Collapsed air sacs and hyaline membranes make the lungs stiff, so breathing takes great effort and oxygen exchange is poor."], ["Blood gases", "Shunting through collapsed lung causes low oxygen, high carbon dioxide and acidosis."], ["Heart and ductus", "Low oxygen and acidosis keep pulmonary pressures high, and a patent ductus arteriosus is common."], ["Brain", "Unstable blood pressure and oxygen in premature babies raise the risk of intraventricular hemorrhage."], ["Eyes", "High or fluctuating oxygen can drive abnormal retinal vessel growth (retinopathy of prematurity)."], ["Long-term lungs", "Ventilation and oxygen can injure developing lungs, leading to bronchopulmonary dysplasia."]],
  symptoms: "Signs appear within minutes to hours of birth. Fast breathing is the baby's attempt to make up for poor gas exchange. Grunting happens because the baby breathes out against a partly closed vocal cord to keep pressure in the lungs and stop the air sacs from collapsing. Nasal flaring and chest retractions show the extra effort needed to inflate stiff lungs. Bluish color (cyanosis) reflects low oxygen. Symptoms often worsen over the first 1 to 2 days before improving as the baby starts making its own surfactant.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is based on a premature baby with breathing distress soon after birth. A chest X-ray shows low lung volumes with a diffuse ground-glass or fine granular pattern and air bronchograms, where air-filled airways stand out against collapsed lung. Blood gases show low oxygen and high carbon dioxide. Infection is considered because early group B strep pneumonia can look identical, so blood cultures are often drawn. Historically, a lecithin to sphingomyelin ratio in amniotic fluid estimated lung maturity before birth.",
  tx: "Prevention starts before birth: antenatal corticosteroids such as betamethasone given to mothers at risk of early delivery speed up fetal surfactant production. After birth, CPAP keeps air sacs open and is often the first support. Exogenous surfactant, such as poractant alfa or beractant, is given directly into the windpipe through a breathing tube or a thin catheter; its phospholipids and proteins spread across the air sacs and lower surface tension within minutes. Mechanical ventilation is used when needed, with oxygen carefully targeted to avoid eye and lung injury. Caffeine citrate stimulates breathing and helps babies come off ventilators. Antibiotics such as ampicillin and gentamicin are often started until infection is ruled out. Warmth, fluids, nutrition and gentle handling support recovery.",
  drugs: ["poractant-alfa", "surfactant-beractant", "betamethasone", "dexamethasone", "caffeine-citrate", "ampicillin", "gentamicin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Surfactant is made by type II pneumocytes, mainly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine; a lecithin to sphingomyelin ratio of 2 or more suggests mature lungs."], ["Risk factor trick", "Maternal diabetes delays surfactant because fetal insulin blocks its production."], ["X-ray pattern", "Ground-glass lungs with air bronchograms and low volumes."], ["Often confused with", "Transient tachypnea of the newborn affects near-term babies after cesarean birth, is caused by retained lung fluid and clears within a day or two."]],
  red: "A newborn with grunting, flaring nostrils, sucking in between the ribs, fast breathing, pauses in breathing or a blue color needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Surfactant replacement was one of the fastest-adopted therapies in modern neonatology, and it helped cut US deaths from RDS dramatically within a decade.",
  src: ["AAP Committee on Fetus and Newborn: Respiratory Support in Preterm Infants at Birth (2014)", "European Consensus Guidelines on the Management of RDS (2022)", "Avery ME, Mead J. Am J Dis Child 1959", "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics"]
},
"hand-foot-mouth": {
  name: "Hand, foot and mouth disease",
  sys: "Children",
  aka: ["HFMD", "coxsackievirus infection", "enterovirus A71", "enterovirus 71"],
  what: "Hand, foot and mouth disease is a common, contagious viral illness in young children. It causes fever, painful sores in the mouth and a rash or small blisters on the hands, feet and sometimes the buttocks, and it usually gets better on its own in about a week.",
  origin: "Coxsackieviruses were discovered in 1948 by Gilbert Dalldorf and Grace Sickles in New York while searching for polio; they named the virus after the town of Coxsackie, where the first samples came from. In 1957 doctors in Toronto described an outbreak of fever with mouth sores and a blistering rash on the hands and feet, caused by coxsackievirus A16. The name hand, foot and mouth disease came from a 1959 to 1960 outbreak in Birmingham, England. Enterovirus A71 was first isolated in California in 1969, and later large outbreaks in Asia, including Malaysia and Taiwan in the late 1990s, showed it can cause severe brain and lung complications. Since about 2008, coxsackievirus A6 has caused more widespread rashes in children and adults and later nail shedding. China approved inactivated EV-A71 vaccines in 2015, but no vaccine is available in the US.",
  timeline: [["1948", "Coxsackieviruses are discovered in New York."], ["1957", "An outbreak in Toronto is linked to coxsackievirus A16."], ["1960", "The name hand, foot and mouth disease is used after a Birmingham outbreak."], ["1969", "Enterovirus A71 is first isolated in California."], ["2015", "China approves the first EV-A71 vaccines."]],
  cell: "HFMD is caused by enteroviruses, small non-enveloped RNA viruses in the Picornaviridae family, most often coxsackievirus A16, coxsackievirus A6 and enterovirus A71. They spread through stool, respiratory droplets and fluid from blisters, and can survive on surfaces. The virus binds receptors such as SCARB2 (for EV-A71 and CVA16) and enters cells of the mouth, throat and intestinal lining, where it replicates in lymphoid tissue such as tonsils and Peyer's patches. A brief viremia seeds the skin and mucous membranes. In the skin, the virus infects keratinocytes and causes cell swelling and death in the epidermis, forming small vesicles surrounded by red inflammation. EV-A71 is neurotropic: it can travel to the brainstem and spinal cord, causing brainstem encephalitis, acute flaccid paralysis and, through a surge of sympathetic activity, neurogenic pulmonary edema. Weeks after infection, temporary arrest of nail growth can lead to nail shedding (onychomadesis).",
  body: [["Mouth and throat", "Painful ulcers on the tongue, gums and inside of the cheeks make eating and drinking hard."], ["Skin", "Red spots and small gray blisters appear on the palms, soles, buttocks and sometimes around the mouth or the diaper area."], ["Fluids", "Mouth pain can lead to refusal to drink and dehydration, the most common reason for hospital care."], ["Nervous system", "Rarely, especially with EV-A71, the virus causes aseptic meningitis, encephalitis or paralysis."], ["Lungs and heart", "Severe EV-A71 disease can cause pulmonary edema and heart failure through brainstem injury."], ["Nails", "Nails may peel or fall off a few weeks later and then grow back normally."]],
  symptoms: "Fever, sore throat and feeling unwell come first, as the virus replicates in the throat and gut and triggers an immune response. Painful mouth sores follow because the virus damages the mucosal lining, so children drool and refuse to eat or drink. The rash and blisters on hands and feet appear as the virus reaches the skin, often not itchy but sometimes tender. Symptoms usually resolve in 7 to 10 days. Signs such as jerking movements, weakness or unusual sleepiness suggest the nervous system is involved.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is usually clinical, based on the typical pattern of mouth ulcers and a rash on the hands and feet in a young child, often during summer and fall outbreaks. Lab testing is rarely needed but PCR of a throat swab, vesicle fluid or stool can identify the virus, which helps in severe cases or outbreaks. Severe neurologic illness is evaluated with spinal fluid testing and MRI.",
  tx: "There is no specific antiviral treatment, so care is supportive. Acetaminophen or ibuprofen relieve fever and mouth pain by reducing prostaglandins in the brain and tissues. Cold drinks, soft foods and frequent small sips help children keep hydrated; aspirin is avoided in children because of the risk of Reye syndrome. Children who cannot drink enough may need IV fluids. Severe EV-A71 disease is treated in intensive care, and IV immune globulin has been used in some severe cases. Handwashing, cleaning toys and surfaces, and keeping children with fever or drooling sores out of childcare until they are better help limit spread.",
  drugs: ["acetaminophen", "ibuprofen"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Coxsackievirus A16 is the classic cause; enterovirus A71 causes the most severe neurologic disease."], ["Often confused with", "Foot-and-mouth disease of cattle is a different virus and does not cause this human illness."], ["Memory trick", "Herpangina is the same virus family but causes sores only at the back of the mouth, without the hand and foot rash."], ["Contagion fact", "The virus can be shed in stool for weeks after symptoms go away."]],
  red: "A child who cannot keep down fluids or has very few wet diapers, or who has a stiff neck, severe headache, jerking movements, weakness, trouble breathing or is hard to wake, needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Coxsackieviruses were found by accident while scientists were searching for the cause of suspected polio cases.",
  src: ["CDC: Hand, Foot, and Mouth Disease", "Red Book: Report of the Committee on Infectious Diseases (AAP)", "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics", "Alsop J et al. Br Med J 1960"]
},
"opioid-overdose": {
  name: "Opioid overdose",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["opioid poisoning", "opioid toxicity", "narcotic overdose", "fentanyl overdose", "heroin overdose"],
  what: "An opioid overdose happens when opioids such as fentanyl, heroin or prescription pain medicines slow breathing so much that the body does not get enough oxygen. It can be fatal within minutes, but the antidote naloxone can quickly reverse it.",
  origin: "Opium has been used and misused since ancient times. In the early 1800s German pharmacist Friedrich Serturner isolated morphine, the first alkaloid drawn from a plant, and the hypodermic needle later made injection common. Heroin was marketed in 1898, and fentanyl was synthesized by Paul Janssen in 1960, far more potent than morphine. In 1961 Jack Fishman and Mozes Lewenstein developed naloxone, a pure opioid antagonist, and filed a patent, and the FDA approved it in 1971. The US opioid crisis came in three waves: prescription opioids from the late 1990s, heroin from around 2010 and illicitly made fentanyl from around 2013, which drove deaths to record levels. Communities responded with naloxone distribution and Good Samaritan laws. A nasal naloxone spray was approved in 2015, and in 2023 the FDA approved it for sale without a prescription. Nalmefene nasal spray was also approved in 2023.",
  timeline: [["1800s", "Friedrich Serturner isolates morphine from opium early in the century."], ["1960", "Paul Janssen synthesizes fentanyl."], ["1971", "The FDA approves naloxone."], ["2013", "Illicit fentanyl begins driving a third wave of US overdose deaths."], ["2015", "Naloxone nasal spray is approved."], ["2023", "Naloxone nasal spray is approved for over-the-counter sale."]],
  cell: "Opioids act on mu opioid receptors, G protein-coupled receptors linked to Gi/o proteins. Activation inhibits adenylyl cyclase and lowers cAMP, opens G protein-gated inwardly rectifying potassium (GIRK) channels that hyperpolarize neurons, and closes voltage-gated calcium channels so less neurotransmitter is released. In the brainstem, mu receptors are dense in the preBotzinger complex, which generates breathing rhythm, and in chemosensitive areas that sense carbon dioxide. Opioids reduce the drive to breathe and blunt the response to rising CO2 and falling oxygen, so breathing becomes slow and shallow and can stop. Hypoxia then causes brain injury, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest. Mu activation also causes sedation, pinpoint pupils via the Edinger-Westphal nucleus, and reduced gut motility. Fentanyl is highly lipid soluble, enters the brain quickly and can cause chest wall rigidity. Combining opioids with alcohol, benzodiazepines or xylazine adds respiratory and CNS depression; xylazine is a sedative that naloxone does not reverse.",
  body: [["Brainstem breathing center", "Breathing slows, becomes shallow and may stop, causing low oxygen and high carbon dioxide."], ["Brain", "Sedation and coma come from widespread mu receptor effects; prolonged hypoxia causes lasting brain injury."], ["Eyes", "Pupils become pinpoint because opioids stimulate parasympathetic output to the iris."], ["Lungs", "Some patients develop noncardiogenic pulmonary edema, and vomiting while unconscious can lead to aspiration."], ["Heart", "Low oxygen leads to slow heart rate, dangerous rhythms and cardiac arrest."], ["Muscles", "Lying still for hours can crush muscle (rhabdomyolysis), releasing myoglobin that injures the kidneys."]],
  symptoms: "The classic triad is decreased consciousness, slow or stopped breathing and pinpoint pupils, all from mu receptor activation. Blue or gray lips and fingertips come from low oxygen. Snoring or gurgling sounds can mean the airway is partly blocked. The person may not respond to voice or pain. Slow heart rate and low blood pressure follow as hypoxia worsens.",
  dx: "Opioid overdose is a clinical diagnosis based on the triad of depressed consciousness, slow breathing and small pupils, often with a history or drug paraphernalia. A quick improvement in breathing after naloxone supports the diagnosis. Standard urine drug screens often miss fentanyl and other synthetic opioids, so negative results do not rule them out. Tests look for complications and co-ingestions, such as a blood gas, glucose, acetaminophen and salicylate levels, an ECG and creatine kinase.",
  tx: "The first goals are oxygen and breathing. Rescuers and clinicians open the airway and give rescue breaths or bag-valve-mask ventilation. Naloxone, a competitive mu receptor antagonist, displaces opioids and restores breathing within minutes; it is given by nasal spray, injection or IV and is adjusted to restore breathing rather than full alertness, because too much can cause abrupt withdrawal with vomiting and agitation. Naloxone often wears off before long-acting opioids, so people are observed for recurring sedation. Nalmefene is a longer-acting antagonist. After the crisis, medications for opioid use disorder lower the risk of future overdose and death: buprenorphine (a partial mu agonist), methadone (a full agonist) and extended-release naltrexone (an antagonist). SAMHSA's National Helpline (1-800-662-4357) offers free, confidential treatment referral. Naloxone in homes and communities saves lives.",
  drugs: ["naloxone", "nalmefene", "buprenorphine", "buprenorphine-naloxone", "buprenorphine-extended-release-injection", "methadone", "naltrexone-extended-release-injection"],
  mem: [["Classic triad", "Coma, respiratory depression and pinpoint pupils."], ["Most-tested fact", "Naloxone is titrated to breathing, not to wakefulness, and its effect may wear off before the opioid's does."], ["NREMT point", "Airway and ventilation come first; give rescue breaths with a bag-valve-mask while naloxone takes effect."], ["Often confused with", "Clonidine, xylazine and some sedatives also cause small pupils and sedation but do not reliably respond to naloxone."]],
  red: "Someone who will not wake up, is breathing slowly or not at all, or has blue lips needs emergency care (call 911); naloxone can be given while waiting. Poison Control is available at 1-800-222-1222, and SAMHSA's National Helpline (1-800-662-4357) offers free help finding treatment.",
  fun: "Most US states have Good Samaritan laws that give some legal protection to people who call 911 to report an overdose.",
  src: ["CDC: Understanding the Opioid Overdose Epidemic", "SAMHSA Overdose Prevention and Response Toolkit (2023)", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "AHA Guidelines for CPR and ECC: Opioid-Associated Emergencies"]
},
"acetaminophen-overdose": {
  name: "Acetaminophen overdose",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["paracetamol overdose", "APAP toxicity", "Tylenol overdose", "acetaminophen poisoning"],
  what: "Acetaminophen overdose happens when someone takes more acetaminophen than the liver can safely process, either at once or over several days. It often causes few symptoms at first, but it can destroy liver cells and cause liver failure unless the antidote is given in time.",
  origin: "Acetaminophen was first made in 1878 by American chemist Harmon Northrop Morse and tried in patients in the 1890s, but it was not widely used until the 1950s, when it was sold in the US as Tylenol. In 1966 doctors in Britain reported fatal liver damage after large overdoses. In 1973 researchers at the US National Institutes of Health, including Jerry Mitchell and James Gillette, showed that a reactive metabolite, later named NAPQI, binds liver proteins once glutathione runs out. That discovery pointed to a cure: in the 1970s Laurie Prescott in Edinburgh showed that N-acetylcysteine, which replenishes glutathione, prevents liver damage if given early. In 1975 Barry Rumack and Henry Matthew published the nomogram still used to decide who needs treatment. Oral N-acetylcysteine was approved in the US for overdose in 1985 and an IV form in 2004. Acetaminophen remains the leading cause of acute liver failure in the US.",
  timeline: [["1878", "Harmon Northrop Morse first synthesizes acetaminophen."], ["1966", "Fatal liver damage from overdose is reported."], ["1973", "NIH researchers describe the toxic metabolite and glutathione mechanism."], ["1975", "Rumack and Matthew publish their nomogram."], ["1985", "Oral N-acetylcysteine is approved in the US for acetaminophen overdose."], ["2004", "IV N-acetylcysteine is approved in the US."]],
  cell: "At normal doses, most acetaminophen is made safe in the liver by glucuronidation and sulfation and excreted in urine. A small fraction is oxidized by cytochrome P450 enzymes, mainly CYP2E1 with CYP3A4 and CYP1A2, into N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine (NAPQI), a highly reactive toxin that is immediately neutralized by glutathione. In overdose, the sulfation and glucuronidation pathways saturate, more drug is shunted to CYP2E1, and glutathione stores become depleted. Free NAPQI binds cysteine groups on cell proteins, especially mitochondrial proteins, causing oxidative stress, activation of the JNK pathway, mitochondrial permeability transition and loss of ATP. Hepatocytes die by necrosis. Damage is worst in zone 3 (centrilobular) hepatocytes around the central vein, which have the most CYP2E1 and the least oxygen. Chronic alcohol use, fasting and malnutrition lower glutathione and can raise risk. The kidneys can also be injured (acute tubular necrosis) because they have their own P450 activity.",
  body: [["Liver", "Centrilobular necrosis causes rising transaminases, then jaundice, bleeding problems and, in severe cases, acute liver failure."], ["Brain", "Liver failure lets ammonia and other toxins build up, causing hepatic encephalopathy and cerebral edema."], ["Blood clotting", "The injured liver stops making clotting factors, so the INR rises and bleeding becomes likely."], ["Kidneys", "Acute tubular necrosis can cause kidney failure, sometimes even without severe liver injury."], ["Metabolism", "Severe toxicity causes low blood sugar and lactic acidosis because the liver cannot make glucose or clear lactate."]],
  symptoms: "In the first 24 hours there may be no symptoms or only nausea, vomiting and sweating, which is why overdoses are easily missed. Between about 24 and 72 hours, right upper belly pain and tenderness develop as liver cells die and liver enzymes climb. By 3 to 4 days, the peak of injury, jaundice, confusion, bleeding and low blood sugar appear in severe cases as the liver fails. Those who survive usually recover liver function fully over the following weeks.",
  dx: "For a single acute ingestion with a known time, an acetaminophen level drawn at 4 hours or later is plotted on the Rumack-Matthew nomogram; levels above the treatment line mean N-acetylcysteine is needed. The nomogram does not apply to repeated ingestions over time, unknown ingestion times or extended-release products, where clinicians use the level plus liver enzymes. Tests include AST and ALT, INR, bilirubin, creatinine, glucose, blood gas and lactate, plus salicylate level and an ECG to look for co-ingestions. King's College criteria help identify patients who may need a liver transplant.",
  tx: "N-acetylcysteine is the antidote. It replenishes glutathione, binds NAPQI directly, supports the safe sulfation pathway and helps protect the liver even after injury has begun; it works best when started within about 8 hours of ingestion but is still given later. It can be given IV or by mouth. Activated charcoal may be given soon after ingestion to bind drug still in the gut. Some toxicology guidelines discuss adding fomepizole, which blocks CYP2E1, for very large ingestions, but this is an off-label use. Severe cases receive intensive care for liver failure, correction of glucose and clotting, and sometimes dialysis or liver transplant. Because many overdoses are intentional, a mental health evaluation and compassionate support are part of care.",
  drugs: ["n-acetylcysteine", "activated-charcoal"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "The toxic metabolite NAPQI is neutralized by glutathione; N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione."], ["Timing rule", "Check the level at 4 hours or later and plot it on the Rumack-Matthew nomogram; NAC is most protective within 8 hours."], ["Zone fact", "Zone 3 (centrilobular) necrosis, because zone 3 has the most CYP2E1."], ["Hidden danger", "Many combination cold, flu and prescription pain products contain acetaminophen, so people can exceed safe amounts without realizing it."]],
  red: "Any suspected acetaminophen overdose needs emergency evaluation right away, even without symptoms (call 911 or Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222). If the overdose was intentional or someone is thinking about self-harm, the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline is available by call or text.",
  fun: "Acetaminophen is called paracetamol in most of the world outside North America; both names are built from parts of its chemical name, N-acetyl-para-aminophenol.",
  src: ["Dart RC et al. Expert Consensus Guidelines for Management of Acetaminophen Poisoning. JAMA Netw Open 2023", "Rumack BH, Matthew H. Pediatrics 1975", "Mitchell JR et al. J Pharmacol Exp Ther 1973", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "AASLD Position Paper: Acute Liver Failure"]
},
"salicylate-poisoning": {
  name: "Salicylate (aspirin) poisoning",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["aspirin overdose", "salicylism", "ASA toxicity", "methyl salicylate poisoning", "oil of wintergreen poisoning"],
  what: "Salicylate poisoning happens when too much aspirin or another salicylate builds up in the body. It disrupts how cells make energy and upsets the blood's acid balance, causing fast breathing, ringing in the ears, vomiting and confusion, and it can be deadly.",
  origin: "Willow bark, which contains salicin, was used for pain and fever since ancient times. In 1763 English clergyman Edward Stone reported to the Royal Society that willow bark eased fevers. In 1897 chemist Felix Hoffmann at Bayer made a stable form of acetylsalicylic acid, sold as Aspirin from 1899, and it became one of the most used drugs in the world. As aspirin filled home medicine cabinets, accidental poisoning in young children became common, and in the 1960s it was a leading cause of child poisoning deaths. The Poison Prevention Packaging Act of 1970 required child-resistant caps, and children's aspirin bottles were limited in size, which sharply cut child deaths. In the 1980s aspirin use in children fell further after its link to Reye syndrome. Today serious poisonings come mostly from intentional overdoses, chronic overuse in older adults and concentrated products such as oil of wintergreen.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Willow bark is used for pain and fever in ancient Egypt and Greece."], ["1763", "Edward Stone reports willow bark for fevers to the Royal Society."], ["1897", "Felix Hoffmann synthesizes stable acetylsalicylic acid at Bayer."], ["1970", "The Poison Prevention Packaging Act requires child-resistant packaging."], ["1980s", "Reye syndrome warnings further reduce aspirin use in children."]],
  cell: "Salicylate first directly stimulates the respiratory center in the medulla, causing hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis. It then uncouples oxidative phosphorylation in mitochondria: the proton gradient leaks away as heat instead of making ATP, so cells burn more fuel, produce heat (hyperthermia) and shift to anaerobic metabolism. Salicylate also inhibits Krebs cycle enzymes and amino acid metabolism. Lactic acid, ketoacids and salicylic acid accumulate, causing an anion gap metabolic acidosis. Adults typically show a mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis. Acidemia is especially dangerous: salicylic acid is a weak acid, and at lower pH more of it is non-ionized and fat-soluble, so it crosses into the brain and other tissues. In the brain this causes confusion, seizures and cerebral edema, and brain glucose can be low even when blood glucose is normal. Increased capillary permeability can cause noncardiogenic pulmonary edema. Vomiting, sweating and fast breathing cause dehydration and potassium loss.",
  body: [["Brainstem", "Direct stimulation of the breathing center causes deep, fast breathing and respiratory alkalosis."], ["Brain", "Salicylate entering the brain causes confusion, agitation, seizures, cerebral edema and low brain glucose."], ["Ears", "Toxicity in the cochlea causes ringing in the ears (tinnitus) and hearing loss."], ["Lungs", "Leaky capillaries can cause noncardiogenic pulmonary edema, a sign of severe poisoning."], ["Stomach", "Direct irritation and brain effects cause nausea and vomiting; tablets can clump into masses that keep releasing drug."], ["Metabolism and temperature", "Uncoupled mitochondria create heat, acidosis and fluid loss."]],
  symptoms: "Fast, deep breathing happens because salicylate stimulates the brainstem breathing center and the body tries to blow off acid. Ringing in the ears and muffled hearing are early clues from effects on the inner ear. Nausea and vomiting come from stomach irritation and brain effects. Fever and sweating reflect heat released by uncoupled mitochondria. Confusion, agitation and seizures signal salicylate entering the brain and are signs of severe poisoning. Chronic poisoning in older adults can look like delirium, sepsis or heart failure, which delays diagnosis.",
  dx: "Diagnosis combines history and a serum salicylate level, repeated every few hours because levels can keep rising from enteric-coated or clumped tablets. A blood gas typically shows respiratory alkalosis with an anion gap metabolic acidosis. Electrolytes, glucose, creatinine and lactate are checked, plus an acetaminophen level for co-ingestion. The level alone does not show severity; symptoms, acid-base status and especially mental status matter most.",
  tx: "Treatment removes salicylate and keeps it out of the brain. Activated charcoal binds drug in the gut. IV sodium bicarbonate alkalinizes the blood, which keeps salicylate ionized so less enters the brain, and alkalinizes the urine, which traps ionized salicylate in the tubules so more is excreted (ion trapping). Potassium is replaced because low potassium blocks urine alkalinization. Dextrose is given for altered mental status because the brain can be low on glucose. IV fluids correct dehydration with care to avoid pulmonary edema. Intubation is avoided when possible because a ventilator may not match the very fast breathing that keeps acid down; if it is needed, ventilation is set high. Hemodialysis removes salicylate and corrects acidosis in severe poisoning, such as altered mental status, pulmonary edema, kidney failure, very high levels or worsening despite treatment. Mental health support is part of care after intentional overdose.",
  drugs: ["sodium-bicarbonate", "activated-charcoal", "potassium-chloride", "dextrose", "normal-saline"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Mixed respiratory alkalosis and anion gap metabolic acidosis, with tinnitus."], ["Memory trick", "Aspirin first makes you breathe fast (alkalosis), then makes you sour (acidosis)."], ["Treatment key", "Sodium bicarbonate keeps salicylate ionized: it traps it in the urine and keeps it out of the brain."], ["Danger point", "Intubation can be deadly if the ventilator fails to match the patient's high breathing rate."]],
  red: "Suspected aspirin or salicylate overdose, including oil of wintergreen ingestion, needs emergency care (call 911 or Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222). Confusion, fast breathing or seizures signal severe poisoning. If someone is thinking about self-harm, the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline is available.",
  fun: "Oil of wintergreen is almost pure methyl salicylate, making it one of the most concentrated salicylate sources found in homes.",
  src: ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "EXTRIP Workgroup: Extracorporeal Treatment for Salicylate Poisoning (2015)", "American Association of Poison Control Centers", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"carbon-monoxide": {
  name: "Carbon monoxide poisoning",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["CO poisoning", "carboxyhemoglobinemia"],
  what: "Carbon monoxide poisoning happens when people breathe in carbon monoxide, an odorless, colorless gas from fires, faulty furnaces, generators and car exhaust. The gas blocks the blood from carrying and releasing oxygen, causing headache, confusion and, at high levels, death.",
  origin: "People have died from fumes of charcoal fires since ancient times. In 1857 French physiologist C. Bernard showed that carbon monoxide poisons by binding to hemoglobin and pushing oxygen out of red blood cells. In the 1890s Scottish physiologist John Scott Haldane studied carbon monoxide in coal mine disasters, measured how tightly it binds hemoglobin and recommended small animals such as canaries as early warning detectors in mines, a practice used in Britain for much of the 20th century. Oxygen therapy became the main treatment, and hyperbaric oxygen chambers were applied to carbon monoxide poisoning from the 1960s. In 2002 a randomized trial led by Lindell Weaver in Utah found that hyperbaric oxygen reduced lasting memory and thinking problems after acute poisoning. Home carbon monoxide alarms, now required in many US states, have prevented many deaths. It is still one of the most common causes of fatal poisoning, especially in winter and after storms when generators are used indoors.",
  timeline: [["1857", "C. Bernard shows that carbon monoxide displaces oxygen from hemoglobin."], ["1890s", "John Scott Haldane studies carbon monoxide in mine disasters."], ["1960s", "Hyperbaric oxygen begins to be used for carbon monoxide poisoning."], ["2002", "A randomized trial shows hyperbaric oxygen reduces cognitive problems after poisoning."]],
  cell: "Carbon monoxide binds the iron in hemoglobin about 200 to 250 times more tightly than oxygen, forming carboxyhemoglobin, so less hemoglobin is free to carry oxygen. It also shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the left, so the remaining oxygen is held more tightly and released less to tissues. Together these cause tissue hypoxia out of proportion to the drop in oxygen-carrying capacity. Carbon monoxide also binds myoglobin in heart and skeletal muscle, reducing their oxygen supply, and binds cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV) in mitochondria, impairing cellular respiration. Beyond hypoxia, it triggers inflammation: platelets release nitric oxide, which forms peroxynitrite, neutrophils release myeloperoxidase and proteases, and lipid peroxidation damages myelin in the brain. This immune-mediated injury helps explain delayed neurologic problems that appear days to weeks later. The globus pallidus and white matter are especially vulnerable. Fetal hemoglobin binds carbon monoxide even more tightly, putting the fetus at extra risk.",
  body: [["Brain", "Hypoxia and inflammation cause headache, confusion, seizures and coma, and can lead to delayed memory, mood and movement problems."], ["Heart", "Low oxygen delivery to heart muscle causes chest pain, arrhythmias and heart muscle injury, even in young people."], ["Muscles", "Carbon monoxide bound to myoglobin causes weakness and can lead to muscle breakdown."], ["Blood", "Carboxyhemoglobin reduces oxygen carrying and release; standard pulse oximeters read it as normal oxyhemoglobin."], ["Fetus", "Fetal hemoglobin holds carbon monoxide tightly, so pregnancy raises the risk of fetal injury or death."], ["Lungs", "When poisoning comes from smoke, airway burns and other toxic gases such as cyanide often add to the injury."]],
  symptoms: "Headache, dizziness, nausea and fatigue are the most common early symptoms because the brain is sensitive to low oxygen; they are often mistaken for the flu, especially when several people in one home are sick at once and feel better away from the house. Confusion, fainting, chest pain and shortness of breath reflect more severe hypoxia of the brain and heart. Seizures and coma occur at high levels. The famous cherry-red skin is rare in living patients. Some people develop new memory or personality problems days to weeks later.",
  dx: "Diagnosis depends on suspecting exposure and measuring carboxyhemoglobin with co-oximetry on an arterial or venous blood gas; non-invasive CO-oximeters can screen. A standard pulse oximeter shows a falsely normal reading because it cannot tell carboxyhemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin. Levels may underestimate severity if oxygen was given before testing. An ECG and troponin look for heart injury, and a pregnancy test is checked. In fire victims, high lactate suggests cyanide poisoning as well.",
  tx: "The first step is removing the person from the source and giving high-flow 100 percent oxygen through a non-rebreather mask or breathing tube. Oxygen competes with carbon monoxide for hemoglobin and shortens its half-life from several hours on room air to roughly an hour or so. Hyperbaric oxygen, breathing pure oxygen in a pressurized chamber, shortens the half-life even more and may reduce delayed brain injury; it is considered for loss of consciousness, neurologic signs, heart injury, pregnancy or very high levels. Smoke inhalation victims with signs of cyanide toxicity may also receive hydroxocobalamin. Prevention is key: working carbon monoxide alarms, maintained heating systems and never running generators, grills or cars in enclosed spaces.",
  drugs: ["hydroxocobalamin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Pulse oximetry is falsely normal in carbon monoxide poisoning; co-oximetry is needed."], ["Curve shift", "Carbon monoxide shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin curve to the left, so oxygen is not released to tissues."], ["Memory trick", "Flu-like symptoms in several family members at once, better when they leave home, think CO."], ["Brain fact", "Bilateral globus pallidus lesions are the classic brain imaging finding."]],
  red: "Headache, dizziness, nausea or confusion in several people in the same building, or a carbon monoxide alarm sounding, means leaving the area at once and calling 911. Poison Control is available at 1-800-222-1222.",
  fun: "Canaries were used in British coal mines as carbon monoxide detectors until 1986, when electronic detectors replaced them.",
  src: ["ACEP Clinical Policy: Carbon Monoxide Poisoning (2017)", "Weaver LK et al. N Engl J Med 2002", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "CDC: Carbon Monoxide Poisoning"]
},
"cyanide-poisoning": {
  name: "Cyanide poisoning",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["cyanide toxicity", "hydrogen cyanide poisoning", "smoke inhalation cyanide"],
  what: "Cyanide poisoning happens when cyanide stops cells from using oxygen to make energy. It can come from smoke in house fires, industrial chemicals or certain plants, and it acts very fast, causing collapse, seizures and cardiac arrest.",
  origin: "Cyanide-containing plants such as bitter almonds, apricot pits and cassava have been known as poisons since ancient times. The pigment Prussian blue was made in the early 1700s, and in 1782 Swedish chemist Carl Wilhelm Scheele isolated hydrogen cyanide from it. In the 1930s American researchers K.K. Chen, C.L. Rose and G.H.A. Clowes developed the nitrite and thiosulfate antidote combination, which became the standard US cyanide kit for decades. Cyanide was later infamous as a chemical weapon and in mass poisonings. In the 1980s and 1990s doctors recognized that cyanide from burning plastics, foam and fabrics is a major killer in house fires, often alongside carbon monoxide. Hydroxocobalamin, a form of vitamin B12 used in France for years, was approved in the US in 2006 and is now the preferred antidote for smoke inhalation because it does not reduce the blood's ability to carry oxygen.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Plants such as bitter almonds and cassava are recognized as poisonous."], ["1782", "Carl Wilhelm Scheele isolates hydrogen cyanide."], ["1930s", "Chen, Rose and Clowes develop the nitrite and thiosulfate antidote."], ["2006", "Hydroxocobalamin is approved in the US as a cyanide antidote."]],
  cell: "Cyanide binds the ferric (Fe3+) iron in cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV) at the end of the mitochondrial electron transport chain. Electrons can no longer be passed to oxygen, so oxidative phosphorylation stops and ATP production collapses, even though plenty of oxygen is delivered. Cells switch to anaerobic glycolysis, producing large amounts of lactate and causing severe anion gap metabolic acidosis. Because tissues cannot extract oxygen, venous blood stays highly oxygenated. Organs with the highest energy needs, the brain and heart, fail first, causing seizures, coma, arrhythmias and cardiovascular collapse. Cyanide also stimulates glutamate release and inhibits other enzymes, adding to brain injury. The body clears small amounts through the mitochondrial enzyme rhodanese, which uses a sulfur donor to convert cyanide to thiocyanate, excreted in urine; this pathway is slow and quickly overwhelmed. Sources include smoke from burning plastics, wool and silk, industrial processes, amygdalin in apricot kernels, poorly processed cassava and prolonged nitroprusside infusion.",
  body: [["Brain", "Energy failure causes headache, confusion, seizures and coma within minutes of heavy exposure."], ["Heart", "Lack of ATP causes low blood pressure, slow or irregular rhythms and cardiac arrest."], ["Lungs and breathing", "Breathing first speeds up from acidosis and then slows and stops as the brainstem fails."], ["Blood and metabolism", "Severe lactic acidosis develops, and venous blood looks bright red because oxygen is not being used."], ["Survivors", "Some survivors develop lasting movement disorders from basal ganglia injury."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms appear within seconds to minutes after inhaling cyanide and more slowly after swallowing it. Early headache, dizziness, anxiety, fast breathing and a racing heart come from cells starved of energy and the body's response to acidosis. Then confusion, seizures and collapse follow as the brain fails. Low blood pressure, slow heart rate and cardiac arrest follow as the heart fails. Skin may look flushed rather than blue because blood stays oxygenated. A bitter almond odor is described, but many people cannot smell it.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical because blood cyanide levels take too long to come back. Clues are sudden collapse after smoke inhalation or a known exposure, severe lactic acidosis, an anion gap metabolic acidosis and a small difference between arterial and venous oxygen levels. In fire victims, a very high lactate strongly suggests cyanide poisoning. Carboxyhemoglobin is checked because carbon monoxide poisoning often occurs at the same time.",
  tx: "Treatment starts with removing the person from the source, decontamination when needed and 100 percent oxygen with airway and circulation support. Antidotes are given based on suspicion, without waiting for tests. Hydroxocobalamin, first-line in the US, uses its cobalt to bind cyanide tightly, forming cyanocobalamin (vitamin B12) that the kidneys excrete; it turns skin and urine red and can interfere with some lab tests. Sodium thiosulfate donates sulfur so rhodanese can convert cyanide to thiocyanate. Sodium nitrite, and inhaled amyl nitrite as a bridge in older kits, turns some hemoglobin into methemoglobin, which pulls cyanide off cytochrome oxidase; nitrites are avoided in smoke inhalation because they further reduce oxygen carrying when carbon monoxide is also present. Sodium bicarbonate may help severe acidosis, and seizures are treated with benzodiazepines.",
  drugs: ["hydroxocobalamin", "sodium-thiosulfate", "sodium-nitrite", "amyl-nitrite", "sodium-bicarbonate", "lorazepam"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Cyanide blocks cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV), so cells cannot use oxygen and lactate rises."], ["Fire victim rule", "House fire plus coma plus high lactate means think cyanide as well as carbon monoxide."], ["Antidote choice", "Hydroxocobalamin is preferred in smoke inhalation because nitrites make methemoglobin, which worsens oxygen delivery alongside carbon monoxide."], ["Nitroprusside link", "Prolonged nitroprusside infusion releases cyanide; thiosulfate can be used to prevent it."]],
  red: "Sudden collapse, seizures or trouble breathing after smoke or chemical exposure needs emergency care (call 911). Rescuers avoid entering an area with toxic fumes without protection. Poison Control is available at 1-800-222-1222.",
  fun: "The ability to smell cyanide's bitter almond odor is thought to be genetic, and a large share of people cannot detect it at all.",
  src: ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Chen KK, Rose CL, Clowes GHA. Proc Soc Exp Biol Med 1933", "CDC: Facts About Cyanide", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"organophosphate-poisoning": {
  name: "Organophosphate and nerve agent poisoning",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["cholinergic crisis", "organophosphate toxicity", "nerve agent exposure", "sarin poisoning", "carbamate poisoning", "SLUDGE syndrome"],
  what: "Organophosphate and nerve agent poisoning happens when insecticides or chemical weapons block the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine. Acetylcholine builds up, causing heavy secretions, wheezing, slow heart rate, muscle twitching, weakness and seizures, and death usually comes from breathing failure.",
  origin: "Organophosphate chemistry dates to the 1800s, but the most toxic compounds were found in the 1930s when German chemist Gerhard Schrader at IG Farben was developing insecticides and discovered tabun in 1936 and sarin in 1938. Their extreme toxicity led Germany to produce them as weapons, though they were not used on the battlefield in World War II. After the war, related compounds such as parathion and malathion became widely used farm insecticides, and pesticide poisoning became a major cause of death worldwide, especially through self-poisoning in rural Asia. In 1955 Irwin Wilson and colleagues developed pralidoxime, an oxime that reactivates the poisoned enzyme. Nerve agents were used in the Iran-Iraq War in the 1980s, in the 1995 Tokyo subway sarin attack and in later attacks, including Novichok poisonings. The Chemical Weapons Convention, in force since 1997, bans their production and use. Military auto-injectors combining atropine and pralidoxime let first responders treat quickly.",
  timeline: [["1936", "Gerhard Schrader discovers tabun while developing insecticides."], ["1955", "Pralidoxime is developed to reactivate acetylcholinesterase."], ["1995", "Sarin is released in the Tokyo subway attack."], ["1997", "The Chemical Weapons Convention enters into force."]],
  cell: "Acetylcholinesterase normally breaks down acetylcholine in the synapse within milliseconds. Organophosphates bind the serine hydroxyl group in the enzyme's active site and phosphorylate it, inactivating the enzyme. Acetylcholine then accumulates and overstimulates all cholinergic receptors. At muscarinic receptors (glands, smooth muscle, heart) it causes secretions, bronchospasm, bronchorrhea, miosis, vomiting, diarrhea, urination and bradycardia. At nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction it first causes fasciculations, then depolarizing blockade with weakness and paralysis, including the diaphragm; nicotinic stimulation of autonomic ganglia and the adrenal medulla can cause tachycardia and hypertension. In the CNS it causes agitation, seizures, coma and central apnea. Over time, the phosphorylated enzyme undergoes aging (loss of an alkyl group), making the bond permanent so oximes no longer work; some nerve agents such as soman age within minutes. Carbamates also inhibit the enzyme but reversibly and without aging. Some organophosphates also inhibit neuropathy target esterase, causing a delayed peripheral neuropathy weeks later.",
  body: [["Lungs", "Bronchorrhea and bronchospasm flood and narrow the airways, while weak breathing muscles and central apnea cause respiratory failure, the main cause of death."], ["Heart", "Muscarinic effects slow the heart and can cause heart block, though nicotinic effects sometimes speed it up; QT prolongation can occur."], ["Glands and gut", "Overstimulation causes heavy sweating, tearing, drooling, vomiting, diarrhea and urination."], ["Eyes", "Constricted pupils and blurred vision are common, especially after vapor exposure."], ["Skeletal muscles", "Twitching (fasciculations) progresses to weakness and paralysis."], ["Brain", "Seizures, confusion and coma result from excess acetylcholine in the CNS, and survivors can have lasting cognitive problems."]],
  symptoms: "The muscarinic signs are remembered as DUMBELS: diarrhea, urination, miosis, bronchorrhea and bronchospasm with bradycardia, emesis, lacrimation and salivation or sweating. These come from acetylcholine flooding muscarinic receptors. Muscle twitching and weakness come from nicotinic overstimulation at the neuromuscular junction. Seizures and coma reflect CNS effects. Vapor exposure acts within seconds to minutes, while skin exposure can be delayed for hours. Some patients develop an intermediate syndrome with neck, limb and breathing muscle weakness one to four days later.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical, based on the cholinergic toxidrome and exposure history, and treatment starts without waiting for tests. Red blood cell acetylcholinesterase activity reflects the enzyme at nerve synapses, while plasma butyrylcholinesterase (pseudocholinesterase) is more widely available but less specific. A garlic or solvent odor may be present with some insecticides. Clinicians monitor oxygen, ECG and electrolytes and watch for recurrent weakness.",
  tx: "Responders protect themselves first with protective equipment and decontaminate the patient by removing clothing and washing the skin, because the chemicals can poison caregivers. Airway support and oxygen are critical. Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors and is given repeatedly and increased until bronchial secretions dry and breathing improves; drying of the lungs, not heart rate or pupil size, is the treatment goal. Pralidoxime reactivates acetylcholinesterase by removing the phosphate group before aging, improving muscle strength, so it is given early. Combined atropine-pralidoxime auto-injectors are used in mass casualty settings. Benzodiazepines such as diazepam or midazolam treat seizures and may protect the brain. Glycopyrrolate can help peripheral secretions but does not reach the brain. Succinylcholine is avoided for intubation because poisoned plasma cholinesterase prolongs paralysis, so a nondepolarizing agent such as rocuronium is used.",
  drugs: ["atropine", "pralidoxime", "atropine-pralidoxime-auto-injector", "diazepam", "midazolam", "lorazepam", "glycopyrrolate", "rocuronium"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "DUMBELS: Diarrhea, Urination, Miosis, Bronchorrhea and Bradycardia, Emesis, Lacrimation, Salivation. The killer B's are bronchorrhea, bronchospasm and bradycardia."], ["Most-tested fact", "Atropine is titrated to drying of bronchial secretions, not to pupils or heart rate."], ["Pralidoxime rule", "Give pralidoxime early, before aging makes enzyme inhibition permanent; it helps nicotinic weakness."], ["Often confused with", "Anticholinergic poisoning is the opposite: dry, hot, red skin, large pupils and fast heart rate."], ["Intubation trap", "Avoid succinylcholine because it is broken down by the same cholinesterases that are poisoned."]],
  red: "Sudden heavy drooling, sweating, wheezing, vomiting, muscle twitching or seizures after insecticide or chemical exposure needs emergency care (call 911). Bystanders avoid touching contaminated clothing or skin without protection. Poison Control is available at 1-800-222-1222.",
  fun: "Many of the most potent nerve agents were discovered by accident during research into new insecticides.",
  src: ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "CHEMM (US HHS) Nerve Agent Guidance", "Eddleston M et al. Lancet 2008: Management of acute organophosphorus pesticide poisoning", "CDC: Facts About Sarin"]
},
"tca-overdose": {
  name: "Tricyclic antidepressant overdose",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["TCA overdose", "TCA toxicity", "amitriptyline overdose", "cyclic antidepressant poisoning"],
  what: "Tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) overdose happens when too much of an older antidepressant such as amitriptyline or nortriptyline is taken. It affects the heart's electrical system, blood pressure and the brain, and can quickly cause seizures, dangerous heart rhythms and death.",
  origin: "In 1957 Swiss psychiatrist Roland Kuhn reported that imipramine, a compound first tested as a possible antipsychotic, lifted severe depression. Imipramine and then amitriptyline became the first widely used antidepressants. Their benefit came with a dangerous downside: a small supply could be lethal in overdose, and TCAs became a leading cause of fatal drug poisoning in the 1970s and 1980s. In 1985 Michael Boehnert and Frederick Lovejoy showed that a widened QRS complex on the ECG predicted seizures and dangerous arrhythmias, giving emergency doctors a simple bedside tool. Sodium bicarbonate, studied in the 1970s and 1980s, became the key treatment for the heart toxicity. When SSRIs, which are much safer in overdose, arrived from the late 1980s, TCA prescribing for depression fell, but TCAs are still used for nerve pain, migraine prevention and some other conditions, so overdoses still occur.",
  timeline: [["1957", "Roland Kuhn reports the antidepressant effect of imipramine."], ["1961", "Amitriptyline is approved in the US."], ["1985", "Boehnert and Lovejoy link QRS widening to seizures and arrhythmias in TCA overdose."], ["1980s", "SSRIs arrive and gradually replace TCAs for depression."]],
  cell: "TCAs have several effects that become dangerous at high levels. Their most lethal action is blocking fast voltage-gated sodium channels in the His-Purkinje system and ventricular muscle, which slows phase 0 depolarization, widens the QRS complex and causes ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation; acidosis and low sodium worsen this block. They also block potassium efflux channels, prolonging the QT interval. Blockade of alpha-1 adrenergic receptors causes vasodilation and hypotension, and sodium channel block reduces cardiac contractility. Strong antimuscarinic effects cause delirium, dilated pupils, dry skin, urinary retention, slowed gut and sinus tachycardia. H1 histamine blockade adds sedation. GABA-A receptor antagonism and other CNS effects lower the seizure threshold, and seizures cause acidosis that worsens sodium channel block. Inhibition of norepinephrine and serotonin reuptake contributes early tachycardia and can deplete norepinephrine later. TCAs are fat soluble with large volumes of distribution, so dialysis does not remove them.",
  body: [["Heart", "Sodium channel blockade widens the QRS complex and causes ventricular arrhythmias, the leading cause of death."], ["Blood vessels", "Alpha-1 blockade and weaker heart contraction cause low blood pressure that can be hard to treat."], ["Brain", "Rapid sedation and coma come from antihistamine and antimuscarinic effects, and seizures can occur suddenly."], ["Skin and eyes", "Antimuscarinic effects cause dry flushed skin and dilated pupils."], ["Gut and bladder", "Slowed gut movement delays absorption and urinary retention develops."]],
  symptoms: "Patients can look fairly well at first and then deteriorate within an hour or two, which is a well-known danger. Drowsiness and confusion come from H1 and muscarinic blockade. Fast heart rate, dry mouth, flushed skin, large pupils and urinary retention are anticholinergic signs. Low blood pressure, wide-complex arrhythmias and seizures follow as sodium channel blockade deepens. The classic summary is the three Cs: coma, convulsions and cardiotoxicity.",
  dx: "The ECG is the key test. A QRS duration over 100 milliseconds predicts seizures and over 160 predicts ventricular arrhythmias, and a tall terminal R wave in lead aVR and a rightward terminal QRS axis are typical. Sinus tachycardia is common. Blood TCA levels do not guide emergency care well. Urine drug screens can give false positives from other drugs with similar structures. Tests also look for co-ingestions such as acetaminophen and check blood gas, glucose and electrolytes.",
  tx: "Treatment centers on the airway, breathing, circulation and the ECG. Activated charcoal may be used when the airway is protected and the ingestion is recent. Sodium bicarbonate is the main antidote for the heart: the extra sodium overcomes the sodium channel block and the alkalosis lowers drug binding to the channel, narrowing the QRS and improving blood pressure. Hypertonic saline is an alternative. Low blood pressure is treated with IV fluids and norepinephrine, a direct alpha agonist. Seizures are treated with benzodiazepines. Lidocaine may be used for arrhythmias that persist despite bicarbonate, while class IA and IC antiarrhythmics are avoided because they also block sodium channels. Physostigmine and flumazenil are avoided because they can trigger seizures or cardiac arrest in TCA overdose. Lipid emulsion and ECMO are used as rescue therapies. Because many overdoses are intentional, compassionate mental health care follows.",
  drugs: ["sodium-bicarbonate", "activated-charcoal", "norepinephrine", "lorazepam", "diazepam", "midazolam", "lidocaine", "lipid-emulsion", "normal-saline"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "The three Cs: Coma, Convulsions, Cardiotoxicity."], ["Most-tested fact", "QRS over 100 ms is the key warning sign, and sodium bicarbonate is the treatment."], ["ECG clue", "A tall terminal R wave in aVR suggests sodium channel blockade."], ["Avoid list", "Avoid physostigmine, flumazenil and class IA or IC antiarrhythmics in TCA overdose."]],
  red: "Any suspected tricyclic antidepressant overdose needs emergency care right away (call 911 or Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222), even if the person seems well, because collapse can be sudden. Anyone thinking about suicide can reach the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline by call or text.",
  fun: "Imipramine was originally tested as a possible treatment for psychosis; its mood-lifting effect was a surprise discovery.",
  src: ["Boehnert MT, Lovejoy FH. N Engl J Med 1985", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "AHA Guidelines for CPR and ECC: Toxicology", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"beta-blocker-overdose": {
  name: "Beta blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["beta blocker toxicity", "calcium channel blocker toxicity", "CCB overdose", "verapamil overdose", "propranolol overdose"],
  what: "Beta blocker and calcium channel blocker overdoses happen when too much of these common heart and blood pressure medicines is taken. Both can make the heart beat dangerously slowly and weakly and drop blood pressure, leading to shock.",
  origin: "Scottish pharmacologist James Black developed propranolol in the early 1960s at ICI to protect the heart from adrenaline, work that earned him a share of the 1988 Nobel Prize. At about the same time German researchers developed verapamil, and Albrecht Fleckenstein showed in the 1960s that these drugs block calcium entry into heart and vessel muscle. Beta blockers and calcium channel blockers became some of the most prescribed drugs in the world, and overdoses became a leading cause of death from cardiovascular drugs. In the 1970s glucagon was found to raise heart rate and contractility in beta blocker poisoning by bypassing beta receptors. In the 1990s animal studies by Jeffrey Kline and colleagues, then human case series, showed that high-dose insulin with glucose improved heart function in calcium channel blocker shock. A 2017 expert consensus guideline recommended high-dose insulin as a first-line therapy for calcium channel blocker poisoning.",
  timeline: [["1960s", "James Black develops propranolol and verapamil is introduced."], ["1970s", "Glucagon is used to treat beta blocker poisoning."], ["1988", "James Black shares the Nobel Prize, in part for beta blockers."], ["1990s", "High-dose insulin euglycemic therapy is developed for calcium channel blocker shock."], ["2017", "Expert consensus recommendations for calcium channel blocker poisoning are published."]],
  cell: "In heart cells, norepinephrine acting on beta-1 receptors activates Gs, raising cAMP and protein kinase A, which opens L-type calcium channels and increases calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Beta blockers stop this chain, while calcium channel blockers block the L-type channel directly. Both reduce calcium entry, so the SA node fires slower, AV conduction slows and contraction weakens. Non-dihydropyridine calcium blockers (verapamil, diltiazem) act strongly on the heart, causing bradycardia, AV block and low output. Dihydropyridines (amlodipine, nifedipine) act mainly on arteries, causing vasodilation and reflex tachycardia, but in large overdoses lose selectivity. Calcium channel blockers also block L-type channels in pancreatic beta cells, reducing insulin release, so hyperglycemia is common and tracks severity. The stressed heart switches from fatty acids to glucose for fuel but becomes insulin resistant, starving it of energy, which is why high-dose insulin helps. Some beta blockers have extra effects: propranolol blocks sodium channels (wide QRS, seizures) and sotalol blocks potassium channels (long QT, torsades).",
  body: [["Heart rhythm", "Slowed SA node and AV conduction cause bradycardia and heart block, sometimes with junctional rhythms."], ["Heart muscle", "Less calcium entry weakens contraction, causing cardiogenic shock."], ["Blood vessels", "Calcium channel blockers dilate arteries, causing severe low blood pressure (vasodilatory shock)."], ["Pancreas and metabolism", "Calcium channel blockers raise blood sugar by blocking insulin release; beta blockers can cause low blood sugar, especially in children."], ["Brain", "Low blood flow causes dizziness, confusion and fainting; propranolol can cause seizures directly."], ["Lungs", "Beta blockers can trigger bronchospasm, and shock can cause fluid in the lungs."]],
  symptoms: "Dizziness, weakness and fainting come from slow heart rate and low blood pressure reducing blood flow to the brain. Calcium channel blocker poisoning often leaves people awake despite very low blood pressure, while beta blocker poisoning, especially with propranolol, more often causes confusion or seizures. Nausea and vomiting are common. Extended-release products can cause symptoms to start many hours after ingestion. Cold, pale skin and low urine output signal shock.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is based on the history and the combination of bradycardia and low blood pressure. The ECG shows bradycardia, heart block, a wide QRS (propranolol) or a long QT (sotalol). Glucose helps tell them apart: high glucose favors calcium channel blockers, while low glucose can occur with beta blockers. Electrolytes, kidney function, lactate and co-ingestant levels are checked. Echocardiography helps show whether shock is mainly from weak pumping or dilated vessels. Patients who took extended-release products are monitored for at least a day.",
  tx: "Treatment supports the heart and blood pressure while the drug wears off. Activated charcoal and, for extended-release products, whole bowel irrigation may limit absorption. Atropine can briefly speed a slow heart, and IV fluids treat low blood pressure. IV calcium (calcium chloride or gluconate) raises extracellular calcium to drive more calcium through remaining channels. High-dose insulin euglycemic therapy uses regular insulin with dextrose to help the heart use glucose and improve contraction, with close glucose and potassium monitoring. Vasopressors such as norepinephrine and epinephrine raise blood pressure and heart rate. Glucagon raises cAMP through its own receptor, bypassing beta receptors, though vomiting is common. Sodium bicarbonate treats wide QRS from propranolol. Refractory cases may receive methylene blue for vasoplegia, lipid emulsion, pacing or ECMO.",
  drugs: ["calcium-chloride", "calcium-gluconate", "regular-insulin", "dextrose", "glucagon", "norepinephrine", "epinephrine", "atropine", "vasopressin", "dopamine", "potassium-chloride", "sodium-bicarbonate", "lipid-emulsion", "methylene-blue", "activated-charcoal", "isoproterenol"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Glucagon works in beta blocker overdose because it raises cAMP through its own receptor, bypassing the blocked beta receptor."], ["Glucose clue", "Calcium channel blocker overdose causes hyperglycemia; beta blocker overdose can cause hypoglycemia."], ["Memory trick", "For calcium channel blocker shock, think Calcium, Catecholamines and high-dose insulin (with glucose)."], ["Drug quirks", "Propranolol widens the QRS and causes seizures; sotalol prolongs the QT."]],
  red: "Suspected overdose of a blood pressure or heart medicine needs emergency care (call 911 or Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222), even before symptoms, because extended-release tablets can act hours later. Anyone thinking about self-harm can reach the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline.",
  fun: "James Black also developed cimetidine, the first H2 blocker for ulcers, making him responsible for two of the most important drug classes of the 20th century.",
  src: ["St-Onge M et al. Experts Consensus Recommendations for the Management of Calcium Channel Blocker Poisoning. Crit Care Med 2017", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "AHA Guidelines for CPR and ECC: Toxicology (2023)", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"benzodiazepine-overdose": {
  name: "Benzodiazepine overdose",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["benzodiazepine toxicity", "benzo overdose", "sedative-hypnotic overdose"],
  what: "Benzodiazepine overdose happens when too much of a sedative such as alprazolam, lorazepam, clonazepam or diazepam is taken. Alone it usually causes deep sleepiness, but combined with alcohol or opioids it can stop breathing.",
  origin: "In 1955 chemist Leo Sternbach at Hoffmann-La Roche synthesized a compound that sat unused on a shelf until 1957, when testing showed it calmed animals. It became chlordiazepoxide (Librium), marketed in 1960, followed by diazepam (Valium) in 1963, which became one of the best-selling drugs in the world. Benzodiazepines replaced barbiturates, which were far more dangerous in overdose, and for a time were seen as nearly risk free. By the 1970s and 1980s, dependence, withdrawal seizures and misuse were recognized. Flumazenil, a benzodiazepine antagonist, was approved in the US in 1991. In 2016 the FDA added a boxed warning about combining opioids and benzodiazepines after overdose deaths involving both rose sharply, and in 2020 it expanded warnings on misuse, addiction and withdrawal. More recently, unapproved designer benzodiazepines such as bromazolam have appeared in the illicit drug supply, often mixed with fentanyl.",
  timeline: [["1955", "Leo Sternbach synthesizes chlordiazepoxide."], ["1960", "Chlordiazepoxide is marketed as the first benzodiazepine."], ["1963", "Diazepam is introduced."], ["1991", "Flumazenil is approved in the US."], ["2016", "The FDA adds a boxed warning on combining opioids and benzodiazepines."], ["2020", "The FDA expands boxed warnings on misuse, dependence and withdrawal."]],
  cell: "Benzodiazepines bind a site on the GABA-A receptor between the alpha and gamma subunits. They are positive allosteric modulators: they do not open the channel on their own but make GABA open it more often, increasing chloride entry and hyperpolarizing neurons. Receptors with alpha-1 subunits mediate sedation, amnesia and some anticonvulsant effects, while alpha-2 and alpha-3 subunits mediate anxiety relief and muscle relaxation. Because their effect depends on natural GABA, benzodiazepines alone have a ceiling and rarely stop breathing in healthy adults. Barbiturates, alcohol and other sedatives act at different GABA-A sites and can open the channel directly at high levels, so combining them produces much deeper CNS and respiratory depression. Opioids add separate mu receptor depression of the brainstem breathing center. Long-term use causes receptor adaptation (tolerance), so stopping suddenly unmasks hyperexcitability and can cause withdrawal seizures. Older adults and people with liver disease clear many benzodiazepines more slowly.",
  body: [["Brain", "Enhanced GABA inhibition causes slurred speech, confusion, poor coordination, amnesia and coma."], ["Breathing", "Breathing is usually only mildly reduced with benzodiazepines alone, but becomes dangerous with alcohol, opioids or lung disease."], ["Airway", "Deep sedation relaxes throat muscles, so the tongue can block the airway and vomit can be inhaled."], ["Muscles", "Muscle relaxation and lying still for long periods can lead to pressure injury and muscle breakdown."], ["Heart and vessels", "Blood pressure and heart rate usually stay fairly normal unless other drugs are involved."]],
  symptoms: "Sleepiness, slurred speech, unsteady walking and confusion happen because GABA inhibition is amplified across the brain. Memory gaps come from effects on the hippocampus. A classic picture is coma with relatively normal vital signs, which helps tell benzodiazepines apart from opioids, where breathing and pupils are more affected. Slow or shallow breathing, snoring sounds and blue lips suggest airway blockage or a co-ingestion such as an opioid or alcohol.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical, based on sedation with fairly normal vital signs and a history of exposure. Standard urine drug screens may miss some benzodiazepines such as clonazepam and lorazepam, and many designer benzodiazepines, so a negative screen does not rule them out. Testing looks for other causes of altered mental status, such as low blood sugar, head injury and co-ingestions, including acetaminophen and salicylate levels and an ECG.",
  tx: "Most benzodiazepine overdoses are managed with supportive care until the drug wears off: positioning, airway protection, oxygen and assisted breathing if needed. Naloxone is given when opioid co-ingestion is possible, which is common. Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine site that can reverse sedation, but it is used mainly to reverse sedation during medical procedures and rarely in unknown overdoses, because it can trigger seizures and withdrawal in people who take benzodiazepines regularly or who also took drugs such as tricyclic antidepressants. Activated charcoal is rarely needed. After recovery, people with dependence are tapered slowly rather than stopped suddenly to prevent dangerous withdrawal. Mental health and substance use care are offered with compassion; SAMHSA's National Helpline (1-800-662-4357) provides free, confidential treatment referral.",
  drugs: ["flumazenil", "naloxone"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Flumazenil can trigger seizures in chronic benzodiazepine users or TCA co-ingestion, so it is rarely used in unknown overdoses."], ["Memory trick", "Benzos increase the Frequency of chloride channel opening; barbiturates increase the Duration (Frenzodiazepines and Barbidurates)."], ["Danger combo", "Benzodiazepines plus opioids or alcohol cause most serious respiratory depression."], ["Clinical picture", "Coma with nearly normal vital signs suggests a benzodiazepine."]],
  red: "Someone who cannot be woken, is breathing slowly or has blue lips needs emergency care (call 911); naloxone can be given if opioids might be involved. Poison Control is available at 1-800-222-1222. For substance use support, SAMHSA's National Helpline is 1-800-662-4357, and anyone thinking about suicide can reach the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline.",
  fun: "The first benzodiazepine was found almost by accident, when a compound that had been set aside for about two years was finally tested during a lab cleanup.",
  src: ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "FDA Drug Safety Communication: Opioids and Benzodiazepines (2016)", "FDA Drug Safety Communication: Benzodiazepine Boxed Warning (2020)", "Katzung's Basic and Clinical Pharmacology"]
},
"digoxin-toxicity": {
  name: "Digoxin toxicity",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["digitalis toxicity", "cardiac glycoside poisoning", "dig toxicity", "foxglove poisoning", "oleander poisoning"],
  what: "Digoxin toxicity happens when levels of digoxin, a heart medicine made from foxglove, or similar plant toxins become too high. It causes nausea, confusion, vision changes and dangerous heart rhythms, and it can be reversed with an antibody antidote.",
  origin: "Plants containing cardiac glycosides, such as foxglove, oleander and squill, have been used as medicines and poisons since ancient times. In 1785 English physician William Withering published his account of foxglove for dropsy, and he also described its toxic effects, including nausea, vomiting, slow pulse and visual disturbances. In 1930 Sydney Smith at Burroughs Wellcome isolated digoxin from the woolly foxglove, Digitalis lanata. As digoxin became one of the most prescribed heart drugs, toxicity became common, especially in older adults with kidney problems. In 1976 Thomas W. Smith and colleagues reported treating life-threatening digoxin poisoning with antibody fragments from immunized sheep, and the antidote was approved in the US in 1986. The 1997 DIG trial showed digoxin reduces hospital stays in heart failure but not death, and its use has since declined.",
  timeline: [["1785", "William Withering describes foxglove, including its toxic effects."], ["1930", "Sydney Smith isolates digoxin from Digitalis lanata."], ["1976", "Digoxin-specific antibody fragments are first used to treat severe poisoning."], ["1986", "Digoxin immune Fab is approved in the US."], ["1997", "The DIG trial clarifies digoxin's role in heart failure."]],
  cell: "Digoxin binds and inhibits the sodium-potassium ATPase pump on the outside of heart cells. Sodium builds up inside, which slows the sodium-calcium exchanger, so more calcium stays in the cell and is stored in the sarcoplasmic reticulum, strengthening contraction. Digoxin also increases vagal tone, slowing the SA node and AV node. In toxicity, calcium overload causes spontaneous calcium release and delayed afterdepolarizations, producing triggered arrhythmias such as frequent PVCs, bigeminy, atrial tachycardia with block and bidirectional ventricular tachycardia. At the same time, high vagal tone and direct effects cause bradycardia and AV block. Pump inhibition throughout the body shifts potassium out of cells, so acute overdose causes hyperkalemia, which predicts mortality. Hypokalemia, by contrast, increases digoxin binding to the pump and worsens chronic toxicity, as do hypomagnesemia and hypercalcemia. Digoxin is cleared by the kidneys, so kidney decline and interacting drugs (amiodarone, verapamil, quinidine, clarithromycin) that block P-glycoprotein raise levels.",
  body: [["Heart", "Increased automaticity and slowed conduction together cause a wide range of arrhythmias, from slow AV block to ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation."], ["Gut and brain", "Stimulation of the chemoreceptor trigger zone causes loss of appetite, nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain, often the first signs."], ["Eyes", "Effects on the retina cause blurred vision, yellow-green tinted vision (xanthopsia) and halos around lights."], ["Brain", "Confusion, fatigue, weakness and delirium are common, especially in older adults."], ["Electrolytes", "Acute overdose raises potassium; chronic toxicity often occurs with low potassium or magnesium from diuretics."], ["Kidneys", "Declining kidney function reduces digoxin clearance and is a common trigger for chronic toxicity."]],
  symptoms: "Nausea, vomiting and loss of appetite are early signs because digoxin acts on the brain's vomiting center. Vision changes, such as yellow or green tints and halos, come from digoxin's effects on retinal cells. Confusion and fatigue are common in older adults. Palpitations, dizziness and fainting reflect arrhythmias or very slow heart rate. Chronic toxicity develops slowly and is easy to mistake for a viral illness or worsening heart failure.",
  dx: "Diagnosis combines symptoms, the ECG and a serum digoxin level drawn at least 6 hours after the last dose, after the drug has distributed into tissues. Levels do not always match severity, especially in chronic toxicity. Potassium, magnesium, calcium and kidney function are checked. The ECG may show almost any arrhythmia; bidirectional ventricular tachycardia and atrial tachycardia with block are especially suggestive. Scooped ST segments (sometimes called the Salvador Dali mustache sign) show digoxin effect, not necessarily toxicity. After antidote treatment, total digoxin levels rise and are no longer useful.",
  tx: "Digoxin immune Fab is the specific antidote. These sheep antibody fragments bind digoxin in the blood, pulling it off sodium-potassium pumps, and the complexes are cleared by the kidneys; effects begin within minutes. Fab is used for life-threatening arrhythmias, high potassium in acute overdose, end-organ problems and very high levels, and it also helps with plant glycosides such as foxglove and oleander. Potassium and magnesium are corrected, since low levels worsen toxicity, and high potassium usually corrects with Fab. Atropine can treat slow heart rates in the meantime. Older teaching warned against IV calcium because of a stone heart effect; more recent studies suggest this risk was overstated, but Fab remains the preferred treatment. Activated charcoal may be used after recent ingestion. Interacting drugs are reviewed, and doses are adjusted for kidney function.",
  drugs: ["digoxin-immune-fab", "atropine", "magnesium-sulfate", "potassium-chloride", "activated-charcoal"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Hyperkalemia is the marker of acute digoxin toxicity; hypokalemia worsens chronic toxicity."], ["Classic rhythm", "Bidirectional ventricular tachycardia and atrial tachycardia with AV block suggest digoxin toxicity."], ["Memory trick", "Digoxin toxicity sees yellow: xanthopsia and halos."], ["Interaction alert", "Amiodarone, verapamil, quinidine and clarithromycin raise digoxin levels."]],
  red: "Palpitations, fainting, severe vomiting, confusion or a very slow pulse in someone taking digoxin, or anyone who swallowed foxglove or oleander, needs emergency care (call 911 or Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222).",
  fun: "Withering's 1785 account of foxglove included careful notes on its overdose effects, making it one of the first detailed descriptions of drug toxicity.",
  src: ["Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "Smith TW et al. N Engl J Med 1976", "Digitalis Investigation Group. N Engl J Med 1997", "Levine M et al. J Emerg Med 2011", "Withering W. An Account of the Foxglove (1785)"]
},
"snakebite": {
  name: "Snakebite envenomation",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["snakebite", "pit viper envenomation", "rattlesnake bite", "crotalid envenomation", "coral snake bite"],
  what: "Snakebite envenomation happens when a venomous snake injects venom through its fangs. In the US most bites come from pit vipers such as rattlesnakes, copperheads and cottonmouths, causing pain, swelling and bleeding problems, while coral snake venom can cause paralysis.",
  origin: "Snakebites have been feared and treated with charms, cutting and herbs since ancient times. Modern treatment began in the 1890s when French physician Albert Calmette, working at the Pasteur Institute in Saigon and later in Lille, immunized animals against cobra venom and produced the first antivenom serum. In 1901 Brazilian physician Vital Brazil founded what became the Butantan Institute, making antivenoms specific to local snakes. In the US, an equine polyvalent antivenom for pit vipers was approved in 1954, but it caused frequent allergic reactions. A purified sheep antibody fragment antivenom, CroFab, was approved in 2000, and an equine F(ab')2 product, Anavip, was approved in 2015. In 2011 US experts published a unified treatment algorithm for pit viper bites. In 2017 the World Health Organization added snakebite envenoming to its list of neglected tropical diseases, since it kills tens of thousands of people each year worldwide, mostly in rural Africa and Asia.",
  timeline: [["1890s", "Albert Calmette produces the first antivenom against cobra venom."], ["1901", "Vital Brazil founds the institute that becomes the Butantan Institute."], ["1954", "An equine pit viper antivenom is approved in the US."], ["2000", "CroFab, a sheep Fab antivenom, is approved in the US."], ["2015", "Anavip, an equine F(ab')2 antivenom, is approved in the US."], ["2017", "The WHO lists snakebite envenoming as a neglected tropical disease."]],
  cell: "Venom is a mix of enzymes and toxins. In pit vipers, snake venom metalloproteinases break down the basement membrane of capillaries and the extracellular matrix, causing bleeding into tissue, swelling and tissue death. Phospholipase A2 enzymes damage cell membranes, destroying muscle (myonecrosis) and causing inflammation. Hyaluronidase helps venom spread through tissue. Thrombin-like enzymes cleave fibrinogen into weak clots that break down quickly, and other components activate or inhibit clotting factors and platelets, causing venom-induced consumptive coagulopathy with low fibrinogen, low platelets and prolonged clotting times. Some rattlesnakes, such as the Mojave rattlesnake, carry a presynaptic phospholipase A2 neurotoxin (Mojave toxin) that can cause weakness. Coral snakes, in the elapid family, inject small alpha-neurotoxins that bind nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction, blocking signals and causing descending paralysis that may start hours after a bite with little local pain. Fluid leaking from damaged vessels can cause shock.",
  body: [["Bite site and limb", "Venom enzymes cause pain, swelling, bruising, blisters and sometimes tissue death spreading up the limb."], ["Blood", "Consumptive coagulopathy lowers fibrinogen and platelets, so bleeding from gums, IV sites or internally can occur."], ["Circulation", "Leaky vessels and venom effects cause low blood pressure and shock in severe bites."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Coral snake and some rattlesnake venoms cause drooping eyelids, double vision, trouble swallowing and weakness, which can stop breathing."], ["Kidneys", "Muscle breakdown, low blood pressure and clotting problems can injure the kidneys."], ["Immune system", "Venom and antivenom can both trigger allergic or anaphylactic reactions."]],
  symptoms: "Pit viper bites cause burning pain and rapidly spreading swelling and bruising because metalloproteinases and phospholipases damage vessels and tissue. Nausea, sweating, a metallic or rubbery taste and muscle twitching (fasciculations) are common systemic signs. Bleeding gums or prolonged bleeding come from venom-induced coagulopathy. Coral snake bites may cause little pain at first, but hours later drooping eyelids, slurred speech, trouble swallowing and weakness appear as neurotoxins block the neuromuscular junction. Some bites are dry bites with no venom injected.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical, based on the history, the snake if safely identified (never by catching it) and the evolving signs. Clinicians mark the edge of swelling and measure it repeatedly to track progression. Blood tests include a platelet count, fibrinogen, PT/INR and blood count, repeated over time because coagulopathy can develop or return later. Creatine kinase and kidney tests look for muscle and kidney injury. For coral snake bites, patients are watched closely for neurologic signs, which can be delayed.",
  tx: "First aid focuses on getting to a hospital quickly: keeping the person calm and still, removing rings, watches and tight clothing before swelling, and calling 911. Tourniquets, cutting the wound, sucking out venom, ice and electric shock are harmful and not used. The key treatment is antivenom. For pit vipers, CroFab (sheep Fab fragments) or Anavip (horse F(ab')2 fragments) bind venom toxins so they cannot act and are cleared; they are given for progressive swelling, coagulopathy or systemic effects, with repeat doses as needed, and Anavip's longer half-life lowers late recurrence. North American coral snake antivenin, made from horse serum, binds neurotoxins and is given early because established paralysis is hard to reverse. Epinephrine and diphenhydramine treat allergic reactions. Pain is treated with opioids, while NSAIDs are avoided because of bleeding risk. Tetanus immunization is updated. Breathing support is provided for paralysis.",
  drugs: ["crotalidae-polyvalent-immune-fab", "crotalidae-immune-fab2-equine", "north-american-coral-snake-antivenin", "epinephrine", "diphenhydramine", "morphine", "fentanyl", "tdap-vaccine", "normal-saline"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Antivenom is the definitive treatment; tourniquets, incision, suction and ice are harmful."], ["Venom types", "Pit vipers are mainly hemotoxic and tissue-damaging; coral snakes are neurotoxic with delayed paralysis."], ["Memory trick", "For US coral snakes: red touches yellow, dangerous fellow; red touches black, venom lack. This rhyme does not work for snakes outside the US."], ["Lab focus", "Watch fibrinogen and platelets for venom-induced consumptive coagulopathy."]],
  red: "Any bite from a venomous or unknown snake needs emergency care (call 911), especially with spreading swelling, bleeding, vomiting, fainting, drooping eyelids, trouble swallowing or trouble breathing. Poison Control (1-800-222-1222) can help guide care.",
  fun: "Albert Calmette, who made the first antivenom, later co-developed the BCG tuberculosis vaccine with Camille Guerin.",
  src: ["Lavonas EJ et al. Unified treatment algorithm for the management of crotaline snakebite in the United States. BMC Emerg Med 2011", "WHO: Snakebite Envenoming Fact Sheet", "Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies", "CDC/NIOSH: Venomous Snakes"]
}
});

;
DB.DIS = DB.DIS || {};
Object.assign(DB.DIS, {
"heat-stroke": {
  name: "Heat stroke",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["exertional heat stroke", "classic heat stroke", "EHS", "sunstroke"],
  what: "Heat stroke is a life-threatening rise in body temperature, usually above 40 C (104 F), with confusion or other brain problems. The body's cooling system is overwhelmed, and heat starts to damage the brain, muscles, liver and kidneys.",
  origin: "Ancient writers described soldiers and laborers who collapsed and died during marches in hot weather, and military medicine has studied heat illness ever since. In the 1950s the US military developed the wet bulb globe temperature (WBGT) index, which combines heat, humidity and sun, to decide when training was too dangerous, and heat deaths among recruits fell. Doctors learned to separate two types: classic heat stroke, which strikes older or ill people during heat waves, and exertional heat stroke, which strikes healthy athletes, soldiers and workers. Deadly heat waves, such as Chicago in 1995 and Europe in 2003, showed how dangerous heat is for people who live alone or without air conditioning. Sports medicine research then showed that fast whole-body cold-water immersion, started on site, saves almost everyone with exertional heat stroke. The guiding rule became cool first, transport second.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "Writers record deaths of soldiers marching in extreme heat."], ["1950s", "The US military develops the WBGT index to guide safe training in the heat."], ["1995", "The Chicago heat wave kills hundreds of people, many older and living alone."], ["2003", "A European heat wave causes tens of thousands of excess deaths."], ["2010s", "Sports medicine groups endorse on-site cold-water immersion as the standard for exertional heat stroke."]],
  cell: "Normally the hypothalamus sheds heat by widening skin blood vessels and making sweat. In heat stroke, heat gain from the environment or from working muscle outruns this system. Above about 40 C (104 F), proteins begin to unfold, cell membranes become leaky and mitochondria fail. Heat shock proteins try to refold damaged proteins but are overwhelmed. Blood is shunted to the skin, so the gut gets too little blood flow; the intestinal barrier breaks down and bacterial endotoxin leaks into the blood. Endotoxin and damaged cells trigger a storm of cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6 and TNF-alpha, much like sepsis. Heat-injured endothelium switches on clotting, which can progress to disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). Skeletal muscle breaks down (rhabdomyolysis), releasing potassium and myoglobin. The result is multiple organ failure that can keep worsening even after cooling.",
  body: [["Brain", "Neurons, especially Purkinje cells of the cerebellum, are very sensitive to heat. Confusion, seizures, unsteady walking and coma reflect this injury, and some survivors have lasting problems with balance or memory."], ["Muscles", "Overheated muscle cells break down (rhabdomyolysis), spilling potassium, phosphate and myoglobin into the blood."], ["Kidneys", "Dehydration, low blood flow and myoglobin clogging the tubules cause acute kidney injury."], ["Liver", "Heat directly injures liver cells, so liver enzymes rise sharply, often peaking a day or two later."], ["Blood and clotting", "Damaged blood vessel lining activates clotting, which can lead to DIC with both clots and bleeding."], ["Heart and circulation", "The heart races to push blood to the skin, and blood pressure can fall as fluid is lost and vessels dilate."]],
  symptoms: "Confusion, odd behavior, slurred speech, seizures or collapse happen because heat directly injures brain cells. The skin is hot; in classic heat stroke it is often dry because sweating has failed, but in exertional heat stroke the person is often still sweating. A fast heart rate and fast breathing reflect the effort to dump heat and the rising metabolic demand. Dark urine comes from myoglobin released by broken-down muscle. Vomiting and diarrhea come from gut injury.",
  dx: "Heat stroke is a clinical diagnosis: a core body temperature above about 40 C (104 F) with central nervous system dysfunction in a person exposed to heat or exercise. A rectal temperature is the reliable field measurement; mouth, ear or forehead readings can be falsely low. Labs look for organ injury: creatine kinase for rhabdomyolysis, electrolytes such as potassium, kidney and liver tests, and clotting studies for DIC. Clinicians also consider infection, drug toxicity (such as stimulants or serotonin syndrome), thyroid storm and malignant hyperthermia.",
  tx: "Treatment is rapid cooling. For exertional heat stroke, whole-body cold-water or ice-water immersion is the fastest method and is started on site before transport when it can be done safely. Other methods include ice packs, cold wet towels, and misting with fans. Cooling stops at a target of about 39 C (102.2 F) to avoid overshooting into hypothermia. IV fluids such as normal saline or lactated Ringer's restore volume and help protect the kidneys from myoglobin. Benzodiazepines such as midazolam or lorazepam control seizures and the shivering that can produce more heat. Antipyretics like acetaminophen do not help, because the hypothalamic set point is normal; the body is simply overwhelmed. Dantrolene is not effective for heat stroke. Intensive care manages kidney failure, high potassium and DIC.",
  drugs: ["normal-saline", "lactated-ringers", "midazolam", "lorazepam", "diazepam", "dextrose", "calcium-gluconate", "regular-insulin", "sodium-bicarbonate"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Heat stroke = core temperature above 40 C (104 F) plus altered mental status. Heat exhaustion has a normal mental state."], ["Memory trick", "Cool first, transport second. Fast cold-water immersion is what saves lives."], ["Often confused with", "Fever: in fever the brain's thermostat is turned up, so acetaminophen works. In heat stroke the thermostat is normal but overwhelmed, so antipyretics do not work."], ["Classic vs exertional", "Classic strikes older or chronically ill people during heat waves, often with dry skin. Exertional strikes young, healthy people during hard exercise, often still sweating."]],
  red: "Confusion, fainting, seizures or a very high body temperature during heat exposure or hard exercise is heat stroke until proven otherwise and needs emergency care (call 911) and immediate cooling.",
  fun: "With cold-water immersion started within minutes, survival from exertional heat stroke is close to 100 percent, which is why many big races keep ice-water tubs at the finish line.",
  src: ["Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for Heat Illness (2019 update)", "American College of Sports Medicine Expert Consensus Statement on Exertional Heat Illness (2023)", "Bouchama A, Knochel JP. Heat stroke. N Engl J Med 2002", "Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine"]
},
"hypothermia": {
  name: "Hypothermia",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["accidental hypothermia", "exposure"],
  what: "Hypothermia means the body's core temperature has dropped below 35 C (95 F). As the body cools, the brain, heart and other organs slow down, and in severe cases the heart can stop.",
  origin: "Cold injury has shaped history for centuries. Dominique Jean Larrey, Napoleon's chief surgeon, described soldiers freezing and dying during the retreat from Moscow in 1812, and noted that rapid warming near fires could be dangerous. The sinking of the Titanic in 1912 showed how quickly cold water kills. In 1953 American researcher John J. Osborn described a distinctive hump on the ECG of cooled dogs, now called the Osborn or J wave. In the same era surgeons began cooling patients on purpose to protect the brain and heart during operations, which taught doctors how the body behaves at low temperatures. Rescue teams later learned that some people with severe hypothermia and cardiac arrest can survive with long CPR and rewarming on heart-lung bypass or ECMO, giving rise to the saying that no one is dead until they are warm and dead.",
  timeline: [["1812", "Larrey describes cold injury and death during Napoleon's retreat from Moscow."], ["1912", "The Titanic disaster shows how fast cold water causes death."], ["1953", "John J. Osborn describes the ECG J wave in cooled animals."], ["1950s", "Surgeons begin using induced hypothermia to protect organs during heart surgery."], ["2000s", "ECMO and bypass rewarming become the standard for hypothermic cardiac arrest at specialized centers."]],
  cell: "Heat comes from metabolism, and cold slows every enzyme reaction; metabolic rate falls by roughly 6 to 7 percent for each degree Celsius of cooling. At first the hypothalamus fights back with shivering and with tight constriction of skin blood vessels, which shifts blood to the core and causes cold diuresis (extra urine). Below about 32 C (89.6 F) shivering fades and the body cools faster. Ion pumps and gap junctions in heart muscle slow down, conduction lengthens and the heart becomes irritable, so atrial fibrillation and then ventricular fibrillation become likely, especially below 28 C (82.4 F). Cold also slows the clotting enzymes, causing bleeding even when lab tests run at 37 C (98.6 F) look normal. The oxygen-hemoglobin curve shifts left, so hemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly. Neurons slow down, which causes confusion and coma but also lowers oxygen need, which is why the cold brain can survive longer without blood flow.",
  body: [["Brain", "Slowed neuron activity causes clumsiness, poor judgment, confusion and eventually coma. Low brain oxygen use can protect it during arrest."], ["Heart", "Slowed conduction causes a slow pulse, J (Osborn) waves and long intervals on the ECG, then atrial fibrillation and ventricular fibrillation or asystole in severe cases."], ["Blood vessels", "Constricted skin vessels keep heat in the core, but rewarming can open them and cause a drop in blood pressure."], ["Kidneys", "Cold diuresis causes fluid loss and dehydration even though the person is not drinking."], ["Blood and clotting", "Clotting enzymes work slowly in the cold, so bleeding worsens, a major problem in injured patients."], ["Skin and limbs", "Low blood flow can combine with freezing to cause frostbite in fingers, toes, ears and nose."]],
  symptoms: "Shivering is the body making heat by contracting muscles, and it fades in moderate hypothermia as energy runs out. Slurred speech, clumsiness and poor decisions come from a cooling brain. Some people undress near the end (paradoxical undressing), likely from a sudden sensation of heat as constricted vessels relax. A slow pulse and slow breathing reflect a slowed metabolism. Frequent urination comes from cold diuresis.",
  dx: "Diagnosis rests on a low core temperature, ideally measured with an esophageal, bladder or rectal probe because regular thermometers often cannot read low enough. Stages are mild (32 to 35 C (89.6-95 F)), moderate (28 to 32 C (82.4-89.6 F)) and severe (below 28 C (82.4 F)); the Swiss staging system uses level of consciousness and shivering when no thermometer is available. An ECG may show J waves and arrhythmias. Labs check glucose, potassium, kidney function and clotting. A very high potassium in arrest suggests death occurred before cooling and helps guide decisions about ECMO.",
  tx: "Care starts with gentle handling, because rough movement can trigger ventricular fibrillation in a cold heart. Wet clothes are removed and the person is insulated. Mild hypothermia is treated with passive rewarming (blankets) and warm sweet drinks if awake. Moderate cases use active external rewarming such as forced-air warming blankets and warmed IV fluids like normal saline or lactated Ringer's. Severe cases may need active internal rewarming, and those in cardiac arrest are best served by ECMO or cardiopulmonary bypass. CPR continues until the person is rewarmed. Cold hearts respond poorly to drugs and defibrillation, so guidelines differ on spacing or holding epinephrine and repeat shocks until the core warms. Dextrose treats low blood sugar, thiamine is given when alcohol use or poor nutrition is suspected, and hydrocortisone or levothyroxine are used if adrenal failure or myxedema caused the cooling.",
  drugs: ["normal-saline", "lactated-ringers", "dextrose", "thiamine", "epinephrine", "amiodarone", "hydrocortisone", "levothyroxine", "glucagon"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "No one is dead until they are warm and dead."], ["Most-tested fact", "The Osborn (J) wave, an extra hump at the end of the QRS complex, is the classic ECG sign of hypothermia."], ["Handle gently", "Rough handling of a cold patient can trigger ventricular fibrillation, so EMS moves them carefully and keeps them horizontal."], ["Afterdrop", "Core temperature can keep falling after rescue as cold blood from the limbs returns to the core."]],
  red: "Confusion, stopping shivering while still cold, very slow breathing or pulse, or unresponsiveness after cold exposure needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Some people have survived cardiac arrest with core temperatures below 14 C (57 F) after hours of CPR and rewarming on bypass.",
  src: ["Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for Out-of-Hospital Evaluation and Treatment of Accidental Hypothermia (2019 update)", "AHA Guidelines for CPR and Emergency Cardiovascular Care: Special Circumstances", "Brown DJA et al. Accidental hypothermia. N Engl J Med 2012", "Osborn JJ. Am J Physiol 1953"]
},
"drowning": {
  name: "Drowning",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["submersion injury", "near drowning (outdated term)"],
  what: "Drowning is breathing trouble caused by being under or in water or another liquid. Water in the airway stops oxygen from reaching the blood, and without oxygen the brain and heart can stop working within minutes.",
  origin: "People have drowned throughout history, and early rescue efforts were often useless or harmful, such as rolling victims over barrels. In 1767 citizens of Amsterdam founded a society to promote the recovery of drowned persons, and in 1774 William Hawes and Thomas Cogan founded the Royal Humane Society in London, which taught rescue breathing and warming. Modern CPR, developed around 1960, combined mouth-to-mouth breathing with chest compressions. Doctors long argued over whether fresh water or salt water drowning differed, but studies showed the difference matters little in people; the main problem is lack of oxygen. In 2002 the World Congress on Drowning in Amsterdam agreed on one simple definition and recommended dropping confusing terms like near drowning and dry drowning. Prevention, through fences around pools, life jackets, swim lessons and supervision, has become the main focus.",
  timeline: [["1767", "A society for the recovery of drowned persons is founded in Amsterdam."], ["1774", "The Royal Humane Society is founded in London to teach resuscitation."], ["1960", "Modern CPR combining rescue breaths and chest compressions is introduced."], ["2002", "The World Congress on Drowning adopts a single, uniform definition of drowning."]],
  cell: "The core injury is hypoxia. When the airway goes under water, the person first holds their breath, then swallows and aspirates water. Water entering the larynx can trigger brief laryngospasm. Aspirated water, fresh or salt, washes out and inactivates surfactant, the lipoprotein that keeps alveoli open. Alveoli collapse, fluid leaks across the damaged alveolar-capillary membrane, and blood flows past airless lung (intrapulmonary shunt). The result is severe hypoxemia that may progress to acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) over hours. In the body, lack of oxygen shuts down aerobic metabolism, causing lactic acidosis. Neurons, the most oxygen-hungry cells, start to die within minutes through energy failure, calcium overload and glutamate excitotoxicity. The heart slows and then stops, usually in a nonshockable rhythm such as bradycardia or asystole. Cold water can protect the brain somewhat by slowing metabolism.",
  body: [["Lungs", "Surfactant loss and fluid in the air sacs cause shunting, low oxygen and stiff lungs. ARDS and pneumonia can develop later."], ["Brain", "Lack of oxygen injures neurons within minutes. Lasting brain injury is the main cause of disability in survivors."], ["Heart", "Hypoxia causes slow heart rhythms and then cardiac arrest, typically asystole or PEA rather than ventricular fibrillation."], ["Body temperature", "Immersion in cold water causes hypothermia, which adds risk but can also protect the brain."], ["Spine", "Diving into shallow water can cause neck and spinal cord injury in some drowning cases."]],
  symptoms: "Coughing, choking and foamy sputum come from water and fluid in the airways. Shortness of breath, fast breathing and blue lips (cyanosis) happen because the lungs cannot move oxygen into the blood. Confusion, drowsiness or unresponsiveness reflect a brain starved of oxygen. Symptoms may worsen over several hours as lung injury and fluid leak progress, which is why people with ongoing breathing symptoms are watched closely.",
  dx: "Drowning is diagnosed from the history of submersion or immersion and the breathing problems that follow. Pulse oximetry and blood gases measure oxygen and acid levels. A chest X-ray may show fluid or patchy infiltrates, though it can be normal early. Clinicians check core temperature, glucose and electrolytes, and look for injuries such as a neck fracture, alcohol or drug use, seizures or a heart rhythm problem that may have caused the event.",
  tx: "The priority is oxygen. Rescuers open the airway and give rescue breaths early, so drowning resuscitation focuses on ventilation, and CPR follows if there is no pulse. Attempts to drain water from the lungs, such as abdominal thrusts, are not recommended. Supplemental oxygen, CPAP or mechanical ventilation with PEEP reopen collapsed alveoli. Epinephrine is used in cardiac arrest, and albuterol can ease bronchospasm. Wet clothes are removed and hypothermia is treated. IV fluids such as normal saline support blood pressure. Antibiotics are not given routinely but are used if pneumonia develops. Prevention is the most effective treatment: supervision, pool fencing, life jackets and swim lessons.",
  drugs: ["epinephrine", "albuterol", "normal-saline", "lactated-ringers", "midazolam", "dextrose"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "In drowning, hypoxia is the problem, so early rescue breaths matter more than in a typical sudden cardiac arrest."], ["Fresh vs salt water", "The type of water rarely changes care; both wash out surfactant and cause hypoxemia."], ["Outdated terms", "Near drowning, dry drowning and secondary drowning are no longer used. A person either drowned and survived, or drowned and died."], ["Prevention", "Drowning is a leading cause of death in US children ages 1 to 4, most often in home swimming pools (CDC)."]],
  red: "Anyone pulled from water who is not breathing normally, is confused, or keeps coughing, struggling to breathe or turning blue needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Drowning is usually silent. A person in trouble often cannot shout or wave because all their effort goes into keeping their mouth above water.",
  src: ["CDC Drowning Facts", "Szpilman D et al. Drowning. N Engl J Med 2012", "AHA Guidelines for CPR and Emergency Cardiovascular Care: Drowning", "Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Treatment and Prevention of Drowning (2019 update)"]
},
"altitude-sickness": {
  name: "Altitude sickness",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["acute mountain sickness", "AMS", "high altitude cerebral edema", "HACE", "high altitude pulmonary edema", "HAPE", "mountain sickness"],
  what: "Altitude sickness is illness caused by going too high too fast, where the air has less oxygen. The mild form causes headache and nausea, and rare severe forms cause swelling of the brain (HACE) or fluid in the lungs (HAPE).",
  origin: "An ancient Chinese text from about 2,000 years ago warned travelers about the Great Headache Mountain and Little Headache Mountain on the route west. In 1590 Jesuit priest Jose de Acosta described severe sickness while crossing the Andes. In 1878 French physiologist Paul Bert showed that the problem was the low pressure of oxygen in thin air, not the cold or the effort. Mountaineers and doctors then documented acute mountain sickness in detail. In 1960 American physician Charles Houston described high altitude pulmonary edema in a young skier, and researchers such as Herbert Hultgren showed it was not heart failure. In the late 1960s a trial showed acetazolamide could prevent mountain sickness. Later studies added dexamethasone for brain swelling and nifedipine for lung swelling, and the Lake Louise score (1991, revised 2018) gave researchers a shared way to measure symptoms.",
  timeline: [["Ancient", "A Chinese text warns of the Headache Mountains on a route to the west."], ["1590", "Jose de Acosta describes altitude sickness in the Andes."], ["1878", "Paul Bert shows low oxygen pressure causes altitude illness."], ["1960", "Charles Houston describes high altitude pulmonary edema."], ["1960s", "A controlled trial shows acetazolamide prevents acute mountain sickness."], ["1991", "The Lake Louise scoring system for acute mountain sickness is introduced."]],
  cell: "At altitude the fraction of oxygen in air is the same, but barometric pressure is lower, so the partial pressure of oxygen falls. Carotid body chemoreceptors sense hypoxemia and increase breathing (the hypoxic ventilatory response), which blows off carbon dioxide and causes respiratory alkalosis. Over days the kidneys excrete bicarbonate to correct this, which is acclimatization. In AMS and HACE, hypoxia widens brain blood vessels and raises VEGF and other signals that loosen the blood-brain barrier, leading to vasogenic edema and, in HACE, a swollen brain. In HAPE, hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction occurs unevenly; parts of the lung get very high pressure and flow, which damages capillary walls (stress failure) and lets protein-rich fluid leak into the alveoli. Reduced nitric oxide and impaired sodium transport (ENaC) that normally clears alveolar fluid add to the problem. HAPE is noncardiogenic pulmonary edema.",
  body: [["Brain", "Mild swelling causes headache. In HACE the brain swells further, causing confusion, poor balance (ataxia) and coma."], ["Lungs", "In HAPE, high pulmonary artery pressure and leaky capillaries flood the alveoli, causing breathlessness, cough and severe hypoxemia."], ["Breathing control", "Faster, deeper breathing raises oxygen but causes respiratory alkalosis; periodic breathing during sleep is common."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys excrete bicarbonate over several days to balance the alkalosis, which is part of acclimatization."], ["Blood", "Over weeks, the kidneys release more erythropoietin, raising red blood cell count to carry more oxygen."]],
  symptoms: "Headache comes from widened, leaky brain vessels. Nausea, poor appetite, fatigue, dizziness and poor sleep are typical of acute mountain sickness and usually start 6 to 12 hours after arrival. Confusion and stumbling (ataxia) mean HACE, because the swelling now affects brain function. Breathlessness at rest, cough, falling exercise tolerance and later pink frothy sputum mean HAPE, because fluid fills the air sacs. Symptoms are worse at night when breathing slows during sleep.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical, based on symptoms after a recent gain in altitude, usually above about 2,500 meters (8,000 feet). The Lake Louise score rates headache, stomach upset, fatigue and dizziness. HACE is suspected with ataxia (failing a heel-to-toe walking test) or altered mental status. HAPE is suspected with breathlessness at rest, crackles and low oxygen saturation that is out of proportion to the altitude. When available, a chest X-ray or lung ultrasound shows patchy fluid in HAPE, and an MRI can show white matter swelling in HACE.",
  tx: "Descent is the most effective treatment for any serious altitude illness. Supplemental oxygen and portable hyperbaric bags, which raise air pressure around the person, buy time when descent is not possible. Acetazolamide, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, makes the kidneys dump bicarbonate, creating a mild metabolic acidosis that drives breathing and speeds acclimatization; it is used for prevention and mild AMS. Dexamethasone, a corticosteroid, reduces brain swelling and is used for moderate AMS and HACE. For HAPE, nifedipine, a calcium channel blocker, lowers pulmonary artery pressure; PDE-5 inhibitors such as tadalafil or sildenafil are used for prevention in some people. Ibuprofen or acetaminophen treats headache, and ondansetron eases nausea. Slow ascent, with rest days, is the best prevention.",
  drugs: ["acetazolamide", "dexamethasone", "nifedipine", "tadalafil", "sildenafil", "ibuprofen", "acetaminophen", "ondansetron"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Climb high, sleep low. Worsening symptoms mean descent, not further ascent."], ["Most-tested fact", "Acetazolamide speeds acclimatization by causing a mild metabolic acidosis that drives breathing (it is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor)."], ["Matching drugs", "Dexamethasone for the brain (HACE), nifedipine for the lungs (HAPE), acetazolamide for prevention."], ["Often confused with", "HAPE is not heart failure. The heart is normal; the problem is high, uneven pulmonary artery pressure that damages lung capillaries."]],
  red: "Confusion, stumbling, severe drowsiness, breathlessness at rest or coughing up frothy sputum at altitude means HACE or HAPE and needs immediate descent and emergency care (call 911 or local rescue).",
  fun: "Tibetan highlanders carry gene variants, including one in EPAS1 (the HIF-2 alpha gene) thought to come from ancient Denisovan humans, that help them live at altitude without dangerously thick blood.",
  src: ["Wilderness Medical Society Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Acute Altitude Illness (2019 and 2024 updates)", "Luks AM, Hackett PH. High altitude illness. N Engl J Med", "Bert P. La Pression Barometrique (1878)", "Houston CS. Acute pulmonary edema of high altitude. N Engl J Med 1960"]
},
"decompression-sickness": {
  name: "Decompression sickness",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["DCS", "the bends", "caisson disease", "divers' disease"],
  what: "Decompression sickness happens when gas bubbles form in the blood and tissues after a fast drop in surrounding pressure, most often when a scuba diver comes up too quickly. The bubbles cause joint pain, rash, and sometimes nerve, lung or brain damage.",
  origin: "The disease first became common in the 1800s, when workers dug bridge foundations inside pressurized chambers called caissons. Many became sick after their shifts. Workers on the Eads Bridge in St. Louis died in the early 1870s, and Washington Roebling, chief engineer of the Brooklyn Bridge, was partly paralyzed by caisson disease. In 1878 Paul Bert showed that the cause was nitrogen bubbles forming as pressure fell, and that slow decompression and breathing oxygen helped. In 1908 J. S. Haldane and colleagues, working for the British Royal Navy, published staged decompression tables that let divers rise in steps. The nickname the bends came from the bent-over posture of affected workers. In the 1960s the US Navy introduced oxygen recompression treatment tables, still the basis of hyperbaric treatment today.",
  timeline: [["1870s", "Caisson disease sickens and kills workers on the Eads Bridge and Brooklyn Bridge."], ["1878", "Paul Bert shows nitrogen bubbles cause decompression sickness."], ["1908", "Boycott, Damant and Haldane publish staged decompression tables for divers."], ["1960s", "The US Navy develops oxygen recompression tables, including Treatment Table 6."]],
  cell: "Henry's law says the amount of gas dissolved in a liquid rises with pressure. During a dive, the diver breathes air under high pressure, and inert nitrogen dissolves into blood and tissues, especially fatty tissue such as nerve myelin. If pressure drops too fast on ascent, the dissolved nitrogen comes out of solution as bubbles, like opening a soda. Bubbles form in tissues and veins. They block small vessels, stretch and tear tissues, and act as foreign surfaces that activate platelets, complement, neutrophils and the clotting cascade. Damaged endothelium leaks fluid, and inflammation adds secondary injury. Venous bubbles are usually filtered by the lungs, but a large load can clog the pulmonary circulation (the chokes), and bubbles can cross into the arteries through a patent foramen ovale and reach the brain or spinal cord. This is different from arterial gas embolism, in which lung overexpansion pushes air directly into the arteries.",
  body: [["Joints and muscles", "Bubbles in and around joints cause deep, aching pain, most often in the shoulders and elbows in divers (type I, the bends)."], ["Skin", "Bubbles in skin vessels cause itching or a mottled, marbled rash (cutis marmorata)."], ["Spinal cord", "The spinal cord's venous drainage is easily blocked by bubbles, causing numbness, weakness, paralysis and loss of bladder control."], ["Brain", "Bubbles that reach the brain cause confusion, vision changes, headache, dizziness and stroke-like symptoms."], ["Lungs", "Many venous bubbles can clog lung vessels, causing chest pain, cough and breathlessness (the chokes)."], ["Inner ear", "Bubbles in the inner ear cause vertigo, ringing and hearing loss."]],
  symptoms: "Symptoms usually start within minutes to a few hours after surfacing, as bubbles grow. Joint pain comes from bubbles stretching tissue. Fatigue and itching come from bubbles in small vessels and tissue inflammation. Numbness, tingling, weakness or trouble urinating mean bubbles are injuring the spinal cord. Breathlessness and chest pain mean bubbles are blocking lung vessels. Flying soon after diving can trigger or worsen symptoms because cabin pressure is lower.",
  dx: "Diagnosis is clinical, based on symptoms after a dive, flight or other pressure change. The dive profile (depth, time, ascent rate), timing of symptoms and a careful neurologic exam are key. No blood test confirms it, and imaging is used mostly to look for other causes such as arterial gas embolism or a pneumothorax. Prompt improvement with recompression supports the diagnosis.",
  tx: "The first step is breathing high-flow oxygen as close to 100 percent as possible. Pure oxygen creates a large gradient that pulls nitrogen out of the bubbles and tissues, and it improves oxygen delivery to injured tissue. Definitive treatment is recompression in a hyperbaric chamber, which shrinks bubbles (Boyle's law) and speeds nitrogen removal, followed by slow, controlled decompression. IV or oral fluids such as normal saline or lactated Ringer's correct dehydration and help blood flow, and the person is kept lying flat. Emergency services and diving medicine networks, such as DAN, help arrange transfer to a chamber. Prevention relies on slow ascents, safety stops, dive tables or computers, and waiting before flying.",
  drugs: ["normal-saline", "lactated-ringers"],
  mem: [["Memory trick", "Shake a soda and open it fast: bubbles. Open it slowly: no bubbles. That is decompression."], ["Most-tested fact", "Henry's law (gas dissolves under pressure) explains DCS; Boyle's law (gas volume shrinks under pressure) explains why recompression works."], ["Often confused with", "Arterial gas embolism: sudden brain symptoms within minutes of surfacing, from lung overinflation pushing air into the arteries. Both are treated with recompression."], ["Type I vs type II", "Type I is pain and skin. Type II is nerves, lungs or inner ear and is more serious."]],
  red: "Numbness, weakness, confusion, trouble breathing, chest pain or severe joint pain after diving needs emergency care (call 911) and 100 percent oxygen; the Divers Alert Network has a 24-hour emergency line to help find a chamber.",
  fun: "Washington Roebling was so disabled by caisson disease that he oversaw much of the Brooklyn Bridge from his window, while his wife Emily Warren Roebling carried his instructions to the site.",
  src: ["US Navy Diving Manual", "Undersea and Hyperbaric Medical Society (UHMS) Best Practice Guidelines", "Vann RD et al. Decompression illness. Lancet 2011", "Boycott AE, Damant GCC, Haldane JS. The prevention of compressed-air illness. J Hyg 1908"]
},
"angioedema": {
  name: "Angioedema",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["Quincke edema", "hereditary angioedema", "HAE", "ACE inhibitor angioedema", "angioneurotic edema"],
  what: "Angioedema is sudden swelling deep in the skin or the lining of the mouth, throat or gut. It can affect the lips, eyes, tongue and airway, and swelling of the throat can block breathing.",
  origin: "In 1882 German physician Heinrich Quincke described episodes of sudden, localized swelling, and the condition is still sometimes called Quincke edema. In 1888 William Osler described a family with inherited swelling attacks across several generations, some fatal from throat swelling, now called hereditary angioedema (HAE). In 1963 Virginia Donaldson and Richard Evans showed that people with HAE lacked a working C1 esterase inhibitor in their blood. In the 1980s doctors recognized that ACE inhibitor blood pressure drugs could cause angioedema by raising bradykinin. Treatment for HAE changed dramatically after 2008, when the FDA began approving C1 inhibitor concentrates, followed by ecallantide, icatibant, the antibody lanadelumab, oral berotralstat, and in 2025 oral sebetralstat for attacks and garadacimab and donidalorsen for prevention.",
  timeline: [["1882", "Heinrich Quincke describes acute localized swelling."], ["1888", "William Osler describes hereditary angioedema in a family."], ["1963", "Donaldson and Evans link HAE to missing C1 esterase inhibitor."], ["1980s", "ACE inhibitors are recognized as a cause of angioedema."], ["2008", "The first C1 inhibitor concentrate for HAE is approved in the US."], ["2018", "Lanadelumab, a kallikrein-blocking antibody, is approved to prevent HAE attacks."]],
  cell: "Angioedema is swelling from leaky blood vessels in the deep dermis and submucosa. Two main pathways cause it. In histaminergic (allergic) angioedema, an allergen cross-links IgE on mast cells, which release histamine, leukotrienes and other mediators that open endothelial junctions; hives and itching often come with it. In bradykinin-mediated angioedema, the contact system is overactive. Factor XIIa converts prekallikrein to kallikrein, which cleaves high-molecular-weight kininogen to release bradykinin. Bradykinin binds B2 receptors on endothelium and makes vessels leaky. C1 esterase inhibitor normally blocks factor XIIa and kallikrein; in HAE a SERPING1 gene mutation causes low (type I) or dysfunctional (type II) C1 inhibitor, so bradykinin builds up. ACE (kininase II) normally breaks down bradykinin, so ACE inhibitors let it accumulate. Bradykinin angioedema has no hives and does not respond to antihistamines or epinephrine reliably.",
  body: [["Airway", "Swelling of the tongue, floor of the mouth and larynx can block breathing, the main cause of death."], ["Face and skin", "Lips, eyelids, hands, feet and genitals swell; the swelling is usually not pitting and may not itch in bradykinin forms."], ["Gut", "Swelling of the bowel wall causes severe cramping abdominal pain, vomiting and sometimes fluid in the abdomen, common in HAE."], ["Blood vessels", "Fluid shifts out of vessels during large attacks, which can lower blood pressure."], ["Complement system", "In HAE, C4 is consumed and low because C1 runs unchecked, a key lab clue."]],
  symptoms: "Swelling comes from fluid leaking out of small vessels into deep tissue, so it feels tight and may burn rather than itch. Allergic angioedema often comes with hives, itching and sometimes wheezing because histamine affects the skin and airways. Bradykinin angioedema builds more slowly over hours, lasts two to five days and has no hives. Hoarseness, trouble swallowing, drooling or a change in voice mean the throat is swelling. Abdominal pain and vomiting in HAE come from swelling of the gut wall.",
  dx: "Diagnosis starts with the history and exam: hives suggest a histamine cause, while no hives plus ACE inhibitor use or a family history suggests a bradykinin cause. Airway is assessed first, sometimes with flexible laryngoscopy. For suspected HAE, labs show a low C4 level and a low or poorly functioning C1 inhibitor. A low C1q level points to acquired C1 inhibitor deficiency, which can come with lymphoma or autoimmune disease. Tryptase may be raised in anaphylaxis.",
  tx: "Airway protection comes first; severe cases may need intubation or a surgical airway. Allergic angioedema is treated like anaphylaxis: epinephrine reverses vessel leak and airway swelling, H1 antihistamines such as diphenhydramine or cetirizine block histamine, H2 blockers such as famotidine add effect, and corticosteroids like methylprednisolone reduce late inflammation. HAE attacks are treated with C1 esterase inhibitor concentrate, the bradykinin B2 receptor blocker icatibant, or kallikrein inhibitors ecallantide or sebetralstat; fresh frozen plasma is a backup. HAE prevention uses C1 inhibitor, lanadelumab, berotralstat, garadacimab, donidalorsen or, less often now, danazol. ACE inhibitor angioedema is managed by stopping the ACE inhibitor for good and supporting the airway; the HAE drugs have not clearly helped it in trials.",
  drugs: ["epinephrine", "diphenhydramine", "cetirizine", "famotidine", "methylprednisolone", "c1-esterase-inhibitor", "icatibant", "ecallantide", "sebetralstat", "lanadelumab", "berotralstat", "garadacimab", "donidalorsen", "danazol"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "ACE inhibitors cause angioedema by blocking bradykinin breakdown. It can appear years after starting the drug."], ["Memory trick", "Hives = histamine. No hives = think bradykinin (ACE inhibitor or HAE)."], ["Lab clue", "Low C4 is the best screening test for hereditary angioedema."], ["Often confused with", "Anaphylaxis: allergic angioedema is often part of it and responds to epinephrine, while HAE does not reliably respond to epinephrine or antihistamines."]],
  red: "Swelling of the tongue or throat, hoarse voice, trouble swallowing or breathing, or drooling needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "ARBs block angiotensin receptors without blocking ACE, so they cause angioedema far less often than ACE inhibitors, though it can still occur.",
  src: ["US Hereditary Angioedema Association Medical Advisory Board Guidelines (2020)", "WAO/EAACI Guideline for the Management of Hereditary Angioedema (2021 revision)", "Donaldson VH, Evans RR. Am J Med 1963", "Osler W. Hereditary angio-neurotic oedema. Am J Med Sci 1888"]
},
"hemorrhagic-trauma": {
  name: "Major trauma and hemorrhage",
  sys: "Poisoning & Emergencies",
  aka: ["hemorrhagic shock", "traumatic hemorrhage", "massive bleeding", "trauma-induced coagulopathy"],
  what: "Major trauma with hemorrhage means a serious injury causes heavy bleeding, either from a visible wound or inside the body. Losing a lot of blood starves organs of oxygen and can cause shock and death within minutes to hours.",
  origin: "Battlefield surgeons have fought bleeding for centuries. In the 1500s French surgeon Ambroise Pare tied off bleeding vessels with ligatures instead of burning wounds with hot oil and cautery. In 1901 Karl Landsteiner discovered the ABO blood groups, making safe transfusion possible, and blood banks spread during the World Wars. In 1978 the first Advanced Trauma Life Support (ATLS) course was held in Nebraska, after surgeon James Styner's family was injured in a 1976 plane crash and received poor rural care. Military experience in Iraq and Afghanistan in the 2000s brought back tourniquets and hemostatic dressings and showed that giving plasma, platelets and red cells together saved lives. The CRASH-2 trial (2010) showed tranexamic acid lowers death from bleeding, and the PROPPR trial (2015) supported balanced transfusion. The Stop the Bleed campaign, launched in 2015, teaches the public to control bleeding.",
  timeline: [["1500s", "Ambroise Pare uses ligatures to tie off bleeding vessels."], ["1901", "Karl Landsteiner discovers ABO blood groups."], ["1978", "The first ATLS course is taught in Nebraska."], ["2000s", "Military experience revives tourniquets and balanced blood product resuscitation."], ["2010", "The CRASH-2 trial shows tranexamic acid reduces bleeding deaths in trauma."], ["2015", "The PROPPR trial reports on balanced transfusion, and Stop the Bleed launches."]],
  cell: "Blood loss lowers venous return and cardiac output, so tissues get less oxygen. Cells switch to anaerobic metabolism and make lactate, causing metabolic acidosis. Baroreceptors trigger a sympathetic surge, so the heart speeds up and vessels constrict to protect the brain and heart. When oxygen debt grows, ATP falls, ion pumps fail and cells swell and die, and ischemia followed by reperfusion releases free radicals and inflammatory signals. Trauma also causes a clotting failure called acute traumatic coagulopathy. Tissue injury and shock activate protein C via thrombomodulin, which breaks down factors Va and VIIIa, and they release tissue plasminogen activator, causing hyperfibrinolysis. The endothelial glycocalyx sheds. Fibrinogen is the first factor to run low. Hypothermia slows clotting enzymes, acidosis weakens them, and low ionized calcium (worsened by citrate in blood products) impairs factor activity, creating a vicious cycle often called the lethal triad (or diamond, adding hypocalcemia).",
  body: [["Heart and circulation", "The heart races and vessels clamp down to keep pressure up. Blood pressure often falls only after large losses, so a normal pressure can be falsely reassuring."], ["Brain", "Less blood flow causes anxiety, then confusion and loss of consciousness."], ["Kidneys", "The kidneys get less blood early, so urine output falls; prolonged shock causes acute kidney injury."], ["Blood and clotting", "Clotting factors, platelets and fibrinogen are lost and consumed, and clot breakdown speeds up, so bleeding worsens."], ["Gut and liver", "Blood is diverted from the gut, which can damage the gut lining and later drive inflammation and organ failure."], ["Lungs", "Shock, inflammation and large transfusions can cause acute lung injury (ARDS or TRALI)."]],
  symptoms: "A fast heart rate is the earliest sign, as the body tries to keep cardiac output up. Pale, cool, clammy skin comes from constricted skin vessels and sympathetic sweating. Fast breathing helps compensate for acidosis. Anxiety and confusion signal reduced brain perfusion. Thirst and low urine output reflect fluid loss and kidney conservation. A narrow pulse pressure appears early, while low blood pressure is a late and ominous sign. Internal bleeding may show as a swollen belly, bruising, or pain in the chest, pelvis or thigh.",
  dx: "Assessment follows a fast primary survey; in trauma, catastrophic bleeding is controlled first (often written as MARCH or C-ABC). ATLS classes of hemorrhage (I to IV) link blood loss to heart rate, blood pressure and mental status. The FAST ultrasound exam looks for blood in the belly and around the heart, and chest and pelvic X-rays look for hidden sources. CT is used in stable patients. Labs include hemoglobin, lactate and base deficit to measure shock, clotting tests and fibrinogen, ionized calcium, and viscoelastic tests (TEG or ROTEM) that show how a clot forms in real time. The shock index (heart rate divided by systolic pressure) helps predict need for massive transfusion.",
  tx: "The goal is to stop the bleeding and replace what was lost. Direct pressure, wound packing, tourniquets for limb bleeding and pelvic binders for pelvic fractures control hemorrhage in the field. Damage control resuscitation uses balanced blood products (red cells, plasma and platelets, or whole blood) through a massive transfusion protocol, while limiting crystalloids such as normal saline, which dilute clotting factors. Permissive hypotension keeps blood pressure lower than normal until the bleeding is controlled, except in head injury. Tranexamic acid blocks plasminogen activation to slow clot breakdown and works best within three hours. Calcium chloride or gluconate replaces calcium bound by citrate. Fibrinogen concentrate or cryoprecipitate replaces fibrinogen. Anticoagulants are reversed: 4F-PCC and vitamin K for warfarin, 4F-PCC for factor Xa inhibitors, idarucizumab for dabigatran and protamine for heparin. Surgery or angioembolization stops the source, and warming prevents hypothermia. Fentanyl or ketamine ease pain, and cefazolin and tetanus shots cover open fractures.",
  drugs: ["tranexamic-acid", "calcium-chloride", "calcium-gluconate", "fibrinogen-concentrate", "prothrombin-complex-concentrate", "phytonadione", "idarucizumab", "protamine", "desmopressin", "fentanyl", "ketamine", "morphine", "normal-saline", "lactated-ringers", "cefazolin", "tdap-vaccine", "tetanus-immune-globulin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Tachycardia is the earliest sign of hemorrhagic shock; hypotension usually appears only after about 30 percent blood volume loss (ATLS class III)."], ["Memory trick", "The lethal triad: cold, acidic and not clotting. Add low calcium to make it a diamond."], ["Field priority", "In major bleeding, stop the bleeding first (tourniquet, packing, pressure), then airway and breathing."], ["Tranexamic acid", "Benefit is greatest when given early; CRASH-2 found giving it after three hours did not help and may harm."]],
  red: "Bleeding that will not stop with firm pressure, spurting blood, a wound in the chest, belly or neck, or signs of shock such as fainting, confusion, or cold, clammy skin need emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Tourniquets were once taught as a last resort, but military data from Iraq and Afghanistan showed that early tourniquet use saved many lives with few lost limbs, changing civilian EMS practice.",
  src: ["American College of Surgeons ATLS Student Course Manual (10th edition)", "CRASH-2 Collaborators. Lancet 2010", "Holcomb JB et al. PROPPR trial. JAMA 2015", "Joint Trauma System Clinical Practice Guidelines: Damage Control Resuscitation", "European guideline on management of major bleeding and coagulopathy following trauma (2023)"]
},
"pancreatic-cancer": {
  name: "Pancreatic cancer",
  sys: "Cancer",
  aka: ["pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma", "PDAC", "cancer of the pancreas"],
  what: "Pancreatic cancer is a cancer that starts in the pancreas, the gland behind the stomach that makes digestive enzymes and insulin. Most cases start in the cells lining the ducts, and it is often found late because early signs are vague.",
  origin: "Doctors in the 1700s and 1800s described hard masses in the pancreas at autopsy, but the organ's deep location made the disease hard to diagnose in life. French physician Armand Trousseau described migrating blood clots linked to hidden cancers in the 1860s, and the sign still bears his name. In 1909 German surgeon Walther Kausch performed the first successful removal of the head of the pancreas and part of the duodenum, and in 1935 American surgeon Allen Whipple and colleagues reported the operation now known as the Whipple procedure. For decades it carried a high death rate until specialized centers made it much safer. Gemcitabine became the standard drug in 1996. In 2011 the FOLFIRINOX combination and in 2013 gemcitabine with nab-paclitaxel improved survival, and in 2019 the POLO trial showed that people with inherited BRCA mutations benefit from PARP inhibitor maintenance.",
  timeline: [["1909", "Walther Kausch performs the first successful pancreaticoduodenectomy (reported in 1912)."], ["1935", "Allen Whipple and colleagues describe the operation that becomes the Whipple procedure."], ["1996", "Gemcitabine is approved in the US for advanced pancreatic cancer."], ["2011", "The FOLFIRINOX regimen improves survival in metastatic disease."], ["2013", "Gemcitabine plus nab-paclitaxel shows a survival benefit in the MPACT trial."], ["2019", "Olaparib is approved as maintenance for germline BRCA-mutated metastatic disease."]],
  cell: "About 90 percent of pancreatic cancers are ductal adenocarcinomas. They grow from precursor lesions called pancreatic intraepithelial neoplasia (PanIN) through a stepwise build-up of mutations. An activating KRAS mutation, found in more than 90 percent of tumors, appears early and keeps the RAS-MAPK growth pathway switched on. Later, loss of the tumor suppressors CDKN2A (p16, a brake on the cell cycle), TP53 and SMAD4 (DPC4, part of TGF-beta signaling) lets cells divide without control and spread. Some people inherit mutations in BRCA1, BRCA2, PALB2 or mismatch repair genes (Lynch syndrome). The tumor builds a dense, scar-like stroma from activated pancreatic stellate cells; this desmoplasia squeezes blood vessels, blocks drug delivery and suppresses immune attack. Tumor cells invade nerves early, which explains pain, and spread to the liver and peritoneum. Smoking, obesity, long-standing diabetes, chronic pancreatitis and family history raise risk.",
  body: [["Bile ducts and liver", "Tumors in the pancreatic head squeeze the common bile duct, causing painless jaundice, dark urine and pale stools. The liver is the most common site of spread."], ["Digestion", "Blocked pancreatic ducts reduce digestive enzymes, causing fatty, greasy stools and weight loss (exocrine insufficiency)."], ["Blood sugar", "Damage to islet cells can cause new-onset diabetes, sometimes before the cancer is found."], ["Nerves", "Invasion of the celiac plexus causes deep pain in the upper belly that bores into the back."], ["Blood clotting", "Tumor cells release procoagulant factors such as tissue factor, raising the risk of deep vein thrombosis and migratory clots (Trousseau sign)."], ["Whole body", "Tumor-driven inflammation causes appetite loss, muscle wasting and weight loss (cachexia)."]],
  symptoms: "Jaundice and itching happen when a tumor in the head of the pancreas blocks the bile duct, so bilirubin backs up into the blood. A painless, enlarged gallbladder with jaundice (Courvoisier sign) points to a slow blockage like a tumor rather than a stone. Upper abdominal pain spreading to the back comes from nerve invasion. Weight loss comes from poor digestion, poor appetite and cachexia. Greasy stools reflect missing enzymes, and new diabetes reflects loss of insulin-making cells.",
  dx: "Diagnosis uses a pancreas-protocol CT scan, which shows the tumor and whether it wraps around major blood vessels. This decides whether the tumor is resectable, borderline or not resectable. Endoscopic ultrasound with fine-needle biopsy confirms the diagnosis. The tumor marker CA 19-9 is often raised and is used to follow treatment, but it is not used alone for screening because it can be high in bile duct blockage and is absent in some people. Liver tests show a cholestatic pattern with jaundice. Guidelines recommend germline genetic testing for everyone with pancreatic cancer and tumor molecular testing in advanced disease.",
  tx: "Surgery is the only potential cure, but only about one in five people can have it at diagnosis. The Whipple procedure removes tumors of the pancreatic head, and distal pancreatectomy removes tumors of the body and tail. Chemotherapy is given before and or after surgery. FOLFIRINOX combines fluorouracil and leucovorin (antimetabolite plus enhancer), irinotecan (topoisomerase I inhibitor) and oxaliplatin (platinum DNA crosslinker). Gemcitabine, a nucleoside analog, is combined with nab-paclitaxel, a microtubule stabilizer. NALIRIFOX uses liposomal irinotecan. Olaparib, a PARP inhibitor, is used as maintenance in germline BRCA-mutated disease. Pembrolizumab is used for rare mismatch repair-deficient tumors, and NTRK inhibitors for rare NTRK fusions. Radiation helps some people. Supportive care is central: bile duct stents for jaundice, pancreatic enzyme replacement, celiac plexus block and opioids for pain, blood thinners for clots, nutrition and early palliative care.",
  drugs: ["gemcitabine", "paclitaxel-protein-bound", "fluorouracil", "leucovorin", "irinotecan", "irinotecan-liposome", "oxaliplatin", "capecitabine", "olaparib", "erlotinib", "pembrolizumab", "larotrectinib", "entrectinib", "pancrelipase", "enoxaparin", "apixaban", "ondansetron", "morphine", "fentanyl"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "KRAS is mutated in more than 90 percent of pancreatic adenocarcinomas; CA 19-9 is the tumor marker."], ["Memory trick", "Courvoisier sign: a palpable, nontender gallbladder with jaundice is unlikely to be from stones (stones scar the gallbladder so it cannot swell)."], ["Trousseau sign of malignancy", "Migratory superficial thrombophlebitis is a classic clue to pancreatic cancer."], ["Biggest risk factor", "Smoking is the leading modifiable risk factor."]],
  red: "New yellowing of the skin or eyes, severe belly pain, vomiting blood or black stools, or a swollen painful leg or sudden breathlessness (possible blood clot) needs prompt or emergency care (call 911 for breathlessness or heavy bleeding).",
  fun: "Armand Trousseau, who described the clotting sign of hidden cancer, later recognized the same sign in himself and correctly predicted his own stomach cancer.",
  src: ["NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma", "American Cancer Society Cancer Facts and Figures", "Conroy T et al. FOLFIRINOX. N Engl J Med 2011", "Von Hoff DD et al. MPACT. N Engl J Med 2013", "Golan T et al. POLO. N Engl J Med 2019", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"ovarian-cancer": {
  name: "Ovarian cancer",
  sys: "Cancer",
  aka: ["epithelial ovarian cancer", "high-grade serous carcinoma", "HGSC", "fallopian tube cancer", "primary peritoneal cancer"],
  what: "Ovarian cancer is a group of cancers that arise in the ovaries, fallopian tubes or the lining of the belly (peritoneum). It often causes only vague symptoms like bloating until it has spread inside the abdomen.",
  origin: "In 1809 Kentucky surgeon Ephraim McDowell removed a huge ovarian tumor from Jane Todd Crawford without anesthesia; she lived for decades, and the operation is remembered as one of the first successful planned abdominal surgeries. Through the 1900s surgeons learned that removing as much tumor as possible (debulking) helped. In 1981 Robert Bast and colleagues developed an antibody that recognized CA-125, and in 1983 they showed the serum CA-125 test tracked ovarian cancer. Cisplatin in the late 1970s and paclitaxel in the 1990s made platinum-taxane chemotherapy the standard. In 1994 the BRCA1 gene was identified, linking many ovarian and breast cancers to inherited DNA repair defects. In the 2000s pathologists found that many high-grade serous cancers actually start in the fallopian tube. PARP inhibitors (first approved in 2014) and antibody-drug conjugates such as mirvetuximab (2022) added new options.",
  timeline: [["1809", "Ephraim McDowell removes an ovarian tumor in one of the first successful abdominal operations."], ["1983", "Robert Bast and colleagues report the CA-125 blood test."], ["1994", "The BRCA1 gene is identified."], ["2000s", "Research shows many high-grade serous cancers begin in the fallopian tube."], ["2014", "Olaparib becomes the first PARP inhibitor approved for ovarian cancer."], ["2022", "Mirvetuximab soravtansine is approved for folate receptor alpha-positive, platinum-resistant disease."]],
  cell: "Most ovarian cancers are epithelial, and the most common and aggressive type is high-grade serous carcinoma. It often begins as serous tubal intraepithelial carcinoma (STIC) in the fimbriae of the fallopian tube, then sheds cells onto the ovary and peritoneum. TP53 is mutated in nearly all high-grade serous cancers. About half have homologous recombination deficiency, often from BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutations; without this double-strand DNA repair pathway, cells become genetically unstable but also very sensitive to platinum drugs and PARP inhibitors (synthetic lethality). Low-grade serous tumors are driven by KRAS or BRAF and grow slowly. Endometrioid and clear cell cancers are linked to endometriosis and ARID1A or PTEN changes, and Lynch syndrome raises risk. Germ cell and sex cord-stromal tumors are separate, rarer groups. Many ovulations over a lifetime may add risk, which is why pregnancy, breastfeeding and birth control pills lower it.",
  body: [["Ovaries and tubes", "Tumors form masses that may be cystic and solid; they can twist or press on nearby organs."], ["Peritoneum", "Cancer cells float through the abdominal fluid and seed the lining of the abdomen and the omentum (omental caking)."], ["Abdomen", "Tumor implants make fluid and block lymph drainage, so ascites builds up and causes swelling and pressure."], ["Bowel", "Implants on the bowel can cause constipation, early fullness and bowel obstruction."], ["Lungs", "Spread to the pleura can cause a pleural effusion and breathlessness."], ["Blood clotting", "Ovarian cancers raise the risk of blood clots, including deep vein thrombosis."]],
  symptoms: "Bloating, a growing belly and feeling full quickly come from ascites and tumor masses pressing on the stomach and bowel. Pelvic or abdominal pain comes from tumor growth and stretching of the peritoneum. Needing to urinate often comes from pressure on the bladder. These symptoms are common in many conditions, but new, persistent symptoms that occur most days for weeks are a warning sign. Weight loss and fatigue come with advanced disease.",
  dx: "Diagnosis starts with a pelvic exam and transvaginal ultrasound, which can show a complex mass with solid parts, septations or ascites. CA-125 is often raised in epithelial cancer, especially after menopause, though it can also rise with endometriosis, fibroids or pregnancy. CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis stages the spread. The diagnosis is confirmed by tissue from surgery or biopsy, and the stage follows the FIGO system. Germline genetic testing for BRCA1, BRCA2 and other genes is recommended for everyone with epithelial ovarian cancer, and tumor testing for homologous recombination deficiency guides therapy. No screening test has been shown to lower deaths in average-risk people.",
  tx: "Treatment combines surgery and chemotherapy. Cytoreductive (debulking) surgery removes as much visible tumor as possible, with the best outcomes when no visible disease remains. Platinum drugs (carboplatin or cisplatin) crosslink DNA, and paclitaxel stabilizes microtubules; together they are the backbone, given before or after surgery. Bevacizumab, an anti-VEGF antibody, slows tumor blood vessel growth. PARP inhibitors (olaparib, niraparib, rucaparib) block DNA single-strand break repair and are used as maintenance, especially in BRCA-mutated or HRD-positive tumors. For platinum-resistant disease, options include liposomal doxorubicin, weekly paclitaxel, topotecan or gemcitabine, and mirvetuximab, which carries a microtubule toxin to folate receptor alpha-positive cells. Hormone therapy such as letrozole is used for low-grade serous cancer. Risk-reducing removal of the tubes and ovaries is offered to people with BRCA mutations.",
  drugs: ["carboplatin", "paclitaxel", "cisplatin", "bevacizumab", "olaparib", "niraparib", "rucaparib", "mirvetuximab-soravtansine", "doxorubicin-liposomal", "topotecan", "gemcitabine", "docetaxel", "letrozole", "tamoxifen", "ondansetron", "aprepitant", "enoxaparin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "BRCA1 and BRCA2 mutations raise ovarian cancer risk, and tumors with these defects respond well to platinum and PARP inhibitors (synthetic lethality)."], ["Tumor marker", "CA-125 follows epithelial ovarian cancer but is not a screening test for average-risk people."], ["Pathology clue", "Psammoma bodies are seen in serous carcinoma; Call-Exner bodies are seen in granulosa cell tumors."], ["Protective factors", "Birth control pills, pregnancy, breastfeeding and tubal ligation all lower risk."]],
  red: "Sudden severe pelvic pain (possible twisting of a mass), vomiting with a swollen belly and no bowel movements (possible bowel blockage), or sudden breathlessness needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Because so many high-grade serous cancers start in the fallopian tube, some guidelines now suggest removing the tubes (opportunistic salpingectomy) during other pelvic surgeries to lower future risk.",
  src: ["NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Ovarian Cancer", "ACOG Committee Opinion on Opportunistic Salpingectomy", "Bast RC Jr et al. N Engl J Med 1983", "Moore K et al. SOLO-1. N Engl J Med 2018", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"bladder-cancer": {
  name: "Bladder cancer",
  sys: "Cancer",
  aka: ["urothelial carcinoma", "transitional cell carcinoma", "NMIBC", "MIBC"],
  what: "Bladder cancer is cancer of the lining of the bladder, the organ that stores urine. The most common first sign is painless blood in the urine, and smoking is the biggest risk factor.",
  origin: "In 1895 German surgeon Ludwig Rehn reported bladder tumors in workers at an aniline dye factory, one of the first links between an occupation, a chemical and cancer. Later work identified aromatic amines such as benzidine and 2-naphthylamine as the culprits, and tobacco smoke, which contains similar chemicals, was found to be the biggest risk factor. In Egypt and other areas, chronic infection with the parasite Schistosoma haematobium was linked to squamous bladder cancer. In 1976 Alvaro Morales and colleagues showed that placing BCG, a weakened tuberculosis vaccine, into the bladder prevented tumors from coming back; it was FDA approved in 1990 and is still the main treatment for high-risk early disease. Cisplatin-based chemotherapy became standard for advanced disease in the 1980s. Immunotherapy and antibody-drug conjugates followed, and in 2023 enfortumab vedotin with pembrolizumab became the new first-line standard for advanced disease.",
  timeline: [["1895", "Ludwig Rehn links bladder tumors to aniline dye work."], ["1976", "Morales and colleagues report intravesical BCG prevents recurrence."], ["1990", "Intravesical BCG is approved by the FDA."], ["2016", "The first PD-L1 checkpoint inhibitor is approved for advanced urothelial cancer."], ["2019", "Erdafitinib becomes the first FGFR inhibitor approved for urothelial cancer."], ["2023", "Enfortumab vedotin with pembrolizumab becomes a first-line standard for advanced disease."]],
  cell: "About 90 percent of bladder cancers in the US are urothelial carcinomas, arising from the urothelium that lines the urinary tract. Carcinogens such as aromatic amines from tobacco are absorbed, activated in the liver, and excreted in urine, so the bladder lining is bathed in them for hours. Two main paths lead to cancer. Low-grade papillary tumors grow like fronds into the bladder space, often carry activating FGFR3 mutations, recur often but rarely invade. Flat carcinoma in situ and high-grade tumors often have TP53 and RB1 loss and tend to invade the muscle wall (muscle-invasive bladder cancer). Because the whole urothelium is exposed (field effect), tumors can appear in many places, including the kidney pelvis and ureters. Many urothelial cancers have a high mutation burden from APOBEC enzymes and tobacco, which helps the immune system see them. Nectin-4 is highly expressed on most urothelial cancer cells, the target of enfortumab vedotin. Schistosomiasis drives squamous cell carcinoma, and a urachal remnant can give rise to adenocarcinoma.",
  body: [["Bladder", "Tumors bleed easily and can irritate the bladder wall, causing frequency and urgency. Invasive tumors grow through the muscle and into fat."], ["Kidneys and ureters", "Tumors near the ureter openings can block urine flow, causing hydronephrosis and kidney damage. The upper urinary tract can grow its own tumors."], ["Lymph nodes", "Invasive cancer spreads first to pelvic lymph nodes."], ["Bones, lungs and liver", "Distant spread causes bone pain, cough or liver problems."], ["Blood", "Ongoing bleeding from the tumor surface can slowly lead to iron deficiency anemia and fatigue."]],
  symptoms: "Painless blood in the urine is the classic sign and happens because tumor surfaces are fragile and bleed. It may come and go, which can falsely reassure. Burning, urgency and frequent urination come from irritation of the bladder wall, especially with carcinoma in situ, and can be mistaken for infection. Flank pain means a ureter is blocked. Pelvic pain, leg swelling, bone pain and weight loss suggest advanced disease.",
  dx: "Unexplained blood in the urine, especially in older adults or smokers, leads to evaluation with cystoscopy, a camera look inside the bladder, and imaging of the upper tract with CT urography. Urine cytology can detect high-grade cancer cells. Transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT) removes the tumor and provides tissue to show the grade and whether it invades the muscle, which is the most important dividing line for treatment. CT or MRI stages invasive disease. Tumor testing for FGFR3 alterations, PD-L1 and HER2 can guide treatment of advanced disease.",
  tx: "Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer is treated with TURBT, often followed by drugs placed into the bladder (intravesical therapy). BCG stimulates a local immune attack. Chemotherapy such as gemcitabine or mitomycin kills tumor cells directly. When BCG fails, options include valrubicin, nadofaragene firadenovec (a gene therapy that makes bladder cells produce interferon), nogapendekin alfa inbakicept (an IL-15 superagonist given with BCG), pembrolizumab, or cystectomy. Muscle-invasive disease is treated with cisplatin-based chemotherapy before radical cystectomy (bladder removal with a new urine pathway), sometimes with durvalumab around surgery, or with bladder-sparing chemoradiation. Advanced disease is treated with enfortumab vedotin (an antibody-drug conjugate targeting Nectin-4) plus pembrolizumab, or platinum chemotherapy followed by avelumab maintenance. Erdafitinib blocks FGFR in tumors with FGFR3 changes. Quitting smoking lowers the chance of new tumors.",
  drugs: ["bcg-live-intravesical", "mitomycin", "gemcitabine", "valrubicin", "nadofaragene-firadenovec", "nogapendekin-alfa-inbakicept", "cisplatin", "carboplatin", "enfortumab-vedotin", "pembrolizumab", "nivolumab", "avelumab", "durvalumab", "erdafitinib", "methotrexate", "vinblastine", "doxorubicin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Painless hematuria in an older smoker is bladder cancer until proven otherwise."], ["Risk factors", "Smoking, aromatic amines (dye, rubber and leather work), cyclophosphamide, and Schistosoma haematobium (squamous cell type)."], ["Key dividing line", "Whether the tumor invades the detrusor muscle decides treatment: bladder-sparing care for non-muscle-invasive disease, cystectomy or chemoradiation for muscle-invasive disease."], ["Memory trick", "BCG is a tuberculosis vaccine turned bladder cancer fighter: it tricks the bladder's immune system into attacking tumor cells."]],
  red: "Passing large clots, being unable to urinate, or heavy bleeding in the urine needs emergency care (call 911 for heavy bleeding with dizziness or fainting).",
  fun: "Bladder cancer is about four times more common in men than in women in the US, partly reflecting past differences in smoking and workplace exposures.",
  src: ["AUA/SUO Guideline on Non-Muscle Invasive Bladder Cancer (2024)", "NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Bladder Cancer", "Morales A et al. J Urol 1976", "Powles T et al. EV-302. N Engl J Med 2024", "American Cancer Society Cancer Facts and Figures"]
},
"kidney-cancer": {
  name: "Kidney cancer",
  sys: "Cancer",
  aka: ["renal cell carcinoma", "RCC", "clear cell renal cell carcinoma", "hypernephroma (old term)"],
  what: "Kidney cancer most often means renal cell carcinoma, a cancer that starts in the tubules of the kidney. Many are found by chance on scans done for other reasons, and small tumors are often cured with surgery.",
  origin: "In 1883 German pathologist Paul Grawitz suggested that kidney tumors came from leftover adrenal tissue, which led to the old name hypernephroma; later work showed they actually come from kidney tubule cells. The disease was long known to resist chemotherapy and radiation. Researchers studying families with von Hippel-Lindau disease, an inherited syndrome of kidney cancers and blood vessel tumors, identified the VHL gene in 1993 and found it is also lost in most sporadic clear cell kidney cancers. That work revealed the cell's oxygen-sensing system and earned the 2019 Nobel Prize for William Kaelin, Peter Ratcliffe and Gregg Semenza. High-dose interleukin-2 was approved in 1992, giving lasting remissions in a few people. Drugs targeting VEGF, starting with sorafenib (2005) and sunitinib (2006), then checkpoint inhibitors (2015 onward) and the HIF-2 alpha blocker belzutifan (2021) transformed treatment.",
  timeline: [["1883", "Paul Grawitz describes kidney tumors he believed came from adrenal rests."], ["1992", "High-dose interleukin-2 (aldesleukin) is approved for metastatic kidney cancer."], ["1993", "The VHL tumor suppressor gene is identified."], ["2005", "Sorafenib, the first VEGF-targeted kinase inhibitor for kidney cancer, is approved."], ["2019", "Kaelin, Ratcliffe and Semenza share the Nobel Prize for discovering how cells sense oxygen."], ["2021", "Belzutifan, a HIF-2 alpha inhibitor, is approved for VHL-associated tumors."]],
  cell: "About 75 percent of renal cell carcinomas are the clear cell type, arising from proximal tubule cells. The key event is loss of both copies of the VHL tumor suppressor on chromosome 3p, through deletion, mutation or silencing. VHL protein normally tags hypoxia-inducible factors (HIF-1 alpha and especially HIF-2 alpha) for destruction when oxygen is present. Without VHL, HIF builds up as if the cell were starved of oxygen. HIF switches on genes for VEGF and PDGF (new blood vessels), GLUT1 (glucose uptake), TGF-alpha and cyclin D1 (growth) and erythropoietin. This makes clear cell tumors full of blood vessels and explains why VEGF and HIF-2 alpha blockers work. Cells fill with lipid and glycogen, giving the clear look. Other common changes include PBRM1, SETD2 and BAP1 mutations. Papillary RCC is linked to MET (type 1) or fumarate hydratase defects, and chromophobe RCC to large chromosome losses. Smoking, obesity, high blood pressure and long-term dialysis raise risk.",
  body: [["Kidney", "The tumor grows as a mass that can bleed into the urine. Removing part or all of the kidney lowers kidney reserve."], ["Veins", "Clear cell tumors tend to grow into the renal vein and can extend up the inferior vena cava, sometimes reaching the heart."], ["Blood", "Some tumors make erythropoietin, causing a high red cell count (polycythemia); others cause anemia."], ["Bones and calcium", "Tumors can make PTH-related protein or spread to bone, raising blood calcium and causing bone pain or fractures."], ["Lungs", "The lungs are the most common site of distant spread, via the blood."], ["Testicular veins", "In men, a left-sided tumor can block the left gonadal vein where it drains into the left renal vein, causing a left varicocele that does not empty when lying down."]],
  symptoms: "Many people have no symptoms, and the tumor is found by imaging. When symptoms occur, blood in the urine comes from tumor invading the collecting system, flank pain from stretching of the kidney capsule, and a mass from tumor size; all three together (the classic triad) are uncommon. Fever, weight loss and fatigue come from cytokines the tumor releases. Paraneoplastic effects include polycythemia from erythropoietin, high calcium from PTHrP, high blood pressure from renin, and abnormal liver tests without liver spread (Stauffer syndrome).",
  dx: "Diagnosis is usually made by contrast CT or MRI, which shows an enhancing solid kidney mass. Many solid enhancing masses are presumed cancer and treated without a biopsy, though biopsy helps when the result would change management. Simple cysts are classified using the Bosniak system. Staging includes chest imaging and blood tests for blood counts, calcium and kidney function. Risk scores such as IMDC guide treatment of metastatic disease. Genetic counseling is offered for young patients, multiple tumors or a family history.",
  tx: "Small localized tumors are treated with partial nephrectomy, which saves kidney function, or radical nephrectomy for larger tumors. Very small tumors in older or frail people may be watched with scans (active surveillance) or treated with ablation. After surgery, pembrolizumab is given to some high-risk patients to lower the chance of return. Advanced clear cell RCC is usually treated with an immune checkpoint inhibitor (pembrolizumab or nivolumab, which block PD-1) combined with a VEGF receptor tyrosine kinase inhibitor (axitinib, cabozantinib or lenvatinib), or with nivolumab plus ipilimumab (CTLA-4 blocker). Later options include other kinase inhibitors such as sunitinib, pazopanib and tivozanib, mTOR inhibitors such as everolimus, and belzutifan, which blocks HIF-2 alpha directly. High-dose interleukin-2 is used rarely at specialized centers. Kidney cancer responds poorly to traditional chemotherapy.",
  drugs: ["pembrolizumab", "nivolumab", "ipilimumab", "axitinib", "cabozantinib", "lenvatinib", "belzutifan", "sunitinib", "pazopanib", "tivozanib", "everolimus", "sorafenib", "temsirolimus", "avelumab", "bevacizumab", "aldesleukin", "zoledronic-acid", "denosumab"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Clear cell RCC = VHL loss on chromosome 3p, leading to HIF buildup and VEGF overproduction."], ["Memory trick", "VHL is the cell's off switch for HIF. Lose the switch and the cell thinks it is suffocating, so it builds blood vessels."], ["Paraneoplastic syndromes", "RCC can make EPO (polycythemia), PTHrP (hypercalcemia), renin (hypertension) and ACTH (Cushing syndrome)."], ["Often confused with", "Angiomyolipoma, a benign kidney tumor containing fat, linked to tuberous sclerosis."]],
  red: "Heavy blood in the urine with dizziness, sudden severe flank pain, or sudden breathlessness or leg swelling (possible clot or tumor in the vein) needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The study of rare families with von Hippel-Lindau disease revealed how every cell in the body senses oxygen, a discovery honored with a Nobel Prize in 2019.",
  src: ["NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Kidney Cancer", "AUA Guideline on Renal Mass and Localized Renal Cancer (2021)", "Latif F et al. Identification of the von Hippel-Lindau disease tumor suppressor gene. Science 1993", "Choueiri TK et al. KEYNOTE-564. N Engl J Med 2021", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"liver-cancer": {
  name: "Liver cancer",
  sys: "Cancer",
  aka: ["hepatocellular carcinoma", "HCC", "hepatoma", "primary liver cancer"],
  what: "Primary liver cancer most often means hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC), a cancer of the main liver cells. It usually grows in a liver already scarred by cirrhosis, hepatitis B or C, alcohol or fatty liver disease.",
  origin: "Liver cancer has long been common in parts of Asia and Africa, but its causes were unclear until the 1900s. In 1963 Garri Abelev found that liver tumors in mice made alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), a fetal protein, and in 1964 Yuri Tatarinov found it in the blood of people with liver cancer, creating one of the first tumor markers. In 1965 Baruch Blumberg discovered the Australia antigen, later shown to be the surface protein of hepatitis B virus, work that earned a Nobel Prize in 1976 and led to a vaccine. Researchers then linked HCC to hepatitis B and C infection and to aflatoxin, a mold toxin on stored grains and nuts. Taiwan began vaccinating all newborns against hepatitis B in 1984, and childhood liver cancer fell, making it the first vaccine shown to prevent a human cancer. Sorafenib (2007) was the first drug to extend survival in advanced HCC, and combinations of immunotherapy, such as atezolizumab with bevacizumab (2020), became the new standard.",
  timeline: [["1964", "Yuri Tatarinov detects alpha-fetoprotein in the blood of people with liver cancer."], ["1965", "Baruch Blumberg discovers the Australia antigen of hepatitis B virus."], ["1984", "Taiwan begins universal newborn hepatitis B vaccination, later shown to cut childhood liver cancer."], ["1996", "The Milan criteria define who benefits most from liver transplant for HCC."], ["2007", "Sorafenib becomes the first drug proven to extend survival in advanced HCC."], ["2020", "Atezolizumab plus bevacizumab is approved as first-line treatment."]],
  cell: "HCC usually develops after years of liver injury, cell death and regeneration. Chronic inflammation from hepatitis B or C, alcohol or metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) keeps hepatocytes dividing in a scarred, oxidative environment, so DNA errors pile up. Cirrhosis is present in most cases. Hepatitis B can cause HCC even without cirrhosis because its DNA integrates into the host genome and the HBx protein disrupts cell control. Aflatoxin B1 is activated by liver enzymes and forms DNA adducts that cause a specific TP53 mutation (codon 249). Common changes in HCC include TERT promoter mutations (letting cells divide endlessly), activating CTNNB1 (beta-catenin) mutations and TP53 loss. Dysplastic nodules progress to cancer. HCC tumors draw their blood supply almost entirely from the hepatic artery, which creates the arterial-phase brightness on imaging and is the basis for embolization treatment. Tumors often invade the portal vein. Cholangiocarcinoma, a separate cancer of the bile ducts, is a less common type of primary liver cancer.",
  body: [["Liver", "The tumor replaces working liver tissue and adds to cirrhosis, worsening jaundice, fluid build-up and clotting problems."], ["Portal vein", "Tumor can grow into the portal vein, raising portal pressure and causing variceal bleeding and ascites."], ["Blood", "Some tumors make erythropoietin (polycythemia) or use up glucose (low blood sugar)."], ["Abdomen", "Large tumors can rupture and bleed into the belly, causing sudden pain and shock."], ["Lungs and bones", "Distant spread most often goes to the lungs and bones."], ["Brain", "Declining liver function lets toxins such as ammonia build up, causing hepatic encephalopathy and confusion."]],
  symptoms: "Early HCC usually causes no symptoms, which is why people with cirrhosis are screened. As it grows, right upper belly pain comes from stretching of the liver capsule, and weight loss and poor appetite come from tumor-driven inflammation. A sudden worsening of cirrhosis, with new ascites, jaundice or confusion, can be the first sign because the tumor destroys remaining liver tissue or blocks the portal vein. Sudden severe pain and lightheadedness can mean tumor rupture with bleeding.",
  dx: "AASLD guidance recommends ultrasound, with or without AFP, about every six months for people with cirrhosis and some people with chronic hepatitis B. A suspicious nodule is studied with multiphase CT or MRI. Unlike most cancers, HCC can be diagnosed without a biopsy in a person with cirrhosis when a nodule shows arterial-phase hyperenhancement and washout on later phases; the LI-RADS system classifies these findings. AFP is often raised but can be normal. The Barcelona Clinic Liver Cancer (BCLC) system links tumor burden, liver function (Child-Pugh) and performance status to treatment choices.",
  tx: "Treatment depends on the tumor and how well the liver works. Curative options include surgical resection for people with good liver function, thermal ablation for small tumors, and liver transplant for those within criteria such as the Milan criteria, which removes both cancer and cirrhosis. Intermediate disease is treated with transarterial chemoembolization or radioembolization, which deliver treatment through the hepatic artery that feeds the tumor. Advanced HCC is treated first with immunotherapy combinations: atezolizumab (anti-PD-L1) with bevacizumab (anti-VEGF), durvalumab with tremelimumab (anti-CTLA-4), or nivolumab with ipilimumab. Kinase inhibitors that block VEGF receptors and other growth signals, such as sorafenib, lenvatinib, regorafenib and cabozantinib, and the VEGFR2 antibody ramucirumab for people with high AFP, are other options. Treating the cause matters: antivirals such as entecavir or tenofovir for hepatitis B, curing hepatitis C, and stopping alcohol. Hepatitis B vaccination prevents many cases.",
  drugs: ["atezolizumab", "bevacizumab", "durvalumab", "tremelimumab", "nivolumab", "ipilimumab", "sorafenib", "lenvatinib", "regorafenib", "cabozantinib", "ramucirumab", "pembrolizumab", "entecavir", "tenofovir", "hepatitis-b-vaccine", "hepatitis-b-immune-globulin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is the classic tumor marker for HCC; cirrhosis is the main risk factor."], ["Imaging clue", "Arterial-phase hyperenhancement with washout in a cirrhotic liver can confirm HCC without a biopsy."], ["Memory trick", "Hepatitis B can cause HCC without cirrhosis because its DNA integrates into liver cell DNA."], ["Toxin link", "Aflatoxin from Aspergillus mold on stored grains and peanuts causes a specific TP53 mutation."]],
  red: "Sudden severe belly pain with lightheadedness, vomiting blood or black stools, new confusion, or rapidly yellowing skin in someone with liver disease needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The hepatitis B vaccine is often called the first anticancer vaccine, because preventing the infection prevents many liver cancers.",
  src: ["AASLD Practice Guidance on Prevention, Diagnosis and Treatment of Hepatocellular Carcinoma (2023)", "NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Hepatobiliary Cancers", "Chang MH et al. N Engl J Med 1997", "Finn RS et al. IMbrave150. N Engl J Med 2020", "Llovet JM et al. SHARP. N Engl J Med 2008"]
},
"thyroid-cancer": {
  name: "Thyroid cancer",
  sys: "Cancer",
  aka: ["papillary thyroid carcinoma", "PTC", "follicular thyroid carcinoma", "medullary thyroid carcinoma", "MTC", "anaplastic thyroid carcinoma"],
  what: "Thyroid cancer is cancer of the thyroid gland in the front of the neck. Most types grow slowly and have excellent cure rates, but a few rare types, like anaplastic thyroid cancer, are very aggressive.",
  origin: "Surgery on the thyroid was once extremely dangerous because of bleeding. In the late 1800s Swiss surgeon Theodor Kocher made thyroidectomy safe and studied what happened when the gland was removed, earning the 1909 Nobel Prize. In 1941 Saul Hertz in Boston first treated an overactive thyroid with radioactive iodine, and in 1946 Samuel Seidlin and colleagues reported shrinking spread thyroid cancer with radioiodine. In 1959 medullary thyroid cancer was described as its own type, later linked to calcitonin-making C cells and, in the 1990s, to RET gene mutations in families with MEN2. After the 1986 Chernobyl nuclear disaster, thyroid cancer in exposed children rose sharply, confirming radiation as a cause. Widespread ultrasound screening, especially in South Korea, later showed that many small cancers were being found that would never have caused harm. Targeted drugs for advanced disease, including RET and BRAF inhibitors, arrived in the 2010s.",
  timeline: [["1909", "Theodor Kocher receives the Nobel Prize for his work on the thyroid."], ["1941", "Saul Hertz first uses radioactive iodine to treat thyroid disease."], ["1946", "Seidlin and colleagues report radioiodine treatment of metastatic thyroid cancer."], ["1986", "The Chernobyl disaster leads to a surge in childhood thyroid cancer."], ["2018", "Dabrafenib plus trametinib is approved for BRAF V600E anaplastic thyroid cancer."], ["2020", "Selpercatinib is approved for RET-altered thyroid cancers."]],
  cell: "Differentiated thyroid cancers come from follicular cells that make thyroid hormone. Papillary carcinoma, about 80 percent of cases, is driven by mutations that turn on the MAPK pathway, most often BRAF V600E or RET/PTC gene rearrangements; childhood radiation exposure strongly favors RET/PTC. Its cells have clear, empty-looking nuclei (Orphan Annie eye nuclei), nuclear grooves and psammoma bodies, and it spreads through lymphatics to neck nodes. Follicular carcinoma is driven by RAS mutations or PAX8-PPARG fusions, spreads through the blood to bone and lung, and is diagnosed by capsular or vascular invasion. Because differentiated cells keep the sodium-iodide symporter and respond to TSH, they take up radioiodine and grow under TSH. Medullary carcinoma comes from parafollicular C cells, makes calcitonin and CEA, shows amyloid, and is caused by RET activation, inherited in MEN2A, MEN2B and familial MTC. Anaplastic carcinoma arises when differentiated tumors gain TP53 and other mutations and lose iodine uptake.",
  body: [["Thyroid gland", "A nodule or mass forms; most nodules are benign, but cancer may feel hard and fixed."], ["Neck lymph nodes", "Papillary cancer often spreads to neck nodes, sometimes as the first sign."], ["Voice and swallowing", "A growing tumor can invade the recurrent laryngeal nerve or press on the esophagus and trachea, causing hoarseness, trouble swallowing or breathing."], ["Bones and lungs", "Follicular cancer spreads through the blood to bone and lung."], ["Gut", "In medullary cancer, high calcitonin and other hormones can cause diarrhea."], ["Parathyroid glands", "Surgery can damage the nearby parathyroid glands, causing low calcium afterward."]],
  symptoms: "Most thyroid cancers cause a painless lump in the neck, often found on exam or imaging done for another reason. Thyroid hormone levels are usually normal, so there are no hormone symptoms. Hoarseness means the tumor may be affecting the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Trouble swallowing or breathing comes from pressure on the esophagus or trachea, and a rapidly growing, hard mass in an older adult suggests anaplastic cancer. Flushing and diarrhea can occur with advanced medullary cancer because of hormones it releases.",
  dx: "Evaluation of a thyroid nodule starts with a TSH test and neck ultrasound. Ultrasound features such as solid texture, darkness (hypoechoic), irregular margins, microcalcifications and being taller than wide raise concern, and systems such as ACR TI-RADS decide which nodules need a biopsy. Fine-needle aspiration is read using the Bethesda system. Molecular testing can help with indeterminate results. Follicular carcinoma cannot be diagnosed on needle biopsy alone, because it requires seeing invasion of the capsule or vessels in the removed tissue. Medullary cancer is suggested by a high calcitonin level, and RET germline testing is done for all patients with medullary cancer.",
  tx: "Most differentiated thyroid cancers are treated with surgery, either lobectomy (removing one side) or total thyroidectomy. Very small, low-risk papillary cancers may be watched with active surveillance. After total thyroidectomy, radioactive iodine (I-131) is used for some patients; thyroid cells take it up through the sodium-iodide symporter and are destroyed by its radiation. Thyrotropin alfa (recombinant TSH) stimulates uptake without stopping hormone pills. Levothyroxine replaces thyroid hormone, and in higher-risk cases keeps TSH low to limit TSH-driven growth. Radioiodine-refractory disease is treated with kinase inhibitors such as lenvatinib and sorafenib (VEGFR and other kinases). Medullary cancer is treated with surgery and, when advanced, RET inhibitors such as selpercatinib, or vandetanib and cabozantinib. Anaplastic cancer with BRAF V600E is treated with dabrafenib plus trametinib. Calcium and calcitriol treat low calcium after surgery. Family members of people with hereditary MTC may have preventive thyroidectomy.",
  drugs: ["levothyroxine", "sodium-iodide-i-131", "thyrotropin-alfa", "lenvatinib", "sorafenib", "cabozantinib", "vandetanib", "selpercatinib", "pralsetinib", "dabrafenib", "trametinib", "larotrectinib", "entrectinib", "calcium-carbonate", "calcitriol", "calcium-gluconate", "liothyronine"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Papillary thyroid cancer: most common type, Orphan Annie eye nuclei and psammoma bodies, spreads to lymph nodes, linked to radiation exposure."], ["Memory trick", "Papillary goes to the Pipes (lymphatics); Follicular goes Far through the blood."], ["Medullary clue", "Medullary cancer makes calcitonin and is linked to RET mutations in MEN2A and MEN2B, so pheochromocytoma must be ruled out before surgery."], ["Often confused with", "Benign follicular adenoma: it looks the same on needle biopsy; only invasion in the removed tissue proves carcinoma."]],
  red: "Rapidly growing neck swelling, trouble breathing or swallowing, or noisy breathing needs emergency care (call 911). After thyroid surgery, neck swelling with breathing trouble or tingling and muscle cramps from low calcium also need urgent care.",
  fun: "After South Korea began widespread thyroid ultrasound screening in the late 1990s, thyroid cancer diagnoses rose about fifteen-fold while deaths stayed flat, a famous example of overdiagnosis.",
  src: ["American Thyroid Association Management Guidelines for Adult Patients with Thyroid Nodules and Differentiated Thyroid Cancer (2015)", "ATA Guidelines for Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma (2015)", "NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Thyroid Carcinoma", "Ahn HS et al. Korea's thyroid-cancer epidemic. N Engl J Med 2014", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"brain-tumors": {
  name: "Brain tumors (glioblastoma)",
  sys: "Cancer",
  aka: ["glioblastoma", "GBM", "glioblastoma multiforme", "glioma", "astrocytoma"],
  what: "Brain tumors are abnormal growths in or around the brain. Glioblastoma is the most common malignant primary brain tumor in adults; it grows fast, spreads into nearby brain tissue and is very hard to cure.",
  origin: "In the 1860s German pathologist Rudolf Virchow described gliomas as tumors of the brain's support cells (glia). In 1926 neurosurgeon Harvey Cushing and pathologist Percival Bailey published a classification of gliomas based on the cell types they resembled, and the name spongioblastoma multiforme, later glioblastoma multiforme, described the most aggressive type with its varied appearance. For decades surgery and radiation gave average survival of about a year. In 2005 Roger Stupp and colleagues showed that adding the oral drug temozolomide to radiation improved survival, and Monika Hegi's team showed that tumors with a silenced MGMT gene benefited most. Bevacizumab was approved for recurrent glioblastoma in 2009, and tumor-treating fields, a device that delivers electric fields through the scalp, were approved later. In 2021 the World Health Organization redefined glioblastoma using molecular markers, limiting it to IDH-wildtype tumors.",
  timeline: [["1860s", "Rudolf Virchow describes gliomas as tumors of glial cells."], ["1926", "Bailey and Cushing publish a classification of gliomas."], ["1996", "Carmustine wafers placed in the tumor cavity are approved."], ["2005", "The Stupp trial establishes radiation plus temozolomide as standard care."], ["2009", "Bevacizumab is approved for recurrent glioblastoma."], ["2021", "The WHO classification defines glioblastoma as an IDH-wildtype, grade 4 astrocytic tumor."]],
  cell: "Glioblastoma is a grade 4 diffuse astrocytic tumor that is IDH-wildtype. It likely arises from neural stem or glial progenitor cells, often near the lateral ventricles. Typical changes include TERT promoter mutation (endless cell division), EGFR amplification or the EGFRvIII variant (constant growth signaling), gain of chromosome 7 with loss of chromosome 10, PTEN loss (unchecked PI3K-AKT signaling), CDKN2A/B deletion (loss of cell cycle brakes) and TP53 or RB pathway defects. Fast growth outstrips the blood supply, so the tumor center dies (necrosis) and tumor cells crowd around it (pseudopalisading). Hypoxic cells release VEGF, which drives abnormal, leaky new vessels (microvascular proliferation) and surrounding vasogenic edema. Tumor cells migrate along white matter tracts, even across the corpus callosum (butterfly glioma), so they cannot all be removed. Silencing of the DNA repair gene MGMT by promoter methylation makes cells more sensitive to temozolomide. Tumors with IDH mutations are now classified separately as astrocytoma or oligodendroglioma.",
  body: [["Brain tissue", "The tumor destroys and pushes on nearby brain, causing weakness, speech or vision problems depending on its location."], ["Pressure inside the skull", "The tumor mass and swelling raise intracranial pressure, causing headache, vomiting and, if severe, brain herniation."], ["Electrical activity", "Irritated cortex near the tumor causes seizures, which are often the first sign."], ["Thinking and personality", "Frontal and temporal tumors can change mood, memory, behavior and judgment."], ["Blood clotting", "Glioblastoma raises the risk of deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism."], ["Eyes", "Raised pressure can cause swelling of the optic disc (papilledema) and blurred vision."]],
  symptoms: "Headache, often worse in the morning or when lying down, comes from rising pressure inside the skull and swelling. Nausea and vomiting come from pressure on the brainstem. Seizures happen because the tumor irritates nearby neurons. Weakness, numbness, trouble speaking or vision loss reflect damage to the specific brain area involved. Personality change, confusion and memory loss are common with frontal or temporal tumors. Symptoms often worsen over weeks, which separates them from a sudden stroke.",
  dx: "MRI with contrast is the main imaging test. Glioblastoma typically appears as a ring-enhancing mass with a dark necrotic center, surrounded by swelling, sometimes crossing the corpus callosum. The diagnosis is confirmed by tissue from surgery or biopsy. The 2021 WHO classification combines microscopy (necrosis and microvascular proliferation) with molecular tests: IDH status, plus TERT promoter mutation, EGFR amplification or chromosome 7 gain and 10 loss, which can establish glioblastoma even without the classic microscopic features. MGMT promoter methylation is tested because it predicts response to temozolomide. Brain metastases from other cancers and lymphoma are important look-alikes.",
  tx: "Treatment begins with maximal safe surgical removal, which reduces mass effect and provides tissue. Radiation follows, given with temozolomide, an oral alkylating drug that adds methyl groups to DNA and triggers cell death; it continues as maintenance afterward. Tumor-treating fields, worn as scalp electrodes, disrupt cell division. At recurrence, options include more surgery, lomustine or carmustine (nitrosoureas that cross the blood-brain barrier), bevacizumab (blocks VEGF, reducing swelling and leaky vessels), re-irradiation and clinical trials. Rare BRAF V600E tumors may respond to dabrafenib plus trametinib. Supportive care is essential: dexamethasone reduces vasogenic edema, levetiracetam controls seizures, antinausea drugs ease treatment, and early palliative care helps with quality of life. Vorasidenib is used for lower-grade IDH-mutant gliomas, not glioblastoma.",
  drugs: ["temozolomide", "lomustine", "carmustine", "bevacizumab", "dexamethasone", "levetiracetam", "ondansetron", "dabrafenib", "trametinib", "vorasidenib", "enoxaparin"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "Glioblastoma: ring-enhancing mass that can cross the corpus callosum (butterfly glioma), with pseudopalisading necrosis on pathology; GFAP positive."], ["Memory trick", "MGMT methylated = Medication Gets More Traction. Silenced MGMT means temozolomide works better."], ["Most common brain tumors", "In adults, metastases are the most common brain tumors overall, meningioma is the most common primary tumor, and glioblastoma is the most common malignant primary tumor."], ["Steroids", "Dexamethasone reduces vasogenic edema around tumors but does not help cytotoxic edema from stroke."]],
  red: "A first seizure, sudden severe headache, sudden weakness or trouble speaking, or drowsiness with vomiting needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "The word multiforme in the old name glioblastoma multiforme refers to how different one part of the tumor can look from another under the microscope, with dead areas, bleeding and many cell shapes.",
  src: ["WHO Classification of Tumours of the Central Nervous System, 5th edition (2021)", "NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Central Nervous System Cancers", "Stupp R et al. N Engl J Med 2005", "Hegi ME et al. N Engl J Med 2005", "Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease"]
},
"tumor-lysis": {
  name: "Tumor lysis syndrome",
  sys: "Cancer",
  aka: ["TLS", "laboratory tumor lysis syndrome", "clinical tumor lysis syndrome"],
  what: "Tumor lysis syndrome happens when large numbers of cancer cells break open at once, usually right after treatment starts, and spill their contents into the blood. The flood of potassium, phosphate and uric acid can damage the kidneys and cause dangerous heart rhythms.",
  origin: "Doctors noticed in the mid-1900s that some people with leukemia and lymphoma developed sudden kidney failure soon after starting chemotherapy, with crystals of uric acid clogging the kidneys. In the 1950s and 1960s, Gertrude Elion and George Hitchings designed allopurinol, a xanthine oxidase inhibitor, which was approved in 1966 and soon used to prevent uric acid buildup during cancer treatment; their work earned the 1988 Nobel Prize. As treatments for fast-growing cancers like Burkitt lymphoma became more powerful, the full picture of high potassium, high phosphate, low calcium and kidney injury was described as tumor lysis syndrome. In 2002 rasburicase, a lab-made enzyme that destroys uric acid, was approved for children, and later for adults. In 2004 Mitchell Cairo and Mitchell Bishop published widely used criteria for laboratory and clinical TLS. Newer targeted drugs such as venetoclax brought careful dose ramp-ups and monitoring to prevent it.",
  timeline: [["1966", "Allopurinol is approved, later used to prevent uric acid kidney injury during chemotherapy."], ["1988", "Gertrude Elion and George Hitchings share the Nobel Prize, partly for allopurinol."], ["2002", "Rasburicase is approved in the US for children receiving cancer therapy."], ["2004", "Cairo and Bishop publish criteria for laboratory and clinical tumor lysis syndrome."]],
  cell: "Cancer cells that die quickly in large numbers release potassium, phosphate and nucleic acids. Purine bases from DNA and RNA (adenine and guanine) are broken down to hypoxanthine and xanthine, then by xanthine oxidase to uric acid. Humans lack the enzyme uricase, so uric acid builds up. In the acidic environment of the distal tubules and collecting ducts, uric acid crystallizes and blocks the tubules. Phosphate binds calcium and precipitates as calcium phosphate crystals in the kidneys and other tissues, which lowers blood calcium (secondary hypocalcemia) and adds to kidney injury. Inflammation, volume depletion and cytokines from dying cells further reduce kidney blood flow. As the kidneys fail, they cannot excrete potassium, phosphate and uric acid, so levels climb further in a vicious cycle. High potassium depolarizes heart muscle cells, risking fatal arrhythmias, and low calcium causes neuromuscular irritability and seizures. Risk is highest with large, fast-growing, treatment-sensitive cancers such as Burkitt lymphoma and acute leukemia with very high white counts, especially with existing kidney problems or dehydration.",
  body: [["Kidneys", "Uric acid and calcium phosphate crystals block tubules, causing acute kidney injury, low urine output and sometimes the need for dialysis."], ["Heart", "High potassium causes peaked T waves, widened QRS and dangerous arrhythmias; low calcium prolongs the QT interval."], ["Nerves and muscles", "Low calcium causes tingling, muscle cramps, spasms (tetany) and seizures."], ["Brain", "Severe electrolyte shifts and kidney failure can cause confusion and seizures."], ["Fluid balance", "Kidney failure leads to fluid overload, swelling and breathlessness."]],
  symptoms: "Many early cases show up only on blood tests. Nausea, vomiting, fatigue and less urine come from kidney injury and rising waste products. Palpitations, weakness or fainting come from high potassium affecting the heart. Muscle cramps, tingling around the mouth, spasms and seizures come from low calcium. Swelling and breathlessness come from fluid the failing kidneys cannot remove. Symptoms usually start within hours to days after cancer treatment begins.",
  dx: "The Cairo-Bishop criteria define laboratory TLS as two or more abnormal values among uric acid, potassium and phosphate (high) and calcium (low), occurring from a few days before to about a week after starting therapy. Clinical TLS adds acute kidney injury, a cardiac arrhythmia, a seizure or death. Monitoring includes frequent electrolytes, uric acid, creatinine, LDH and urine output, and an ECG for potassium effects. Samples for uric acid in patients receiving rasburicase are kept on ice, because the drug keeps breaking down uric acid in the tube.",
  tx: "Prevention is the main strategy. Patients at risk get aggressive IV fluids such as normal saline to increase urine flow and wash out uric acid and phosphate. Allopurinol, a xanthine oxidase inhibitor, blocks new uric acid production and is used for intermediate risk; febuxostat is an alternative. Rasburicase, a recombinant urate oxidase, converts existing uric acid into soluble allantoin and is used for high risk or established TLS; it is avoided in G6PD deficiency because its hydrogen peroxide by-product causes hemolysis and methemoglobinemia. High potassium is managed with calcium to stabilize the heart, regular insulin with dextrose and albuterol to shift potassium into cells, and potassium binders or loop diuretics to remove it. Phosphate binders such as sevelamer lower phosphate. Calcium is given only for symptomatic low calcium, because extra calcium can worsen calcium phosphate crystal formation. Urine alkalinization is no longer routine. Dialysis is used for severe kidney failure or electrolyte problems that do not respond.",
  drugs: ["rasburicase", "allopurinol", "febuxostat", "normal-saline", "calcium-gluconate", "calcium-chloride", "regular-insulin", "dextrose", "albuterol", "sodium-zirconium-cyclosilicate", "patiromer", "sodium-polystyrene-sulfonate", "furosemide", "sevelamer", "sodium-bicarbonate"],
  mem: [["Most-tested fact", "TLS: high potassium, high phosphate, high uric acid and low calcium after starting treatment for a fast-growing cancer such as Burkitt lymphoma or ALL."], ["Memory trick", "Three go up, one goes down: K, phos and uric acid rise; calcium falls."], ["Rasburicase warning", "Check for G6PD deficiency before rasburicase, because it can cause severe hemolysis and methemoglobinemia."], ["Allopurinol vs rasburicase", "Allopurinol stops new uric acid from forming; rasburicase destroys uric acid that is already there."]],
  red: "During or just after cancer treatment, a racing or irregular heartbeat, fainting, seizures, muscle spasms, little or no urine, or severe weakness needs emergency care (call 911).",
  fun: "Humans and other great apes lost the uricase gene millions of years ago, so rasburicase is borrowed from a mold, Aspergillus flavus, that still has it.",
  src: ["Cairo MS, Bishop M. Tumour lysis syndrome: new therapeutic strategies and classification. Br J Haematol 2004", "Howard SC, Jones DP, Pui CH. The tumor lysis syndrome. N Engl J Med 2011", "NCCN Guidelines and Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine: Oncologic Emergencies", "British Society for Haematology guideline on TLS (2015)"]
}
});

